message.content:
{
    "short_answer": "American Psychiatric Association",
    "long_answer": "After MindFreedom International carried out a 21-day hunger strike in 2003 challenging claims about a simple “chemical imbalance” cause of mental disorders, the American Psychiatric Association (APA) released an official position statement and letter answering the group’s questions. The APA’s document clarified that while biological factors are involved in mental illness, the chemical-imbalance slogan is an oversimplification, thus directly responding to the hunger strikers’ demands."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystinosis",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome of inadequate proximal-tubular reabsorption is Fanconi syndrome. In children the commonest aetiology is the inherited lysosomal storage disorder cystinosis, in which CTNS gene defects cause cystine accumulation in renal tubular cells leading to generalized proximal-tubular dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Avascular necrosis",
    "long_answer": "When a bone fracture damages the arteries supplying the bone, the resulting loss of perfusion can lead to death of bone tissue; this ischemic bone death is known as avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical repair",
    "long_answer": "Encephaloceles are neural-tube defects in which brain tissue and meninges protrude through a skull defect. Because the underlying problem is a physical opening in the cranium, no medication or supportive therapy can correct it. Definitive management requires neurosurgical closure of the skull defect, excision or repositioning of herniated tissue, and reconstruction of the dura and bone. Timely surgical repair is therefore the only effective treatment recognized in clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-TL1 A3243G",
    "long_answer": "The A3243G point mutation in the mitochondrial tRNALeu(UUR) gene (MT-TL1) is the prototypical genetic lesion in MELAS and has also been documented in patients who present with Chronic Progressive External Ophthalmoplegia. Thus, this single mitochondrial DNA mutation is shared by both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kurtus",
    "long_answer": "The genus Kurtus—commonly called nurseryfish—includes species (e.g., Kurtus gulliveri) in which the male bears a bony supra-occipital hook projecting from the forehead; after spawning, the adhesive egg mass is threaded onto this hook, and the male carries the clustered eggs externally until they hatch."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intervention",
    "long_answer": "The reality-documentary series “Intervention” (aired on A&E) included a widely discussed episode focusing on a woman named Allison whose primary addiction was huffing computer dust-remover (compressed-air duster)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neu-Laxova syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The PSPH gene encodes phosphoserine phosphatase, the last enzyme in the L-serine biosynthesis pathway. Loss-of-function mutations that markedly reduce this enzyme’s activity cause a severe congenital serine-deficiency disorder classified as Neu-Laxova syndrome. Clinically, Neu-Laxova syndrome is characterized by intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, limb anomalies and, importantly, a distinctive congenital ichthyosis-like skin thickening. Thus, the syndrome associated with PSPH enzyme deficiency and linked to ichthyosis is Neu-Laxova syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELN",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ELN gene, which encodes the structural protein elastin, are a well-established cause of autosomal-dominant cutis laxa. Elastin is the principal component of elastic fibers that give skin its stretch and recoil. Defective or insufficient elastin owing to ELN mutations disrupts elastic-fiber assembly, leading to the characteristic loose, inelastic skin seen in cutis laxa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Mature red blood cells expel all of their mitochondria during erythropoiesis, so they contain no mitochondria at all. Because mitochondrial diseases involve defects in mitochondrial DNA or oxidative-phosphorylation machinery, assays that rely on the presence and function of mitochondria cannot be performed in erythrocytes, preventing detection of mitochondrial disorders in these cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIH Office of Rare Diseases",
    "long_answer": "Pilotto syndrome is listed in the database maintained by the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH) Office of Rare Diseases, which designates disorders that affect fewer than 200,000 people in the United States as rare diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOST gene",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SOST gene, which encodes the osteocyte-secreted protein sclerostin, underlie the congenital high-bone-mass disorders sclerosteosis and Van Buchem disease. Without sclerostin’s normal inhibition of Wnt/β-catenin signalling, osteoblast activity is unchecked, producing greatly increased bone density and characteristic cranio-facial deformities (frontal bossing, mandibular overgrowth, distorted skull). Thus, SOST mutation is the established genetic cause of these human conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stanford University",
    "long_answer": "Investigators at Stanford University proposed that, in Rosai–Dorfman disease, circulating monocytes that migrate into the inflammatory lesion may themselves secrete macrophage colony-stimulating factor (M-CSF).  Local production of M-CSF would drive the proliferation and activation of the characteristic S-100–positive histiocytes, providing a cytokine-mediated explanation for the massive histiocytic accumulation seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CFTR gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, such as the common ΔF508 allele, disrupt epithelial chloride and bicarbonate transport—an essential metabolic process for salt-water homeostasis in airways. Individuals carrying CFTR mutations (e.g., patients with cystic fibrosis or CFTR-related disorders) exhibit thick, poorly cleared mucus and are several-fold more likely to develop colonisation by Aspergillus fumigatus and the ensuing hypersensitivity reaction that defines allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. Thus, CFTR gene mutations link disordered ion metabolism with a markedly increased risk of ABPA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "In February 2015 the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted marketing authorization for 23andMe’s Bloom Syndrome Carrier Screen, the first direct-to-consumer genetic test cleared to identify carriers of the Bloom syndrome–associated BLM gene mutation. The authorization followed FDA review confirming the test’s analytical accuracy and clinical validity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the ABCC9 gene, which encodes the sulfonylurea receptor 2 subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, produce Cantú syndrome (hypertrichotic osteochondrodysplasia). Affected individuals transmit the disorder to offspring in a vertical pattern and males and females are equally affected, establishing an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IQSEC2",
    "long_answer": "IQSEC2 (IQ motif and Sec7 domain-containing 2) is an X-chromosomal gene whose product possesses a Sec7 guanine-nucleotide exchange factor domain that activates members of the ADP-ribosylation factor (ARF) family of small GTPases. Pathogenic variants in IQSEC2 cause an X-linked intellectual disability syndrome, firmly establishing this gene’s role in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paresis",
    "long_answer": "Paresis is the neurological term for partial loss or impairment of voluntary muscle movement, manifesting clinically as muscle weakness. It denotes reduced motor strength rather than complete paralysis (plegia) and is commonly used to describe focal deficits (e.g., facial paresis) or generalized weakness resulting from injury or disease affecting nerves, muscles, or the neuromuscular junction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicular lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "The t(14;18)(q32;q21) translocation juxtaposes the BCL2 gene from chromosome 18 next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain (IgH) locus on chromosome 14. This causes BCL2 over-expression, inhibiting apoptosis and driving the pathogenesis of follicular lymphoma, the classic non-Hodgkin B-cell lymphoma associated with this chromosomal abnormality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamazepine",
    "long_answer": "The lancinating neuropathic pain that accompanies Ramsay Hunt syndrome type 2 (geniculate herpes zoster) is commonly managed with the anticonvulsant carbamazepine. Carbamazepine stabilizes inactivated sodium channels in sensory neurons, dampening ectopic firing and thereby reducing the neuralgic pain characteristic of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Progressive Cardiac Conduction Defect is a degenerative disorder of the heart’s electrical conduction system, often managed with pacemaker implantation and other rhythm-focused interventions. The medical specialty responsible for diagnosing and treating such conduction disturbances is cardiology, specifically the cardiac electrophysiology subspecialty within cardiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia, caused by an autosomal-recessive deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase, prevents conversion of galactose-1-phosphate to UDP-galactose. Toxic metabolites accumulate when lactose-containing milk is ingested, leading to vomiting, diarrhea, hepatic dysfunction and hypoglycemia. The resulting malabsorption and metabolic stress produce failure to thrive and poor growth in affected infants, making this inherited enzyme deficiency a well-recognized cause of poor growth during infancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses (MPS) are a group of lysosomal storage diseases caused by inherited deficiencies of specific lysosomal hydrolases that degrade glycosaminoglycans. The resulting accumulation of undegraded material leads to characteristic coarse facial features, organomegaly, skeletal abnormalities, and other systemic manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IFT122 gene (3q21.3) underlie cranioectodermal dysplasia/Sensenbrenner syndrome. Affected individuals carry biallelic pathogenic variants inherited from heterozygous carrier parents, demonstrating an autosomal recessive pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is an anterior horn cell disease characterized by progressive degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons, including those located in the spinal cord’s anterior horns. Because it famously affected baseball player Lou Gehrig, ALS is commonly called Lou Gehrig’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whole-exome sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Researchers applied whole-exome sequencing (NGS of all coding regions) to DNA from individuals/families with renal agenesis. Analysis of the exome data revealed segregating loss-of-function and missense variants in the GREB1L gene, establishing its link to renal agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electron-transfer flavoprotein dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Glutaric acidemia type II (multiple-acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) is most commonly caused by deficiency of mitochondrial electron-transfer flavoprotein dehydrogenase (ETF-QO, encoded by ETFDH). This enzyme accepts electrons from electron-transfer flavoprotein and passes them to ubiquinone; when it is missing, all FAD-dependent acyl-CoA dehydrogenases cannot feed electrons into the respiratory chain, producing the characteristic metabolic block seen in GA-II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluorescence in situ hybridization",
    "long_answer": "Pallister–Killian syndrome results from mosaic tetrasomy 12p carried on a small supernumerary isochromosome 12p. Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) using chromosome-12p–specific probes on fibroblast or buccal‐cell preparations can directly visualize and verify this extra marker chromosome, thereby confirming the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DEPDC5",
    "long_answer": "DEPDC5 encodes a core component of the GATOR1 complex, which normally acts as a negative regulator of mTORC1 signalling in response to amino-acid availability. Loss-of-function mutations in DEPDC5 hyper-activate mTORC1 and are the most frequent cause of familial focal epilepsies, with seizure penetrance approaching 90 %. Thus, DEPDC5 is both strongly linked to a high prevalence of epilepsy and mechanistically tied to regulation of mTORC1 signalling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-set ears",
    "long_answer": "Case descriptions of porencephaly-cerebellar hypoplasia-internal malformations syndrome note dysmorphic facial features that include low-set, often posteriorly rotated ears. Auricular low-set positioning is a very common, non-specific craniofacial sign that recurs in numerous genetic syndromes such as Down, Turner, Noonan, and DiGeorge syndromes, hence its identification as a shared feature across many conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAR1A",
    "long_answer": "Subsequent genetic testing of tissue from Cushing’s 1914 patient revealed a germ-line mutation in PRKAR1A, the gene encoding the type-1α regulatory subunit of protein kinase A. Loss-of-function mutations in PRKAR1A are the principal molecular cause of Carney complex, explaining the patient’s combination of endocrine tumors, myxomas and pigmentation anomalies that define this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystrophin",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophin is a large cytoskeletal protein that anchors the intracellular actin cytoskeleton to the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex in the sarcolemma of muscle fibers, thereby preserving membrane stability during contraction. Loss-of-function mutations in the DMD gene that encodes dystrophin disrupt this linkage, making muscle cell membranes fragile and leading to the progressive muscle degeneration seen in Duchenne muscular dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystoid macular edema",
    "long_answer": "In the late phase of Berlin’s edema (commotio retinae) the blood-retinal barrier can become disrupted, allowing extracellular fluid to collect in the outer plexiform layer of the macula. This pattern of fluid accumulation produces the characteristic cyst-like spaces of cystoid macular edema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectrodactyly-ectodermal dysplasia-cleft (EEC) syndrome",
    "long_answer": "EEC syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene. TP63 encodes a transcription factor critical for ectodermal development; its disruption produces the triad of limb malformations (ectrodactyly), ectodermal dysplasia with hypohidrosis (reduced sweating, sparse hair, abnormal teeth), and orofacial clefting. The presence of hypohidrosis in combination with TP63 mutations identifies EEC syndrome as the relevant disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alopecia–intellectual disability syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia–intellectual disability syndrome (also called alopecia-mental retardation syndrome, APMR) is a rare developmental disorder in which congenital or early-onset baldness (alopecia) is coupled with proportionate dwarfism (short stature) and moderate-to-severe intellectual disability. The condition is typically inherited in an autosomal-recessive manner; pathogenic variants have been identified in genes such as REXO4 or LSS that disrupt normal hair‐follicle development and growth as well as neurodevelopment, explaining the characteristic triad of baldness, short stature, and cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HCCS",
    "long_answer": "Microphthalmia with Linear Skin Defects Syndrome (MLS; OMIM #309801) is an X-linked dominant, male-lethal disorder. The disease-causing mutations are loss-of-function variants or whole-gene deletions in the HCCS gene (holocytochrome-c synthase) located at Xp22.2. HCCS encodes the mitochondrial holocytochrome-c synthase enzyme; its deficiency disrupts oxidative phosphorylation, leading to ocular malformations (severe microphthalmia/anophthalmia) and characteristic linear dermal aplasia along Blaschko’s lines. Numerous clinical and molecular studies have firmly established HCCS as the key gene responsible for MLS syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PHF6 gene (plant homeodomain finger protein 6) on Xq26 have been identified in families with Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome and in several other pedigrees with X-linked syndromic or non-specific intellectual disability, establishing PHF6 as the shared genetic cause across these X-linked mental retardation phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mass psychogenic illness",
    "long_answer": "Mass psychogenic illness (also called mass sociogenic illness or mass hysteria) describes the rapid spread of real, functional symptoms—such as tics, fainting, or nausea—among members of a group in the absence of any infectious or toxic agent. Recent reports have shown that social media platforms can facilitate this spread by exposing large audiences to videos of people displaying symptoms, leading susceptible viewers to develop similar manifestations through psychological and social contagion rather than biological transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Massive hemoptysis",
    "long_answer": "In patients with unilateral absence of a pulmonary artery, high-pressure systemic collaterals supply the affected lung. These fragile vessels can rupture, producing sudden, profuse intrapulmonary bleeding—clinically termed massive hemoptysis—which is recognized as a life-threatening complication of this congenital anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Friedrich Albert von Zenker",
    "long_answer": "German pathologist Friedrich Albert von Zenker (1825–1898) first elucidated the life cycle of Trichinella spiralis—the nematode responsible for trichinosis. In 1860 he demonstrated that ingesting larvae encysted in pork leads to adult worms in the intestine, whose newborn larvae then disseminate via the bloodstream and encyst in skeletal muscle. Zenker’s work linked the clinical disease to the parasite’s full developmental cycle and established the basis for meat-inspection and preventive measures against trichinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Viral infection",
    "long_answer": "Vestibular neuritis is most often triggered by a viral infection—commonly reactivation of herpes simplex virus type 1—which provokes an inflammatory immune response in the vestibular (VIII cranial) nerve, causing the acute vertigo characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autism spectrum disorder",
    "long_answer": "In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), the previously separate diagnoses of childhood disintegrative disorder, Asperger syndrome, autistic disorder, and pervasive developmental disorder–NOS were merged under a single umbrella diagnosis called “Autism spectrum disorder.” This term reflects the current consensus that these conditions represent variations along a common neurodevelopmental spectrum distinguished by deficits in social communication and restricted, repetitive behaviors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is the combined neuropsychiatric disorder that encompasses the acute phase of Wernicke encephalopathy and the chronic amnestic phase of Korsakoff syndrome. It is caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, most commonly due to chronic alcohol misuse, and can lead to profound memory impairment and other signs of alcohol-related dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zona glomerulosa",
    "long_answer": "The CYP11B2 gene encodes aldosterone synthase, the key enzyme that converts 11-deoxycorticosterone to aldosterone. Aldosterone is produced exclusively in the outermost layer of the adrenal cortex—the zona glomerulosa—so this zone uniquely expresses CYP11B2, whereas deeper zones (fasciculata and reticularis) do not."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sir Samuel Wilks",
    "long_answer": "After Thomas Hodgkin’s original 1832 description, the disorder’s existence as a distinct clinic-pathological entity was confirmed in 1865 by the English physician Sir Samuel Wilks. Wilks re-examined Hodgkin’s cases at Guy’s Hospital, recognized the characteristic lymph node pathology, and published the series naming the condition “Hodgkin’s disease,” thereby establishing its eponym and securing its place in medical nosology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratoacanthoma",
    "long_answer": "Keratoacanthoma is a benign but rapidly enlarging cutaneous tumour that arises from the pilosebaceous (hair-follicle) epithelium. Clinically it grows over weeks, forming a dome-shaped lesion with a central keratin plug, and is therefore classically described as a rapidly-growing skin tumour originating from the hair follicle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zinc toxicity",
    "long_answer": "Zinc toxicity—also referred to as zinc poisoning—is the clinical condition produced by ingesting or absorbing excessive amounts of zinc. Acute or chronic overdose overwhelms normal homeostatic mechanisms, leading to gastrointestinal distress, impaired copper absorption, anemia, and neurologic or immune dysfunction. Because the disorder’s name begins with the letter “Z” and is directly caused by zinc overdose, it is the disease that fits the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zavesca",
    "long_answer": "Zavesca is the proprietary (brand) name for miglustat, the only medicine currently licensed in Europe and other regions to slow the progressive neurological manifestations of Niemann-Pick disease type C. Miglustat is a small-molecule analogue of a naturally occurring monosaccharide; by inhibiting glucosylceramide synthase it reduces the build-up of glycosphingolipids in neurons, thereby stabilising or delaying neurological decline in NPC patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meiotic nondisjunction",
    "long_answer": "Small supernumerary marker chromosomes such as the inv-dup(22) that causes Cat eye syndrome typically originate when homologous chromosomes fail to separate correctly during meiosis (meiotic nondisjunction). The mis-segregated chromosome undergoes breakage and inverted duplication, yielding an extra, structurally abnormal chromosome fragment—an sSMC—that is retained in the gamete and subsequently in the embryo, producing the characteristic partial trisomy/tetrasomy seen in Cat eye syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked dominant CGG expansion",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome is inherited in an X-linked dominant manner. It results from a pathological expansion of the CGG trinucleotide repeat (> 200 repeats, the “full-mutation” allele) in the 5′ UTR of the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome, which leads to hypermethylation and silencing of the gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease",
    "long_answer": "Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease (PMD) is an X-linked leukodystrophy caused by pathogenic variants in the PLP1 gene. The mutation impairs production of proteolipid protein 1, leading to hypomyelination throughout the central nervous system. Clinically, affected individuals develop early nystagmus, hypotonia that progresses to spasticity, and motor impairment; the chronic muscle imbalance and weakness frequently give rise to progressive scoliosis. Thus, PMD is a genetic disorder characterized by CNS hypomyelination that can also cause scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention",
    "long_answer": "Surveillance data compiled by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) documented more than 32,000 reported cases of Legionnaires’ disease during the period 1995-2005, making the CDC the organization that issued this figure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMX1B",
    "long_answer": "Nail–patella syndrome is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the LMX1B gene. LMX1B maps to chromosome 9q34, the exact chromosomal band that also contains the ABO blood-group gene, placing both loci on the same chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DST (dystonin)",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type 6 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the DST gene, which encodes the cytolinker protein dystonin (also called BPAG1). Dystonin connects microtubules, actin, and intermediate filaments; when it is mutated, this cytoskeletal cross-linking is lost, impairing axonal transport along the cytoskeleton and leading to the severe neuropathy characteristic of HSAN-6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia",
    "long_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia is a neuronal-migration disorder in which cortical neurons arrest in transit, forming ectopic islands or bands of gray matter in abnormal brain locations (e.g., periventricular nodules or subcortical bands). Because these heterotopic neurons are dysplastic and disrupt normal cortical circuitry, patients commonly present with refractory epilepsy and other neurological deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome results from a terminal or interstitial deletion of the distal short arm of chromosome 4 (region 4p16.3). The chromosomal loss disrupts multiple genes critical for craniofacial development, producing the characteristic 'Greek-warrior helmet' facies that includes marked hypertelorism, a broad nasal bridge, and ocular anomalies, along with growth retardation and developmental delay. Thus, the hypertelorism-associated syndrome caused by a chromosome 4 deletion is Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple myeloma",
    "long_answer": "Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) is a premalignant plasma-cell disorder that progresses to multiple myeloma at a rate of about 1% per year. In multiple myeloma, clonal plasma cells infiltrate the bone marrow, replacing normal hematopoietic tissue and thereby causing normocytic, normochromic anemia. Thus, the anemia commonly seen in patients who progress from MGUS is attributable to the development of multiple myeloma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paruresis",
    "long_answer": "Paruresis, also called shy-bladder syndrome, is characterized by marked anxiety and inability to initiate or sustain urination in the real or imagined presence of others (e.g., public restrooms). DSM-5 subsumes this condition under Social Anxiety Disorder, because the core fear is negative evaluation by others during a social situation. The urinary difficulty is therefore viewed as a specific performance-type manifestation of social anxiety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RECQL4 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "RAPADILINO syndrome is produced by autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the RECQL4 DNA helicase gene (chromosome 8q24). The common founder change is a splice-site deletion (c.1390+2delT) that removes exon 7, impairing RECQL4 function and leading to the radial-ray and patellar hypoplasia typical of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "George Pepperdine",
    "long_answer": "George Pepperdine, the businessman and philanthropist who established George Pepperdine College (now Pepperdine University) in 1937, lost his first wife during the 1929-1930 global psittacosis (“parrot fever”) outbreak. She contracted and succumbed to the zoonotic Chlamydia psittaci infection that spread worldwide at that time, an event frequently noted in biographical accounts of Pepperdine’s life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "All reported cases of Scalp defects-postaxial polydactyly syndrome have occurred in siblings born to unaffected, often-consanguineous parents, and male and female children are affected equally. This segregation pattern is characteristic of autosomal recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Wernicke encephalopathy is an acute neurological syndrome caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency that presents with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and global confusion. Because it affects the central nervous system and requires neurological assessment, imaging and management (e.g., intravenous thiamine), its diagnosis and treatment are handled by the medical specialty of Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IKBKG (NEMO)",
    "long_answer": "Incontinentia pigmenti is an X-linked dominant disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the IKBKG gene, also called NEMO (NF-κB essential modulator). Defective IKBKG impairs NF-κB signaling, leading to apoptosis of ectoderm-derived cells and the characteristic staged skin lesions and other ectodermal abnormalities observed in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Congenital or familial hypoparathyroidism is a genetic disorder in which the parathyroid glands produce little or no parathyroid hormone. The resulting chronic hypocalcaemia can cause bilateral congenital cataracts, making hypoparathyroidism the classic genetic condition that links cataracts with decreased parathyroid function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal stage",
    "long_answer": "After the 8th week of embryonic development, the conceptus enters the fetal stage (fetal period). From this point until birth it is termed a fetus. Disorders such as Saethre-Chotzen syndrome, noted for craniosynostosis and blepharophimosis, arise or become evident during this fetal stage when rapid cranial and facial growth occurs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMARCAD1",
    "long_answer": "The congenital absence of fingerprints seen in Baird syndrome (familial adermatoglyphia) is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SMARCAD1 gene. These mutations affect a skin-specific isoform of the chromatin-remodelling factor SMARCAD1 during embryonic development, preventing normal formation of epidermal ridges and thereby leading to the complete absence of fingerprints."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYH3 gene, which encodes embryonic skeletal-muscle myosin heavy chain 3, cause several distal arthrogryposis syndromes, including Freeman-Sheldon (DA2A) and Sheldon-Hall (DA2B). These mutations impair myosin function during fetal muscle development, reduce fetal movement, and result in the fixed joint contractures that define this form of arthrogryposis, making MYH3 a well-established gene of interest in medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Twitter",
    "long_answer": "In December 2016, Newsweek journalist Kurt Eichenwald, who has photosensitive epilepsy, suffered a seizure after a rapidly flashing strobe GIF was deliberately sent to him through the Twitter platform. The incident prompted an FBI investigation and highlighted the risk of seizure-inducing visual stimuli on social media."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sepsis",
    "long_answer": "Sepsis—the systemic inflammatory response to infection—is the single most common precipitating factor for acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). During sepsis, circulating bacterial or fungal toxins and host cytokines trigger widespread endothelial injury in the pulmonary microvasculature, leading to increased capillary permeability, non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and the diffuse alveolar damage characteristic of ARDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Suprachiasmatic nucleus",
    "long_answer": "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the anterior hypothalamus is the brain’s master circadian pacemaker. It orchestrates daily sleep–wake cycles by integrating light input from the retina and synchronizing peripheral clocks. Familial advanced sleep phase syndrome (FASPS) is caused by mutations in core clock genes (e.g., PER2, CK1δ) whose oscillations are generated and coordinated in the SCN, linking this nucleus directly to the genetic regulation of the altered sleep timing seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Hypervalinemia arises when the mitochondrial branched-chain α-keto acid dehydrogenase (BCKDH) complex is deficient. BCKDH catalyzes the oxidative decarboxylation of the α-keto acid produced from valine (as well as leucine and isoleucine). Loss of this enzyme blocks valine catabolism, causing plasma accumulation of valine and its keto-acid, a biochemical hallmark of hypervalinemia (a maple-syrup-urine disease variant)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OMIM",
    "long_answer": "The Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man (OMIM) database is the authoritative resource that compiles and curates detailed information on the genes and specific pathogenic variants responsible for Joubert syndrome, including phenotype descriptions, allelic variants, and relevant literature references."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABHD5",
    "long_answer": "Jordans' anomaly—lipid-filled vacuoles in circulating granulocytes—is a hallmark of neutral lipid storage disease, especially the ichthyotic form (Chanarin-Dorfman syndrome). This disorder is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ABHD5 gene (also known as CGI-58), whose product activates adipose triglyceride lipase. ABHD5 mutations impair triglyceride hydrolysis, leading to systemic neutral-lipid accumulation and the characteristic vacuolated leukocytes seen as Jordans' anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychoneuroimmunology",
    "long_answer": "Robert Ader, along with Nicholas Cohen, introduced the term 'psychoneuroimmunology' in the late 1970s to describe the interdisciplinary field that investigates how psychological processes (psycho-), the nervous system (neuro-), and the immune system (immunology) interact and influence health and disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pan American Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "On 29 April 2015, the Pan American Health Organization (PAHO)—the World Health Organization’s regional office for the Americas—announced that endemic rubella virus transmission had been eliminated throughout North, Central, and South America, officially declaring the region free of rubella transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phosphomannomutase-2",
    "long_answer": "The most frequently diagnosed form of congenital disorder of glycosylation is PMM2-CDG (formerly CDG-Ia). It results from loss-of-function mutations in the PMM2 gene, which encodes the enzyme phosphomannomutase-2. This enzyme catalyzes the conversion of mannose-6-phosphate to mannose-1-phosphate, a critical early step in the dolichol-linked pathway for N-linked glycan assembly. Deficiency of phosphomannomutase-2 thus disrupts formation of the lipid-linked oligosaccharide precursor, leading to multisystem hypoglycosylation that characterizes the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "West syndrome",
    "long_answer": "West syndrome, also called infantile spasms, is an epilepsy syndrome that begins in the first year of life. It is marked by clusters of brief tonic spasms (infantile spasms), a chaotic EEG pattern known as hypsarrhythmia, and is frequently accompanied by developmental arrest or regression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniotic band syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Amniotic band syndrome (also called amniotic band sequence) is a congenital disorder characterized by fibrous amniotic bands that entangle the fetus. These bands can wrap around extremities, producing circumferential constriction rings, distal swelling, and even limb amputations. Affected infants frequently have intrauterine growth restriction, leading to low birth weight."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Duplications at cytoband Xp11.2 lie on the X chromosome. Reported families show affected hemizygous males with intellectual disability, whereas heterozygous carrier females are typically unaffected or only mildly affected because of X-inactivation. This segregation pattern is characteristic of X-linked recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Haemophilus ducreyi",
    "long_answer": "Chancroid is a sexually transmitted ulcerative disease caused by the Gram-negative coccobacillus Haemophilus ducreyi. This organism is highly fastidious and cannot grow on routine laboratory media; it requires enriched, specialized media (e.g., chocolate agar supplemented with IsoVitalex or Mueller-Hinton agar with added growth factors) and microaerophilic conditions. Its strict nutritional demands and slow growth necessitate these specialized culture conditions, distinguishing it from less fastidious bacteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Axanthism",
    "long_answer": "Axanthism is a recessive pigment-cell mutation that eliminates or greatly reduces xanthophores, the cells that synthesize yellow pteridine pigments in amphibian skin. With the yellow layer absent, only the structural blue light reflected by underlying iridophores remains visible, so the animals appear blue instead of their normal green coloration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enzyme replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy is caused by a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase A. An available therapeutic approach is enzyme replacement therapy, in which recombinant arylsulfatase A is administered to supply the missing enzyme and reduce sulfatide accumulation, aiming to slow or halt disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The disorder described is characteristic of nail-patella syndrome, in which bilateral hypoplasia or absence of the nails of the thumb, index, and middle fingers is typical. Nail-patella syndrome results from heterozygous mutations in the LMX1B gene and is transmitted with high penetrance in an autosomal dominant fashion. Therefore, its inheritance pattern is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Auxotroph",
    "long_answer": "An auxotroph is a mutant organism that has lost the ability to synthesize a specific organic compound essential for its growth; it therefore requires that compound to be supplied externally. Certain laboratory-derived or naturally occurring strains of Pseudomonas stutzeri exhibit such nutritional requirements, classifying them as auxotrophs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP53",
    "long_answer": "TP53, which encodes the p53 tumor-suppressor protein, is mutated in the great majority of small-cell lung carcinomas (≈ 75-95%) and in more than half of all human cancers overall. Loss-of-function mutations in TP53 abolish p53’s roles in DNA-damage response, cell-cycle arrest, and apoptosis, thereby promoting malignant transformation across many tissue types."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral-facial-digital syndrome 1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OFD1 gene (located on Xp22) cause Oral-facial-digital syndrome type 1, an X-linked dominant ciliopathy. Affected individuals present with malformations of the face (e.g., hypertelorism, broad nasal bridge), oral cavity (cleft tongue, cleft palate, multiple frenula), and digits (brachydactyly, syndactyly, polydactyly), fitting the description of the congenital disorder in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chanarin–Dorfman syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Chanarin–Dorfman syndrome (neutral lipid storage disease with ichthyosis) is an autosomal-recessive storage disorder in which neutral lipids accumulate in many tissues. Triglyceride deposition in the liver produces marked hepatomegaly that can progress to steatohepatitis, fibrosis and cirrhosis, leading to growth failure and variable intellectual disability. Patients present at birth with diffuse ichthyosiform hyperkeratosis and often have congenital sensorineural hearing loss—skin and auditory findings that closely resemble those seen in Hystrix-like Ichthyosis with Deafness (HID) syndrome—making Chanarin–Dorfman syndrome the condition described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tennis elbow",
    "long_answer": "Inflammation of the lateral epicondyle of the humerus—technically called lateral epicondylitis—results from repetitive overuse of the wrist extensor tendons (especially extensor carpi radialis brevis). Because this overuse injury is frequent in people who repeatedly perform backhand strokes in tennis, the condition is popularly known as “tennis elbow.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The combination of congenital heart defects, characteristic cranio-facial dysmorphism, and intellectual disability is typical of Kabuki (Niikawa-Kuroki) syndrome. The disorder is caused most often by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KMT2D gene (and less commonly KDM6A), and familial cases show vertical transmission with equal sex distribution. These features establish an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance as the suspected genetic mechanism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMP22 gene deletion",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsy (HNPP) is almost always caused by a heterozygous 1.5-Mb deletion on chromosome 17p11.2 that removes one copy of the PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein-22) gene. Loss of PMP22 expression (haploinsufficiency) disrupts myelin maintenance in peripheral nerves, producing the pressure-sensitive neuropathy characteristic of HNPP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutropenia",
    "long_answer": "Cohen syndrome frequently features neutropenia, a hematologic condition in which circulating neutrophil levels are abnormally low. Neutrophils are the most abundant type of white blood cell and are essential for innate immune defense; their deficiency predisposes affected individuals to recurrent infections, a recognized complication of Cohen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-encephalitic parkinsonism",
    "long_answer": "Post-encephalitic parkinsonism, the extrapyramidal syndrome that can follow encephalitis lethargica, typically affects children or young adults. In contrast to idiopathic Parkinson’s disease, it seldom shows the classic resting “pill-rolling” tremor; rigidity, bradykinesia and dystonic posturing predominate instead."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diathesis–stress model",
    "long_answer": "The diathesis–stress model posits that a person carries an inherited biological or genetic susceptibility (diathesis) and that onset of disorders such as schizophreniform disorder occurs when environmental stressors act on this vulnerability. This interaction of genetic risk with precipitating environmental events explains how the illness can be triggered in susceptible individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parental consanguinity",
    "long_answer": "Lamellar ichthyosis is inherited in an autosomal-recessive manner, so the risk rises markedly when the parents are blood relatives (e.g., first-cousins). Consanguineous couples are more likely to carry the same pathogenic allele and therefore have a higher chance of having a child affected by lamellar ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Photosensitive epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Photosensitive epilepsy, in which flashing or flickering light provokes seizures, is the most prevalent form of reflex epilepsy. The seizures it elicits are typically generalized—most often generalized tonic-clonic, myoclonic, or absence attacks—reflecting widespread cortical hyperexcitability to visual stimuli. Because of its high frequency relative to other stimulus-induced epilepsies (e.g., reading, musicogenic, hot-water), it is recognized as the commonest reflex epilepsy involving generalized seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrichosis pinnae auris",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrichosis pinnae auris, a Y-linked form of hypertrichosis, leads to dense terminal hair growth confined to the helix of the external ear (pinna). Because the causative allele is carried on the Y chromosome, the trait is transmitted solely through males and manifests as excessive hair on this specific auricular region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein (BMP)",
    "long_answer": "During limb formation, Bone Morphogenetic Proteins—particularly BMP-2, BMP-4 and BMP-7—are expressed in the interdigital mesenchyme. Their signaling triggers programmed cell death (apoptosis) in this tissue, eliminating the web between the forming digits. Failure of BMP-mediated apoptosis results in persistence of interdigital tissue and produces syndactyly (fused digits). Thus, BMPs are the key proteins responsible for the apoptosis that prevents syndactyly in human fetal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPG20 (spartin)",
    "long_answer": "Troyer syndrome is the SPG20 type of complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia (HSP). It results from loss-of-function mutations in the SPG20 gene, which encodes the protein spartin; the original Amish kindred carry a homozygous 1110delA frameshift in SPG20. Disruption of spartin impairs endosomal trafficking and mitochondrial function, producing the characteristic combination of childhood-onset spastic paraparesis, distal muscle wasting, dysarthria and short stature seen in Troyer syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the hepatic transporter genes SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3 cause Rotor syndrome (conjugated hyperbilirubinemia). The disorder appears only when both copies of the relevant genes are defective, and unaffected parents can transmit the mutation to offspring, establishing an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAMA2",
    "long_answer": "The LAMA2 gene, located on chromosome 6q22-q23, encodes the laminin-α2 chain of the skeletal-muscle basement membrane. Pathogenic LAMA2 mutations classically cause merosin-deficient congenital muscular dystrophy, but milder or partial-deficiency variants present clinically as an autosomal-recessive limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (historically designated LGMD2U, now LGMD R23). Hence, the gene on chromosome 6 whose mutation produces a form of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy is LAMA2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gross (large) gene deletions",
    "long_answer": "Mutations causing Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS-III) include many missense, nonsense, splice-site and small insertion/deletion variants in SGSH, NAGLU, HGSNAT or GNS. However, no gross or large-scale deletions/duplications of these genes have ever been documented in human patients, making large genomic deletions the mutation type associated with the disorder that has not yet been observed in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is a chromosomal disorder in which affected individuals characteristically have proportionate short stature—overall height is reduced but body segments remain in normal proportion. Another common minor anomaly in Down syndrome is clinodactyly, especially incurving of the fifth finger due to mid-phalanx hypoplasia. The simultaneous presence of proportionate short stature and clinodactyly therefore points to Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein 4 (BMP4)",
    "long_answer": "During limb bud formation, BMP4 is secreted in the interdigital mesenchyme. Its signaling activates the intrinsic apoptotic pathway in the cells between developing digits, causing programmed cell death that removes the webbing. Failure of BMP4-mediated apoptosis results in persistent webbing and thus syndactyly, so BMP4 is the key protein directing this developmental apoptosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VACTERL-H syndrome",
    "long_answer": "VACTERL-H (VACTERL association with Hydrocephalus) is a malformation complex that combines the usual VACTERL limb abnormalities—most often radial aplasia or dysplasia—with additional central-nervous-system defects such as hydrocephalus produced by arachnoid cysts. Lower-limb involvement can include complete absence of the tibia. The triad of radial dysplasia, absent tibia and arachnoid cyst–related hydrocephalus is therefore characteristic of VACTERL-H syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rett syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rett syndrome is an X-linked dominant neurodevelopmental disorder caused most commonly by mutations in the MECP2 gene. It disrupts maturation of neurons in the brain and spinal cord, leading to loss of purposeful hand use, motor incoordination, language regression and social withdrawal. Because early developmental regression and impaired social communication closely resemble features of autism spectrum disorder, many young girls with Rett syndrome are initially misdiagnosed as having autism before the correct genetic diagnosis is made."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EWS-WT1 fusion protein",
    "long_answer": "Desmoplastic small-round-cell tumor is driven by the t(11;22)(p13;q12) translocation that fuses the EWSR1 gene with the Wilms tumor 1 (WT1) gene. The resulting EWS-WT1 fusion protein joins the strong N-terminal transactivation domain of EWSR1 to the C-terminal zinc-finger DNA-binding domain of WT1, giving the chimeric oncoprotein sequence-specific DNA-binding capability that underlies its pathogenic transcriptional activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heritability",
    "long_answer": "Heritability is the statistical measure that quantifies how much of the observed variation in risk for bipolar disorder within a population can be attributed to inherited genetic differences among individuals. Twin, family, and genome-wide studies consistently show high heritability for bipolar disorder, indicating that genetic variability substantially influences its development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ICSH",
    "long_answer": "The International Council (formerly Committee) for Standardization in Haematology (ICSH) issued the consensus guidelines for bone-marrow iron staining in which it formally defined the four sideroblast categories—Type 0 (no granules), Type 1 (few scattered ferritin granules), Type 2 (numerous coarse granules not encircling the nucleus) and Type 3 (ring sideroblasts with ≥5 granules surrounding ≥1⁄3 of the nuclear circumference). These definitions underpin the diagnostic criteria for sideroblastic anemias used in clinical hematology and by the WHO classification of myelodysplastic syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia is produced by a deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT). When GALT is absent, galactose-1-phosphate cannot enter normal metabolism, so free galactose rises. Aldose reductase converts this excess galactose to the polyol galactitol, which accumulates in tissues (lens, liver, brain), leading to cataracts and other metabolic–endocrine complications. Thus, GALT deficiency is the specific defect that results in galactitol buildup in galactosemic patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yul Brynner",
    "long_answer": "Yul Brynner, who first shaved his head for the 1951 stage role of the King of Siam in “The King and I” and retained the look throughout his film career, is widely credited with making the completely shaved head a fashionable, confident style choice. His high-profile adoption of a smooth scalp helped destigmatize male pattern baldness and established the shaved head as a visible, socially accepted option for men experiencing hair loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Current authoritative classifications of Usher syndrome recognize three clinical types (I, II and III). Extensive genetic and clinical studies carried out since the 1990s—particularly by groups at the University of Iowa (Carver Laboratory of Dr. Edwin M. Stone and colleagues) and corroborated by other academic centers—showed that cases once proposed as a separate 'type IV' were in fact atypical presentations of the known types and did not represent a distinct genetic entity. Consequently, the international medical and genetic consensus no longer acknowledges an Usher syndrome type IV. Therefore, the statement that there is no Usher syndrome type IV is supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gaucher disease",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GBA gene on chromosome 1q21 impair the lysosomal enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase. The resulting glycolipid accumulation in macrophages causes Gaucher disease, a lysosomal storage disorder marked by hepatosplenomegaly, cytopenias, and characteristic skeletal manifestations such as bone pain, osteopenia, and osteonecrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heterochromia iridum",
    "long_answer": "The presence of two differently coloured irises in the same individual is called heterochromia iridum (or heterochromia iridis). It results from variations in melanin distribution or sympathetic innervation of the iris and can be a feature associated with disorders such as Parry–Romberg syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PAX2 gene disrupt closure of the embryonic optic (choroid) fissure. This failure produces an ocular coloboma, a cleft or gap that can involve the iris, retina, choroid, or optic disc. PAX2-related renal-coloboma syndrome is the prototypic condition in which such defects are observed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alternative splicing",
    "long_answer": "The CDKN2A locus uses alternative splicing (with separate first exons driven by different promoters and reading frames) to generate distinct mRNA transcripts. These transcripts encode different proteins—p16INK4a and p14ARF—so the process of alternative splicing enables a single CDKN2A gene to give rise to multiple protein isoforms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patience Dudley",
    "long_answer": "Colonial court and church records from Massachusetts Bay Colony (1630s) describe Governor Thomas Dudley’s daughter, Patience Dudley, as a “natural idiot”—the seventeenth-century term for a person with significant cognitive limitations. Because these documents pre-date any other surviving reports, historians of disability identify Patience Dudley as the first clearly documented individual with an intellectual disability in the Colonial United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phlebotomy",
    "long_answer": "The cornerstone first-line therapy for polycythemia vera is periodic phlebotomy (also called venesection), in which units of blood are removed to lower the hematocrit (< 45 %), reduce blood viscosity, and diminish thrombotic risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FSHB",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FSHB gene, which encodes the β-subunit of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), impair production of biologically active FSH. Because FSH is the pituitary gonadotropin that normally acts on Sertoli cells, its deficiency results in normosmic congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism without olfactory defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase",
    "long_answer": "The condition that is frequently mistaken for Hurler syndrome is I-cell disease. I-cell disease results from a deficiency of the Golgi enzyme N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase, which is required to add mannose-6-phosphate tags to lysosomal hydrolases. Without this enzyme, hydrolases are mis-routed outside the cell, leading to lysosomal storage of undegraded material, coarse facial features, and skeletal abnormalities resembling Hurler syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NBEAL2",
    "long_answer": "Gray platelet syndrome is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NBEAL2 gene. NBEAL2 encodes a BEACH-domain–containing protein required for the formation of α-granules during megakaryocyte maturation. When NBEAL2 is defective, megakaryocytes fail to package proteins into α-granules, producing large, pale (‘gray’) platelets that lack these granules and leading to the thrombocytopenia and bleeding tendency characteristic of Gray platelet syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Annular pancreas",
    "long_answer": "Annular pancreas is a congenital malformation in which a ring of pancreatic tissue encircles the second part of the duodenum. This constricts the duodenal lumen, often causing duodenal obstruction, which in utero leads to impaired swallowing of amniotic fluid and resultant polyhydramnios. Therefore, polyhydramnios together with a pancreatic ring around the duodenum characterises annular pancreas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encephalocele",
    "long_answer": "Frontonasal dysplasia often presents with a midline cranial defect in which brain tissue and meninges herniate through an opening in the skull. This neural tube defect, called an encephalocele, produces a sac-like protrusion of the brain and is a recognized, frequent manifestation of frontonasal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-DPB1 Glu69 allele",
    "long_answer": "Most patients who develop chronic beryllium disease carry HLA-DPB1 alleles that encode a glutamic acid at position 69 of the HLA-DP β-chain (the “Glu69” polymorphism). This amino-acid substitution alters the peptide-binding groove, making it highly effective at presenting beryllium to CD4⁺ T-cells, which drives the granulomatous immune response characteristic of the disease. Individuals with the HLA-DPB1 Glu69 allele therefore have a markedly increased risk of chronic beryllium disease after exposure compared with those lacking this variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Affect intensity",
    "long_answer": "Borderline Personality Disorder is characteristically marked by very strong, rapidly shifting emotions. In personality research, this dispositional tendency to experience emotions with unusually high strength is termed ‘affect intensity.’ Studies using the Affect Intensity Measure (AIM) consistently show that people with BPD score well above normative levels, confirming that heightened affect intensity is a core emotional feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A5",
    "long_answer": "The X-linked dominant form of Alport syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the COL4A5 gene on chromosome Xq22, which encodes the α5 chain of type IV collagen. Mutations in COL4A5 disrupt the assembly of the collagen IV network in glomerular, cochlear, and ocular basement membranes, producing the characteristic renal, auditory, and ocular manifestations of Alport syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human placental lactogen",
    "long_answer": "Placental site trophoblastic tumor (PSTT) is derived from intermediate trophoblasts that secrete large amounts of human placental lactogen (hPL) but relatively little β-hCG. Immunohistochemical staining for hPL therefore shows strong, diffuse positivity in PSTT, whereas other gestational trophoblastic neoplasms (e.g., choriocarcinoma, hydatidiform mole) are weak or negative for hPL. This marked expression of human placental lactogen is routinely used by pathologists to distinguish PSTT from the other forms of gestational trophoblastic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CACNA1H",
    "long_answer": "CACNA1H encodes the Cav3.2 α1-subunit of voltage-gated T-type calcium channels. Mutations and polymorphisms in CACNA1H that alter channel gating or increase channel current have been identified in patients with childhood absence epilepsy, linking this gene—and the T-type calcium channel activity it regulates—to the generation of thalamocortical oscillations that underlie absence seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Major depressive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Major depressive disorder (clinical depression) is frequently linked to increased procrastination; the low mood, anhedonia and reduced motivation characteristic of MDD make task initiation and completion difficult. By definition, MDD produces pervasive changes in a person’s thoughts, behaviours, emotions and overall sense of well-being, matching the description given in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mevalonic aciduria",
    "long_answer": "Mevalonic aciduria is the severe, autosomal-recessive form of mevalonate kinase deficiency. Loss of the enzyme mevalonate kinase disrupts the early steps of cholesterol and isoprenoid biosynthesis, resulting in accumulation of mevalonic acid and a shortage of downstream products. Clinically, this manifests in infancy with developmental delay, marked hypotonia, failure to thrive, dysmorphic features, and recurrent inflammatory crises, making mevalonic aciduria the prototypic disorder linked to mevalonate kinase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Borrelia",
    "long_answer": "Lyme disease is caused by spirochetes of the genus Borrelia, most notably Borrelia burgdorferi sensu lato. Comparative genomic analyses reveal that Borrelia species possess numerous linear and circular plasmids and exhibit extensive sequence variation across strains, underscoring the genus’s exceptional genetic diversity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly",
    "long_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome) is produced by mutations in the FBN2 gene and presents with tall stature, long thin limbs, arachnodactyly, scoliosis, and joint laxity—features that closely mimic those of Marfan syndrome. Unlike Marfan syndrome, however, it characteristically lacks ocular involvement, so lens dislocation (ectopia lentis) is not a feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LRPPRC",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the LRPPRC (leucine-rich pentatricopeptide repeat-containing) gene on chromosome 2p21 causes Leigh syndrome, French Canadian type (LSFC). Carriers of the same LRPPRC mutation have been shown to display dyslipidaemia with elevated LDL-cholesterol and triglycerides, which translates into a higher incidence of premature atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease in this population. Thus, the LRPPRC gene mutation links both LSFC and increased cardiovascular risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Petrous temporal bone",
    "long_answer": "Vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) almost always arise within the internal auditory (acoustic) canal, and this canal is located in the petrous portion of the temporal bone of the skull."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enthesitis",
    "long_answer": "Enthesitis refers to inflammation of the entheses—the sites where tendons, ligaments, or joint capsules attach to bone. It is a hallmark manifestation of the spondyloarthritis group of disorders (including ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic arthritis, and others), arising from immune-mediated inflammation at these insertion points and leading to pain, swelling, and possible new bone formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wheat germ oil",
    "long_answer": "Wheat germ oil is recognized as the most concentrated natural source of vitamin E, providing roughly 149 mg of α-tocopherol per 100 g of oil according to standard food-composition data."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Locked-in syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Locked-in syndrome is a rare neurological condition, usually caused by damage to the ventral pons, that leaves patients completely paralyzed (quadriplegic) and mute (anarthric) while preserving consciousness, cognition, and vertical eye movements, fitting the description of individuals who are unable to move or speak despite being fully aware."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOTCH1",
    "long_answer": "NOTCH1 functions as a tumour-suppressor in epidermal keratinocytes. Loss-of-function mutations in NOTCH1, common in cutaneous squamous-cell carcinoma, remove Notch-mediated transcriptional repression of the keratinocyte growth-factor receptor gene FGFR2. Consequently, NOTCH1 mutation leads to marked overexpression of FGFR2 in these tumours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SEPN1 (SELENON)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SEPN1 gene (also called SELENON), which encodes selenoprotein N, are a recognized cause of congenital fiber-type disproportion and can likewise lead to the condition known as SEPN1-related congenital muscular dystrophy. The same loss-of-function mutations in SEPN1 impair selenoprotein N-mediated redox homeostasis in muscle, explaining their role in both phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Scimitar syndrome is a rare form of partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection with associated right-sided cardiac and pulmonary malformations. Because it is a congenital heart defect, its detection (echocardiography, cardiac CT/MRI) and ongoing medical and interventional management are carried out by specialists in pediatric cardiology, the branch of cardiology dedicated to congenital heart diseases in infants and children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive inheritance",
    "long_answer": "Dejerine–Sottas disease (hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy type III) is most often transmitted in an autosomal recessive manner, in which affected individuals inherit two pathogenic alleles—one from each carrier parent—leading to the severe early-onset demyelinating neuropathy characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Normal 46,XX or 46,XY",
    "long_answer": "Schinzel–Giedion syndrome is caused by de-novo heterozygous mutations in the autosomal gene SETBP1 (18q12.3); it is not linked to the sex chromosomes. Affected individuals therefore have the standard human sex-chromosome complement—46,XX for females and 46,XY for males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carpenter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder whose core clinical triad includes craniosynostosis, polysyndactyly (fusion of digits), and soft-tissue abnormalities. Affected individuals typically develop early-onset obesity, global developmental delay/intellectual disability, and generalized hypotonia or muscle weakness. The presence of syndactyly together with obesity and intellectual disabilities thus points to Carpenter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VLDLR",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the VLDLR gene (encoding the very-low-density lipoprotein receptor) cause the ‘dysequilibrium syndrome’ characterized by non-progressive cerebellar hypoplasia, ataxia and intellectual disability. Because the children present with early motor delay, spasticity and poor balance, many affected families in the 1970s were given a diagnosis of cerebral palsy until the VLDLR mutation was identified and the syndrome was reclassified."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nasal polyps",
    "long_answer": "Anosmia (loss of smell) can occur when benign, noncancerous growths called nasal polyps develop on the lining of the nasal passages or paranasal sinuses. These polyps physically obstruct airflow to the olfactory epithelium and can also promote chronic inflammation, both of which interfere with olfactory receptor function and lead to diminished or absent smell sensation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kniest dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Kniest dysplasia is an autosomal-dominant skeletal dysplasia caused by pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene encoding type II collagen. It involves abnormal development of the spine (spondylo-), the epiphyses and the metaphyses of long bones, so it is classified as a spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia. Clinically it presents with short stature, kyphoscoliosis, joint deformities and characteristic radiographic changes, fulfilling the definition of this subgroup of bone disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HFE",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the HFE gene—most commonly the C282Y or H63D variants—impair regulation of intestinal iron absorption, leading to iron overload (hereditary hemochromatosis). Excess iron promotes the formation of uroporphyrinogen‐oxidizing reactive species that inhibit uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, the key enzymatic defect in porphyria cutanea tarda. Consequently, individuals carrying pathogenic HFE mutations have a significantly higher risk of developing porphyria cutanea tarda while the gene’s normal role is to modulate iron availability for hemoglobin synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FOXC2 transcription-factor gene cause lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome, an autosomal-dominant disorder characterized by primary lymphatic dysfunction (usually lower-limb lymphedema) and an extra row of eyelashes (distichiasis). FOXC2 is crucial for lymphatic valve and vessel development; loss-of-function mutations impair lymphatic flow, producing the clinical lymphedema phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylmalonic acidemia",
    "long_answer": "The MUT gene encodes the enzyme methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Loss-of-function mutations in MUT impair conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, leading to accumulation of methylmalonic acid in blood and tissues. This inborn error of metabolism, known as methylmalonic acidemia (or methylmalonic aciduria), presents with metabolic acidosis, failure to thrive, and often hepatomegaly due to toxic metabolite buildup and secondary liver enlargement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Felty’s syndrome is a complication of long-standing rheumatoid arthritis characterized by the triad of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia. Its epidemiology and pathophysiology are linked to autoimmune mechanisms and chronic inflammation, not to hemoglobinopathies. Sickle cell disease, by contrast, is an inherited disorder caused by a mutation in the β-globin gene leading to hemoglobin S. It is most prevalent in individuals of African, Middle-Eastern, or Mediterranean ancestry and is unrelated to autoimmune rheumatoid disease.\n\nThere is no established epidemiological or pathophysiologic connection between Felty’s syndrome and sickle cell disease, and the two conditions are not commonly found together in the same patients. Case reports of their coexistence are exceedingly rare and considered coincidental rather than indicative of a true association. Therefore, the statement that sickle cell disease is commonly found in individuals with Felty’s syndrome is not supported by current medical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Therapeutic phlebotomy",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary haemochromatosis leads to excess iron accumulation. The standard treatment is therapeutic phlebotomy, where 450–500 mL of blood is removed at regular intervals—exactly the same venesection procedure used when people donate blood. Each session eliminates iron contained in red blood cells, progressively lowering body iron stores and preventing organ damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missense mutation",
    "long_answer": "Freeman–Sheldon syndrome (distal arthrogryposis type 2A) is caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the MYH3 gene, which alter single amino acids in the embryonic myosin heavy-chain protein and disrupt its motor function, producing the characteristic contractures and facial features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked primary immunodeficiency characterized by the classic triad of micro-thrombocytopenia, eczema, and recurrent infections. A distinguishing laboratory feature is reduced activity of the T-cell surface enzyme 5'-nucleotidase, reflecting the underlying defect in the WAS protein that disrupts cytoskeletal organization and immune cell signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Excessive calcium influx",
    "long_answer": "ADNFLE-linked mutations in neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors make the channels open more easily and stay open longer, raising their permeability to Ca2+. The resulting excessive influx of calcium through the mutant receptors drives abnormal depolarization and transmitter release in frontal-lobe neurons, producing the hyperexcitability that characterises autosomal dominant nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLN3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CLN3 gene, mapped to chromosome 16p12.1, cause the juvenile form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (Batten disease). Patients typically show characteristic cerebral and cerebellar atrophy and periventricular white-matter signal changes on MRI, findings that help clinicians recognize and link the disease to the CLN3 locus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Copper indium gallium selenide",
    "long_answer": "Copper indium gallium selenide (CIGS) is a thin-film semiconductor composed of Cu, In, Ga and Se. Because the Ga/In ratio can be adjusted, its bandgap can be tuned continuously from about 1.0 eV (CuInSe2) up to ~1.7 eV (CuGaSe2). The presence of selenium in the diselenide lattice and this variable bandgap make CIGS a widely used absorber layer in high-efficiency thin-film solar cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Classic familial hypercholesterolemia is caused by mutations in the LDL-receptor (LDLR) gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 19 (region 19p13). Defects in this chromosome-19 gene impair LDL clearance, leading to the elevated plasma cholesterol that defines the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oxford University Press",
    "long_answer": "Oxford University Press published the earliest dedicated monograph that focused on therapeutic management of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Churg-Strauss syndrome), making it the first book specifically indicated for treating this rare vasculitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cowden syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the tumor-suppressor gene PTEN on chromosome 10 cause Cowden syndrome, also called multiple hamartoma syndrome. Affected individuals develop numerous hamartomas in the skin, mucous membranes, gastrointestinal tract, and other tissues, and they have elevated risks of breast, thyroid, and endometrial cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNTNAP2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in CNTNAP2, which encodes the axonal cell-adhesion protein CASPR2, have been reported in patients with focal cortical dysplasia types 2a and 2b. CASPR2 localises to the juxtaparanodes of myelinated axons where it clusters Kv1 potassium channels that regulate saltatory conduction. Loss-of-function CNTNAP2 mutations therefore disturb the propagation of action potentials along axons, directly affecting the axonal transmission of electrical impulses observed in these forms of cortical dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NRAS",
    "long_answer": "Neurocutaneous melanosis is driven by post-zygotic activating mutations in the NRAS proto-oncogene, most commonly at codon 61. These mosaic NRAS mutations arise in neural crest–derived melanocyte precursors, leading to proliferation of congenital melanocytic nevi in the skin and melanotic lesions in the leptomeninges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frederick William Hope",
    "long_answer": "The term “myiasis” for parasitic infestation of the body by fly larvae was introduced in 1840 by the British entomologist Frederick William Hope, who proposed it to replace the older term “scholechiasis.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALMS1",
    "long_answer": "Alström syndrome is an autosomal-recessive ciliopathy produced by loss-of-function mutations in the ALMS1 gene on chromosome 2p13. In addition to obesity, retinal dystrophy, cardiomyopathy and diabetes, ALMS1 mutations consistently lead to progressive sensorineural hearing loss, making ALMS1 the causative gene for both Alström syndrome and its characteristic auditory impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNE gene",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary inclusion body myopathy (HIBM/Nonaka myopathy) is produced by autosomal-recessive mutations in the GNE gene, which encodes the bifunctional enzyme UDP-N-acetyl-glucosamine-2-epimerase/N-acetyl-mannosamine kinase. Loss of GNE activity reduces sialic-acid synthesis, leading to hyposialylation of podocyte surface proteins; this triggers focal segmental glomerular sclerosis, the characteristic renal lesion seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF5A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the kinesin motor‐protein gene KIF5A are a recognized cause of autosomal-dominant hereditary spastic paraplegia (SPG10). Different pathogenic variants in KIF5A have also been linked to other neurological disorders, including axonal Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, illustrating how alterations in this single gene can give rise to multiple, distinct clinical conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder",
    "long_answer": "In DSM-5, the condition formerly labeled “conversion disorder” was renamed to Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder (FNSD). The new term emphasizes that the patient presents with neurological-type symptoms (e.g., weakness, movement abnormalities, sensory loss, seizures) that are inconsistent with recognized neurological disease, reflecting a functional disturbance rather than structural pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "London",
    "long_answer": "The published case of a 5-year-old girl diagnosed with Revesz syndrome who additionally exhibited bilateral retinal detachment and retinitis pigmentosa was documented by clinicians in London, thereby identifying that city as the location of the reported case."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shatapatha Brahmana",
    "long_answer": "The earliest detailed narration of the Vāmana (dwarf) incarnation of Lord Vishnu occurs in the Shatapatha Brahmana, a prose ritual text of the Yajur Veda. In this Brahmana, Vishnu assumes the form of a dwarf to win back the three worlds from the asura Bali by taking three great strides, a motif that later becomes the full Vāmana avatar story in the Puranas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Voretigene neparvovec",
    "long_answer": "The FDA-approved gene therapy marketed as Luxturna contains voretigene neparvovec-rzyl, an adeno-associated virus vector that delivers a functional copy of the human RPE65 gene to retinal pigment epithelial cells. By restoring RPE65 activity, it treats vision loss in patients with Leber congenital amaurosis (or other inherited retinal dystrophy) caused by biallelic RPE65 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDL",
    "long_answer": "Intermediate-density lipoprotein (IDL), the remnant of VLDL, carries ApoB-100 together with several copies of apolipoprotein E (ApoE), the ligand that allows hepatic receptor uptake. Homozygosity for the defective ApoE2 isoform blocks this clearance, causing accumulation of IDL/β-VLDL particles and resulting in familial dysbetalipoproteinemia (Type III hyperlipoproteinemia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactic acid",
    "long_answer": "In Kearns–Sayre syndrome, defective mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation forces cells to rely more on anaerobic glycolysis, leading to excess production of lactate. This causes lactic acid to accumulate in the bloodstream, resulting in the characteristic elevation of blood lactate levels seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein",
    "long_answer": "In limb development the interdigital mesenchyme expresses bone morphogenetic proteins (notably BMP-2/BMP-4). These BMP signals trigger caspase-dependent apoptosis of the cells between the forming digits, removing the intervening tissue so the fingers separate. If BMP-driven apoptosis is blocked, the webbing persists, producing syndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (45,X gonadal dysgenesis) is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea. Affected girls have streak ovaries that produce little or no estrogen, leading to failure of breast development and other secondary sexual characteristics, and consequently no onset of menses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital heart defect",
    "long_answer": "Ectopia cordis frequently co-occurs with congenital heart defects—structural abnormalities of the heart or great vessels that arise during embryogenesis and are present at birth, such as ventricular or atrial septal defects and tetralogy of Fallot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MCT8",
    "long_answer": "Allan–Herndon–Dudley syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC16A2 gene, which encodes the monocarboxylate transporter 8 (MCT8). MCT8 is a high-affinity, neuron-specific transporter responsible for importing the active thyroid hormone triiodothyronine (T3) into developing and mature neurons; its deficiency disrupts cerebral thyroid hormone availability and leads to the neurological and endocrine manifestations of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL11A2",
    "long_answer": "Otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the COL11A2 gene, which encodes the α2 chain of type XI collagen. Defective type XI collagen impairs cartilage and inner-ear extracellular matrix structure, producing the characteristic skeletal abnormalities and severe hearing loss seen in OSMED."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver transplantation; liver",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis produces chronic inflammation and fibrosis of the intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts, ultimately leading to biliary cirrhosis. The only definitive, curative therapy for this condition is orthotopic liver transplantation, because the organ principally damaged by the disease is the liver itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complex partial seizure",
    "long_answer": "In focal (partial) seizures, when the abnormal electrical activity spreads to a broader region of one cerebral hemisphere, consciousness becomes impaired. This presentation is classified as a complex partial seizure (now termed a focal-onset impaired-awareness seizure). It contrasts with simple partial seizures, where awareness is preserved and the discharge remains limited to a smaller cortical area."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anotia",
    "long_answer": "Anotia is a congenital malformation in which the external ear (pinna) and often the external auditory canal are completely absent. Because the sound-conducting structures of the outer ear are missing, affected individuals typically experience conductive hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BMPR1B",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the BMPR1B gene (Bone Morphogenetic Protein Receptor Type 1B) cause brachydactyly type A2. BMPR1B is a key receptor in BMP signaling that drives chondrocyte differentiation during limb development, and it is specifically required for normal formation and elongation of the middle and proximal phalanges of the digits. Loss-of-function variants disrupt this process, producing the characteristic shortening of fingers and toes seen in brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylketonuria",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in both alleles of the PAH gene—even if the two mutant alleles are different (compound heterozygosity)—reduce or abolish phenylalanine hydroxylase activity. This autosomal-recessive loss of enzyme function leads to accumulation of phenylalanine and the clinical phenotype known as phenylketonuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A. Siewert",
    "long_answer": "The earliest published description of the triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis—the disorder now called Kartagener syndrome—was by the German physician A. Siewert in 1904; Manes Kartagener later popularised the condition in 1933, but Siewert’s case report was the first on record."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paramyxoviridae",
    "long_answer": "Human parainfluenza viruses (HPIV types 1-4) are classified in the family Paramyxoviridae. All paramyxoviruses are enveloped, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA viruses, meaning their entire genome is composed of RNA rather than DNA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pachydermoperiostosis",
    "long_answer": "Pachydermoperiostosis—also known as Touraine-Solente-Golé syndrome—is the primary form of hypertrophic osteo-arthropathy. It is defined by periosteal new bone formation (abnormal bone growth), marked thickening of the skin (pachydermia) and can include ocular manifestations such as corneal clouding or leukoma. These features match the description of abnormal growth of bone and skin together with corneal opacification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive retinal atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Progressive retinal atrophy (PRA) is an inherited ocular disorder in dogs in which both retinas undergo progressive, bilateral degeneration of the photoreceptors, ultimately leading to blindness. Genetic linkage studies have mapped one of the PRA loci to the ninth canine chromosome (CFA9), confirming its hereditary basis and chromosomal location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Beare–Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome results from gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR2 gene. Mutations in a single allele are sufficient to produce the phenotype, so the disorder follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance, with most affected individuals carrying a de novo mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transposition of the great arteries",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent congenital heart defect causing cyanosis in the first days of life is complete transposition of the great arteries (d-TGA). In this lesion the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, producing an abnormal spatial arrangement of the great vessels and parallel, rather than serial, circulations. Because it is both common in the neonatal period and defined by malposition of the great arteries, d-TGA is the prototypical and most common congenital heart lesion fitting the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alan Lyell",
    "long_answer": "Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is often called Lyell’s syndrome because it was first described and delineated as a distinct clinical entity in 1956 by the Scottish dermatologist Alan Lyell."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Characidae",
    "long_answer": "A classic model for the study of albinism in fish is the Mexican tetra, Astyanax mexicanus. Both surface-dwelling pigmented individuals and naturally occurring albino cave forms exist within this single species. Astyanax mexicanus is taxonomically placed in the family Characidae (the characins or tetras), making Characidae the family to which this albino-capable fish species belongs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NPC1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Niemann–Pick disease type C, the form that presents with neurological features such as dystonia, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NPC1 gene. NPC1 encodes a lysosomal membrane protein required for intracellular cholesterol and lipid trafficking; its dysfunction leads to lipid accumulation in neurons and consequent movement disorders including dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bilirubin",
    "long_answer": "Hemoglobin from senescent red blood cells is degraded to biliverdin and then to bilirubin. This pigment enters the gut via bile, where intestinal bacteria act on it. When bilirubin accumulates in the bloodstream—as can occur in newborns whose livers are not yet efficient at conjugating and excreting it—it produces the yellowish skin and eye discoloration characteristic of neonatal jaundice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "Liver cirrhosis—a progressive, irreversible scarring of the liver resulting from chronic insults such as viral hepatitis, alcohol misuse, and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease—is the leading indication for liver transplantation worldwide. Because it causes both premature mortality and prolonged morbidity, cirrhosis accounts for millions of disability-adjusted life years (DALYs), making it a major contributor to the global disease burden."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein conformational diseases",
    "long_answer": "Amyloidosis belongs to the broader class of protein conformational (protein-misfolding) diseases, in which normally soluble proteins adopt aberrant conformations, misfold, and aggregate into insoluble deposits that disrupt tissue function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Morquio syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV) is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disease caused by deficiency of either N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase (type IVA) or β-galactosidase (type IVB). Accumulation of keratan sulfate and related glycosaminoglycans in lysosomes disrupts endochondral bone growth, producing the characteristic short-trunk dwarfism and skeletal dysplasia. Thus, it is both a genetic cause of dwarfism and a well-recognized lysosomal storage disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin D deficiency",
    "long_answer": "The bone disorder in question is rickets/osteomalacia, which features poor mineralization and defective bone development and is frequently seen in patients with chronic renal disease. The fundamental mechanism is a deficiency of active vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxy-vitamin D), whether from inadequate intake/sunlight or impaired renal hydroxylation; this deficiency prevents normal calcium and phosphate absorption, leading to the characteristic bone defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia most commonly results from mutations in the EDA gene (ectodysplasin A). EDA encodes a signaling protein essential for the embryonic development of skin appendages such as sweat glands, hair follicles, and teeth; loss-of-function mutations disrupt these pathways, producing the characteristic skin, hair, and sweat-gland defects of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cartilage-hair hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Cartilage-hair hypoplasia (CHH) is an autosomal-recessive metaphyseal chondrodysplasia caused by mutations in the RMRP gene. It presents with disproportionate short stature, bowing of the long bones due to metaphyseal dysplasia, sparse fine hair, and a variable T- and B-cell defect that can manifest as severe combined immunodeficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia is caused in most patients by biallelic pathogenic variants in one of several FA pathway genes (e.g., FANCA, FANCC, FANCG). These mutant alleles must be inherited from both parents before disease manifests, fitting the classical autosomal recessive inheritance pattern. Although a rare X-linked form exists (FANCB), the predominant mode of transmission for Fanconi anemia is autosomal recessive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Geroderma osteodysplasticum",
    "long_answer": "Geroderma osteodysplasticum is a very rare autosomal-recessive connective-tissue and bone dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GORAB gene. A hallmark skeletal feature of the disorder is progressive metaphyseal malalignment that produces severe genu valgum, which often first appears or markedly worsens in the rapid growth period just after puberty. The condition is therefore noted for ‘post-puberty genu valgum’ together with osteopenia, joint laxity and prematurely aged, wrinkled skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DST (dystonin)",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary sensory neuropathy type 6 (HSAN-VI) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the DST gene, which encodes the cytoskeletal linker protein dystonin/BPAG1. Dystonin is essential for the trafficking and maturation of autophagosomes; DST mutations block autophagic flux, causing accumulation of autophagic vacuoles in neurons and thereby driving the HSAN-VI phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurocutaneous melanosis",
    "long_answer": "Neurocutaneous melanosis is a neurocutaneous disorder characterized by large or multiple giant congenital melanocytic nevi on the skin together with proliferations of melanocytes (melanocytic tumors) within the leptomeninges and other parts of the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lowe syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Lowe syndrome (oculocerebrorenal syndrome) is an X-linked disorder caused by mutations in the OCRL gene. A characteristic triad is present: (1) bilateral congenital cataracts, (2) intellectual disability from central nervous system involvement, and (3) proximal renal tubular dysfunction that produces low-molecular-weight proteinuria along with other Fanconi-type losses. Hence, the syndrome that links congenital cataracts and intellectual disability with proteinuria is Lowe syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poly-L-lactic acid",
    "long_answer": "Cutis verticis gyrata can be managed cosmetically by filling the deep scalp furrows. Poly-L-lactic acid (PLLA) is a biodegradable synthetic polymer injectable (marketed as Sculptra) widely used to restore facial volume. Case reports have shown that injecting PLLA into the scalp folds stimulates collagen deposition and elevates the depressions, improving the appearance of cutis verticis gyrata, making PLLA a proposed minimally-invasive treatment option."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angelman syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Angelman syndrome arises when the maternally inherited 15q11-q13 region is deleted or otherwise inactivated. Because several genes in this imprinted region are normally expressed only from the maternal allele (the paternal alleles are silenced), loss of the maternal copy eliminates their expression, producing the characteristic neurodevelopmental disorder known as Angelman syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The Versican gene (VCAN, formerly CSPG2), whose mutations cause Wagner’s vitreoretinopathy (Wagner’s disease), is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 at band 5q14.3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MECP2 gene, which is located on the X chromosome, are the primary cause of classic Rett syndrome. MECP2 encodes methyl-CpG-binding protein 2, a protein that binds methylated DNA to regulate chromatin structure and gene transcription. Beyond its transcriptional role, MECP2 participates in the DNA damage response by interacting with chromatin-remodeling and repair complexes, thereby contributing to genome maintenance. Hence, MECP2 mutations link Rett syndrome with DNA repair processes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX",
    "long_answer": "Langer Mesomelic Dysplasia results from complete loss-of-function mutations or deletions of the SHOX (short stature homeobox) gene located in the pseudo-autosomal region of the X and Y chromosomes. Homozygous or compound heterozygous defects in SHOX abolish its transcription factor activity in growth-plate chondrocytes, leading to the severe mesomelic shortening characteristic of this dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX3",
    "long_answer": "The PAX3 transcription-factor gene is classically cited as pleiotropic: it is required in several tissues during development. Complete (homozygous) loss-of-function of PAX3 in mice prevents closure of the cranial neural tube, producing exencephaly/anencephaly. In heterozygotes the same mutation affects melanocyte and inner-ear development, causing pigmentation and hearing defects (analogous to Waardenburg syndrome in humans). Thus PAX3 inactivation gives multiple, seemingly unrelated phenotypes, exemplifying pleiotropy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈500,000–650,000 bp",
    "long_answer": "Koolen–De Vries syndrome results from the recurrent microdeletion at chromosome 17q21.31 that spans roughly 500–650 kilobases, i.e., about 500,000 to 650,000 base pairs, invariably including the KANSL1 gene whose haplo-insufficiency produces the syndrome’s clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "Leptospira species commonly infect the liver, producing jaundice in severe leptospirosis (Weil’s disease). The liver is also central to digestion and nutrient absorption because it secretes bile for fat emulsification and metabolizes carbohydrates, lipids and proteins absorbed from the intestine. Thus, the liver is both a target organ in leptospirosis and a key organ in digestion and nutrient processing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinemia type II is an inborn error of tyrosine metabolism due to tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency. Management centers on genetic and metabolic evaluation, dietary restriction of tyrosine and phenylalanine, and long-term monitoring of systemic complications—tasks routinely coordinated by physicians trained in medical genetics (metabolic genetics). Consequently, patients with Tyrosinemia type II are most often managed by medical geneticists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Perseus",
    "long_answer": "In Greek mythology, the hero Perseus slew the only mortal Gorgon, Medusa. When Perseus decapitated Medusa, her blood gave rise to the winged horse Pegasus and the warrior Chrysaor, fulfilling the mythological account."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incomplete penetrance",
    "long_answer": "Rosselli–Gulienetti syndrome is produced by heterozygous (autosomal-dominant) mutations in TP63. Not every individual who carries the mutation develops the characteristic ectodermal-limb defects; only a subset of carriers are clinically affected. This indicates that the mutation shows incomplete (reduced) penetrance rather than full penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipedema",
    "long_answer": "The condition once labeled “adiposis dolorosa” when it presents as symmetric, bilateral enlargement of the lower limbs is now called lipedema. Lipedema is a chronic disorder of adipose tissue seen almost exclusively in women, characterized by abnormal deposition and proliferation of subcutaneous fat from the hips to the ankles, sparing the feet. This leads to disproportionate enlargement of both legs, tenderness, and easy bruising, distinguishing it from simple obesity or lymphedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ellis-van Creveld syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ellis-van Creveld syndrome results from biallelic mutations in the EVC or EVC2 genes, both situated at 4p16 on chromosome 4. The encoded proteins localize to the base of primary cilia and are essential for Hedgehog signaling; their loss disrupts ciliary function, so the disorder is classified among the human ciliopathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive-behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "Carcinophobia is categorized as a specific phobia that manifests as intense, persistent anxiety about cancer. Evidence-based clinical guidelines for both cancer-related anxiety and specific phobias recommend cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT)—particularly its components of cognitive restructuring and graduated exposure—as the first-line, non-pharmacologic treatment. CBT helps patients identify and challenge catastrophic thoughts about cancer, learn relaxation and coping skills, and gradually reduce avoidance behaviors, thereby effectively managing anxiety in cancer patients with carcinophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "I-cell disease",
    "long_answer": "I-cell disease (mucolipidosis II) is a lysosomal storage disorder in which a defect in N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase prevents addition of the mannose-6-phosphate tag that targets hydrolase enzymes to lysosomes. Because the enzymes are mis-routed and inactive inside lysosomes, undegraded substrates accumulate, producing coarse facial features, gingival hyperplasia, growth delay and other systemic manifestations characteristic of this metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Medical genetics is the branch of medicine devoted to identifying, explaining, and managing hereditary and chromosomal disorders. Conditions such as Trisomy 9 result from an extra copy of chromosome 9; evaluating their origin, inheritance patterns, recurrence risk, and clinical consequences falls squarely within the remit of medical genetics specialists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMARCAL1",
    "long_answer": "The rare autosomal-recessive disorder first described by Schimke is Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia (SIOD). Pathogenic variants in the SMARCAL1 gene (SWI/SNF-related, matrix-associated, actin-dependent regulator of chromatin subfamily A-like 1) are responsible for SIOD. Molecular confirmation of the disease is therefore achieved by testing for SMARCAL1 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Joker: Folie à Deux",
    "long_answer": "The follow-up to the 2019 film “Joker” is officially titled “Joker: Folie à Deux.” The subtitle uses the medical term for shared delusional disorder (folie à deux), indicating the sequel’s thematic focus on that psychiatric condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enzyme",
    "long_answer": "Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) is an enzyme that catalyzes the esterification of free cholesterol in plasma lipoproteins, principally HDL. Loss-of-function mutations in this enzyme cause familial LCAT deficiency and the milder Fish-eye disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinemia type II (Richner–Hanhart syndrome) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TAT gene on chromosome 16. Because disease manifestations occur only when both alleles are defective, the trait is transmitted in an autosomal recessive manner: affected individuals inherit one mutant allele from each carrier parent, while heterozygous carriers are typically asymptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Remission",
    "long_answer": "In clinical gastroenterology, the complete resolution of rectal bleeding, urgency, pain, and endoscopic inflammation in proctitis is termed 'remission.' This word is universally used to denote the total disappearance of symptoms and signs of inflammatory bowel conditions after effective therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PR-B",
    "long_answer": "The PR-B isoform of the progesterone receptor is essential for normal mammary gland development, driving ductal side-branching and alveolar formation. In breast cancer, PR-B activation up-regulates genes that enhance cell proliferation, survival, and metastatic potential, thereby promoting tumour progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A and zinc supplementation",
    "long_answer": "In its cost-benefit analysis of global interventions, the Copenhagen Consensus ranked providing young children with vitamin A and zinc tablets as one of the four most cost-effective solutions, because these micronutrients rapidly correct zinc deficiency, lower infection-related morbidity and mortality, and promote healthy growth at minimal cost."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacrum",
    "long_answer": "Maternal diabetes mellitus is strongly linked to caudal regression (sacral agenesis), a congenital malformation in which the caudal part of the vertebral column fails to develop. About one-third of infants with this disorder have diabetic mothers. The primary structure affected is the sacrum (lower spine), leading to lower-body neurologic and musculoskeletal defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HMBS gene",
    "long_answer": "Acute Intermittent Porphyria results from mutations in the HMBS gene (also called porphobilinogen deaminase, PBGD) located on chromosome 11q23. Loss-of-function variants reduce hepatic porphobilinogen deaminase activity, leading to accumulation of ALA and PBG and producing the characteristic autosomal-dominant porphyric attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. In late April – early May 2020, the Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health (RCPCH) published interim clinical guidance for UK clinicians on a new paediatric inflammatory condition that was emerging during the COVID-19 pandemic. The document, titled “Paediatric Multisystem Inflammatory Syndrome temporally associated with SARS-CoV-2 (PIMS-TS),” describes diagnostic criteria for children presenting with persistent fever, elevated inflammatory markers and features that overlap with, or mimic, classic and atypical Kawasaki disease and Kawasaki-shock syndrome. The guidance outlines the clinical case definition, recommended laboratory tests, differential diagnosis and management principles. It was widely circulated to help paediatricians recognise and treat this Kawasaki-like illness linked to SARS-CoV-2 infection. Hence, the RCPCH has indeed issued guidance for diagnosing a Kawasaki-like disease associated with COVID-19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL3A1 gene, which encodes type III collagen, cause the vascular form of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Defective type III collagen weakens blood-vessel and tissue integrity, leading to hallmark features such as easy bruising, translucent skin, arterial and organ fragility, making vascular EDS the connective-tissue disorder linked to COL3A1 variation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST (SPG4)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPAST gene, also known as SPG4 and encoding the microtubule-severing protein spastin, are the single most frequent cause of hereditary spastic paraplegia. Large clinical–genetic series consistently show that SPAST mutations explain about 40 % of autosomal-dominant HSP and roughly 25 % of all HSP cases across inheritance patterns, making it the predominant gene implicated in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nondisjunction",
    "long_answer": "Pentasomy X (49,XXXXX) arises when one or more meiotic divisions fail to separate the X chromosomes properly—a process called nondisjunction. This meiotic nondisjunction produces an egg (or, rarely, a sperm) containing extra X chromosomes. When it is fertilized, the resulting zygote carries five X chromosomes instead of the normal two, leading to the chromosomal disorder Pentasomy X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome",
    "long_answer": "David Middleton Greig is commemorated in the eponymous Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome, a chromosomal condition caused by pathogenic variants in the GLI3 gene on chromosome 7p13, characterized by craniofacial enlargement (cephalo-), extra and fused digits (poly- and syndactyly)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDOB (Aldolase B) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary fructose intolerance is an autosomal-recessive metabolic disorder produced by pathogenic mutations in the ALDOB gene, which encodes the hepatic enzyme fructose-1-phosphate aldolase (aldolase B). Defective aldolase B prevents proper cleavage of fructose-1-phosphate, leading to its toxic accumulation in liver, kidney and intestine, and causing the characteristic hypoglycemia, vomiting, and hepatic/renal dysfunction seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Francis I of France",
    "long_answer": "Francis I (reigned 1515-1547) kept the famous court jester Triboulet, who was markedly deformed—contemporaries describe an abnormally small, misshapen head and dwarf-stature that today would be classified as microcephaly. The congenital disorder microcephaly was already recognized in early modern medical writings, so Triboulet’s condition was noted and discussed by physicians of the time. Thus, the French king associated with a jester exhibiting the physical deformity characteristic of known microcephaly is Francis I of France."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTEN",
    "long_answer": "Cowden syndrome is most commonly caused by germ-line mutations in the PTEN gene. PTEN encodes a lipid phosphatase that dephosphorylates PIP3, thereby antagonizing the PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling cascade that is activated downstream of receptor tyrosine kinases. Loss-of-function PTEN mutations thus dysregulate this receptor tyrosine kinase pathway, leading to the hamartomas and cancer predisposition characteristic of Cowden syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xp22.12",
    "long_answer": "Coffin-Lowry syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the RPS6KA3 (RSK2) gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band Xp22.12 on the short arm of the X chromosome. This Xp22.12 locus is therefore the chromosomal location linked to the X-linked intellectual disability phenotype seen in Coffin-Lowry syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATXN7",
    "long_answer": "The ATXN7 gene encodes ataxin-7, a core component of the SAGA (Spt-Ada-Gcn5 acetyltransferase) transcriptional co-activator complex. Within SAGA, ataxin-7 helps coordinate histone acetyltransferase and deubiquitinase activities that modify histones and thereby regulate gene transcription. CAG repeat expansion in ATXN7 causes spinocerebellar ataxia type 7, an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercapnia",
    "long_answer": "In centrilobular emphysema, destruction of terminal bronchioles and loss of alveolar surface area impair gas exchange and ventilation. This can lead to retention of carbon dioxide, producing hypercapnia (elevated PaCO₂) as a significant complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-HTTLPR short allele",
    "long_answer": "Possession of the short (s) allele of the serotonin-transporter-linked polymorphic region (5-HTTLPR) in the SLC6A4 gene has been consistently associated with a higher likelihood of developing social anxiety disorder. The short allele reduces serotonin-transporter expression, heightening amygdala reactivity to social threat cues and thereby increasing vulnerability to social anxiety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VLDLR",
    "long_answer": "Mutations that disrupt the VLDLR gene (encoding the very-low-density-lipoprotein receptor) cause the autosomal-recessive condition now called VLDLR-associated cerebellar hypoplasia, a form of dysequilibrium syndrome (CAMRQ type 1). This disorder—characterised by non-progressive ataxia, mental retardation, and impaired balance—was first reported in the 1970s in Hutterite families and initially classified as an unusual variant of cerebral palsy. Subsequent genetic studies showed that loss-of-function mutations in VLDLR underlie the syndrome, establishing the gene’s causal role." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The genetic disorders characterized by premature fusion of the skull’s sutures (e.g., craniosynostosis syndromes such as Crouzon, Apert, and Muenke) are transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion—only one mutated allele is sufficient to cause the condition, and it can be passed from an affected parent to offspring with a 50% probability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BTK gene",
    "long_answer": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia results from loss-of-function mutations in the BTK (Bruton’s tyrosine kinase) gene. BTK is a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase essential for signaling downstream of the pre-B-cell receptor; its absence blocks B-cell maturation, leading to profoundly reduced serum immunoglobulins characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly-hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "The condition called brachydactyly-hypoplasia is a congenital limb malformation in which brachydactyly (abnormally short fingers or toes) occurs together with hypoplasia, the incomplete or deficient development of the associated bones, soft tissues, or other organs. Thus it simultaneously embodies digit shortening and tissue underdevelopment within the same developmental defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apert syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome (acrocephalosyndactyly type I) is defined by early fusion of cranial sutures that produces an oxycephalic or tower-shaped skull and by characteristic mid-facial abnormalities concentrated around the nasal base and lips (depressed nasal bridge, short columella, thick everted lips, cleft palate in some cases). These findings, together with syndactyly of the hands and feet, make Apert syndrome the prototypic condition linking oxycephaly with nasolabial facial anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "United Nations",
    "long_answer": "World Down Syndrome Day (21 March) was formally recognized in 2012 when the United Nations General Assembly adopted resolution A/RES/66/149, designating the date as an international day of awareness for Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC9",
    "long_answer": "The ABCC9 gene (ATP-binding cassette sub-family C member 9), which encodes the SUR2 subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, resides on chromosome 12p12.1—the short arm of chromosome 12. Gain-of-function mutations in ABCC9 are the predominant cause of Cantú syndrome. Variants in the same locus have also been reported in genetic studies of spinal anomalies, including scoliosis, linking this 12p gene to both Cantú syndrome and scoliosis phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CABIN syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The described constellation of findings corresponds to CABIN syndrome – an acronym for Cerebellar Ataxia, Brachydactyly, Intellectual disability, and Nystagmus. Reported patients present with shortened digits (brachydactyly) together with ocular motor abnormalities such as nystagmus and strabismus, marked cerebellar ataxia, and global developmental/intellectual impairment, matching all features listed in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The SLCO2A1 gene, whose mutations cause the primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy/Pachydermoperiostosis phenotype, is located on human chromosome 3 (cytogenetic band 3q22)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ocular hypertelorism",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome with multiple lentigines (formerly LEOPARD syndrome) is characterized by the mnemonic L-E-O-P-A-R-D, where the “O” stands for ocular hypertelorism. This refers to an abnormally increased distance between the eyes, giving a wide-spaced appearance that is readily visible on physical examination and is one of the hallmark ocular manifestations of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain",
    "long_answer": "Rocky Mountain spotted fever (caused by Rickettsia rickettsii) can produce vasculitis that involves the central nervous system, leading to complications such as encephalitis, seizures, and altered mental status. The affected CNS structure is the brain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chigger mites",
    "long_answer": "Scrub typhus is caused by the bacterium Orientia tsutsugamushi and is transmitted to humans through the bite of infected larval trombiculid mites, commonly called chiggers (especially of the genus Leptotrombidium). These mites acquire the bacterium from small mammals and pass it to people when they feed, thereby spreading the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT II) deficiency is an inherited fatty-acid oxidation disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the CPT2 gene. Defective CPT II impairs transport of long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria, limiting energy production during prolonged exercise or fasting. The energy deficit leads to episodic muscle pain (myalgia), weakness, and rhabdomyolysis, especially under metabolic stress, making CPT II deficiency a well-known genetic cause of muscle pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST",
    "long_answer": "The SPAST gene (also known as SPG4), which encodes the microtubule-severing protein spastin, is the most common cause of hereditary spastic paraplegia. Pathogenic SPAST variants are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern and are estimated to account for roughly 25 % of all HSP cases (and ~40 % of autosomal-dominant HSP), making it the principal genetic contributor to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TUBB4A gene (Tubulin β-4A), mapped to chromosome 19p13.3, cause the DYT4 form of dystonia, classically presenting as the rare ‘whispering’ or spasmodic dysphonia. The p.Arg2Gly substitution in TUBB4A disrupts β-tubulin function, leading to the characteristic laryngeal dystonia phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Möbius syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Möbius syndrome is classically characterized by congenital palsy of cranial nerves VI and VII, leading to weakness or paralysis of the extra-ocular (especially abducens-controlled) muscles and facial muscles. Limb anomalies, including clubfoot and generalized arthrogryposis (multiple joint contractures), are frequently associated with this syndrome, making Möbius syndrome the best-established arthrogryposis-associated disorder that involves extraocular muscle weakness or paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ochoa syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ochoa syndrome, also called urofacial syndrome, was first reported by Ochoa and Gorlin in 1968. The condition is characterized by severe functional abnormalities of the lower urinary tract (neurogenic bladder, vesicoureteral reflux, hydroureteronephrosis) often leading to recurrent urinary-tract infections and renal failure. These genitourinary defects occur together with a pathognomonic ‘inverted’ facial grimace, and the disorder is now known to result from recessive mutations in genes such as HPSE2 or LRIG2. Thus, the syndrome squarely involves genitourinary system abnormalities and was indeed first described in 1968."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Functional neurological disorder",
    "long_answer": "Functional neurological disorder (formerly called conversion disorder) presents with seizure-like episodes that mimic epilepsy and motor symptoms such as paralysis, tremor, or gait disturbance. It is diagnosed only after investigations show no anatomical or physiological neurological lesion, indicating the symptoms arise from abnormal nervous-system functioning rather than structural disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPTLC1",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant form of hereditary sensory neuropathy (HSN type I) is most commonly caused by missense mutations in the SPTLC1 gene, which encodes the long-chain‐base subunit-1 of serine palmitoyltransferase. These mutations reduce normal enzyme activity, leading to accumulation of neurotoxic deoxysphingolipids and progressive sensory neuropathy in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naturism",
    "long_answer": "Naturism (also called social nudism) is a lifestyle and cultural movement whose adherents practice non-sexual social nudity, often for body-acceptance and health reasons; this exposure to unclothed bodies would be particularly difficult for someone with gymnophobia, the clinical fear of nudity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bartter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bartter syndrome is a salt-wasting renal tubulopathy caused by loss-of-function mutations in transport proteins (e.g., NKCC2, ROMK, CLC-Kb) that reside in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. A rare familial form of Bartter syndrome has been reported in the same kindreds that manifest the very uncommon neurological–ocular disorder termed spastic ataxia–corneal dystrophy syndrome, establishing an association between the two conditions. Thus, Bartter syndrome satisfies both features mentioned in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Small cell lung carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Ectopic production of antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) by tumor cells is most commonly seen in small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC). Up to 10–15 % of patients with SCLC develop SIADH, and the majority of paraneoplastic SIADH cases are linked to this cancer, making it the classic malignancy associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis–renal dysfunction–cholestasis (ARCS) syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene, which encodes a Sec1/Munc18-like protein essential for intracellular vesicle trafficking. Pathogenic variants in VPS33B disrupt apical–basolateral protein sorting in epithelial cells, leading to the characteristic triad of congenital contractures, renal tubular dysfunction, and cholestatic liver disease observed in this genodermatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the paired-box transcription factor PAX2 are found in patients with either bilateral or unilateral renal agenesis. During early kidney development PAX2 up-regulates the anti-apoptotic protein BCL-2, ensuring survival of ureteric-bud and metanephric-mesenchyme cells. When PAX2 is mutated this protective effect is lost, excessive apoptosis occurs, and renal development arrests, leading to BRA or URA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "c.521delT mutation",
    "long_answer": "The single-nucleotide deletion c.521delT in the SGCA gene (historically also reported as 525delT) produces a frameshift leading to premature truncation of α-sarcoglycan. This recurrent founder mutation has been identified in many populations and is estimated to represent up to one-third of all mutant SGCA alleles observed in sarcoglycanopathy (LGMD2D) patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Positive-sense single-stranded RNA",
    "long_answer": "Kyasanur Forest disease virus is a member of the Flaviviridae family. All flaviviruses possess a monopartite, positive-sense, single-stranded RNA genome of about 11 kilobases, which serves directly as mRNA for viral protein synthesis. Therefore, the virus that causes Kyasanur Forest disease has a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pericarditis",
    "long_answer": "Tricuspid valve dysplasia in dogs is frequently associated with pericarditis, an inflammatory condition of the pericardium—the fibrous sac that surrounds the heart. Pericarditis causes thickening, fluid accumulation, and adhesions around the heart, which can exacerbate the hemodynamic consequences of the malformed tricuspid valve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Androgen insensitivity syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Androgen insensitivity syndrome is an X-linked genetic disorder caused by mutations in the androgen-receptor (AR) gene. Because target tissues cannot respond to androgens, 46,XY individuals develop female external genitalia but lack a uterus and upper vagina. Without a functional uterus they cannot menstruate, so the primary presentation is absence of menstruation (primary amenorrhoea) despite normal or high circulating androgen levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Huron",
    "long_answer": "Iosheka (more often spelled Iouskeha/Ioskeha) is the culture-hero in Huron mythology who fashioned the first man and woman. The Huron, also called Wendat, formed the Huron Confederacy and were longtime enemies of the Iroquois Confederacy, against whom they fought repeatedly in the 17th-century Beaver Wars. Thus, the Indigenous nation associated with the creator figure Iosheka is the Huron."
}

{
    "short_answer": "United States",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is widely called “Lou Gehrig’s disease” after the famed American baseball player who was diagnosed with ALS in 1939; this eponym is specific to U.S. usage and is not the standard term in most other countries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Actinobacillosis",
    "long_answer": "The bacterial disease actinobacillosis, caused by Actinobacillus lignieresii, commonly produces large, soft granulomatous abscesses on the head, neck and oral region of cattle (the classic “wooden tongue” or cutaneous form). These abscesses arise when the organism, a normal inhabitant of the bovine oral cavity, enters tissues through minor wounds, leading to chronic pyogranulomatous inflammation and marked swelling in those areas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ITM2B",
    "long_answer": "Both Familial British dementia (FBD) and Familial Danish dementia (FDD) are autosomal-dominant disorders produced by distinct mutations in the same gene, ITM2B (also called BRI2). In FBD a stop-codon point mutation (E693Q) in ITM2B creates the amyloidogenic peptide ABri, while in FDD a 10-base-pair duplication in ITM2B generates the peptide ADan. Thus, pathogenic mutations in the ITM2B gene underlie both dementias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dose–response relationship",
    "long_answer": "In Oppositional Defiant Disorder, increasing numbers or severity of oppositional-defiant symptoms are systematically associated with proportionally greater functional impairment; this graded correspondence is referred to as a dose–response relationship."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heart failure",
    "long_answer": "Joseph Joachim died in Berlin on 15 August 1907 after a protracted period of chronic heart disease; the immediate terminal event recorded was acute heart failure, which ended the celebrated Hungarian violinist’s life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Livedo reticularis",
    "long_answer": "The mottled, net-like discoloration of the skin seen in livedo reticularis resembles the pattern of cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita (CMTC), but it arises from transient or chronic reduction of blood flow in cutaneous arterioles and capillaries (vasospasm or intravascular obstruction). This hypoperfusion produces the characteristic violaceous, reticular pattern, distinguishing livedo reticularis from the congenital vascular malformation of CMTC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The Lancet Infectious Diseases",
    "long_answer": "Georgios Pappas’ account of the event—described as the largest laboratory accident ever recorded in infectious-disease history and caused by aerosolised Brucella bacteria—was published as part of his review article in the journal The Lancet Infectious Diseases. This journal issue documented the magnitude of the accident and its public-health implications, making it the authoritative source for that report."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology a “syndrome” is the label given to a specific, recurrent constellation of characteristic signs and symptoms that occur together. Al-Gazali-Sabrinathan-Nair syndrome represents one such defined pattern, so the correct term for its characteristic clinical grouping is simply a syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Goodpasture syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome is caused by genetic defects in type IV collagen, a critical component of the glomerular basement membrane. The autoimmune disease Goodpasture syndrome (also called anti-glomerular basement membrane disease) specifically targets the α3 chain of the same type IV collagen, leading to rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and pulmonary hemorrhage. Thus, Goodpasture syndrome targets the same collagen type involved in Alport syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "About 70–80 % of nonsyndromic congenital (birth-defect) deafness is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, in which affected children receive two pathogenic alleles—one from each carrier parent—while carriers have normal hearing. Other patterns (autosomal dominant, X-linked, mitochondrial) occur less frequently."
}

{
    "short_answer": "46,XY agonadism",
    "long_answer": "46,XY agonadism (pure 46,XY gonadal agenesis) is the condition in which a person with a normal male karyotype completely lacks both gonads. Without testicular tissue no androgens are produced, so masculinisation of the external genitalia does not occur and, in rare cases, this results in penile agenesis (aphallia), the intersex variation where the phallus is absent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple organ failure",
    "long_answer": "Extreme hyperthermia (as in heat stroke) can trigger systemic inflammatory and coagulative responses that damage the liver, kidneys, heart, brain, and other tissues, culminating in multiple organ failure—a life-threatening syndrome in which several vital organs lose function simultaneously."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The clinical triad of aortic arch anomaly, characteristic facial features, and intellectual disability is produced by a hemizygous chromosomal deletion; because one copy of the affected autosomal region is sufficient to cause the phenotype, the disorder segregates in families with a 50 % risk to each child of an affected parent. Hence, despite many de-novo cases, the established mode of inheritance is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FTSJ1",
    "long_answer": "The FTSJ1 gene, located on Xp11.23, is implicated in nonsyndromic X-linked intellectual disability. It encodes a protein homologous to bacterial FtsJ rRNA methyltransferases and is believed to act as an S-adenosyl-L-methionine–dependent 2′-O-ribose methyltransferase for ribosomal RNA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NHS",
    "long_answer": "The X-linked form of nystagmus that occurs together with congenital zonular cataract is caused by pathogenic variants in the NHS gene on Xp22. Loss-of-function mutations in NHS (the Nance-Horan syndrome gene) impair normal lens development and ocular motor pathways, producing the characteristic combination of congenital zonular cataract and infantile nystagmus that segregates in an X-linked pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GAPO syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A homozygous deletion in the ANTXR1 (anthrax toxin receptor 1) gene disrupts its function and produces the autosomal-recessive disorder known as GAPO syndrome—Growth retardation, Alopecia, Pseudoanodontia, and Optic atrophy. The optic atrophy component represents an optic neuropathy, making GAPO syndrome the genetic disorder linked to ANTXR1 deletions and optic nerve damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOS1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary gingival fibromatosis (HGF) is most commonly linked to an autosomal-dominant activating mutation in the SOS1 gene (Son-of-Sevenless-1). This mutation leads to excessive proliferation of gingival fibroblasts and overproduction of extracellular matrix, producing the characteristic gingival overgrowth seen by dental clinicians."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome type III is an autosomal-recessive deaf-blindness disorder most often caused by biallelic mutations in the CLRN1 gene (clarin-1). Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type I) is a lysosomal-storage disease produced by biallelic mutations in an entirely different gene, IDUA, which encodes the enzyme α-L-iduronidase. These two disorders involve distinct genes located on different chromosomes, with unrelated molecular functions and disease mechanisms. No single pathogenic variant is known to produce both clinical entities, and established medical genetics literature treats them as separate, gene-specific conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kidney",
    "long_answer": "Aspartylglucosaminuria results from lack of the lysosomal enzyme aspartylglucosaminidase, causing storage of glyco-asparagines in many tissues. Accumulation is especially noted in the kidneys, which are responsible for filtering the blood to remove waste and form urine. Thus, the kidney is both a principal organ affected by the enzyme deficiency and the body’s primary blood-filtering organ."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gynecomastia",
    "long_answer": "Gynecomastia is the benign (non-cancerous) enlargement of male breast glandular tissue. It is frequently seen in obese males because excess adipose tissue elevates peripheral conversion of androgens to estrogens, tipping the hormonal balance toward breast tissue proliferation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHACE syndrome",
    "long_answer": "PHACE syndrome (Posterior fossa malformations, Hemangioma, Arterial abnormalities, Cardiac defects, Eye anomalies) is a congenital neurocutaneous disorder in which the posterior fossa component often manifests as a Dandy–Walker malformation. Alongside this cerebellar/brain anomaly, affected infants frequently display cardiovascular defects such as coarctation of the aorta or other arterial malformations and a range of ocular abnormalities (e.g., coloboma, microphthalmia), matching the brain, cardiovascular, and eye involvement noted in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital syphilis",
    "long_answer": "National surveillance data released after China ended its zero-COVID-19 restrictions showed a marked uptick in newborns diagnosed with congenital syphilis—an in-utero infection with Treponema pallidum passed from untreated mothers. The rebound of adult syphilis transmission that accompanied the lifting of movement and testing controls translated into more vertical (mother-to-child) infections, increasing reported cases of this congenital condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCS1L mutation",
    "long_answer": "GRACILE syndrome is an autosomal-recessive mitochondrial disorder characterized by growth retardation, amino-aciduria, cholestasis, iron overload, lactic acidosis and early death. It is caused by pathogenic mutations in the BCS1L gene, which encodes a chaperone required for assembly of mitochondrial respiratory-chain complex III. Deficiency of complex III activity can be demonstrated by spectrophotometric enzyme assays in patient tissues, providing a biochemical diagnosis that points to BCS1L mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy (especially the infantile form known as Werdnig–Hoffmann disease) is caused by homozygous deletions or loss-of-function mutations in the SMN1 gene on chromosome 5q13. This neurodegenerative disorder should not be confused with Hoffmann syndrome, which is an unrelated hypothyroid myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lethal congenital anomaly",
    "long_answer": "Conditions such as full trisomy 16 are classified as lethal congenital anomalies—developmental defects so severe that they are incompatible with sustaining life, leading to miscarriage, stillbirth, or death shortly after birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "D4Z4 repeat contraction",
    "long_answer": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (FSHD) is most commonly caused by contraction (deletion) of the D4Z4 macrosatellite repeat array on chromosome 4q35. Reducing the repeat array to ≤10 units, in the presence of a permissive 4qA allele, allows ectopic expression of the DUX4 transcription factor in skeletal muscle, leading to the characteristic progressive muscle weakness of FSHD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CALR mutation",
    "long_answer": "In essential thrombocythemia, approximately 50–60 % of patients carry the JAK2 V617F mutation and about 3–5 % harbor MPL exon 10 mutations. Among the remaining JAK2- and MPL-negative cases, frameshift mutations in the calreticulin gene (CALR), usually in exon 9, are found in roughly two-thirds of such patients, making CALR the second most frequent driver mutation overall and the predominant mutation in the JAK2/MPL-negative subgroup."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAB Cooperative Group",
    "long_answer": "The French-American-British (FAB) Cooperative Group created the morphologic L1/L2/L3 classification that is still cited for subtyping acute lymphoblastic leukemia. This international consortium of French, American, and British hematopathologists analyzed blast cell morphology and cytochemistry to devise the standardized ALL subtypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesomelic dwarfism",
    "long_answer": "Mesomelic dwarfism is characterized by mesomelia, a disproportionate shortening of the middle limb segments—the radius-ulna in the forearms and the tibia-fibula in the lower legs—while the proximal (humerus, femur) and distal (hands, feet) segments are relatively less affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iceland",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CHST6 gene cause macular corneal dystrophy (MCD). Epidemiological studies have shown that MCD is unusually common in Iceland—much more frequent there than in most other populations—making Iceland the country with a particularly high prevalence of this CHST6-related genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Robinow syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Robinow syndrome is a skeletal dysplasia in which growth retardation is most pronounced in the middle segments of the limbs, producing mesomelic dwarfism. A distinctive “fetal face” with frontal bossing, wide-set eyes, depressed nasal bridge, and other craniofacial anomalies is typical, and affected individuals commonly have hypoplastic external genitalia (e.g., micropenis, clitoral hypoplasia). This triad of mesomelic limb shortening, head-and-face abnormalities, and genital malformations is characteristic of Robinow syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipoic-acid synthesis defects",
    "long_answer": "Some patients with glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinemia) who lack mutations in the usual glycine-cleavage system genes (GLDC, AMT, GCSH) actually have pathogenic variants in genes required for mitochondrial lipoic-acid biosynthesis or attachment (e.g., LIAS, LIPT1/LIPT2, BOLA3). Because the H-protein of the glycine-cleavage complex must be lipoylated to function, defective lipoic-acid synthesis or transfer inactivates the whole complex, producing the same biochemical and clinical picture despite normal coding sequences in GLDC or AMT." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "ARC syndrome (arthrogryposis-renal dysfunction-cholestasis) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene, which encodes a protein essential for vesicle trafficking and apical–basolateral polarity in epithelial cells. Defects in VPS33B disrupt skin barrier formation, joint development, and bile canalicular transport, producing the characteristic skin abnormalities, congenital contractures, and cholestatic liver disease seen in ARC patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MYO6 gene, which encodes the unconventional motor protein myosin VI and is located on human chromosome 6q14, are a well-established cause of autosomal dominant (DFNA22) and autosomal recessive (DFNB37) nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness. Altered myosin VI disrupts hair-cell stereocilia function in the inner ear, leading to progressive hearing loss without other syndromic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Signs",
    "long_answer": "In clinical terminology, objective indications of a disease or disorder that can be detected by physical examination, imaging, or other measurable means are called “signs.” In the context of di-ethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure, physicians look for specific physical signs—such as cervical collar deformities, vaginal adenosis, or clear-cell carcinoma lesions—that are objectively observable rather than subjectively reported by the patient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Retinoblastoma is linked to loss or mutation of the RB1 tumor-suppressor gene located on the long arm of chromosome 13, specifically at band 13q14. Deletions or rearrangements involving this chromosomal region are characteristic of the disease, establishing chromosome 13q14 as the critical site associated with retinoblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angelman syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Angelman syndrome occurs when the maternal copy of the imprinted 15q11-q13 region is deleted (or otherwise inactivated), removing expression of the maternally active UBE3A and related genes. Because the paternal alleles in this region are normally silenced by imprinting, loss of the maternal allele leaves no functional gene expression, producing the characteristic neurodevelopmental disorder. (Paternal deletions of the same region cause Prader-Willi syndrome.)"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Organic acids",
    "long_answer": "In β-ketothiolase (acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase) deficiency, the block in isoleucine catabolism and ketone-body utilization causes organic keto-acids—particularly 2-methylacetoacetate, 2-methyl-3-hydroxybutyrate and related metabolites—to accumulate. During ketoacidotic crises these organic acids rise to toxic concentrations in the blood, leading to the high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leber congenital amaurosis",
    "long_answer": "Both Angela Montenegro and Jack Hodgins are portrayed as carriers of a recessive mutation that causes Leber congenital amaurosis (LCA), an inherited retinal disorder that leads to severe vision loss or blindness from infancy. Because LCA follows an autosomal-recessive pattern, when two carriers conceive, each pregnancy carries a 25 % risk that the child will inherit two mutated alleles and manifest the disease."  
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bevacizumab",
    "long_answer": "Bevacizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to vascular endothelial growth factor-A (VEGF-A), preventing its interaction with VEGF receptors on endothelial cells. By inhibiting VEGF-mediated angiogenesis, bevacizumab reduces tumor blood-supply growth and is approved for combination treatment of advanced or metastatic non-small-cell lung cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OMIM",
    "long_answer": "The Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man (OMIM) database is the authoritative, curated resource that catalogs human genes and their pathogenic variants, linking each mutation to its associated genetic disorder. OMIM contains a dedicated entry for the LDHA gene and details the specific mutations responsible for glycogen storage disease XI, making it the appropriate database for comprehensive information on this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Oppositional Defiant Disorder is not named as a separate disability category in the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), but a child who carries an ODD diagnosis can still receive special-education services and classroom accommodations if the disorder substantially interferes with learning. Under IDEA, students whose behavioral or emotional condition \"adversely affects educational performance\" may be served in one of two eligibility categories most commonly used for ODD: (1) Emotional Disturbance (ED) and, in some cases, (2) Other Health Impairment (OHI). If ODD-related behaviors—such as chronic defiance, anger, or disruptive conduct—meet the IDEA criteria (impact on academic or social functioning and need for specially designed instruction), the child is eligible for an Individualized Education Program (IEP) and the associated accommodations and supports. Therefore, a diagnosis of ODD can indeed lead to IDEA services, provided the statutory eligibility standards are met."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant DYT4 (“whispering” or spasmodic dysphonia) is produced by missense mutations in the TUBB4A gene, which encodes β-tubulin 4A and maps to chromosome 19p13.2; thus, TUBB4A mutation is the chromosome 19–linked cause of this form of spasmodic dysphonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Feingold syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Germline loss-of-function mutations in the MYCN proto-oncogene cause Feingold syndrome (also called Feingold syndrome type 1). This autosomal-dominant developmental disorder is characterized by digital anomalies such as clinodactyly and brachymesophalangy, along with microcephaly, short stature and gastrointestinal atresias, firmly linking MYCN mutations to the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural crest cells",
    "long_answer": "During eye development, neural crest cells migrate into the anterior part of the optic cup where they differentiate into the corneal endothelium and stroma, iris stroma, trabecular meshwork and other anterior-segment tissues. If this neural crest–cell migration is defective, these structures form abnormally, producing malformations such as iridogoniodysgenesis. Hence, abnormal migration of neural crest cells underlies this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AASS gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AASS gene, which encodes the bifunctional enzyme lysine-ketoglutarate reductase/saccharopine dehydrogenase (LKR/SDH) responsible for the first two steps of lysine degradation, impair lysine catabolism. This enzymatic defect leads to accumulation of lysine in plasma, producing the metabolic disorder hyperlysinemia characterized by abnormally high blood lysine levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N. Scott Adzick",
    "long_answer": "The NIH-funded Management of Myelomeningocele Study (MOMS), which compared prenatal (fetal) versus postnatal repair of spina bifida, was led by N. Scott Adzick, MD, Surgeon-in-Chief at the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia. Dr. Adzick served as the principal investigator who oversaw the multicenter randomized trial and authored the landmark 2011 NEJM report demonstrating the benefits of prenatal repair."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Royal Statistical Society",
    "long_answer": "In the Sally Clark murder trial, paediatrician Sir Roy Meadow’s expert-witness claim that the chance of two natural cot deaths in one family was 1 in 73 million was heavily criticised. The professional body that publicly challenged this misuse of statistics—and thereby became centrally involved in the controversy over Munchausen syndrome by proxy evidence—was the Royal Statistical Society."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRCA2",
    "long_answer": "BRCA2 is one of the two major tumor-suppressor genes involved in hereditary breast–ovarian cancer syndrome. Germ-line pathogenic variants in BRCA2 not only confer a high lifetime risk of breast and ovarian cancers but are also well documented to elevate the risk of pancreatic adenocarcinoma, making BRCA2 mutation carriers several-fold more susceptible to pancreatic cancer than the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gliosis",
    "long_answer": "Neuropathological studies of Cockayne syndrome consistently report widespread reactive gliosis—an increase in astrocyte number and hypertrophy—throughout cortical and subcortical regions. This glial response is a hallmark of the disorder’s neurodegenerative changes, appearing alongside neuronal loss and demyelination in affected brains."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Luxturna",
    "long_answer": "Luxturna (voretigene neparvovec-rzyl) is the first FDA-approved in vivo gene therapy for patients with biallelic RPE65 mutation–associated retinal dystrophy, which clinically manifests as Leber congenital amaurosis type 2 and certain forms of retinitis pigmentosa. The adeno-associated virus vector delivers a functional RPE65 gene to retinal pigment epithelial cells, restoring the visual cycle and improving vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAPT",
    "long_answer": "The microtubule-associated protein tau gene (MAPT) resides on chromosome 17q21. Association studies have reported that a deletion polymorphism within MAPT (tagging the H1/H2 haplotype block) is over-represented in patients with multiple system atrophy, suggesting that loss of this MAPT segment can contribute to MSA susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Highgate",
    "long_answer": "Before Earlswood was built, Andrew Reed’s National Asylum for Idiot Children operated at Park House in the London suburb of Highgate. This Highgate school was one of the first British institutions to apply Edouard Séguin’s educational and training methods for people with intellectual disability, and John Langdon Down studied its practices closely. The experience and ideas he encountered at Highgate directly shaped the therapeutic, educational and humanitarian programme he later implemented as medical superintendent at Earlswood Asylum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is an autosomal-dominant heart disease most often produced by missense mutations in the β-myosin heavy chain gene (MYH7) located on chromosome 14q. These mutations lead to abnormal sarcomeric function, causing asymmetric ventricular hypertrophy that presents clinically with palpitations, arrhythmias, syncope and risk of sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOD1",
    "long_answer": "Missense mutations in the superoxide dismutase-1 (SOD1) gene are well known in familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. The mutant SOD1 protein acquires toxic properties that disrupt axonal transport and its interaction with cytoskeletal elements, causing neurofilament proteins to accumulate within the axons of affected neurons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Partial trisomy 10q",
    "long_answer": "Having three copies of the distal portion of the long (q) arm of chromosome 10 represents a duplication of that segment, producing a partial trisomy. This condition is classically termed partial trisomy 10q (also called distal 10q duplication syndrome), reflecting the presence of an extra copy of genetic material from the distal 10q region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2 (Gordon syndrome) typically presents with hypertension, hyperkalemia, and a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis in which the fall in bicarbonate is offset by a rise in serum chloride. This acid–base disturbance is therefore termed hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, the condition marked by the elevated blood chloride levels referenced in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichothiodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "The described triad—brittle (fragile) sulfur-deficient hair, marked photosensitivity of the brows and lashes, and intellectual disability—is the classic clinical presentation of trichothiodystrophy (TTD). TTD is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by mutations in nucleotide-excision-repair/transcription genes (e.g., ERCC2/XPD, ERCC3/XPB). The sulfur-poor matrix proteins make the hair shaft extremely fragile, while the DNA-repair defect produces cutaneous photosensitivity and neurodevelopmental impairment. TTD is therefore the accepted alternative name for this syndrome (also historically termed PIBIDS/Tay syndrome)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Total anomalous pulmonary venous return",
    "long_answer": "In total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR), all four pulmonary veins fail to connect to the left atrium and instead drain into the right atrium or systemic venous circulation. This misrouting sends oxygen-rich pulmonary blood back to the right side of the heart, leading to mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood and causing cyanosis and right-sided volume overload—hallmarks of this congenital heart defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beriberi",
    "long_answer": "Christiaan Eijkman investigated the neurological disease beriberi among soldiers and prisoners in the Dutch East Indies. He showed that the disorder was linked to diets based on polished white rice and could be prevented by unpolished rice, thereby revealing a nutritional deficiency. This work ultimately led to the identification of vitamin B₁ (thiamine) as the essential factor whose absence causes beriberi."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ADGRV1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ADGRV1 gene, which encodes very large G-protein-coupled receptor-1 (VLGR1), underlie Usher syndrome type IIC. ADGRV1 is strongly expressed not only in cochlear hair cells and retina but also in the developing cerebral cortex. Animal and human studies show that loss-of-function ADGRV1 mutations disturb neuronal migration and synaptic maturation within the auditory cortex, leading to altered cortical circuit formation, seizures and impaired central auditory processing. Thus the ADGRV1 mutation associated with Usher syndrome specifically affects auditory cortex development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Texas",
    "long_answer": "The reported case involves Lisa Alamia, a woman from Rosenberg, Texas, who underwent jaw surgery to correct an overbite and subsequently developed Foreign Accent Syndrome—a rare speech disorder that alters pronunciation patterns. Clinical evaluation confirmed the condition, making Texas the state associated with this widely publicized case."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly-Ichthyosis-Deafness syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The described constellation matches the rare Brachydactyly-Ichthyosis-Deafness (BIDS) syndrome. BIDS presents with congenital ichthyosis, sensorineural deafness, moderate intellectual disability and multiple skeletal anomalies; the skeletal changes typically include brachydactyly of the fingers and toes, short stature and other bone malformations. These core findings align precisely with the features listed in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Queen’s University",
    "long_answer": "A research team at Queen’s University (Kingston, Ontario, Canada) identified the genetic basis of Quebec platelet disorder, demonstrating that the disease is caused by a 74-kb duplication of the urokinase plasminogen activator gene (PLAU) on chromosome 10. The duplication leads to marked over-expression of urokinase in platelet α-granules, explaining the disease’s characteristic bleeding and hyperfibrinolysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal failure",
    "long_answer": "Feigenbaum-Bergeron-Richardson syndrome (also called retinal vasculopathy with cerebral leukodystrophy, TREX1-related) is a systemic microangiopathy. Besides the characteristic retinal and cerebral lesions, many affected individuals develop progressive kidney involvement that leads to renal failure, making renal failure a well-documented and common complication of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4,977 bp deletion",
    "long_answer": "Kearns–Sayre syndrome is most often caused by the mitochondrial DNA “common deletion,” a loss of 4,977 base pairs (m.8470_13446del4977) that removes several oxidative-phosphorylation genes; this 4.9-kb deletion is the deletion most frequently identified in patients with KSS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary haemochromatosis excess iron is deposited in the pancreatic islets, damaging β-cells and inducing insulin resistance. This disruption of insulin production and action frequently leads to secondary diabetes mellitus—often called “bronze diabetes”—making it one of the most common glucose-metabolism complications of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OMIM (Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man)",
    "long_answer": "OMIM is a curated database that assigns stable, unique six-digit identification numbers to Mendelian genetic disorders, their phenotypes, and the corresponding genes. These OMIM IDs are the standard references used in research, clinical genetics, and informatics to unambiguously link diseases with their associated gene entries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bird",
    "long_answer": "Andrea Arnold’s 2024 feature “Bird,” selected for the main competition at the 77th Cannes Film Festival, centres on a socially isolated teenage girl who forges an unexpected bond that helps her discover purpose and direction in her life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermis",
    "long_answer": "In scleredema, histopathology reveals marked thickening of the dermis—especially the reticular dermis—where broad, swollen collagen bundles are separated by clear mucin-filled spaces, a characteristic diagnostic feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tomato flu",
    "long_answer": "Tomato flu – also nicknamed “tomato fever” because of the red, tomato-like blisters it produces – has been reported in parts of India and virologic testing has shown the causal agent to be Coxsackie A viruses that normally produce hand, foot, and mouth disease (HFMD). Because the clinical picture and virology overlap with HFMD, tomato flu is regarded as a localized or variant presentation of hand, foot, and mouth disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paranuclear eosinophilic inclusions",
    "long_answer": "Infantile digital fibromatosis is characterized by fibroblasts/myofibroblasts that contain distinctive round-to-oval, brightly eosinophilic inclusion bodies located just beside the nucleus. These inclusions are aggregates of actin microfilaments and are not seen in other cutaneous fibrous proliferations, making them the key histologic feature that distinguishes this lesion from other fibrous skin diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (XLHED) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EDA gene, which encodes the signaling protein ectodysplasin-A required for normal development of sweat glands, hair, and teeth. Disruption of EDA signaling underlies the phenotype targeted in Edimer Pharmaceuticals’ clinical trials."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triad",
    "long_answer": "A set of three characteristic clinical findings—blepharochalasis, double lip, and non-toxic thyroid enlargement—defines Ascher’s syndrome; such a grouping of three signs or symptoms is termed a medical triad."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZMPSTE24",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the human gene ZMPSTE24, which encodes the zinc-metalloprotease responsible for the final endoproteolytic cleavage of pre-lamin A, block normal lamin A processing. Accumulation of unprocessed (farnesylated) pre-lamin A leads to progeroid disorders such as restrictive dermopathy and mandibuloacral dysplasia, establishing ZMPSTE24 as the critical gene whose loss of function causes a lamin A–processing defect and a progeroid phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kansas City",
    "long_answer": "The pathologist who first described X-linked lymphoproliferative disease was Dr. David T. Purtilo. According to published biographies and obituaries, Dr. Purtilo was born in Kansas City, Kansas; it is therefore this city that is his birthplace."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edward N. Zalta",
    "long_answer": "The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, a scholarly online encyclopedia featuring peer-reviewed articles in philosophy, was founded by philosopher Edward N. Zalta, who has served as its Principal Editor since its inception in 1995."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nick Knowles",
    "long_answer": "Nick Knowles, presenter of the BBC renovation series “DIY SOS: The Big Build,” led the programme’s team in rebuilding the home of a British family whose youngest son has the rare genetic disorder Prader–Willi syndrome. The project adapted the house to meet the boy’s strict medical and dietary needs, illustrating Knowles’s direct involvement in the rebuild for this Prader–Willi syndrome–affected family."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ulnar–mammary syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ulnar–mammary syndrome is an autosomal dominant malformation disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TBX3 transcription factor. Affected individuals typically show congenital ulnar-side limb defects, hypoplasia of apocrine glands and mammary tissue, genital anomalies and other variable craniofacial or dental malformations, fitting the description of an autosomal dominant malformation disorder beginning with the letter “U.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Johnson & Johnson",
    "long_answer": "In July 2021 the U.S. FDA revised the Emergency Use Authorization for the Janssen (Johnson & Johnson) COVID-19 vaccine, instructing the company to add a safety warning about an observed increased risk of Guillain–Barré syndrome. This placed the responsibility on Johnson & Johnson to include the GBS risk in its vaccine labeling and patient fact sheets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Horizontal gene transfer",
    "long_answer": "Trypanosoma cruzi can integrate fragments of its DNA, including retrotransposon-like sequences, into host chromosomes through horizontal (lateral) gene transfer. This evolutionary mechanism moves genetic material between unrelated organisms—rather than by normal parent-to-offspring inheritance—allowing the parasite’s DNA to become stably inserted in the human genome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome (MHC class II deficiency) is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by mutations in transcription factors such as CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP or RFXANK. These defects abolish surface expression of HLA-DR, ‑DP and ‑DQ molecules on antigen-presenting cells, preventing CD4⁺ T-cell development and leading to a severe combined immunodeficiency phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cataract",
    "long_answer": "Wagner’s disease (autosomal-dominant vitreoretinopathy caused by VCAN/versican mutations) frequently leads to early lens opacification; development of cataracts is one of the most common ocular complications reported in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L-DOPA (levodopa)",
    "long_answer": "6-Pyruvoyltetrahydropterin synthase deficiency lowers tetrahydrobiopterin levels, crippling tyrosine hydroxylase and reducing formation of L-DOPA, the immediate precursor of dopamine. This leads to brain dopamine deficiency; clinical management therefore supplies the missing precursor, L-DOPA, which is converted to dopamine in the CNS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive cutis laxa type II",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATP6V0A2 gene, which encodes the a2 subunit of the vacuolar H⁺-ATPase required for proper protein glycosylation, cause autosomal recessive cutis laxa type II (ARCL II). Patients typically present with loose, inelastic skin (cutis laxa) and multisystem involvement, including neurologic manifestations such as developmental delay, ataxia, and dystonia. Thus ARCL II is the genetic disorder linked to ATP6V0A2 defects that features both cutis laxa and dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic (usually homozygous) loss-of-function variants in the SMOC1 gene are a recognised cause of the autosomal-recessive syndrome characterised by anophthalmia / severe microphthalmia with limb and craniofacial anomalies (often referred to as Waardenburg anophthalmia syndrome or microphthalmia-with-limb-anomalies syndrome). Since the first gene description in 2010–2011, affected kindreds have been published from a number of different countries. The peer-reviewed literature and the OMIM entry for SMOC1 list families originating from Turkey, Brazil, Italy, China, Japan and Lebanon (as well as additional pedigrees from other regions). Thus, the condition caused by homozygous SMOC1 mutations has indeed been reported in families from all of the countries cited in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarah E. Pitlyk",
    "long_answer": "U.S. District Judge Sarah E. Pitlyk has publicly recounted that her youngest child was born with an encephalocele—a rare neural-tube defect in which brain tissue protrudes through an opening in the skull. Soon after birth, the baby underwent neurosurgical repair at Boston Children’s Hospital, one of the foremost centers for treating such craniofacial and neurological congenital anomalies. These facts are cited in biographical and testimonial pieces Mrs. Pitlyk has shared about her family’s medical journey."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lenz–Majewski syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the PTDSS1 gene, which encodes phosphatidylserine synthase 1, cause Lenz–Majewski syndrome (also called Lenz–Majewski hyperostotic dwarfism). This rare syndrome is characterized by skeletal hyperostosis, growth retardation, distinctive craniofacial features, and digital anomalies including syndactyly, linking the gene defect directly to the clinical finding specified in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rickettsia rickettsii",
    "long_answer": "Rocky Mountain Spotted Fever is caused by the gram-negative, obligate intracellular bacterium Rickettsia rickettsii, a member of the spotted fever group of rickettsiae transmitted primarily by hard ticks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RT-PCR for HEV RNA",
    "long_answer": "Reverse-transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) amplification of hepatitis E virus (HEV) RNA in serum or stool directly demonstrates the viral genome, thereby definitively confirming the presence of the virus that causes hepatitis E."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iduronate-2-sulfatase",
    "long_answer": "Hunter syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type II) results from a hereditary deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme iduronate-2-sulfatase, which is required to degrade the glycosaminoglycans heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate. Lack of this enzyme leads to their accumulation in tissues, producing the characteristic clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome) results from pathogenic variants in the FBN2 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-2. The FBN2 gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q23-q31), so the causative gene for this disorder is located on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Albrecht von Graefe",
    "long_answer": "The earliest recorded description of the association between retinitis pigmentosa and sensorineural (“perceptive”) deafness was made in 1858 by the German ophthalmologist Albrecht von Graefe. He noted that some patients with the retinal degeneration also had congenital hearing loss; Charles Usher later established its hereditary nature, but von Graefe was the first to report the combined phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IFIH1",
    "long_answer": "Singleton-Merten syndrome type 1 is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the IFIH1 gene. IFIH1 encodes the cytosolic pattern-recognition receptor MDA5 (melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5), a helicase that senses viral double-stranded RNA and activates type I interferon signaling. Pathogenic IFIH1 variants lead to constitutive MDA5 activation and the autoimmune manifestations characteristic of Singleton-Merten syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC) is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in TSC1 or TSC2. It is defined by the development of multiple benign hamartomas in several organs. In the kidneys, hamartomatous angiomyolipomas and cysts enlarge the kidneys, producing nephromegaly. In the lungs, hamartomatous proliferation of smooth-muscle cells leads to lymphangioleiomyomatosis with multiple cystic spaces. Thus, TSC uniquely links benign hamartomatous cysts in both lung and kidney and the clinical finding of nephromegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF1C",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the kinesin-family motor protein gene KIF1C, located on human chromosome 17p13, cause the autosomal-recessive form of hereditary spastic paraplegia designated SPG58. Loss-of-function mutations impair axonal transport, leading to progressive lower-limb spasticity and weakness that characterize hereditary spastic paraplegia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Variable expressivity",
    "long_answer": "Synpolydactyly type 1 is caused by the same pathogenic HOXD13 mutation in affected family members, yet their clinical manifestations range from mild webbing to extensive digit fusion and duplication. This difference in degree, despite an identical genotype, is a classic example of variable expressivity—the genetic phenomenon in which a single mutant allele produces a spectrum of phenotypic severity among individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "90%",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological data from the United Kingdom show that amblyopia (\"lazy eye\") is by far the commonest reason children are seen in eye clinics: it accounts for roughly nine-tenths of all paediatric visual-health appointments. Although only ~2–3 % of children have amblyopia, each affected child requires repeated follow-up for diagnosis, patching or atropine therapy, and monitoring of visual development, so in aggregate these visits comprise about 90 % of the total eye-care appointments in UK children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aspartylglucosaminuria",
    "long_answer": "Aspartylglucosaminuria is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by deficiency of aspartylglucosaminidase (AGA). It leads to progressive cognitive decline and intellectual disability. Owing to a founder effect, its prevalence in Finland (~1 : 18 000 births) makes it the country’s third most common genetic cause of intellectual disability after trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) and fragile X syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA gene",
    "long_answer": "Metageria is one of the progeroid (premature-aging) skin disorders caused by pathogenic variants in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear-envelope proteins lamin A and lamin C. LMNA mutations disrupt nuclear-lamina integrity, leading to accelerated tissue ageing and the characteristic prematurely aged cutaneous appearance seen in Metageria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF5A",
    "long_answer": "KIF5A encodes the neuronal heavy-chain isoform of kinesin-1. Germ-line mutations in KIF5A are a well-established cause of autosomal-dominant hereditary spastic paraplegia type 10 (SPG10). Exome/targeted sequencing studies of ACTH-secreting pituitary adenomas, the tumours responsible for Cushing’s disease, have identified somatic KIF5A missense mutations in a subset of cases, showing that the same gene altered in HSP can also contribute to corticotroph tumourigenesis and Cushing’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mast cell",
    "long_answer": "Mast cells are immune cells packed with histamine-containing granules that mediate immediate-type allergic reactions. They constitutively express the KIT (CD117) receptor tyrosine kinase, the same molecule that is frequently mutated and activated in gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GISTs). Because of this shared KIT dependence, mast cells are often cited in discussions of GIST biology and therapeutics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSM-IV",
    "long_answer": "Sadistic Personality Disorder was deleted because research and field trials for the next manual showed low diagnostic reliability, little evidence for distinct validity (large overlap with Antisocial and other personality disorders), and worries that the label was pejorative or could be misused in court to excuse violent acts. Its removal occurred with publication of the DSM-IV in 1994, the first edition after DSM-III-R that no longer listed it even as a proposed diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folie à Deux",
    "long_answer": "The X-Files Season 5 episode titled “Folie à Deux” is explicitly based on the psychiatric syndrome folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder). The story centers on a telemarketer who believes his supervisor is a literal monster and whose delusion becomes ‘contagious,’ mirroring the clinical phenomenon where two or more individuals share the same paranoid belief. The episode’s title and narrative directly reference this condition, making it the series’ installment inspired by folie à deux."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL1RAPL1",
    "long_answer": "The IL1RAPL1 gene (Interleukin-1 Receptor Accessory Protein-Like 1) is located at Xp21.3–Xp22.1 on the X chromosome. Loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions in IL1RAPL1 disrupt synapse formation and neuronal signaling, and are a well-established cause of non-syndromic X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMACHC",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MMACHC gene (the cause of the cblC type of methylmalonic acidemia) block the intracellular conversion of vitamin B12 to its active cofactors. The resulting functional B12 deficiency impairs DNA synthesis in bone-marrow precursors, leading to megaloblastic anemia and decreased production of white blood cells (leukopenia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein Data Bank",
    "long_answer": "The Protein Data Bank (PDB) is the central repository for three-dimensional structural data of biological macromolecules. Experimentally solved crystal and NMR structures of antithrombin have been deposited in the PDB, where they can be accessed by researchers worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BamA",
    "long_answer": "Treponema pallidum BamA (gene TP0326) is the organism’s homolog of the Omp85 outer-membrane-protein family. As the β-barrel assembly machinery component, BamA inserts and folds other surface proteins that mediate adhesion, immune evasion and nutrient uptake. Proper outer-membrane biogenesis driven by BamA is therefore essential for membrane integrity and directly augments the pathogen’s virulence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Diabetes mellitus is frequently present in people who suffer cardiac arrest because chronic hyperglycaemia accelerates coronary artery disease and electrical instability of the heart; epidemiologic studies report diabetes in roughly one-quarter of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest cases. The same disease is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease worldwide, producing diabetic nephropathy through glomerular hyperfiltration, advanced glycation and microvascular injury. Thus diabetes is the common underlying condition that links both cardiac arrest and chronic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDL receptor mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypercholesterolemia is classically caused by mutations in the LDL receptor (LDLR) gene. Loss-of-function mutations in LDLR impair hepatic uptake of apoB- and apoE-containing lipoproteins. Besides markedly raising plasma LDL-cholesterol, this disrupted trafficking decreases the transfer of surface phospholipids and apolipoproteins to nascent HDL particles, so HDL-cholesterol concentrations fall as a secondary consequence. Thus, an LDL receptor mutation is the genetic change in FH that alters circulating HDL levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Invasion",
    "long_answer": "In malignant transformation, cancer cells acquire the ability to breach the basement membrane and actively penetrate adjacent normal tissues—a process called invasion. This local tissue invasion is a key step in the progression of oral leukoplakia when it evolves into invasive squamous cell carcinoma, enabling tumor expansion and setting the stage for possible metastasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 9",
    "long_answer": "Hypoplastic left heart syndrome is one of the congenital heart defects documented in infants who carry an extra copy of chromosome 9. This aneuploid condition—known as Trisomy 9 (often mosaic)—produces a characteristic multiple-anomaly syndrome that includes severe cardiac malformations such as hypoplastic left heart."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Agenesis of the corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "When the corpus callosum fails to develop during fetal brain formation, the condition is termed agenesis of the corpus callosum (ACC). ACC can occur as an isolated anomaly or as part of various genetic syndromes, leading to a spectrum of neurological and cognitive outcomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "COL9A2, which encodes the α2 chain of type IX collagen, is sited on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (1p34.2-p33). Pathogenic variants in COL9A2 are a recognised cause of Stickler syndrome (type V) and are likewise responsible for autosomal multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (type 2), linking the two disorders to this chromosome locus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5–50 µm",
    "long_answer": "In prion-related transmissible spongiform encephalopathies, the characteristic spongiform change consists of numerous round, clear vacuoles in neurons and the surrounding neuropil. Histopathological studies describe these cortical holes as measuring roughly 5 to 50 micrometres in diameter, large enough to be visible at light-microscope magnification and to impart the ‘sponge-like’ appearance of affected brain tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diplogasteria",
    "long_answer": "The subclass Diplogasteria, which includes many diplogasterid nematodes, is sometimes treated as an independent subclass, but in the Classical (Chitwood-type) phylogenetic system it is merged with the rhabditid line and therefore considered to belong in the subclass Rhabditia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKHD1",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive polycystic kidney disease is caused chiefly by mutations in the PKHD1 gene. The PKHD1 product, fibrocystin/polyductin, localises to the basal body–centrosome complex and interacts with centrosomal proteins such as pericentrin. Functional studies have shown that loss of fibrocystin disrupts normal centrosome duplication, leading to supernumerary centrosomes and aberrant mitotic spindle formation. Thus, PKHD1 is the ARPKD-associated gene that directly participates in regulating centrosome duplication during cell division."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unesterified cholesterol",
    "long_answer": "Niemann–Pick disease type C is caused by defects in NPC1 or NPC2 proteins, which normally shuttle cholesterol out of late endosomes/lysosomes. Loss of their function traps unesterified (free) cholesterol within these compartments, making cholesterol the principal storage material that accumulates in lysosomes in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cockayne syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome (CS) is an autosomal-recessive DNA-repair disorder that presents with growth failure, neuro-developmental delay, photosensitivity and characteristic facial appearance. Ocular involvement is common; affected infants can be born with or soon develop dense congenital cataracts alongside pigmentary retinopathy and optic atrophy. Because the condition’s name begins with the letter “C,” it is the syndrome listed under diseases that start with ‘C’ and is classically associated with congenital cataracts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Locus heterogeneity",
    "long_answer": "Diamond–Blackfan anemia can result from pathogenic variants in many different ribosomal-protein genes (e.g., RPS19, RPL5, RPL11, RPS26, and others). The existence of multiple, distinct genetic loci whose mutation produces the same clinical phenotype is termed locus heterogeneity, which is the form of genetic variability characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia variabilis",
    "long_answer": "Mendes da Costa syndrome is the historical name for erythrokeratodermia variabilis (EKV), a genodermatosis characterized by transient erythematous patches and fixed hyperkeratotic plaques. Pathogenic mutations in the gap-junction genes GJB3 (encoding connexin-31) and GJB4 (encoding connexin-30.3) have been repeatedly demonstrated in affected families, establishing these two genes as the molecular basis of EKV."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC6A19",
    "long_answer": "Hartnup disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC6A19 gene, which lies on chromosome 5p15.33. SLC6A19 encodes the B0AT1 neutral amino-acid transporter; its dysfunction impairs intestinal and renal uptake of tryptophan, leading to niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency. The resulting pellagra-like state can produce neuropsychiatric manifestations, including dementia-like cognitive impairment, thereby linking mutations in SLC6A19 to the observed symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental isodisomy",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental isodisomy occurs when both copies of a particular chromosome are inherited from the same parent and are genetically identical. This makes the child homozygous for all alleles on that chromosome, so any recessive pathogenic variant carried by that parent will be expressed, leading to otherwise rare autosomal-recessive disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noonan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant RASopathy caused by pathogenic variants in genes of the RAS/MAPK signaling cascade (e.g., PTPN11, SOS1, RAF1, KRAS). A major clinical feature is proportionate short stature, and recombinant human growth hormone is an approved treatment to improve linear growth in affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UpToDate",
    "long_answer": "UpToDate is an evidence-based, peer-reviewed online medical knowledge base that contains material on dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica (Trevor disease). Comparative evaluations of point-of-care resources repeatedly show UpToDate to have the highest content accuracy among major clinical knowledge systems, making it the recognised source that fits both criteria in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Healthline",
    "long_answer": "The health-information website Healthline describes gender euphoria as the “deep joy” or happiness a person experiences when their internal gender identity is affirmed by, and matches, their outward gender expression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bruton’s tyrosine kinase",
    "long_answer": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the gene encoding Bruton’s tyrosine kinase (BTK). BTK is a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase essential for signaling downstream of the pre-B-cell receptor; its absence halts maturation at the pre-B stage, producing a profound block in B-cell development and resulting in markedly reduced or absent immunoglobulins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In medicine, a collection of characteristic signs and symptoms that tend to occur together and define a particular disorder is called a syndrome. Warburg Micro syndrome is an example of such a grouping; therefore, the general term for any similar constellation of clinical features is “syndrome.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome is an imprinting disorder caused by loss of paternal gene expression on chromosome 15q11-q13. Clinically it presents with neonatal hypotonia (muscle weakness), mild-to-moderate intellectual disabilities, characteristic craniofacial/oral anomalies that can include cleft lip or cleft palate, and, from early childhood, uncontrolled hyperphagia leading to severe obesity. These hallmark features align with the syndrome described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Freeman–Sheldon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Freeman–Sheldon syndrome—also called distal arthrogryposis type 2A or “whistling-face” syndrome—is a genetic disorder caused most often by dominant mutations in the MYH3 gene. The altered myosin heavy-chain protein disrupts muscle and connective-tissue development, leading to multiple congenital contractures of the face and distal limbs that are present at birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Dr. Jacqueline A. Noonan was working as a pediatric cardiologist caring for children with congenital heart disease when she noticed a recurring pattern of characteristic facial features, short stature, and pulmonary valve stenosis. Her observations, presented at a cardiology meeting in the early 1960s, led to the first description of what became known as Noonan syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EPM2A",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive Lafora disease is most commonly produced by loss-of-function mutations in the EPM2A gene, which encodes laforin, a glycogen phosphatase. Absence of functional laforin disrupts normal dephosphorylation of glycogen, causing accumulation of insoluble, poorly branched polyglucosan inclusions (Lafora bodies) and leading to the progressive myoclonic epilepsy characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Indonesia",
    "long_answer": "After the discovery of an atypical Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (mad-cow disease) case in a Californian dairy cow in April 2012, the Indonesian government announced an immediate suspension of U.S. beef imports to protect its food supply, making Indonesia the country that halted imports in response to that incident."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sotos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is characterized by pre- and postnatal overgrowth with pronounced macrocephaly. Individuals often show skeletal and connective-tissue anomalies, including chest wall deformities such as pectus excavatum, scoliosis, and joint laxity. Nail abnormalities—described as dysplastic or broad, often with ridging—are also a recognized minor feature. The coexistence of macrocephaly, pectus excavatum, and dysplastic nails therefore points to Sotos syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pericardial effusion",
    "long_answer": "In Hashimoto’s thyroiditis, the resulting hypothyroid state causes increased capillary permeability and glycosaminoglycan accumulation, allowing protein-rich fluid to collect in the pericardial sac. This pericardial effusion is a well-documented cardiac complication and can progress to tamponade if untreated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caregivers and Veterans Omnibus Health Services Act of 2010",
    "long_answer": "The Caregivers and Veterans Omnibus Health Services Act of 2010 (Public Law 111-163) explicitly acknowledged the crucial role of family caregivers for injured or ill veterans and created a comprehensive VA caregiver support program that provides training, respite care, counseling, health coverage, and monthly stipends to eligible caregivers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fucosidosis",
    "long_answer": "Fucosidosis is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme α-L-fucosidase. Accumulation of fucose-containing glycoproteins and glycolipids produces progressive neurodegeneration (worsening intellectual disability, spasticity, seizures) and characteristic angiokeratoma corporis diffusum—clusters of angiokeratomas that typically appear on the lower trunk, buttocks, and upper thighs. This combination of progressive neurologic decline and angiokeratomas matches the clinical picture described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folinic acid",
    "long_answer": "In autosomal-recessive GTP cyclohydrolase I deficiency, some patients develop secondary cerebral folate deficiency. The recommended folate replacement is folinic acid (5-formyl-tetrahydrofolate), a reduced, bioactive form that can cross the blood-brain barrier and restore cerebrospinal fluid 5-methyltetrahydrofolate levels more effectively than ordinary folic acid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain MRI",
    "long_answer": "Progressive hemifacial atrophy (Parry–Romberg syndrome) can be accompanied by seizures, headaches, or other focal deficits. Because intracranial white-matter changes, cortical atrophy, or vascular malformations are often detectable, current clinical guidance advises performing a magnetic resonance imaging scan of the brain whenever a Parry–Romberg patient reports neurological symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LEMD3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LEMD3 gene (also known as MAN1) cause osteopoikilosis, a sclerosing bone dysplasia manifesting as numerous bone islands, and the same gene defects underlie Buschke–Ollendorff syndrome, which combines osteopoikilosis with connective-tissue nevi."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome—an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by complete deficiency of the bilirubin-conjugating enzyme UGT1A1—is exceedingly rare worldwide, yet founder mutations and high consanguinity have produced a cluster of cases in several Israeli Arab villages, giving this population a markedly higher incidence of the disease compared with other ethnic groups in Israel and elsewhere."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy (I-cell disease) results from mutations in the GNPTAB gene that eliminate activity of N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase. Without this enzyme, lysosomal hydrolases fail to acquire the mannose-6-phosphate targeting signal, are secreted extracellularly, and lysosomes accumulate undegraded carbohydrates, lipids and proteins, forming the characteristic intracellular inclusion bodies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SOST gene, which encodes the Wnt pathway antagonist sclerostin, cause high-bone-mass disorders such as sclerosteosis and Van Buchem disease. These conditions are only expressed when both alleles are mutated, and carriers are clinically unaffected, demonstrating an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "Metastatic insulinomas are pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors that secrete large amounts of insulin independent of physiologic regulation. The excess insulin drives glucose out of the bloodstream into tissues, leading to abnormally low blood glucose levels. This hypoglycemia is the hallmark clinical manifestation of insulin-secreting tumors and typically presents with neuroglycopenic (confusion, seizures) and adrenergic (sweating, palpitations) symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melanopsin-expressing ipRGCs",
    "long_answer": "In SARDS the rods and cones are rapidly lost, abolishing the fast, photoreceptor-driven component of the pupillary light reflex. The remaining slow, sustained constriction is mediated by melanopsin-expressing intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells (ipRGCs), which survive the photoreceptor degeneration and retain intrinsic photosensitivity, thereby preserving the slow phase of the reflex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Influenza",
    "long_answer": "Influenza (‘the flu’) is an acute infectious disease caused by influenza viruses. Virologically, four primary antigenic types of influenza virus are recognized—Type A, Type B, Type C, and Type D—each differing in host range, epidemiology, and pathogenic potential. This clear division into four types makes influenza the well-known infectious disease that begins with the letter “I” and consists of four primary types."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein-losing enteropathy",
    "long_answer": "In Ménétrier’s disease the hypertrophied gastric mucosa leaks large amounts of serum proteins into the gastric lumen. This excessive gastrointestinal loss of plasma proteins is termed protein-losing enteropathy (or protein-losing gastropathy) and leads to hypoalbuminemia and edema, making it a hallmark complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pendred",
    "long_answer": "Pendred syndrome results from biallelic mutations in the PDS (now SLC26A4) gene encoding the anion exchanger pendrin. The disorder is named after the British physician Vaughan Pendred, who first reported the combination of congenital deafness and goitre in 1896."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAI1",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Magenis syndrome is most commonly caused by a heterozygous deletion or point mutation of the RAI1 (Retinoic Acid-Induced 1) gene on chromosome 17p11.2. Loss of RAI1 produces the craniofacial, neurobehavioral, and developmental features characteristic of the syndrome. RAI1 is a transcriptional regulator that modulates expression of several core clock genes (e.g., CLOCK, BMAL1), thereby influencing circadian rhythm entrainment and sleep-wake cycles. Thus, mutation of RAI1 links the hallmark clinical features of Smith–Magenis syndrome with disruption of normal circadian regulation." }

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophages",
    "long_answer": "In Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity, antigenic peptides are processed and displayed on MHC class II molecules by macrophages acting as professional antigen-presenting cells. Memory CD4⁺ Th1 cells recognize these macrophage-presented antigens, become activated, secrete cytokines (e.g., IFN-γ), and orchestrate the ensuing inflammatory response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Rud syndrome has been reported in association with recurrent seizures, i.e., epilepsy. Epilepsy is likewise common in the broader population of people with intellectual disability, occurring in roughly 20–30 % of such individuals. Thus, epilepsy is the shared neurological condition linked both to Rud syndrome and to a significant proportion of those with intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "World Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "In the 5th edition of the WHO Classification of Tumours: Soft Tissue and Bone Tumours published in 2020, the World Health Organization formally listed Infantile Digital Fibromatosis as its own benign fibroblastic/myofibroblastic tumor entity. This WHO classification update distinguishes IDF from other pediatric fibrous lesions and standardizes its diagnostic category worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NR0B1 (DAX1)",
    "long_answer": "X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita and associated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism are both caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NR0B1 gene, also called DAX1, located on Xp21. NR0B1 encodes a nuclear receptor essential for adrenal cortex development and hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis regulation; its disruption leads to adrenal insufficiency and impaired gonadotropin production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesenteric fat necrosis",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic sclerosing mesenteritis is thought to begin with necrosis of the mesenteric adipose tissue; the damaged fat releases lipid breakdown products that trigger persistent inflammatory cell infiltration, which over time promotes fibroblast activation and collagen deposition, culminating in chronic inflammation and dense fibrosis of the mesentery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bougainvillea",
    "long_answer": "Bougainvillea is a genus of woody climbing plants whose most widely grown cultivars (e.g., B. × buttiana) are periclinal chimeras derived from two species. These vines are adapted to arid conditions, tolerating drought once established, and in warm climates they can carry colourful bracts and flowers almost year-round, giving them an exceptionally long flowering period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. The National Research Council Canada (NRC), through its Certified Reference Materials (CRM) program at the Metrology Research Centre (formerly the Institute for National Measurement Standards), is one of the principal international producers of reference materials that carry certified values for methylmercury. Well-known NRC CRMs such as DORM-5 (fish protein), DOLT-5 (dogfish liver), TORT-3 (lobster hepatopancreas) and others include certified concentrations of both total mercury and methylmercury and are widely used worldwide to validate analytical methods, calibrate instruments, and support quality assurance in environmental and food testing laboratories. Hence, NRC Canada does indeed produce certified reference materials specifically intended for methylmercury analysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mixotrophs",
    "long_answer": "Mixotrophic organisms (mixotrophs) are capable of both autotrophy—producing their own energy via photosynthesis or chemosynthesis—and heterotrophy—obtaining nutrients by consuming other organisms. Many planktonic protists are mixotrophs, switching between photosynthesis and phagotrophy, and carnivorous plants are photosynthetic yet supplement nutrient intake by digesting prey, fitting the mixotrophic lifestyle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAMA",
    "long_answer": "The 50-year follow-up investigation assessing the lifelong physical health of individuals with idiopathic scoliosis was reported in the Journal of the American Medical Association (JAMA), which published the landmark natural-history study showing that, decades after diagnosis, most subjects had overall health, function and survival comparable to the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The human DHCR7 gene, which encodes 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase and whose loss-of-function mutations cause Smith–Lemli–Opitz syndrome, is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 11 (region 11q13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RSPO2",
    "long_answer": "Two genes are known to cause autosomal-recessive tetra-amelia. Loss-of-function mutations in WNT3 abolish limb formation but lung buds still arise, whereas loss-of-function of RSPO2 (R-spondin-2) disrupts WNT/β-catenin signalling required for early foregut endoderm budding; as a result, both limb buds and the embryonic lung buds fail to form. Therefore, RSPO2 mutations uniquely prevent lung formation in tetra-amelia syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome arises when the paternally expressed genes clustered in the imprinted 15q11-q13 region of chromosome 15 are lost or inactivated (through paternal deletion, maternal uniparental disomy, or imprinting-center defects). Loss of function of these specific genes (e.g., SNRPN, NDN, MAGEL2) leads to the characteristic hypotonia, hyperphagia, obesity, and neurodevelopmental abnormalities of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Night blindness",
    "long_answer": "Decreased activity of ornithine aminotransferase causes gyrate atrophy of the choroid and retina. The earliest and most frequent presenting complaint is nyctalopia, or night blindness, reflecting early rod photoreceptor dysfunction before other visual deficits appear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Medical genetics is the clinical specialty devoted to diagnosing and managing hereditary disorders. Clinicians in this field evaluate genetic causes, perform molecular testing, counsel families, and guide treatment for conditions such as apolipoprotein B deficiency and many other inherited metabolic, neurologic, and developmental diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate kinase",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PKLR gene impair the activity of the erythrocyte–liver isozyme of pyruvate kinase. This enzyme catalyzes the final step of glycolysis, converting phosphoenolpyruvate + ADP to pyruvate + ATP. In red blood cells, which depend entirely on glycolysis for energy, pyruvate kinase deficiency reduces ATP production from ADP, leading to hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Brunner syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) gene located on the X chromosome (Xp11.3). Because males have only one X chromosome, hemizygous males with the mutation express the phenotype, while heterozygous females are typically unaffected carriers. This pattern—mutation on the X chromosome with clinical manifestation primarily in males and carrier status in females—defines an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In lysosomal acid lipase (LAL) deficiency, massive cholesterol-ester and triglyceride accumulation leads to cirrhosis and hepatic failure. When an affected infant progresses to end-stage liver disease, the only definitive therapy capable of replacing the failed organ and providing a new source of LAL activity is orthotopic liver transplantation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis",
    "long_answer": "In vertically (perinatally) HIV-infected children, lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) is a characteristic HIV-associated syndrome. Besides chronic cough and diffuse pulmonary infiltrates, the disorder reflects generalized lymphoid hyperplasia; thus, affected children commonly show generalized lymphadenopathy, parotid gland enlargement, and prominent hepatosplenomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSD11B2",
    "long_answer": "Apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HSD11B2 gene, which encodes 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2. Deficient 11β-HSD2 fails to convert cortisol to inactive cortisone in renal tubules, so cortisol continuously activates mineralocorticoid receptors, leading to sodium retention, hypokalemia, and the severe monogenic hypertension characteristic of AME."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple synostoses syndrome 2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the GDF5 gene cause Multiple synostoses syndrome 2 (SYNS2), an autosomal-dominant skeletal disorder characterized by progressive bony fusions. A hallmark of SYNS2 is synostosis (coalition) of the carpal bones in the wrists, often accompanied by fusion of other joints, symphalangism of the fingers, and conductive hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor-2 (FGFR2) gene drive craniosynostosis syndromes such as Apert and Crouzon, in which signaling is overactive in osteogenic cells. The resulting excess FGF signaling accelerates ossification of cranial sutures, leading to premature closure of the skull bones during embryonic and early postnatal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Portland",
    "long_answer": "Oregon Health & Science University (OHSU) in Portland, Oregon, is one of the clinical trial sites where patients with Niemann–Pick disease type C receive intrathecal hydroxypropyl-β-cyclodextrin (HPβCD) therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MECP2 gene, located on Xq28, produce Rett syndrome, a well-established X-linked dominant neurodevelopmental disorder observed in humans and modeled in other mammals. Pathogenic variants in MECP2 disrupt its role as a methyl-CpG–binding transcriptional regulator, leading to wide-spread gene-expression abnormalities and the characteristic phenotype transmitted in an X-linked dominant pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren’s syndrome is an autoimmune disorder characterized by lymphocytic infiltration of the salivary and lacrimal glands, producing the classic sicca symptoms of dry mouth (xerostomia) and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca). It is also well-recognized that patients with Sjögren’s can present with chronic urticaria (chronic hives), reflecting its broader autoimmune involvement and association with other autoantibody-mediated cutaneous manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipid peroxidation",
    "long_answer": "Reactive oxygen species initiate lipid peroxidation of the skeletal-muscle sarcolemma and organelle membranes. This oxidative damage increases membrane permeability, disrupts calcium pumps, and allows excess Ca2+ influx. Elevated intracellular calcium activates proteases and phospholipases, depletes ATP, and leads to myofiber necrosis, thereby precipitating rhabdomyolysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Eisenmenger syndrome is a late complication of unrepaired congenital heart defects that produces severe pulmonary arterial hypertension, right-to-left shunting, and cyanosis. Management focuses on hemodynamics, arrhythmias, heart failure, and pulmonary vascular resistance—core areas of cardiovascular medicine. Therefore, patients are primarily cared for by cardiologists, especially those specializing in adult congenital heart disease and pulmonary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cigarette smoking",
    "long_answer": "Cigarette smoking is a well-established risk factor for ectopic pregnancy, likely because nicotine and other tobacco constituents impair tubal motility and ciliary function, increasing the chance that a fertilized ovum implants outside the uterus. The same exposure is also a recognized risk factor for multiple gastrointestinal diseases, including peptic ulcer disease, Crohn’s disease, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and several GI cancers. Thus, smoking represents a shared, modifiable risk factor for both ectopic pregnancy and gastrointestinal disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren syndrome is an autoimmune connective-tissue disorder characterized by lymphocytic destruction of exocrine glands producing dry eyes and mouth. It frequently occurs as “secondary” Sjögren syndrome in patients who already have another autoimmune disease, with rheumatoid arthritis being the most common associated condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nevus sebaceous",
    "long_answer": "Nevus sebaceous (also called nevus sebaceous of Jadassohn) is a congenital organoid epidermal nevus in which there is a marked hamartomatous overgrowth of sebaceous glands, often accompanied by proliferations of other adnexal structures. This characteristic sebaceous hyperplasia distinguishes it from other forms of nevi."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occipitocervical fusion",
    "long_answer": "Occipitocervical fusion surgically stabilises the junction between the occiput and upper cervical vertebrae. In Klippel–Feil syndrome it is used when congenital vertebral fusion leads to adjacent-segment instability, neural compression or basilar invagination. The same procedure is the standard operative management for craniocervical instability because it rigidly fixes the skull to the cervical spine, preventing pathologic motion and protecting the brainstem and upper spinal cord."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lowe syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Lowe syndrome (oculocerebrorenal syndrome) is an X-linked disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the OCRL gene. A hallmark of the disease is congenital glaucoma produced by goniodysgenesis (maldevelopment of the anterior chamber angle). Affected boys also have global developmental delay/intellectual disability and post-natal growth failure that results in short stature. The coexistence of goniodysgenesis, intellectual disability, and short stature is therefore characteristic of Lowe syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyloid aggregates",
    "long_answer": "Familial British dementia is characterized by extracellular deposits of amyloid fibrils composed of the ABri peptide. These amyloid aggregates, similar in morphology to the β-amyloid plaques of Alzheimer’s disease, accumulate in cerebral vessels and parenchyma, driving neurodegeneration in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Single umbilical artery",
    "long_answer": "In VACTERL association, the umbilical cord often contains only one artery rather than the normal two. This single umbilical artery is a well-established accompanying finding in many infants with the vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal and limb defects that define VACTERL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease is transmitted in an autosomal dominant pattern: a single mutant allele of the HTT gene on chromosome 4 is sufficient to cause the disorder, and it can be passed from an affected parent to offspring with a 50% probability regardless of sex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent pulmonary infections",
    "long_answer": "The submucosal bony and cartilaginous nodules of tracheobronchopathia osteochondroplastica protrude into the airway lumen and disrupt normal mucociliary clearance. This stagnation of secretions predisposes patients to repeated bacterial colonisation and inflammation, making them notably prone to developing recurrent lower-respiratory-tract (pulmonary) infections such as pneumonias and bronchitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone deficiency",
    "long_answer": "In Klinefelter syndrome, the extra X chromosome causes primary testicular failure, leading to markedly reduced testosterone production at puberty. This testosterone deficiency impairs normal masculinization, resulting in the characteristic physical changes such as small testes, scant facial/body hair, gynecomastia, and reduced muscle mass."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Persistent deficient sexual desire",
    "long_answer": "DSM-5 defines hypoactive sexual desire disorder as a persistent (≥6 months) or recurrent deficiency or absence of sexual thoughts/fantasies and desire for sexual activity that causes clinically significant personal distress, is not better explained by another mental disorder, medical condition, relationship difficulties, or substance/medication effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "c.525delT",
    "long_answer": "The single-nucleotide deletion c.525delT in the SGCA gene (also historically called 521delT) produces a frameshift and premature stop codon in α-sarcoglycan. This founder mutation is the most prevalent SGCA defect worldwide and is reported to constitute roughly 25–33 %—i.e., up to one-third—of all mutated SGCA alleles identified in sarcoglycanopathy (LGMD2D) patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBC1D24",
    "long_answer": "DOOR (or DOORS) syndrome is inherited in an autosomal-recessive manner and has been shown to result from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TBC1D24 gene located on chromosome 16p13.3. Pathogenic variants in TBC1D24 disrupt a Rab-GAP–related protein important for neuronal and skeletal development, explaining the combined features of deafness, onychodystrophy, osteodystrophy, intellectual disability and seizures that characterize the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baricitinib",
    "long_answer": "In June 2022 the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved baricitinib, an oral Janus kinase-1/2 inhibitor, as the first systemic therapy specifically indicated for adults with severe alopecia areata. Approval was based on the phase 3 BRAVE-AA1 and BRAVE-AA2 trials, in which daily oral baricitinib significantly improved scalp-hair regrowth compared with placebo, establishing it as the FDA-approved systemic treatment for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antisocial personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "Antisocial personality disorder shows the strongest and most consistent comorbidity with substance-related disorders; individuals with this disorder have markedly elevated rates of alcohol and drug misuse compared with other personality disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3-bp GAG deletion",
    "long_answer": "Early-onset primary torsion dystonia (DYT1) is classically produced by a spontaneous in-frame deletion of three nucleotides (GAG) in exon 5 of the TOR1A (DYT1) gene. This 3-bp GAG deletion removes a single glutamic-acid residue from the torsin-A protein and is the well-established de novo mutation responsible for most sporadic cases of torsion dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1. The FBN1 gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 15 at band 15q21.1; mutations in this locus disrupt fibrillin-1 structure and microfibril formation, leading to the connective-tissue manifestations characteristic of Marfan syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coumarin",
    "long_answer": "Dahlberg-Borer-Newcomer syndrome is a form of primary (hereditary) lymphedema. Management of the limb swelling in this syndrome classically includes the benzopyrone drug coumarin, which promotes macrophage degradation of interstitial proteins and thereby reduces the high-protein edema characteristic of lymphedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spasticity",
    "long_answer": "In metachromatic leukodystrophy, demyelination damages upper motor neuron pathways in the central nervous system, producing spasticity—an abnormal, velocity-dependent increase in skeletal muscle tone characteristic of many CNS lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nucleotide excision repair endonuclease",
    "long_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum results from mutations in genes (XPA–XPG, XPV) that encode the nucleotide excision repair (NER) endonuclease complex responsible for detecting and excising UV-induced DNA lesions. The loss of this DNA repair enzyme prevents removal of thymine dimers, leading to the extreme UV sensitivity and cancer predisposition characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin D",
    "long_answer": "Seasonal Affective Disorder often occurs during months with limited sunlight, which lowers cutaneous synthesis of vitamin D. Numerous studies have shown that serum 25-hydroxy-vitamin D levels fall in winter and that low levels are correlated with depressive symptoms characteristic of SAD; supplementation or light therapy that restores vitamin D status can alleviate these symptoms. Thus, vitamin D deficiency is the vitamin deficiency associated with Seasonal Affective Disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells",
    "long_answer": "Histologic and immunohistochemical studies of Kikuchi (histiocytic necrotizing) lymphadenitis consistently show a marked predominance of activated CD8-positive cytotoxic T lymphocytes surrounding and infiltrating the necrotic foci. These cells express cytotoxic molecules such as perforin and granzyme B, and their apoptotic activity is thought to drive the characteristic necrosis and karyorrhexis seen in the disease. Consequently, CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells are considered central to the pathogenesis of Kikuchi disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rifampicin",
    "long_answer": "Rifampicin (rifampin) is a first-line antituberculous antibiotic that strongly induces hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes. This induction up-regulates δ-aminolevulinate synthase, the rate-limiting step in heme synthesis, leading to excess accumulation of porphyrin precursors and precipitating an acute porphyric attack in susceptible individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Necrosis",
    "long_answer": "Marchiafava–Bignami disease, an alcohol-related disorder affecting the corpus callosum, shows widespread demyelination accompanied by necrosis. Necrosis is a form of cell injury resulting in the premature, unregulated death of cells and surrounding tissue, typically triggered by external insults such as toxins or ischemia—precisely the pathological change described in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Northern epilepsy syndrome (CLN8-related neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis) presents with focal seizures beginning in childhood. Clinical studies and Finnish case series have shown that the seizures are usually well-controlled with the broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug valproic acid (sodium valproate), which is therefore the standard pharmacologic therapy chosen for these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TREX1",
    "long_answer": "Retinal vasculopathy with cerebral leukoencephalopathy (RVCL-S) is an autosomal-dominant microvascular disease produced by heterozygous C-terminal frameshift or nonsense mutations in the TREX1 gene (three-prime repair exonuclease 1). In addition to retinal and cerebral small-vessel pathology, the same TREX1 mutations damage visceral microvessels, predisposing patients to life-threatening gastrointestinal bleeding. Thus, a pathogenic TREX1 mutation links RVCL with the occurrence of gastrointestinal hemorrhage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome (CdLS) is a multisystem developmental disorder in which heterozygous pathogenic variants in the NIPBL gene account for the majority of cases. NIPBL encodes a cohesin-loading factor critical for chromosomal cohesion. Skeletal anomalies, including congenital radioulnar synostosis of the forearm, are well-documented manifestations of CdLS, linking this syndrome to the specified skeletal finding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis for L/S ratio",
    "long_answer": "Sampling the amniotic fluid by amniocentesis and measuring the lecithin-to-sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio (or other surfactant markers) allows assessment of fetal lung maturity; when the ratio indicates adequate surfactant production (≈ ≥2.0), delivery can safely be undertaken, whereas a low ratio prompts delaying birth to give the lungs more time to develop, thereby guiding timing of delivery in a fetus at high risk of pulmonary hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Historical military medical records from World War II show that persistent enuresis (bed-wetting) was explicitly listed among the conditions reviewed by Army neuropsychiatrists. U.S. War Department directives (e.g., Circular Letter No. 74, 1943; Technical Bulletin MED 203, 1945) instructed examiners to record nocturnal or diurnal enuresis because it was regarded as a possible indicator of emotional instability, psychoneurosis, or other neuropsychiatric disorders. Soldiers or inductees with chronic adult enuresis could be classified under ‘Psychoneurosis, enuresis’ and were sometimes discharged on those grounds. Similar attention to enuresis appears in British and German military neuropsychiatric manuals of the period. Therefore, enuresis was indeed considered during neuropsychiatric evaluation of soldiers in World War II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sclerosis is an acquired demyelinating disease of the central nervous system. Demyelinating plaques can involve the auditory pathways in the brainstem or cochlear nerve, producing episodes of sudden or progressive sensorineural hearing loss. Thus, this myelin-targeting disorder is a recognized neurologic cause of hearing impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC12A3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SLC12A3 gene (encoding the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter) cause Gitelman syndrome. This salt-wasting tubulopathy produces chronic intravascular volume depletion, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and leading to secondary hyperaldosteronism. Several reported Gitelman patients with SLC12A3 mutations have also exhibited left renal vein compression (nutcracker syndrome), linking the mutation to both secondary hyperaldosteronism and nutcracker syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the CHD7 gene (Chromodomain Helicase DNA-binding protein 7) are the primary genetic cause of CHARGE syndrome. Because heart malformations—most commonly conotruncal and atrioventricular septal defects—are a cardinal feature of CHARGE, CHD7 mutations are also strongly linked to congenital heart defects. Thus, CHD7 is the gene whose mutation underlies both CHARGE syndrome and the associated congenital cardiac anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Traumatic brain injury (TBI) is a well-recognized cause of autonomic nervous system disturbances. After moderate-to-severe TBI, up to 10–30 % of patients develop a syndrome variably called dysautonomia, autonomic storming, or paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity. Manifestations include episodic tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, diaphoresis, and postural blood-pressure instability. These signs are attributed to damage or disconnection within central autonomic control networks (hypothalamus, brainstem, cortical-subcortical pathways) produced by the brain injury. The association is documented in neurology and rehabilitation guidelines, making the statement that TBI can lead to dysautonomia consistent with established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turpentine oil",
    "long_answer": "Instilling or painting turpentine oil over the skin lesion occludes the larva’s breathing spiracles; the ensuing hypoxia drives the fly larva toward the surface, allowing it to be grasped and extracted. Turpentine is therefore routinely used to force larvae out in cutaneous myiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome 2 (CCDS2) is the autosomal-recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the GAMT gene, which encodes guanidinoacetate N-methyltransferase, the enzyme that catalyzes the final step in creatine biosynthesis. SLC6A10P, on the other hand, is a pseudogene related to the solute carrier family 6 and has no demonstrated protein-coding function. There is no evidence or consensus linking SLC6A10P variants to CCDS2 or to any of the creatine deficiency syndromes. Hence, mutations in SLC6A10P are not a recognized cause of cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transthoracic echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "The first-line test for a suspected sinus of Valsalva aneurysm is transthoracic echocardiography (TTE). TTE is non-invasive, rapidly available, and can visualize the aortic root and adjacent cardiac chambers, allowing detection of a dilated sinus and any abnormal flow jets if rupture has occurred. More detailed studies (e.g., transesophageal echo, CT, or MRI) are pursued after this initial screening imaging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated LDL cholesterol",
    "long_answer": "In hypercholesterolemia, excess low-density lipoprotein (LDL) particles circulate in the blood. These LDL particles readily penetrate the arterial endothelium, become oxidized, and are taken up by macrophages to form foam cells. The accumulation of these lipid-laden foam cells initiates fatty streaks that progress to fibrofatty plaques, i.e., atheromas. Thus, the primary driver of atheroma formation is the persistently elevated level of LDL cholesterol in the bloodstream."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase",
    "long_answer": "Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (FBPase) is a lithium-sensitive phosphatase that contains the same conserved catalytic sequence motif found in inositol-1-phosphatase. FBPase catalyzes the hydrolysis of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate to fructose-6-phosphate, a key regulated step of hepatic and renal gluconeogenesis, thereby linking it functionally to glucose production while sharing structural features with inositol-1-phosphatase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "αvβ3 integrin",
    "long_answer": "The αvβ3 integrin expressed on osteoclast precursors binds RGD-containing bone matrix proteins and delivers ‘outside-in’ signals that cooperate with RANKL signaling. Engagement of αvβ3 activates c-Src, Syk and ITAM-bearing adaptors (DAP12, FcRγ), which in turn trigger Vav/Rho GTPases, PLCγ2, Ca2+ oscillations and NFATc1 induction—events that are essential for osteoclast differentiation, cytoskeletal organization and survival. Mice or cells lacking αvβ3 show defective osteoclast formation and function, demonstrating that this integrin is a central regulator of osteoclastogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Tuberculosis (TB) is classified as a notifiable disease in virtually all national and international public-health regulations. Health-care providers and laboratories are legally required to report suspected or confirmed cases of TB to public-health authorities so that contact tracing, surveillance, and control measures can be implemented. This requirement is stipulated by agencies such as the World Health Organization, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, and equivalent bodies in most countries. Therefore, according to established global public-health consensus, tuberculosis is indeed a notifiable disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myeloperoxidase stain",
    "long_answer": "The cytochemical myeloperoxidase (MPO) stain detects the peroxidase enzyme present in the granules of myeloid blasts. AML cells are MPO-positive, whereas lymphoid blasts in other leukemias such as ALL are MPO-negative, allowing reliable differentiation of acute myeloid leukemia from other leukemic subtypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radius",
    "long_answer": "Radial dysplasia (radial longitudinal deficiency or radial club hand) is a congenital malformation in which the radius bone of the forearm fails to develop normally; it may be hypoplastic, shortened, or completely absent, producing the characteristic radial deviation and forearm shortening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Biopesticides",
    "long_answer": "Products whose active ingredients come from natural sources—plants, microbes, minerals—are sold as “biopesticides.” This label is widely used in advertising to position these consumer products as gentler, more environmentally friendly, and safer than conventional synthetic chemical pesticides, even though they still exert pesticidal activity and are regulated by the EPA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zinc",
    "long_answer": "Zinc supplementation is commonly used to treat dysgeusia because inadequate zinc impairs taste-bud function. Zinc is also an essential trace element for the immune system, supporting development and activity of neutrophils, natural killer cells, and T-lymphocytes. Thus, zinc addresses taste disturbances while simultaneously contributing to normal immune defense."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrology",
    "long_answer": "Medullary sponge kidney is a congenital malformation of the renal collecting ducts. Because it is primarily a disorder of kidney anatomy and function—with complications such as hematuria, urinary tract infections, and nephrolithiasis—the condition is medically evaluated and managed by a nephrologist, the physician specialist in nephrology who treats diseases of the kidneys."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Neurologists (often pediatric neurologists) are the specialists who evaluate and manage central and peripheral nervous system problems such as hydrocephalus, seizures, spinal cord compression, and cognitive decline that occur in mucopolysaccharidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Perimetry",
    "long_answer": "Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy primarily damages the retinal ganglion cells serving the papillomacular bundle, producing central or centro‐cecal scotomas. Automated or manual visual-field perimetry (e.g., Humphrey or Goldmann perimetry) quantitatively maps these scotomas, so perimetry is the standard test used to detect the visual field loss characteristic of LHON."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism",
    "long_answer": "Masses in the hypothalamic–pituitary region (e.g., craniopharyngioma, germinoma) can damage or compress GnRH-secreting neurons. The resulting deficiency of gonadotropin-releasing hormone leads to low LH and FSH levels—a state termed hypogonadotropic hypogonadism—which prevents normal activation of the gonads and manifests clinically as delayed or absent puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Byssinosis",
    "long_answer": "Inhaling dust from processed cotton fibers in textile mills can inflame and obstruct the airways, leading to the occupational lung disease called byssinosis (often termed “brown-lung disease”). It is characterized by chest tightness and chronic airflow limitation in cotton, flax, or hemp workers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disease transmission",
    "long_answer": "Beyond the irrational fear termed musophobia, many people are concerned that mice and rats can spread zoonotic pathogens – e.g., hantaviruses, Leptospira, Salmonella and plague bacteria – to humans and domestic animals. This public-health worry about disease transmission is separate from the psychological phobia itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Histamine",
    "long_answer": "Histamine is a classic inflammatory mediator stored in mast cells and basophils. Upon IgE-mediated activation by egg allergens, these cells degranulate and release histamine, leading to vasodilation, bronchoconstriction, pruritus and hypotension. Elevated histamine release is therefore directly associated with the severity of allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, in individuals with egg allergy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Small-cell lung carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "The paraneoplastic form of chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction is classically linked to small-cell lung carcinoma. In this setting, anti-neuronal (anti-Hu/ANNA-1) antibodies generated against the tumor attack enteric neurons, producing severe dysmotility that mimics mechanical obstruction. Among malignancies, small-cell lung carcinoma is by far the most frequently reported and well-documented cancer associated with chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CA1 region",
    "long_answer": "Selective lesions confined to the CA1 region of the hippocampus, such as those seen in patient R.B. or following transient ischemia, reliably produce profound anterograde memory loss. CA1 pyramidal neurons are critical for consolidating new declarative memories, so damage here disrupts memory formation while sparing many other cognitive functions, establishing CA1 as the hippocampal subfield most closely linked to lesion-induced amnesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin",
    "long_answer": "Recurrent subareolar (periductal) abscesses of the breast have been linked to hyperprolactinaemia. Excess production of the pituitary-derived protein hormone prolactin increases ductal secretions, promotes duct ectasia and stasis, and thereby predisposes the subareolar ducts to bacterial infection and chronic abscess formation. Normalisation of prolactin levels in affected patients has been reported to reduce recurrence, supporting the causal role of excessive prolactin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Institute of Dental Research",
    "long_answer": "Dr. James F. Bosma reported his landmark observations on congenital arrhinia while he was a clinician-scientist at the U.S. National Institute of Dental Research (NIDR, now NIDCR) of the National Institutes of Health. His NIDR case descriptions led to recognition of the disorder later termed Bosma arhinia microphthalmia syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of Cornelia de Lange syndrome are caused by mutations in the NIPBL gene, which is located on chromosome 5 (band 5p13.2). Hence, chromosome 5 is the chromosome implicated in the primary gene mutation underlying this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Usher syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome is an autosomal-recessive genetic disorder in which mutations in ciliary or photoreceptor genes cause retinitis pigmentosa. Degeneration of peripheral rod photoreceptors produces progressive constriction of the visual field, leading to the characteristic ‘tunnel vision’ seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose testosterone",
    "long_answer": "Studies and clinical case series have shown that adding a small (physiologic female-range) dose of testosterone to the hormone regimen of transgender women with markedly suppressed androgen levels can restore libido and improve other symptoms of hypoactive sexual desire disorder without reversing feminization. The androgen supplementation raises circulating testosterone into the low-female range, which is sufficient to enhance central sexual drive mechanisms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triatomine bug",
    "long_answer": "Chagas disease is transmitted by triatomine bugs, commonly called “kissing bugs.” These reduviid insects are nocturnal; they leave their daytime hiding places in cracks or thatched roofs and feed on the blood of sleeping humans and animals at night. During this nighttime feeding they defecate, and the Trypanosoma cruzi parasites in their feces enter the bite site or mucous membranes, initiating infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strange Situation Procedure",
    "long_answer": "Mary Ainsworth devised the Strange Situation Procedure in 1969 as a standardized laboratory paradigm to evaluate the quality of attachment between a caregiver and a 12- to 18-month-old child. By observing the child’s behavior during a series of separations and reunions with the caregiver and the introduction of a stranger, clinicians and researchers can classify attachment styles (secure, anxious-avoidant, anxious-resistant, disorganized). This method is routinely employed in clinical and research settings to assess attachment patterns in typical development as well as in conditions such as reactive attachment disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholesterol crystals",
    "long_answer": "In Coats’ disease the leaky, telangiectatic retinal vessels allow serum lipoproteins to accumulate in and under the retina. These lipids precipitate, and within the subretinal exudate characteristic needle-shaped, birefringent cholesterol crystals are found on histologic examination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amygdala",
    "long_answer": "The amygdala, located in the anterior medial temporal lobe, is a key limbic structure that degenerates early in many cases of frontotemporal dementia (especially the behavioural variant) and whose bilateral damage is well-known to cause social-emotional agnosia—an inability to recognise and respond to others’ emotional expressions. Thus the amygdala is the common brain area implicated in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mosaic trisomy 18",
    "long_answer": "When only a proportion of an individual’s cells carry a third copy of chromosome 18, the condition is called mosaic trisomy 18. This form of Edwards syndrome arises from a post-zygotic mitotic error, producing two cell lines—one with the normal chromosomal complement and another with trisomy 18—resulting in a mosaic distribution of the extra chromosome throughout the body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strongyloides stercoralis",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion; infections that themselves cause marked eosinophilia must first be ruled out. Strongyloides stercoralis is specifically emphasized because it can produce persistent, sometimes intermittent eosinophilia and, if unrecognized, administration of corticosteroids (commonly used to treat HES) can precipitate life-threatening disseminated strongyloidiasis. Therefore, stool examination or serologic testing for Strongyloides stercoralis is obligatory before confirming hypereosinophilic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "USH2A",
    "long_answer": "The USH2A gene, encoding the protein usherin, is mutationally linked to Usher syndrome type II and is also known to cause autosomal recessive nonsyndromic hearing loss (designated DFNB3A). Pathogenic variants in USH2A disrupt inner-ear hair-cell function, leading to sensorineural deafness characteristic of both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Keloid scars are an overgrowth of dermal fibrous tissue, so their evaluation and management—using intralesional corticosteroids, cryotherapy, laser therapy, pressure or silicone sheets, and, when needed, surgical excision—falls primarily within the expertise of dermatologists; therefore, the specialty of dermatology most commonly treats keloid scars."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Vasculitis is an inflammatory, often immune-mediated process affecting blood vessels. The cornerstone of therapy is systemic corticosteroids (glucocorticoids such as prednisone or methylprednisolone), which rapidly suppress immune activation and vessel wall inflammation, leading to symptom control and prevention of tissue damage across most vasculitic syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "In a congenital diaphragmatic hernia, abdominal viscera herniate through the defective diaphragm into the thoracic cavity. The occupying abdominal organs compress the developing fetal lungs, limiting lung growth and alveolar branching. This mechanical restriction leads to under-development of lung tissue—pulmonary hypoplasia—which is the major pathophysiological consequence and principal cause of respiratory distress in affected neonates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal tubular transport",
    "long_answer": "All three conditions are hereditary defects of the kidney’s tubular transport mechanisms. In iminoglycinuria the proximal tubule fails to reabsorb glycine and imino acids; in renal tubular acidosis the tubules cannot secrete H⁺ or reclaim HCO₃⁻ properly; and in Gitelman syndrome mutations in the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter impair salt reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule. Thus the disrupted process common to these disorders is renal tubular solute transport (reabsorption/secretion)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candida esophagitis",
    "long_answer": "In achalasia the retained food and stasis of secretions create an ideal setting for fungal overgrowth; super-infection of the stagnant esophageal lumen by Candida species produces mucosal inflammation, and this candidal esophagitis manifests clinically with odynophagia (painful swallowing), a symptom not usually caused by achalasia itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "F68.12",
    "long_answer": "In ICD-10-CM, the diagnosis ‘Factitious disorder imposed on another’—commonly known as Munchausen syndrome by proxy, where a caregiver deliberately fabricates or induces illness in someone under their care—is coded as F68.12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Gangliosidoses such as GM1 and GM2 (Tay-Sachs, Sandhoff) are inherited lysosomal storage disorders produced by congenital enzyme deficiencies. Diagnosis, management, and genetic counseling for these hereditary metabolic diseases are handled within the specialty of Medical genetics, the branch of medicine devoted to disorders caused by genetic mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Axonal polyneuropathy",
    "long_answer": "Diabetes mellitus most often produces a chronic, length-dependent sensorimotor neuropathy in which the primary pathological change is degeneration of nerve axons. Nerve-conduction studies show low amplitudes with relatively normal conduction velocities, the hallmark of an axonal rather than a demyelinating process, so diabetic peripheral neuropathy is classified as an axonal polyneuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystinosis",
    "long_answer": "Abderhalden–Kaufmann–Lignac syndrome is the historical name for cystinosis, a rare autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by mutations in the CTNS gene. The defect prevents the transport of cystine out of lysosomes, leading to crystal accumulation in tissues and the characteristic nephropathic Fanconi syndrome, growth failure, rickets, and ocular involvement described by Abderhalden, Kaufmann, and Lignac."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type II pseudohypoaldosteronism",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoaldosteronism type II (Gordon syndrome) is characterized by hypertension, hyperkalemia, and a normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. The acidosis results from impaired distal tubular secretion of hydrogen and potassium ions secondary to increased sodium–chloride cotransporter (NCC) activity. Hence, the hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis seen in pseudohypoaldosteronism is specifically associated with type II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Old English “stígend”",
    "long_answer": "The English word “stye” (eye-lid swelling) descends from Old English “stígend,” a noun derived from the verb “stígan,” meaning “to rise.” The sense of ‘a riser’ or ‘something that rises up’ was applied to the small raised, inflamed bump on the eyelid, giving the modern medical term stye (hordeolum)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear envelope proteins lamin A and lamin C, cause the autosomal-dominant form of Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (EDMD type 2). LMNA mutations disrupt nuclear-lamina integrity in muscle and cardiac cells, predisposing patients to conduction system disease and life-threatening arrhythmias that are characteristic cardiac complications of EDMD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4th–6th weeks",
    "long_answer": "During the 4th to 6th weeks of embryonic development, the cloaca is partitioned by the uro-rectal septum, separating the urogenital sinus from the rectum and isolating the genital (phallus) tubercle that will elongate into a single penis. Interference with this 4- to 6-week morphogenetic interval can duplicate the genital tubercle, producing diphallia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLRA1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the GLRA1 gene, which encodes the α1 subunit of the inhibitory glycine receptor, are the most common and conclusively established cause of hereditary hyperekplexia. Loss-of-function or dominant-negative mutations in GLRA1 impair glycinergic neurotransmission in the spinal cord and brainstem, leading to the exaggerated startle response and muscle stiffness characteristic of the disorder. GLRA1 is routinely detected by clinical genetic testing and is the definitive gene linked to hyperekplexia in human patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autoimmune disease",
    "long_answer": "Morphea, also called localized scleroderma, is characterized by immune-mediated inflammation and excessive collagen deposition in the skin and underlying tissues. Like systemic scleroderma, it is classified as an autoimmune connective-tissue disorder in which dysregulated immune responses target the body’s own tissues, leading clinicians to regard morphea as an autoimmune disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risk of sudden hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Pendred syndrome is associated with an enlarged vestibular aqueduct in the inner ear; even minor head trauma can cause fluid pressure changes that abruptly damage the cochlea. This can trigger a rapid, often irreversible worsening of the sensorineural hearing loss already present, so affected individuals are advised to avoid head injuries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain",
    "long_answer": "Menkes (kinky-hair) disease results from mutations in the ATP7A copper-transporting ATPase, preventing intestinal absorption and systemic distribution of copper. The consequent copper deficiency most profoundly damages the brain, where loss of copper-dependent enzymes leads to severe neurodegeneration and neurologic dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-TK",
    "long_answer": "Myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibers (MERRF) is classically caused by pathogenic mutations in the mitochondrial DNA gene MT-TK, which encodes the mitochondrial tRNA for lysine. Defects in this tRNA impair mitochondrial protein synthesis and oxidative phosphorylation, producing the neuromuscular symptoms and ragged-red fibers characteristic of MERRF."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Barium swallow",
    "long_answer": "If the endoscope cannot be advanced or tolerated, a contrast radiographic study of the esophagus—the barium swallow (contrast esophagography)—can still outline mucosal irregularities, eccentric filling defects, and shouldering that are characteristic of esophageal carcinoma, allowing the tumor to be detected even when endoscopy fails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital heart defects",
    "long_answer": "More than half of children who have Jacobsen syndrome (the 11q terminal deletion disorder) are born with congenital heart defects—particularly left-sided obstructive lesions such as hypoplastic left heart, coarctation of the aorta, or ventricular septal defects—making structural congenital heart disease the most common cardiovascular complication in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Audiologist",
    "long_answer": "An audiologist is a doctoral-level healthcare professional specially trained in audiology to evaluate the auditory system, perform comprehensive hearing tests, determine the type and degree of hearing loss, and administer or coordinate appropriate treatments such as hearing aids, rehabilitative therapy, or referral for medical or surgical care when needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fragile X syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome results from expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat (> ~200 copies) in the 5′-untranslated region of the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome (Xq27.3). The expansion leads to hypermethylation and transcriptional silencing of FMR1, loss of the FMRP protein, and manifests clinically with intellectual disability, characteristic facial features and behavioral findings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Left cardiac sympathetic denervation",
    "long_answer": "In catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CPVT), excessive adrenergic stimulation precipitates life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias. Surgical left cardiac sympathetic denervation removes the lower half of the left stellate ganglion and upper thoracic sympathetic ganglia, thereby diminishing cardiac sympathetic outflow. This reduces catecholamine-mediated triggers and has been shown to lower arrhythmia burden and sudden death risk in CPVT patients who remain symptomatic despite optimal drug therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibial tuberosity",
    "long_answer": "Osgood–Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis that occurs at the tibial tuberosity—the bony prominence on the proximal anterior tibia where the patellar tendon inserts. Repetitive stress from quadriceps contraction during growth leads to inflammation and pain in this specific structure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teratogens",
    "long_answer": "Teratogens are environmental agents—such as certain chemicals, drugs, infections, or radiation—that interfere with normal embryonic or fetal development. Exposure during critical periods of gestation can disrupt limb formation, resulting in congenital malformations like ectrodactyly (split-hand/foot). Thus, substances that produce birth defects are collectively termed teratogens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution prenatal ultrasonography can reveal tell-tale signs of esophageal atresia, such as marked polyhydramnios and a small or absent fetal stomach bubble, allowing suspicion of the anomaly before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Walker–Warburg syndrome is a severe congenital muscular dystrophy accompanied by major brain and eye malformations. Because it primarily involves the nervous system and skeletal muscle, diagnosis (clinical evaluation, neuro-imaging, muscle/brain pathology, genetic testing) and ongoing management of seizures, hypotonia, feeding, and respiratory issues are overseen by a neurologist—specifically a paediatric neurologist in infancy—who coordinates the multidisciplinary care these patients require."
}

{
    "short_answer": "International Society for Sexual Medicine",
    "long_answer": "The peer-reviewed Journal of Sexual Medicine, which features clinical and translational studies on conditions such as hypoactive sexual desire disorder, is published by the International Society for Sexual Medicine (ISSM)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitosis",
    "long_answer": "Chondroblastoma cells are somatic (non-germline) tumor cells; their growth and expansion occur through ordinary mitotic cell division, the process by which one diploid parent cell duplicates its genome and divides to form two genetically identical daughter cells. No meiotic division is involved in the proliferation of chondroblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMARCAD1",
    "long_answer": "Adermatoglyphia—the congenital absence of fingerprints—is caused by a splice-site mutation in the skin-specific isoform of the SMARCAD1 gene. SMARCAD1 resides on chromosome 4 (band 4q22); the mutation disrupts proper mRNA splicing, leading to loss of ridge formation in the epidermis and thereby producing the characteristic lack of fingerprints."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Axial osteosclerosis is a heritable bone-density disorder in which excessive sclerosis is confined chiefly to the axial skeleton (skull, vertebrae, pelvis). Reported families show vertical transmission through successive generations with affected males and females, and parent-to-child passage without skipped generations, establishing an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric surgery",
    "long_answer": "VACTERL association involves congenital malformations—such as anal atresia, tracheoesophageal fistula, and other defects—that typically require operative correction soon after birth. The specialty that routinely evaluates and surgically manages these anomalies is pediatric surgery, making pediatric surgeons the physicians most likely to treat patients with VACTERL association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prompt intravenous glucose infusion",
    "long_answer": "In MCADD, patients cannot adequately oxidize medium-chain fatty acids, so during catabolic stress they must be supplied with an immediate, continuous source of carbohydrates. The standard management in any illness is to avoid fasting and start a dextrose-containing intravenous infusion (e.g., 10% glucose) to maintain blood glucose and prevent reliance on fatty-acid oxidation, thereby averting hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and metabolic decompensation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "In Niemann–Pick disease type B, deficiency of acid sphingomyelinase causes sphingomyelin-laden macrophages to accumulate within the pulmonary interstitium and alveoli. This leads to interstitial lung disease, reduced diffusing capacity, and characteristic radiographic and histologic lung pathology, showing that the lungs are a major organ affected by the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary adenoma",
    "long_answer": "Enlarging pituitary adenomas grow upward from the sella turcica and compress the overlying optic chiasm and adjoining optic nerves, producing characteristic visual field disturbances such as bitemporal hemianopsia. This mass effect on the visual pathway is a well-recognized complication of pituitary tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gingival bleeding",
    "long_answer": "Glanzmann's thrombasthenia is characterized by defective platelet aggregation owing to absent or dysfunctional GPIIb/IIIa complexes. This leads to mucocutaneous hemorrhage; a very common manifestation in the mouth is persistent or excessive bleeding from the gums (gingival bleeding), especially after minor trauma such as tooth-brushing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome is an autosomal-dominant neurocristopathy in which defective neural crest cell migration causes both congenital sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary abnormalities such as heterochromia of the iris, patchy skin depigmentation, and the classic white forelock (poliosis). These features make it the prototypical genetic syndrome associating congenital deafness with pigmentation defects and poliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Klippel-Feil syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function variants in the GDF6 gene are a recognized cause of autosomal-dominant Klippel-Feil syndrome. This congenital connective-tissue disorder is characterised by cervical vertebral fusion and frequently accompanies generalized joint hypermobility or laxity. Thus, Klippel-Feil syndrome is the connective tissue disorder directly linked to pathogenic GDF6 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow stroma",
    "long_answer": "Primary myelofibrosis is characterized by fibrous connective-tissue proliferation within the bone marrow stroma, leading to replacement of normal hematopoietic marrow with collagen/reticulin fibers produced by activated fibroblasts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal smoking during pregnancy",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies show that cigarette smoking by the mother while pregnant (prenatal nicotine exposure) increases the odds of later schizophrenia in the offspring and is one of the strongest modifiable risk factors for sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS). Nicotine and other tobacco constituents impair fetal brain development and cardiorespiratory regulation, mechanisms implicated in both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Congenital cytomegalovirus can infect the inner ear’s cochlea and spiral ganglion, damaging hair cells and auditory neurons. This injury may not manifest at birth but can progress, leading to permanent sensorineural hearing loss—one of the most common and well-documented long-term sequelae of cCMV infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHM and POU3F4",
    "long_answer": "Ayazi syndrome is a contiguous-gene deletion at Xq21; the deletion removes the CHM gene (whose loss causes choroideremia) and the adjacent POU3F4 gene (whose loss causes X-linked deafness type 3). The simultaneous absence of CHM and POU3F4 produces the combined phenotype of choroideremia, deafness and intellectual disability that defines Ayazi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease is caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in one allele of the HTT gene on chromosome 4. Because a single mutated copy is sufficient to produce the disorder and it is transmitted equally through males and females with vertical transmission in every generation, the disease follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MED12",
    "long_answer": "MED12 is an X-linked gene (locus Xq13) that encodes a core subunit of the Mediator transcriptional-regulator complex. Pathogenic variants in MED12 were first recognised as the cause of Opitz-Kaveggia (FG) syndrome, characterised by intellectual disability, hypotonia and distinctive facial and gastrointestinal anomalies in males. Subsequent reports have shown that de-novo or mosaic loss-of-function variants in the same gene can produce a clinical picture fulfilling diagnostic criteria for typical or atypical Rett syndrome in females who are negative for MECP2, linking MED12 mutations to both disorders." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polymicrogyria",
    "long_answer": "Polymicrogyria is a neuronal-migration cortical malformation in which the affected region of cerebral cortex is abnormally folded into many small gyri and shows disordered lamination with only four cortical layers instead of the normal six."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Guizar-Vásquez-Sánchez-Manzano syndrome is an extremely rare, inherited malformation/ skeletal-dysplasia syndrome. Because it is genetic in origin and requires chromosome or molecular studies, genetic counselling, and coordination of multidisciplinary care, its diagnosis and ongoing management fall within the medical specialty of clinical or medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Angiomatosis most commonly refers to retinal capillary hemangiomas (angiomatosis retinae), the earliest and hallmark manifestation of von Hippel–Lindau disease. Evaluation, laser or cryo-ablation, and ongoing surveillance of these vascular retinal lesions are carried out by ophthalmologists; thus, ophthalmology is the specialty primarily involved in treating patients with angiomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia, Strudwick type, is one of the type II collagenopathies. It is produced by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1-chain of type II collagen essential for normal cartilage and skeletal development. Mutations in COL2A1 disrupt collagen structure, leading to the characteristic skeletal abnormalities of the Strudwick subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T-wave inversion in leads V1–V3",
    "long_answer": "In arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy, the ECG finding observed most frequently is T-wave inversion in the right-precordial leads (V1–V3). This reflects repolarization abnormalities caused by fibrofatty replacement of right ventricular myocardium and is present in the majority of affected patients, making it the commonest ECG abnormality in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Soft palate",
    "long_answer": "In Lujan–Fryns syndrome, hypernasal speech is caused by velopharyngeal insufficiency—an inability of the soft palate (velum) to close properly against the posterior pharyngeal wall. This dysfunction of the soft palate lets excess air escape through the nasal passages during speech, producing the characteristic hypernasal resonance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid follicular cells",
    "long_answer": "Thyroid follicular cells (thyrocytes) line the thyroid follicles, synthesize thyroglobulin, iodinate it, and secrete the thyroid hormones T3 and T4 into the circulation. Follicular thyroid carcinoma originates from these same hormone-producing follicular epithelial cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palmoplantar keratoderma",
    "long_answer": "Naxos disease is an autosomal-recessive syndrome caused by plakoglobin mutations that combines arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy with characteristic cutaneous findings. Affected individuals develop woolly hair at birth and, during early childhood, a diffuse thickening of the skin on the palms and soles—palmoplantar keratoderma—which is the hallmark skin condition linked to the cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke",
    "long_answer": "Within the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH), the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS) is the branch that provides funding and scientific oversight for research on cerebellar hypoplasia as well as a wide range of other neurological disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fornix",
    "long_answer": "The fornix is a key white-matter tract that links the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies and other limbic structures. Interruption of the fornix impairs the Papez memory circuit, producing anterograde amnesia. In hydrocephalus, the enlarged lateral ventricles frequently stretch or compress the fornix, leading to memory deficits. Thus, injury or dysfunction of the fornix underlies the memory loss observed in both hydrocephalus and classical amnesic syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cryptorchidism-arachnodactyly-intellectual disability syndrome (also called CRANDID syndrome) is a very rare congenital disorder whose core phenotypic triad is (1) cryptorchidism in affected males, (2) arachnodactyly/long slender limbs and fingers, and (3) mild-to-moderate intellectual disability. Case reports additionally describe dolichocephaly, high-arched palate, hyperextensible joints, scoliosis, and a tall, slender habitus. A consistent feature across published descriptions is elongation of the digits (arachnodactyly), not shortening. Brachydactyly (short digits) has not been reported as part of this syndrome and would be phenotypically opposite to the characteristic arachnodactyly. Therefore, the statement that the syndrome includes brachydactyly is not supported by the established clinical descriptions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Symptom reduction",
    "long_answer": "Regular, graded physical activity has been shown in clinical trials and guideline reviews to lessen the intensity and frequency of bodily complaints in somatic symptom disorder. Exercise improves mood, decreases anxiety and catastrophizing, enhances endogenous pain-inhibitory pathways, and restores physical conditioning, which together translate into fewer and less severe somatic symptoms and better overall functioning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Failure of medial nasal prominence fusion",
    "long_answer": "Frontonasal dysplasia produces a midline (median) facial cleft because, during embryogenesis, the two medial nasal prominences fail to merge in the midline. These prominences normally unite to form the intermaxillary segment, giving rise to the philtrum of the upper lip, the primary palate, and the nasal tip. When this fusion does not occur, a median cleft lip/nose develops, the hallmark cleft of frontonasal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Varicella-zoster virus",
    "long_answer": "Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) is the herpesvirus that produces chickenpox during primary infection and shingles upon reactivation. When VZV involves the auditory system—such as in congenital varicella infection or in Ramsay Hunt (herpes zoster oticus)—it can damage the cochlea or auditory nerve, leading to permanent sensorineural hearing loss in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Metacarpal synostosis is a congenital fusion of hand bones that often requires evaluation and possible surgical correction of the skeletal deformity. Such musculoskeletal malformations are routinely managed by orthopedic surgeons—especially those specializing in hand or pediatric orthopedic surgery—making orthopedics the specialty most likely to treat patients with FGF16-related metacarpal synostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type 0 results from loss-of-function mutations in the liver glycogen synthase gene GYS2, which is located on chromosome 12 (cytogenetic band 12p12.2). Hence, chromosome 12 is the chromosome affected in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Ring chromosome 20 syndrome results from the formation of a ring-shaped structural abnormality involving chromosome 20. The ring chromosome replaces one of the normal chromosome 20 homologues, leading to the characteristic epilepsy and developmental features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteochondritis dissecans",
    "long_answer": "Osteochondritis dissecans is a joint disorder in which subchondral bone loses its blood supply, leading to separation and cracking of the overlying articular cartilage. The resulting cartilage-and-bone fragments can shear vascular structures inside the joint, producing bleeding into the joint cavity (hemarthrosis). Thus, cartilage damage in osteochondritis dissecans commonly gives rise to hemarthrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "California",
    "long_answer": "In January 2002, the State of California prohibited all pharmaceutical preparations of lindane (γ-hexachlorocyclohexane), including lotions and shampoos formerly used to treat scabies and head lice, because of environmental contamination and human‐health concerns. No other U.S. state has enacted a comparable ban on lindane for scabies treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)",
    "long_answer": "Lactate dehydrogenase is a ubiquitous cytosolic enzyme present in virtually every cell type. When red blood cells or other tissues are damaged or lysed, LDH is released into the plasma. In hereditary spherocytosis, ongoing intravascular and extravascular hemolysis causes elevated serum LDH levels, making it a useful biochemical marker of the degree of hemolysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oil pulling",
    "long_answer": "Oil pulling—an Ayurvedic practice in which individuals swish edible oils such as sesame or coconut oil in the mouth for prolonged periods—has been documented in multiple case reports to cause exogenous lipoid pneumonia. Small amounts of the oil can be aspirated into the lungs during or after the practice, where the non-metabolizable lipid accumulates in alveoli and triggers an inflammatory reaction characteristic of lipid pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly–ectodermal dysplasia–cleft lip/palate (EEC) syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor. p63 is crucial for epithelial, limb, and genitourinary development; consequently, TP63 mutations not only produce the classic ectodermal and limb defects but also disrupt urinary-tract morphogenesis, leading to kidney anomalies such as renal agenesis, hydronephrosis, and vesicoureteral reflux in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vomiting",
    "long_answer": "Because situs ambiguus often places the stomach in an abnormal midline or right-sided location, associated malrotation and volvulus impede normal gastric emptying and favor gastro-oesophageal reflux. This anatomic derangement commonly produces recurrent vomiting in affected patients, making it one of the characteristic gastrointestinal symptoms linked directly to the stomach’s abnormal positioning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH7 (β-myosin heavy chain)",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is most often caused by pathogenic variants in sarcomeric protein genes. The classic mutation located on chromosome 14q (specifically 14q11–q12) affects the MYH7 gene, which encodes the β-myosin heavy chain. Altered MYH7 disrupts sarcomere contractile function, producing left-ventricular hypertrophy that can precipitate outflow obstruction, arrhythmias, and clinical manifestations such as syncope."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve",
    "long_answer": "Chvostek's sign is elicited by tapping over the course of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) just anterior to the ear. In hypoparathyroidism, the resulting hypocalcaemia increases neuromuscular excitability; stimulation of the facial nerve therefore provokes twitching of the ipsilateral facial muscles, producing the characteristic sign."
}

{
    "short_answer": "18q deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with 18q deletion syndrome (monosomy of the long arm of chromosome 18, also called De Grouchy syndrome) frequently exhibit neuro-developmental problems; population studies show a markedly higher prevalence of autism spectrum disorders among those carrying the 18q– deletion compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis is a congenital disorder characterized by combined capillary-vascular malformations (e.g., port-wine stains) and pigmentary skin lesions (e.g., dermal melanocytosis, nevus of Ota). Because its principal manifestations are cutaneous and diagnosis and management (laser therapy, monitoring for associated skin findings) fall within the realm of skin disease, the specialty most commonly responsible for evaluating and treating these patients is dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant cylindroma",
    "long_answer": "In CYLD cutaneous syndrome, the numerous benign cylindromas that develop on the scalp and face may undergo malignant transformation, producing a malignant cylindroma (also called cylindrocarcinoma). This aggressive neoplasm can locally invade and metastasize, making malignant cylindroma a recognized and serious complication of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypocalcemia",
    "long_answer": "Hypocalcemia is defined as an abnormally low concentration of calcium in the blood. Because calcium is essential for neuromuscular excitability, reduced serum calcium can manifest with perioral or limb numbness and tingling, carpopedal spasms, tetany, and generalized seizures. One of the most frequent endocrine causes of persistent hypocalcemia is hypoparathyroidism, in which inadequate parathyroid hormone production diminishes renal calcium reabsorption and intestinal calcium absorption, leading directly to low serum calcium levels and the associated neurologic and muscular symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAS/MAPK pathway",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome belongs to the group of ‘RASopathies’. Germ-line gain-of-function mutations in PTPN11, SOS1, KRAS, RAF1, RIT1 and related genes disrupt the normal control of the Ras–mitogen-activated protein kinase (RAS/MAPK) signalling cascade, causing an attenuated (dysregulated) pathway output that underlies the characteristic developmental, cardiac and growth abnormalities of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercalcemic crisis",
    "long_answer": "When a parathyroid adenoma decompensates, it can abruptly oversecrete parathyroid hormone, driving serum calcium to very high levels. The resulting life-threatening endocrine emergency is a hypercalcemic crisis (also called parathyroid crisis), characterised by severe hypercalcaemia with dehydration, renal failure, neuro-cognitive changes and cardiac dysrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology",
    "long_answer": "Sinus barotrauma results from pressure-related injury to the paranasal sinuses during changes in ambient pressure experienced by pilots and aircrews. Diagnosis and management of disorders of the sinuses, middle ear, and related aeromedical problems fall under the ear, nose, and throat specialty—formally called otolaryngology. Therefore, ENT/otolaryngology physicians are the specialists who treat sinus barotrauma in aviators."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellar abiotrophy",
    "long_answer": "Cerebellar abiotrophy (often termed cerebellar cortical abiotrophy) is the progressive form of cerebellar degeneration. In this disorder Purkinje cells and other cerebellar neurons undergo post-natal, ongoing degeneration, so clinical signs such as ataxia and tremor worsen with time. In contrast, congenital cerebellar hypoplasia is non-progressive. Therefore cerebellar abiotrophy is recognized as the progressive type of cerebellar degeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unique",
    "long_answer": "Families dealing with distal trisomy 10q can turn to Unique – the Rare Chromosome & Genomic Disorder Support Group. Unique maintains an international registry, produces disorder-specific information sheets, and hosts online and local peer-support networks that connect families facing rare chromosomal duplications such as distal trisomy 10q."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Primary cutaneous amyloidosis is characterized by the deposition of amyloid protein limited to the skin, leading to pruritic papules, macules, or plaques without systemic involvement. Because it is a disorder of the skin’s structure and appearance, diagnosis (e.g., skin biopsy) and management (topical therapies, phototherapy, systemic agents when needed) fall within the expertise of dermatologists. Therefore, dermatology is the most relevant medical specialty for treating this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Postpartum hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Because hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome involves collagen‐related tissue fragility and poor vascular/uterine recoil, women with hEDS are at higher risk of excessive bleeding after delivery. Impaired collagen cross-linking can reduce uterine contractility and weaken blood-vessel support, predisposing to postpartum hemorrhage as well as other labor issues such as rapid labor, perineal tears, and pelvic-organ prolapse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravitreal methotrexate",
    "long_answer": "The current standard first-line therapy for primary intraocular lymphoma is serial intravitreal injections of methotrexate (typically 400 µg/0.1 mL given twice weekly then tapered). The antimetabolite achieves high local drug levels within the vitreous and retina, induces rapid regression of lymphoma cells, spares surrounding ocular structures, and reduces the risk of CNS dissemination better than external-beam radiotherapy alone. Thus, intravitreal methotrexate is accepted as the initial treatment of choice for PIOL without concurrent CNS disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Daentl-Townsend-Siegel syndrome is the eponym originally applied to the developmental disorder now best known as oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD). In the literature it is also called the Meyer-Schwickerath syndrome. All three names describe the same autosomal dominant condition produced most often by pathogenic variants in the GJA1 (connexin-43) gene, characterised by ocular anomalies (microcornea, glaucoma), distinctive dentition (enamel hypoplasia, small peg-shaped teeth) and digital malformations (syndactyly, camptodactyly)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dura mater",
    "long_answer": "The optic nerve is an extension of the central nervous system and is surrounded by meningeal layers. Its outer fibrous sheath is a direct continuation of the cranial dura mater, which fuses with the sclera at the back of the eyeball. Hence, it is the dura mater that ensheathes the optic nerve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Propranolol",
    "long_answer": "Propranolol is a non-selective β-adrenergic blocker commonly prescribed to relieve symptoms in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by slowing heart rate, reducing myocardial oxygen demand, and lessening left-ventricular outflow obstruction. The same drug is also an established, FDA-approved agent for the prophylactic treatment of migraine headaches, where its central β-blockade helps dampen cortical excitability and trigeminovascular activation. Thus, propranolol serves both as a therapy for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and as a preventive medication for migraines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB4 (MDR3)",
    "long_answer": "The ABCB4 protein, also called MDR3, is a canalicular ATP-binding cassette transporter that flips phosphatidylcholine from the inner to the outer leaflet of the apical (canalicular) membrane of hepatocytes, allowing its secretion into bile. Mutations in ABCB4 cause progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 3, underscoring its key role in phospholipid transport across the hepatocyte apical membrane."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hawaii",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, autochthonous human infections with the rat lung-worm Angiostrongylus cantonensis, presenting as eosinophilic meningitis, have been documented almost exclusively in Hawaii. The parasite’s life-cycle is established there in rats and gastropods, and multiple confirmed clinical cases have been reported from the islands, making Hawaii the recognized U.S. state with documented eosinophilic meningitis caused by A. cantonensis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In alpha-mannosidosis, an allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) is used to replace the patient’s defective marrow with donor-derived multipotent hematopoietic stem cells. These engrafted cells differentiate into macrophages and other blood-borne cells that secrete functional α-mannosidase, thereby supplying enzyme to affected tissues and slowing or halting disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chediak-Higashi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Chediak-Higashi syndrome is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal trafficking disorder in which defective melanosome formation leads to profound hypopigmentation. As a result, the retinal pigment epithelium lacks melanin and appears essentially absent on ophthalmoscopy, producing the striking pale fundus, photophobia and reduced visual acuity characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "World Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "The Fredrickson classification (Types I–V) of familial hyperlipidemias, originally proposed by Donald Fredrickson and colleagues in 1967, was formally adopted as the standard nomenclature for these disorders by the World Health Organization (WHO). This WHO endorsement established the classification’s global use in clinical and research settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrichosis",
    "long_answer": "In the film, Lila Jute is described as being covered in dense hair on virtually every part of her body, a hallmark of the rare disorder hypertrichosis (sometimes nicknamed “werewolf syndrome”). Hypertrichosis is characterized by excessive growth of terminal hair in areas that are not normally hairy, resulting from genetic or sporadic alterations affecting hair-follicle cycling. This condition, rather than common androgen-dependent hirsutism, best explains the generalized, thick body hair portrayed in the character."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO5A",
    "long_answer": "Elejalde syndrome (neuroectodermal melanolysosomal disease) is allelic to Griscelli syndrome type 1 and results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the MYO5A gene, which encodes the motor protein myosin-Va essential for melanosome and neuronal vesicle transport. Defects in MYO5A lead to the characteristic silvery hair pigmentation and severe neurologic dysfunction seen in Elejalde syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Terminally differentiated hair cells",
    "long_answer": "In the human (and more broadly mammalian) organ of Corti the auditory hair cells, as well as their adjacent supporting cells, permanently exit the cell-division cycle shortly after birth under the control of inhibitors such as p27^Kip1. Because these cells are terminally differentiated, no mitotic progenitor pool remains and the supporting cells cannot transdifferentiate into new hair cells as they do in birds or fish. Consequently, once cochlear sensory hair cells are damaged or lost with aging, they cannot be regenerated, leading to presbycusis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CASK",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function variants in the X-linked CASK gene cause the syndrome ‘microcephaly with pontine and cerebellar hypoplasia’ (MICPCH), a form of pontocerebellar hypoplasia. A cardinal clinical feature of CASK-related MICPCH is congenital hypotonia, alongside severe developmental delay and microcephaly, linking the gene to both pontocerebellar hypoplasia and hypotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electromyography (EMG)",
    "long_answer": "Neuromyotonia is identified by the characteristic pattern of continuous, high-frequency spontaneous discharges (doublets, triplets, myokymic and neuromyotonic discharges) seen on electromyography. EMG therefore provides the definitive physiological evidence of peripheral nerve hyperexcitability that is essential for diagnosing neuromyotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "When lipomas in familial multiple lipomatosis are unusually large, situated deep to fascia, or suspected of infiltrating muscle fibres or neurovascular structures, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the imaging modality of choice. MRI offers superior soft-tissue contrast, delineates the extent of fatty tissue, and accurately shows any intramuscular or neurovascular involvement, making it the recommended diagnostic tool in such cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Holocarboxylase synthetase",
    "long_answer": "The HLCS gene encodes holocarboxylase synthetase, the biotin-protein ligase that attaches biotin to the four mitochondrial carboxylases. Pathogenic mutations in HLCS diminish the catalytic activity of holocarboxylase synthetase itself, leading to the clinical picture of holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency (multiple carboxylase deficiency)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLUT2 (SLC2A2)",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi–Bickel syndrome, also classified as glycogen storage disease type XI, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC2A2 gene, which encodes the facilitative glucose transporter 2 (GLUT2). Absence or severe reduction of functional GLUT2 in hepatocytes, pancreatic β-cells, renal tubular cells, and enterocytes disrupts glucose and galactose transport, leading to hepatomegaly, renal Fanconi syndrome, and the characteristic clinical features of Fanconi–Bickel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Mitral stenosis is a valvular heart disease; its evaluation with echocardiography, medical management (e.g., rate control, anticoagulation), percutaneous balloon valvotomy, and referral for valve surgery are all coordinated by cardiologists—the specialists in cardiology—who are trained to diagnose and treat heart valve disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase deficiency porphyria is an inborn error of heme biosynthesis, a pathway central to red-blood-cell and hemoglobin metabolism. Disorders of heme synthesis are classically evaluated and managed by hematologists, who specialize in diseases of the blood and its constituents; therefore, the hematology specialty is responsible for diagnosing and treating this form of porphyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonidine",
    "long_answer": "Shapiro syndrome is characterized by recurrent episodes of spontaneous hypothermia accompanied by profuse sweating (hyperhidrosis). Multiple case reports have shown that the α2-adrenergic agonist clonidine attenuates or abolishes these temperature-dysregulation attacks, presumably by modulating hypothalamic autonomic output. Consequently, clonidine is the medication most commonly employed to control the hypothermia in Shapiro syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor necrosis factor-α",
    "long_answer": "Tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α), a key member of the tumor necrosis factor cytokine family, is released in large amounts by macrophages exposed to bacterial endotoxin. TNF-α triggers widespread vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, fever, and activation of the coagulation cascade, driving the systemic inflammatory response that culminates in septic shock."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Speech-language pathologist",
    "long_answer": "Both vocal cord dysfunction (paradoxical vocal fold movement) and puberphonia (mutational falsetto) are voice disorders primarily managed with behavioral voice therapy and breathing retraining rather than surgery or medication. These interventions are delivered by a speech-language pathologist (speech therapist), the specialist who evaluates and treats disordered voice production and teaches the specific vocal and respiratory techniques needed for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antibiotics",
    "long_answer": "Chorioretinitis secondary to bacterial infection is managed with antibiotic therapy—commonly systemic and sometimes intravitreal broad-spectrum antibiotics (e.g., vancomycin, ceftazidime). These agents eradicate the causative bacteria, allowing resolution of the retinal–choroidal inflammation and preventing vision-threatening complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bupropion",
    "long_answer": "Bupropion is a norepinephrine–dopamine reuptake inhibitor that is frequently added to SSRIs or SNRIs to augment treatment-resistant persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia). The same dopaminergic and noradrenergic activity makes it a well-established non-stimulant option for managing attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, so it is used in both clinical contexts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myofibril",
    "long_answer": "Central core disease is a congenital myopathy in which ‘cores’—regions lacking normal oxidative enzyme activity—form within the contractile apparatus of skeletal muscle fibers. These cores arise in the myofibrils, the basic rod-like contractile organelles composed of repeating sarcomeres. Thus, the myofibril is the organelle specifically affected in central core disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Acute leukemia is defined by the rapid proliferation of immature hematopoietic blast cells. Because these blasts quickly crowd out normal marrow elements, symptoms (anemia, infections, bleeding) appear abruptly, and the untreated disease progresses over only weeks to a few months, giving it a recent onset and short clinical course compared with the more indolent chronic leukemias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RRM2B",
    "long_answer": "RRM2B (also called p53R2) is a p53-inducible small subunit of ribonucleotide reductase. It is directly transactivated by the TP53 gene product, and pathogenic variants in RRM2B cause a form of mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome, confirming its association with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 10–14 days",
    "long_answer": "Measles has an incubation period of roughly 10–14 days; most patients develop the initial prodromal symptoms (fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) about 10–12 days after exposure, with the characteristic rash following a few days later. This timeframe is consistently reported by CDC and WHO guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "9p deletion",
    "long_answer": "Loss of genetic material from the short arm of chromosome 9 (monosomy 9p) causes a well-defined syndrome characterized by intellectual disability and a high frequency of genital malformations such as ambiguous genitalia or gonadal dysgenesis, making the 9p deletion the classic chromosomal lesion associated with this clinical combination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectodermal dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Tooth and nail (Witkop) syndrome is a hereditary form of ectodermal dysplasia in which development of ectoderm-derived structures—specifically the teeth (leading to hypodontia) and nails (thin, brittle, dysplastic nails)—is impaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Europe",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar hydatid disease, caused by the tapeworm Echinococcus multilocularis, is increasingly reported in Europe because red fox (Vulpes vulpes) populations have expanded after successful anti-rabies vaccination and urbanization. The larger fox reservoir raises environmental contamination with parasite eggs, leading to emerging human cases across several European countries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ascaris",
    "long_answer": "Larvae of the nematode genus Ascaris (especially Ascaris lumbricoides) migrate from the intestine through the bloodstream to the lungs, where they can provoke an intense eosinophilic inflammatory response. This pulmonary phase manifests clinically as eosinophilic pneumonia (Löffler’s syndrome) characterized by transient pulmonary infiltrates and marked eosinophilia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankle-foot orthosis",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsy (HNPP) often causes foot-drop and distal muscle weakness due to compressive peripheral nerve palsies. An ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) is a lightweight brace that stabilises the ankle and foot during gait, prevents excessive plantar-flexion, and reduces tripping, thereby alleviating functional impairment associated with HNPP. Consequently, clinicians commonly prescribe an AFO to help manage the motor symptoms of HNPP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incomplete spinal cord injury",
    "long_answer": "In an incomplete spinal cord injury some, but not all, motor pathways are disrupted. This partial damage can leave certain muscles totally denervated (paralysis) while others retain limited descending input, resulting in residual voluntary movement that is weaker than normal. Thus an incomplete lesion characteristically produces a combination of paralysis in some muscle groups and muscle weakness in others."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exposure therapy",
    "long_answer": "Equinophobia—a specific phobia involving an intense, irrational fear of horses—is most effectively treated with exposure therapy, a form of cognitive-behavioral therapy in which the patient is gradually and systematically exposed to horses or horse-related stimuli while employing relaxation and coping techniques. Repeated controlled exposure leads to desensitization and extinction of the pathological fear response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Agenesis of the corpus callosum is typically identified in utero with prenatal ultrasonography, which can visualize the absence of the corpus callosum and associated ventricular changes safely during pregnancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "Rombo syndrome is a rare genetic skin disorder whose clinical triad includes vermiculate atrophoderma, multiple basal-cell carcinomas, and prominent telangiectasia. Telangiectasia consists of visibly dilated superficial blood vessels that appear as persistent red streaks or patches on the skin, making it the redness-characterized skin condition associated with Rombo syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sudeck’s atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Prolonged immobilisation of a limb after trauma can precipitate Sudeck’s atrophy, now termed complex regional pain syndrome type I. The condition is characterised by sympathetic dysregulation that produces progressive muscle wasting, oedema, joint stiffness and severe limb pain despite the absence of ongoing tissue damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bird-like facies",
    "long_answer": "Al-Gazali-Donnai-Mueller syndrome is characterized by severe microcephaly and a narrow face with a beak-shaped nose and receding chin. This distinctive configuration is classically termed a “bird-like facies,” the standard dysmorphologic descriptor for such an abnormal facial shape."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6 (IL-6)",
    "long_answer": "Interleukin-6 is markedly increased in the aqueous humour of patients with uveitis. IL-6 is classically produced by immune cells and drives acute-phase and T-cell–mediated inflammatory responses, qualifying it as a pro-inflammatory cytokine. Conversely, when released from contracting skeletal muscle, it functions as a myokine that can exert systemic anti-inflammatory effects by inducing IL-10 and inhibiting TNF-α production. This dual role and its documented elevation in ocular inflammation make IL-6 the cytokine described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cesarean section",
    "long_answer": "Elective cesarean delivery performed before rupture of membranes minimizes the neonate’s exposure to infected maternal genital secretions, thereby preventing intrapartum transmission of herpes simplex virus from mother to fetus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Deafness-vitiligo-achalasia syndrome is a very rare disorder affecting several neural-crest–derived tissues. Reported families show biallelic (homozygous) loss-of-function mutations in endothelin-signalling genes, and the trait appears only when both parents contribute the mutant allele, indicating classic autosomal-recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4–6 years",
    "long_answer": "For children with Binder’s syndrome (maxillonasal dysplasia), surgical reinsertion of the nasolabial muscles is generally advised in the early mixed-dentition period, about 4 to 6 years of age, to optimize mid-facial growth and nasal contour while taking advantage of the child’s remaining facial development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutis laxa",
    "long_answer": "Familial benign copper deficiency (the mild ‘occipital horn’ variant of Menkes disease) impairs the copper-dependent enzyme lysyl oxidase. Inadequate cross-linking of elastin and collagen causes loose, sagging, inelastic skin—clinically known as cutis laxa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "46,XX",
    "long_answer": "Pure gonadal agenesis (also called pure gonadal dysgenesis) is seen in individuals with either a 46,XX or a 46,XY chromosomal complement, but epidemiologic series show that the 46,XX karyotype is the more frequent form—accounting for roughly two-thirds of reported cases. Thus, the 46,XX karyotype is the one most commonly associated with (i.e., shows the higher prevalence of) gonadal agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy",
    "long_answer": "A trisomy is a chromosomal abnormality in which an embryo possesses three copies of a particular chromosome instead of the normal two. The resulting gene-dosage imbalance commonly disrupts early development; many trisomies (e.g., trisomy 16) are incompatible with life and are a leading genetic cause of spontaneous miscarriage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus is a frequent cause of necrotizing fasciitis, particularly in monomicrobial (often MRSA-associated) cases, and it is also one of the most prevalent early pathogens isolated from burn wounds. Its array of virulence factors—such as cytolytic toxins, superantigens, and biofilm-forming capacity—facilitates rapid tissue invasion and destruction in necrotizing fasciitis and promotes colonization and infection of the damaged skin barrier in burn injuries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treponema carateum",
    "long_answer": "Pinta is a non-venereal treponemal infection. It is caused by the spirochete bacterium Treponema carateum, which, like other treponemes, exhibits a spiral (helically coiled) morphology observable under dark-field microscopy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankyrin-B syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Long-QT syndrome type 4 is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the ANK2 gene, which encodes the membrane-adapter protein ankyrin-B. The resulting clinical picture—including prolonged QT, sinus-node dysfunction and other arrhythmias—is collectively termed Ankyrin-B syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is a systemic connective-tissue disorder that affects the cardiovascular, ocular, skeletal, pulmonary, skin, and nervous systems. Because it involves so many organ systems, clinicians have catalogued far more than 30 distinct clinical features.  Examples include aortic-root dilatation, aortic dissection, mitral-valve prolapse, ectopia lentis, high myopia, retinal detachment, tall stature, increased arm-span-to-height ratio, arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum, pectus carinatum, scoliosis, kyphosis, protrusio acetabuli, joint hypermobility, wrist and thumb signs, high-arched palate, malar hypoplasia, spontaneous pneumothorax, apical blebs, dural ectasia, lumbosacral meningocele, stretch marks (striae), hernias, early osteoarthritis, and sleep-disordered breathing, among many others.  Standard diagnostic schemes such as the revised Ghent criteria list dozens of specific findings that can contribute to the diagnosis.  Therefore, the syndrome is associated with well over 30 recognized signs and symptoms, and the statement that it has fewer than 30 is not correct."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Warthin–Starry stain",
    "long_answer": "Treponema pallidum, the spirochete responsible for syphilis, does not take up the Gram stain well. It can be visualized in tissue sections with silver impregnation techniques, most classically the Warthin–Starry stain, which deposits silver on the bacterial cell wall and renders the slender spirochetes black against a pale background."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VACTERL association",
    "long_answer": "VACTERL association (also called VATER) is a non-random constellation of congenital anomalies: Vertebral defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac malformations, Tracheo-Esophageal fistula with Esophageal atresia, Renal anomalies, and Limb abnormalities. The classic combination includes esophageal atresia together with vertebral and cardiac defects, fitting the condition described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diarrhoea",
    "long_answer": "Diarrhoea is characterized by the passage of frequent, loose or watery stools. The excessive loss of water and electrolytes in these stools rapidly depletes body fluids, making dehydration a common and potentially dangerous consequence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrophic vaginitis",
    "long_answer": "Removal of the ovaries eliminates the main source of estrogen. The consequent estrogen deficiency leads to atrophy and thinning of the vaginal epithelium, decreased lubrication, and increased fragility—collectively termed atrophic vaginitis (now included in the genitourinary syndrome of menopause). These changes commonly produce painful intercourse (dyspareunia) in women after an oophorectomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teratogenicity",
    "long_answer": "Isotretinoin is a potent human teratogen; exposure during pregnancy can cause severe, life-threatening fetal malformations and embryonic loss. Therefore, it is contraindicated in women who are or could become pregnant, making it unsuitable for treating hirsutism in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osmotic fragility test",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis is characterized by defects in red blood-cell membrane proteins, making the cells more prone to lysis in hypotonic solutions. The osmotic fragility test exposes erythrocytes to progressively dilute saline; spherocytes lyse at higher tonicities than normal cells, revealing the membrane abnormality. Because of this ability to detect increased membrane fragility, the osmotic fragility test is traditionally and widely used to help confirm hereditary spherocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPS6KA3 gene",
    "long_answer": "Coffin–Lowry syndrome is an X-linked disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the RPS6KA3 gene (also called RSK2) on Xp22.2. RPS6KA3 encodes ribosomal S6 kinase 2, a downstream effector of the MAPK/ERK pathway; pathogenic variants disrupt this kinase’s activity, leading to the characteristic intellectual disability, facial features, and skeletal anomalies of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ochlophobia",
    "long_answer": "Because people with phonophobia chronically shun concerts, stadiums, and other noisy gatherings, their fear can generalize from loud sounds to the crowded settings where those sounds occur. This conditioned generalization may give rise to ochlophobia—the specific phobia of crowds."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tutankhamun",
    "long_answer": "Computed-tomography and genetic studies of the mummy published in 2010 (JAMA, Hawass et al.) showed that the 18th-dynasty pharaoh Tutankhamun had a congenital clubfoot (talipes equinovarus) of the left foot, probably worsened by Köhler disease II. More than one hundred walking canes found in his tomb corroborate that he needed support for walking, confirming that this pharaoh suffered from a clubfoot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mast cells",
    "long_answer": "In allergic conjunctivitis, IgE-sensitized mast cells residing in the conjunctival mucosa degranulate upon allergen exposure, releasing histamine and other mediators. The histamine they release produces vasodilation, itching, tearing, and other characteristic ocular symptoms of the allergic reaction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Hoyeraal–Hreidarsson syndrome is a severe variant of dyskeratosis congenita that characteristically leads to progressive bone-marrow failure and immunodeficiency. Because management centers on monitoring and treating cytopenias and performing hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, the specialty most routinely responsible for these patients’ care is hematology (typically pediatric hematology/oncology)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Live attenuated vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Phase I clinical studies of human parainfluenza virus type 3 (HPIV-3) have employed a live attenuated, cold-passaged/temperature-sensitive HPIV-3 strain (e.g., the cp45 virus) administered intranasally. This live attenuated vaccine was shown to be safe and immunogenic in adults and young children during those early-phase trials."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniotic band syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Amniotic band syndrome (also called amniotic band sequence or Streeter dysplasia) is a congenital condition in which fibrous amniotic strands entangle parts of the developing fetus. The strands create circumferential constriction rings around limbs or digits—hence the term constriction ring syndrome—and can also disrupt normal craniofacial development, leading to anomalies such as cleft lip and/or cleft palate. Thus, amniotic band syndrome is characteristically associated with both constriction rings and cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin C",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of vitamin C (ascorbic acid) weakens collagen synthesis in vascular connective tissue, making capillary walls fragile. This vascular fragility leads to perifollicular petechiae and larger skin and mucosal hemorrhages that clinically present as purpura."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CACNA1A",
    "long_answer": "Spinocerebellar ataxia type 6 (SCA6) is caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the CACNA1A gene on chromosome 19p13, which encodes the α1A subunit of the P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channel. Expansion of this polyglutamine tract disrupts channel function in cerebellar Purkinje cells, leading to progressive cerebellar ataxia characteristic of SCA6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous normal saline",
    "long_answer": "In diabetic ketoacidosis, the first-line treatment for severe dehydration is rapid rehydration with isotonic 0.9 % sodium chloride solution (normal saline). Large-volume IV saline restores intravascular volume, improves renal perfusion, and helps lower blood glucose and ketone levels before insulin therapy is optimized."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lithium therapy",
    "long_answer": "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus frequently arises in patients treated with lithium salts for bipolar disorder; lithium interferes with aquaporin-2 expression in collecting-duct cells, making the kidneys unresponsive to antidiuretic hormone and producing marked polyuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Dyscalculia is a brain-based learning disorder linked to atypical function of parietal and associated cortical networks that handle numerical processing. Because it involves the structure and function of the nervous system, its clinical evaluation and management are handled within the medical specialty of Neurology, which diagnoses and treats such neurocognitive conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLB1",
    "long_answer": "GM1 gangliosidosis is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the GLB1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-galactosidase-1. Mutations in GLB1 reduce or eliminate β-galactosidase activity, leading to accumulation of GM1 ganglioside in neurons and other tissues and producing the clinical features of GM1 gangliosidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "Berylliosis (chronic beryllium disease) is an immune-mediated response to inhaled beryllium that primarily affects the lungs, leading to formation of non-caseating granulomas within pulmonary tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inguinal hernia",
    "long_answer": "Polydactyly-myopia syndrome is a rare hereditary disorder marked mainly by post-axial polydactyly and severe early-onset myopia. Clinical case series describing the syndrome consistently record the presence of a protrusion of abdominal contents through the inguinal canal—an inguinal hernia—as one of its associated somatic findings. Hence, the characteristic hernia seen in this syndrome is an inguinal hernia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondrial DNA point mutation",
    "long_answer": "Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) is caused by missense point mutations in mitochondrial DNA—most commonly in the ND4, ND6, or ND1 genes that encode complex I subunits of the respiratory chain. Because the pathogenic variants reside in the mitochondrial genome, LHON shows maternal inheritance and does not follow Mendelian nuclear patterns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Microcephaly-lymphoedema-chorioretinal dysplasia is usually produced by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the KIF11 gene. Because only one altered copy of KIF11 is sufficient to cause the phenotype, the syndrome follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern, with affected individuals having a 50 % chance of transmitting the disorder to each child."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Collecting duct",
    "long_answer": "In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis the defect is in the α-intercalated cells that normally reside in the cortical portion of the renal collecting duct. These cells fail to secrete hydrogen ions, preventing urine acidification and producing the characteristic metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Classic Stickler syndrome is caused by heterozygous mutations in collagen genes such as COL2A1, COL11A1 and COL11A2. These mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, so a single altered allele is sufficient to cause the disorder and it can pass from an affected parent to offspring with a 50 % recurrence risk. Although rare recessive forms exist, the established and predominant inheritance pattern of Stickler syndrome is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Severe respiratory depression",
    "long_answer": "Benzodiazepines enhance GABA-mediated CNS inhibition, while tricyclic antidepressants have strong sedative and anticholinergic effects. When taken together their depressant actions are additive, markedly increasing the likelihood of profound sedation and life-threatening respiratory depression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochrane Collaboration",
    "long_answer": "A systematic review carried out by the Cochrane Collaboration’s Occupational Safety and Health Review Group concluded that workplace noise-control legislation lowered average noise exposures by several decibels, demonstrating the effectiveness of such laws."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptozocin",
    "long_answer": "Streptozocin (streptozotocin) is a nitrosourea antibiotic that is preferentially taken up by pancreatic islet cells and alkylates their DNA, leading to selective necrosis of these cells. Because glucagonomas arise from the glucagon-secreting alpha cells of the islets, streptozocin is used as a cytotoxic agent to damage these alpha cells and treat malignant glucagon-secreting tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diarrhea",
    "long_answer": "Giardiasis, caused by the protozoan Giardia lamblia/duodenalis, most frequently presents with profuse, foul-smelling watery or greasy diarrhea. This symptom results from parasite-induced malabsorption of fats and carbohydrates in the small intestine and is reported as the most common and prominent clinical feature in epidemiologic and clinical studies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Albright hereditary osteodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (pseudohypoparathyroidism type Ia) is a congenital disorder caused by GNAS-gene mutations. A key skeletal feature is brachydactyly that characteristically affects both the 4th and 5th metacarpals and the homologous metatarsals, so the toes are shortened as well as the fingers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin dysregulation",
    "long_answer": "During pregnancy, high estradiol enhances expression of tryptophan-hydroxylase and serotonin transporters, boosting central serotonergic tone. The rapid fall in estradiol immediately after delivery withdraws this support, leading to reduced serotonin synthesis, release and receptor signalling in mood-regulating brain regions (e.g., raphe-prefrontal and limbic circuits). This abrupt serotonin dysregulation can precipitate low mood, anhedonia and other symptoms characteristic of postpartum depression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic adrenaline injection",
    "long_answer": "In Mallory-Weiss syndrome, most tears stop bleeding spontaneously, but persistent or severe hemorrhage is controlled endoscopically. Injecting dilute adrenaline (epinephrine) around and into the tear causes local vasoconstriction and tamponade, achieving rapid hemostasis without the need for surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercalcemia",
    "long_answer": "Jansen’s metaphyseal chondrodysplasia is caused by constitutive activation of the PTH/PTHrP receptor, producing persistent, PTH-independent hypercalcemia. Sustained hypercalcemia leads to increased urinary calcium excretion, renal calcium deposition (nephrocalcinosis), and, over time, progressive chronic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parental consanguinity",
    "long_answer": "In a number of reported Dubowitz syndrome families, the mother and father are blood relatives (often first-cousins). This consanguinity among parents supports an autosomal-recessive mode of inheritance for the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Influenza",
    "long_answer": "The word “influenza” (the common ‘flu’) is taken directly from the Italian term influenza, which literally means “influence.” In the 16th–17th centuries Italian physicians used the word for diseases they believed were brought about by the influence of the stars; English later adopted it specifically for the acute viral respiratory illness we now call influenza."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-carbohydrate meals",
    "long_answer": "In hypokalemic periodic paralysis, a large carbohydrate load provokes an insulin surge that drives potassium from the bloodstream into muscle cells, rapidly lowering serum potassium and precipitating weakness or paralysis. Therefore, patients are advised to avoid high-carbohydrate meals—especially those rich in sugars or refined starches—to reduce the risk of an attack."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MBTPS2",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans is an X-linked genodermatosis caused by pathogenic variants in the MBTPS2 gene, which encodes the site-2 metalloprotease involved in activating sterol-regulatory element–binding proteins. Missense mutations in MBTPS2 disrupt epidermal lipid homeostasis and hair-follicle differentiation, producing the characteristic follicular hyperkeratosis, scarring alopecia, and photophobia seen in this rare disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Russell-Silver syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The American pediatrician Henry K. Silver, who co-founded the first pediatric nurse-practitioner education program in 1965, also described a form of severe proportionate intrauterine and postnatal growth retardation that now bears his name together with that of British physician A. Russell. This congenital growth disorder, known as Russell-Silver syndrome (or Silver-Russell syndrome), is characterized by intrauterine growth restriction, postnatal short stature, relative macrocephaly, and distinctive cranio-facial features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NY-ESO-1",
    "long_answer": "In clinical trials of adoptive T-cell therapy for myxoid liposarcoma, patients receive autologous T cells genetically engineered to express a high-affinity T-cell receptor that specifically recognizes the cancer-testis antigen NY-ESO-1 (encoded by the CTAG1B gene). These modified T cells target tumor cells presenting NY-ESO-1–derived peptides in the context of HLA-A*02:01, leading to selective tumor cell killing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stuttering",
    "long_answer": "Damage or hemorrhage produced by a cerebral arteriovenous malformation can injure cortical–subcortical speech-motor circuits (e.g., left frontal operculum, basal ganglia–thalamic pathways). Such lesions are a well-documented organic trigger for sudden-onset, neurogenic stuttering—characterised by involuntary sound or syllable repetitions, blocks and prolongations—making stuttering a recognized speech disorder that can result from a cerebral AVM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sphincter pupillae",
    "long_answer": "Parasympathetic fibers originating in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel with the oculomotor nerve, synapse in the ciliary ganglion, and reach the eye via short ciliary nerves. These axons innervate the sphincter pupillae muscle in the iris, whose contraction constricts the pupil (miosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STAR",
    "long_answer": "Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia is almost always caused by loss-of-function mutations in the STAR gene (steroidogenic acute regulatory protein) located on chromosome 8p11.2. STAR is essential for transporting cholesterol into mitochondria, the first step of steroid hormone biosynthesis; its absence blocks steroid production, leading to adrenal insufficiency and lipid accumulation characteristic of the lipoid form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncology",
    "long_answer": "Mismatch repair cancer syndrome (also called constitutional mismatch-repair deficiency) presents with multiple early-onset malignancies—brain tumours, hematologic cancers, colorectal and other solid tumours. The pattern is usually first recognised by oncologists, who order tumour MMR/MSI testing, arrange confirmatory germ-line genetic studies, and then deliver or coordinate cancer-directed therapy and surveillance. Consequently, the medical specialty most commonly responsible for diagnosing and treating MMRCS is oncology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Normansfield",
    "long_answer": "After leaving his post at the Royal Earlswood Asylum in 1868, John Langdon Down founded a private residential institution for people with developmental and intellectual disabilities at Normansfield in Teddington, Middlesex. This ‘Normansfield Training Institution’ became a model facility for the humane care and education of individuals with what is now called Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DAXX",
    "long_answer": "Genomic studies of esthesioneuroblastoma have identified recurrent, loss-of-function mutations in the DAXX gene, which is located on human chromosome 6p21.3. DAXX encodes a multifunctional chromatin-associated protein involved in transcriptional regulation and telomere maintenance; its mutation is thought to contribute to the oncogenesis of this neuroendocrine tumour."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediastinum",
    "long_answer": "Although germ cell tumors usually arise in the testes or ovaries, misplaced primordial germ cells can lodge along the midline during embryogenesis; the most common extragonadal site where they subsequently give rise to tumors is the anterior mediastinum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3R1",
    "long_answer": "SHORT syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PIK3R1 gene, which encodes the p85α regulatory subunit of class IA phosphoinositide-3-kinase (PI3K). Defective PIK3R1 impairs PI3K activity, leading to reduced downstream AKT and mTOR signalling, a pathway crucial for cell growth and proliferation. Thus, PIK3R1 mutations directly disrupt the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signal route implicated in SHORT syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baclofen",
    "long_answer": "Baclofen, a GABA-B receptor agonist, is widely prescribed to reduce muscle spasticity in children with spastic cerebral palsy and is likewise a first-line symptomatic treatment (orally or via intrathecal pump) for the lower-limb spasticity characteristic of hereditary spastic paraplegia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Koilonychia",
    "long_answer": "Koilonychia—spoon-shaped, thin, concave nails—is a classic dermatologic manifestation of chronic iron-deficiency anemia. Low iron disrupts normal keratin synthesis in the nail matrix, producing fragile nails that curve upward at the edges. Clinically, the presence of koilonychia often prompts evaluation for underlying iron deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Both chorioretinitis and multifocal choroiditis with panuveitis are inflammatory disorders of the posterior segment of the eye. Systemic or local corticosteroids (e.g., oral prednisone, peri- or intravitreal steroid injections) are routinely used to suppress ocular inflammation, reduce tissue damage, and preserve vision in both conditions, making corticosteroids the standard, shared therapeutic approach."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elimination",
    "long_answer": "Within the hierarchy of hazard controls, completely eliminating the noise source or removing workers from exposure is the most effective way to prevent occupational noise-induced hearing loss, because it removes the hazard rather than merely shielding workers from it."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuropathology",
    "long_answer": "Chronic traumatic encephalopathy can be definitively confirmed only after death by microscopic examination of brain tissue. This evaluation—looking for the characteristic perivascular accumulations of hyper-phosphorylated tau in specific cortical regions—is carried out by a neuropathologist, the pathology subspecialty dedicated to diagnosing diseases of the nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Rasburicase converts uric acid to allantoin while generating hydrogen peroxide. In patients lacking glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, red blood cells cannot replenish reduced glutathione to neutralize this oxidative stress, leading to rapid oxidative damage, hemolytic anemia, and methemoglobinemia. Therefore rasburicase is contraindicated in G-6-PD–deficient individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenic flexure",
    "long_answer": "The splenic flexure (left colic flexure) lies at the watershed between the terminal branches of the superior mesenteric artery and the inferior mesenteric artery. Because it relies on this dual, end-arterial blood supply, it is particularly susceptible to hypoperfusion, making it the colon segment most prone to ischemic colitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Barth syndrome is an X-linked disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the TAZ (tafazzin) gene that disrupt cardiolipin remodeling. The clinical picture is dominated by cardiac involvement: infants and children with Barth syndrome commonly develop dilated cardiomyopathy and/or left-ventricular non-compaction, often accompanied by heart failure and life-threatening arrhythmias. Cardiomyopathy is considered one of the major and most severe manifestations of the disease, accounting for significant morbidity and mortality in affected males. Therefore, cardiomyopathy is indeed recognized as a severe complication of Barth syndrome by the medical and scientific community."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic intestinal pseudo-obstruction",
    "long_answer": "When no underlying anatomic, metabolic, or systemic disorder can be identified to explain chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction, the condition is designated idiopathic intestinal pseudo-obstruction (also termed primary CIPO)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galen",
    "long_answer": "Galen of Pergamon, the eminent physician practicing in the Roman Empire (2nd century CE), taught that an excess of the blood humour produced a state of vascular \"plethora\" manifested by a strong, bounding pulse—what modern medicine recognises as hypertension. To correct this excess, Galen specifically recommended phlebotomy (bloodletting), making him the classical authority associated with using bloodletting to treat elevated blood pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrocephalus",
    "long_answer": "In Chiari malformation, herniation of cerebellar tissue through the foramen magnum narrows or occludes the outlets of the fourth ventricle and obstructs cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) exit into the subarachnoid space. This blockage produces a non-communicating buildup of CSF within the ventricular system, resulting in obstructive hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L-methylfolate",
    "long_answer": "The C677T polymorphism in the MTHFR gene reduces activity of methylenetetrahydro­folate reductase, limiting conversion of folic acid to the bioactive methyl donor 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. Supplementation with L-methylfolate (5-methyltetrahydrofolate) bypasses this enzymatic step, normalizes folate-dependent methylation and homocysteine metabolism, and is therefore the appropriate therapeutic form of folate for individuals carrying the C677T variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMARCA2",
    "long_answer": "Nicolaides–Baraitser syndrome is almost invariably caused by heterozygous de-novo mutations in the SMARCA2 gene, which encodes the BRM ATP-dependent helicase subunit of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodeling complex. Pathogenic SMARCA2 variants disrupt chromatin remodeling and gene transcription, leading to the characteristic facial features, intellectual disability, and sparse hair seen in Nicolaides–Baraitser syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folic acid",
    "long_answer": "High-dose folic acid supplementation (≈5 mg daily begun before conception and continued through early pregnancy) has been shown to lower the incidence of congenital malformations, particularly neural tube defects, in the offspring of women with pre-gestational diabetes. Adequate folate availability supports proper neural tube closure and other embryonic developmental processes, counteracting the teratogenic risk associated with maternal hyperglycaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "The Shwartzman phenomenon is an endotoxin-mediated reaction that produces microvascular thrombosis followed by hemorrhage, mirroring the pathophysiologic process seen in disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). DIC is characterized by systemic activation of the coagulation cascade, leading to widespread microthrombi formation and consumption of clotting factors and platelets, which in turn results in simultaneous clotting and severe bleeding throughout the body. Hence, DIC is the condition classically associated with the Shwartzman phenomenon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PEX7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PEX7 gene, which encodes the peroxisomal targeting signal-2 (PTS2) receptor, cause rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1 (RCDP1) and can also produce a Refsum disease phenotype. Defective PEX7 impairs import of several PTS2-containing enzymes into peroxisomes, leading to the biochemical abnormalities characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 16",
    "long_answer": "About half of first-trimester miscarriages carry chromosomal errors; autosomal trisomies predominate, and the single most common specific abnormality found in products of conception is trisomy 16. Embryos with trisomy 16 are not viable beyond the early weeks, making this aneuploidy the one most frequently identified in early pregnancy loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The ‘heart-hand’ syndrome first reported by Ruiz de la Fuente and colleagues (often called the Spanish type) appears in successive generations of affected families, with both males and females transmitting the trait to their offspring. This vertical transmission without sex bias and with roughly 50 % risk to children is characteristic of autosomal dominant inheritance, which is therefore the mode of inheritance generally ascribed to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benzene",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and mechanistic studies show that occupational or environmental exposure to benzene markedly increases the incidence of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Benzene metabolites damage hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow, induce chromosomal aberrations (e.g., monosomy 7, translocations), and generate oxidative DNA lesions, all of which drive leukemogenic mutations that culminate in AML."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enamel micro-abrasion",
    "long_answer": "Mild to moderate dental fluorosis causes superficial enamel discoloration (white or brown mottling). Enamel micro-abrasion, which uses an acid–pumice slurry to remove a thin outer layer of enamel, eliminates or lightens these surface stains and leaves a polished surface, markedly improving the cosmetic appearance of fluorosed teeth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-Aminolevulinic acid",
    "long_answer": "Orally administered 5-aminolevulinic acid (5-ALA) is taken up and converted to the fluorescent molecule protoporphyrin IX, which preferentially accumulates in high-grade glioma cells. Under blue-violet surgical illumination, the tumor emits a bright red-pink fluorescence, allowing surgeons to distinguish malignant tissue more clearly and achieve a more complete resection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Koilocytosis",
    "long_answer": "Genital warts (condylomata acuminata) induced by human papillomavirus show characteristic koilocytosis—squamous epithelial cells with enlarged, irregular nuclei surrounded by a perinuclear clear halo—reflecting HPV-induced cytopathic changes and serving as a diagnostic histopathologic hallmark."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kosovo",
    "long_answer": "Surveillance reports and epidemiologic studies show that Kosovo recorded repeated, large-scale outbreaks of tularemia caused by Francisella tularensis from 1984 through 2012, involving hundreds of human cases and making it one of the most notable European hotspots for the disease during that interval."
}

{
    "short_answer": "German Shepherd",
    "long_answer": "The German Shepherd breed has a well-documented genetic predisposition to canine hip dysplasia because of its large size and conformational traits. At the same time, German Shepherds have long been favored as guide (seeing-eye) dogs due to their intelligence, trainability, and steady temperament. This combination makes the German Shepherd the breed that fits both aspects of the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pebbly ivory-colored skin lesions",
    "long_answer": "Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is uniquely characterized by clusters of firm, pebbly, ivory-colored papules and nodules located mainly between the scapulae and on the extensor surfaces of the upper arms and thighs. These distinctive cutaneous lesions are considered pathognomonic, meaning their presence strongly indicates Hunter syndrome and is not seen in other mucopolysaccharidoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fever",
    "long_answer": "Patients with acute myeloid leukemia frequently present with fever, reflecting either infection or cytokine-mediated inflammation. Because AML causes neutropenia and impaired immune function, infections are common, making elevated body temperature (fever) one of the hallmark presenting symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human chorionic gonadotropin",
    "long_answer": "Gestational trophoblastic diseases (e.g., hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma) secrete very high amounts of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). Because hCG shares an α-subunit with thyroid-stimulating hormone and can weakly bind the TSH receptor, markedly elevated hCG can stimulate the thyroid gland, raising circulating thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) levels and causing gestational hyperthyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APUD cell",
    "long_answer": "An apudoma is a neuroendocrine tumor that originates from APUD cells—cells capable of Amine Precursor Uptake and Decarboxylation. These neural-crest–derived endocrine cells are scattered throughout the body (e.g., in the gastrointestinal tract, pancreas, and lungs) and give rise to various hormonal tumors collectively termed apudomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysphagia",
    "long_answer": "After Nissen fundoplication, the gastric fundus is wrapped 360° around the distal esophagus to reinforce the lower esophageal sphincter. If the wrap is too tight or edema develops, luminal narrowing can occur, leading to postoperative dysphagia—difficulty swallowing solid or even liquid food—which is one of the most frequently reported complications of the procedure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Bruck syndrome is characterized by severe bone fragility and congenital joint contractures, requiring fracture care, deformity correction, and musculoskeletal rehabilitation. These interventions lie chiefly within the expertise of orthopedic (especially pediatric orthopedic) specialists, who manage conditions involving bones, joints, and the locomotor system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus oleronius",
    "long_answer": "Bacillus oleronius has been isolated from Demodex folliculorum mites on human skin. Proteins from this bacterium can trigger strong neutrophil and T-cell inflammatory responses in people with rosacea, suggesting that its presence on Demodex mites can aggravate or exacerbate rosacea lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Receptor antagonist",
    "long_answer": "A receptor antagonist is a drug that binds to a receptor without activating it, thereby blocking the action of the receptor’s natural ligand and interfering with normal receptor function. Because substances that hinder iodine uptake or action (e.g., perchlorate, thiocyanate) are termed iodine antagonists, the general pharmacological class involved is a receptor antagonist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoporosis",
    "long_answer": "Kallmann syndrome causes congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, leaving patients with chronically low testosterone or oestrogen levels. Sex steroids are essential for building and preserving bone mineral density; when they are deficient, bone resorption exceeds formation, leading to decreased bone mass and a markedly higher risk of osteoporosis and related fragility fractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Knobloch syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Knobloch syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused most often by mutations in the COL18A1 gene. It is classically characterized by a midline skull defect—most frequently an occipital encephalocele—and severe ocular involvement, including extreme (high) myopia, vitreoretinal degeneration and recurrent retinal detachment that lead to profound loss of vision. The simultaneous presence of occipital encephalocele and severe eyesight problems therefore points to Knobloch syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cartilage-hair hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Cartilage-hair hypoplasia (metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, McKusick type) is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by RMRP mutations. In addition to short-limb dwarfism and sparse hair, the disease often involves bone-marrow dysfunction; affected individuals can develop varying degrees of neutropenia, predisposing them to recurrent infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANK2 (ankyrin-B) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Long QT syndrome type 4 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ANK2 gene, which encodes ankyrin-B. Defective ankyrin-B disrupts the proper localization of multiple ion channels and transporters in cardiomyocytes, leading to delayed ventricular repolarization and the prolonged QT interval characteristic of LQT4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Lhermitte–Duclos disease is frequently seen as part of Cowden syndrome, in which germline mutations occur in the PTEN tumor-suppressor gene. PTEN is mapped to chromosome 10q23, so chromosome 10 is the chromosome typically implicated in patients with Lhermitte–Duclos disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mast cell",
    "long_answer": "Mast cells are immune cells that store large quantities of histamine in their cytoplasmic granules. During IgE-mediated allergic reactions they degranulate, releasing histamine and other mediators that cause typical allergy symptoms. Excessive or dysregulated mast-cell activation likewise underlies histamine intolerance, where spontaneous or triggered histamine release produces similar clinical manifestations. Thus the mast cell is centrally involved in both classic allergic responses and histamine-related intolerance syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Lassa fever is an acute viral hemorrhagic illness caused by Lassa virus, an Arenavirus that is endemic in several West African countries (including Nigeria, Sierra Leone, Liberia, Guinea, and others). Recurrent outbreaks occur every year in this region, and the disease is recognized by the World Health Organization as a significant public-health threat in West Africa. Therefore, Lassa fever is indeed a health issue in West Africa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is defined by malignant proliferation and accumulation of immature lymphoid precursor cells—lymphoblasts—in the bone marrow and peripheral blood. The unchecked expansion of these lymphoblasts impairs normal hematopoiesis, making ALL the prototypical leukemia characterized specifically by lymphoblast proliferation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brainstem glioma",
    "long_answer": "In children, tumors of the brainstem—most notably diffuse intrinsic pontine (brainstem) gliomas—often involve or compress the abducens (sixth cranial) nerve or its nucleus. This makes brainstem glioma one of the common tumorous causes of sixth nerve palsy in the pediatric population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) involves abnormal development of the first and second pharyngeal (branchial) arches, resulting in hypoplastic or absent middle-ear ossicles—the malleus, incus and/or stapes. The malformed or missing ossicles prevent efficient sound conduction, producing the characteristic conductive hearing loss seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ceftriaxone",
    "long_answer": "In pre-pubertal children epididymitis is most often produced by Gram-negative urinary-tract bacteria. Current paediatric urology and infectious-disease guidelines therefore recommend a third-generation cephalosporin; ceftriaxone is preferred because it provides reliable coverage of the usual enteric pathogens, is safe in children, and can be given as a single daily dose for 10-14 days."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Megaloblastic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Lubani-Al Saleh-Teebi syndrome is the original description of the disorder now called thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anemia (TRMA, Rogers syndrome). The hallmark hematologic finding in this condition is a profound megaloblastic anemia caused by defective DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors secondary to loss-of-function mutations in the thiamine transporter gene SLC19A2; the anemia corrects with high-dose thiamine. Hence, the type of anemia associated with the syndrome is megaloblastic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutis laxa",
    "long_answer": "Cutis laxa is an inherited connective-tissue disorder in which deficient elastic fiber formation causes loose, prematurely wrinkled skin at birth. In the autosomal-recessive forms (e.g., ATP6V0A2-related ARCL type 2), affected individuals frequently develop neurological complications such as developmental delay and epileptic seizures later in childhood or adolescence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome is classically caused by specific activating mutations (most often S252W or P253R) in the FGFR2 gene (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2). These FGFR2 mutations disrupt normal signaling during embryonic development, producing the characteristic combination of craniosynostosis (premature fusion of cranial sutures) and syndactyly (fusion of digits)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratosis pilaris",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis pilaris is a follicular hyperkeratinization disorder in which excess keratin builds up and plugs the openings of hair follicles, producing rough, ‘chicken-skin’ papules. Dermatologic surveys and clinical criteria for the hypermobile, classical, and other Ehlers-Danlos syndromes list keratosis pilaris as a common cutaneous manifestation, reflecting the altered collagen and skin barrier physiology in these connective-tissue disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EEC syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal Dysplasia-Cleft lip/palate (EEC) syndrome is a p63-related disorder whose core features include the ectodermal-dysplasia phenotype (sparse hair, abnormal teeth, hypohidrosis).  Congenital malformations of the urinary tract are common in EEC (hydronephrosis, vesicoureteral reflux, ureterocele, renal agenesis), and they can precipitate acute or rapidly progressive renal insufficiency that may become life-threatening.  Thus the syndrome simultaneously presents ectodermal dysplasia and the potential for sudden, severe deterioration of kidney function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trigonocephaly",
    "long_answer": "Say–Meyer syndrome is characterised by trigonocephaly, a triangular deformation of the forehead. Trigonocephaly results from premature fusion of the metopic suture—metopic synostosis—so the same cranial shape abnormality seen in Say–Meyer syndrome can also arise directly from metopic synostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric surgery",
    "long_answer": "Duodenal atresia is a congenital obstruction of the duodenum that presents immediately after birth and requires definitive operative correction (e.g., duodenoduodenostomy). Neonatal stabilization is important, but the curative treatment is surgical and is performed by specialists trained in operating on newborns with congenital anomalies—namely pediatric surgeons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporin A",
    "long_answer": "In cells from patients with Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy (a collagen VI-related myopathy), mitochondria show an abnormal permeability-transition response when challenged with the complex-I inhibitor rotenone. Treatment with the cyclophilin inhibitor cyclosporin A closes the permeability-transition pore, restoring membrane potential and preventing the excessive depolarization induced by rotenone; thus cyclosporin A normalizes the mitochondrial response and underlies its therapeutic use in this disease model."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudo-nitzschia",
    "long_answer": "Pseudo-nitzschia is a diatom—an algal microorganism whose cell wall (frustule) is made of hydrated silica. Certain Pseudo-nitzschia species produce the neurotoxin domoic acid, which accumulates in shellfish and can cause amnesic shellfish poisoning in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radium",
    "long_answer": "Radium (Ra) is an alkaline-earth metal whose isotopes (e.g., 223Ra, 224Ra) are intensely radioactive and have half-lives of only days. As a divalent cation (Ra2+), it behaves chemically like Ca2+ and Ba2+ and is readily sequestered by the hexadentate chelator EDTA, forming Ra-EDTA complexes; this is the same chelation mechanism by which EDTA binds other heavy-metal ions in medical detoxification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin E deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Prolonged malnutrition can deplete the fat-soluble antioxidant vitamin E. Lack of vitamin E leads to degeneration of the posterior columns and spinocerebellar tracts of the spinal cord as well as Purkinje cell loss in the cerebellum, producing a characteristic spinocerebellar ataxia. Therefore, vitamin E deficiency is a well-recognized, nutrition-related cause of spinocerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "The corpus callosum is the principal commissural fibre tract that enables communication between the left and right cerebral hemispheres. Neuro-imaging studies of patients with the X-linked syndrome of ocular albinism with late-onset sensorineural deafness consistently demonstrate hypoplasia or other abnormalities of the corpus callosum, indicating that this interhemispheric structure is affected in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked intellectual disability",
    "long_answer": "Mietens syndrome is catalogued in OMIM as \"Mental retardation, X-linked, syndromic, Mietens type\" (MRXS). The condition is transmitted through the X chromosome and presents in affected males as a syndromic form of intellectual disability. Therefore, the intellectual disability associated with Mietens syndrome is classified as X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Zieve's syndrome is a complication of acute alcoholic hepatitis characterized by jaundice, hemolytic anemia, and hyperlipidemia. Because it arises from alcohol-induced liver injury, its recognition and management—alcohol withdrawal care, monitoring of hepatic function, and treatment of associated metabolic and hematologic abnormalities—fall primarily within the scope of gastroenterologists (hepatologists), the specialists who diagnose and treat liver diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulsed radiofrequency ablation",
    "long_answer": "Pre-clinical studies in pigs assessing pulsed radiofrequency (PRF) applied to the lumbar dorsal-root ganglion—a procedure proposed for treating radicular pain—have shown axonal injury with myelin breakdown typical of Wallerian degeneration in the treated lumbar nerve roots. These histopathological findings indicate that the potential clinical treatment, pulsed radiofrequency ablation, can induce Wallerian degeneration in porcine lumbar nerve-root models."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FRAS1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FRAS1 gene, which encodes an extracellular matrix protein crucial for embryonic epithelial–mesenchymal adhesion, are the classical cause of autosomal-recessive Fraser syndrome. Independent studies have also shown that FRAS1 mutations can present with ocular malformations including isolated or syndromic coloboma, linking the same gene defect to both Fraser syndrome and coloboma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parkinsonism",
    "long_answer": "Both Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) and Parkinson’s disease share the cardinal motor syndrome of parkinsonism—characterised clinically by bradykinesia, muscular rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability—because both disorders involve α-synuclein Lewy body pathology in the nigro-striatal dopaminergic system. Consequently, patients with either condition often present with the same parkinsonian motor features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type IV hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "Pityriasis lichenoides chronica is believed to arise from a T-cell-mediated, delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity reaction, most likely triggered by infectious antigens. Histology shows CD8⁺ T-cell infiltration and keratinocyte apoptosis, supporting a cell-mediated immune mechanism rather than an IgE-mediated (type I) or immune-complex (type III) process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methotrexate and azathioprine",
    "long_answer": "Lymphomatoid granulomatosis is an Epstein–Barr-virus-driven B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder that can appear in the setting of iatrogenic immunosuppression. Multiple case reports document its development in patients receiving the disease-modifying antirheumatic drug methotrexate or the purine-analogue immunosuppressant azathioprine (e.g., in rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, and organ-transplant recipients). Withdrawal of these agents has led to regression of the lesions in several cases, underscoring their causal role."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome is caused by a partial deletion of the distal short arm of chromosome 4 (region 4p16.3), not chromosome 9. This 4p deletion leads to the characteristic craniofacial features, growth retardation, intellectual disability, and seizure tendency seen in the syndrome. No established medical literature links the canonical form of Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome to deletions on chromosome 9, so the statement is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest tube",
    "long_answer": "In a pneumothorax, air accumulates in the pleural space and can collapse the lung. Placement of a chest tube (tube thoracostomy) provides continuous drainage of that air, re-expands the lung, and normalizes intrapleural pressure, making it the standard device for relieving pressure in this situation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The inherited split-foot malformation (ectrodactyly) seen at high frequency in the vaDoma people maps to the SHFM1 locus on chromosome 7q21, making chromosome 7 the chromosomal site linked to the trait."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Middle cranial fossa",
    "long_answer": "Hypergeusia (abnormally heightened taste perception) is most often produced by lesions or tumours that involve the gustatory cortex situated in the insular-opercular region of the temporal lobe. The temporal lobes occupy the middle cranial fossa; consequently, pathology in the middle cranial fossa is classically associated with hypergeusia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDF5",
    "long_answer": "Christian brachydactyly corresponds to brachydactyly type C (OMIM 113100). Multiple families with this limb malformation have heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the Growth Differentiation Factor 5 gene (GDF5), a BMP family ligand essential for cartilage and phalangeal development. Disruption of GDF5 signaling alters chondrogenesis in the developing hand, producing the characteristic shortening of the middle phalanges seen in Christian brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pus in the fallopian tubes",
    "long_answer": "Visualization or aspiration of frank purulent exudate (pus) within the fallopian tubes or pouch of Douglas—most commonly detected at laparoscopy or culdocentesis—is regarded as a definitive diagnostic sign of pelvic inflammatory disease, because the presence of pus confirms active upper-genital-tract infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A",
    "long_answer": "Several case reports and small series have shown that high-dose systemic vitamin A (retinol) can lead to marked improvement of the intensely pruritic papules and nodules of prurigo nodularis. The benefit is attributed to retinoid effects on epidermal differentiation and inflammation, and vitamin A was one of the first agents reported to control this chronic dermatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen receptor agonist",
    "long_answer": "Diethylstilbestrol (DES) is a synthetic non-steroidal estrogen that binds to estrogen receptors (ERα/ESR1 and ERβ/ESR2) with high affinity and activates their transcriptional signaling. Thus, DES functions as an agonist of the estrogen receptor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Biotin",
    "long_answer": "Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency causes a failure to attach the vitamin biotin to several carboxylase enzymes, producing a multiple-carboxylase deficiency. High-dose biotin therapy supplies abundant free vitamin that residual enzyme activity can use, rapidly correcting the metabolic derangements. Egg yolks are one of the richest natural sources of biotin, so this vitamin is the essential nutrient for treating the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAG1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Alagille syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the JAG1 gene, which encodes the Jagged-1 ligand in the Notch signaling pathway. Defective JAG1 signaling disrupts intrahepatic bile duct development, leading to chronic cholestasis that can progress to fibrosis and ultimately cirrhosis in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Levator palpebrae superioris",
    "long_answer": "In oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy the characteristic ptosis (eyelid drooping) is caused by myopathic weakness of the levator palpebrae superioris, the striated skeletal muscle that normally elevates the upper eyelid. Degeneration of this muscle fibers in OPMD leads directly to the drooping appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STXBP2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in STXBP2, which encodes the vesicle-fusion regulator Munc18-2, are the cause of familial hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis type 5. The same defect disrupts apical membrane trafficking in intestinal epithelial cells, producing the villus atrophy and intracellular microvillus inclusions characteristic of microvillus inclusion disease. Hence STXBP2 mutations are associated with both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD8+ cytotoxic T cell",
    "long_answer": "In oral lichen planus, the basal keratinocytes of the oral epithelium are targeted and destroyed mainly by CD8-positive cytotoxic T lymphocytes. These CD8+ T cells recognize antigen–MHC class I complexes on basal cells and release perforin-granzyme and Fas-mediated apoptotic signals, leading to basal cell apoptosis characteristic of the lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cigarette smoking",
    "long_answer": "Inhalation of cigarette smoke can damage alveolar basement membranes, exposing the α3 chain of type IV collagen and promoting formation of anti-glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM) antibodies. This environmental lung injury is a well-established trigger that contributes to the development of Goodpasture syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 16",
    "long_answer": "The single most common chromosomal abnormality identified in first-trimester spontaneous abortions is trisomy 16. Conceptuses with an extra copy of chromosome 16 occur frequently, but the condition is uniformly lethal and therefore leads to early pregnancy loss rather than live birth, making it a frequent cause of first-trimester miscarriage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmic artery",
    "long_answer": "In giant cell (temporal) arteritis, granulomatous inflammation can extend from the extracranial branches of the carotid into the ophthalmic artery; thrombosis or severe luminal narrowing of this artery (or its posterior ciliary branches) compromises blood flow to the optic nerve and retina, producing acute, often irreversible vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iris",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent type of uveitis is anterior uveitis (iritis), in which inflammation chiefly involves the iris—the anterior part of the uveal tract—producing pain, photophobia, and a cells-and-flare reaction in the anterior chamber."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematogenous dissemination",
    "long_answer": "In chromoblastomycosis, a rare but serious complication is hematogenous dissemination, where the dematiaceous fungi enter the bloodstream and are carried through blood vessels to distant organs such as the lungs or brain. This blood-borne spread distinguishes it from the usual localized, cutaneous-lymphatic progression of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-blocker therapy",
    "long_answer": "Case reports and small series describing neurologic sequelae of chronic toluene inhalation have shown that non-selective β-adrenergic blockers (e.g., propranolol) lessen the intention tremor and partially improve cerebellar ataxia once exposure is stopped. The symptomatic benefit is attributed to the tremor-suppressing action of β-blockade on peripheral and central β-adrenergic receptors, making β-blocker therapy the treatment of choice for relief of toluene-induced tremor and ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occlusive small-vessel vasculitis",
    "long_answer": "In Behçet’s disease, acute optic neuropathy is almost always produced by an occlusive, immune-mediated vasculitis affecting the small vessels that supply the optic nerve head. This vascular inflammation and thrombosis cause ischemia of the optic nerve, leading to sudden visual loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex encephalitis is an acute viral infection of the brain that produces focal neurological deficits, seizures, and altered consciousness. Its diagnosis relies on neurologic evaluation, brain MRI, EEG, and cerebrospinal fluid PCR, and treatment includes intravenous acyclovir plus management of seizures and intracranial pressure. Because the condition primarily affects the central nervous system, these diagnostic and therapeutic responsibilities fall under the specialty of Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2q37 deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is a cardinal manifestation of 2q37 deletion syndrome (also called brachydactyly-mental retardation syndrome). The condition results from a sub-telomeric deletion on the long arm of chromosome 2 (region q37) and is characterized by brachydactyly type E, developmental delay/intellectual disability, distinctive facial features and frequently obesity. The loss of genes within the 2q37 region disrupts normal skeletal development, leading to the shortened digits seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiothoracic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Dextrocardia with situs ambiguus is a complex congenital malposition of the heart that usually requires operative correction of associated intracardiac and great-vessel anomalies. These operations are performed by specialists trained in cardiothoracic (cardiac) surgery, the surgical discipline dedicated to repairing structural heart defects and thoracic vessels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycine",
    "long_answer": "Glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinemia) results from defects in the glycine cleavage system, causing marked accumulation of the inhibitory neurotransmitter glycine in cerebrospinal fluid and plasma. Elevated CSF glycine levels are a diagnostic hallmark and drive the neurological symptoms of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium ions",
    "long_answer": "The fluid that bathes and surrounds the body’s cells—interstitial (extracellular) fluid—contains various electrolytes. Its principal intracellular-to-extracellular signalling cation is potassium (K⁺). Hypokalemia is defined as an abnormally low concentration of potassium in this extracellular fluid, making potassium ions the component directly altered by the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacrococcygeal region",
    "long_answer": "Pilonidal disease almost always presents in the midline of the natal (intergluteal) cleft over the sacrum and coccyx—collectively termed the sacrococcygeal region—where hair and friction predispose to sinus and cyst formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome characterised by camptodactyly together with mesomelic (middle-segment) limb shortening is Feingold syndrome. Feingold syndrome results from heterozygous loss-of-function variants or deletions of the MYCN gene on chromosome 2p24 and shows clear vertical (parent-to-child) transmission. Consequently, its well-established inheritance pattern is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chylothorax",
    "long_answer": "When Gorham-Stout disease involves the ribs, spine, or thoracic duct, abnormal lymphatic proliferation can rupture or obstruct the thoracic duct, allowing chyle to leak into the pleural space. The resulting chylothorax leads to massive pleural effusions, respiratory compromise, and significant protein-calorie loss, making it one of the most serious, potentially fatal complications of Gorham’s disease in the chest cavity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibial hemimelia-syndactyly",
    "long_answer": "The congenital limb‐malformation syndrome called tibial hemimelia-syndactyly (classified as syndactyly type IX) is characterised by partial or complete absence or marked hypoplasia of the tibia together with cutaneous or osseous syndactyly (webbing/fusion) of the toes, satisfying both features described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The human MANBA gene, which encodes lysosomal β-mannosidase and whose loss-of-function mutations cause the lysosomal storage disorder β-mannosidosis, is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 4 (cytogenetic band 4q24)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunodeficiency-centromeric instability-facial anomalies (ICF) syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CDCA7 gene cause one subtype of Immunodeficiency-centromeric instability-facial anomalies (ICF) syndrome. ICF syndrome is characterized by variable immunodeficiency, chromosomal instability of pericentromeric regions, and distinctive craniofacial features that frequently include macroglossia (enlarged tongue). CDCA7 mutations define the subtype known as ICF type 3, linking this gene directly to the macroglossia-associated genetic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macular coloboma-brachydactyly syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The disorder is a rare autosomal-dominant syndrome in which patients present with bilateral macular coloboma causing impaired central vision together with brachydactyly type B (absence or hypoplasia of the distal phalanges and nails). This ocular–skeletal combination is designated the macular coloboma-brachydactyly type B syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Synpolydactyly is usually caused by polyalanine expansions or other mutations in the HOXD13 gene. A single mutated allele is sufficient to produce the limb-malformation phenotype, and affected individuals can transmit the trait to offspring of either sex with a 50 % probability. These features define an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern, albeit with variable expressivity and occasional incomplete penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Petrus Gonsalvus",
    "long_answer": "The earliest well-documented instance of hypertrichosis in medical and historical records is that of Petrus Gonsalvus (also known as Pedro González), born in 1537 on Tenerife. His generalized excessive hair growth was carefully chronicled by Renaissance physicians and European courts, making him the first recorded case of the condition during the late medieval/early modern period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beemer-Langer syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Renal dysplasia-limb defects syndrome is most commonly referred to as Beemer-Langer syndrome, a lethal congenital disorder characterized by severe renal dysplasia or agenesis together with major limb reduction defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stannosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic inhalation of tin or tin-oxide dust leads to the benign, non-fibrotic pneumoconiosis known as stannosis. In affected workers (e.g., tin smelters, grinders), tin particles accumulate in alveolar macrophages and lung parenchyma, producing dense radiopaque nodules on chest imaging but causing little or no fibrotic reaction, distinguishing it from fibrogenic pneumoconioses such as silicosis or asbestosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibia vara",
    "long_answer": "Blount's disease is commonly known as tibia vara, reflecting the characteristic progressive bowing (varus deformity) of the proximal tibia that defines the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vomiting",
    "long_answer": "Panayiotopoulos syndrome is an autonomic childhood epilepsy in which ictal emesis is a hallmark feature; roughly two-thirds of affected children experience nausea and forceful vomiting during the seizure. Vomiting can also appear after the seizure has ended, making it a recognizable postictal symptom as well."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LPIN2",
    "long_answer": "Majeed syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis, congenital dyserythropoietic anemia and neutrophilic dermatosis. It is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LPIN2 gene, which encodes the phosphatidic-acid phosphatase lipin-2 involved in lipid metabolism and innate immune regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "The standard test to confirm a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is transthoracic echocardiography, usually with color-flow Doppler. This non-invasive ultrasound imaging visualizes the ductus between the aorta and pulmonary artery and demonstrates the characteristic continuous left-to-right shunt, making it the primary diagnostic procedure for PDA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac troponin T",
    "long_answer": "Cardiac troponin T (cTnT) is incorporated into the Mayo staging system for light-chain and transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis, where rising serum levels reflect greater myocardial infiltration and worse prognosis. The identical protein is a cornerstone biomarker in coronary artery disease and acute myocardial ischemia, because injury or ischemic necrosis of cardiomyocytes causes release of troponin T into the circulation. Thus, cTnT serves both to gauge the severity of cardiac amyloidosis and to signal ischemic heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dissociative identity disorder",
    "long_answer": "Shirley Ardell Mason – the patient portrayed in the book and film “Sybil” – was said by her psychiatrist to have multiple personality disorder, the term now replaced by dissociative identity disorder (DID). DID is grouped within the dissociative disorders and is characterized by profound episodes of amnesia in which distinct identity states take executive control; for this reason many clinical classifications describe it as falling under the broader category, or subset, of dissociative amnesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Delayed antibody production",
    "long_answer": "In rickettsial infections, patient antibodies (IgM/IgG) do not reach detectable levels until about 7–10 days or more after symptom onset; therefore serologic assays performed early in the illness are often negative, making them unreliable for early diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toronto",
    "long_answer": "Stephen Christmas was living in Toronto, Canada, and was being treated at the Hospital for Sick Children there when his blood was studied and the distinct deficiency of clotting Factor IX was identified. This led to the recognition of a new form of haemophilia—later named Haemophilia B or “Christmas disease”—making Toronto the city where he was first diagnosed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paratyphi A, B, C",
    "long_answer": "Human paratyphoid fever is produced by the three Salmonella enterica subspecies enterica serovars Paratyphi A, Paratyphi B, and Paratyphi C. These antigenically distinct serotypes—collectively referred to as S. Paratyphi—are the well-established etiologic agents of paratyphoid fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Denosumab",
    "long_answer": "Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL used to treat post-menopausal osteoporosis and other bone-loss conditions, lists “pain in extremity (limb pain)” among its common adverse effects in clinical trials and prescribing information."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microcytic hypochromic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Individuals carrying the hemoglobin E trait (heterozygous HbAE) typically exhibit a mild, thalassemia-like picture characterized by reduced mean corpuscular volume and mean corpuscular hemoglobin. This produces a microcytic, hypochromic anemia, even though most carriers are clinically well."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluoroquinolones",
    "long_answer": "Fluoroquinolone antibiotics (e.g., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin) carry an FDA black-box warning for potentially irreversible peripheral neuropathy. Clinical reports and post-marketing surveillance have shown that this class can damage peripheral nerves, leading to numbness, pain, or weakness that may persist long after the drug is stopped."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq27.3",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome, the prototypic syndromic form of X-linked intellectual disability, is caused by CGG-repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene located in chromosomal region Xq27.3. The expanded repeat becomes hyper-methylated and recruits repressive histone marks (e.g., H3K9me3, loss of H3/H4 acetylation), leading to epigenetic silencing of FMR1 and the associated cognitive and physical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome is a generalized defect of the proximal renal tubules. The impaired reabsorption of bicarbonate produces a type II (proximal) renal tubular acidosis, and the simultaneous loss of filtered glucose from the same nephron segment results in glycosuria despite normal blood glucose levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochrane Collaboration",
    "long_answer": "A systematic review conducted by the Cochrane Collaboration’s Infectious Diseases Group reported only weak (low-certainty) evidence that ivermectin treatment might lessen chorioretinal lesions in people with onchocercal eye disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low intracellular cAMP",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia areata has been shown to involve a marked reduction in adenylate-cyclase activity, leading to abnormally low levels of cyclic adenosine 3′,5′-monophosphate (cAMP) within lymphocytes and hair-follicle cells. This defect in cAMP generation disrupts normal immunoregulatory and growth-control signalling, and is considered a central metabolic abnormality in the disease’s pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET",
    "long_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is caused by activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase. Confirmation of a RET mutation in the index patient establishes the molecular diagnosis of MEN2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gain or loss of cysteine",
    "long_answer": "CADASIL-causing NOTCH3 mutations alter the number of cysteine residues within individual extracellular EGF-like repeats, producing an odd cysteine count. This disrupts normal disulfide-bond formation, leads to receptor misfolding and aggregation in vascular smooth-muscle cells, and underlies the vascular pathology of CADASIL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "46,XY sex reversal",
    "long_answer": "The DMRT1 and DMRT2 genes lie in a critical region of chromosome 9p24 that is required for normal testis determination. Deletion of this segment removes both genes, abolishing the DMRT1–DMRT2 transcriptional activity needed for Sertoli-cell and testis differentiation. As a result, embryos with a 46,XY karyotype fail to form testes and instead develop streak gonads and female or ambiguous external genitalia. Clinically, this presentation is termed 46,XY sex reversal (complete gonadal dysgenesis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Megaureter is an abnormal dilatation of the ureter that may require procedures such as ureteral tapering and reimplantation. These operations involve the urinary tract and are performed by surgeons trained in diseases of the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra—i.e., urologists (often pediatric urologists). Thus, the surgical management of megaureter belongs to the specialty of Urology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia commonly causes optic-nerve hypoplasia and other ocular abnormalities that impair vision; evaluation and management of these visual deficits are carried out by an ophthalmologist, who specializes in diagnosing and treating eye and vision disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shigella dysenteriae type 1",
    "long_answer": "Shigella dysenteriae type 1, the classic cause of bacillary dysentery, produces Shiga toxin that injures vascular endothelium. This toxin-mediated damage in the renal microvasculature can precipitate the triad of hemolytic–uremic syndrome (microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury). Hence infection with S. dysenteriae type 1 is a well-recognized antecedent of HUS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Potter syndrome (oligohydramnios sequence) results from severe lack of amniotic fluid, usually because of bilateral renal agenesis. The oligohydramnios compresses the fetus, causing marked intrauterine growth delay and, critically, pulmonary hypoplasia—lungs that are small and underdeveloped—together with characteristic facial and limb deformities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gerota’s fascia",
    "long_answer": "In Wunderlich syndrome, spontaneous bleeding usually collects in the sub-capsular or perirenal space. This haemorrhage is initially contained by the renal (Gerota’s) fascia that envelops the kidney. If Gerota’s fascia ruptures, the containment is lost, the blood dissects into the retroperitoneum, and a massive, acute haemorrhage can occur. Thus, rupture of Gerota’s fascia is the event that converts a confined renal bleed into the life-threatening form of Wunderlich syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colchicine",
    "long_answer": "Colchicine binds to tubulin and prevents proper spindle-fiber formation during mitosis; this blocks chromosome segregation, so the replicated chromosomes remain within a single nucleus, effectively doubling the chromosome number. Because of this spindle-poison action, colchicine is routinely used in plant breeding and in vitro cell culture to induce polyploidy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "124,884 nucleotides",
    "long_answer": "The varicella-zoster virus (VZV, human herpesvirus-3) possesses a single, linear double-stranded DNA genome that is 124,884 nucleotides long in the prototypical Dumas reference strain (GenBank NC_001348), corresponding to roughly 125 kilobase pairs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DYNC1H1",
    "long_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy with lower extremity predominance 1 (SMALED1) is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the DYNC1H1 gene, which encodes the cytoplasmic dynein 1 heavy chain. Mutations impair dynein-mediated axonal transport, leading to selective weakness and atrophy that predominates in the lower limbs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Limiting dairy products",
    "long_answer": "In people with mucopolysaccharidosis, milk and other dairy foods can increase the thickness and volume of respiratory secretions. Clinicians therefore often advise reducing or eliminating dairy products to lessen mucus production and ease airway clearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GCH1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Dopamine-responsive dystonia (Segawa disease) most commonly results from heterozygous mutations in the GCH1 gene, which encodes GTP-cyclohydrolase I—the rate-limiting enzyme for tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis. BH4 is an essential cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase in dopamine production; reduced BH4 from GCH1 mutations lowers dopamine levels, producing the characteristic dystonia that improves with levodopa. GCH1 mutations account for the majority of inherited DRD cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Axial skeleton",
    "long_answer": "Partial or complete trisomy of chromosome 15q consistently produces malformations of vertebrae, ribs and other midline structures, leading to scoliosis and related spinal/rib anomalies. These structures belong to the axial skeleton, showing that axial skeletal development is predominantly affected by chromosome 15q trisomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kupffer cells",
    "long_answer": "In cold agglutinin disease, IgM antibodies bind red blood cells at low temperatures, fix complement, and leave C3b on the erythrocyte surface. These C3b-opsonized cells are cleared primarily in the liver by Kupffer cells, the resident hepatic macrophages that possess complement receptors and phagocytose the coated red blood cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Prolactin secretion is normally kept in check by tonic dopaminergic inhibition from the hypothalamus. Any situation that decreases hypothalamic dopamine or increases hypothalamic-pituitary stimulatory inputs (e.g., serotonin, TRH, vasoactive intestinal peptide) can raise serum prolactin concentrations. Both experimental and clinical studies have shown that physical or psychological stress activates central serotonergic and corticotropin-releasing pathways, which in turn reduce dopaminergic tone and stimulate prolactin release. Endocrinology texts and consensus guidelines therefore list “stress (acute or chronic)” among the physiologic and secondary causes of hyperprolactinemia. Although stress-related elevations are often modest and may be transient, persistent or repeated chronic stress can maintain prolactin levels above the normal reference range, contributing to clinically significant hyperprolactinemia in some individuals. Consequently, clinicians are advised to measure prolactin after the patient has rested for at least 15–30 minutes and to repeat testing if an initial value is only mildly elevated, precisely to exclude stress-induced elevations. Thus, chronic stress is a recognized contributing factor to hyperprolactinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS13B",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the VPS13B gene (also known as COH1) are the established molecular cause of classical Cohen syndrome. Subsequent genetic studies showed that Mirhosseini-Holmes-Walton syndrome represents the same allelic disorder: patients diagnosed with this phenotype carry loss-of-function mutations in VPS13B. Thus, the VPS13B gene is the common genetic basis for both Cohen syndrome and Mirhosseini-Holmes-Walton syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNTNAP2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in CNTNAP2, the gene that encodes the axonal cell-adhesion molecule CASPR2, have been reported in autosomal-recessive hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type II. The same molecule (CASPR2) is the principal target of the pathogenic process in Morvan’s syndrome, and CNTNAP2 mutations or CASPR2 dysfunction can reproduce the Morvan phenotype of peripheral nerve hyper-excitability, severe insomnia and autonomic instability. Hence CNTNAP2 mutation is a recognised genetic link between HSAN II and Morvan’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iron overload",
    "long_answer": "Patients with β-thalassemia often need repeated red-blood-cell transfusions to correct severe anemia; each transfused unit carries 200–250 mg of elemental iron and the body lacks a regulated excretory pathway for iron. Additionally, ineffective erythropoiesis in β-thalassemia suppresses hepcidin, increasing dietary iron absorption. The resulting progressive iron accumulation (iron overload) can damage the heart, liver, and endocrine organs, so iron chelation therapy is required to remove the excess iron and prevent organ toxicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Mucha–Habermann disease (pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta) is an inflammatory skin disorder characterized by acute eruptions of papules that may ulcerate or become necrotic. Because it primarily involves the skin and requires clinical examination, differential diagnosis of cutaneous eruptions, and skin-directed therapies (e.g., topical steroids, phototherapy, systemic immunomodulators), its diagnosis and management fall within the expertise of the medical specialty of dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis",
    "long_answer": "Mick Mars has publicly stated that he was diagnosed in his late teens with ankylosing spondylitis, a chronic inflammatory autoimmune disorder that primarily targets the sacro-iliac joints and spinal ligaments. The persistent inflammation causes pain, stiffness, and progressive fusion of the vertebrae and pelvis, ultimately limiting mobility—features that match the disease description in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Jackson–Weiss syndrome is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder caused by activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (FGFR2) gene located on chromosome 10q26. FGFR2 gain-of-function variants accelerate osteoblast differentiation and lead to premature fusion of cranial sutures, producing the characteristic craniosynostosis seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clinical child psychologists",
    "long_answer": "In practice, children with possible reactive attachment disorder are most commonly first seen by clinical child psychologists, who conduct the initial behavioral and developmental assessments. Their evaluations often uncover the attachment-related symptoms and prompt referral for further psychiatric or multidisciplinary investigation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radial club hand",
    "long_answer": "A single transverse palmar (simian) crease often accompanies congenital radial deficiency, also called radial club hand. Because the radius and radial‐side soft tissues are under-developed, fetal hand movement is altered, and the normal two palmar flexion creases fail to form, leaving a single transverse crease. Hence radial club hand is the congenital limb deformity most commonly associated with a single transverse palmar crease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spironolactone",
    "long_answer": "Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic and aldosterone-receptor antagonist. In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis it is prescribed to limit renal potassium and hydrogen-ion loss, thereby helping correct the chronic metabolic acidosis. Separate clinical studies and case reports show that blocking mineralocorticoid receptors with spironolactone can reduce manic symptoms and stabilize mood, so the drug is also used as an adjunctive treatment in bipolar and other mood disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital rubella syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Maternal infection with rubella virus during the first trimester can cross the placenta and disrupt ocular development in the fetus, producing congenital rubella syndrome. Classic ocular manifestations include cataracts, glaucoma, and microphthalmia, making this syndrome a well-established viral cause of microphthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Third metacarpal",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly (split-hand/split-foot malformation) characteristically involves loss of the central digital ray. Radiographic and clinical studies show that the third metacarpal—the middle or central metacarpal— is the bone most consistently absent or hypoplastic, making it the metacarpal most commonly affected in individuals with ectrodactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPS1",
    "long_answer": "Trichorhinophalangeal Syndrome Type I (TRPS I) is caused by pathogenic variants or deletions in the TRPS1 gene, which encodes a GATA-type zinc-finger transcription factor essential for hair, craniofacial, and skeletal development. Loss-of-function mutations in TRPS1 disrupt transcriptional regulation of chondrocyte and hair follicle differentiation, producing the characteristic craniofacial features, sparse hair, and cone-shaped epiphyses seen in TRPS I patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Barakat syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Barakat syndrome, also called HDR (Hypoparathyroidism-Deafness-Renal dysplasia) syndrome, is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GATA3 gene. Its defining triad includes hypoparathyroidism, sensorineural deafness, and a spectrum of renal abnormalities such as dysplasia, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, and chronic kidney disease, all of which can manifest with proteinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tricuspid and mitral valves",
    "long_answer": "In dogs and cats, 'heart valve dysplasia' refers to congenital malformations of the atrioventricular valves. The lesions most commonly involve the tricuspid valve (tricuspid valve dysplasia) and the mitral valve (mitral valve dysplasia), where abnormal leaflets, chordae tendineae, or papillary muscles lead to regurgitation and, less often, stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Megalocornea",
    "long_answer": "In Anophthalmia-megalocornea-cardiopathy-skeletal anomalies (AMCS) syndrome, the characteristic ocular abnormality that accompanies the cardiac and skeletal defects is megalocornea—an abnormally enlarged corneal diameter present from birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tricyclic antidepressants",
    "long_answer": "Tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., amitriptyline, nortriptyline) frequently produce orthostatic hypotension because they block peripheral α1-adrenergic receptors. This α1 blockade prevents normal reflex vasoconstriction on standing, leading to a drop in blood pressure and dizziness, making orthostatic hypotension a well-recognized side effect of the TCA drug class."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "The clinical picture couples sensorineural inner-ear damage (cochleosaccular degeneration) with progressive cataract formation. While the hearing loss may require otologic input, the active, treatable problem is the lens opacity; cataract progression threatens vision and is addressed surgically and medically by an ophthalmologist. Consequently, the specialty most directly responsible for ongoing management is Ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systolic ejection murmur",
    "long_answer": "In canine subvalvular aortic stenosis the fixed obstruction below the aortic valve causes turbulent blood flow during ventricular ejection, producing a harsh, crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur best heard at the left heart base."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Major depressive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Distorted or negative body image often precipitates major depressive disorder, and epidemiologic studies indicate that more than half of individuals with anorexia nervosa experience a major depressive episode, making depression one of the most prevalent comorbid psychological conditions in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Colorectal cancer originates from the gland-forming epithelial cells lining the colon and rectum. Because it develops from these mucus-secreting glandular tissues, it is classified histologically as an adenocarcinoma—a subtype of carcinoma that arises from glandular epithelium and accounts for the vast majority of colorectal tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-7",
    "long_answer": "Interleukin-7 (IL-7) delivers essential survival and proliferation signals to developing T- and early B-cell precursors. Loss-of-function mutations in the IL-7 receptor α-chain (IL7R) prevent this signaling, resulting in a form of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) characterized by markedly reduced or absent T lymphocytes and profound cellular and humoral immune defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angerona",
    "long_answer": "In ancient Rome, the throat-constricting illness now recognised as peritonsillar abscess (then called angina or quinsy) was entrusted to the protection of the goddess Angerona. Romans invoked Angerona at the Divalia festival to relieve the choking pain and swelling of quinsy; her very name derives from the Latin verb “angere” (to strangle or choke), reflecting her role as the divine curer of this throat condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gut dysbiosis",
    "long_answer": "Studies show patients with Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome have an altered intestinal microbial community—reduced diversity, depletion of anti-inflammatory genera such as Faecalibacterium, and over-representation of pro-inflammatory taxa. This dysbiosis increases intestinal permeability, allows bacterial products (e.g., LPS) to enter the bloodstream, and drives systemic immune activation and metabolic disturbances that mirror and exacerbate ME/CFS symptoms. Thus, an imbalanced gut microbiota (dysbiosis) is considered a contributing factor in the pathogenesis of ME/CFS rather than a mere consequence of the illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nystagmus",
    "long_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia involves optic-nerve hypoplasia, and the resulting sensory deficit frequently leads to infantile nystagmus—rhythmic, involuntary oscillations of the eyes—which is the eye movement disorder most commonly reported in patients with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomal β-lactamase production",
    "long_answer": "Yersinia enterocolitica carries chromosomally encoded β-lactamases (e.g., BlaA) that are constitutively expressed and efficiently hydrolyze penicillin G’s β-lactam ring. This enzymatic inactivation renders the organism intrinsically resistant to penicillin G and related narrow-spectrum β-lactam antibiotics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ornithine",
    "long_answer": "Lysinuric protein intolerance is caused by defective transport of cationic amino acids (lysine, arginine, and ornithine). Ornithine, a key intermediate that shuttles between the cytosol and mitochondria during the urea cycle, is therefore poorly absorbed in affected individuals, contributing to hyperammonemia after protein intake."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human T-cell lymphotropic virus type 1 (HTLV-1)",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous T-cell lymphomas, particularly variants such as mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome, have been epidemiologically linked to infection with the retrovirus HTLV-1. This virus preferentially infects CD4⁺ T-cells, promotes chronic T-cell activation and clonal expansion, and can drive malignant transformation, explaining its association with T-cell lymphoproliferative disorders including cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC2A2",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi–Bickel syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder of glucose homeostasis and renal proximal-tubular function. It is consistently caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC2A2 gene, which encodes the facilitative glucose transporter GLUT2. Defective GLUT2 impairs glucose transport in liver, kidney and pancreas, producing the characteristic glycogen accumulation, proximal renal tubular dysfunction and metabolic disturbances seen in Fanconi–Bickel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "In November 1989, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) ordered a nationwide recall of all L-tryptophan dietary supplements after epidemiologic investigations linked these products to the outbreak of eosinophilia–myalgia syndrome (EMS). The FDA’s action removed the contaminated tryptophan, later traced to a single manufacturer, from the market to protect public health."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Germinoma",
    "long_answer": "In the pineal region, intracranial germ cell tumours are the predominant neoplasms, and the germinoma subtype constitutes the majority of these. Numerous clinicopathologic series show that germinomas make up roughly half of all pineal gland tumours, making them the single most common tumour arising in this gland."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX3",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the TBX3 gene cause ulnar-mammary syndrome, a disorder marked by complete or partial absence of the mammary glands (amastia) together with ulnar-side limb malformations such as hypoplastic forearms, missing digits, or other limb deformities. TBX3 encodes a T-box transcription factor critical for both limb bud and mammary ridge development, explaining why its disruption leads to the combined phenotype of amastia and limb abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strongylida",
    "long_answer": "The infraorder Strongylida comprises a large group of parasitic nematodes—such as hookworms and trichostrongyles—that infect vertebrates. In modern nematode taxonomy, Strongylida is treated as an infraorder within the order Rhabditida (phylum Nematoda), distinguishing it from other rhabditid infraorders that are primarily free-living."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Oculofaciocardiodental (OFCD) syndrome is an X-linked dominant disorder that is typically lethal in males and manifests in affected females with characteristic ocular, facial, cardiac, and dental anomalies. Pathogenic variants in the BCOR gene (BCL-6 corepressor), located on Xp11.4, disrupt normal gene repression during embryonic development, leading to the constellation of malformations seen in OFCD syndrome. Hence, mutations in BCOR are the established genetic cause of Oculofaciocardiodental syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bardet–Biedl syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bardet–Biedl syndrome is a ciliopathy classically defined by polydactyly, obesity, retinal dystrophy, hypogonadism and renal anomalies. It is now distinguished from the clinically related Laurence–Moon syndrome, which features spastic paraplegia instead of polydactyly. Thus, when polydactyly is present and paraplegia is absent, the correct diagnosis is Bardet–Biedl syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Copper deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Lysyl oxidase is a copper-dependent enzyme that catalyzes the cross-linking of collagen and elastin. When dietary or metabolic copper is deficient, lysyl oxidase activity falls, impairing structural integrity of the vascular wall and predisposing to arterial aneurysms, as classically observed in conditions such as Menkes disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enzyme replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher’s disease results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase. Treatment involves periodic intravenous infusions of recombinant human β-glucocerebrosidase (e.g., imiglucerase, velaglucerase alfa), which raise the circulating and intracellular level of the missing enzyme. This approach—supplying the patient with the functional enzyme to compensate for the genetic deficiency—is termed enzyme replacement therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmoplakin (DSP)",
    "long_answer": "Naxos–Carvajal syndrome combines arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy with cutaneous signs, notably woolly hair and palmoplantar keratoderma. The syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the desmoplakin (DSP) gene, a key desmosomal protein; defective desmoplakin disrupts hair‐shaft anchoring, producing the characteristic tightly curled (woolly) hair observed in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory depression",
    "long_answer": "Barbiturates are potent central nervous system depressants; in overdose they markedly suppress the brainstem respiratory centers, leading to hypoventilation, apnea, and ultimately respiratory failure. This respiratory depression is a well-recognized, life-threatening complication of barbiturate overdose."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meat",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is supplied almost exclusively by animal-derived foods; ordinary meat—such as beef, pork, or poultry—is a well-known, widely consumed source that provides far more than the daily 2–3 µg requirement. Adequate intake from meat prevents vitamin B12 deficiency and thereby protects against pernicious anemia, which arises when B12 absorption or supply is insufficient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gabapentin",
    "long_answer": "Gabapentin, an oral anticonvulsant prescribed to relieve the neuropathic itch of notalgia paresthetica, is well-documented to produce cutaneous adverse drug reactions such as morbilliform or fixed drug eruptions. Therefore, among the therapies used for notalgia paresthetica, gabapentin is the one specifically noted for its potential to cause a drug eruption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TINF2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Revesz syndrome is a severe, telomere-biology disorder within the dyskeratosis congenita spectrum. Nearly all reported cases carry heterozygous missense mutations in the TINF2 gene, which encodes the shelterin complex protein TIN2. These TINF2 mutations critically shorten telomeres, impairing hematopoietic stem-cell function and resulting in the profound bone marrow failure characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency blocks conversion of pyruvate to oxaloacetate, so excess pyruvate is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. The resulting buildup of lactate leads to a systemic metabolic acidosis—classically described as lactic acidosis—in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MP-12",
    "long_answer": "MP-12 is a live-attenuated strain of Rift Valley fever virus produced by serial mutagenesis. In controlled laboratory and pre-clinical studies it has induced strong neutralising antibody responses and protective immunity in multiple animal models with a favourable safety profile, identifying it as a promising vaccine candidate against Rift Valley fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal X-chromosome transmission",
    "long_answer": "X-linked ichthyosis is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the STS gene on the X chromosome. Affected males must receive their single X chromosome from their mother, while their father contributes a Y chromosome. Because fathers do not pass an X chromosome to their sons, male offspring can only acquire the mutant X from a carrier mother, making maternal inheritance the predominant route."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adjunctive therapy",
    "long_answer": "In the management of temporomandibular joint dysfunction, treatments such as occlusal splints, medications, or surgery are considered primary modalities. Additional measures—e.g., physical‐therapy modalities, behavioral techniques, acupuncture, or biofeedback—are classified collectively as adjunctive (adjuvant) therapy. These adjunctive therapies are applied in conjunction with the primary treatments to enhance pain relief, improve joint function, and shorten recovery time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maine, Malaysia, and Siberia",
    "long_answer": "Georges Gilles de la Tourette compared pathological startle and tic phenomena in three geographically distinct groups: the “Jumping Frenchmen of Maine” in the U.S., the Southeast Asian “Latah” cases he traced to Malaysia, and the Siberian “Myriachit” sufferers. By examining startle-provoked movements and echophenomena in Maine, Malaysia, and Siberia, he sought common features that informed his description of Tourette’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuro-oncologist",
    "long_answer": "An optic nerve glioma is a low-grade astrocytic tumor arising in the optic pathway, part of the central nervous system. Tumors of the brain and optic pathways are managed by a cancer specialist with expertise in nervous-system tumors—a neuro-oncologist—who oversees chemotherapy, radiation, and coordinates care with neurosurgeons and ophthalmologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dexamethasone",
    "long_answer": "Dexamethasone, a potent glucocorticoid, is given to climbers with high-altitude pulmonary edema because it lowers pulmonary artery pressure and capillary leak, helping resolve the edema. The RECOVERY trial showed that the same drug at 6 mg daily reduces mortality in hospitalized COVID-19 patients who require supplemental oxygen or mechanical ventilation, and it is therefore recommended in treatment guidelines for severe COVID-19 requiring respiratory support."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant mesothelioma",
    "long_answer": "Both asbestos fibers and the naturally occurring fibrous zeolite erionite are established carcinogens that induce malignant mesothelioma, a cancer arising from the mesothelial lining of the pleura or peritoneum. Epidemiological studies show high mesothelioma incidence in workers exposed to asbestos and in populations living in Turkish villages with erionite-containing rocks, confirming the link for this specific cancer type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRT2 gene sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Ichthyosis bullosa of Siemens is caused by pathogenic missense mutations in the keratin 2 (KRT2) gene. Direct molecular genetic testing—i.e., sequencing the KRT2 gene from the patient’s DNA—can identify these mutations and thus definitively confirm the genetic etiology of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1",
    "long_answer": "The infantile form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (CLN1 disease) results from mutations in the PPT1 gene that abolish activity of the lysosomal enzyme palmitoyl-protein thioesterase-1. Loss of this enzyme prevents removal of palmitate residues from proteins, leading to lysosomal storage of ceroid lipofuscin and the characteristic neurodegenerative phenotype seen in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immune checkpoint inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Treatment with immune-checkpoint-blocking antibodies (e.g., nivolumab, pembrolizumab, ipilimumab) can iatrogenically trigger hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis by unleashing excessive T-cell activation and cytokine release, leading to the hyper-inflammatory state characteristic of HLH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tardive dyskinesia",
    "long_answer": "Tardive dyskinesia is a drug-induced hyperkinetic movement disorder that arises after prolonged exposure to antipsychotic (dopamine-blocking) medications. It produces involuntary, repetitive motor and sometimes phonic movements that can resemble the motor and vocal tics of Tourette syndrome, leading to potential misdiagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion",
    "long_answer": "In Fragile X syndrome, the FMR1 gene contains an abnormally large expansion of CGG trinucleotide repeats (> 200 copies) in its 5′ untranslated region. This expanded CGG repeat triggers DNA hypermethylation of the adjacent promoter, causing transcriptional silencing of FMR1 and loss of its protein product (FMRP), which underlies the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYOC (myocilin)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYOC gene, which encodes the trabecular-meshwork protein myocilin, are the most firmly established Mendelian risk factor for primary open-angle glaucoma. More than 100 missense or truncating MYOC mutations impair myocilin folding and secretion, causing toxic accumulation in the trabecular meshwork, elevated intra-ocular pressure, and markedly increased glaucoma risk—especially in juvenile- and early-onset forms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis is classically driven by a type III hypersensitivity reaction, in which circulating antigen–antibody immune complexes deposit in post-capillary venules of the skin, activate complement, recruit neutrophils, and cause vessel wall inflammation and damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Halal syndrome—also called holoprosencephaly-polydactyly or pseudotrisomy 13—shows normal karyotypes in affected infants but recurs in siblings born to unaffected parents. Multiple reported familial cases and equal sex distribution indicate an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance, so it is classified as an autosomal recessive genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS gene",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the GNAS gene (encoding the Gs-alpha protein) underlie Albright hereditary osteodystrophy and related forms of pseudohypoparathyroidism. One hallmark of this condition is brachydactyly type E, characterized by shortening and other abnormalities of the fourth metacarpal and fourth metatarsal bones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Galactosialidosis is a lysosomal storage disorder that frequently produces neurological manifestations such as cerebellar ataxia, myoclonus, seizures, and progressive neuro-cognitive decline. Symptomatic control of these central-nervous-system complications falls under the care of the medical specialty of Neurology; neurologists coordinate anticonvulsant therapy, manage movement disorders, and monitor neurodevelopment as part of the multidisciplinary management of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Family history of alopecia areata",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia areata has a strong genetic component; approximately 10–20 % of affected individuals report that a first-degree relative also has the disease. This familial clustering and HLA-linked susceptibility loci make a positive family history one of the most common and well-established risk factors for developing alopecia areata."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Pain in many inherited disorders is usually managed symptomatically; first-line therapy typically involves nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen or naproxen. NSAIDs inhibit cyclo-oxygenase enzymes, lowering prostaglandin synthesis and thereby reducing inflammation and nociceptive signaling, and are widely recommended as an initial, general approach to pain control in a variety of genetic diseases (e.g., sickle-cell disease, Ehlers–Danlos syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocardial fibroelastosis",
    "long_answer": "HEC syndrome is a rare congenital association whose letters stand for Hydrocephalus, Endocardial fibroelastosis, and Cataracts. The cardiac component of the triad is endocardial fibroelastosis, a disorder characterized by diffuse fibro-elastic thickening of the endocardium that leads to impaired ventricular function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dor fundoplication",
    "long_answer": "After a laparoscopic Heller myotomy for achalasia, surgeons routinely add a partial anterior 180–200° fundoplication—known as the Dor fundoplication—to protect the exposed mucosa and reduce postoperative gastro-oesophageal reflux while avoiding the dysphagia risk of a full (Nissen) wrap. This combination (Heller myotomy plus Dor fundoplication) is the standard, guideline-supported procedure for achalasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Twitching motility",
    "long_answer": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae moves across solid surfaces by twitching motility, a type-IV pilus–driven process. The bacterium repeatedly polymerizes and depolymerizes its polymeric type IV pili, extending the pilus fiber, attaching its tip to the substrate, and then retracting it to pull the cell forward."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic vascular inflammation",
    "long_answer": "Chlamydia pneumoniae can infect endothelial cells, macrophages and vascular smooth-muscle cells, establishing a persistent infection that continuously triggers production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, adhesion molecules and oxidative radicals. This chronic vascular inflammation promotes LDL oxidation, recruitment of monocytes, foam-cell formation and smooth-muscle proliferation, all of which accelerate atherosclerotic plaque initiation and progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cat eye syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cat eye syndrome is caused by a small supernumerary marker chromosome derived from chromosome 22 (inv-dup(22)(q11)), resulting in partial tetrasomy of 22p and 22q11. Among its classic clinical features are ocular coloboma, anal atresia, and characteristic ear anomalies—specifically preauricular skin tags or pits—making it the syndrome classically linked to a small supernumerary marker chromosome that presents with preauricular pits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bisphenol A",
    "long_answer": "Bisphenol A (BPA) is an industrial chemical used in polycarbonate plastics and epoxy resins. It acts as an endocrine-disrupting compound with estrogen-like activity, and epidemiological studies have linked higher BPA exposure—measured by urinary concentrations—to earlier onset of puberty and menarche in girls."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plastic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Bartsocas-Papas syndrome is a severe cranio-facial and limb pterygium disorder that presents with cleft lip/palate, ankyloblepharon, webbed limbs and other malformations requiring staged reconstructive and cleft repair operations. Management therefore falls chiefly to cranio-facial / pediatric plastic surgeons, the specialty that performs these reconstructive procedures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rhinotillexomania",
    "long_answer": "When habitual nose-picking escalates into a compulsive, uncontrolled body-focused repetitive behavior (BFRB) it is termed rhinotillexomania. This diagnosis parallels other BFRBs such as trichotillomania and excoriation disorder, reflecting a pathological, often stress-relieving urge to pick the nasal mucosa despite negative consequences."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4–6 years",
    "long_answer": "In congenital maxillonasal dysplasia (Binder’s syndrome), early soft-tissue surgery is recommended; the nasolabial muscles (e.g., orbicularis oris, levator labii) are detached from their aberrant insertion and reattached to the columella–philtral region at approximately 4–6 years of age, before significant midfacial growth and school attendance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valacyclovir",
    "long_answer": "Valacyclovir, the orally bio-available pro-drug of acyclovir, blocks viral DNA polymerase after phosphorylation by virus-encoded thymidine kinase. Continuous oral valacyclovir suppressive therapy has been shown in clinical trials to markedly decrease genital Herpes simplex virus-2 shedding in humans, and experimental and field studies in macaques have demonstrated that the same regimen significantly lowers or eliminates shedding of Macacine herpesvirus 1 (B virus)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Leukemia patients who fail to achieve durable remission with conventional chemotherapy are often treated with bone-marrow (hematopoietic stem cell) transplantation, in which healthy donor stem cells are infused after high-dose conditioning to re-establish normal blood cell production and eradicate residual malignant clones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cigarette smoking",
    "long_answer": "Cigarette smoking is an established modifiable risk factor for diabetic retinopathy. Smoking accelerates microvascular damage through oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, and increased blood–retinal barrier permeability, thereby compounding the hyperglycemia-induced vascular injury seen in diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chancroid",
    "long_answer": "Chancroid, caused by the bacterium Haemophilus ducreyi, produces soft, painful genital ulcers that often appear on the labia minora in women. The lesions are characteristically tender with undermined edges and may be associated with inguinal lymphadenitis, distinguishing it from other sexually transmitted infections such as syphilis (painless chancre) or herpes (viral, not bacterial)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "10 exons",
    "long_answer": "Mevalonate kinase deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the mevalonate kinase (MVK) gene on chromosome 12q24. Molecular characterization of MVK shows that its genomic DNA is organized into exactly 10 exons spread over roughly 19–21 kb. This 10-exon structure is consistently reported in reference sequences (e.g., NM_000431) and genetic studies describing MVK-related disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bowel perforation",
    "long_answer": "In Hirschsprung’s disease, the aganglionic segment causes functional obstruction, leading to massive dilation and pressure build-up in the proximal colon. This elevated pressure can weaken and eventually rupture the bowel wall, producing an actual hole (perforation) in the gastrointestinal tract—known clinically as bowel or colonic perforation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningoencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Although primarily a cartilaginous autoimmune disease, relapsing polychondritis can involve small-vessel vasculitis that extends to the central nervous system; this may produce an inflammatory, aseptic meningoencephalitis that presents with headache, fever, confusion or focal deficits. Such CNS involvement is a well-documented but uncommon neurological association of relapsing polychondritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1",
    "long_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN 1) is a hereditary syndrome in which parathyroid hyperplasia or adenomas frequently cause primary hyperparathyroidism, and pancreatic gastrin-secreting tumors (gastrinomas) lead to recurrent peptic ulcer disease (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Thus MEN 1 is a recognized risk factor for primary hyperparathyroidism and is concurrently implicated in peptic ulcer disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemoptysis",
    "long_answer": "Choriocarcinoma frequently metastasizes hematogenously to the lungs, where the vascular, friable metastatic nodules can bleed into the airways. This pulmonary involvement commonly manifests clinically as hemoptysis (coughing up blood), making it a typical symptom in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Mycosis fungoides is the prototypic and most common form of primary cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL), a mature (peripheral) T-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by malignant CD4-positive skin-homing T lymphocytes that produce patch, plaque, and tumor lesions in the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunotherapy",
    "long_answer": "In bladder cancer, immunotherapy (also called biologic therapy) is employed to stimulate the patient’s own immune system—using agents such as intravesical Bacillus Calmette-Guérin or immune-checkpoint inhibitors—to recognize and attack tumor cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Middle-aged adults",
    "long_answer": "Large cross-sectional and longitudinal studies show a curvilinear age trajectory for death anxiety: it rises from young adulthood, peaks in middle age (roughly 40–60 years), and then declines in late life. Women consistently report higher scores than men across ages, whereas very old adults (> 65–70 years) usually show the lowest levels. Thus, the demographic group with the highest death-anxiety levels is middle-aged adults, especially middle-aged women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Tennis Elbow (lateral epicondylitis) is an overuse injury characterized by localized inflammation and pain. The standard pharmacologic approach to reduce that inflammation is the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). NSAIDs inhibit cyclo-oxygenase enzymes, lowering prostaglandin synthesis, which in turn diminishes swelling, pain, and other inflammatory signs in musculoskeletal conditions such as Tennis Elbow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an inherited SERPINA1 gene disorder that causes panacinar emphysema; ruptured emphysematous bullae often lead to spontaneous pneumothorax. Misfolded alpha-1 antitrypsin also accumulates in hepatocytes, producing neonatal hepatitis, chronic cholestasis, cirrhosis, and risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. Thus, the same genetic defect is well known for combining pneumothorax with clinically significant liver disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma most frequently spreads hematogenously via the portal venous system, and the liver is therefore the predominant site of distant (secondary) pancreatic cancer. At diagnosis or during disease progression, the majority of patients—often more than half—develop hepatic metastases, making the liver the most common organ involved in secondary pancreatic cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ocular conformers",
    "long_answer": "Infants born with microphthalmia are fitted with a series of progressively larger ocular conformers—smooth acrylic or silicone shells placed in the conjunctival sac. By gradually increasing their size, the conformers exert gentle, continuous pressure that stimulates bony and soft-tissue growth of the orbit, helping the eye socket expand symmetrically during early facial development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lesinurad",
    "long_answer": "Lesinurad, marketed as Zurampic (and in combination as Duzallo), is a selective inhibitor of the renal urate transporters URAT1 and OAT4 used to lower serum uric acid in gout patients. AstraZeneca voluntarily discontinued its marketing in the United States in 2019, ending its commercial availability despite no new safety concerns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "X-linked hypertrichosis is inherited as an X-linked trait because the causal genomic alteration lies on the X chromosome (classically mapped to region Xq24–Xq27.1). Therefore, the condition is specifically associated with the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunohistochemistry",
    "long_answer": "Immunohistochemistry (IHC) employs antigen-specific antibodies linked to chromogenic or fluorescent labels to demonstrate the presence of characteristic markers (e.g., CD5, CD117, cytokeratins) in resected or biopsied thymic tissue. Visualization of these antigens in situ allows pathologists to confirm the diagnosis of thymic carcinoma and distinguish it from other mediastinal tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Itraconazole",
    "long_answer": "Oomycete infections such as pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum) and lagenidiosis (Lagenidium spp.) are commonly managed, in addition to surgical debulking, with the triazole antifungal itraconazole. Although these organisms are not true fungi, itraconazole shows the best documented in-vivo activity among available systemic antifungals and is therefore the drug most often selected for medical treatment of both diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothalamic glioma",
    "long_answer": "Diencephalic syndrome classically occurs in infants and young children with low-grade astrocytomas arising in the hypothalamic/optic chiasm region—commonly called hypothalamic (optic-pathway) gliomas. These tumors interfere with hypothalamic centers regulating appetite and metabolism, leading to profound failure to thrive despite normal or increased caloric intake."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYB",
    "long_answer": "In most adenoid cystic carcinomas, the hallmark genomic alteration is a t(6;9)(q22; p23) translocation that fuses the MYB transcription-factor gene to NFIB. This rearrangement leads to constitutive over-expression and activation of the oncogenic transcription factor MYB, which drives tumorigenesis in adenoid cystic carcinoma, making MYB the key genomic event in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium nitroprusside",
    "long_answer": "Overdose of ergot alkaloids, which act as potent 5-HT2A receptor agonists, can precipitate intense peripheral vasospasm and ischemia (ergotism). Intravenous sodium nitroprusside donates nitric oxide, producing direct arterial and venous smooth-muscle relaxation, and is the standard agent used clinically to promptly reverse this severe vasoconstriction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygosity",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental disomy can give a child two identical copies of a chromosome (isodisomy) inherited from the same parent. If that parent carries a recessive pathogenic allele, the child becomes homozygous for the mutation, thereby expressing a rare autosomal-recessive disorder. Thus, the critical zygosity created by UPD that allows these disorders to manifest is homozygosity for the recessive allele."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Appendicular skeleton",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy of the long arm of chromosome 15 (15q duplication) consistently produces limb-related skeletal malformations—limb-reduction defects, abnormal long-bone growth, and multiple digital anomalies. These changes involve the bones of the arms, legs, shoulder, and pelvic girdles, i.e., the appendicular skeleton, distinguishing them from axial-skeleton disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Summer season",
    "long_answer": "In India, cholera shows a clear seasonal trend, with most outbreaks clustering in the hot summer months (roughly April-June) just before the monsoon rains. High temperatures and water scarcity during this period promote contamination of drinking water sources, creating optimal conditions for Vibrio cholerae transmission and resulting in the characteristic summer surge of cholera cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brunner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brunner syndrome, an X-linked disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MAOA gene, leads to deficient monoamine oxidase A activity. The resulting excess of serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine produces behavioral disturbances such as impulsive aggression, mild intellectual disability and documented episodes of hypersexuality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaline phosphatase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Lyngstadaas syndrome is a dental–skeletal disorder that belongs to the spectrum of hypophosphatasia. It is produced by markedly reduced activity of the tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNAP) enzyme. The enzyme deficiency impairs mineralization of bone and root cementum, leading to weak bones and premature exfoliation of teeth—findings characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microphthalmia",
    "long_answer": "Microphthalmia—development of abnormally small eyes—is one of the characteristic ocular findings in Norrie disease. The same malformation can also be produced by congenital viral infections such as rubella or cytomegalovirus, by teratogenic drugs (e.g., thalidomide, isotretinoin), and by maternal vitamin A deficiency, all of which disrupt early eye morphogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Quinidine",
    "long_answer": "In short QT syndrome, the class IA anti-arrhythmic quinidine has been administered to patients who developed atrial fibrillation. By blocking the rapid delayed-rectifier potassium current (IKr), quinidine lengthens the abnormally short QT interval seen in this channelopathy and has been reported to suppress atrial fibrillation episodes in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bromocriptine",
    "long_answer": "Nelson's syndrome arises after bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing disease and is marked by rapid enlargement of an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma. The dopamine-agonist bromocriptine can inhibit ACTH secretion by corticotroph cells and has been shown to slow or reduce further growth of these pituitary adenomas, making it a useful medical treatment option for Nelson's syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glial cytoplasmic inclusions",
    "long_answer": "Multiple system atrophy is pathologically defined by α-synuclein-positive glial cytoplasmic inclusions, also known as Papp-Lantos bodies, within oligodendrocytes. These inclusions distinguish MSA from other synucleinopathies such as Parkinson disease, where neuronal Lewy bodies are typical."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchiectasis",
    "long_answer": "In tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome), marked dilatation and atony of the trachea and main bronchi impair mucociliary clearance, leading to chronic pooling of secretions and repeated infections. Over time, this produces permanent dilatation and destruction of distal bronchi—i.e., bronchiectasis—which is reported as one of the most frequent and characteristic complications of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asia",
    "long_answer": "Although neurasthenia has been removed from the DSM in North America and is seldom used in Europe, it is still listed in the ICD-10 (code F48.0) and remains an accepted clinical diagnosis in several Asian countries, particularly China and other East-Asian nations. Thus, the continent that continues to recognize neurasthenia as a diagnosable condition is Asia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Sotos syndrome is an inherited over-growth and developmental disorder caused most often by pathogenic variants in the NSD1 gene. Definitive diagnosis relies on recognizing the characteristic pattern of features and confirming it with molecular genetic testing, and long-term management requires coordinated surveillance for associated complications. These tasks are central to the expertise of clinical/medical geneticists, the specialty responsible for diagnosing genetic syndromes, interpreting DNA tests, counseling families, and directing multidisciplinary care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FZD4",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FZD4 gene (Frizzled-4), a key component of the Norrin-Wnt signalling pathway required for retinal vascular and foveal development, are a recognised cause of familial exudative vitreoretinopathy that often shows foveal/macular hypoplasia. Independent studies have also demonstrated that rare FZD4 mutations are over-represented in infants who develop severe retinopathy of prematurity, linking the same gene to both macular hypoplasia and ROP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ethylene glycol",
    "long_answer": "Ethylene glycol, the main constituent of automotive antifreeze, is nephrotoxic when ingested. Its metabolites—particularly glycolic acid and oxalic acid—damage renal tubular epithelial cells, leading to calcium oxalate crystal deposition and acute tubular necrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MLYCD",
    "long_answer": "Malonic aciduria (malonyl-CoA decarboxylase deficiency) is produced by biallelic mutations in the MLYCD gene located on chromosome 16q23-q24. Loss of functional malonyl-CoA decarboxylase prevents conversion of malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA, leading to accumulation of malonic acid in blood and urine—that characteristic biochemical hallmark of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Warkany syndrome 2 corresponds to trisomy 8 mosaicism, a chromosomal abnormality identifiable on a fetal karyotype. Obtaining fetal cells through amniocentesis permits cytogenetic analysis, revealing the extra chromosome 8 and thus diagnosing the syndrome prenatally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fourth metacarpals",
    "long_answer": "Biemond syndrome is characterized by congenital shortening (brachymetacarpia/-metatarsia) that is most pronounced in the fourth ray; the fourth metacarpal bones are notably shortened, producing the typical hand anomaly seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Digital subtraction angiography",
    "long_answer": "Catheter-based digital subtraction angiography is the diagnostic imaging technique used for fibromuscular dysplasia. It outlines the arterial lumen in real time, revealing the pathognomonic ‘string of beads’ appearance produced by alternating segments of stenosis and aneurysmal dilatation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) is closely linked to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome. Patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus, characterized by systemic insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia, show markedly higher hepatic fat accumulation and inflammation, making T2DM a well-established risk factor for the development and progression of NASH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Entire colon",
    "long_answer": "Hirschsprung’s disease typically involves the rectum and sigmoid colon, but in about 5–10 % of cases the aganglionic segment extends proximally to include the whole large intestine, producing total colonic aganglionosis. Thus, the entire colon can be affected in these rare presentations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edwards syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18) is a chromosomal aneuploidy in which neural-tube defects, particularly spina bifida and encephalocele, occur far more frequently than in the general population; up to 5–10 % of affected fetuses demonstrate spina bifida, making this genetic disorder a well-recognized risk factor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Morvan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Morvan syndrome (also called Morvan fibrillary chorea) can arise in patients with acquired neuromyotonia. It is characterized by peripheral nerve hyper-excitability plus autonomic and central nervous system involvement, notably severe sleep disturbances such as almost complete insomnia (agrypnia excitata)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Genetic studies have shown that Juberg–Hayward syndrome is linked to a locus on chromosome 7p (near the HOXA gene cluster), while hereditary triphalangeal thumb maps to a regulatory element within the LMBR1 region on chromosome 7q36. Thus, both conditions involve pathogenic variants located on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Coeliac disease shows a markedly higher prevalence in people with Down syndrome, a congenital chromosomal disorder (trisomy 21). Down syndrome is frequently accompanied by developmental anomalies of the gastrointestinal tract—such as duodenal atresia and general small-bowel hypoplasia—reflecting underdevelopment of the small intestine. Hence, when coeliac disease is found together with an underdeveloped small intestine, an associated congenital condition to consider is Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "McArdle disease",
    "long_answer": "Exercise-induced myogenic hyperuricemia is classically seen in McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V), caused by deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase. In this disorder, inability to mobilize glycogen during exercise forces muscle cells to degrade adenine nucleotides, generating excess purine metabolites that are converted to uric acid, leading to marked post-exercise hyperuricemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dandy–Walker malformation",
    "long_answer": "Marden–Walker syndrome frequently includes structural brain anomalies, most notably the Dandy–Walker malformation—a constellation of cerebellar vermis hypoplasia/aplasia, cystic dilatation of the fourth ventricle, and enlargement of the posterior fossa—reported in multiple confirmed cases of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trypanophobia",
    "long_answer": "Trypanophobia is the specific phobic anxiety disorder characterized by an intense, irrational fear of medical procedures involving injections or hypodermic needles. Although sometimes colloquially grouped with aichmophobia (fear of sharp objects), trypanophobia is the clinically recognized term for needle-related phobia, leading to avoidance of vaccinations, blood draws, or any needle use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peroxisomal disorder",
    "long_answer": "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata is caused by mutations in genes (e.g., PEX7) needed for peroxisome biogenesis and plasmalogen synthesis. Because its primary defect lies in peroxisomal function, it is classified among the peroxisomal inborn errors of metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX3",
    "long_answer": "Growth hormone deficiency can follow an X-linked pattern when it is caused by pathogenic variants in the SOX3 gene. SOX3 lies on the Xq27 region and encodes a transcription factor critical for pituitary and hypothalamic development. Loss-of-function mutations or duplications of SOX3 disrupt pituitary organogenesis, leading to isolated or combined pituitary hormone deficiencies in hemizygous males, most prominently growth hormone deficiency; carrier females are usually unaffected or mildly affected, consistent with X-linked inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strabismus",
    "long_answer": "Heterophoria is a latent ocular misalignment that is normally kept in check by the patient’s fusional vergence system. If fusional vergence cannot compensate, the latent deviation becomes manifest, producing a constant eye turn known as strabismus (heterotropia), such as an esotropia or exotropia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Factitious disorder",
    "long_answer": "In factitious disorder (e.g., Munchausen syndrome), individuals intentionally create or worsen physical signs—such as skin lesions—by self-treatment (applying caustics, picking, cutting) in order to assume the sick role. The lesions are therefore self-induced rather than arising from a primary dermatologic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈0.1 % (1/1,000)",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies show that primary hyperparathyroidism affects roughly one in every thousand adults, giving it a prevalence of about 0.1 % in the general population (higher in post-menopausal women)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azacitidine",
    "long_answer": "Azacitidine is a hypomethylating cytidine analogue approved as first-line therapy for higher-risk myelodysplastic syndrome. Because it can induce DNA hypomethylation and promote differentiation or apoptosis of malignant myeloid cells, it is also widely used—especially in older or unfit patients—for the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Berdazimer",
    "long_answer": "Zelsuvmi is the FDA-approved brand name for a topical gel whose active pharmaceutical ingredient is berdazimer (also called berdazimer sodium). Berdazimer is a nitric-oxide–releasing macromolecule that, when applied to skin lesions, delivers nitric oxide to exert antiviral activity against the molluscum contagiosum virus, leading to lesion clearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "In multiple system atrophy (MSA), degeneration commonly involves the cerebellum, a hind-brain structure that fine-tunes motor activity and maintains posture, coordination, and balance. Damage to cerebellar neurons and their pathways produces the ataxia and gait instability characteristic of MSA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Twinkle helicase",
    "long_answer": "Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO) is frequently caused by mutations in the nuclear gene C10orf2/PEO1. This gene encodes Twinkle helicase, the nuclear-encoded mitochondrial DNA helicase that unwinds the mitochondrial genome at the replication fork. Twinkle is therefore indispensable for normal mitochondrial DNA replication and maintenance; when it is defective, multiple mtDNA deletions accumulate, producing the CPEO phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sirolimus",
    "long_answer": "Sirolimus (rapamycin) is an oral inhibitor of the mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR). By blocking mTOR-mediated signaling, sirolimus suppresses endothelial-cell proliferation and angiogenic pathways that drive hemangioma growth. Clinical case series have shown that sirolimus can shrink multiple visceral and cutaneous lesions in infants with diffuse neonatal hemangiomatosis, making it a targeted therapeutic option for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missense mutation",
    "long_answer": "Björnstad syndrome is caused by autosomal-recessive missense mutations in the BCS1L gene; these single amino-acid substitutions reduce, but do not abolish, BCS1L chaperone activity, leading to the characteristic pili torti and sensorineural deafness seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonketotic hyperglycinemia",
    "long_answer": "Nonketotic hyperglycinemia (also called glycine encephalopathy) is a neurometabolic disorder in which deficiency of the glycine cleavage system leads to markedly elevated glycine concentrations in cerebrospinal fluid and plasma. The excess glycine overstimulates NMDA receptors in the brain, producing neonatal or infantile seizures, lethargy, hypotonia, and encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemiparesis",
    "long_answer": "In Sturge-Weber syndrome, leptomeningeal vascular malformations can cause recurrent cerebral ischemia and seizures that damage the motor cortex and corticospinal tract. This often produces hemiparesis—weakness or partial paralysis affecting one side of the body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VAC chemotherapy and conservative surgery",
    "long_answer": "Sarcoma botryoides (botryoid embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma) of the vagina in young children is now managed with a multimodal protocol: initial systemic chemotherapy—typically the VAC regimen of vincristine, dactinomycin, and cyclophosphamide—followed by conservative, organ-preserving surgery (and radiotherapy only if margins remain positive). This approach achieves high survival while maintaining vaginal and bladder function, replacing the historically radical exenterative procedures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Most common adult leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) accounts for roughly 25–30 % of all leukemias diagnosed in North America and Europe, giving it the highest prevalence of any adult leukemia in the Western world."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Capnocytophaga canimorsus",
    "long_answer": "Capnocytophaga canimorsus is a Gram-negative rod that colonizes the oral flora of dogs and cats. When transmitted to humans—often through bites, licks, or close contact—it can produce fulminant septicemia characterized by disseminated intravascular coagulation and bilateral adrenal hemorrhage, the hallmark of Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pollex",
    "long_answer": "In anatomical nomenclature derived from Medical Latin, the thumb—the first (lateral-most) digit of the human hand—is termed the “pollex.” This name is used universally in clinical anatomy to distinguish the thumb from the other digits (index, medius, annularis, minimus)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nadolol",
    "long_answer": "Nadolol is a long-acting, non-selective β-adrenergic blocker routinely prescribed for hypertension and angina pectoris. By blunting catecholamine-induced adrenergic stimulation, nadolol lowers the incidence of exercise- or stress-triggered ventricular arrhythmias, making it a first-line pharmacologic therapy for patients with Catecholaminergic Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia (CPVT)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatitis B virus",
    "long_answer": "Polyarteritis nodosa is classically associated with chronic infection by Hepatitis B virus (HBV). Circulating immune complexes containing hepatitis B surface antigen and antibody deposit in medium-sized arterial walls, triggering necrotizing vasculitis. Historically, 10–30 % of PAN cases have been linked to HBV infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoketotic hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA (HMG-CoA) lyase catalyzes the final step of ketogenesis and leucine degradation. When this enzyme is deficient, ketone bodies cannot be synthesized during fasting, and glucose is rapidly consumed, producing a clinical picture of profound hypoglycemia with absent or very low ketone bodies—i.e., hypoketotic hypoglycemia—often accompanied by metabolic acidosis and organic aciduria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coxa vara",
    "long_answer": "In spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita the proximal femoral epiphysis ossifies abnormally, leading to a reduced femoral neck–shaft angle. This progressive varus angulation of the hip—coxa vara—is the characteristic hip joint deformity reported in most affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Persistent or intractable hiccups are frequently associated with gastrointestinal conditions such as gastro-oesophageal reflux, hiatal hernia, or esophageal irritation. Because these underlying GI disorders are common reversible causes, patients are often referred to a gastroenterologist for diagnostic endoscopy, pH studies, and pharmacologic treatment (e.g., proton-pump inhibitors, baclofen) aimed at the esophagus and stomach. Thus, gastroenterology is the specialty that typically evaluates and treats intractable hiccups."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polycystic ovary syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is regarded as the most prevalent endocrine disorder among women of reproductive age, affecting roughly 6–15 % of this population worldwide. Characterised by hyperandrogenism, oligo- or anovulation, and polycystic ovarian morphology, PCOS is a leading cause of infertility and metabolic disturbances, which is why it is frequently the subject of review articles in endocrinology and reproductive medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium ion (Ca²⁺)",
    "long_answer": "In rhabdomyolysis leading to myoglobinuria, failure of ATP-dependent ion pumps allows Ca²⁺ to flood into skeletal-muscle cells. The excess intracellular calcium activates proteases and phospholipases that damage contractile proteins and membranes, causing muscle fibre necrosis and the release of myoglobin, which is subsequently filtered into urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Levator palpebrae superioris",
    "long_answer": "In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies impair neuromuscular transmission at skeletal muscles rich in nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. The muscle that elevates the upper eyelid is the levator palpebrae superioris (innervated by the oculomotor nerve). Weakness of this muscle prevents proper eyelid elevation, producing the characteristic ptosis observed in many patients with myasthenia gravis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congestive heart failure",
    "long_answer": "Severe aplastic anemia produces profound anemia. To compensate for the drastically reduced oxygen-carrying capacity, the heart increases cardiac output chronically. This sustained high-output state eventually decompensates, leading to congestive (high-output) heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressant prescribed for prevention of organ-transplant rejection and, at lower doses, for severe rheumatoid arthritis. One of its characteristic adverse effects is hypertrichosis (hirsutism), leading to noticeably increased hair growth in many patients due to cyclosporine-induced stimulation of the hair follicle growth cycle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Omaveloxolone",
    "long_answer": "Omaveloxolone (brand name Skyclarys), approved by the U.S. FDA in 2023 as the first treatment for Friedreich's ataxia, is an Nrf2 pathway activator that enhances antioxidant gene expression, thereby lowering neuronal oxidative stress and inflammation and improving mitochondrial function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematogenous spread",
    "long_answer": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis typically disseminates through the bloodstream from a primary pulmonary (or other) focus. This hematogenous spread seeds a small subpial or subependymal ‘Rich focus’ in the brain, which later ruptures into the subarachnoid space, allowing bacilli to enter the meninges and produce tuberculous meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ITGA6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ITGA6 gene, which encodes the integrin α6 subunit (integrin alpha-6), are a known cause of junctional epidermolysis bullosa with pyloric atresia. Thus, ITGA6 links both the disease (JEB-PA) and the integrin α6 protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disaccharidase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Genetic sucrase-isomaltase deficiency (GSID) is an inherited lack or severe reduction of the intestinal brush-border enzyme complex sucrase-isomaltase. Because sucrase-isomaltase is one of the key disaccharidases that split sucrose, maltose, and isomaltose, its absence places GSID within the group of congenital disaccharidase deficiencies, leading to primary sucrose intolerance and starch-derived oligosaccharide malabsorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal-dominant keratin gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pachyonychia congenita is caused by heterozygous (autosomal-dominant) missense mutations in epithelial keratin genes—classically KRT6A, KRT6B, KRT6C, KRT16, or KRT17. These pathogenic keratin mutations disrupt filament assembly in nail, skin, and mucosal epithelia, producing the characteristic thickened nails and palmoplantar keratoderma of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diplopia",
    "long_answer": "When the visual axes of the two eyes are not properly aligned—such as in strabismus—the brain receives images from slightly different points on each retina. Because corresponding retinal points do not match, the visual cortex cannot fuse the two inputs into a single percept, leading to the experience of seeing two separate images of the same object, a phenomenon called diplopia (double vision)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The GBA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase implicated in Gaucher’s disease, is mapped to chromosome 1 at band 1q21. Mutations in this chromosomal locus disrupt enzyme function, leading to the accumulation of glucocerebroside that characterizes the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated C-reactive protein",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with periodontal disease typically show higher circulating levels of systemic inflammation markers, most consistently C-reactive protein (CRP); the chronic oral infection provokes a low-grade inflammatory response that spills over into the bloodstream, raising CRP and related cytokines such as IL-6 in proportion to periodontal severity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRAPS",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TNFRSF1A gene cause TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS), an autoinflammatory disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of high fever, migratory rash, myalgia, and other systemic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Three to six lobes",
    "long_answer": "Oligomeganephronia is a form of renal hypoplasia in which each kidney is markedly reduced in size and develops far fewer pyramids/calyces than normal. Gross pathology typically shows only about 3–6 renal lobes (pyramids), compared with the usual 8–18 lobes seen in a normal adult kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroglossal duct",
    "long_answer": "The foramen cecum is the small pit at the apex of the sulcus terminalis on the dorsal surface of the tongue. During early development, this spot is where the thyroid diverticulum invaginates from the floor of the primitive pharynx and descends into the neck through the thyroglossal duct. When the duct normally involutes, its proximal opening on the tongue persists as the foramen cecum, making it a remnant of the embryonic thyroglossal duct."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azathioprine",
    "long_answer": "For chronic or recurrent cutaneous leukocytoclastic (small-vessel) vasculitis, first-line anti-inflammatory agents are colchicine or dapsone. When lesions persist or recur despite these drugs, immunosuppressive therapy is indicated. Azathioprine, a purine-synthesis-inhibiting antimetabolite, is the standard next-line agent; it down-regulates lymphocyte proliferation and immune complex–mediated vascular injury, and numerous clinical series document its efficacy in colchicine- or dapsone-refractory cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmin-related myopathy",
    "long_answer": "Desmin-related myopathy (also called desminopathy) is a myofibrillar muscle disease caused by pathogenic variants in the DES gene that encodes the intermediate-filament protein desmin. Because its name begins with the letter ‘D’, it is included in alphabetical lists of diseases under the letter ‘D’, fulfilling the query for a desmin-associated disease in that category."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler–Weber–Rendu syndrome) is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by mutations in genes such as ENG or ACVRL1 that regulate TGF-β signaling in vascular endothelial cells. The defect leads to development of telangiectasias and arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) throughout the body, including the brain. Cerebral AVMs can bleed or irritate surrounding cortex, markedly increasing the risk of epileptic seizures in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vertebral column (spine)",
    "long_answer": "Solitary plasmacytoma of bone most frequently arises in the axial skeleton, with the vertebral bodies—particularly in the thoracic spine—being the single most common site. Roughly half of all SPB cases involve a vertebra, reflecting the high marrow content and plasma-cell homing to this region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rho(D) immune globulin",
    "long_answer": "Rh-negative mothers are given Rho(D) immune globulin (commonly known by the brand name RhoGAM) during pregnancy and shortly after delivery. This preparation of anti-D antibodies binds any fetal Rh-positive red blood cells that enter the maternal circulation, thereby preventing the mother's immune system from becoming sensitized and forming anti-D antibodies that could cause hemolytic disease of the newborn in current or future pregnancies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "McArdle’s disease (glycogen-storage disease type V) is caused by a deficiency of the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, also called myophosphorylase. Without this enzyme, skeletal muscle cannot cleave glycogen to release glucose-1-phosphate during exercise. The resulting energy shortage forces adenylate kinase (myokinase) to convert 2 ADP → ATP + AMP, markedly increasing AMP levels. Thus, the deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase is what leads to the excess AMP production observed during exertion in patients with McArdle’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystoid macular edema",
    "long_answer": "Cystoid macular edema (CME) is an ocular condition in which fluid accumulates within the layers of the macula, producing numerous tiny cyst-like spaces in the retina. These microscopic cysts disrupt the normal retinal architecture and can impair central vision, making CME the classic eye disease characterized by the formation of tiny retinal cysts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is well known for its high incidence of congenital heart defects: up to 50% of affected infants have cardiac anomalies, most commonly atrioventricular septal defects, ventricular septal defects, and atrial septal defects. The extra copy of chromosome 21 disrupts genes involved in cardiac morphogenesis, leading to these characteristic septal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alopecia areata",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia areata is an autoimmune disorder that produces an abrupt, often diffuse loss of anagen hairs. The sudden shedding and characteristic ‘exclamation-point’ hairs can closely mimic the anagen effluvium that follows ingestion of heavy metals such as thallium or arsenic, leading clinicians to suspect toxic poisoning. Because its clinical presentation overlaps with these toxic ingestions, alopecia areata is a recognised hair disorder that may be misdiagnosed as a case of toxic exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "The atrophin-1 gene (ATN1), whose CAG-repeat expansion causes dentatorubral–pallidoluysian atrophy (DRPLA), is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 12 (region 12p13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autonomic nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Both fibromyalgia and gastrointestinal activity show dysregulation of the autonomic nervous system (ANS). In fibromyalgia, altered sympathetic-parasympathetic balance contributes to widespread pain and fatigue, while the ANS (via sympathetic and parasympathetic/vagal pathways) is the primary neural controller of gut motility, secretion and sensitivity. Thus, autonomic nervous system dysfunction links fibromyalgia with gastrointestinal functional disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dialysis",
    "long_answer": "Uremia occurs when failing kidneys can no longer remove nitrogenous wastes and excess electrolytes. The standard, immediately effective therapy is dialysis—most commonly hemodialysis—which mechanically filters the patient’s blood to remove uremic toxins, correct electrolyte and acid-base imbalances, and thereby relieves the clinical syndrome until renal function is restored or a kidney transplant is performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schwann cell",
    "long_answer": "In the peripheral nervous system, Schwann cells wrap their membranes around axons to form the myelin sheath. The optic nerve, however, is part of the central nervous system, where myelination is carried out by oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells. Thus, Schwann cells myelinate peripheral nerves but do not participate in optic nerve myelination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Diamond–Blackfan anemia is often managed initially with systemic corticosteroids (such as prednisone), which stimulate erythropoiesis and can raise hemoglobin levels in many patients, thereby reducing transfusion requirements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kidney stones",
    "long_answer": "Primary hyperparathyroidism raises blood and urinary calcium levels, predisposing to calcium-containing renal calculi. Nephrolithiasis is the single most frequent clinical manifestation of the disease and is readily identified on imaging studies such as non-contrast CT, ultrasound, or plain abdominal X-ray."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis)",
    "long_answer": "Eumycetoma is a chronic fungal mycetoma that begins after traumatic inoculation of fungal elements into the subcutaneous tissue. The organisms proliferate in the hypodermis, producing grains and triggering a granulomatous inflammatory response; only secondarily does the process extend upward to the dermis/epidermis or downward to bone. Thus, the primary integumentary layer involved is the subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missense mutation",
    "long_answer": "In X-linked myotubular myopathy, missense mutations in the MTM1 gene generally preserve some myotubularin function; this residual activity correlates with milder clinical phenotypes and longer survival compared with truncating (nonsense or frameshift) mutations that abolish protein function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), hepatic glycogen phosphorylase is deficient, so liver glycogen cannot be mobilised to maintain blood glucose. During periods of fasting, this impaired glycogenolysis can lead to a fall in plasma glucose levels, resulting in hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium bicarbonate",
    "long_answer": "Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis is marked by defective hydrogen-ion secretion in the distal nephron, producing a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with possible hypokalemia. Chronic oral alkali replacement with sodium bicarbonate restores systemic pH, mitigates potassium losses, and is the standard therapeutic approach for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laryngoscope",
    "long_answer": "Laryngeal papillomatosis is diagnosed by directly inspecting the larynx; this is done with a laryngoscope—a rigid or flexible fiber-optic instrument that allows visualization of papillomatous lesions on the vocal folds and other laryngeal structures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tadpole gill-ventilation hypothesis",
    "long_answer": "The hypothesis, advanced by Straus and colleagues, proposes that the mammalian hiccup is an evolutionary vestige of the buccal-pump reflex used by tadpoles to draw water across their gills. The motor pattern of a hiccup—rapid diaphragmatic (in tadpoles, buccal-floor) contraction followed by abrupt glottic closure—closely matches this amphibian respiration pattern, suggesting today’s hiccups are a retained ancestral reflex rather than a functionally useful behaviour in mammals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal pain",
    "long_answer": "Liddle’s syndrome frequently presents with abdominal pain caused by hypokalaemia-induced intestinal ileus; abdominal pain is likewise one of the hallmark complaints in patients suffering from constipation, making it a symptom common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Sorbsy (Sorsby) syndrome refers to an inherited disorder that primarily affects the eyes—classically Sorsby fundus dystrophy or the blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus complex. Because the condition produces structural or degenerative ocular changes that require diagnosis, monitoring, and often surgical or medical eye care, it is managed by ophthalmologists, the medical specialists devoted to diseases of the eye and visual system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Auricular cartilage",
    "long_answer": "Primrose syndrome, caused by pathogenic variants in the ZBTB20 gene, is characteristically associated with progressive calcification and true ossification of the external ear’s auricular cartilage (the pinna). This hardening of the pinnae is repeatedly reported in clinical series and is considered a distinctive skeletal-dysplastic feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dorothy Reed",
    "long_answer": "The American pathologist Dorothy Reed (later Reed-Mendenhall) published a landmark 1902 paper on Hodgkin’s disease that included meticulous microscopic drawings of the now-eponymous Reed–Sternberg giant cells. Her illustrations clarified the characteristic histology and helped differentiate Hodgkin lymphoma from tuberculosis, thereby providing crucial support for early, accurate descriptions of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale",
    "long_answer": "The Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS) is a 14-item instrument with separate subscales for anxiety and depression that is widely applied in chronic pain disorders, including fibromyalgia, to quantify the severity of patients’ anxiety and depressive symptoms in clinical and research settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2q37 deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A microdeletion of the terminal band of chromosome 2 (2q37) causes 2q37 deletion syndrome—also called brachydactyly-mental retardation syndrome. Patients typically show brachydactyly type E, intellectual disability, obesity, and characteristic facial features, linking the presence of brachydactyly directly to the 2q37 chromosomal deletion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor, are the commonest cause of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility. Because the mutant channel leaks calcium, physical exertion can also trigger a mild hypermetabolic state, producing symptoms such as inappropriate sinus tachycardia during exercise in carriers. Thus an RYR1 mutation links both malignant hyperthermia and exercise-induced sinus tachycardia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "UniProt’s entry for the human TBX22 gene (UniProt ID: O43566) lists its genomic location as chromosome X (cytogenetic band Xq21.1). Thus, TBX22 is an X-linked gene situated on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Glanzmann's thrombasthenia is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the ITGA2B or ITGB3 genes that encode the platelet membrane glycoprotein IIb/IIIa complex. Disease manifests only when both alleles are defective, while heterozygous carriers are clinically normal, and it affects males and females equally—features characteristic of an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of congenital adrenal hyperplasia cases are caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency due to mutations in the CYP21A2 gene. CYP21A2 is located in the major-histocompatibility-complex (MHC class III) region on chromosome 6. The labia majora’s male homologue is the scrotum; the MHC (and thus CYP21A2) lies on the same chromosome that carries the HLA genes classically remembered with the word “major,” linking the two structures. Hence, CYP21A2 is the CAH-related gene found on the same chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology",
    "long_answer": "Surgical management of laryngomalacia, such as endoscopic supraglottoplasty, is performed by otolaryngologists (ear, nose and throat surgeons), the medical specialty trained to operate on disorders of the larynx and pediatric airway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylketonuria",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inborn error of amino-acid metabolism caused by deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase. Failure to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine leads to toxic accumulation of phenylalanine and its metabolites in the brain, resulting in severe intellectual and other developmental disabilities if the condition is not detected early and managed with a low-phenylalanine diet."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-restorative sleep",
    "long_answer": "A cardinal clinical feature of myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS) is that patients awaken feeling just as tired as before they slept. This is referred to as non-restorative (or unrefreshing) sleep, and it is listed alongside post-exertional malaise and cognitive impairment as a core symptom in modern diagnostic criteria for ME/CFS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈5 %",
    "long_answer": "Clinic-based studies show that about one child in twenty who presents with reduced vision diagnosed as amblyopia actually has functional (non-organic) visual loss; thus, the prevalence of functional visual loss among amblyopic patients in general ophthalmology clinics is approximately 5 %."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benzodiazepines",
    "long_answer": "Olney’s lesions are excitotoxic vacuolar/cytotoxic changes in cortical neurons that appear in rodents after exposure to high-dose NMDA-receptor antagonists such as ketamine or PCP. Enhancing GABAergic inhibition counteracts the NMDA-block–induced cortical hyperexcitability that underlies these lesions. Experimental studies have shown that co-administration of benzodiazepines (e.g., diazepam, clonazepam) markedly suppresses or prevents the formation of Olney’s lesions, demonstrating that this drug class can protect against the phenomenon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midgut volvulus",
    "long_answer": "Midgut volvulus is a life-threatening complication in which the small intestine twists around the superior mesenteric vessels, cutting off its blood supply. The resulting ischemia and necrosis often necessitate extensive resection of non-viable small bowel; removal of this large segment can leave the patient with inadequate absorptive surface, producing short bowel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ponseti method",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent foot deformity in arthrogryposis is congenital clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The established non-surgical way to correct this deformity is the Ponseti method, which uses gentle manipulation followed by weekly serial plaster casts (often with a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy and later bracing) to progressively realign the foot without open surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Aase syndrome features hypoplastic (underdeveloped) bone marrow that causes congenital anemia. The standard way to document marrow cellularity and architecture is a bone marrow biopsy (often with aspiration), which allows direct microscopic examination of hematopoietic tissue and confirms marrow underdevelopment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mean systemic filling pressure",
    "long_answer": "Guytonian circulatory physiology holds that the pressure that drives venous return is the gradient between right-atrial pressure and the mean systemic filling pressure (Pmsf) – the equilibrated pressure that would exist throughout the systemic vasculature if the heart were stopped. Because measuring it would require cessation of blood flow, Pmsf is difficult to localize and cannot be directly measured at the bedside; nevertheless, it is regarded as the upstream, driving pressure for venous return."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obstetrics & Gynecology",
    "long_answer": "Game-Friedman-Paradice syndrome was described as a disorder of abnormal labor progression; its recognition, monitoring and management (e.g., labor augmentation or operative delivery) fall squarely within the practice of obstetrics. Consequently, an obstetrician-gynecologist is the specialist who would diagnose and treat this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T4 endonuclease V",
    "long_answer": "Topical liposome-encapsulated T4 endonuclease V is applied to the skin of xeroderma pigmentosum patients to treat and prevent actinic keratoses. The enzyme recognizes UV-induced pyrimidine dimers and initiates nucleotide-excision repair, compensating for the defective DNA repair in these patients and reducing the number of new precancerous lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medulla oblongata",
    "long_answer": "In basilar invagination the odontoid process of the axis is pushed upward through the foramen magnum, narrowing the space at the cranio-cervical junction. This upward displacement can directly compress the medulla oblongata—the lower part of the brainstem that lies just above the foramen magnum—leading to brainstem and long-tract neurological signs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Pyoderma gangrenosum is an autoinflammatory neutrophilic dermatosis that often responds to systemic immunosuppression. Cyclosporine, a calcineurin-inhibitor immunosuppressant, has demonstrated efficacy comparable to systemic corticosteroids in randomized trials and is widely accepted in clinical guidelines as a first-line systemic agent for treating pyoderma gangrenosum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Omalizumab",
    "long_answer": "Omalizumab is an anti-IgE monoclonal antibody that binds circulating IgE, preventing its interaction with FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. By lowering free IgE and down-regulating receptor expression, it reduces mediator release that drives both allergic asthma and chronic spontaneous urticaria. Consequently, it is FDA- and EMA-approved for moderate-to-severe allergic asthma and for chronic urticaria unresponsive to antihistamines, making it a single medication effective in treating both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGMS2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SGMS2 gene (encoding sphingomyelin-synthase-2) are the established cause of calvarial doughnut lesions-bone fragility syndrome. Defective SGMS2 impairs sphingomyelin production in osteoblasts, producing low bone mass, recurrent fractures and, in more severe frameshift or nonsense variants, marked growth failure that results in very short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Inflammatory breast cancer (IBC) presents with diffuse dermal lymphatic invasion and nearly always involves the entire breast skin envelope. Because of this extensive, multifocal involvement, breast-conserving operations such as segmental resection (lumpectomy) cannot achieve adequate local-disease control or negative margins. Current evidence-based guidelines (e.g., NCCN, ASCO/ABS, ESMO) therefore recommend a modified radical mastectomy with axillary staging following neoadjuvant systemic therapy, not segmental resection. Segmental resection is considered inadequate and is generally contraindicated for IBC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AREDS antioxidant vitamins with zinc",
    "long_answer": "Randomized controlled trials from the Age-Related Eye Disease Study (AREDS) demonstrated that a high-dose combination of antioxidant vitamins (vitamin C, vitamin E, β-carotene or, in later formulations, lutein/zeaxanthin) together with zinc (plus copper to prevent deficiency) significantly reduces the risk of progression from intermediate to advanced age-related macular degeneration. This supplement regimen is therefore recommended to slow AMD progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aseptic (viral) meningitis",
    "long_answer": "The poliovirus is an enterovirus. Enteroviruses frequently infect the meninges and produce inflammation without bacterial pathogens being present, a clinical picture termed aseptic—or viral—meningitis. Thus, infection with the poliovirus can lead to aseptic (viral) meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiazide diuretic",
    "long_answer": "In hypoparathyroidism, calcium and active vitamin D replacement can raise serum calcium but also increase urinary calcium, predisposing to nephrolithiasis. Thiazide diuretics (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide) lower urinary calcium excretion by enhancing distal tubular calcium reabsorption, thereby helping to prevent kidney stones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PEX7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PEX7 gene, which encodes the peroxisomal targeting signal-2 (PTS2) receptor, disrupt peroxisomal matrix protein import. Loss-of-function variants in PEX7 cause rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1 and can also produce a peroxisomal form of Refsum’s disease, giving this gene a well-established link to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hypertension",
    "long_answer": "In sarcoidosis, granulomatous inflammation and fibrosis can affect the pulmonary vasculature and parenchyma, increasing resistance in the pulmonary arteries. This leads to pulmonary hypertension, a recognized complication marked by abnormally elevated blood pressure within the lungs' arterial system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈5 percent",
    "long_answer": "Large series and guideline reviews show that only a small minority of adrenal incidentalomas prove to be malignant (primary adrenocortical carcinoma or metastatic disease). Across unselected incidentally discovered adrenal masses, malignancy is identified in roughly 5 % of cases, with the remainder being benign adenomas or other non-malignant lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "8p23.1 duplication syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Partial syndactyly of the toes is a recurrent physical finding in individuals who carry a recurrent microduplication of the 8p23.1 region. This chromosomal rearrangement, known as 8p23.1 duplication syndrome, produces partial trisomy for genes within distal short arm of chromosome 8 and is a recognized genomic disorder with features that include mild-to-moderate developmental delay, congenital heart defects, and characteristic digital anomalies such as partial fusion of the second and third toes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN5",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked CLCN5 gene, which encodes the renal chloride/proton exchanger CLC-5, are the prototypical cause of Dent’s disease (Dent disease type 1). These loss-of-function variants impair proximal tubule endocytosis, leading to low-molecular-weight proteinuria, hypercalciuria, nephrocalcinosis, and progressive tubular injury. Consequently, many affected males develop chronic kidney disease that can advance to end-stage renal failure, establishing CLCN5 mutations as a shared genetic driver of both Dent’s disease and chronic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene are the most common cause of Waardenburg syndrome type 2A. MITF encodes a basic helix-loop-helix leucine-zipper transcription factor that controls expression of genes needed for melanocyte differentiation and for progression through the cell cycle, thereby directly influencing cell division. Loss-of-function variants disrupt these processes, producing the pigmentary abnormalities and sensorineural deafness characteristic of WS2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydralazine",
    "long_answer": "Hydralazine, an arterial vasodilator prescribed for difficult-to-control hypertension, is a well-documented cause of hypersensitivity (eosinophilic) myocarditis. In susceptible patients the drug provokes an immune-mediated reaction that leads to dense eosinophilic infiltration of the myocardium, manifesting as acute heart failure or cardiogenic shock. Numerous case reports and reviews identify hydralazine as a classic antihypertensive agent capable of this severe immunological adverse effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PVC",
    "long_answer": "PVC is the shared abbreviation for premature ventricular contractions—an ectopic heart arrhythmia characterized by early beats originating in the ventricles—and polyvinyl chloride, one of the world’s most widely manufactured synthetic polymers used in pipes, cables, and packaging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orientia",
    "long_answer": "Scrub typhus is caused by the obligate intracellular bacterium Orientia tsutsugamushi. This pathogen belongs to the genus Orientia (previously classified within the genus Rickettsia), which encompasses bacteria responsible for scrub-typhus–type illnesses transmitted by larval trombiculid mites."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNAH11",
    "long_answer": "Mutation of the DNAH11 gene, which encodes the left–right dynein heavy chain essential for motile cilia in the embryonic node, disrupts the left–right axis and ciliary motility. This leads to primary ciliary dyskinesia with situs inversus, the classic presentation of Kartagener syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MELAS syndrome",
    "long_answer": "MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes) is a maternally inherited mitochondrial DNA disorder, most often caused by the m.3243A>G mutation in the tRNALeu(UUR) gene. Because mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation is impaired in many organs, patients develop multisystem disease that typically includes premature atherosclerotic vascular changes, sensorineural hearing loss, diabetes mellitus, epileptic seizures, and progressive nephropathy (often focal segmental glomerulosclerosis). These co-occurring features make MELAS the classic genetic disorder fitting the combination listed in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronal craniosynostosis",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is most characteristically linked to premature fusion of the coronal sutures—often bilaterally—resulting in brachycephaly or turricephaly. While other sutures can be involved, coronal craniosynostosis is the hallmark pattern seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Germline mosaicism",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta is usually autosomal-dominant, so an affected parent is expected. When two clinically normal parents have more than one child with osteogenesis imperfecta, the usual explanation is germline (gonadal) mosaicism: a pathogenic COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutation is present only in a subset of one parent’s germ cells, leaving the parent phenotypically unaffected but allowing the mutation to recur in multiple offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Potter sequence arises when bilateral renal agenesis produces severe oligohydramnios. Normal kidney formation depends on branching morphogenesis of the ureteric bud, a process driven by signaling through the RET receptor tyrosine kinase. Pathogenic mutations in the RET gene abolish this signaling, so the ureteric bud fails to branch and kidneys do not develop, leading to the renal agenesis that precipitates Potter sequence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Dentatorubral–pallidoluysian atrophy produces marked degeneration of the cerebellar dentate nucleus and its connections. The cerebellum is the brain region that fine-tunes motor commands and coordinates movement, so its involvement in DRPLA explains the characteristic gait ataxia, tremor, and other coordination deficits seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatic glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease) results from a deficiency of hepatic glycogen phosphorylase, the liver-specific isoform of the enzyme that cleaves α-1,4-glycosidic bonds in glycogen. Without adequate hepatic glycogen phosphorylase activity, the liver cannot mobilize glycogen to glucose during fasting, leading to accumulation of glycogen in hepatocytes, hepatomegaly, and mild fasting hypoglycaemia characteristic of GSD VI."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kartagener syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kartagener syndrome is a hereditary form of primary ciliary dyskinesia in which immotile or dyskinetic cilia lead to defective mucociliary clearance. This results in recurrent upper- and lower-respiratory infections manifesting as chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis. Disrupted ciliary motion during embryogenesis also causes reversed left-right organ positioning (situs inversus). The classic triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis therefore defines Kartagener syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrology",
    "long_answer": "Xanthine oxidase deficiency causes xanthinuria, crystal formation, and recurrent xanthine kidney stones that can progress to nephrolithiasis and renal failure. Management therefore lies primarily with nephrologists, the specialists who diagnose and treat metabolic disorders of the kidney and renal stone disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pastinaca sativa",
    "long_answer": "The species Pastinaca sativa, commonly called wild parsnip, produces high levels of photoactive furanocoumarins (e.g., psoralens) in its sap. When the sap contacts skin and is subsequently exposed to ultraviolet-A sunlight, the compounds form DNA cross-links that trigger an inflammatory blistering reaction known as phytophotodermatitis. This effect is well documented in dermatological and plant-toxicity literature, making P. sativa the canonical Pastinaca species associated with phytophotodermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abnormal neuroblast migration",
    "long_answer": "During cortical development, neuroblasts generated in the ventricular zone must migrate along radial glia to reach the cortical plate and form the normal six-layered cortex. In polymicrogyria, this migratory process is disrupted—neuroblasts either arrest prematurely or arrive disorganized—so the cortex becomes poorly laminated and overfolded into many small gyri. Thus, defective migration of neuroblasts is a key pathogenic step in producing polymicrogyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Allopolyploidy",
    "long_answer": "In crops such as wheat and sugarcane the extra chromosome sets come from hybridisation between different species (e.g., the A, B and D genomes in hexaploid wheat; genomes of Saccharum officinarum and S. spontaneum in sugarcane). This inter-specific origin defines allopolyploidy, the form of polyploidy most frequently encountered in monocotyledon crops."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical repair",
    "long_answer": "Encephaloceles are corrected by neurosurgical intervention in which the herniated brain tissue and meninges are reduced or excised and the cranial defect is closed; no pharmacologic therapy can address the structural defect, so definitive surgical repair is the primary treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystrophin",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophin is a key cytoskeletal protein that links actin filaments inside muscle fibers to the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex in the sarcolemma. When dystrophin is absent because of mutations in the DMD gene, the muscle cell membrane becomes fragile, leading to repeated fiber damage, impaired regeneration, and progressive weakness characteristic of Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophies and related skeletal-muscle impairments."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholesteatoma",
    "long_answer": "A cholesteatoma is an abnormal keratinizing epithelial growth in the middle ear and mastoid that progressively erodes surrounding bone, including the bony canal of the facial nerve. If the lesion is not removed, this erosion can expose or compress the facial nerve, leading to peripheral facial nerve paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory alkalosis",
    "long_answer": "During sepsis the systemic inflammatory response stimulates the respiratory centre, producing hyperventilation. The excessive ventilation lowers arterial CO₂ (PaCO₂), which in turn raises blood pH, producing the acid–base disturbance known as respiratory alkalosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meige syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brueghel’s syndrome is an older synonym for Meige syndrome, a segmental cranial dystonia that combines blepharospasm with oromandibular (jaw-opening) dystonia. The disorder was first described by the French neurologist Henri Meige; the alternative name arose from the wide-open mouth seen in a painting by Pieter Brueghel that resembles the clinical jaw posture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "A congenital or acquired deficiency of complement component C5 can manifest with cutaneous conditions such as vasculitic rashes or lupus-like skin lesions. Physicians who are specifically trained to diagnose, treat and follow patients with diseases of the skin, hair and nails are dermatologists; therefore, a dermatologist is the specialist responsible for managing skin disorders linked to C5 deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is a chromosomal disorder whose characteristic craniofacial dysmorphisms include flat facies, up-slanting palpebral fissures and prominent epicanthal folds, small nose with a low nasal bridge, and other facial deformities. The epicanthal folds are a well-recognized diagnostic feature of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellar atrophy",
    "long_answer": "In PMM2-CDG (formerly CDG-Ia), brain MRI typically reveals marked cerebellar atrophy or hypoplasia—especially of the vermis and hemispheres. This consistent finding is considered a radiological hallmark of the disorder and aids in its clinical recognition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood-retinal barrier",
    "long_answer": "The blood-retinal barrier, formed by tight junctions between retinal vascular endothelial cells (inner BRB) and the retinal pigment epithelium (outer BRB), limits leukocyte and macromolecule movement from the circulation into retinal tissue. This immune-privileged barrier impedes normal trafficking of lymphocytes, so malignant cells can accumulate within the eye, rendering intraocular lymphoma puzzling in the context of systemic immune surveillance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Beyond its approved use for severe acne, isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is employed off-label for basal cell carcinoma, the commonest form of skin cancer. As a systemic retinoid, it normalizes keratinocyte differentiation and decreases proliferation, which can reduce the growth and recurrence of basal cell carcinoma lesions, particularly in high-risk or refractory cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal epidermis",
    "long_answer": "In Epidermolysis bullosa simplex, mutations in keratin 5 or 14 weaken the structural integrity of basal keratinocytes. Mechanical stress therefore causes cytolysis within these basal cells, producing intra-epidermal blisters that remain confined to the basal layer of the epidermis, just above the dermo-epidermal junction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Chronic diabetes mellitus causes diffuse peripheral nerve damage (diabetic peripheral neuropathy) through metabolic and microvascular injury to axons and Schwann cells. The same disease can involve autonomic nerves that supply the anal sphincter and rectum, reducing sphincter tone and impairing rectal sensation, which leads to fecal incontinence. Thus, diabetes mellitus is a well-established common cause of both peripheral neuropathy and fecal incontinence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMM2-CDG",
    "long_answer": "The phosphomannomutase-2 deficiency subtype (PMM2-CDG, formerly CDG-Ia) frequently involves the retina; electroretinography in affected patients shows markedly decreased scotopic responses, demonstrating reduced rod function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tetracyclines",
    "long_answer": "The first-line therapy for Ehrlichiosis is doxycycline, which belongs to the tetracycline class of antibiotics. Tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis by binding the 30S ribosomal subunit of Ehrlichia species, and early administration markedly reduces morbidity and mortality, making this antibiotic class the standard recommended treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Among the clinical features of 3p deletion syndrome, recurrent epileptic seizures are well documented. Loss of genes on the short arm of chromosome 3 disrupts normal neuronal development and excitability, and as a result many affected individuals present with epilepsy, making it a characteristic neurological manifestation of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clozapine",
    "long_answer": "Clozapine, an atypical antipsychotic with strong 5-HT2A and moderate dopamine-D2 antagonism, has been reported in several case studies to markedly reduce the visual trails, halos, and after-images characteristic of Hallucinogen Persisting Perception Disorder when other agents either failed or worsened symptoms. Its serotonergic blockade is thought to counter the cortical 5-HT2A hypersensitivity believed to underlie HPPD, making clozapine the antipsychotic that has shown the most promising benefit for these visual disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naegeli–Franceschetti–Jadassohn syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Congenital absence of dermatoglyphics (adermatoglyphia) is a hallmark of Naegeli–Franceschetti–Jadassohn syndrome, a rare autosomal-dominant ectodermal dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the keratin-14 (KRT14) gene. The defect disrupts normal epidermal ridge formation during embryogenesis, so patients are born without the distinctive fingerprint patterns normally present on the hands."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heavy menstrual bleeding",
    "long_answer": "Iron deficiency in pre-menopausal women is most often due to excessive blood loss during menstruation—clinically termed heavy menstrual bleeding or menorrhagia. This same heavy or prolonged bleeding is recognized as one of the possible menstrual disturbances seen in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), where irregular endometrial shedding can lead to episodically very heavy periods. Thus, heavy menstrual bleeding is both a frequent cause of iron-deficiency anaemia in women and a documented symptom of PCOS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemivertebrae",
    "long_answer": "In VACTERL association, the vertebral component most frequently consists of segmentation defects; the single-sided failure of vertebral body formation known as hemivertebrae is the classic and most commonly reported anomaly, often leading to congenital scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genomic imprinting",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome arises from abnormal genomic imprinting at chromosome 11p15.5. Loss of the normal maternal imprint (or paternal uniparental disomy) removes silencing of the IGF-2 locus so that both alleles are expressed, leading to IGF-2 overactivity and the characteristic fetal over-growth features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediastinal lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "Unicentric Castleman disease is characterized by enlargement of a single nodal station, and in the majority of reported cases the isolated mass arises from mediastinal (often paratracheal or hilar) lymph nodes. These mediastinal nodes are therefore the classic site involved in unicentric Castleman disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bartonella quintana",
    "long_answer": "Trench fever is an acute relapsing febrile illness classically seen in soldiers of World War I. It is caused by the Gram-negative bacillus Bartonella quintana (formerly Rochalimaea quintana), which is transmitted from person to person by the human body louse (Pediculus humanus corporis). The louse becomes infected while feeding on bacteremic humans, and the bacteria are then passed in louse feces that contaminate skin abrasions, leading to systemic infection and the characteristic relapsing fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Albendazole",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar echinococcosis, produced by the tapeworm Echinococcus multilocularis, is managed primarily with long-term benzimidazole therapy. Albendazole is the drug of choice because it inhibits parasite microtubule assembly via β-tubulin binding, suppressing larval growth and reducing cyst viability. Continuous albendazole treatment—often for months to years and combined with surgery when possible—has been shown to improve survival and disease control in alveolar echinococcosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laryngomalacia",
    "long_answer": "In infants, the most frequent reason for persistent inspiratory (chronic) stridor is laryngomalacia, a congenital softening and inward collapse of the supraglottic laryngeal structures during inspiration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Haloperidol",
    "long_answer": "Haloperidol is a high-potency, first-generation (typical) antipsychotic whose strong D2-receptor blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces prominent extrapyramidal side-effects. Shortly after initiation it commonly causes acute dystonia—sustained, painful muscle contractions of the face, neck or limbs. With long-term use, the same potent dopamine antagonism can lead to ‘rabbit syndrome,’ a rhythmic, perioral tremor that resembles rapid chewing motions. Because haloperidol is well documented to precipitate both of these movement disorders, it is the antipsychotic most closely associated with both acute dystonia and rabbit syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mowat-Wilson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mowat-Wilson syndrome is a congenital disorder produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the ZEB2 gene. A cardinal clinical triad includes: (1) Hirschsprung’s disease or intestinal aganglionosis, seen in roughly half of affected individuals; (2) distinctive facial dysmorphism with broad eyebrows, uplifted earlobes, wide nasal tip, and pointed chin; and (3) hypoplastic or dystrophic nails—especially of the great toes and fifth fingers. This combination of Hirschsprung’s disease, nail hypoplasia, and abnormal facial features is therefore diagnostic of Mowat-Wilson syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 50 percent",
    "long_answer": "Studies of monozygotic twins show a concordance rate of roughly one-half (≈50 %) for alopecia areata, indicating that if one identical twin is affected the co-twin has about a 50 % chance of developing the disease. This figure is far higher than the general-population risk but well below 100 %, reflecting both strong genetic predisposition and the need for additional non-genetic triggers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disability-adjusted life years",
    "long_answer": "The overall burden of lymphatic filariasis is typically expressed in disability-adjusted life years (DALYs), a metric that combines years of life lost due to premature death with years lived with disability, providing a single figure that captures both mortality and morbidity impacts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3–17 days",
    "long_answer": "After inhalation of Histoplasma capsulatum microconidia, symptoms of histoplasmosis typically develop in about 3 to 17 days (roughly 1–3 weeks). This incubation period reflects the time required for the spores to convert to the yeast phase in the lungs, multiply within macrophages, and provoke the host immune response that produces clinical manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNQ1",
    "long_answer": "The KCNQ1 gene, encoding the Kv7.1 potassium channel α-subunit that forms the cardiac IKs current, is situated on chromosome 11p15.5 and is the principal gene for Long QT syndrome type 1. Pathogenic variants in KCNQ1 prolong ventricular repolarization, producing the prolonged QT interval characteristic of Long QT syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "The sight-threatening uveitis that occurs in Behçet's disease is most commonly treated with corticosteroids. Glucocorticoids rapidly suppress the autoimmune-mediated inflammation of the ocular vasculature, reducing pain, preventing structural damage, and preserving vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karim Nader",
    "long_answer": "Neuroscientist Karim Nader, featured in an NPR segment on memory, described his pioneering research on memory reconsolidation. He showed that when a stored memory is re-activated, its molecular trace becomes temporarily unstable and must be ‘re-saved,’ a process that allows new information to be incorporated. This means a memory can literally be rewritten each time it is recalled."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMP2 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Winchester syndrome—a rare connective-tissue disorder marked by progressive, severe osteolysis (bone resorption) of the limbs and other skeletal sites—is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the MMP2 gene (matrix metalloproteinase-2). Defective MMP-2 disrupts collagen and extracellular-matrix remodeling, leading to the characteristic bone and connective-tissue abnormalities recognized in medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nutritional yeast",
    "long_answer": "Nutritional yeast is an inactive form of Saccharomyces cerevisiae grown on molasses, dried, and sold as yellow flakes or powder. Its naturally high content of free glutamic acid gives it a strong savory (“umami”) flavor reminiscent of cheese, which is why it is widely used by vegans and vegetarians as a cheese substitute in sauces, soups, and sprinkled on foods."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Schamberg's disease is a chronic pigmented purpuric dermatosis that presents with characteristic copper-brown patches and petechiae on the skin. Because it is a cutaneous vascular disorder diagnosed primarily by clinical inspection of skin lesions (often confirmed with a skin biopsy), the specialist most commonly responsible for recognizing and diagnosing it is a dermatologist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inflammatory bowel disease",
    "long_answer": "Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)—principally ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease—can produce upper-gastrointestinal symptoms such as heartburn because inflammation may involve the esophagus or promote gastro-oesophageal reflux.  More importantly, long-standing colonic inflammation in IBD markedly elevates colorectal cancer risk; patients with pancolitis have a several-fold higher incidence of colon cancer after 8–10 years of disease compared with the general population.  Thus, IBD is a well-recognized gastrointestinal disorder that both causes heartburn and predisposes to colon cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) is a mitochondrial genetic disorder that most often manifests in late teens to early 30-s.  A previously healthy person—classically a young adult man—develops painless, sudden-to-subacute loss of central vision in one eye, and the fellow eye is usually affected within days to a few months.  Thus the disease characteristically produces an abrupt (“immediate” or acute/subacute) onset of vision loss during young adulthood, which is well documented in epidemiologic and clinical studies.  Although the exact timing can vary and some patients present in childhood or later adulthood, the typical presentation matches the statement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antidiuretic hormone",
    "long_answer": "Damage to the hypothalamus can impair production of antidiuretic hormone (ADH, also called arginine vasopressin). ADH normally promotes water re-absorption in the renal collecting ducts; when it is deficient, large volumes of dilute urine are excreted, obliging the patient to drink excessively to maintain water balance. Thus ADH deficiency is the classic hypothalamic cause of increased urine output (polyuria) and thirst (polydipsia) characteristic of central diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OEIS complex",
    "long_answer": "Imperforate anus can be part of the OEIS complex – Omphalocele, Exstrophy of the cloaca (bladder/cloacal exstrophy), Imperforate anus, and Spinal defects. In this malformation sequence the bladder is split and exposed (cloacal/bladder exstrophy), directly involving the urinary bladder along with the anorectal atresia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type II is caused by pathogenic variants in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene, which is mapped to chromosome 22q12. Loss or mutation of this chromosome 22–located gene leads to the characteristic vestibular schwannomas and other tumors seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endothelin-1",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary arterial hypertension is often driven by excessive production of the potent vasoconstrictor peptide endothelin-1 in the pulmonary vasculature. Endothelin-1 causes sustained pulmonary arterial constriction and vascular remodeling. Drugs such as bosentan, sitaxentan, and ambrisentan directly antagonize endothelin receptors (ETA and/or ETB), thereby counteracting the effects of endothelin-1 and improving pulmonary hemodynamics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiofrequency catheter ablation",
    "long_answer": "In patients with Wolff–Parkinson–White (WPW) syndrome, a catheter is threaded to the heart and radiofrequency energy is delivered to heat and destroy (“ablate”) the accessory atrioventricular pathway responsible for the pre-excitation. This procedure, known as radiofrequency catheter ablation, eliminates the abnormal conduction route and is the definitive curative treatment for WPW."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Intermittent exotropia is a form of strabismus in which one eye intermittently deviates outward. Diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment—whether via glasses, occlusion therapy, orthoptic exercises, or strabismus surgery—are managed by physicians trained in eye diseases. Therefore, a child with intermittent exotropia is typically evaluated and treated by an ophthalmologist, often one subspecialized in pediatric ophthalmology and strabismus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatal period",
    "long_answer": "In combined malonic and methylmalonic acidemia (CMAMMA), the most frequently encountered form of methylmalonic acidemia, the earliest clinical manifestations can emerge in the neonatal period—literally within the first days of life—when affected newborns may develop metabolic acidosis, vomiting, and lethargy. Although some individuals remain asymptomatic until later childhood or even adulthood, the disease is first capable of producing symptoms immediately after birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Foix–Alajouanine syndrome is a subacute necrotizing myelopathy caused by a spinal dural arteriovenous malformation, presenting with progressive neurological deficits such as paraparesis and sensory loss. Evaluation relies on neurological examination, MRI, and spinal angiography, and initial medical management and coordination of definitive neurosurgical or endovascular treatment are overseen by a neurologist. Therefore, a neurologist is the specialist most commonly responsible for diagnosing and treating this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome is a genetic contiguous-gene deletion involving LIS1 on 17p13.3 that produces classical lissencephaly with pachygyria. Nearly all affected infants develop early-onset epilepsy, and the cortical malformation makes the seizures frequently refractory to multiple anti-epileptic drugs, leading to medically drug-resistant epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pompe disease",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II, acid α-glucosidase deficiency) is a lysosomal glycogen storage disorder in which defective acid maltase leads to massive glycogen accumulation in striated muscle, including the muscles of the tongue. The resulting intralysosomal glycogen build-up causes marked enlargement of the tongue (macroglossia), a classic clinical feature of infantile-onset Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A",
    "long_answer": "The TUBB4A gene, located on chromosome 19p13, encodes the β-tubulin 4A protein. Pathogenic variants in TUBB4A are well established as the cause of DYT4 dystonia (also called whispering dysphonia), linking this chromosome-19 gene to dystonic movement disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Current clinical evidence does not support the claim that aspirin is effective in reducing mortality in patients who already have acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Although platelet activation contributes to ARDS pathophysiology and some early observational studies suggested that pre-hospital aspirin use might lower the risk of developing ARDS, subsequent randomized controlled trials (e.g., the LIPS-A trial) failed to show a significant benefit either in preventing ARDS or in improving survival once ARDS is established. Major critical-care guidelines (e.g., ATS/ESICM ARDS management recommendations) do not list aspirin as a mortality-reducing therapy. Standard care for ARDS continues to focus on lung-protective ventilation strategies, prone positioning, and other supportive measures, not on antiplatelet agents such as aspirin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN9A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SCN9A gene, which encodes the voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.7, underlie both conditions: gain-of-function mutations produce hyper-excitable nociceptors causing familial primary erythromelalgia, while loss-of-function mutations abolish Nav1.7 activity and lead to congenital insensitivity to pain. Thus, SCN9A is the common genetic link between erythromelalgia and complete lack of pain sensation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematocele",
    "long_answer": "Following blunt or penetrating injury to the testis, rupture of the tunica albuginea or scrotal vessels can cause blood to collect within the tunica vaginalis sac. This post-traumatic accumulation of blood around the testis is termed a hematocele, presenting as a tender scrotal swelling that transilluminates poorly because it contains blood rather than clear fluid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63 (p63)",
    "long_answer": "EEC (Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal dysplasia-Clefting) syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor. TP63 is crucial for ectodermal and craniofacial development, so its mutation can disrupt normal eyelid morphogenesis, producing the characteristic eyelid abnormalities seen in EEC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesentery",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic sclerosing mesenteritis is a rare fibro-inflammatory disorder that primarily involves the adipose tissue of the small-bowel mesentery, leading to fibrosis, inflammation, and mass-like thickening of the mesentery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Abnormal and excessive growth of tissue is termed neoplasia. In gestational trophoblastic disease, the trophoblastic cells undergo neoplastic proliferation, giving rise to lesions such as hydatidiform mole, invasive mole, and choriocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipofuscin",
    "long_answer": "Vitelliform macular dystrophy (Best disease) is characterized by abnormal accumulation of lipofuscin-rich, yellowish material within and beneath the retinal pigment epithelium at the macula. This buildup of the autofluorescent pigment, caused by mutations such as those in the BEST1 gene that impair RPE ion transport and phagocytic function, produces the classic ‘egg-yolk’ (vitelliform) lesion seen clinically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome, also called 22q11.2 deletion syndrome, is caused by a microdeletion on chromosome 22 and is well known for its strong association with conotruncal cardiac defects, especially tetralogy of Fallot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transcranial direct current stimulation",
    "long_answer": "Several controlled studies have shown that applying anodal transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS) to left-hemisphere language cortex, often combined with naming therapy, significantly improves word-retrieval in post-stroke patients with anomic aphasia. By modulating cortical excitability non-invasively, tDCS enhances neural plasticity in perilesional language networks, leading to faster and more durable gains in naming accuracy compared with sham stimulation—hence it is regarded as a promising treatment for anomic aphasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Available generically",
    "long_answer": "Valproate (valproic acid/divalproex) is long off-patent and is produced by multiple manufacturers worldwide, so it is fully available as a generic drug rather than being restricted to a single branded product."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholesteatoma",
    "long_answer": "A cholesteatoma—an abnormal growth of keratinizing squamous epithelium in the middle ear—can erode the bony labyrinth, most often the lateral semicircular canal, creating a labyrinthine fistula. This abnormal opening allows perilymph leakage and abnormal stimulation of vestibular receptors, leading to balance disruption and episodic vertigo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yes",
    "long_answer": "Numerous epidemiological studies have shown that children and adults with spina bifida have a markedly elevated prevalence of IgE-mediated natural-rubber latex allergy—often 20–65 %, compared with <2 % in the general population. The heightened risk is attributed to their repeated early-life exposures to latex-containing surgical gloves, catheter balloons, and other medical devices during the multiple surgeries and catheterizations commonly required for spina bifida care, leading to sensitization and clinical allergy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(11;22) translocation",
    "long_answer": "Ewing sarcoma characteristically harbors a somatic chromosomal translocation, most often t(11;22)(q24;q12), which fuses the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 with the FLI1 gene on chromosome 11. The resulting EWSR1-FLI1 fusion oncogene drives aberrant transcriptional activity and is present in about 85% of Ewing sarcoma cases, making this specific translocation the mutation most commonly associated with the tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dopamine",
    "long_answer": "Dopamine physiologically inhibits prolactin release from pituitary lactotrophs via D2 receptors. Pharmacologic dopamine agonists such as bromocriptine or cabergoline are therefore administered to lower elevated prolactin levels, reverse hyperprolactinemia-induced suppression of GnRH, and correct the resulting acquired hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Brain magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is often ordered during the diagnostic work-up for suspected bipolar disorder because it can reveal structural lesions, tumours, strokes, demyelination, or other neurological abnormalities whose symptoms can mimic mood disturbances. A normal MRI helps clinicians rule out these medical or neurological causes, supporting a primary psychiatric diagnosis of bipolar disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Canine distemper virus",
    "long_answer": "Paget’s disease of bone has been linked to persistent infection by a paramyxovirus; immunohistochemical and molecular studies have detected nucleocapsid proteins and RNA of canine distemper virus in pagetic osteoclasts. The same virus, a Morbillivirus within the Paramyxoviridae family, is the well-established etiologic agent of canine distemper, thereby connecting it with both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Halos",
    "long_answer": "Keratoconus thins and distorts the cornea, causing incoming light to scatter before it reaches the retina. This stray light produces bright circular rings—“halos”—around point-light sources such as headlights or streetlamps, an effect that closely resembles the lens-flare artefact seen in photography."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLURP1",
    "long_answer": "Meleda disease (mal de Meleda) is an autosomal-recessive palmoplantar keratoderma caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLURP1 gene, which encodes the secreted Ly-6/uPAR-related protein-1 important for epidermal homeostasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NLRP3 (CIAS1)",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the NLRP3 gene (also called CIAS1, encoding the inflammasome protein cryopyrin) underlie the spectrum of cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes. These mutations are responsible for the most severe form, neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (NOMID/CINCA), and have also been identified in patients diagnosed with Marshall syndrome, placing both conditions on the same genetic and pathophysiological continuum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis is classically caused by autosomal-dominant mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel (Nav1.4). These gain-of-function mutations impair channel inactivation, leading to sustained sodium influx, membrane depolarization, and episodic muscle weakness precipitated by elevated serum potassium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shauna Rae",
    "long_answer": "Shauna Rae, star of the TLC series “I Am Shauna Rae,” developed pituitary-related growth hormone deficiency after chemotherapy for a childhood brain cancer damaged her endocrine system, stunting her growth to 3′10″ despite otherwise normal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Necrosis",
    "long_answer": "Leukoplakia can show areas where epithelial cells die through necrosis. Necrosis is a pathological form of cell death in which plasma-membrane integrity is lost, leading to leakage of cellular contents into the surrounding tissue. This uncontrolled release provokes inflammation and distinguishes necrosis from the orderly, membrane-contained process of apoptosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LEMD3 (MAN1)",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the LEMD3 gene, which encodes the inner nuclear membrane protein MAN1, are the established genetic cause of both osteopoikilosis and Buschke–Ollendorff syndrome. Defective LEMD3 disrupts TGF-β/BMP signaling in bone and skin, producing the characteristic sclerotic bone spots of osteopoikilosis and the elastin-rich skin papules seen in Buschke–Ollendorff syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stratified squamous epithelium",
    "long_answer": "In pemphigus vulgaris, pathogenic IgG auto-antibodies target the desmosomal cadherin desmoglein-3 (and often desmoglein-1), which are adhesion proteins specific to desmosomes of stratified squamous epithelium in the skin and mucous membranes. Loss of desmosomal adhesion within this epithelial layer produces the characteristic intraepidermal blisters and erosions seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria cutanea tarda can occur as a paraneoplastic manifestation of hepatocellular carcinoma; the skin photosensitivity and uroporphyrin accumulation may precede or accompany the liver tumor, making PCT a recognized, although infrequent, paraneoplastic phenomenon of hepatocellular carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "99th percentile",
    "long_answer": "The QT-interval threshold used to diagnose congenital Long QT syndrome in females (≈480 ms QTc) is set at the upper 1 % of the normal population distribution; in other words, the cutoff corresponds to the 99th percentile of QT intervals in healthy women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GDF6 gene (Growth Differentiation Factor 6) cause a form of Klippel–Feil syndrome. GDF6 is a bone morphogenetic protein that directs early skeletal patterning, promoting vertebral bone formation and helping set up the joint boundaries that separate individual bones. Disruption of this gene impairs vertebral segmentation, leading to the congenital fusion characteristic of Klippel–Feil syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastric adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Pernicious anemia results from autoimmune atrophic gastritis that destroys parietal cells, producing chronic inflammation, achlorhydria, and intestinal metaplasia of the gastric mucosa. These changes markedly increase the risk of developing gastric adenocarcinoma (stomach cancer), making it the gastrointestinal malignancy classically associated with pernicious anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Upper gastrointestinal series",
    "long_answer": "Both duodenal atresia and midgut volvulus may show a “double-bubble” sign on plain abdominal radiography. An upper gastrointestinal (UGI) contrast series tracks the passage of contrast through the duodenum and proximal jejunum; in duodenal atresia contrast stops abruptly at the atretic segment, whereas in midgut volvulus the study demonstrates abnormal duodenal position, corkscrew/twisting of the bowel, or a spiral ‘coiled-spring’ appearance. Thus an UGI series reliably differentiates the two conditions and is considered the diagnostic gold standard for suspected midgut volvulus." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "AKT1",
    "long_answer": "Proteus syndrome is caused by a post-zygotic activating mutation in the AKT1 gene (most commonly c.49G>A, p.E17K). The mosaic gain-of-function mutation constitutively activates the PI3K–AKT signalling pathway, driving the disproportionate, segmental overgrowth of bone, connective tissue and other organs that characterises the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiazolidinediones",
    "long_answer": "A subset of follicular thyroid carcinomas carries the t(2;3)(q13;p25) translocation that fuses PAX8 to the nuclear receptor PPARγ. Because the fusion protein leaves the PPARγ ligand-binding domain intact, activating this receptor with PPARγ agonists can drive redifferentiation and growth arrest. Thiazolidinediones (e.g., pioglitazone, rosiglitazone) are well-known PPARγ agonists and therefore represent a rational therapeutic class for these translocation-positive tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Novosibirsk Oblast",
    "long_answer": "Phylogeographic analysis of Omsk hemorrhagic fever virus genomes shows that one genetic clade—the so-called eastern or Novosibirsk lineage—traces its most recent common ancestor to Novosibirsk Oblast in western Siberia, Russia, before spreading to other regions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute myeloid leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia causes defective DNA interstrand cross-link repair, leading to chromosomal breakage and bone-marrow failure. This genomic instability markedly increases the risk of hematologic malignancy, particularly progression to acute myeloid leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ring chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Ring chromosome 15 syndrome is caused by the formation of a ring-shaped chromosome derived from chromosome 15. During cell division, the ends of chromosome 15 break and the broken arms fuse, creating a circular (ring) chromosome. This structural abnormality—specifically a ring chromosome involving chromosome 15—leads to loss of genetic material and the characteristic clinical features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topical gentamicin",
    "long_answer": "An aminoglycoside antibiotic (0.1% gentamicin ointment) is applied to skin lesions in junctional epidermolysis bullosa because it promotes translational read-through of premature stop-codon mutations in genes such as LAMB3 or COL17A1, restoring laminin-332 or type XVII collagen at the dermal-epidermal junction and thereby reducing blistering and improving wound healing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Witch’s milk",
    "long_answer": "In some newborns, circulating maternal estrogens and prolactin transiently stimulate the infant’s mammary glands, leading to a small secretion of milk-like fluid. This phenomenon is medically termed “witch’s milk” (also called neonatal milk or neonatal galactorrhea). The secretion is harmless, self-limited, and reflects temporary hormonal influence rather than true lactation pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deficient sexual desire",
    "long_answer": "DSM-5 defines male hypoactive sexual desire disorder as a condition in which a man experiences persistently or recurrently deficient (or absent) sexual/erotic thoughts, fantasies, and desire for sexual activity for at least six months, on most occasions, leading to clinically significant distress and not attributable to another medical, psychiatric, or substance-related cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Valproic acid, an anticonvulsant that also functions as a histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor, has been evaluated in pre-clinical studies and clinical trials to increase SMN2 gene expression and ameliorate symptoms in patients with spinal muscular atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synovial membrane of joints",
    "long_answer": "In hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, the disease process includes marked villous (finger-like) proliferation of the synovial membrane that lines synovial joints, producing joint effusions and arthritic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Subcortical dementia",
    "long_answer": "Subcortical dementia is marked by atrophy and ischemic or degenerative lesions in cerebral white matter and deep gray nuclei, disrupting fronto-subcortical circuits. The resulting disconnection chiefly impairs executive functions (planning, set-shifting, processing speed) while sparing cortical language and visuospatial skills, fitting the pattern described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Choking on fish bones",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with ichthyophobia often avoid eating fish because they fear that small, concealed bones could become lodged in their throat and cause choking. This anxiety about fish bones is a well-documented, common component of the phobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reactive arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Reactive arthritis, historically called Reiter syndrome, classically presents with a triad of asymmetric arthritis together with urethritis and conjunctivitis. It is a post-infectious spondyloarthropathy in which joint inflammation follows genitourinary or gastrointestinal infections, explaining the concurrent inflammation of the urethra and conjunctiva."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyrrolizidine alkaloids",
    "long_answer": "Hepatic veno-occlusive disease was first described in the 1920s in Jamaican patients who had consumed ‘bush teas’ or grain contaminated with Crotalaria/Senecio plants. These plants contain pyrrolizidine alkaloids, which are metabolically activated in the liver to reactive pyrroles that injure sinusoidal endothelial cells and narrow or obliterate terminal hepatic venules. Thus, ingestion of pyrrolizidine alkaloids is the earliest reported cause of hepatic veno-occlusive disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCX gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the DCX (doublecortin) gene disturb microtubule-dependent neuronal migration. In affected males this produces the X-linked lissencephaly spectrum, whose neuroimaging hallmark is pachygyria/lissencephaly, while clinically they often display craniofacial dysmorphism such as hypertelorism. Hence a DCX gene mutation is classically linked to the coexistence of pachygyria and hypertelorism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF-κB signaling",
    "long_answer": "Repeated inflammatory episodes in the ovary/fallopian tube bathe epithelial cells in cytokines (e.g., IL-1β, IL-6, TNF-α) and reactive oxygen species. These mediators chronically activate the NF-κB signaling pathway, which drives transcription of genes that induce cell proliferation, inhibit apoptosis, create a pro-angiogenic milieu, and exacerbate oxidative DNA damage. The combination of accumulated genetic lesions and NF-κB-dependent survival/proliferation of altered cells promotes malignant transformation, linking chronic inflammation directly to ovarian cancer development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma is an inherited disorder of keratinization that causes thickened, blister-prone skin on the palms and soles. Because it is a primary disease of the epidermis, diagnosis, genetic counseling, and management (e.g., keratolytics, retinoids, wound care) are handled by the medical specialty that deals with skin disorders—dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muenke syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Muenke syndrome is a craniosynostosis disorder produced by the recurrent FGFR3 Pro250Arg mutation. Although best known for coronal synostosis, the mutation can also lead to premature fusion of the metopic suture, resulting in trigonocephaly. Thus, among the syndromes featuring trigonocephaly, the one specifically caused by an FGFR3 mutation is Muenke syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "In humans the HMGCL gene, which encodes the enzyme 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase responsible for the final step of leucine catabolism and ketone-body formation, is mapped to cytogenetic band 1p36.11 on chromosome 1. Mutations in this locus cause 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nitazoxanide",
    "long_answer": "Nitazoxanide is a broad-spectrum thiazolide antiparasitic agent. Clinical studies show it is effective against the cestode Hymenolepis nana, the principal cause of hymenolepiasis, and it is the only drug approved specifically for treating diarrhoeal disease caused by the protozoan Cryptosporidium parvum. Thus, the same medication can be used for both infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Duplex ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "Superficial thrombophlebitis is confirmed in practice with duplex ultrasonography, a non-invasive test that combines B-mode imaging and color Doppler to visualize superficial veins, demonstrate intraluminal thrombus, assess blood flow, and rule out extension into the deep venous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Listeriosis",
    "long_answer": "Listeriosis, caused by the food-borne bacterium Listeria monocytogenes, can cross the placental barrier during pregnancy. Maternal infection often produces only mild flu-like symptoms, but fetal infection can lead to miscarriage, pre-term delivery, or stillbirth because the organism proliferates in the placenta and fetus, provoking severe sepsis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Salmonella infection",
    "long_answer": "Patients with sickle-cell anemia are especially susceptible to osteomyelitis caused by Salmonella species; defects in splenic function and complement opsonization increase the frequency of Salmonella bacteremia, which can seed bone and lead to osteomyelitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-1β",
    "long_answer": "Muckle-Wells syndrome is a cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome caused by gain-of-function mutations in the NLRP3 gene, which lead to over-activation of the inflammasome. This results in excessive cleavage and secretion of the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-1β (IL-1β), driving the fever, rash, arthralgia and other inflammatory manifestations characteristic of the disorder. Therapeutic blockade of IL-1β (e.g., with anakinra, canakinumab) markedly ameliorates symptoms, underscoring IL-1β’s central role in the disease’s inflammatory response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lorazepam",
    "long_answer": "Lorazepam, a benzodiazepine that enhances GABA-A receptor activity, is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for catatonia. Given intramuscularly or intravenously, it rapidly reduces catatonic symptoms and produces calming sedation, so it is also used in acutely agitated patients with brief psychotic disorder to prevent them from hurting themselves or others."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teduglutide",
    "long_answer": "Teduglutide is a recombinant analog of glucagon-like peptide-2 (GLP-2) that is approved for the treatment of short bowel syndrome. By binding to GLP-2 receptors on intestinal epithelial cells, it stimulates mucosal growth, increases intestinal absorptive capacity, and thereby reduces or eliminates the need for parenteral nutrition in patients with SBS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glass blowing",
    "long_answer": "Laryngocele is an air-filled dilation of the laryngeal saccule that develops when intralaryngeal pressure is repeatedly and forcefully raised. People who blow glass for a living habitually generate high, sustained intralaryngeal pressures during the blowing process, and this chronic pressure promotes herniation of the saccule, markedly increasing their risk of laryngocele formation compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leaky scanning",
    "long_answer": "Parvoviruses place two in-frame AUG start codons on the same capsid mRNA. Because the first AUG sits in a weak Kozak context, many ribosomes bypass it and initiate translation at the downstream AUG. Initiation at the upstream codon yields the longer VP1 protein, while initiation at the downstream codon yields the shorter VP2 protein. This ribosomal bypassing of a sub-optimal start site is called leaky scanning, and it allows different proteins to be produced from one mRNA molecule."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pruritus",
    "long_answer": "The medical term for an unpleasant cutaneous sensation that elicits a strong urge to scratch is pruritus. Pruritus is frequently reported by patients with lichen planus, where immune-mediated skin and mucosal inflammation stimulates itch-specific C-fiber nerve endings, producing the characteristic desire to scratch."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miniature Wirehaired Dachshund",
    "long_answer": "Canine Lafora disease, a progressive myoclonic epilepsy caused by autosomal-recessive EPM2B (NHLRC1) mutations leading to polyglucosan body accumulation, is classically reported in and genetically characterized within the Miniature Wirehaired Dachshund breed, making this breed uniquely predisposed to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Leiner’s disease is an infantile erythroderma regarded as an extreme form of seborrhoeic dermatitis. Because it primarily presents with widespread scaling, erythema and other cutaneous manifestations, its recognition, differential diagnosis and management fall within the domain of dermatology; hence a dermatologist (often with paediatric expertise) is the specialist most commonly responsible for diagnosing and treating the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolonged fasting",
    "long_answer": "Metabolic crises in long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency typically arise when the body depends on long-chain fatty-acid oxidation for energy—most commonly during periods of prolonged fasting (or other catabolic stresses such as intercurrent illness). The defective enzyme cannot meet the increased β-oxidation demand, leading to hypoketotic hypoglycaemia and other acute symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crohn’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Crohn’s disease, an inflammatory bowel disease that occurs more frequently in patients with coeliac disease, produces transmural gastrointestinal inflammation that can involve the stomach or proximal small bowel. Active Crohn’s inflammation disrupts normal neuromuscular function and can cause stricturing or edema, leading to impaired motility and clinically demonstrable delayed gastric emptying (gastroparesis). Thus, Crohn’s disease is the coeliac-associated gastrointestinal disorder that can cause both inflammation and delayed gastric emptying."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clubfoot Seven",
    "long_answer": "In 'Once Upon a Time in China III' the character known as Clubfoot Seven (also called Seven Chiu-Tsat) is depicted with a congenital clubfoot deformity, which is central to his nickname and to his storyline within the film."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
    "long_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) is an endoscopic-fluoroscopic procedure in which contrast is injected into the bile ducts through the ampulla of Vater, allowing direct radiographic visualization of strictures or masses caused by cholangiocarcinoma. During the same session therapeutic measures—such as brush cytology/biopsy for diagnosis, balloon dilation, and placement of biliary stents to relieve obstruction—can be performed, making ERCP both a diagnostic and therapeutic tool for bile duct cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "21 %",
    "long_answer": "In the 2017 JAMA neuropathology study of 202 deceased American-football players, only 14 had played solely at the high-school level; chronic traumatic encephalopathy was identified in 3 of those 14 brains, yielding a prevalence of 21 percent among tested high-school players."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incomplete penetrance",
    "long_answer": "In genetics, penetrance is the proportion of individuals carrying a pathogenic variant who actually express the associated phenotype. When some people with the HOGA1 mutation that causes primary hyperoxaluria type 3 remain clinically asymptomatic, the gene shows less than 100 % penetrance—termed incomplete (or reduced) penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome (RTS) is a rare genodermatosis caused most often by mutations in the RECQL4 helicase gene. Patients characteristically develop poikiloderma, growth deficiency, and skeletal anomalies such as radial‐ray defects and clinodactyly (curved fifth fingers). Importantly, RTS confers a markedly increased lifetime risk of osteogenic sarcoma (bone cancer), making it the syndrome classically associated with both clinodactyly and heightened bone cancer susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myxoid liposarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Among the histologic subtypes of liposarcoma, the myxoid variant is defined by abundant gelatinous (myxoid) extracellular matrix with delicate, arborizing capillaries and scattered lipoblasts. This myxoid stroma is the hallmark feature that distinguishes myxoid liposarcoma from other forms such as well-differentiated, dedifferentiated, or pleomorphic liposarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ESCO2",
    "long_answer": "Both Juberg–Hayward syndrome and Roberts/SC phocomelia syndrome are autosomal-recessive limb-reduction disorders that have been shown to be allelic. Sequencing studies identified pathogenic mutations in the ESCO2 gene (Establishment of Sister Chromatid Cohesion N-acetyltransferase 2) in families diagnosed with either condition. Loss-of-function mutations in ESCO2 impair sister-chromatid cohesion during cell division, producing the characteristic growth retardation, limb malformations and craniofacial anomalies seen in both syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nausea",
    "long_answer": "Radiation sickness (acute radiation syndrome) can follow excessive exposure during therapeutic radiation treatments. One of its earliest and most characteristic symptoms is nausea, which arises within hours as rapidly dividing gastrointestinal epithelial cells are injured by ionizing radiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-resolution computed tomography",
    "long_answer": "Acute interstitial pneumonitis is diagnosed by demonstrating diffuse alveolar damage; the standard way to visualise this in vivo is with high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest, which reveals the characteristic bilateral ground-glass opacities and consolidation needed for diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Ramos-Arroyo syndrome is a very rare multiple-congenital-anomaly disorder originally described by C. Ramos-Arroyo and subsequently catalogued in OMIM (OMIM #601186). Core clinical findings include blepharophimosis/ptosis with characteristic facial dysmorphism, sensorineural hearing loss, intestinal malrotation or spontaneous perforation, growth deficiency and other systemic anomalies. Published case reports and reviews consistently note mild to moderate developmental delay or intellectual disability in the majority of affected individuals. Because the cognitive impairment occurs as one feature of a broader pattern of congenital anomalies, the condition is classified as a syndromic form of intellectual disability rather than a non-syndromic/isolated ID. Thus, the statement that Ramos-Arroyo syndrome is associated with intellectual disability and considered a syndromic intellectual disability is supported by established medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epidermis",
    "long_answer": "Epidermal nevus syndrome is characterized by hamartomatous overgrowths (epidermal nevi) that originate within and are confined to the epidermis—the outermost layer of the skin. These lesions involve proliferating keratinocytes of the epidermal layer, distinguishing them from conditions that affect the dermis or subcutis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Oropharyngeal dysphagia often arises when the striated muscles of the oropharynx and upper esophageal sphincter are too weak to initiate and coordinate swallowing. The autoimmune neuromuscular-junction disorder myasthenia gravis causes fatigable weakness of these skeletal muscles, leading to impaired bolus propulsion and pharyngeal clearance, and is therefore a well-recognized cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia due to muscle weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tafamidis",
    "long_answer": "The U.S. FDA approved tafamidis (marketed as Vyndaqel and Vyndamax) in 2019 as the first disease-modifying therapy for cardiomyopathy caused by transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis (both hereditary and wild-type). Tafamidis binds to and stabilizes the transthyretin tetramer, preventing its dissociation into monomers that misfold and form amyloid fibrils, thereby slowing progression of ATTR cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "6D70 Mild neurocognitive disorder",
    "long_answer": "In the ICD-11, mild cognitive impairment is classified in the Mental, Behavioural and Neurodevelopmental Disorders chapter under the block of Neurocognitive disorders. The specific code is 6D70, titled “Mild neurocognitive disorder,” which encompasses clinical presentations commonly referred to as mild cognitive impairment (MCI)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mepolizumab",
    "long_answer": "Mepolizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody against interleukin-5 (IL-5), is used in hypereosinophilic syndrome to lower circulating eosinophil counts. Clinical trials have shown that adding mepolizumab enables significant tapering or discontinuation of systemic glucocorticoids while maintaining disease control, providing a steroid-sparing treatment option for HES."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARX",
    "long_answer": "OMIM lists mutations in the X-chromosomal ARX (Aristaless-related homeobox) gene as the cause of the severe fore-brain malformation termed X-linked aprosencephaly (also called hydranencephaly/lissencephaly with abnormal genitalia). Loss-of-function ARX variants disrupt early neurodevelopment, abolishing formation of the prosencephalon and producing the aprosencephaly phenotype that characterises the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Family history assessment",
    "long_answer": "Tracing one’s genealogy allows the collection of a detailed family history, revealing how frequently type 2 diabetes and related metabolic disorders occur among blood relatives. Recognizing these familial patterns helps individuals gauge their inherited risk, prompts earlier glucose screening, and encourages preventive lifestyle changes—actions known to delay or mitigate type 2 diabetes progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Advanced age",
    "long_answer": "Increasing chronological age is a well-established risk factor for both conditions: older adults have markedly higher incidence rates of delirium—especially during acute illness or after surgery—and the probability of developing most cancers rises steeply with advancing age because of cumulative genetic and epigenetic damage and diminished repair capacity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC12A3",
    "long_answer": "Gitelman syndrome is classically produced by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC12A3 gene, which encodes the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter (NCC) of the distal convoluted tubule. Impaired NCC activity leads to renal salt wasting, hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, changes that precipitate the characteristic muscle cramps experienced by affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dopamine dysregulation",
    "long_answer": "In schizophrenia, loss of normal dopaminergic homeostasis leads to over-activity of dopamine transmission in the mesolimbic pathway and under-activity in the mesocortical pathway. The hyperdopaminergic state drives positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, while hypodopaminergia in prefrontal circuits contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. Thus, disruption of dopamine balance directly underlies the characteristic clinical manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutaneous mucinoses",
    "long_answer": "Lichen myxedematosus (also called papular mucinosis) is classified within the cutaneous mucinoses—a group of skin disorders characterized by abnormal dermal deposition of mucin (glycosaminoglycans) produced by fibroblasts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parvovirus B19",
    "long_answer": "Parvovirus B19 infection is a well-established viral cause of acute, often symmetric polyarthritis that can mimic rheumatoid arthritis, particularly in adults. Immune-complex formation during Parvovirus B19 viremia leads to inflammation of multiple joints, producing the clinical picture of polyarthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patau syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Patau syndrome, the clinical condition caused by trisomy of chromosome 13, is well-documented to produce multiple ocular malformations, including congenital cataracts. The extra copy of chromosome 13 disrupts normal ocular development, predisposing affected infants to lens opacities alongside other eye anomalies such as microphthalmia and coloboma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gemfibrozil",
    "long_answer": "Gemfibrozil is a fibrate antihyperlipidemic agent that activates the nuclear receptor PPAR-α, leading to up-regulation of extrahepatic lipoprotein lipase (LPL). Enhanced LPL activity accelerates the hydrolysis and clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins such as VLDL, thereby lowering plasma triglyceride levels and treating hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3-O-methyldopa",
    "long_answer": "Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC) deficiency blocks the decarboxylation of L-DOPA, leading to accumulation of its O-methylated product, 3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD), in blood and urine. Quantifying 3-OMD in dried blood spots or plasma reliably distinguishes affected individuals from healthy controls and is therefore used as a biochemical screening test for AADC deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose atropine",
    "long_answer": "Randomized controlled trials such as the ATOM1, ATOM2 and LAMP studies have shown that nightly administration of very low-concentration atropine eye drops (≈0.01–0.05%)—an antimuscarinic agent—reduces the rate of axial elongation and refractive change in school-aged children, thereby slowing the progression of myopia without the significant side-effects seen with full-strength atropine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "50 percent",
    "long_answer": "Studies evaluating the in-vivo intramuscular halothane injection test found that only about half of the people with a positive result were truly malignant-hyperthermia susceptible when confirmed by the gold-standard caffeine–halothane contracture test; hence its positive predictive value is roughly 50 %, leading to abandonment of the method for clinical diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Optic neuropathy is damage or dysfunction of the optic nerve that leads to visual loss. Disorders of the optic nerve are diagnosed and managed primarily by ophthalmologists—often those with subspecialty training in neuro-ophthalmology—because they specialize in diseases of the eye and its neural pathways."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography",
    "long_answer": "After a hip dislocation is reduced, the standard recommendation is to obtain a computed tomography (CT) scan of the pelvis/hip. Thin-section CT images confirm that the femoral head is concentrically reduced and allow detection of occult acetabular or femoral head fractures, intra-articular loose bodies, or other post-reduction complications more sensitively than plain radiographs. Therefore, CT is the imaging technique used to rule out complications following hip-dislocation reduction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis",
    "long_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis is the most common sexually transmitted pathogen responsible for cervicitis. The bacteria infect the columnar epithelial cells of the endocervix, triggering an inflammatory immune response that leads to cervical erythema, friability, and mucopurulent discharge. If untreated, chlamydial cervicitis can ascend, causing pelvic inflammatory disease and infertility, underscoring its clinical significance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mevalonate",
    "long_answer": "Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate. By blocking mevalonate formation—an essential early precursor in the biosynthetic pathway—statins suppress downstream cholesterol synthesis and thereby lower plasma cholesterol levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nitrofurantoin",
    "long_answer": "Nitrofurantoin is an antibiotic routinely prescribed for prophylaxis and treatment of urinary tract infections. A recognized adverse effect of this drug is a hypersensitivity-mediated pulmonary reaction that presents as acute or subacute eosinophilic pneumonia, with infiltrates, hypoxemia, and peripheral eosinophilia that typically resolve once nitrofurantoin is discontinued."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP1A3",
    "long_answer": "CAPOS syndrome (Cerebellar Ataxia, Areflexia, Pes cavus, Optic atrophy, Sensorineural hearing loss) is caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the ATP1A3 gene, which encodes the α3-subunit of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase. Dysfunction of this neuronal ion pump disrupts cerebellar and brainstem circuitry, producing cerebellar ataxia and the associated ataxic dysarthria observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "In Orofaciodigital syndrome type 1, central-nervous-system malformations are common, the most characteristic and variably present abnormality being agenesis or hypoplasia of the corpus callosum. Different individuals with OFD1 may show complete, partial, or no involvement of this midline cerebral structure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parietal bone vertex",
    "long_answer": "In Adams–Oliver syndrome, the aplasia cutis congenita presents as congenital scalp defects localized over the vertex of the skull, involving the overlying skin and underlying parietal bone in that region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Passive diffusion",
    "long_answer": "Dietary retinyl esters are hydrolyzed to free retinol, which enters mixed bile-salt micelles. The unesterified retinol then leaves the micelle and crosses the brush-border membrane of enterocytes by simple, concentration-dependent passive diffusion—no dedicated transporter or energy input is required—after which it is re-esterified and packaged into chylomicrons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mammary ridge",
    "long_answer": "During the sixth week of embryogenesis, bilateral ectodermal thickenings called the mammary ridges (milk lines) form and give rise to the mammary buds. If these mammary ridges fail to form or regress completely in the pectoral region, no mammary buds develop, resulting in complete absence of breast tissue, nipple, and areola— a condition termed amastia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sonic hedgehog",
    "long_answer": "The ZRS (zone of polarizing activity regulatory sequence) is a limb-specific enhancer located in an intron of the LMBR1 gene that controls transcription of the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) signaling molecule. Mutations or duplications within ZRS cause ectopic or elevated SHH expression during limb bud development, producing mirror-image or extra digits (polysyndactyly). Thus, SHH is the signaling molecule whose expression is regulated by the ZRS and whose mis-expression leads to polysyndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is a hereditary disorder of calcium-sensing receptor signaling that presents with lifelong mild hypercalcemia and inappropriately low urinary calcium. Evaluation of calcium, parathyroid hormone, and renal calcium excretion, along with genetic testing, falls within the expertise of an endocrinologist, the medical specialist who diagnoses and manages metabolic and parathyroid disorders. Therefore, the specialty most likely to diagnose this condition is Endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematologist",
    "long_answer": "Megaloblastic anemia is a blood disorder, and when it arises from the inborn error of pyrimidine synthesis known as orotic aciduria (UMP-synthase deficiency), management focuses on correcting the hematologic defect (e.g., giving uridine). Such evaluation and treatment fall within the expertise of a hematologist, the specialist who diagnoses and treats disorders of the blood and bone marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Long bones of the limbs",
    "long_answer": "Thanatophoric dysplasia is a lethal FGFR3-related skeletal disorder characterized by extreme micromelia—the long bones of the arms and legs are markedly shortened and bowed (the classic “telephone-receiver” femora). This profound limb involvement is the most conspicuous anatomical abnormality, leading to the dwarfing phenotype and distinguishing the condition clinically and radiologically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV) is a lysosomal storage disorder characterized by severe skeletal dysplasia, progressive airway obstruction, restrictive lung disease, and cervical spine instability. Most published natural-history series and reviews report that death usually results from: 1) progressive respiratory failure caused by upper-airway obstruction, tracheobronchomalacia, and restrictive lung mechanics; 2) cardiac or pulmonary complications such as cor pulmonale; or 3) catastrophic cervical spinal cord injury. Bacterial or viral infections can precipitate decompensation, but they are not cited as the leading, independent cause of death. Therefore, infection is not considered the primary cause of mortality in patients with Morquio syndrome according to established clinical data and expert consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Allogeneic bone marrow (hematopoietic stem-cell) transplantation can repopulate the patient with healthy donor-derived hematopoietic precursors, which then establish functional blood cell production even before full intramedullary space is restored. This treatment can correct the bone-marrow failure of infantile osteopetrosis by supplying osteoclast precursors and enabling effective hematopoiesis outside the patient’s sclerotic marrow cavities until normal marrow spaces reopen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oligodendrocytes",
    "long_answer": "In the central nervous system, myelin sheaths around axons are produced by oligodendrocytes. In Cockayne syndrome, neuropathological studies often show marked loss or absence of these myelin sheaths in cerebral white matter, reflecting dysfunction or loss of oligodendrocytes, the cells responsible for CNS myelination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acetaminophen (paracetamol)",
    "long_answer": "In Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome, supportive therapy focuses on hydration, skin care, and comfort measures. For fever control in children, the recommended antipyretic is acetaminophen (paracetamol) because it is effective, well-tolerated, and avoids the Reye-syndrome risk associated with aspirin and the potential renal effects of NSAIDs in dehydrated patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercalcemia",
    "long_answer": "Marked elevation of serum calcium depresses renal tubular responsiveness to vasopressin, decreasing aquaporin-2 insertion in the collecting duct and producing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with polyuria. The same hypercalcemic state can affect neuronal excitability, and very high calcium levels are a well-recognized metabolic trigger for generalized seizures. Thus hypercalcemia is the electrolyte imbalance that can cause both nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE gene",
    "long_answer": "The ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) gene has been one of the most intensively investigated loci in essential hypertension research. ACE encodes the enzyme that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor that elevates peripheral vascular resistance and stimulates aldosterone release. Increased ACE activity—whether from gene overexpression or common polymorphisms such as the I/D variant—raises angiotensin II levels and reliably elevates blood pressure, establishing its central role in essential hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endothelial dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "In HELLP syndrome, placental-derived factors damage maternal vascular endothelium, producing microangiopathic hemolysis, platelet consumption and organ ischemia. In acute kidney injury, ischemic or toxic insults first injure the renal microvascular endothelium, causing vasoconstriction, reduced perfusion and subsequent tubular injury. Therefore, injury and dysfunction of the vascular endothelium is the key pathogenic mechanism common to both HELLP syndrome and acute kidney injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Gerodermia osteodysplastica is an autosomal-recessive connective-tissue disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the GORAB gene (Golgi-associated Rab2 binding protein). The GORAB locus is situated on chromosome 1 at band 1q24, making chromosome 1 the chromosomal site associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the modality of choice for malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours because its excellent soft-tissue contrast allows precise localisation of the nerve-based mass, definition of its extent along the nerve and relationship to surrounding structures, thereby guiding diagnosis and surgical planning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Linkage analyses have mapped both autosomal-dominant familial eosinophilia and susceptibility loci for childhood asthma to the 5q31–q33 cytokine gene cluster on chromosome 5, which contains IL-3, IL-4, IL-5 and other Th2-related genes that drive eosinophilic inflammation and asthma pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urethral sphincter electromyography",
    "long_answer": "Fowler’s syndrome is characterized by complex, high-amplitude electrical discharges in the external urethral sphincter. These discharges are detected with urethral sphincter electromyography (EMG), performed using a concentric needle electrode placed in the sphincter muscle. The EMG test reveals the pathognomonic abnormal activity, making it the key diagnostic technique for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diverticulitis",
    "long_answer": "Diverticulitis, the inflammatory complication of diverticulosis, is linked to an increased incidence of adenocarcinoma in the same (usually left-sided/sigmoid) segment of colon. Because inflammation or an occult tumour can underlie or mimic diverticulitis, patients who develop diverticulitis are known to have a higher risk of left-sided colorectal cancer and are routinely advised to undergo colonoscopic surveillance after an attack."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cranial nerves VI and VII",
    "long_answer": "Möbius syndrome is defined by congenital palsy of the abducens (VI) and facial (VII) nerves. These two cranial nerves fail to develop fully—typically from hypoplasia or agenesis of their brain-stem nuclei—leading to impaired ocular abduction (VI) and bilateral facial paralysis (VII), the hallmark clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scapula",
    "long_answer": "Frank–Ter Haar syndrome (caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in SH3PXD2B) produces a distinctive skeletal dysplasia; among its radiographic hallmarks are marked abnormalities of the scapulae, which are enlarged, dysplastic and hook-shaped at the acromion. The shoulder blades are therefore one of the most prominently and characteristically affected bones in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CT angiography",
    "long_answer": "Contrast-enhanced computed tomography angiography (CTA) provides high-resolution images of both vascular structures and the skull base. In cases of carotid agenesis it shows complete absence of the carotid lumen and the corresponding bony carotid canal, making CTA a reliable, widely adopted tool for diagnosing this congenital anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pancreatic β-cells",
    "long_answer": "Insulinomas are neuroendocrine tumours that arise from the β-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. β-cells are the endocrine cells that physiologically synthesize and secrete insulin; when they undergo neoplastic transformation, they overproduce insulin, leading to the hypoglycaemia characteristic of insulinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "During pregnancy, high-resolution prenatal ultrasonography can reveal the characteristic constellation of findings seen in fetuses with trisomy 9—such as severe intra-uterine growth restriction, craniofacial and limb malformations, cardiac defects and cystic hygroma. Detection of these structural anomalies on prenatal ultrasound raises suspicion for trisomy 9 and leads to confirmatory cytogenetic testing (e.g., amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Collins–Pope syndrome has been described in affected siblings born to clinically normal parents, with no vertical transmission, a pattern that requires both copies of the causative gene to be mutated for the phenotype to appear. This pattern is characteristic of an autosomal-recessive genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midbrain",
    "long_answer": "Progressive supranuclear palsy is a 4-repeat tauopathy in which hyper-phosphorylated tau aggregates build up predominantly in neurons and glia of the midbrain (especially the subthalamic nucleus, substantia nigra and related brain-stem structures). Degeneration of this midbrain region produces the vertical gaze palsy and postural instability characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior fossa",
    "long_answer": "Dandy–Walker malformation features hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle. The dilated ventricle expands backward, so cerebrospinal fluid accumulates and markedly enlarges the posterior cranial fossa—the space at the back of the skull that normally houses the cerebellum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heightened ototoxicity risk",
    "long_answer": "Aminoglycosides bind to mitochondrial 12S rRNA, inhibiting mitochondrial protein synthesis. In individuals with pre-existing mitochondrial defects (e.g., m.1555A>G mutation), this greatly worsens mitochondrial dysfunction in cochlear hair cells, producing rapid, irreversible sensorineural hearing loss. Therefore, aminoglycosides are avoided because they carry an unusually high risk of ototoxicity in patients with mitochondrial diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive inheritance",
    "long_answer": "SCARF syndrome is believed to follow an autosomal-recessive pattern, meaning both healthy parents can carry one mutated allele and have a 25 % chance of having another affected child in each pregnancy. Genetic counseling helps identify carrier status, explain this recurrence risk, and discuss options such as prenatal or pre-implantation testing, enabling informed reproductive decisions for families with a history of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "The DSM-5 defines obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) as a Cluster C personality disorder marked by pervasive preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, mental and interpersonal control, and rigidity at the expense of flexibility and efficiency. Maladaptive perfectionism—striving for flawlessness, excessive self-criticism, and reluctance to delegate—is a core clinical feature and a key reason OCPD is strongly linked to dysfunctional perfectionistic traits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crush injury",
    "long_answer": "Severe crush injury to skeletal (striated) muscle causes extensive muscle fiber necrosis (rhabdomyolysis), releasing large amounts of the oxygen-binding protein myoglobin into the circulation. Because myoglobin is freely filtered by the glomerulus, the excess load enters the urine, producing characteristic myoglobinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandible",
    "long_answer": "In autosomal dominant and recessive forms of osteopetrosis, the dense, poorly vascularized bone is prone to infection; approximately 10 % of patients develop osteomyelitis of the mandible, usually precipitated by dental infections or extractions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burning",
    "long_answer": "Dermatitis often produces a burning sensation in the inflamed skin. This “burning” is not only listed among the classic symptoms of contact, atopic, and other forms of dermatitis, but it is also classified in pain medicine as a specific quality of pain (burning pain), typically mediated by nociceptive C-fibres activated during cutaneous inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RELN",
    "long_answer": "Norman–Roberts syndrome, also designated lissencephaly-2 (LIS2), is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the RELN gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein reelin. Reelin is critical for proper neuronal migration and layering of the cerebral cortex and cerebellum; its absence disrupts these processes, giving rise to the characteristic lissencephaly with cerebellar hypoplasia that defines the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rh(D) alloimmunization",
    "long_answer": "Hydrops fetalis can be immune‐mediated when maternal antibodies destroy fetal red cells. The only immune form that is effectively preventable is Rh(D) alloimmunization (Rh incompatibility) between an Rh-negative mother and an Rh-positive fetus. Administration of anti-D IgG to Rh-negative women during pregnancy and after delivery prevents formation of maternal anti-D antibodies, thereby averting hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn and the development of hydrops fetalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HFE gene testing",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis is almost always caused by pathogenic variants—most commonly C282Y and H63D—in the HFE gene. Molecular testing of the HFE gene in peripheral blood can identify these mutations and definitively confirm the diagnosis in a patient who already shows biochemical iron overload and has a suggestive family history of liver disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giant cell arteritis and Takayasu arteritis",
    "long_answer": "Large vessel vasculitis predominantly affects the aorta and its major branches. According to international vasculitis classifications (e.g., the 2012 Chapel Hill Consensus Conference), the two prototypic and most common entities in this category are giant cell arteritis (affecting older adults, especially the temporal arteries) and Takayasu arteritis (occurring in younger individuals and involving the aorta and its branches)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metronidazole",
    "long_answer": "Alfred Donné first described the protozoan parasite Trichomonas vaginalis in 1836, the causative agent of trichomoniasis. The standard treatment for this infection is the nitroimidazole antibiotic metronidazole, which is highly effective because, once reduced inside the anaerobic parasite, it generates reactive intermediates that damage DNA and other critical biomolecules, eradicating the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (45,X) is classically associated with congenital cystic hygroma. The underlying lymphatic maldevelopment causes fluid accumulation, leading to marked nuchal cystic hygromas and peripheral lymphedema (edema of the hands and feet) in affected fetuses and newborns. Thus, edema is a characteristic symptom of Turner syndrome in the context of cystic hygroma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Co-inheritance of HFE-related hereditary hemochromatosis causes abnormally increased intestinal iron absorption. When a patient with hereditary spherocytosis also carries pathogenic HFE mutations (e.g., C282Y or H63D), the added iron uptake combines with the iron released from chronic hemolysis and any transfusional iron, leading to clinically significant iron overload."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dopamine",
    "long_answer": "Dopamine signaling is central to both conditions: hyperactivity of mesolimbic dopamine pathways is a well-established mechanism underlying psychotic symptoms, while reduced dopaminergic transmission, particularly in mesocortical and striatal circuits, is linked to the anhedonia, psychomotor retardation and other features of melancholic depression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Collecting duct",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 (distal) renal tubular acidosis results from impaired H⁺ secretion by the α-intercalated cells in the cortical collecting duct. This segment of the nephron is also a major site for potassium handling—principal cells there secrete K⁺ under aldosterone control—so dysfunction of the collecting duct disrupts both acid–base balance and potassium regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibula",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple exostoses (multiple osteochondromas) produce osteocartilaginous outgrowths near the growth plates of long bones. In the leg, these lesions most often involve and stunt growth of the fibula, making the fibula the bone classically associated with this inherited disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wilms tumor",
    "long_answer": "Hemihyperplasia (formerly called hemihypertrophy) is a congenital over-growth disorder that confers a markedly higher risk of embryonal renal tumors, most notably Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma). Because of this well-documented association, children with isolated hemihyperplasia undergo periodic abdominal ultrasonography to screen specifically for Wilms tumor during early childhood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epidermolytic hyperkeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Killer Croc is portrayed as having been born with a mutation that makes his skin thicken into dark, plate-like scales reminiscent of a reptile. The real-world disorder that produces very similar hard, hyperkeratotic, ‘crocodile-like’ scaling—and is classified within the ichthyoses—is epidermolytic hyperkeratosis, a keratin-gene (KRT1/KRT10) mutation resulting in congenital ichthyosis and extensive hyperkeratosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PKD1 or PKD2 genes cause autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). The numerous renal cysts and distorted collecting system produced by ADPKD promote urinary stasis and stone formation, creating a favorable environment for bacterial growth. Consequently, individuals with ADPKD have a well-documented, significantly higher incidence of recurrent urinary tract and cyst infections compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alexander disease",
    "long_answer": "Alexander disease is a rare hereditary leukodystrophy produced by dominant-acting mutations in the gene encoding glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP). These mutations disrupt intermediate filament assembly in astrocytes, leading to Rosenthal fiber accumulation and progressive demyelination characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Ascariasis, caused by the intestinal roundworm Ascaris lumbricoides, is one of the most common soil-transmitted helminth infections worldwide. According to the World Health Organization and numerous epidemiologic studies, high prevalences are found in regions with warm, humid climates and inadequate sanitation—conditions that occur widely throughout much of Latin America and the Caribbean. Surveys from countries such as Brazil, Bolivia, Peru, Guatemala, and Honduras consistently demonstrate infection rates ranging from 10 % to over 50 % in school-age children, and the region is included in WHO mass drug-administration (MDA) programs targeting soil-transmitted helminths. Because of these documented burdens, Latin America is recognized as one of the geographic areas heavily affected by ascariasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cranial Doppler ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "In neonates and young infants the open fontanelle allows cranial (trans-fontanellar) Doppler ultrasonography to show a midline cystic structure with turbulent, high-velocity flow characteristic of a Vein of Galen aneurysmal malformation. Because it is rapid, bedside, and non-invasive, this Doppler ultrasound technique is particularly useful for diagnosing VGAM in infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Septic shock",
    "long_answer": "Septic shock is a severe, life-threatening condition that develops when a severe infection triggers a systemic inflammatory response; persistent tachycardia is one of the compensatory cardiovascular signs. The combination of a serious infection with marked tachycardia reflects systemic vasodilation, capillary leak, and inadequate tissue perfusion that characterize septic shock."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Jansky–Bielschowsky disease, a late-infantile form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in genes such as CLN2 (TPP1). Because the disorder manifests only when both alleles are mutated and carriers are typically unaffected, it follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NR0B1",
    "long_answer": "X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita is caused by mutations in the NR0B1 gene (also called DAX-1) on chromosome Xp21. NR0B1 encodes a nuclear receptor that regulates transcriptional programs essential for proper development and function of the adrenal cortex and the gonads; loss-of-function mutations therefore impair adrenal formation and disrupt ovarian (and testicular) development, producing the characteristic clinical picture of X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skeletal muscle",
    "long_answer": "Benign fasciculation syndrome is characterized by involuntary muscle twitches (fasciculations) that arise in voluntary, striated muscles—such as those of the limbs, face, and trunk. These twitches originate in the lower motor neuron units innervating skeletal muscle fibers; neither smooth nor cardiac muscle is involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Lucey-Driscoll syndrome (transient familial neonatal hyperbilirubinemia) is produced by loss-of-function variants in the UGT1A1 gene that are inherited on both parental alleles. Because two defective copies are required to impair bilirubin conjugation and cause the neonatal jaundice phenotype, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Oculocutaneous albinism frequently causes ocular abnormalities such as nystagmus, reduced visual acuity, photophobia, and foveal hypoplasia. The medical specialty dedicated to diagnosing and managing disorders of the eye and visual system is ophthalmology; ophthalmologists evaluate and treat these vision problems in albinism patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lenz-Majewski syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Lenz-Majewski syndrome is a very rare skeletal dysplasia marked by generalized progressive hyperostosis and craniofacial overgrowth. Genetic studies have shown that it is caused by heterozygous gain-of-function missense mutations in the PTDSS1 gene, which encodes phosphatidylserine synthase 1. These mutations increase phosphatidylserine synthase activity, leading to the characteristic bone overgrowth seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic capillary leak syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Systemic capillary leak syndrome (SCLS, Clarkson disease) is a rare disorder marked by sudden, massive leakage of plasma from the vascular space, leading to hypotension, hemoconcentration, and hypo-albuminemia. Because its presentation mimics septic shock, it is frequently misdiagnosed as sepsis. Infectious triggers, including viral hemorrhagic fevers such as dengue and Ebola, are well-documented precipitants of acute SCLS attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia (also termed cryptogenic organizing pneumonia) is one of the idiopathic interstitial pneumonias. It is classified within the spectrum of interstitial lung disease because its primary pathology involves organizing fibrous tissue in the alveolar ducts, alveoli, and surrounding interstitium, leading to a diffuse, restrictive, ILD-type pattern on imaging and pulmonary function testing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatomegaly",
    "long_answer": "In mucolipidosis type I (sialidosis), deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme neuraminidase leads to accumulation of sialyl-oligosaccharides in multiple organs, including the liver. This storage causes enlargement of the liver—clinically recognized as hepatomegaly—which is one of the characteristic systemic signs of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked ABCD1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the X-linked ABCD1 gene disable a peroxisomal membrane transporter needed to β-oxidize very-long-chain fatty acids. Their accumulation in neural tissue produces adrenoleukodystrophy, wherein excess fatty acids trigger inflammatory demyelination and progressive destruction of the myelin sheath."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SF3B4",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome (acrofacial dysostosis, Nager type) is most frequently caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SF3B4 gene, which encodes the splicing factor 3B subunit 4 located on chromosome 1q21. Variants in SF3B4 disrupt pre-mRNA splicing, leading to the craniofacial, limb, and other developmental anomalies characteristic of the syndrome. Pathogenic SF3B4 mutations are identified in the majority of molecularly confirmed Nager syndrome cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "12-lead ECG",
    "long_answer": "In Kounis syndrome the presence of myocardial ischemia is demonstrated with a 12-lead electrocardiogram; ST-segment and T-wave abnormalities (or new Q waves) on the ECG provide objective confirmation that ischemia is occurring in the setting of the allergic reaction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid hormone",
    "long_answer": "A deficiency of thyroid hormones (hypothyroidism) is well documented to produce neuropsychiatric manifestations, including anxiety. Reduced thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) impair central nervous system metabolism and neurotransmitter balance, leading many hypothyroid patients to experience anxiety, irritability or panic that improve when thyroid hormone levels are restored."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain radiography",
    "long_answer": "Subungual exostosis is confirmed with a simple plain X-ray of the affected digit, which clearly demonstrates the pedunculated bony outgrowth arising from the dorsal surface of the distal phalanx, making conventional radiography the standard diagnostic imaging technique."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Connective tissue disorder",
    "long_answer": "De Barsy syndrome is a hereditary form of cutis laxa characterized by defective elastic fibers and lax skin, classifying it as a connective tissue disorder. Repetitive strain injuries (also called cumulative trauma disorders) involve chronic damage to tendons, ligaments, and other soft connective tissues caused by overuse. Thus, both conditions fall under the category of connective tissue disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenytoin",
    "long_answer": "The anticonvulsant phenytoin can produce nystagmus as a dose-related adverse effect. At therapeutic concentrations it typically causes horizontal nystagmus, but when serum levels become toxic (generally >20–30 µg/mL) vestibulo-cerebellar dysfunction worsens and vertical nystagmus may appear. The presence of vertical nystagmus is therefore a recognized clinical sign of phenytoin overdose or toxicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phasmophobia",
    "long_answer": "The persistent, excessive, and irrational fear of ghosts or supernatural beings is termed phasmophobia, derived from the Greek “phasma” (apparition) and “phobos” (fear). It is classified as a specific phobia within anxiety disorders, characterized by marked fear and avoidance of situations or stimuli related to ghosts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate carboxylase cofactor",
    "long_answer": "Biotin is required as a covalently bound coenzyme for pyruvate carboxylase, the mitochondrial enzyme that catalyzes the ATP-dependent carboxylation of pyruvate to oxaloacetate—the first committed step of gluconeogenesis. Without biotin, this carboxylation reaction cannot proceed, blocking formation of oxaloacetate and thereby preventing the gluconeogenic pathway from synthesizing glucose."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital heart disease",
    "long_answer": "More than 90 % of infants with Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome) are born with serious congenital heart defects—most commonly ventricular septal defects, atrial septal defects, and patent ductus arteriosus. The resulting cardiac failure is the leading cause of early mortality in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis, a prototypical form of axial spondyloarthritis, produces chronic inflammation and ossification of the spine. Progressive enthesitis and syndesmophyte formation fuse the vertebrae, reducing spinal flexibility and abolishing the normal inward curvature of the lower back. This leads to a characteristic flattening or loss of the lumbar lordosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenocopy",
    "long_answer": "A phenocopy is a trait or malformation produced entirely by environmental factors that reproduces the appearance of a heritable genetic disorder. Maternal exposure to warfarin, for example, can lead to skeletal changes that closely resemble the genetic disorder X-linked recessive chondrodysplasia punctata, even though the underlying genotype is normal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningioma",
    "long_answer": "A meningioma, a usually benign tumor arising from arachnoid cap cells of the meninges, often infiltrates or stimulates the adjacent calvarial bone. This provokes marked hyperostosis and thickening of the involved skull segment, producing conspicuous focal enlargement—sometimes described radiologically and clinically as ‘local gigantism’ of the skull."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ichthyosis–deafness–intellectual disability syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ichthyosis–deafness–intellectual disability syndrome (IDMR) is an autosomal-recessive disorder whose core triad is congenital ichthyosis, profound sensorineural deafness and moderate-to-severe intellectual disability. Reported cases frequently show additional skeletal anomalies, notably fifth-finger clinodactyly, along with short stature, nail dystrophy and seizures, making clinodactyly a recognized component of the syndrome’s phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Lenz microphthalmia syndrome is transmitted in an X-linked recessive manner: affected males inherit the pathogenic allele from carrier mothers, while heterozygous females are usually unaffected or only mildly affected, consistent with classic X-linked recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synovial sarcoma",
    "long_answer": "The chromosomal translocation t(X;18)(p11.2;q11.2) creates an SS18-SSX fusion gene that is pathognomonic for synovial sarcoma, a malignant soft-tissue tumor. This genetic hallmark is used diagnostically, and standard management often combines wide surgical excision with adjuvant or neoadjuvant radiation therapy, to which the tumor is relatively radiosensitive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human chorionic gonadotropin",
    "long_answer": "In prepubertal children, a diagnostic hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) stimulation test is performed: exogenous hCG provokes Leydig‐cell production of testosterone and its 5α-reduced product dihydrotestosterone (DHT). In 5α-reductase 2 deficiency, DHT rises inadequately and the post-stimulation testosterone-to-DHT ratio becomes markedly elevated, confirming the diagnosis. Thus, the hormone used for diagnosis is human chorionic gonadotropin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal lobe",
    "long_answer": "Intracranial meningiomas most frequently arise along the parasagittal or convexity dura over the frontal region; consequently, the frontal lobe is the lobe most commonly associated with these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "McArdle’s disease (glycogen storage disease type V) is caused by biallelic mutations in the PYGM gene, which encodes the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase. The PYGM gene is mapped to chromosome 11, band q13, establishing chromosome 11 as the chromosomal location associated with McArdle’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMP2",
    "long_answer": "Winchester syndrome is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MMP2 gene, which encodes matrix metalloproteinase-2. In addition to progressive osteolysis and joint contractures, MMP2 deficiency frequently produces cutaneous changes such as patchy or diffuse hyperpigmentation, linking the same gene mutation to both Winchester syndrome and skin hyperpigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "The photosensitive form of trichothiodystrophy results from mutations in the ERCC2 gene, also known as XPD. ERCC2 encodes a helicase subunit of the TFIIH complex essential for both nucleotide-excision DNA repair and transcription initiation; loss-of-function mutations impair DNA repair of UV-induced damage, leading to cutaneous photosensitivity characteristic of this TTD subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endovascular coiling",
    "long_answer": "Randomized controlled trials such as ISAT and BRAT consistently show that aneurysms treated with endovascular coiling recur (or require retreatment) significantly more often than those treated with microsurgical clipping, making coiling the modality with the higher recurrence rate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corah Dental Anxiety Scale",
    "long_answer": "The Corah Dental Anxiety Scale (DAS) is a validated four-item psychometric instrument designed to quantify the intensity of dental fear and anxiety. By scoring patients’ responses to common dental scenarios on a 1–5 scale, it yields a total score from 4 (no anxiety) to 20 (severe anxiety), providing an objective measure of how fearful or anxious a person is about dental treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurotoxicity",
    "long_answer": "Bites from several elapid snakes (e.g., cobras, kraits, coral snakes, mambas) can produce neurotoxicity: their venoms contain presynaptic and postsynaptic neurotoxins that block neuromuscular transmission, causing progressive paralysis and potentially respiratory failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) alters craniofacial anatomy: mid-face hypoplasia and short, horizontally oriented Eustachian tubes impair ventilation and mucous clearance from the middle ear and contiguous sinuses. This poor drainage predisposes affected individuals to recurrent otitis media and chronic/recurrent sinus infections, making Down syndrome the well-established genetic condition linked to this clinical problem."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ETV6–NTRK3 fusion",
    "long_answer": "The cellular variant of congenital mesoblastic nephroma typically harbors the chromosomal translocation t(12;15)(p13;q25) that creates the ETV6–NTRK3 fusion gene. This fusion constitutively activates the NTRK3 tyrosine-kinase domain, driving tumorigenesis. Larotrectinib, a selective pan-TRK inhibitor, has produced dramatic and durable responses in multiple solid tumors bearing NTRK fusions, including those with the ETV6–NTRK3 rearrangement, confirming the clinical relevance of this target."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Third-hand hypothesis",
    "long_answer": "The third-hand hypothesis proposes that early hominins used their anterior teeth like an extra hand or vise to hold, pull or process materials (e.g., hides, plant fibres). This chronic non-dietary loading would increase functional stress on the tooth roots, stimulating compensatory cementum deposition and thus explaining the marked hypercementosis seen in some prehistoric dentitions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MCPH1 (Microcephalin)",
    "long_answer": "MCPH1 encodes microcephalin, a centrosomal DNA-damage–response protein whose loss-of-function mutations cause primary autosomal-recessive microcephaly. Independent candidate-gene and genome-wide studies have also shown that common variants and copy-number changes in MCPH1 are associated with increased risk of schizophrenia, making it a gene linked to both microcephaly and schizophrenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Truncating PKHD1 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Patients who carry at least one truncating mutation (nonsense or frameshift) in the PKHD1 gene tend to present with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease in the prenatal or perinatal period, indicating an earlier and more severe disease onset compared with individuals who harbor only missense variants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Short-limb skeletal dysplasia associated with combined immunodeficiency (e.g., cartilage-hair hypoplasia or Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia) is produced by biallelic pathogenic variants in the responsible gene, so it is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NUAK2",
    "long_answer": "Bruno Reversade’s team showed that individuals carrying biallelic (homozygous) loss-of-function mutations in the serine/threonine kinase gene NUAK2 lack NUAK2 activity, which impairs actomyosin-mediated apical constriction during neural tube closure. This failure to close the cranial neural tube produces the lethal neural tube defect anencephaly in humans, establishing NUAK2 inactivation as a genetic cause of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Capsular contracture",
    "long_answer": "After breast implantation, the body forms a fibrous capsule around the prosthesis. In some patients this capsule progressively tightens and thickens, compressing the implant and making the breast feel firm, distorted, or painful—a complication known as capsular contracture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syncytiotrophoblastic barrier",
    "long_answer": "The continuous syncytiotrophoblast layer of the placenta forms a physical and immunological barrier that keeps maternal blood in the intervillous space from directly contacting fetal capillaries, thereby largely blocking hepatitis B virus passage and preventing most in-utero transmission to the fetus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EEC syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor crucial for ectodermal development, cause EEC (Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal Dysplasia-Cleft lip/palate) syndrome. These p63 mutations disrupt limb, skin, hair, nail, and craniofacial formation, producing the characteristic triad of limb malformations (ectrodactyly), ectodermal dysplasia, and clefting defects that define the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsense mutation",
    "long_answer": "Abruzzo–Erickson syndrome (the X-linked cleft-palate/ankyloglossia disorder) is produced by loss-of-function lesions in the TBX22 gene. In the pedigrees first characterised, sequencing showed a premature stop-codon (nonsense) change in TBX22, which truncates the T-box transcription factor and eliminates its normal activity. This nonsense mutation is the canonical molecular lesion associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plateletpheresis",
    "long_answer": "In life-threatening or acute settings of essential thrombocythemia, therapeutic plateletpheresis (thrombocytapheresis) is performed to physically remove circulating platelets. This extracorporeal apheresis procedure can lower the platelet count by hundreds of thousands per microliter within hours, providing rapid cytoreduction while more durable pharmacologic treatments (e.g., hydroxyurea) take effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hormone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Many girls and women with tetrasomy X develop premature ovarian failure, leading to estrogen deficiency. Standard care is to prescribe hormone replacement therapy—typically cyclical estrogen with or without progesterone—to induce and maintain secondary sexual characteristics, regulate menses, protect bone mineral density, and alleviate hypo-estrogenic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Chédiak–Higashi syndrome have defective neutrophil lysosomal trafficking, leading to impaired killing of catalase-positive pyogenic bacteria. Clinical series consistently show that Staphylococcus aureus is the pathogen isolated most frequently in their recurrent skin, soft-tissue, and respiratory infections, making it the commonest infectious cause in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GM2 ganglioside",
    "long_answer": "Tay–Sachs disease is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme β-hexosaminidase A, which normally degrades GM2 ganglioside in neuronal lysosomes. When the enzyme is absent or defective, GM2 ganglioside accumulates within neurons, leading to the neurodegeneration characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTCP",
    "long_answer": "Hepatitis B virus gains entry into human hepatocytes by binding the Sodium Taurocholate Co-transporting Polypeptide (NTCP, gene SLC10A1) on the cell surface. Structural and micrographic studies have shown the HBV pre-S1 domain engaging NTCP, confirming it as the functional receptor mediating viral attachment and internalization into liver cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The FMR1 (fragile X mental retardation 1) gene is situated on the long arm of the human X chromosome at band Xq27.3. Mutations or CGG-repeat expansions in this locus cause fragile X syndrome, underscoring its X-linked inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bright light therapy",
    "long_answer": "In sighted patients, non-24-hour sleep–wake disorder is best treated by scheduled exposure to high-intensity morning light (bright light therapy), which acts as a powerful zeitgeber to entrain the circadian clock to the 24-hour day."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutropenia",
    "long_answer": "Felty's syndrome is classically characterized by the triad of long-standing rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia. The enlarged spleen sequesters and destroys neutrophils, leading to persistently low circulating neutrophil counts. This neutropenia is the hallmark hematologic abnormality in patients with Felty's syndrome and predisposes them to recurrent infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastric bypass surgery",
    "long_answer": "Patients who have undergone gastric bypass (bariatric) surgery are at increased risk for nutritional optic neuropathy because postoperative malabsorption can lead to deficiencies of vitamin B12, folate, thiamine, and other micronutrients essential for optic-nerve mitochondrial function. Without supplementation, these deficiencies can damage the papillomacular bundle and produce the bilateral, painless vision loss characteristic of nutritional optic neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caffeine–halothane contracture test",
    "long_answer": "The definitive diagnostic procedure for malignant hyperthermia susceptibility is the caffeine–halothane contracture test (CHCT). A fresh skeletal-muscle biopsy is exposed to caffeine and halothane in vitro; excessive contracture in response to these triggering agents indicates an abnormal ryanodine receptor–mediated calcium release and confirms susceptibility to malignant hyperthermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Potter syndrome (the oligohydramnios sequence) occurs when severe loss of amniotic fluid—classically from bilateral renal agenesis—compresses the fetus. The resulting ‘Potter facies’ include a broad, flattened (bulky) nose, low-set dysplastic or malformed ears, epicanthal folds and micrognathia, along with limb contractures and pulmonary hypoplasia. Thus, the syndrome marked by a bulky, flattened nose and malformed ears is Potter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Renal dysplasia-limb defects syndrome (also called Ulbright–Hodes syndrome) has been reported almost exclusively in siblings born to unaffected, usually non-consanguineous parents, with no vertical transmission. This recurrence among siblings and absence in parents is the classical pattern for autosomal recessive inheritance, which is the consensus classification given in OMIM and other medical genetics references."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysosome",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses are lysosomal storage disorders caused by inherited deficiencies of specific lysosomal hydrolases that normally degrade glycosaminoglycans. When these enzymes are absent or inactive, undegraded mucopolysaccharides accumulate within lysosomes, disrupting cellular function. Thus, malfunction of the lysosome is central to the pathogenesis of all mucopolysaccharidoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phospholamban",
    "long_answer": "In cardiac muscle, calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum by the SERCA2a Ca²⁺-ATPase. The small trans-membrane protein phospholamban binds to SERCA2a in its dephosphorylated state and lowers the pump’s affinity for Ca²⁺, thereby inhibiting calcium uptake. Phosphorylation of phospholamban by PKA relieves this inhibition, demonstrating its regulatory role."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TEK (TIE2) gene",
    "long_answer": "Harvard Medical School ophthalmic geneticist Dr. Janey L. Wiggs and colleagues discovered that loss-of-function mutations in the TEK (also called TIE2) receptor tyrosine-kinase gene cause primary congenital glaucoma. Their work showed that TEK mutations disrupt Schlemm’s canal development and aqueous outflow, firmly linking TEK gene defects to the pathogenesis of primary congenital glaucoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "Evans syndrome is characterized by autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia that arise from loss of self-tolerance. Studies show defective regulation of T lymphocytes—particularly helper and regulatory T cells—leads to aberrant activation of autoreactive B cells and production of pathogenic anti-erythrocyte and anti-platelet antibodies. Thus, dysregulated T lymphocytes are the key white-blood-cell population driving the immune disturbance in Evans syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Physiotherapist",
    "long_answer": "Orthostatic intolerance is often managed with graded exercise, tilt-table training, and conditioning programs that improve venous return and autonomic regulation. These interventions are typically planned and supervised by a physiotherapist (physical therapist), the rehabilitation specialist trained to design exercise and positional therapies for circulatory and autonomic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Y chromosome microdeletion",
    "long_answer": "Microdeletions in the AZF (azoospermia factor) regions of the Y chromosome cause Y chromosome microdeletion, a genetic disorder that disrupts spermatogenesis and leads to severe oligospermia or complete azoospermia (near-absence of sperm) in affected men."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cohen syndrome is indeed frequently associated with neutropenia; affected individuals often have persistently or intermittently low absolute neutrophil counts. However, by definition neutropenia is characterized by an abnormally LOW number of neutrophils (a type of white blood cell), not a high number. Therefore the statement, which links neutropenia both to Cohen syndrome and to an abnormally high white-blood-cell count, is partially incorrect and overall not accurate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest computed tomography (CT)",
    "long_answer": "A chest CT scan can distinguish a hemothorax from other pleural effusions because it quantifies the X-ray attenuation of the pleural fluid in Hounsfield units (HU). Acute blood has a higher density (roughly 30–70 HU) than serous or transudative effusions (usually <10–20 HU). Measuring this attenuation on CT images therefore allows clinicians to identify a hemothorax reliably and separate it from non-bloody effusions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endothelial tissue",
    "long_answer": "Deep-soft-tissue angiosarcomas originate from the endothelial tissue that lines blood (and lymphatic) vessels; malignant transformation of these vascular endothelial cells gives rise to the tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Todd's paralysis",
    "long_answer": "After a seizure, some patients develop Todd's paralysis—a transient, post-ictal neurological deficit. Because the cortical region that governs language can be temporarily exhausted, Todd's paralysis may manifest as aphasia or other speech deficits that usually resolve within hours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ataxia",
    "long_answer": "In scrapie-affected sheep, degeneration of central nervous system neurons leads to proprioceptive deficits, producing ataxia—an uncoordinated, staggering gait that is a hallmark clinical sign of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "α-intercalated (Type A) cells",
    "long_answer": "Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis arises when the α-intercalated, or Type A, cells of the cortical collecting duct fail to secrete hydrogen ions via the H+-ATPase/H+-K+-ATPase pumps. This impairment prevents acidification of the urine, leading to systemic metabolic acidosis characteristic of distal RTA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Barrett’s esophagus is an intestinal‐type columnar metaplasia of the distal esophageal squamous mucosa caused by chronic gastro-esophageal reflux. This metaplastic epithelium follows a well-defined dysplasia–carcinoma sequence that markedly increases the risk of developing esophageal adenocarcinoma, the principal malignancy arising in Barrett’s esophagus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma",
    "long_answer": "Among the palmoplantar keratodermas, the punctate (also called papular) form is characterized by numerous small keratotic papules that often have overlying scale on the palms and soles. This clinicopathologic pattern distinguishes it from the diffuse or focal types, which present with uniform thickening or pressure-site plaques rather than discrete papules with scales."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RECQL4",
    "long_answer": "RAPADILINO syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the RECQL4 gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase. Defective RECQL4 impairs DNA replication and repair, leading to growth retardation; affected individuals characteristically present with marked short stature in addition to the limb and skeletal anomalies that define RAPADILINO. Thus, RECQL4 mutation explains both the syndrome and its associated short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C19orf12",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the C19orf12 gene, which encodes a mitochondrial membrane protein, are the established cause of mitochondrial membrane protein-associated neurodegeneration (MPAN), a form of neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation. The same C19orf12 variants have been identified in patients originally diagnosed with Behr syndrome (optic atrophy with spastic paraparesis and other neurologic signs), demonstrating that this gene links the two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxic shock syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus can produce superantigen exotoxins such as TSST-1, which trigger toxic shock syndrome. This fulminant condition causes widespread cytokine release, leading to systemic inflammation, hypotension and ischemia, and is a well-recognized precipitant of multiple organ dysfunction syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis causes chronic cholestasis, reducing bile delivery to the intestine. Inadequate bile impairs micelle formation and absorption of fat, leading to poor uptake of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K; hence patients often require supplemental vitamins to prevent deficiencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Saint Gertrude of Nivelles",
    "long_answer": "Saint Gertrude of Nivelles (7th-century abbess) has been traditionally invoked to protect people and homes against infestations of rats and mice. Because of this longstanding association with repelling rodents, devotional practice also designates her as the patron saint for individuals who suffer from musophobia—the pathological fear of mice or rats."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cisplatin",
    "long_answer": "Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapeutic agent used against a broad range of solid tumours—including testicular, ovarian, lung, bladder, and head-and-neck cancers. Its dose-limiting toxicity is nephrotoxicity: the drug accumulates in renal proximal tubular epithelial cells, induces oxidative stress, inflammation and apoptosis, and can precipitate acute kidney injury (AKI) in cancer patients. Because this risk is well documented, aggressive IV hydration and renal monitoring are standard when cisplatin is administered."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric surgery",
    "long_answer": "Splenogonadal fusion-limb defects-micrognathia syndrome is a congenital malformation complex that typically requires operative correction of splenogonadal fusion/cryptorchidism and often additional procedures for associated limb anomalies. Surgical management in infants and children is coordinated by specialists trained in operative care of congenital anomalies—pediatric surgeons—making pediatric surgery the specialty most directly responsible for treating affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Joint hypermobility syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In joint hypermobility syndrome (also called benign joint hypermobility syndrome), the connective-tissue laxity leaves ligaments too weak to stabilise joints. To keep the joints aligned, surrounding muscles must contract almost constantly, and this chronic compensatory activity produces premature muscle fatigue and aching."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR on Xp11.4",
    "long_answer": "Oculofaciocardiodental (OFCD) syndrome is an X-linked dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the BCOR gene. BCOR (BCL6 co-repressor) resides on the short arm of the X chromosome at cytogenetic band Xp11.4; pathogenic mutations at this locus disrupt transcriptional repression during embryogenesis, producing the characteristic ocular, facial, cardiac and dental anomalies of OFCD syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pick’s disease",
    "long_answer": "In the mid-1990s Robert W. Floyd was diagnosed with Pick’s disease, a frontotemporal dementia characterized by progressive degeneration of neurons in the frontal and temporal lobes. This neurodegenerative disorder gradually impairs behavior, language and cognition, and it was the condition from which Floyd suffered during the last years of his life until his death in 2001."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 50–60 years",
    "long_answer": "Salla disease, the milder form of free sialic acid storage disease, usually permits survival well into adulthood; most affected individuals live beyond 40 years and many reach their fifth or sixth decade, giving an approximate life expectancy of 50–60 years—only slightly reduced compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "West Yorkshire",
    "long_answer": "All reported kindreds with Jancar syndrome come from a small geographic cluster in West Yorkshire, England, where several consanguineous families with the characteristic triad of intellectual disability, early-onset epilepsy and spastic paraparesis were first described, suggesting a founder effect restricted to that region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "In cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis, immune-complex deposition activates complement; the generated C5a chemoattractant and Fc receptor engagement recruit neutrophils to the post-capillary venules, where these cells infiltrate and damage the vessel wall."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus (particularly methicillin-resistant strains, MRSA) is one of the leading ICU pathogens. It frequently colonises the upper airways and invasive devices, making it a major cause of hospital-acquired (including ventilator-associated) pneumonia, and it is also the most common organism isolated from healthcare-associated bloodstream infections such as central-line–associated bacteremia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroencephalography (EEG)",
    "long_answer": "Electroencephalography records the brain’s electrical activity in real time. In patients presenting with dissociative amnesia, an EEG can be performed to evaluate cortical function and to rule out seizure activity or other neurological abnormalities, thereby providing an objective measure of brain activity associated with memory disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candida",
    "long_answer": "Among fungal pathogens, species of the genus Candida are the most frequently documented cause of myocarditis worldwide. Candida yeasts commonly cause bloodstream infections; once in the circulation they can seed the myocardium, producing focal or diffuse inflammatory lesions. Numerous clinical series and autopsy studies show Candida spp. (especially C. albicans) accounting for the majority of reported cases of fungal myocarditis, particularly in immunocompromised or critically ill patients, making this genus the best-established fungal cause of myocarditis globally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "McIndoe vaginoplasty",
    "long_answer": "In Müllerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome) the upper vagina and uterus are absent. A widely used surgical option to construct a functional neovagina is the McIndoe vaginoplasty, in which a space is dissected between the rectum and bladder and lined with a split-thickness skin graft over a stent, creating a patent, functional vaginal canal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromhidrosis",
    "long_answer": "Chromhidrosis is a rare sweat-gland disorder in which sweat appears yellow, orange, green, blue or black. In its eccrine form, water-soluble pigments from certain ingested drugs or dyes (e.g., rifampin, quinacrine, levodopa) are excreted through the sweat glands, giving the perspiration a distinct color."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Japanese encephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Japanese encephalitis, caused by Japanese encephalitis virus (a mosquito-borne flavivirus), is recognized as the foremost cause of viral encephalitis across Asia. It accounts for tens of thousands of cases annually and leads to a high burden of mortality and long-term neurologic sequelae, making it a major contributor to disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) in the region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reactive hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "After a carbohydrate-rich meal, an exaggerated insulin surge can drive plasma glucose below normal within 2–4 hours, producing postprandial or reactive hypoglycemia. This condition is defined by symptomatic low blood sugar (e.g., sweating, tremor, palpitations) that follows a meal and is relieved by carbohydrate intake."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketogenic diet",
    "long_answer": "In glucose transporter‐1 (GLUT1) deficiency encephalopathy, impaired transport of glucose into the brain produces seizures and cerebellar ataxia. Switching the patient to a ketogenic diet—very high fat, low carbohydrate—supplies ketone bodies that cross the blood-brain barrier independently of GLUT1, providing an alternative energy source for neurons. This metabolic bypass often lessens or resolves the ataxia associated with the encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Tsukahara syndrome (often reported as Roulet-Tsukahara cataract-microcornea-mental-retardation syndrome) is a rare congenital disorder whose hallmark manifestations are dense congenital cataracts, microcornea, and other ocular malformations. Definitive management depends on timely eye surgery and life-long visual rehabilitation, so the specialty that most commonly treats affected patients is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Holt–Oram syndrome is characterized by congenital heart defects such as atrial or ventricular septal defects and conduction disturbances. Diagnosis, medical management, and decisions about catheter-based or surgical repair of these cardiac anomalies are the domain of cardiologists (particularly pediatric or congenital heart cardiology). Therefore, the medical specialty most involved in treating the heart abnormalities of Holt-Oram syndrome is cardiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stereotactic radiosurgery",
    "long_answer": "Vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) are often managed with stereotactic radiosurgery, such as Gamma Knife or CyberKnife systems. This form of high-precision radiotherapy delivers a single or few highly focused doses of ionizing radiation to the tumor while sparing surrounding neural structures, making it a preferred non-invasive option for small to medium-sized vestibular schwannomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Howie Mandel",
    "long_answer": "Comedian and television host Howie Mandel has repeatedly discussed his severe obsessive-compulsive disorder and accompanying mysophobia (fear of germs) in interviews and his autobiography. His germ-avoidant behaviors, such as preferring fist-bumps to handshakes while judging on ‘America’s Got Talent,’ have made his struggle with mysophobia widely known to the public."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "AREDYLD syndrome is a lipodystrophic disorder characterized by acromegaloid facies, renal anomalies, deafness and lipoatrophy. The loss of adipose tissue produces marked insulin resistance, so affected patients develop early-onset, non-insulin-dependent (type 2) diabetes mellitus rather than the autoimmune type 1 form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gene therapy",
    "long_answer": "Gene therapy delivers a functional copy of the defective gene (or directly edits the mutant gene) into a patient’s cells—often via adeno-associated or lentiviral vectors—so the correct enzyme is transcribed and translated. By restoring or modulating gene expression, this modern approach can correct the underlying enzymatic deficiency responsible for many inborn errors of metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital atransferrinemia",
    "long_answer": "Congenital atransferrinemia (complete transferrin deficiency) is an autosomal-recessive iron-overload disorder in which the absence of transferrin leaves circulating iron unbound; this ‘free’ iron is deposited in the liver, heart and endocrine organs while erythropoiesis is iron-starved, producing severe, early-onset microcytic anemia. Affected infants and young children present with poor growth and delayed developmental milestones alongside hepatosplenomegaly and hemosiderosis—the developmental delay results from chronic severe anemia and systemic iron toxicity. Hence, congenital atransferrinemia is the iron-overload condition classically associated with delayed milestones in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inferior oblique",
    "long_answer": "The inferior oblique extra-ocular muscle elevates the globe (especially when the eye is adducted). When this muscle is overactive or otherwise dysfunctional, its excessive elevator action can pull the affected eye upward, producing a vertical strabismus known as hypertropia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren–Larsson syndrome is an autosomal-recessive neurocutaneous disorder caused by mutations in the ALDH3A2 gene, which encodes the microsomal enzyme fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase (FALDH). Loss of FALDH activity impairs oxidation of long-chain fatty aldehydes to fatty acids, leading to the characteristic triad of congenital ichthyosis, spasticity and intellectual disability seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Johnson–McMillin syndrome is a rare congenital disorder in which abnormalities of the inner ear are part of the core phenotype. These cochlear/vestibular defects lead to bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, the form of deafness consistently documented in individuals with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WT1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line deletion or mutation of the WT1 tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 11p13 causes WAGR syndrome. Because the deletion usually spans both WT1 and the adjacent PAX6 locus, affected children develop congenital aniridia (from PAX6 loss) and have a high risk of Wilms’ tumor (from WT1 inactivation), linking WT1 abnormalities to the coexistence of these two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydronephrosis",
    "long_answer": "Hydronephrosis is the dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces resulting from obstruction of urine outflow—most commonly kidney stones. The acute distention often produces intense flank or abdominal pain (renal colic). Urinary stasis created by the obstruction predisposes the kidney to bacterial ascent and infection, so untreated hydronephrosis can progress to acute pyelonephritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CJC-1295",
    "long_answer": "CJC-1295 is a synthetic analogue of growth-hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) that athletes and bodybuilders inject to raise endogenous growth hormone and IGF-1 levels. Chronic exposure to this peptide can produce sustained GH excess and acromegaloid changes; documented cases describe scalp thickening and folding characteristic of secondary cutis verticis gyrata developing after prolonged CJC-1295 use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX3",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome most commonly results from loss-of-function mutations in the PAX3 gene. PAX3 encodes a paired-box homeodomain transcription factor essential for neural-crest cell development; its disruption impairs melanocyte formation, leading to pigmentary anomalies (white forelock, heterochromia, patchy skin depigmentation) and to defective melanocytes in the stria vascularis of the inner ear, causing congenital sensorineural hearing loss characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST2",
    "long_answer": "Setleis syndrome (focal facial dermal dysplasia type 3) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TWIST2 gene, which encodes a basic helix–loop–helix transcription factor crucial for dermal and craniofacial development. Because early skin lesions of Setleis syndrome can mimic those of incontinentia pigmenti, it is included in the differential diagnosis of that disorder; demonstrating a pathogenic TWIST2 mutation confirms Setleis syndrome rather than incontinentia pigmenti."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Humoral theory",
    "long_answer": "For centuries, rheumatism was interpreted through the lens of the ancient Greco-Roman humoral theory, which held that diseases arose from imbalances among the body’s four humors (blood, phlegm, yellow bile, black bile). Pain and stiffness were attributed to an excess of viscous, “flowing” humors (rheuma), and therapies aimed to restore balance by purging or altering these humors. This Galenic framework dominated medical thought about rheumatic disorders well into the early modern era."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "RECQL4 (RecQ-like helicase 4) is the gene in which biallelic pathogenic variants cause the majority of cases of Rothmund–Thomson syndrome (RTS, particularly Type 2). Cytogenetic mapping places RECQL4 on the long arm of chromosome 8, specifically at 8q24.3. It is not located on chromosome 9. Therefore the statement that the RECQL4 gene is on chromosome 9 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus",
    "long_answer": "Infection with Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) is an established risk factor for several sub-types of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, including Burkitt lymphoma and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. EBV can immortalize B-cells, drive their uncontrolled proliferation, and create genomic instability, thereby increasing the likelihood of malignant lymphoid transformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bullous pemphigoid",
    "long_answer": "Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune blistering disorder in which IgG auto-antibodies target hemidesmosomal proteins (BP180 and BP230) at the dermo-epidermal junction. This immune attack produces separation beneath the epidermis, yielding tense subepidermal blisters that are classically accompanied by marked pruritus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temple-Baraitser syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Temple-Baraitser syndrome is a rare congenital disorder marked by hypoplasia or aplasia of the distal phalanges and absent or small nails of the thumbs and great toes. Pathogenic missense mutations in the voltage-gated potassium channel gene KCNH1 are the established molecular cause of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothalamus",
    "long_answer": "Neuroimaging and clinical studies show activation of the posterior hypothalamus during cluster headache attacks, making this region central to their pathophysiology. The hypothalamus also houses autonomic control centers that send descending sympathetic fibers which modulate pupillary dilatation and interact with parasympathetic pathways that constrict the pupil. Thus, the hypothalamus is the shared brain structure implicated in both cluster headaches and the regulation of pupil size."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen Storage Disease type I, deficient glucose-6-phosphatase prevents release of free glucose. During prolonged hypoglycemia, accumulated glucose-6-phosphate is shunted into glycolysis, generating excess pyruvate that is reduced to lactate. The rising lactate produces a high-anion-gap lactic acidosis, a characteristic metabolic complication of extended fasting in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPA1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OPA1 gene—best known for causing autosomal dominant optic atrophy—can also produce the Behr-syndrome phenotype, which includes early-onset optic atrophy together with neurological features such as cerebellar ataxia. OPA1 encodes a mitochondrial inner-membrane GTPase that is essential for mitochondrial fusion; loss-of-function mutations disrupt mitochondrial dynamics and energy production in neurons, leading to degeneration of cerebellar pathways and the observed ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC6A8",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC6A8 gene, which encodes the X-linked creatine transporter 1, lead to cerebral creatine deficiency syndrome 1. Because SLC6A8 is located on the X chromosome, its loss-of-function variants cause an X-linked form of intellectual disability accompanied by seizures, speech delay, and brain creatine depletion detectable on MR spectroscopy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primaquine",
    "long_answer": "Primaquine, an 8-aminoquinoline antimalarial used for radical cure of Plasmodium vivax and P. ovale, produces oxidative stress in erythrocytes. Patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency lack sufficient NADPH to regenerate reduced glutathione, so primaquine triggers acute hemolytic anemia. For this reason current guidelines require G6PD testing and recommend avoiding or dose-modifying primaquine in deficient individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin-eosin stain",
    "long_answer": "Routine histopathology with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining shows Ewing sarcoma as sheets of uniform, densely packed cells with scant cytoplasm whose nuclei take up hematoxylin and appear dark blue. This classic H&E picture of ‘small round blue cells’ is a key morphologic feature that allows pathologists to recognize and diagnose Ewing sarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sexual contact",
    "long_answer": "Syphilis, caused by Treponema pallidum subspecies pallidum, is transmitted chiefly through direct sexual contact with infectious lesions on the genitals, mouth, or anus—making vaginal, oral, and anal sex the primary routes of spread."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP2U1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the cytochrome P450 gene CYP2U1 cause hereditary spastic paraplegia type 56. CYP2U1 encodes a P450 enzyme that ω- and (ω–1)-hydroxylates long-chain fatty acids, especially arachidonic acid, linking this gene’s defect to disturbed arachidonic acid metabolism and the neurodegeneration seen in HSP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Pneumoconiosis is a form of interstitial lung disease caused by inhalation of mineral dusts (e.g., silica, asbestos, coal). Because it primarily involves the structure and function of the lungs, its diagnosis and management fall under pulmonology (respiratory medicine), the medical specialty dedicated to diseases of the respiratory system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "John Ritter",
    "long_answer": "John Ritter, who was born with an iris coloboma in his right eye (a congenital defect that leaves a key-hole-shaped gap in ocular tissue), was the lead actor portraying Jack Tripper in the popular sitcom “Three’s Company.” His coloboma is a well-documented physical trait, making him a notable public figure with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polydactyly",
    "long_answer": "Mikhail Tal was born with polydactyly—an extra, supernumerary finger on his right hand. Polydactyly is a congenital limb malformation in which the developing limb bud produces more than the normal five digits, usually owing to altered expression of patterning genes such as SHH. Although the additional finger was surgically removed in childhood, the condition left a visible deformity of Tal’s right hand."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a demyelinating disease that can involve any cranial nerve pathway, so lesions in the pontine facial-nerve nucleus or root may produce an acute peripheral facial weakness clinically indistinguishable from Bell’s palsy. Epidemiologic studies also show that migraine (especially migraine without aura) is significantly more prevalent in people with MS than in the general population, and migraine attacks are often temporally related to MS activity. Thus, MS is a neurological disorder that is linked to both Bell’s-type facial palsy and migraine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sebelipase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Sebelipase alfa is a recombinant human lysosomal acid lipase (LAL) used as enzyme-replacement therapy for lysosomal acid lipase deficiency (LAL-D). Marketed under the trade name Kanuma, it received regulatory approval (FDA and EMA) in 2015. By supplying functional LAL, sebelipase alfa reduces pathological accumulation of cholesteryl esters and triglycerides in tissues, thereby treating both infantile-onset Wolman disease and later-onset cholesteryl ester storage disease forms of LAL-D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAB23",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is most often caused by biallelic mutations in the RAB23 gene. The clinical spectrum produced by RAB23 variants includes craniosynostosis, polydactyly and notably brachydactyly, linking RAB23 directly to the shortened digits seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the COL4A1 gene, which encodes the α1-chain of type IV collagen in cerebral vessel basement membranes, weaken small intracranial vessels. This predisposes the fetus or neonate to intracerebral haemorrhage that can cavitate into porencephalic cysts (hereditary porencephaly). The same structural brain injury can trigger very-early-onset epileptic encephalopathy, and several reported infants with COL4A1 mutations have fulfilled the electro-clinical criteria of Ohtahara syndrome. Thus, COL4A1 mutation is a recognised genetic cause common to both porencephaly and Ohtahara syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets",
    "long_answer": "X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (vitamin-D–resistant rickets) is an inherited disorder in which mutations in the PHEX gene raise FGF23 levels, causing inadequate renal tubular reabsorption of phosphate. The resulting chronic hypophosphatemia prevents proper mineralization of osteoid, producing rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Non-ossifying fibromas (NOFs) are very common, benign developmental lesions of bone that occur mainly in children and adolescents. They are self-limited, often regress spontaneously as the skeleton matures, and have no documented potential to undergo malignant transformation. Large lesions can weaken bone and predispose to pathologic fracture, but progression to any form of sarcoma has not been reported in the medical literature. Therefore, the consensus in orthopedic pathology is that non-ossifying fibromas do not become malignant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatitis C infection",
    "long_answer": "Chronic infection with hepatitis C virus is frequently associated with oral lichen planus, and epidemiologic studies show that patients who have both oral lichen planus and hepatitis C have a significantly higher rate of malignant transformation to oral squamous cell carcinoma compared with those without the viral infection. The persistent immune-mediated inflammation and mucosal oxidative stress induced by HCV are thought to promote carcinogenesis in the affected oral epithelium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palovarotene",
    "long_answer": "Palovarotene, a selective retinoic-acid-receptor-γ agonist marketed as Sohonos, became the first medication formally approved (FDA, Aug 2023; Health Canada, 2022) for Fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva. By modulating retinoid signaling, it suppresses aberrant bone morphogenetic protein (BMP) pathway activity and thereby reduces heterotopic ossification in FOP patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Domoic acid",
    "long_answer": "Amnesic shellfish poisoning (ASP) occurs after ingestion of seafood contaminated with domoic acid, a neurotoxin produced by certain marine diatoms (e.g., Pseudo-nitzschia species). Domoic acid mimics glutamate, over-activating AMPA/kainate receptors in the brain and leading to neuronal excitotoxicity, particularly in the hippocampus, which manifests clinically as memory loss and other neurological symptoms characteristic of ASP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arch support orthotic",
    "long_answer": "Accessory navicular syndrome is aggravated by excess pronation and traction on the posterior tibial tendon. A rigid or semi-rigid arch support orthotic (often custom-made) lifts and stabilizes the medial longitudinal arch, off-loads the accessory navicular, and reduces tensile stress on the tendon, thereby relieving pain and inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Townes–Brocks syndrome is an autosomal-dominant multiple-malformation disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the SALL1 gene. Because its diagnosis, genetic testing, family counseling, and coordination of surveillance for multisystem anomalies (renal, auditory, gastrointestinal, limb) fall under the expertise of clinical geneticists, the specialty most commonly responsible for managing such patients is Medical Genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peritoneal dialysis",
    "long_answer": "In Epstein syndrome, progressive renal insufficiency can require dialysis. The modality commonly employed is peritoneal dialysis, in which the patient’s own peritoneal membrane lining the abdominal cavity serves as the semi-permeable membrane that filters waste and excess fluid from the blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Long arm of chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Distal trisomy 10q is characterized by partial trisomy of the q (long) arm of chromosome 10, meaning that the distal segment of chromosome 10’s long arm is present in triplicate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAM20A",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FAM20A gene are well documented to produce an autosomal-recessive syndrome that includes hypoplastic amelogenesis imperfecta together with pronounced gingival hyperplasia (gingival fibromatosis). FAM20A encodes a secretory-pathway pseudokinase that regulates enamel matrix protein phosphorylation and ectopic calcification; when mutated, it disrupts enamel formation and promotes gingival overgrowth, explaining the concurrent presentation of both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "No clear evidence of benefit",
    "long_answer": "The 2016 Cochrane systematic review concluded that existing randomized controlled trial data are too limited and of very low certainty to show any clinically meaningful advantage of Nd:YAG laser peripheral iridotomy in pigment dispersion syndrome or pigmentary glaucoma; the procedure did not demonstrably reduce intra-ocular pressure, prevent conversion to glaucoma, or slow visual-field loss compared with observation or standard care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX15",
    "long_answer": "Cousin syndrome is a recessive skeletal dysplasia with arthrogryposis and short stature. It is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in the TBX15 gene, which encodes the T-box transcription factor 15 that regulates early mesodermal patterning and limb and skeletal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Negative selection",
    "long_answer": "Comparative sequence analyses of the VP2 capsid gene of Parvovirus B19 show a very low ratio of nonsynonymous to synonymous substitutions (dN/dS < 1). This pattern indicates that deleterious amino-acid–changing mutations are systematically removed, a hallmark of purifying (negative) selection. Thus, negative selection is the evolutionary force responsible for ‘purifying’ VP2 codons in Parvovirus B19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "African Americans",
    "long_answer": "U.S. surveillance studies of endemic‐area states (e.g., Wisconsin, Arkansas, Mississippi) consistently show that Black/African-American persons develop blastomycosis and die from it at rates several-fold higher than White persons, indicating a disproportionate incidence and mortality burden in this racial group."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunodeficiency",
    "long_answer": "Malnutrition—especially protein-energy and micronutrient deficiencies—impairs both innate and adaptive immune responses (e.g., reduced T-cell activity, antibody production, and barrier integrity). This nutrition-induced immunodeficiency makes malnourished people far more susceptible to infections, explaining their higher infectious comorbidity even when universal health care is available."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary microcephaly",
    "long_answer": "Primary microcephaly denotes congenital brain malformations that arise from genetic defects acting during early neurogenesis. These mutations impair the proliferation of neural progenitor cells, so fewer neurons are produced; as a result, the whole brain (especially the cerebral cortex) is abnormally small at birth while overall cortical architecture is relatively preserved. Thus, primary microcephaly is the prototypical malformation produced by primary alterations in the neurogenesis stage of brain development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute phase",
    "long_answer": "The common cold is classified as an acute viral upper-respiratory infection; its acute phase refers to the early symptomatic period that usually resolves spontaneously within a few days to, at most, a couple of weeks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poliovirus",
    "long_answer": "Patients with X-linked (Bruton) agammaglobulinemia lack mature B cells and therefore have almost no circulating immunoglobulins. Neutralizing antibody is crucial for controlling enteroviruses. Without it, attenuated or wild-type poliovirus can disseminate and invade the central nervous system, producing paralytic poliomyelitis or chronic meningoencephalitis. Hence, poliovirus—an enterovirus—is notorious for causing severe CNS disease in individuals with this immunoglobulin deficiency, and live polio vaccine is contraindicated in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary siderosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic inhalation of fine iron or iron-oxide (rust) dust in workplaces such as welding, grinding, or mining leads to pulmonary siderosis, a form of pneumoconiosis. Iron particles are deposited in the lungs, producing characteristic radiographic opacities; hence pulmonary siderosis is the occupational lung disease associated with rust dust exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX",
    "long_answer": "The SHOX (short-stature homeobox) gene lies in the pseudoautosomal region 1 (PAR1) shared by the X and Y chromosomes. Because individuals with Klinefelter syndrome carry an extra sex chromosome (47,XXY), they possess three copies of the SHOX gene instead of the usual two. This gene-dosage excess contributes to characteristic features of Klinefelter syndrome, such as tall stature and long limbs, making SHOX the pseudoautosomal gene most clearly associated with the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DLX5",
    "long_answer": "Split-hand/split-foot malformation (SHFM) type 1 maps to chromosome 7q21-q22. Haploinsufficiency or dysregulation of the homeobox gene DLX5, located in this region, has been repeatedly demonstrated in affected families. Deletions, point mutations, or regulatory disruptions involving DLX5 impair limb bud patterning, producing the characteristic ectrodactyly phenotype. Thus, DLX5 is the chromosome 7 gene strongly associated with SHFM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fenobam",
    "long_answer": "Fenobam is a selective, non-competitive (negative allosteric) modulator of the metabotropic glutamate receptor type 5 (mGluR5). In early clinical studies with adults who have fragile X syndrome, single-dose fenobam produced measurable improvements in sensory gating, anxiety and other behavioral endpoints, providing the first promising clinical evidence that mGluR5 inhibition can ameliorate fragile X–related symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder that primarily affects the epidermis and dermis of genital and extragenital sites. Diagnosis relies on characteristic cutaneous findings, sometimes confirmed by skin biopsy, and first-line therapy is high-potency topical corticosteroids—procedures routinely performed within dermatology. Therefore, dermatologists are the specialists most commonly responsible for diagnosing and managing lichen sclerosus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metabolic specialist",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia is an inborn error of carbohydrate metabolism and, after the initial neonatal stabilization, patients require lifelong dietary restriction of galactose and monitoring for metabolic and developmental complications. This ongoing care is overseen by a pediatric metabolic specialist (often a biochemical geneticist) who is trained in the management of inherited metabolic disorders, coordinates dietary therapy, and monitors long-term sequelae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Third-spacing",
    "long_answer": "During acute pancreatitis inflammatory mediators make capillaries highly permeable, allowing large volumes of plasma and enzyme-rich exudate to leak from the vascular bed into the interstitium. The interstitial space therefore serves as a ‘third space’ that sequesters fluid, producing hypovolaemia, shock, organ oedema (lungs, kidneys, gut) and other systemic complications characteristic of severe acute pancreatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The HBB gene, which encodes the β-globin subunit of hemoglobin and whose mutations cause β-thalassemia, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 at band p15.5 within the β-globin gene cluster."
}

{
    "short_answer": "H2A.X",
    "long_answer": "H2A.X is a specialized H2A histone variant that becomes rapidly phosphorylated (forming γ-H2AX) at sites of DNA double-strand breaks, where it recruits and coordinates DNA-repair proteins. Germ-line mutations in the H2AFX gene encoding H2A.X have been identified in families displaying X-linked intellectual disability, tying this DNA-repair histone variant to the XLID clinical spectrum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Hajdu–Cheney syndrome is caused by heterozygous, gain-of-function mutations in the terminal exon of the NOTCH2 gene. Because a single mutated allele is sufficient to produce the phenotype and the mutation can be transmitted from an affected parent to offspring with a 50 % risk, the disorder follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance, even though many cases arise de novo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome is an autosomal-recessive limb malformation caused by loss-of-function mutations in WNT7A. A defining clinical combination is ectrodactyly (split hand/foot malformation) together with postaxial polydactyly, often accompanied by fibular aplasia-hypoplasia and femoral bowing. No other congenital limb disorder consistently shows both ectrodactyly and postaxial polydactyly, making Fuhrmann syndrome the classic diagnosis when these two features coexist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ambroise Paré",
    "long_answer": "The French barber-surgeon Ambroise Paré (1510–1590), a leading surgical authority of the 16th century, documented congenital hand and foot anomalies, including the fusion of digits now termed syndactyly, in his surgical writings on ‘monstrous’ births and malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metabolic (lactic) acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Pearson syndrome is a mitochondrial DNA deletion disorder that severely impairs oxidative phosphorylation. The resulting energy failure causes excessive lactate production, and most affected infants experience episodes of profound lactic (metabolic) acidosis—especially during infections or other stresses—which is the primary cause of death in the majority of cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mat Fraser",
    "long_answer": "Mat Fraser, born in 1962, is a British actor and performance artist who has bilateral phocomelia caused by his mother’s use of thalidomide during pregnancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis–renal dysfunction–cholestasis (ARC) syndrome is an autosomal-recessive multisystem disorder most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene, which encodes a Sec1/Munc18 family protein essential for vesicle trafficking. Defective VPS33B disrupts intracellular protein sorting, leading to the characteristic musculoskeletal contractures, renal tubular dysfunction, and neonatal cholestasis seen in ARC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippocampus",
    "long_answer": "Neuronal migration disorders such as lissencephaly and cortical heterotopias frequently disrupt the normal layering and cytoarchitecture of the hippocampus. This medial temporal-lobe structure is essential for consolidating short-term information into long-term declarative memory, so maldevelopment of the hippocampus can impair memory formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Odynophagia (painful swallowing) is a characteristic presenting symptom of cancer arising in the esophagus. When this malignancy is detected at a localized stage, the recommended curative treatment is surgical resection—an esophagectomy that removes the tumor-bearing segment of the esophagus and regional lymph nodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary valve stenosis can be detected before birth by performing a fetal echocardiogram—high-resolution ultrasound imaging of the fetal heart—which visualizes the pulmonary valve morphology and Doppler flow patterns in utero."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-6",
    "long_answer": "In fibrous dysplasia, GNAS-mutated bone-marrow stromal cells show chronically elevated cAMP levels, which drive massive over-secretion of the cytokine-hormone interleukin-6 (IL-6). The excess IL-6 promotes fibroblast proliferation, osteoclast activation and marrow fibrosis, replacing normal bone with fibrous tissue characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Bauxite fibrosis is a type of pneumoconiosis caused by inhalation of bauxite (aluminium ore) dust, leading to fibrotic changes in the lungs. Disorders of the airways and lung parenchyma are evaluated and managed by pulmonologists, who perform imaging, pulmonary function tests, bronchoscopy, and prescribe therapies for interstitial lung disease. Hence, the specialty most directly responsible for diagnosing and treating bauxite fibrosis is pulmonology (respiratory medicine)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langer mesomelic dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Langer mesomelic dysplasia is a rare mesomelic dwarfism caused by biallelic loss-of-function of the SHOX gene. A defining radiographic feature is the presence of markedly bowed, thin, “serpentine” fibulae (often accompanied by similar bowing of the radii), and patients present clinically with profound short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor triatriatum",
    "long_answer": "Cor triatriatum is a rare congenital heart defect in which a fibromuscular membrane partitions the left atrium into two chambers. The proximal (posterior-superior) chamber receives all pulmonary venous blood, while the distal (anterior-inferior) chamber is contiguous with the mitral valve and left atrial appendage. This dividing membrane can obstruct pulmonary venous outflow and is the hallmark anatomical feature of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant gene",
    "long_answer": "Pedigree studies of families in which individuals are born without gluteal muscles show vertical transmission through successive generations and equal occurrence in males and females, indicating that the causal allele is dominant and located on an autosome. Therefore, the congenital absence of the gluteal muscles is attributed to an autosomal dominant gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Band keratopathy",
    "long_answer": "Primary hyperparathyroidism produces chronic hypercalcaemia; the excess calcium can precipitate in the corneal epithelial basement membrane and Bowman’s layer, forming a horizontal calcific opacity across the inter-palpebral zone of the cornea. This characteristic corneal calcification is known as band keratopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatal lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Infants born to mothers with systemic lupus erythematosus may develop neonatal lupus erythematosus, a disorder caused by transplacental passage of maternal anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB autoantibodies. In addition to cutaneous lesions and congenital heart block, these autoantibodies can provoke hepatic inflammation leading to hepatomegaly, cholestasis, and secondary splenomegaly, thus producing hepatosplenomegaly in the neonate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippocampus",
    "long_answer": "Lead neurotoxicity is well-documented to damage the hippocampus, a medial temporal-lobe structure crucial for the encoding and consolidation of new memories. Experimental and epidemiological studies show that lead exposure reduces synaptic plasticity, impairs long-term potentiation, and causes neuronal loss specifically in the hippocampus, which explains the learning and memory deficits observed in lead-poisoned individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathyroid tumor",
    "long_answer": "A parathyroid neoplasm is an abnormal proliferation of cells forming a tumor in the parathyroid glands; it may be benign (adenoma) or malignant (carcinoma), but in all cases it is classified simply as a parathyroid tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain MRI",
    "long_answer": "Marchiafava–Bignami disease is usually identified by magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, which reveals characteristic demyelination, necrosis, and atrophy of the corpus callosum; MRI is far more sensitive and specific than clinical findings or CT, so it is the standard diagnostic modality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nodules",
    "long_answer": "In hidradenitis suppurativa, the lesions that present as small, firm, palpable lumps larger than 1 cm are classified as nodules. Dermatologically, a nodule is a solid, raised lesion >1 cm in diameter, which fits the described morphology seen early in HS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyproheptadine",
    "long_answer": "Familial cold urticaria can trigger massive histamine-mediated systemic reactions when the skin is chilled. Regular prophylaxis with cyproheptadine, an H1-antihistamine that also blocks serotonin receptors, blunts mast-cell mediator release and is recommended to prevent life-threatening anaphylactic episodes in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis can extend beyond the axial skeleton and cause apical pulmonary fibrosis, so the lungs are the organ that may become fibrotic in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peroxisome",
    "long_answer": "Alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGXT) must be targeted to the peroxisomal matrix of hepatocytes, where it converts glyoxylate to glycine. Proper peroxisomal localization prevents cytosolic glyoxylate accumulation and subsequent oxalate overproduction, thereby averting primary hyperoxaluria type 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium iodide",
    "long_answer": "Administering potassium iodide (stable, non-radioactive iodine) quickly saturates the thyroid gland with iodine, competitively blocking uptake of inhaled radioactive iodine isotopes such as I-131. This prevents the thyroid from concentrating the radioisotope, thereby reducing radiation dose to the gland and lowering the risk of thyroid injury or cancer. Potassium iodide is the standard medical countermeasure recommended by health authorities for radiation exposure involving radioactive iodine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT)",
    "long_answer": "LCAT is the plasma enzyme that esterifies free (unesterified) cholesterol on lipoproteins. When LCAT is deficient, cholesterol cannot be converted to cholesteryl esters, so free cholesterol accumulates in tissues such as the cornea, kidneys, and reticuloendothelial system, producing the clinical features of familial LCAT deficiency and fish-eye disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM6",
    "long_answer": "Spinocerebellar ataxia type 35 (SCA35) is caused by pathogenic variants in the TGM6 gene, which encodes transglutaminase 6. Multiple independent families with SCA35 have been shown to carry missense or truncating mutations in TGM6, establishing this gene as the causal locus for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLUT2",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi–Bickel syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC2A2 gene, which encodes the glucose transport protein GLUT2 (Glucose Transporter 2). Defective GLUT2 impairs glucose and galactose transport in hepatocytes, pancreatic β-cells, renal tubular cells, and intestinal epithelium, producing the characteristic hepatomegaly, renal Fanconi syndrome, and dysglycemia seen in Fanconi–Bickel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lionel Sweeney",
    "long_answer": "In the movie, Robert Downey Jr.’s character Lionel Sweeney is depicted as a man whose entire body is densely covered with hair due to congenital hypertrichosis, the rare genetic condition that produces excessive terminal hair growth over the face and body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glomerular filtration",
    "long_answer": "In the kidneys, blood first enters the glomeruli of each nephron, where plasma is forced across the glomerular capillary walls into Bowman’s capsule. This pressure-driven step, called glomerular filtration, removes water, electrolytes, and small solutes from the bloodstream, producing the filtrate that is subsequently modified along the nephron to become urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kawasaki disease",
    "long_answer": "In developed countries, Kawasaki disease is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children. This acute, self-limited vasculitis can damage the coronary arteries, leading to aneurysms and long-term cardiac morbidity. With the decline of rheumatic fever in high-income nations, Kawasaki disease has become the principal source of pediatric acquired heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Farnese Hercules",
    "long_answer": "Madelung’s disease (multiple symmetric lipomatosis) produces large, bilateral fat masses around the neck, shoulders and upper trunk, forming a ‘horse-collar’ that gives patients an exaggerated, muscular-looking habitus. This pseudo-athletic build has been classically likened to the ancient marble statue of the Farnese Hercules, whose heavily bulked neck, shoulders and arms resemble the symmetrical lipomatous enlargements seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase",
    "long_answer": "Nitisinone is the standard therapy for hereditary tyrosinemia type I, a disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH). By inhibiting 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase upstream in the tyrosine-catabolic pathway, nitisinone prevents accumulation of toxic metabolites that arise when FAH is lacking."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WTX101",
    "long_answer": "Wilson Therapeutics’ lead product for treating Wilson’s disease is WTX101, the bis-choline salt of tetrathiomolybdate, a potent copper-binding agent designed to reduce toxic copper accumulation characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Xanthine kidney stones are a form of urolithiasis (urinary tract stones). The specialty that routinely evaluates, images, medically manages, and surgically removes urinary calculi—including rare types such as xanthine stones—is urology. Urologists are trained to perform stone-specific metabolic work-ups, order imaging, prescribe stone-prevention regimens, and carry out procedures such as ureteroscopy or percutaneous nephrolithotomy when necessary."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scotophobia",
    "long_answer": "Fear of the dark is medically termed nyctophobia, but an accepted synonym is scotophobia. Derived from the Greek root ‘skotos’ meaning darkness plus ‘phobos’ for fear, scotophobia literally translates to a dread of darkness. The name begins with the four-letter sequence “Scot”, matching the first four letters of the country name Scotland, thus satisfying the clue in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbimazole",
    "long_answer": "Carbimazole, an antithyroid drug used to treat hyperthyroidism (it is converted to methimazole in vivo), readily crosses the placenta. Exposure during the first trimester is teratogenic and produces the well-described “carbimazole/methimazole embryopathy,” which includes aplasia cutis congenita, choanal or oesophageal atresia, and nipple–breast anomalies such as amastia or athelia. Hence maternal use of carbimazole in pregnancy can result in congenital absence of breasts (amastia) in the infant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TFE3",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar soft part sarcoma is driven by the recurrent chromosomal translocation t(X;17)(p11;q25), which fuses the TFE3 gene on chromosome Xp11.2 with the ASPSCR1 (ASPL) gene on chromosome 17q25. TFE3, an X-linked transcription factor, contributes the DNA-binding domain of the oncogenic ASPSCR1-TFE3 fusion protein that characterizes this sarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Target cells",
    "long_answer": "In hemoglobin C disease, red blood cell morphology is notable for numerous target cells (codocytes). The altered hemoglobin C promotes dehydration and increased surface-to-volume ratio of erythrocytes, producing the characteristic bull’s-eye appearance seen on blood smears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fragile X syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome, caused by CGG-repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome, is a common inherited neurodevelopmental disorder. Typical craniofacial features include a long narrow face, large protruding ears, and a high-arched palate, along with intellectual disability, autism spectrum traits, and macroorchidism in post-pubertal males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "Mixed connective tissue disease is an autoimmune connective-tissue disorder that combines features of systemic lupus erythematosus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis. The diagnosis and management of systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases fall under the medical specialty of rheumatology, whose physicians (rheumatologists) are trained to interpret serologic markers such as anti-U1 RNP antibodies, assess multi-system involvement, and prescribe immunomodulatory therapy. Therefore, rheumatology is the specialty responsible for diagnosing and treating MCTD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gluten",
    "long_answer": "Gluten is the mixture of storage proteins (gliadins and glutenins) found in wheat that elicits IgE-mediated wheat allergy and other immune reactions. Because it is distributed throughout the grain, gluten remains in the outer layers that become wheat bran, so bran products contain the same allergenic gluten proteins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "Tracheal agenesis can be detected before birth by prenatal ultrasonography: real-time fetal ultrasound shows the characteristic findings of congenital high airway obstruction syndrome (bilaterally enlarged, echogenic lungs, dilated airways below the obstruction, flattened diaphragm and ascites), allowing diagnosis of tracheal agenesis in utero."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "Central (secondary) hypothyroidism occurs when the pituitary fails to produce adequate thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). Pituitary apoplexy—hemorrhagic or ischemic infarction of the pituitary—often destroys TSH-secreting cells, producing the same TSH deficiency. Thus lack of thyroid-stimulating hormone links both hypothyroidism and pituitary apoplexy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXTL3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Recessive loss-of-function mutations in the EXTL3 gene, which encodes a glycosyl-transferase required for heparan-sulfate biosynthesis, impair endochondral bone growth (causing short-limb skeletal dysplasia) and block normal T- and B-cell development, producing a form of severe combined immunodeficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylglucosamine 6-sulfatase",
    "long_answer": "Sanfilippo syndrome type III D results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme N-acetylglucosamine 6-sulfatase (encoded by the GNS gene). This enzyme normally removes the 6-sulfate group from N-acetylglucosamine residues during the stepwise degradation of heparan sulfate. When the enzyme is absent or markedly reduced, heparan sulfate cannot be fully broken down and consequently accumulates within tissues, producing the characteristic clinical features of MPS III D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Substantia nigra",
    "long_answer": "Hypokinesia—as exemplified in Parkinson’s disease—results from degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta. Loss of this mid-brain basal-ganglia nucleus diminishes dopamine delivery to the striatum, impairing the direct motor pathway and producing the characteristic reduction in voluntary movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHACE syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sternal cleft (a midline chest wall defect) together with large segmental facial hemangiomas is characteristic of PHACE syndrome, a neurocutaneous disorder defined by the association of Posterior fossa brain malformations, large facial Hemangiomas, Arterial cerebrovascular anomalies, Cardiac defects/coarctation of the aorta, Eye anomalies, and Sternal defects. The presence of both a sternal cleft and extensive facial hemangioma is therefore classically linked to PHACE syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomal microdeletion",
    "long_answer": "Kurukawa-Takagi-Nakao syndrome is a contiguous-gene disorder caused by the loss of a small segment of a chromosome—a microdeletion—which removes several neighboring genes and produces the characteristic clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18) is strongly associated with congenital heart malformations, the most frequent of which is a ventricular septal defect (VSD). Autopsy and echocardiographic studies show VSDs in the majority of affected infants, often accompanied by other lesions, but the interventricular septal opening is the single most common cardiac defect observed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 8",
    "long_answer": "A well-defined subtype of multicentric Castleman disease is driven by infection with Human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8, also called Kaposi sarcoma–associated herpesvirus). The virus infects B cells and produces viral interleukin-6, leading to the characteristic systemic inflammatory symptoms and lymph node hyperplasia seen in HHV-8–associated Castleman disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycyl-tRNA synthetase",
    "long_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 2D is caused by dominant mutations in the GARS1 gene, which encodes glycyl-tRNA synthetase (GlyRS). GlyRS charges tRNAGly during protein synthesis and is ubiquitously required, but studies in animal and cell models show that altered GlyRS disrupts neuromuscular junction development and neurotransmission. Because normal GlyRS activity is indispensable for maintaining synaptic structure and function, the protein is considered essential for synaptic function and its dysfunction underlies the neuropathy in CMT2D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ascitic fluid cytology",
    "long_answer": "Collecting ascitic fluid by paracentesis and performing cytological examination—ascitic fluid cytology—allows microscopic evaluation of the cells present. Detection of malignant cells, tuberculous granulomas, or inflammatory cell patterns helps pinpoint the underlying aetiology of the ascites, making it a key diagnostic method for determining its cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGLT1",
    "long_answer": "Glucose-galactose malabsorption is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the sodium/glucose cotransporter 1 (SGLT1), encoded by the SLC5A1 gene. SGLT1 is normally embedded in the apical membrane of intestinal microvilli, where it couples Na⁺ influx to active uptake of dietary glucose and galactose. When this transporter is defective or absent, these monosaccharides cannot be absorbed, leading to their accumulation in the intestinal lumen and the characteristic osmotic diarrhea of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Granulomatous mastitis",
    "long_answer": "Sarcoidosis is a systemic granulomatous disease that may involve the breast, producing non-caseating granulomas and leading to granulomatous mastitis. This chronic inflammatory breast condition occurs independently of pregnancy or lactation, distinguishing it from the common lactational mastitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gout",
    "long_answer": "Gout is a crystal-induced arthropathy characterized by recurrent acute inflammatory attacks affecting joints and periarticular tissues. Monosodium urate crystal deposition triggers intense synovitis, leading to acute pain, redness, and swelling; during these flares a true joint effusion with inflammatory synovial fluid is commonly present."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DOCK8",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal recessive form of Job's syndrome (hyper-IgE syndrome) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the DOCK8 (Dedicator of Cytokinesis 8) gene. DOCK8 deficiency leads to impaired actin cytoskeleton regulation in immune cells, resulting in defective T-cell and B-cell function, recurrent infections, eczema and markedly elevated serum IgE. In contrast, the autosomal dominant form is linked to STAT3 mutations, making DOCK8 the characteristic gene for the recessive variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The MYCN proto-oncogene is mapped to cytogenetic band 2p24.3 on chromosome 2. Mutations or deletions of MYCN on this chromosome cause Feingold syndrome, linking the gene’s chromosomal location directly to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis",
    "long_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by germ-line mutations in the APC gene. Affected individuals develop hundreds to thousands of adenomatous colonic polyps in childhood or adolescence and have a markedly increased risk of extracolonic tumors; among these, hepatoblastoma is the most characteristic malignant tumor seen in young children with FAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sperm maturation and storage",
    "long_answer": "In Young's syndrome the epididymis becomes obstructed, blocking passage of sperm. The epididymis’ normal physiological role is to provide an environment in which spermatozoa mature, gain motility, are stored, and are conveyed from the testis to the vas deferens for eventual ejaculation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AFF2 (FMR2)",
    "long_answer": "Fragile XE mental retardation (FRAXE) is caused by expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat in the AFF2 gene—also known as FMR2—situated on the long arm of the X chromosome at Xq27–Xq28. This location and gene involvement are well-established in clinical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins)",
    "long_answer": "Statins inhibit hepatic HMG-CoA reductase, lowering the liver’s intracellular cholesterol pool. The fall in hepatic cholesterol triggers up-regulation of LDL receptors on hepatocyte surfaces, which markedly increases hepatic reuptake and clearance of circulating LDL particles, thereby lowering plasma LDL-cholesterol levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Costochondritis is an inflammatory condition of the costochondral junctions, so first-line treatment targets both pain and inflammation. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen or naproxen are routinely recommended because they inhibit cyclo-oxygenase enzymes, reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and thereby relieving the characteristic chest wall pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral hypoxia",
    "long_answer": "A myocardial infarction can drastically reduce cardiac output or precipitate cardiac arrest, cutting off oxygenated blood to the brain. This oxygen deprivation leads to cerebral hypoxia (also called anoxic/hypoxic brain injury), a condition in which brain cells are damaged or die because they are starved of oxygen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Porphyria",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria refers to a group of heme-biosynthesis disorders in which defective enzymes cause porphyrins and their precursors to accumulate in the body. The resulting neuro-visceral attacks commonly present with abdominal pain, autonomic disturbances, and gastrointestinal symptoms—constipation being a frequent feature. Thus, constipation linked to a build-up of porphyrins is characteristic of porphyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Diabetes mellitus is well-documented to cause anorgasmia through autonomic and peripheral neuropathy, vascular impairment, and hormonal dysregulation that interfere with normal sexual response. Beyond its sexual-function effects, diabetes is one of the top ten leading causes of death worldwide, responsible for millions of deaths each year due to its systemic complications such as cardiovascular disease, renal failure, and infections. Hence, diabetes mellitus fits both criteria posed in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic alcohol abuse",
    "long_answer": "The commonest precipitating factor for spinocerebellar (cerebellar–spinal) degeneration is prolonged, excessive intake of ethanol. Chronic alcohol abuse selectively damages Purkinje cells in the anterior cerebellar vermis, producing progressive gait and limb ataxia that is clinically recognised as alcoholic spinocerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polyuria",
    "long_answer": "Both nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and uncontrolled diabetes mellitus lead to marked increases in urine output. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, the kidneys cannot respond to antidiuretic hormone, causing excessive loss of dilute urine, while in uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, osmotic diuresis from glucosuria produces large volumes of urine. Thus, the characteristic symptom common to both conditions is polyuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nocardia asteroides",
    "long_answer": "Nocardia asteroides is a filamentous, weakly acid-fast, Gram-positive bacterium that is the principal pathogen responsible for nocardiosis. The infection most often begins in the lungs, where N. asteroides commonly produces a subacute or chronic pneumonia before it may disseminate to other organs. Thus, the same organism is well recognized as a cause of both nocardiosis and pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDNRB mutation",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg-Shah syndrome (type IV Waardenburg) involves loss-of-function mutations in the endothelin receptor type B (EDNRB) gene. This disrupts neural-crest–derived enteric ganglion cell migration, producing aganglionosis of the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus and resulting in Hirschsprung’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MUT",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent form of methylmalonic acidemia (the mut0 / mut– type) results from mutations in the MUT gene, which encodes the mitochondrial enzyme methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needed to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. Loss-of-function variants in MUT disrupt this step of propionate metabolism, leading to accumulation of methylmalonic acid and the characteristic clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trimethadione",
    "long_answer": "Fetal trimethadione syndrome is a well-described teratogenic pattern that occurs when the anticonvulsant drug trimethadione is taken during pregnancy. Maternal exposure to trimethadione, used for absence (petit mal) seizures, leads to characteristic fetal anomalies including craniofacial defects, cardiac malformations, and developmental delay. Therefore, trimethadione is the anticonvulsant specifically associated with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fragile X premutation",
    "long_answer": "Carriers of the FMR1 gene CGG-repeat premutation (55–200 repeats) are at risk for Fragile X-associated primary ovarian insufficiency (FXPOI). About one in five female premutation carriers lose ovarian function before age 40, and published cases document amenorrhea and estrogen deficiency beginning in adolescence—as early as 15 years old—due to accelerated depletion of ovarian follicles caused by the premutation’s RNA-mediated toxicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function variants in the FOXL2 gene – which encodes a forkhead/winged-helix transcription factor critical for peri-ocular mesenchyme and eyelid formation – are the canonical cause of blepharophimosis, ptosis, epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES). In several patients, especially those with larger deletions or severe missense changes of FOXL2, the same mutation also disrupts early ocular morphogenesis, producing congenital microphthalmia. Hence mutation of the single gene FOXL2 can give rise to both microphthalmia and the full BPES phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melanin",
    "long_answer": "Albinism results when animals fail to synthesize melanin—the brown-black pigment produced by tyrosinase from tyrosine in melanocytes. Without melanin, the underlying tissues and structural color mechanisms may still scatter light, but the usual pigmentary background is missing, giving an overall pale or white appearance characteristic of albinism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The RET (\"REarranged during Transfection\") proto-oncogene that is implicated in both Hirschsprung’s disease and multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is located on chromosome 10, band q11.2. It is not found on chromosome 18. Therefore, the statement that the RET gene associated with Hirschsprung’s disease appears on chromosome 18 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Budd-Chiari syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Budd-Chiari syndrome refers to thrombosis or obstruction of the hepatic veins. The resulting hepatic venous outflow block produces hepatic congestion, hepatomegaly, ascites, abdominal pain, and portal hypertension—clinical features that closely resemble those seen in portal vein thrombosis, but the clot is located in the hepatic veins rather than the portal vein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemarthrosis",
    "long_answer": "Hemophilia commonly produces spontaneous bleeding into joints—hemarthrosis—because the deficiency of clotting factors allows minor trauma to cause intra-articular hemorrhage. Osteochondritis dissecans can likewise present with hemarthrosis when the subchondral bone fragment and overlying cartilage separate, tearing small vessels and releasing blood into the joint space. Thus, hemarthrosis is a clinical feature shared by both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Motile cilia",
    "long_answer": "Primary ciliary dyskinesia results from structural or functional defects in motile cilia—the hair-like organelles whose coordinated beating clears mucus from airways and propels sperm. Mutations affecting axonemal dynein arms or other ciliary components impair ciliary motion, leading to the characteristic respiratory, fertility, and laterality disorders of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinologist",
    "long_answer": "Hormonal dysregulation in ME/CFS can involve the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, thyroid function, and sex-steroid or metabolic hormones. An endocrinologist is the physician who specializes in diagnosing and treating disorders of these endocrine glands and their hormones, making this specialist the most appropriate for evaluating and managing such imbalances in ME/CFS patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tacrolimus",
    "long_answer": "HIV-associated eosinophilic folliculitis has been shown to improve with topical tacrolimus, an immunosuppressive calcineurin inhibitor that blocks T-cell activation and the release of cytokines driving eosinophilic inflammation. Clinical case reports document rapid reduction of pruritus and follicular papules in HIV-positive patients treated with tacrolimus ointment, supporting its use in this setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Blackwater fever is a severe form of intravascular haemolysis and haemoglobinuria that occurs in some patients with Plasmodium falciparum malaria. Historically, it was most often seen in non-immune individuals who had received repeated or prolonged courses of quinine. Numerous case series from the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and occasional modern reports, show that episodes of blackwater fever typically began within hours to days after quinine ingestion, and recurred when quinine was re-administered. Although the exact mechanism is still debated (immune-mediated haemolysis, oxidative stress, possible G6PD deficiency), the epidemiologic association is well established; substitution of other antimalarials has caused the syndrome to become rare. Therefore, quinine is accepted in standard medical literature as a precipitating trigger of blackwater fever in malaria patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A Gun for Sale",
    "long_answer": "Graham Greene’s 1936 novel “A Gun for Sale” centres on Raven, a professional assassin born with a congenital cleft lip—a common cranio-facial malformation resulting from incomplete fusion of the upper lip during embryonic development. The deformity is mentioned throughout the book and shapes both Raven’s appearance and his outsider status, making this the Greene work that explicitly features a character with a cleft lip."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase",
    "long_answer": "In gluconeogenesis, the cytosolic or mitochondrial enzyme phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK) catalyzes the decarboxylation and phosphorylation of oxaloacetate, using GTP (or ATP in some organisms), to generate phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) and carbon dioxide. This reaction bypasses the irreversible pyruvate kinase step of glycolysis and is a key regulatory point in glucose synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Digoxin",
    "long_answer": "When infants with a ventricular septal defect develop congestive heart-failure symptoms, first-line medical therapy is the cardiac glycoside digoxin. Digoxin enhances myocardial contractility, decreases heart rate, and improves cardiac output, thereby reducing pulmonary over-circulation and relieving heart-failure signs until the defect closes spontaneously or is surgically repaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "In 46,XX (pure) gonadal dysgenesis the streak gonads fail to produce estradiol, so patients do not undergo normal pubertal development. Treatment therefore begins with exogenous estrogen (e.g., oral or transdermal estradiol/conjugated estrogens) to induce secondary sexual characteristics and promote bone health; once the endometrium is primed, a progestin is cycled with the estrogen. Thus the essential hormone replacement is estrogen therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichinosis",
    "long_answer": "Trichinosis (trichinellosis) is a parasitic infection caused by Trichinella nematodes acquired by eating under-cooked pork or wild game that contains encysted larvae. After the larvae are released in the intestine, they disseminate and encyst in skeletal muscle, producing characteristic muscle pain, fever, and myalgia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2 duplication",
    "long_answer": "A recurrent duplication of the Xq28 region that includes the MECP2 gene (the MECP2 duplication) produces MECP2-duplication syndrome, an X-linked condition characterized by severe intellectual disability and additional syndromic features such as hypotonia, seizures, and recurrent infections. Thus, the MECP2 duplication is the chromosomal duplication classically associated with the syndromic form of X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Attention and learning deficits",
    "long_answer": "Prenatal exposure to cocaine and alcohol disrupts fetal brain development, especially in frontal-striatal and hippocampal circuits. Long-term follow-up studies show children have lower IQ scores, poorer language and memory, difficulty with executive functions, and behavioral patterns resembling ADHD—marked by inattention, impulsivity, and hyperactivity. These neurocognitive and self-regulation problems collectively manifest as persistent attention and learning deficits throughout childhood and adolescence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WHSC1, WHSC2, LETM1, FGFRL1",
    "long_answer": "Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome results from a hemizygous deletion in the 4p16.3 ‘WHS critical region’. Functional studies of patients with differently sized deletions have shown that loss of four genes within this segment—WHSC1 (also called NSD2/MMSET), WHSC2 (NELFA), LETM1 and FGFRL1—accounts for the cardinal phenotype. WHSC1 and WHSC2 deletions correlate with the typical facial appearance, growth delay and intellectual disability; LETM1 haploinsufficiency is linked to the characteristic epileptic seizures; and FGFRL1 contributes to craniofacial and skeletal anomalies. Thus, these genes collectively underlie the major clinical features of Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absence of mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Mature human red blood cells expel all their mitochondria during erythropoiesis. Without these organelles they lack mitochondrial DNA and the oxidative-phosphorylation machinery affected in mitochondrial disorders. Consequently, defects that cause mitochondrial diseases have no cellular target to manifest in red blood cells, so such diseases are not detected in this cell type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Tucker syndrome is an extremely rare, congenital multisystem disorder, so evaluation of its inheritance pattern, genetic etiology, and the coordination of multidisciplinary care are handled primarily by the medical genetics (clinical genetics) specialty. Geneticists perform the diagnostic work-up, arrange molecular testing, counsel families, and guide referrals to other specialists as needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mansonella streptocerca",
    "long_answer": "Of the human-infecting filarial worms in the genus Mansonella, Mansonella streptocerca is geographically restricted to the equatorial rain-forest region of Central Africa, essentially the Congo Basin. In contrast, M. perstans occurs widely in Africa and parts of South America, and M. ozzardi is found in the New World. Hence, the only Mansonella species confined to the Congo Basin is Mansonella streptocerca."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroencephalography",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures characteristically show a distinctive EEG pattern called theta pointu alternant. This rhythmic, alternating theta activity can only be observed by recording the brain’s electrical activity; therefore, an electroencephalogram (electroencephalography, EEG) is the diagnostic tool used to detect this pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome is typically transmitted in an autosomal-dominant manner, meaning the causative SF3B4 mutation resides on one of the non-sex (autosomal) chromosomes rather than on the X or Y chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Defective peroxisomal β-oxidation",
    "long_answer": "Zellweger syndrome is a peroxisome-biogenesis disorder; without functional peroxisomes, the β-oxidation steps that occur in these organelles cannot shorten C27 bile-acid intermediates to the normal C24 primary bile acids. This block in peroxisomal β-oxidation prevents completion of the bile acid synthetic pathway, so patients have defective bile acid synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Morquio A (MPS IVA) results from pathogenic variants in the GALNS gene. During amniocentesis, fetal cells present in the amniotic fluid are collected (typically at 15–18 weeks’ gestation) and their DNA can be analyzed for these GALNS mutations, allowing prenatal identification of the gene malfunction responsible for Morquio A syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gelsolin (GSN) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Type II lattice corneal dystrophy that accompanies Familial Amyloidosis, Finnish Type, is caused by missense mutations (most commonly Asp214Asn or Asp214Tyr) in the gelsolin (GSN) gene on chromosome 9q34. The mutant gelsolin protein is abnormally cleaved, forming amyloid deposits in the cornea and other tissues, producing the characteristic corneal lattice lines and systemic manifestations of this hereditary amyloidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus commonly co-occurs with autoimmune adrenal insufficiency (Addison’s disease) and autoimmune thyroid disease as part of Autoimmune Polyglandular Syndrome type II (Schmidt syndrome). In this syndrome, the same underlying loss of self-tolerance leads to auto-reactive T-cells and antibodies that attack pancreatic β-cells, the adrenal cortex, and thyroid tissue, linking all three disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Prednisone, a systemic corticosteroid, is routinely added to the antiparasitic regimen for toxoplasmic chorioretinitis to suppress the host-mediated inflammatory response in the retina and choroid, thereby minimizing tissue damage and preserving visual function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chylomicrons",
    "long_answer": "Chylomicrons are large, triglyceride-rich lipoproteins formed in the intestinal mucosa that circulate to peripheral tissues. Via the action of endothelial lipoprotein lipase, they unload free fatty acids that skeletal and cardiac muscle cells oxidize for energy, while adipose tissue takes up the same fatty acids for storage. Thus, chylomicrons are the lipoproteins that interact with muscle tissue for energy utilization and facilitate lipid storage elsewhere."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Coronary artery aneurysms are abnormalities of the coronary arteries and therefore fall under the purview of cardiology. Cardiologists—particularly those trained in interventional cardiology—provide the definitive management, which may include antithrombotic medical therapy, covered-stent placement, or referral for surgical repair when necessary."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tetra-amelia syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hirotada Ototake’s limb absence is due to tetra-amelia syndrome, a rare autosomal-recessive genetic disorder most often linked to loss-of-function mutations in the WNT3 gene. The condition is characterized by complete agenesis of all four limbs and, in some cases, additional malformations. Tetra-amelia precisely accounts for being born without both arms and legs, matching the clinical description of Ototake’s congenital presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase",
    "long_answer": "The commonest form of homocystinuria (type I) results from deficiency of the enzyme cystathionine β-synthase (CBS). CBS normally converts homocysteine to cystathionine and requires pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6) as an essential co-factor. Because of this dependency, giving pharmacologic doses of pyridoxal phosphate can enhance residual CBS activity in many patients, lowering plasma homocysteine and ameliorating clinical manifestations. Hence, CBS deficiency is the form of homocystinuria that is treatable with pyridoxal phosphate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNNM4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CNNM4 gene, which encodes a magnesium transporter expressed in retina and tooth enamel–forming cells, are the established cause of Jalili syndrome (amelogenesis imperfecta with cone-rod dystrophy) and have also been reported in families presenting with isolated cone-rod dystrophy. Thus, CNNM4 mutations link both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KAL1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Kallmann syndrome is most classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the KAL1 gene (now termed ANOS1), which encodes the extracellular adhesion protein anosmin-1. Anosmin-1 is essential for the embryonic migration of olfactory bulb neurons and gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) neurons from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus. Disrupted KAL1 function prevents this migration, producing the characteristic combination of anosmia (absent sense of smell) and hypothalamic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism seen in Kallmann syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (bone-marrow transplantation) has been reported to reduce the macrophage-derived lipid storage, leading to regression of subcutaneous granulomas and decreased systemic inflammation in patients with Farber disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Signs",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, objective findings detected by a clinician on examination—such as cells or flare in the anterior chamber, keratic precipitates, or posterior synechiae in uveitis—are referred to as signs, distinguishing them from subjective symptoms reported by the patient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAR1A",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is a multiple-neoplasia syndrome characterized by spotty skin pigmentation, cardiac and cutaneous myxomas, and endocrine overactivity. In approximately two-thirds of affected families the disorder is caused by inactivating germ-line mutations in the PRKAR1A gene, which encodes the regulatory subunit type I-α of protein kinase A. Loss of PRKAR1A function leads to dysregulated cyclic-AMP/PKA signaling and the characteristic phenotype, making PRKAR1A the gene most commonly mutated in Carney complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "PAX3 is a transcription factor best known for its role in neural-crest and myogenic development and for the PAX3-FOXO1 fusion found in alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma. In surgical pathology, PAX3 immunohistochemistry is occasionally applied to help identify tumors of skeletal-muscle or melanocytic lineage, but it is not a standard or recommended marker for thyroid neoplasms. In contrast, the paired-box family member that is routinely used in thyroid pathology is PAX8, which remains positive in most differentiated and many anaplastic thyroid carcinomas and therefore helps confirm thyroid origin. Current diagnostic algorithms, reference textbooks, and guideline documents for anaplastic (undifferentiated) thyroid carcinoma do not list PAX3 as an immunohistochemical marker for this disease. Consequently, PAX3 is not used to diagnose anaplastic thyroid cancer by immunohistochemistry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Socrates",
    "long_answer": "Socrates held that admitting one’s own ignorance is the essential first step toward true knowledge. His famous claim that he was wise only because he knew he knew nothing expresses the idea that recognising ‘stupidity’ (ignorance) can give birth to wisdom; thus, in his philosophy, stupidity is the mother of wisdom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticonvulsant medications",
    "long_answer": "Drugs originally developed to control seizures—such as the anticonvulsants gabapentin and pregabalin—are frequently recommended as first-line therapy for neuropathic pain in peripheral neuropathy because they dampen ectopic nerve firing and reduce aberrant pain signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylalanine",
    "long_answer": "Spirulina is extremely protein-rich and contains large amounts of the amino acid phenylalanine. People with phenylketonuria lack functional phenylalanine hydroxylase, so they cannot metabolize phenylalanine efficiently; excess intake leads to neurotoxic accumulation. Therefore spirulina’s high phenylalanine content makes it unsuitable for individuals with phenylketonuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate",
    "long_answer": "Crystal arthropathy such as pseudogout arises when calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) microcrystals deposit within articular and fibro-cartilage. These crystals trigger inflammatory responses in the joint, leading to chondrocalcinosis and acute or chronic arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporin A",
    "long_answer": "In collagen VI–deficient Ullrich congenital muscular dystrophy, mitochondria are abnormally sensitive to permeability-transition opening and show an altered membrane-potential/NADH response when challenged with the complex I inhibitor rotenone. Exposure of patient muscle or fibroblasts to the cyclophilin-D inhibitor cyclosporin A corrects this defect, restoring a normal mitochondrial reaction to rotenone; this rescuing effect underpins the use of cyclosporin A (and non-immunosuppressive analogs) as a therapeutic approach for UCMD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miglustat",
    "long_answer": "Cipaglucosidase alfa (a recombinant human acid α-glucosidase) is administered together with miglustat, an oral iminosugar that acts as a pharmacological chaperone. Miglustat binds and stabilises cipaglucosidase alfa in circulation, enhancing its delivery to lysosomes and enzymatic activity, and the fixed-dose combination (AT-GAA; Pombiliti + Opfolda) is approved for the treatment of adults with late-onset Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apomixis",
    "long_answer": "Polyploid species in the genus Crepis typically produce seeds asexually by apomixis (agamospermy), bypassing normal meiosis and fertilization. This mode of reproduction is characteristic of many polyploid Crepis taxa and contrasts with the sexual reproduction observed in their diploid relatives."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Envelope (E) glycoprotein",
    "long_answer": "West Nile virus relies on its envelope (E) glycoprotein, embedded in the viral lipid membrane, to attach to cellular receptors and trigger low-pH-induced fusion of the viral envelope with endosomal membranes. This E protein–mediated binding and fusion step is indispensable for the virus’s entry into host cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "In lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, the primary site of injury is the principal cells of the cortical and medullary collecting ducts. Lithium enters these cells through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) and interferes with vasopressin (ADH)-stimulated cyclic-AMP signaling, leading to down-regulation of aquaporin-2 water channels and an inability to concentrate urine. The proximal tubule is not the site responsible for the water-handling defect that characterizes lithium-induced NDI. Although isolated reports describe other lithium-related tubular abnormalities, the development of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus specifically reflects collecting-duct dysfunction, not proximal-tubular involvement. Therefore, stating that the proximal tubule is affected in lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is not consistent with established scientific consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Urology is the medical and surgical specialty dedicated to the study, diagnosis, and treatment of disorders of the urinary tract in both sexes (kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra) and the male reproductive organs. Conditions such as nephrolithiasis (kidney stones) fall squarely within the scope of urology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Dyschromatosis symmetrica hereditaria is caused by pathogenic variants in the ADAR1 (adenosine deaminase acting on RNA 1) gene, which has been precisely mapped to the 1q21 region of chromosome 1. Therefore, the ADAR1 gene—and thus the molecular defect underlying this pigmentary disorder—is located on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydroxocobalamin",
    "long_answer": "Methylmalonic acidemia results from defects in the pathway that converts methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, a reaction requiring the coenzyme adenosylcobalamin (vitamin B12). Parenteral hydroxocobalamin is the treatment of choice because it is efficiently taken up by cells and converted intracellularly to adenosylcobalamin, lowering methylmalonic acid levels in patients with cobalamin-responsive forms of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POEMS syndrome",
    "long_answer": "POEMS syndrome is defined by the coexistence of Polyneuropathy and a plasma-cell disorder producing a Monoclonal gammopathy; these two findings are the mandatory diagnostic criteria. The acronym stands for Polyneuropathy, Organomegaly, Endocrinopathy, Monoclonal protein, and Skin changes, highlighting the characteristic association of monoclonal gammopathy with a demyelinating peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSM-III-R",
    "long_answer": "The 1987 revised third edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-III-R) was the first version that formally listed the new category ‘sadistic personality disorder’ (as a provisional Axis II diagnosis) and also contained the ‘adjustment disorder’ category within its diagnostic framework, thereby introducing both conditions in that edition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aortic root dilatation",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome commonly weakens the elastic media of the proximal aorta, leading to progressive widening of the aortic root—the segment of the ascending aorta immediately above the aortic valve. This condition is termed aortic root dilatation (or aneurysm of the aortic root) and is a well-recognized cardiovascular complication in Marfan patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral cortex",
    "long_answer": "Microcephaly results from impaired proliferation and survival of neural progenitor cells during fetal brain development, leading to a marked reduction in the volume and surface area of the cerebral cortex—the cerebral hemispheres—while other regions such as the brainstem or cerebellum are relatively less affected. This cortical underdevelopment is what produces the characteristically small head circumference seen in individuals with microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heat intolerance",
    "long_answer": "In epidermolytic ichthyosis the thick hyperkeratotic scales can block eccrine sweat ducts, leading to reduced or absent sweating (hypohidrosis). This impairs heat dissipation and can result in heat intolerance or even hyperthermia/heat-stroke episodes, making disordered sweating a significant complication of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is an inherited connective-tissue disorder caused by mutations in genes encoding type I, III or V collagen (e.g., COL5A1, COL5A2, COL3A1). The resulting defective or insufficient collagen weakens ligaments, tendons and joint capsules, producing marked joint hypermobility and predisposing patients to early-onset degenerative joint disease such as osteoarthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Familial forms of Type III membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis have been linked to the 1q32 region that contains complement-regulatory genes such as CFHR5; therefore, the candidate gene for this disease is located on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benign adipose tissue disorder",
    "long_answer": "Pelvic lipomatosis is a rare, non-neoplastic condition marked by diffuse overgrowth of mature fat in the pelvic retroperitoneum. Because the adipose proliferation is benign and lacks malignant potential, it is classified as a benign disorder of adipose tissue rather than a true tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow",
    "long_answer": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia originates in and disrupts the bone marrow, the body’s principal site of hematopoiesis where new red cells, white cells, and platelets are normally produced."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANKH",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ANKH gene (encoding the progressive ankylosis protein) reduce export of inorganic pyrophosphate, lowering extracellular pyrophosphate levels. Because pyrophosphate normally inhibits hydroxyapatite deposition, its depletion leads to excessive mineralization and marked thickening of tooth root cementum (hypercementosis). Thus, ANKH dysfunction directly links pyrophosphate regulation to the hypercementosis phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "Cyanosis—a bluish discoloration of the skin and digits caused by reduced oxygenated blood in cutaneous vessels—is one of the most obvious clinical signs of Raynaud’s phenomenon during vasospastic attacks. The same bluish or violaceous mottling (livedo) is also characteristic of livedoid dermatitis/vasculopathy, where sluggish blood flow and microthrombi produce localized tissue hypoxia. Thus, cyanosis is a shared, readily visible symptom in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Granulomatous mastitis",
    "long_answer": "Granulomatous mastitis is a rare, benign inflammatory disorder of the breast that can be associated with systemic granulomatous diseases such as sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and Wegener’s granulomatosis. Unlike the common puerperal (lactational) mastitis that occurs during breastfeeding, granulomatous mastitis arises in non-pregnant, non-lactating women and is characterized histologically by non-caseating granulomas within breast lobules. Thus, it represents breast inflammation that is not related to pregnancy or lactation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clindamycin or ampicillin-sulbactam",
    "long_answer": "Aspiration pneumonia is usually caused by oral anaerobes and mixed aerobic Gram-negative flora. Recommended empiric therapy therefore uses agents with reliable anaerobic coverage: either clindamycin (good activity against oral anaerobes and streptococci) or a β-lactam/β-lactamase-inhibitor such as ampicillin-sulbactam (or its oral counterpart amoxicillin-clavulanate). These regimens are endorsed in IDSA / ATS guidelines and have proven clinical efficacy in aspiration pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medial longitudinal arch",
    "long_answer": "Flat feet (pes planus) occur when the medial longitudinal arch—the high, inner arch running from the heel through the navicular and cuneiforms to the first three metatarsals—collapses, bringing the mid-foot down so the sole rests almost wholly on the ground."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prevent endometrial hyperplasia",
    "long_answer": "In females with Kallmann syndrome, estrogen is first given to induce pubertal development; once sufficient estrogenization has built an endometrium, cyclic progesterone is added. The progesterone’s key role is to convert the proliferative endometrium to a secretory phase and trigger withdrawal bleeding, thereby preventing unopposed-estrogen-induced endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX10",
    "long_answer": "Yemenite deaf-blind hypopigmentation syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the SOX10 gene, which encodes an SRY-box transcription factor essential for neural-crest-derived melanocytes and enteric nervous system development. The same SOX10 mutations are a well-established cause of Shah-Waardenburg syndrome (Waardenburg syndrome type IV), linking the two disorders through a common genetic mechanism affecting neural-crest cell differentiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurosurgery",
    "long_answer": "Gliomas are primary brain tumors arising from glial cells of the central nervous system. Initial and often definitive management involves maximal safe surgical resection or biopsy, procedures performed by neurosurgeons; consequently, the specialty of neurosurgery is the one most commonly responsible for treating patients with glioma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "28–40 weeks gestation",
    "long_answer": "By WHO-accepted definition, the perinatal period begins at 28 completed weeks of gestation and ends 7 days after birth; therefore the fetus is considered at risk of perinatal asphyxia from 28 weeks until delivery (through term, i.e., 40 weeks)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caffeine",
    "long_answer": "Caffeine, the widely consumed methylxanthine found in coffee, tea, energy drinks and many medications, stimulates the central nervous system by antagonizing adenosine receptors and promoting catecholamine release. This heightened sympathetic activity can increase physiologic tremor amplitude and exacerbate essential or other action tremors in susceptible individuals, making caffeine a recognized, common trigger of tremors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Integumentary system",
    "long_answer": "After the initial pulmonary infection, Blastomyces dermatitidis can disseminate via the bloodstream to the integumentary system. Involvement of the skin produces characteristic verrucous or ulcerative cutaneous lesions, reflecting hematogenous spread of the fungus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "In hypereosinophilic syndrome, persistent eosinophil infiltration of the lung parenchyma releases cytotoxic granule proteins that injure alveolar and interstitial tissue. Repeated injury and healing can lead to progressive scarring of the lung—pulmonary fibrosis—which causes severe, irreversible restrictive respiratory failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "When red blood cells are destroyed at an accelerated rate—known as hemolysis—the resulting surge in unconjugated bilirubin exceeds the liver’s ability to conjugate and excrete it. This accumulation of bilirubin in the blood and tissues produces the yellow discoloration characteristic of jaundice. The clinical condition marked by this increased destruction of red cells and subsequent jaundice is hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peptidoglycan",
    "long_answer": "For many years Chlamydia trachomatis was considered to lack a classical peptidoglycan cell wall (‘the chlamydial anomaly’). Using fluorescent D-amino-acid probes and mass-spectrometry, later studies showed that a thin peptidoglycan layer is synthesized and localized specifically at the division septum of the reticulate body. Thus the structural component once thought absent—peptidoglycan—is in fact present in the chlamydial division site."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Centrosome duplication pathway",
    "long_answer": "Most genes mutated in autosomal-recessive primary microcephaly (e.g., MCPH1, WDR62, ASPM, CDK5RAP2, CEP152, STIL, CENPJ) encode centrosomal or centriolar proteins that act together in the centrosome duplication/mitotic-spindle pathway. This pathway governs accurate centrosome biogenesis, spindle formation and symmetric divisions of neural progenitor cells, thereby controlling cerebral cortical expansion. Disruption of the centrosome duplication pathway limits neural progenitor proliferation and produces primary microcephaly, demonstrating its key role in normal brain development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemorrhagic complications",
    "long_answer": "Yellow fever frequently produces hepatic failure, coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia, placing patients at high risk of bleeding. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs inhibit platelet aggregation and damage gastric mucosa, thereby markedly increasing the likelihood and severity of hemorrhagic complications in these already coagulopathic patients; therefore NSAIDs should be avoided."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Although lymphomatoid papulosis is usually benign and self-healing, long-term follow-up studies show that about 10-20 % of patients later develop a true malignant lymphoma, most often a form of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma such as mycosis fungoides or primary cutaneous anaplastic large-cell lymphoma. Thus, lymphomatoid papulosis can evolve into cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography (CT)",
    "long_answer": "Chest computed tomography is the imaging modality of choice for lung hamartomas, because CT clearly delineates the well-circumscribed ‘coin-lesion’ and can reveal the characteristic fat and popcorn-like calcifications that distinguish hamartomas from malignant pulmonary nodules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior open bite",
    "long_answer": "Chronic tongue-thrust swallowing pushes the incisors apart, preventing their eruption and producing an anterior open bite. The same malocclusion is often seen in skeletal Class III patterns, where excessive mandibular growth (mandibular prognathism) contributes to the vertical separation of the anterior teeth. Thus anterior open bite can result from tongue thrusting and is also characteristically associated with mandibular prognathism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peroxisomal disorder",
    "long_answer": "Pipecolic acidemia results from a deficiency of the peroxisomal enzyme pipecolic-acid oxidase, causing accumulation of pipecolic acid in body fluids. Because the defect involves a single peroxisomal enzyme and impairs peroxisomal amino-acid (lysine) catabolism, it is classified among the peroxisomal disorders (specifically, a single-enzyme peroxisomal defect)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Steatocystoma multiplex is a cutaneous disorder characterized by numerous sebum-filled dermal cysts derived from sebaceous glands. Diagnosis, medical management, and surgical or laser removal of these cysts fall under the expertise of dermatologists; therefore, the specialty most commonly treating this condition is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomal deletion",
    "long_answer": "A 2q deletion arises from a chromosomal deletion mutation—a structural rearrangement in which a segment of DNA on the long arm (q) of chromosome 2 is lost, leading to partial monosomy for the genes within that region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Traumatic neuroma",
    "long_answer": "A traumatic neuroma (also called an amputation neuroma) forms when a peripheral nerve is cut or injured during a surgical procedure. The proximal axons attempt to regenerate and proliferate together with Schwann cells and fibrous tissue, producing a disorganized nodule that often contains both sensory and sympathetic autonomic nerve fibers. Thus it is a lesion arising from surgical trauma to a nerve and involves the sympathetic nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLB1",
    "long_answer": "GM1 gangliosidosis is a lysosomal storage disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the GLB1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme acid β-galactosidase. Mutations in GLB1 reduce or eliminate enzyme activity, leading to accumulation of GM1 ganglioside and the characteristic neurovisceral manifestations of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dietitian",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary fructose intolerance requires lifelong elimination of fructose, sucrose and sorbitol; this is achieved through individualized dietary planning and education provided by a dietitian (often a registered dietitian specialized in metabolic disorders), who guides food selection, label reading and nutrient adequacy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Megakaryocytes",
    "long_answer": "Bernard–Soulier syndrome is an inherited platelet-adhesion defect caused by absence of the glycoprotein Ib/IX/V receptor on platelets. The defective platelets are removed from the circulation, producing thrombocytopenia with abnormally large (giant) platelets. The bone marrow compensates by ramping up platelet production, which is reflected by an increased number of their precursors—megakaryocytes—in marrow aspirates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aplysia californica",
    "long_answer": "The marine mollusc Aplysia californica (the California sea hare) is the classic model used to study the gill-and-siphon withdrawal reflex that underlies work on learning and memory by Kandel and others. Like other animals, Aplysia’s cholinergic motor neurons depend on synaptic vesicle release, which can be blocked by botulinum neurotoxin; therefore Aplysia preparations are susceptible to the paralytic effects of botulism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphangiography",
    "long_answer": "Lymphangiography—radiographic imaging of lymphatic vessels after contrast injection—is the standard method used to confirm a lymphangioma. By opacifying and outlining the lymphatic channels, it demonstrates the characteristic, abnormal dilated lymphatic spaces that define a lymphangioma, allowing definitive diagnosis and distinction from vascular or cystic lesions of other origins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STRA6 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the STRA6 gene, which encodes the cell-surface receptor that imports vitamin A (retinol) from circulating retinol-binding protein, block fetal vitamin A uptake. When maternal vitamin A levels are low during pregnancy, this defect produces a severe retinoic-acid deficiency in the embryo, disrupting eye field development and causing congenital anophthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniotes",
    "long_answer": "Paranasal sinuses are characteristic of many amniotes—the clade that encompasses all mammals (synapsids) as well as reptiles, which include birds and crocodilians. Fossil and anatomical studies show that these air-filled cavities arose within the amniote lineage and are retained across its major branches, explaining their presence in most mammals, birds, and crocodilians."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optic atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Optic neuropathy refers to damage or degeneration of the optic nerve. When this damage leads to loss of nerve fibers and pallor of the optic disc, the condition is commonly termed optic atrophy. Thus, “optic atrophy” is another established name for optic neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schistosomiasis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic infection with Schistosoma species (especially S. mansoni or S. japonicum) produces peri-portal fibrosis and portal hypertension. The resulting venous congestion classically leads to pronounced, often massive, splenomegaly—making schistosomiasis a well-recognized ‘S’ disease commonly associated with an enlarged spleen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occupational hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "According to surveillance data from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics and NIOSH, hearing loss caused by chronic exposure to hazardous noise levels is the single most frequently recorded work-related illness in the manufacturing sector. Continuous noise above about 85 dB damages cochlear hair cells, producing permanent, sensorineural hearing loss, which is why noise-induced occupational hearing loss tops the list of reported manufacturing-related illnesses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uveitis",
    "long_answer": "Children with Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis are prone to developing chronic anterior uveitis—an inflammation of the uveal tract (iris and ciliary body). Because the ocular inflammation is often asymptomatic, it can smolder undetected, leading to cataract, glaucoma, and retinal damage; without timely ophthalmologic screening and treatment, this uveitis can ultimately cause irreversible blindness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LEMD3",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic loss-of-function mutations in the LEMD3 gene (also known as MAN1) are the established molecular cause of osteopoikilosis. LEMD3 encodes an inner nuclear membrane protein that antagonizes TGF-β/BMP signalling; its disruption leads to the characteristic sclerotic bone islands seen in osteopoikilosis and related disorders such as Buschke-Ollendorff syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia",
    "long_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is an autosomal-recessive disorder, most commonly due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency, that impairs cortisol synthesis in the adrenal cortex. The resultant ACTH-driven shunting of precursors into the androgen pathway causes markedly elevated circulating androgens from birth, leading to virilisation, hirsutism and other androgen-excess features. Because CAH is inherited and characteristically involves high androgen levels, it is the hereditary condition classically associated with androgen excess."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β)",
    "long_answer": "Transforming growth factor-β is the principal profibrotic cytokine. It binds to TGF-β receptors on fibroblasts, activating SMAD signaling that drives their proliferation, differentiation into contractile myofibroblasts, and overproduction of extracellular matrix proteins such as collagen, thereby promoting tissue fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIT D816V mutation",
    "long_answer": "More than 90 % of patients with systemic mastocytosis carry the activating KIT D816V point mutation, a substitution of aspartic acid with valine at codon 816 of the KIT proto-oncogene that drives constitutive signaling and mast-cell proliferation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta is an inherited connective-tissue disorder, most commonly due to mutations in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 genes, that leads to deficient or abnormal type I collagen. The resulting brittle bones fracture easily, often at different ages and stages of healing, which can mimic the radiologic pattern seen in non-accidental trauma, making it a well-recognized condition that can be mistaken for child abuse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX1",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome) results from a hemizygous deletion that removes several genes. Extensive genetic and animal-model studies show that loss of one copy of the T-box transcription factor gene TBX1 (i.e., TBX1 haploinsufficiency) is chiefly responsible for many of the syndrome’s cardinal manifestations, including conotruncal heart defects, thymic and parathyroid hypoplasia, and craniofacial anomalies. Thus, TBX1 haploinsufficiency is considered a primary molecular cause of the clinical features observed in DiGeorge syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-resolution CT",
    "long_answer": "In chronic beryllium disease (berylliosis), high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest is the preferred imaging modality because it can reveal the fine nodules, ground-glass opacities, and interstitial changes that may be missed on a standard chest radiograph, enabling sensitive detection of lung abnormalities associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nystagmus",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome frequently involves ocular abnormalities; one well-documented manifestation is nystagmus, an involuntary, rhythmic oscillation of the eyes. Clinical surveys of patients with Noonan syndrome report nystagmus among the characteristic eye findings alongside hypertelorism and ptosis, making it the recognized involuntary eye-movement disorder associated with this genetic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythema infectiosum",
    "long_answer": "Parvovirus B19 is the etiologic agent of erythema infectiosum (also called fifth disease or slapped-cheek syndrome). When a pregnant woman acquires this infection, the virus can cross the placenta, destroy fetal erythroid precursors, cause severe fetal anemia and hydrops fetalis, and ultimately lead to miscarriage or stillbirth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T2*-weighted MRI",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging, particularly T2*-weighted or R2* sequences, is highly sensitive to paramagnetic iron deposits. Excess iron shortens T2/T2* relaxation times, allowing MRI to non-invasively quantify hepatic iron concentration and thus detect iron overload in hereditary haemochromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibroblast",
    "long_answer": "Fibroblasts are the principal connective-tissue cells that secrete and maintain the extracellular matrix. They synthesize large amounts of collagen as well as other matrix proteins and growth factors that are essential for the normal function and structural integrity of many human cell and tissue types."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1961 Capitola seabird attack",
    "long_answer": "Alfred Hitchcock was inspired by the real August 18, 1961 incident in Capitola–Santa Cruz, California, when thousands of disoriented sooty shearwaters, apparently poisoned by algal-toxin–contaminated anchovies, slammed into houses, streets and cars. The bizarre, unexplained coastal bird onslaught—reported in local newspapers—prompted Hitchcock to base his 1963 thriller ‘The Birds’ on the event."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cervical insufficiency",
    "long_answer": "Ehlers–Danlos syndrome involves defective collagen and connective-tissue fragility, making the cervical stroma weak and easily distensible during pregnancy. This predisposes affected women to cervical insufficiency (incompetent cervix), in which the cervix dilates painlessly in the mid-trimester, leading to miscarriage or pre-term birth. Thus, cervical insufficiency is the pregnancy complication of the cervix classically linked to Ehlers–Danlos syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systematic desensitization",
    "long_answer": "Specific phobias, such as an irrational fear of caimans, are most effectively treated with behavioural techniques that involve graduated exposure to the feared stimulus while teaching relaxation skills. Systematic desensitization—a form of exposure-based cognitive-behavioural therapy—progressively presents the caiman (or representations of it) in a controlled hierarchy, allowing the patient to replace anxiety with relaxation, and is therefore the accepted treatment of choice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SF3B1",
    "long_answer": "SF3B1 mutations are recurrently detected in Waldenström macroglobulinemia. SF3B1 encodes a core component of the U2 snRNP complex that mediates pre-mRNA splicing, a crucial post-transcriptional mechanism controlling gene expression. Alterations in SF3B1 therefore link the disease to disrupted post-transcriptional regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RB1",
    "long_answer": "The RB1 (retinoblastoma 1) tumor-suppressor gene lies at cytogenetic band 13q14 on human chromosome 13. Deletions that remove one copy of RB1 are a hallmark of the 13q deletion syndrome, producing the typical combination of congenital anomalies and a high risk of retinoblastoma observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal aorta",
    "long_answer": "Most horseshoe kidneys are supplied by one or more renal arteries that arise directly from the abdominal aorta just below the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery. Although accessory branches can come from other vessels (e.g., common or internal iliac, inferior mesenteric), the abdominal aorta is by far the most frequent primary source of arterial blood to a horseshoe kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cathelicidin LL-37",
    "long_answer": "In rosacea, keratinocytes and infiltrating immune cells overproduce the cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide LL-37. Elevated LL-37 levels drive inflammation and vascular changes characteristic of the disease, and its abnormal processing by serine proteases is a well-established pathogenic mechanism documented in rosacea patients’ skin and serum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GSN (gelsolin)",
    "long_answer": "Finnish-type familial amyloidosis, which manifests ophthalmologically as lattice corneal dystrophy type II, is caused by pathogenic mutations in the GSN gene encoding the actin-modulating protein gelsolin. Mutant gelsolin fragments aggregate as amyloid, producing the characteristic systemic and corneal deposits seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "House, M.D.",
    "long_answer": "In the medical drama series House, M.D., one of Dr. Gregory House’s patients is ultimately diagnosed with Muckle–Wells syndrome—a rare autosomal-dominant cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome (CAPS) characterized by recurrent urticarial rash, sensorineural hearing loss, fevers and risk of amyloid A amyloidosis. The episode highlights House’s differential process and the need for interleukin-1–blocking therapy once the Muckle–Wells diagnosis is established."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aicardi-Goutières syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Aicardi-Goutières syndrome (AGS) is an inherited encephalopathy that typically manifests in early infancy with recurrent, unexplained fevers, severe neurologic impairment (irritability, developmental regression, seizures, intracranial calcifications), and characteristic chilblain-like lesions on the fingers, toes, ears, or nose. These cutaneous, neurologic, and systemic features together are classic for AGS, distinguishing it from other causes of chilblains or febrile illnesses in infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluoxetine",
    "long_answer": "Fluoxetine, a selective serotonin-reuptake inhibitor (SSRI), is often prescribed to patients with Kleine–Levin syndrome to lessen co-existing anxiety or mood symptoms. By enhancing central serotonergic transmission, fluoxetine reduces anxiety without significantly aggravating the disorder’s hypersomnolence, providing symptomatic relief during and between episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultraviolet A radiation",
    "long_answer": "Actinic keratoses arise after chronic solar exposure. Among solar wavelengths, Ultraviolet A (320–400 nm) penetrates deeply and induces oxidative stress rather than direct DNA photolesions. UVA energy excites endogenous chromophores, producing reactive oxygen species (e.g., singlet oxygen, superoxide), which then damage lipids, proteins and DNA in keratinocytes, driving the development of actinic keratosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edinburgh",
    "long_answer": "Ellis–Van Creveld syndrome was first characterized in 1940 by the British physician Richard W. B. Ellis, who practiced in and was associated with the city of Edinburgh, Scotland, together with the Dutch paediatrician Simon van Creveld of Amsterdam."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Cat eye syndrome is caused by the presence of an extra, small supernumerary marker chromosome derived from chromosome 22—typically an inverted duplicated segment encompassing 22pter-q11.2—leading to partial trisomy or tetrasomy of this region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Only a few families with Cousin–Walbraum–Cegarra syndrome have been reported, and the affected children were siblings born to unaffected, often consanguineous parents. This recurrence in siblings with healthy parents and equal sex distribution fits the classical autosomal-recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pars interarticularis",
    "long_answer": "The most common type of spondylolisthesis is the isthmic (lytic) form, which results from a stress fracture or congenital defect in the pars interarticularis—the bony segment between the superior and inferior articular facets of a vertebra. Weakening of this specific region allows the vertebral body to slip forward while the posterior elements remain behind, producing the characteristic anterior displacement seen in spondylolisthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAM134B",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FAM134B (RETREG1) gene cause hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type IIB. The profound impairment of pain and temperature sensation produced by this mutation leads to repeated unnoticed injuries, chronic infections and tissue necrosis, culminating in spontaneous loss of digits and limbs (autoamputation)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry",
    "long_answer": "Beginning in the early 1980s, routine analysis of patients’ urine by gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC-MS) for organic acids and fatty-acid oxidation intermediates demonstrated that many children thought to have Reye syndrome actually had inborn errors of metabolism (e.g., MCAD deficiency, organic acidemias). The widespread use of GC-MS metabolic screening therefore greatly reduced the number of cases labeled as Reye syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colchicine",
    "long_answer": "Colchicine is the standard first-line therapy for familial Mediterranean fever (FMF), where it reduces inflammatory attacks and prevents progression to AA (serum amyloid A–derived) amyloidosis. By suppressing neutrophil-mediated inflammation and lowering serum amyloid A levels, continuous colchicine therapy also treats existing AA amyloidosis and limits further amyloid deposition, making it the medication of choice for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal SGLT2 inhibition",
    "long_answer": "SGLT2 inhibitor drugs block the sodium-glucose co-transporter-2 in the proximal renal tubule, preventing re-absorption of filtered glucose; this increases urinary glucose excretion and thereby corrects hyperglycaemia and associated glycosuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHGDH, PSAT1 and PSPH",
    "long_answer": "Neu-Laxova syndrome is now known to result from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the three enzymes that catalyse de-novo L-serine biosynthesis—PHGDH (phosphoglycerate dehydrogenase), PSAT1 (phosphoserine aminotransferase-1) and PSPH (phosphoserine phosphatase). Case series documenting pathogenic variants in each of these genes report the typical lethal NLS phenotype together with mid-face anomalies, and several affected fetuses/newborns showed cleft lip and/or cleft palate. Thus mutations in PHGDH, PSAT1 and PSPH are genetically linked to both Neu-Laxova syndrome and the occurrence of orofacial clefts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythromycin",
    "long_answer": "Erythromycin, a macrolide antibiotic, is effective against atypical pathogens such as Mycoplasma pneumoniae—the cause of ‘walking pneumonia’—and Legionella pneumophila, the agent of Legionnaires’ disease. Its ability to penetrate host cells and inhibit bacterial 50 S ribosomal subunit protein synthesis makes it the traditional first-line treatment for both infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Pashayan syndrome shows autosomal dominant inheritance: the trait is transmitted from an affected parent to about 50 % of offspring of either sex, indicating that a single mutant allele on one of the autosomes is sufficient to cause the characteristic cranio-facial and skeletal features described by Pashayan and colleagues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central scotoma",
    "long_answer": "Cone dystrophy primarily damages cone photoreceptors concentrated in the macula; loss of macular function produces a loss of sensitivity in the central visual field. Accordingly, automated perimetry in cone dystrophy typically shows a central scotoma—an area of reduced or absent vision confined to the central field."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystonia",
    "long_answer": "Tranebjaerg–Svejgaard syndrome (better known today as Mohr-Tranebjaerg or deafness-dystonia-optic neuronopathy syndrome) is an X-linked disorder caused by TIMM8A gene mutations. After early-onset sensorineural deafness, affected individuals characteristically develop progressive generalized dystonia—a movement disorder marked by sustained, involuntary muscle contractions and abnormal postures. Thus, dystonia is the neurological disorder classically associated with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac muscle",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune-mediated congenital heart block arises when maternal anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB antibodies cross the placenta and damage the fetal atrioventricular node and surrounding conduction tissue. These structures are composed of specialized cardiomyocytes, i.e., cardiac muscle tissue, leading to fibrosis and interruption of electrical impulse propagation in the heart."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood serum",
    "long_answer": "Laboratory confirmation of Lassa fever is usually made by testing the patient’s blood serum: viral antigen or RNA can be detected by ELISA or RT-PCR during the acute phase, and Lassa-specific IgM/IgG antibodies appear later. Therefore, serum is the standard body fluid submitted for definitive diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution thyroid ultrasonography is the standard imaging technique used to visualise the gland’s size, echotexture and the presence of nodules or lymphocytic infiltration, allowing detection of structural abnormalities associated with autoimmune thyroid disease in patients with autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-N-acetyl neuraminidase",
    "long_answer": "Sialidosis is a lysosomal storage disorder produced by inherited deficiency of the lysosomal sialidase alpha-N-acetyl neuraminidase (also called neuraminidase-1, NEU1). Lack of this enzyme blocks removal of terminal sialic acid residues from glycoproteins and oligosaccharides, causing their accumulation in lysosomes throughout the body. The visceral buildup leads to organ enlargement, and hepatosplenomegaly—splenomegaly in particular—is a recognized clinical consequence of this neuraminidase-1 deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patau syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Patau syndrome (trisomy 13) is a chromosomal disorder whose classic phenotypic triad includes severe intellectual disability, marked microcephaly and midline facial defects such as cleft lip and/or cleft palate. These craniofacial anomalies, together with other features like polydactyly and congenital heart defects, define the syndrome clinically, making Patau syndrome the well-recognized condition characterized by both microcephaly and cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSM-5",
    "long_answer": "In the fifth edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5), body dysmorphic disorder was moved from the somatoform disorders section and placed in the new chapter titled “Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders,” recognizing its close phenomenological and neurobiological relationship to obsessive-compulsive spectrum conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Localized scleroderma (morphea)",
    "long_answer": "Localized scleroderma, also called morphea, is an autoimmune skin disorder in which excessive collagen deposition produces isolated, well-circumscribed patches or plaques of hardened (sclerotic) skin. Unlike systemic sclerosis, it is confined to the skin and underlying tissues and does not involve internal organs. Epidemiological studies show it occurs 2–3 times more frequently in women than in men, fitting the description given."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Branch retinal artery occlusion",
    "long_answer": "In Susac's syndrome, an autoimmune microangiopathy targets precapillary arterioles. Occlusion of the retinal branch arterioles produces focal retinal ischemia, leading to scotomas and partial vision loss. Thus, branch retinal artery occlusion is the direct cause of the visual impairment seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palliative care",
    "long_answer": "For patients with end-stage (stage D) heart failure who remain symptomatic despite optimal medical, device, and surgical therapy, clinical guidelines recommend transition to palliative care. This focuses on comfort, symptom relief, and quality-of-life support—often including hospice services—when curative or disease-modifying measures have been exhausted."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eisenmenger syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A large unrepaired aortopulmonary window causes a massive left-to-right shunt that rapidly elevates pulmonary arterial pressure. Persistent high flow and pressure injure pulmonary arterioles, leading to irreversible pulmonary vascular obstructive disease and shunt reversal— the pathophysiologic state known as Eisenmenger syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "SARS-CoV-2 infection can cause diffuse alveolar damage and aberrant repair that may evolve into pulmonary fibrosis, leaving permanent scarring and reduced lung compliance. Pulmonary fibrosis is likewise the hallmark outcome of many interstitial lung diseases, where chronic inflammation and fibroblast activation remodel the interstitium. Thus, fibrotic remodeling of lung tissue represents a shared long-term complication in both COVID-19 survivors and patients with interstitial lung disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MELAS syndrome",
    "long_answer": "MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes) is a maternally inherited mitochondrial disorder that damages cerebral white matter through respiratory-chain failure, producing a tissue-energy deficit that mimics hypoxic injury. Consequently, it is listed among hereditary leukoencephalopathies (because it is caused by mtDNA mutations) and among hypoxic/ischemic leukoencephalopathies (because its pathophysiology involves cellular hypoxia-like energy failure)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tetrabenazine",
    "long_answer": "Tetrabenazine is prescribed to suppress chorea and other hyperkinetic movement disorders by inhibiting the vesicular monoamine transporter-2 (VMAT2), thereby depleting presynaptic dopamine. Because this dopamine depletion mimics the dopaminergic deficit seen in Parkinson’s disease, tetrabenazine can produce drug-induced parkinsonism—manifesting as rigidity, bradykinesia, resting tremor, and other parkinsonism-like symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Tel Hashomer camptodactyly syndrome is a rare congenital disorder that has been observed in multiple families where affected individuals are born to clinically normal, consanguineous parents and where both sexes are equally affected. These pedigree patterns and the requirement for two carrier parents are characteristic of an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Platelets act as immune sentinels",
    "long_answer": "Besides stopping bleeding, platelets patrol the bloodstream as innate-immune sentinels. They carry pattern-recognition receptors (e.g., Toll-like receptors), FcγRIIA, integrins such as GPIIb/IIIa, and, upon activation, P-selectin. By engaging these receptors with their cognate ligands—bacterial PAMPs, immune-complex Fc regions, fibrinogen, or PSGL-1 on leukocytes—platelets bind, signal, and release cytokines and microbicidal peptides, recruit and activate neutrophils and monocytes, and form platelet–leukocyte or platelet–pathogen aggregates. Ligand-receptor binding therefore allows platelets to translate vascular surveillance into inflammatory and antimicrobial responses." }

{
    "short_answer": "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita is characterized by congenital, non-progressive joint contractures present at birth. Pathologically, many affected muscles show replacement of normal muscle fibers by dense fibrous and fatty tissue, leading to abnormal fibrosis of the muscle and restricted joint movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Incentive spirometer",
    "long_answer": "Because pleuritic chest pain makes patients take shallow, protective breaths, they are at risk of atelectasis and secondary pneumonia. Regular use of an incentive spirometer provides visual feedback that motivates the patient to take slow, deep inspirations, thereby promoting lung expansion and preventing complications associated with pleurisy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medial fibroplasia",
    "long_answer": "In fibromuscular dysplasia, the medial fibroplasia subtype is defined histologically by replacement of smooth-muscle cells within the arterial media by collagen, producing localized thickening of the media that alternates with areas of thinning. Thus, it is the form of FMD that involves both medial thickening and collagen formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crouzon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome—also known as craniofacial dysostosis—is an autosomal-dominant hereditary craniosynostosis disorder. Premature fusion of cranial sutures leads to a high, tower-shaped skull (turricephaly) and distinctive cranio-facial dysmorphisms, including mid-face hypoplasia, shallow orbits with proptosis, and a beaked nasal profile."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "The liver is the body’s primary detoxification organ, metabolizing and clearing drugs, toxins and metabolic by-products. In Sézary disease (Sézary syndrome, a leukemic variant of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma), malignant T lymphocytes can disseminate beyond the skin and blood to infiltrate visceral organs; hepatic infiltration is well documented, leading to hepatomegaly and impaired liver function. Thus, the detoxifying organ affected in Sézary disease is the liver."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aichmophobia",
    "long_answer": "The clinically recognized specific phobia describing an irrational, persistent fear of pointed or sharp objects is called aichmophobia, from the Greek word “aichmē” meaning spear or point and “phobos” meaning fear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling",
    "long_answer": "When a familial EFNB1 mutation is suspected, the recommended fetal diagnostic approach is to obtain chorionic villus tissue (usually at 10–13 weeks’ gestation) and perform targeted molecular analysis for the known EFNB1 pathogenic variant. This prenatal genetic test can confirm or exclude craniofrontonasal dysplasia before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Goiter",
    "long_answer": "Hyperplasia of thyroid follicular cells causes enlargement of the entire gland; this diffuse or nodular enlargement is clinically termed a goiter. The increased cell number expands thyroid volume, producing the characteristic neck swelling recognized as goiter."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autoimmunity",
    "long_answer": "Psoriasis arises when autoreactive T-cells and other immune mediators mistakenly attack the body’s own skin tissues, exemplifying an autoimmune malfunction rather than allergy or immunodeficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L-methylfolate",
    "long_answer": "Individuals carrying the C677T (677T) variant of the MTHFR gene have reduced activity of methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase, the enzyme that converts folic acid into the bioactive form 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. Supplementing directly with L-methylfolate (5-methyltetrahydrofolate) bypasses this enzymatic step, normalizing folate-dependent methylation and homocysteine metabolism; therefore, L-methylfolate is the form of folate recommended for treating people with the 677T MTHFR variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superior oblique",
    "long_answer": "Chronic Progressive External Ophthalmoplegia is a mitochondrial myopathy that produces symmetrical, slowly-progressive weakness of the extra-ocular muscles, most prominently the four recti and the levator palpebrae. The thin, energy-efficient superior oblique muscle is characteristically spared (or only becomes involved very late), so a small amount of depression in adduction/intorsion typically remains when other eye movements are markedly limited."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Ichthyosis linearis circumflexa is a rare keratinization disorder of the skin, classically seen in Netherton syndrome. Because it manifests as characteristic erythematous, serpiginous, scaly plaques and involves diagnosis and management of cutaneous barrier defects, it falls squarely within the expertise of dermatologists. Therefore, the medical specialty most likely to treat this condition is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plaque reduction neutralization test",
    "long_answer": "Confirmation of a suspected yellow-fever case is achieved by performing a plaque reduction neutralization test (PRNT) on the patient’s blood plasma. This assay measures the ability of antibodies in the plasma to neutralize yellow-fever virus, providing highly specific evidence of infection and distinguishing it from cross-reactive flaviviruses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Impaired cell-mediated immunity",
    "long_answer": "Clearance of dermatophytes relies mainly on T-lymphocyte–driven (Th1) cell-mediated immunity and the resulting delayed-type hypersensitivity response in the skin. In immunodeficient individuals—especially those with T-cell defects (e.g., HIV infection, immunosuppressive therapy)—this arm of the immune system is weakened, so the host cannot mount an effective cellular response against the fungi. The reduced cytokine production and impaired recruitment of neutrophils/macrophages allow dermatophytes to persist and spread, making such patients more prone to chronic or disseminated dermatophytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis",
    "long_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is an autoimmune blistering disorder marked by intensely pruritic grouped vesicles and blisters. It results from IgA autoantibodies against epidermal transglutaminase that deposit in the dermal papillae, and it occurs almost exclusively in patients with gluten sensitivity/celiac disease; removing gluten from the diet typically improves both the skin lesions and the underlying enteropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunohistochemistry",
    "long_answer": "Definitive diagnosis of congenital mesoblastic nephroma is made by performing immunohistochemical staining on the resected renal tissue. The spindle-cell tumour shows diffuse immunoreactivity for vimentin and smooth-muscle actin and is typically WT1-negative, a profile that distinguishes it from Wilms tumour and confirms congenital mesoblastic nephroma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase",
    "long_answer": "The common (type I) form of homocystinuria is caused by deficiency of cystathionine β-synthase (CBS). Because this block stops conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine and cysteine, patients have low cysteine and therefore low glutathione. Supplying cysteine donors (e.g., N-acetyl-cysteine) or otherwise boosting intracellular glutathione can partially correct the oxidative stress and clinical manifestations of CBS-deficient homocystinuria. Thus, increasing glutathione levels is useful specifically in cystathionine β-synthase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated erythrocyte protoporphyrin",
    "long_answer": "In erythropoietic protoporphyria, excess protoporphyrin IX circulates within red blood cells and is excreted via the hepatobiliary system. Patients whose red-cell (or plasma) protoporphyrin levels are particularly high deliver larger amounts of protoporphyrin to bile, predisposing it to supersaturation and precipitation of protoporphyrin-rich gallstones. Thus a markedly elevated erythrocyte protoporphyrin concentration is a recognized risk factor for cholelithiasis in EPP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Throat swab culture",
    "long_answer": "A throat swab sent for bacterial culture is considered the gold-standard test to confirm a bacterial etiology of pharyngitis, because it can definitively identify Group A β-hemolytic Streptococcus—the most common bacterial cause—by growing the organism from the pharyngeal specimen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DMP1",
    "long_answer": "The DMP1 (dentin matrix acidic phosphoprotein 1) gene is highly expressed in osteocytes and encodes a matrix protein that controls phosphate homeostasis and bone biomineralization. Loss-of-function mutations in DMP1 disrupt osteocytic signaling, increase FGF23 secretion, produce hypophosphatemia, and impair mineral deposition, demonstrating its key regulatory role in phosphate balance and skeletal mineralization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fever",
    "long_answer": "Yersinia pestis infection (plague) typically begins after a 2–7-day incubation with the sudden onset of high fever and chills, making fever one of the earliest and most common manifestations before other characteristic signs such as buboes appear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ΔF508 CFTR mutation",
    "long_answer": "Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens is an atypical manifestation of cystic fibrosis and is almost always linked to mutations in the cystic-fibrosis-transmembrane-conductance-regulator (CFTR) gene. The most prevalent and well-known change is the ΔF508 (Phe508del) deletion, a three–base-pair loss that removes phenylalanine at position 508, disrupting CFTR protein folding and chloride transport. Carriers or compound heterozygotes for this ΔF508 CFTR mutation commonly present with both cystic fibrosis and absent vas deferens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Krissy Taylor’s sudden death was attributed to arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC), a rare inherited heart disease in which mutations in desmosomal proteins (e.g., plakophilin-2, desmoplakin, plakoglobin) weaken cell-to-cell adhesion in the ventricular myocardium. This leads to fibrofatty replacement of right-ventricular muscle and predisposes affected individuals, especially young athletes, to lethal arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sweet’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sweet’s syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis) is a rare inflammatory skin disorder characterized by abrupt onset of tender, erythematous plaques rich in dermal neutrophils. Besides high fever and leukocytosis, up to one-third of patients develop painful polyarthritis or migratory arthralgia, making polyarthritis a well-documented systemic manifestation of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgeon",
    "long_answer": "Carpal tunnel syndrome results from compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel of the wrist. Evaluation and management of hand and wrist musculoskeletal conditions—including nerve-entrapment syndromes—fall under the scope of an orthopedic surgeon, particularly those with hand-surgery training. These specialists perform the physical examination, order or interpret nerve-conduction studies, initiate conservative measures (splinting, injections), and, when required, carry out the definitive surgical procedure (carpal tunnel release)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polycystic ovary syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is characterized by the presence of multiple small cysts in the ovaries, chronic anovulation, and hyper-androgenism. The resulting disruption of normal follicular development and ovulation makes PCOS one of the most common endocrine disorders and a leading cause of infertility in women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystal deposition disease (pseudogout) is an inflammatory arthropathy caused by CPP crystal accumulation in joints. Diagnosis requires joint-focused evaluation, synovial fluid analysis, and imaging, and management involves anti-inflammatory pharmacotherapy and long-term joint care—expertise that lies within rheumatology. Therefore, a rheumatologist is the specialist who routinely diagnoses and treats this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clauss fibrinogen assay",
    "long_answer": "The Clauss fibrinogen assay is the standard quantitative coagulation test that directly measures functional fibrinogen concentration in plasma; markedly reduced values on this assay confirm the low fibrinogen levels characteristic of congenital hypofibrinogenemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disability-adjusted life years (DALYs)",
    "long_answer": "Global assessments of cirrhosis burden, such as the World Health Organization’s Global Burden of Disease studies, use disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) as the key metric. DALYs combine years of life lost because of premature death with years lived with disability, thus capturing both mortality and morbidity to quantify the total health loss attributable to cirrhosis worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the CHD7 gene (chromodomain helicase DNA-binding protein 7) are the primary molecular cause of CHARGE syndrome. Because CHD7 plays a role in embryonic genital development, males with CHD7 mutations frequently present with external genital anomalies such as micropenis or hypospadias. Thus, the same CHD7 mutation that produces the multisystem features of CHARGE syndrome can also be directly associated with hypospadias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4",
    "long_answer": "The WNT4 gene, located on chromosome 1p36, encodes a signalling protein essential for Müllerian-duct formation. Pathogenic variants in WNT4 disrupt this pathway, leading to uterine development disorders such as Müllerian aplasia (e.g., Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome) and SERKAL syndrome. Hence, WNT4 on chromosome 1 is directly linked to abnormal uterine development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitiligo",
    "long_answer": "Monosomy 18p (18p- deletion syndrome) has been repeatedly reported together with the acquired depigmenting disorder vitiligo. Case series and individual reports describe children and adults with 18p- syndrome who subsequently developed generalized or segmental vitiligo, indicating a true association between loss of genetic material on the short arm of chromosome 18 and susceptibility to the autoimmune destruction of melanocytes that characterizes vitiligo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXT1 and EXT2",
    "long_answer": "Osteochondromas almost always harbor loss-of-function mutations in the exostosin genes EXT1 or EXT2, whereas lesions of Trevor disease (dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica) do not. Molecular analysis of EXT1 and EXT2 therefore distinguishes the two conditions: detection of an EXT1/EXT2 mutation supports osteochondroma, while absence of such mutations favors Trevor disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Filaggrin",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FLG gene, which encodes the epidermal barrier protein filaggrin, are a well-established cause of ichthyosis vulgaris and strongly predispose carriers to developing atopic dermatitis. The defective filaggrin impairs stratum corneum integrity and hydration, linking the same genetic defect to both skin disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PORCN",
    "long_answer": "Focal dermal hypoplasia (Goltz syndrome) is an X-linked dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the PORCN gene, which lies on chromosome Xp11.23. PORCN encodes an O-acyltransferase required for palmitoylation and secretion of Wnt proteins, a signaling pathway essential for the proper development of ectoderm-derived tissues such as skin, hair, nails, and teeth. Loss-of-function mutations in PORCN disrupt Wnt signaling, leading to the characteristic cutaneous, skeletal, and ectodermal defects seen in focal dermal hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oikophobia",
    "long_answer": "The correct term is “oikophobia,” derived from the Greek ‘oikos’ (house) and ‘phobos’ (fear). In clinical and popular psychology it denotes an irrational fear or aversion to the home environment, including household appliances or familiar domestic objects. In social-political discourse, philosophers such as Roger Scruton have extended the word to describe hostility toward or rejection of one’s own cultural roots. Thus oikophobia uniquely covers both the literal fear of items in the home and the figurative renunciation of one’s native culture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "In medical usage the term “birth defect” (or congenital anomaly) is applied to any structural, functional or metabolic abnormality that arises during fetal development and is already present at birth. Mickleson (often written as Michelson/Michels) syndrome is a rare, genetically-determined multiple-anomaly syndrome that is present from the moment of birth and is characterised by a typical pattern of congenital malformations (e.g., distinctive cranio-facial features, ocular and skeletal abnormalities, developmental delay, etc.). Because the disorder originates in embryonic development and the abnormalities are evident at or soon after delivery, it is classified by clinicians as a congenital disorder—that is, a birth defect. Therefore, Mickleson syndrome is considered a birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loin pain",
    "long_answer": "In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, chronically elevated urinary calcium and phosphate lead to nephrolithiasis/nephrocalcinosis. Passage or impaction of renal stones produces characteristic flank- or loin-localized pain, making loin pain a recognized symptom of distal RTA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arthralgia",
    "long_answer": "In Lyme disease, patients often develop arthralgia—a medical term that literally means pain in one or more joints. This migratory joint pain is a hallmark systemic manifestation of Borrelia burgdorferi infection, reflecting inflammation of the joints during the early disseminated or late stages of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin",
    "long_answer": "When serum prolactin levels become abnormally high (hyperprolactinaemia), prolactin exerts a negative feedback effect on the hypothalamus, suppressing pulsatile secretion of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). Reduced GnRH output diminishes luteinizing hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone release from the pituitary, which can result in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium valproate",
    "long_answer": "Photosensitive reflex epilepsy is most effectively managed with broad-spectrum antiepileptic drugs that suppress the photoparoxysmal response on EEG. Sodium valproate (valproic acid) is widely considered the first-line medication because it enhances inhibitory GABAergic transmission and stabilises neuronal firing across cortical networks, thereby reducing seizures triggered by flashing lights. Clinical guidelines and multiple studies cite sodium valproate as the standard treatment for photosensitive epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Catalase",
    "long_answer": "Catalase is a ubiquitous antioxidant enzyme located in peroxisomes that rapidly decomposes hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) into water and molecular oxygen, preventing oxidative damage to cellular components."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IGF2",
    "long_answer": "In Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, dysregulation of the imprinted 11p15.5 region often leads to biallelic expression and overactivity of the IGF2 (insulin-like growth factor 2) gene. Excess IGF2 promotes somatic overgrowth and markedly elevates the risk of embryonal childhood cancers such as Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma, making IGF2 overexpression a central oncogenic driver in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin E functions as a lipid-soluble antioxidant that protects erythrocyte membranes from oxidative injury. In deficiency, unchecked reactive oxygen species peroxidize red-cell membrane lipids, leading to membrane fragility and premature destruction of erythrocytes, manifesting clinically as hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isoprinosine",
    "long_answer": "Isoprinosine (inosine pranobex) is an antiviral immunomodulator that, when started early—particularly in stage 1 of subacute sclerosing panencephalitis—can slow neurological deterioration and prolong survival. Clinical studies and neurology guidelines list oral isoprinosine as the principal pharmacologic option for SSPE in its initial stage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carpenter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (also called acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is classically characterized by a combination of craniofacial malformations due to craniosynostosis, limb anomalies that include both pre- or post-axial polydactyly and syndactyly, and shortened digits (brachydactyly). This distinctive triad places Carpenter syndrome among the syndromes in which brachydactyly co-occurs with craniofacial abnormalities and polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Egypt, United States, Canada",
    "long_answer": "Only three very small series have been published. The syndrome was first described in two Egyptian siblings (Temtamy et al.). A second sib-pair was later reported from the United States (Centerwall et al.), and an isolated case was documented in Canada. No other countries have recorded confirmed occurrences, so cases have been reported solely in Egypt, the United States, and Canada."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Passive-aggressive personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "Passive-aggressive personality disorder (also called negativistic personality disorder) had been an official Axis II diagnosis in DSM-III-R, but in DSM-IV it was removed from the main list and relegated to Appendix B (\"Criteria Sets and Axes Provided for Further Study\") because of inconsistent empirical support and controversy about its validity. Therefore, it was the disorder moved to Appendix B pending further research."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Visual hallucination",
    "long_answer": "Perceptual disturbances describe abnormalities in the way external stimuli are experienced. The hallmark and most frequently noted manifestation of this disturbance is the visual hallucination—seeing people, objects, or lights that are not actually present. Visual hallucinations arise from aberrant activity in the visual processing pathways of the brain and are classically reported in conditions such as delirium, toxic-metabolic encephalopathies, and certain psychotic disorders, making them the prototypical symptom of a perceptual disturbance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lumbar spine",
    "long_answer": "To confirm idiopathic intracranial hypertension, clinicians perform a lumbar puncture—introducing a needle into the sub-arachnoid space between lumbar vertebrae (usually L3–L4 or L4–L5)—and the opening cerebrospinal fluid pressure is recorded. Thus, the procedure involves the lumbar region of the spine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 7",
    "long_answer": "In kidney (renal) transplant recipients, reactivation or primary infection with Human herpesvirus 7 (HHV-7) often precedes and stimulates cytomegalovirus (CMV) replication. Clinical studies show that HHV-7 antigenaemia emerges first, and its presence is a strong predictor for subsequent CMV viremia or disease, indicating a causal link between HHV-7 activity and CMV reactivation in this patient group."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTPN22",
    "long_answer": "The protein tyrosine phosphatase non-receptor type 22 gene (PTPN22) maps to chromosome 1p13.3. Variants in PTPN22, particularly the R620W (1858C>T) polymorphism, are strongly associated with several autoimmune disorders, including autoimmune thyroid diseases such as Hashimoto’s thyroiditis and Graves’ disease, making PTPN22 the chromosome 1p13 gene linked to these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Membranous labyrinth",
    "long_answer": "Labyrinthitis is an inflammatory disorder of the inner ear that primarily involves the membranous labyrinth—the delicate, fluid-filled system of ducts and sacs (cochlear duct, utricle, saccule, semicircular ducts) that resides inside the surrounding bony labyrinth. Thus, the part of the inner ear within the bony labyrinth that is affected is the membranous labyrinth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bartter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bartter syndrome in infants and children produces polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration episodes, failure to thrive, hypokalaemia and nephrocalcinosis—clinical features that closely resemble those seen in distal renal tubular acidosis. The key biochemical distinction is that Bartter syndrome causes hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, whereas distal RTA causes a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, but the presenting symptoms are sufficiently alike that the two conditions are often considered together in the differential diagnosis." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-Amyloid protein",
    "long_answer": "In Northern epilepsy syndrome (the CLN8 form of neuronal ceroid-lipofuscinosis) neurons accumulate intralysosomal material that is immunoreactive for β-amyloid (Aβ) peptides. The same β-amyloid peptides aggregate extracellularly to form the characteristic senile plaques of Alzheimer’s disease. Hence, abnormal accumulation of β-amyloid protein is a pathological feature common to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Simian virus 40 (SV40)",
    "long_answer": "Simian virus 40 (SV40) is a member of the Polyomaviridae family. Its large-T antigen has potent oncogenic properties, and SV40 DNA sequences and T-antigen protein have been detected in certain human brain tumors such as ependymomas and choroid-plexus papillomas, linking the virus to brain tumor development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pork",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies show that frequent consumption of pork—especially in processed, salted or smoked forms—raises exposure to nitrosamines and other carcinogens, and is consistently linked to a higher incidence of gastric (stomach) cancer. The U.S. Department of Agriculture categorizes all pork as red meat, making pork the red-meat dietary factor associated with increased stomach-cancer risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectopia lentis et pupillae",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the ADAMTSL4 gene cause the autosomal-recessive ocular disorder known as ectopia lentis et pupillae. This condition is characterised by displacement of the crystalline lens and abnormal position of the pupil; the zonular instability and high myopia that result can precipitate serious complications such as cataract, glaucoma, and retinal detachment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Adrenocortical hyperfunction is an endocrine disorder caused by excess secretion of adrenal cortical hormones (e.g., cortisol, aldosterone). The medical specialty of Endocrinology focuses on diseases of the hormone-producing glands, including the adrenal cortex. Endocrinologists perform hormonal testing, imaging, and manage medical or surgical treatment of conditions such as Cushing’s syndrome or primary hyperaldosteronism, making Endocrinology the appropriate specialty for diagnosis and therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Subcutaneous tissue",
    "long_answer": "Chromoblastomycosis is classified as a subcutaneous (or cutaneous–subcutaneous) mycosis; the causative dematiaceous fungi are introduced by trauma and establish chronic granulomatous infection primarily in the subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis) beneath the dermis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complete surgical excision",
    "long_answer": "Nail matrix tumors such as onychomatricoma or subungual glomus tumor are effectively managed by completely excising the lesion, including the involved portion of the nail matrix. Surgical removal eliminates the neoplastic tissue, prevents recurrence, and allows the nail unit to regenerate normally, making complete surgical excision the standard recommended treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitors and calcium-channel blockers",
    "long_answer": "Several case reports and series have shown that interstitial granulomatous dermatitis with arthritis (IGDA) can be precipitated by drugs, most commonly antihypertensive agents. Among these, angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors (e.g., captopril, enalapril) and calcium-channel blockers (e.g., diltiazem, verapamil) are the medications most consistently linked to the development of IGDA. Withdrawal of the offending drug often leads to improvement of the cutaneous granulomatous lesions and associated arthritis, underscoring the causal relationship."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric surgery",
    "long_answer": "Hirschsprung’s disease involves congenital absence of enteric ganglion cells and is definitively treated with bowel resection and pull-through procedures, which are performed by surgeons trained to operate on infants and children. Therefore, management of Hirschsprung’s disease–type D brachydactyly syndrome in pediatric patients falls under the specialty of pediatric surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lumbar puncture",
    "long_answer": "Tuberculous meningitis is confirmed by performing a lumbar puncture, which withdraws cerebrospinal fluid for laboratory analysis (cell counts, protein, glucose, acid-fast bacilli smear/culture or PCR) to detect Mycobacterium tuberculosis and characteristic CSF changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syndactyly",
    "long_answer": "Syndactyly is the congenital fusion of two or more fingers or toes. This digit fusion is a documented phenotypic feature of Jancar syndrome, appearing alongside the syndrome’s neurological and developmental abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burosumab",
    "long_answer": "Burosumab is a human monoclonal antibody that binds and inhibits excess fibroblast growth factor-23 (FGF23), the pathogenic driver of phosphate wasting in X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH). By neutralizing FGF23, burosumab restores renal phosphate reabsorption and increases circulating 1,25-dihydroxy-vitamin D levels, directly addressing the underlying molecular cause of XLH rather than only treating symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Numerous mitotic figures",
    "long_answer": "High-grade (WHO grade III/IV) astrocytomas are defined histologically by the presence of frequent mitoses—seen as numerous mitotic figures—indicating brisk, uncontrolled cell division. This elevated mitotic activity is one of the key criteria that distinguishes high-grade astrocytoma from lower-grade tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proximal phalanx",
    "long_answer": "In most cases of polydactyly, the supernumerary finger or toe is only partly developed.  When skeletal tissue is present, the element that ossifies first and is therefore most frequently seen is the proximal phalanx.  Metacarpals, metatarsals or more distal phalanges are duplicated far less often, so the proximal phalanx is the bone most commonly found within an accessory digit."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caudate nucleus",
    "long_answer": "Neuro-imaging and neuropathological studies show that the caudate nucleus—a major component of the basal ganglia—exhibits structural and functional abnormalities in both conditions. Children with specific language impairment typically have reduced caudate grey-matter volume and altered activation during language tasks, while enlarged or otherwise atypical caudate morphology is a consistent finding in several genetic and developmental forms of intellectual disability (e.g., Fragile X syndrome). Thus, the caudate nucleus is a common neural substrate implicated in both intellectual disability and specific language impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ifosfamide",
    "long_answer": "Ifosfamide, an alkylating chemotherapy agent, is well-recognized for causing Fanconi-type proximal renal tubular dysfunction. This injury impairs bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule, leading to bicarbonaturia and the characteristic metabolic acidosis of proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Before the 2011 discovery of the somatic activating AKT1 (p.E17K) mutation on chromosome 14, several groups had proposed that Proteus syndrome might belong to the PTEN-hamartoma tumour spectrum because of clinical overlap. PTEN is located at 10q23.31, so chromosome 10 was the chromosomal locus previously suggested to be linked to Proteus syndrome, a hypothesis later disproved by the identification of the AKT1 mosaic mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlorine",
    "long_answer": "Chlorine, the halogen element employed in sodium hypochlorite and other chlorine-based bleaches and disinfectants used to clean brewing equipment, can be inhaled as chlorine gas or hypochlorite aerosols. This airway irritant is a well-documented cause of irritant-induced and occupational asthma in brewery workers and other cleaning personnel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathyroid hormone",
    "long_answer": "Parathyroid hormone (PTH), secreted by the parathyroid glands, is the principal regulator of serum calcium. It stimulates osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, releasing calcium into the bloodstream, and also enhances renal calcium reabsorption and activates vitamin D synthesis. These actions collectively maintain normal circulating calcium levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reactive arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Reactive arthritis (formerly called Reiter’s syndrome) is classically characterized by the triad of arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis. Thus, inflammation of the conjunctiva is a well-known extra-articular manifestation of this form of arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome type II is a severe combined immunodeficiency caused by lack of MHC-class II expression on antigen-presenting cells. Replacing the patient’s hematopoietic system with donor stem cells that can normally express MHC-II reconstitutes functional CD4⁺ T-cell–mediated immunity, so allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell (bone marrow) transplantation is the established therapeutic option."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Internal tibial torsion",
    "long_answer": "In children who are walking but still in the toddler age range (≈1–3 years), the leading anatomic reason for in-toeing gait is internal (medial) torsion of the tibia. Metatarsus adductus predominates in infants, and excessive femoral anteversion becomes more evident after about 3–4 years, but for toddlers the most frequent etiology is internal tibial torsion, in which the tibia is rotated inward relative to the knee, producing the classic pigeon-toed appearance when walking."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infective endocarditis",
    "long_answer": "The cat-scratch disease pathogen Bartonella henselae is not limited to causing lymphadenitis; it can invade vascular endothelium and cardiac valves, producing culture-negative infective endocarditis—a severe, life-threatening inflammation of the heart’s endocardial lining and valves."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nocardia infection",
    "long_answer": "Nocardia species (gram-positive, branching, weakly acid-fast bacteria) often begin as a pulmonary infection that can hematogenously disseminate to the central nervous system, producing one or multiple brain abscesses. Because Nocardia grows slowly and relapses are common, effective management requires prolonged, high-dose antibiotic therapy—typically trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for 6–12 months—distinguishing it from most other bacterial brain abscess pathogens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vemurafenib",
    "long_answer": "Vemurafenib is a selective inhibitor of the BRAF V600E kinase approved for unresectable or metastatic melanoma with this mutation. Clinical trials and post-marketing data show frequent dermatologic toxicities, and hyperkeratosis is a well-documented adverse effect occurring in a substantial proportion of treated patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arthralgia",
    "long_answer": "Chikungunya virus infection is characteristically accompanied by intense arthralgia—pain in the joints without objective signs of joint inflammation. This non-inflammatory joint pain can persist for weeks to months and is one of the hallmark symptoms used clinically to differentiate chikungunya from other arboviral illnesses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urea",
    "long_answer": "In mammals, excess nitrogen from amino-acid catabolism is converted in the liver to urea via the ornithine (urea) cycle; this urea is then transported to the kidneys and excreted in urine, making it the principal nitrogen-containing constituent of mammalian urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oliver–McFarlane syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Oliver–McFarlane syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the PNPLA6 gene. Clinically it is distinguished by striking trichomegaly (excessively long eyelashes) together with ocular anomalies such as high myopia, chorioretinal degeneration, and progressive macular atrophy. These combined features make Oliver–McFarlane syndrome the prototypical genetic condition ‘characterized by ocular anomalies and trichomegaly.’"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pacman dysplasia is a lethal skeletal/osteochondrodysplasia documented in the medical literature (e.g., OMIM #261370) as being inherited in an autosomal recessive manner—affected infants inherit two defective alleles, one from each heterozygous carrier parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CFC1",
    "long_answer": "The CFC1 (Cripto/FRL-1/cryptic family 1) gene, situated on chromosome 2q21, has been implicated in syndromic forms of biliary atresia that occur with laterality defects. Pathogenic variants in CFC1 disrupt NODAL signaling essential for bile duct and visceral laterality development, linking this chromosome-2 gene to the pathogenesis of biliary atresia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Galactosialidosis is a lysosomal storage disorder that can produce ocular manifestations such as cherry-red spots, corneal clouding, and progressive vision loss. Management of these vision problems requires an ophthalmologist, the medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating eye diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "Peripheral T-cell lymphomas originate from mature (post-thymic) T lymphocytes located in peripheral lymphoid tissues, most commonly the lymph nodes, rather than in the thymus or bone marrow. Sézary disease is one such lymphoma arising in these peripheral lymph nodes before malignant T cells disseminate to blood and skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Action myoclonus",
    "long_answer": "Patients with sialidosis type II frequently develop action myoclonus—sudden, involuntary muscle jerks that are precipitated or intensified by purposeful movement. Action myoclonus is a recognized clinical subtype of myoclonus and is well-documented as a common neurological manifestation in the natural history of sialidosis type II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polycythemia",
    "long_answer": "In von Hippel-Lindau disease (historically called hereditary neurocutaneous angioma), some cerebellar or retinal hemangioblastomas secrete excess erythropoietin, producing secondary polycythemia. The resulting increase in blood viscosity and arterial pressure heightens the likelihood of vessel rupture, making polycythemia a recognized risk factor for cerebral (intracranial) bleeding in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caroli disease",
    "long_answer": "Caroli disease, a congenital dilatation of the intra-hepatic bile ducts (type V choledochal cyst), is frequently found together with congenital hepatic fibrosis (Caroli syndrome). The accompanying portal tract fibrosis obstructs portal venous flow, so patients commonly develop clinically significant portal hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome is caused by trisomy 21, where individuals have three copies of human chromosome 21 instead of the usual two, leading to the characteristic clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hodgkin lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Acquired ichthyosis (ichthyosis acquisita) commonly appears as a paraneoplastic dermatosis, and the malignancy most classically linked to it is Hodgkin lymphoma. Numerous case series and reviews have documented that onset of ichthyosiform scaling in adulthood often precedes or parallels the diagnosis or relapse of Hodgkin lymphoma, making this cancer the best-established association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-functioning pituitary adenoma",
    "long_answer": "Pickardt syndrome refers to the combination of mild-to-moderate hyperprolactinaemia (the “stalk effect”) and other pituitary–hypothalamic deficits that arise when the pituitary stalk (infundibulum) is compressed. The tumour most often responsible is a non-functioning pituitary adenoma: as it enlarges upward from the sella turcica, it mechanically compresses the infundibulum, interrupting dopaminergic inhibition of prolactin release and transport of hypothalamic releasing hormones. This mechanism produces the endocrine constellation characteristic of Pickardt syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "King syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The inherited cancer predisposition formerly called Hereditary breast–ovarian cancer syndrome (HBOC) is increasingly referred to as King syndrome, honoring geneticist Mary-Claire King, who first mapped and identified the BRCA1 gene and, together with subsequent work on BRCA2, established their role in familial breast and ovarian cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KMT2D",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in KMT2D (also called MLL2), which encodes the histone-3 lysine-4 methyltransferase 2D, are the predominant genetic cause of Kabuki syndrome type 1 and have also been classified as causing ‘syndromic microphthalmia 11’ (MCOPS11). Thus, pathogenic KMT2D variants link both the characteristic Kabuki phenotype and the microphthalmia-containing syndromic spectrum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Relapsing polychondritis frequently co-exists with other autoimmune disorders; among these, rheumatoid arthritis is reported most often. Case series show that 10–30 % of patients with relapsing polychondritis have concomitant rheumatoid arthritis, making it the single most common autoimmune association, likely reflecting shared autoimmune mechanisms against cartilage-related antigens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Neoplasia refers to the uncontrolled, abnormal and excessive proliferation of cells that forms a neoplasm (tumor). Such neoplastic changes in the oral mucosa can progress from benign lesions to malignant transformation, ultimately resulting in oral cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gabapentin",
    "long_answer": "Gabapentin is an anticonvulsant widely prescribed for neuropathic pain syndromes such as diabetic neuropathy and post-herpetic neuralgia; however, clinical trials and guideline reviews show it has little or no demonstrated efficacy in classical trigeminal neuralgia, in contrast to agents like carbamazepine or oxcarbazepine that reliably alleviate the paroxysmal facial pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin rash",
    "long_answer": "Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) commonly presents with cutaneous involvement, most frequently a diffuse erythematous-maculopapular skin rash, reflecting the systemic immune hyperactivation characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3 Gly380Arg mutation",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia is almost always caused by a single point mutation in the FGFR3 gene, in which glycine at codon 380 is replaced by arginine (p.Gly380Arg, c.1138G>A/C). This activating FGFR3 mutation impairs endochondral bone growth, leading to shortened vertebral pedicles and narrowing of the spinal canal. The resulting congenital spinal stenosis frequently produces back pain and neurogenic symptoms in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Because the single functioning kidney must compensate by hyperfiltration, individuals with unilateral renal agenesis have an increased risk of developing systemic hypertension later in life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq28 region",
    "long_answer": "Most genes that produce syndromic X-linked intellectual disability are clustered at the distal long arm of the X chromosome, cytogenetic band Xq28. Multiple well-characterised syndromic XLID genes (e.g., MECP2, L1CAM, IKBKG) reside in Xq28, making this chromosomal region the one classically associated with the syndromic form of X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is characterized by abnormally short digits, and the shortening is primarily due to underdevelopment or premature fusion of the phalangeal bones (the tubular bones within the fingers and toes). Defective growth of these phalanges leads to the overall reduced length of the digits observed in the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "18F-FDG PET/CT",
    "long_answer": "Whole-body 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose positron-emission tomography combined with computed tomography (18F-FDG PET/CT) can reveal increased metabolic uptake in inflamed cartilage before structural changes become apparent on routine imaging, allowing the early detection and assessment of relapsing polychondritis activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "OSLAM (osteosarcoma, limb anomalies, and erythroid macrocytosis) syndrome has been reported in multiple affected siblings born to unaffected parents, with no evidence of vertical transmission. This pattern—affected children of two clinically normal carriers—matches the classical autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasminogen activator inhibitor-2 (PAI-2)",
    "long_answer": "PAI-2 is the plasminogen activator inhibitor synthesized and released by placental trophoblasts. Maternal plasma concentrations of PAI-2 rise markedly throughout gestation, becoming one of the dominant fibrinolytic inhibitors in late pregnancy, whereas PAI-1 is chiefly endothelial-derived. Thus, PAI-2 is the inhibitor secreted by the placenta and present in significant amounts during pregnancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APC",
    "long_answer": "The adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) tumor-suppressor gene is altered in approximately 70–80 % of sporadic colorectal cancers and in virtually all familial adenomatous polyposis–associated tumors, making it the single most frequently mutated gene in colorectal cancer. Loss-of-function APC mutations initiate the classic adenoma–carcinoma sequence by dysregulating the Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathway, thereby driving early neoplastic transformation in the colon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest pain",
    "long_answer": "Annuloaortic ectasia, a dilatation of the aortic root that can progress to aneurysm or dissection, frequently presents with chest pain owing to aortic wall stress and possible ischemia. Palpitations—awareness of an abnormal heartbeat—are often accompanied by chest pain when the underlying rhythm disturbance induces myocardial oxygen mismatch or simply heightens cardiac awareness. Thus, chest pain is a symptom that characteristically appears in both annuloaortic ectasia and episodes of palpitations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cat eye syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cat eye syndrome is classically caused by a super-numerary marker chromosome derived from chromosome 22 that results in partial tetrasomy of the short arm (22pter) and proximal long arm (22q11). This chromosomal abnormality produces a constellation of congenital malformations—including ocular coloboma, anal atresia, ear tags/pits, cardiac and renal defects—making it the hallmark disorder associated with tetrasomy of the p arm of chromosome 22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1 premutation",
    "long_answer": "Up to 5-10% of women with primary ovarian insufficiency carry a premutation (55–200 CGG repeats) in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. This premutation can expand to a full mutation (>200 repeats) during oogenesis, so carriers have a significantly increased risk of giving birth to a child affected by fragile X syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heart transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In the reported case of familial eosinophilia complicated by eosinophil-mediated endomyocardial fibrosis and advanced heart failure, the definitive therapy was orthotopic heart transplantation. Immunosuppressive post-transplant therapy controlled graft rejection while the transplant replaced the irreversibly damaged myocardium, providing clinical recovery despite persistent hereditary eosinophilia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aortic arch",
    "long_answer": "In annuloaortic ectasia the aneurysmal dilatation involves the aortic annulus, sinuses of Valsalva and proximal ascending aorta up to the origin of the brachiocephalic (innominate) artery, but the aortic arch beyond this point is characteristically spared and remains of normal calibre."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pentosan polysulfate sodium",
    "long_answer": "Pentosan polysulfate sodium (marketed as Elmiron) is the only oral drug approved specifically for interstitial cystitis/bladder pain syndrome. It acts as a synthetic sulfated polysaccharide that adheres to and replenishes the bladder’s glycosaminoglycan layer, thereby helping repair the damaged urothelial lining and reducing pain and urinary urgency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PITX2 and FOXC1",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of the autosomal-dominant Axenfeld–Rieger syndrome are caused by pathogenic variants or dosage alterations in two transcription-factor genes: PITX2 on chromosome 4q25 and FOXC1 on chromosome 6p25. Mutations in either gene disrupt anterior segment and craniofacial development, producing the characteristic ocular and systemic features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheostomy",
    "long_answer": "Infants with Kniest dysplasia often develop upper-airway obstruction from laryngo- or tracheomalacia caused by the type II collagen defect. Establishing a tracheostomy bypasses the collapsible segment, secures the airway, and reliably alleviates the associated respiratory distress."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Right-sided mirror placement",
    "long_answer": "In dextrocardia the heart lies in the right hemithorax, so to record a normal-looking tracing the ECG electrodes are applied as a mirror image: the precordial leads (V1–V6) are positioned on the right chest in the same intercostal spaces and relative distances (labelled V1R–V6R), and the right- and left-arm limb electrodes are swapped. This right-sided, mirror placement restores the usual anteroposterior and left-right vectors, preventing the false appearance of global infarction or axis reversal that standard left-sided placement would produce."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare",
    "long_answer": "The opportunistic pathogen Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare (collectively termed Mycobacterium avium complex, MAC) frequently causes disseminated, systemic infections in people with advanced AIDS, especially when CD4⁺ T-cell counts fall below ~50 cells/µL. Due to severe cell-mediated immunodeficiency, these patients cannot contain the normally low-virulence organism, leading to bacteremia, lymphadenitis, and multi-organ involvement characteristic of MAC infections in AIDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rotavirus vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Rotavirus is the leading worldwide cause of severe, dehydrating gastroenteritis in infants and young children. Oral rotavirus vaccines (e.g., Rotarix, RotaTeq) are recommended in infancy to induce mucosal immunity against the major circulating rotavirus strains, thereby markedly reducing hospitalizations and deaths from severe diarrheal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose aspirin",
    "long_answer": "Starting low-dose aspirin (≈75–150 mg daily) before 16 weeks’ gestation has been shown in randomized trials and guidelines to lower the risk of placenta-mediated complications, including placental abruption, by improving trophoblastic invasion and reducing platelet aggregation within the uteroplacental circulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcinosis Raynaud phenomenon Esophageal dysmotility Sclerodactyly Telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "The limited cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis is classically referred to as CREST syndrome. The acronym CREST expands to the five cardinal clinical features: Calcinosis (deposition of calcium in the skin), Raynaud phenomenon (episodic digital ischemia), Esophageal dysmotility (impaired oesophageal peristalsis and reflux), Sclerodactyly (skin tightening of the fingers), and Telangiectasia (dilated cutaneous capillaries)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Acute myeloblastic leukemia (FAB M2) is a malignant disorder of blood-forming cells; its diagnosis, chemotherapeutic management, and supportive care are handled by the medical specialty of hematology (often within combined hematology-oncology practice), which focuses on diseases of the blood and bone marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Paramyotonia congenita is an inherited neuromuscular channelopathy caused by mutations in the skeletal-muscle sodium-channel gene (SCN4A). Diagnosis relies on neurologic examination, electromyography, and confirmatory genetic testing, and treatment involves drugs such as mexiletine or acetazolamide plus lifestyle counseling. These evaluations and interventions fall within the expertise of a neurologist—typically one with a subspecialty in neuromuscular medicine—who is therefore the appropriate specialist to diagnose and manage paramyotonia congenita."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Urban–Rogers–Meyer syndrome is a rare congenital (present-at-birth) genetic disorder characterized by multiple structural and functional anomalies such as absent or hypoplastic patellae, genital malformations, distinctive facial features, skeletal abnormalities and intellectual disability. Because these abnormalities arise during embryonic development and are already present at birth, the syndrome is classified among congenital anomalies—commonly referred to as birth defects—by clinical genetics references and dysmorphology texts. Therefore, Urban–Rogers–Meyer syndrome is indeed considered a birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurulation",
    "long_answer": "Neural tube defects like spina bifida arise when the neural folds fail to fuse during neurulation, the process that forms the neural tube in the 3rd–4th week of embryonic development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chloramphenicol",
    "long_answer": "Chloramphenicol is well-known for its potential to produce idiosyncratic bone-marrow toxicity. In susceptible individuals it can suppress hematopoiesis, leading to simultaneous decreases in red cells, white cells, and platelets—that is, pancytopenia—sometimes progressing to aplastic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shortness of breath",
    "long_answer": "Atrioventricular septal defect leads to excessive pulmonary blood flow and heart failure, producing dyspnea—commonly described as shortness of breath. Dyspnea is one of the top chief complaints prompting emergency department visits across the United States, making it both a hallmark symptom of this congenital heart defect and a leading reason for seeking urgent care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrichosis of the palms and soles (hypertrichosis palmaris et plantaris, sometimes called “hairy palms and soles”) is a rare genodermatosis. Pedigrees that have been reported in the medical literature show vertical transmission with affected individuals in successive generations and male-to-male transmission, the classic pattern of autosomal-dominant inheritance. OMIM (catalogue number 239200) also lists the disorder as autosomal dominant. Although isolated sporadic cases can occur, the inherited form is not regarded as autosomal recessive. Therefore the statement that hairy palms and soles due to hypertrichosis are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cetaceans and sirenians",
    "long_answer": "Hyperphalangy—the presence of more phalanges per digit than the mammalian norm—is a hallmark of the fully aquatic flippers of cetaceans (whales, dolphins, porpoises) and sirenians (manatees, dugongs). In both groups the number of phalanges in the central digits can double the typical 2-3-3-3-3 mammalian pattern, an adaptation that stiffens and elongates the paddle-like forelimbs for efficient swimming."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALX1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the homeobox transcription factor gene ALX1 have been shown to disturb midline craniofacial patterning. Such mutations produce the characteristic median facial cleft, orbital hypertelorism and nasal malformations that define autosomal-recessive frontonasal dysplasia (FND type 3), establishing ALX1 as a causal gene for frontonasal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgeon",
    "long_answer": "Congenital limb deformities involve structural abnormalities of the bones, joints, or soft tissues present at birth. Evaluation and correction—whether via casting, bracing, growth-modulation procedures, or complex reconstructive surgery—require detailed knowledge of musculoskeletal anatomy and surgical techniques. These responsibilities fall within the expertise of an orthopedic surgeon (often a pediatric orthopedic subspecialist), the physician trained to diagnose and treat congenital and acquired disorders of the limbs and skeleton."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Lassa fever is caused by the Lassa virus, whose natural reservoir is the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis). The principal route of human infection is environmental exposure to the rat’s excreta—especially urine and feces (\"droppings\"). People become infected when they inhale aerosolized particles, ingest food or water contaminated by these droppings, or when the excreta come into contact with broken skin or mucous membranes. Although secondary (person-to-person) transmission can occur via direct contact with the blood or body fluids of infected patients, the primary mode of transmission recognized by epidemiologic studies and WHO/CDC guidance remains contact with or ingestion/inhalation of infected rat droppings or urine. Therefore, the statement is accurate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAG2",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome, especially when accompanied by hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, is frequently caused by mutations in the PRKAG2 gene, which encodes the γ2 regulatory subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase. Genetic testing for a PRKAG2 mutation is therefore part of the diagnostic evaluation of patients with familial or syndromic WPW."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichorrhexis invaginata",
    "long_answer": "Nathalie (Comèl-Netherton) syndrome is classically characterized by a distinctive hair-shaft defect called trichorrhexis invaginata, often described as “bamboo hair.” This abnormality, which makes the hair brittle and easily broken, is regarded as the cardinal clinical sign of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inhalation of aerosolized rodent excreta",
    "long_answer": "Argentine hemorrhagic fever, caused by Junín virus, is acquired chiefly when people inhale dust or aerosols contaminated with the urine, saliva, or feces of its natural rodent reservoir (Calomys musculinus). This airborne exposure to rodent excreta is the principal route by which the virus is transmitted from rodents to humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravaginal metronidazole",
    "long_answer": "If a woman cannot tolerate oral nitroimidazoles because of nausea, metronidazole can be administered intravaginally (e.g., 0.75 % gel or suppository inserted once or twice daily for 5 days). Delivering the drug directly into the vagina bypasses gastrointestinal absorption, minimizing systemic side-effects while providing sufficient local concentrations to eradicate Trichomonas vaginalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxocariasis",
    "long_answer": "Toxocariasis, a helminth infection caused by the dog and cat roundworms Toxocara canis and Toxocara cati, is recognized as a neglected disease of poverty in the United States. Seroprevalence studies show the highest rates among children living in inner-city neighborhoods where soil contamination with infective eggs and limited access to veterinary care for pets are common. This urban poverty linkage makes toxocariasis the paradigmatic neglected disease prevalent in U.S. inner-city areas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psoriatic arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthritis that occurs in patients with psoriasis. A hallmark clinical feature is dactylitis—diffuse, sausage-like swelling of entire fingers or toes—resulting from inflammation of joints, tendons, and their sheaths."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PAX2 transcription factor cause the papillorenal (renal-coloboma) syndrome. Because PAX2 is critical for optic stalk and optic nerve development, its disruption produces optic disc/retinal colobomas and, in many patients, optic nerve hypoplasia. Thus a PAX2 mutation is classically associated with the coexistence of coloboma and optic nerve hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Flock worker's lung",
    "long_answer": "Flock worker's lung is an occupational interstitial lung disease that develops in workers who inhale microscopic ‘flock’ fibers (typically nylon, rayon, or polyester) used to give fabrics and other materials a velvet-like texture. Chronic exposure to these short synthetic fibers triggers lymphocytic bronchiolitis and interstitial pneumonitis, leading to restrictive lung impairment characteristic of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis",
    "long_answer": "Homocystinuria can weaken the physis and surrounding bone through altered collagen cross-linking and osteoporosis, predisposing children and adolescents to slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE). Consequently, SCFE is a recognized orthopedic condition that may occur in patients with homocystinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AMER1",
    "long_answer": "Osteopathia striata with cranial sclerosis is an X-linked dominant skeletal dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the AMER1 gene (also known as WTX/FAM123B). AMER1 encodes a negative regulator of Wnt/β-catenin signaling; its inactivation disrupts osteoblast differentiation and leads to the characteristic linear striations and cranial sclerosis of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ivermectin",
    "long_answer": "Infections with the cattle-grub larvae Hypoderma lineatum and Hypoderma bovis are routinely treated with ivermectin. This macrocyclic lactone reaches therapeutic levels in tissue and kills the migrating first- and second-stage larvae by opening glutamate-gated chloride channels, leading to paralysis and death of the parasite. A single oral or parenteral dose of ivermectin is highly effective and is the standard treatment recommended in veterinary and occasional human cases of hypodermosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Paul Karason, known for the argyria that turned his skin blue, underwent coronary artery bypass graft surgery for heart disease. In the period leading up to his death in 2013, he was hospitalized after developing pneumonia, a severe lung infection that can complicate recovery after major cardiac surgery and contribute to respiratory failure. This bout of pneumonia was one of the acute conditions he suffered immediately before his death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Prednisone, a systemic corticosteroid, is first-line therapy in immune thrombocytopenic purpura because it suppresses autoantibody-mediated platelet destruction. The same broad anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects make prednisone a standard drug for controlling joint inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis and for inducing remission in Crohn’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orexin (hypocretin)",
    "long_answer": "Narcolepsy, especially the form with cataplexy, is caused by the selective loss of orexin-producing (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. This orexin deficiency disrupts the maintenance of wakefulness and REM-sleep control. Wake-promoting agents such as modafinil indirectly enhance orexin signaling and compensate for the missing orexin drive, helping patients stay awake during the day."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PC gene abolish or reduce the activity of the mitochondrial enzyme pyruvate carboxylase. Without this enzyme, pyruvate cannot be efficiently converted to oxaloacetate, causing excess pyruvate to be shunted to lactate and resulting in severe lactic acidosis. Therefore, lactic acidosis due to PC gene mutations is characteristic of pyruvate carboxylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome (Brachmann-de Lange syndrome) is a congenital cohesinopathy caused most often by pathogenic variants in NIPBL or related cohesin-complex genes. A hallmark is the characteristic “mephistophelian” facies—highly arched, often confluent eyebrows, long eyelashes, and down-turned mouth corners. Typical dysmorphic features also include low-set posteriorly rotated ears, anteverted nares, and micrognathia, making this syndrome the classic genetic disorder described with both a mephistophelian appearance and low-set ears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preimplantation genetic diagnosis",
    "long_answer": "Carrier couples for alpha-thalassemia can undergo in-vitro fertilization with preimplantation genetic diagnosis (PGD); embryos are biopsied and only those without the pathogenic HBA1/HBA2 deletions are implanted, thereby avoiding the birth of a child with alpha-thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacterial infection",
    "long_answer": "Endocarditis is most frequently an infective process caused by bacteria that colonize and inflame the endocardial surfaces of the heart, especially the cardiac valves. Organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus viridans are classic culprits, making bacterial infection the condition most commonly associated with endocarditis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diagnostic probes",
    "long_answer": "In congenital disorders of glycosylation, lectins serve as laboratory probes that bind defined sugar motifs on patient glycoproteins. By comparing lectin-binding patterns (e.g., lectin blotting of serum transferrin) to normal controls, clinicians can detect abnormal or missing glycans and thereby diagnose and subtype CDG. Thus, lectins function as diagnostic tools rather than pathogenic factors in these disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coxiella burnetii",
    "long_answer": "Coxiella burnetii is the Gram-negative bacterium that causes Q fever. Although long regarded as an obligate intracellular pathogen, it can now be propagated outside host cells in specialized axenic media such as acidified citrate cysteine medium (ACCM), demonstrating its ability to grow in a cell-free culture system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Avoidance of pork",
    "long_answer": "Trichinosis is contracted by eating raw or under-cooked pork that contains Trichinella spiralis larvae. Jewish dietary (kosher) laws forbid consumption of pork altogether, eliminating this major exposure route and thus helping to prevent the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudomembranous colitis",
    "long_answer": "Because treating Pseudomonas aeruginosa often requires prolonged, broad-spectrum or combination antibiotic regimens, the normal intestinal microbiota can be severely disrupted. This ecological imbalance permits overgrowth of toxigenic Clostridioides (Clostridium) difficile, whose toxins injure the colonic mucosa and produce the characteristic inflammatory plaques of pseudomembranous colitis—an important, potentially life-threatening adverse effect of intensive antipseudomonal therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycine-to-cysteine",
    "long_answer": "In osteogenesis imperfecta, point mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2 often replace the obligatory glycine residue in the Gly-X-Y collagen repeat with a bulkier amino acid; the classic and most frequently cited change is substitution of glycine by cysteine, which disrupts triple-helix formation and weakens type I collagen, producing the brittle-bone phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dental ankylosis",
    "long_answer": "Dental ankylosis is the pathological fusion of a tooth’s cementum or dentin directly to the surrounding alveolar bone, resulting in obliteration of the periodontal ligament space. This union prevents normal eruptive movement and can lead to infra-occlusion or difficulty in tooth extraction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AKT1 E17K",
    "long_answer": "Proteus syndrome is caused by a somatic, activating missense mutation in the serine-threonine kinase gene AKT1—specifically the c.49G>A (p.Glu17Lys, E17K) substitution. This mutation constitutively activates AKT1 within the PI3K–AKT signaling pathway, driving the segmental overgrowth and tissue dysregulation characteristic of Proteus syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clinical mimicry",
    "long_answer": "Psychosis precipitated by stimulants such as amphetamine or cocaine produces positive symptoms—paranoid delusions, auditory hallucinations, thought disorder—that are virtually indistinguishable from an acute episode of paranoid schizophrenia. Because the phenomenology, course, and neurochemical basis (excess dopaminergic transmission) are so similar, stimulant psychosis is often used as an experimental model for schizophrenia, and heavy stimulant use can precipitate or unmask latent schizophrenic illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCHE gene",
    "long_answer": "Pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) deficiency results from variant alleles of the BCHE gene, which encodes the plasma enzyme butyrylcholinesterase. Mutations such as the ‘atypical’ (Dibucaine-resistant) and ‘fluoride-resistant’ alleles reduce or abolish enzyme activity, leading to prolonged apnea after succinylcholine or other ester anesthetics. Thus, allelic variants of BCHE are the molecular basis of pseudocholinesterase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Addison's disease",
    "long_answer": "Eugene Merle Shoemaker was diagnosed with Addison's disease, a primary adrenal insufficiency in which the adrenal cortex fails to produce adequate cortisol and aldosterone. Because astronauts must tolerate extreme physical stress and cannot risk an adrenal crisis, this chronic adrenal gland disorder disqualified him from astronaut training, thereby preventing him from becoming the first geologist to walk on the Moon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ORC1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in ORC1, which encodes the largest subunit of the Origin-Recognition Complex (a core component of the DNA pre-replication complex), have been shown to cause a form of primordial dwarfism known as Meier-Gorlin syndrome. Defective ORC1 impairs origin licensing during S-phase entry, leading to global reductions in cell proliferation and the extreme proportionate growth failure characteristic of primordial dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Desmin-related myofibrillar myopathy (desminopathy) is a rare genetic neuromuscular disorder. The primary specialist who evaluates, diagnoses and follows these patients is a neuromuscular physician—typically a neurologist, clinical geneticist or physiatrist with expertise in muscle disease. The diagnostic work-up centers on history and neurologic examination, serum creatine kinase, electromyography, muscle biopsy with histopathology/immunohistochemistry, and confirmatory DES gene sequencing. Muscle MRI or CT can provide supportive information on patterns of muscle involvement, and those images are interpreted by a radiologist; however, radiology plays an ancillary role. The radiologist is therefore not considered the primary specialist responsible for making the diagnosis. Consequently, stating that a radiologist would be the primary specialist involved in diagnosing desmin-related myofibrillar myopathy is not consistent with established clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-Mi-2 antibodies",
    "long_answer": "Dermatomyositis is frequently associated with the presence of anti-Mi-2 autoantibodies. These IgG antibodies target the Mi-2 component of the nucleosome-remodeling deacetylase (NuRD) complex in cell nuclei. Because anti-Mi-2 antibodies are found in a significant subset of dermatomyositis patients and are highly specific for this inflammatory myopathy, they serve as reliable serological biomarkers for diagnosis and disease subset classification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Multiple well-documented lifestyle factors heighten the likelihood of diabetic nephropathy in people with diabetes. Cigarette smoking accelerates intrarenal hemodynamic changes and independently predicts faster decline in glomerular filtration rate. Persistently poor glycaemic control—often related to unhealthy diet, physical inactivity, and obesity—directly increases hyper-filtration injury and the development of micro-albuminuria. High blood pressure, which can be aggravated by excess dietary sodium, contributes synergistically to renal damage. Epidemiologic and interventional studies consistently show that patients who quit smoking, maintain tight glycaemic control through balanced diet and regular exercise, limit sodium, and keep body weight and blood pressure in recommended ranges have a significantly lower incidence and slower progression of diabetic nephropathy. Therefore, certain modifiable lifestyle habits do increase the risk of developing the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "When lipomas in familial multiple lipomatosis become large, lie deep, or appear to infiltrate muscle fibres or neurovascular structures, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is employed because it provides superior soft-tissue contrast and delineates the extent of intramuscular or neurovascular involvement, guiding diagnosis and surgical planning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome is an autosomal-recessive DNA-repair disorder characterized clinically by growth failure, microcephaly, photosensitivity, neurodevelopmental delay and premature ageing. Pathological studies show diffuse white-matter hypomyelination, basal-ganglia and cerebellar calcifications, cortical and cerebellar atrophy, neuronal loss and astrogliosis. Deposition of β-amyloid and formation of Alzheimer-type neuritic (senile) plaques are not a recognized or common feature of Cockayne syndrome. Amyloid plaques are instead a hallmark of Alzheimer disease and certain other amyloidogenic disorders, not of Cockayne syndrome. Therefore, the statement that amyloid plaques are commonly seen in patients with Cockayne syndrome is not supported by established biomedical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent urinary tract infections",
    "long_answer": "In a megaureter the ureter is markedly dilated and its distal segment exhibits impaired peristalsis; this functional obstruction causes urinary stasis. Stagnant urine provides an excellent milieu for bacterial growth, so patients are prone to repeated ascending infections—i.e., recurrent urinary tract infections—which is one of the main recognized complications of an obstructive megaureter."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SF3B4 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Most individuals with Nager syndrome carry heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions in the SF3B4 gene, which encodes the splicing factor 3B subunit 4 involved in pre-mRNA processing. Disruption of SF3B4 impairs normal craniofacial and limb development, making these mutations the genetic alteration most often linked to Nager acrofacial dysostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cimetidine",
    "long_answer": "Dermatographic urticaria (dermographism) is mediated by histamine released from cutaneous mast cells. While H1-antihistamines are first-line therapy, adding an H2-receptor antagonist can improve control. Cimetidine, a well-studied H2 antagonist, blocks histamine H2 receptors on dermal vasculature and mast cells, thereby reducing wheal formation and pruritus in dermatographic urticaria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningoencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Tick-borne encephalitis virus may progress from an initial febrile phase to infection of the central nervous system; involvement of both the meninges and brain parenchyma produces meningoencephalitis. This severe complication is marked by high fever, altered consciousness, seizures, and can leave permanent neurological deficits or be fatal, making it one of the gravest outcomes of tick-borne encephalitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL-3, IL-5, GM-CSF",
    "long_answer": "In eosinophilic gastroenteritis a Th2-skewed immune response leads to over-expression of the cytokines interleukin-3 (IL-3), interleukin-5 (IL-5) and granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF).  These three cytokines share a common β-receptor chain and together promote eosinophil chemotaxis, activation, survival and degranulation in the gastrointestinal mucosa, explaining the tissue eosinophilia characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STAC3",
    "long_answer": "Native American myopathy is a congenital myopathy first described in the Lumbee tribe and is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the STAC3 gene (SH3 and cysteine-rich domain 3). STAC3 is critical for skeletal-muscle excitation-contraction coupling, and the recurrent STAC3 p.Trp284Ser variant segregates with the characteristic myopathy and malignant hyperthermia susceptibility seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meckel-Gruber syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Meckel-Gruber syndrome is an autosomal-recessive malformation (lethal ciliopathy) whose core findings include severe internal hydrocephalus/occipital encephalocele, cystic dysplastic kidneys and post-axial polydactyly. Autopsy studies show that affected female fetuses frequently have Müllerian duct fusion defects, most commonly a bicornuate or uterus didelphys. Thus the simultaneous presence of a bicornuate uterus with hydrocephalus internus is characteristic of Meckel-Gruber syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coagulopathy",
    "long_answer": "The trauma triad of death is composed of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy. Coagulopathy refers to impaired blood coagulation, leading to uncontrolled bleeding after severe trauma. It develops when massive blood loss, dilution of clotting factors, hypothermia, and acidosis disrupt the normal clotting cascade, making it a pivotal—and often fatal—element of the triad."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant COL1A1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Over 90 % of osteogenesis imperfecta cases are the mild, Type I form, caused by autosomal-dominant loss-of-function mutations in the COL1A1 gene encoding the α1 chain of type I collagen. These null (often nonsense or frameshift) mutations reduce the amount of normal collagen produced, leading to the characteristic bone fragility of the commonest form of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome",
    "long_answer": "Apolipoprotein B deficiency results from mutations in the APOB gene located on chromosome 2 (region 2p24.2). Chromosome 2 is one of the numbered, non-sex chromosomes—an autosome—so the disorder is inherited in an autosomal (typically autosomal-recessive) pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Focal clonic seizures",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures (also termed benign familial neonatal epilepsy) characteristically present in healthy full-term newborns with frequent, brief focal clonic seizures that may migrate from one side of the body to the other and can secondarily generalize. These clonic motor events, typically starting on day 2–3 of life and resolving within weeks, are the hallmark seizure type associated with this inherited KCNQ2/KCNQ3 channelopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Potassium-aggravated myotonia is one of the ‘sodium channel myotonias.’ It is produced by missense mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel α-subunit (NaV1.4). These gain-of-function mutations impair channel inactivation, making muscle fibers hyper-excitable; elevated extracellular potassium further increases this excitability, provoking myotonic stiffness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuromuscular blocking agents",
    "long_answer": "During general anesthesia, IgE-mediated anaphylaxis is most commonly triggered by neuromuscular blocking agents (e.g., succinylcholine, rocuronium, vecuronium). Their quaternary ammonium groups can cross-link pre-existing IgE on mast cells, provoking rapid histamine release and systemic anaphylaxis, making this drug class the leading cause of peri-operative allergic reactions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Six-hour caffeine cutoff",
    "long_answer": "Controlled sleep-laboratory studies (e.g., Drake et al., J Clin Sleep Med 2013) demonstrate that ingesting caffeine up to six hours before bedtime significantly shortens total sleep time and lowers sleep efficiency. Consequently, sleep-hygiene guidelines recommend a ‘six-hour caffeine cutoff’—ceasing caffeine consumption at least six hours prior to intended sleep—to minimize caffeine’s disruptive effects on sleep quality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease",
    "long_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy) produces progressive distal muscle atrophy and sensory loss. The two most common genetic forms involve the PMP22 gene duplication on chromosome 17 (CMT1A) and mutations in the MPZ gene on chromosome 1 (CMT1B), linking the disorder to abnormalities on chromosomes 17 and 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Saethre-Chotzen syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Saethre-Chotzen syndrome (acrocephalosyndactyly type III) is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder produced by pathogenic variants in TWIST1. Clinically it combines: 1) conductive hearing loss from external and middle-ear malformations; 2) characteristic eyelid ptosis; and 3) skeletal anomalies such as craniosynostosis, brachydactyly and syndactyly. This constellation of features makes Saethre-Chotzen syndrome the well-established diagnosis that fits the triad described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TYR (tyrosinase)",
    "long_answer": "The albino phenotype in guinea pigs is produced by mutations at the classical ‘c-locus’, which encodes the enzyme tyrosinase (gene symbol TYR). Loss-of-function alterations in TYR block the first step of melanin biosynthesis, preventing pigment formation in hair, skin and eyes and thereby causing albinism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SKI gene",
    "long_answer": "Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the SKI gene, which is located on chromosome 1p36. Mutations in SKI disrupt TGF-β signalling, producing the craniosynostosis–marfanoid phenotype of the syndrome. Cardiac manifestations of SKI mutations include aortic root dilatation and mitral valve prolapse, linking SKI to both Shprintzen-Goldberg syndrome and mitral valve disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Balloon pulmonary valvuloplasty",
    "long_answer": "For severe pulmonic (pulmonary) stenosis, the evidence-based first-line therapy is percutaneous balloon pulmonary valvuloplasty, in which a balloon catheter is advanced across the stenotic pulmonary valve and inflated to split the fused commissures, relieving the outflow obstruction. It achieves high success rates, low complication risk, and has largely replaced surgical valvotomy as the preferred treatment when the valve is anatomically suitable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXIT procedure",
    "long_answer": "The Ex Utero Intrapartum Treatment (EXIT) procedure is a specialized cesarean-based delivery in which the fetus is partially delivered but kept on placental support while surgeons secure the airway (e.g., intubation, tracheostomy) before complete delivery. It is specifically employed for fetuses with airway-compressing lesions such as large congenital pulmonary airway malformations, ensuring ventilation is established before cord clamping."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ellis-van Creveld syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ellis-van Creveld syndrome is an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia characterized by mesomelic limb shortening, polydactyly, and cardiac anomalies. It results from loss-of-function mutations in the EVC gene (or the adjacent EVC2), which disrupt ciliary Hedgehog signaling required for normal endochondral bone growth. Hence, the disease associated with mesomelic dwarfism due to an EVC mutation is Ellis-van Creveld syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Esophageal varices are dilated submucosal veins in the distal esophagus that develop as a complication of portal hypertension and can cause life-threatening upper gastrointestinal bleeding. Their diagnosis (upper endoscopy) and primary treatments—endoscopic band ligation, sclerotherapy, variceal surveillance, and medical therapy with non-selective β-blockers—are all core procedures of gastroenterologists. Therefore, the specialty most commonly responsible for managing and treating esophageal varices is gastroenterology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Weaver syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the EZH2 gene cause Weaver syndrome, an over-growth disorder characterized by advanced bone age, macrocephaly and distinctive craniofacial features. Among these facial findings are alterations of the palpebral fissures—typically down-slanting or widened—that reflect the syndrome’s impact on peri-ocular development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schwann cells",
    "long_answer": "In peripheral nerve grafting after facial injury, autologous Schwann cells from a donor nerve (e.g., sural nerve) are implanted because these glial cells myelinate peripheral axons and secrete neurotrophic and guidance factors that direct and accelerate regrowth of injured facial nerve fibers, thereby restoring nerve function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Cartilage–hair hypoplasia is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the non-coding RNA gene RMRP (RNA component of mitochondrial RNA-processing endoribonuclease), which is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 9 (9p). Therefore, the disease-associated gene resides on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liposuction",
    "long_answer": "Angiolipomas are benign tumors composed largely of adipose tissue; one accepted management option—particularly when multiple lesions are present—is liposuction, a procedure that removes the fatty component of the tumor through suction-assisted aspiration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vestibular aqueduct",
    "long_answer": "In individuals with Marfanoid habitus (including many with Marfan syndrome), CT of the temporal bone often reveals an enlarged vestibular aqueduct. This structural malformation disrupts endolymphatic fluid dynamics in the inner ear and is a well-recognised cause of early-onset sensorineural hearing impairment in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paediatric neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Walker–Warburg syndrome is a severe congenital muscular dystrophy characterised by profound brain and eye malformations. Disorders of muscle and central nervous system development are diagnosed and followed chiefly by a paediatric neurologist, who performs the neuromuscular examination, orders brain MRI and genetic testing, and coordinates multidisciplinary care for the affected infant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Cystic hygroma (a congenital lymphatic malformation) can enlarge the neck and upper thorax, and in the neonatal period it may cause airway obstruction, respiratory distress, or other complications that demand immediate advanced resuscitation and surgical or ENT intervention. Current perinatal guidelines therefore recommend that fetuses with a prenatally diagnosed cystic hygroma be delivered in a tertiary-care center with a neonatal intensive care unit and ready access to pediatric surgery/airway specialists (sometimes employing an EXIT procedure). Hence, arranging delivery in a NICU-equipped facility is considered appropriate and standard of care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Subcutaneous adipose tissue",
    "long_answer": "Familial partial lipodystrophy is characterized by selective loss of body fat from specific regions. The fat that disappears is the adipose tissue located in the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer) of the integumentary system, leaving the epidermis and dermis largely intact."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketosis",
    "long_answer": "In glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), the inability to mobilize hepatic glycogen limits glucose availability. When carbohydrates are deficient, the body compensates by oxidizing fatty acids, generating excess ketone bodies; hence affected individuals can develop ketosis (and occasionally ketoacidosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American College of Medical Genetics",
    "long_answer": "The American College of Medical Genetics (ACMG) convened an expert panel that, in 2006, issued the Recommended Uniform Screening Panel for U.S. newborns. That list of 29 core disorders specifically includes 3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency, making ACMG the organization that formally recommends newborn screening for this inborn error of leucine metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Broca’s area",
    "long_answer": "Broca’s area, located in the dominant (usually left) posterior inferior frontal gyrus, subserves expressive language. Lesions here not only abolish the ability to speak but also eliminate the ability to write (agraphia) because writing is another form of language output. The region lies immediately anterior to the primary motor cortex for the face and arm; therefore strokes or other damage that involve Broca’s area typically extend into adjacent motor cortex, producing contralateral limb or facial paralysis. Thus the same lesion produces both agraphia and paralysis, linking these two deficits to Broca’s area."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinal pigment epithelium",
    "long_answer": "Central serous chorioretinopathy is caused by focal defects in the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE). Breakdown of the RPE barrier allows choroidal fluid to accumulate under the neurosensory retina, producing the characteristic serous retinal detachment seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine A",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine A, a calcineurin-inhibitor immunosuppressant, has been reported in case studies to induce remission of steroid-refractory eosinophilic cystitis. By blocking T-cell activation and downstream cytokine release, cyclosporine dampens the eosinophil-driven inflammatory response in the bladder wall, leading to symptomatic and histologic improvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bartonella quintana",
    "long_answer": "Bartonella quintana, the agent of trench fever, is well-documented as causing recurrent bloodstream infections and endocarditis in marginalized populations, especially homeless individuals and intravenous drug users who are frequently exposed to body-louse infestation, the main vector of this species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy",
    "long_answer": "When actinomycosis does not respond adequately to prolonged high-dose antibiotic regimens, hyperbaric oxygen therapy can be added. Exposing tissues to 100% oxygen at increased atmospheric pressure raises local oxygen tensions, directly inhibiting the anaerobic Actinomyces organisms and enhancing the bactericidal activity of penicillins, thereby improving outcomes in refractory cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homocystine",
    "long_answer": "Homocystinuria is most commonly caused by deficiency of the enzyme cystathionine β-synthase. This blocks conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine, leading to accumulation of free homocysteine that readily oxidizes to its disulfide form, homocystine. The excess homocystine is water-soluble and is excreted in large amounts in the urine, making it the hallmark substance detected in patients with homocystinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papillitis",
    "long_answer": "Papillitis is the anterior form of optic neuritis in which the inflammatory process involves the optic nerve head (optic disc), producing disc swelling visible on fundoscopic examination. This distinguishes it from retrobulbar neuritis, where inflammation is posterior to the disc and the optic nerve head appears normal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midodrine",
    "long_answer": "Midodrine, an oral alpha-1 adrenergic agonist, is commonly prescribed for patients with recurrent vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope who continue to faint despite non-pharmacologic measures such as hydration, salt loading, and physical counter-pressure maneuvers. By increasing peripheral vascular resistance and venous return, midodrine helps prevent the hypotension and bradycardia that trigger vasovagal episodes, and is therefore recommended when other treatments are insufficient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), particularly with gadolinium contrast, is the first-line and most widely used modality for evaluating suspected spinal tumors because it provides high-resolution, multiplanar visualization of the spinal cord, vertebrae, and surrounding soft tissues without ionizing radiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Levothyroxine therapy",
    "long_answer": "Kocher-Debré-Semelaigne syndrome presents in children with profound hypothyroidism and generalized skeletal-muscle hypertrophy (‘pseudohypertrophy’). The definitive treatment is replacement of the deficient thyroid hormone with levothyroxine; restoring euthyroidism reverses the muscular enlargement and other hypothyroid features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ishihara test",
    "long_answer": "The Ishihara pseudo-isochromatic plate test is the standard clinical tool for detecting and grading color-vision deficiencies. It is routinely used in patients with retinal disorders, including age-related macular degeneration, to document acquired color vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hamate–capitate coalition",
    "long_answer": "Ellis–Van Creveld (chondroectodermal dysplasia) is characterized radiographically by congenital fusion of the hamate and capitate carpal bones. This hamate–capitate coalition is a well-recognized skeletal marker of the syndrome and helps in its diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Overwhelming postsplenectomy infection",
    "long_answer": "Because the spleen is absent, the person is prone to sudden, fulminant sepsis from encapsulated bacteria (e.g., Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae). A medical identification tag instantly informs clinicians of the patient’s asplenia, prompting urgent empiric antibiotics and other prophylactic measures, thereby reducing the high mortality risk of overwhelming postsplenectomy infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTCH1",
    "long_answer": "Nevoid basal-cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome most commonly arises from germ-line mutations in the PTCH1 tumor-suppressor gene, a key receptor in the Hedgehog signaling pathway. Loss-of-function PTCH1 mutations not only predispose to multiple basal-cell carcinomas but are also frequently found in sporadic and syndrome-associated medulloblastomas, especially the desmoplastic subtype, linking the same genetic defect to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Compression stockings",
    "long_answer": "Klippel–Trénaunay syndrome causes venous malformations and limb swelling; wearing graduated compression stockings applies external pressure that improves venous return, limits edema, and reduces pain, making these compression garments a standard conservative measure to relieve KTS symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrioventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "An atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD), also known as an endocardial cushion defect, is frequently linked to chromosomal abnormalities—most notably trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) and other aneuploidies. The combined atrial and ventricular septal gaps with common AV valve allow a large left-to-right shunt, leading to pulmonary over-circulation and heart failure. This volume overload manifests clinically as tachypnea and shortness of breath in infants and children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus infection",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus is the classic cause of a skin infection that can progress from superficial lesions (such as folliculitis, impetigo, or cellulitis) into subcutaneous tissue, fascia, and muscle. If not controlled, the bacterium can spread contiguously or via the bloodstream to the underlying bone, where it provokes an inflammatory reaction called osteomyelitis. Its virulence factors (e.g., protein A, toxins, biofilm formation) enable this deep-tissue invasion and persistence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the TWIST2 gene have been reported in individuals with Ablepharon-macrostomia syndrome. TWIST2 encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor that participates in cranial suture and facial mesenchyme development; consequently, the same mutations have also been documented to cause premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis) in affected patients. Thus, TWIST2 mutation links both Ablepharon macrostomia syndrome and craniosynostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX gene",
    "long_answer": "Léri–Weill dyschondrosteosis is a mesomelic form of dwarfism caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the SHOX (short stature homeobox) gene located in the pseudoautosomal region of the X and Y chromosomes; disruption of SHOX impairs skeletal growth, producing the characteristic short stature and Madelung deformity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Degos disease (malignant atrophic papulosis) typically presents first with pathognomonic porcelain-white, atrophic papules on the skin. These distinctive cutaneous lesions are within the domain of dermatology, and dermatologists are trained to recognize and diagnose such papular eruptions; therefore, a dermatologist is the specialist most likely to make the initial diagnosis of Degos disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gongylonemiasis",
    "long_answer": "Members of the nematode genus Gongylonema (order Spirurida), such as Gongylonema pulchrum, parasitize the upper digestive tract of mammals and occasionally humans. Infection with these ‘gullet worms’ produces the disease termed gongylonemiasis, characterized by serpiginous tunnels in the oral, esophageal or gastric mucosa and associated irritation or foreign-body sensation. Thus, the pathological condition resulting from Gongylonema infection is called gongylonemiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious Disease",
    "long_answer": "Viral hemorrhagic fevers (e.g., Ebola, Lassa, Marburg, Crimean-Congo) are caused by highly contagious viruses that require specialized infection control, isolation, antiviral therapy, and public-health coordination. These responsibilities fall under the internal-medicine subspecialty of Infectious Disease, whose physicians are trained to diagnose, manage, and contain serious viral infections and their complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Blepharospasm is a focal dystonia of the eyelid that is treated with interventions such as botulinum-toxin injections and, if needed, eyelid surgery. These diagnostic and therapeutic procedures are routinely performed by ophthalmologists—especially those trained in oculoplastic surgery—making ophthalmology the specialty responsible for managing blepharospasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Imperforate anus",
    "long_answer": "During normal embryogenesis the urorectal septum splits the common cloaca into a ventral urogenital sinus (future bladder) and a dorsal anorectal canal (future anus). Malformation of this partitioning or failure of the anal membrane to perforate produces an imperforate anus, often with recto-urinary fistulas, making this the characteristic birth defect linked to the process of separating the anus from the bladder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dihydropteridine reductase",
    "long_answer": "Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) is regenerated from its oxidized form by the enzyme dihydropteridine reductase (DHPR). If DHPR is deficient, BH4 levels fall, impairing the BH4-dependent enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase. This blocks conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, so phenylalanine accumulates in the blood (hyperphenylalaninemia), producing a form of ‘malignant’ phenylketonuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exotropia",
    "long_answer": "Exophoria is a latent heterophoria in which the visual axes have a tendency to move outward (away from the midline). When this outward (divergent) deviation becomes manifest, it is termed an exotropia—sometimes called a divergent strabismus or exodeviation. Thus, the specific word that denotes the outward deviation associated with exophoria is “exotropia.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood transfusion",
    "long_answer": "Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria causes rapid complement-mediated destruction of red blood cells. When the hemolysis is brisk enough to produce severe, symptomatic anemia, the standard supportive therapy is transfusion of compatible packed red blood cells to quickly restore oxygen-carrying capacity while hemolysis is controlled by warming and treating any underlying trigger."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandibular second premolar",
    "long_answer": "In cases of hypodontia (congenital absence of one or more teeth, excluding third molars), epidemiological studies consistently show that the mandibular (lower) second premolar is the single tooth most frequently missing. Large population surveys rank it ahead of the maxillary lateral incisor and maxillary second premolar, making the lower second premolar the commonest tooth absent in hypodontia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fragile X syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome is caused by expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome, leading to methylation-mediated silencing of the gene and loss of FMRP protein. It is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability and autism spectrum features. Because it follows X-linked dominant inheritance with variable penetrance (full mutation, premutation, carrier states), families benefit greatly from genetic counseling to assess carrier status, recurrence risk, and related conditions such as Fragile X–associated tremor/ataxia syndrome (FXTAS) and primary ovarian insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling",
    "long_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) obtains placental (chorionic-villous) tissue at 10–12 weeks’ gestation, providing fetal DNA that can be molecularly tested for the SMN1 gene deletion/mutation that causes spinal muscular atrophy, allowing definitive prenatal diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticonvulsants",
    "long_answer": "The neuropathic facial or limb pain produced by a syringobulbia‐related brain-stem cavity is commonly treated with anticonvulsant drugs (e.g., carbamazepine, gabapentin). These agents dampen aberrant neuronal firing by modulating voltage-gated sodium or calcium channels and are well-established first-line therapy for central neuropathic pain syndromes, including the pain arising from syringobulbia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gomori trichrome stain",
    "long_answer": "MELAS is a mitochondrial myopathy that produces ‘ragged-red fibers’ in skeletal muscle. These abnormal subsarcolemmal accumulations of mitochondria become distinctly red when a muscle biopsy is stained with the modified Gomori trichrome stain, making this histological stain the standard test for confirming MELAS and other mitochondrial myopathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "All three germ layers",
    "long_answer": "Teratomas arise from totipotent germ cells; consequently, they can differentiate into and contain mature tissues derived from ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. Classic examples include skin or neural tissue (ectoderm), cartilage or bone (mesoderm), and gut-type epithelium (endoderm). Thus, the tumor’s components reflect contributions from all three embryonic germ layers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aspergillus fumigatus",
    "long_answer": "Patients with chronic granulomatous disease lack a functional NADPH oxidase, impairing their ability to kill catalase-positive microbes. Aspergillus fumigatus is a catalase-positive mold and is the most frequently encountered fungal pathogen in these patients, leading to invasive pulmonary and systemic infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysphagia",
    "long_answer": "Leukodystrophies damage the myelin of central and cranial motor pathways, leading to bulbar muscle weakness and incoordination. This commonly produces dysphagia—difficulty swallowing—which is a well-documented clinical manifestation in many leukodystrophy subtypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clozapine-induced agranulocytosis",
    "long_answer": "In schizoaffective disorder, clozapine is sometimes prescribed when standard antipsychotics are inadequate. Clozapine can suppress bone-marrow function, leading to agranulocytosis or severe neutropenia. A complete blood count is therefore obtained at baseline and monitored regularly to detect any fall in white-blood-cell or absolute neutrophil counts early and prevent life-threatening infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Budesonide",
    "long_answer": "Budesonide, a locally acting glucocorticoid, is the evidence-based first-line treatment for lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis. The same drug is routinely prescribed, in controlled-release oral or rectal formulations, for inflammatory bowel diseases—specifically mild-to-moderate Crohn’s disease (particularly ileocecal involvement) and for ulcerative colitis via oral MMX tablets or enemas—because it delivers high mucosal anti-inflammatory activity with minimal systemic exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optical coherence tomography (OCT)",
    "long_answer": "Macular (foveal) hypoplasia is typically confirmed with optical coherence tomography, which provides high-resolution cross-sectional images of the retina. OCT reveals the characteristic absence of the normal foveal pit and persistent inner retinal layers, making it the standard imaging modality for diagnosing this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antipsychotic drugs",
    "long_answer": "Acute dystonia is a well-recognized extrapyramidal side effect of antipsychotic (neuroleptic) drugs, particularly the typical D2-receptor antagonists such as haloperidol, chlorpromazine, and fluphenazine. By blocking dopamine signaling in the nigrostriatal pathway, these agents disrupt the normal balance between dopaminergic and cholinergic tone in the basal ganglia, leading to painful, sustained muscle contractions characteristic of dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NADPH oxidase",
    "long_answer": "Chronic granulomatous disease is caused by inherited defects in the phagocyte NADPH oxidase complex (most often the gp91-phox subunit). Without functional NADPH oxidase, neutrophils and macrophages cannot generate the superoxide anion and downstream reactive oxygen species that drive the respiratory burst, so ingested bacteria and fungi are not killed effectively."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACSF3",
    "long_answer": "In combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (CMAMMA), the mitochondrial enzyme acyl-CoA synthetase family member 3 (ACSF3) normally catalyses the reversible thio-ester reaction between free methylmalonic acid and CoA, i.e. the interconversion of methylmalonyl-CoA and methylmalonic acid (and similarly for malonate/malonyl-CoA). Loss-of-function mutations in ACSF3 prevent this step, so methylmalonyl-CoA is hydrolysed to, and accumulates as, methylmalonic acid, producing the characteristic organic-aciduria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Specific phobia",
    "long_answer": "Medication (pharmaco-) phobia is considered a specific phobia—one of the DSM-5 anxiety-disorder subtypes—because the core symptom is an excessive, irrational fear confined to a particular object or situation, namely taking prescribed drugs, which leads to avoidance and clinically significant distress."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NINDS",
    "long_answer": "Within the U.S. National Institutes of Health, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS) is the branch that supports and funds basic, translational, and clinical research on encephaloceles and a broad range of other brain and nervous system disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "Two-dimensional and Doppler echocardiography is the standard test to visualise the pulmonary valve, assess leaflet motion, and measure the systolic pressure gradient across the valve; these findings definitively confirm pulmonary valve stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen-storage disease type II) is caused by pathogenic variants in the GAA gene, which encodes acid α-glucosidase. The GAA gene is mapped to chromosome 17, band q25.3; mutations here lead to deficient lysosomal glycogen breakdown and the clinical manifestations of Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stridor",
    "long_answer": "After a laryngospasm episode in patients with spinal and bulbar muscular atrophy (SBMA), partial reopening of the glottis causes turbulent airflow through a still-narrowed upper airway. This turbulent, high-frequency, inspiratory sound is clinically termed stridor, a hallmark of upper-airway obstruction following laryngeal spasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothyroidism",
    "long_answer": "In neutral lipid storage disease—particularly the Chanarin-Dorfman (ichthyotic) form—lipid droplets accumulate in many organs, including the thyroid gland. This infiltration often impairs thyroid function, leading to reduced synthesis of thyroid hormones and a clinical picture of hypothyroidism, which is therefore a well-recognized and frequent endocrine complication of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Moebius syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Moebius syndrome is a congenital cranial nerve disorder classically involving the VI (abducens) and VII (facial) nerves. The VI-nerve palsy produces horizontal ophthalmoparesis/ophthalmoplegia, while reduced fetal facial and limb movement can lead to multiple congenital contractures, i.e., arthrogryposis. Thus, this syndrome links arthrogryposis with ophthalmoparesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Epidermolysis bullosa simplex is an inherited blistering disorder of the skin; its diagnosis, wound care, and long-term management fall under the expertise of dermatologists, the medical specialists who treat diseases of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatal cerebral edema",
    "long_answer": "In familial hemiplegic migraine, especially with CACNA1A or ATP1A2 mutations, even a minor head trauma can trigger massive, diffuse brain swelling. This malignant or fatal cerebral edema may evolve rapidly after a mild concussion, leading to coma and high mortality, making it a recognized severe complication in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthostatic hypotension",
    "long_answer": "Multiple system atrophy commonly leads to neurogenic orthostatic hypotension because of severe autonomic failure. When a patient also has essential hypertension, antihypertensive therapy and impaired baroreflexes can further widen the blood-pressure swing between the supine and standing positions, making orthostatic hypotension more pronounced and symptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prostaglandin E1",
    "long_answer": "In infants with an interrupted aortic arch, systemic blood flow depends on continued patency of the ductus arteriosus. Intravenous infusion of Prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil) relaxes ductal smooth muscle, preventing closure or reopening the ductus, thereby maintaining adequate systemic perfusion until definitive surgical repair can be performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Craniofacial anomaly",
    "long_answer": "Arrhinia, the congenital absence of the nose, arises from disrupted mid‐facial development during embryogenesis. Because it affects the formation of facial structures derived from the craniofacial complex, it is classified as a craniofacial anomaly (a subset of craniofacial malformations)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iatrogenic disease",
    "long_answer": "An iatrogenic disease is any disorder, injury, or complication that arises as a direct result of medical intervention—such as surgery, drugs, or diagnostic procedures. Infections like postoperative osteomyelitis exemplify iatrogenic conditions because they are provoked by the treatment itself rather than the underlying illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the heart’s endocardial surface and valves, so its diagnosis (e.g., physical examination for murmurs, echocardiography, Duke criteria) and overall management (hemodynamic assessment, valve-related complications, coordination with cardiac surgery) are directed by cardiology specialists, who work with infectious disease consultants for antibiotic selection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deciduous teeth",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Down syndrome frequently exhibit delayed exfoliation of the primary (deciduous) teeth. Root resorption and eruption timing are generally slowed in this chromosomal condition, so baby teeth are retained longer than expected before permanent successors emerge."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) is a 4-repeat tauopathy, so abnormal accumulation of phosphorylated tau protein is a core pathological feature. However, the brain regions predominantly affected are the brain-stem (especially the midbrain), basal ganglia structures such as the globus pallidus and subthalamic nucleus, and frontal cortical areas—not the cerebellum. Although the cerebellar dentate nucleus can show some tau pathology, it is not the primary or most severely affected site, nor does cerebellar dysfunction drive the characteristic clinical picture of axial rigidity, vertical gaze palsy and early falls. Therefore the statement that PSP “primarily affects the cerebellum due to tau protein accumulation” is inconsistent with established neuropathological consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EED",
    "long_answer": "Cohen-Gibson syndrome is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the EED gene. EED encodes a core component of Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2); pathogenic variants impair PRC2’s histone H3K27 methyltransferase activity, leading to the dysregulated gene expression and overgrowth features characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Childhood",
    "long_answer": "Recurrent aphthous stomatitis (canker sores) characteristically has its first onset during childhood, often before the second decade of life, and new cases are uncommon after about age 30."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tau protein",
    "long_answer": "In Alzheimer’s disease, aggregation of hyper-phosphorylated tau protein inside neurons forms neurofibrillary tangles. PET imaging and CSF assays that quantify tau are closely monitored because regional tau burden correlates strongly with neuronal loss and cerebral cortex atrophy, making tau a key protein tracked for its connection to brain volume loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation",
    "long_answer": "In pheochromocytoma, excess circulating catecholamines act on both α- and β-adrenergic receptors. Giving a β-blocker first removes the vasodilatory/chronotropic β-receptor effects, leaving catecholamines to act solely on α-receptors. This unopposed α-adrenergic stimulation causes intense systemic vasoconstriction, abrupt hypertensive crisis, and possible cardiovascular collapse, so β-blockers are avoided unless adequate α-blockade is established beforehand."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aberrant right subclavian artery",
    "long_answer": "In this anomaly—also called arteria lusoria—the right fourth aortic arch and proximal right dorsal aorta involute, so the right subclavian artery instead develops from the left dorsal aorta distal to the left subclavian. The vessel then courses posterior to the trachea-esophagus to reach the right arm, making it a classic congenital aortic arch variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Links actin to sarcolemma",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophin forms a key part of the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex, binding cortical actin filaments inside the muscle fiber to transmembrane β-dystroglycan, which in turn connects to the extracellular matrix. This linkage stabilizes the sarcolemma and distributes mechanical stress during muscle contraction and relaxation; its absence leads to membrane fragility and the myofiber degeneration seen in Duchenne muscular dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDKN2A (p16)",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma almost universally harbors inactivating mutations or deletions in the CDKN2A gene, which encodes the p16INK4A tumor-suppressor protein. p16 is a key regulator of cell-cycle control: it inhibits cyclin-dependent kinases 4 and 6 (CDK4/6), maintaining the retinoblastoma (RB) protein in its active, hypophosphorylated state and thereby enforcing the G1-to-S checkpoint. Loss of CDKN2A disrupts this pathway, permitting unchecked cell-cycle progression and contributing to pancreatic tumorigenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperammonemia",
    "long_answer": "Excess blood ammonia (hyperammonemia) arises when the urea cycle does not function properly. This can occur congenitally from inborn deficiencies of urea-cycle enzymes such as ornithine transcarbamylase, carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase I or others. It can also be acquired in severe liver disease (e.g., cirrhosis, fulminant hepatitis), because the diseased liver cannot convert ammonia to urea efficiently. Thus the same metabolic disturbance—hyperammonemia—results from both a genetic enzyme deficiency and liver dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "In dysautonomia, malfunction of the autonomic nervous system can cause excessive sympathetic drive or inadequate parasympathetic modulation. This often produces an inappropriately rapid heart rate—tachycardia—especially when changing posture (as in postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome, POTS). Hence, episodic or sustained tachycardia is a well-recognized, common cardiac symptom of dysautonomia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C→T transition",
    "long_answer": "In Wieacker (Wieacker‐Wolff) syndrome, the most severe – frequently prenatal-lethal – presentations are produced by nonsense mutations in the X-linked ZC4H2 gene. These lethal nonsense alleles typically arise from a cytosine-to-thymine (C→T) single-base transition, which converts an arginine-coding CGA triplet into the premature stop codon TGA, truncating the protein and leading to the lethal phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal phalanges",
    "long_answer": "The osteodystrophy seen in DOOR syndrome chiefly involves hypoplasia or absence of the distal phalanges of the fingers and toes—the terminal bones underlying the nails—leading to the characteristic nail and skeletal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tenosynovial giant cell tumor",
    "long_answer": "Tenosynovial giant cell tumor (TGCT)—historically called pigmented villonodular synovitis—is a soft-tissue neoplasm driven by a t(1;2) translocation that places the CSF1 gene under control of COL6A3 regulatory elements. The resulting overexpression of colony-stimulating factor 1 (CSF1) recruits and stimulates macrophages, producing the characteristic tumor mass in synovium, tendon sheath, or adjacent soft tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Borrelial lymphocytoma",
    "long_answer": "Borrelial lymphocytoma (also called lymphadenosis benigna cutis) is a benign, localized B-cell–rich skin infiltrate caused by Borrelia burgdorferi infection in Lyme disease. Although it more often appears on the earlobe or nipple, in adult males it can develop on the scrotum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercalcemia",
    "long_answer": "Persistent elevation of serum calcium (hypercalcemia) can impair the kidney’s ability to concentrate urine by down-regulating aquaporin-2 water channels in the collecting duct and reducing the responsiveness of tubular cells to antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin). This electrolyte disturbance is therefore a well-recognized, established cause of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Patrick Henry Hughes was born with arthrogryposis multiplex congenita, a disorder causing multiple congenital joint contractures that limit movement of the arms and legs. Management of arthrogryposis— including corrective surgery, bracing, and long-term musculoskeletal care— is handled by physicians trained in orthopedic surgery (often pediatric orthopedics). Therefore, the medical specialty most likely to treat his condition is orthopedic surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "hip bone",
    "long_answer": "The hip bone (os coxae) is formed by the fusion of the ilium, ischium, and pubis. It is one of the skeletal sites where hereditary multiple osteochondromas (also called hereditary multiple exostoses) frequently develop, producing cartilage-capped bony outgrowths along its surface."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome is a chromosomal disorder in which affected females have a complete or partial monosomy of the X chromosome (karyotype 45,X or variants). Because many individuals have mild or non-specific physical findings, the diagnosis is frequently made incidentally during evaluation for short stature, primary amenorrhea, or prenatal genetic screening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchodilators",
    "long_answer": "In lymphangioleiomyomatosis, proliferation of abnormal smooth-muscle cells can narrow small airways and cause airflow obstruction. Inhaled bronchodilators—typically β2-adrenergic agonists or other bronchodilator agents—are often prescribed to relax bronchial smooth muscle and improve expiratory flow, thereby alleviating obstructive lung symptoms such as dyspnea and wheezing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rash",
    "long_answer": "Infection with Sindbis or Sindbis-like alphaviruses (e.g., Ockelbo or Pogosta strains) commonly produces a febrile illness marked by a maculopapular skin rash; this cutaneous eruption is one of the most frequent and characteristic symptoms alongside fever and joint pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)",
    "long_answer": "In hyperprolactinemia, elevated prolactin suppresses hypothalamic GnRH release, resulting in reduced anterior-pituitary secretion of the gonadotropins. One of these, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), is essential for promoting the growth and maturation of ovarian follicles; therefore its secretion is inhibited when prolactin levels are abnormally high."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Putamen",
    "long_answer": "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (caused by JC polyomavirus) produces demyelinating lesions that extend from sub-cortical white matter into deep gray structures. Among the basal ganglia, the putamen is a well-documented site of involvement, showing characteristic T2/FLAIR hyperintensity on MRI in PML patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Paresis is a partial loss of motor strength that arises from injury or disease of the central or peripheral nervous system. Neurologists are the specialists trained to evaluate neurological deficits, perform diagnostic tests such as neuro-imaging and electromyography, determine the underlying cause, and manage or treat conditions that produce paresis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unilateral cerebral atrophy",
    "long_answer": "In Rasmussen's encephalitis, the chronic T-cell–mediated inflammation selectively attacks one cerebral hemisphere. This ongoing immune assault destroys cortical neurons and supporting glia, leading to progressive shrinkage of that hemisphere—i.e., unilateral cerebral atrophy—accompanied by hemiparesis, cognitive decline, and focal seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxic megacolon",
    "long_answer": "In ulcerative colitis, massive inflammatory damage can paralyze the colon, causing marked dilation (≥6 cm), systemic toxicity, and risk of perforation—a condition called toxic megacolon. Because medical therapy often fails and mortality rises rapidly once perforation or sepsis ensues, urgent subtotal colectomy is the standard life-saving surgical treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Argininosuccinic aciduria is an inborn error of the urea-cycle caused by deficiency of argininosuccinate lyase. Diagnosis, dietary and pharmacologic nitrogen-scavenging therapy, and genetic counseling for this metabolic disorder are typically coordinated by clinicians trained in medical/clinical genetics (often titled metabolic geneticists or pediatric metabolic specialists). Therefore, the specialty most likely to manage these patients is medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Merkel cell polyomavirus",
    "long_answer": "Merkel cell polyomavirus (MCPyV) is clonally integrated and drives most Merkel cell carcinomas, and viral DNA has also been detected in lesions of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (e.g., mycosis fungoides/Sezary syndrome), linking the same virus to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathyroid hormone 1 receptor (PTH1R)",
    "long_answer": "Blomstrand chondrodysplasia is an autosomal-recessive, perinatally lethal skeletal disorder that results from loss-of-function mutations in the parathyroid hormone/parathyroid hormone-related peptide type-1 receptor (PTH1R). The defective PTH1R impairs signaling of both PTH and PTHrP, leading to premature endochondral ossification and the characteristic skeletal abnormalities seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "REM sleep behavior disorder",
    "long_answer": "REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is one of the four core clinical features in the diagnostic criteria for dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB). In RBD, the normal muscle atonia of REM sleep is lost, leading patients to act out their dreams; its presence strongly supports a diagnosis of DLB because Lewy body pathology frequently affects brainstem structures that regulate REM-sleep atonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TorsinA",
    "long_answer": "The DYT1 gene (TOR1A) encodes the protein torsinA. TorsinA is an evolutionarily conserved member of the AAA+ (ATPases Associated with diverse cellular Activities) family, and the characteristic GAG-deletion (ΔE) mutation in DYT1 dystonia alters this ATPase, leading to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesenteric angiography",
    "long_answer": "Selective mesenteric angiography is considered the gold-standard test for acute intestinal (mesenteric) ischemia because it directly visualizes the superior and inferior mesenteric arterial flow, detects arterial occlusion, emboli, or vasospasm in real time, and can simultaneously permit endovascular therapy. No other modality matches its diagnostic accuracy in the acute setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Procarbazine",
    "long_answer": "Procarbazine, an alkylating-like agent used in Hodgkin’s lymphoma regimens such as MOPP, is well documented to induce secondary myelodysplastic changes and therapy-related (subleukemic) acute myeloid leukemia years after treatment, making it a recognized leukemogenic risk factor in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS13B (COH1)",
    "long_answer": "Cohen syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13B gene (also known as COH1). VPS13B encodes a large transmembrane protein involved in Golgi trafficking; its disruption leads to the characteristic clinical features of Cohen syndrome, including ocular manifestations such as high myopia, retinal dystrophy and chorioretinitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superior mesenteric artery syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rapid weight loss in anorexia nervosa depletes the mesenteric fat pad, narrowing the angle between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. This compresses the third portion of the duodenum, producing Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA) syndrome—a gastrointestinal obstruction classically associated with anorexia nervosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPA1",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant optic atrophy is most commonly caused by pathogenic mutations in the OPA1 gene, which encodes a mitochondrial dynamin-like GTPase. OPA1 is highly expressed in retinal ganglion cells and is essential for mitochondrial fusion and maintenance of mitochondrial membrane potential. Loss-of-function OPA1 mutations disrupt mitochondrial dynamics, leading to selective degeneration of retinal ganglion cells and the characteristic visual loss seen in dominant optic atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SERPING1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary angioedema (HAE) types I and II result from deficiency or dysfunction of the C1 esterase inhibitor. Both forms are caused by pathogenic variants in the SERPING1 gene, which encodes the C1-inhibitor protein; therefore, detecting a SERPING1 mutation is the molecular diagnostic hallmark for HAE types I and II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular zone",
    "long_answer": "In the embryonic brain, neural stem cells that give rise to cortical neurons reside along the wall of the cerebral ventricles in a germinal region called the ventricular zone. Mutations causing lissencephaly disrupt these ventricular-zone radial glial stem cells, impairing their ability to proliferate and guide neuronal migration, which leads to the characteristic smooth (agyral) cortex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly-nystagmus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly-nystagmus syndrome (OMIM #609346) is a rare hereditary condition in which congenital brachydactyly is accompanied by congenital nystagmus. Clinical follow-up of affected families has shown that many patients later manifest progressive cerebellar ataxia, establishing cerebellar involvement as a characteristic component of the disorder’s phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nodular sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "In developed countries, the nodular sclerosis variant of classical Hodgkin lymphoma accounts for the largest proportion of cases—typically around 60–70 %. Its characteristic lacunar Reed-Sternberg cells and collagen banding distinguish it histologically, and epidemiologic surveys consistently show it as the predominant subtype in these populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium channel blockers",
    "long_answer": "In idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension, acute vasodilator testing identifies a small subset (≈5–10%) of patients who are vasoreactive. For these responders, high-dose oral calcium channel blockers such as nifedipine, diltiazem, or amlodipine are recommended because they produce sustained pulmonary arterial vasodilation and improve hemodynamics and symptoms. Other drug classes are reserved for non-responders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Queens",
    "long_answer": "Rickettsialpox was first identified after a 1946 outbreak among residents of an apartment complex in Kew Gardens, Queens, New York City. Investigation by Dr. Robert Huebner traced the illness to the mite-borne bacterium Rickettsia akari, making Queens the borough where the initial outbreak occurred."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basilar invagination",
    "long_answer": "Basilar invagination (also called basilar impression) is a cranio-vertebral junction abnormality in which the odontoid process and body of the C2 vertebra migrate upward through the foramen magnum. This superior displacement narrows the foramen magnum lumen and can compress the medulla and upper cervical spinal cord."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sushruta",
    "long_answer": "The 6th-century BCE Indian surgeon Sushruta, author of the classical treatise Sushruta Samhita, gave one of the earliest systematic descriptions of leprosy (‘kushtha’). He classified its clinical forms, discussed its contagious nature, and prescribed therapies such as purification procedures and chaulmoogra-seed preparations, making him the ancient physician most often credited with the first detailed account and treatment guidelines for leprosy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The human CPOX gene (coproporphyrinogen oxidase), whose variants cause hereditary coproporphyria, has been mapped to the long arm of chromosome 3 (region q11-q12)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Froehlich syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Froehlich syndrome (also called adiposogenital dystrophy) occurs when a tumor or other lesion of the hypothalamus disrupts its regulatory control over appetite and anterior pituitary function. Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamic nuclei removes the normal satiety signal, producing marked hyperphagia and obesity, while interference with hypothalamic releasing hormones lowers secretion of pituitary gonadotropins and other trophic hormones—reflected historically by very low urinary excretion of these pituitary hormones. Hence, a hypothalamic tumor producing increased appetite together with low urinary pituitary hormones typifies Froehlich syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious disease",
    "long_answer": "Bolivian hemorrhagic fever is a viral hemorrhagic fever caused by the Machupo arenavirus. Diagnosis requires specialized virologic testing and management involves high-level infection control, antiviral or supportive care, and outbreak containment—all core responsibilities of physicians trained in the specialty of infectious diseases (often with tropical medicine expertise)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary xanthinuria is a rare purine-metabolism disorder caused by the congenital absence or severe deficiency of the enzyme xanthine oxidoreductase (also called xanthine dehydrogenase/oxidase). Loss of this enzyme blocks the conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid, resulting in markedly low serum and urinary uric acid and accumulation of xanthine that can precipitate as renal or urinary tract stones. Type I xanthinuria is due solely to xanthine oxidase deficiency, while Type II also involves aldehyde oxidase. Thus, xanthinuria is indeed characterized by xanthine oxidase deficiency, in accord with established biochemical and clinical descriptions of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Lipodystrophy is a syndrome in which adipose tissue is partially or totally lost (or degenerates) in different regions of the body. The resulting severe insulin resistance, hypertriglyceridaemia and renal involvement frequently produce secondary (non-essential) hypertension, so lipodystrophy is recognized as one of the endocrine/metabolic causes of secondary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Helicobacter pylori",
    "long_answer": "Helicobacter pylori is the spiral-shaped bacterium that colonises the stomach lining and is the main infectious cause of peptic (stomach) ulcers in humans. While human-to-human spread is most common, several studies have demonstrated that domestic cats can carry H. pylori in their gastric mucosa and shed the organism, allowing zoonotic transmission from cats to people."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burkitt lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Burkitt lymphoma is a high-grade B-cell lymphoma with a well-established association with latent Epstein–Barr virus infection (particularly in the endemic African form). Standard curative regimens (e.g., CODOX-M/IVAC) include the nucleoside analog cytarabine (ara-C), and other nucleoside analogs are active in this disease, so EBV-driven Burkitt lymphoma is characteristically treated with nucleoside-analog–containing chemotherapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function variants in the DNMT3A gene cause Tatton-Brown-Rahman syndrome (TBRS), a congenital overgrowth–intellectual disability disorder. Clinical descriptions of TBRS repeatedly note seizures, particularly febrile seizures, in affected children. Thus, TBRS is the DNMT3A-associated genetic disorder that can present with febrile seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leflunomide",
    "long_answer": "Leflunomide, an immunosuppressive agent used in transplant medicine and rheumatology, inhibits pyrimidine synthesis and has demonstrated direct antiviral activity against BK virus. It is therefore employed as a potential therapeutic option for BK virus-associated nephropathy while simultaneously allowing some maintenance of immunosuppression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula",
    "long_answer": "Foix–Alajouanine syndrome is a progressive congestive myelopathy that results from a spinal dural arteriovenous fistula (a type of arteriovenous malformation). The abnormal shunting of arterial blood into the spinal venous system produces venous hypertension, impaired cord perfusion, and ultimately necrotizing myelopathy, which defines the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Phosphofructokinase (PFK) deficiency—glycogen-storage disease type VII—has an infantile variant in which the enzyme defect affects not only muscle and red blood cells but also heart and brain. Published case reports describe infants with generalized seizures that fulfill diagnostic criteria for epilepsy; this neurological disorder is therefore listed among the recognized symptoms of the infantile form of PFK deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellar ataxia",
    "long_answer": "Premature fusion of cranial sutures can restrict posterior-fossa growth, elevate intracranial pressure, or produce a Chiari I malformation, all of which may impair cerebellar function. The resulting neurological complication is cerebellar ataxia—uncoordinated gait and limb movements that arise when the cerebellum is compromised."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dura mater",
    "long_answer": "Although infantile myofibromatosis most often involves skin, muscle, and bone, the tumor can occasionally extend into the central nervous system; when it does, it characteristically forms masses attached to the dura mater, the outer meningeal layer surrounding the brain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain",
    "long_answer": "Acute myeloid leukemia can infiltrate the central nervous system, most notably the brain. The brain is enclosed and protected by the meninges (dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater), making it the body part that satisfies both conditions specified in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDF5",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly type C is most commonly caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the GDF5 gene (Growth Differentiation Factor 5), located on chromosome 20q11.2. GDF5 encodes a bone morphogenetic protein that is critical for cartilage and bone development; mutations disrupt signaling required for digital growth, leading to the shortened middle phalanges and characteristic hand anomalies seen in brachydactyly type C."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTPA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TTPA gene, which encodes the α-tocopherol transfer protein, prevent normal incorporation of dietary vitamin E into plasma lipoproteins. Although the individual consumes adequate vitamin E, the defect causes systemic vitamin E deficiency that leads to a progressive neurodegenerative disorder known as ataxia with vitamin E deficiency (AVED)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmoid tumor",
    "long_answer": "Gardner’s syndrome, a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis, characteristically includes desmoid tumors—also called aggressive fibromatoses. These are benign, non-metastasizing tumors composed of proliferating fibroblasts and collagen that arise from musculo-aponeurotic fibrous tissue. Their strong association with Gardner’s syndrome makes the desmoid tumor the recognized benign fibrous tumor in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNN4",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary xerocytosis (dehydrated hereditary stomatocytosis) can result from gain-of-function mutations in the KCNN4 gene. KCNN4 encodes the intermediate-conductance, calcium-activated potassium channel KCa3.1 (also called the Gardos channel). Because the channel opens in response to intracellular Ca²⁺, pathogenic KCNN4 variants increase K⁺ efflux from red blood cells, leading to cellular dehydration and the xerocytosis phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Photophobia",
    "long_answer": "In Meige’s syndrome, patients often report photophobia, an abnormal intolerance or sensitivity to light that accompanies the condition’s characteristic blepharospasm and other cranial dystonias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Congenital Dyserythropoietic Anemia type II is caused by intrinsic defects in erythroid precursors (typically SEC23B mutations). Because the underlying marrow produces defective red cells, replacing the patient’s hematopoietic system with donor stem cells can eliminate the disease. Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation provides a new, genetically normal erythropoiesis and is therefore considered the only potentially curative therapy for CDA II, whereas supportive measures like transfusion or iron chelation merely mitigate complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "In cutis laxa, the abnormal elastin in connective tissues can involve internal organs as well as the skin. The lungs are commonly affected, leading to early-onset emphysema because their elastic fibers are crucial for normal alveolar recoil and gas exchange. Thus, the lungs—whose primary function is to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide—are an organ that can be compromised in cutis laxa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nystagmus",
    "long_answer": "Split hand split foot-nystagmus syndrome is a rare congenital disorder characterized by ectrodactyly (split hand/foot malformation) together with the ocular finding of congenital nystagmus—an involuntary, rhythmic oscillation of the eyes. The presence of nystagmus is a defining ophthalmic feature consistently reported in patients with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Opportunistic infections",
    "long_answer": "High-dose corticosteroids and other potent immunosuppressants used to treat severe graft-versus-host disease markedly impair cellular and humoral immunity, allowing latent or environmental pathogens (e.g., CMV, EBV, invasive fungi, Pneumocystis) to reactivate or invade. Consequently, life-threatening opportunistic infections are a well-recognized complication of the intense immunosuppression required to control GVHD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liebenberg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Liebenberg syndrome is an autosomal-dominant limb malformation disorder produced by deletions or translocations in the regulatory region located upstream of the PITX1 gene. These structural variants dysregulate PITX1 expression during limb development, leading to features such as elbow dysplasia, radial head dislocation, brachydactyly and characteristic finger flexion deformities (camptodactyly)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-resolution computed tomography",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease is most readily identified non-invasively by high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the chest, which characteristically shows interlobular septal thickening, centrilobular ground-glass opacities and mediastinal lymphadenopathy—findings that support the diagnosis without the need for lung biopsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cowden syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cowden syndrome (PTEN hamartoma-tumor syndrome) is characterized by mucocutaneous manifestations such as papillomatous papules, cobblestone oral mucosa, and a markedly fissured tongue. Germ-line mutations in the PTEN tumor-suppressor gene underlie the syndrome. Carriers have a greatly elevated lifetime risk of several malignancies, most notably female breast cancer (up to 50–85% risk), making Cowden syndrome the established link between fissured tongue and increased breast-cancer susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Succinyl-CoA",
    "long_answer": "Propionyl-CoA is first carboxylated to methylmalonyl-CoA, which is then rearranged by the enzyme methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. This mutase requires adenosyl-cobalamin (vitamin B12) as a cofactor to convert methylmalonyl-CoA into the citric-acid-cycle intermediate succinyl-CoA. Thus, succinyl-CoA is the TCA cycle intermediate whose synthesis from propionyl-CoA depends on vitamin B12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAPSS2",
    "long_answer": "The Pakistani type of spondylo-epimetaphyseal dysplasia is an autosomal-recessive skeletal disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PAPSS2 gene (3′-phosphoadenosine 5′-phosphosulfate synthase 2). Defective PAPSS2 impairs synthesis of the universal sulfate donor PAPS, reducing sulfation of cartilage proteoglycans and leading to the characteristic epiphyseal, metaphyseal, and vertebral abnormalities seen in this form of SEMD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Patterson (Kelly-Patterson/Plummer-Vinson) syndrome—characterized by iron-deficiency anaemia, dysphagia and esophageal webs—confers a high risk of malignant change in the upper aerodigestive tract, most characteristically development of post-cricoid and upper esophageal squamous cell carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Under 1 per million",
    "long_answer": "AREDYLD syndrome is extraordinarily rare; Orphanet and the NIH Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center list its prevalence as “<1/1,000,000.” At that frequency, fewer than one person per million residents—only a handful of individuals at most—would be expected to have the disorder in the entire United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium acetate",
    "long_answer": "Soaking calcium carbonate (e.g., limestone or eggshells) in vinegar (acetic acid) converts it to calcium acetate. Orally, calcium acetate serves as a phosphate-binding agent, lowering serum phosphate levels in patients with chronic kidney disease and thereby helping prevent renal osteodystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "In severe or malignant forms, hypertension—especially in obstetric settings such as pre-eclampsia/eclampsia—can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). DIC is marked by systemic activation of the coagulation cascade, forming microthrombi throughout the vasculature and consuming platelets and clotting factors, which paradoxically leads to both widespread clotting and severe bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metabolic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Neonatal infection (sepsis) can cause tissue hypoxia and increased lactic acid production, consuming bicarbonate buffers. The accumulation of hydrogen ions drives arterial and venous blood pH below the normal range (< 7.35), producing the acid–base disturbance known as metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Churg-Strauss syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Churg-Strauss syndrome, now termed eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, is an ANCA-associated small-vessel autoimmune vasculitis classically characterized by severe asthma, peripheral eosinophilia, and systemic granulomatous inflammation. Asthma is often the earliest and most persistent clinical feature, linking this autoimmune disease directly with the respiratory condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome is a chromosomal disorder caused by the presence of an extra copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21). It is the most frequent live-born aneuploidy, readily identified through karyotyping or targeted prenatal screening, and is routinely managed in medical genetics clinics because of its characteristic physical features, intellectual disability, and associated health complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "In infants with situs ambiguus (heterotaxy), cyanosis—a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes—signals systemic hypoxemia caused by the congenital heart malformations that frequently accompany this condition. Because cyanosis results from inadequate oxygenated blood reaching the systemic circulation, it reflects a circulatory rather than a gastrointestinal or hepatic problem, making it the key symptom that indicates a systemic or circulatory issue in situs ambiguus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Hydrops-ectopic calcification-moth-eaten skeletal dysplasia (also called Greenberg dysplasia) is a rare, usually lethal, autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia caused by mutations in the LBR gene. Diagnosing and managing such congenital skeletal disorders, providing molecular testing, and offering genetic counselling for affected families are the domain of clinical/medical geneticists; therefore, a paediatric medical genetics service would be the specialty most often involved in these cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryptococcal meningitis",
    "long_answer": "Cryptococcus neoformans infection of the meninges (cryptococcal meningitis) is a fungal disease that invades the central nervous system. When the fungus proliferates in the brain and meninges it raises intracranial pressure, commonly producing headache, nausea, and vomiting along with other neurologic signs. This well-documented clinical presentation makes cryptococcal meningitis the fungal infection known to cause nausea when it affects the brain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Developmental dyscalculia",
    "long_answer": "Developmental dyscalculia is a neurodevelopmental disorder in which functional imaging consistently shows abnormal (usually reduced) activation of right-hemisphere parietal circuits, especially the right intraparietal sulcus that supports number processing. Clinically it produces acalculia, finger-representation problems and frequent left–right disorientation—the very components of Gerstmann syndrome—so it is considered to share core symptoms with that classic parietal-lobe syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-RNR1",
    "long_answer": "The mitochondrial 12S ribosomal RNA gene, MT-RNR1, is encoded entirely within the mitochondrial genome. Pathogenic point mutations in MT-RNR1 (e.g., m.1555A>G) are a well-established cause of maternally inherited nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness, often with heightened susceptibility to aminoglycoside-induced hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NFKB2",
    "long_answer": "DAVID syndrome (Deficient Anterior pituitary with Variable Immune Deficiency) is caused by heterozygous, typically truncating, mutations in the NFKB2 gene. NFKB2 encodes the p100/p52 subunit of the non-canonical NF-κB transcription factor pathway; loss-of-function mutations act in a dominant-negative fashion, impairing B-cell development (leading to common variable immunodeficiency) and disrupting pituitary ACTH production, yielding the combined endocrine-immune phenotype characteristic of DAVID syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Central core disease is a congenital myopathy in which muscle biopsy examined by light microscopy shows centrally located oxidative-deficient ‘cores’ in the fibers. Almost all familial and sporadic cases with these histological cores carry pathogenic mutations in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor (calcium-release channel) on chromosome 19. Thus, the characteristic light-microscopy finding points to an underlying RYR1 mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methotrexate",
    "long_answer": "Both invasive mole and choriocarcinoma fall under gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, which is highly chemosensitive. Single-agent methotrexate (often with folinic-acid rescue) is the standard first-line therapy used to eradicate trophoblastic tissue in both conditions, achieving high cure rates while preserving fertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "In primary ovarian insufficiency the ovaries produce little estrogen and inhibin, removing the normal negative feedback on the pituitary. As a result, gonadotropin secretion rises, and serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) becomes markedly elevated (often >40 IU/L), a key laboratory hallmark of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Lumbar spinal stenosis is most accurately assessed with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), which provides high-resolution, multiplanar images of the spinal canal, vertebral bodies, intervertebral discs, ligaments, and neural elements, allowing visualization of the characteristic narrowing that defines the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Astrocytic proliferation",
    "long_answer": "Glial scars in multiple system atrophy arise through reactive gliosis, in which astrocytes become activated, enlarge, and divide. This astrocytic proliferation (also called astroglial hypertrophy and hyperplasia) lays down dense networks of glial filaments and extracellular matrix that constitute the scar tissue observed in affected brain regions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARID1B",
    "long_answer": "Coffin–Siris syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in ARID1B, a core component of the SWI/SNF (BAF) chromatin-remodeling complex. Pathogenic ARID1B mutations account for the clear majority of genetically confirmed CSS cases, so diagnostic molecular testing panels for the syndrome routinely begin with, or focus on, ARID1B sequencing and copy-number analysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schinzel–Giedion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Schinzel–Giedion syndrome is a rare congenital malformation disorder that combines characteristic skeletal defects, notably hypoplastic or shortened ulnae, with central-nervous-system anomalies such as a large retrocerebellar arachnoid (meningeal) cyst. The co-occurrence of ulnar abnormalities and a posterior-fossa arachnoid cyst is regarded as a hallmark radiological–clinical constellation of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Musculoskeletal contractures, malalignments, and bone deformities that develop in children with cerebral palsy are most commonly corrected by orthopedic surgery, which includes tendon-lengthening, muscle releases, and osteotomies to improve alignment and function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hormone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Menopausal hormone replacement therapy, particularly estrogen-containing regimens, elevates vaginal estrogen levels. Estrogen increases glycogen deposition in the vaginal epithelium, providing a nutrient source for Candida species and fostering an acidic environment that promotes their growth. Consequently, women receiving estrogen-based HRT have a higher incidence of vulvovaginal candidiasis compared with those not on this therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Alexander disease is caused by dominant mutations in the GFAP (glial fibrillary acidic protein) gene. The GFAP gene is mapped to band q21 on the long arm of chromosome 17 (17q21), so the pathogenic variants responsible for Alexander disease are located on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karel Frederik Wenckebach",
    "long_answer": "The Wenckebach block (second-degree atrioventricular block type I) is named after Dutch physician and physiologist Karel Frederik Wenckebach, who first described the characteristic progressively lengthening PR intervals followed by a dropped ventricular beat in 1899."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactate dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Under anaerobic conditions, the enzyme lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) catalyzes the reduction of pyruvate to lactate while oxidizing NADH to NAD⁺. This reaction regenerates NAD⁺ needed for glycolysis to continue in the absence of sufficient oxygen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ichthyosis",
    "long_answer": "Neu–Laxova syndrome, a rare lethal congenital malformation disorder, consistently features a generalized thickening and lamellar scaling of the skin that resembles congenital ichthyosis. This ichthyosiform hyperkeratosis is one of the characteristic clinical findings reported in virtually all described cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cohen-Gibson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous de-novo missense variants in the EED gene, a core component of the Polycomb Repressive Complex 2, cause Cohen-Gibson syndrome. This overgrowth–intellectual disability disorder is clinically recognised by dysmorphic facial features (broad forehead, hypertelorism, full lips, etc.), variable developmental delay and other congenital anomalies. Identification of recurrent de-novo EED missense mutations established Cohen-Gibson syndrome as the EED-associated phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gene therapy",
    "long_answer": "Researchers are testing gene therapy for Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy: an adeno-associated viral (AAV) vector is used to deliver a functional copy of the mitochondrial ND4 gene to retinal ganglion cells, aiming to restore or preserve vision lost from the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Insufficient ionized calcium in the extracellular fluid (hypocalcaemia) lowers the threshold for action-potential generation in peripheral nerves and muscle fibres. This heightened neuromuscular excitability manifests clinically as painful skeletal muscle cramps and tetany, a well-documented consequence of calcium deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bevacizumab",
    "long_answer": "Bevacizumab, a monoclonal antibody that inhibits vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), is well documented to precipitate posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES). VEGF blockade by bevacizumab can produce severe hypertension and endothelial dysfunction, leading to breakdown of the blood-brain barrier and the vasogenic edema characteristic of PRES. Multiple clinical case reports and pharmacovigilance data link bevacizumab therapy—given intravenously for cancer or, less often, intravitreally for ocular disease—to the development of PRES."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tram-track calcifications",
    "long_answer": "In Sturge–Weber syndrome, leptomeningeal angiomas cause chronic venous stasis and dystrophic mineral deposition along the cerebral cortical gyri. On CT or skull radiographs this appears as parallel, curvilinear ‘tram-track’ cortical calcifications. Detection of these gyriform tram-track calcifications in the cerebral cortex is a classic, highly suggestive sign of Sturge–Weber syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AKT1",
    "long_answer": "Proteus syndrome is caused by a mosaic, activating mutation in the AKT1 gene—most commonly the c.49G>A (p.E17K) substitution—which leads to constitutive activation of the PI3K-AKT signaling pathway and the asymmetric, progressive overgrowth characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EZH2",
    "long_answer": "Weaver syndrome is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the EZH2 gene. EZH2 encodes the catalytic subunit of the Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2), a histone-methyltransferase that regulates gene expression. Beyond its role in growth and development, EZH2 is critically involved in lymphopoiesis: it controls chromatin remodeling during B- and T-cell differentiation and germinal-center formation. Thus, the gene linked to Weaver syndrome that functions in lymphopoiesis is EZH2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rod–cone dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Bardet–Biedl syndrome is a ciliopathy in which almost all affected individuals develop a progressive rod–cone retinal dystrophy (clinically akin to retinitis pigmentosa). Degeneration of rod photoreceptors causes early night-blindness and peripheral vision loss, followed by cone involvement leading to central vision impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function variants in the WNT4 gene are a recognised cause of Müllerian (MRKH) agenesis. The disorder follows an autosomal-dominant inheritance pattern in which several heterozygous carriers—particularly males and some females—show no genital tract malformation, illustrating the gene’s incomplete penetrance and variable expressivity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giardiasis",
    "long_answer": "Patients with common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) frequently develop chronic infection with the protozoan Giardia lamblia. The defect in IgA- and IgG-mediated mucosal immunity allows persistent giardial colonisation of the small intestine, leading to chronic diarrhoea and malabsorption. Hence, giardiasis is a characteristic gastrointestinal complication of CVID."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptococcus suis infection",
    "long_answer": "Streptococcus suis is a zoonotic bacterium carried by pigs; humans can acquire the infection by handling or eating raw or undercooked contaminated pork. Once in the bloodstream it readily crosses the blood-brain barrier, frequently producing severe neurological disease—most characteristically acute bacterial meningitis, often accompanied by sepsis and permanent hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital kidney disease",
    "long_answer": "Kidneys that develop abnormally in utero—such as renal agenesis, dysplasia, or multicystic kidneys—are classified collectively as congenital kidney disease. These conditions arise from malformation of the urinary tract during fetal development, distinguishing them from kidney disorders acquired after birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interstitial (atypical) pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "In interstitial, or “atypical,” pneumonia the inflammatory reaction is largely confined to the alveolar septa and pulmonary interstitium; the airspaces themselves remain mostly free of the purulent alveolar exudate that characterizes typical bacterial (lobar or bronchopneumonia) infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spironolactone and testolactone",
    "long_answer": "Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis) is managed with the combined use of spironolactone, an anti-androgen that blocks androgen receptors, and testolactone, an aromatase inhibitor that suppresses estrogen synthesis. By lowering estrogen production, testolactone slows bone maturation, while spironolactone counteracts the excess testosterone, together mitigating the precocious puberty phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked dominant",
    "long_answer": "Most reported cases of Orofaciodigital syndrome correspond to type I, which is caused by pathogenic variants in the OFD1 gene on the X chromosome. Type I is inherited in an X-linked dominant manner and is typically lethal in hemizygous males, making X-linked dominant inheritance the pattern seen in the majority of Orofaciodigital syndrome cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPS1 and EXT1",
    "long_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome (trichorhinophalangeal syndrome type II) is a contiguous-gene deletion disorder caused by an 8q24.11-q24.13 microdeletion that removes the TRPS1 gene and the EXT1 gene. Loss of TRPS1 leads to the characteristic hair, facial, and skeletal abnormalities, while loss of EXT1 contributes to multiple exostoses. Thus, affected individuals are missing both TRPS1 and EXT1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatology",
    "long_answer": "Hepatology is the subspecialty of medicine focused on liver diseases; hepatologists (often within gastroenterology) diagnose and treat conditions such as hepatomegaly and hepatic cirrhosis, so they would be the appropriate specialists for managing the liver manifestations seen in Senter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis type 5",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis type 5 (DA5) is a recognised syndromic form of arthrogryposis in which congenital contractures of the hands and feet are accompanied by ocular motility defects. Affected individuals frequently show ptosis, strabismus and partial external ophthalmoplegia/ophthalmoparesis due to limited extra-ocular muscle function. Pathogenic variants in the mechanosensitive ion-channel gene PIEZO2 underlie DA5, explaining the combined limb-muscle and eye-movement phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zika virus",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and experimental studies show that maternal infection with Zika virus during pregnancy can cross the placenta, infect fetal neural progenitor cells, disrupt neurodevelopment, and markedly increase the risk of babies being born with congenital microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Measles virus",
    "long_answer": "Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a rare, fatal neurodegenerative complication that arises years after primary infection with the measles virus (a Morbillivirus of the Paramyxoviridae family). Persistent, mutant measles virus remains in the central nervous system, leading to progressive inflammation, demyelination, and neuronal degeneration characteristic of SSPE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKP2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (often termed arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy) is most commonly linked to pathogenic variants in the plakophilin-2 (PKP2) gene, which encodes a desmosomal protein essential for myocyte adhesion. Identifying a PKP2 mutation during the work-up helps distinguish this inherited cardiomyopathy from other causes of ventricular arrhythmias in the differential diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystic fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis, caused by CFTR gene mutations, produces viscous secretions that obstruct pancreatic ducts. This obstruction destroys the exocrine tissue, leading to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, and with progressive loss of islet cells many patients develop cystic-fibrosis–related diabetes mellitus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Geist classification",
    "long_answer": "The accessory navicular is traditionally categorized by the Geist classification, which separates these ossicles into three anatomical types: Type I (a small sesamoid bone within the posterior tibial tendon), Type II (a larger triangular or heart-shaped ossicle united to the navicular by a synchondrosis), and Type III (an enlarged, fused ‘cornuate’ navicular)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Fine–Lubinsky syndrome is a rare, inherited neuro-developmental and craniofacial disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the CLP1 gene. Because its diagnosis relies on recognizing a characteristic pattern of congenital anomalies and confirming the causative mutation with molecular testing, and because ongoing care involves genetic counseling and multisystem management, the specialty that most commonly evaluates and manages patients with this syndrome is medical (clinical) genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxoplasma gondii",
    "long_answer": "Toxoplasmosis is caused by the protozoan parasite Toxoplasma gondii. This obligate intracellular apicomplexan infects warm-blooded animals, with felids as the definitive host. Human infection occurs via ingestion of oocysts from cat feces, consumption of tissue cysts in undercooked meat, or transplacental transmission, leading to congenital or acquired toxoplasmosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rome IV criteria",
    "long_answer": "Cyclic vomiting syndrome is defined clinically according to the Rome IV functional gastrointestinal disorder criteria: at least two or more stereotypical, discrete episodes of intense nausea and vomiting occurring in the previous 6 months, lasting hours to days, with complete return to baseline between attacks and no alternative medical explanation. These Rome IV criteria are the accepted standard for diagnosing cyclic vomiting syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningococcal ACWY vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Saudi Arabia mandates that all pilgrims undertaking Umrah present a certificate of vaccination with a quadrivalent meningococcal conjugate or polysaccharide vaccine (covering serogroups A, C, W-135, and Y) given within the specified period before entry, to prevent outbreaks of meningococcal disease during the pilgrimage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1",
    "long_answer": "The NF1 gene (neurofibromin-1) is situated on chromosome 17q11.2. Pathogenic variants in NF1 cause neurofibromatosis type 1, a genetic skin disorder characterized by café-au-lait macules, cutaneous neurofibromas and other pigmentary/dermal findings. Thus, NF1 is the skin-condition–related gene located on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "The vast majority of primary esophageal malignancies are squamous cell carcinomas, arising from the squamous epithelium that lines most of the esophagus. Epidemiologic data show strong links between classic risk factors (tobacco, alcohol, caustic injury, achalasia, Plummer-Vinson syndrome) and development of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, making this histologic type the one classically associated with esophageal cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tunica media and adventitia",
    "long_answer": "In cerebral amyloid angiopathy, amyloid-β peptide is deposited within the vessel wall itself—specifically in the tunica media and extending into the adventitia—of small- to medium-sized cortical and leptomeningeal arteries, arterioles, and capillaries. Accumulation in these layers weakens the vessel wall and underlies the characteristic risk of lobar intracerebral hemorrhage seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renovascular hypertension",
    "long_answer": "When the renal arteries become narrowed (renal artery stenosis), renal perfusion falls. The juxtaglomerular apparatus responds by releasing excess renin, activating the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system. The resulting systemic vasoconstriction and sodium-water retention produce a secondary form of high blood pressure termed renovascular hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "The interferon regulatory factor 6 gene (IRF6) on chromosome 1q32 has been repeatedly mapped and functionally validated as a major susceptibility locus for nonsyndromic cleft lip with or without cleft palate as well as Van der Woude syndrome. Mutations or risk variants in IRF6 disrupt epithelial differentiation during craniofacial development, leading to the clefting phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radical inguinal orchiectomy",
    "long_answer": "The presence of a seminoma cannot be reliably distinguished from other testicular tumours by imaging or serum markers alone. The accepted method is removal of the testis through an inguinal approach (radical inguinal orchiectomy), after which histopathological examination of the specimen provides definitive confirmation of seminoma while avoiding tumour seeding that could occur with scrotal biopsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic anhydrase II mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CA2 gene, encoding carbonic anhydrase II, cause a syndrome that includes autosomal-recessive distal renal tubular acidosis. Because CA II is crucial for CO₂/HCO₃⁻ handling in many tissues, its deficiency also impairs brain acid–base balance, leading to abnormal calcium deposition and characteristic cerebral (intracranial) calcifications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Koilonychia is an abnormal nail finding; diagnosis and treatment of nail disorders fall under dermatology. Board-certified dermatologists are trained to examine nails, identify causes such as iron-deficiency anemia or systemic disease, and manage the condition or refer for additional care, making them the appropriate specialists for koilonychia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNNT1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Amish nemaline myopathy is caused by a homozygous loss-of-function mutation in the TNNT1 gene, which encodes slow-skeletal-muscle troponin T. The truncating mutation abolishes functional troponin T1, disrupts thin-filament regulation, and leads to the severe congenital muscle weakness and nemaline bodies characteristic of this disorder in the Old Order Amish population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium benzoate",
    "long_answer": "Sodium benzoate, widely used as a food preservative, is administered pharmacologically to patients with glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinemia). It binds free glycine to form hippurate, which is readily excreted by the kidney, thereby lowering systemic and cerebrospinal glycine concentrations. When begun early and combined with other supportive measures, this benzoate therapy can reduce seizures, apnea, and neuro-developmental injury in certain milder or attenuated forms of the disorder, improving clinical outcomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oxybutynin",
    "long_answer": "Urinary urgency, frequency and incontinence that often accompany hereditary spastic paraplegia can be managed with the antimuscarinic agent oxybutynin. By blocking M3 muscarinic receptors in the detrusor muscle, oxybutynin reduces involuntary bladder contractions and improves storage capacity, thereby relieving these urinary symptoms in HSP patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spleen",
    "long_answer": "In infectious mononucleosis, Epstein-Barr virus infects B lymphocytes and triggers marked lymphoid and reticulo-endothelial hyperplasia. The spleen, a major lymphoid organ in the abdomen, consequently undergoes congestion and proliferation of immune cells, leading to splenomegaly that is clinically evident in many patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The PTGER4 gene, whose variants confer increased susceptibility to ankylosing spondylitis, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 5 (region 5p13.1). Genome-wide association studies have repeatedly linked this chromosomal locus to elevated disease risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a coagulopathy frequently triggered by acute promyelocytic leukaemia and other leukemic states. In DIC, widespread activation of coagulation leads to formation of microvascular thrombi, consuming platelets and clotting factors; this paradoxically produces both excessive clotting and a high risk of hemorrhage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rho(D) immune globulin",
    "long_answer": "Hydrops fetalis of immune origin is usually caused by maternal anti-Rh(D) antibodies that destroy Rh-positive fetal red cells. Administering Rho(D) immune globulin (anti-D antibody) to Rh-negative mothers during pregnancy and after delivery binds any fetal Rh-positive erythrocytes in the maternal circulation, preventing maternal sensitization and the subsequent hemolytic disease that can progress to immune hydrops fetalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transthyretin (TTR) mutation",
    "long_answer": "The overwhelmingly common genetic cause of restrictive cardiomyopathy is amyloid deposition from mutant transthyretin. Pathogenic variants in the TTR gene destabilize the transthyretin tetramer, leading to misfolded monomers that aggregate as amyloid fibrils in the myocardium, producing the classic stiff-walled, diastolic-dysfunction phenotype of restrictive cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen-storage diseases are categorized by the specific enzyme that is deficient. Glycogen storage disease type IV (Andersen disease) is caused by deficiency of the glycogen branching enzyme (also called GBE1). Phosphorylase kinase deficiency produces a different form of glycogen-storage disease, historically classified as type VIII or more commonly type IX (subtypes IXa, IXb, IXc, etc.). Therefore, phosphorylase kinase deficiency does not cause glycogen storage disease type IV."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palpable purpura",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss) is a small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis; when it involves dermal post-capillary venules it produces leukocytoclastic vasculitis, leading to the characteristic palpable purpura that can be seen on the skin of affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low back pain",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Stiff-person syndrome commonly develop persistent rigidity and spasm of the axial and paraspinal muscles, which produces chronic low back pain. This musculoskeletal pain is one of the most frequently reported symptoms of the disorder and is recognized clinically as a prevalent form of chronic pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EC 6.3.4.5",
    "long_answer": "Type I citrullinemia results from mutations in the ASS1 gene, which encodes argininosuccinate synthase. This urea-cycle enzyme (argininosuccinate synthetase) is classified under Enzyme Commission number EC 6.3.4.5, catalyzing the ATP-dependent condensation of citrulline and aspartate to form argininosuccinate. Deficiency of this EC 6.3.4.5 enzyme impairs ammonia detoxification, producing the clinical phenotype of type I citrullinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital hyperinsulinism",
    "long_answer": "Congenital hyperinsulinism (CHI) is a rare genetic disorder in which mutations in genes that regulate pancreatic β-cell KATP channels (most commonly ABCC8 or KCNJ11) cause uncontrolled insulin secretion. The resulting persistent, often severe hypoglycemia in neonates and infants can lead to seizures, brain injury, and long-term neurodevelopmental disability if not rapidly recognized and treated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1",
    "long_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome results from an inherited deficiency of the hepatic enzyme UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1), which normally conjugates unconjugated bilirubin with glucuronic acid. Without sufficient UGT1A1 activity, bilirubin cannot be adequately conjugated for excretion, leading to the severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenectomy",
    "long_answer": "Surgical removal of the spleen (splenectomy) is often employed to treat the severe hemolytic anemia in hereditary pyropoikilocytosis, because eliminating splenic sequestration markedly decreases the premature destruction of the fragile, spectrin-deficient red blood cells characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant mutation",
    "long_answer": "Blepharoptosis-myopia-ectopia lentis (BMEL/BHME) is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner: affected individuals carry a single heterozygous pathogenic mutation that is sufficient to cause the syndrome and shows vertical inheritance through successive generations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bannayan–Riley–Ruvalcaba syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bannayan–Riley–Ruvalcaba syndrome (a PTEN-hamartoma tumour syndrome) is classically characterized by congenital macrocephaly together with tall, slender ‘Marfanoid’ body habitus, joint laxity and other features such as lipomas and intestinal hamartomatous polyps. The coexistence of macrocephaly and Marfanoid habitus in the same genetic disorder distinguishes BRRS from other overgrowth conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Semaphorin F (SEMA5A)",
    "long_answer": "Cri du chat syndrome results from deletion of the 5p15 region. Within this critical segment lies the semaphorin F gene (SEMA5A), which guides axonal growth and cortical development. Loss of SEMA5A disrupts normal cerebral development, contributing to the microcephaly and intellectual disability characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increased mechanical load",
    "long_answer": "Obesity adds extra body weight that places additional mechanical stress on muscles and joints already weakened by prior poliomyelitis. This overload accelerates muscle fatigue, joint degeneration, pain, and respiratory effort, thereby intensifying the characteristic weakness, fatigue, and musculoskeletal pain seen in post-polio syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lamotrigine",
    "long_answer": "Lamotrigine is an antiepileptic drug that is also widely used as a mood-stabiliser in bipolar disorder. A recognised adverse effect is a hypersensitivity reaction known as DRESS (Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms), in which striking peripheral eosinophilia is typical. Thus, lamotrigine can cause eosinophilia as a side effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPG11",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPG11 gene (encoding the protein spatacsin) are a frequent cause of autosomal-recessive hereditary spastic paraplegia type 11. Beyond progressive lower-limb spasticity, SPG11‐related disease typically presents as a ‘complex’ form of HSP marked by thinning of the corpus callosum and variable cognitive impairment; many patients exhibit learning difficulties or frank intellectual disability. Thus, SPG11 links hereditary spastic paraplegia with intellectual disability in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6) are responsible for Van der Woude syndrome, which is recognized as the most common syndromic form of orofacial clefting (cleft lip and/or palate). Loss-of-function variants in IRF6 disrupt epithelial differentiation during craniofacial development, leading to the characteristic lip pits and clefts that define this prevalent clefting syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST2",
    "long_answer": "Barber-Say syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the TWIST2 gene, which encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor involved in dermal and craniofacial development. Disruption of TWIST2 alters fibroblast function and extracellular-matrix production, producing clinical features such as redundant or atrophic skin, hypertrichosis, and facial dysmorphism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Craniotomy",
    "long_answer": "Removal of a glioblastoma almost always involves a craniotomy, in which a section of the skull is temporarily opened so neurosurgeons can microsurgically resect (debulk) as much of the tumor as is safely possible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholestyramine",
    "long_answer": "Cholestyramine is a bile-acid sequestrant resin that treats hyperlipidemia by binding bile acids in the intestinal lumen. This prevents bile-acid reabsorption, increases hepatic conversion of cholesterol to bile acids, and thereby lowers circulating LDL cholesterol levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus",
    "long_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) is epidemiologically and mechanistically linked to multiple sclerosis; almost all MS patients show prior EBV infection and elevated EBV-specific antibodies, and recent longitudinal studies indicate seroconversion to EBV markedly raises MS risk. EBV can also infect NK/T cells, and its reactivation in these cells underlies the rare but well-documented 'severe mosquito bite allergy,' characterized by exaggerated local and systemic reactions after mosquito bites. Thus, EBV is the virus connecting both phenomena."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The ROR2 gene, mutations of which cause the autosomal recessive form of Robinow syndrome, is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 9 (cytogenetic band 9q22.31)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune hepatitis is primarily managed with immunosuppressive therapy, most commonly systemic corticosteroids such as prednisone. These drugs dampen the aberrant immune response attacking hepatocytes, leading to reduced inflammation, normalization of transaminases, and prevention of progressive liver damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth retardation",
    "long_answer": "In children with hemosiderosis, excess iron deposits in endocrine organs—especially the pituitary—impair growth-hormone secretion and other hormonal axes, leading to slowed or stunted physical growth. Consequently, growth retardation is a well-recognized developmental problem in pediatric patients with iron overload (hemosiderosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric urology",
    "long_answer": "Posterior urethral valves are congenital obstructing leaflets in the male infant urethra. Their diagnosis and definitive management—endoscopic valve ablation, vesicostomy, or other urinary tract reconstructive procedures—are performed by physicians trained in pediatric urology, the subspecialty of urology focused on surgical and medical care of genitourinary disorders in infants and children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling",
    "long_answer": "Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme iduronate-2-sulfatase. Fetal chorionic-villus tissue obtained by chorionic villus sampling (CVS) at 10–12 weeks’ gestation can be assayed directly for iduronate-2-sulfatase activity. Demonstrating markedly reduced or absent enzyme activity in the chorionic villi provides a definitive prenatal diagnosis of Hunter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRCA1 and BRCA2",
    "long_answer": "Germline mutations in the tumor-suppressor genes BRCA1 and BRCA2 greatly elevate lifetime risk of epithelial ovarian cancer—up to ~40-60 % for BRCA1 and ~10-25 % for BRCA2 carriers—because loss of their DNA-repair (homologous recombination) function promotes genomic instability and malignant transformation in ovarian epithelial cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retina",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome causes progressive vision loss due to retinitis pigmentosa, in which the light-sensing photoreceptor cells of the retina degenerate. Damage to the retina, particularly the rod and later cone photoreceptors, leads to night blindness and constricted visual fields characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hannibal Lecter",
    "long_answer": "Thomas Harris’s fictional serial killer Hannibal Lecter is sometimes (incorrectly) cited as an example of a person with elective, or selective, mutism brought on by childhood trauma. In the canon Lecter is only briefly silent after witnessing his sister’s murder; he soon speaks fluently and shows no clinical picture of selective-mutism. Thus the popular attribution of elective mutism to this character is mistaken."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Decibels (dB)",
    "long_answer": "The perceived loudness of tinnitus is quantified by matching the tinnitus sound to an external tone and recording its intensity in decibels (dB), typically referenced to the patient’s hearing threshold (dB SL or dB HL). Decibels are the standard acoustic unit for sound intensity and therefore for tinnitus loudness measurement in clinical audiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome results from a deletion of genetic material on the short arm of chromosome 4 (4p16.3). A hallmark craniofacial feature of this contiguous-gene-deletion disorder is marked hypertelorism, along with a broad nasal bridge and “Greek-warrior-helmet” facies, linking the syndrome to the clinical finding in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fas (CD95) receptor",
    "long_answer": "In lichen planus, activated CD8⁺ cytotoxic T lymphocytes accumulate at the dermal-epidermal junction and induce apoptosis of basal keratinocytes. A key step is the binding of Fas ligand (FasL) on these T cells to the Fas (CD95/APO-1) death receptor expressed on keratinocytes. Engagement of the Fas receptor triggers the caspase cascade, leading to keratinocyte apoptosis and the characteristic interface dermatitis of lichen planus. Thus, the Fas (CD95) receptor is centrally involved in the disease’s immunopathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "March",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, March is officially recognized by Congress and public health agencies as National Colon Cancer Awareness Month, dedicated to increasing awareness of colorectal cancer screening, prevention, and research."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diagnostic laparoscopy",
    "long_answer": "Peritonitis can be definitively confirmed by diagnostic laparoscopy, a minimally invasive procedure in which a laparoscope (camera-equipped endoscope) is introduced through small abdominal incisions, allowing direct visual inspection of the peritoneal cavity and, if necessary, collection of peritoneal fluid or biopsies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "The PEP-C regimen is an all-oral combination used for relapsed mantle cell lymphoma; it consists of Prednisone, Etoposide, Procarbazine, and Cyclophosphamide. Thus, Prednisone is one of the medications included in this regimen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary edema",
    "long_answer": "In mitral stenosis, left-atrial and pulmonary venous pressures rise, increasing capillary hydrostatic pressure in the lungs. When this hydrostatic force exceeds plasma oncotic pressure, fluid is driven from the pulmonary capillaries into the interstitium and alveoli, producing pulmonary edema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sapropterin dihydrochloride",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal recessive GTP-cyclohydrolase I deficiency causes a shortage of the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4). Sapropterin dihydrochloride is a synthetic analogue of BH4 that replaces the missing cofactor, restoring phenylalanine hydroxylase and aromatic amino-acid hydroxylase activity. Therefore, sapropterin dihydrochloride is the standard BH4 analogue used in treating this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Charles procedure",
    "long_answer": "In advanced lymphedema the limb develops fibrotic, chronic non-pitting edema that no longer responds to conservative measures. The classical surgical option for such cases is the Charles procedure, an excisional operation in which the diseased skin and subcutaneous tissue are removed en bloc down to the deep fascia and the area is resurfaced with split-thickness skin grafts. By excising the fibrotic, lymph-laden tissue, the Charles procedure can markedly reduce the chronic non-pitting edema characteristic of end-stage lymphedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ischemic stroke",
    "long_answer": "When a thrombus forms in a cerebral artery, it can block (obstruct) blood flow to brain tissue, producing focal cerebral ischemia. Neurons deprived of oxygen and glucose—especially in memory-critical regions such as the hippocampus and temporal cortex—are damaged or die, leading to symptoms that commonly include acute memory loss. This pathophysiologic event is clinically termed an ischemic stroke (cerebrovascular accident due to thrombosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "African trypanosomiasis",
    "long_answer": "Tsetse flies (Glossina spp.) transmit the protozoan parasite Trypanosoma brucei, which causes African trypanosomiasis—also called African sleeping sickness. This vector-borne illness is entirely separate from encephalitis lethargica, a viral-associated encephalitic condition that caused epidemics in the early 20th century."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WT1 gene",
    "long_answer": "The WT1 (Wilms-tumor 1) tumor-suppressor gene resides at chromosome band 11p13, on the short arm (p) of chromosome 11. Germ-line or somatic mutations or deletions of WT1 are found in many cases of Wilms’ tumor, establishing its central role in the pathogenesis of this pediatric kidney cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laparoscopic cholecystectomy",
    "long_answer": "For patients with symptomatic cholelithiasis, current surgical guidelines recommend laparoscopic cholecystectomy—the minimally invasive removal of the gallbladder—as the preferred treatment. This technique reliably eliminates recurrent biliary colic, prevents complications such as acute cholecystitis or pancreatitis, and is associated with less postoperative pain, shorter hospital stay, and faster recovery compared with open cholecystectomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Focal palmoplantar and gingival keratosis is a keratinization disorder that principally affects the skin of the palms and soles and, secondarily, the oral mucosa. Disorders of keratinization are diagnosed and treated by dermatologists, who specialize in diseases of the skin, hair, nails, and related mucous membranes. Consequently, the appropriate medical specialist to manage this condition is a dermatologist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "In pedigrees reported for the syndrome of zonular (lamellar) congenital cataract with congenital nystagmus, only males manifest the disease while transmission is through phenotypically normal carrier mothers. This sex-linked pattern, with no male-to-male transmission and skipping of generations via carrier females, is characteristic of an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIT D816V mutation",
    "long_answer": "Urticaria pigmentosa, the most common form of cutaneous mastocytosis, is frequently driven by an activating point mutation in the KIT proto-oncogene, most classically the Asp816Val (D816V) substitution. This gain-of-function mutation constitutively activates the KIT tyrosine kinase on mast cells, leading to their clonal proliferation and accumulation in the skin that characterizes the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucose (dextrose)",
    "long_answer": "Intravenous or oral glucose (dextrose) is routinely administered to correct hypoglycemia and is also given at the start of therapy for alcohol withdrawal to treat or prevent the frequent concomitant low blood sugar seen in alcohol-dependent patients. Thus, glucose is a standard component of management in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial infantile epilepsy is typically caused by heterozygous mutations in genes such as PRRT2 and follows an autosomal-dominant inheritance pattern, with affected individuals in successive generations and no sex bias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blue sclera",
    "long_answer": "Viljoen–Kallis–Voges syndrome (brittle-cornea syndrome) and kyphoscoliotic Ehlers–Danlos syndrome are both collagen-related connective-tissue disorders in which thinning of the ocular sclera makes it translucent. The underlying uveal pigment then shows through, giving the characteristic appearance of blue sclera, a clinical sign reported in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aortic valve",
    "long_answer": "Whipple’s disease, caused by the bacterium Tropheryma whipplei, can manifest as culture-negative infective endocarditis. In reported cases this infection shows a strong predilection for the aortic valve, leading to aortic valvulitis and insufficiency; hence the aortic valve is the valve characteristically affected in Whipple’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Upper lung lobes",
    "long_answer": "In rare primary pulmonary sporotrichosis, inhaled Sporothrix spores lodge in the lungs and typically produce chronic fibronodular or cavitary lesions that are most prominent in the upper lobes, analogous to the apical involvement seen in pulmonary tuberculosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTPN11",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PTPN11 gene (encoding the tyrosine-phosphatase SHP-2) are the established molecular cause of the cartilage tumour disorder metachondromatosis. Pathogenic variants in the same gene have also been reported in patients with Maffucci syndrome, showing that PTPN11 alterations can underlie both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Depression",
    "long_answer": "Vision impairment from cataracts limits daily activities, independence, and social interaction. This functional decline and isolation markedly increase the risk of developing depression; large epidemiologic studies show higher rates of depressive symptoms in people with cataract-related visual loss, which often improve after cataract surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is characterized by abnormally short fingers and toes because the phalanges—the small tubular bones that form the digits—fail to grow to normal length during development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC)",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomyxoma peritonei is typically managed with cytoreductive surgery followed by hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC). During HIPEC, chemotherapy drugs—most commonly mitomycin C or oxaliplatin—are circulated within the abdominal cavity at 41-43 °C for 60-120 minutes. The heat enhances drug penetration and cytotoxicity against residual mucinous tumor cells while limiting systemic exposure, making HIPEC the standard heated-chemotherapy component of treatment for this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR1",
    "long_answer": "Somatic activating mutations in the FGFR1 (fibroblast growth factor receptor-1) gene have been identified as the molecular cause of encephalocraniocutaneous lipomatosis. Aberrant FGFR1 signalling also disrupts early ocular development, and pathogenic FGFR1 variants are a recognised cause of congenital eye defects such as microphthalmia. Thus, FGFR1 mutation links both encephalocraniocutaneous lipomatosis and microphthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ceftazidime",
    "long_answer": "First-line therapy for acute, severe melioidosis consists of an ‘intensive phase’ of intravenous antibiotics; international guidelines recommend ceftazidime as the standard drug, given for at least 10–14 days before switching to oral eradication therapy. Its proven activity against Burkholderia pseudomallei and superiority over earlier regimens make ceftazidime the most commonly used agent in this phase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LONP1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the LONP1 gene (Lon peptidase 1, mitochondrial) cause CODAS (Cerebral, Ocular, Dental, Auricular, Skeletal) syndrome. LONP1 encodes the major ATP-dependent protease in the mitochondrial matrix, and its expression/turnover is modulated by the mitochondrial transcription factor TFAM, which integrates mitochondrial DNA transcription with proteostasis. Thus, deficiency of LONP1 leads to the multisystem disorder CODAS, and the gene is functionally connected to regulation by TFAM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium cyanide",
    "long_answer": "John B. McLemore ingested potassium cyanide, a salt that rapidly liberates cyanide ions in the body. Cyanide binds to cytochrome c oxidase in mitochondria, halting cellular respiration and leading to rapid, often fatal, systemic hypoxia— the classic mechanism of cyanide poisoning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAGE3",
    "long_answer": "Among the genes known to cause Galloway–Mowat syndrome (GAMOS), LAGE3 is located on the X chromosome (Xp11.23). Pathogenic variants in LAGE3 lead to GAMOS type 2 and are transmitted in an X-linked recessive manner, distinguishing it from the other GAMOS genes that follow autosomal-recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Inherited optic neuropathies such as Leber hereditary optic neuropathy and dominant optic atrophy arise from pathogenic mutations in mitochondrial DNA or in nuclear genes (e.g., OPA1, MFN2) that encode mitochondrial proteins. These mutations impair mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation and disrupt retinal ganglion cell energy supply, leading to degeneration of the optic nerve. Hence, the cellular organelle implicated is the mitochondrion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is a hereditary disorder of calcium homeostasis caused by inactivating mutations in the calcium-sensing receptor, leading to lifelong hypercalcemia and altered parathyroid hormone regulation. Disorders of calcium and parathyroid function fall within the scope of endocrinology, the medical specialty that diagnoses and manages hormonal and metabolic diseases. Therefore, an endocrinologist is the clinician most likely to evaluate, distinguish FHH from primary hyperparathyroidism, and guide long-term management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome is a rare congenital disorder caused most often by pathogenic variants in the CREBBP or EP300 genes. Core clinical features include proportionate short stature, characteristic broad thumbs and big toes, intellectual disability, and congenital heart defects that can include dextrocardia. One of its classic diagnostic hallmarks is abnormal dermatoglyphics—such as persistent fetal fingertip pads and distinctive fingerprint ridge patterns—first emphasized in the original description of the syndrome. Thus, Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome is the rare disorder that combines short stature, dextrocardia, and dermatoglyphic abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciliary ganglion",
    "long_answer": "Holmes-Adie (Adie) syndrome is produced by selective degeneration of the post-ganglionic parasympathetic neurons in the ciliary ganglion and their short ciliary nerves. Loss of this parasympathetic input to the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle leads to the characteristic tonic, poorly light-reactive pupil and associated accommodation abnormalities seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "cromolyn sodium",
    "long_answer": "Case reports describe clinical and endoscopic remission of Cronkhite–Canada syndrome after administration of cromolyn sodium (sodium cromoglicate). Cromolyn sodium is a well-known mast cell stabilizer that inhibits the degranulation of mast cells and subsequent release of inflammatory mediators, providing the mechanistic rationale for its proposed use in this rare polyposis syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) arises when homologous chromosome 21 pairs fail to separate during maternal or paternal meiosis I or II. The resulting gamete carries two copies of chromosome 21; after fertilization with a normal gamete, the zygote contains three copies (47,XX+21 or 47,XY+21). Thus the chromosomal abnormality is produced by a meiotic nondisjunction event that becomes manifest at fertilization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Roux-en-Y gastric bypass",
    "long_answer": "Contemporary cases of adult-onset nesidioblastosis—which presents as persistent hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia due to β-cell hyperplasia—have been documented almost exclusively after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery for morbid obesity. The altered gastrointestinal anatomy after this bariatric procedure greatly increases post-prandial incretin (e.g., GLP-1) release, stimulating pancreatic β-cell proliferation and insulin secretion, thereby inducing the modern form of nesidioblastosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vicarious conditioning",
    "long_answer": "A child who repeatedly watches a parent or peer react with marked fear or avoidance toward a neutral object (e.g., dogs) can acquire the same fear without any direct traumatic event. The observed person’s fearful behavior functions as the unconditioned stimulus; through associative observational learning (vicarious conditioning, or modeling), the previously neutral stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus that elicits a phobic response in the child."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lamellar ichthyosis",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of keratinocyte transglutaminase (transglutaminase-1, encoded by the TGM1 gene) causes lamellar ichthyosis, an autosomal-recessive form of congenital ichthyosis. Because it is inherited recessively, its prevalence is higher in populations with frequent consanguineous marriages."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS13B",
    "long_answer": "Cohen syndrome is classically caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13B (also known as COH1) gene. While its hallmark ocular findings are high myopia and progressive retinal dystrophy, several reported patients with VPS13B mutations have additionally presented with ocular coloboma, demonstrating that pathogenic variants in VPS13B can underlie both Cohen syndrome and colobomatous defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMBR1",
    "long_answer": "Tibial hemimelia-polysyndactyly-triphalangeal thumb syndrome is an autosomal-dominant limb malformation that maps to chromosome 7q36. Pathogenic point mutations or duplications in the limb-specific Sonic Hedgehog enhancer (ZRS) located within intron 5 of the LMBR1 gene disrupt normal anterior–posterior patterning, producing tibial hemimelia together with polysyndactyly and triphalangeal thumbs. Thus, the syndrome is genetically linked to the LMBR1 gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroretinography",
    "long_answer": "Electroretinography (ERG) measures the electrical responses of retinal photoreceptors and inner retinal cells. In Usher syndrome, ERG shows markedly reduced or extinguished scotopic and photopic responses, allowing early detection of the retinitis pigmentosa–type retinal degeneration characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic",
    "long_answer": "In the majority of patients with eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman syndrome), no definite precipitating factor can be identified. Although some cases follow strenuous exercise, infection, trauma, or certain drugs, most are classified as idiopathic—meaning the cause is unknown."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bilateral renal agenesis",
    "long_answer": "Potter sequence is classically produced by congenital bilateral renal agenesis, in which the ureteric buds fail to form. Because the ureteric bud arises from the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct, its absence can simultaneously eliminate other Wolffian-derived male structures, including the seminal vesicles, leading to their congenital absence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Pyruvate carboxylase normally channels pyruvate into oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis. When the enzyme is deficient, pyruvate is instead reduced to lactate, producing severe, recurrent lactic acidosis. This profound acidaemia can damage multiple organs; in affected infants it frequently precipitates progressive hepatic dysfunction that can culminate in acute liver failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ornithine",
    "long_answer": "Ornithine aminotransferase normally converts ornithine to pyrroline-5-carboxylate in the ornithine–proline pathway. When the enzyme is deficient, ornithine cannot be catabolized and accumulates in body fluids. Consequently, plasma and urinary levels of the amino acid ornithine become markedly elevated in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Therapeutic phlebotomy",
    "long_answer": "Hemochromatosis type 4 (ferroportin disease) produces systemic iron overload. The established way to deplete these excess iron stores is repeated therapeutic phlebotomy (regular removal of blood), which forces the body to mobilize and export iron from hepatic and other tissues to make new red blood cells, thereby lowering tissue iron and serum ferritin to safe levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP27A1",
    "long_answer": "Cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis (CTX) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CYP27A1 gene, which encodes sterol 27-hydroxylase, a key enzyme in the bile-acid synthetic pathway. Deficient sterol 27-hydroxylase lowers chenodeoxycholic-acid production, leading to accumulation of cholesterol/cholestanol in tissues (producing the neurologic and tendon xanthomas of CTX) and to supersaturation of bile with cholesterol, a well-documented mechanism that increases the risk of cholesterol gallstone formation. Thus, CYP27A1 mutations link CTX and a heightened propensity for gallstones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone",
    "long_answer": "Adiposogenital dystrophy (Fröhlich’s syndrome) arises from hypothalamic lesions that impair secretion of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH, also called LHRH). GnRH is synthesized in the arcuate/preoptic areas of the hypothalamus and stimulates pituitary release of LH and FSH. Loss of GnRH output lowers LH/FSH, leading to hypogonadism, delayed sexual development, and the characteristic obesity of adiposogenital dystrophy. Thus, diminished hypothalamic GnRH is the hormonal defect associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myxidium",
    "long_answer": "The genus Myxidium is classified within the family Myxidiidae of the phylum Myxozoa. Numerous Myxidium species are parasitic in the kidneys, gall-bladders, or other organs of marine and freshwater fish, where they produce myxospores and can cause pathological conditions collectively termed myxidiosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD20",
    "long_answer": "Standard therapy for gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma includes rituximab (e.g., R-CHOP). Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody that binds the CD20 antigen on B-cells, a surface protein encoded by the MS4A1 (CD20) gene. Thus, the treatment specifically targets the CD20 gene product expressed by the malignant B-cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Executive dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "The Stroop colour-word test probes executive control processes—especially response inhibition and selective attention mediated by the prefrontal cortex. Difficulty completing the task therefore indicates executive dysfunction, the cognitive impairment involving deficient inhibitory control, set-shifting and attention regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "Atelectasis reduces alveolar ventilation, leading to hypoxemia. When arterial oxygen saturation falls, de-oxygenated hemoglobin imparts a bluish color to skin and mucous membranes; this visible bluish discoloration—cyanosis—is a characteristic symptom signaling insufficient oxygen in the blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High tibial osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "When a pathologic bow-leg (genu varum) persists after medical management of rickets or in infantile tibia vara (Blount’s disease), the deformity is surgically corrected by a high (proximal) tibial osteotomy, in which the upper tibia is cut and realigned into valgus to restore normal mechanical axis of the limb."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric neurology",
    "long_answer": "Polymicrogyria is a congenital malformation of cortical development that typically presents in infancy or childhood with seizures, developmental delay, and motor deficits. Evaluation requires detailed neurological examination, brain MRI interpretation, and ongoing management of epilepsy and neuro-developmental issues—all core functions of pediatric neurologists. Therefore, the medical specialty that diagnoses and treats polymicrogyria is pediatric neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triple A syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Achalasia is classically linked to Triple A (Allgrove) syndrome, an autosomal-recessive genetic disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the AAAS gene. The syndrome is defined by the triad of achalasia of the oesophagus, alacrima (absent tear production), and adrenocorticotropic hormone–resistant adrenal insufficiency, making it the well-established hereditary condition most commonly associated with achalasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Senile plaques",
    "long_answer": "Although Cockayne syndrome features progressive neurodegeneration, brain tissue from affected individuals characteristically lacks the β-amyloid-containing senile (amyloid) plaques that are common in Alzheimer disease and normal ageing. Thus, senile plaques are not typically seen in patients with Cockayne syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EMD and LMNA",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy arise from pathogenic variants in the X-linked EMD gene (coding for emerin) or the autosomal LMNA gene (coding for lamin A/C). Because these two genes account for the great majority of genetically confirmed EDMD, clinical molecular testing panels routinely focus on sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis of EMD and LMNA to establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLI2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the GLI2 gene, a transcription factor in the Hedgehog signalling pathway, are a recognised cause of growth hormone deficiency owing to pituitary hypoplasia. The same mutations frequently produce holoprosencephaly-spectrum malformations, polydactyly, cleft palate and other midline anomalies—well-established congenital birth defects—linking GLI2 to both endocrine deficiency and structural developmental defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse",
    "long_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse is characterized by one or both mitral valve leaflets billowing (prolapsing) back into the left atrium during systole. This abnormal displacement can cause mitral regurgitation, reducing forward cardiac output and producing symptoms such as shortness of breath, palpitations, or chest discomfort."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peptic ulceration",
    "long_answer": "Indometacin is a potent non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug. By inhibiting cyclo-oxygenase-1 it markedly reduces gastric prostaglandin synthesis, weakening the mucosal barrier and increasing acid injury. This can lead to the development of peptic ulceration, a serious gastrointestinal condition that may progress to bleeding or perforation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EFHC1",
    "long_answer": "The EFHC1 gene (also known as MYOCLONIN-1) is one of the principal genes linked to Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy. EFHC1 encodes a microtubule- and centrosome-associated protein that regulates neuronal migration and promotes dendrite formation and branching. Experimental knock-down or pathogenic EFHC1 mutations impair dendritogenesis in developing cortical neurons, providing mechanistic insight into how EFHC1 dysfunction contributes to the neurodevelopmental defects underlying Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX gene",
    "long_answer": "Langer mesomelic dysplasia arises from homozygous or compound-heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the SHOX (short stature homeobox) gene located in the pseudo-autosomal region of the X and Y chromosomes. Complete absence of functional SHOX disrupts endochondral bone growth in the limbs, producing the severe mesomelic dwarfism characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Occipital epilepsy is a focal seizure disorder originating in the occipital lobe of the brain. Diagnosis requires neurological evaluation with EEG and neuro-imaging, and treatment involves antiepileptic medications or other neuro-targeted therapies—all of which fall under the expertise of neurologists (often subspecialized epileptologists)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium",
    "long_answer": "Hyperprolactinemia in prolactinoma lowers gonadal steroids (estrogen/testosterone), accelerating bone resorption and predisposing to osteoporosis. Ensuring a high dietary intake of the bone-building mineral calcium (about 1,000–1,500 mg/day) helps maintain bone mineral density and mitigate this risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "G6PC3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the G6PC3 gene (glucose-6-phosphatase catalytic subunit 3) cause autosomal-recessive severe congenital neutropenia type 4. Patients with G6PC3 deficiency not only have profound neutropenia but also characteristically develop cardiovascular abnormalities, including pulmonary and/or systemic hypertension, making G6PC3 mutations the established genetic link between severe congenital neutropenia and hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jalili syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Jalili syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the CNNM4 gene. It combines cone-rod dystrophy, which produces photophobia, reduced visual acuity and nystagmus, with amelogenesis imperfecta affecting tooth enamel. This distinctive association of ocular and dental features defines the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Second metatarsal",
    "long_answer": "Freiberg disease (Freiberg infraction) is an osteochondrosis/avascular necrosis of a metatarsal head. Over 75 % of reported cases involve the head of the second metatarsal, making it by far the most commonly affected bone, followed distantly by the third metatarsal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sudden, purposeful travel",
    "long_answer": "Dissociative fugue is distinguished from dissociative amnesia by the individual’s sudden, purposeful travel or wandering away from home/work, often with confusion about or assumption of a new identity, whereas dissociative amnesia involves only the memory loss without such travel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "central nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Gliosarcoma behaves like glioblastoma and almost always remains confined to the neuraxis; spread beyond the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) is exceptionally uncommon, so it rarely metastasizes outside the CNS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysine acetyltransferase 6B",
    "long_answer": "Genitopatellar syndrome is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KAT6B gene. KAT6B encodes lysine acetyltransferase 6B, a MYST-family histone acetyltransferase that catalyzes acetylation of specific lysine residues on histone H3. This histone-modifying enzyme regulates chromatin structure and gene transcription, and its dysfunction underlies the developmental abnormalities seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
    "long_answer": "Caroli disease is characterized by segmental, saccular dilatation of the intra-hepatic bile ducts. The most definitive, routinely used imaging study to demonstrate these enlarged ducts is endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), which directly opacifies the biliary tree and clearly reveals the cystic, communicating ductal dilatations that establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "While several observational studies have explored whether maternal exposure to the herbicide atrazine might increase the risk of abdominal-wall defects such as gastroschisis or omphalocele, the findings have been inconsistent and generally limited by ecological exposure metrics, small numbers of cases, and potential confounding. Regulatory reviews by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, the European Food Safety Authority, the World Health Organization, and major teratology and epidemiology committees conclude that the current evidence is insufficient to establish a causal or “conclusive” link between atrazine and these specific birth defects. Thus, the scientific consensus is that a definitive causal relationship has not been demonstrated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitor",
    "long_answer": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, such as captopril or enalapril, reduce intraglomerular pressure and stabilize the glomerular filtration barrier. By diminishing efferent arteriolar resistance and attenuating angiotensin II-mediated permeability changes, they can markedly reduce or even abolish proteinuria in some patients with glomerulosclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Women who have systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) carry a substantially higher risk of developing pre-eclampsia. SLE is an autoimmune disease characterised by circulating auto-antibodies and immune-complex deposition, which can damage renal and placental vascular endothelium. This endothelial injury and complement activation promote the hypertension, proteinuria and placental insufficiency that define pre-eclampsia, making SLE a well-established risk factor in obstetric guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROR2",
    "long_answer": "The ROR2 gene (receptor tyrosine-kinase–like orphan receptor 2) is causally implicated in both conditions: biallelic loss-of-function variants produce the recessive form of Robinow syndrome, characterized by mesomelic limb shortening and craniofacial anomalies, while heterozygous gain-of-function or dominant-negative mutations in ROR2 give rise to autosomal dominant brachydactyly type B1, marked by hypoplastic or absent distal phalanges. Thus, ROR2 links these two skeletal disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin (5-HT)",
    "long_answer": "Opioid exposure activates descending facilitatory pathways from the rostral ventromedial medulla; these fibres release serotonin in the spinal cord, and excess spinal 5-HT—especially acting on 5-HT3 receptors—is a key mechanism in opioid-induced hyperalgesia. Cholecystokinin (acting via CCK2 receptors in the same brainstem region) excites these serotonergic ON-cells and increases serotonin release, thereby amplifying pain sensitivity. Hence, serotonin is the neurotransmitter both promoted by cholecystokinin and central to the development of opioid-induced hyperalgesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly-renal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Siegler and colleagues first delineated brachydactyly-renal syndrome in 1980.  It is an autosomal-dominant disorder marked by pronounced underdevelopment of the digits (brachydactyly due to absent or shortened middle phalanges) together with renal ectopia or other structural kidney anomalies.  The concurrence of digital hypoplasia and malpositioned kidneys they reported became the defining clinical constellation for this genetic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "MRI of the brain, especially with high-resolution T2-weighted or cine CSF-flow sequences, is the modality of choice for demonstrating narrowing or blockage of the cerebral aqueduct and associated ventricular changes, making it the best technique for detecting aqueductal stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Omovertebral bone",
    "long_answer": "In Sprengel's deformity (congenital elevation of the scapula), about one-third of patients have an accessory bar of bone called the omovertebral bone that bridges the superomedial angle of the scapula to one of the lower cervical vertebrae (usually C4–C7). This anomalous bony connection restricts scapular descent and contributes to the characteristic high position of the scapula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial hypobetalipoproteinemia",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypobetalipoproteinemia (FHBL) is an autosomal-codominant disorder caused most often by truncating mutations in the APOB gene. The defect markedly lowers apoB-containing lipoproteins, producing hypolipoproteinemia. Because apoB is required to assemble and secrete VLDL, its absence leads to lipid retention within hepatocytes, resulting in hepatic steatosis, steatohepatitis and, in some cases, cirrhosis. Thus FHBL is the genetic hypolipoproteinemia that is well established as a cause of liver disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinologist",
    "long_answer": "Insulinomas are pancreatic neuro-endocrine tumors that autonomously secrete insulin and disturb glucose homeostasis. Because their management requires expertise in hormone-related pathophysiology, control of hypoglycemia, and coordination of surgical or oncologic treatments, the care is overseen by an endocrinologist, the specialist in disorders of endocrine glands such as the pancreas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tyrosinase (Tyr)",
    "long_answer": "Albinism in standard laboratory rats is caused by a loss-of-function mutation in the tyrosinase (Tyr) gene. Tyrosinase encodes the rate-limiting enzyme for melanin biosynthesis; when the gene is mutated, melanin cannot be produced, leading to the characteristic absence of pigment in the fur, eyes and skin of albino rats."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ΔFosB",
    "long_answer": "ΔFosB, a truncated splice variant of the FosB gene, is a highly stable AP-1 transcription factor that accumulates in the nucleus accumbens after repeated opioid exposure. Its persistent overexpression alters the expression of downstream genes that regulate synaptic plasticity and reward circuitry, thereby promoting the behavioral sensitization and compulsive drug-seeking characteristic of opioid addiction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus type 1",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex encephalitis (HSE) is overwhelmingly caused by herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1) in immunocompetent children and adults. HSV-1 accounts for roughly 90% of sporadic, non-epidemic encephalitis cases, infecting the temporal and frontal lobes via reactivation of latent virus in trigeminal or olfactory pathways. HSV-2 more commonly causes neonatal encephalitis, but HSV-1 is the dominant etiologic agent in older age groups."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Because patients with Nijmegen breakage syndrome have a profound combined immunodeficiency and a very high risk of lymphoid malignancies, allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (bone-marrow/cord-blood HSCT) is considered as a curative approach. Transplanted donor stem cells can reconstitute normal DNA-repair–competent lymphoid and myeloid lineages, improving immune function and lowering cancer risk, so HSCT is the treatment option discussed for NBS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMX1B",
    "long_answer": "Nail–patella syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the LMX1B gene, which encodes a LIM-homeodomain transcription factor crucial for limb, nail and renal development. Defects in LMX1B can impair glomerular basement-membrane integrity, leading to proteinuria and progressive chronic kidney disease—a recognized contributor to secondary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Emphysema",
    "long_answer": "Chronic bronchitis almost always co-exists with emphysema. Both disorders arise from long-term airway irritation (most often cigarette smoking) and together constitute the two principal components of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Thus, emphysema is the most common chronic condition found in patients who have chronic bronchitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iodine-131",
    "long_answer": "Graves’ disease, an autoimmune hyperthyroidism, is commonly treated with radioiodine ablation. The therapeutic isotope employed is Iodine-131 (¹³¹I), which is taken up by thyroid follicular cells via the sodium-iodide symporter. Its β-particle emissions selectively destroy hyperfunctioning thyroid tissue while sparing most extrathyroidal tissues, making ¹³¹I the standard radioisotope for definitive treatment of Graves’ disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythema",
    "long_answer": "The medical term for redness of the skin, produced by dilation and congestion of superficial capillaries and commonly observed in viral exanthems like roseola, is erythema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Profuse watery diarrhea",
    "long_answer": "Diarrheic shellfish poisoning is caused by okadaic-acid toxins that inhibit intestinal protein phosphatases, leading to chloride and water hypersecretion in the gut. The resulting copious, watery diarrhea removes large volumes of fluid and electrolytes from the body, and this fluid loss is the direct, primary reason affected individuals become dehydrated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLUT2",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi-Bickel syndrome, the glycogen storage disease described by Guido Fanconi, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC2A2 gene that encodes the glucose transporter protein GLUT2. Defective GLUT2 impairs hepatic and renal glucose transport, leading to the characteristic hepatomegaly, renal Fanconi syndrome, and glycogen accumulation seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "MELAS stands for Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes. The brain manifestation of the syndrome is an encephalopathy—a disorder of brain function caused by mitochondrial DNA mutations that impair energy production, leading to seizures, cognitive decline, and stroke-like episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Long-term warfarin",
    "long_answer": "Patients who have a lupus anticoagulant and have already suffered a thrombotic event are at high risk for recurrent venous or arterial thrombosis. Evidence-based guidelines therefore recommend lifelong oral anticoagulation with the vitamin K antagonist warfarin, titrated to an INR of about 2.0–3.0 (or slightly higher in some protocols), as the standard preventive treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIPBL",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NIPBL gene, which encodes a cohesin-loading factor, are the main cause of Cornelia de Lange syndrome. The same NIPBL mutations—sometimes in mosaic form—have repeatedly been reported in patients who exhibit congenital radioulnar synostosis, either as an isolated limb anomaly or within the CdLS phenotype. Hence, alteration of NIPBL links radioulnar synostosis and Cornelia de Lange syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "mTOR",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC) tumors arise when mutations in TSC1 or TSC2 cause constitutive activation of the serine/threonine protein kinase mTOR (mechanistic Target Of Rapamycin). Clinically, mTOR inhibitors such as sirolimus and everolimus are used to shrink or stabilize TSC-related tumors (e.g., renal angiomyolipomas, subependymal giant-cell astrocytomas), confirming mTOR as the key kinase targeted in their treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinal ischemia",
    "long_answer": "Retinal ischemia occurs when inadequate blood flow deprives retinal tissue of oxygen and nutrients. The resulting hypoxia can damage photoreceptors and ganglion cells, trigger pathologic neovascularization, and ultimately lead to partial or permanent vision impairment if not promptly addressed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Hay–Wells syndrome, also called AEC syndrome, belongs to the p63-associated ectodermal dysplasia disorders. Missense mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor crucial for ectodermal and craniofacial development, disrupt enamel, dentin and tooth morphogenesis, leading to the dental abnormalities characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Differential diagnosis",
    "long_answer": "Clinicians distinguish avoidant personality disorder from other disorders (e.g., social anxiety disorder, schizoid, dependent or paranoid personality disorders) through a systematic differential diagnosis. By applying DSM criteria in structured clinical interviews, they compare the full pattern, chronicity and pervasiveness of avoidance, hypersensitivity and interpersonal functioning, thereby ruling out conditions that share some overlapping features but differ in course or scope."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole",
    "long_answer": "Isosporiasis, caused by the coccidian parasite Cystoisospora (Isospora) belli, is most effectively eradicated with the folate-antagonist combination trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (cotrimoxazole). This drug blocks sequential steps in the parasite’s folic-acid synthesis pathway, leading to rapid clinical and parasitological cure and is therefore considered the treatment of choice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Baller–Gerold syndrome results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the RECQL4 DNA helicase gene. The RECQL4 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 8q24.3, locating the causative mutations for this syndrome on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MANBA",
    "long_answer": "MANBA encodes lysosomal β-mannosidase. Loss-of-function variants in MANBA eliminate β-mannosidase activity, causing the lysosomal storage disorder beta-mannosidosis. Similar MANBA mutations have been identified in patients clinically diagnosed with Sanfilippo (mucopolysaccharidosis type III)–like disease, so the MANBA gene is considered to underlie both beta-mannosidosis and certain cases labelled as Sanfilippo syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the modality of choice for Sturge-Weber syndrome because it clearly delineates leptomeningeal angiomas, cortical atrophy, and other intracranial abnormalities characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMP22 gene deletion",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsy (HNPP) is most commonly caused by a heterozygous 1.5-Mb deletion encompassing the PMP22 gene on chromosome 17p11.2. Loss of one PMP22 copy reduces peripheral myelin protein 22 dosage by ~50 %, leading to the pressure-sensitive demyelinating neuropathy characteristic of HNPP. (In contrast, a duplication of PMP22 causes Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A.)"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whipple procedure",
    "long_answer": "Tropheryma whipplei causes Whipple’s disease. The same eponym is used for the standard pancreaticoduodenectomy, universally referred to as the Whipple procedure, so the operation shares its name with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xerosis cutis",
    "long_answer": "Grover’s disease (transient acantholytic dermatosis) commonly develops or flares in settings of very dry skin. The condition most often linked to this trigger is xerosis cutis, a disorder defined by excessively dry, rough skin. Clinical and epidemiologic studies note that patients with Grover’s disease frequently have marked xerosis, and improving skin hydration can help prevent or lessen outbreaks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type III",
    "long_answer": "The Glanzmann thrombasthenia-like bleeding seen in some patients with leukocyte adhesion deficiency occurs in LAD type III. In this subtype, mutations in the FERMT3 gene abolish expression of kindlin-3, a protein required for inside-out activation of β-integrins on both leukocytes and platelets. Loss of kindlin-3 prevents activation of platelet αIIbβ3 integrin (the same pathway defective in Glanzmann thrombasthenia), producing a similar bleeding diathesis while simultaneously impairing leukocyte adhesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brucella bacteria",
    "long_answer": "Brucellosis is an infectious disease produced by bacteria of the genus Brucella, small Gram-negative coccobacilli that infect animals such as cattle, sheep, goats, and pigs and can be transmitted to humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiographic X-ray",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is confirmed by obtaining plain radiographic X-ray images of the hands and/or feet; the films clearly show the shortened phalanges or metacarpals, allowing objective identification of the congenital skeletal anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "S100, CD68, CD163",
    "long_answer": "Rosai–Dorfman disease is confirmed when the characteristic plasmacytoid histiocytes show strong reactivity for S100 protein together with macrophage‐lineage markers CD68 and CD163, while lacking CD1a or langerin. This immunophenotype distinguishes RDD from Langerhans cell histiocytosis and other histiocytic disorders, making S100, CD68 and CD163 the standard diagnostic stains."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-communicable disease",
    "long_answer": "Cancer is classified as a non-communicable disease (NCD) because it arises from genetic and cellular changes within an individual—such as DNA mutations that lead to uncontrolled cell proliferation—and it cannot be transmitted from person to person like infectious (communicable) diseases. Global health authorities, including the World Health Organization, list cancer alongside cardiovascular disease, chronic respiratory disease, and diabetes as one of the major NCDs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-galactosidase A",
    "long_answer": "Fabry disease is an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-galactosidase A (encoded by the GLA gene). Mutations in GLA produce a misfolded or unstable alpha-galactosidase A protein that is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than properly trafficked to lysosomes, leading to accumulation of misfolded enzyme and its undegraded substrate, globotriaosylceramide (Gb3/GL-3). Thus, alpha-galactosidase A deficiency is the enzyme defect responsible for the protein misfolding seen in Fabry disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transient epileptic amnesia",
    "long_answer": "Transient epileptic amnesia is a focal epilepsy syndrome in which very brief, recurrent partial seizures cause isolated episodes of amnesia. Because the attacks resemble the sudden-onset, short-lived memory loss seen in transient global amnesia, patients are often misdiagnosed until EEG or treatment response confirms the epileptic origin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused most often by pathogenic variants in EYA1, SIX1, or SIX5. It presents with branchial cleft anomalies in the neck, external/middle/inner ear malformations with hearing loss, and a spectrum of renal defects. The renal component frequently includes hypoplasia or dysplasia; histologically this can appear as oligomeganephronia, where the kidney is small with markedly reduced nephron number and enlarged residual glomeruli. Because BOR syndrome simultaneously affects the kidneys, ears and neck and is a well-documented cause of renal hypoplasia leading to oligomeganephronia, it is the genetic disorder requested."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paradoxical respiration",
    "long_answer": "In severe infantile hypophosphatasia the poorly mineralised ribs make the chest wall unstable, so during inspiration the thorax moves inward instead of outward – a phenomenon called paradoxical respiration (paradoxical chest wall movement). The same abnormal inward-outward motion is characteristic of patients with flail chest caused by multiple rib fractures, making paradoxical respiration a shared respiratory complication of both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Chronic large-plaque parapsoriasis and related parapsoriasis conditions carry a recognized risk of malignant transformation into cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, most commonly the mycosis fungoides variant. Histologic progression from the inflammatory parapsoriasis patches to clonal proliferation of skin-homing T lymphocytes underlies this evolution to lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "iPLA2β",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PLA2G6 gene cripple the enzyme calcium-independent phospholipase A2β (iPLA2β). iPLA2β normally hydrolyzes phospholipids, recycling fatty acids to preserve neuronal cell-membrane integrity. Loss of its activity in infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy leads to defective phospholipid remodeling and progressive axonal degeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nucleic acid amplification test",
    "long_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis is most accurately detected by nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), such as PCR, which identify pathogen-specific DNA or RNA directly in urine or genital swab specimens with high sensitivity and specificity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDH18A1",
    "long_answer": "Leao-Teles et al. described individuals with the clinical picture of De Barsy syndrome who carried biallelic pathogenic variants in ALDH18A1, the gene encoding Δ¹-pyrroline-5-carboxylate synthetase (P5CS). Their work pointed to ALDH18A1 deficiency—known to disrupt proline and ornithine synthesis—as a molecular cause linking this gene to the cutis-laxa/De Barsy phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMARCB1 (INI1)",
    "long_answer": "Germline loss-of-function mutations in the SMARCB1 (also known as INI1) gene, located on chromosome 22q11.2, are a well-established cause of schwannomatosis, a hereditary syndrome characterized by the development of multiple cutaneous and peripheral nerve schwannomas without vestibular involvement. The SMARCB1 protein is a core component of the SWI/SNF chromatin-remodeling complex; its inactivation promotes tumorigenesis by dysregulating cell-cycle and growth-control genes, leading to the formation of numerous cutaneous schwannomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mogamulizumab",
    "long_answer": "Mogamulizumab is a humanized IgG1 monoclonal antibody directed against the CCR4 receptor expressed on malignant T lymphocytes. In 2018 it received FDA approval for adult patients with relapsed or refractory mycosis fungoides (and Sézary syndrome) after at least one prior systemic therapy, providing a targeted biologic option for this cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Hypoplasia is a congenital condition characterized by the incomplete or under-development of a tissue or organ, and it can result in shortened bones of the fingers or toes, manifesting clinically as brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a chronic inflammatory and fibrosing disease of the intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts that leads to progressive cholestatic liver injury and often requires endoscopic evaluation, medical management of cholestasis, surveillance for cholangiocarcinoma, and consideration for liver transplantation. These diagnostic and therapeutic tasks fall within the expertise of gastroenterologists, particularly those with hepatology training, so patients with PSC are typically managed by the gastroenterology specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Excitation-contraction uncoupling",
    "long_answer": "CACNA1S encodes the α1-subunit of the skeletal-muscle L-type Ca2+ channel (DHPR), which initiates Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum during an action potential. Disease-causing mutations alter the channel’s voltage sensor, generating an abnormal ‘gating-pore’ leak current. In hypokalaemia this leak depolarises the muscle membrane, inactivates Na+ channels, blocks action-potential propagation and thereby prevents Ca2+ influx–triggered release. The loss of this Ca2+ signal disconnects excitation from contraction, producing the transient muscle weakness of hypokalaemic periodic paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langerhans cells",
    "long_answer": "Histopathologic studies of Fox-Fordyce disease show a perifollicular infiltrate rich in Langerhans cells (dendritic histiocytes). These antigen-presenting cells participate in the immune-inflammatory response that follows rupture of the obstructed apocrine duct, leading to the pruritic papules typical of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "In severe pulmonary (pulmonic) stenosis the right-ventricular pressure rises enough to cause right-to-left shunting of venous blood through a patent foramen ovale, delivering desaturated blood to the systemic circulation. This produces visible cyanosis—bluish discoloration of the lips, tongue and skin—which is a key clinical sign in babies that the stenosis is severe."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folic acid",
    "long_answer": "Periconceptional folic-acid supplementation (the standard 400 µg or more given in prenatal vitamins) lowers the incidence of caudal regression/caudal dysgenesis. Adequate maternal folate promotes proper neurulation and caudal spinal development, and studies show that women with diabetes who take folic acid before and during early pregnancy have a significantly reduced risk of bearing infants with this malformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "In glycogen storage disease type III (Cori/Forbes disease), the deficiency of the glycogen debranching enzyme limits liberation of free glucose from stored glycogen during fasting. This impaired glycogenolysis produces abnormally low blood glucose levels, and the clinical manifestation of that low glucose is recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest x-ray",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary siderosis (“welder’s lung”) is classically identified radiologically: a plain chest x-ray reveals numerous small, diffuse, metallic-density nodular opacities throughout the lung fields, reflecting inhaled iron-oxide deposits. This characteristic radiographic pattern makes the chest x-ray the standard imaging technique used to diagnose pulmonary siderosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray",
    "long_answer": "The initial imaging study for suspected interstitial lung disease is a plain chest radiograph (chest X-ray). It is inexpensive, quick, and widely available, allowing clinicians to screen for reticular, nodular, or ground-glass changes before proceeding to more sensitive tests such as high-resolution CT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Congenital absence of the cervix occurs as part of Müllerian agenesis, classically known as Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser (MRKH) syndrome. In MRKH, failed development of the Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts leads to agenesis or severe hypoplasia of the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina, while ovarian function and external genitalia remain normal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Menkes disease",
    "long_answer": "Menkes disease (Menkes kinky-hair syndrome) is an X-linked disorder of the ATP7A copper-transport protein. Impaired copper delivery cripples copper-dependent enzymes such as lysyl-oxidase, leading to marked cutis laxa and other connective-tissue defects, and also disrupts neuroendocrine enzymes (e.g., dopamine β-hydroxylase, peptidylglycine α-amidating mono-oxygenase), producing profound neurological and endocrine dysfunction. Hence the syndrome is classically recognized by loose, redundant skin together with combined nervous-system and endocrine involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ovarian theca cells",
    "long_answer": "In the ovary, theca-interna cells respond to luteinizing hormone (LH) to synthesize androgens (e.g., androstenedione), which granulosa cells subsequently convert to estrogens. In hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, circulating LH is low, so theca cells receive inadequate stimulation, leading to diminished ovarian androgen production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oligodendroglioma",
    "long_answer": "In neuro-imaging, oligodendrogliomas characteristically contain intratumoral calcifications. Up to 70–90 % of these gliomas show coarse or speckled calcium deposits that appear hyperdense on CT and markedly hypointense on MRI, making calcification a classic diagnostic clue for this type of brain cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Imperforate anus",
    "long_answer": "Imperforate anus (an anorectal malformation) is a congenital defect in which the anal opening is absent, misplaced, or obstructed because of abnormal development of the cloacal membrane and urorectal septum. This anomaly is well documented to occur in approximately 1 out of every 5,000 live births."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Molybdenum",
    "long_answer": "Nitrogenase, the enzyme complex that converts atmospheric N₂ to ammonia in legume root nodules, contains a molybdenum–iron (Mo-Fe) cofactor at its active site. Adequate molybdenum is therefore indispensable for proper nitrogenase assembly and catalytic activity; without this mineral, legumes cannot efficiently fix nitrogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonate",
    "long_answer": "The term “neonate” (or newborn) designates an infant from birth up to 28 days of life. Disorders that present during this period, such as neonatal hepatitis, therefore affect a neonate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Curry-Jones syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Curry-Jones syndrome is a very rare developmental disorder produced by activating mosaic mutations in the SMO gene located on chromosome 7q32. SMO encodes the Smoothened receptor of the Hedgehog signalling pathway; gain-of-function mutations disturb embryonic patterning and lead to the characteristic constellation of anomalies in Curry-Jones syndrome. Clinical features include craniosynostosis, polysyndactyly, cutaneous mosaicism, gastrointestinal malformations, and eye defects such as ocular coloboma. The established link between SMO mutations and this phenotype makes Curry-Jones syndrome the genetic disorder requested."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Niemann-Pick disease type C",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NPC1 gene account for the majority of cases of Niemann-Pick disease type C, an autosomal-recessive lysosomal cholesterol-trafficking disorder. The neuro-ophthalmologic manifestations of this disease include vertical supranuclear gaze palsy and oculomotor dysfunction; eyelid drooping (ptosis) is a recognized feature that can appear in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatology",
    "long_answer": "Dr. Telfer B. Reynolds was a pioneer in the study and care of liver diseases.  In the 1960s he organized one of the world’s first dedicated hepatology (liver-disease) programs, helping to establish hepatology as a distinct medical specialty and advancing clinical research on conditions such as hepatic encephalopathy and the overlap disorder later called Reynolds syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sweet’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sweet’s syndrome, also called acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis, presents with tender erythematous skin plaques and fever. It is classically associated with hematologic malignancies, especially acute myeloid leukemia, occurring as a paraneoplastic manifestation driven by cytokine-mediated neutrophil infiltration of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enterovirus 71; nausea",
    "long_answer": "Among the viruses that cause hand, foot, and mouth disease, Enterovirus 71 (EV-A71) is the one most often linked to severe complications such as aseptic meningitis, encephalitis, and acute flaccid paralysis. A symptom that EV-A71 HFMD commonly produces—especially when the nervous system is involved—is nausea (often with vomiting). Nausea is likewise a frequent accompanying symptom of many types of headaches, particularly migraines, so it is shared by both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skeletal dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Short rib–polydactyly syndrome is one of the lethal skeletal dysplasias: disorders in which the growth and development of cartilage and bone are fundamentally abnormal, producing markedly shortened ribs and limbs together with polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Athetosis is a form of involuntary writhing movement that arises from dysfunction of the basal ganglia and other central nervous system structures. Because it is fundamentally a neurological movement disorder, diagnosis and management are typically handled by neurologists, the specialists trained to evaluate and treat diseases of the nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypokalemia",
    "long_answer": "Hypokalemia is a state of abnormally low serum potassium concentration (<3.5 mmol/L). Adequate potassium is essential for maintaining the resting membrane potential and excitability of skeletal muscle cells; when potassium falls, membrane hyperpolarization impairs muscle fiber contraction, leading to symptoms such as generalized muscle weakness or paralysis. Therefore, hypokalemia directly explains muscle weakness caused by low potassium levels in the blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kartagener syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kartagener syndrome, a form of primary ciliary dyskinesia caused by autosomal-recessive defects in dynein-arm proteins of motile cilia, impairs the left–right flow generated by embryonic nodal cilia. This randomizes laterality and can produce complete situs inversus in roughly half of affected individuals, including documented cases in monozygotic (identical) twins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CVP regimen",
    "long_answer": "The CVP chemotherapy protocol—Cyclophosphamide, Vincristine (the “V” component), and Prednisone/Prednisolone—is a standard regimen for treating indolent non-Hodgkin lymphomas such as follicular lymphoma. Vincristine’s microtubule-disrupting action is a key part of this combination therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome type 2 (WS2) is most frequently caused by mutations in the MITF gene, which encodes the microphthalmia-associated transcription factor crucial for melanocyte development. Loss-of-function MITF variants impair melanocyte formation in the inner ear and skin, producing the characteristic pigmentary changes and congenital sensorineural hearing loss seen in WS2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "In mixed connective tissue disease, high-dose glucocorticoids are combined with a potent immunosuppressant when life- or organ-threatening manifestations (e.g., rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease, severe myositis, pulmonary hypertension) occur. If methotrexate cannot be used, the standard alternative for such severe presentations is cyclophosphamide, an alkylating agent with proven efficacy in aggressive connective-tissue disorders by powerfully inhibiting proliferating lymphocytes and dampening auto-immune inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "eMedicine (now incorporated into Medscape Reference) maintains a large, physician-written encyclopedia of diseases and clinical topics. Among its orthopaedic and sports-medicine entries is a dedicated article titled \"Chondromalacia Patellae\" (sometimes categorized under Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome) that reviews the condition’s pathophysiology, clinical presentation, imaging findings, and management options. Therefore, the eMedicine knowledge base does include information on chondromalacia patellae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arginase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the urea-cycle enzyme arginase (ARG1) impairs the conversion of arginine to ornithine and urea. This blocks the final step of the urea cycle, leading to accumulation of arginine and resulting hyperammonemia in the blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatocytes",
    "long_answer": "In Type I tyrosinemia, deficiency of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase leads to accumulation of toxic metabolites (fumarylacetoacetate and succinylacetone). These metabolites cause oxidative stress and DNA damage that primarily injure hepatic parenchymal cells—hepatocytes—resulting in liver necrosis, cirrhosis, and risk of hepatocellular carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROR2",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-recessive form of Robinow syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ROR2 gene (Receptor Tyrosine Kinase-Like Orphan Receptor 2). Although patients often have genital hypoplasia, the gonads and endocrine function are typically normal, so both males and females generally retain normal fertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Percutaneous cholecystostomy",
    "long_answer": "When acute cholecystitis cannot be managed with prompt cholecystectomy, image-guided percutaneous cholecystostomy—placement of a catheter through the abdominal wall into the gallbladder—is recommended. The drain decompresses the obstructed gallbladder, allows bile outflow, and, together with antibiotics, rapidly controls infection and inflammation until definitive surgery can be safely performed or serves as definitive therapy in high-risk patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Kostmann syndrome (severe congenital neutropenia) are routinely treated with recombinant granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF, e.g., filgrastim). G-CSF acts on the bone marrow to stimulate proliferation, differentiation, and survival of neutrophil precursors, markedly elevating circulating neutrophil counts and reducing infection risk in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive muscular atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Duchenne–Aran disease is the historical eponym for progressive muscular atrophy, a motor-neuron disorder characterized by selective degeneration of anterior horn cells leading to steadily worsening muscle wasting without sensory loss. Hence, in lists organized alphabetically under the letter “P,” it appears as progressive muscular atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gomori trichrome stain",
    "long_answer": "Muscle biopsies from patients with chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia characteristically show \"ragged-red fibers,\" which are accumulations of abnormal mitochondria at the muscle-fiber periphery. These inclusions are visualised with the modified Gomori trichrome histochemical stain, making the Gomori trichrome stain the standard tool for confirming the diagnosis of CPEO."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD), pre-symptomatic patients in the late-infantile form can benefit from hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT). Transplanting healthy donor or gene-corrected autologous hematopoietic stem cells supplies progeny that migrate to the central nervous system and provide functional arylsulfatase A, the enzyme deficient in MLD, thereby slowing or preventing the accumulation of sulfatides and delaying disease progression when performed before clinical symptoms appear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SUMF1 gene disrupt the formylglycine-generating enzyme required to activate all sulfatases, leading to multiple sulfatase deficiency. This disorder is transmitted only when both copies of SUMF1 are altered, so affected individuals are typically born to carrier parents—an inheritance pattern that is classically autosomal recessive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adrenal cortex",
    "long_answer": "Familial glucocorticoid deficiency type 1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ACTH (melanocortin-2) receptor, making the cortisol-producing cells of the adrenal cortex—particularly the zona fasciculata and reticularis—unresponsive to ACTH. Consequently, the adrenal cortex is the primary part of the gland affected, while the medulla and mineralocorticoid-secreting zona glomerulosa are largely spared."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Broad-spectrum antibiotics",
    "long_answer": "Both noma (cancrum oris) and necrotizing fasciitis are fulminant polymicrobial soft-tissue infections. In the early stage, before extensive tissue loss occurs, the cornerstone of management for both conditions is the prompt administration of broad-spectrum systemic antibiotics—classically high-dose penicillin (often combined with agents such as metronidazole or clindamycin)—to rapidly control the mixed anaerobic and aerobic bacterial load and halt disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rhabditida",
    "long_answer": "The nematode family Bunonematidae is placed within the order Rhabditida, as recognized by standard nematode taxonomic systems (e.g., ITIS, IRMNG). Rhabditida encompasses several free-living and parasitic nematode families, including those in the superfamily Bunonematoidea to which Bunonematidae belongs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculomotor nerve",
    "long_answer": "Tolosa–Hunt syndrome produces painful ophthalmoplegia due to granulomatous inflammation in the cavernous sinus or superior orbital fissure, most frequently involving the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III); this nerve’s paresis is the commonest cranial‐nerve deficit reported in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplant",
    "long_answer": "Malignant infantile osteopetrosis is caused by dysfunctional osteoclasts, which originate from the hematopoietic lineage. Transplanting donor hematopoietic stem cells can repopulate the patient with functional osteoclasts and halt the disease. The same procedure is also employed for certain skeletal dysplasias—such as mucopolysaccharidoses (e.g., Hurler syndrome)—where donor-derived cells supply the missing lysosomal enzyme and ameliorate bone and cartilage abnormalities. Thus, hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is a shared, disease-modifying treatment option for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Cytogenetic studies of patients with the Gollop-Wolfgang complex (characterised by tibial agenesis and femoral bifurcation) have repeatedly demonstrated a micro-duplication involving the long arm of chromosome 17 (17q22–q23). Gain of material in this region alters the regulation of limb-patterning genes, producing the characteristic malformations seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Warkany syndrome 1 was described as a growth-retardation and malformation disorder seen only in males born to clinically normal mothers. The trait was transmitted through carrier females, never from an affected male to his sons, which is characteristic of an X-linked recessive (sometimes lethal in hemizygous males) mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spirometry",
    "long_answer": "Spirometry is the standard pulmonary function test used to confirm chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). By measuring forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1), forced vital capacity (FVC), and the FEV1/FVC ratio, spirometry quantifies the degree of airflow limitation characteristic of COPD and distinguishes it from other respiratory conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Keratolytic winter erythema (Oudtshoorn skin disease) has been genetically mapped to the 8p22-p23 region of human chromosome 8. The same chromosomal arm harbours loci (e.g., 8p11-p12, 8p22-p23, and 8q24) that are frequently amplified, deleted, or otherwise altered in several subtypes of breast cancer, making chromosome 8 a recognised hotspot in breast-tumour genetics. Hence, chromosome 8 is the chromosome associated with both keratolytic winter erythema and certain forms of breast cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonazepam",
    "long_answer": "Clonazepam (a benzodiazepine that potentiates GABA-A receptor–mediated inhibition) is the only antiepileptic drug with an FDA label specifically allowing its use as monotherapy for myoclonic seizures, including those occurring in Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsies (PME)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRSS1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary pancreatitis is classically caused by mutations in the cationic trypsinogen gene, PRSS1, located on chromosome 7q35. Pathogenic variants such as R122H and N29I lead to inappropriate intrapancreatic activation of trypsin, triggering recurrent episodes of pancreatitis and progressive pancreatic damage, thereby establishing PRSS1 as the gene responsible for this inherited disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "In Becker muscular dystrophy, long-term treatment with corticosteroids (glucocorticoids such as prednisone or deflazacort) is recommended because these drugs decrease inflammation in dystrophic muscle, thereby slowing both skeletal and cardiac muscle degeneration and prolonging ambulation and cardiac function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mozambique",
    "long_answer": "Mozambique, located in Southeastern Africa, consistently reports new-case detection rates for leprosy well above the World Health Organization’s elimination threshold of 1 case per 10 000 population, making it one of the African countries with the highest leprosy prevalence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMP14",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the MMP14 gene (encoding membrane-type 1 matrix metalloproteinase) are a known cause of autosomal-recessive Winchester syndrome. MT1-MMP is required for collagen degradation and activation of MMP-2 during bone remodeling; its absence impairs normal matrix turnover, producing generalized osteopenia in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mumps orchitis",
    "long_answer": "In post-pubertal males, infection with the mumps virus can produce orchitis, an inflammatory involvement of the testes. The resulting edema, increased intratesticular pressure, and immune-mediated damage selectively destroy the germinal epithelium of the seminiferous tubules, halting maturation of germ cells. This viral orchitis is a well-documented cause of spermatogenesis arrest and consequent subfertility or infertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distraction osteogenesis",
    "long_answer": "In both Hemifacial microsomia and Parry–Romberg syndrome, severe mandibular (jaw) underdevelopment or asymmetry can be surgically corrected by distraction osteogenesis—a procedure that slowly separates a surgically cut bone segment so new bone forms in the gap, lengthening and reshaping the jaw to restore facial symmetry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Restless legs syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Damage to peripheral sensory nerves, such as in diabetic or other forms of peripheral neuropathy, can produce uncomfortable paresthesias in the legs that worsen during inactivity and at night. This triggers the characteristic irresistible urge to move the limbs that defines restless legs syndrome, a sleep-related movement disorder that frequently disrupts sleep."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cold agglutinin disease",
    "long_answer": "Cold agglutinin disease (CAD) is an autoimmune hemolytic anemia in which patients produce very high titers of cold-reactive IgM antibodies (cold agglutinins). These antibodies bind to red blood cells at low temperatures, activate complement, and cause intravascular hemolysis, defining CAD as a cold-sensitive, antibody-mediated form of hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial dysautonomia",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysautonomia (Riley-Day syndrome, HSAN III) is an exceedingly rare hereditary sensory-autonomic neuropathy caused by ELP1 (IKBKAP) mutations. Impaired development of autonomic nerves produces pronounced orthostatic hypotension, while hypotonia and connective-tissue laxity give rise to generalized joint hypermobility. Thus this disorder uniquely links hypermobility with a severe, rare form of dysautonomia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus frequently involves the lungs, leading to pleuritis, interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary hypertension. The lungs are the primary organ for gas exchange, transferring oxygen to the blood and removing carbon dioxide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Betulin",
    "long_answer": "The topical gel (Oleogel-S10 / Filsuvez) developed for junctional epidermolysis bullosa contains a 10 % dry extract of birch (Betula) bark. The pharmacologically active constituent of this extract is the pentacyclic triterpene betulin, which predominates (>80 %) in the bark and promotes re-epithelialisation and wound healing in JEB lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAMA2 gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LAMA2 gene, which encodes the laminin-α2 (merosin) chain, cause merosin-deficient congenital muscular dystrophy type 1A. Because laminin-α2 is also critical for brain white-matter integrity, its absence can produce cerebral white-matter abnormalities that manifest as seizures and significant learning disabilities in many patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Pellagra is caused by a deficiency of niacin (vitamin B₃) or its precursor tryptophan. Whole-kernel maize contains some niacin, but most of it is bound in a form (niacytin) that is nutritionally unavailable unless the grain is treated with alkali (e.g., traditional nixtamalization). In addition, a large fraction of the maize’s free niacin and tryptophan resides in the germ and outer layers of the kernel. Modern roller-milling and degermination remove these parts to improve shelf life, producing refined cornmeal/grits that are markedly lower in niacin and tryptophan. Populations whose staple food is such milled, untreated corn—and who lack alternative protein or niacin sources—are therefore at higher risk of developing pellagra. Historical outbreaks in the U.S. South and parts of Europe were linked directly to the widespread adoption of degerminated corn products. Hence, the process of milling corn, by stripping away nutrient-rich fractions, can indeed contribute to the development of pellagra when the refined corn becomes a dietary staple."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a neurocutaneous disorder characterized by tumor growth along peripheral and central nerves, café-au-lait macules, and neurologic complications. Because it primarily affects the nervous system and requires ongoing neurologic assessment and management, patients are typically treated and followed by the medical specialty of Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurosurgery",
    "long_answer": "Trigonocephaly is a form of craniosynostosis caused by premature fusion of the metopic suture. Definitive management requires surgical cranial-vault remodeling to reopen and reshape the fused suture line, a procedure routinely performed by pediatric neurosurgeons (usually in conjunction with a craniofacial plastic surgeon). Hence, the medical specialty most directly responsible for treating trigonocephaly is neurosurgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plakophilin-2",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent genetic cause of arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy is loss-of-function mutation in the desmosomal protein plakophilin-2 (encoded by PKP2). Plakophilin-2 is essential for maintaining mechanical coupling between cardiomyocytes; pathogenic variants weaken desmosomal adhesion, promoting myocyte detachment, fibrofatty replacement of ventricular myocardium, and the ventricular arrhythmias characteristic of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiologist",
    "long_answer": "Variant (Prinzmetal) angina is caused by coronary artery vasospasm, producing transient myocardial ischemia. Diagnosis relies on cardiac-specific tests such as ECG monitoring, provocative coronary angiography, and management with nitrates or calcium-channel blockers—procedures and therapies that fall within the expertise of a cardiologist. Therefore, a cardiologist is the appropriate specialist to diagnose and treat variant angina."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Injection-site abscess",
    "long_answer": "BCG is a live attenuated mycobacterial vaccine that must be deposited within the dermis. If it is injected subcutaneously the organisms multiply in the fatty tissue, leading to extensive local necrosis and caseation; this typically presents as a cold, suppurative abscess at the injection site. To avoid this complication the vaccine is therefore given intradermally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GARS",
    "long_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 2D (CMT2D) is an autosomal-dominant axonal neuropathy caused by pathogenic variants in the GARS gene, which encodes glycyl-tRNA synthetase. Mutations in GARS disrupt tRNA charging and impair axonal maintenance, leading to the characteristic peripheral neuropathy of CMT2D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is an autosomal-dominant genetic disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene. The mutation promotes formation of multiple neurofibromas—benign Schwann-cell tumours—that grow along peripheral nerves and within the spinal canal. As these tumours enlarge they mechanically compress the affected nerves and spinal cord, leading to muscle weakness, loss of coordination, and other motor deficits. Thus NF1 exemplifies a genetic disorder in which nerve compression directly produces impaired motor function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Catheter ablation for atrial fibrillation",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary vein stenosis is a recognized iatrogenic complication of radiofrequency catheter ablation used to isolate the pulmonary veins in patients with atrial fibrillation. Thermal injury and scarring at the ostia of the veins during this procedure can progressively narrow the vein lumen, producing clinically significant pulmonary vein stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARX",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ARX (aristaless related homeobox) gene, which is located on the X chromosome at Xp22.13, are a well-established cause of X-linked infantile epileptic spasms syndrome (also called West syndrome). Pathogenic variants in ARX disrupt neuronal development and lead to the characteristic infantile spasms and developmental encephalopathy seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD4+ T helper cells",
    "long_answer": "Individuals who develop Whipple’s disease exhibit an underlying defect in cell-mediated immunity characterized by abnormally low numbers of circulating CD4⁺ T-helper lymphocytes, particularly Th1 memory cells. The reduced CD4+ pool limits interferon-γ–mediated activation of macrophages, allowing the intracellular bacterium Tropheryma whipplei to survive and disseminate, predisposing these patients to Whipple’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Safe-in-Sound Excellence in Hearing Loss Prevention Award",
    "long_answer": "The Safe-in-Sound Excellence in Hearing Loss Prevention Award, established by NIOSH together with the National Hearing Conservation Association, specifically honors companies and organizations that have demonstrated outstanding success in preventing occupational noise-induced hearing loss through effective noise control and hearing conservation programs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kerosene",
    "long_answer": "Kerosene (paraffin oil), a hydrocarbon fuel commonly kept in homes for cooking, lighting or heating, readily dissolves epidermal lipids when it contacts the skin. The resulting defatting and barrier disruption lead to erythema, dryness and cracking, producing a well-recognized form of irritant contact dermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome is characterized by congenital sensorineural hearing loss together with retinitis pigmentosa, a progressive retinal degeneration that causes night blindness and loss of peripheral (tunnel) vision, eventually leading to severe visual impairment or blindness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MKRN3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the imprinted MKRN3 gene, situated in the Prader-Willi critical region on chromosome 15q11-q13, remove an inhibitory signal on GnRH secretion. When the mutated allele is paternally inherited, this leads to early activation of the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis and familial central precocious puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroschisis",
    "long_answer": "Gastroschisis is a congenital defect in the anterior abdominal wall that allows fetal intestines (and sometimes other viscera) to protrude freely outside the abdomen without a covering sac. It is distinct from omphalocele and has a known, though typically weak, association with jejuno-ileal or other forms of intestinal atresia, likely because the same vascular insult that produces the abdominal wall defect can also disrupt intestinal blood supply during development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Classical conditioning",
    "long_answer": "If a toddler repeatedly experiences the pain or fright of falling (unconditioned stimulus) while being in high places (initially neutral stimulus), the brain can form an association between heights and fear. Over time the previously neutral cue of elevation becomes a conditioned stimulus that automatically elicits a fear response. This associative learning process—classical conditioning—is a well-established pathway for the development of specific phobias such as acrophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The TSC2 gene, whose loss-of-function mutations cause tuberous sclerosis complex, is mapped to chromosome 16, specifically the 16p13.3 region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gynecologist",
    "long_answer": "Hematocolpos is the retention of menstrual blood in the vagina due to an outflow obstruction such as an imperforate hymen or transverse vaginal septum. Disorders of the female reproductive tract, including their surgical correction (e.g., hymenectomy), are within the scope of obstetrics-gynecology. Therefore, a gynecologist is the specialist who evaluates and treats patients with hematocolpos."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Biemond syndrome (also called Mohr-Tranebjaerg/Biemond type II) is produced by pathogenic variants in the TIMM8A gene located on the X chromosome. Affected males manifest childhood-onset sensorineural deafness followed by dystonia and optic atrophy, while carrier females are usually asymptomatic. This pattern—full expression in hemizygous males and carrier status in heterozygous females—defines X-linked recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gynecology",
    "long_answer": "Sarcoma botryoides is an embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma that typically arises in the vagina or cervix of infants and young girls, presenting as a grape-like (botryoid) mass protruding from the introitus. Because it originates in the female reproductive tract, its diagnosis, surgical management, and coordination of adjuvant therapy are handled primarily by gynecologic specialists; in children this falls under pediatric and adolescent gynecology/gynecologic oncology, a subspecialty of gynecology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Institutes of Health (NIH)",
    "long_answer": "Diagnostic criteria for dysplastic nevus syndrome (also called familial atypical multiple-mole melanoma syndrome) were formulated at the National Institutes of Health Consensus Development Conference in 1992. The NIH conference set the widely accepted standards—multiple (often >50) melanocytic nevi with several clinically atypical or histologically dysplastic lesions, together with a personal or family history of melanoma—used to recognize and manage the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastric lavage",
    "long_answer": "In acute tricyclic antidepressant overdose, activated charcoal is typically instilled into the stomach through a large-bore orogastric/nasogastric tube at the end of gastric lavage. The lavage removes gastric contents, and the activated charcoal adsorbs any residual drug, reducing further absorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schistosomiasis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic infection with Schistosoma species (e.g., S. mansoni or S. japonicum) can provoke progressive pulmonary vascular remodeling and obliteration, and is officially classified by the World Health Organization as a cause of group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension. Thus, schistosomiasis is a well-established parasitic risk factor for developing pulmonary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pressure ulcer",
    "long_answer": "Pressure ulcers (decubitus or bedsore wounds) form when prolonged external pressure over bony prominences causes repeated cycles of tissue ischemia followed by reperfusion. These ischemia–reperfusion episodes generate oxidative stress and inflammatory damage, making pressure ulcers the prototypical wounds linked to this mechanism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytotoxic CD8+ T cells",
    "long_answer": "In toxic epidermal necrolysis, large numbers of activated cytotoxic CD8+ T lymphocytes infiltrate the epidermis and release granulysin, perforin/granzyme B, and Fas ligand, triggering widespread apoptosis of keratinocytes. These CD8+ T cells are therefore considered the principal effector cells responsible for keratinocyte death in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis–ptosis–epicanthus inversus syndrome (especially type I, which includes premature ovarian failure) is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the FOXL2 gene on chromosome 3q23. FOXL2 encodes a forkhead box transcription factor critical for eyelid development and ovarian folliculogenesis; when mutated, it leads to the eyelid malformations of BPES and, in many female patients, premature (secondary) ovarian failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "Diseases that cause thickening and stiffening of the interstitium—collectively called interstitial lung diseases or pulmonary fibrosis—affect the lung parenchyma. The interstitium is the delicate tissue surrounding the alveoli, so its scarring directly impairs lung function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperventilation",
    "long_answer": "At high altitude the fall in atmospheric O₂ lowers arterial PO₂, stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies. The homeostatic response is reflex hyperventilation—an increase in the rate and depth of breathing—which raises alveolar and arterial O₂ partial pressure and helps restore oxygen delivery, even though it produces a secondary respiratory alkalosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD8+ cytotoxic T cells",
    "long_answer": "In Hashimoto’s autoimmune thyroiditis the thyroid parenchyma is infiltrated by autoreactive CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes. These cells recognize thyroid-specific antigens presented on MHC class I molecules of follicular epithelial cells and induce their apoptosis through perforin–granzyme release and Fas–FasL interactions, leading to progressive destruction of the gland."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Executive dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "Neuropsychological testing in primary lateral sclerosis commonly reveals subtle but measurable deficits in frontal-lobe executive functions—impaired verbal fluency, set-shifting, working memory and other frontotemporal tasks—while general intellect remains largely intact, indicating the presence of executive dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperkalemia",
    "long_answer": "Elevated serum potassium (hyperkalemia) can develop after cardiac arrest because global ischemia and cell lysis release intracellular K⁺ and renal perfusion falls, impairing potassium excretion. In metabolic acidosis, excess H⁺ enters cells in exchange for K⁺, shifting potassium into the extracellular fluid. Thus both cardiac arrest and metabolic acidosis can independently lead to the same condition—hyperkalemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Generalized Arterial Calcification of Infancy causes widespread calcification and stenosis of large- and medium-sized arteries, leading to severe neonatal hypertension and heart failure. Infants typically present with cardiovascular compromise, and diagnosis relies on echocardiography, vascular imaging, and management of heart failure—all core activities of pediatric cardiology. Consequently, pediatric cardiologists are the specialists most often responsible for recognizing and treating GACI, including initiating bisphosphonate therapy and coordinating multidisciplinary care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxoplasmosis",
    "long_answer": "Primary infection with the protozoan parasite Toxoplasma gondii during pregnancy can cross the placenta and cause congenital toxoplasmosis, which is well-documented to result in miscarriage or stillbirth as well as severe fetal abnormalities. Therefore, toxoplasmosis is the parasitic infection that can lead to stillbirth if acquired while pregnant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "China",
    "long_answer": "Familial progressive hyperpigmentation is an extremely rare genodermatosis, but the greatest number of reported pedigrees originate from China. Case series and genetic studies consistently note that the disorder is seen predominantly in Chinese families, making China the country with the highest documented prevalence of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum",
    "long_answer": "True diphallia represents complete duplication of the penile shaft, which requires duplication of all three erectile bodies within the penis: the paired corpora cavernosa and the single corpus spongiosum (with its enclosed urethra). Thus, these erectile structures in the body of the penis are the anatomical elements involved in the formation of true diphallia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Mucolipidosis type IV commonly causes eye problems such as corneal clouding, retinal degeneration, strabismus, and optic nerve atrophy. Management of these ocular manifestations is the domain of ophthalmology, the medical specialty dedicated to diagnosing and treating diseases of the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia variabilis",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the gap-junction genes GJB3 (connexin-31) and GJB4 (connexin-30.3) cause the autosomal dominant skin disorder erythrokeratodermia variabilis (EKV). EKV is typified by transient erythematous patches accompanied by persistent or migrating hyperkeratotic plaques, so patients commonly present with areas of marked hyperkeratosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≥55 mg/g",
    "long_answer": "In the TDx-FLM surfactant-to-albumin (S/A) assay, an S/A ratio of 55 mg surfactant per g albumin or higher is the established cutoff that signifies fetal lung maturity. At this level the fetal lungs contain adequate surfactant, greatly lowering the risk of infant respiratory distress syndrome (IRDS). Values <40 mg/g are considered immature, 40–54 mg/g transitional, and ≥55 mg/g mature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parotid gland",
    "long_answer": "The parotid gland’s excretory (Stensen’s) duct passes across the masseter muscle, pierces the buccinator, and opens into the oral cavity on the buccal mucosa opposite the crown of the maxillary second molar."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX19 (TPIT)",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TBX19 gene, which encodes the T-pit transcription factor required for pro-opiomelanocortin expression in corticotrophs, produce congenital isolated adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) deficiency. The resulting cortisol lack leads to a characteristic peripheral lymphocytosis that is repeatedly documented in affected neonates and infants. Thus, TBX19 mutation is the recognized genetic cause that links ACTH deficiency and lymphocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type 4 renal tubular acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Hypoaldosteronism (or aldosterone resistance) reduces Na⁺ reabsorption and impairs distal tubular secretion of H⁺ and K⁺. The resulting hyperkalemia limits ammonium generation, producing a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis characteristic of Type 4 renal tubular acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STS",
    "long_answer": "X-linked ichthyosis is caused by deletions or loss-of-function mutations in the STS (steroid sulfatase) gene on chromosome Xp22.3. STS encodes the enzyme steroid sulfatase, which hydrolyzes cholesterol sulfate in the epidermis; its deficiency leads to abnormal retention of cholesterol sulfate, impaired corneocyte shedding, and the characteristic generalized scaling of X-linked ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Geographic tongue",
    "long_answer": "Geographic tongue (benign migratory glossitis) presents as multiple erythematous, depapillated areas with whitish serpiginous borders that migrate over time, giving the dorsum of the tongue a map-like appearance. Although its precise etiology is unknown, clinical studies note higher prevalence in individuals with parafunctional oral habits such as bruxism and tongue thrusting, suggesting these habits may contribute to or exacerbate the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Klumpke's paralysis is a lower brachial plexus neuropathy (injury of the C8–T1 nerve roots). Disorders of peripheral nerves are diagnosed and managed primarily by specialists in neurology, who evaluate the extent of nerve damage, order electrodiagnostic studies, and guide both medical and rehabilitative treatment. Therefore, the medical specialty most likely to treat Klumpke's paralysis is neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Persistent truncus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) disrupts development of the third and fourth pharyngeal (branchial) pouches, leading to malformation of the cardiac outflow tract derived from neural‐crest cells. The hallmark congenital heart defect linked to this mechanism is persistent truncus arteriosus, in which a single great vessel fails to septate into the aorta and pulmonary artery. This defect is classically and most commonly cited in association with DiGeorge syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arylsulfatase B",
    "long_answer": "Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type VI) results from inherited deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase). Without functional arylsulfatase B, sulfate groups cannot be removed from dermatan sulfate and related glycosaminoglycans, leading to their intralysosomal accumulation and the characteristic clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type IV hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "The intestinal inflammation of tropical sprue is driven predominantly by a T-cell–mediated, delayed-type (Type IV) hypersensitivity reaction. Activated lymphocytes release cytokines that recruit macrophages and cause villous atrophy and mucosal injury, accounting for the malabsorption and inflammatory changes characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive symmetric erythrokeratoderma",
    "long_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia with ataxia is considered a variant of progressive symmetric erythrokeratoderma, one of the two classic forms of hereditary erythrokeratodermias (disorders of keratinization/ichthyosis). Patients show the characteristic bilaterally symmetrical hyperkeratotic erythematous plaques of PSEK together with cerebellar ataxia caused by ELOVL4 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) is a lysosomal storage disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme arylsulfatase A. Without this enzyme, sulfatides—sulfated glycolipids (a category of fats/lipids)—accumulate in cells of the nervous system and other tissues. The buildup of these lipid molecules leads to progressive destruction of myelin and the neurological symptoms characteristic of the disease. Thus, MLD is indeed associated with the pathological accumulation of fats (lipids) in the body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lithiasis",
    "long_answer": "Lithiasis is the medical term for the pathological formation of calculi (stones) in the body. Depending on location, it is subclassified as nephrolithiasis or urolithiasis for kidney/urinary stones and cholelithiasis for gallbladder stones, but the overarching process of stone crystallization and aggregation from supersaturated bodily fluids is called lithiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Necrotizing fasciitis is a rare, rapidly progressive soft-tissue infection most often precipitated by trauma, surgery, varicella lesions, or underlying illnesses such as diabetes or immunosuppression. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are widely used analgesic and antipyretic agents. A few observational reports—particularly in children with varicella—have suggested that NSAID use might delay recognition of worsening soft-tissue infection or, by modulating host immune responses, potentially facilitate progression to invasive bacterial disease. However, large epidemiologic studies and systematic reviews have failed to demonstrate a consistent, causal link between NSAID exposure and the development of necrotizing fasciitis in the general population. Current clinical guidelines do not list NSAID therapy as an independent, universal risk factor for the disease. Therefore, it is not accurate to state that NSAID use always increases the risk of necrotizing fasciitis; at most, an association is unproven, limited to specific contexts, and remains controversial."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Propofol",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by volatile halogenated anesthetics and depolarizing neuromuscular blockers, not by intravenous induction agents. Propofol, an intravenous anesthetic, does not activate the ryanodine receptor–mediated Ca2⁺ release responsible for malignant hyperthermia episodes, making it a well-established safe choice in patients with malignant hyperthermia susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intestinal tumors",
    "long_answer": "Short bowel syndrome usually arises after extensive surgical resection of the small intestine. One of the common reasons surgeons remove large segments of bowel is the presence of benign or malignant intestinal tumors—abnormal proliferations of tissue. Because these neoplastic growths must be excised en bloc with surrounding healthy bowel, intestinal tumors are a well-recognized, common cause of short bowel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synovial membrane",
    "long_answer": "In synovial (osteo)chondromatosis, the cartilaginous transformation originates from metaplasia of the synovial membrane (synovium) lining the joint, resulting in formation of intra-articular cartilaginous nodules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Johns Hopkins Hospital",
    "long_answer": "The inaugural description of propionic acidemia (then called propionic aciduria) was published in 1961 by Barton Childs, William Nyhan and colleagues working at Johns Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore. Their report of two siblings with severe metabolic acidosis and massive urinary excretion of propionic acid introduced the disorder as a new inborn error of metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blue nevus",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is a hereditary syndrome (often due to PRKAR1A mutations) characterized by spotty skin pigmentation, myxomas, and endocrine tumors. Among its cutaneous manifestations are multiple pigmented melanocytic lesions, classically the blue nevus—especially the epithelioid (pigmented epithelioid melanocytoma) variant—making this type of nevus a recognized feature of Carney complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conversion disorder",
    "long_answer": "Conversion disorder, now formally termed functional neurological symptom disorder, presents with neurological symptoms such as weakness, sensory loss, or seizures that are precipitated or exacerbated by psychological stress. Neuroimaging and other investigations show no structural brain lesion or neurodegenerative process, distinguishing it from organic neurological disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Therapeutic hypothermia",
    "long_answer": "The established treatment for neonatal encephalopathy due to birth asphyxia (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) is therapeutic hypothermia—either whole-body or selective head cooling to ~33–34 °C initiated within 6 hours of birth and maintained for about 72 hours. Cooling lowers cerebral metabolism and mitigates excitotoxicity, inflammation, and apoptotic pathways, significantly improving survival and neurodevelopmental outcomes in multiple randomized controlled trials."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cathepsin S",
    "long_answer": "The CTSS gene encodes the lysosomal cysteine protease Cathepsin S. Studies of biomarker profiles in Gaucher’s disease—a lysosomal storage disorder caused by glucocerebrosidase deficiency—show that Cathepsin S concentration is markedly increased in patient plasma and tissue, reflecting macrophage activation and altered lysosomal function characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RNase H2 complex",
    "long_answer": "Aicardi–Goutières syndrome can be caused by mutations in any of the three genes RNASEH2A, RNASEH2B or RNASEH2C, which encode the heterotrimeric ribonuclease H2 (RNase H2) complex. RNase H2 is a trimer made of the A, B and C subunits; its loss of function leads to accumulation of ribonucleotides in DNA, aberrant nucleic-acid sensing and the type I interferon signature characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "CHARGE syndrome is genetically confirmed in most patients by detecting heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the CHD7 gene, which encodes a chromatin-remodeling ATP-dependent helicase. Accordingly, BGI Genomics’ diagnostic panel for CHARGE syndrome focuses on sequencing and analyzing CHD7 to identify pathogenic variants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease) results from a deficiency of the hepatic isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, the enzyme that cleaves α-1,4-glycosidic bonds to release glucose-1-phosphate from liver glycogen. Loss of this enzyme impairs glycogenolysis, leading to hepatomegaly, mild hypoglycaemia, and accumulation of glycogen in the liver—hallmarks of GSD VI."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Desmin-related myofibrillar myopathy is a hereditary neuromuscular disorder that presents with progressive skeletal muscle weakness and can involve cardiomyopathy. Evaluation requires a neurologic examination, electromyography, nerve-conduction studies, and muscle biopsy—all procedures typically directed by a neurologist (often with neuromuscular subspecialty training). Hence, the medical specialty responsible for diagnosing this condition is Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conduction aphasia",
    "long_answer": "An infarction within the left Sylvian (lateral) fissure jeopardises the subcortical white-matter fibres of the arcuate fasciculus and adjacent supramarginal cortex that link Broca’s and Wernicke’s language areas. Damage to these fibres produces conduction aphasia, in which speech is fluent and comprehension is relatively preserved but repetition is markedly impaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia is most commonly caused by mutations in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor calcium channel. Defective RYR1 channels open excessively when exposed to triggering agents such as volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine, releasing large amounts of Ca²⁺ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The resulting sustained muscle contraction drives rapid ATP consumption, heat production, lactic acidosis, and the systemic hypermetabolic state characteristic of malignant hyperthermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmoid tumor",
    "long_answer": "Kevin Reilly was diagnosed with a desmoid tumor—an uncommon, locally aggressive fibrous neoplasm that arises from musculo-aponeurotic connective tissue. Because the tumor infiltrated the soft tissues of his shoulder girdle and chest wall, surgeons had to perform a radical resection that removed his left arm, shoulder, and five ribs to achieve disease control."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brugada syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Brugada syndrome is an inherited arrhythmia disorder most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SCN5A gene that encodes the cardiac fast sodium channel (NaV1.5). The defective sodium current alters ventricular depolarization and repolarization, producing the characteristic ECG pattern and creating a substrate for malignant arrhythmias, particularly polymorphic ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation, which can lead to sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Avoidant personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "Avoidant personality disorder, listed in the DSM-IV-TR, is sometimes called the “anxious” or “panphobic” personality because affected individuals exhibit a pervasive fear of virtually all social interactions and potential negative evaluation. This broad, undifferentiated fearfulness (panphobia) is a core descriptive feature of the disorder, making Avoidant personality disorder the DSM-IV-TR personality disorder most closely linked with panphobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "In hypopituitarism, deficiency of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) from the anterior pituitary reduces stimulation of the thyroid gland, leading to decreased synthesis and release of thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3). This central (secondary) lack of thyroid hormone production manifests clinically as hypothyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Forebrain (prosencephalon)",
    "long_answer": "Holoprosencephaly occurs when the embryonic forebrain, known as the prosencephalon, fails to divide into the paired cerebral hemispheres during the 3rd–4th week of gestation. Defective midline patterning signals (e.g., reduced Sonic hedgehog from prechordal tissue) in this organ lead to the characteristic single-lobed cerebrum and associated midline facial anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin",
    "long_answer": "Botulinum toxin, produced by Clostridium botulinum, cleaves SNARE proteins in motor nerve terminals, preventing presynaptic release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. This blockade causes flaccid paralysis of skeletal muscles, and weakness of the levator palpebrae superioris leads to the characteristic ptosis seen in botulism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperphosphatemia",
    "long_answer": "Excess phosphate in the blood (hyperphosphatemia) drives the precipitation of calcium-phosphate complexes, which deposit in renal tubules and interstitium. This calcium-phosphate deposition is a well-recognized mechanism leading to nephrocalcinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior fossa decompression",
    "long_answer": "Syringomyelia secondary to an Arnold-Chiari (Chiari I) malformation is most effectively treated by posterior fossa (foramen magnum) decompression—typically a suboccipital craniectomy with C1 laminectomy and duraplasty—which enlarges the foramen magnum, restores cerebrospinal fluid flow at the craniocervical junction, and allows the syrinx to regress."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondrial DNA mutation analysis",
    "long_answer": "Sequencing or targeted PCR-based analysis of mitochondrial DNA identifies the characteristic point mutations—m.3243A>G in tRNALeu(UUR) for MELAS and m.8344A>G in tRNALys for MERRF—thereby reliably distinguishing the two syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELANE mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the ELANE gene (formerly called ELA2), which encodes neutrophil elastase, are the most frequent and well-established genetic cause of both severe congenital neutropenia (Kostmann disease) and cyclic neutropenia. Different heterozygous ELANE variants disrupt neutrophil maturation, leading either to persistent profound neutropenia (SCN) or the characteristic 21-day oscillations of neutrophil counts seen in cyclic neutropenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase whose deficiency causes Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type I), is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 4 at band 4p16.3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL1B",
    "long_answer": "The IL1B gene encodes the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-1β. Polymorphisms that increase IL-1β expression enhance gastric mucosal inflammation in response to Helicobacter pylori infection, thereby predisposing individuals to peptic ulcer disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blepharophimosis syndrome (BPES)",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis syndrome, formally called blepharophimosis–ptosis–epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES), is classically defined by the triad of congenital ptosis, epicanthus inversus, and a shortened horizontal palpebral fissure (blepharophimosis). These ocular features are pathognomonic for the syndrome and arise from developmental defects in the eyelids, most often due to pathogenic variants in the FOXL2 gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertonia",
    "long_answer": "Prenatal exposure to cocaine can disrupt central neurotransmitter regulation of motor pathways. As a result, some neonates and young infants show hypertonia—abnormally increased skeletal-muscle tone—manifesting as rigid or stiff extremities and exaggerated reflexes during early development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The RET proto-oncogene, mutations of which are a well-established cause of Hirschsprung’s disease, is located on the long arm of chromosome 10 at cytogenetic band 10q11.2—not on chromosome 15. Therefore, the statement that the RET gene associated with Hirschsprung’s disease is on chromosome 15 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 2",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is an autosomal-dominant tumor-predisposition syndrome caused by pathogenic variants in the NF2 gene encoding merlin. Individuals with NF2 characteristically develop multiple central-nervous-system tumors, including vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal or intracranial ependymomas; thus the syndrome is classically linked to both brain tumors in general and ependymomas specifically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Toxic shock syndrome can precipitate disseminated intravascular coagulation and other coagulative disturbances, leading to a markedly decreased circulating platelet count. This low-platelet state is termed thrombocytopenia, and it is a recognized hematologic complication of toxic shock syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urinary incontinence",
    "long_answer": "Excess body weight increases intra-abdominal pressure, which in turn raises pressure on the bladder and urethral sphincter. Over time this mechanical strain weakens pelvic floor support and can impair sphincter closure, leading to episodes of involuntary urine leakage—urinary incontinence, particularly the stress subtype. Numerous epidemiologic studies show a direct, dose-dependent rise in incontinence prevalence with higher body-mass index, establishing overweight as a causal risk factor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome, also called the \"heart-hand\" syndrome, is an autosomal-dominant disorder in which skeletal anomalies of the upper limbs—especially thumb/radial defects and brachydactyly—occur together with congenital heart disease, most commonly atrial or ventricular septal defects. Thus it is the classic congenital syndrome that links brachydactyly with structural cardiac malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMR vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Women who may become pregnant are advised to receive the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine, which contains a live-attenuated rubella component. Vaccination at least 1 month before conception induces protective antibodies against rubella, preventing primary maternal infection and thereby averting congenital rubella syndrome in the fetus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Porphyrinogenic risk",
    "long_answer": "Gliclazide is considered porphyrinogenic: it can induce hepatic enzymes that accelerate heme synthesis, raising porphyrin precursors and thereby triggering an acute porphyric attack. For this reason it is listed as unsafe and should be avoided in patients with acute porphyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), especially with gadolinium contrast, is the modality of choice for diagnosing spinal nerve sheath tumors such as schwannomas and neurofibromas. MRI provides high-resolution visualization of soft tissues, spinal cord, and nerve roots, allowing precise delineation of tumor size, extent, and relationship to adjacent neural structures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia",
    "long_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia—most often due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency—is an autosomal-recessive genetic disorder that impairs cortisol synthesis in the adrenal cortex. The resulting ACTH-driven adrenal hyperplasia leads to overproduction of adrenal androgens. In females this androgen excess commonly manifests as oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, and virilization, making CAH the classic genetic cause of oligomenorrhea linked to excessive androgen production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whole-lung lavage",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis is characterized by intra-alveolar accumulation of surfactant lipoproteins. The accepted first-line and most effective therapy is whole-lung lavage, in which each lung is sequentially filled and drained with warm saline under general anesthesia to wash out the proteinaceous material, thereby restoring alveolar ventilation and gas exchange."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Separation anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Persistent fear of the dark (nyctophobia), especially in children, is often viewed as a symptom of separation anxiety disorder. In DSM anxiety-disorder nosology, excessive distress when alone or at bedtime and refusal to sleep without an attachment figure are core features of separation anxiety, so nighttime darkness fears are interpreted as a manifestation of this disorder rather than a distinct condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt",
    "long_answer": "The transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) procedure is performed to decompress portal hypertension by creating a portosystemic channel within the liver. While effective for bleeding or refractory ascites, TIPS diverts portal blood away from hepatocyte detoxification, raising systemic ammonia and other neurotoxic substances. This increase can precipitate or worsen hepatic encephalopathy, a well-recognized complication in patients—including those with gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE)—who undergo the procedure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Salicylic acid",
    "long_answer": "Lichen spinulosus is a follicular keratotic disorder; therapy aims to dissolve the compact keratin plugs. Topical keratolytics such as 3–6 % salicylic acid ointment are routinely recommended because salicylic acid breaks down inter-cellular keratin, smooths the papules and improves the rough plaques characteristic of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STXBP2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the STXBP2 gene, which encodes the vesicle-fusion regulator Munc18-2, impair apical membrane trafficking in enterocytes, producing Microvillous Inclusion Disease, and simultaneously block cytotoxic-granule exocytosis in NK and cytotoxic T cells, causing Familial Hemophagocytic Lymphohistiocytosis type 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AGK",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic pathogenic variants in the AGK gene (acylglycerol kinase) are the established cause of classical Sengers syndrome. Loss of AGK impairs mitochondrial protein import and phospholipid metabolism, leading to mitochondrial myopathy that manifests clinically as generalized muscle weakness and hypotonia, together with the syndrome’s other hallmarks (congenital cataracts, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, lactic acidosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Achondroplasia",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia accounts for the majority of dwarfism cases worldwide. It results from activating mutations in the FGFR3 gene, leading to impaired endochondral bone growth and the characteristic disproportionate short stature. Clinically and epidemiologically, it is recognized as the single most common form of dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNJ5 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line or somatic mutations in the KCNJ5 gene, which encodes the GIRK4 potassium channel, cause familial hyperaldosteronism type III. The mutant channel allows excess sodium influx, stimulating aldosterone over-production. The resulting hyperaldosteronism leads to hypokalaemia, producing muscle weakness along with hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toupee",
    "long_answer": "A toupee is a partial wig designed to cover localized areas of hair loss, such as the crown or front scalp commonly affected in androgenetic (male-pattern) baldness. It is tailored to blend with the wearer’s existing hair, providing cosmetic coverage without treating the underlying follicular miniaturization driven by dihydrotestosterone (DHT)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Levodopa",
    "long_answer": "Levodopa (often given with the peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor carbidopa) remains the gold-standard therapy for Parkinson’s disease because it is the metabolic precursor of dopamine, crosses the blood-brain barrier, and replenishes striatal dopamine levels, providing the greatest and most reliable symptomatic relief of bradykinesia and rigidity compared with any other pharmacologic treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Remotivation therapy",
    "long_answer": "Remotivation therapy is a structured group-based intervention used chiefly with confused or cognitively impaired patients, such as those suffering from organic brain syndrome. By engaging the patient in guided discussion of factual, concrete topics (e.g., current events, past interests), it stimulates attention, orientation, and social interaction, thereby helping to re-establish or improve mental functions that have been diminished by the organic brain disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN5A",
    "long_answer": "The SCN5A gene encodes the cardiac sodium-channel α-subunit (NaV1.5). Pathogenic SCN5A variants are the most frequent genetic cause of Brugada syndrome (≈20–30 % of cases) and have also been repeatedly identified in families with arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia/cardiomyopathy (classified as ARVD10). Loss-of-function mutations in SCN5A reduce inward sodium current, producing conduction delay in the right ventricular outflow tract that underlies the arrhythmic substrate in both disorders, making SCN5A the gene most commonly linked to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CSTB",
    "long_answer": "The most common progressive myoclonus epilepsy beginning with the letter “U” is Unverricht–Lundborg disease (EPM1). This disorder is caused by loss-of-function mutations—most often an unstable dodecamer repeat expansion in the promoter—of the CSTB gene, which encodes the protease inhibitor cystatin B. Defective CSTB leads to neuronal dysfunction and the characteristic myoclonus-epilepsy phenotype of Unverricht–Lundborg disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vertigo",
    "long_answer": "Patients with familial progressive vestibulocochlear dysfunction frequently experience vertigo—a spinning or whirling sensation that arises from vestibular system impairment. Vertigo is one of the most common clinical forms of dizziness and is therefore recognized as both a hallmark vestibular symptom of this hereditary disorder and a widely encountered type of dizziness in general clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is defined by an abnormal shortening of the digits that results from congenital shortening of the phalangeal bones (proximal, middle, and/or distal phalanges) of the fingers; these shortened phalanges give the hand its characteristically short appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periventricular nodular heterotopia",
    "long_answer": "A balanced translocation t(X;16)(q28;q11.2) can disrupt the FLNA gene on Xq28. Loss-of-function of FLNA causes the X-linked neuronal migration disorder known as periventricular nodular heterotopia (PVNH). Patients with PVNH, beside epilepsy due to ectopic grey matter lining the ventricles, often show mild craniofacial anomalies such as hypertelorism, broad nasal bridge and high-arched palate. Thus, the genetic disorder associated with the Xq28/16q11.2 translocation and presenting with hypertelorism is periventricular nodular heterotopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The standard tendon-transfer procedure for persistent foot-drop (loss of tibialis anterior function) is to reroute the tibialis posterior tendon to the dorsum of the foot. This operation is well described for causes such as common-peroneal-nerve palsy, Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease and distal myopathies. The fibularis (peroneus) longus tendon is not the usual or recommended donor in these cases; it is generally left intact to preserve eversion strength and lateral-column stability, and it is itself often weakened in many neuromuscular disorders. Therefore, established surgical consensus does not involve “repurposing” the fibularis longus to act as the tibialis anterior in the treatment of foot drop due to distal myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Deficiencies in early classical-pathway complement components—especially C1q, C2, and C4—impair clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic debris. This promotes loss of self-tolerance and the development of the multisystem autoimmune disease systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Consequently, inherited or acquired deficiency of these complement proteins is strongly and uniquely linked to an increased risk of SLE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris",
    "long_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune blistering disease in which IgG auto-antibodies target desmoglein proteins within desmosomes of stratified squamous epithelium. Loss of desmosomal integrity leads to acantholysis and secondary disruption of adjacent gap junctions, producing fragile blisters and erosions that characteristically involve the oral and other mucous membranes as well as skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teeth bleaching",
    "long_answer": "Intrinsic stains from dental fluorosis are often lightened with professional teeth bleaching, typically using hydrogen- or carbamide-peroxide gels. The oxidizing agents penetrate the enamel and break down the pigmented molecules responsible for the brown or mottled discoloration, improving tooth color without removing additional tooth structure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Flint",
    "long_answer": "Flint is a hard form of microcrystalline quartz (crystalline silica) that was historically knapped into stone tools and struck to produce sparks for fire-starting. When flint is mined, cut, or ground, respirable crystalline silica dust is released; chronic inhalation of this dust produces the occupational lung disease silicosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium channel blockers",
    "long_answer": "Magnesium physiologically antagonizes voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx into excitable cells. Medications known as calcium channel blockers (e.g., verapamil, diltiazem, dihydropyridines) exert the same mechanism pharmacologically, and are therefore used to treat many of the same calcium-dependent conditions such as hypertension, certain arrhythmias and migraine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Kinking hair is a disorder of the hair shaft. Medical doctors who specialize in conditions of the skin, hair, and nails are dermatologists. Their training includes diagnosing structural hair abnormalities such as acquired progressive kinking, pili torti, or other shaft disorders and implementing appropriate medical or procedural management. Therefore, a dermatologist is the specialist equipped to evaluate and treat kinking hair."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parkinson’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Parkinson’s disease is characterized by intracellular Lewy bodies composed largely of aggregated alpha-synuclein and is strongly linked to mitochondrial dysfunction in dopaminergic neurons, both of which contribute to the neurodegeneration seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive dysphagia",
    "long_answer": "The earliest and most frequent presenting complaint in esophageal cancer is progressive dysphagia—difficulty swallowing that starts with solid foods and gradually involves liquids as the tumor narrows the esophageal lumen. Tumor growth obstructs the esophageal passage, so patients notice swallowing problems before other symptoms such as weight loss or pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALK",
    "long_answer": "Familial cases of neuroblastoma most often arise from germ-line activating mutations in the ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) proto-oncogene. These autosomal-dominant ALK mutations drive constitutive tyrosine-kinase signaling, predisposing carriers to the development of neuroblastoma in multiple family members."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome is frequently associated with congenital cyanotic heart defects such as truncus arteriosus and tetralogy of Fallot. These malformations reduce arterial oxygen saturation, leading to hypoxemia. The clinical manifestation of this oxygen deficiency is cyanosis—a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preeclampsia",
    "long_answer": "Large cohort studies show that women who develop preeclampsia—a hypertensive disorder of pregnancy characterized by hypertension and proteinuria—have a markedly higher risk of subsequently developing chronic kidney disease and progressing to end-stage renal disease (kidney failure). Endothelial injury and persistent renal microvascular damage during preeclampsia are believed to underlie this long-term renal risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PEX7",
    "long_answer": "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1 (RCDP-1) is an autosomal-recessive peroxisome biogenesis disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the PEX7 gene, which encodes the peroxisomal targeting signal-2 (PTS2) receptor required for importing specific enzymes into peroxisomes. Loss-of-function mutations in PEX7 disrupt plasmalogen synthesis and other peroxisomal pathways, producing the skeletal, neurological, and ocular features characteristic of RCDP-1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Collecting duct",
    "long_answer": "Distal (type I) renal tubular acidosis results from impaired H⁺ secretion by the α-intercalated cells of the cortical collecting duct—the terminal portion of the nephron responsible for final urine acidification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vertical transmission",
    "long_answer": "In dual inheritance (gene–culture co-evolution) theory, cultural information can be passed vertically—from parents to their offspring—as opposed to horizontal (peer-to-peer) or oblique (older generation to younger, non-offspring) transmission. The same term, vertical transmission, is used in infectious disease biology to denote pathogens that are transmitted from a mother to her child during pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding. Thus, “vertical transmission” is the shared term for parent-to-offspring transfer in both cultural and biomedical contexts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Bart–Pumphrey syndrome, characterized by leukonychia, palmoplantar keratoderma, and sensorineural hearing loss, is caused by heterozygous mutations in the GJB2 gene (connexin-26) and is transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNQ2",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of benign familial neonatal seizures are caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KCNQ2 gene, which encodes the Kv7.2 subunit of the neuronal voltage-gated potassium channel mediating the M-current. Disruption of this channel lowers the threshold for neuronal firing, producing the characteristic self-limited seizures in otherwise healthy neonates. Although mutations in KCNQ3 and a few other genes can also cause this syndrome, KCNQ2 mutations are by far the most prevalent and well-established etiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "In DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion), the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches fail to develop properly, leading to aplasia or hypoplasia of the parathyroid glands. Loss of parathyroid tissue causes deficient parathyroid hormone secretion—i.e., congenital hypoparathyroidism—resulting in hypocalcemia. Thus, hypoparathyroidism is the classic endocrine disease associated with DiGeorge syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Large-scale mitochondrial DNA deletion",
    "long_answer": "Kearns–Sayre syndrome is almost always caused by a spontaneous, single large deletion (typically 1–10 kb) in mitochondrial DNA. This deletion removes multiple contiguous genes needed for oxidative phosphorylation, leading to the characteristic multisystem features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplantation",
    "long_answer": "For malignant infantile osteopetrosis, allogeneic bone marrow (hematopoietic stem-cell) transplantation replaces the defective osteoclast lineage with normal donor cells, restoring bone resorption and hematopoiesis. This intervention achieves long-term survival and clinical improvement in a substantial proportion of affected infants, making it the only treatment judged satisfactory in many cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pterin-4 α-carbinolamine dehydratase deficiency is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the PCBD1 gene. Because disease manifests only when both alleles are affected, and carriers with one mutant allele are asymptomatic, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epinephrine",
    "long_answer": "Intramuscular epinephrine is the first-line, life-saving treatment for any form of anaphylaxis, including exercise-induced anaphylaxis and other severe systemic allergic reactions. It rapidly reverses airway constriction, hypotension, and urticaria by stimulating α- and β-adrenergic receptors, and patients at risk are advised to carry an epinephrine auto-injector."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Barrett's esophagus is a premalignant metaplasia of the distal esophagus that commonly progresses through dysplasia to esophageal adenocarcinoma. Numerous studies show that DNA-methylation, histone-modification and other epigenetic alterations accumulate in Barrett’s epithelium and its dysplastic stages, driving the neoplastic transformation that culminates in esophageal adenocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fabry disease",
    "long_answer": "Fabry disease, an X-linked sphingolipidosis caused by α-galactosidase A deficiency with accumulation of globotriaosylceramide, classically presents with clusters of cutaneous angiokeratomas (especially in a ‘bathing-trunk’ distribution), making the presence of multiple angiokeratomas a hallmark clinical sign of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laser photocoagulation",
    "long_answer": "In severe retinopathy of prematurity, peripheral retinal laser photocoagulation is applied to the avascular retina. By destroying the ischemic tissue that releases VEGF, the laser treatment causes regression of the pathologic neovascular tufts and prevents further growth of abnormal blood vessels, making it the standard vision-saving therapy for advanced ROP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZFPM2 (FOG2)",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the ZFPM2 gene, which encodes the transcriptional co-factor FOG2, have been repeatedly identified in patients and families with congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH). ZFPM2 interacts with GATA transcription factors to regulate diaphragmatic and pulmonary development; disruption of this gene impairs formation of the pleuroperitoneal membranes, producing the characteristic diaphragmatic defect seen in CDH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alveolar soft part sarcoma",
    "long_answer": "To date, the U.S. FDA has granted checkpoint-inhibitor approval only for alveolar soft part sarcoma: in 2021–2022 atezolizumab (anti-PD-L1) was approved for unresectable or metastatic ASPS in adults and children ≥2 years. No other soft-tissue sarcoma subtype has a dedicated immunotherapy approval, so ASPS remains the sole soft-tissue sarcoma with regulatory authorization for immune-checkpoint therapy as of 2023."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Olfactory reference syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Olfactory reference syndrome (ORS) is a psychiatric disorder in which individuals hold a persistent, unshakeable belief that they emit a foul or abnormal body odor that others can smell, despite no objective evidence. This false belief (often delusional or obsessional in nature) causes marked distress, social anxiety, and avoidance behavior, and is recognized in contemporary classifications as an obsessive-compulsive and related disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary edema",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary edema is the accumulation of fluid within the lung interstitium and alveoli, most commonly precipitated by systemic fluid overload and elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (as in left-sided heart failure). Loop diuretics such as furosemide are standard therapy because they remove excess intravascular volume, lower pulmonary capillary pressure, and thereby resolve the fluid in the lungs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coma",
    "long_answer": "A profound reduction in central nervous system activity can occur in both severe hypothermia and marked hyperglycemia. When core body temperature falls markedly, neuronal metabolism slows, leading to progressive loss of consciousness that culminates in coma. Likewise, extreme hyperglycemia (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state) produces hyper-osmolarity, dehydration, and acidosis that depress cerebral function, also resulting in coma. Thus, coma is a potential outcome of both hypothermia and hyperglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jaundice",
    "long_answer": "Jaundice is a yellow discoloration of the skin (and mucous membranes) caused by elevated circulating bilirubin. Because bilirubin metabolism depends on normal hepatic uptake, conjugation, and excretion, jaundice frequently signals systemic disorders such as liver disease, biliary obstruction, or hemolysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otitis media",
    "long_answer": "The common cold can cause swelling of the nasopharyngeal mucosa and Eustachian tube dysfunction, which traps secretions behind the tympanic membrane. This predisposes to inflammation and infection of the middle ear, clinically termed otitis media—one of the most frequent complications of viral upper-respiratory infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azoospermia",
    "long_answer": "Both XX male syndrome (de la Chapelle syndrome) and cryptorchidism commonly lead to azoospermia—complete absence of sperm in the ejaculate. In XX male individuals, lack of the Y-chromosome AZF spermatogenesis regions results in non-obstructive azoospermia. In cryptorchidism, failure of testicular descent exposes germ cells to higher abdominal temperature, causing germ-cell loss and often culminating in azoospermia, especially when bilateral or untreated. Thus, azoospermia is the condition associated with both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood clotting",
    "long_answer": "Thrombocytopenia involves a decreased number of platelets (cell fragments derived from megakaryocytes). The primary function of these platelets is to initiate hemostasis by adhering to damaged vessel walls, aggregating with one another, and providing a phospholipid surface for the coagulation cascade, thereby forming a blood clot to stop bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adolf von Strümpell",
    "long_answer": "The German neurologist Adolf von Strümpell first described the familial form of spastic paraparesis in the 1880s and introduced the designation “hereditary spastic paraplegia” (hereditäre spastische Spinalparalyse). His coinage established the term that is still used to refer to this group of inherited disorders characterised by progressive lower-limb spasticity and weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Management of diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis is mainly symptomatic. For the characteristic axial pain and stiffness, first-line therapy is the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), which reduce prostaglandin-mediated inflammation at entheseal ossification sites and provide analgesia, thereby improving mobility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tension headache",
    "long_answer": "When Paget's disease affects the skull, the thickened, enlarged bone can irritate periosteal pain fibers and adjacent soft tissues, producing a dull, tight, band-like pain characteristic of a tension headache—the most common primary headache type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tocilizumab",
    "long_answer": "Tocilizumab is a humanised monoclonal antibody that blocks the interleukin-6 (IL-6) receptor. Because excessive IL-6 signalling drives the lymphoproliferation seen in unicentric Castleman disease, international guidelines recommend tocilizumab as the first-line systemic (medical) therapy when the lesion is not amenable to curative surgery. The same drug is widely used and licensed for rheumatoid arthritis, where IL-6 also plays a central role in joint inflammation, making tocilizumab a shared treatment option for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myoclonic-astatic epilepsy (Doose syndrome)",
    "long_answer": "Myoclonic-astatic epilepsy, also known as Doose syndrome, is an idiopathic generalized epilepsy syndrome whose hallmark seizure type is the myoclonic-astatic (or atonic) seizure, characterized by a brief myoclonic jerk immediately followed by a sudden loss of muscle tone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wiktionary",
    "long_answer": "Bathophobia is listed in Wiktionary, the free-content, collaboratively edited dictionary project maintained by the Wikimedia Foundation. Wiktionary provides open-license definitions for English and many other languages, and its entry for “bathophobia” defines the term as an irrational fear of depths."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B8",
    "long_answer": "Non-paraneoplastic (autoimmune) Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome shows a strong genetic association with the HLA-B8 haplotype (often linked to the DR3 and DQ2 alleles). This HLA subtype increases susceptibility to developing the calcium-channel autoantibodies that underlie the neuromuscular transmission defect characteristic of Lambert–Eaton syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngologist (ENT specialist)",
    "long_answer": "Spasmodic dysphonia is a focal laryngeal dystonia, and its diagnosis relies on direct examination of the vocal folds during phonation. An otolaryngologist—often a laryngologist/ENT specialist—performs flexible or rigid laryngoscopy and stroboscopy to observe the task-specific, involuntary spasms that characterize the disorder, making this physician the key professional in establishing the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Coagulopathy can trigger widespread microvascular thrombosis and impaired perfusion, leading to failure of multiple organs. The clinical condition defined as “multiple organ dysfunction syndrome” (MODS) is characterized by altered organ function in an acutely ill patient such that homeostasis cannot be maintained without intervention."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor prognosis",
    "long_answer": "Vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome is associated with fragile arteries and hollow organs that can rupture spontaneously; most patients experience life-threatening complications in young adulthood, giving a median life expectancy of roughly 40–50 years, so overall survival outlook is poor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autoimmune disorder",
    "long_answer": "Although the exact cause of achalasia remains unclear, accumulating evidence—such as lymphocytic infiltration of the myenteric plexus, association with HLA class II alleles, and coexistence with other autoimmune diseases—suggests that achalasia may represent an autoimmune disorder in which immune-mediated destruction of inhibitory myenteric neurons leads to impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter and esophageal aperistalsis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal lobe",
    "long_answer": "In porencephaly, the cystic cavity most often develops within the temporal region of the cerebral hemisphere. The temporal lobe houses Wernicke’s area, essential for language comprehension, and medial temporal structures such as the hippocampus, which are central to memory formation. Therefore, the temporal lobe links both the porencephalic cyst location and the functions of language comprehension and memory."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Omega-3 fatty acids",
    "long_answer": "Increasing dietary intake of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (e.g., from oily fish or fish-oil supplements) can speed recovery in polymyalgia rheumatica. EPA and DHA compete with arachidonic-acid metabolism, lowering production of pro-inflammatory eicosanoids and cytokines, which diminishes pain and stiffness and can reduce the steroid dose and duration needed for remission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chloroma",
    "long_answer": "A chloroma, also called a granulocytic or myeloid sarcoma, is a solid mass composed of immature leukemic (most often myeloid) cells. Although these tumors usually arise in bone or soft tissues, they can, on rare occasions, form within the central nervous system, representing an extramedullary manifestation of acute leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ocular larva migrans",
    "long_answer": "After humans ingest Toxocara canis or Toxocara cati eggs, hatchling larvae can enter the bloodstream and migrate to the eye. Their presence in ocular tissues provokes a granulomatous inflammatory reaction, producing the condition known as ocular larva migrans (ocular toxocariasis), which may present as retinal/chorioretinal granuloma, posterior uveitis, or endophthalmitis and can lead to unilateral vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epiphyseal cartilage",
    "long_answer": "Trevor disease, formally known as dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica, is characterized by unilateral, localized overgrowth of epiphyseal cartilage. This cartilaginous overgrowth forms osteochondroma-like projections from the epiphysis, leading to joint deformity and asymmetry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), hepatic glycogen phosphorylase is deficient, so liver glycogen cannot be mobilized to release glucose during periods without food intake. Consequently, during fasting these patients can develop hypoglycemia because endogenous glucose production is impaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Clinical guidelines for epididymitis recommend nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen or naproxen to relieve scrotal pain and reduce inflammation; NSAIDs inhibit cyclo-oxygenase enzymes, lowering prostaglandin synthesis and thereby diminishing pain and swelling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epidermis",
    "long_answer": "Stevens–Johnson syndrome is a severe mucocutaneous reaction characterized by widespread apoptosis of keratinocytes leading to full-thickness necrosis and detachment of the epidermis at the dermo-epidermal junction, making the epidermal layer the primary site of pathologic injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric dentistry",
    "long_answer": "Fissured (scrotal) tongue is a benign developmental variation of the oral mucosa. It is usually detected during routine inspection of the mouth and, when management is needed, consists mainly of oral-hygiene instructions and reassurance. The specialists specifically trained to examine, diagnose, and manage conditions of the oral cavity in children are pediatric dentists (the field of pediatric dentistry), so this specialty is most likely to handle fissured tongue in paediatric patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Individual fit-testing",
    "long_answer": "The most reliable way to guarantee that a hearing protection device (earplug or earmuff) really attenuates workplace noise to a safe level is to perform individual fit-testing (field attenuation measurement) on each worker. Fit-testing quantifies the actual sound attenuation achieved in that person’s ear, yields a Personal Attenuation Rating (PAR), and confirms that the device is inserted or worn correctly; if the measured attenuation is inadequate, the worker can be retrained or issued a different protector. This evidence-based procedure ensures the effectiveness of hearing protection and prevents noise-induced hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEN1",
    "long_answer": "Somatic mutations in the MEN1 gene, which encodes the tumor-suppressor protein menin, are the mutations most often identified in sporadic prolactin-secreting pituitary adenomas (prolactinomas). Loss of menin function promotes unchecked proliferation of lactotroph cells, making MEN1 the mutation most commonly linked to sporadic prolactinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome is an autosomal-dominant ‘hand-heart’ disorder caused by mutations in TBX5. It presents with upper-limb skeletal anomalies such as brachydactyly, triphalangeal or absent thumbs, and radial-ray defects, and carries a high incidence of congenital heart disease—most commonly atrial or ventricular septal defects and cardiac conduction abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lady Lamb",
    "long_answer": "The American singer-songwriter Lady Lamb (Aly Spaltro) revealed in early 2020 that she had been diagnosed with Chiari malformation type I and subsequently underwent posterior-fossa decompression brain surgery to correct the herniation of her cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum, a standard surgical treatment for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wayson stain",
    "long_answer": "Plague due to Yersinia pestis is rapidly diagnosed by applying the Wayson stain to a smear of bubo aspirate, blood, or sputum; the stain reveals the characteristic bipolar “safety-pin” appearance of the organism, confirming the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "No intervention",
    "long_answer": "When cor triatriatum is discovered incidentally in a patient who has no symptoms and no hemodynamic obstruction, no surgical or medical therapy is required; the condition is simply observed because it carries little risk when asymptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bradykinin accumulation",
    "long_answer": "ACE inhibitors block angiotensin-converting enzyme, which normally degrades bradykinin and substance P. Inhibition of this breakdown causes bradykinin to accumulate in the respiratory mucosa, stimulating vagal afferents and producing the characteristic persistent, dry cough seen in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Athetosis is an involuntary movement disorder arising from dysfunction in the basal ganglia and other motor pathways of the central nervous system. The diagnosis, differential assessment, and management of such movement disorders fall within the domain of Neurology, the medical specialty devoted to diseases of the brain, spinal cord, nerves, and muscles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fasting hypoglycaemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen Storage Disease type I (von Gierke disease) the first feature that typically leads to clinical recognition is recurrent, severe fasting hypoglycaemia. Despite very low glucose levels, affected patients show relatively mild neurological symptoms because the disease causes chronic hyper-lactataemia; the elevated circulating lactate (and accompanying ketone bodies) can be taken up and oxidised by the brain, supplying energy and thereby mitigating the usual neuroglycopenic effects of hypoglycaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Almost invariably fatal",
    "long_answer": "Whole-body radiation doses exceeding ~8 Gy destroy hematopoietic, gastrointestinal and neurovascular stem cells so extensively that even aggressive supportive therapy cannot reverse the damage; death typically occurs within days to a few weeks. Therefore, the prognosis for acute radiation syndrome at >8 Gy is essentially certain death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-17",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APS-1/APECED) is characterised by high-titer neutralising autoantibodies directed against the Th17 cytokine interleukin-17 (IL-17A and IL-17F). Loss of functional IL-17 signalling impairs mucosal immunity, contributes to the chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis typical of APS-1, and forms an essential part of the syndrome’s autoimmune pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "In classic Guillain–Barré syndrome (GBS) the cerebrospinal fluid shows the characteristic 'albuminocytologic dissociation': an elevated protein concentration with a normal or only minimally increased white-cell count (usually ≤5–10 cells/µL). A true pleocytosis (marked elevation of leukocytes) is not typical for GBS and, if present, should prompt consideration of alternative or accompanying diagnoses such as infectious polyradiculitis (e.g., HIV, CMV, Lyme disease) or meningeal inflammation. Therefore, Guillain–Barré syndrome does not typically present with pleocytosis in cerebrospinal fluid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central nervous system",
    "long_answer": "In Sandhoff disease, deficient β-hexosaminidase activity prevents GM2 ganglioside degradation, causing its progressive accumulation in neurons of the central nervous system, leading to widespread neurodegeneration of the brain and spinal cord."
}

{
    "short_answer": "H syndrome",
    "long_answer": "H syndrome is an autosomal-recessive condition due to biallelic SLC29A3 mutations. First documented in a consanguineous Iranian (Jewish) family, the disorder presents with the characteristic triad of cutaneous hyperpigmentation with hypertrichosis and systemic findings that spell the letter “H” (hepatosplenomegaly, hearing loss, heart anomalies, hypogonadism, hyperglycaemia, short stature). Several reported patients also developed progressive renal involvement, leading to chronic renal impairment or failure, making kidney dysfunction a recognised component of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "THRB gene",
    "long_answer": "The majority of cases of generalized resistance to thyroid hormone (Refetoff syndrome) are caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the THRB gene, which encodes the thyroid hormone receptor-β. These mutations impair receptor binding or co-activator interactions, decreasing tissue responsiveness to circulating T3 and T4 despite normal or elevated hormone levels, and hence are most commonly linked to thyroid hormone resistance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasma cells",
    "long_answer": "In neurosyphilis, Treponema pallidum infection of the central nervous system elicits a chronic meningeal and perivascular inflammatory reaction. Histopathology consistently shows dense perivascular cuffing by plasma cells—the antibody-producing end-stage B-lymphocytes. This plasma cell-rich infiltrate is a classic hallmark of the immune response in neurosyphilis."
}

{  
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis causes chronic cholestasis and reduced bile flow, which impairs the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K). Patients may therefore need vitamin supplementation because of UNDER-absorption and resulting deficiencies—not because of over-absorption. Hence, the premise of the question is incorrect."  
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystine precipitation in urine",
    "long_answer": "In cystinuria, defective renal tubular re-uptake of cystine causes very high cystine concentrations in the urine. Because cystine is poorly soluble, especially in acidic urine, it becomes supersaturated and precipitates, leading to the aggregation of crystals that form cystine kidney stones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic pneumonia—whether idiopathic, drug-induced, or associated with an underlying malignancy—resolves promptly with systemic glucocorticoid therapy. Corticosteroids suppress eosinophil-mediated inflammation in the lung, leading to rapid improvement in respiratory symptoms, radiographic infiltrates, and peripheral eosinophilia even when the condition is precipitated by cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST (spastin)",
    "long_answer": "The SPAST gene, which encodes the microtubule-severing protein spastin, is mapped to chromosome locus 2p22.3. Pathogenic variants in SPAST are the most common cause of autosomal-dominant hereditary spastic paraplegia (designated SPG4), leading to progressive lower-limb spasticity through impaired axonal transport and microtubule dynamics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Permanent pacemaker",
    "long_answer": "Progressive cardiac conduction defect leads to inexorably worsening atriovioentricular block and bundle-branch disease. No drugs halt this degeneration; the only intervention shown to prevent syncope and sudden death is implantation of a permanent pacemaker to provide reliable electrical pacing when native conduction fails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous antibiotics",
    "long_answer": "Infective endocarditis is routinely treated with prolonged courses of high-dose intravenous antibiotics, which deliver bactericidal drug levels directly into the bloodstream to eradicate the organisms adherent to the cardiac endocardium and valves."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central auditory nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Auditory processing disorder is not caused by damage to the ears themselves; instead, it involves abnormal neural processing of sound within the brain. Specifically, the disorder affects structures and pathways of the central auditory nervous system—brainstem, midbrain, thalamic, and cortical auditory centers—where acoustic signals are decoded and interpreted. Dysfunction in these central pathways leads to the characteristic difficulties in sound discrimination and comprehension seen in APD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pallister-Hall syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pallister-Hall syndrome, named after geneticists Philip Pallister and Judith Hall, is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the GLI3 gene. GLI3 encodes a zinc-finger transcription factor that mediates sonic hedgehog signaling; pathogenic variants disrupt normal embryonic patterning, giving rise to the characteristic anomalies of Pallister-Hall syndrome such as hypothalamic hamartoma, polydactyly and bifid epiglottis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP1B1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CYP1B1 gene are the commonest genetic cause of primary congenital glaucoma (GLC3A). The elevated intra-ocular pressure produced by CYP1B1 defects leads to photophobia, tearing and reflex blepharospasm, so the same CYP1B1 mutation that produces congenital glaucoma can clinically manifest with blepharospasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis",
    "long_answer": "The 45-letter word “pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis,” coined at a 1935 meeting of the National Puzzlers’ League, is accepted by that organization and by major English dictionaries as the longest established word in the language. It names a lung disease caused by inhaling very fine siliceous volcanic dust, and no longer dictionary-listed English word exceeds its length."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The FBN2 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-2 and whose pathogenic variants cause congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome), is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 (region 5q23–q31)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Torricelli barometer experiment",
    "long_answer": "In 1643 Evangelista Torricelli filled a sealed glass tube with mercury, inverted it into a mercury basin, and observed that the column fell to about 76 cm while a vacuum formed above it. The only force able to hold that heavy mercury column was the weight of the surrounding air pressing on the reservoir, proving that air has mass and exerts measurable pressure (atmospheric pressure)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pheochromocytoma",
    "long_answer": "A pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting neuroendocrine tumor that arises from chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla. Excess release of epinephrine and norepinephrine produces classic adrenergic symptoms such as episodic or sustained hypertension, tachycardia, palpitations, headache, and sweating."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Setleis syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Blepharo-naso-facial syndrome is the original descriptive name for Setleis syndrome, a rare focal facial dermal dysplasia caused by TWIST2 mutations. Affected infants present with characteristic facial abnormalities including bilateral scar-like depressions in the temples, abnormal eyelids/eyelashes, a flattened nasal bridge and other mid-face anomalies—hence the blepharo (eyelid)-naso (nose)-facial designation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorda tympani nerve",
    "long_answer": "The chorda tympani nerve, a branch of the facial nerve that conveys taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, has been implicated in burning mouth syndrome. Injury or hypofunction of this nerve can diminish or distort gustatory input, and clinical studies show that such damage is associated with dysgeusia and the oral burning sensations characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triangular face",
    "long_answer": "Bangstad syndrome is a rare growth-retardation disorder in which patients typically show a characteristic triangular facial shape—broad forehead that tapers to a small, pointed chin. This distinctive triangular face is repeatedly documented as one of the most common cranio-facial findings in the syndrome’s clinical descriptions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone",
    "long_answer": "Crandall syndrome combines sensorineural deafness and pili torti with hypogonadism. Laboratory studies on affected males consistently show markedly reduced circulating testosterone, reflecting gonadotropin-related hypogonadism. Thus low serum testosterone is a characteristic finding in individuals with Crandall syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenotype",
    "long_answer": "The phenotype encompasses all observable characteristics of an organism—morphology, development, biochemical or physiological properties, and clinical conditions. Congenital anomalies such as hemifacial microsomia are part of this outward expression of the underlying genotype interacting with environmental influences, and thus are classified as phenotypic traits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Neonatal herpes simplex virus infection can invade the central nervous system, leading to herpes encephalitis—an inflammation of brain parenchyma that may cause seizures, neurologic deficits, and long-term neurodevelopmental impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Paramyotonia congenita is an autosomal-dominant skeletal-muscle channelopathy produced by missense mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the α-subunit (NaV1.4) of the voltage-gated sodium channel in skeletal muscle. These mutations impair normal channel inactivation, leading to the cold- or exercise-induced myotonia and episodic weakness characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "The VAC combination protocol for rhabdomyosarcoma consists of Vincristine, Actinomycin D (dactinomycin), and Cyclophosphamide; the alkylating agent cyclophosphamide provides DNA-cross-linking cytotoxicity that complements the microtubule inhibition of vincristine and the transcription inhibition of actinomycin D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX6 gene are a recognized cause of Peters' Anomaly. PAX6 encodes a paired-box/homeodomain transcription factor that is indispensable for early eye morphogenesis; it controls evagination of the optic vesicle and its subsequent invagination to form the optic cup. Loss-of-function or missense variants in PAX6 disrupt this optic cup development, leading to anterior segment dysgenesis that manifests clinically as Peters' Anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL-23/IL-17 axis",
    "long_answer": "Axial spondyloarthritis is strongly driven by the IL-23/IL-17 cytokine pathway: genetic variants in IL23R and related genes increase risk, IL-23 promotes differentiation of Th17 cells that secrete IL-17A/F, and elevated IL-17 is found in inflamed entheses and serum of patients. Therapeutic blockade of IL-17A (e.g., secukinumab, ixekizumab) or IL-23 markedly improves disease, confirming the pivotal pathogenic role of this pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary capillary hemangiomatosis is a rare vascular lung disease that causes severe pulmonary hypertension and progressive respiratory failure. Its diagnosis, medical therapy, and evaluation for lung transplantation are handled by physicians trained in pulmonary and critical care medicine—i.e., pulmonologists—who specialize in disorders of the pulmonary vasculature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenomegaly",
    "long_answer": "Enlargement of the spleen is medically termed splenomegaly. It occurs when pathologic processes—such as hematologic malignancies like leukemia or storage disorders such as Gaucher’s disease—lead to congestion, infiltration, or hyperplasia of splenic tissue, increasing the organ’s size and weight."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucinous carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomyxoma peritonei is classically produced by mucin-secreting (mucinous) carcinomas of the appendix or ovary, whose abundant extracellular mucin spills into the peritoneal cavity. The same histologic tumor type—mucinous (colloid) carcinoma—also occurs as a distinct subtype of breast cancer. Thus, mucinous carcinoma is the cancer that can give rise to pseudomyxoma peritonei and can likewise affect the breast."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "In diffuse neonatal hemangiomatosis, numerous cutaneous hemangiomas are often accompanied by visceral lesions. Among internal organs, the liver is by far the most frequently involved site, typically presenting with multiple hepatic hemangiomas that can lead to complications such as high-output cardiac failure. This hepatic predilection is well documented in neonatal vascular tumor literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum",
    "long_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum is an autosomal-recessive disorder in which mutations in any of several XPA–XPG genes disable the nucleotide excision repair pathway. The defective DNA repair proteins cannot remove UV-induced pyrimidine dimers, leading to extreme photosensitivity and a markedly increased risk of skin cancers. Thus, the disease is classically defined by a defect in DNA repair proteins and autosomal-recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nabilone",
    "long_answer": "Nabilone is a synthetic analogue of Δ9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) used as an antiemetic and appetite stimulant. By stimulating central CB1 cannabinoid receptors it can impair cerebellar and vestibular motor control, so clinical trials and prescribing information list dizziness, drowsiness and frank ataxia as characteristic CNS adverse effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Venturi effect",
    "long_answer": "According to the Bernoulli (Venturi) principle, rapid laminar flow in the fetal descending aorta creates a localized fall in static pressure at the aortic end of the ductus arteriosus. Because pressure in the pulmonary artery is higher (owing to the high pulmonary vascular resistance in utero), this pressure drop ‘siphons’ or draws blood through the ductus from the pulmonary artery into the aorta, bypassing the non-functioning fetal lungs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene (encoding fibrillin-1) underlie several phenotypes. Missense substitutions clustered in the TB5 domain of FBN1 cause acromicric dysplasia, while C-terminal truncating variants that delete the asprosin-encoding region of FBN1 produce Marfanoid–progeroid–lipodystrophy syndrome. Thus the same gene, FBN1, is genetically linked to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iron-deficiency anemia",
    "long_answer": "Most dietary iron is absorbed in the duodenum and proximal jejunum of the small intestine. Diseases or surgical loss of these segments cause inadequate iron uptake, depleting body iron stores. Insufficient iron impairs hemoglobin synthesis, producing the characteristic microcytic, hypochromic picture of iron-deficiency anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Contiguous-gene deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The combination of aniridia, cerebellar ataxia, renal agenesis and psychomotor retardation arises when several neighbouring genes on chromosome 11p (including PAX6 for iris development and other nearby loci for cerebellar and renal development) are simultaneously lost. Such disorders, produced by a microdeletion that removes a stretch of adjacent genes, are classified as contiguous-gene deletion (microdeletion) syndromes rather than single-gene or polygenic traits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SRRT (ARS2) gene cause a neuro-developmental disorder manifested by microcephaly, seizures, hearing problems and other neurological deficits. These clinical signs reflect primary dysfunction of brain development and maintenance, showing that the disease chiefly affects the brain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Chronic helminth infections (e.g., schistosomiasis, soil-transmitted nematodes, filarial worms) can influence the course of HIV infection. Helminths drive persistent immune activation and a Th2-skewed cytokine profile, which can up-regulate HIV co-receptors, increase viral replication, and hasten CD4-cell decline. Epidemiological studies in co-endemic regions have repeatedly shown that HIV-positive individuals who are also infected with helminths tend to have higher plasma HIV RNA levels and progress to symptomatic disease more quickly than helminth-free controls. Small randomized and quasi-experimental trials further support this relationship: anthelmintic treatment (e.g., praziquantel, albendazole, ivermectin) often leads to a modest but measurable fall in HIV viral load and, in some studies, slower CD4 loss. Systematic reviews (e.g., Cochrane 2012, updated 2015) and WHO technical reports conclude that, although the magnitude of benefit varies by parasite species and study quality, the overall evidence indicates that helminthiasis can adversely affect HIV progression and that deworming is a reasonable adjunctive intervention in co-infected patients. Therefore the established biomedical consensus is that helminthiasis does affect the progression of HIV."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2 gene",
    "long_answer": "Benign meningiomas arising at the skull base often carry loss-of-function mutations of the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 22q, which encodes the merlin protein. Growth of an NF2-mutated skull-base meningioma around the cavernous sinus or sphenoid/clival region can compress cranial nerves III, IV, or VI, producing ocular motor palsy and the clinical symptom of diplopia (double vision). Thus, an NF2 mutation is the characteristic genetic alteration linked to these tumors and their ability to cause diplopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAB23",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RAB23 gene are the established molecular cause of Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II). Independent reports have shown that the same RAB23 mutations can present with isolated digit shortening, i.e., brachydactyly, indicating that defective RAB23— a small GTP-binding protein that modulates Hedgehog signalling— underlies both brachydactyly and the full Carpenter syndrome phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous immunoglobulin",
    "long_answer": "Current consensus guidelines and case-series data show that monthly high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG, typically 1–2 g/kg) markedly reduces the frequency and severity of systemic capillary leak syndrome (Clarkson disease) episodes and improves survival, making IVIG the recommended first-line prophylactic therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11p15",
    "long_answer": "The commonest genetic alteration in embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is loss of heterozygosity at the imprinted region on the short arm of chromosome 11 (11p15). This LOH, often accompanied by paternal uniparental disomy, disrupts normal imprinting of genes such as IGF2 and H19 and is a hallmark molecular event in this tumour subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome (types I and II) results from pathogenic mutations in the UGT1A1 gene that abolish or markedly reduce the activity of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyl-transferase. Without this enzyme, bilirubin cannot be efficiently conjugated in the liver, leading to severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and sustained jaundice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygous (biallelic)",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in both copies of the SLC22A5 (OCTN2) gene. Affected individuals therefore carry pathogenic variants on each allele—either the same mutation on both chromosomes (homozygous) or two different mutations (compound heterozygous). Heterozygous carriers have one normal allele and are typically asymptomatic, so clinical disease requires a biallelic (homozygous/compound heterozygous) state."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cushing’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Achard–Thiers syndrome couples type 2 diabetes mellitus with adrenal hypercortisolism (Cushing’s syndrome). The excess adrenal androgens present in Cushing’s syndrome cause virilization in women, and this androgen-driven virilization can enlarge the clitoris, producing clitoromegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femoral head",
    "long_answer": "Developmental hip dysplasia chiefly affects the proximal femur: the femoral head is poorly seated in, or displaced from, the acetabulum, leading to abnormal shaping of both structures. Thus, the part of the femur involved is its head—the articular portion that normally fits into the hip socket."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
    "long_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) is regularly employed when cholangiocarcinoma is suspected because the endoscope can be advanced to the biliary tree, permitting brush cytology or forceps biopsies for histologic confirmation while simultaneously allowing stent placement to decompress the obstructed bile ducts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacrum (lower spine)",
    "long_answer": "Sacral agenesis is characterized by partial or complete absence of the sacrum—the group of vertebrae forming the base of the spine and back of the pelvis—so it primarily affects the lower (caudal) portion of the spinal column."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDKN2A",
    "long_answer": "Familial atypical multiple mole melanoma (FAMMM) syndrome is most commonly caused by germ-line mutations in the CDKN2A tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 9p21. Loss-of-function in CDKN2A (encoding the p16INK4A and p14ARF proteins) disrupts cell-cycle control, greatly increasing the risk of cutaneous melanoma in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Pleurisy—an inflammation of the pleural membranes causing sharp chest pain—is a well-recognized manifestation of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Immune-complex deposition and complement activation in SLE trigger serosal inflammation, making pleuritis one of the most common pulmonary complications of this autoimmune disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis",
    "long_answer": "At 45 letters, pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis is listed in major authoritative dictionaries such as the Oxford English Dictionary and Merriam-Webster. Coined in 1935, it denotes a form of lung disease caused by inhalation of very fine silica dust. No longer word has a verified entry in any mainstream English dictionary, making it the longest word formally recognized in the language."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kidney transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Once autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease progresses to end-stage kidney disease, definitive management requires renal replacement therapy. The preferred and most effective modality is kidney (renal) transplantation, which restores renal function and survival more effectively than long-term dialysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonadoblastoma",
    "long_answer": "Gonadoblastoma is a rare germ-cell/sex-cord stromal tumor that almost exclusively arises in abnormal gonads containing a Y chromosome. It is classically seen in individuals with gonadal dysgenesis (streak or dysgenetic gonads) and in patients with complete androgen insensitivity (testicular feminization) whose intra-abdominal or inguinal testes are dysgenetic. Thus, the presence of these abnormal gonads is strongly associated with the development of gonadoblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylphenidate",
    "long_answer": "Methylphenidate is a benzyl-substituted piperidine derivative that acts as a norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor. It is an FDA-approved first-line pharmacotherapy for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) in adults, improving attention and impulse control by increasing synaptic catecholamine levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "D016409",
    "long_answer": "The MeSH descriptor D016409 corresponds to “Lymphoma, Follicular, Small Cleaved-Cell,” the classic follicular (centrocytic) lymphoma subtype characterized by small cleaved B-cells and the hallmark t(14;18)(q32;q21) translocation that juxtaposes IGH and BCL2, leading to BCL2 over-expression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is an autoimmune blistering skin disorder that is closely linked to celiac disease. Its diagnosis typically involves skin examination, direct immunofluorescence of a skin biopsy, and serologic tests for celiac-associated antibodies. Management usually includes a gluten-free diet and dermatologic agents such as dapsone. These evaluations and treatments fall within the expertise of dermatologists (for the skin manifestations) and gastroenterologists (for the underlying gluten-sensitive enteropathy). Cardiologists specialize in diseases of the heart and vascular system and are not the physicians who ordinarily diagnose or manage dermatologic or gastrointestinal autoimmune conditions like dermatitis herpetiformis. Therefore, a cardiologist is not the specialist most likely to diagnose and treat this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Negative selection",
    "long_answer": "Within the thymic medulla, immature T cells that bind self-antigens with high affinity undergo apoptosis in a process called negative selection (central tolerance). By deleting these autoreactive T cells before they enter the circulation, negative selection reduces the likelihood that donor T cells will mount harmful immune responses against host tissues, thereby helping to prevent graft-versus-host disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TMEM67",
    "long_answer": "COACH syndrome is one of the Joubert-related ciliopathies. Sequencing studies have shown that biallelic loss-of-function mutations in TMEM67 (also known as MKS3 or meckelin) account for the majority of COACH syndrome cases—well over half of all reported families. Mutations in a few other ciliary genes (e.g., CC2D2A, RPGRIP1L) can also cause the disorder, but TMEM67 is by far the most frequently mutated gene in this autosomal-recessive condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Werner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Werner syndrome (adult-onset progeria) is a progeroid disorder characterized by premature aging features such as early cataracts, short stature, scleroderma-like skin atrophy, and, notably, dystrophic calcinosis of the skin and subcutaneous tissues. The presence of calcinosis is a well-recognized hallmark helping to distinguish Werner syndrome from other progeroid conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Lipodystrophy is characterized by abnormal loss or redistribution of adipose tissue and is frequently accompanied by severe insulin resistance, dyslipidemia, and other endocrine-metabolic derangements. Because endocrinologists specialize in diagnosing and managing disorders of metabolism, lipid handling, and insulin action, patients with suspected lipodystrophy are most often evaluated and diagnosed within the field of Endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metoclopramide",
    "long_answer": "Metoclopramide is a dopamine-2 receptor antagonist with prokinetic properties. It is prescribed to enhance upper-gut motility in chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction and is also a well-established antiemetic for the treatment of nausea and vomiting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Graves disease",
    "long_answer": "Pretibial myxedema (thyroid dermopathy) is a characteristic extrathyroidal manifestation of Graves disease, an autoimmune hyperthyroidism in which thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins activate the TSH receptor. These autoantibodies also stimulate fibroblasts in the skin, especially over the shins, leading to mucopolysaccharide deposition and the localized edema known as pretibial myxedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythropoietin",
    "long_answer": "During the pubertal growth spurt, rising erythropoietin (EPO) levels stimulate increased erythroid heme synthesis. In erythropoietic protoporphyria, the ferrochelatase defect blocks the final step of heme formation, so the higher EPO-driven heme demand leads to greater accumulation of free protoporphyrin IX and the first appearance or worsening of photosensitivity symptoms at puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced exercise tolerance",
    "long_answer": "Dilated cardiomyopathy weakens the left ventricle, lowering stroke volume and cardiac output. The resulting chronic heart-failure symptoms—fatigue, dyspnea on exertion, and easy tiring—markedly reduce patients’ exercise tolerance and capacity to perform everyday activities, leading to substantially poorer health-related quality-of-life scores."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial myxoma",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is a familial syndrome characterized by skin pigmentation, endocrine tumors, and a high incidence of cardiac (usually left-atrial) myxomas. These myxomas can prolapse through the mitral valve or obstruct the mitral orifice during diastole, producing dyspnea, orthopnea, and a diastolic murmur that closely resemble the clinical picture of rheumatic mitral stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iris",
    "long_answer": "Within the uveal (vascular) layer of the eye, the iris contains the sphincter pupillae and dilator pupillae smooth muscles that receive parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation, respectively. These autonomic inputs constrict or dilate the pupil, demonstrating direct control of the iris by the autonomic nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPA gene",
    "long_answer": "DeSanctis–Cacchione syndrome is an extreme neurocutaneous form of xeroderma pigmentosum. It is most often produced by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the XPA gene (excision-repair cross-complementation group 1). XPA encodes a key protein of the nucleotide-excision DNA-repair pathway; when it is defective, UV-induced DNA damage accumulates, causing the severe photosensitivity, multiple skin cancers, microcephaly, dwarfism and progressive neurological degeneration that characterise DeSanctis–Cacchione syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse",
    "long_answer": "Several clinical series have shown that patients with Chiari (Arnold-Chiari) malformations, especially type I, have an increased incidence of mitral valve prolapse. Both conditions are linked to underlying connective-tissue laxity: the collagen abnormalities that allow downward herniation of the cerebellar tonsils can also cause redundant, floppy mitral leaflets, leading to prolapse. Thus, mitral valve prolapse is a recognized cardiac condition associated with Chiari malformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the KCNQ2 gene classically cause benign familial neonatal seizures and related epileptic encephalopathies. These disorders are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner: a single mutated copy of KCNQ2 is sufficient to produce disease, and affected individuals have a 50% chance of passing the mutation to each offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome type 3",
    "long_answer": "Fused fingers (syndactyly) are a limb anomaly characteristic of Waardenburg syndrome type 3, also called Klein-Waardenburg syndrome, which combines the usual pigmentation and hearing features of Waardenburg syndrome with upper-limb malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "S-100 protein",
    "long_answer": "Paragangliomas characteristically show S-100–positive sustentacular cells surrounding the nests (zellballen) of chief cells. This S-100 staining pattern is absent in most other neuroendocrine tumors, making S-100 immunostaining a key marker for distinguishing paraganglioma from other neuroendocrine neoplasms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Robinow syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The condition originally described by Meinhard Robinow in 1969 is characterized by mesomelic shortening of the limbs together with genital hypoplasia, distinctive ‘fetal-face’ craniofacial features, and vertebral anomalies. Because of its hallmark combination of mesomelic dwarfism and small external genitalia, it has long been referred to in the medical genetics literature as the “mesomelic dwarfism-small genitalia syndrome,” now universally known as Robinow syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scapula",
    "long_answer": "A characteristic skeletal feature of Cohen syndrome is marked hypoplasia of the scapulae: the shoulder blades are conspicuously small and under-developed on radiographs, serving as a helpful diagnostic clue in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L1CAM",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the L1CAM gene cause the X-linked form of hereditary spastic paraplegia (SPG1/MASA syndrome). L1CAM encodes the L1 cell-adhesion molecule, a membrane glycoprotein that mediates fasciculation and guidance of developing axons; it is a key component of the axon pathfinding machinery during nervous-system development. Thus, the same gene implicated in hereditary spastic paraplegia is directly involved in axonal pathfinding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dynein arms",
    "long_answer": "Ciliary motion is generated by axonemal dynein arms—ATP-dependent motor protein complexes (outer and inner dynein arms) attached to the microtubule doublets of the axoneme. Mutations or absence of dynein-arm components (e.g., DNAH5, DNAI1) impair ciliary beating and are a well-established cause of primary ciliary dyskinesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple osteochondromas (also called hereditary multiple exostoses) is characterized by the development of cartilaginous outgrowths on multiple long bones. In the upper extremity the forearm is a classic site of involvement, and the disease typically affects both bones of the forearm—the ulna and the radius. The distal ulna is often shortened while the radius bows, producing deformity and functional problems. Because involvement of both forearm bones is a well-described and common feature of the disorder, it is correct to state that more than one bone in the forearm is affected in hereditary multiple osteochondromas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis pilaris is a benign disorder of keratinization affecting the hair-follicle openings of the skin. Conditions of the skin, hair and nails fall under the expertise of dermatologists; therefore the specialty of Dermatology is most commonly responsible for diagnosing and managing keratosis pilaris through topical keratolytics, emollients, and patient education."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatomegaly",
    "long_answer": "A characteristic clinical feature of Katz syndrome is hepatomegaly—an abnormal enlargement of the liver—reflecting the hepatic involvement commonly reported in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray",
    "long_answer": "Standard evaluation of respiratory complaints in people with HIV/AIDS begins with a chest radiograph. A chest X-ray can reveal the infiltrates, interstitial patterns, or diffuse ground-glass opacities typical of bacterial pneumonia or Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, making it the most commonly used first-line diagnostic tool in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-contrast CT scan",
    "long_answer": "The first-line test for detecting a subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured intracranial aneurysm is a non-contrast computed tomography (CT) scan of the head; it rapidly visualizes acute blood in the subarachnoid spaces with high sensitivity within the first 24 hours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bromocriptine",
    "long_answer": "Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC) deficiency blocks conversion of L-DOPA and 5-hydroxytryptophan into dopamine and serotonin, leaving patients with very low monoamine neurotransmitter levels. Bromocriptine, a dopamine-receptor agonist, is routinely prescribed because it can directly stimulate postsynaptic dopamine receptors, thereby pharmacologically substituting for the missing endogenous dopamine and ameliorating many of the motor and autonomic symptoms of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) is a white-matter injury that mainly affects premature infants and is a well-recognized cause of cerebral palsy and other neurodevelopmental sequelae. Multiple cohort and case-series studies have shown that children with PVL have a markedly higher incidence of epilepsy than the general pediatric population. Reported epilepsy rates in PVL cohorts typically range from about 15 % to 40 %, depending on the severity and extent of the white-matter damage and associated cortical involvement. This contrasts with an epilepsy prevalence of roughly 0.5 %–1 % in the overall child population. The increased seizure risk is biologically plausible because PVL frequently coexists with cortical and thalamic dysmaturation and secondary grey-matter injury, which disrupt neuronal circuitry and lower the seizure threshold. Therefore, within the context of established clinical evidence, epilepsy is indeed considered a prevalent and clinically significant comorbidity in children who have periventricular leukomalacia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Erythroplakia is an oral mucosal lesion characterized by a red, velvety patch with a high rate of epithelial dysplasia. Histologic studies show that a large proportion of erythroplakic lesions already harbor severe dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, or invasive cancer. The malignancy that most commonly arises from these lesions is oral squamous cell carcinoma, making erythroplakia a well-recognized premalignant condition for this cancer type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Texas",
    "long_answer": "In 2017 the Texas Department of State Health Services and CDC investigated a cyclosporiasis cluster in which the great majority of patients reported eating at a Mediterranean-style restaurant chain in the Dallas–Fort Worth area. Epidemiologic evidence linked the illnesses to fresh produce served by that chain, making Texas the state that experienced the 2017 restaurant-associated Cyclospora outbreak."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHFM1",
    "long_answer": "Split hand/split foot malformation type 1 (SHFM1) is the form linked to microdeletions or mutations at chromosome 7q21 involving the DLX5/DLX6 region; affected individuals commonly present with limb ectrodactyly together with congenital sensorineural hearing loss, making SHFM1 the SHFM subtype characteristically associated with deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In nephropathic cystinosis, cystine accumulates in proximal renal tubular cells, producing a generalized proximal tubulopathy called Fanconi syndrome. This defect causes massive urinary losses of bicarbonate, phosphate, glucose, amino acids and other solutes; the bicarbonate wasting manifests as a type II (proximal) renal tubular acidosis, leading to systemic metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesoderm",
    "long_answer": "Hanhart syndrome (hypoglossia-hypodactylia) involves congenital defects of the tongue musculature and distal limbs, both of which originate mainly from mesodermal tissue (paraxial and lateral-plate mesoderm). Consequently, the disorder is attributed to an early developmental disturbance of the embryonic mesoderm germ layer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GBS-CIDP Foundation International",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, patient advocacy and support for Multifocal Motor Neuropathy (MMN) are provided by the GBS-CIDP Foundation International, a charitable nonprofit organization that assists individuals affected by Guillain-Barré syndrome, chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, and related variants such as MMN through education, patient networking, and research funding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Hay-Wells syndrome (also called AEC: ankyloblepharon-ectodermal defects-cleft lip/palate) is caused by heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor critical for epithelial and craniofacial development. Disruption of TP63 impairs palatal shelf fusion, so the same mutations that produce Hay-Wells syndrome frequently manifest with cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seminal vesicles",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is caused by PKD1/PKD2 mutations that trigger cyst formation in epithelial-lined organs. While both sexes can develop renal and hepatic cysts, men with ADPKD frequently form cysts in the seminal vesicles, a male-specific accessory reproductive organ. These seminal-vesicle cysts are a well-documented extrarenal manifestation seen on ultrasound or MRI in affected males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinous-process step-off",
    "long_answer": "During lumbar spine palpation, the examiner runs a finger down the midline; anterior displacement of one vertebra causes its spinous process to be recessed relative to the one above, creating a palpable ‘step-off.’ This characteristic discontinuity is the classic physical-examination sign used to detect spondylolisthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary edema",
    "long_answer": "In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease the pulmonary venules are obstructed, so downstream outflow is fixed. Endothelin receptor antagonists vasodilate the precapillary pulmonary arteries, suddenly increasing blood flow and hydrostatic pressure in capillaries that cannot empty. This triggers acute, massive pulmonary edema, which can rapidly progress to respiratory failure and death, making the drugs potentially fatal in PVOD patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prodromal phase",
    "long_answer": "In many neurological disorders, including epilepsy, a set of vague or nonspecific symptoms can appear hours to days before the first major clinical event; this preliminary symptom period is termed the prodromal phase (or prodrome). Recognizing prodromal symptoms can help predict and manage impending seizures or other neurological episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giardiasis",
    "long_answer": "Giardiasis, caused by the flagellated protozoan Giardia lamblia (G. intestinalis), often produces crampy abdominal pain along with bloating and diarrhea. The standard way to confirm infection is examination of stool for Giardia cysts or trophozoites (or by stool antigen tests), making stool microscopy the key diagnostic method."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium-channel blockers",
    "long_answer": "Antihypertensive therapy with calcium-channel blockers (e.g., nitrendipine, amlodipine) has been shown to cut the incidence of dementia that develops before an overt stroke. By lowering blood pressure and improving cerebral perfusion, these agents lessen small-vessel ischemic damage and microinfarcts, thereby preventing the vascular cognitive decline often termed pre-stroke dementia. Large trials such as the Syst-Eur study demonstrated about a 50 % reduction in new-onset dementia among older hypertensive patients treated with a calcium-channel blocker regimen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Constitutively active LH receptor",
    "long_answer": "Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis) is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the luteinizing hormone/chorionic gonadotropin receptor (LHCGR). The mutated receptor is constitutively active in Leydig cells, driving testosterone production without any need for pituitary LH stimulation. GnRH agonists lower or abolish LH secretion, but because the LH receptor is already autonomously active, testicular steroidogenesis continues unabated, making GnRH-agonist therapy ineffective."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osseous (bony) tissue",
    "long_answer": "In congenital syngnathia, the maxilla and mandible are united by an actual bridge of bone; the adhesion is formed by osseous tissue rather than by fibrous or mucosal material."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dercum disease",
    "long_answer": "Adiposis dolorosa is now most commonly referred to as Dercum disease, a rare disorder characterized by multiple painful subcutaneous lipomas accompanied by obesity and other systemic symptoms such as fatigue and cognitive disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "In the human genome, the CCR5 chemokine receptor gene is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 3 at cytogenetic band 3p21.31. This chromosomal location has been confirmed by linkage analysis and physical mapping studies, and is the site where well-known variants such as the CCR5-Δ32 deletion occur."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunofixation electrophoresis",
    "long_answer": "After the serum cryoprecipitate is isolated, immunofixation electrophoresis is performed to separate the immunoglobulins and probe them with heavy- and light-chain-specific antisera. This makes any monoclonal immunoglobulin band visible, allowing precise identification of its heavy-chain class (e.g., IgM, IgG) and light-chain type (κ or λ), which is essential for typing the monoclonal antibodies that cause cryoglobulinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral edema",
    "long_answer": "In Waldmann disease (primary intestinal lymphangiectasia), proteins such as albumin are lost into the intestinal lumen. The resulting hypoalbuminemia lowers plasma oncotic pressure, leading to fluid shift from the intravascular compartment into the interstitial space. This manifests clinically as peripheral (often generalized) edema, which is one of the hallmark signs of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarpa salpa",
    "long_answer": "The salema porgy (Sarpa salpa), nick-named the “dreamfish,” inhabits shallow coastal waters of the Mediterranean and eastern Atlantic. When its flesh—particularly the viscera—is eaten, it can trigger ichthyoallyeinotoxism, a poisoning syndrome that produces vivid, LSD-like hallucinations due to indole alkaloids accumulated from the algae it consumes. Thus, Sarpa salpa is the coastal fish species known to cause LSD-like hallucinations in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reed–Sternberg cell",
    "long_answer": "On histologic examination of an excised lymph node, identification of multinucleated B-cell–derived Reed–Sternberg cells is pathognomonic and therefore required for a definitive diagnosis of Hodgkin lymphoma, the classical form of lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "PELVIS syndrome (an acronym for Perineal hemangioma, External genital malformations, Lipomyelomeningocele, Vesicorenal abnormalities, Imperforate anus and Skin tag) is a clinically recognised congenital syndrome. The hallmark feature is a large segmental cutaneous infantile hemangioma in the perineal or lumbosacral skin, and this cutaneous vascular anomaly is present from birth. Because the syndrome is defined around this congenital skin lesion and its associated developmental anomalies, it is classified by dermatologists and pediatricians as one of the cutaneous congenital anomaly syndromes (along with related entities such as LUMBAR and SACRAL syndromes). Hence, PELVIS syndrome is indeed considered a type of congenital cutaneous anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increased outdoor time",
    "long_answer": "Multiple epidemiological and interventional studies show that children who spend more hours outdoors have a significantly lower incidence and slower progression of myopia. High ambient light outdoors is thought to stimulate retinal dopamine release, which inhibits excessive axial elongation of the eye, thereby reducing myopia risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked dominant",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis follicularis spinulosa decalvans is a rare genodermatosis caused by pathogenic variants in the X-chromosomal gene MBTPS2. The disorder follows an X-linked dominant mode of inheritance: hemizygous males are fully and often severely affected, while heterozygous females can show milder, patchy disease because of random X-chromosome inactivation (lyonisation). This clinical pattern is characteristic of X-linked dominant conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The MATN3 gene, mutations of which cause multiple epiphyseal dysplasia type 5, is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 2 (region 2p24-p23). This chromosomal assignment is confirmed by genetic linkage and physical mapping studies in the OMIM and Ensembl databases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Creatine phosphokinase",
    "long_answer": "After venomous snake bites, markedly elevated serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) reflects extensive myonecrosis and tissue edema. Clinical studies show that high CPK levels correlate with rising intracompartmental pressures, marking patients at increased risk for developing compartment syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningioma",
    "long_answer": "Meningiomas are benign primary brain tumors arising from arachnoid cap cells. Because they often grow slowly and remain clinically silent, they are frequently detected only when magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) or computed tomography (CT) is performed for unrelated reasons; such unsuspected, asymptomatic lesions are termed brain 'incidentalomas'."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giardia lamblia",
    "long_answer": "Giardia lamblia (also called Giardia intestinalis or G. duodenalis) is a flagellated protozoan parasite that infects the human small intestine. Ingestion of its environmentally resistant cysts—typically through contaminated water—leads to giardiasis, a disease characterized by watery, foul-smelling diarrhea, abdominal cramps, and malabsorption. Because it is a single-celled protozoan eukaryote, not a bacterium or virus, Giardia lamblia is the prototypical protozoan pathogen responsible for diarrheal illness in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3R1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "SHORT syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous mutations in the PIK3R1 gene, which encodes the p85α regulatory subunit of phosphoinositide-3-kinase (PI3K). These mutations impair PI3K–AKT–mTOR signaling, a central pathway that governs cell growth, proliferation and metabolism, explaining the growth retardation and other features seen in SHORT syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caesarean section",
    "long_answer": "Although juvenile-onset laryngeal papillomatosis is usually acquired from maternal genital human papillomavirus (HPV) at delivery, studies show that the absolute risk is very low and elective Caesarean section does not reliably prevent infection. Therefore obstetric guidelines do not recommend performing a caesarean section solely to prevent transmission of laryngeal papillomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lingual thyroid",
    "long_answer": "A lingual thyroid is ectopic thyroid tissue that remains at the base of the tongue because the thyroid primordium fails to descend during development—a form of thyroid dysgenesis. The mass can obstruct oropharyngeal structures, leading to dysphonia or difficulty talking, as well as dysphagia and airway compromise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothalamus",
    "long_answer": "Langerhans cell histiocytosis frequently infiltrates the hypothalamic–pituitary region, producing manifestations such as central diabetes insipidus. The hypothalamus is also the primary neural regulator of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, where it secretes corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) that drives downstream pituitary ACTH release. Therefore, the hypothalamus is both commonly affected in LCH and the key control center of the HPA axis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium benzoate therapy",
    "long_answer": "In non-ketotic hyperglycinemia (glycine encephalopathy), high-dose sodium benzoate is given to conjugate excess glycine to hippurate, which is then renally excreted. This lowers plasma and cerebrospinal-fluid glycine levels, and in some patients leads to better neurologic function and survival."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC38A8",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SLC38A8 gene (solute carrier family-38 member-8) disrupt maturation of the fovea centralis, resulting in foveal hypoplasia that clinically presents with congenital nystagmus. Because of its critical role in forming the foveal pit, SLC38A8 is the gene most directly linked to nystagmus through abnormal development of the fovea centralis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ranula",
    "long_answer": "A ranula is a mucous extravasation cyst arising from the sublingual (or less commonly minor) salivary glands. When it is confined to the sublingual space it presents as a ‘simple’ ranula in the floor of the mouth, but if the cyst dissects through or around the mylohyoid muscle it becomes a ‘plunging’ ranula that manifests as a cystic mass in the neck. Thus the same lesion can be encountered both in the floor of the mouth and in the neck."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyaline membranes",
    "long_answer": "In Infant Respiratory Distress Syndrome, insufficient pulmonary surfactant leads to alveolar collapse and epithelial injury. Plasma proteins and necrotic epithelial cells exude into the airspaces, condensing into eosinophilic, glassy ‘hyaline membranes’ that line the bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli—this is the characteristic histopathological finding in lungs of affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zika virus",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and laboratory studies show that maternal infection with Zika virus can cross the placenta, infect fetal neural progenitor cells, and disrupt neurodevelopment. During the 2015–2016 outbreaks in Brazil and other regions, a marked rise in babies born with congenital microcephaly was traced to vertical (mother-to-child) transmission of Zika virus, establishing it as the causative agent of this birth defect now termed “congenital Zika syndrome.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "TREX1",
    "long_answer": "Retinal vasculopathy with cerebral leukoencephalopathy and systemic manifestations (RVCL-S) is caused by heterozygous, usually C-terminal frameshift or truncating, mutations in the TREX1 gene. Besides the characteristic retinal and white-matter vascular lesions, TREX1 mutations can produce systemic vasculopathy affecting the liver, where cholestatic injury often leads to raised liver enzymes, including elevated alkaline phosphatase levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEOX1",
    "long_answer": "Genetic analysis of Tutankhamun’s mummy detected a pathogenic variant in the MEOX1 (Mesenchyme Homeobox 1) gene. Loss-of-function mutations in MEOX1 are a well-established cause of Klippel–Feil syndrome, a disorder characterised by congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae. The presence of this mutated gene in Tutankhamun’s DNA provides a molecular explanation for skeletal anomalies observed in CT scans of his spine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frank–Ter Haar syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Frank–Ter Haar syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in SH3PXD2B. Its clinical picture includes markedly enlarged corneas (megalocornea) and distinctive facial dysmorphism, notably hypertelorism and epicanthic folds. The concurrence of these two ocular features—megalocornea and epicanthic folds—makes Frank-Ter Haar syndrome the well-established genetic condition that fulfils the question’s criteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective IgA deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Selective IgA deficiency predisposes to giardiasis because IgA is the principal immunoglobulin protecting the intestinal mucosa. Without sufficient secretory IgA, mucosal defense against the protozoan parasite Giardia lamblia is weakened, leading to a higher incidence and chronicity of giardial infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atherosclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Both peripheral artery disease and cerebrovascular disease arise from atherosclerosis—the progressive build-up of lipid-rich plaques and inflammatory changes in medium- and large-sized arteries. This atheromatous process narrows or occludes blood vessels, reducing blood flow to the limbs in PAD and to the brain in cerebrovascular disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peritonitis",
    "long_answer": "When an inflamed diverticulum ruptures, intestinal bacteria escape into the normally sterile peritoneal cavity. The resulting infection and inflammation of the peritoneal lining is called peritonitis, a serious complication of diverticulitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "Dermatomyositis is driven by a humoral, immune-complex–mediated attack on the small vessels of muscle and skin. Circulating autoantibodies deposit in endomysial capillaries, activate complement, and form membrane-attack complexes that cause microvascular injury and perifascicular muscle fiber atrophy—mechanisms characteristic of a Type III (immune complex) hypersensitivity reaction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glial cytoplasmic inclusions",
    "long_answer": "Multiple system atrophy is pathologically defined by abundant α-synuclein–positive glial cytoplasmic inclusions (Papp-Lantos bodies) in oligodendrocytes, which constitute the characteristic histopathologic hallmark of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meiosis II nondisjunction",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental isodisomy arises when sister chromatids fail to separate during the second meiotic division (meiosis II nondisjunction). The resulting gamete carries two identical copies of a single parental chromosome; after fertilization and subsequent trisomy or monosomy rescue, the embryo retains these two identical chromosomes from one parent, producing isodisomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonadoblastoma",
    "long_answer": "In 45,X/46,XY mosaicism, the presence of Y-chromosome material in dysgenetic or streak gonads markedly increases the risk of developing gonadoblastoma—a germ-cell tumour that commonly arises in such abnormal gonadal tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-1β",
    "long_answer": "Mutations that reduce mevalonate kinase activity lead to accumulation of unprenylated proteins, activation of the NLRP3 inflammasome, and marked overproduction of the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-1β (IL-1β). Elevated IL-1β is a hallmark of mevalonate-kinase deficiency (e.g., hyper-IgD syndrome) and drives the fever and systemic inflammation characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diltiazem",
    "long_answer": "Chest pain from coronary vasospasm (variant/Prinzmetal angina) is best relieved and prevented by calcium-channel blockers. Diltiazem, a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker, relaxes coronary arterial smooth muscle, abolishing the spasm and alleviating the ischemic chest pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroconvulsive therapy",
    "long_answer": "Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is widely regarded as one of the safest treatments for severely depressed pregnant women because it produces rapid symptom relief without exposing the fetus to psychotropic drugs. Decades of obstetric and psychiatric data show minimal teratogenic risk; fetal heart rate usually remains stable, and no increase in congenital malformations has been documented. Therefore, when depression is life-threatening or medication poses unacceptable risks, ECT is considered the least harmful option for the fetus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 is a genetic disorder of aldosterone resistance that leads to severe salt-wasting, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis in infancy. Because it involves dysfunction of an adrenal hormone and requires expertise in hormonal regulation, electrolyte management, and genetic counseling, patients are primarily managed by an endocrinologist (commonly a pediatric endocrinologist)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin C",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin C is a potent antioxidant, but randomized controlled trials and meta-analyses have shown that supplementing vitamin C does not significantly change lung function, exacerbation rates, or overall symptoms in patients with asthma, indicating no meaningful clinical benefit despite its antioxidant properties."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metacarpal head osteonecrosis",
    "long_answer": "Dieterich's disease is the eponym for idiopathic avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis) of the metacarpal head, most often the second metacarpal. It represents ischemic bone death in this region and is therefore also called metacarpal head osteonecrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dietary essentiality",
    "long_answer": "In human nutrition, linoleic and α-linolenic acids are true essential fatty acids because the body cannot synthesize them and deficiency impairs health. In contrast, the term \"essential\" in essential oils simply means they contain the plant’s aromatic essence; they are not required for any physiological function. Therefore, only essential fatty acids are nutritionally indispensable, whereas essential oils are optional for human health."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary fructose intolerance, deficiency of the hepatic enzyme aldolase B causes fructose-1-phosphate to accumulate in liver cells, depleting inorganic phosphate and ATP. The resulting chronic hepatocellular injury and fibrosis can progress to end-stage liver disease—manifesting clinically as liver cirrhosis, a serious, potentially fatal condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "In severe leptospirosis a life-threatening complication is diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage, also called leptospiral pulmonary hemorrhagic syndrome. Bleeding into the alveolar spaces can manifest clinically as hemoptysis, often accompanied by acute respiratory distress and hypoxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dopaminergic dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "Periodic limb movement disorder, like restless legs syndrome, is linked to impaired dopamine neurotransmission in the central nervous system. Evidence includes the disorder’s marked clinical improvement with L-dopa or dopamine-agonist therapy and imaging/biochemical data pointing to reduced dopaminergic activity in basal ganglia pathways. Thus, dysfunction of the dopamine system is the characteristic neurotransmitter abnormality in PLMD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatocellular carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "In acquired dysfibrinogenemia, structurally abnormal fibrinogen is produced by diseased or malignant hepatocytes. Hepatocellular carcinoma—cancer of the liver cells that normally synthesize fibrinogen—can secrete dysfunctional fibrinogen molecules, leading to prolonged clotting times despite normal antigenic levels. Thus, hepatocellular carcinoma is a well-documented malignancy that causes acquired dysfibrinogenemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The main chemical of concern used during the Vietnam War was the herbicide mixture known as Agent Orange, which was contaminated with the dioxin TCDD.  Numerous evaluations by the U.S. Institute of Medicine/National Academies (now the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine), the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC), and the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs have reviewed all available epidemiologic studies of veterans, civilian applicators, and industrial workers exposed to Agent Orange or TCDD.

Those reviews conclude that the evidence is inadequate or insufficient to determine an association between exposure to Agent Orange (or its dioxin contaminant) and acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults.  Consequently, AML is not on the VA’s list of diseases that are presumed to be caused by Agent Orange exposure.  While TCDD is considered a human carcinogen overall, the data specifically linking it to AML do not show a consistent or statistically significant increase in risk.

Therefore, current scientific consensus does NOT support the statement that a chemical used during the Vietnam War (such as Agent Orange) is associated with an increased risk of developing acute myeloid leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Large anterior fontanelle",
    "long_answer": "Infants with Yunis–Varon syndrome characteristically have a markedly enlarged anterior fontanelle, reflecting delayed ossification and wide cranial sutures that typify the cleidocranial-like skeletal defects of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pelvic osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "In the first stage of the modern staged repair of classic bladder exstrophy, the surgeon must bring the widely separated pubic bones together to achieve a tension-free bladder and abdominal wall closure. To accomplish this, bilateral pelvic (usually posterior iliac) osteotomies are commonly performed, allowing medial rotation of the hemipelvis and successful approximation of the symphysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypereosinophilic syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In hypereosinophilic syndrome, persistent high eosinophil counts infiltrate the endocardium; the ensuing inflammation and thrombus formation evolve into fibrotic scarring—termed eosinophilic endomyocardial fibrosis (Löffler endocarditis). This stiff fibrotic endocardium restricts ventricular filling, producing a classic restrictive cardiomyopathy often seen in affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypokalemia",
    "long_answer": "Low serum potassium (hypokalemia) reduces membrane excitability in skeletal muscle, producing generalized muscle weakness or paralysis, and alters cardiac myocyte repolarization, leading to a variety of arrhythmias (e.g., premature beats, torsades de pointes, ventricular tachycardia). Hence hypokalemia is a single condition that explains both muscle weakness and cardiac rhythm disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasmin",
    "long_answer": "Psychogenic purpura (Gardner-Diamond syndrome) is characterized by painful, spontaneous bruising. Studies of lesional skin show marked hyper-fibrinolysis: levels of the fibrin-degrading protease plasmin are locally elevated, leading to degradation of fibrin and vessel wall proteins and thereby facilitating hemorrhage. This localized increase in plasmin activity explains why antifibrinolytic drugs (e.g., ε-aminocaproic acid, tranexamic acid) can lessen attacks, supporting plasmin’s pathogenic role in psychogenic purpura."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1 CGG expansion",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome results from a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. When the repeat number exceeds ~200 (‘full mutation’), the gene is methylated and silenced, abolishing production of FMRP, a protein essential for normal synaptic development. Because males have only one X chromosome, this mutation commonly leads to moderate-to-severe intellectual disability and characteristic physical and behavioral features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLN3",
    "long_answer": "The juvenile form of Batten disease (a neuronal ceroid-lipofuscinosis) is caused by mutations in the CLN3 gene, which is located on chromosome 16p12. Defects in CLN3 disrupt lysosomal function, leading to the neurodegenerative symptoms characteristic of Batten disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The TYR gene, encoding the enzyme tyrosinase that is defective in Oculocutaneous albinism type I, is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 11 (cytogenetic location 11q14.3). This chromosomal position is consistently reported in human genomic databases and genetic studies elucidating the molecular basis of OCA1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APC gene",
    "long_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis is caused by germ-line mutations in the APC (adenomatous polyposis coli) tumor-suppressor gene. Because virtually all individuals with an APC mutation will develop hundreds to thousands of colonic adenomas that inevitably progress to colorectal carcinoma, prophylactic surgical removal of the colon (colectomy) is the standard preventive treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gradient-echo MRI",
    "long_answer": "Lobar intracerebral hemorrhages caused by cerebral amyloid angiopathy are best detected with T2*-weighted gradient-echo magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), which is highly sensitive to hemosiderin deposits and small bleeds characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) is characterized by elevated intracranial pressure without a mass lesion or hydrocephalus. First-line management includes weight loss in overweight patients and carbonic-anhydrase-inhibiting diuretics such as acetazolamide to lower cerebrospinal fluid production, directly addressing the raised pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "House M.D.",
    "long_answer": "The Fox television medical drama \"House M.D.\" includes a Season 4 episode titled \"Ugly\" in which Dr. House and his team evaluate a 16-year-old boy whose massive craniofacial overgrowth is first suspected to be craniodiaphyseal dysplasia (“lionitis”). The rare skeletal disorder, characterized by excessive bone deposition in the skull and facial bones, drives the diagnostic storyline of that episode, making House M.D. the series that features a teenage patient with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crizotinib",
    "long_answer": "ALK-rearranged lung adenocarcinomas are treated with crizotinib, an oral small-molecule tyrosine-kinase inhibitor that specifically blocks the activity of the anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) fusion protein (e.g., EML4-ALK). By inhibiting the aberrant ALK signaling that drives tumor growth, crizotinib produces marked responses and is an established first-line therapy for ALK-positive non-small-cell lung cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophage activation syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In the systemic subtype of Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis, a life-threatening complication called macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) can develop. MAS is a form of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis characterized by uncontrolled activation of macrophages and cytotoxic T cells, leading to high fever, cytopenias, liver dysfunction, coagulopathy, and a cytokine storm. It occurs in a notable minority of systemic JIA patients and requires prompt recognition and treatment because of its high mortality risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior spinal artery",
    "long_answer": "Normally a single anterior spinal artery courses in the midline and supplies the anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord. In diastematomyelia the cord is split, and each hemicord retains its own longitudinal ventral vessel; thus, each hemicord is vascularised by its own branch of the anterior (ventral) spinal artery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in GREB1L are typically heterozygous and segregate with unilateral or bilateral renal agenesis and other CAKUT phenotypes in families in an autosomal dominant manner, showing variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "In humans, the FOXL2 gene maps to chromosome 3, specifically at cytogenetic band 3q23. Pathogenic variants or deletions of this gene on chromosome 3 are a well-established cause of blepharophimosis, ptosis and epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES), confirming its chromosomal location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLI3",
    "long_answer": "Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome is classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GLI3 gene, which encodes a zinc-finger transcription factor of the Sonic Hedgehog signaling pathway. Although Carpenter syndrome is most often linked to RAB23, several documented patients with a Carpenter-like phenotype have been found to carry pathogenic GLI3 variants, demonstrating that GLI3 mutations can underlie both Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome and Carpenter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC17A5",
    "long_answer": "The most severe form of sialic acid storage disease—infantile free sialic acid storage disease—results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC17A5 gene. SLC17A5 encodes the lysosomal membrane transporter ‘sialin,’ which is responsible for exporting free sialic acid from lysosomes. Loss-of-function mutations in SLC17A5 lead to intralysosomal accumulation of free sialic acid, producing the characteristic clinical and biochemical manifestations of the severe infantile disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgM",
    "long_answer": "Pathogen-specific IgM antibodies are used because maternal IgG readily crosses the placenta, whereas IgM does not. Therefore, the presence of IgM directed against a given pathogen in a newborn’s serum must have been produced by the infant itself, indicating an in-utero (vertically transmitted) infection such as the TORCH group diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fever",
    "long_answer": "Patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) frequently present with fever, a non-specific systemic response to inflammation or infection. Fever is also one of the most ubiquitous symptoms seen across a wide range of infectious diseases, reflecting activation of cytokine-mediated pyrogenic pathways. Thus, fever is a shared, common symptom between APL and many infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB11 (BSEP)",
    "long_answer": "In progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 2, mutations in the ABCB11 gene (encoding the bile-salt export pump, BSEP) lead to absent or markedly reduced canalicular BSEP protein. Immunohistochemical staining for BSEP on a liver biopsy therefore allows direct recognition of an ABCB11 mutation–related disease, making this the PFIC gene defect that can be identified through liver biopsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "North Carolina",
    "long_answer": "National occupational‐mortality surveillance data for 1996-2005 show that North Carolina, home to the largest concentration of cotton-textile mills in the United States, registered the greatest number of deaths in which byssinosis was the underlying or contributing cause. The state’s long-standing textile industry resulted in more workers chronically exposed to cotton dust, explaining why North Carolina accounted for the highest byssinosis-related mortality during that period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kernicterus",
    "long_answer": "In untreated pernicious anemia the intense intramedullary destruction of megaloblastic red cells can raise unconjugated bilirubin to toxic levels. When bilirubin crosses the blood–brain barrier it deposits in basal ganglia and brain-stem nuclei, producing bilirubin encephalopathy, classically called kernicterus. The identical pathology occurs in newborns with severe jaundice, whose high serum unconjugated bilirubin and immature blood–brain barrier make them especially vulnerable to kernicterus. Thus, kernicterus is a neurological complication that can arise in both situations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PORCN",
    "long_answer": "Focal dermal hypoplasia (Goltz syndrome) is an X-linked dominant disorder. Pathogenic variants are consistently found in the PORCN gene (porcupine O-acyltransferase) located on the X chromosome (Xp11.23). Mutations in PORCN disrupt Wnt protein palmitoylation and secretion, leading to the characteristic skin, skeletal, and ectodermal abnormalities seen in focal dermal hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schistosomiasis",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic cystitis can be precipitated by infection with the trematode Schistosoma haematobium. The parasite’s eggs lodge in the bladder wall, provoking a marked eosinophil-rich inflammatory response that produces the characteristic eosinophilic cystitis. Urinary schistosomiasis is effectively managed with the antiparasitic drug praziquantel, linking this infection both etiologically and therapeutically to eosinophilic cystitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD2",
    "long_answer": "De-novo loss-of-function variants in the CHD2 gene (chromodomain helicase DNA-binding protein 2), which is situated on chromosome 15q26.1, have been repeatedly shown to cause epileptic encephalopathy characterised by myoclonic–atonic (myoclonic astatic) seizures. Hence, CHD2 is the chromosome-15 gene specifically linked to myoclonic astatic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obesity",
    "long_answer": "In Prader–Willi syndrome, hypothalamic dysfunction produces hyperphagia and reduced energy expenditure. Unless caloric intake is carefully restricted, affected children and adults commonly develop severe, central (truncal) obesity; this weight gain is regarded as the most frequent and well-recognized sequela of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A. Bernard Ackerman",
    "long_answer": "Ackerman syndrome takes its eponym from the American dermatologist-dermatopathologist A. Bernard Ackerman, who first reported the characteristic association of gingival fibromatosis, ocular (juvenile-glaucoma) changes, and other craniofacial anomalies. His initial description established the condition as a distinct clinical-genetic entity, and subsequent case-series have continued to cite his original work."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III cryoglobulinemia",
    "long_answer": "Type III (mixed polyclonal) cryoglobulinemia is the form most frequently associated with systemic autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus, and Sjögren’s syndrome. In this type, polyclonal IgM with rheumatoid-factor activity complexes with polyclonal IgG, and the immune complex–mediated process overlaps with the pathophysiology of these autoimmune conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rapid protein C depletion",
    "long_answer": "In the first 24–72 hours of warfarin therapy the vitamin K–dependent anticoagulant protein C (half-life ~8 h) falls more quickly than the longer-lived procoagulant factors II, IX and X. This transient imbalance produces a hypercoagulable state with microvascular thrombosis in subcutaneous fat, leading to the characteristic early warfarin-induced skin necrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conradi–Hünermann–Happle syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the EBP gene disrupt cholesterol biosynthesis and cause X-linked dominant chondrodysplasia punctata type 2, also called Conradi–Hünermann–Happle syndrome. The disorder produces asymmetric epiphyseal stippling and vertebral malformations, predisposing affected individuals to progressive spinal curvature (scoliosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diencephalon",
    "long_answer": "During embryogenesis the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) forms as a ventral outgrowth of neuroectoderm from the floor of the diencephalon. This diencephalic infundibulum unites with Rathke’s pouch from oral ectoderm to create the complete pituitary gland, making the diencephalon the brain region directly involved in its development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuroblastoma",
    "long_answer": "Neuroblastoma, a pediatric tumor of neural crest–derived adrenal medulla or sympathetic ganglia, characteristically secretes excess catecholamines. Their metabolites (vanillylmandelic acid and homovanillic acid) accumulate and can be measured as markedly elevated catecholamine levels in the patient’s urine, serving as a key diagnostic marker for this cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EFNB1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Craniofrontonasal dysplasia (CFND) is an X-linked craniosynostosis syndrome caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EFNB1 gene, which encodes the cell-surface signaling protein ephrin-B1. EFNB1 mutations disrupt ephrin-Eph signaling critical for craniofacial suture patterning, leading to distinctive features such as coronal craniosynostosis, hypertelorism, and maxillary hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epiglottis",
    "long_answer": "In laryngomalacia, the floppy, omega-shaped epiglottis collapses inward during inspiration, producing supraglottic airway obstruction and the characteristic inspiratory stridor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "Succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (SSADH) deficiency often presents in the neonatal period with neurologic signs such as seizures, arising from accumulation of γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA) and γ-hydroxybutyrate that disturb neuronal excitability. Seizures are likewise a well-recognized clinical manifestation of neonatal hypoglycemia, produced when the glucose-deprived brain becomes hyper-excitable. Thus, seizures are a symptom common to both SSADH deficiency and hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "The only disease-modifying therapy shown to improve survival and neurologic outcome in Krabbe disease is hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (bone marrow or umbilical-cord blood transplant). When performed presymptomatically, donor-derived cells supply functional galactocerebrosidase to the central nervous system, slowing demyelination and disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In several Zika virus outbreaks (e.g., French Polynesia 2013–2014, Brazil 2015–2016) a marked rise in Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) cases was documented. Zika infection can trigger an autoimmune response that attacks peripheral nerve myelin or axons, producing rapid, ascending limb weakness and paralysis—hallmark features of GBS and the source of its impaired motor function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiovascular disease",
    "long_answer": "Cardiovascular disease—including coronary heart disease, myocardial infarction, and stroke—accounts for the largest proportion of mortality and years-lived-with-disability in high-income, developed nations. Epidemiologic data show it causes roughly one-third to one-half of all deaths and represents the greatest single contributor to disability-adjusted life years in these countries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "Microscopic polyangiitis is an ANCA-associated small-vessel vasculitis that requires systemic immunosuppressive therapy (e.g., high-dose corticosteroids, cyclophosphamide or rituximab). The diagnosis, monitoring, and adjustment of these disease-modifying and immune-modulating treatments are core responsibilities of rheumatologists, the specialty that routinely manages systemic autoimmune vasculitides."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate",
    "long_answer": "Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates block farnesyl-pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, thereby preventing production of the downstream isoprenoid geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate (GGPP). Lack of GGPP impairs protein prenylation, triggers inflammasome activation, and is considered a central mechanism in the inflammatory pathogenesis of mevalonate kinase deficiency. Hence, GGPP synthesis is both inhibited by bisphosphonates and critical to the disease process in mevalonate kinase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trigonocephaly",
    "long_answer": "Premature fusion of the metopic suture (metopic craniosynostosis) restricts lateral frontal growth and produces a keel-shaped, triangular forehead; this characteristic cranial deformity is termed trigonocephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPD (ERCC2)",
    "long_answer": "The XPD protein, encoded by the ERCC2 gene, is a helicase subunit of the transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision repair (NER) complex TFIIH. Pathogenic variants in XPD can produce the skin-cancer-prone disorder xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) and, in other patients, the combined XP/Cockayne syndrome phenotype, showing that the same DNA repair protein is implicated in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric oncology",
    "long_answer": "Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignant soft-tissue sarcoma that occurs almost exclusively in children. Management requires systemic chemotherapy, radiation, and coordinated multidisciplinary care, which is led by specialists in pediatric oncology—physicians trained to treat cancers in the pediatric population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior longitudinal ligament",
    "long_answer": "In Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) the pathological process is exuberant calcification and ossification of spinal entheses. The most characteristic site is the anterior longitudinal ligament that runs along the front of the vertebral bodies; it becomes thickened and ossified, producing the flowing anterior bony bridges seen on radiographs. Other ligaments can be involved secondarily, but the anterior longitudinal ligament is the primary structure affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Cowden syndrome, one of the PTEN-hamartoma tumor syndromes caused most commonly by germline pathogenic variants in the tumor-suppressor gene PTEN, is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner. Thus a single mutated allele is sufficient to confer risk, and the disorder can appear in each generation with a 50 % chance of being passed from an affected parent to offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal-dominant COL1A1 null mutation",
    "long_answer": "The commonest form of osteogenesis imperfecta (type I) is almost always caused by heterozygous loss-of-function (nonsense/frameshift or splice-site) mutations in the COL1A1 gene. These autosomal-dominant null mutations halve the production of normal type I collagen, leading to the characteristic bone fragility of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paralympic Games",
    "long_answer": "The Paralympic Games are an international multisport event specifically organized for athletes with a wide range of physical disabilities, including musculoskeletal conditions such as arthrogryposis. Competitors with joint contractures and related mobility limitations are eligible to compete under the event’s classification system, making the Paralympics the appropriate international stage for such athletes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autoimmune disease",
    "long_answer": "Evidence such as HLA class-II associations, circulating anti-myenteric antibodies, and coexistence with disorders like type 1 diabetes or thyroiditis suggest that the neuronal loss seen in achalasia may result from immune-mediated attack. Therefore, achalasia is often considered related to, or a form of, an autoimmune disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spironolactone",
    "long_answer": "Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that also acts as an anti-androgen: it competitively blocks androgen receptors and weakly inhibits 5-α-reductase. Because it lowers the effect of circulating androgens, it is routinely used to treat hyperandrogenic states such as hirsutism and acne in polycystic ovary syndrome, and the same mechanism makes it useful (off-label) for androgen-related hair loss (androgenetic alopecia), particularly in women. Thus one drug can address both hyperandrogenism and hair loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Order",
    "long_answer": "Spirurida is classified at the rank of an order within the phylum Nematoda; it groups together several families of parasitic roundworms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ichthyosis",
    "long_answer": "Warkany syndrome 2 is the clinical name for mosaic trisomy 8. Among its recognised cutaneous manifestations are abnormalities of keratinisation; many patients show a generalized dry, thick, scaly skin phenotype that is classified as ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peroxisomal enzymes",
    "long_answer": "Zellweger syndrome is a peroxisome-biogenesis disorder caused by mutations in PEX genes that prevent import of matrix proteins into peroxisomes. As a result, the enzymes normally located in peroxisomes are absent or non-functional, leading to the characteristic metabolic abnormalities (e.g., accumulation of very-long-chain fatty acids, reduced plasmalogen synthesis). Thus, individuals with Zellweger syndrome are specifically deficient in peroxisomal enzymes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "<1 in 1,000,000",
    "long_answer": "MacDermot–Winter syndrome is an ultra-rare X-linked neurodevelopmental disorder; only a handful of families have ever been documented in the literature. Orphanet and other rare-disease registries list its population prevalence as less than 1 per 1,000,000 individuals, reflecting the extremely limited number of confirmed cases worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5 years old",
    "long_answer": "International guidelines (e.g., DSM-5 and the International Children’s Continence Society) define nocturnal enuresis as nighttime urinary incontinence in a child who has reached a developmental age of at least 5 years, because normal physiologic attainment of consistent nighttime bladder control is expected by this age."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic giant-cell myocarditis often begins abruptly with malignant ventricular arrhythmias; sustained ventricular tachycardia is reported as one of the most frequent initial clinical manifestations. Because ventricular tachycardia is a rapid rhythm originating below the atrioventricular node (an arrhythmia), its occurrence early in the disease commonly alerts clinicians to the presence of giant-cell myocarditis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "International Headache Society",
    "long_answer": "In 2004 the diagnostic criteria for Tolosa–Hunt syndrome were formalized in the second edition of the International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-II), a document issued by the International Headache Society, which defines standardized clinical criteria for headache-related disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amblyopia",
    "long_answer": "In congenital ankyloblepharon, the partial or complete fusion of the upper and lower eyelid margins can obstruct the visual axis during the critical period of visual development. This sensory deprivation frequently leads to stimulus-deprivation amblyopia, which is considered the principal and most clinically significant complication if the adhesions are not released promptly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNS stimulants",
    "long_answer": "Central nervous system (CNS) stimulants—such as amphetamine, dextro-amphetamine and methylphenidate—markedly increase synaptic dopamine and noradrenaline. At high doses or with chronic misuse this dopaminergic surge can precipitate florid psychotic symptoms (paranoia, hallucinations, agitation), a syndrome termed stimulant psychosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleural cavity (around the lungs)",
    "long_answer": "In hydrops fetalis, pleural effusion refers to an abnormal accumulation of fluid within the pleural cavity—the space that surrounds and lines the lungs—thereby affecting the fetal lungs and thoracic compartment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Primary congenital glaucoma is an ocular disorder characterized by developmental anomalies of the trabecular meshwork leading to elevated intra-ocular pressure in infants. Diagnosis and management—including gonioscopy, tonometry, and angle surgery—fall within the expertise of ophthalmologists, particularly those subspecialized in pediatric or glaucoma care; therefore, the medical specialty most likely to treat primary congenital glaucoma is Ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sympathetic autonomic nervous system",
    "long_answer": "In Long QT syndrome, life-threatening ventricular tachyarrhythmias such as torsades de pointes are typically precipitated by surges of adrenergic tone (e.g., exercise, emotional stress, sudden fright). This implicates the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, whose catecholaminergic activation accentuates the abnormal repolarization inherent to Long QT mutations and triggers the arrhythmia; accordingly, β-blockers that blunt sympathetic output are first-line therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant connective-tissue disorder caused chiefly by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene. Because its pathogenesis, diagnosis, counseling, and management revolve around hereditary mechanisms, the medical specialty that focuses on inherited diseases—medical genetics—specializes in the study of Marfan syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cockles",
    "long_answer": "Cockles are small edible bivalve molluscs traditionally gathered along the Welsh coast and served with bacon and laverbread as part of a classic Welsh cooked breakfast. In Cambodia, cockles are widely sold by street vendors—often steamed or stir-fried with chili and basil—making them a well-known and popular street food there."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iodized salt",
    "long_answer": "Fortifying table salt with iodine (iodized salt) is the standard population-level dietary measure used worldwide to supply adequate iodine, thereby preventing the thyroid hormone deficiency and goiter that lead to hypothyroidism in iodine-deficient regions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Roberts syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the ESCO2 gene disrupt sister-chromatid cohesion, and this genetic defect is the established cause of Roberts syndrome (also known as SC phocomelia). The syndrome is characterized by severe, congenital abnormalities of limb, craniofacial, and other tissue development that are evident from birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amsler grid",
    "long_answer": "Patients with age-related macular degeneration are commonly given an Amsler grid—a simple square grid with a central fixation dot—to use at home. By looking at the grid daily, they can quickly notice new distortions, blurred areas, or missing lines, which signal progression toward neovascular (wet) AMD and warrant prompt clinical evaluation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Pashayan syndrome (OMIM 265120) is a rare cranio-facial malformation syndrome characterized by blepharophimosis, ptosis, telecanthus, mid-face hypoplasia and ear anomalies. In the original and subsequent case series the disorder was seen in multiple successive generations, with affected males and females transmitting the trait to their children. This vertical transmission pattern, together with the roughly 50 % risk to offspring, is consistent with autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expressivity. There is no credible evidence that the condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern. Therefore the statement that Pashayan syndrome is autosomal recessive is not supported by established genetic knowledge."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary valve stenosis",
    "long_answer": "In Noonan syndrome, congenital heart disease occurs in the majority of patients, and the lesion seen most frequently—reported in roughly 50-70 % of affected individuals—is pulmonic (pulmonary valve) stenosis, typically with a dysplastic valve. Other defects such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or atrial septal defect are less common, making pulmonary valve stenosis the predominant cardiac abnormality in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ilium",
    "long_answer": "Boomerang dysplasia, caused by pathogenic FLNB mutations, is characterized by markedly deficient ossification of several skeletal elements. Radiographs consistently show underossification of the iliac portions of the pelvis, with the ilia appearing poorly mineralized or almost absent compared with normal fetal development. This hypo-ossification of the ilium is a defining pelvic feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AIRE gene testing",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 (APS-1/APECED) is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the AIRE gene. Sequencing or targeted mutation analysis of the AIRE gene in peripheral blood DNA is therefore the definitive diagnostic procedure: identifying pathogenic AIRE variants confirms the clinical suspicion of APS-1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yorkshire Terrier",
    "long_answer": "Legg–Calvé–Perthes disease is an avascular necrosis of the femoral head that appears almost exclusively in immature toy and small-breed dogs. Epidemiologic studies consistently reveal a marked over-representation of Yorkshire Terriers, indicating a breed-specific genetic susceptibility. Thus, Yorkshire Terriers are regarded as the breed most prone to this orthopedic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3q26.33 SOX2 deletion",
    "long_answer": "Loss of the SOX2 gene through a microdeletion at chromosome 3q26.33 causes SOX2-anophthalmia syndrome. This well-characterised defect disrupts early eye morphogenesis, producing unilateral or bilateral anophthalmia, and impairs hypothalamic–pituitary development, leading to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism in males that manifests as micropenis (often with cryptorchidism). Thus, a 3q26.33 microdeletion encompassing SOX2 is the recognised chromosomal lesion that links anophthalmia and micropenis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Long (tubular) bones",
    "long_answer": "Kozlowski-Krajewska syndrome is a type of spondylometaphyseal/metaphyseal chondrodysplasia in which the metaphyses of the long tubular bones of the limbs are primarily involved, producing characteristic deformity and short-limb dwarfism. Thus the disorder chiefly affects the long bones rather than flat or craniofacial bones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polymerase chain reaction",
    "long_answer": "Amplifying viral genomes by PCR on an endomyocardial-biopsy sample allows direct detection and typing of enteroviruses, adenovirus, parvovirus B19 and other agents, thereby pinpointing a viral etiology of myocarditis that routine histology or serology cannot confirm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystonia",
    "long_answer": "Dystonia is a movement disorder marked by involuntary, sustained muscle contractions that produce twisting motions and abnormal postures. When it affects the lower limbs, these contractions can cause the legs and feet to assume odd positions, leading to dragging of the foot, inward turning of the ankle, or scissoring of the legs. Such abnormal leg movements make walking difficult, slow, and uncomfortable, so dystonia fits the description of a movement disorder that impairs gait through odd postures and leg movements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tryptophan",
    "long_answer": "In hypertryptophanemia, hepatic tryptophan-2,3-dioxygenase activity is deficient, so the normal degradation of tryptophan is blocked. The resulting marked elevation of plasma tryptophan exceeds renal re-uptake capacity, leading to pronounced urinary loss of the unchanged amino acid. Thus, tryptophan itself is the compound that is substantially excreted in this metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "<100 cells/µL",
    "long_answer": "Microsporidial infections in HIV most often appear in patients with advanced immunosuppression; clinical series show that virtually all cases occur when the peripheral CD4+ T-lymphocyte count has fallen below 100 cells per microlitre. Hence, a CD4+ count under this threshold marks the level at which HIV-infected individuals become susceptible to microsporidiosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Exposure to alcohol during pregnancy can cause a range of neurodevelopmental problems collectively termed fetal alcohol spectrum disorders (FASD). Well-documented long-term outcomes in affected children include difficulties with executive function, learning, impulse control and sustained attention. Numerous epidemiologic and clinical studies show that children with prenatal alcohol exposure have a markedly higher prevalence of attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) or clinically significant attention deficits compared with unexposed peers. These attention problems are considered part of the characteristic cognitive and behavioral profile of FASD and can persist into adolescence and adulthood. Therefore, attention-deficit disorder is indeed a recognized long-term cognitive and behavioral effect of in-utero alcohol exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Matrix metalloproteinase-2 (MMP-2)",
    "long_answer": "Angiostatin is generated by limited proteolytic cleavage of plasminogen. One of the key enzymes that carries out this cleavage is the zinc-dependent protease matrix metalloproteinase-2 (MMP-2, gelatinase A). MMP-2 cuts plasminogen within its kringle domain region, releasing the kringle-containing fragment termed angiostatin, which functions as a potent endogenous inhibitor of angiogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loss-of-function mutation",
    "long_answer": "Coffin–Lowry syndrome arises when the RPS6KA3 gene (encoding the RSK2 kinase) acquires mutations that abolish or greatly reduce its protein’s activity. These loss-of-function changes—typically nonsense, frameshift, splice-site or other truncating alterations—eliminate functional RSK2, producing the characteristic neurological and skeletal features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2 tumor-suppressor gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene (located on chromosome 22q) greatly increase the risk of developing meningiomas, as seen in neurofibromatosis type 2 patients. NF2 inactivation is also characteristic of many radiation-associated meningiomas, and studies of individuals with frequent or early-life dental X-ray exposure—a source of ionizing radiation—show a higher incidence of meningioma in the presence of NF2 gene defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epsilon aminocaproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Bernard–Soulier syndrome is a platelet-adhesion disorder; during mucosal or postsurgical bleeding episodes antifibrinolytic therapy helps stabilise the clot. The antifibrinolytic drug epsilon aminocaproic acid binds plasminogen and prevents fibrin breakdown, thereby controlling bleeding in patients with Bernard–Soulier syndrome when platelet transfusion is not immediately required."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sub-Saharan Africa",
    "long_answer": "Noma (cancrum oris) predominantly affects severely malnourished children living in extreme poverty, and epidemiological studies show that the vast majority of reported cases occur in sub-Saharan Africa, particularly within the Sahel belt. Other regions report only sporadic cases, making sub-Saharan Africa the region most heavily burdened by this neglected tropical disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM5",
    "long_answer": "Acral peeling skin syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the TGM5 gene, which encodes epidermal transglutaminase 5. Defective TGase-5 impairs cross-linking of proteins in the cornified envelope, weakening adhesion in the outer epidermis and causing the characteristic painless peeling of acral skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin type A",
    "long_answer": "Blepharospasm is characterized by involuntary contractions of the orbicularis oculi muscles. Local injections of botulinum toxin type A (e.g., Botox) are the established first-line therapy because the toxin cleaves SNAP-25, blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, and thereby temporarily weakens the overactive eyelid muscles, relieving spasms for several months."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATRX",
    "long_answer": "The ATRX gene (Alpha-thalassemia/mental retardation syndrome X-linked) is recurrently inactivated in undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma, where its loss promotes the alternative lengthening of telomeres pathway. Germ-line mutations in the same X-chromosomal gene cause ATR-X syndrome, an X-linked mental retardation disorder accompanied by alpha-thalassemia, firmly linking ATRX to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome (Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome) is a dominantly inherited disorder caused mainly by pathogenic variants in KMT2D or KDM6A. Its hallmarks are characteristic cranio-facial dysmorphisms (long palpebral fissures, arched eyebrows, large ears) and multiple skeletal anomalies (vertebral and hip malformations, brachydactyly, persistent fetal fingertip pads). Renal structural and tubular defects that are common in Kabuki syndrome frequently result in hyperuricemia and can produce early-onset arterial hypertension, making this condition the well-established genetic disorder that matches the clinical triad described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Brown-Séquard syndrome results from damage to one half of the spinal cord; demyelinating lesions restricted to a hemicord can produce the characteristic ipsilateral motor/proprioceptive loss and contralateral pain-temperature loss. Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune, CNS-demyelinating disease in which focal plaques can form in the spinal cord. When an MS plaque affects only one lateral half of the cord, it can trigger a clinical Brown-Séquard presentation. Thus, multiple sclerosis is a well-documented autoimmune cause of Brown-Séquard syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve (CN VII)",
    "long_answer": "Ramsay Hunt syndrome type 2, also called herpes zoster oticus, results from reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the geniculate ganglion. This ganglion belongs to the facial nerve, cranial nerve VII, and its involvement produces the characteristic triad of ear pain, vesicular rash of the external ear/canal, and ipsilateral peripheral facial paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylalanine hydroxylase",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary disorder phenylketonuria (PKU) results from deficiency of the hepatic enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH), which normally converts phenylalanine to tyrosine. Lack of PAH activity leads to accumulation of phenylalanine in the blood, producing the markedly elevated phenylalaninemia characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydronephrosis",
    "long_answer": "A urinary tract infection can produce inflammation or obstruction that blocks normal urine outflow. The resulting back-pressure elevates hydrostatic pressure within the kidney, leading to dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces—a pathologic state known as hydronephrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis is a prototypic axial spondyloarthritis in which chronic inflammation causes progressive fusion and rigidity of the vertebral column (the “bamboo spine”). This leads to intense spinal and sacro-iliac pain, restricted chest expansion that impairs breathing, forward spinal curvature that can compress abdominal organs and disturb digestion, and extra-skeletal inflammation of the aortic root or cardiac conduction system, producing cardiovascular irregularities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome (NBS) is a congenital DNA-repair disorder caused by biallelic NBN mutations. Cells from people with NBS are hypersensitive to ionising radiation and to DNA-damaging cytotoxic drugs, especially alkylating (radiomimetic) agents such as cyclophosphamide, busulfan, chlorambucil and others. Because of this extreme sensitivity, standard management of NBS avoids or markedly reduces exposure to alkylating chemotherapy; the drugs are not used to treat the syndrome itself and, when an NBS patient develops cancer, treatment protocols generally substitute or greatly dose-reduce alkylators to minimise life-threatening toxicity. Therefore, alkylating antineoplastic agents are not considered part of the therapeutic approach to Nijmegen breakage syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose radiotherapy",
    "long_answer": "Clinical studies of Ledderhose disease (plantar fibromatosis) show that administering low-dose external-beam radiotherapy to the plantar nodules arrests growth and relieves pain in roughly 80 % of treated patients, making this modality the most consistently effective nonsurgical treatment reported."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACADM",
    "long_answer": "ACADM (medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase) lies on chromosome 1p31 and encodes a key mitochondrial enzyme that catalyzes the first dehydrogenation step in the β-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAG trinucleotide repeat expansions",
    "long_answer": "Most spinocerebellar ataxias are autosomal-dominant disorders produced by unstable CAG trinucleotide repeat expansions in specific genes (e.g., ATXN1, ATXN2, ATXN3). The expanded CAG tract encodes an abnormally long polyglutamine stretch, leading to toxic protein aggregation and progressive degeneration of cerebellar and spinal pathways, which manifests clinically as spinocerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fewer than 1,000 cases",
    "long_answer": "Waldmann disease, also called primary intestinal lymphangiectasia, is classified as an ultra-rare disorder. According to the U.S. NIH Genetic and Rare Diseases (GARD) database, its exact prevalence is unknown, but estimates indicate fewer than 1,000 affected individuals nationwide—well under 1 per 100,000 population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rubella vaccination",
    "long_answer": "Immunizing children and women of child-bearing age with the live-attenuated rubella vaccine induces long-lasting neutralising antibodies, blocks wild-type rubella virus circulation through herd immunity, and therefore prevents primary maternal infection during pregnancy—the sole cause of congenital rubella syndrome—leading to a marked decline or elimination of CRS in the population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren-Larsson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren-Larsson syndrome is an autosomal-recessive neurocutaneous disorder caused by mutations in the ALDH3A2 gene. It classically presents with the triad of hereditary spastic paraplegia (progressive spasticity and weakness of the lower limbs), congenital ichthyosis manifesting as dry, thickened, scaly skin, and mild to moderate intellectual disability. The coexistence of spastic paraparesis with ichthyosis makes Sjögren-Larsson syndrome the well-established answer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZIC2",
    "long_answer": "The ZIC2 gene, located at chromosome 13q32, is a well-established cause of holoprosencephaly. Pathogenic variants or deletions in ZIC2 disrupt transcription factor functions crucial for forebrain midline patterning, leading to the spectrum of holoprosencephaly malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "The classic X-linked recessive form of hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia results from pathogenic variants in the EDA gene, which encodes the signaling protein ectodysplasin A on the X chromosome. Because males have a single X chromosome, hemizygous EDA mutations manifest the disease, while carrier females are usually asymptomatic or mildly affected, confirming the X-linked recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal artery",
    "long_answer": "Fibromuscular dysplasia most frequently involves the renal arteries, where the characteristic medial fibroplasia produces segmental stenoses. Narrowing of a renal artery lowers renal perfusion, activates the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system, and precipitates secondary (renovascular) hypertension. Approximately two-thirds of FMD cases affect the renal arteries, making them the classic and most common site of disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2 gene",
    "long_answer": "Papillorenal (renal-coloboma) syndrome in humans is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the PAX2 gene. In zebrafish embryogenesis the Sonic hedgehog (Shh) signaling pathway positively regulates pax2a expression, linking Shh control to the same developmental pathway that, when disrupted in humans, leads to papillorenal syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophage activation syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) is a severe, potentially fatal hyper-inflammatory illness in which cytotoxic T lymphocytes and macrophages undergo uncontrolled proliferation and activation, leading to massive cytokine release and hemophagocytosis. Clinically and pathophysiologically, MAS is considered a form of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) and is most commonly triggered in patients with systemic-onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis (sJIA)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRIT1 (CCM1)",
    "long_answer": "The KRIT1 gene, also known as CCM1, is one of the three major genes linked to cerebral cavernous malformations. Mouse studies show that complete (homozygous) loss of KRIT1 results in severe vascular defects and mid-gestation lethality, causing embryos to die around embryonic day 9–11. Thus, KRIT1 deficiency can lead to embryonic death in mice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin K1",
    "long_answer": "Leafy green vegetables are especially rich in vitamin K1 (phylloquinone) because this molecule is an essential electron carrier in the photosystem I complex of photosynthesis. Its role in the chloroplast makes it abundant in green leaves, leading to the high vitamin K content characteristic of leaf vegetables."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EYA1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic heterozygous mutations in the EYA1 gene (oto-faciocervical syndrome type 1 locus) are well documented to produce the otofaciocervical syndrome phenotype. EYA1 defects disturb branchial arch development, leading to external ear anomalies such as preauricular pits, sinuses or cysts, alongside the characteristic facial, cervical and auditory malformations of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX",
    "long_answer": "Short stature in Turner syndrome is largely attributed to haploinsufficiency of the SHOX (short stature homeobox) gene. SHOX resides in the pseudoautosomal region 1 (PAR1) of both the X and Y chromosomes, so individuals with 45,X karyotype lack one functional copy. Loss of this second allele reduces SHOX dosage, disrupting growth-plate chondrocyte function and leading to the characteristic short stature observed in Turner syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antihistamines",
    "long_answer": "Allergic conjunctivitis is primarily driven by histamine release from ocular mast cells. Topical or oral antihistamine preparations that block H1-histamine receptors rapidly relieve itching, redness, and tearing, making antihistamines the standard pharmacologic treatment for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otitis media",
    "long_answer": "The Eustachian (auditory) tube normally equalizes pressure and allows drainage from the middle ear into the nasopharynx. When this tube is obstructed—by inflammation, infection, allergy, or enlarged adenoids—air cannot enter the middle ear, negative pressure develops, and fluid accumulates. This stagnant fluid provides a medium for bacterial growth, leading to inflammation and infection of the middle-ear cavity, a condition clinically known as otitis media."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 1",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (the Unna-Thost form) is most often produced by heterozygous mutations in the KRT1 gene. Keratin 1 is a type II (basic) epidermal keratin expressed in the suprabasal layers of palm and sole skin; pathogenic KRT1 variants cause the marked palmoplantar hyperkeratosis seen in this disorder without the epidermolysis characteristic of the keratin-9–related (Vörner) epidermolytic form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Megaloblastic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Folate (vitamin B9) is essential for DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells, including erythroid precursors in the bone marrow. When folate is deficient, nuclear maturation is impaired while cytoplasmic development proceeds, producing abnormally large, immature red-cell precursors called megaloblasts. The resulting circulating red cells are macrocytic, giving rise to the classic macrocytic-megaloblastic anemia characteristic of folate deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 1 and Keratin 16",
    "long_answer": "Nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma results from mutations in specific epithelial keratins. Diffuse nonepidermolytic PPK (Unna-Thost type) is produced by mutations in keratin 1 (KRT1), while focal nonepidermolytic PPK seen in pachyonychia congenita is most often caused by mutations in keratin 16 (KRT16, usually together with its partner KRT6). Thus, keratin 1 and keratin 16 are the key keratins linked to nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thumb metacarpophalangeal joints",
    "long_answer": "In Léri’s pleonosteosis the thumbs deviate radially (valgus) at the metacarpophalangeal joints, so these MCP joints of the thumbs are the hand joints that become characteristically affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolonged apnea",
    "long_answer": "Succinylcholine is normally hydrolyzed within minutes by plasma pseudo- (butyryl-) cholinesterase. In individuals who lack or have deficient enzyme activity, the drug persists, causing a markedly extended depolarizing neuromuscular block that presents as prolonged apnea/respiratory paralysis after anesthesia. Management is supportive: secure the airway and provide mechanical ventilation with sedation (and, if needed, give fresh frozen plasma to supply functional cholinesterase) until the succinylcholine is metabolized and spontaneous respiration returns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RECQL4",
    "long_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome type II is most often caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the RECQL4 gene. RECQL4 encodes a DNA helicase of the RecQ family and is located on chromosome 8 at band q24.3, so mutations in RECQL4 on chromosome 8 are the established genetic basis of this form of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kosovo",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic surveillance data show that Kosovo experienced a prolonged and sizable tularemia outbreak, with hundreds of human cases recorded during 1984–2012. The zoonotic agent Francisella tularensis circulated widely in small-mammal and rodent populations after the Balkan conflicts, leading to repeated human infections and making Kosovo one of Europe’s most affected areas in that period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III hypersensitivity and chills",
    "long_answer": "Farmer’s lung is a classic example of a Type III (immune-complex–mediated) hypersensitivity reaction triggered by inhaled mould antigens from hay. In its acute phase it produces systemic manifestations such as chills/rigors, the same shivering sensation that commonly accompanies the onset of a febrile (fever) response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ASAH1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ASAH1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme acid ceramidase, cause both Farber lipogranulomatosis (Farber disease) and the distinct phenotype of spinal muscular atrophy with progressive myoclonic epilepsy (SMA-PME). Loss-of-function variants in ASAH1 reduce acid ceramidase activity, leading to toxic ceramide accumulation that underlies the pathophysiology of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylmalonic acidemia",
    "long_answer": "Methylmalonic acidemia is an organic acidemia in which deficiency of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (or its cobalamin cofactor) causes accumulation of methylmalonic and related organic acids. Affected infants present with recurrent vomiting, lethargy and metabolic acidosis. Diagnosis is confirmed by gas-chromatography/mass-spectrometry analysis of a urine sample showing markedly elevated methylmalonic and other organic acids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skeletal muscle",
    "long_answer": "Several metabolic myopathies—such as glycogen-storage diseases and fatty-acid oxidation defects—are inborn errors of metabolism that predominantly impair skeletal muscle, leading to exercise intolerance, muscle weakness or rhabdomyolysis. Hence, skeletal muscle tissue is the type affected by these inherited metabolic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clarithromycin",
    "long_answer": "Clarithromycin, a macrolide antibiotic, strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein—the main pathways that metabolize and transport colchicine. Coadministration therefore markedly increases colchicine plasma concentrations, risking severe toxicity (e.g., myelosuppression, rhabdomyolysis). Because of this well-documented interaction, clarithromycin is contraindicated or requires extreme caution when a patient is taking colchicine for gout."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Adenylosuccinate lyase deficiency results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the ADSL gene on chromosome 22. Individuals develop the disorder only when they inherit one mutated allele from each carrier parent, while carriers with a single mutant copy are typically asymptomatic. This pattern—disease expression in homozygotes or compound heterozygotes and equal risk in males and females—defines autosomal recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "African ancestry",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary elliptocytosis is markedly more prevalent in people of African ancestry—especially West Africans and African-Americans—where carrier frequencies can approach 1 %, far higher than in other populations. This increased occurrence is thought to persist because spectrin-related membrane defects confer a selective advantage against Plasmodium falciparum malaria common in Africa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse tenosynovial giant cell tumor (formerly called pigmented villonodular synovitis) produces chronic joint swelling, pain and synovial hypertrophy that resemble inflammatory arthritis. Lacking characteristic MRI or histologic evaluation, clinicians frequently attribute these symptoms to rheumatoid arthritis, making RA one of the most common initial misdiagnoses before the correct identification of diffuse TGCT is established."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAK2 V617F mutation",
    "long_answer": "The JAK2 V617F gain-of-function mutation, present in about half of essential thrombocythemia cases, enhances JAK-STAT signaling, producing hyper-reactive platelets and a pro-thrombotic state. Carriers of this mutation have a significantly higher rate of arterial thrombotic events—including acute coronary syndrome—than ET patients without the mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Axon",
    "long_answer": "After replicating in peripheral mucocutaneous sites, herpes simplex virus enters sensory nerve endings and travels by retrograde axonal transport along the axon toward the trigeminal (and olfactory) ganglia and into the brain. Thus, the axon is the neuronal component responsible for conveying the virus to the CNS in herpes simplex encephalitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasmodium sporozoites",
    "long_answer": "When an infected female Anopheles mosquito takes a blood meal, it probes the skin and releases saliva to prevent clotting. That saliva contains Plasmodium sporozoites that have developed in the mosquito’s salivary glands. These sporozoites enter the human bloodstream and travel to the liver, initiating the malaria infection. Thus, transmission occurs through the injection of Plasmodium sporozoites during the mosquito’s bite."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absent umbilical cord",
    "long_answer": "In limb-body wall complex, prenatal ultrasound typically shows the fetus fixed to the placenta because the umbilical cord is missing or extremely short. This absent (or rudimentary) umbilical cord is regarded as a hallmark finding that helps distinguish LBWC from other ventral wall defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RARS2",
    "long_answer": "The gene RARS2 encodes mitochondrial arginyl-tRNA synthetase. Pathogenic variants in RARS2 cause pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 6. Because RARS2 is required for mitochondrial protein synthesis, its impairment disrupts mitochondrial translation and oxidative phosphorylation, linking the pontocerebellar hypoplasia phenotype directly to defective mitochondrial function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dementia with Lewy bodies",
    "long_answer": "Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) is defined neuropathologically by cortical Lewy bodies composed of α-synuclein and clinically by fluctuating cognition, parkinsonism, visual hallucinations, and prominent autonomic dysfunction (e.g., orthostatic hypotension, urinary incontinence). The coexistence of Lewy bodies and autonomic failure distinguishes DLB from other dementias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A3243G MT-TL1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "More than 80 % of MELAS cases carry an A→G transition at nucleotide 3243 of mitochondrial DNA, affecting the tRNALeu(UUR) gene MT-TL1. This A3243G point mutation impairs mitochondrial protein synthesis and oxidative phosphorylation, producing the characteristic encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes seen in MELAS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures are classically inherited in an autosomal-dominant manner. Most families carry a heterozygous pathogenic variant in potassium channel genes such as KCNQ2 or KCNQ3, and a single mutated allele passed from an affected parent is sufficient to produce the seizure phenotype, yielding dominant transmission with high penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive-behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "Nyctophobia is classified as a specific phobia. The intervention with the strongest evidence for specific phobias is cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT), particularly the exposure-based component in which the patient is gradually and systematically exposed to darkness while learning coping and cognitive restructuring techniques. Controlled trials show CBT reliably reduces avoidance and fear responses, making it the standard recommended treatment for nyctophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peritoneal adhesions",
    "long_answer": "Megaduodenum can arise when peritoneal adhesions—fibrous bands that form between adjacent organs, loops of bowel, or the abdominal wall after inflammation, surgery, or injury—externally constrict or kink the duodenum. These fibrous bands impede duodenal outflow, causing progressive dilation (megaduodenum) proximal to the obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rapid heartbeat",
    "long_answer": "People with scopophobia often experience autonomic arousal when they believe they are being watched; one of the most reported physical signs is a rapid heartbeat (palpitations). The same sympathetic surge that underlies a panic attack also produces palpitations, making an accelerated heart rate a hallmark symptom shared by both scopophobia and panic attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gaucher disease and Niemann-Pick disease",
    "long_answer": "Among the sphingolipidoses, Gaucher disease (glucocerebrosidase deficiency) and Niemann-Pick disease caused by acid sphingomyelinase deficiency both have approved recombinant enzyme replacement therapies and show a markedly higher prevalence in the Ashkenazi Jewish population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dilated cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Children with Kocher–Debre–Semelaigne syndrome (the myopathic form of severe congenital hypothyroidism) can develop dilated cardiomyopathy as a result of prolonged thyroid-hormone deficiency. Several genetic subtypes of limb–girdle muscular dystrophy—particularly those involving sarcoglycan or lamin A/C defects—also frequently manifest dilated cardiomyopathy because the same proteins are required for normal cardiac muscle integrity. Thus, dilated cardiomyopathy is a well-documented comorbid condition shared by both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced mutation rate",
    "long_answer": "Because hypogammaglobulinemia eliminates most circulating antibodies, the hepatitis C virus faces little antibody-mediated immune pressure. With this selective force removed, escape variants are not favored, so far fewer new mutations accumulate; the viral population therefore evolves much more slowly than in immunocompetent hosts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anthracite coal",
    "long_answer": "Caplan’s syndrome—rheumatoid arthritis accompanied by multiple pulmonary nodules on a background of pneumoconiosis—was first described in Welsh miners chronically exposed to anthracite (“hard”) coal dust. The immunologic lung reaction occurs most characteristically in workers mining or processing anthracite coal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Overlapping clinical features",
    "long_answer": "Both alcohol-related dementia and Alzheimer’s disease present with progressive cognitive decline, memory impairment, executive dysfunction, and cortical atrophy on imaging; because their neuropsychological profiles and structural brain changes are so alike, routine clinical assessment cannot easily separate the two conditions, making differential diagnosis difficult."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Family therapy",
    "long_answer": "When a child’s conduct disorder stems from parental rejection, neglect, or other dysfunctional family interactions, evidence-based guidelines recommend family therapy (e.g., parent-management training, systemic family interventions). By directly involving parents and caregivers, this approach targets the underlying relational problems, improves parenting skills and supervision, and reduces antisocial behaviors more effectively than child-focused treatment alone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisolone",
    "long_answer": "Current international and U.S. clinical practice guidelines (EASL 2018; AASLD 2020) advise a 28-day course of the corticosteroid prednisolone (typically 40 mg/day) for patients with severe alcoholic hepatitis (e.g., Maddrey discriminant function ≥ 32 or MELD > 20) who have no contraindications, because randomized trials and meta-analyses show it improves 28-day survival compared with placebo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRAF V600E",
    "long_answer": "Erdheim–Chester disease is a clonal histiocytic disorder in which approximately 50–60 % of cases harbor the activating BRAF V600E point mutation in the BRAF proto-oncogene. This somatic mutation can be detected by molecular analysis of lesional tissue, including bone marrow biopsy specimens, confirming its diagnostic utility in Erdheim–Chester disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris",
    "long_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune blistering disorder in which IgG auto-antibodies target desmoglein 3 (and often desmoglein 1), the cadherin-type adhesion molecules in desmosomes of keratinocytes. Loss of desmoglein-mediated cell adhesion (acantholysis) causes intra-epidermal blister formation, producing the characteristic flaccid bullae and erosions seen clinically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders (HAND) are neurological complications of HIV infection that affect cognitive, motor, and behavioral functions. Their evaluation and management fall primarily within the scope of Neurology, the specialty dedicated to diagnosing and treating disorders of the nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oseltamivir",
    "long_answer": "Oseltamivir, an oral neuraminidase inhibitor marketed as Tamiflu, is the recommended antiviral for severe or complicated influenza A and B infections. By blocking the viral neuraminidase enzyme, it prevents release of new virions from infected cells, reducing viral spread and improving clinical outcomes in hospitalized or high-risk patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Abdallat–Davis–Farrage syndrome is inherited in an autosomal-recessive manner: affected individuals are born only when both parents carry one mutated allele of the responsible gene, and the trait appears in siblings of either sex while typically skipping earlier generations. This inheritance pattern was deduced from reports of the syndrome in multiple children of consanguineous parents and the absence of vertical transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tau protein",
    "long_answer": "In Alzheimer’s disease, neuronal degeneration releases tau into the cerebrospinal fluid. Raised CSF tau (total and phosphorylated forms) is consistently linked to cortical and hippocampal volume loss; higher concentrations track faster brain atrophy and disease progression. Thus, the presence of tau protein in CSF is a biomarker of the atrophic changes characteristic of Alzheimer’s pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Egg allergy",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological surveys of U.S. children consistently show that allergy to cow’s milk is the most prevalent food allergy, and allergy to hen’s egg ranks immediately after it. Nationally representative studies conducted by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and other cohorts (e.g., NHANES, Consortium of Food Allergy Research) report egg allergy in roughly 1–2 % of children, making it the second-most common food allergy after milk and ahead of peanut, tree-nut, soy, wheat, fish, and shellfish allergies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis can cause bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia (BOOP), an inflammatory process in which granulation tissue fills the distal airways and alveoli. Clinically and radiographically BOOP mimics infectious pneumonia—patients present with cough, dyspnea, fever, and patchy pulmonary infiltrates that appear like pneumonia on chest imaging—yet it is a sterile, immune-mediated complication linked to rheumatoid disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDH1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary diffuse gastric cancer is most commonly caused by germ-line mutations in the CDH1 gene, which encodes the cell-adhesion molecule E-cadherin. Because of its central role in familial diffuse gastric cancer predisposition, CDH1 is one of the key genes being analyzed in the International Cancer Genome Consortium’s gastric cancer genome projects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Goodpasture syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ernest William Goodpasture was a pathologist who pioneered the cultivation of viruses in fertilized chicken embryos, a breakthrough that revolutionized virology and vaccine production. In 1919 he also provided the first clinical and pathological description of the autoimmune pulmonary-renal disorder now called Goodpasture syndrome (anti-glomerular basement membrane disease). Hence, the autoimmune disease first described by this pathologist is Goodpasture syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatitis B virus X protein",
    "long_answer": "The Hepatitis B virus X protein (HBx) is encoded by the X open reading frame (ORF) present in all members of the Hepadnaviridae family. Unlike the surface, core, and polymerase products that form structural or enzymatic components of the virion, HBx functions as a regulatory, multifunctional protein that modulates transcription, signaling, and viral replication; consequently, it is classified as a non-structural protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pure-tone audiometry",
    "long_answer": "Pure-tone audiometry measures hearing thresholds across frequencies, allowing clinicians to plot an audiogram that specifies both the degree (mild to profound) and the type (conductive, sensorineural, or mixed) of hearing loss. It is therefore the standard test for evaluating nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium",
    "long_answer": "In U.S. hospitals, serious infections caused by the vancomycin-resistant strain of Enterococcus faecium (designated VRE faecium) are frequently managed with linezolid because this oxazolidinone retains activity against enterococci that are resistant to vancomycin and often multiple other antibiotics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Malignant fibrous histiocytoma is no longer recognized as a distinct diagnostic category in the WHO classification of bone tumors. With improved histologic, immunohistochemical and molecular analyses, lesions previously labeled MFH are now grouped under the entity ‘undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma (UPS) of bone,’ reflecting their lack of specific line-age differentiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryoprecipitate",
    "long_answer": "Bleeding episodes in congenital afibrinogenemia are commonly treated with cryoprecipitate transfusion. Cryoprecipitate is the cold-insoluble fraction of plasma that is rich in fibrinogen and also carries other clotting factors such as factor VIII, factor XIII and von Willebrand factor, thereby replenishing the missing fibrinogen while supplying additional pro-coagulant proteins to control hemorrhage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis is a seronegative spondyloarthritis strongly associated with the HLA-B27 allele. It characteristically produces inflammation at entheses (enthesitis, e.g., at the Achilles tendon and sacroiliac ligaments) and frequently manifests extra-articular features such as acute anterior uveitis. The concurrence of HLA-B27 positivity, enthesitis, and uveitis is classic for ankylosing spondylitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "B/Yamagata lineage",
    "long_answer": "For the 2024–2025 influenza season, WHO and national health agencies revised the vaccine composition and eliminated the influenza B/Yamagata lineage component, leaving only the A(H1N1)pdm09, A(H3N2), and B/Victoria lineage viruses. The B/Yamagata lineage has not been detected in global surveillance for several years, so it was removed from seasonal flu vaccine recommendations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic ankle instability",
    "long_answer": "After an ankle sprain, the ligaments (especially the anterior talofibular and calcaneofibular) may heal in a lengthened, lax state, leaving the joint mechanically and functionally unstable. This chronic ankle instability is the commonest long-term disability reported after an ankle sprain. Joint instability is likewise a recognised sequel of developmental (congenital) hip dysplasia, where abnormal acetabular morphology permits recurrent subluxation or dislocation of the femoral head. Thus, persistent instability is the shared and most frequent residual problem in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jarcho-Levin syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Spondylocostal dysostosis is classically referred to as Jarcho-Levin syndrome, a rare genetic disorder characterized by abnormal segmentation of the vertebrae and malformation of the ribs leading to a short trunk and thoracic insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maurice John Parsonage and William Aldren Turner",
    "long_answer": "Parsonage–Turner syndrome (acute brachial neuritis) is named after the two British neurologists who first gave a detailed clinical description of the disorder in a 1948 Lancet paper: Maurice John Parsonage (1915–1989) and William Aldren Turner (1864–1945). Their work on ‘neuralgic amyotrophy’ linked the sudden onset of shoulder pain with subsequent patchy paralysis of the brachial plexus, and the eponym Parsonage–Turner has been used ever since to honor their contribution."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ADGRG1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ADGRG1 gene (also called GPR56) cause bilateral frontoparietal polymicrogyria. Beyond its role in cortical development, ADGRG1 is expressed on oligodendrocyte precursor cells and regulates their proliferation and differentiation, making it critical for proper neuronal myelination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNF-α and IL-1",
    "long_answer": "Asbestosis develops when inhaled asbestos fibers activate alveolar macrophages, which then release the pro-inflammatory cytokines tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α) and interleukin-1 (IL-1). These immune system proteins stimulate fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition in lung parenchyma, driving the progressive pulmonary fibrosis characteristic of asbestosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3R1",
    "long_answer": "SHORT syndrome is almost invariably caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PIK3R1 gene, which encodes the p85α regulatory subunit of phosphatidylinositol-3-kinase. Sequencing of PIK3R1 identifies these pathogenic variants and is therefore the definitive genetic test used to confirm a diagnosis of SHORT syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phakomatosis pigmentokeratotica",
    "long_answer": "Phakomatosis pigmentokeratotica is a rare neurocutaneous syndrome defined by the coexistence of two distinct cutaneous lesions: an organoid sebaceous (epidermal) nevus and a speckled lentiginous nevus (nevus spilus). This characteristic combination, often following mosaic lines of Blaschko, underlies the diagnosis of this specific phakomatosis, which may be accompanied by neurologic, ocular, or skeletal anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loss of appetite",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis commonly causes systemic inflammation that elevates cytokines such as TNF-α and IL-6, suppressing hypothalamic hunger signals. This leads to loss of appetite (anorexia), a frequent extra-articular symptom in RA. Reduced caloric intake from this symptom can produce unintentional weight loss and contribute to rheumatoid cachexia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHST6",
    "long_answer": "The macular corneal dystrophy that is part of Spastic ataxia-corneal dystrophy syndrome is produced by pathogenic mutations in the CHST6 gene, which encodes corneal N-acetyl-glucosamine-6-sulfotransferase; loss of this enzyme impairs keratan-sulfate sulfation, leading to the typical macular corneal stromal deposits seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Gerodermia osteodysplastica is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the GORAB gene (also called SCYL1BP1), which is located on chromosome 1q24. There is no established linkage of this disorder to chromosome 7. Therefore, saying that chromosome 7 is associated with Gerodermia osteodysplastica is not consistent with current genetic evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenoxybenzamine",
    "long_answer": "Phenoxybenzamine, an irreversible non-selective alpha-adrenoceptor antagonist, is the standard pre-operative medication used to block alpha receptors in patients with pheochromocytoma. By preventing catecholamine-induced vasoconstriction, it stabilises blood pressure and reduces the risk of intra-operative hypertensive crises."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paget disease of bone",
    "long_answer": "Paget disease of bone (osteitis deformans) is characterized by disordered bone remodeling that leads to focal bony overgrowth and cortical thickening. When the skull is affected, expansion of cranial bones increases head circumference—patients may notice that their hats no longer fit—and the thickened bone can cause headaches and other cranial nerve symptoms. These clinical features make Paget disease the classic cause of increased hat size and skull-related headaches."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type I interferon response",
    "long_answer": "Experimental and clinical studies show that induction of type I interferons (IFN-α/β) and the downstream interferon-stimulated gene program is essential for controlling chikungunya virus replication. Mice deficient in the type I IFN receptor or signaling develop overwhelming viremia and severe disease, whereas early IFN-α/β production restricts viral spread and promotes recovery, demonstrating that the type I interferon response is a key component of host defense against chikungunya infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eczema",
    "long_answer": "Intense pruritus from pediculosis pubis often causes repeated scratching. Continuous mechanical trauma to the skin disrupts the epidermal barrier and triggers an inflammatory response, leading to an eczematous dermatitis (eczema) in the affected area."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ezetimibe",
    "long_answer": "Ezetimibe is added when maximally tolerated statin therapy fails to achieve adequate LDL-cholesterol reduction in familial hypercholesterolemia. By inhibiting the NPC1L1 transporter in the intestinal brush border, it decreases dietary and biliary cholesterol absorption, providing an additional 15–25 % LDL-C lowering on top of statins and improving lipid control in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is an imprinting-related overgrowth disorder in which dysregulated IGF2 and other growth genes cause macrosomia, organomegaly and neonatal hyperinsulinism leading to hypoglycaemia. Children with this syndrome have a markedly elevated risk of embryonal tumours such as Wilms tumour and hepatoblastoma, linking congenital hyperinsulinism with overgrowth and childhood cancer predisposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karyotyping",
    "long_answer": "Pentasomy X is a numerical chromosomal abnormality (49,XXXXX). A standard cytogenetic karyotype allows direct visualization and counting of all chromosomes in metaphase spreads, thereby confirming the presence of five X chromosomes and establishing the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Keratomalacia is a degenerative softening and ulceration of the cornea that occurs in severe vitamin A deficiency. Because it primarily affects the cornea and other ocular tissues, management—including corneal protection, topical therapy, and surgical evaluation—is handled by specialists in ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP1B1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Primary congenital glaucoma is most commonly linked to loss-of-function mutations in the CYP1B1 gene, which encodes the cytochrome P450 1B1 enzyme. These mutations impair normal development of the trabecular meshwork and anterior chamber angle, leading to elevated intraocular pressure characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RBP4",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RBP4 gene, which encodes serum retinol-binding protein 4, can cause microphthalmia/anophthalmia. Because RBP4 is required to deliver vitamin A (retinol) from the maternal circulation to the embryo, a mother who is vitamin A–deficient provides even less retinol to a fetus carrying an RBP4 mutation. This gene–environment interaction worsens retinoic-acid deprivation during early eye development, increasing the likelihood and severity of anophthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Prader–Willi syndrome is a genomic imprinting disorder caused most often by deletion of the paternally derived 15q11-q13 region. Core clinical features include neonatal hypotonia, poor sucking that evolves into hyperphagia, short stature (from growth-hormone deficiency), characteristic facies, hypogonadism, and mild–moderate learning difficulties. Thus, the syndrome matches the triad of short stature, learning disability, and hypotonia described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genu varum",
    "long_answer": "Phosphate diabetes (familial hypophosphatemic or vitamin-D-resistant rickets) causes chronic renal phosphate wasting, leading to defective mineralization of growing long bones. The result is bowing of the lower limbs, most characteristically the knees becoming widely separated—clinically termed genu varum (bow legs)—which is the deformity most commonly associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncology",
    "long_answer": "Polymorphous low-grade adenocarcinoma is a malignant tumour of the salivary glands; as a cancer, its diagnosis, treatment planning (surgery, radiotherapy), and follow-up fall under the domain of oncology, the medical specialty dedicated to the management of neoplastic diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rifampicin",
    "long_answer": "Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis often causes severe pruritus because accumulated bile acids and other pruritogens are not excreted. Rifampicin – an antibiotic of the rifamycin class – is routinely used to relieve cholestatic itch: it induces hepatic microsomal enzymes and up-regulates transporters such as the bile-salt export pump, thereby enhancing detoxification and excretion of pruritogenic substances. Clinical guidelines list rifampicin as an effective pharmacologic option for pruritus in PFIC and other cholestatic liver diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-Hydroxytryptophan",
    "long_answer": "Dihydropteridine reductase deficiency prevents recycling of tetrahydrobiopterin, the co-factor for tryptophan hydroxylase, so the conversion of tryptophan to 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP) and thus serotonin is impaired. Administering 5-hydroxytryptophan bypasses this blocked enzymatic step, replenishing central serotonin levels and correcting the serotonin deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Lesch–Nyhan syndrome is characterized by severe neurological manifestations such as dystonia, choreoathetosis, spasticity, and self-injurious behavior. The evaluation and management of these motor and behavioral complications are the domain of neurology, the medical specialty focused on disorders of the nervous system. Neurologists coordinate pharmacologic and supportive therapies aimed at alleviating the syndrome’s central-nervous-system symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Depressed mood",
    "long_answer": "In DSM-5, nicotine withdrawal is characterized by several symptoms, one of which is ‘depressed mood.’ The same term—persistent depressed mood—is a cardinal diagnostic criterion for depressive disorders (e.g., major depressive disorder). Thus, ‘depressed mood’ is the symptom common to both nicotine withdrawal and depressive disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "All reported cases of Morse–Rawnsley–Sargent syndrome have occurred in siblings born to unaffected, non-consanguineous parents, with no vertical transmission and equal involvement of males and females. This segregation pattern is the hallmark of autosomal recessive inheritance, indicating that two carrier parents each contribute one mutated allele for the disorder to manifest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gnathostoma",
    "long_answer": "Migratory subcutaneous swellings known as “creeping disease” or cutaneous larva migrans in humans are classically produced by the wandering third-stage larvae of nematodes belonging to the genus Gnathostoma (order Spirurida), most notably Gnathostoma spinigerum. These larvae migrate through the skin, creating serpiginous tracks and eosinophilic inflammation characteristic of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bitemporal hemianopsia",
    "long_answer": "A pituitary tumor expanding upward can compress the optic chiasm, where fibers from the nasal retina of both eyes decussate. This selective compression causes loss of peripheral (temporal) visual fields in both eyes, producing the classic visual defect known as bitemporal hemianopsia, a hallmark symptom suggesting a pituitary mass impinging on the optic nerve/chiasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute myelomonocytic leukemia",
    "long_answer": "In the French-American-British (FAB) system for classifying acute myeloid leukemia (AML), the designation “M4” corresponds to acute myelomonocytic leukemia, characterized by proliferating myeloid precursors that show both granulocytic and monocytic differentiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcolemma",
    "long_answer": "The dystrophin-glycoprotein-associated complex is a membrane-spanning protein assembly located in the sarcolemma of skeletal muscle fibres. When components of this sarcolemmal complex are dysfunctional, the linkage between the cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix is lost, destabilising the muscle-cell membrane. This membrane instability leads to muscle weakness and fetal akinesia, which manifest clinically as joint contractures (arthrogryposis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin",
    "long_answer": "In many cases of acquired hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, serum prolactin levels are abnormally high. Hyperprolactinemia suppresses hypothalamic neurons that secrete gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), leading to reduced pituitary secretion of LH and FSH and consequent gonadal failure. Thus, prolactin is the hormone both commonly elevated and mechanistically responsible for inhibiting GnRH release in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellar Purkinje-cell neurotoxicity",
    "long_answer": "Lead accumulates in the cerebellum, where it generates oxidative stress, interferes with calcium-dependent signaling, and depresses GABAergic transmission. These insults injure or kill Purkinje cells, the main inhibitory neurons that regulate cerebellothalamocortical output; their loss produces rhythmic oscillatory activity that clinically appears as essential tremor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wernicke aphasia",
    "long_answer": "The posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus, bordering the Sylvian (lateral) fissure, is known as Wernicke’s area. Lesions in this temporal-lobe region disrupt language comprehension while sparing fluent speech production, producing the classic fluent or receptive language deficit called Wernicke aphasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-neuraminidase",
    "long_answer": "Mucolipidosis type I (sialidosis) is caused by a hereditary deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-neuraminidase (also called sialidase). Lack of this enzyme leads to intralysosomal accumulation of sialylated glycoproteins in neurons and retinal ganglion cells, producing the characteristic cherry-red macular spots seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR2",
    "long_answer": "The majority of catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia cases—around 50–60%—result from pathogenic variants in the RYR2 gene, which encodes the cardiac ryanodine receptor responsible for calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Mutant RYR2 channels become hypersensitive to catecholamine stimulation, producing diastolic calcium leak and the bidirectional or polymorphic ventricular tachyarrhythmias characteristic of CPVT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Pili torti is a structural hair-shaft disorder characterized by flattened, irregularly twisted hairs. Disorders of hair growth and hair shaft morphology are evaluated with scalp and skin examination, dermoscopy, and sometimes light microscopy—all core competencies of dermatologists. Therefore, diagnosis and management of pili torti fall within the medical specialty of dermatology, often by a dermatologist or subspecialist trichologist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syringocystadenoma papilliferum",
    "long_answer": "Syringocystadenoma papilliferum is a benign adnexal tumor that arises from apocrine or eccrine sweat glands. Because it forms papillary and cystic structures similar to those seen in papillary hidradenoma, it can be misdiagnosed as the latter. Histologically, syringocystadenoma papilliferum shows characteristic papillary projections lined by two layers of epithelium with a dense plasma-cell infiltrate in the stroma, confirming its origin as a non-malignant sweat-gland neoplasm rather than papillary hidradenoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "In cardiology the term “ventricular aneurysm” almost always refers to an out-pouching of the ventricular wall that develops after transmural myocardial infarction, trauma, infection, or cardiac surgery. Because it arises secondarily to damage that occurs after birth, it is classified as an acquired lesion, not as a congenital heart defect.  

There is a very rare entity called a congenital ventricular aneurysm or diverticulum that is present at birth, but these cases are exceptional and are specifically labeled “congenital ventricular aneurysm.” In routine medical usage, however, when clinicians speak of a ventricular aneurysm they are referring to the far more common acquired form, which is not grouped with the spectrum of congenital heart diseases.  

Therefore, ventricular aneurysms are not generally considered congenital heart defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interventricular foramina",
    "long_answer": "Each cerebral hemisphere’s lateral ventricle communicates with the midline third ventricle through a narrow channel called an interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro). These paired openings allow cerebrospinal fluid produced in the lateral ventricles to flow into the third ventricle, maintaining normal CSF circulation within the ventricular system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiamine deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Lack of thiamine (vitamin B1) is a well-known cause of Wernicke’s encephalopathy, whose ocular hallmark is ophthalmoparesis/ophthalmoplegia with nystagmus. The same nutritional deficiency can damage the optic nerves, producing a painless, symmetric optic neuropathy often termed nutritional optic neuropathy. Therefore, thiamine deficiency is a single vitamin deficit capable of manifesting both optic neuropathy and ophthalmoparesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OCLN",
    "long_answer": "The OCLN gene, which encodes the tight-junction protein occludin, maps to chromosome 5q13.2. Biallelic pathogenic variants in OCLN have been shown to produce the interferon-mediated encephalopathy classified as Aicardi–Goutières syndrome type 12, characterised by neuroinflammation, intracranial calcifications and elevated type I interferon signatures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sigmoid colon",
    "long_answer": "In patients with an imperforate anus, the standard initial surgical step is creation of a divided colostomy in the sigmoid segment of the large intestine. This diversion through the sigmoid colon decompresses the distal bowel and keeps the future operative field clean, making it the part of the large intestine specifically involved in the surgical management of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aneuploidy",
    "long_answer": "If a sperm carries an abnormal number of chromosomes (owing to meiotic nondisjunction) and fertilizes a normal egg, the resulting zygote gains or loses one or more chromosomes. This incorrect chromosome count is called aneuploidy, producing conditions such as trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) or monosomy X (Turner syndrome)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Developmental delay",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with glycine encephalopathy (non-ketotic hyperglycinemia) frequently show global developmental delay—slow acquisition of cognitive, motor and language milestones. Developmental delay is also a defining neuro-behavioral feature of intellectual disability, where reduced intellectual functioning manifests as delayed attainment of age-appropriate skills. Thus, developmental delay is the shared neurological symptom in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ulcerative colitis",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis is strongly associated with inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis; up to 70% of PSC patients have or will develop it. Colonoscopy is therefore recommended to detect or monitor ulcerative colitis (and the increased colorectal cancer risk it confers) early."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Madeira",
    "long_answer": "Madeira, an autonomous Portuguese archipelago famous worldwide for its fortified Madeira wine, was the first European territory to record locally mosquito-borne transmission of Zika virus, confirming autochthonous cases on the island."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberculosis",
    "long_answer": "Current WHO and UNAIDS data show that tuberculosis remains the foremost killer of people living with HIV. Immunosuppression caused by HIV greatly increases susceptibility to Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection and progression to active disease; in 2023 TB accounted for roughly one-third of all HIV-related deaths worldwide, making it the leading cause of death in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis is most often caused by missense mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel α-subunit (NaV1.4). These gain-of-function mutations alter channel inactivation, leading to sustained sodium influx, membrane depolarization, and episodic muscle weakness precipitated by elevated extracellular potassium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Speech-language pathologist",
    "long_answer": "A speech-language pathologist (speech therapist) is trained to evaluate and treat the dysarthria, hypophonia, and dysphagia that commonly arise in Parkinson’s disease. Through targeted voice exercises (e.g., Lee Silverman Voice Treatment) and swallowing strategies, they help maintain intelligible speech and safe, efficient swallowing, thereby improving communication and reducing aspiration risk in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Canada",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological surveys compiled by the Multiple Sclerosis International Federation’s Atlas of MS and other peer-reviewed studies consistently show that Canada has the world’s highest national prevalence of multiple sclerosis, estimated at roughly 290 cases per 100,000 population. This exceptionally high rate is attributed to a combination of genetic susceptibility in Northern European–descended populations and environmental factors linked to high-latitude regions, such as reduced lifetime UV-B exposure and resultant vitamin D insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Sugarman syndrome is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern: two pathogenic alleles (one from each carrier parent) are required for the condition to manifest. This places it among the class of autosomal recessive genetic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "End-stage renal disease",
    "long_answer": "In some patients with Bartter syndrome, chronic salt-wasting, hypokalemia, and nephrocalcinosis progressively damage renal tissue, eventually causing end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Once ESRD develops, kidney function is lost and patients often need renal replacement therapy—dialysis or kidney transplantation—to survive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In autosomal-dominant Hyper-IgE syndrome, also called Job syndrome, defective STAT3 signaling impairs neutrophil chemotaxis and Th17 responses. As a result, patients develop recurrent Staphylococcus aureus skin infections that form ‘cold’ abscesses—abscesses lacking the usual warmth, redness and pain—despite active bacterial infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric surgery",
    "long_answer": "Sirenomelia (mermaid syndrome) is a rare congenital malformation in which the lower limbs are fused and multiple genitourinary and gastrointestinal defects are present. Management requires complex, staged corrective operations and intensive peri-operative care, tasks performed by specialists trained in surgical treatment of congenital anomalies in children—namely pediatric surgeons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptococcus pyogenes",
    "long_answer": "Erysipelas is classically caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, a Group A β-haemolytic streptococcus. Beyond the sharply demarcated skin infection, the bacterium’s pyrogenic exotoxins and the resulting systemic inflammatory response often produce constitutional symptoms such as fever, chills, and headache, making headache a well-recognized secondary symptom of S. pyogenes erysipelas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Small cell lung carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Hemeralopia (day-blindness) occurs in the paraneoplastic syndrome known as cancer-associated retinopathy. The syndrome is most frequently linked to small cell lung carcinoma, whose onconeural antibodies (e.g., anti-recoverin) damage cone photoreceptors, producing photophobia and hemeralopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical luxation",
    "long_answer": "Clinical evidence shows that carefully luxating the ankylosed tooth to fracture the bony fusion re-establishes periodontal-ligament function. Once the ankylosis is broken, normal vertical and mesio-distal tooth movement resumes, allowing orthodontic forces to regain the space lost by tipping of adjacent teeth and permitting the delayed eruption of the successor. Repeated in many orthodontic case reports, surgical luxation is therefore the treatment confirmed to recover lost space and facilitate eruption in tooth ankylosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aspirin",
    "long_answer": "Sneddon's syndrome is characterized by recurrent cerebral ischemic events, and long-term antiplatelet therapy with aspirin is routinely prescribed to reduce platelet aggregation and vascular thrombosis. The same antiplatelet effect of daily low-dose aspirin is the standard secondary-prevention strategy after a transient ischemic attack (TIA), where it lowers the risk of progression to a major ischemic stroke by inhibiting thromboxane-mediated platelet activation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cat eye syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cat eye syndrome is classically caused by a supernumerary marker chromosome derived from chromosome 22 that produces partial tetrasomy of 22p and 22q11.2. One of its major clinical features is anal atresia/imperforate anus, linking the condition to the question’s phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rasmussen encephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Rasmussen encephalitis is a rare pediatric neurological disorder in which chronic, immune-mediated inflammation is confined to one cerebral hemisphere. The resulting unilateral cortical damage leads to frequent, severe focal seizures (often epilepsia partialis continua) and progressive loss of motor and cognitive functions on the opposite side of the body, matching the clinical features described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2",
    "long_answer": "The GJB2 gene, encoding the gap-junction protein connexin 26, is situated on human chromosome 13q12. Pathogenic variants in GJB2 are a well-established cause of autosomal-dominant palmoplantar keratoderma (often accompanied by sensorineural deafness), linking this chromosome 13 gene directly to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Empty sella syndrome is identified by visualizing the herniation of cerebrospinal fluid into the sella turcica and the resultant flattening of the pituitary gland. High-resolution magnetic resonance imaging of the sellar and parasellar region is the modality of choice because it provides superior soft-tissue contrast, clearly delineating CSF, pituitary tissue, and surrounding structures, thereby confirming the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Depo-Provera",
    "long_answer": "Depo-Provera (injectable medroxyprogesterone acetate) is a progestin-only contraceptive whose androgen-like hormonal activity can shift hair follicles into the telogen (resting) phase, leading to diffuse scalp hair shedding or worsening of androgenetic alopecia in susceptible women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysosomes",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II) results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme acid α-glucosidase. Without this enzyme, glycogen cannot be degraded inside lysosomes, leading to its progressive accumulation within these organelles in muscle and other tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSV DNA polymerase",
    "long_answer": "Molecular studies of skin lesions in herpes simplex virus-associated erythema multiforme show that keratinocytes contain DNA sequences encoding the HSV DNA polymerase enzyme (the UL30 gene). Detection of this viral polymerase gene, rather than full infectious virus, supports the idea that viral DNA persists in keratinocytes and triggers the immune reaction characteristic of erythema multiforme."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "CHIME (coloboma–heart defects–ichthyosiform dermatosis–mental retardation–ear anomalies) syndrome is caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in the PIGL gene, and affected individuals inherit one pathogenic allele from each parent. The requirement for two mutated copies defines an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPR143",
    "long_answer": "Ocular albinism with late-onset sensorineural deafness (OASD) is an X-linked disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the GPR143 gene (formerly called the OA1 gene) on Xp22. Mutations in GPR143 disrupt the melanosome-associated G-protein-coupled receptor required for normal pigment biogenesis in the eye and inner ear, leading to the characteristic ocular albinism and the progressive, late-onset sensorineural hearing loss seen in OASD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diplopia",
    "long_answer": "A pituitary adenoma can expand laterally and compress cranial nerves III, IV, or VI within the cavernous sinus. Impaired function of these ocular motor nerves leads to misalignment of the eyes, producing the clinical symptom of double vision (diplopia); its presence in a patient with pituitary disease points to tumor extension and is a warning sign accompanying hypopituitarism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elosulfase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Morquio syndrome Type A (MPS IVA) is caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme N-acetyl-galactosamine-6-sulfatase. The approved disease-specific treatment is enzyme-replacement therapy with elosulfase alfa (brand name Vimizim), a recombinant form of this enzyme that is infused intravenously to restore enzymatic activity and reduce glycosaminoglycan accumulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia results from an inherited deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT). Without GALT, dietary galactose cannot be efficiently converted to UDP-galactose; instead, galactose and galactose-1-phosphate accumulate in blood and tissues, producing the characteristic galactosemia and its clinical manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Cardiocranial syndrome, Pfeiffer type has been reported predominantly in siblings born to unaffected, often consanguineous parents, with equal incidence in males and females. This recurrence pattern without vertical transmission points to an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PDX1 (IPF1)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the pancreatic and duodenal homeobox-1 gene, PDX1 (also called IPF1), can present as permanent neonatal diabetes. When both alleles are severely affected, PDX1 mutations prevent pancreatic development, resulting in complete pancreatic agenesis. Hence the same PDX1 gene defect is recognized as a cause of both neonatal diabetes and pancreatic agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "60 years",
    "long_answer": "Under the NHS Bowel Cancer Screening Programme in England, people are routinely invited for colorectal (bowel) cancer screening from the age of 60 up to 74 years, with a faecal immunochemical test offered every two years. Therefore, the recommended starting age for colorectal cancer screening according to NHS England is 60 years."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxoplasma-specific IgM assay",
    "long_answer": "Because maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta but IgM antibodies do not, detection of Toxoplasma gondii–specific IgM in a newborn’s serum proves the infant has produced the antibody itself. This confirms in utero infection and is therefore considered the definitive laboratory diagnosis of congenital toxoplasmosis in neonates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Decreases renal uric acid excretion",
    "long_answer": "During ketosis, elevated ketone bodies (acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate) compete with urate for renal tubular secretion. This competition reduces the kidneys’ ability to clear uric acid, thereby lowering its excretion and predisposing to transient hyperuricemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketoconazole",
    "long_answer": "Ketoconazole is an imidazole antifungal frequently formulated as a shampoo or cream for seborrhoeic dermatitis. When given orally, however, it carries a well-documented risk of serious hepatotoxicity, including fulminant hepatic failure, which has led regulatory agencies (e.g., FDA, EMA) to restrict or withdraw its systemic use. This characteristic makes ketoconazole the antifungal agent in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal artery",
    "long_answer": "Definitive diagnosis of giant cell arteritis is made by performing a temporal artery biopsy; histology of the superficial temporal artery shows granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells in the vessel wall."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Mandibuloacral dysplasia is produced by biallelic (homo- or compound heterozygous) pathogenic variants in genes such as LMNA or ZMPSTE24. Because two mutant alleles—one from each parent—are required for the disorder to manifest, the disease is transmitted in an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Titanium",
    "long_answer": "In Jeune syndrome (asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy), surgeons implant a Vertical Expandable Prosthetic Titanium Rib (VEPTR) to widen the rigid, hypoplastic thorax and improve respiratory capacity. The device is manufactured from titanium because this metal is light, strong, corrosion-resistant and highly biocompatible, making it ideal for chest wall reconstruction in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD18 (β2 integrin)",
    "long_answer": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 (LAD-1) is caused by mutations in the ITGB2 gene that encodes the β2 integrin subunit, known as CD18. Loss or marked reduction of CD18 prevents formation of the functional β2 integrins (e.g., LFA-1, Mac-1) required for leukocyte adhesion to endothelial ICAMs, leading to the characteristic immunodeficiency of LAD-1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anaplastic ependymoma",
    "long_answer": "Among the World Health Organization-classified ependymal tumors, anaplastic ependymoma (WHO grade III) is the form considered malignant. It shows high cellularity, brisk mitotic activity, microvascular proliferation and necrosis, correlating with more aggressive clinical behavior and poorer prognosis compared with lower-grade (grade I myxopapillary or subependymoma, grade II classic) ependymomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Excess growth hormone and IGF-1 in childhood gigantism produce cardiovascular changes—salt and water retention, increased cardiac output, and vascular smooth-muscle hypertrophy—that frequently manifest as systemic arterial hypertension. Elevated blood pressure is one of the most consistently reported medical complications in pediatric patients with gigantism and contributes significantly to their long-term morbidity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infantile sialic acid storage disease",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC17A5 gene, which encodes the lysosomal sialic-acid transporter sialin, prevent normal efflux of free sialic acid from lysosomes. The resulting buildup of sialic acid causes infantile sialic acid storage disease, a lysosomal storage disorder presenting in early infancy with hypotonia, developmental delay, coarse facial features, and characteristic hepatomegaly (often with splenomegaly)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholesterol",
    "long_answer": "Cortisol biosynthesis in the adrenal cortex begins with cholesterol. Cholesterol is transported into the mitochondria of zona fasciculata cells, where the enzyme cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme (desmolase, CYP11A1) converts it to pregnenolone. This initiates the steroidogenic pathway that ultimately produces cortisol. Thus, cholesterol is the obligatory precursor molecule for cortisol synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RHBDF2",
    "long_answer": "Howel–Evans syndrome (tylosis with oesophageal cancer) is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the RHBDF2 gene, which encodes the inactive rhomboid protease iRhom2. Variants in the same RHBDF2 gene can disturb epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) ligand shedding in the inner ear, leading to progressive sensorineural hearing loss; hence RHBDF2 mutations are implicated in both Howel–Evans syndrome and deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Ionizing radiation is a well-established teratogen. When a pregnant woman is exposed to sufficient doses—particularly during the critical period of brain development (about gestational weeks 8-25)—radiation can damage rapidly dividing neural precursor cells, resulting in a permanent reduction in brain volume and head size. Epidemiological studies of in-utero survivors of the Hiroshima and Nagasaki atomic bombs, as well as data from therapeutic and accidental medical exposures, consistently show a dose-related increase in microcephaly and other central nervous system deficits. The effect is considered a deterministic (non-stochastic) outcome with a threshold in the range of ~0.1–0.2 Gy and a steep rise in incidence at doses ≥0.5 Gy. Therefore, microcephaly can indeed be observed in children born to women who were exposed to significant levels of radiation during pregnancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Good (favorable) prognosis",
    "long_answer": "Mucinous cystadenocarcinoma of the pancreas, when surgically resected, carries a markedly better outcome than conventional pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, with reported 5- to 10-year survival rates around 50–60 %. Thus it is generally considered to have a good or favorable prognosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Willis-Ekbom disease",
    "long_answer": "Restless Legs Syndrome (RLS) is also known as Willis-Ekbom disease, acknowledging the early descriptions by Thomas Willis and later detailed characterization by Karl-Axel Ekbom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chrousos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Primary generalized glucocorticoid resistance, known as Chrousos syndrome, is a hereditary disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the glucocorticoid receptor (NR3C1). Impaired receptor signaling makes tissues resistant to cortisol, prompting chronic ACTH over-secretion. The excess ACTH drives adrenal hyperplasia and overproduction of adrenal androgens, resulting in clinical hyperandrogenism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyloid",
    "long_answer": "The word “amyloid” was coined by the German pathologist Rudolf Virchow in 1854. Observing the extracellular deposits that we now know are protein fibrils, he noted that they stained blue-violet with iodine—just as starch (Greek: amylon; Latin: amylum) does—and therefore named them “amyloid,” meaning “starch-like.” Although the material was later shown to be protein, the term has remained to describe the protein aggregates characteristic of amyloidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 20%",
    "long_answer": "Prospective and retrospective series of patients with chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction consistently report that roughly one-fifth of affected individuals die from complications such as malnutrition, sepsis, and intestinal failure, giving an estimated overall mortality rate of about 20 %."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX10",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SOX10 gene are known to cause Waardenburg syndrome type 2E. SOX10 encodes a transcription factor crucial for neural crest-derived cell lineages, including melanocytes and Schwann cells, and is also essential for the development and maturation of oligodendrocytes that form myelin in the central nervous system. Consequently, pathogenic SOX10 variants can lead to the pigmentary and auditory features of Waardenburg syndrome as well as defects in CNS myelination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondrial complex II deficiency is an inherited metabolic disorder; definitive diagnosis relies on specialized biochemical assays and genetic testing that are typically performed and interpreted by physicians trained in medical genetics (often pediatric/biochemical geneticists), the specialty responsible for diagnosing mitochondrial diseases in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Angiolipomas are benign tumors composed of adipose tissue and proliferating blood vessels that arise in the skin or subcutaneous tissue. Because they present as cutaneous or subcutaneous nodules, their evaluation (clinical examination, possible biopsy) and definitive management (often surgical excision) usually fall within the expertise of dermatology, the medical specialty dedicated to diseases of the skin and its appendages."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "T-lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma is a malignant neoplasm of immature T-cell precursors that arises in the bone marrow and lymphoid tissues; disorders of the blood-forming and lymphatic systems are managed by hematologists (often hematologist-oncologists), who direct diagnosis, chemotherapy, and stem-cell transplantation for this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "50%",
    "long_answer": "The so-called Gorlin sign—the ability to extend the tongue to touch the tip of the nose—is present in roughly one half of individuals with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (about 50 %), compared with only ~10 % of the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycerate kinase",
    "long_answer": "D-glyceric aciduria results from deficiency of glycerate kinase (encoded by GLYCTK). This cytosolic enzyme normally phosphorylates D-glycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate in serine/glycine and glycerate metabolism. When glycerate kinase activity is absent or greatly reduced, D-glycerate cannot be further metabolized and accumulates in blood, urine, cerebrospinal fluid, and tissues, especially during catabolic states, producing the characteristic elevation of D-glyceric acid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome is a rare congenital disorder (also called Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome) in which characteristic facial dysmorphisms—long palpebral fissures with everted lower lids, arched eyebrows, prominent ears—are hallmarks. In addition to growth delay and intellectual disability, affected individuals commonly display musculoskeletal findings such as joint laxity/hypermobility stemming from connective-tissue involvement, making joint hypermobility a recognized symptom of this genetic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Warkany syndrome 2, better known as trisomy 8 (most often mosaic trisomy 8), is classically associated with a variety of cutaneous pigmentary anomalies. Numerous clinical series and reviews describe streaky or patchy areas of both hypo- and hyperpigmentation distributed along the lines of Blaschko or in a mosaic pattern. These pigmentary changes are considered one of the characteristic, although variably expressed, features of the syndrome. Consequently, the presence of hyperpigmented skin areas is a documented and accepted manifestation in individuals with Warkany syndrome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Congenital nystagmus is an involuntary oscillation of the eyes that affects visual function. Evaluation, optical correction, and possible surgical or pharmacologic management of nystagmus are the domain of ophthalmologists—especially pediatric or neuro-ophthalmology subspecialists. Therefore, when Karsch–Neugebauer syndrome presents with congenital nystagmus, the medical specialty primarily responsible for treatment is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "F",
    "long_answer": "In ICD-10, both Disinhibited Attachment Disorder (coded F94.2) and Hospitalism/Reactive Attachment Disorder (coded F94.1) fall under Chapter V, the ‘F’ category for Mental and Behavioural Disorders (codes F00–F99)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medullary thyroid carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Medullary thyroid carcinoma is the thyroid cancer linked to multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A, a syndrome that also causes parathyroid hyperplasia. The hyperplastic parathyroids secrete excess parathyroid hormone, leading to markedly elevated serum PTH levels in patients with this form of thyroid cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Articaine",
    "long_answer": "Patients who have hypokalemic sensory overstimulation frequently experience inadequate pulpal anesthesia with common agents such as lidocaine or mepivacaine. Clinical case series have shown that switching to the thiophene-containing amide local anesthetic articaine (e.g., 4 % articaine with epinephrine) reliably achieves effective anesthesia for dental procedures in these individuals, making articaine the recommended local anesthetic for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is classically an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by mutations in sarcomeric protein genes (e.g., β-myosin heavy chain, myosin-binding protein C). Because most cases arise from these heritable mutations, HCM is considered the cardiomyopathy that is usually inherited."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RARS2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in RARS2, which encodes the mitochondrial arginyl-tRNA synthetase, cause pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 6. The defective enzyme disrupts mitochondrial protein synthesis and oxidative phosphorylation, so patients typically show elevated blood or CSF lactate levels—a hallmark of mitochondrial dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fasciculations",
    "long_answer": "Fasciculations are brief, spontaneous, involuntary contractions of small groups of muscle fibers that produce visible twitching under the skin. They arise from hyper-excitable or damaged lower motor neurons in peripheral neuropathy, making them a characteristic symptom of many peripheral nerve disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary cardiac pacemaker",
    "long_answer": "The sinoatrial (SA) node is the heart’s primary pacemaker; it initiates the electrical impulses that depolarise the right (and left) atrium. In right atrial enlargement, the atrial muscle mass increases, producing larger-amplitude P waves on ECG, but the SA node’s fundamental role—generating the rhythmic impulses that start each heartbeat—remains unchanged."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome is an autosomal-recessive limb-malformation disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WNT7A gene. Its core skeletal findings include fibular aplasia or severe hypoplasia together with additional digital anomalies such as brachydactyly, oligodactyly and polydactyly, making it the well-established genetic condition that combines fibular aplasia with brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electrocardiogram",
    "long_answer": "Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is a form of congenital long-QT syndrome. The characteristic diagnostic finding is a markedly prolonged QT interval on the heart’s electrical tracing, which is detected non-invasively by a standard surface electrocardiogram (ECG)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Romantic drama",
    "long_answer": "Several films, notably the Japanese movie “A Song to the Sun” (Taiyō no Uta, 2006) and its U-S. remake “Midnight Sun” (2018), depict a teenager with xeroderma pigmentosum—an inherited DNA-repair defect that makes ordinary sunlight lethal. Both titles are categorized as romantic drama movies, so the film genre featuring a character with xeroderma pigmentosum is the romantic drama genre."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Erosive pustular dermatitis of the scalp is an uncommon inflammatory skin disorder characterized by erosions, pustules, crusting, and eventual scarring alopecia. Because it is a cutaneous disease requiring expertise in diagnosis and management of skin and scalp conditions, treatment is typically overseen by a dermatologist, the medical specialist trained in disorders of the skin, hair, and nails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "Gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging is the preferred imaging modality for detecting leukemic infiltration of the central nervous system; it can demonstrate meningeal thickening or enhancement and parenchymal lesions produced by circulating leukemic cells, confirming CNS spread."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Rett syndrome is almost always caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MECP2 gene, which encodes the methyl-CpG-binding protein 2 that regulates chromatin and gene expression in neurons. MECP2-knockout mice replicate the neurological features of Rett syndrome, confirming the causal role of MECP2 mutations in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous larva migrans is a parasitic skin infestation by hookworm larvae that manifests as pruritic, serpiginous tracks in the epidermis. Because it is primarily a dermatologic condition diagnosed and managed through skin examination and topical or systemic anti-helminthic therapy, patients are most commonly evaluated and treated by dermatologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "21-hydroxylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "In 46,XX infants, the leading cause of virilized or ambiguous external genitalia is congenital adrenal hyperplasia caused by autosomal-recessive deficiency of the steroid-biosynthetic enzyme 21-hydroxylase. The enzyme defect blocks cortisol (and often aldosterone) synthesis, diverting adrenal precursors into excess androgen production, which masculinizes the external genitalia while the karyotype and internal reproductive organs remain female. This accounts for the vast majority of ambiguous genitalia cases in otherwise genotypically normal female newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Porokeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the mevalonate-pathway enzymes phosphomevalonate kinase (PMVK) and mevalonate diphosphate decarboxylase (MVD) are a recognised cause of porokeratosis, a keratinisation disorder that presents clinically as annular papules and plaques with a raised, keratotic rim (cornoid lamella)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Riboflavin (vitamin B2)",
    "long_answer": "High-dose riboflavin (400 mg/day) is an evidence-based prophylactic treatment that lowers the frequency of migraine attacks in adults. Biochemically, riboflavin is converted to the coenzymes FMN and FAD, which are required for converting tryptophan to niacin, pyridoxine to its active form (pyridoxal-5′-phosphate, vitamin B6), and for folate interconversions. Thus, riboflavin both reduces migraine incidence and participates in the metabolism of niacin, vitamin B6, and folate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IHH",
    "long_answer": "Angel-shaped phalango-epiphyseal dysplasia (also called acro-capitofemoral dysplasia) is produced by homozygous or compound-heterozygous mutations in the IHH gene (Indian hedgehog). Loss of normal IHH signalling disturbs chondrocyte proliferation and differentiation in the growth plate, leading to the characteristic ‘angel-shaped’ middle phalanges and generalized epiphyseal dysplasia seen on hand radiographs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythropenia",
    "long_answer": "Erythropenia (also called erythrocytopenia) is the medical term for an abnormally low number of circulating red blood cells. Because anemia is defined as any condition in which red cell mass or number is reduced, erythropenia is itself classified as a form of anemia, distinguished specifically by the quantitative decrease in erythrocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tentorium cerebelli",
    "long_answer": "The tentorium cerebelli is a horizontal fold of the dura mater that forms a rigid roof over the posterior cranial fossa. It stretches between the occipital lobes of the cerebral hemispheres above and the cerebellum below, thereby physically and functionally separating the cerebrum from the cerebellum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Van der Woude syndrome is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6). PCR-based amplification and sequencing of IRF6 exons allowed investigators to identify these causative mutations, establishing IRF6 mutation as the genetic basis of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicular lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "The chromosomal translocation t(14;18)(q32;q21) juxtaposes the BCL2 gene on chromosome 18 next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain locus on chromosome 14, causing over-expression of the anti-apoptotic BCL2 protein. This genetic hallmark is classically seen in follicular lymphoma, a germinal-center B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asexual reproduction",
    "long_answer": "In the soil, Coccidioides species grow as filamentous molds whose hyphae fragment into barrel-shaped arthroconidia; this is an asexual mode of reproduction, with no sexual stage observed in nature. The airborne arthroconidia are the infectious propagules that cause coccidioidomycosis when inhaled."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) have a markedly elevated risk of acute myeloid leukemia, particularly the megakaryoblastic subtype. The extra copy of chromosome 21 alters hematopoietic stem-cell biology and cooperates with acquired mutations (e.g., GATA1) to drive leukemogenesis, making AML up to 100-fold more common in this genetic disorder than in the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAB23",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the RAB23 gene are known to cause autosomal dominant brachydactyly type B2 and, in the homozygous or compound-heterozygous state, autosomal recessive Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II). Thus RAB23 is the shared gene underlying both brachydactyly and Carpenter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3q26.3 deletion",
    "long_answer": "A micro-deletion of chromosome 3q26.3 removes one copy of the SOX2 gene. SOX2 haplo-insufficiency is the best-known cause of bilateral anophthalmia/microphthalmia and, because SOX2 is also required for maintenance of spermatogonial stem cells, the same deletion impairs sperm development and can lead to male infertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX2 gene, a transcription factor critical for early kidney development, lead to renal hypoplasia/dysplasia in humans (e.g., renal-coloboma syndrome) and produce the same dysplastic renal phenotype in animal models such as mice, rats, and certain dog breeds. Thus, PAX2 is the gene consistently linked with renal dysplasia across both human and veterinary species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLA2G6",
    "long_answer": "Infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy (INAD) is the classic phenotype of PLA2G6-associated neurodegeneration (PLAN). Pathogenic variants in the PLA2G6 gene, which encodes group VI calcium-independent phospholipase A2, are the established cause of INAD. A characteristic clinical feature of PLA2G6 mutations is early‐onset nystagmus in addition to progressive psychomotor regression and cerebellar atrophy. Thus, mutations in PLA2G6 are linked to both infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy and nystagmus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Craniospinal irradiation",
    "long_answer": "Standard therapy for medulloblastoma includes craniospinal irradiation—radiation delivered to the entire brain and spinal axis. While effective at controlling tumor spread, this exposure damages developing neural tissue and the cochlea, leading to well-documented late effects such as global cognitive decline, impaired memory and processing speed, and sensorineural hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyst (infective stage)",
    "long_answer": "Balantidiasis is acquired by the fecal-oral ingestion of the mature cyst stage of Balantidium coli. The cysts can survive outside the host, resist gastric acidity, and excyst in the large intestine, where released trophozoites colonize and invade the mucosa to produce disease. Trophozoites themselves are not infective when swallowed because they are destroyed in the stomach, making the cyst the required form for human infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alström syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Alström syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder (ALMS1 mutations) whose clinical picture includes truncal obesity beginning in early childhood, mild intellectual disability, and subtle craniofacial dysmorphism (round face, deep-set eyes, thick lips). The ocular hallmark is an early-onset cone-rod dystrophy in which cone dysfunction predominates, producing photophobia and hemeralopia (day blindness). Thus Alström syndrome is the genetic syndrome that combines obesity, mental retardation, craniofacial abnormalities, and hemeralopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is an immune-mediated platelet disorder that requires expert evaluation of platelet counts, antibody testing, and selection of non-heparin anticoagulants (e.g., argatroban, bivalirudin). These diagnostic and therapeutic decisions fall within the domain of hematology, the specialty that manages blood and clotting disorders; therefore, patients with HIT are typically treated by hematologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benign bone tumor",
    "long_answer": "Chondroblastoma, also known as Codman’s tumor, is a rare neoplasm that arises from cartilage-producing chondroblasts in the epiphysis of long bones. Because it is locally aggressive yet lacks metastatic potential, it is categorized within orthopaedic oncology as a benign bone tumor, specifically a benign cartilage-forming tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amenorrhea",
    "long_answer": "Women with anorexia nervosa commonly develop amenorrhea—cessation of menstrual periods—because severe caloric restriction and low body fat suppress hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal activity, leading to reduced GnRH pulsatility, low LH/FSH, and hypoestrogenism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal pain",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatoblastoma often presents with non-specific gastrointestinal complaints, and abdominal pain is one of the most commonly reported symptoms in affected patients. Abdominal pain is also one of the leading chief complaints that bring people to emergency departments worldwide, making it a symptom shared by pancreatoblastoma and a frequent ED presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ranidae",
    "long_answer": "The term ranidaphobia is derived from the zoological name Ranidae, the taxonomic family that contains the “true frogs.” Thus, the specific phobia ranidaphobia refers to an intense, irrational fear of frogs belonging to the family Ranidae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperglycemia-induced microvascular damage",
    "long_answer": "In diabetes, chronic hyperglycaemia and insulin resistance trigger formation of advanced glycation end-products, oxidative stress and inflammation that injure cerebral micro-vessels. This microvascular damage reduces brain perfusion and blood–brain-barrier integrity, while altered insulin signalling facilitates β-amyloid accumulation and tau hyperphosphorylation. The combined vascular insufficiency and neurodegenerative changes accelerate neuronal loss, thereby raising the risk of cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obstetric forceps",
    "long_answer": "Setleis syndrome produces bilateral, scar-like depressions of the temporal skin that mimic the marks left on a newborn’s face when delivery is assisted with obstetric forceps; hence, the facial defects are described as resembling forceps marks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Itching",
    "long_answer": "Pruritus is the medical term for itching of the skin. Generalized or persistent itching without an obvious rash is a recognized ‘B-symptom’ in many patients with Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma, occurring because cytokines released by malignant lymphocytes stimulate cutaneous nerve endings. Thus, the lymphoma symptom referred to as pruritus is simply itching."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus",
    "long_answer": "Lymphomatoid granulomatosis is an EBV-driven lymphoproliferative disorder in which the neoplastic component consists of Epstein–Barr virus–infected B cells surrounded by a reactive T-cell infiltrate. Detection of EBV DNA/RNA in the malignant B cells is a diagnostic hallmark, establishing Epstein–Barr virus as the causative viral agent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemiballismus",
    "long_answer": "Hemiballismus is a movement disorder characterized by violent, involuntary flinging movements of one side of the body. It results from a lesion of the contralateral subthalamic nucleus, which can be caused by vascular events such as rupture or compression from an intracranial aneurysm. Thus, an aneurysm can precipitate hemiballismus, which presents clinically with involuntary limb movements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "9 weeks",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution transvaginal sonography has demonstrated the characteristic body-wall and limb defects of Limb Body Wall Complex as early as the 9th gestational week, making 9 weeks the earliest time at which LBWC can be reliably diagnosed by prenatal ultrasound."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced serum uric acid",
    "long_answer": "Castration eliminates testicular androgens, which normally suppress renal urate excretion. Without these androgens, eunuchs clear uric acid more efficiently, so their serum uric-acid levels stay low, making gout—which depends on hyperuricemia—rare in this group."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angiostrongylus cantonensis",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic meningitis is classically produced by helminth larvae that invade the central nervous system. Worldwide, the rat lung-worm Angiostrongylus cantonensis is by far the most frequent culprit: humans acquire the parasite by eating raw or under-cooked snails, slugs, or contaminated vegetables, and the migrating larvae provoke a characteristic cerebrospinal-fluid eosinophilia. Consequently, A. cantonensis is recognized as the most common cause of eosinophilic meningitis in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "Blood levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) are routinely measured to evaluate thyroid function. TSH is secreted by the anterior pituitary and regulates the thyroid gland’s production of thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3); even small changes in thyroid hormone output alter TSH through negative feedback, making it the most sensitive and widely accepted marker for detecting hypo- or hyperthyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST (spastin)",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spastic paraplegia (HSP) is genetically heterogeneous, but the single most frequently mutated gene is SPAST, which encodes the microtubule-severing protein spastin (locus SPG4). SPAST mutations account for roughly 40% of autosomal-dominant and about 20–25% of all HSP cases, making it the gene most commonly associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperthyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Onycholysis (separation of the distal nail plate from the nail bed, often called 'Plummer’s nails') is classically associated with hyperthyroidism. In thyrotoxic states, excess thyroid hormone heightens sympathetic nervous system activity and peripheral blood flow changes, weakening the adhesion between the nail plate and bed. This sympathetic overactivity is the recognized mechanism linking hyperthyroidism to onycholysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatal lethal",
    "long_answer": "The severest form of nemaline myopathy is the neonatal-onset (severe congenital) variant, which typically leads to profound hypotonia and respiratory failure that is lethal in the newborn period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticonvulsants",
    "long_answer": "Neuropathic pain from syringobulbia is commonly managed with anticonvulsant drugs such as gabapentin or pregabalin; these agents modulate neuronal excitability and reduce central neuropathic pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC40A1 Q248H",
    "long_answer": "African iron overload (also called Bantu siderosis) has been linked to a missense mutation in the ferroportin-1 gene, SLC40A1. The common African variant, Q248H, impairs hepcidin-mediated regulation of ferroportin, leading to increased intestinal iron absorption and hepatic iron accumulation characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (also called pseudotumor cerebri) is defined by elevated intracranial pressure without an intracranial mass or hydrocephalus. The increased cerebrospinal fluid pressure can transmit pulsations to the dural venous sinuses and middle-ear structures, producing rhythmic vascular noise perceived as pulsatile tinnitus. Thus IIH is a classic cause of pulsatile tinnitus in addition to headaches and papilledema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGCE (epsilon-sarcoglycan)",
    "long_answer": "Myoclonus–dystonia is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the SGCE gene, which encodes the epsilon-sarcoglycan protein. Mutations in SGCE disrupt the sarcoglycan complex, leading to the characteristic combination of myoclonic jerks and dystonia. No other sarcoglycan genes show the consistent, well-established association seen with SGCE in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Zamzam–Sheriff–Phillips syndrome has been reported in multiple siblings born to consanguineous parents, with unaffected parents and both male and female children involved, a pattern that fits an autosomal-recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "12 weeks’ gestation",
    "long_answer": "Because the malformations of Limb Body Wall Complex are extensive and easily visualised, high-resolution prenatal ultrasound can identify the syndrome during the routine first-trimester scan; reports consistently show reliable diagnosis at about 12 weeks of gestation (11–13 + 6 weeks), well before the second trimester."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTRK3",
    "long_answer": "The neurotrophic tyrosine kinase receptor type 3 gene (NTRK3) lies on chromosome 15q25.3. NTRK3 encodes Trk-C, the high-affinity receptor for neurotrophin-3 that participates in neural crest-derived enteric nervous system development. Case-control and sequencing studies have shown that variants in NTRK3 increase susceptibility to Hirschsprung’s disease, identifying it as the Hirschsprung-associated gene mapped to chromosome 15."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle biopsy",
    "long_answer": "In McArdle’s disease (glycogen-storage disease type V), frozen-section muscle biopsy followed by histochemical staining for phosphorylase activity is employed. The stain normally colors myophosphorylase-positive fibers; its complete absence in the specimen confirms the lack of the enzyme in skeletal muscle, establishing the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papulopustules",
    "long_answer": "Perioral dermatitis typically presents with numerous tiny inflammatory papulopustules—small erythematous papules and pustules clustered around the mouth (often sparing the vermilion border). These papulopustular lesions are the characteristic skin finding of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(X;18)(p11.2;q11.2)",
    "long_answer": "Synovial sarcoma is molecularly defined by the reciprocal chromosomal translocation t(X;18)(p11.2;q11.2), which fuses the SS18 (SYT) gene on chromosome 18 to an SSX gene (usually SSX1 or SSX2) on the X chromosome. Detection of this SS18-SSX fusion is considered diagnostic for synovial sarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cricopharyngeal muscle",
    "long_answer": "Zenker's diverticulum forms just above the upper esophageal sphincter when the cricopharyngeal muscle (the sphincteric portion of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor) fails to relax or spasms during swallowing. This cricopharyngeal dysfunction increases intraluminal pressure, forcing pharyngeal mucosa to herniate through Killian’s dehiscence, creating the diverticulum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clubfoot Seven",
    "long_answer": "In Once Upon a Time in China III, the character nick-named “Clubfoot Seven,” portrayed by Xiong Xin-xin, is distinguished by having a deformed foot (a clubfoot) that influences both his gait and fighting style; no other character in the film is depicted with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sex-chromosome aneuploidy",
    "long_answer": "In 48,XXYY syndrome, each cell carries two extra sex chromosomes—one additional X and one additional Y—resulting in a 48,XXYY karyotype. This sex-chromosome aneuploidy disrupts normal testicular development and hormone production, impairing the formation of male primary sex characteristics such as normal-sized testes and sufficient testosterone levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deferoxamine",
    "long_answer": "Sideroblastic anemia often produces marked systemic iron overload, which can be exacerbated by the repeated transfusions many patients require. Deferoxamine is a high-affinity iron-chelating agent that binds excess ferric iron and promotes its renal and biliary excretion, thereby lowering body iron stores and preventing organ damage. For this reason, deferoxamine is routinely used as the pharmacologic treatment for iron overload in patients with sideroblastic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDKN2A (p16)",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma almost uniformly shows inactivation of the CDKN2A tumor-suppressor gene, which encodes the p16INK4a protein. p16 normally blocks cyclin-dependent kinases 4 and 6, enforcing the G1-to-S checkpoint. Mutation or deletion of CDKN2A therefore disrupts cell-cycle control, driving uncontrolled proliferation in pancreatic cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurulation",
    "long_answer": "In MeSH, the developmental process in which the embryonic neural plate folds and closes to form the neural tube is indexed under the descriptor “Neurulation.” This term covers the sequence of morphogenetic events—neural plate thickening, neural groove formation, elevation of neural folds, and their fusion—that generate the neural tube, the precursor of the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi–Bickel syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In 1949 Horst Bickel and Guido Fanconi first described the disorder now known as Fanconi–Bickel syndrome, which is classified as glycogen storage disease type XI. The syndrome involves mutations in the SLC2A2 gene encoding the glucose transporter GLUT2, leading to massive glycogen accumulation in liver and kidneys along with a generalized proximal renal Fanconi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac arrest",
    "long_answer": "Profound hypocalcemia can prolong the QT interval and trigger malignant ventricular arrhythmias that culminate in cardiac arrest. When arrest occurs, the recommended emergency management is immediate cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) with electrical defibrillation to re-establish an effective rhythm while intravenous calcium is given to correct the underlying electrolyte disturbance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Valproic acid (valproate) is an antiepileptic drug that enhances brain GABA levels. It is frequently prescribed to lessen the myoclonic jerks seen in myoclonus-dystonia and is also an established first-line therapy for generalized epilepsies as well as a mood-stabilising agent approved for the treatment of bipolar disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal pain",
    "long_answer": "Bile duct hamartomas (Von Meyenburg complexes) are typically incidental findings, but in the minority of patients who become symptomatic the most often reported complaint is diffuse or right-upper-quadrant abdominal pain. Abdominal pain is also among the leading chief complaints that drive patients to seek care in emergency departments, making it a shared and well-documented symptom in both contexts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Haemophilus influenzae",
    "long_answer": "Haemophilus influenzae (especially type b) was historically one of the leading bacterial causes of meningitis in young children, and nontypeable strains commonly colonize the nasopharynx. These same strains ascend the Eustachian tube and are among the principal bacterial pathogens isolated in acute otitis media, making H. influenzae a pathogen that links both meningitis and middle-ear infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obesity",
    "long_answer": "Excess body weight (obesity) is recognized as an independent risk factor for calciphylaxis in patients with chronic kidney disease and is also a well-established major risk factor for developing type 2 diabetes, making obesity a common contributor to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek: a- (‘without’) + plasis (‘formation’)",
    "long_answer": "The word ‘aplasia’ is derived from classical Greek roots: the privative prefix a- meaning ‘without’ or ‘lack of,’ combined with plasis, from plassein, meaning ‘formation’ or ‘molding.’ Hence, aplasia literally signifies the absence or failure of tissue or organ formation, which is exactly how the term is used in medical contexts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CKAP2L",
    "long_answer": "Whole-exome next-generation sequencing of families with the characteristic syndactyly, craniofacial anomalies and growth deficiency of Filippi syndrome revealed biallelic loss-of-function variants in CKAP2L (Cytoskeleton Associated Protein 2-Like). CKAP2L encodes a spindle-associated protein required for proper chromosome segregation during mitosis; its disruption explains the developmental defects seen in Filippi syndrome. Thus, CKAP2L is the gene that next-generation sequencing has definitively linked to Filippi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu disease) is an autosomal-dominant vascular disorder characterized by multiple telangiectasias and arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) in organs such as the brain, lungs, and liver. Cerebral AVMs formed in this condition can rupture, and when they do so into the subarachnoid space they produce a subarachnoid hemorrhage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatology is the medical specialty devoted to the diagnosis and treatment of rheumatic disorders—autoimmune and inflammatory conditions that frequently affect the joints, spine, ligaments, and surrounding tissues. Because chronic back pain can arise from rheumatic diseases such as ankylosing spondylitis or psoriatic arthritis, its evaluation and management fall under the expertise of rheumatologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1 in 16 000 live births",
    "long_answer": "Population-based studies from Great Britain and Ireland show biliary atresia occurs in roughly 1 infant for every 15 000–17 000 live births (≈0.6 per 10 000), giving a prevalence close to 1 in 16 000 across the British Isles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Batrachophobia",
    "long_answer": "The specific phobia characterized by an irrational and persistent fear of amphibians—such as frogs, toads, salamanders, and newts—is called batrachophobia. Derived from the Greek word “batrachos” (frog) and “phobos” (fear), it is classified under animal-type specific phobias in psychiatric nomenclature. Individuals with batrachophobia experience marked anxiety or avoidance when confronted with, or even thinking about, amphibians."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "According to current scientific and medical consensus, chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) can be diagnosed with certainty only by microscopic examination of brain tissue obtained at autopsy. While research groups have proposed clinical criteria and use brain imaging or fluid biomarkers to identify individuals who may have CTE during life, these tools remain investigational and lack the specificity and validation required for a definitive diagnosis. Therefore, a conclusive, gold-standard diagnosis of CTE cannot yet be made in living individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C9ORF72",
    "long_answer": "The GGGGCC hexanucleotide repeat expansion in the C9ORF72 gene is the single most common genetic cause of frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD), responsible for roughly one-third of familial FTLD cases and a significant proportion of sporadic cases. Pathogenic expansions in C9ORF72 lead to both loss-of-function of the normal protein and toxic gain-of-function mechanisms (RNA foci and dipeptide-repeat proteins), driving neurodegeneration characteristic of FTLD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amblyopia",
    "long_answer": "When strabismus persists throughout much of childhood, the brain suppresses input from the deviating eye to avoid double vision. This chronic suppression prevents normal visual-cortical development in that eye, leading to amblyopia (\"lazy eye\"), a lasting reduction in visual acuity that cannot be corrected by glasses alone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray, ultrasound, CT",
    "long_answer": "Situs inversus is usually identified by imaging studies that reveal mirror-image placement of thoracic and abdominal viscera. A plain chest or abdominal X-ray shows the cardiac apex and gastric bubble on the right side, suggesting organ reversal. Abdominal or cardiac ultrasound then confirms right-left inversion of the liver, spleen, and heart chambers, while computed tomography (CT) provides detailed cross-sectional confirmation of the reversed organ anatomy. These three modalities are therefore routinely used to diagnose situs inversus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Becker muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the dystrophin (DMD) gene, which is situated on the X chromosome (Xp21). Because the gene is X-linked, the disorder follows an X-linked recessive pattern of inheritance, primarily affecting males while carrier females are usually asymptomatic or mildly affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Factor V Leiden mutation",
    "long_answer": "Several clinical series have shown that Proteus syndrome patients who carry the heterozygous Factor V Leiden (G1691A) mutation in the F5 gene experience a markedly higher incidence of deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. This pro-coagulant variant increases activated factor V resistance to protein C, predisposing to venous thromboembolism, and when present in the already thrombosis-prone milieu of Proteus syndrome it further elevates DVT risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Art therapy",
    "long_answer": "Art therapy is an expressive, adjunctive treatment that engages patients in creative activities such as drawing, painting, and sculpting. For people with Bipolar II disorder, these structured creative processes provide a non-verbal channel to externalize complex emotions, reduce mood-related distress, and foster greater insight and self-awareness, complementing standard pharmacologic and psychotherapeutic interventions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Anencephaly is typically detected during routine obstetric ultrasonography, often as early as the first-trimester scan. Absence of the cranial vault and major portions of the brain creates a characteristic ‘frog-eye’ or ‘Mickey Mouse’ appearance on ultrasound images, making the diagnosis clear and reliable before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "21-Hydroxylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "The commonest cause of virilization and ambiguous genitalia in chromosomal females (46,XX) is congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency. Loss of this cortisol-synthesizing enzyme diverts adrenal steroid precursors into androgen pathways, leading to excess fetal androgen production and masculinization of the external genitalia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frederick William Eurich",
    "long_answer": "Frederick William Eurich (1867–1933), a British bacteriologist serving the Bradford Anthrax Investigation Board, systematically studied Bacillus anthracis in imported wool and developed effective sterilisation and hygiene procedures for the local wool industry. His work greatly reduced the incidence of cutaneous (woolsorters’) anthrax among Bradford textile workers, making him the key figure in combating the disease there."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methyldopa",
    "long_answer": "Methyldopa, an α-2 adrenergic agonist prescribed for systemic hypertension (notably in pregnancy), is a well-documented drug cause of retroperitoneal fibrosis. Chronic exposure can trigger an immune-mediated fibro-inflammatory reaction in the retroperitoneum, leading to ureteral obstruction and other complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Panhypopituitarism",
    "long_answer": "In panhypopituitarism the anterior pituitary fails to secrete LH and FSH, leading to markedly reduced testicular testosterone production. The fall in circulating androgens causes loss of secondary sexual hair, decreased muscle bulk and other male secondary sexual characteristics—clinical changes described as a reversal of virilization in adult males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydroquinone",
    "long_answer": "Exogenous ochronosis is a paradoxical blue-black dermal pigmentation that can develop after prolonged use of skin-lightening creams containing hydroquinone (usually ≥2%). Chronic topical application of this phenolic compound leads to accumulation and polymerization of homogentisic-like pigments within dermal connective tissue, producing the characteristic ochronotic discoloration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-1 β (IL-1β)",
    "long_answer": "Interleukin-1 β is a prototypic pro-inflammatory cytokine that is synthesized and secreted chiefly by activated macrophages. In patients with Hantavirus hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS), elevated IL-1β levels have been documented, and this cytokine drives the inflammatory cascade and vascular permeability that characterize the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome type II",
    "long_answer": "Albinism–deafness syndrome (also called Tietz or Ziprkowski-Krasilnikova syndrome) combines generalized hypopigmentation with congenital sensorineural deafness. The condition is caused by dominant mutations in the MITF gene, the same gene mutated in Waardenburg syndrome type II (WS2). Because of the shared genetic basis and overlapping pigmentary–auditory features, it is generally regarded as a severe variant of Waardenburg syndrome type II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome",
    "long_answer": "The ZAP70 gene is situated on human chromosome 2 (region 2q11-q12). Chromosome 2 is one of the 22 pairs of autosomes, meaning it is not a sex chromosome (X or Y). Therefore, ZAP70 resides on an autosomal chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cuboid bone",
    "long_answer": "The posterior tibial tendon fans out on the plantar aspect of the foot, sending slips not only to the navicular but also to several other tarsal bones, including the cuboid. The cuboid is the bone that becomes malaligned or irritated in the clinical entity termed “cuboid syndrome,” making it the common link between the tendon’s attachment and the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈2.4 million DALYs",
    "long_answer": "Analyses of national audiometric surveillance and burden-of-disease data by NIOSH investigators estimate that occupational noise-exposed U.S. workers collectively lose about 2.4 million disability-adjusted life-years (DALYs) from noise-related hearing impairment across all industries. The figure reflects the years lived with disability attributable to permanent hearing loss (virtually no years of life lost from mortality) and represents the total health burden for the U.S. noise-exposed working population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypogonadism",
    "long_answer": "Hypogonadism is the condition in which the gonads (testes or ovaries) fail to produce normal amounts of sex steroids—testosterone in males and estrogen-progesterone in females. This hormonal insufficiency impairs spermatogenesis in men and disrupts ovulation and menstrual cycling in women, so individuals of either genetic sex can experience reduced fertility or complete infertility as a consequence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal adhesions",
    "long_answer": "Fibrous scar-like bands (adhesions) that form between peritoneal surfaces after inflammation, surgery, or congenital malrotation can tether and obstruct the duodenum. This obstruction causes chronic stasis and marked dilatation, producing a megaduodenum. Thus, abdominal adhesions are a well-recognized fibrous-band condition that can lead to megaduodenum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superior oblique muscle",
    "long_answer": "The superior oblique is one of the extra-ocular muscles; its primary action is to depress the eye when it is adducted. Injury to the muscle itself (or to its trochlear nerve supply) produces ophthalmoparesis, leading to vertical diplopia and an inability to look downward effectively. Thus, damage to the superior oblique muscle causes the characteristic depression deficit in eye movements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome, characterized by upper-limb anomalies such as long thumbs and by cardiac septal and conduction defects, is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner: a single pathogenic allele of the TBX5 gene is sufficient to cause the disorder and it can appear in successive generations with equal frequency in males and females."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CEP290",
    "long_answer": "The CEP290 gene, first identified in Meckel-Gruber syndrome type 4, encodes a centrosomal/transition-zone protein essential for cilium formation. Pathogenic CEP290 variants are also well-known causes of several other ciliopathies—including Joubert syndrome, Leber congenital amaurosis, nephronophthisis, and Bardet–Biedl syndrome—demonstrating its shared role across multiple cilium-related disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II (MOPD II) appears in several official rare-disease lists maintained or funded by government bodies. In the United States, the National Institutes of Health—through its Office of Rare Diseases Research (ORDR) and the Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center (GARD)—includes MOPD II in its federally curated Rare Diseases Database. Likewise, Orphanet, which is supported by the European Commission and many European national health ministries, also classifies MOPD II as an orphan (rare) disease. Because it is formally catalogued in these government-backed rare-disease registries, the condition is officially recognized as a rare disease by government agencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYCN",
    "long_answer": "Activating amplification or mutation of the MYCN proto-oncogene, which encodes the N-Myc transcription factor, has been shown to drive a subset of retinoblastomas in the absence of RB1 loss. Because MYCN acts through gain-of-function alterations that promote proliferation, it is classified as a bona fide oncogene, and its mutation/amplification can directly lead to the development of retinoblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Serpiginous choroiditis is classified as a posterior uveitis that primarily affects the choriocapillaris, retinal pigment epithelium, and overlying retina in the posterior pole. In classic descriptions and large clinical series, there is usually little or no inflammatory activity in the anterior segment; if present, anterior chamber cells are minimal and not a defining or common feature. Therefore, anterior chamber involvement is not usually seen in serpiginous choroiditis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "200–400 nm",
    "long_answer": "Members of the family Poxviridae are among the largest viruses; their brick-shaped (or ovoid) virions typically measure roughly 200–400 nanometres in length, substantially larger than most other DNA viruses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "External-beam pelvic radiotherapy",
    "long_answer": "Radiation-induced lumbar plexopathy is a late complication seen after external-beam radiotherapy directed at pelvic malignancies (e.g., cervical, endometrial, prostate, or rectal cancer). The ionizing radiation delivered to the pelvic field can injure the lumbar plexus through microvascular damage and progressive fibrosis, leading to the characteristic delayed neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Veterinarians",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous listeriosis is usually an occupational infection acquired when Listeria-infected animals (e.g., aborting sheep or cattle) are examined or assisted during delivery. Veterinarians frequently handle these diseased animals, giving them a recognized risk for skin inoculation and localized Listeria infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Odynophagia (painful swallowing) is a classic presenting symptom of esophageal carcinoma. When the tumor is confined to the esophagus without distant spread, standard management includes surgical resection (esophagectomy) as the preferred curative therapy. Thus, localized esophageal carcinoma is treated by surgical removal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methimazole",
    "long_answer": "Maternal treatment with the antithyroid drug methimazole (or its pro-drug carbimazole) during early pregnancy is a well-documented teratogen; fetal exposure can disrupt mid-line skin development, leading to the characteristic scalp defect known as aplasia cutis congenita in the newborn."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYCN",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome type 1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the MYCN proto-oncogene. Independent of the full syndrome, pathogenic variants in MYCN have been identified in patients with isolated or syndromic esophageal atresia/tracheoesophageal fistula, making MYCN a recognized genetic risk factor for this congenital malformation. Thus, the MYCN gene links Feingold syndrome and susceptibility to esophageal atresia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Distal convoluted tubule",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 2 (Gordon syndrome) results from mutations in the WNK4 kinase gene that dysregulate the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter (NCC). This transporter operates in the distal convoluted tubule of the nephron, so that segment is the site chiefly affected by WNK4 mutations, leading to excessive sodium reabsorption, hyperkalemia, and hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congestive heart failure",
    "long_answer": "If a patent ductus arteriosus is not closed, the continuous left-to-right shunt creates chronic volume overload of the left atrium and ventricle, eventually leading to ventricular dilation and systolic dysfunction—i.e., congestive heart failure. Heart failure is one of the most frequent complications of an untreated PDA and is also among the leading causes of cardiomegaly observed on chest imaging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of malignant hyperthermia–susceptible individuals carry mutations in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor calcium-release channel. Pathogenic RYR1 variants make the channel abnormally sensitive to volatile (inhalational) anesthetics and depolarizing muscle relaxants, leading to uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release, muscle rigidity, hypermetabolism, and the malignant hyperthermia crisis. Hence, RYR1 mutation is the mutation most commonly linked to anesthetic-triggered malignant hyperthermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGFB1",
    "long_answer": "Camurati-Engelmann disease (progressive diaphyseal dysplasia) is caused by autosomal-dominant mutations in the TGFB1 gene (transforming growth factor-β1) on chromosome 19q13. Excessive activity of TGF-β1 from these mutations leads to hyperostosis and cortical thickening of the diaphyses of long bones, producing the characteristic bone pain and limb abnormalities seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "myxoedema",
    "long_answer": "In British medical usage, the clinical state of severe thyroid-hormone deficiency is called ‘myxoedema’ (spelled with the digraph ‘oe’). This term refers to the characteristic accumulation of mucopolysaccharides in the skin and other tissues seen in advanced hypothyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intestinal atresia",
    "long_answer": "In fetuses with gastroschisis, the exposed mid-gut can undergo vascular compromise and twisting while floating free in amniotic fluid. This frequently results in segmental loss or obliteration of the bowel lumen, producing jejuno-ileal intestinal atresia, which is found in roughly 10–20 % of infants with gastroschisis and is the most common postoperative complication requiring additional surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Costello syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Costello syndrome is a congenital (‘birth-defect’) disorder in the RAS-MAPK pathway that was originally described as a Noonan-like condition. Patients show distinctive craniofacial malformations—coarse facial features, large mouth with thick lips, nasal papillomas, and macrocephaly—together with cardiac defects and developmental delay. Because of these clinical similarities to Noonan syndrome, Costello syndrome is classified among the RASopathies but is distinguished by its characteristic craniofacial phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylketonuria",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal-recessive inborn error of phenylalanine metabolism. Absence or deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase causes toxic accumulation of phenylalanine in the brain, which disrupts neurotransmitter synthesis. Without dietary restriction of phenylalanine, affected children typically develop progressive neurotoxicity manifested by intellectual disability, behavioral problems and recurrent seizures. Early detection and strict dietary treatment therefore prevent the seizure disorder associated with untreated PKU."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Classic Stickler syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in collagen-encoding genes such as COL2A1, COL11A1, or COL11A2. These mutations produce disease when only one copy of the gene is altered, and affected individuals have a 50 % chance of transmitting the disorder to each child. Thus, the disorder is characteristically inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, although rare autosomal-recessive forms have been reported."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTC7A gene on chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TTC7A (tetratricopeptide repeat domain 7A) gene. The human TTC7A locus is situated on chromosome 2p16.3; pathogenic variants in this gene disrupt epithelial cell polarity and survival during gut development, leading to the characteristic widespread intestinal atresias seen in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palliative care",
    "long_answer": "Multiple system atrophy is an incurable, progressively disabling neurodegenerative disorder. As symptoms such as autonomic failure, parkinsonism, ataxia and dysphagia worsen, guidelines urge transition to palliative care to focus on symptom control, comfort measures and quality-of-life support rather than disease-modifying treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microtia",
    "long_answer": "Microtia is a congenital malformation in which the external ear (pinna) is small or rudimentary. Because the first and second branchial arch structures develop abnormally, the external auditory canal and middle-ear ossicles are often underdeveloped or absent, producing canal atresia and conductive hearing loss. Additional skeletal anomalies that may accompany this defect include bilateral parietal foramina in the parietal bones of the skull."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Os",
    "long_answer": "In Latin-based anatomical nomenclature, the term ‘os’ (plural ‘ossa’) is the word for a bone, appearing as a prefix in bone names such as os coxae, os frontale, and os temporale. Thus, the anatomical term for bone that employs the prefix “os” is simply “os.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type 3 von Willebrand disease",
    "long_answer": "Type 3 von Willebrand disease is characterized by an almost complete absence of von Willebrand factor because both alleles are defective. Without vWF, circulating factor VIII levels fall drastically and platelet adhesion is profoundly impaired, producing a bleeding phenotype similar to severe hemophilia A. Consequently, patients with type 3 VWD are the ones who most commonly experience deep-tissue and life-threatening internal hemorrhage, whereas types 1 and 2 usually present with milder mucocutaneous bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "According to the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) and other public-health authorities, placing an infant on their back (supine position) for every sleep is regarded as the single most effective, evidence-based measure to reduce the risk of sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS). The “Back to Sleep” campaign, launched in the 1990s on the basis of this finding, led to a dramatic decline in SIDS rates. While many additional recommendations (e.g., avoidance of soft bedding, room-sharing without bed-sharing, smoke-free environment, breastfeeding, pacifier use during sleep) also lower SIDS risk, none is considered more effective than the supine sleep position. Therefore, stating that putting a child under one year old on their back is only the second most effective method contradicts established scientific consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Niacin",
    "long_answer": "Niacin (nicotinic acid, vitamin B3) is routinely prescribed to treat hypoalphalipoproteinemia because at pharmacologic doses it reduces hepatic clearance of apoA-I and lowers VLDL production, leading to a 15–35 % increase in circulating HDL cholesterol."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes 3 and 11",
    "long_answer": "The case report on the Japanese girl with Pai syndrome described a balanced reciprocal translocation designated t(3;11)(q26.3;p11.2). Thus, the rearrangement involves chromosome 3 (band q26.3) and chromosome 11 (band p11.2), linking these two chromosomes to the phenotype observed in Pai syndrome for that patient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrocephalus",
    "long_answer": "In neurocysticercosis, cysticerci can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid pathways—especially within the ventricular system—leading to hydrocephalus, an accumulation of CSF that raises intracranial pressure inside the skull."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARX",
    "long_answer": "Abruzzo–Erickson syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-disability disorder. The causative mutations that have been identified in affected families occur in the ARX gene (Aristaless-related homeobox) located on chromosome Xp22.13, establishing ARX as the X-chromosomal gene responsible for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncology",
    "long_answer": "Sézary disease is an aggressive cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, a form of cancer originating from malignant T-lymphocytes. The medical specialty responsible for the diagnosis, staging, and treatment of cancers—including lymphomas—is oncology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serine",
    "long_answer": "Factor XIa is a serine protease; its catalytic activity relies on an active-site serine residue (Ser195 in chymotrypsin numbering). This serine performs the nucleophilic attack that cleaves the Arg145-Ala146 and Arg180-Val181 bonds in factor IX, thereby converting factor IX to the active enzyme factor IXa. Hence, the amino acid serine is directly involved in the activation of factor IX by factor XIa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sperm maturation and storage",
    "long_answer": "Even in males with Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome, the epididymis serves its normal role: it receives testicular sperm, allows them to mature and acquire motility, and stores them until they are propelled onward through the vas deferens during ejaculation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APC codon 1309 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis results from germ-line mutations in the APC tumour-suppressor gene. Frameshift (5-bp deletion) mutations at APC codon 1309 truncate the protein early and abolish its β-catenin–regulating function, leading to thousands of adenomatous polyps at an earlier age than other APC mutations; this codon 1309 defect therefore produces the most severe, ‘profuse’ form of FAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis is an autosomal-recessive disorder, most commonly due to HFE gene mutations, that leads to excessive intestinal iron absorption. The resulting systemic iron overload causes iron deposition in multiple organs; when excess iron accumulates in the myocardium it produces dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy and heart failure, making hemochromatosis a well-recognized genetic cause of iron-overload cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Schneckenbecken dysplasia produces striking skeletal abnormalities such as the characteristic \"snail-shell\" pelvis and markedly shortened long bones. These structural changes can be visualised in utero with high-resolution prenatal ultrasonography, allowing the condition to be detected during routine or targeted fetal anomaly scans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxic shock syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is a severe illness triggered by exotoxin-producing Staphylococcus aureus or group A Streptococcus infections. The superantigen toxins drive a massive cytokine release that produces high fever, hypotension, rash and multi-organ involvement—clinical features that closely mirror the hyperinflammatory presentation seen in multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningoencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "In tick-borne encephalitis, the infection can progress from isolated meningitis to simultaneous inflammation of both the meninges and brain parenchyma—termed meningoencephalitis. This combined meningeal and cerebral involvement constitutes the severe neurologic complication of tick-borne encephalitis, associated with higher morbidity, prolonged recovery, and risk of permanent neurological deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clarithromycin",
    "long_answer": "Clarithromycin, a macrolide that binds the 50 S ribosomal subunit and blocks mycobacterial protein synthesis, is the drug of choice for Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare infections when surgical excision is not feasible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proteus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Proteus syndrome results from a mosaic activating mutation in AKT1 (most commonly c.49G>A, p.E17K). In addition to its characteristic asymmetric, segmental overgrowth of skin, bone and other tissues, affected individuals frequently show cerebral cortical malformations, and hemimegalencephaly is a well-documented, recurring brain manifestation of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Abnormal heart rhythms (cardiac arrhythmias) such as ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, high-grade atrioventricular block, or rapid supraventricular tachycardias can drastically reduce the heart’s ability to pump blood effectively. The resulting drop in cerebral perfusion can cause transient loss of consciousness, commonly termed syncope. This pathophysiological link between significant arrhythmias and loss of consciousness is well-documented in cardiology guidelines and clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homocystinuria",
    "long_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase (CBS) is the key enzyme that converts homocysteine to cystathionine in methionine metabolism. Inherited CBS deficiency causes the inborn‐error disorder homocystinuria. Accumulated homocysteine and its metabolites damage ocular tissues, predisposing patients to lens dislocation and early-onset cataracts, along with other systemic features. Thus, homocystinuria is the metabolic disorder in which CBS deficiency leads to cataract formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proton pump inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Zollinger–Ellison syndrome causes marked gastric acid hypersecretion from gastrin-producing tumors. High-dose proton pump inhibitors (e.g., omeprazole, pantoprazole) are the treatment of choice because they irreversibly inhibit the parietal-cell H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, thereby markedly reducing acid production and controlling the associated peptic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonorchis sinensis",
    "long_answer": "Infection with the liver fluke Clonorchis sinensis chronically irritates and inflames the biliary epithelium; this prolonged inflammation and epithelial turnover significantly increase the risk of malignant transformation, making clonorchiasis a well-established risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Second-degree (partial-thickness) burn",
    "long_answer": "A second-degree, or partial-thickness, burn commonly produces fluid-filled blisters in the epidermis/upper dermis, creating thin-walled bullae that closely resemble the vesicles and bullae seen in bullous impetigo caused by Staphylococcus aureus exfoliative toxins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Watchful waiting",
    "long_answer": "Because indolent non-Hodgkin lymphomas such as follicular lymphoma grow very slowly and may remain asymptomatic for years, randomized trials have shown no survival advantage to immediate chemotherapy. Therefore, the standard initial management for patients with low-tumor-burden, asymptomatic indolent lymphoma is watchful waiting (active surveillance) until clinical symptoms or disease progression warrant treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nocturia",
    "long_answer": "Primary aldosteronism (Conn’s syndrome) produces hypokalaemia and loss of renal concentrating ability, leading to increased urine volume. This often manifests as nocturia – the need to pass excessive amounts of urine during the night – and is a well-described symptom associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acquired hemophilia A",
    "long_answer": "In elderly patients a common hematologic cause of spontaneous hematuria is acquired hemophilia A, an autoimmune disorder in which auto-antibodies neutralize coagulation factor VIII. The resulting severe deficiency of factor VIII impairs the intrinsic coagulation pathway, leading to spontaneous mucosal and soft-tissue bleeding, including bleeding from the urinary tract that manifests as hematuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tandem mass spectrometry",
    "long_answer": "In expanded newborn screening, dried-blood-spot samples are analyzed by tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS). This technique detects the characteristic acyl-carnitine pattern—especially elevated 3-hydroxy-isovalerylcarnitine (C5-OH)—that signifies 3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is a chromosomal disorder characterized by the presence of an extra copy of chromosome 21. A hallmark of affected newborns is generalized hypotonia (“floppy infant”), together with characteristic facial features and varying degrees of intellectual disability. The hypotonia results from altered neuromuscular development linked to the gene dosage imbalance on chromosome 21."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "Genuphobia is classified as a specific phobia—an anxiety disorder involving an irrational, persistent fear of knees or kneeling. Diagnosis and evidence-based treatments such as cognitive-behavioral therapy, exposure therapy, and, when indicated, pharmacotherapy (e.g., SSRIs, anxiolytics) are overseen by physicians trained in mental-health disorders. These interventions fall squarely within the scope of the medical specialty of psychiatry, making it the most appropriate field for treating genuphobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumoconiosis",
    "long_answer": "Pneumoconiosis is a group of occupational lung diseases that develop when workers chronically inhale inorganic dusts (e.g., coal, silica, asbestos). The inhaled particles deposit in the alveoli, triggering macrophage activation, fibrosis, and progressive impairment of lung function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlamydia psittaci",
    "long_answer": "Chlamydia psittaci is the etiologic agent of psittacosis (parrot fever) contracted from infected birds, and chronic infection with this same bacterium has been repeatedly linked to ocular adnexal mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma, a subtype of extranodal marginal zone lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lichenoid drug eruption",
    "long_answer": "A drug-induced lichenoid eruption (drug-induced lichenoid dermatitis) produces the same interface-lichenoid histopathology as idiopathic cutaneous lichen planus—including a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate, basal cell vacuolar change, and saw-toothed epidermal hyperplasia—so the two conditions are often histopathologically indistinguishable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thalamus",
    "long_answer": "The thalamus is the brain’s principal relay station, transferring nearly all sensory information (except olfaction) and many motor signals to the cerebral cortex. Damage to its medial/anterior nuclei disrupts the limbic memory circuits, producing profound anterograde amnesia—as classically observed in Korsakoff syndrome or bilateral thalamic infarcts—while its central role is to relay sensory and motor impulses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiothoracic surgery",
    "long_answer": "A bicuspid aortic valve predisposes to severe aortic stenosis, regurgitation, and—most importantly—ascending aortic aneurysm or dissection due to intrinsic weakness of the aortic media. Definitive management of these complications requires surgical valve replacement and/or repair or replacement of the ascending aorta, operations performed by specialists in cardiothoracic (cardiac) surgery; thus this specialty is most commonly responsible for treating the major complications of a bicuspid aortic valve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nager syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in SF3B4 reduce the amount of this spliceosomal subunit (haploinsufficiency), disrupting RNA splicing during development and producing the craniofacial and limb anomalies that define Nager syndrome, also called Nager acrofacial dysostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KAT6B",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis-intellectual disability syndrome, SBBYS type (also called Say-Barber-Biesecker-Young-Simpson syndrome) is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function or truncating mutations in the KAT6B gene, which encodes the lysine acetyltransferase 6B histone-modifying enzyme. Pathogenic variants in KAT6B disrupt normal chromatin acetylation and gene regulation during development, leading to the characteristic craniofacial features, blepharophimosis and global developmental delay seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Placido’s disc",
    "long_answer": "Placido’s disc (photokeratoscope) is a circular plate bearing alternating black-and-white concentric rings that are projected onto the anterior corneal surface. The pattern of the reflected rings is then inspected or analyzed; any distortion or irregular spacing reveals abnormal corneal curvature, making the instrument a classic tool for detecting and diagnosing keratoconus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Pyaemia is a form of septicemia characterized by numerous metastatic abscesses throughout the body. The condition is classically caused by Staphylococcus aureus, a Gram-positive, pus-forming (pyogenic) bacterium whose virulence factors (e.g., coagulase, leukocidins) promote abscess formation and hematogenous spread, producing the clinical picture of pyaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Familial progressive hyperpigmentation is consistently reported to segregate through multiple generations with male-to-male transmission and without sex bias, indicating autosomal inheritance. Linkage and mutation studies have identified pathogenic variants in the KITLG (KIT ligand) gene on chromosome 12q21, and affected individuals show disease in every generation, all hallmarks of an autosomal dominant genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaline phosphatase",
    "long_answer": "During the secretory stage of amelogenesis, secretory ameloblasts express high levels of alkaline phosphatase. This enzyme hydrolyses organic phosphate esters, increasing the local concentration of inorganic phosphate and favouring the nucleation of hydroxyapatite crystals. The activity of alkaline phosphatase therefore supports the initial, partial (≈30 %) mineralization of the enamel matrix that precedes the later maturation phase in which full mineral density is achieved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow",
    "long_answer": "Aase syndrome is characterized by hypoplastic (under-developed) bone marrow, the main post-natal site of hematopoiesis. Because the bone marrow fails to produce adequate red blood cells, affected individuals present with congenital hypoplastic anemia and other blood-cell deficiencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP7A gene",
    "long_answer": "Menkes kinky hair syndrome is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene, which encodes a copper-transporting P-type ATPase. Defective ATP7A impairs systemic copper transport, leading to the characteristic neurologic degeneration, connective-tissue defects, and ‘kinky’ hair seen in Menkes disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "In pseudohypoparathyroidism, chronic hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia lead to ectopic calcium-phosphate deposition in the brain. The most characteristic site of these intracranial calcifications is the basal ganglia—particularly the globus pallidus and other lenticular nuclei—visible on CT or MRI and associated with movement or neurocognitive symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonadal dysgenesis",
    "long_answer": "Malouf syndrome, also known as the dilated cardiomyopathy-hypergonadotropic hypogonadism syndrome, is classically characterized by dilated cardiomyopathy in association with gonadal dysgenesis (streak or under-developed ovaries leading to primary ovarian failure)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the LMNA gene (encoding lamin A/C) cause a form of familial dilated cardiomyopathy that is notable for early-onset conduction system disease, frequent malignant ventricular arrhythmias, and rapid progression to end-stage heart failure; consequently, carriers have a high incidence of ventricular arrhythmia and often require cardiac transplantation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plummer–Vinson syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Plummer–Vinson syndrome is defined by the triad of upper-esophageal webs that cause dysphagia, chronic iron-deficiency anemia, and associated mucosal changes. Patients with this syndrome may also develop systemic manifestations of longstanding iron deficiency, including reactive or congestive splenomegaly, thereby increasing their risk of an enlarged spleen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fecal–oral transmission",
    "long_answer": "Shigellosis is spread when Shigella bacteria from an infected person’s feces reach another person’s mouth. Disposable or cloth diapers that become contaminated with stool create a ready source of bacteria; during changing, caregivers’ hands, changing tables, or surrounding surfaces can be contaminated. If hands are not thoroughly washed and surfaces are not disinfected, the organisms are easily transferred to food, toys, or directly to another person, enabling fecal–oral transmission and facilitating outbreaks, especially in daycare settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution renal ultrasound is routinely used to pick up nephrocalcinosis at an early stage because fine calcium deposits make the renal pyramids appear brightly echogenic on sonography long before they are dense enough to be seen on plain radiographs or other modalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rho(D) immune globulin",
    "long_answer": "Immune-mediated hydrops fetalis most commonly results from Rh incompatibility, in which maternal anti-Rh(D) antibodies attack fetal red blood cells. Administering Rho(D) immune globulin (e.g., RhoGAM) to Rh-negative mothers during pregnancy and after delivery neutralizes any fetal Rh-positive erythrocytes that enter the maternal circulation, preventing maternal sensitization and the consequent antibody-mediated hemolysis that leads to hydrops fetalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYCN",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome (oculodigitoesophagoduodenal syndrome) is an autosomal-dominant disorder most commonly produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the MYCN gene on chromosome 2p24.3. MYCN encodes the N-Myc transcription factor; its haploinsufficiency disrupts embryonic development, leading to the characteristic digital anomalies, microcephaly, facial features, and gastrointestinal atresias seen in Feingold syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fasting lipid panel",
    "long_answer": "Hypertriglyceridemia is identified by quantifying triglyceride concentrations in the blood. This is routinely done with a fasting lipid panel (also called a fasting serum lipid profile), a laboratory blood test that measures triglycerides, total cholesterol, HDL-C and LDL-C. Elevated fasting triglyceride values on this test establish the diagnosis of hypertriglyceridemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD) is classically caused by heterozygous mutations in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-43. Defects in connexin-43 disrupt intercellular communication within skeletal and otic tissues; in the middle ear this can impair ossicular development or mobility, producing a conductive hearing loss. Thus, a pathogenic mutation in GJA1 is the recognized genetic alteration that links ODDD to conductive deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthotic shoe inserts",
    "long_answer": "Custom or prefabricated foot orthoses (orthotic shoe inserts) are commonly prescribed to redistribute pressure and improve alignment of the first metatarsophalangeal joint, thereby lessening mechanical stress and relieving the pain produced by bunions (hallux valgus)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Refraction test",
    "long_answer": "Myopia is confirmed during a comprehensive eye examination by performing a refraction test (objective retinoscopy and/or automated/subjective refraction). This procedure measures how light focuses through the cornea and lens onto the retina, quantifying the eye’s refractive error; a negative diopter value indicates myopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen therapy",
    "long_answer": "Girls and women with Turner syndrome usually have ovarian failure, leading to markedly low circulating estrogen. Administering exogenous estrogen (estrogen replacement therapy, typically low-dose estradiol begun around the normal age of puberty) stimulates secondary sexual characteristics. The added estrogen drives breast tissue growth and the typical female fat distribution that widens the hips, thereby promoting normal pubertal development in Turner syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MLL (KMT2A) rearrangement",
    "long_answer": "In infants (younger than 12 months) with acute lymphoblastic leukaemia, the single most frequent genetic alteration is a rearrangement of the MLL gene—now designated KMT2A—on chromosome 11q23, usually the t(4;11)(q21;q23) MLL-AF4 fusion. These KMT2A/MLL rearrangements are present in roughly 70–80 % of infant ALL cases, far surpassing any other lesion. Because infants with KMT2A-rearranged ALL often show leukaemic skin infiltrates (\"blue-berry muffin\" lesions), hepatosplenomegaly and other readily observable findings, the abnormality can first be suspected during the physical examination and then confirmed with cytogenetic or molecular tests."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemarthrosis",
    "long_answer": "Hemarthrosis—bleeding into joints—is a hallmark of hemophilia A and B, arising from deficient clotting factor activity. Although rare, patients with congenital Factor XIII deficiency can experience the same joint hemorrhages because Factor XIII is essential for stabilizing the fibrin clot; without it, clots are fragile and recurrent bleeding into joints occurs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryopyrin",
    "long_answer": "Muckle-Wells syndrome is one of the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS). It is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the NLRP3 gene, which encodes the protein cryopyrin. Mutant cryopyrin hyperactivates the inflammasome, driving excessive maturation and release of the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-1β, thereby producing the characteristic recurrent inflammatory episodes of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 2",
    "long_answer": "Pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 2 (PCH-2) is an autosomal-recessive neurodegenerative disorder, most often caused by biallelic TSEN54 mutations. It features marked hypoplasia of the pons and cerebellum and commonly presents with progressive microcephaly, severe motor and cognitive impairment, and early-onset epileptic seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAK2 V617F mutation",
    "long_answer": "The V617F point mutation in the Janus kinase-2 (JAK2) gene is frequently found in patients with splanchnic and portal vein thrombosis. It drives latent myeloproliferative neoplasms, leading to increased erythrocyte and platelet production, hyperviscosity and a pro-thrombotic state; up to half of idiopathic portal vein thrombosis cases carry this mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hormone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Because the streak gonads in XX gonadal dysgenesis do not produce ovarian sex steroids, exogenous sex‐steroid (estrogen followed by cyclic estrogen-progestin) hormone replacement therapy is given to supply the missing estrogen and progesterone, thereby triggering pubertal development and establishing withdrawal menstruation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eisenmenger syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A large unrepaired aortopulmonary window produces a substantial left-to-right shunt, chronically overloading the pulmonary circulation. Persistent high pulmonary blood flow leads to progressive pulmonary vascular remodeling and irreversible pulmonary hypertension. Once pulmonary pressures equal or exceed systemic levels, shunt reversal (right-to-left flow) occurs, causing cyanosis and the constellation known as Eisenmenger syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute renal failure",
    "long_answer": "Large amounts of free hemoglobin filtered into the renal tubules during hemoglobinuria precipitate with Tamm-Horsfall proteins and generate oxidative injury, producing acute tubular necrosis. This pigment-induced nephropathy can rapidly lead to acute renal failure (acute kidney injury), a serious complication frequently seen in ICU patients after massive intravascular hemolysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Germline mosaicism",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome is usually caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in SF3B4 and therefore follows an autosomal-dominant model, yet most cases are sporadic while occasional families show multiple affected siblings born to unaffected parents. This apparent discrepancy is best explained by germline (gonadal) mosaicism: a new SF3B4 mutation arises in one parent’s germ-cell line, leaving the parent phenotypically normal but capable of transmitting the mutation to more than one child, producing the observed inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly",
    "long_answer": "The condition in which a person is born with unusually short fingers and/or toes is called brachydactyly. It is a congenital skeletal malformation in which the phalanges, metacarpals, or metatarsals are shortened, leading to overall reduced digit length."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ocular coloboma",
    "long_answer": "Most patients reported with Kapur–Toriello syndrome exhibit ocular coloboma—an embryologic defect leading to a cleft or gap in structures such as the iris, retina, or optic nerve. Case series and reviews describe coloboma in the majority of individuals with this rare multiple-anomaly syndrome, making it the characteristic eye-related finding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skellefteå",
    "long_answer": "In Sweden, familial amyloid polyneuropathy caused by a transthyretin (TTR) gene mutation is particularly common in and around the city of Skellefteå in Västerbotten County. This ‘Skellefteå variant’ (often TTR Val30Met) has been extensively documented, giving the city one of the world’s highest regional prevalences of TTR-FAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Induction of apoptosis",
    "long_answer": "Ebola virus infection triggers programmed cell death (apoptosis) in many cell types, including lymphocytes and endothelial cells. Viral proteins such as the surface glycoprotein (GP) and VP24/VP35 disrupt intracellular signaling and mitochondrial integrity, activating caspases and leading to apoptotic cell death, which contributes to tissue damage and the severe pathology of Ebola virus disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLCG2",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the PLCG2 gene (encoding phospholipase C-γ2) produce a common-variable-immunodeficiency–like disorder called PLAID/APLAID. A hallmark of this condition is cold-induced urticaria, manifested as wheals and itching that mimic typical hives. Thus, a PLCG2 mutation links CVID with hive-like (urticarial) symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Visual agnosia",
    "long_answer": "After surgery restores his eyesight, Virgil can see light, color and shape, but his brain cannot transform these raw visual signals into meaningful objects or faces. This higher-order perceptual deficit—intact vision paired with an inability to recognize what is seen—is the clinical picture of visual agnosia, the very condition Oliver Sacks described in the case story on which the film is based."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNA mismatch repair",
    "long_answer": "Lynch syndrome (hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer) is caused by germ-line mutations in genes such as MLH1, MSH2, MSH6 and PMS2. These proteins must form MutSα (MSH2-MSH6) and MutLα (MLH1-PMS2) heterodimers to recognize and repair base-pair mismatches and small insertion–deletion loops arising during DNA replication. Loss of functional dimers impairs the DNA mismatch repair process, leading to microsatellite instability and tumor predisposition characteristic of Lynch syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Swyer–James syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Post-infectious obliterative bronchiolitis due to adenovirus (especially serotypes 3 and 7) can produce unilateral lung hypoplasia with air-trapping and diminished vascularity; this clinical–radiologic entity is called Swyer–James (or Swyer–James–MacLeod) syndrome, a rare complication of adenoviral bronchiolitis obliterans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET",
    "long_answer": "The RET proto-oncogene, mapped to chromosome 10q11.2, undergoes activating rearrangements (RET/PTC) that are a well-established driver event in papillary thyroid cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Breast carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Among solid tumours, carcinoma of the breast has a particular predilection for metastasising to the axial skeleton. In the bone marrow it seeds discrete aggregates of malignant epithelial cells, producing nodular deposits that can be recognised histologically or on imaging. Thus, nodular formations detected within bone marrow are classically associated with metastatic breast carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(11;22) translocation",
    "long_answer": "Both Ewing sarcoma and peripheral primitive neuroectodermal tumor belong to the Ewing-family tumors and harbor the characteristic chromosomal translocation t(11;22)(q24;q12), which fuses the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 to the FLI1 gene on chromosome 11. This EWSR1-FLI1 fusion is the defining genetic event common to both tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crouzon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome (autosomal-dominant craniofacial dysostosis caused mainly by FGFR2 mutations) involves premature fusion of multiple cranial sutures. This early synostosis produces a small cranium (micro-/brachycephaly), thinning of the calvarial bone, and the characteristic “beaten-copper” or digital impressions—generalized depressions most evident along the frontoparietal and occipitoparietal sutures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase",
    "long_answer": "Morquio A syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV A) is caused by a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase (encoded by the GALNS gene). Lack of this sulfatase prevents proper degradation of the glycosaminoglycans keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate, leading to their accumulation and the characteristic skeletal dysplasia and systemic manifestations of Morquio A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human chorionic gonadotropin",
    "long_answer": "Froehlich syndrome (adiposogenital dystrophy) results from hypothalamic-pituitary damage that lowers secretion of the gonadotropins LH and FSH, causing hypogonadism and delayed puberty. Administering human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) provides an exogenous LH-like stimulus to the gonads, promoting testosterone production and pubertal development in affected children, thereby serving as an appropriate hormone-replacement therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2 (connexin-26)",
    "long_answer": "Keratitis-Ichthyosis-Deafness (KID) syndrome and many forms of nonsyndromic autosomal recessive sensorineural hearing loss share pathogenic mutations in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-26. Altered connexin-26 disrupts intercellular ion homeostasis in the epidermis and cochlea, producing the characteristic skin lesions and profound hearing impairment observed in these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "X-linked intellectual disability results from pathogenic variants in genes located on the X chromosome; because males have a single X chromosome, mutations there readily manifest as intellectual disability with an X-linked pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow biopsy",
    "long_answer": "In Aase syndrome, the congenital anemia results from bone-marrow hypoplasia, so a bone marrow biopsy (often with aspiration) is the standard diagnostic test used to directly examine marrow cellularity and erythroid precursors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercortisolism",
    "long_answer": "In Cushing's syndrome, chronically elevated cortisol (hypercortisolism) suppresses hypothalamic GnRH secretion and pituitary release of LH and FSH. This disruption of the normal gonadotropin-estrogen/progesterone axis can halt the menstrual cycle, leading to amenorrhea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superior vena cava syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Large anterior mediastinal teratomas may compress or invade the superior vena cava. Obstruction of this major vein impedes venous return from the head, neck, and upper limbs, producing facial and upper-extremity edema, distended neck veins, and dyspnea. Because it alters venous return and right-atrial filling, the lesion directly disrupts normal hemodynamics— the clinical entity known as superior vena cava syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenovirus infection",
    "long_answer": "Swyer–James (MacLeod) syndrome is classically a post-infectious obliterative bronchiolitis that develops after a severe lower-respiratory adenovirus infection—most often adenovirus serotype 7—in infancy or early childhood. The viral damage to small airways prevents normal lung growth and results in the characteristic unilateral hyperlucent, hypoplastic lung seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STAC3",
    "long_answer": "Native American myopathy (NAM) is caused by pathogenic variants in the STAC3 gene, which encodes the SH3 and cysteine-rich domain-containing protein 3. STAC3 is essential for excitation–contraction coupling in skeletal muscle; loss-of-function mutations (e.g., the recurrent p.Trp284Ser substitution found in the Lumbee tribe) disrupt calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, producing the characteristic muscle weakness, facial dysmorphism, and malignant hyperthermia susceptibility seen in NAM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLRN1",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome type III is caused by pathogenic mutations in the CLRN1 gene, which encodes the tetraspan membrane protein clarin-1. Loss-of-function variants (e.g., p.Y176X, p.M122K) disrupt clarin-1’s role in inner-ear hair cells and photoreceptors, producing the progressive hearing loss and retinitis pigmentosa characteristic of Usher type III."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy with progressive myoclonic epilepsy (SMA-PME) combines motor neuron degeneration with seizure disorders, both of which fall under disorders of the nervous system. Diagnosis and management—covering motor weakness, electrophysiologic studies, antiepileptic therapy, and supportive neurologic care—are therefore primarily the responsibility of a neurologist (often a pediatric neurologist in children)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Philtrum",
    "long_answer": "During embryogenesis the medial nasal (nasomedial) processes fuse with the adjacent maxillary processes to create the vertical mid-line groove of the upper lip known as the philtrum. In Aarskog–Scott syndrome this structure is characteristically elongated or prominent, making the philtrum the facial feature in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Samsung Medical Center",
    "long_answer": "Before undergoing her groundbreaking tracheal transplant, Hannah Warren survived for more than two years in the neonatal intensive-care unit of Samsung Medical Center in Seoul, where mechanical ventilation kept her alive despite being born without a functional trachea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ascending urinary tract infection",
    "long_answer": "Because the bladder mucosa is exposed to the external environment in bladder exstrophy, enteric bacteria such as Escherichia coli can readily contaminate the open bladder and travel up the ureters. This continual contamination makes ascending urinary tract infection the single most frequent infectious problem seen in patients with bladder exstrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intestinal malrotation",
    "long_answer": "Intestinal (mid-gut) malrotation is a congenital defect in which the intestines fail to rotate and fix properly during embryologic development. The abnormal positioning predisposes to duodenal obstruction by Ladd bands and to mid-gut volvulus, both of which commonly present with acute, often bilious, vomiting in neonates and infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pregnancy",
    "long_answer": "Pregnancy is a well-established host risk factor for progression to severe dengue. The physiologic expansion of maternal blood volume and extracellular fluid in pregnancy amplifies the capillary-plasma leakage characteristic of dengue infection, predisposing pregnant women to haemoconcentration, hypovolaemic shock and severe disease manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Giant congenital melanocytic nevi are large pigmented skin lesions present at birth. Because they are cutaneous abnormalities with potential for malignant melanoma transformation, their diagnosis, monitoring, and initial management fall under the expertise of dermatologists, the medical specialists devoted to skin disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Michaelis–Gutmann bodies",
    "long_answer": "Malakoplakia is characterized histologically by sheets of macrophages (von Hansemann cells) that contain round, basophilic, intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies composed of partially digested bacterial remnants complexed with calcium and iron. These pathognomonic inclusions are called Michaelis–Gutmann bodies, and their presence is diagnostic of malakoplakia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Burning mouth syndrome occurs predominantly in post-menopausal women, and one leading hypothesis links it to the fall in circulating estrogen after menopause. Estrogen deficiency can alter oral mucosal integrity, salivary composition, and sensory nerve function, creating the persistent burning sensation characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Connective tissue growth factor (CTGF)",
    "long_answer": "Connective tissue growth factor (CTGF), also known as CCN2, is a well-established downstream mediator of tissue fibrosis that promotes extracellular-matrix deposition and myofibroblast activation. After central nervous system injury, astrocytes strongly up-regulate CTGF, and this protein drives the proliferation and matrix production that are essential for glial scar formation. Thus, CTGF links fibrotic processes with the generation of the glial scar."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplant",
    "long_answer": "The disease is osteopetrosis, in which defective osteoclasts fail to resorb bone, leading to abnormally dense yet fragile (brittle) bones. Osteoclasts arise from hematopoietic stem cells, so replacing the patient’s marrow with donor hematopoietic stem cells provides functional osteoclasts and reverses the pathophysiology. Thus, allogeneic bone marrow (hematopoietic stem cell) transplantation is the definitive, disease-modifying treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aldose reductase",
    "long_answer": "Aldose reductase is the key enzyme of the polyol pathway that reduces glucose to sorbitol. In chronic hyperglycaemia, sorbitol accumulates in nerves and retinal cells, producing osmotic stress and oxidative damage that contribute to diabetic neuropathy and diabetic retinopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Addison disease",
    "long_answer": "Primary adrenal insufficiency, known as Addison disease, results in inadequate production of cortisol (and often aldosterone) by the adrenal cortex. Cortisol normally supports vascular tone and responsiveness to catecholamines; when it is deficient, systemic vascular resistance falls, leading to dizziness and blood-pressure drops upon standing—classic orthostatic hypotension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLG gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Ligneous conjunctivitis is most commonly caused by congenital type-1 plasminogen deficiency, an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the PLG gene that encodes plasminogen. The impaired plasmin-mediated fibrinolysis leads to accumulation of fibrin-rich, woody (ligneous) pseudomembranes on mucous membranes, including the conjunctiva."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Congenital inguinal hernias are frequently seen in patients with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, a hereditary connective-tissue disorder caused by defects in collagen synthesis. The abnormal collagen weakens the abdominal wall and inguinal canal, predisposing affected infants to develop inguinal hernias at birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgE",
    "long_answer": "Immediate (Type I) hypersensitivity reactions—such as anaphylaxis, asthma, and common allergies—are mediated by immunoglobulin E (IgE). Allergen-specific IgE binds to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils; upon re-exposure, cross-linking of these IgE molecules triggers rapid degranulation and release of histamine and other mediators, producing the characteristic immediate allergic response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type II collagen",
    "long_answer": "Stickler syndrome is an autosomal-dominant arthro-ophthalmopathy that arises from pathogenic variants in genes such as COL2A1, COL11A1, COL11A2 and related loci. These genes encode type II (and related type XI) collagen, the principal structural protein of cartilage and the ocular vitreous. Mutated or deficient type II collagen disrupts normal development of cartilage, joints, mid-facial bones and the vitreous body, producing the characteristic skeletal, craniofacial and ocular manifestations of Stickler syndrome. Thus, abnormal type II collagen is assumed to be the key substance involved in its cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acetylcholine",
    "long_answer": "Organophosphate compounds inhibit acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that normally breaks down the neurotransmitter acetylcholine. This inhibition causes acetylcholine to accumulate at cholinergic synapses, overstimulating muscarinic receptors in salivary glands and leading to profuse salivation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2",
    "long_answer": "Palmoplantar keratoderma-deafness syndrome is most often produced by missense mutations in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin 26. Altered connexin 26 disrupts intercellular communication in both the epidermis (leading to hyperkeratosis of palms and soles) and the cochlea (producing sensorineural hearing loss), establishing GJB2 as the causal gene for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ICD-10",
    "long_answer": "The Tenth Revision of the International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10) explicitly lists myiasis under code B87 in Chapter I, ‘Certain infectious and parasitic diseases’, thus providing a specific classification for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium",
    "long_answer": "Calcium ions are indispensable for skeletal and cardiac muscle contraction because they bind to troponin-C, permitting actin-myosin interaction. Mutations in genes that encode neuronal calcium channels, particularly CACNA1A, produce spinocerebellar ataxia (e.g., SCA type 6). Thus, the element whose normal physiology underlies muscle contraction and whose gene defects cause spinocerebellar ataxia is calcium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5q- syndrome",
    "long_answer": "5q- syndrome is a distinct subtype of myelodysplastic syndrome produced by an interstitial deletion on the long arm (q) of chromosome 5. Patients typically present with a severe, isolated macrocytic anemia, relatively preserved or elevated platelet counts, and characteristic dysplastic megakaryocytes. The chromosomal deletion leads to haploinsufficiency of genes such as RPS14, contributing to ineffective erythropoiesis and the macrocytic anemia seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal lobe",
    "long_answer": "Quadrantanopia classically arises when a lesion involves Meyer's loop—the inferior optic radiations that course through the temporal lobe. Damage here disrupts visual information from the superior contralateral visual quadrant, producing the characteristic ‘pie-in-the-sky’ quadrantanopia most frequently tested and reported in clinical neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is a well-recognized cause of microcephaly, which is defined as a head circumference that is smaller than expected for gestational age and sex. CMV can infect the developing fetal brain, leading to disruption of neuronal proliferation and migration, intracranial calcifications, and overall impaired brain growth. These effects frequently manifest at birth as a smaller-than-normal head size (microcephaly) in affected newborns. This association is documented in authoritative sources such as the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), the American Academy of Pediatrics (Red Book), and numerous peer-reviewed studies on congenital CMV. Therefore, congenital CMV can indeed lead to a smaller head size in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elbow joint",
    "long_answer": "In Nager syndrome (acrofacial dysostosis), congenital radioulnar synostosis frequently fuses the radius and ulna near the elbow, limiting flexion-extension and pronation-supination. This abnormality makes the elbow joint stiff and results in poor movement of the upper limb."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor pulmonale",
    "long_answer": "In chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction and destruction of pulmonary capillary beds raise pulmonary arterial pressure, leading to pulmonary hypertension. The sustained pressure overload causes right-ventricular hypertrophy and eventual right-sided heart failure, a condition termed cor pulmonale. This cardiovascular complication is among the most common and clinically significant sequelae of COPD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tissue preconditioning",
    "long_answer": "The onion-like concentric rings of alternating demyelinated and myelinated tissue in Baló’s concentric sclerosis are thought to arise from a hypoxia-like ‘tissue preconditioning’ phenomenon: an initial ischemic-type insult triggers protective responses in adjacent white-matter zones, so successive waves of injury and relative protection produce the characteristic concentric lamellae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papilledema",
    "long_answer": "Papilledema is swelling of the optic disc caused by raised intracranial pressure. In cavernous sinus thrombosis, venous congestion and impaired cerebrospinal fluid drainage elevate intracranial pressure, which can lead to papilledema visible on fundoscopic examination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Sonic hedgehog (SHH) is a developmental morphogen; pathogenic germ-line variants are classically associated with midline developmental defects such as holoprosencephaly, craniofacial anomalies and limb patterning disorders. Current medical and genetic literature does not show an association between SHH mutations and mastocytosis, a clonal mast-cell disease that is typically driven by activating mutations in the KIT receptor (most commonly KIT D816V). Likewise, while hedgehog signalling participates in skeletal development, SHH mutations have not been implicated as a recognised genetic cause of osteoporosis in humans; osteoporosis is instead linked to variants in genes such as COL1A1/2, LRP5, WNT1, and others, as well as secondary hormonal and environmental factors. Therefore, genetic mutations in SHH are not considered to be associated with either mastocytosis or osteoporosis according to established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal artery biopsy",
    "long_answer": "The definitive confirmation of giant cell arteritis is obtained by histopathologic examination of a temporal artery biopsy, which reveals granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells and disruption of the internal elastic lamina. Because this tissue diagnosis directly demonstrates the characteristic vascular lesions, temporal artery biopsy is regarded as the gold-standard diagnostic procedure for GCA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carcinoid syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Appendiceal neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors can release large amounts of serotonin and related mediators into the circulation, producing carcinoid syndrome. These bioactive substances stimulate fibroblast proliferation, leading to dense fibrosis in the mesentery, peritoneum, and right-sided heart valves. The resulting fibrotic plaques can kink or obstruct bowel loops, impair nutrient transit, and compromise organ function, making carcinoid syndrome the condition in which tumor-induced fibrosis hampers digestion or other visceral activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis",
    "long_answer": "A cervical Pap smear collected during prenatal care can be tested for Chlamydia trachomatis. Identifying and treating this often-asymptomatic infection with antibiotics before delivery markedly lowers the risk that the organism will be passed from mother to infant during passage through the birth canal, thereby preventing neonatal conjunctivitis and pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isoelectric focusing",
    "long_answer": "Diagnosis of congenital disorders of glycosylation relies on isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin. This electrophoretic technique separates transferrin glycoforms according to their net charge, which changes with the number of sialic acid–terminated N-glycans. Abnormal banding patterns on transferrin isoelectric focusing reveal hypoglycosylated forms characteristic of CDG, making it the standard assay to assess transferrin glycosylation status."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal trisomy",
    "long_answer": "Cytogenetic studies of products of conception show that extra copies of an autosome (autosomal trisomies—most often trisomy 16) account for roughly 50–60 % of all chromosomal abnormalities found in first-trimester miscarriages, making autosomal trisomy the single most frequent chromosomal anomaly responsible for early pregnancy loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NRAS mutation",
    "long_answer": "Neurocutaneous melanosis is driven by post-zygotic, activating mutations of the NRAS oncogene—most commonly substitutions at codon 61 (e.g., Q61K/Q61R). These NRAS mutations are found both in the large/giant congenital melanocytic naevi of the skin and in the leptomeningeal melanocytic proliferations, indicating a shared somatic mosaic origin and underlying the pathogenesis of this phakomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBA1A",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TUBA1A gene, which encodes the α-1A-tubulin subunit, disturb microtubule dynamics needed for neuronal migration, producing a form of lissencephaly (\"LIS3\"). Because TUBA1A is also critical for corticospinal-tract development, affected individuals frequently show hypertonia and muscle spasticity in addition to the smooth-brain malformation, so the same TUBA1A mutation can present with both lissencephaly and muscle spasticity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine, a calcineurin-inhibitor immunosuppressant, is employed in refractory or severe cases of urticarial vasculitis because it down-regulates T-cell activation and cytokine release, thereby diminishing the immune-complex–mediated vascular inflammation characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vomiting",
    "long_answer": "Vomiting is a classic gastrointestinal symptom seen in disorders such as gastroenteritis, gastritis, and peptic-ulcer disease. Clinical descriptions of pneumonic plague note that, in addition to its hallmark fever, cough, and rapidly progressive pneumonia, patients frequently experience systemic symptoms that include nausea and vomiting. Thus vomiting is a symptom shared by many gastrointestinal diseases and by pneumonic plague."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fainting",
    "long_answer": "Both dysautonomia—dysfunction of the autonomic nervous system—and reflex (vasovagal) syncope can lead to transient loss of consciousness caused by sudden drops in blood pressure and/or heart rate. This manifests clinically as fainting, the hallmark symptom they share."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1 case per 10 million people per year",
    "long_answer": "VIPoma is an exceedingly rare neuroendocrine tumour; epidemiologic studies and reviews (e.g., UpToDate, major endocrine oncology texts) place its incidence at roughly one new case per 10 million population annually."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinologist",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile osteoporosis is a metabolic bone disorder requiring evaluation of calcium–phosphate balance, vitamin-D status, and hormonal influences on bone turnover. These systemic, endocrine-mediated factors are routinely assessed and managed by an endocrinologist (ideally a pediatric endocrinologist), the specialist trained to diagnose and treat metabolic bone diseases in children and adolescents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular endothelial growth factor",
    "long_answer": "In POEMS syndrome, monoclonal plasma cells secrete excessive amounts of vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). The elevated VEGF drives abnormal angiogenesis and increased capillary permeability, producing many hallmark features of the syndrome such as edema, organomegaly, skin changes, and peripheral neuropathy. Serum VEGF levels correlate closely with disease activity, confirming that VEGF overproduction underlies the clinical manifestations of POEMS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arthroscopic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Elbow dysplasia in dogs is commonly managed by arthroscopic surgery, where a small arthroscope and instruments are inserted into the joint to locate and remove fragmented cartilage or bone (e.g., a fragmented medial coronoid process) and smooth the joint surface. This minimally invasive technique reduces postoperative pain, allows thorough joint evaluation, and is widely accepted as the surgical treatment of choice for canine elbow dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Navicular bone",
    "long_answer": "Ischiopatellar dysplasia (small-patella syndrome) is caused by TBX4 mutations and affects bones derived from the hind-limb bud. In addition to hypoplastic or absent patellae, mid-foot involvement is characteristic: the navicular bone frequently shows delayed ossification, marked hypoplasia or complete absence. This selective navicular deficiency is a well-described radiological hallmark of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood",
    "long_answer": "Haemophilia A is caused by mutations in the F8 gene that lower or eliminate coagulation-factor VIII activity. Factor VIII functions in the intrinsic coagulation cascade within the circulating blood, and its absence leads to defective clot formation. Thus the primary organ/system affected by this genetic deficiency is the blood (haematologic system), producing the characteristic bleeding tendency of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cocaine",
    "long_answer": "Smoking or inhaling cocaine fumes (especially crack cocaine) is a well-documented precipitant of acute eosinophilic pneumonia. The irritant combustion products and drug itself injure the alveolar epithelium, triggering release of Th2-type cytokines (IL-5, eotaxin) and massive pulmonary eosinophil recruitment, leading to the characteristic febrile, hypoxic presentation and eosinophil-rich infiltrates on bronchoalveolar lavage or lung biopsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Livedo reticularis",
    "long_answer": "Relapsing polychondritis can be accompanied by livedo reticularis, a cutaneous vascular phenomenon that produces a lace-like, purplish (reticular) mottling of the skin. This pattern results from impaired blood flow in the dermal vessels and is commonly seen in systemic autoimmune or vasculitic disorders, including relapsing polychondritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a congenital over-growth disorder characterized by features such as macroglossia, organomegaly and hemihyperplasia. Children with this imprinting disorder have a markedly increased risk of embryonal tumors, especially Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma, making routine abdominal ultrasound surveillance standard care in the first years of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydatidiform mole",
    "long_answer": "A hydatidiform mole (molar pregnancy) is a gestational trophoblastic disease characterized by abnormal proliferation of trophoblastic tissue. The trophoblasts secrete very high amounts of beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (β-hCG), often far exceeding levels seen in normal pregnancy. These markedly elevated β-hCG concentrations stimulate maternal estrogen and thyroid pathways, provoking severe nausea and vomiting—clinically termed hyperemesis gravidarum. Thus, the disease most classically linked to high β-hCG levels and intense gestational nausea is the hydatidiform mole."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein-4",
    "long_answer": "During limb development, BMP-4, a TGF-β family member produced in the apical ectodermal ridge and interdigital mesenchyme, activates caspase-dependent programmed cell death in the interdigital tissue. This apoptosis removes the webbing between the digits, allowing separate fingers and toes to form; blocking BMP-4 signaling prevents this cell death and results in syndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1.5 × 10⁹ cells/L",
    "long_answer": "In adults, a normal absolute neutrophil count spans roughly 1.5–8.0 × 10⁹ cells per litre (1,500–8,000 cells/µL). Counts below 1.5 × 10⁹ cells/L define neutropenia; therefore 1.5 × 10⁹ cells/L (≈1,500 cells/µL) is accepted as the lower limit of the reference range."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guanfacine",
    "long_answer": "Guanfacine is an antihypertensive drug that functions as an α-2A adrenergic receptor agonist. Beyond lowering blood pressure, its presynaptic α-2A activation enhances prefrontal cortical regulation of attention and impulse control. Because of this mechanism, an extended-release formulation of guanfacine (Intuniv) is FDA-approved and widely used as a non-stimulant treatment for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL1A1 and COL1A2",
    "long_answer": "Over 85–90 % of osteogenesis imperfecta (all classical types I–IV) are caused by dominant pathogenic variants in the collagen type I genes COL1A1 and COL1A2, which encode the α1(I) and α2(I) chains of type I collagen. Defective or reduced type I collagen disturbs bone matrix formation and strength, producing the characteristic brittle-bone phenotype. Therefore, these two genes are the ones most commonly linked to osteogenesis imperfecta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous pyelogram",
    "long_answer": "An intravenous pyelogram (IVP) is performed by injecting radiopaque contrast medium into a vein; the contrast is filtered by the kidneys and outlines the renal collecting systems, ureters, and bladder on sequential X-ray images. This contrast-enhanced imaging specifically highlights the urinary tract anatomy and function, making it a standard diagnostic study in evaluating malformations such as cloacal exstrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD70",
    "long_answer": "The CD70 gene (TNFSF7) encodes the CD70 ligand that binds directly to the costimulatory receptor CD27. Biallelic CD70 deficiency is a recognized cause of Epstein-Barr virus–driven primary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis. Thus, among genes linked to primary HLH, CD70 is the one whose product physiologically interacts with the protein CD27."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders",
    "long_answer": "Dissociative disorders are classified in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), the authoritative diagnostic handbook produced and periodically revised by the American Psychiatric Association. The DSM provides standardized criteria and coding for mental health conditions, including dissociative identity disorder, dissociative amnesia, and depersonalization/derealization disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Locus heterogeneity",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome exemplifies locus heterogeneity, where pathogenic variants in several different genes (e.g., PTPN11, SOS1, RAF1, KRAS, NRAS, etc.) all produce the same clinical disorder. Thus, a single phenotype arises from mutations at multiple distinct genetic loci."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Performance anxiety",
    "long_answer": "Helen Singer Kaplan emphasized that performance anxiety—persistent worry about one’s sexual adequacy and self-monitoring during intercourse—activates sympathetic arousal and distracts attention from erotic stimuli. This anxiety can inhibit the parasympathetic vasocongestive responses required for vaginal lubrication and genital swelling, thereby precipitating or maintaining female sexual arousal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLAGL1 (ZAC)",
    "long_answer": "Transient neonatal diabetes mellitus (TNDM) is most commonly caused by overexpression of the imprinted gene PLAGL1 (also called ZAC) located at chromosome 6q24. Paternal uniparental disomy, paternal duplication, or loss of maternal methylation at this locus increases PLAGL1 expression, leading to the transient diabetic phenotype in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNAH11",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DNAH11 gene, which encodes an outer-dynein-arm heavy chain, impair the motility of nodal (embryonic) cilia. During early embryogenesis these cilia generate the left-ward fluid flow that establishes left–right body asymmetry. Loss-of-function DNAH11 variants therefore cause primary ciliary dyskinesia accompanied by laterality defects such as situs inversus, illustrating their critical role in nodal cilia function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prophylactic salpingo-oophorectomy",
    "long_answer": "Women who carry deleterious BRCA1 mutations are advised to undergo risk-reducing (prophylactic) bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy—surgical removal of both ovaries and fallopian tubes—after completion of childbearing, typically by age 35–40. This intervention eliminates most of the ovarian and fallopian-tube epithelium where high-grade serous carcinomas originate, lowering ovarian cancer incidence by roughly 80–90% and improving overall survival in this high-risk population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal MRI",
    "long_answer": "Gómez–López-Hernández syndrome is characterized by the cerebellar malformation rhombencephalosynapsis. High-resolution fetal magnetic resonance imaging can depict this mid-hind-brain anomaly in utero, making fetal MRI the imaging technique capable of establishing the diagnosis prenatally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guanosine triphosphate (GTP)",
    "long_answer": "In Lesch–Nyhan syndrome the loss of HGPRT blocks the purine-salvage pathway, so guanine cannot be recycled to GMP and then to GTP. The resulting cerebral deficiency of GTP (a guanine nucleotide) impairs tetrahydrobiopterin and dopamine synthesis in the basal ganglia, which is believed to underlie the characteristic neurological and behavioral abnormalities of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burning mouth syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Burning mouth syndrome (BMS) is a chronic condition in which patients experience a persistent burning or scalding sensation—often on the tongue, lips, palate, or entire oral mucosa—despite normal clinical examination and laboratory findings. Because no local or systemic medical cause can be identified, it is classified as an idiopathic orofacial pain disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "In the lateral cervical region near the nerve ring",
    "long_answer": "Deirids are a pair of small cuticular sensory papillae found in Secernentea nematodes on the lateral lines of the neck (cervical area), typically at or just posterior to the level of the oesophageal nerve ring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs",
    "long_answer": "In Caplan's syndrome, the rheumatoid arthritis component is managed with disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs). These agents—such as methotrexate, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, or biologic DMARDs—slow the underlying autoimmune inflammation, limit joint destruction, and improve long-term function, while the pulmonary nodules usually require no specific therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are commonly prescribed for hypochondriasis (now termed illness-anxiety disorder). By blocking the serotonin transporter, they prevent reuptake of serotonin into presynaptic neurons, thereby raising extracellular serotonin levels in the brain and alleviating anxiety-related symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The very first clinical report of human uniparental disomy (Spence et al., 1988) described a child with cystic fibrosis who had inherited two maternal copies of chromosome 7 (maternal isodisomy 7). This case established chromosome 7 as the chromosome involved in the first documented instance of uniparental disomy causing human disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cleft palate",
    "long_answer": "Trimethadione is a well-established teratogen. Maternal use during early pregnancy produces the fetal trimethadione syndrome, whose hallmark craniofacial defect is a cleft palate (often with or without cleft lip), caused by disruption of palatal shelf fusion in the first trimester."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasminogen deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Ligneous conjunctivitis occurs in people with congenital plasminogen (type I) deficiency. Because plasmin is required to lyse fibrin and remodel extracellular matrix, lack of plasminogen prevents normal fibrinolysis during tissue repair, leading to fibrin-rich ‘woody’ pseudomembranes and markedly impaired wound healing on the conjunctiva and other mucous membranes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen insensitivity syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The condition is called estrogen insensitivity (or estrogen resistance) syndrome. It is an extremely rare disorder caused by inactivating mutations in the ESR1 gene that render estrogen receptors non-functional, so tissues cannot respond to circulating estrogens. This receptor failure underlies the intersex/sexual-development abnormalities seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renin",
    "long_answer": "In scleroderma renal crisis, vascular injury in the kidney stimulates juxtaglomerular cells to release the enzyme renin. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, initiating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system cascade that drives severe vasoconstriction, sodium retention, and malignant hypertension characteristic of the crisis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX1",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge (22q11.2 deletion) syndrome commonly results from haploinsufficiency of the TBX1 gene. TBX1 encodes a T-box transcription factor that directs patterning and morphogenesis of the pharyngeal arches and their derivatives—including the thymus, parathyroids, and great cardiac vessels—explaining the characteristic craniofacial, cardiac, and immunologic defects seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Type I cryoglobulinemia is classically linked to B-cell lymphoproliferative disorders. Among the leukemias, chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is the one most consistently associated because the malignant B-cells secrete a monoclonal immunoglobulin that precipitates in the cold, forming cryoglobulins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor and whose mutations cause Hay–Wells (ankyloblepharon-ectodermal defects-cleft lip/palate) syndrome, is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 3 at band 3q28."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polysplenia syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Polysplenia syndrome, a form of heterotaxy, is defined by the presence of multiple small accessory spleens rather than a single normal spleen. It results from disturbed left–right body patterning, a developmental process that depends on motile embryonic cilia. Defects in ciliary structure or function (primary ciliary dyskinesia) therefore predispose to laterality errors such as polysplenia, making the two conditions frequently associated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carpenter syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is an autosomal-recessive craniosynostosis syndrome caused by mutations in RAB23 or MEGF8. Its cardinal features include craniosynostosis, pre- and post-axial polysyndactyly/syndactyly, early-onset obesity, and proportionate short stature, fitting the clinical picture described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "James B. Pollack",
    "long_answer": "James B. Pollack, the noted NASA planetary scientist who studied the atmospheres of Mars, Venus and Titan, was afflicted with esophageal achalasia—a rare motility disorder in which degeneration of Auerbach’s plexus prevents relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter. The resultant dysphagia and aspiration contributed to the respiratory complications that led to his death in 1994."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Relative bradycardia",
    "long_answer": "In untreated typhoid fever the heart rate is often slower than would be expected for the degree of fever—a phenomenon called relative bradycardia (Faget sign). This pulse-temperature dissociation is a well-described clinical finding in typhoid, attributed to Salmonella Typhi endotoxin effects on the cardiac conduction system and autonomic regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary mucoepithelial dysplasia is an autosomal-dominant ectodermal disorder that primarily affects the skin, hair, nails, and mucous membranes; management centers on monitoring and treating these cutaneous and mucosal manifestations. Consequently, dermatologists—specialists in diseases of the skin and its appendages—are the medical professionals most directly involved in the ongoing care of patients with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Activates adenylyl cyclase",
    "long_answer": "When the luteinizing hormone/choriogonadotropin receptor is stimulated, it couples to the Gs heterotrimeric G protein. The G-protein’s alpha subunit (Gαs) exchanges GDP for GTP, dissociates from the βγ subunits, and directly activates membrane adenylyl cyclase, leading to increased intracellular cyclic AMP and downstream signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Fechtner syndrome is an Alport-related MYH9 disorder that, in addition to nephritis, sensorineural deafness, cataracts and leukocyte inclusions, is distinguished by a reduced platelet count—i.e., thrombocytopenia (often manifesting as macrothrombocytopenia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating chemotherapeutic agent included in standard mantle cell lymphoma regimens such as R-CHOP and VR-CAP. Its hepatic metabolism produces the urotoxic metabolite acrolein, which can damage the bladder epithelium and lead to hemorrhagic cystitis, a characteristic dose-limiting adverse effect that is often prevented with vigorous hydration and MESNA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Congenital absence (aplasia) of the gluteal muscles occurring in association with spina bifida occulta has been reported mainly in affected siblings born to unaffected parents, a pattern characteristic of autosomal-recessive inheritance. No sex bias or parent-to-child transmission has been documented, further supporting an autosomal recessive mode of transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pilocarpine eye drops",
    "long_answer": "Adie (tonic) pupils respond to topical cholinergic miotics; dilute pilocarpine eye drops stimulate the denervated sphincter muscle, constricting the pupil and improving accommodation, thus treating the pupillary symptoms of Adie syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMM2-CDG (CDG-Ia)",
    "long_answer": "The most prevalent form of congenital disorder of glycosylation, CDG type Ia caused by phosphomannomutase-2 (PMM2) deficiency, typically manifests with neurological features dominated by cerebellar involvement; patients characteristically develop truncal and limb ataxia along with hypotonia and developmental delay. Thus, PMM2-CDG is the subtype most commonly associated with ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ataxia-telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "Ataxia-telangiectasia (ATM gene mutation) is the most frequently encountered inherited disorder that presents with both cerebellar ataxia and extrapyramidal choreiform movements. The progressive cerebellar degeneration produces ataxia, while basal-ganglia involvement gives rise to chorea/athetosis, making it a characteristic and relatively common inherited cause of the combined symptom pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi anemia",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia is an inherited chromosomal-instability syndrome that markedly increases the risk of malignancies (especially acute myeloid leukemia and solid tumors). Among its characteristic congenital anomalies are radial-ray defects, including hypoplastic radius, absent thumbs, and congenital radioulnar synostosis, making Fanconi anemia the tumor-predisposition syndrome classically associated with radioulnar synostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EEC syndrome",
    "long_answer": "EEC (Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal Dysplasia-Cleft) syndrome is a well-described autosomal-dominant disorder in which affected individuals can show complete or partial absence of the tibia together with orofacial clefts, most often cleft lip and/or cleft palate. The syndrome’s core triad is limb malformations (classically split hand/foot or other long-bone defects such as tibial aplasia), ectodermal dysplasia features, and cleft lip/palate, making it the canonical condition linking absent tibia with a cleft lip."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reed–Sternberg cells",
    "long_answer": "Hodgkin lymphoma is diagnosed pathologically by finding Reed–Sternberg cells—large, often binucleate or multinucleate B-cell–derived cells with prominent eosinophilic nucleoli—in an appropriate reactive inflammatory background on lymph node biopsy. Their presence is considered pathognomonic for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Ethanol metabolism raises the cytosolic NADH/NAD⁺ ratio, driving pyruvate toward lactate. If the enzyme pyruvate dehydrogenase is deficient (as can occur in thiamine-dependent alcoholics or in genetic PDH complex defects), pyruvate cannot enter the tricarboxylic acid cycle, accumulates, and is further reduced to lactate, producing marked lactic acidosis during ethanol poisoning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Choroidal neovascularization",
    "long_answer": "In punctate inner choroiditis, disruption of Bruch’s membrane and the retinal pigment epithelium frequently triggers growth of abnormal choroidal blood vessels—choroidal neovascularization (CNV). This sight-threatening complication is also well documented in other white dot syndromes such as multifocal choroiditis and presumed ocular histoplasmosis, making CNV the shared and most common serious sequela."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Boucher-Neuhäuser syndrome is a hereditary neurodegenerative disorder whose core triad includes cerebellar ataxia. Because the prominent, progressive neurological manifestation is cerebellar dysfunction, patients are most commonly evaluated and followed by a neurologist, with additional subspecialty input (endocrinology for hypogonadism and ophthalmology for retinal dystrophy) as needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPC1",
    "long_answer": "GPC1 (glypican-1) has been identified by genome-wide association studies as a susceptibility gene for biliary atresia. Glypican-1 is a cell-surface heparan-sulfate proteoglycan that modulates Hedgehog ligand presentation and signalling, and it also participates in the control of inflammatory responses. Thus, GPC1 links biliary atresia risk with regulation of inflammation and Hedgehog pathway activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Quadriceps femoris",
    "long_answer": "Osgood–Schlatter disease is produced by repetitive traction of the patellar tendon where the quadriceps attach to the tibial tubercle. Conditioning programmes that focus on strengthening the quadriceps femoris (especially the vastus medialis) improve knee-extensor control and reduce the relative tensile load on the apophysis, thereby helping to lessen pain and facilitate recovery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Postcapillary venules",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis targets the smallest veins in the skin—the postcapillary venules. Immune-complex deposition occurs predominantly in these vessels, leading to neutrophilic inflammation and vessel wall necrosis that produce the characteristic palpable purpura."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystine",
    "long_answer": "Cystine stones arise in patients with cystinuria, a hereditary defect in renal re-absorption of the dibasic amino acids. Poorly soluble cystine (the oxidized dimer of cysteine) precipitates in urine, leading to recurrent kidney stones. Thus, the amino acid cystine is directly linked to an increased risk of nephrolithiasis through formation of characteristic cystine calculi."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edwards syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome, also called trisomy 18, is caused by the presence of an extra (third) copy of chromosome 18. The chromosomal imbalance leads to multiple congenital anomalies; a hallmark prenatal finding is severe intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mumps virus infection",
    "long_answer": "Primary ovarian insufficiency can be a late consequence of mumps virus infection. The paramyxovirus that causes mumps can produce oophoritis (inflammation of the ovaries), and in some women this inflammatory damage results in loss of follicular reserve and premature cessation of ovarian function, leading to primary ovarian insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary biliary cholangitis",
    "long_answer": "Primary biliary cholangitis (formerly primary biliary cirrhosis) is the autoimmune liver disease characteristically accompanied by non-caseating granulomas within portal tracts. These granulomatous lesions reflect the immune-mediated destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts that defines the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coracidium stage",
    "long_answer": "After Diphyllobothrium eggs reach fresh water they hatch into a ciliated, free-swimming larva called the coracidium. This coracidium is physiologically adapted to an external aquatic environment, whereas the adult worm lives attached to the mucosa of the definitive host’s small intestine. Thus the coracidium stage is the life-cycle phase adapted to a markedly different environment from that of the adult form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital muscular dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "In congenital muscular dystrophy, infants and children often develop severe joint contractures and progressive scoliosis. Orthopaedic procedures such as tendon-release or spinal fusion are therefore frequently undertaken to correct these deformities, lessen pain, and enhance mobility, thereby improving overall quality of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystoid macular edema",
    "long_answer": "In intermediate uveitis the chronic inflammation and breakdown of the blood-retinal barrier often lead to cystoid macular edema (CME). Persistent CME can destroy photoreceptors and cause irreversible macular scarring, making it one of the main causes of permanent vision loss in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Congenital hypoprothrombinemia results from pathogenic variants in the coagulation factor II (F2) gene. Cytogenetic mapping places the F2 gene on the short arm of chromosome 11 (band 11p11-q12), making chromosome 11 the chromosomal location implicated in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈35 %",
    "long_answer": "Only about one-third of people who show a positive local contracture after an intramuscular halothane injection are confirmed to be malignant-hyperthermia-susceptible when retested with the standard in-vitro caffeine/halothane contracture test; studies place the positive predictive value at roughly 35 %, highlighting the test’s limited reliability for diagnosing MH susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Trichostasis spinulosa is a follicular disorder of the skin and hair shafts. Diagnosis relies on clinical examination and dermatoscopic or histologic evaluation of the pilosebaceous unit, and management may include topical keratolytics or laser therapy. These evaluations and treatments fall squarely within the expertise of a dermatologist—a physician specially trained in disorders of the skin, hair, and nails—making a dermatologist the appropriate specialist to diagnose and treat trichostasis spinulosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Night blindness",
    "long_answer": "Nyctalopia (night blindness) is a hallmark of retinitis pigmentosa because rod photoreceptors, which mediate dim-light vision, degenerate first. In the cone-rod type of congenital amaurosis with congenital hypertrichosis, rod dysfunction is likewise present early, so affected individuals also experience profound difficulty seeing in low-light conditions. Therefore, night blindness is a common symptom shared by both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Generalized vulvodynia",
    "long_answer": "Generalized vulvodynia—also called dysesthetic vulvodynia—is characterized by diffuse, spontaneous vulvar pain described as burning, stinging, or electric-shock sensations. These neuropathic, neuralgia-like symptoms distinguish it from localized or provoked forms of vulvodynia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Congenital ichthyosiform erythroderma is a genetic disorder characterized by generalized scaling and erythroderma of the skin from birth. Because it primarily affects the skin’s structure and function, its diagnosis, genetic counseling, and long-term management fall under the expertise of dermatology, the medical specialty dedicated to disorders of the skin, hair, and nails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prilocaine",
    "long_answer": "Among the local anesthetics, prilocaine is well known for producing clinically significant methemoglobinemia. Prilocaine is metabolized to o-toluidine, an oxidizing agent that converts the ferrous iron (Fe2+) in hemoglobin to the ferric form (Fe3+), generating methemoglobin that cannot carry oxygen. High doses or topical/IV regional use of prilocaine have repeatedly produced this complication in clinical reports and are specifically cautioned against for this reason."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superficial, attention-seeking interactions",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with histrionic personality disorder typically have adequate social skills but deploy them primarily to attract attention and approval. Their interactions are often dramatic, rapidly shifting, and shallow, which can make relationships superficial and less authentic despite apparent sociability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ascope Province",
    "long_answer": "In 2010, Peru’s Ministry of Health and the World Health Organization reported an outbreak of plague caused by Yersinia pestis in the La Libertad Region. Pneumonic cases were specifically documented in the Chocope District of Ascope Province, leading to a health emergency declaration for that province."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothermia",
    "long_answer": "Shapiro syndrome classically presents with recurrent episodes of spontaneous hypothermia (marked falls in core body temperature). Hypothermia is likewise the primary factor that limits the duration a person can safely swim in cold water, because prolonged exposure causes a dangerous drop in body temperature. Thus, hypothermia is both a hallmark symptom of Shapiro syndrome and the major physiological limitation during cold-water swimming."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Terminal ileum",
    "long_answer": "Hirschsprung’s disease is almost always confined to the rectum-sigmoid region, but in rare ‘long-segment’ or ‘total colonic aganglionosis’ variants the aganglionic segment extends proximally through the whole colon and can reach the terminal ileum of the small intestine. Thus, in these uncommon cases the terminal ileum is the intestinal segment that becomes involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Male hemizygosity for the X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Males possess only one X chromosome (are hemizygous); if that single X carries a dominant pathogenic mutation, every cell expresses the defective gene with no normal allele to counterbalance it. Females have two X chromosomes, so a normal copy on the second X and mosaicism from random X-inactivation typically dampen the mutation’s impact, making the condition less severe and less often lethal in females."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemangioblastoma",
    "long_answer": "Hemangioblastomas are highly vascular tumors of the central nervous system that characteristically arise in the cerebellum, brainstem, or spinal cord and occur with increased frequency in patients with Von Hippel–Lindau (VHL) syndrome due to germ-line VHL gene mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinemia type II is an inherited inborn error of metabolism caused by deficiency of tyrosine aminotransferase. Diagnosis requires biochemical and molecular genetic testing, and treatment involves specialized dietary management under metabolic protocols. These activities are routinely performed by physicians trained in medical genetics (often called metabolic geneticists), the specialty that manages hereditary metabolic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eosinophils",
    "long_answer": "Langerhans cell histiocytosis lesions contain proliferating Langerhans-type dendritic cells admixed with large numbers of eosinophils; the abundance of this granulocyte led to the historical term “eosinophilic granuloma.” Thus, eosinophils are the granulocytes characteristically involved in the formation of LCH lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "Social anxiety disorder (social phobia) is most effectively and safely treated first-line with cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT). High-quality trials and guidelines (e.g., NICE, APA) show that individual CBT using exposure, cognitive restructuring, and social-skills training produces durable remission and outperforms placebos and many pharmacologic agents, making it the recommended initial intervention."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Dextrocardia with situs ambiguus (heterotaxy) is a congenital malposition of the thoracic and abdominal organs that is typically managed by pediatric cardiologists and cardiothoracic or general pediatric surgeons, because the major surgical issues involve complex intracardiac repairs, great-vessel rerouting, and occasionally abdominal organ malrotation or splenic anomalies. Otolaryngology–head and neck (ENT) surgeons specialize in diseases of the ear, nose, throat, and related head-and-neck structures; they do not operate on the heart, great vessels, or abdominal viscera. Therefore, an ENT surgeon would not ordinarily be part of the surgical team treating dextrocardia with situs ambiguus unless there were an unrelated ear-, nose-, or airway-specific problem. Consequently, the statement that an ENT surgeon would be involved in the surgical treatment of dextrocardia situs ambiguus is not supported by standard medical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bayer",
    "long_answer": "The German pharmaceutical company Bayer synthesized suramin (originally called “Bayer 205”) in 1916; this drug proved to be the first reliably effective and safe therapy for human African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness), replacing the highly toxic arsenical atoxyl that had been used previously."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amylase",
    "long_answer": "Serum amylase is a digestive enzyme produced mainly by the pancreas (and salivary glands). When pancreatic tissue is injured or inflamed—such as in acute pancreatitis—cellular contents leak into the bloodstream, raising circulating amylase levels. Because amylase is released whenever these tissues are damaged, an elevated serum amylase concentration is a recognized biochemical indicator of tissue damage and is routinely used in the diagnosis and monitoring of pancreatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parakeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Parakeratosis refers to abnormal keratinization in which pyknotic nuclei are retained within the cells of the stratum corneum. This histological change is a characteristic feature of several papulosquamous dermatoses and can be seen in lesions associated with lichen planus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-traumatic stress disorder",
    "long_answer": "Reactive attachment disorder (RAD) is grouped with trauma- and stressor-related disorders in DSM-5 because its core pathology arises from early severe neglect or abuse. Neurobiological studies show that children with RAD exhibit alterations in stress-response systems—blunted or dysregulated hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis activity, abnormal cortisol rhythms, and limbic (amygdala/hippocampal) changes—patterns that closely mirror findings in post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Hence PTSD is often cited as having a similar neurobiological basis to RAD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SATB2",
    "long_answer": "SATB2 (Special AT-rich sequence-binding protein 2) is located at chromosome 2q33.1. Haplo-insufficiency or mutation of SATB2 disrupts craniofacial development, giving rise to cleft lip and/or cleft palate. 2q32-q33 microdeletions that remove SATB2 were first described in patients diagnosed with acrofrontofacionasal dysostosis, establishing this gene as the molecular link between the dysostosis and the clefting phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Labetalol",
    "long_answer": "Intravenous labetalol, a combined α- and β-adrenergic blocker, is recommended for rapid blood-pressure reduction in hypertensive emergencies such as hypertensive encephalopathy. It effectively lowers systemic vascular resistance without markedly decreasing uteroplacental blood flow and has an established safety profile in pregnancy, making it a preferred agent for pregnant women with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "White matter",
    "long_answer": "Canavan’s disease is a leukodystrophy caused by ASPA deficiency that leads to accumulation of N-acetylaspartate and spongy degeneration of the cerebral white matter, damaging the myelin that normally insulates nerve fibers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal process of the maxilla",
    "long_answer": "Traumatic telecanthus arises when the medial canthal ligament loses its normal bony attachment. The ligament is anchored to the anterior lacrimal crest, which is part of the frontal process of the maxilla. Fracture or displacement of this portion of the maxilla (as in naso-orbito-ethmoidal injuries) allows the ligament and medial eyelids to splay laterally, producing the increased intercanthal distance characteristic of traumatic telecanthus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neisseria",
    "long_answer": "Members of the genus Neisseria are gram-negative diplococci; Neisseria gonorrhoeae is the agent of gonorrhea, while its close relative Neisseria meningitidis is a leading cause of bacterial meningitis. Thus the Neisseria genus encompasses organisms capable of causing both diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Michael Cohen Jr.",
    "long_answer": "The current standard clinical classification of Pfeiffer syndrome into types I (classic), II (with cloverleaf skull) and III (severe without cloverleaf skull) was proposed and published by the craniofacial geneticist Michael Cohen Jr. in the early 1990s; his scheme remains the most widely accepted framework for describing the syndrome’s clinical variability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrectomy",
    "long_answer": "In fulminant or necrotizing pyelonephritis where the renal tissue is extensively destroyed and infection cannot be controlled by antibiotics or drainage, surgical removal of the affected kidney (nephrectomy) may be required to eliminate the source of sepsis and preserve overall health."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dura mater",
    "long_answer": "Experimental and clinical studies show that the underlying dura mater directs osteogenic signaling at cranial sutures; abnormalities in its signaling can trigger premature osteoblastic activity and fusion, making the dura mater the meningeal layer implicated in craniosynostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenomegaly",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease is characterized by systemic inflammation, widespread lymph-node involvement, and enlargement of reticuloendothelial organs. One of the most frequent organ manifestations is splenic enlargement (splenomegaly), reported in more than half of iMCD patients and incorporated as a minor diagnostic criterion in current consensus guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polycystic ovary syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fowler’s syndrome, a cause of urinary retention in young women due to abnormal urethral sphincter activity, shows a strong association with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). PCOS is the most common endocrine disorder in women of reproductive age, characterized by hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction, and polycystic ovarian morphology. Studies report a high prevalence of PCOS among women diagnosed with Fowler’s syndrome, linking the two conditions clinically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Viral infection",
    "long_answer": "Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood (TEC) typically appears several weeks after a self-limited febrile illness. Most studies implicate a preceding viral infection—parvovirus B19, enteroviruses, adenovirus, EBV, and others—as the stimulus that transiently suppresses erythroid precursors. Thus, a nonspecific viral infection is considered the usual trigger for TEC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loading dose",
    "long_answer": "A loading dose is an initial, larger-than-maintenance dose administered to rapidly raise the plasma concentration of a drug to the therapeutic range. In conditions such as pheochromocytoma, where prompt blockade of excess catecholamine effects is crucial, a loading dose accelerates attainment of effective drug levels before subsequent maintenance dosing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain MRI",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain is often obtained to exclude structural or neurological lesions (tumors, strokes, demyelination, frontal-temporal abnormalities) that can mimic or precipitate manic episodes, thereby helping to rule out secondary causes of mania in patients presumed to have Bipolar I disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CASR gene",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia results from inactivating germ-line mutations in the calcium-sensing receptor (CASR) gene. The defective receptor perceives serum calcium as low, shifting the set-point for parathyroid hormone secretion and renal calcium reabsorption, producing lifelong mild hypercalcaemia with relative hypocalciuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zika virus",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies during the 2015–2016 outbreaks in Brazil and other countries showed a sharp rise in infants born with microcephaly that correlated with maternal infection by Zika virus during pregnancy. Virological and pathological investigations demonstrated that Zika virus crosses the placenta, infects fetal neural progenitor cells, and disrupts neurodevelopment, establishing a causal link between Zika virus infection and congenital microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome results from micro-deletions at chromosome 11p13 that encompass the PAX6 gene. Loss of one copy of PAX6 not only causes the aniridia seen in WAGR but also disrupts brain development, leading to neurological manifestations such as intellectual disability, epilepsy, and structural brain malformations. Thus, deletion of PAX6 on chromosome 11 links WAGR syndrome with neurological conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MDM2",
    "long_answer": "Atypical lipomatous tumors (well-differentiated liposarcomas) almost uniformly show high-level amplification of the MDM2 oncogene, which resides on chromosome 12q15. Detection of MDM2 amplification by FISH or PCR is a standard diagnostic marker distinguishing these tumors from benign lipomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reactive arthritis (Reiter syndrome)",
    "long_answer": "Reactive arthritis—also known as Reiter syndrome—is an autoimmune spondyloarthropathy that typically presents with the classic triad of arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis. The autoimmune inflammation is triggered by a preceding gastrointestinal or genitourinary infection, and ocular involvement commonly manifests as conjunctivitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Macrophagic myofasciitis (MMF) is a specific histopathological lesion seen in muscle biopsies, characterized by dense aggregates of PAS-positive macrophages that contain aluminum hydroxide adjuvant crystals within their cytoplasm. The diagnosis is supported by special stains (e.g., aluminon), energy-dispersive X-ray microanalysis, or electron microscopy demonstrating aluminum—not by detection of potassium iodide. Potassium iodide plays no role in the pathologic identification or confirmation of MMF. Therefore, the statement that MMF is confirmed by the presence of potassium iodide is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHH gene",
    "long_answer": "The Sonic Hedgehog (SHH) gene encodes the SHH morphogen that is secreted by the zone of polarizing activity in the developing limb bud. If the DNA sequence of the SHH gene itself is mutated or deleted, transcription of the gene is reduced or the mRNA/protein produced is defective, leading to decreased SHH protein levels and consequent disturbances in limb patterning along the anterior-posterior axis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unknown (idiopathic)",
    "long_answer": "Cronkhite–Canada syndrome has no firmly established causative agent; current evidence indicates an idiopathic origin, with autoimmune mechanisms only suspected but unproven."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral blood smear",
    "long_answer": "A Wright-Giemsa–stained peripheral blood smear allows direct microscopic examination of red cells, where the DNA remnants known as Howell-Jolly bodies appear as dark, round inclusions. In hereditary spherocytosis, hyposplenism or postsplenectomy reduces splenic clearance of these inclusions, so the smear is the standard test that reveals Howell-Jolly bodies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phase 1/2 clinical trial",
    "long_answer": "Efficacy of the mRNA therapy for methylmalonic acidemia is being investigated in an interventional Phase 1/2 clinical trial in affected patients, where dosing, safety, pharmacodynamics and therapeutic benefit (e.g., reductions in methylmalonic acid levels) are systematically measured."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrocalcinosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic therapy for hypoparathyroidism relies on high-dose oral calcium and active vitamin D. Because parathyroid hormone is absent, the kidneys cannot efficiently reabsorb calcium; the excess filtered calcium leads to persistent hypercalciuria. Over years, this promotes calcium deposition within the renal parenchyma, causing nephrocalcinosis, a recognized long-term complication of conventional hypoparathyroidism treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycoproteins",
    "long_answer": "Fucosidosis is caused by a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme α-L-fucosidase. Without this enzyme, fucose-containing oligosaccharide side-chains cannot be removed and degraded, leading to the intralysosomal build-up of fucose-rich glycoproteins (and related glycolipids). Thus, the principal macromolecules that accumulate in tissues and body fluids in fucosidosis are glycoproteins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Activates p53-mediated DNA-damage program",
    "long_answer": "Doxorubicin intercalates DNA and poisons topoisomerase II, creating double-strand breaks that activate ATM/ATR kinases. This rapidly stabilises and activates p53, leading to a characteristic transcriptional programme: up-regulation of DNA-damage-response, cell-cycle-arrest and apoptotic genes (e.g., CDKN1A/p21, GADD45, BAX) and concurrent repression of many proliferation-associated transcripts. Thus, the net effect on the cellular transcriptome is a p53-dependent shift toward damage-response and death pathways while suppressing genes needed for growth and division."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gorlin syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Gorlin syndrome (also called nevoid basal-cell carcinoma syndrome) has been historically described in the pathology literature as “cystic hamartoma of the lung and kidney,” reflecting the multiple hamartomatous cysts that can develop in both organs alongside the better-known skin and jaw manifestations caused by germ-line PTCH1 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPN1MW",
    "long_answer": "The most prevalent type of colour-blindness is red-green (specifically deuteranomaly/deuteranopia), which arises from mutations or unequal recombination affecting the medium-wavelength (green-sensitive) opsin gene OPN1MW located at Xq28 on the X chromosome. Defects in OPN1MW disrupt normal green-cone photopigment function, producing the characteristic red-green vision deficiency that is most common in the population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune neuromuscular disorder in which antibodies target acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. This impairs synaptic transmission, producing fluctuating skeletal-muscle weakness. Bulbar muscles that control swallowing and speech are commonly affected, so patients frequently experience dysphagia and difficulty chewing, especially after prolonged activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant connective-tissue disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix glycoprotein fibrillin-1. Defective fibrillin-1 disrupts microfibril formation, leading to the characteristic skeletal, ocular (lens dislocation), and cardiovascular (aortic root dilatation) manifestations of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whitaker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AIRE (Autoimmune Regulator) gene lead to autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1, historically known as Whitaker syndrome. Loss-of-function variants in AIRE impair central T-cell tolerance, resulting in multiorgan autoimmunity (chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, etc.). Hence, the disease beginning with “W” tied to AIRE mutations is Whitaker syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "B lymphocyte",
    "long_answer": "Hodgkin lymphoma arises from malignant transformation of germinal-center B lymphocytes; the characteristic Reed–Sternberg cells that define the disease are clonally derived from these B cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOD2 on chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Blau syndrome is an autosomal-dominant granulomatous disease produced by gain-of-function mutations in the NOD2 (also known as CARD15) gene. This gene resides at chromosomal band 16q12, so pathogenic NOD2 variants on chromosome 16 are responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Real-time prenatal ultrasonography can directly visualize amniotic (constriction) bands encircling fetal limbs or digits and the resulting distal edema or deformities, thereby confirming constriction ring syndrome in utero."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma",
    "long_answer": "In the punctate form of palmoplantar keratoderma, the lesions present as numerous discrete hyperkeratotic papules scattered over the palms and soles. This distinguishes it from the diffuse and focal forms, which show continuous thickening or localized plaques rather than individual papules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucosal biopsy",
    "long_answer": "The definitive way to diagnose and confirm oral lichen planus is to take an incisional mucosal biopsy of the affected area and examine the tissue histopathologically; the characteristic band-like lymphocytic infiltrate and basal cell degeneration seen microscopically establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatology",
    "long_answer": "Andersen’s disease (glycogen storage disease type IV) leads to abnormal glycogen deposition in the liver, progressing to hepatomegaly, cirrhosis, and liver failure. The medical specialty focused on diagnosing and managing liver disorders—hepatology (a branch of gastroenterology)—is therefore the primary specialty involved in treating these hepatic complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STAR",
    "long_answer": "Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia results from loss-of-function mutations in the STAR gene, which encodes the steroidogenic acute regulatory protein that transports cholesterol into mitochondria for steroid biosynthesis. The STAR gene is mapped to chromosome 8p11.2, and its disruption abolishes adrenal and gonadal steroid production, producing the classic lipoid CAH phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenic vein",
    "long_answer": "Banti’s syndrome—chronic congestive (hypersplenic) portal hypertension with massive splenomegaly—classically arises when the splenic vein becomes obstructed or thrombosed. Blockage of this vein impedes venous outflow from the spleen, producing sustained splenic congestion, portal-hypertensive changes, cytopenias and the clinical picture described by Banti."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Aniridia is a congenital disorder characterized by partial or complete absence of the iris and associated ocular abnormalities such as glaucoma, cataract, and foveal hypoplasia. Management of these eye‐specific problems falls under the expertise of ophthalmologists, the physicians who diagnose and treat diseases of the eye. Therefore, the relevant medical specialty is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obstructive hydrocephalus",
    "long_answer": "The cerebral aqueduct connects the third and fourth ventricles; if it becomes occluded after a traumatic brain injury, cerebrospinal fluid can no longer pass downstream. This causes CSF to accumulate in the lateral and third ventricles, enlarging them and raising intracranial pressure—a pattern characteristic of non-communicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an inherited (codominant) disorder in which misfolded α1-antitrypsin accumulates in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum. The resulting chronic hepatitis and cirrhosis markedly raise the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma, making this genetic disease a well-established predisposing factor for HCC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GBE1",
    "long_answer": "Andersen’s disease, the classic glycogen storage disease type IV, is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the GBE1 gene, which encodes the glycogen-branching enzyme. The same GBE1 mutations have been documented in families presenting with congenital arthrogryposis multiplex congenita (fetal akinesia deformation sequence), showing that defects in this single gene can lead to both Andersen’s disease and a form of arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurocutaneous syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Phakomatosis pigmentovascularis is a rare congenital neurocutaneous syndrome characterized by the simultaneous presence of vascular malformations (capillary malformations) and pigmentary skin lesions (nevus flammeus with dermal melanocytosis). As a member of the phakomatoses, it involves developmental anomalies of tissues derived from the neuroectoderm, linking skin findings with potential ocular or neurologic manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tietze syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Tietze syndrome is an inflammatory disorder of the costal cartilage that, like costochondritis, causes sharp chest wall pain. However, it is distinguished by visible or palpable swelling at the affected costochondral junctions, a feature typically absent in costochondritis. Because the pain locations and character are similar, the two conditions are frequently confused clinically, but the presence of localized swelling points to Tietze syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Irreversible brain damage",
    "long_answer": "During ventricular fibrillation the ventricles quiver instead of pumping, so cardiac output and cerebral blood flow fall to near zero. Neurons tolerate only a few minutes without oxygen; after about 3–5 minutes of this global cerebral hypoxia-ischemia, neuronal death becomes permanent, producing irreversible brain damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Charles Howard Usher",
    "long_answer": "The Scottish ophthalmologist Charles Howard Usher systematically studied families in which retinitis pigmentosa occurred together with congenital deafness and, in a 1914 report, established this combination as a single inherited disorder. His work defined the condition now called Usher syndrome—retinitis pigmentosa linked with deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laryngospasm",
    "long_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism leads to hypocalcaemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and can cause tetany of the laryngeal muscles. This may precipitate laryngospasm, producing stridor and acute airway obstruction, a recognised respiratory complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cheilitis",
    "long_answer": "Isotretinoin markedly reduces sebaceous gland activity, causing mucocutaneous dryness. More than 90 % of patients develop cheilitis—dry, inflamed, and sometimes fissured lips—as a characteristic and very common adverse effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROR2",
    "long_answer": "Robinow syndrome can arise from mutations in several genes; the autosomal-recessive form is most often due to mutations in the ROR2 gene. ROR2 (receptor tyrosine kinase-like orphan receptor 2) is located on chromosome 9q22, making it the Robinow-associated gene situated on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Among malignancies linked to ionizing radiation, leukemia (particularly acute myeloid and acute lymphoblastic forms) shows the strongest, well-documented association. High-dose radiation can damage hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow, induce DNA double-strand breaks, and generate chromosomal translocations (e.g., t(15;17), t(9;22)), leading to malignant clonal expansion and the development of leukemia. Epidemiological evidence from atomic-bomb survivors, radiotherapy patients, and nuclear-industry accidents consistently demonstrates a markedly increased leukemia incidence after substantial ionizing radiation exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 3–5 years",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is a relentlessly progressive interstitial lung disease; numerous cohort studies show a median survival (and thus average life expectancy) of roughly three to five years from the time of diagnosis, making its prognosis comparable to many malignant cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Lichen myxedematosus (scleromyxedema) is a primary cutaneous mucinosis characterized by papular skin lesions and dermal mucin deposition. Diagnosis and management of this skin-limited or systemic disorder are handled by dermatologists; thus, the relevant medical specialty is dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratolytic therapy",
    "long_answer": "Calluses are thickened areas of stratum corneum. Keratolytic therapy employs agents such as salicylic acid or urea that break down keratin in this outer layer, softening the hyperkeratotic tissue so it loosens and sloughs off, thereby reducing the callus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paranthropus robustus",
    "long_answer": "Dental remains of the South African australopithecine Paranthropus robustus consistently exhibit generalized, symmetrically distributed pitting and thinning of the enamel on many teeth. The uniform, bilateral pattern across the entire dentition matches the diagnostic presentation of the hereditary enamel defect amelogenesis imperfecta, leading paleo-anthropologists to conclude that this extinct hominid species suffered from that condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGF signaling pathway",
    "long_answer": "During gastrulation, cells leaving the primitive streak must undergo an epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) to become the loose embryonic mesenchyme that will give rise to mesodermal tissues. Fibroblast-growth-factor (FGF) signals—particularly FGF8 acting through FGFR1 and the downstream MAPK/ERK cascade—are required for this process. FGF signaling suppresses E-cadherin expression, promotes cell migration, and enables the epiblast cells to delaminate and adopt a mesenchymal phenotype. Loss or inhibition of FGF signaling blocks EMT and mesenchyme formation, underscoring its essential role in embryonic mesenchyme development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDF5",
    "long_answer": "Acromesomelic dysplasia of the Grebe and Hunter-Thompson types is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the GDF5 (growth differentiation factor 5) gene. GDF5 encodes a bone morphogenetic protein crucial for chondrogenesis; it directs proliferation and differentiation of growth-plate chondrocytes and the formation of cartilage and joints. When GDF5 is mutated, normal cartilage development is impaired, resulting in the severe limb-shortening characteristic of acromesomelic dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BMPR2",
    "long_answer": "BMPR2 (bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 2) is the most commonly mutated gene in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension. It encodes a receptor for bone morphogenetic proteins, key regulators of osteoblast differentiation and overall bone formation and resorption, linking the gene directly to bone metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C203R mutation",
    "long_answer": "Both Blue cone monochromacy (BCM) and X-linked cone dystrophy have been repeatedly linked to the same missense change in the X-chromosomal cone opsin genes, a substitution of cysteine with arginine at position 203 (C203R, p.Cys203Arg) in the OPN1LW/OPN1MW opsins. This single point mutation disrupts proper folding and trafficking of the L- and M-cone photopigments, leading to absent or progressively dysfunctional red/green cones—producing the clinical pictures of BCM and cone dystrophy, respectively."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SATB2",
    "long_answer": "SATB2 (Special AT-rich Sequence-Binding Protein 2) encodes a chromatin-organizing transcription factor that recruits histone-modifying enzymes, thereby influencing epigenetic regulation of gene expression during craniofacial development. Pathogenic variants or altered expression of SATB2 have been repeatedly linked to abnormal palatogenesis, leading to cleft lip and/or cleft palate in both syndromic and nonsyndromic cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiculopathy",
    "long_answer": "Compression of spinal nerves or their roots leads to damage and dysfunction of those nerve roots, producing a neuropathy known as radiculopathy. In radiculopathy, mechanical pressure or inflammation at the spinal nerve root impairs sensory and/or motor function in the corresponding dermatome or myotome, distinguishing it from peripheral mononeuropathies or polyneuropathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inversely proportional",
    "long_answer": "Venous compliance reflects how easily veins expand to store blood. When compliance is high, veins accommodate more blood at a given pressure, so less blood is propelled back to the right atrium, lowering venous return. Conversely, when compliance falls (e.g., via venoconstriction), veins become stiffer, their volume decreases, central venous pressure rises, and a larger fraction of blood is returned to the heart. Thus, venous return changes inversely with venous compliance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMAD4",
    "long_answer": "Myhre syndrome is caused by heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the SMAD4 gene, a key intracellular mediator of TGF-β signalling. Independent pathogenic variants in this same gene have also been reported in families with autosomal-dominant keratosis pilaris atrophicans, a recognized subtype of keratosis pilaris. Thus, SMAD4 is the common genetic link between Myhre syndrome and that specific form of keratosis pilaris."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase (the lysosomal enzyme encoded by the GALNS gene) catalyzes the hydrolysis of the 6-sulfate group from N-acetyl-D-galactosamine-6-sulfate residues that occur in certain glycosaminoglycans, chiefly keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate. Removal of this sulfate group is an essential step in the lysosomal degradation (breakdown) pathway of these molecules. When the enzyme is deficient, as in Morquio A (mucopolysaccharidosis IVA), undegraded keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate accumulate within cells. Therefore, by carrying out this desulfation reaction, N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase indeed promotes the normal breakdown of chondroitin sulfate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KAL1 (ANOS1)",
    "long_answer": "Congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism accompanied by anosmia characterizes Kallmann syndrome. The classical and best-established genetic cause of this syndrome is loss-of-function mutations in the KAL1 (now termed ANOS1) gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein anosmin-1 required for migration of olfactory and GnRH neurons. Disruption of KAL1/ANOS1 impairs olfactory bulb development, producing anosmia, and prevents GnRH neurons from reaching the hypothalamus, leading to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamazepine",
    "long_answer": "Carbamazepine, a sodium-channel–blocking anticonvulsant used for focal and generalized tonic-clonic seizures, is contraindicated in absence (petit mal) epilepsy because it can increase thalamocortical burst firing and has been repeatedly shown to precipitate or worsen absence seizures in both clinical studies and practice guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNFRSF1A",
    "long_answer": "TNF receptor associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS) is an autosomal-dominant autoinflammatory disorder caused by pathogenic mutations in the TNFRSF1A gene, which encodes the type 1 tumor necrosis factor receptor (TNF-R1). These mutations impair normal TNF-R1 folding and signaling, leading to recurrent fever episodes and systemic inflammation characteristic of TRAPS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cushing syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cushing syndrome is characterized by chronic excess glucocorticoids, producing central (truncal) obesity and violaceous abdominal striae. Cortisol also exerts mineralocorticoid-like actions and increases vascular responsiveness to catecholamines, leading to secondary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTH1R",
    "long_answer": "An activating mutation in the parathyroid hormone 1 receptor gene (PTH1R), most often the R150C variant, has been documented in patients with both Metachondromatosis and Ollier disease. This gain-of-function change alters PTH/PTHrP signalling in growth-plate cartilage, promoting the formation of multiple enchondromas and osteochondromas that characterize these disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome is a granulomatous disorder marked by recurrent episodes of facial (VII cranial nerve) paralysis, often accompanied by orofacial edema and fissured tongue. It is part of the spectrum of orofacial granulomatosis and can occur in association with other granulomatous diseases such as Crohn’s disease or sarcoidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A2",
    "long_answer": "Achondrogenesis type 1B is an autosomal-recessive, lethal chondrodysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC26A2 gene (also known as DTDST). SLC26A2 encodes a sulfate transporter that is essential for proper sulfation of cartilage proteoglycans; defective sulfate transport disrupts cartilage matrix formation, leading to the severe skeletal abnormalities characteristic of achondrogenesis type 1B."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita is an autoimmune blistering disorder of the skin and mucous membranes. Diagnosis relies on dermatologic evaluation, skin biopsy with immunofluorescence, and serologic testing, and treatment typically involves topical or systemic immunosuppressive therapies overseen by dermatologists. Therefore, the specialty most directly responsible for diagnosing and managing EBA is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoporosis",
    "long_answer": "In hyperprolactinemic women, elevated prolactin suppresses hypothalamic GnRH release, lowering LH and FSH secretion. The resulting hypoestrogenism reduces bone mineral density through increased bone resorption and decreased formation, leading to osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious disease",
    "long_answer": "Brazilian hemorrhagic fever is caused by the Sabiá arenavirus, a highly contagious viral hemorrhagic fever. Management requires expertise in diagnosing, treating, and containing severe viral infections, which falls under the purview of infectious disease specialists, who coordinate antiviral therapy, isolation precautions, and public-health measures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Because the patients are children, the specialty that focuses on the diagnosis and management of health problems in this age group is Pediatrics. Pediatricians are trained to evaluate and treat sleep-related disorders in children, including those precipitated by substances such as caffeine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angiogenesis inhibitor",
    "long_answer": "Drugs that block angiogenesis—most commonly anti-VEGF angiogenesis inhibitors such as bevacizumab—suppress the action of vascular endothelial growth factor, thereby preventing new blood-vessel formation. This same mechanism is used to treat choroidal neovascularization in presumed ocular histoplasmosis syndrome and to restrict tumor blood supply in several cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SRCAP",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous truncating mutations in the SRCAP (SNF2-related CREBBP Activator Protein) gene have been identified in individuals diagnosed with Nasodigitoacoustic (Keipert) syndrome. These loss-of-function variants disrupt SRCAP’s role as a chromatin-remodelling co-activator of CREBBP, leading to the characteristic triad of a broad nasal tip (naso-), brachydactyly/syndactyly (digito-) and sensorineural hearing loss (acoustic). The detection of the same SRCAP mutations in both classic Floating-Harbor syndrome and patients labelled as Nasodigitoacoustic syndrome has established SRCAP haploinsufficiency as the causal mechanism for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAH",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria is most commonly caused by mutations in the PAH gene, which encodes phenylalanine hydroxylase. Loss-of-function variants in PAH impair conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to toxic phenylalanine accumulation. If untreated, this neurotoxicity results in severe cognitive impairment and other mental disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femur-fibula-ulna syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Several dysmorphology reviews note that proximal femoral focal deficiency (PFFD) represents the lower-limb manifestation of, and is probably identical to, the femur-fibula-ulna (FFU) syndrome/complex. Both conditions show congenital absence or severe hypoplasia of the proximal femur, often accompanied by fibular and, variably, ulnar deficiencies, suggesting they are part of the same malformation spectrum rather than separate entities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-traumatic stress disorder",
    "long_answer": "People with post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) commonly experience recurrent, vivid nightmares that replay traumatic events. These persistent distressing dreams can lead to an intense fear of dreaming itself—oneirophobia—because the person dreads reliving the trauma each time they sleep."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wnt/β-catenin pathway",
    "long_answer": "Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy is most often caused by mutations in NDP, FZD4, LRP5 or TSPAN12, all of which encode components of the canonical Wnt (Norrin-FZD4-LRP5) signaling cascade. This Wnt/β-catenin pathway is essential for normal eye morphogenesis and for guiding retinal vascular development; disruption of its signaling leads to the abnormal, incomplete blood vessel growth that characterizes FEVR."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectodermal dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Ectodermal dysplasia is a group of inherited disorders in which tissues derived from the embryonic ectoderm—most notably hair, teeth, nails, and sweat glands—develop abnormally. Mutations in genes such as EDA, EDAR, or EDARADD disrupt signaling pathways (e.g., ectodysplasin-NF-κB) that guide ectodermal organ formation, leading to sparse or absent hair (hypotrichosis), missing or cone-shaped teeth, dystrophic nails, and reduced or absent sweat glands (hypohidrosis or anhidrosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase",
    "long_answer": "The vast majority of classic homocystinuria cases result from a deficiency of the enzyme cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), which catalyzes the conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine in the trans-sulfuration pathway. Loss of CBS activity leads to accumulation of homocysteine and its metabolites, producing the characteristic vascular, ocular, skeletal, and neurodevelopmental manifestations of homocystinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX10",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SOX10 gene, which encodes a transcription factor essential for neural-crest development, are a recognized cause of Waardenburg syndrome type 2E. Because SOX10 is also required for proper maturation of glial cells and neural circuitry, loss-of-function mutations can lead to additional neurodevelopmental abnormalities, and several patients with SOX10 mutations have presented with autism-like behavioral and cognitive symptoms alongside the characteristic pigmentary changes and sensorineural deafness of Waardenburg syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPG7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPG7 gene (encoding the mitochondrial protein paraplegin) are a well-established cause of autosomal-recessive hereditary spastic paraplegia type 7. Numerous clinical and genetic studies have shown that the same SPG7 mutations frequently produce cerebellar ataxia—either isolated or combined with spasticity—demonstrating that this single gene is involved in both hereditary spastic paraplegia and cerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CT scan",
    "long_answer": "A cranial computed tomography (CT) scan rapidly visualizes brain structures and clearly delineates the ventricular system; enlargement of the ventricles on CT indicates cerebrospinal fluid accumulation, making it the standard imaging test for diagnosing hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Quetiapine",
    "long_answer": "Case reports and small clinical observations indicate that the atypical antipsychotic quetiapine can markedly reduce the visual distortions, after-images, and ‘trailing’ phenomena seen in Hallucinogen Persisting Perception Disorder (HPPD). Improvement is thought to stem from quetiapine’s antagonism of 5-HT2A receptors (implicated in psychedelic-induced visual symptoms) together with its relatively low, transient dopamine-D2 occupancy, making it better tolerated than other antipsychotics that often exacerbate HPPD. Thus, quetiapine has emerged as the antipsychotic showing the most promise for treating HPPD-related visual disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periostitis (cortical hyperostosis)",
    "long_answer": "When prostaglandin E1 is infused for prolonged periods to keep the ductus arteriosus open, neonates can develop periostitis—subperiosteal new-bone formation and cortical hyperostosis of the long bones. This bone reaction appears after days to weeks of continuous therapy and regresses once the prostaglandin is discontinued, making it a recognized adverse effect of long-term use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endovascular embolization",
    "long_answer": "For vein of Galen aneurysmal malformations, the current standard of care is endovascular embolization, in which a microcatheter is navigated into the feeding vessels and occlusive materials (e.g., liquid adhesive, coils, or Onyx) are deliberately delivered to create an intravascular embolus. This targeted occlusion reduces the arteriovenous shunt, alleviates high-flow cardiac load, and preserves surrounding brain tissue, making it the preferred treatment over open surgery or conservative management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asfotase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Asfotase alfa is a recombinant form of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase used as enzyme-replacement therapy. It is FDA- and EMA-approved for perinatal/infantile-onset and juvenile-onset hypophosphatasia, where it supplements the deficient alkaline phosphatase activity, reduces accumulation of inorganic pyrophosphate, and thereby promotes normal bone mineralization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Early-onset variant",
    "long_answer": "Current psychiatric consensus (e.g., DSM-5) places selective mutism within the anxiety-disorder spectrum because the vast majority of affected children fulfill criteria for social anxiety disorder. Selective mutism is therefore regarded as an early-onset or more severe variant of social anxiety disorder, not a separate, unrelated condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Camptodactyly and kyphoscoliosis",
    "long_answer": "The hallmarks of Spondylocamptodactyly syndrome are (1) congenital or early-onset camptodactyly—fixed flexion deformity of the fingers—and (2) progressive vertebral dysplasia producing kyphosis/kyphoscoliosis. These two skeletal abnormalities are consistently present and are used clinically to recognise the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amusia",
    "long_answer": "Amusia is a neurological disorder in which individuals cannot perceive or recognize musical tones, melodies, or rhythms, even though their peripheral hearing, language skills, and general intellectual abilities are normal. The condition can be congenital or acquired after brain damage, but in all cases the hallmark is a specific deficit in music perception or production without broader auditory impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Modified acid-fast stain",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporiasis is caused by the coccidian protozoan Cyclospora cayetanensis. Its spherical oocysts in stool become variably acid-fast and appear pink-red when a modified Ziehl-Neelsen (modified acid-fast) stain is applied, allowing definitive microscopic identification of the pathogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Direct (classical) conditioning",
    "long_answer": "According to Rachman’s three-pathway theory and subsequent dental‐anxiety research, most people acquire dental fear after experiencing a painful or traumatic dental visit themselves; this direct personal experience establishes a conditioned association between dental treatment and pain/distress. Therefore, the classical (direct) conditioning pathway is the commonest route through which dental fear develops."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine A",
    "long_answer": "Multicentric carpotarsal osteolysis syndrome frequently evolves into a focal-segmental glomerulosclerosis–type nephropathy. Case reports show that treatment with the calcineurin-inhibitor immunosuppressant cyclosporine A reduced proteinuria and helped stabilise renal function in affected patients, so this drug is recommended when nephropathy develops."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Hemolytic anemia causes accelerated destruction of red blood cells, generating excess unconjugated bilirubin that produces hyperbilirubinemia and jaundice. The increased clearance of damaged RBCs in the reticulo-endothelial system, especially the spleen, leads to splenic hypertrophy and clinically evident splenomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemoglobin A₂",
    "long_answer": "In β-thalassemia, deficient β-globin synthesis causes the body to compensate by increasing production of the δ-globin chains that form Hemoglobin A₂ (α₂δ₂). As a result, patients show a persistently elevated Hb A₂ fraction (typically >3.5% of total hemoglobin), which is the classic laboratory hallmark used in screening and diagnosing β-thalassemia trait and disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STK11",
    "long_answer": "Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a hereditary polyposis and cancer-predisposition disorder caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the STK11 gene (also called LKB1) on chromosome 19p13.3. STK11 encodes a serine/threonine kinase that acts as a tumor-suppressor by regulating cellular polarity and AMPK signaling; its inactivation leads to the hamartomatous polyps and increased malignancy risk characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy",
    "long_answer": "In men with type 1 diabetes, chronic hyperglycaemia often damages the autonomic nerves that supply the penile tissues. This diabetic autonomic neuropathy impairs the neuro-vascular signals required to initiate and maintain an erection, making it one of the most frequent biologic causes of erectile dysfunction in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FREM1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FREM1 gene (FRAS1-related extracellular matrix protein 1) cause the syndrome known as “bifid nose, renal agenesis, and anorectal malformations” (BNAR). Patients with FREM1 mutations characteristically present with a midline bifid nose together with anorectal anomalies (e.g., imperforate anus) and renal defects (such as unilateral or bilateral renal agenesis), establishing FREM1 as the causal gene for this triad of abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMO",
    "long_answer": "Curry–Jones syndrome is caused by a recurrent post-zygotic missense mutation in the SMO gene (Smoothened, a Hedgehog-signaling receptor). Mosaic activation of SMO (e.g., p.Leu412Phe) drives the characteristic craniofacial, limb, and cutaneous abnormalities observed in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "The most severe form of von Willebrand disease is Type 3, which results from virtually complete absence of von Willebrand factor. Type 3 VWD is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner: affected individuals carry pathogenic variants in both alleles of the VWF gene, while heterozygous carriers are typically asymptomatic or only mildly affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Émile Achard and Joseph Thiers",
    "long_answer": "The constellation of type II diabetes mellitus accompanied by clinical signs of androgen excess (such as hirsutism and virilization) is called Achard–Thiers syndrome. It is named after the French physicians Émile Achard and Joseph Thiers, who first described the condition in 1921."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP19A1",
    "long_answer": "Aromatase excess syndrome is caused by gain-of-function genomic rearrangements (duplications, inversions or promoter fusions) that increase expression of the CYP19A1 gene on chromosome 15q21.2, which encodes the aromatase enzyme. Overexpression of CYP19A1 elevates estrogen synthesis, producing the clinical features of the syndrome such as gynecomastia and advanced bone age."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Injection drug use",
    "long_answer": "In industrialized countries, stringent screening of blood products has largely removed transfusion-related risk. Today, the great majority of new hepatitis C infections arise from percutaneous exposure to contaminated blood when people share needles, syringes, or other injection equipment—i.e., injection drug use is the dominant transmission route."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. The most widely used laboratory method for confirming schistosomiasis is microscopic detection of Schistosoma eggs in excreta. Eggs of Schistosoma mansoni, S. japonicum and related intestinal species are sought in stool samples (e.g., Kato-Katz thick smear), while eggs of Schistosoma haematobium are typically detected in urine (e.g., urine filtration or sedimentation techniques). These parasitological examinations remain the diagnostic gold standard in endemic areas, and positive findings in either stool or urine definitively establish infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "In many hospitalized alcohol-dependent patients, portal hypertension from alcoholic liver disease produces splenomegaly. The enlarged spleen sequesters and prematurely destroys circulating platelets (hypersplenism), leading to a reduced platelet count—thrombocytopenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diamond-Blackfan anemia",
    "long_answer": "Diamond-Blackfan anemia (DBA) is a congenital hypoplastic anemia in which erythroid precursors in the bone marrow are markedly reduced, leading to pure red-cell aplasia. Besides hematologic manifestations, children with DBA commonly show growth retardation; several studies and case reports document true growth hormone deficiency in a subset of these patients. Thus, DBA is the congenital disorder of red cell precursor deficiency that can also present with growth hormone deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPS14",
    "long_answer": "In the 5q- syndrome, deletion of the chromosome segment 5q33 removes one copy of the ribosomal protein gene RPS14. Haploinsufficiency of RPS14 impairs ribosome biogenesis in erythroid precursors, activates p53-mediated apoptosis, and thereby produces the characteristic macrocytic anemia seen in this deletion syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Under the U.S. Orphan Drug Act a condition is classified as a rare disease when it affects fewer than 200,000 people nationwide. Arachnoiditis—including adhesive arachnoiditis—is listed by the National Institutes of Health’s Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center (GARD) and by the National Organization for Rare Disorders (NORD) as a rare disorder. Epidemiologic estimates place its prevalence well below the 200,000-person threshold, and authoritative sources such as the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke describe it as a “rare” condition. Therefore, within the United States arachnoiditis is officially regarded as a rare disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrocephalus",
    "long_answer": "Saal Greenstein syndrome has been associated with episodes of hydrocephalus, a disorder in which cerebrospinal fluid accumulates within the brain’s ventricles, causing their expansion and raising intracranial pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GATA1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "In newborns and infants with Down syndrome, somatic mutations in the GATA1 gene (producing the truncated “GATA1-short” protein) drive transient abnormal myelopoiesis and Down-syndrome–associated acute megakaryoblastic leukemia. The altered GATA1 impairs normal megakaryocyte differentiation and leads to excessive proliferation of platelet precursor (megakaryocytic) cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain pelvic X-ray",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis is confirmed when bilateral sacroiliitis is seen on imaging. The standard diagnostic technique is a plain anteroposterior X-ray of the pelvis (sacroiliac joints), which visualises joint-space narrowing, sclerosis, and erosions characteristic of sacroiliitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colchicine",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis usually resolves spontaneously, but lesions persisting beyond about 3–4 weeks are commonly treated with colchicine. Colchicine inhibits microtubule-dependent neutrophil chemotaxis and degranulation, reducing the immune-complex–driven inflammation in the small dermal vessels and hastening clearance of the rash."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Sugarman syndrome is another name for familial juvenile hyperuricaemic nephropathy, a kidney disorder most often caused by pathogenic variants in the UMOD gene (and less commonly REN or MUC1). Reported families show vertical transmission with affected individuals in successive generations and equal involvement of males and females, establishing an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Linezolid",
    "long_answer": "Linezolid, an oxazolidinone antibiotic used for serious Gram-positive infections (e.g., MRSA, VRE), is a well-documented cause of black hairy tongue. The drug alters normal oral flora and promotes keratin accumulation on the filiform papillae, leading to the characteristic dark, hairy appearance of the tongue. This adverse effect is listed in the FDA label and multiple clinical reports."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increases bone resorption",
    "long_answer": "Excess vitamin A activates osteoclasts and suppresses osteoblast function, accelerating bone turnover and thereby increasing the rate of bone resorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Independent prognostic factor",
    "long_answer": "In oral squamous cell carcinoma, the depth of invasion (tumor depth) is one of the strongest independent predictors of outcome. As the tumor extends more deeply into underlying tissues, the likelihood of occult cervical lymph-node metastasis, local recurrence, and reduced disease-specific or overall survival rises markedly. Consequently, depth of invasion is now incorporated into the AJCC/UICC staging system and guides decisions on elective neck dissection and adjuvant therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)",
    "long_answer": "Olfactory Reference Syndrome is viewed as an obsessive-compulsive–spectrum disorder. The first-line pharmacologic treatment mirrors OCD management, with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors—such as fluoxetine, sertraline, paroxetine, or fluvoxamine—consistently shown to reduce the intrusive odor preoccupation and associated anxiety. Thus, SSRIs are the drug class most commonly prescribed for ORS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The PKD1 gene, whose mutations are responsible for the majority of autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease cases, is located on the short arm of chromosome 16 (region 16p13.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BDNF",
    "long_answer": "In some individuals with WAGR syndrome the chromosomal deletion extends telomeric to PAX6 and removes one copy of the BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) gene. Haploinsufficiency of BDNF disrupts central appetite-regulating pathways, producing marked hyperphagia and early-onset obesity, hence the designation “WAGRO” (WAGR-obesity) when BDNF is deleted."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B27",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of idiopathic acute anterior uveitis—the most common form of autoimmune uveitis—is strongly associated with the class I major histocompatibility complex antigen HLA-B27. Approximately one-half to two-thirds of patients with this uveitis carry the HLA-B27 allele, and its presence markedly increases the risk of developing recurrent autoimmune ocular inflammation as well as related spondyloarthropathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APOL1",
    "long_answer": "Risk variants (G1 and G2) of the APOL1 gene, located on chromosome 22q13, are strongly associated with ‘hypertensive’ nephropathy—kidney failure historically attributed to hypertension, particularly in individuals of African ancestry. These APOL1 variants increase susceptibility to progressive glomerulosclerosis and ESRD independent of blood-pressure level, explaining much of the excess risk previously ascribed to hypertensive nephropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus weihenstephanensis",
    "long_answer": "Comparative genomic and DNA–DNA hybridization studies of the Bacillus cereus group demonstrated that strains classified as Bacillus weihenstephanensis are genetically indistinguishable from Bacillus mycoides. On this basis, taxonomists concluded that B. weihenstephanensis is not a separate species but a heterotypic synonym of Bacillus mycoides."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa",
    "long_answer": "Rud syndrome—a rare constellation of congenital ichthyosis, hypogonadism, short stature and intellectual disability—has been variably associated with ocular findings. Several case reports describe retinal degeneration typical of retinitis pigmentosa, but other documented patients lack any retinal changes. Because this pigmentary retinopathy is present in only a subset of reported cases, retinitis pigmentosa is considered an inconsistently reported manifestation of Rud syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Headache",
    "long_answer": "Both presbyopia, the age-related loss of accommodation, and eye strain (asthenopia) commonly present with headaches—typically frontal or temporal pain that appears after prolonged near-vision tasks—because excessive or ineffective accommodative effort fatigues the ciliary and extra-ocular muscles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteon",
    "long_answer": "The osteon, or Haversian system, is the basic functional unit of compact bone. Each osteon consists of concentric lamellae of mineralized matrix surrounding a central (Haversian) canal that houses blood vessels and nerves. During craniofacial bone healing and regeneration, new osteons are laid down by osteoblasts, remodeling the bone and restoring its mechanical strength and vascular supply, making the osteon critical to successful craniofacial regeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphedema–distichiasis syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the FOXC2 transcription-factor gene cause the autosomal-dominant disorder called lymphedema–distichiasis syndrome. A hallmark of the condition is distichiasis—a second row of eyelashes—together with primary lymphatic dysfunction that produces chronic swelling of the limbs (lymphedema), typically beginning in adolescence. The FOXC2 gene’s role in lymphatic valve and eyelid development explains these characteristic clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked dominant",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PHEX gene, located on the X chromosome (Xp22.1), cause X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets. Because the pathogenic allele on one X chromosome is sufficient to produce disease in both males (hemizygous) and heterozygous females, the trait follows an X-linked dominant pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caroli disease",
    "long_answer": "Caroli disease is a congenital disorder characterized by segmental cystic dilatation of the intra-hepatic bile ducts. It represents a ductal-plate malformation that often coexists with congenital hepatic fibrosis (the combination is termed Caroli syndrome) and is frequently seen in patients who have autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease. Thus, Caroli disease is the liver condition associated with both congenital hepatic fibrosis and polycystic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. Inborn errors of metabolism are hereditary disorders caused by pathogenic variants in genes encoding metabolic enzymes or transporters. Modern clinical practice guidelines recommend genetic testing—ranging from single-gene analysis to multigene panels, whole-exome or whole-genome sequencing—as a key tool for confirming a biochemical diagnosis, guiding treatment, and enabling family counseling. Licensed physicians can order these tests through certified clinical laboratories and interpret the results in the context of the patient’s clinical and biochemical findings. Thus, performing (i.e., ordering, overseeing, and utilizing) genetic testing for suspected inborn errors of metabolism is a standard component of medical care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary pyropoikilocytosis (HPP) results when an individual inherits two deleterious alleles—often involving α- or β-spectrin mutations—leading to severe membrane instability of red blood cells. Because disease expression requires biallelic (homozygous or compound heterozygous) mutations, HPP follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome (an X-linked combined immunodeficiency caused by mutations in the WAS gene) is classically marked by the triad of eczema, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and bleeding manifestations such as bloody diarrhea. Lymphocytes from affected patients show abnormally low 5′-nucleotidase activity, a laboratory clue that helps distinguish this disorder from other immunodeficiencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EEC syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rüdiger originally described the EEC (Ectrodactyly–Ectodermal-dysplasia–Cleft lip/palate) syndrome. This condition is characterized by limb split-hand/foot malformations, ectodermal dysplasia features and an orofacial cleft, most frequently a cleft palate. Hence, the cleft-palate-containing syndrome associated with Rüdiger syndrome is EEC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling, amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Craniofrontonasal dysplasia is caused by pathogenic variants in the EFNB1 gene. Definitive prenatal confirmation therefore requires molecular analysis of fetal DNA obtained through invasive sampling procedures. Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) in the first trimester or amniocentesis in the second trimester provides fetal cells that can be tested for the EFNB1 mutation known in the family, thereby confirming the diagnosis before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Constitutional growth delay",
    "long_answer": "The majority of otherwise unexplained cases of short stature accompanied by late onset of pubertal development are due to Constitutional Growth Delay, a benign variant in which children grow at a normal velocity but follow a delayed growth curve, entering puberty and achieving final adult height later than their peers without underlying endocrine or genetic pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa is an inherited blistering disorder caused by defects in type VII collagen that make the skin extremely fragile. Because the manifestations are primarily cutaneous—recurrent blisters, erosions, scarring, and chronic wounds—the specialty most directly responsible for diagnosis, ongoing management, and coordination of wound care is dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with block",
    "long_answer": "The electrocardiographic finding considered virtually pathognomonic for digoxin toxicity is paroxysmal atrial tachycardia accompanied by second-degree (usually 2:1) atrioventricular block. Digoxin increases atrial automaticity while simultaneously enhancing vagal tone at the AV node; this unique combination produces a rapid atrial rhythm that is intermittently blocked at the AV node, yielding the characteristic pattern on ECG."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesomelic short stature",
    "long_answer": "Wallis–Zieff–Goldblatt syndrome is a rare skeletal dysplasia in which growth deficiency predominantly affects the middle segments of the limbs (forearms and lower legs). This pattern of disproportionate limb shortening is classified as mesomelic, so the syndrome produces mesomelic short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphadenopathy",
    "long_answer": "In AIDS dysmorphic syndrome, enlargement or abnormal firmness of lymph nodes—termed lymphadenopathy—is frequently observed. This reflects chronic immune activation and viral infection of lymphoid tissues, making lymphadenopathy a characteristic clinical finding in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Measles virus",
    "long_answer": "Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH) is mediated by Donath–Landsteiner IgG auto-antibodies that cause complement-dependent hemolysis. The chronic form was classically linked to tertiary syphilis, whereas the acute, self-limited form occurs mainly in children after certain viral illnesses. Among these, measles virus infection is the prototypical and most frequently cited precipitating infection in the literature, making measles virus the virus classically associated with the acute form of PCH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "International Prognostic Index",
    "long_answer": "For aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphomas, especially diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, clinicians use the International Prognostic Index (IPI). The IPI combines five readily measured clinical factors—age, serum LDH, performance status, Ann Arbor stage, and number of extranodal sites—to stratify patients into risk groups and predict overall survival and treatment outcome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute intermittent porphyria",
    "long_answer": "Acute intermittent porphyria is an autosomal-dominant porphyrin metabolism disorder caused by deficient porphobilinogen deaminase. The enzymatic block leads to accumulation of porphobilinogen and other porphyrin precursors that precipitate acute neurovisceral attacks, characterized by severe abdominal pain, autonomic instability, and dramatic neurological crises such as motor neuropathy, seizures, and psychiatric symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. NYU Grossman School of Medicine (part of NYU Langone Health) operates the Dysautonomia Center, which includes the Familial Dysautonomia Treatment & Research Center. Founded more than four decades ago, this multidisciplinary program—directed by faculty such as Dr. Horacio Kaufmann—provides comprehensive clinical care, genetic counseling, and research specifically for patients with familial dysautonomia (Riley–Day syndrome). It is one of the world’s principal referral and treatment centers for this rare hereditary autonomic neuropathy, confirming that NYU Grossman School of Medicine does indeed have a dedicated treatment center for familial dysautonomia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the CYP21A2 gene. CYP21A2, located in the HLA class III region on chromosome 6p21.3, encodes the cytochrome P450 21-hydroxylase enzyme required for cortisol and aldosterone synthesis. Loss-of-function mutations disrupt steroidogenesis, leading to the classic and non-classic forms of CAH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin",
    "long_answer": "Brunner syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MAOA gene, which normally degrades monoamines. The resulting MAOA deficiency prevents normal breakdown of serotonin, leading to markedly elevated serotonin levels; this excess serotonin is a hallmark biochemical feature of the disorder and is linked to the impulsive aggression and other neurobehavioral symptoms seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urocanase",
    "long_answer": "In histidine catabolism, urocanase (urocanate hydratase) converts urocanic acid to 4-imidazolone-5-propionic acid. When urocanase is deficient, this step is blocked, causing urocanic acid to accumulate and be excreted in excess in the urine (urocanic aciduria)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital clubfoot",
    "long_answer": "The Ponseti method is a serial casting technique specifically designed for correcting congenital clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). Clubfoot is a common foot deformity seen in infants with arthrogryposis multiplex congenita, and numerous clinical studies confirm that Ponseti casting followed by bracing effectively corrects the equinus, cavus, adductus, and varus components of the deformity in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome—also called Kabuki make-up syndrome—is a congenital disorder whose hallmark facial features include long palpebral fissures with eversion of the lateral third of the lower eyelid and frequent upper-eyelid ptosis. A consistent systemic manifestation is mild-to-moderate intellectual disability. The combination of ptosis and cognitive impairment, together with post-natal growth retardation and skeletal anomalies, forms the classic clinical picture of Kabuki syndrome, which is most often caused by pathogenic variants in the KMT2D or KDM6A genes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage diseases predominantly involve the liver, the body’s central organ for glycogen metabolism. Hepatocytes synthesize glycogen after meals (glycogenesis) and break it down during fasting (glycogenolysis); defects in these pathways lead to hepatic enlargement, hypoglycemia, and other features characteristic of glycogen storage disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uterine cervix",
    "long_answer": "Placental site nodules are benign residual foci of intermediate trophoblast that most commonly arise in the endocervical canal; thus, the uterine cervix is the part of the female reproductive system that can be affected in this form of gestational trophoblastic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Purine nucleotide cycle",
    "long_answer": "Because skeletal muscle glycogenolysis is blocked in McArdle disease, contracting muscle must obtain ATP by adenylate kinase (2 ADP → ATP + AMP). The excess AMP is then deaminated by myoadenylate deaminase, producing inosine-5′-monophosphate (IMP) and releasing free ammonia. This AMP-to-IMP deamination constitutes the key step of the purine nucleotide cycle, making this pathway the principal source of the exercise-induced ammonia seen in individuals with McArdle disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈5 kb",
    "long_answer": "The BK polyomavirus possesses a circular double-stranded DNA genome of roughly 5,100 base pairs, commonly described as about 5 kilobases in length."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optic nerve",
    "long_answer": "The optic nerve (cranial nerve II) carries the axons of retinal ganglion cells, conveying visual signals from the retina through the optic chiasm and optic tracts to central visual relay nuclei in the brain, making it the cranial nerve responsible for transmitting visual information."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGFB1",
    "long_answer": "Camurati-Engelmann disease (progressive diaphyseal dysplasia) is most often caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the TGFB1 gene, which encodes transforming growth factor-β1. Excessive TGFB1 signaling leads to hyperostosis of the diaphyses; the resulting thickened cortical bone can compress adjacent neurovascular structures, producing nerve damage such as cranial nerve palsies or peripheral neuropathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-free diet",
    "long_answer": "Type III galactosemia results from UDP-galactose-4-epimerase deficiency, which blocks normal galactose metabolism. Accumulated galactose and its toxic metabolite galactitol damage the liver, leading to jaundice. Eliminating dietary sources of galactose—primarily lactose-containing milk and dairy—prevents substrate buildup, thereby averting hepatic injury and jaundice. Hence, a strict galactose-free (lactose-free) diet is the recommended management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "Runs of premature ventricular contractions can degenerate into ventricular tachycardia. In ventricular tachycardia the ventricles beat rapidly and ineffectively, causing a sudden drop in cardiac output and cerebral perfusion, which often produces hypotension and syncope."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heyde’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Heyde’s syndrome describes the well-established association in which severe aortic stenosis causes high-shear fragmentation of von Willebrand factor multimers, leading to an acquired type 2A von Willebrand disease and resultant bleeding from intestinal angiodysplasias. Thus, the coexistence of aortic stenosis and gastrointestinal bleeding is recognized clinically as Heyde’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tokophobia",
    "long_answer": "Tokophobia is a specific phobic anxiety disorder characterized by an intense, pathological fear of pregnancy and childbirth. In affected women, this fear can become so overwhelming that they request an elective cesarean section to avoid labor, despite the absence of obstetric indications. Tokophobia is recognized in psychiatric and obstetric literature as a clinically significant condition that can arise in pregnancy, often requiring psychological support or counseling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tis N0 M0",
    "long_answer": "A localized abnormal-cell phenomenon in the pancreas corresponds to carcinoma in situ, which in the TNM system is classified as Tis (tumor in situ), with no regional lymph-node involvement (N0) and no distant metastasis (M0)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C8 and T1 nerves",
    "long_answer": "Klumpke's paralysis is produced by injury to the lower trunk of the brachial plexus, which is formed by the ventral rami of the eighth cervical (C8) and first thoracic (T1) spinal nerves; damage to C8-T1 roots causes the characteristic intrinsic hand muscle weakness and claw hand deformity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes 17 and 1",
    "long_answer": "About 70 % of Charcot–Marie–Tooth cases (type 1A) are caused by a duplication of the PMP22 gene on chromosome 17p11.2, and a further 10–15 % (type 1B) result from mutations in the MPZ gene on chromosome 1q22. These two chromosomes therefore account for the majority of mutation-related CMT cases reported."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Goitre",
    "long_answer": "Both thyroid hormone resistance and primary goitre typically present with an enlarged thyroid gland—clinically termed goitre. In thyroid hormone resistance, impaired feedback keeps TSH relatively high, stimulating thyroid hyperplasia; in goitre, the gland is by definition enlarged. Thus thyroid enlargement (goitre) is the shared symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mondor's disease",
    "long_answer": "Superficial thrombophlebitis of the subcutaneous veins of the breast or anterior chest wall is called Mondor's disease, first described in 1939 by the French surgeon Henri Mondor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchoalveolar lavage",
    "long_answer": "Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) involves washing the distal airways and alveoli via a bronchoscope and analyzing the recovered fluid. In hypersensitivity pneumonitis, BAL typically shows a marked lymphocytic alveolitis, providing direct evidence of active inflammatory processes in the lung parenchyma. Therefore, BAL is a useful diagnostic procedure to detect and quantify pulmonary inflammation in patients suspected of having hypersensitivity pneumonitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polydactyly",
    "long_answer": "Stratton-Parker syndrome belongs to the short-rib polydactyly group of lethal osteochondrodysplasias. A defining skeletal hallmark of these disorders, including the Stratton-Parker subtype, is the presence of post-axial polydactyly—extra fingers and/or toes accompanying the markedly shortened ribs and limbs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Duodenal atresia",
    "long_answer": "The classic prenatal and neonatal “double-bubble” sign—two adjacent fluid-filled bubbles representing the stomach and proximal duodenum—is characteristic of duodenal atresia. Epidemiologic studies show that this congenital intestinal obstruction occurs in roughly 20–40% of infants with trisomy 21, making duodenal atresia a well-established malformation associated with Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Organophosphates",
    "long_answer": "Organophosphate insecticides (e.g., malathion, naled, temephos) are widely used agricultural pesticides and are also applied in mosquito-control programs. By inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, they rapidly kill larval and adult mosquitoes, thereby lowering vector populations and cutting the transmission of arboviruses such as dengue, Zika, chikungunya and West Nile virus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation",
    "long_answer": "When conventional ventilation, surfactant, and inhaled nitric oxide fail to oxygenate a newborn with severe pulmonary hypoplasia, the definitive rescue therapy is extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO). ECMO temporarily bypasses the underdeveloped lungs, oxygenating and removing CO₂ from the blood extracorporeally, giving the infant time for pulmonary and cardiorespiratory stabilization; thus, it is considered the last-resort treatment in these critical cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior horn",
    "long_answer": "Both spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) selectively degenerate the lower motor neurons located in the anterior (ventral) horn of the spinal cord. Loss of these anterior-horn motor neurons leads to progressive muscle weakness and atrophy characteristic of these motor neuron diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈49 hours",
    "long_answer": "Acitretin, the active metabolite of etretinate prescribed for severe psoriasis, is eliminated slowly from the body; pharmacokinetic studies show an average terminal elimination half-life of about 49 hours (commonly reported range ~33–96 hours)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TPM2",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis (particularly type 1) is most often caused by pathogenic variants in the TPM2 gene, which encodes β-tropomyosin of fast-twitch skeletal muscle. TPM2 is situated at the chromosomal locus 9p13.2; mutations at this defined position disrupt thin-filament regulation during muscle contraction and lead to the congenital limb contractures that characterise distal arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lou Gehrig",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis is commonly called “Lou Gehrig’s disease,” a name derived from the famed New York Yankees baseball player Lou Gehrig, who was diagnosed with ALS in 1939 and publicized the illness, leading to the eponymous designation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis is an inherited hemolytic anemia caused by defects in red-cell membrane proteins. Because it is a disorder of the blood, patients are typically evaluated and managed by hematologists, the medical specialists who diagnose and treat diseases of the blood and hemopoietic organs, including congenital hemolytic anemias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DLX3",
    "long_answer": "Screwdriver-shaped (conical) incisors are a typical dental feature of tricho-dento-osseous syndrome. This syndrome results from pathogenic mutations in the DLX3 (distal-less homeobox 3) gene on chromosome 17q21. DLX3 loss-of-function alters epithelial-mesenchymal signaling during tooth morphogenesis, leading to enamel hypoplasia and the characteristic tapering, screwdriver-like shape of the incisors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae",
    "long_answer": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a common cause of so-called “atypical” or walking pneumonia and characteristically lacks a peptidoglycan cell wall. Instead, its plasma membrane contains sterols, rendering it intrinsically resistant to β-lactam antibiotics and giving rise to its pleomorphic, cell-wall-deficient morphology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Perrault syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Perrault syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by sensorineural deafness together with bilateral ovarian agenesis/ovarian dysgenesis in phenotypic 46,XX females, leading to primary amenorrhoea and infertility. Hence it is the syndrome classically associated with bilateral absence of functional ovaries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal hyperthyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Excess maternal thyroid hormone (hyperthyroidism) accelerates fetal bone maturation, promoting premature fusion of the metopic suture; this early synostosis produces the triangular forehead characteristic of trigonocephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rodents",
    "long_answer": "Martinican pit vipers (Bothrops lanceolatus) commonly prey on small mammals such as rats and mice. These rodents are well-recognized reservoirs for numerous zoonotic pathogens (e.g., Leptospira, Hantaviruses, and Yersinia pestis), making them noteworthy in both ecological and public-health contexts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Headache",
    "long_answer": "Choroid plexus papillomas over-produce cerebrospinal fluid, leading to hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure; the most frequent clinical manifestation of this pressure increase is persistent headache."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aromatization",
    "long_answer": "Testosterone is converted into the potent estrogen estradiol through the enzymatic process called aromatization, catalyzed by the cytochrome P450 enzyme aromatase (CYP19A1). In postmenopausal women, peripheral aromatization of androgens in adipose and other tissues becomes the primary source of estrogens, and the resulting estradiol can drive the growth of hormone-receptor–positive breast cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "During colonisation of the human nasopharyngeal mucosa, Neisseria meningitidis is first met by polymorphonuclear neutrophils. These innate immune phagocytes migrate into the mucosal epithelium and attempt to engulf the bacteria; meningococcal capsule, LOS and other factors help the pathogen resist neutrophil-mediated killing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acetaminophen",
    "long_answer": "After incision and curettage of a chalazion, the discomfort is usually mild; routine practice is to advise an oral non-antiplatelet analgesic. Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is recommended because it provides adequate postoperative pain control without increasing bleeding risk, unlike aspirin or other NSAIDs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chemophobia and chemical synthesis",
    "long_answer": "The phenomenon of an unfounded or exaggerated fear of chemicals is termed chemophobia. The purposeful, laboratory-based carrying out of chemical reactions to create desired compounds is known as chemical synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncocytes",
    "long_answer": "Both oncocytomas and so-called eosinophilic (Warthin) tumours are composed of oncocytes—large, granular, mitochondria-rich epithelial cells with intensely eosinophilic (oxyphilic) cytoplasm. Thus the cell type common to the two lesions is the oncocyte."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WAGR syndrome",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome is a contiguous-gene deletion on chromosome 11p13 involving PAX6 and WT1. Its cardinal clinical tetrad is Wilms tumor predisposition, congenital aniridia (absence of the iris), genitourinary anomalies, and mental/psychomotor retardation. The combination of missing irises and psychomotor delay therefore points to WAGR syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mevalonate kinase (MVK)",
    "long_answer": "Linear porokeratosis is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the mevalonate-kinase gene (MVK). The very same MVK mutations that create the porokeratotic clone have repeatedly been identified in squamous-cell carcinomas that arise within these lesions, showing a direct genetic link between MVK mutation and cutaneous SCC development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Reactive (post-prandial) hypoglycaemia is usually managed first with non-pharmacologic measures. Clinical guidelines and endocrinology texts consistently recommend dietary modification as the cornerstone of therapy. Patients are advised to:  • eat small, frequent meals (every 3–4 h) rather than large single meals;  • emphasise complex carbohydrates with high fibre, adequate protein and healthy fats;  • avoid or limit rapidly absorbable/simple sugars, especially on an empty stomach;  • include protein and/or fat with carbohydrate to slow gastric emptying and glucose absorption;  • limit alcohol and caffeine that can precipitate swings in glucose;  • consider bedtime snacks if nocturnal symptoms occur.  These measures reduce the rapid glucose peaks that trigger exaggerated insulin release and subsequent hypoglycaemia. Pharmacologic therapy is rarely required unless dietary measures fail. Therefore, according to established medical practice, recommending dietary changes for patients with reactive hypoglycaemia is standard and evidence-based."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Xanthine oxidase is the enzyme that normally converts hypoxanthine to xanthine and subsequently converts xanthine to uric acid. In hereditary or acquired xanthine oxidase deficiency (classically seen in the inborn error of metabolism called xanthinuria), this enzymatic step is blocked. Because xanthine can no longer be efficiently oxidized to uric acid, both hypoxanthine and, more prominently, xanthine accumulate in blood and urine. Clinically, this leads to markedly low serum and urinary uric-acid levels and the formation of xanthine stones in the urinary tract. Therefore, a deficiency of xanthine oxidase does indeed cause a build-up of xanthine in the body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Familial male-limited precocious puberty is caused by activating mutations in the LHCGR gene that are transmitted on an autosome. A single mutant allele is sufficient for the trait to be expressed, but clinical manifestations are limited to males; therefore, the disorder shows an autosomal dominant pattern with sex-limited (male-only) expression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatigue",
    "long_answer": "Both essential thrombocythemia and anemia frequently present with fatigue. In anemia, reduced hemoglobin diminishes oxygen delivery to tissues, leading to tiredness and low energy. In essential thrombocythemia, chronic inflammation and altered blood flow from markedly elevated platelet counts likewise produce systemic fatigue. Thus, fatigue is a well-recognized symptom that the two conditions share."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DMD (dystrophin)",
    "long_answer": "X-linked dilated cardiomyopathy classically results from mutations in the DMD gene that encodes the sarcolemmal protein dystrophin; because the gene is located on the X chromosome, the trait shows X-linked inheritance, with affected males and carrier females."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selection for reduced virulence",
    "long_answer": "With vertical transmission, a pathogen’s spread is tied to the host’s ability to survive, reproduce, and pass the infection to offspring. If the pathogen severely harms or kills the parent, it sharply lowers its own chance of reaching the next generation. Natural selection therefore favors strains that keep the host healthy enough to reproduce, leading to evolution toward lower virulence in vertically transmitted infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A3, COL4A4, COL4A5 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome results from pathogenic variants in the genes encoding the type IV collagen α-chains of the glomerular basement membrane. Molecular testing most often detects mutations in COL4A5 (X-linked form) and, less commonly, in COL4A3 or COL4A4 (autosomal recessive or dominant forms). Identifying a disease-causing variant in any of these three genes confirms the diagnosis and guides inheritance counseling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperbaric Medicine",
    "long_answer": "Decompression sickness (\"the bends\" or Caisson disease) is treated primarily with hyperbaric oxygen therapy, which involves placing the patient in a pressurized chamber to recompress them and accelerate inert-gas elimination. Physicians trained in the specialty of Hyperbaric Medicine (often termed Undersea and Hyperbaric Medicine) are specifically qualified to manage these cases, making this the medical specialty most likely to treat decompression sickness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Prophylaxis of hypokalemic periodic paralysis commonly involves carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., acetazolamide or dichlorphenamide). These drugs induce a mild metabolic acidosis and modify ionic fluxes in skeletal muscle fibers, thereby reducing the frequency and severity of paralytic attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Third-stage (L3) larvae",
    "long_answer": "Humans acquire dracunculiasis by drinking water that contains copepods (water fleas) harboring the infective third-stage (L3) larvae of Dracunculus medinensis. These L3 larvae are released in the stomach, penetrate the intestinal wall, and begin their migration and maturation, initiating infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caveolin-3 (CAV3)",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant mutations in the CAV3 gene, which encodes the muscle membrane protein caveolin-3, often present as isolated or asymptomatic hyper-CK-aemia. Affected individuals show chronically elevated serum creatine kinase despite minimal or absent clinical muscle weakness, because the altered caveolin-3 disrupts sarcolemmal membrane stability, allowing leakage of intracellular CK into the blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal pain",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary angioedema, swelling of the gastrointestinal mucosa produces acute abdominal pain; this painful intestinal edema commonly provokes nausea and can culminate in vomiting during an attack."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Optic-nerve complications of Behçet’s disease are ocular manifestations that require detailed eye examination, visual-field testing, and imaging of the optic disc; these evaluations and the initiation of anti-inflammatory or immunosuppressive therapy are carried out by an ophthalmologist (often a neuro-ophthalmology subspecialist), the specialist trained to diagnose and treat diseases of the eye and optic nerve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombin",
    "long_answer": "Congenital afibrinogenemia is characterized by an absence of fibrinogen (coagulation factor I). During normal hemostasis, the serine protease thrombin (activated factor II, or factor IIa) cleaves fibrinogen to release fibrinopeptides A and B, thereby converting soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin, its active clot-forming form. Thus, thrombin is the enzyme that activates the protein lacking in individuals with congenital afibrinogenemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of congenital cicatricial alopecia is Marie-Unna hereditary hypotrichosis. This disorder is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the U2HR regulatory region of the hairless (HR) gene, which resides on chromosome 8p21-22; therefore, chromosome 8 is the chromosomal location classically associated with this congenital scarring alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "Tachycardia is defined as a resting heart rate greater than 100 beats per minute. Excess catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) stimulate β-adrenergic receptors in the sinoatrial node, increasing its firing rate and producing the rapid heartbeat characteristic of tachycardia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central scotoma",
    "long_answer": "Cone dystrophy primarily damages macular cone photoreceptors, producing loss of central retinal function. Perimetric testing therefore most often shows a central scotoma—a localized defect in the visual field corresponding to the macula—making it the characteristic visual field abnormality in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia 2B",
    "long_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2B (MEN 2B) is a RET-mutated syndrome that classically presents with a marfanoid body habitus. Because of its underlying connective-tissue abnormality, patients frequently develop axial skeletal deformities, including kyphosis and scoliosis, in addition to hallmark features such as mucosal neuromas, medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy",
    "long_answer": "Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease is the prototypical form of hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy (HMSN), a group of inherited peripheral nerve disorders. In HMSN, genetic mutations impair myelin or axonal proteins in peripheral nerves, producing the progressive motor weakness and sensory loss characteristic of Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial hypobetalipoproteinemia",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypobetalipoproteinemia is an inherited disorder—most often caused by truncating mutations in the APOB gene—that produces marked hypolipoproteinemia (very low LDL-cholesterol and apoB levels). The same biochemical picture can also occur secondarily in severe malnutrition or cachectic ‘wasting’ states, so wasting can produce a form of hypobetalipoproteinemia that mimics the genetic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Crohn's disease is a form of inflammatory bowel disease that affects the gastrointestinal tract; its diagnosis, medical management, and endoscopic monitoring are handled chiefly by the medical specialty of gastroenterology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "inv(20)(p11q13)",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures segregate with a pericentric inversion of chromosome 20, cytogenetically designated inv(20)(p11q13). The inversion acts as a marker for the disease locus on 20q13 where the KCNQ2 gene that underlies most cases of this benign neonatal epilepsy is located."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systematic desensitization",
    "long_answer": "Herpetophobia is classified as a specific phobia. The evidence-based, first-line behavioral treatment for specific phobias is systematic desensitization (graduated exposure therapy), in which the patient is gradually and repeatedly exposed to reptile-related stimuli while practicing relaxation techniques, leading to extinction of the fear response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Indican",
    "long_answer": "In blue diaper syndrome, malabsorption of tryptophan leads to excess production of indole in the gut; the liver converts indole to indoxyl-sulfate (indican), which is excreted in the urine. When the urine in the infant’s diaper is exposed to air, indican is oxidized to the blue dye indigo, producing the characteristic bluish discoloration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrotic syndrome",
    "long_answer": "An acquired deficiency of prekallikrein can occur in nephrotic syndrome because the damaged glomerular filtration barrier allows excessive urinary loss of plasma proteins, including the contact-system protein prekallikrein (Fletcher factor). The continual loss of this protein in the urine lowers its circulating level, producing the observed deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia frequently includes hypopituitarism, leading to deficiencies of growth hormone, cortisol, thyroid hormone and others. Management of these endocrine deficits requires hormone replacement therapy and ongoing monitoring, tasks that fall within the expertise of a pediatric endocrinologist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Throat culture",
    "long_answer": "Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America and the American Academy of Pediatrics state that if a rapid antigen detection test (RADT) for Group A Streptococcus is negative in a child or adolescent with suspected streptococcal pharyngitis, a conventional throat culture of a tonsillopharyngeal swab should be performed. Throat culture provides higher sensitivity (≈95%) than RADT and is the reference standard for confirming or excluding Group A streptococcal infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arg46Trp (R46W)",
    "long_answer": "The form of spondyloenchondrodysplasia with immune dysregulation is most often linked to a homozygous ACP5 point mutation (c.136C>T) that replaces arginine with tryptophan at residue 46 of tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (p.Arg46Trp). This missense change eliminates TRAP activity, leading to the skeletal dysplasia and immune abnormalities characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Horse phobia",
    "long_answer": "In Sigmund Freud’s famous 1909 case study of the five-year-old boy known as “Little Hans,” the central clinical focus was the child’s intense, persistent fear of horses. Freud interpreted this equinophobia as a manifestation of underlying Oedipal and castration anxieties, using the case to support his psychoanalytic theory of psychosexual development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deferoxamine",
    "long_answer": "Iron overload in congenital dyserythropoietic anemia is managed with iron-chelation therapy. Deferoxamine binds excess ferric iron and promotes its urinary and biliary excretion, thereby lowering body iron stores and preventing organ damage. It is the standard chelator historically recommended for CDA patients who accumulate iron from ineffective erythropoiesis and transfusions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ferdinand-Jean Darier and James Clarke White",
    "long_answer": "Darier’s disease, also called Darier-White disease or keratosis follicularis, was independently described in 1889 by the French dermatologist Ferdinand-Jean Darier and soon after by the American dermatologist James Clarke White. Because both physicians characterized the same inherited keratinization disorder, their surnames are attached to the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Surgical removal of congenital cataracts is performed by an ophthalmologist—a physician specialized in ophthalmology who is trained to diagnose and surgically treat disorders of the eye, including congenital lens opacities seen in cataract-ataxia-deafness syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chylothorax",
    "long_answer": "When Gorham-Stout disease extends to the ribs or thoracic vertebrae, the abnormal lymphatic proliferation can disrupt the thoracic duct and pleural lymphatics, allowing chyle to leak into the pleural space. The resulting chylothorax leads to massive pleural effusions, respiratory failure, and is the principal life-threatening complication of chest involvement in Gorham’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroencephalogram (EEG)",
    "long_answer": "An electroencephalogram records the brain’s electrical activity through scalp electrodes, allowing clinicians to visualize abnormal discharges such as focal or generalized slowing, periodic complexes, or epileptiform spikes that commonly occur in encephalitis. Because encephalitic inflammation disrupts normal neuronal firing patterns, EEG is routinely employed to detect and monitor these pathological electrical changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Touton giant cell",
    "long_answer": "In lipoid (lipid) pneumonia the alveoli become packed with lipid-laden (foamy) macrophages. Fusion of these lipid-filled macrophages produces Touton giant cells—multinucleated cells with a ring of nuclei surrounding a central lipid-rich cytoplasm—which are characteristic of lesions rich in neutral fat and cholesterol."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cranial nerves VI and VII",
    "long_answer": "Möbius syndrome is characterized by congenital hypoplasia or absence of the abducens (VI) and facial (VII) cranial nerve nuclei/nerve fibers. This underdevelopment causes the hallmark findings of impaired lateral eye movement and bilateral facial paralysis seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankle-foot orthosis",
    "long_answer": "Because myotonic dystrophy produces distal leg weakness and foot-drop, clinicians commonly prescribe an ankle-foot orthosis (AFO). The rigid or hinged brace supports the ankle in neutral position, prevents excessive plantar-flexion, and stabilizes gait, reducing the energy cost of walking and lowering fall risk in patients with this neuromuscular disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Distal trisomy 10q (duplication of the distal segment of chromosome 10q) is a well-described chromosomal duplication syndrome. Reported individuals show multiple congenital anomalies, growth and developmental delay, characteristic craniofacial features, and abnormal muscle tone—most commonly neonatal hypotonia that may later be followed by hypertonia or spasticity. Normal or “typical” muscle tone is not a defining or characteristic finding in this condition. Hence the statement that distal trisomy 10q is characterized by typical (i.e., normal) muscle tone is not consistent with the established clinical description of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dandy–Walker malformation",
    "long_answer": "Dandy–Walker malformation (also called Dandy–Walker syndrome) is a congenital brain malformation characterized by hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis and cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle. It is a recognized feature of Marden–Walker syndrome and is frequently accompanied by agenesis or hypogenesis of the corpus callosum, linking all three conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibialis posterior",
    "long_answer": "In surgical correction of foot drop caused by distal myopathy, the tendon of the tibialis posterior muscle is detached, routed through the interosseous membrane, and attached to the dorsum of the foot. Once transferred, tibialis posterior can contract to dorsiflex the ankle, effectively substituting for the weakened or absent tibialis anterior muscle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bartonella henselae",
    "long_answer": "Cat-scratch disease is an infectious lymphadenitis produced by the Gram-negative, intracellular bacterium Bartonella henselae, which is transmitted to humans mainly through scratches or bites from cats harboring this pathogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NTRK1 gene cause congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis (Hereditary Sensory and Autonomic Neuropathy type IV). Affected individuals are typically homozygous or compound-heterozygous for loss-of-function mutations, while each parent is an asymptomatic carrier, demonstrating the classic autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin (5-HT)",
    "long_answer": "Serotonin, released mainly from enterochromaffin cells in the gut wall, activates receptors on enteric neurons to stimulate smooth-muscle contraction and coordinate colonic motility patterns, thereby regulating colonic motor activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome, also known as Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome, was first described in 1981 in Japanese patients and families. The multisystem disorder is marked by characteristic facial features, growth delay, intellectual disability, skeletal anomalies, and genitourinary malformations; hypospadias is one of its common urogenital manifestations. Pathogenic variants in KMT2D or, less frequently, KDM6A underlie most cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Celiac disease",
    "long_answer": "Selective immunoglobulin A (IgA) deficiency is strongly associated with celiac disease: up to 10–15 % of IgA-deficient individuals develop this autoimmune enteropathy, compared with ≈1 % in the general population. The lack of mucosal IgA alters gut immune tolerance, facilitating an autoimmune response to gluten, making celiac disease significantly more common in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midodrine",
    "long_answer": "Midodrine, prescribed for symptomatic orthostatic hypotension, is administered as an inactive prodrug that is rapidly converted in vivo to the active metabolite desglymidodrine, an α1-adrenergic agonist responsible for its blood-pressure-raising effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selamectin",
    "long_answer": "Selamectin is an avermectin applied as a monthly ‘spot-on’ solution (e.g., Revolution) that is licensed and widely used to eliminate Sarcoptes scabiei infestations in dogs; the drug is absorbed transdermally after topical application and kills the mites systemically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clindamycin",
    "long_answer": "Clindamycin is a lincosamide antibiotic with proven activity against Toxoplasma gondii. It is commonly employed as an alternative to the standard pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine regimen for ocular toxoplasmosis, particularly in patients who cannot tolerate sulfonamides. Oral or intravitreal clindamycin, often combined with pyrimethamine or used alone intravitreally, achieves therapeutic intraocular concentrations and has been shown in clinical studies to control active retinochoroiditis effectively."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elosulfase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Elosulfase alfa, marketed as Vimizim, is a recombinant form of the missing enzyme N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase. It received FDA approval in 2014 as an enzyme-replacement therapy specifically for patients with Morquio syndrome Type A (mucopolysaccharidosis IVA), addressing the underlying enzyme deficiency that leads to glycosaminoglycan accumulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) is classified as a mood disorder. Diagnosis, pharmacologic management (e.g., SSRIs, SNRIs), and coordination of psychotherapy for mood disorders fall within the domain of psychiatry, the medical specialty dedicated to treating mental illnesses. Therefore, psychiatrists are the specialists most likely to manage patients with persistent depressive disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALAS2",
    "long_answer": "X-linked dominant protoporphyria is produced by gain-of-function (hyperactive) mutations in the erythroid-specific aminolevulinate synthase 2 gene (ALAS2) located on the X chromosome. The mutant ALAS2 enzyme increases heme precursor synthesis, leading to excess protoporphyrin IX accumulation and the characteristic photosensitive phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor pulmonale",
    "long_answer": "In chronic mountain sickness, sustained hypoxia causes pulmonary vasoconstriction, producing chronic pulmonary hypertension. Persistently elevated pressure overloads the right ventricle, leading to right-sided ventricular hypertrophy and eventual failure—a condition termed cor pulmonale."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hurler syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type I) is an autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder caused by complete deficiency of the enzyme α-L-iduronidase. Lack of this enzyme leads to accumulation of dermatan and heparan sulfate, producing coarse facies, corneal clouding, hepatosplenomegaly, developmental delay, and characteristic umbilical or inguinal hernias, making it the lysosomal disease classically associated with hernias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Factor XIIIa immunostain",
    "long_answer": "Granuloma annulare is characterized histologically by palisading aggregates of dermal epithelioid histiocytes (dermal dendrocytes). These histiocytic cells strongly express transglutaminase Factor XIIIa. Immunohistochemical staining for Factor XIIIa is therefore the standard method used to highlight and examine the dermal epithelioid histiocytes in granuloma annulare, aiding in both identification and differential diagnosis from other granulomatous conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal tubular acidosis",
    "long_answer": "Medical historians have suggested that Tiny Tim’s small stature, bone deformities, muscle weakness, need for a crutch, and potentially fatal but reversible course are most consistent with type I (distal) renal tubular acidosis. This disorder causes chronic metabolic acidosis, leading to rickets-like skeletal changes and growth failure; with appropriate alkali therapy—plausibly affordable after Scrooge’s transformation—his condition could have improved, fitting Dickens’ narrative that Tim survives once the family’s finances improve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Factitious disorder imposed on another; diagnosis",
    "long_answer": "When a caregiver deliberately fabricates or induces illness in someone under their care, the psychiatric condition is called Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another (historically termed Munchausen syndrome by proxy). The systematic medical procedure used to identify the illness or condition that best explains a patient’s signs and symptoms is known as diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical removal of tumor",
    "long_answer": "The condition described is Meigs syndrome; ascites and pleural effusion disappear once the benign ovarian mass (usually a fibroma) is surgically excised, making operative removal of the ovarian tumor the definitive treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectrodactyly",
    "long_answer": "Jancar syndrome is one of the eponymous names for the ectrodactyly–mental retardation (EMR) syndrome. The hallmark skeletal anomaly in this disorder is ectrodactyly (split-hand/foot malformation or “lobster-claw” deformity), in which central digits are absent and the hand or foot appears cleft. This distinctive limb defect is consistently reported in affected individuals and is considered characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chondrosarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Chondrosarcoma is the malignant cartilage-forming tumor that characteristically infiltrates surrounding bone and soft tissues and can metastasize—most often to the lungs—distinguishing it from benign cartilage tumors, which remain localized."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibialis posterior",
    "long_answer": "Tibialis posterior, a deep posterior-compartment muscle of the leg, is innervated by the tibial nerve. It functions as the principal dynamic stabiliser of the lower leg and foot, supporting the medial longitudinal arch and controlling pronation during gait, thereby providing key stability to the lower limb."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamazepine",
    "long_answer": "Carbamazepine is an anticonvulsant that is also used as a mood-stabilizing agent in bipolar disorder. A well-documented, though uncommon, hematologic adverse effect of carbamazepine therapy is agranulocytosis (severe neutropenia), thought to result from immune-mediated or direct toxic suppression of granulocyte precursors. Consequently, periodic blood count monitoring is recommended in patients receiving carbamazepine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral vascular disease",
    "long_answer": "In the United States the majority of limb amputations are performed because of dysvascular disease—peripheral arterial occlusive disease, most often associated with diabetes mellitus. This peripheral vascular disease accounts for more amputations than trauma, tumors, infections, or congenital abnormalities, making it the single most common cause of amputation in the U.S."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic stress",
    "long_answer": "Weight-based stigma functions as a persistent psychosocial stressor. Repeated exposure to teasing, discrimination, and social rejection activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, elevating cortisol and sympathetic tone. This chronic stress promotes visceral fat deposition, insulin resistance, systemic inflammation, hypertension, and dyslipidemia, while also discouraging healthcare seeking and physical activity. Consequently, the stress pathway triggered by obesity stigma directly amplifies the metabolic and cardiovascular risks already linked to excess adiposity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "Real-time prenatal ultrasonography (including high-resolution or 3-D ultrasound) can visualize amniotic bands encircling a fetal limb and the resulting distal edema or deformity, allowing definitive confirmation of constriction ring (amniotic band) syndrome while the pregnancy is still ongoing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Müllerian agenesis",
    "long_answer": "After gonadal dysgenesis (e.g., Turner syndrome), the next most frequent reason a girl fails to menstruate despite reaching the age threshold is Müllerian agenesis, also called Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome. In this condition the Müllerian ducts do not develop, so the uterus and upper vagina are absent, yet ovarian function and secondary sexual characteristics are normal, leading to primary amenorrhea. Multiple epidemiologic reviews list it as the second-most common etiology of primary amenorrhea, accounting for roughly 15–20 % of cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norepinephrine",
    "long_answer": "Neuroblastoma cells express the norepinephrine transporter of sympathetic-derived tissue. This allows them to actively take up norepinephrine (and its radiolabeled analogue ¹²³I- or ¹³¹I-MIBG), a property exploited in diagnostic scintigraphy and staging of neuroblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desquamation",
    "long_answer": "Tunga penetrans (the jigger flea) burrows into the skin, leading to local inflammation and subsequent peeling or sloughing off of the superficial epidermis. This shedding of dead keratinized cells is medically termed desquamation, the characteristic skin condition associated with tungiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wyburn-Mason syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Wyburn-Mason syndrome (Bonnet-Dechaume-Blanc syndrome) is a rare neuro-oculo-cutaneous disorder characterized by large arteriovenous malformations of the retina, midbrain and diencephalon. These intracranial AVMs frequently lie in the midline and drain into, or compress, the deep venous system, thereby directly or indirectly involving the Great Cerebral Vein of Galen and its outflow into the straight sinus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "In the perineum",
    "long_answer": "In complete penoscrotal transposition the scrotum lies cephalad, and the penis is displaced posterior-inferiorly so that it emerges from the perineum, behind/below the scrotal sacs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aniridia",
    "long_answer": "In WAGR syndrome, the characteristic ocular defect is aniridia—the partial or complete absence of the iris. Lack of iris tissue disrupts normal light regulation and is commonly accompanied by foveal hypoplasia, nystagmus, cataract, and glaucoma, all of which can significantly reduce visual acuity. Thus, aniridia is the eye condition in WAGR syndrome that leads to decreased vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Purkinje shift",
    "long_answer": "In low-light (scotopic) conditions the retina relies mainly on rod photoreceptors, which are relatively insensitive to long-wavelength red light; this spectral sensitivity shift toward blue-green, called the Purkinje shift, makes red objects appear much darker. People with Stargardt disease lose central cone function and depend even more on rod-mediated vision in dim illumination, so the Purkinje shift further impairs their ability to detect red objects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGF3",
    "long_answer": "Otodental syndrome is most often produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions involving the FGF3 gene on chromosome 11q13. FGF3 encodes fibroblast growth factor 3, a signaling protein required for normal inner-ear morphogenesis; its haplo-insufficiency impairs cochlear development and results in the characteristic sensorineural hearing loss seen in otodental syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brad Carter",
    "long_answer": "Brad Carter—an American guitarist and actor—was diagnosed with essential tremor and later underwent deep-brain stimulation surgery. He recorded a public-service announcement describing how the neurological disorder affected his ability to play music and how treatment helped, thereby raising awareness about living with essential tremor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azumolene",
    "long_answer": "Azumolene is a water-soluble analogue of dantrolene that has been investigated experimentally for malignant hyperthermia. Studies in susceptible animal models and isolated muscle preparations show that azumolene inhibits excessive calcium release from the ryanodine receptor with potency comparable to dantrolene, thereby preventing or reversing the hypermetabolic crisis characteristic of malignant hyperthermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome (the “heart-hand” syndrome) is caused by pathogenic variants in the TBX5 gene. Patients typically show hand anomalies such as clinodactyly of the 5th finger together with congenital fusion (coalition) of the carpal bones in the wrist. These limb findings accompany the cardiac septal defects that characterize the condition, making Holt-Oram the classic syndrome combining clinodactyly with carpal bone fusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac arrhythmia",
    "long_answer": "Berk–Tabatznik syndrome denotes the bradycardia–tachycardia (sick-sinus) pattern in which episodes of supraventricular tachycardia alternate with marked sinus bradycardia or pauses. Because it arises from dysfunction of the heart’s conduction system and produces abnormal heart rhythms, it is classified as a cardiac arrhythmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Hyperimmunoglobulin E syndrome (Job’s syndrome) is characterized by markedly elevated serum IgE levels and defective neutrophil chemotaxis. This immune defect predisposes patients to recurrent ‘cold’ skin and pulmonary abscesses that are classically caused by the gram-positive coccal bacterium Staphylococcus aureus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Vaginismus (now classified under genito-pelvic pain/penetration disorder in DSM-5) is understood to arise from an interaction of psychological and physical factors. Established clinical literature and consensus guidelines list several pre-disposing or precipitating factors, including:  • Anxiety disorders or high trait anxiety/fear of pain and penetration.  • Prior pelvic or genital injury (e.g., childbirth trauma, episiotomy, accidents) that conditions the individual to expect pain.  • Prior pelvic or gynecologic surgery (e.g., hymenectomy, vaginal or vulvar procedures) that can create pain memories or physical sensitivity.  These factors are repeatedly cited in gynecology and sexual-medicine texts and in reviews by the International Society for the Study of Women’s Sexual Health (ISSWSH) as contributors to both primary and secondary vaginismus. Therefore, anxiety disorders, prior injury, and prior surgery are indeed considered recognized risk factors for developing vaginismus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 (fibrillin-1)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1, are the well-established genetic cause of Marfan syndrome. Distinct heterozygous missense mutations in the same gene—especially those altering residues near the RGD integrin-binding motif—have also been shown to cause Stiff skin syndrome. Thus, pathogenic variants in FBN1 are implicated in both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose oral prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Polymyalgia rheumatica is highly sensitive to glucocorticoids; guidelines recommend starting with a low daily dose of an oral corticosteroid, typically prednisone 10–20 mg. This rapidly relieves the characteristic proximal muscle pain and stiffness and confirms the diagnosis, after which the dose is tapered based on clinical response and inflammatory markers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metoclopramide",
    "long_answer": "Metoclopramide is a dopamine-D2 receptor antagonist frequently prescribed as a pro-kinetic agent for gastro-oesophageal reflux and heartburn. By blocking hypothalamic D2 receptors it lifts dopamine’s normal inhibitory control over pituitary lactotrophs, leading to increased prolactin secretion. Consequently, metoclopramide is a well-documented cause of drug-induced hyperprolactinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Great toe",
    "long_answer": "Onychocryptosis, commonly called an ingrown toenail, occurs overwhelmingly on the hallux (great toe). The great toe endures the highest mechanical pressure from footwear and gait, making its nail plate most prone to penetrating the periungual skin, so it is cited as the digit most frequently affected in clinical series."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious disease",
    "long_answer": "Rickettsia are obligate intracellular bacteria that cause infections such as Rocky Mountain spotted fever and epidemic typhus. Diagnosis, selection of appropriate antimicrobials (e.g., doxycycline), and management of systemic complications fall under the expertise of physicians trained in the medical specialty of infectious disease, making this specialty the one most involved in treating rickettsial illnesses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Avoidant personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and clinical studies of personality–disorder comorbidity consistently show that avoidant personality disorder co-occurs at very high rates with both paranoid personality disorder (cluster A) and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (cluster C). Correlation matrices from large samples (e.g., Torgersen et al., 2001; Pulay et al., 2009) demonstrate strong comorbidity coefficients between avoidant PD and paranoid PD, and similarly between avoidant PD and OCPD, making avoidant personality disorder the disorder most characteristically shared with both of these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "Echocardiography—particularly transesophageal echocardiography (TEE)—is essential for diagnosing infective endocarditis because it directly visualizes valvular vegetations, abscesses, and prosthetic-valve involvement, findings that constitute a major Duke diagnostic criterion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute otitis media",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Gustavson syndrome frequently experience acute otitis media—recurrent middle-ear infections that typically arise during or shortly after viral upper-respiratory tract infections. This symptom is repeatedly described in the clinical literature on the X-linked Gustavson (hypotonic facies–seizures) syndrome, making acute otitis media a characteristic and common manifestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluorescent in situ hybridization",
    "long_answer": "1p36 deletion syndrome is confirmed by demonstrating loss of the subtelomeric region of chromosome 1p using fluorescent in situ hybridization (FISH) probes (or equivalent chromosomal microarray). FISH directly visualizes the absence of the 1p36 signal, providing definitive molecular cytogenetic evidence of the deletion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homotetramer",
    "long_answer": "The HMGCL gene encodes mitochondrial 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase, which crystallographic and biochemical studies have shown assembles into a homotetramer—four identical subunits arranged as a dimer of dimers. This quaternary structure is required for its catalytic cleavage of HMG-CoA in ketogenesis and leucine degradation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oligodactyly",
    "long_answer": "Oligodactyly is a congenital limb malformation characterized by the presence of fewer than the normal five digits on a hand or foot. It arises from disruptions in early limb bud development, often due to genetic mutations or vascular insults, leading to the absence or failure of formation of one or more fingers or toes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nerve biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Confirmation of chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy can be obtained by performing a nerve biopsy—most commonly a sural nerve biopsy—which allows histopathological examination of the peripheral nerve tissue for segmental demyelination, remyelination, and inflammatory infiltrates characteristic of CIDP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basophils",
    "long_answer": "Chronic myelogenous (myeloid) leukemia typically shows marked basophilia—an abnormally high number of basophils in the peripheral blood. While many myeloid lineage cells are elevated, persistent elevation of basophils is characteristic and helps distinguish CML from other leukocytoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fixed mid-dilated pupil",
    "long_answer": "In acute angle-closure glaucoma the iris becomes ischemic and the pupil often becomes mid-dilated and fails to constrict when light is shone into either eye. Checking the pupillary light reflex therefore reveals a fixed (non-reactive), mid-dilated pupil, a classic and common sign of an acute attack."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Huo Qubing",
    "long_answer": "Historical chronicles of the Western Han state that the celebrated cavalry general Huo Qubing (died 117 BCE) suddenly fell ill after returning from a northern campaign and succumbed to a rapidly fatal ‘epidemic pestilence’ (瘟疫). Modern historians identify this description with bubonic plague, making Huo Qubing the first documented Han-dynasty figure recorded as dying from plague contamination."
}

{
  "short_answer": "Meningitis",
  "long_answer": "Al-Gazali-Donnai-Mueller syndrome is a severe variant of frontonasal dysplasia characterized by midline facial clefts and dermoid or encephalocele tracts that can communicate between the nasal cavity and the cranial cavity. Because these tracts provide a direct pathway for bacteria to reach the central nervous system, bacterial meningitis is a well-documented and potentially life-threatening complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teratogenicity",
    "long_answer": "Thalidomide is a powerful human teratogen; exposure during pregnancy can cause severe congenital malformations such as limb reduction defects. Therefore, when thalidomide is used to treat HIV-associated ulcers or wasting, the major risk is the potential induction of serious birth defects if a patient becomes pregnant, necessitating stringent contraception and pregnancy-prevention measures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mepolizumab",
    "long_answer": "Mepolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets interleukin-5 (IL-5). By blocking IL-5 signaling, it reduces eosinophil development and survival, thereby lowering the pathogenic eosinophil count characteristic of hypereosinophilic syndrome. Clinical trials demonstrated its efficacy and safety, leading to regulatory approval specifically for the treatment of hypereosinophilic syndrome in patients who do not respond adequately to conventional therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Podiatry",
    "long_answer": "Onychophosis is a hyperkeratotic thickening that develops in the nail folds—most commonly of the toes—leading to pain and difficulty with footwear. Because it is a disorder of the toenails and surrounding skin, diagnosis and definitive care (debridement, pressure‐relief, footwear advice) are routinely provided by podiatrists, the medical specialists dedicated to conditions of the foot and toenails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "The FRAXD locus corresponds to the folate-sensitive rare fragile site fra(X)(q27.3)D. Cytogenetic and sequence mapping place this gene at band q27.3 of the human X chromosome; hence FRAXD is located on chromosome X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant connective-tissue disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene, which encodes fibrillin-1. Defective fibrillin weakens elastic fibers throughout the body, including the lung pleura, predisposing patients to apical blebs and an elevated incidence of spontaneous pneumothorax."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meiotic nondisjunction",
    "long_answer": "Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) most commonly arises when the X chromosomes fail to separate properly during meiosis I or II in either the mother’s oogenesis or the father’s spermatogenesis. This meiotic nondisjunction produces a gamete with an extra X chromosome; when it is fertilized by a normal gamete, the resulting zygote carries the XXY karyotype characteristic of Klinefelter syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis is most commonly caused by mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel (Nav1.4). Pathogenic variants in SCN4A disrupt sodium channel inactivation, leading to sustained depolarization of muscle fibers and episodic weakness triggered by elevated serum potassium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Petrolatum (petroleum jelly)",
    "long_answer": "Petrolatum, commonly called petroleum jelly, is a widely used ingredient in skin-care creams and ointments. It forms an occlusive barrier on the epidermis that prevents transepidermal water loss, making it a first-line moisturizer for xeroderma (abnormally dry skin). Its barrier and emollient properties also protect minor wounds and promote healing, explaining its common recommendation for both skin protection and repair."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aedes aegypti",
    "long_answer": "Aedes aegypti is the primary mosquito vector that transmits dengue virus to humans. Its day-biting behavior, preference for human blood, and ability to breed in urban water containers make it the chief driver of dengue fever outbreaks worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MUT",
    "long_answer": "OMIM identifies mutations in the MUT gene, which encodes the enzyme methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, as the cause of the classic ‘mut’ type (mut0/mut–) methylmalonic acidemia. This mutational class accounts for the majority of methylmalonic acidemia cases, making MUT the gene most commonly responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aulus Cornelius Celsus",
    "long_answer": "In his 1st-century AD medical encyclopedia De Medicina, the Roman encyclopaedist Aulus Cornelius Celsus advised patients with nyctalopia (night blindness) to eat liver—an organ later found to be exceptionally rich in vitamin A, the nutrient whose deficiency causes the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitch Clarke",
    "long_answer": "In the television series “Zoey’s Extraordinary Playlist,” Zoey’s father, Mitch Clarke, is depicted as living with progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP). The storyline shows the characteristic neurological decline of PSP—loss of voluntary eye movement, motor impairment, speech and swallowing difficulties—highlighting the disease’s progressive neurodegenerative nature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supraventricular tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is a rapid heart rhythm that originates in tissue located at or just above the atrioventricular (AV) node. In SVT, ectopic pacemaker cells or re-entrant circuits near the AV node fire repeatedly and much faster than the normal sino-atrial pacemaker, driving the heart rate well above normal limits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN5",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked chloride channel gene CLCN5 are the classic cause of Dent’s disease (Dent disease type 1). Loss-of-function of CLCN5 impairs proximal-tubule endocytosis, leading to phosphaturia as well as low-molecular-weight proteinuria and hypercalciuria. The resulting chronic renal phosphate wasting can produce the skeletal picture of hypophosphatemic rickets, so CLCN5 mutations are recognised as a genetic cause of both Dent’s disease and hypophosphatemic rickets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamazepine",
    "long_answer": "Carbamazepine is an anticonvulsant that blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, thereby reducing repetitive neuronal firing. By dampening peripheral nerve hyperexcitability in neuromyotonia (Isaacs’ syndrome), it lessens the continuous muscle fiber activity responsible for spasms, stiffness, and neuropathic pain, and is therefore commonly used as first-line symptomatic therapy for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exchange transfusion",
    "long_answer": "In Crigler–Najjar syndrome, unconjugated bilirubin can rise to neurotoxic levels. When this hyperbilirubinemia becomes severe and must be lowered rapidly, an exchange transfusion is employed to remove bilirubin-laden blood and replace it with donor blood, thereby quickly reducing serum bilirubin concentrations and preventing kernicterus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Hyperimmunoglobulin E (Job) syndrome are prone to recurrent cutaneous and pulmonary infections caused mainly by Staphylococcus aureus. Continuous or intermittent prophylaxis with the antistaphylococcal antibiotic trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the standard first-line measure to prevent these infections, as it provides reliable activity against S. aureus and has demonstrated reduction in infection frequency in this patient population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alcohol withdrawal",
    "long_answer": "Abrupt cessation of chronic heavy alcohol use produces Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome, in which CNS hyper-excitability from down-regulated GABAergic and up-regulated glutamatergic signaling can precipitate generalized tonic-clonic seizures. These withdrawal-induced seizures may merge into prolonged, recurrent seizures without recovery of consciousness—i.e., status epilepticus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Necrotizing enterocolitis",
    "long_answer": "Infants born with gastroschisis frequently develop necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) because their exposed, inflamed intestines have impaired perfusion and motility, predisposing them to ischemia and bacterial translocation. Preterm infants likewise have immature intestinal barriers and altered gut perfusion, making NEC one of their most feared gastrointestinal complications. Thus, NEC is a shared and well-recognized complication in both populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-resolution ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution ultrasound can non-invasively visualize the median nerve within the carpal tunnel, allowing assessment of its cross-sectional area, echogenicity, and swelling. These sonographic findings correlate with carpal tunnel syndrome, making ultrasound a widely accepted imaging tool for its diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Celiac disease",
    "long_answer": "Celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) is an autoimmune condition triggered by gluten that leads to IgA autoantibody production. The same IgA-mediated immunity underlies two extra-intestinal manifestations: dermatitis herpetiformis, a pruritic vesicular rash pathognomonic for celiac disease, and Henoch–Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis), in which IgA immune-complex deposition produces palpable purpura. Thus, celiac disease is the autoimmune disorder epidemiologically and mechanistically linked to both Henoch–Schönlein purpura and dermatitis herpetiformis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATXN7",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia type II corresponds to spinocerebellar ataxia type 7, a disorder caused by CAG-repeat expansion in the ATXN7 gene. Mutant ATXN7 protein leads to neurodegeneration that affects both the cerebellum and the retina, producing the characteristic combination of progressive ataxia and pigmentary retinal degeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risk of irreversible blindness",
    "long_answer": "Giant cell arteritis causes inflammatory narrowing and thrombosis of cranial arteries, especially the ophthalmic and posterior ciliary branches of the carotid. Without prompt high-dose corticosteroid treatment, this can abruptly occlude blood flow to the optic nerve and retina, leading to sudden, permanent vision loss—often in both eyes—within hours to days. Because this sight-threatening complication is preventable only with immediate therapy, the condition is treated as a medical emergency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nintedanib",
    "long_answer": "Nintedanib is a small-molecule tyrosine-kinase inhibitor that targets PDGFR, FGFR and VEGFR signaling. It has been evaluated in pre-clinical and early clinical studies for plexiform neurofibromas and NF1-associated malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors. Independently, nintedanib is an approved antifibrotic agent for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, where it slows the decline in lung function by blocking the same pro-fibrotic receptor pathways. Thus, it is the drug investigated for neurofibromas and also licensed for treating idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography",
    "long_answer": "Adenomatoid odontogenic tumors characteristically contain small intralesional calcifications that can be difficult to appreciate on plain dental radiographs. Computed tomography (CT) provides high-resolution cross-sectional images that clearly display the well-defined radiolucent lesion and its fine calcified flecks, allowing confident diagnosis and precise assessment of the tumor’s extent and relation to adjacent structures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "Cyclophosphamide, a nitrogen-mustard alkylating agent that cross-links DNA and blocks cell replication, is part of standard combination regimens (e.g., CAP: cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, cisplatin) used to treat advanced or malignant thymoma when surgery alone is insufficient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blood–brain barrier",
    "long_answer": "In adrenoleukodystrophy the destructive demyelination occurs within the central nervous system; therefore, any therapeutic agent must penetrate the blood–brain barrier to reach the affected white-matter tissue and exert its effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Factor X deficiency is a rare bleeding disorder caused by reduced or dysfunctional coagulation factor X. Evaluation requires specialized coagulation studies (e.g., PT, aPTT, factor assays) and management often involves plasma‐derived or recombinant factor X replacement and monitoring for bleeding complications—all core competencies of a hematologist. Therefore, the medical specialty most involved in diagnosing and treating Factor X deficiency is Hematology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mansonella streptocerca",
    "long_answer": "Mansonella streptocerca is a filarial nematode whose microfilariae inhabit the skin. Their presence provokes papular dermatitis and marked pruritus in infected individuals, making this species well-known for causing itching in mansonellosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease",
    "long_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) is a hereditary peripheral neuropathy that has been linked to pathogenic variants in more than 100 different genes. Although classically characterized by progressive distal muscle weakness and sensory loss, certain CMT subtypes also involve the auditory nerve or cochlea, leading to sensorineural hearing loss. The extraordinary genetic heterogeneity of CMT—spanning genes encoding myelin proteins, axonal cytoskeletal components, mitochondrial factors, and more—makes it one of the most genetically diverse neurological disorders known, with over a hundred gene mutations documented to date."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Triple-A (Allgrove) syndrome includes primary adrenal insufficiency, a hormonal disorder of the adrenal cortex. Management of adrenal hormone deficiencies—diagnosis, glucocorticoid/mineralocorticoid replacement, and long-term endocrine monitoring—is the domain of the medical specialty of Endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congo red",
    "long_answer": "Primary cutaneous amyloidosis is confirmed histologically by demonstrating amyloid deposits in skin biopsy sections. The standard stain for amyloid is the diazo dye Congo red, which binds β-pleated sheet–rich amyloid fibrils and produces the characteristic apple-green birefringence under polarized light, allowing definitive diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heliotrope",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile dermatomyositis is characterized by a distinctive heliotrope rash—a violet-to-lilac (purple) discoloration—that can appear not only on the eyelids but also on extensor surfaces such as the elbows. This heliotrope hue helps distinguish the disease’s cutaneous findings from ordinary erythematous rashes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zika virus",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological and laboratory studies have shown that maternal infection with Zika virus during pregnancy can cross the placenta and disrupt fetal neurodevelopment, leading to microcephaly and other congenital abnormalities collectively termed Congenital Zika Syndrome. The surge in microcephaly cases during the 2015–2016 Zika outbreaks in Brazil and other regions firmly established this association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reversible forward trunk flexion",
    "long_answer": "Camptocormia is diagnosed clinically by the presence of pronounced thoracolumbar (forward) flexion—typically >30–45°—that appears when the patient is standing or walking but disappears almost completely when the patient lies supine. This characteristic, reversible forward trunk flexion serves as the key diagnostic indicator distinguishing camptocormia from other causes of postural muscle weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital syphilis",
    "long_answer": "Hutchinson’s teeth—centrally notched, peg-shaped permanent incisors—form one element of Hutchinson’s triad and are considered pathognomonic for congenital syphilis, the transplacental infection with Treponema pallidum acquired in utero."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T-cell–mediated delayed hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "Granuloma annulare is thought to arise from a type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity reaction, in which activated T lymphocytes orchestrate a cellular immune response that leads to dermal granulomatous inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophage activation syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A life-threatening hyper-inflammatory state known as macrophage activation syndrome (a form of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis) can develop in children with systemic-onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis. It is characterised by excessive activation and proliferation of macrophages and T cells, leading to pancytopenia, liver dysfunction, coagulopathy, very high serum ferritin and multi-organ failure, and is recognised as the most serious complication of this subtype of JIA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bellevue Hospital",
    "long_answer": "The TV series’ New Amsterdam Hospital is modeled on Bellevue Hospital in New York City. Bellevue’s East-Coast location, with visiting patients and staff from endemic regions such as the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys, makes histoplasmosis—caused by inhalation of Histoplasma capsulatum spores carried on dust or bird/bat guano—a potential clinical concern, aligning real epidemiology with the show’s medical scenarios."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytokeratin",
    "long_answer": "In anaplastic (undifferentiated) thyroid carcinoma the neoplastic cells almost invariably retain epithelial cytokeratin filaments. Demonstrating cytokeratin immunoreactivity (using pancytokeratin/AE1-AE3 antibodies) is therefore a standard immunohistochemistry step to confirm the diagnosis and to distinguish anaplastic thyroid cancer from mimics such as sarcoma or lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The Anatomy of Melancholy",
    "long_answer": "Robert Burton’s 1621 treatise 'The Anatomy of Melancholy' devotes extensive sections to hypochondriasis, analysing its causes, symptoms, and suggested treatments within the broader discussion of melancholy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging is routinely used to detect the cerebral abnormalities of phenylketonuria, such as diffuse white-matter hyperintensities and impaired myelination, because its high-contrast resolution allows non-invasive visualization of structural brain changes caused by elevated phenylalanine levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAPN3",
    "long_answer": "Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by mutations in the CAPN3 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle specific calcium-dependent protease calpain-3. Deficiency or dysfunction of calpain-3 disrupts muscle fiber maintenance, leading to the characteristic pelvic and shoulder-girdle muscle weakness of LGMD2A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arch of the aorta",
    "long_answer": "The arch of the aorta develops mainly from the left fourth aortic arch. Because it lies to the left and anterior of the trachea in the superior mediastinum, enlargement or aneurysm of this arterial arch can press on the trachea and push it toward the right side."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphatic system",
    "long_answer": "After inhalation of Paracoccidioides spp. spores, the fungus establishes a primary pulmonary focus and then disseminates predominantly through the lymphatic system. Lymphatic spread carries the organism to regional lymph nodes and other reticulo-endothelial tissues, accounting for the characteristic mucocutaneous and nodal lesions seen in paracoccidioidomycosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary ciliary dyskinesia",
    "long_answer": "Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by mutations that disrupt the dynein arms and motility of cilia. Because embryonic nodal cilia fail to generate the normal left-ward flow that establishes left–right body asymmetry, laterality is randomized: roughly 50 % of individuals with PCD develop complete situs inversus (the Kartagener variant). Donated bodies affected by PCD therefore provide valuable material for anatomical and ultrastructural studies of ciliary defects and visceral laterality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tonsillar carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil frequently presents with odynophagia (painful swallowing) because the lesion lies in the oropharyngeal swallowing pathway. While advanced oropharyngeal cancers are often treated with radiotherapy or chemoradiation, an early-stage lesion that is confined (isolated) to the tonsil can be managed surgically by radical or partial tonsillectomy. This makes tonsillar carcinoma the carcinoma that both causes painful swallowing and may require surgery if the tumour is isolated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Japan",
    "long_answer": "All published cases of the rare association between congenital scalp defects (aplasia cutis) and severe myopia—the so-called Aplasia cutis-myopia syndrome—originate from Japan, where the syndrome was first described and subsequently reported in additional Japanese patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Nearly all classic organic acidemias—such as propionic acidemia, methylmalonic acidemia, isovaleric acidemia, and glutaric acidemia—result from loss-of-function mutations in enzymes of branched-chain amino-acid or odd-chain fatty-acid catabolism. Two non-functional alleles are required to abolish enzyme activity; therefore the disorders are transmitted in an autosomal recessive pattern, which is the predominant mode of inheritance observed for organic acidemias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microwave rotational spectroscopy",
    "long_answer": "The geometry of gaseous formaldehyde (H₂CO) was deduced from its high-resolution microwave rotational spectrum. Analysis of the rotational constants (A, B, C) obtained by microwave spectroscopy yields accurate bond lengths and bond angles, allowing a precise determination of the molecular structure in the gas phase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cri-du-chat syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cri-du-chat syndrome (5p deletion syndrome) results from a partial deletion of the short arm of chromosome 5. Besides its hallmark high-pitched “cat-like” cry, affected infants often show craniofacial anomalies such as micrognathia, hypertelorism, and low-set or malformed ears. Ear malformations frequently include preauricular skin tags and preauricular fistulas, making this chromosomal deletion disorder a recognized cause of these external ear anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)",
    "long_answer": "Endodermal sinus (yolk-sac) tumor cells characteristically synthesize and secrete alpha-fetoprotein. Immunohistochemical staining for AFP shows strong cytoplasmic positivity in the tumor, making the AFP stain the routine histological marker used to confirm the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mavoglurant",
    "long_answer": "Mavoglurant (AFQ056) is a negative allosteric modulator of the metabotropic glutamate receptor-5 (mGluR5). It entered clinical trials to reduce levodopa-induced dyskinesia in Parkinson’s disease and, because excessive mGluR5 signaling is also implicated in the neurobiology of Fragile X syndrome, the same compound has been evaluated in clinical studies for Fragile X patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noonan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome, an autosomal-dominant RASopathy, typically presents with chest wall deformities such as pectus excavatum and congenital heart disease, most characteristically pulmonary valve stenosis. These hallmark features link the syndrome to both musculoskeletal and cardiac manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "18F-FDG PET/CT scan",
    "long_answer": "Fluorodeoxyglucose positron-emission tomography combined with computed tomography (18F-FDG PET/CT) is the preferred imaging modality for Letterer-Siwe disease (multisystem Langerhans-cell histiocytosis) because it can reveal metabolically active lesions throughout the body—bone, liver, spleen, lung, lymph nodes, and other organs—thereby mapping the extent of multi-organ involvement in a single study."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pecten oculi",
    "long_answer": "Birds possess a highly vascular, pigmented structure called the pecten oculi that projects from the optic disc into the vitreous body. Because the avian retina is largely avascular, the pecten oculi serves as the primary source of oxygen and nutrients for retinal tissue, allowing clear vision without intraretinal blood vessels that would otherwise scatter light."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medullary thyroid carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN 2) is caused by germ-line activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene. These mutations drive the development of medullary thyroid carcinoma originating from parafollicular C-cells, making medullary thyroid carcinoma the characteristic thyroid malignancy seen in virtually all MEN 2 patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "May-Hegglin anomaly is an inherited platelet disorder in which leukocytes—most conspicuously neutrophils—show distinctive Döhle-like cytoplasmic inclusion bodies composed of altered myosin heavy-chain protein. These inclusions are a hallmark for diagnosing the anomaly and are routinely observed in peripheral blood smears of affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryotherapy",
    "long_answer": "Benign cutaneous fibromas can be eliminated by cryotherapy (cryosurgery), in which the lesion is rapidly frozen—typically with liquid nitrogen—causing cellular ice-crystal formation, vascular stasis, and subsequent tissue necrosis. The devitalized fibroma then sloughs off, providing a simple, minimally invasive means of removal for these benign tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whipple disease",
    "long_answer": "Whipple disease is a very rare systemic infectious disorder caused by the bacterium Tropheryma whipplei. Although it classically presents with weight loss, diarrhea, and arthralgia, the infection can disseminate to many organs, including the eyes. Ocular involvement frequently manifests as anterior or posterior uveitis, sometimes preceding gastrointestinal symptoms. Recognition of uveitis in the context of this rare multisystem infection is therefore important for early diagnosis and treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sign",
    "long_answer": "In clinical terminology, an objective, observable manifestation of a disorder—such as the visible bony prominence characteristic of a bunion—is called a “sign.” Signs differ from symptoms, which are the patient’s subjective experiences."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ascitic fluid cytology",
    "long_answer": "Obtaining ascitic fluid by diagnostic paracentesis and performing cytological examination allows microscopic evaluation of the cells present. Detecting malignant cells, inflammatory cells, or infectious organisms in this cytology helps pinpoint whether the ascites is due to cancer, infection, or other pathologies, thereby revealing the underlying cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Metabolic syndrome consists of insulin resistance, central obesity, dyslipidemia and hypertension—abnormalities of energy, glucose and lipid homeostasis that fall under hormonal and metabolic regulation. These disorders are classically evaluated and managed by physicians trained in Endocrinology, the specialty devoted to diseases of metabolism such as diabetes and obesity; therefore endocrinologists are the specialists most commonly responsible for treating patients with metabolic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "George H. Whipple",
    "long_answer": "George Hoyt Whipple’s experiments showed that a diet rich in liver rapidly restored hemoglobin and red-blood-cell levels in anemic dogs. These findings led directly to the use of liver therapy in human patients with pernicious anemia, work for which Whipple shared the 1934 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Mounier-Kuhn syndrome is a disorder of marked dilatation and structural weakness of the trachea and main bronchi that leads to chronic cough, recurrent lower-respiratory infections, and bronchiectasis. Evaluation requires chest imaging, pulmonary function testing, and often bronchoscopy, and treatment focuses on airway clearance, infection control, and respiratory support. These diagnostic and therapeutic tasks fall within the expertise of pulmonologists (respiratory medicine specialists), making Pulmonology the specialty most commonly responsible for recognizing and managing the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edema",
    "long_answer": "Achondrogenesis type 2 often presents with generalized edema, an abnormal accumulation of fluid in the body’s interstitial tissues (sometimes manifesting prenatally as hydrops fetalis). This fluid build-up is frequently noted on prenatal ultrasound and is a well-documented clinical feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciprofloxacin",
    "long_answer": "Topical ciprofloxacin (0.3 % ophthalmic solution) is routinely prescribed for bacterial keratitis because this fluoroquinolone achieves high corneal concentrations and has broad-spectrum activity against common causative organisms, including Pseudomonas, Gram-negative rods, and many Gram-positive cocci."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Austria",
    "long_answer": "One of the very first physicians to describe and name encephalitis lethargica was the Austrian neuro-psychiatrist Constantin von Economo, who published his seminal observations from Vienna in 1916–1917. Therefore, Austria was the country home to this pioneering researcher of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytogenetic karyotyping",
    "long_answer": "Conventional cytogenetic karyotyping of bone-marrow metaphase chromosomes is routinely used in acute myeloid leukemia to detect balanced translocations (e.g., t(8;21), inv(16)), deletions, trisomies, and other chromosomal abnormalities, providing critical information for diagnosis, risk stratification, and treatment planning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asplenia (postsplenectomy)",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis is often treated by splenectomy. Removal of the spleen abolishes splenic macrophage-mediated clearance and IgM-mediated opsonization of encapsulated bacteria, so patients lacking a functional spleen cannot effectively clear Streptococcus pneumoniae, placing them at high risk for invasive pneumococcal infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microneurography",
    "long_answer": "Microneurography involves inserting fine microelectrodes into a peripheral nerve to record single-unit action potentials in awake humans. This technique has been applied to complex regional pain syndrome to measure spontaneous and evoked activity in sensory and sympathetic fibers, providing direct information on abnormal nerve impulses that contribute to CRPS pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandible",
    "long_answer": "Hallermann–Streiff syndrome is characterized by marked mandibular hypoplasia (micrognathia). Because the lower jaw is under-developed, patients may suffer airway obstruction, feeding difficulties, and malocclusion, making surgical reconstruction of the mandible (e.g., distraction osteogenesis or grafting) a common corrective procedure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dexamethasone suppression test",
    "long_answer": "Cushing’s syndrome produced by an autonomous, cortisol-secreting adrenocortical adenoma is confirmed with a dexamethasone suppression test: cortisol secretion fails to suppress after dexamethasone because it is ACTH-independent, distinguishing the adrenal tumor from pituitary- or ectopic-ACTH–driven hypercortisolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial chylomicronemia",
    "long_answer": "Familial chylomicronemia (Type I hyperlipoproteinemia) is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused most often by lipoprotein lipase or apolipoprotein C-II deficiency. Because chylomicrons cannot be efficiently hydrolyzed, patients exhibit massive fasting hypertriglyceridemia and markedly elevated circulating chylomicrons, the hallmark biochemical features described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Byssinosis",
    "long_answer": "Byssinosis—often called 'brown-lung disease'—is a respiratory occupational disease seen predominantly in textile workers who inhale cotton, flax or hemp dust. Repeated exposure triggers airway inflammation, bronchoconstriction and chronic obstructive changes, leading to chest tightness and progressive dyspnoea, especially at the beginning of the work week. Thus, inhalation of cotton dust is the established cause of byssinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MTHFR",
    "long_answer": "Mutations—especially the common 677C>T missense variant—in the MTHFR (methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase) gene impair folate metabolism, raising homocysteine levels and reducing methyl-folate needed for DNA synthesis and methylation during early embryogenesis. This disruption of folate-dependent pathways interferes with neural tube closure and is a well-documented genetic risk factor for neural tube defects, including anencephaly, in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NDP gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Norrie disease is classically caused by mutations in the X-linked NDP gene, which encodes the secreted signaling protein norrin. Genetic analyses have shown that identical NDP mutations responsible for Norrie disease are present in a subset of premature infants who develop severe retinopathy of prematurity, indicating a shared pathogenic mechanism involving defective norrin/β-catenin retinal vascular signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCS1L",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the BCS1L gene, which encodes a mitochondrial chaperone required for assembly of respiratory-chain complex III, give rise to two distinct phenotypes: Björnstad syndrome (characterised by pili torti and sensorineural deafness) and the severe metabolic disorder GRACILE syndrome. Thus, mutation of BCS1L is the shared genetic cause of both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dyspareunia",
    "long_answer": "Dyspareunia is the medical term for painful sexual intercourse. Post-partum hormonal changes (low estrogen), perineal tears or episiotomy scars, and vaginal tissue trauma can lead to dyspareunia after childbirth, making intercourse uncomfortable or painful until healing and hormonal balance are restored."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Persistent fatigue",
    "long_answer": "After the acute infection with Rickettsia australis (Queensland tick typhus) has resolved, many patients report a prolonged period of marked tiredness or lack of energy—often termed post-infective or persistent fatigue. This lingering fatigue is well documented as the most common convalescent symptom and can last for weeks to months in a subset of recovered individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urinary oligosaccharide chromatography",
    "long_answer": "Analyzing the pattern of oligosaccharides excreted in urine by thin-layer or other chromatography distinguishes the two disorders: alpha-mannosidosis shows a broad ‘mannose-rich’ oligosaccharide pattern, whereas beta-mannosidosis produces a different, usually single disaccharide peak. Thus urinary oligosaccharide chromatography clearly differentiates alpha- from beta-mannosidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Double outlet right ventricle",
    "long_answer": "In the congenital heart defect known as double outlet right ventricle (DORV), both great arteries—the aorta and the pulmonary trunk—originate predominantly or entirely from the right ventricle instead of one arising from each ventricle. This anatomic malposition defines the lesion and distinguishes it from other outflow-tract defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trismus-pseudocamptodactyly syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Trismus-pseudocamptodactyly syndrome (also classified as Distal Arthrogryposis type 7) is characterized by congenital contractures of the fingers and limitations in mouth opening. It is well established that heterozygous missense mutations in the MYH8 gene, which encodes the perinatal skeletal muscle myosin heavy-chain, are the causal genetic defect leading to these contractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO5B",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MYO5B gene (progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 6) disrupt myosin-Vb–mediated apical membrane trafficking in both hepatocytes and intestinal enterocytes. In the liver this causes low-GGT cholestasis characteristic of PFIC, while in the intestine it produces the epithelial defect seen in microvillus inclusion disease, leading to profuse watery diarrhea. Thus the same MYO5B mutation can manifest with progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis and severe watery diarrhea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "Uveal (ocular) melanoma is the most common primary intra-ocular cancer in adults. Because its venous drainage enters the systemic circulation via the hepatic portal route, metastatic spread occurs predominantly to the liver, which is the first and by far the most frequent site of distant disease (≈90% of metastases)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "The SLC25A15 gene encodes the mitochondrial ornithine transporter-1. Pathogenic variants that abolish or markedly reduce the activity of this transporter prevent ornithine from entering the mitochondrial matrix, where the first two reactions of the urea cycle occur. As a result, carbamyl-phosphate cannot be combined with ornithine to form citrulline, leading to accumulation of carbamyl-phosphate, hyperammonemia, hyperornithinemia, and homocitrullinuria—the triad that defines Hyperornithinemia-Hyperammonemia-Homocitrullinuria (HHH) syndrome. HHH syndrome is classified among the inherited urea-cycle disorders in standard biomedical references such as OMIM, GeneReviews, and MedlinePlus (the consumer-oriented portal of the U.S. National Library of Medicine, the world’s largest medical library). Therefore, loss-of-function mutations in SLC25A15 are an established, causative genetic basis of this urea-cycle disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genzyme Corporation",
    "long_answer": "The approved enzyme replacement therapy for mucopolysaccharidosis type I is laronidase (marketed as Aldurazyme). This recombinant human α-L-iduronidase was developed jointly by BioMarin and Genzyme, and Genzyme Corporation manufactures and commercializes the product, providing the enzyme needed to replace the deficient α-L-iduronidase in MPS I patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural crest cells",
    "long_answer": "Frontonasal dysplasia arises when neural crest cells—ectoderm-derived mesenchyme that normally migrates into the frontonasal prominence—fail to migrate or proliferate properly. This defective neural crest cell migration prevents normal fusion of the midline facial structures, producing the characteristic midline facial cleft."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isocyanates",
    "long_answer": "Isocyanates, particularly compounds such as toluene di-isocyanate and hexamethylene di-isocyanate, are added to enamel coatings and polyurethane paints. Inhalation of isocyanate vapours or aerosols can trigger an immune-mediated inflammatory reaction in the lung parenchyma, leading to hypersensitivity pneumonitis (also called isocyanate-induced extrinsic allergic alveolitis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippocampus",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 episodic ataxia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the Kv1.1 potassium channel (KCNA1). Kv1.1 is densely expressed on hippocampal mossy-fiber axons and interneurons, where it dampens repetitive firing. When the channel is defective, hippocampal neurons become hyper-excitable, generating spontaneous, synchronized discharges that propagate as seizures. Thus, altered firing in the hippocampus is regarded as the main driver of the epileptic episodes associated with EA1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAK2 V617F mutation",
    "long_answer": "Primary myelofibrosis is a BCR-ABL–negative myeloproliferative neoplasm, and about 50–60 % of cases harbor the somatic gain-of-function point mutation V617F in the JAK2 tyrosine-kinase gene. This activating JAK2 mutation drives constitutive JAK-STAT signaling, leading to clonal myeloid proliferation and reactive marrow fibrosis, and is therefore a hallmark molecular abnormality associated with primary myelofibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "University of California, San Diego",
    "long_answer": "William L. Nyhan, the pediatrician and biochemical geneticist known for describing Lesch–Nyhan syndrome, is Professor Emeritus of Pediatrics at the University of California, San Diego School of Medicine; this is his current institutional affiliation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrology",
    "long_answer": "Multicentric carpotarsal osteolysis syndrome frequently progresses to progressive renal dysfunction and eventual kidney failure. The medical specialty that focuses on the diagnosis and treatment of kidney disease and renal failure is nephrology; nephrologists manage complications such as declining glomerular filtration rate, proteinuria, hypertension, and the need for dialysis or transplantation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marek’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Marek’s disease is a highly contagious herpesvirus infection of chickens caused by Gallid herpesvirus-2. It produces a T-cell lymphoma that infiltrates peripheral nerves and viscera; enlargement of the sciatic and brachial plexus leads to leg or wing paralysis, while visceral tumors confirm the diagnosis. The hallmark finding of neoplastic, transformed CD4+ T lymphocytes distinguishes Marek’s disease from other poultry neuropathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The familial (hereditary) form of Caffey disease, also called infantile cortical hyperostosis, is transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait. Mutations in the COL1A1 gene (e.g., the Arg836Cys substitution) segregate in affected families, showing vertical transmission with variable expressivity but without sex linkage, confirming autosomal dominant inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Massive fetal hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Ulceration of the umbilical cord, often seen in association with intestinal (particularly jejunal-ileal) atresia, can erode the cord vessels. When an umbilical vein or artery ruptures, the fetus can lose a large volume of blood into the amniotic cavity, leading to massive fetal hemorrhage that may cause shock, exsanguination, and intrauterine or early neonatal death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hand radiography",
    "long_answer": "In suspected brachydactyly, a standard X-ray of the hand (hand radiography) is obtained; the radiograph permits precise caliper measurement of the bony length of the third digit and comparison with overall hand length, thereby confirming abnormal shortness of the finger."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder",
    "long_answer": "Large U.S. epidemiologic surveys (e.g., the National Epidemiologic Survey on Alcohol and Related Conditions) show that Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD) has the highest lifetime prevalence—about 7–8% of U.S. adults—exceeding that of borderline, narcissistic, antisocial, and other personality disorders. Thus, OCPD is the most common personality disorder in the United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) is a systemic, non-inflammatory condition characterized by excessive ossification of ligaments and entheses, most prominently along the spine. Because it is a musculoskeletal disorder that overlaps with other spondyloarthropathies, diagnosis and non-surgical management (e.g., analgesia, physical therapy, evaluation of metabolic comorbidities) are typically handled by a rheumatologist, the specialist in diseases of the joints, bones, and connective tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function or missense mutations in the FOXL2 (Forkhead Box L2) transcription factor cause Blepharophimosis–Ptosis–Epicanthus Inversus Syndrome (BPES). In BPES type I, the ocular malformations are accompanied by premature ovarian insufficiency/ovarian failure in affected females, linking FOXL2 dysfunction to both eyelid development and ovarian follicle maintenance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Long COVID",
    "long_answer": "Persistent health problems—such as fatigue, dyspnea, and cognitive impairment—that last for weeks to months after the acute SARS-CoV-2 infection are collectively termed “Long COVID” (also called post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection, PASC)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proteus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Proteus syndrome is a rare mosaic disorder in which activating AKT1 mutations drive disproportionate, progressive overgrowth of skin, skeletal elements, and other tissues composed of hamartomas. The condition, often evoking the story of the ‘Elephant Man,’ was profiled in Season 10 of the TV series ‘Mystery Diagnosis,’ and its hallmark is exactly the combination of skeletal and hamartous tissue overgrowth noted in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 8",
    "long_answer": "Kaposi’s sarcoma is etiologically linked to infection with Human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also called Kaposi sarcoma–associated herpesvirus (KSHV). HHV-8 is a gamma-herpesvirus whose latent and lytic gene products promote endothelial cell proliferation and cytokine dysregulation, events central to the development of Kaposi’s sarcoma lesions, particularly in immunocompromised individuals such as those with HIV/AIDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "Liver cirrhosis markedly reduces portal venous flow and creates a hypercoagulable milieu, making it the single most frequent predisposing factor for portal vein thrombosis in adults. At the same time, cirrhosis—regardless of underlying etiology (e.g., chronic hepatitis B or C, alcohol, NASH)—is the dominant substrate for hepatocellular carcinoma, accounting for the great majority of HCC cases. Thus, cirrhosis is both a common cause of portal vein thrombosis and a leading cause of hepatocellular carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase",
    "long_answer": "The photosensitive blistering disorder described is Porphyria Cutanea Tarda. It results from a deficiency of the heme-biosynthetic enzyme uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD). Reduced UROD activity leads to accumulation of uroporphyrins, which generate reactive oxygen species on sunlight exposure, producing fragile skin and painful blisters in sun-exposed areas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sirolimus",
    "long_answer": "Sirolimus (Rapamune) is an mTOR inhibitor that was granted FDA approval specifically for lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM). By blocking aberrant mTOR-mediated smooth-muscle proliferation, sirolimus reduces lung cyst progression and markedly decreases chylous effusions such as chylothorax, making it the standard pharmacologic therapy for LAM-associated chylothorax."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MSX1",
    "long_answer": "MSX1 encodes a homeobox transcription factor essential for craniofacial and tooth development. Pathogenic variants in MSX1 are a well-established cause of congenital anodontia/hypodontia and have also been repeatedly linked to nonsyndromic cleft lip and/or palate, demonstrating its dual involvement in both dental agenesis and craniofacial clefting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TorsinA",
    "long_answer": "Early-onset generalized (DYT1) torsion dystonia results from a characteristic GAG deletion in the DYT1 (TOR1A) gene on chromosome 9, which encodes the AAA+ ATPase protein torsinA. The mutation produces a defective torsinA protein that disrupts neuronal function in motor pathways, leading to the clinical dystonia phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈10 years",
    "long_answer": "In the natural history of severe mucopolysaccharidosis type I (Hurler syndrome), progressive cardiorespiratory failure and neurologic decline lead most patients to death in early childhood; epidemiologic studies place the median survival at roughly the end of the first decade of life—about ten years—unless treated with HSCT or enzyme-replacement therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of vitamin B12 (cobalamin) impairs DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing hematopoietic precursors, leading to ineffective erythropoiesis and pancytopenia. Because many patients have malabsorption (e.g., pernicious anemia lacking intrinsic factor), treatment is typically intramuscular injections of vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin or hydroxocobalamin) to bypass the gastrointestinal tract and restore blood cell counts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deutetrabenazine",
    "long_answer": "Deutetrabenazine is a vesicular monoamine transporter-2 (VMAT2) inhibitor. It received U.S. FDA approval for the treatment of chorea associated with Huntington’s disease in 2017 and was subsequently approved for the treatment of tardive dyskinesia. Thus, it is the medication that carries indications for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chemotherapy",
    "long_answer": "For mycosis fungoides—a cutaneous T-cell lymphoma—one of the standard management options is chemotherapy, the administration of cytotoxic anti-cancer drugs such as methotrexate, mechlorethamine, gemcitabine, or multi-agent regimens (e.g., CHOP). These drugs target and kill the malignant T cells, making chemotherapy a well-established treatment choice when skin-directed measures are insufficient or the disease has progressed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the WNT4 gene are a well-documented cause of Müllerian duct (MRKH) agenesis. WNT4 lies on chromosome 1p36.23, and loss-of-function mutations disrupt WNT signalling needed for Müllerian duct formation, leading to uterine and vaginal aplasia in 46,XX individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lumbar puncture",
    "long_answer": "A lumbar puncture allows cerebrospinal fluid analysis, typically revealing albuminocytologic dissociation—elevated protein with few or no white blood cells—which is characteristic of Guillain–Barré syndrome and helps confirm the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleomorphic type",
    "long_answer": "Within the architectural classifications of hepatocellular carcinoma, the pleomorphic (or giant-cell) type is distinguished by marked cellular pleomorphism: tumor cells vary greatly in size and shape, often forming bizarre, multinucleated or giant cells. This pleomorphic cytology is the defining histologic feature of the pleomorphic type, setting it apart from the more uniform trabecular or pseudoglandular patterns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inborn error of metabolism caused by deficiency or absence of the hepatic enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase. Without this enzyme, phenylalanine cannot be efficiently converted to tyrosine and therefore accumulates in the bloodstream. Consequently, patients with untreated PKU have markedly elevated—not decreased—blood phenylalanine concentrations. Reducing phenylalanine levels requires dietary restriction, but the disorder itself intrinsically raises them. Thus, the statement that phenylketonuria decreases the levels of phenylalanine in the blood is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Masson’s trichrome",
    "long_answer": "Masson’s trichrome stain differentially colors collagen (blue-green) and smooth muscle/epithelium (red), so on colonic biopsies it allows the pathologist to see whether a thick subepithelial collagen band is present. This distinction is essential when evaluating microscopic colitis: collagenous colitis shows a thickened collagen band, whereas lymphocytic colitis does not. Thus Masson’s trichrome is the routinely used stain to differentiate collagen from muscle in this diagnostic setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "Hypokinesia—marked reduction in the speed and amplitude of voluntary movements, as seen in Parkinson’s disease—arises from dysfunction of the basal ganglia circuitry. Damage or dopamine loss in basal ganglia nuclei (notably the substantia nigra and striatum) disrupts their modulatory output to the motor cortex, leading to impaired initiation and scaling of movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscular dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Sam Schröder was born with muscular dystrophy, a group of inherited genetic disorders in which mutations disrupt the production of key muscle-structural proteins (such as dystrophin), leading to progressive muscle weakness and loss of function. The resulting impairment in all four limbs places him in the quad division of wheelchair tennis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acetylcholinesterase",
    "long_answer": "Metrifonate (trichlorfon) is an organophosphate prodrug that is spontaneously converted in vivo to dichlorvos. Dichlorvos irreversibly phosphorylates and thereby inhibits the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, blocking the breakdown of acetylcholine; this cholinesterase inhibition is responsible for metrifonate’s therapeutic and toxic effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zollinger-Ellison syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by gastrin-secreting tumors (gastrinomas) that markedly increase circulating gastrin levels. The excess gastrin stimulates the parietal cells of the stomach to secrete large amounts of hydrochloric acid (hyperchlorhydria), overwhelming normal mucosal defenses and producing recurrent, severe peptic ulcers, often in the stomach or proximal duodenum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ornithine transcarbamylase",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC), a key mitochondrial enzyme of the urea cycle that converts carbamoyl-phosphate and ornithine to citrulline, blocks the normal disposal of nitrogen generated from protein catabolism. With the urea cycle stalled, ammonia accumulates in the bloodstream (hyperammonemia), producing the characteristic clinical picture seen in OTC deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperphosphatemia",
    "long_answer": "In tumor lysis syndrome, rapid destruction of lymphoma cells releases large amounts of intracellular phosphate into the bloodstream, producing marked hyperphosphatemia. This electrolyte disturbance is a characteristic laboratory finding of tumor lysis syndrome and has been repeatedly documented in patients with AIDS-related lymphoma who develop this complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Von Hippel-Lindau disease",
    "long_answer": "Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) disease results from germ-line mutations of the VHL tumor-suppressor gene located on chromosome 3p25-26. This hereditary cancer syndrome predisposes affected individuals to develop multiple tumors, including retinal and cerebellar hemangioblastomas, clear-cell renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma. The link between the chromosome 3 mutation and the occurrence of pheochromocytoma is a hallmark of VHL disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluorescein angiography",
    "long_answer": "In presumed ocular histoplasmosis syndrome (POHS) the major sight-threatening complication is choroidal neovascularization. Fundus fluorescein angiography is routinely performed at follow-up visits because the intravenous dye highlights areas of neovascular leakage, allowing early detection and monitoring of CNV activity and treatment response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basilar artery",
    "long_answer": "Basilar-type (brainstem) migraine was historically attributed to transient vasospasm or dysfunction in the basilar artery and its brainstem–cerebellar branches, which explains the characteristic bilateral visual, vestibular, and brainstem aura symptoms seen in this migraine subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Barth syndrome commonly leads to a severe, life-threatening cardiomyopathy—often dilated or left-ventricular non-compaction—that impairs the heart’s pumping ability, predisposing patients to congestive heart failure and sudden cardiac death. This cardiac involvement is the major complication responsible for most morbidity and mortality in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuroplasticity",
    "long_answer": "Complex regional pain syndrome involves maladaptive neuroplasticity—activity-dependent reorganization of central nervous system circuits that alters somatosensory and motor cortical maps and amplifies pain signalling. This aberrant plasticity underlies the persistent, disproportionate pain and sensory–motor disturbances characteristic of CRPS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "8%",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies in the United States consistently report that prostatitis syndromes account for about 8 percent of all outpatient visits made by men to urologists, making it one of the most frequent urologic diagnoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valproate",
    "long_answer": "Northern epilepsy syndrome (progressive epilepsy with mental retardation, CLN8 disease) is typically managed with valproate (valproic acid/sodium valproate). Valproate’s antiepileptic action includes blockade of voltage-gated sodium ion channels, which limits high-frequency neuronal firing and helps control the generalized tonic-clonic seizures characteristic of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrin",
    "long_answer": "Congenital nephrotic syndrome (especially the Finnish-type) is caused by mutations in the NPHS1 gene that encodes nephrin. Nephrin is a key structural protein of the slit diaphragm situated between podocyte foot processes in the filtration slits of the glomerular basement membrane, where it maintains the size-selective permeability barrier. Loss or dysfunction of nephrin disrupts this barrier, leading to the massive proteinuria characteristic of congenital nephrotic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arterial switch operation",
    "long_answer": "For the usual (d-type) complete transposition of the great arteries, definitive repair is achieved with the arterial switch operation (Jatene procedure). This neonatal surgery anatomically restores the left ventricle-to-aorta and right ventricle-to–pulmonary artery connections by transecting the great arteries above the valves, exchanging their positions, and re-implanting the coronary arteries into the neoaorta, thereby correcting the parallel circulations and providing physiologic, long-term outcomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Underdiagnosis of mild cases",
    "long_answer": "Tourette syndrome often presents with mild, transient, or easily suppressed tics that do not cause significant impairment and therefore never prompt a medical visit or formal diagnosis. Epidemiological studies that depend on clinical records or self-report miss these unrecognized cases, so the true population prevalence is higher than reported."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orange juice",
    "long_answer": "Orange juice is naturally rich in vitamin C (ascorbic acid). Adequate dietary vitamin C is essential for collagen synthesis; without it, connective-tissue defects lead to scurvy. Regular consumption of vitamin C–rich orange juice therefore helps prevent scurvy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1 in 30,000 births",
    "long_answer": "Radial dysplasia (radial club hand) is the congenital anomaly that causes characteristic radial deviation of the wrist and may occur in the same individual or family with ulnar dysplasia. Epidemiologic studies place its incidence at roughly one case per 30,000 live births, making it far more common than ulnar longitudinal deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "Epilepsy is defined by recurrent seizures, and Crome syndrome—a rare congenital disorder sometimes termed myoclonic dwarfism—also characteristically includes epileptic or myoclonic seizures among its clinical features. Therefore, seizures are a symptom common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type IV hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "Erythema multiforme is driven by a delayed-type immune response in which cytotoxic T lymphocytes recognize viral or drug-related antigens on keratinocytes and release perforin-granzyme and Th1 cytokines. This T-cell–mediated, cell-dependent mechanism classifies it as a Type IV hypersensitivity reaction in the Gell-Coombs scheme."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus (HSV) is responsible for neonatal herpes, acquired by the infant during delivery, and it also produces a severe ocular infection known as herpetic keratoconjunctivitis, a serious form of conjunctivitis that can threaten vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandible",
    "long_answer": "In acromegaly, excess growth hormone stimulates disproportionate bone growth, especially in facial bones. The mandible—the lower jawbone—often enlarges, producing characteristic prognathism. As the primary movable bone in the masticatory apparatus that bears the lower teeth and provides attachment for major chewing muscles, the mandible is both commonly affected in acromegaly and indispensable for effective chewing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudomonas stutzeri",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomonas stutzeri was originally isolated and described by Burri and Stutzer in 1895 (initially as Bacillus denitrificans). It is a non-fluorescent, Gram-negative, denitrifying Pseudomonas species that occurs widely in soil and water but can infect humans. Although infections are uncommon, P. stutzeri is recognized as an opportunistic pathogen responsible for bacteraemia, endocarditis, wound infections and other illnesses, particularly in immunocompromised or hospitalized patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isoproterenol",
    "long_answer": "Isoproterenol is a non-selective β-adrenergic agonist. β1-stimulation increases heart rate and AV-node conduction, making it a recognized treatment for digoxin-induced sinus bradycardia or high-grade AV block. β2-stimulation drives potassium into cells via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activation, acutely lowering elevated serum potassium; intravenous or high-dose inhaled β-agonists (including isoproterenol) are standard adjuncts in the emergency management of hyperkalemia. Thus a single drug—Isoproterenol—can address both digoxin-related bradyarrhythmias and hyperkalemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vaginal dilation",
    "long_answer": "Women with Müllerian agenesis (MRKH syndrome) typically lack the upper vagina. Regular use of graduated vaginal dilators (the Frank method) applies gentle pressure to stretch the vaginal vestibule and create an adequate-length neovagina, allowing comfortable penetrative sexual intercourse without the need for surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaptonuria",
    "long_answer": "Alkaptonuria is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by deficiency of homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase in the tyrosine/phenylalanine degradation pathway. The resulting accumulation of homogentisic acid leads to its deposition in body fluids and connective tissues, producing dark pigmentation of sweat, urine, breast milk, and cartilage (ochronosis). Therefore, homogentisic acid–induced dark discoloration of sweat and breast milk is characteristic of alkaptonuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EFHC1",
    "long_answer": "EFHC1 (myoclonin-1) is a well-established susceptibility gene for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy. Its protein localizes to centrosomes and microtubules, helping organize the mitotic spindle during cell division and mediating Ca²⁺-dependent signaling that directs neuroblast migration. Disruption of these functions by EFHC1 mutations contributes to the pathogenesis of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caffeine",
    "long_answer": "Caffeine is a widely consumed central-nervous-system stimulant found in coffee, tea, and many beverages. It is well documented to provoke or worsen myokymia (such as eyelid twitching) due to its excitatory effect on peripheral nerves and muscles. At the same time, caffeine is commonly taken to enhance alertness, attention, and other aspects of cognitive performance, making it the archetypal stimulant linked to both phenomena."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heller myotomy",
    "long_answer": "In achalasia microcephaly syndrome, the esophageal component is classical achalasia—failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax. The established surgical therapy for achalasia is a Heller myotomy (often performed laparoscopically), which divides the LES muscle fibers to restore esophageal emptying and relieve dysphagia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enzyme replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Fabry disease is caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme α-galactosidase A. The standard pharmacologic treatment is enzyme replacement therapy, in which recombinant α-galactosidase A (e.g., agalsidase β or agalsidase α) is infused to restore the missing enzymatic activity and clear accumulated globotriaosylceramide, thereby slowing or preventing organ damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "In XX gonadal dysgenesis the ovaries are replaced by non-functional ‘streak’ gonads, so estrogen and inhibin feedback to the hypothalamus–pituitary is lost. The pituitary responds by markedly increasing secretion of the gonadotropins, with follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) showing the most pronounced and consistent rise. Thus serum FSH levels are characteristically elevated in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Around 2 %",
    "long_answer": "Population studies and meta-analyses of thousands of incidentally detected adrenal masses show that only about 1.9-2 % prove to be malignant (most often adrenocortical carcinoma or metastatic deposits). The remaining >95 % are benign lesions such as non-functioning cortical adenomas, myelolipomas, or cysts, which is why the consensus is that malignancy occurs in roughly two percent of adrenal incidentalomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroretinography (ERG)",
    "long_answer": "Electroretinography records the electrical responses of retinal photoreceptors. In cone dystrophy the photopic (cone-mediated) ERG amplitudes diminish before ophthalmoscopic or structural changes are obvious, making ERG a sensitive test for detecting early retinal involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Holt-Oram syndrome (‘heart-hand’ syndrome) is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the TBX5 gene. It is characterized by skeletal abnormalities of the upper limbs—classically brachydactyly, triphalangeal or absent thumbs—and a markedly increased incidence of congenital heart defects such as atrial or ventricular septal defects and conduction disturbances. Hence, this syndrome links brachydactyly with a high risk of congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoblasts",
    "long_answer": "In myositis ossificans, fibroblasts in the injured muscle undergo metaplasia into osteoblasts. These osteoblasts synthesize osteoid and mineralize it, creating mature bone within the soft tissue, so they are the cells directly responsible for the heterotopic bone formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT5A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the WNT5A gene cause autosomal-dominant Robinow syndrome. WNT5A encodes a Wnt family glycoprotein that is synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum, processed in the Golgi, and secreted into the extracellular space, where it functions as a morphogen in non-canonical Wnt signaling. Its biology is therefore directly tied to cellular secretion processes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholinesterase inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "For patients with early- to mid-stage Alzheimer’s disease, clinical guidelines recommend cholinesterase inhibitors (e.g., donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine). By inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, these drugs raise synaptic acetylcholine levels in remaining cholinergic neurons, which can modestly improve or stabilize memory, cognition, and daily functioning, thereby alleviating neurological symptoms in this stage of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medium-chain triglyceride",
    "long_answer": "In long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, oxidation of long-chain fatty acids is blocked. Dietary supplementation with medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) provides fatty acids that enter mitochondria independently of carnitine and are oxidised by enzymes that remain functional, supplying ketones and energy while bypassing the defective long-chain pathway. Therefore, MCT oil is the standard triglyceride preparation used in treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Impaired carnitine shuttle",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency depletes intracellular carnitine, so long-chain acyl-CoA molecules cannot be transported into mitochondria via the carnitine shuttle. Without mitochondrial entry, fatty-acid β-oxidation cannot occur, acetyl-CoA is not generated, and the liver has no substrate for ketogenesis; therefore, patients cannot produce ketone bodies during metabolic stress."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic hepatitis B",
    "long_answer": "Papular acrodermatitis (Gianotti-Crosti syndrome) is classically linked to persistent infection with hepatitis B virus. The acral papular eruption represents an immune response to chronic HBV infection, making chronic hepatitis B the well-established systemic condition associated with acrodermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carboplatin",
    "long_answer": "Carboplatin is a second-generation platinum compound specifically developed as a less nephro-, oto- and neuro-toxic analogue of cisplatin. In hematologic oncology it is incorporated into regimens such as ICE (ifosfamide, carboplatin, etoposide), which are widely used for relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphomas, providing cisplatin-like antitumor efficacy with a more favorable toxicity profile."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-aquaporin-4 antibody",
    "long_answer": "Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder is characterized in the great majority of cases by serum IgG autoantibodies that target the aquaporin-4 water channel on astrocytic foot processes. These anti-aquaporin-4 (AQP4-IgG) antibodies are detected in roughly 70–80 % of NMOSD patients, are highly specific for the disease, and their binding initiates complement-mediated astrocyte injury that drives the characteristic optic nerve and spinal cord demyelination. Therefore, the anti-aquaporin-4 antibody is the autoantibody most commonly associated with NMOSD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacteria",
    "long_answer": "Peritonitis is most commonly an infectious inflammation of the peritoneum produced by bacterial invasion—often enteric Gram-negative or Gram-positive bacteria that reach the peritoneal cavity via perforation, translocation, or catheter contamination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HPSE2",
    "long_answer": "Missense, nonsense, or truncating mutations in the HPSE2 gene, mapped to chromosome 10q23-q24, cause urofacial (Ochoa) syndrome. This autosomal-recessive disorder is characterized by a congenital neurogenic bladder with detrusor–sphincter dyssynergia, leading to urinary retention, reflux, and renal damage. Thus, HPSE2 mutation on 10q23q24 is the established genetic cause of this neurogenic bladder condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Feeding obturator",
    "long_answer": "A feeding obturator is a small palatal prosthesis that temporarily seals the cleft in infants with cleft palate. By separating the oral and nasal cavities, it enables the infant to generate negative intra-oral pressure for effective sucking, reduces milk regurgitation through the nose, and facilitates adequate nutrition until surgical repair is performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leptin",
    "long_answer": "In congenital or acquired lipodystrophy, the loss of adipose tissue drastically lowers circulating leptin, an adipocyte-derived hormone that normally regulates appetite and lipid handling. Leptin deficiency promotes hyperphagia, ectopic fat accumulation and impaired insulin signalling, thereby driving the severe insulin resistance characteristic of lipodystrophy; metreleptin replacement therapy improves glucose tolerance, confirming leptin’s causal role."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenic suspensory ligaments",
    "long_answer": "A wandering spleen occurs when the suspensory (peritoneal) ligaments that normally anchor the spleen—chiefly the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments—become congenitally absent or pathologically weakened. Loss of this ligamentous support allows the spleen to migrate freely within the abdomen or pelvis, producing the clinical entity known as a wandering spleen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Germline mosaicism",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta is usually autosomal-dominant, caused by pathogenic variants in COL1A1 or COL1A2. If a mutation occurs post-zygotically in the precursor germ cells of one parent, that parent’s somatic tissues remain normal, but a subset of sperm or oocytes carries the defect—this is called germline (gonadal) mosaicism. A child who receives one of these mutant gametes develops osteogenesis imperfecta even though neither parent shows the disease, explaining sporadic cases in otherwise unaffected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia (MH) episodes are precipitated almost exclusively by volatile inhalational anesthetics (e.g., sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane, halothane, enflurane) and the depolarizing neuromuscular blocker succinylcholine in genetically susceptible individuals. Extensive clinical experience, laboratory testing of susceptible muscle, and position statements from the Malignant Hyperthermia Association of the United States (MHAUS) and other anesthesia‐safety organizations classify amide local anesthetics—including mepivacaine, lidocaine, bupivacaine, and ropivacaine—as ‘non-triggering’ agents. These drugs do not activate the abnormal ryanodine receptor 1 (RYR1)–mediated calcium release that underlies an MH crisis. They are therefore considered safe for use in patients known or suspected to be MH-susceptible. Although local anesthetics can cause their own systemic toxicities (e.g., CNS or cardiac toxicity at high plasma levels), they have not been shown to provoke malignant hyperthermia.\n\nConsequently, the statement that mepivacaine can trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible individuals is not supported by current scientific consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine A",
    "long_answer": "Pure red cell aplasia is often autoimmune in origin; suppressing aberrant T-cell activity can restore erythropoiesis. The calcineurin inhibitor Cyclosporine A is the standard immunosuppressant for this purpose and induces hematologic remission in a high proportion of PRCA patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral myelin protein 22 (PMP22)",
    "long_answer": "Duplications or point mutations in the PMP22 gene, which encodes peripheral myelin protein 22, are the classical molecular cause of Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease type 1A. The same PMP22 alterations are also found in patients diagnosed with Roussy–Lévy syndrome, making PMP22 the shared protein whose gene mutations underlie both neuropathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "In cold agglutinin disease, IgM antibodies fix complement on red blood cells, coating them primarily with C3 fragments. These C3-opsonized erythrocytes are recognized and removed by Kupffer cells that possess complement receptors in the hepatic sinusoids, making the liver the principal site of clearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PITX2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PITX2 homeobox transcription factor disrupt embryonic development of both the anterior segment of the eye and the developing dentition. Patients with PITX2 mutations (e.g., in Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome) therefore present with ocular hypoplasia/anterior segment dysgenesis and dental hypoplasia or hypodontia, reflecting the gene’s pivotal role in ocular and dental tissue differentiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clobetasol propionate",
    "long_answer": "Clinical protocols for vitiligo commonly pair narrow-band UVB phototherapy with the super-potent topical corticosteroid clobetasol propionate. Applied to depigmented skin between light-exposure sessions, clobetasol’s strong anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects limit autoimmune melanocyte damage, while NB-UVB stimulates melanocyte proliferation and migration. Numerous trials have shown that this combination yields higher and faster repigmentation rates than either modality used alone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conductive hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Anotia—the congenital absence of the external ear and often the external auditory canal—prevents sound waves from reaching the middle ear. This mechanical blockage produces a conductive, not sensorineural, deficit, so the hearing impairment associated with anotia is classified as conductive hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLNB",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FLNB gene (filamin B) cause the classic form of Larsen syndrome. Filamin B is a cytoskeletal actin-binding protein that regulates signaling pathways crucial for vertebral segmentation and overall skeleton morphogenesis during embryonic development, explaining its link to the skeletal abnormalities seen in Larsen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aicardi-Goutières syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Aicardi-Goutières syndrome (AGS) is a very rare autosomal recessive/dominant interferonopathy of infancy caused by mutations in genes such as TREX1, RNASEH2A-C, SAMHD1, or ADAR1. Affected babies develop recurrent, chilblain-like acral lesions together with severe neurologic disturbances (encephalopathy, calcifications, developmental delay) and episodes of unexplained, sterile fevers. These clinical features make AGS the classic inherited condition linking chilblain lesions with profound neurologic and febrile manifestations in early life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hand-foot-and-mouth disease",
    "long_answer": "Onychomadesis—proximal detachment and shedding of the nail plate—has been repeatedly documented 4-8 weeks after infection with hand-foot-and-mouth disease, most commonly caused by Coxsackie A6/A10 enteroviruses. The viral insult temporarily halts matrix activity at the proximal nail fold, leading to periodic idiopathic nail shedding that starts at the proximal end."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Fournier gangrene is a necrotizing infection of the scrotum, penis, and perineum that demands rapid surgical debridement and genitourinary reconstruction. Because it primarily involves the external genitalia and perineal urogenital structures, the specialty most commonly responsible for its operative management and ongoing care is urology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Dyschromatosis universalis hereditaria is a rare genodermatosis characterized by generalized mottled hyper- and hypopigmented macules of the skin. Because it is primarily a pigmentary skin disorder, diagnosis, evaluation and ongoing care fall under the expertise of a dermatologist, the specialist trained to manage diseases of skin, hair and nails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the BCOR gene (BCL-6 corepressor) located on Xp11.4 have been demonstrated to cause two distinct X-linked conditions: Lenz microphthalmia syndrome (MCOPS1) and oculofaciocardiodental (OFCD) syndrome. Multiple independent studies have shown loss-of-function mutations or frameshifts in BCOR segregating with the clinical features of both disorders, establishing BCOR as the shared genetic etiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antihistamines",
    "long_answer": "Both physical urticaria and IgE-mediated food allergies involve mast-cell degranulation with release of histamine; H1-receptor antihistamines are therefore first-line symptomatic therapy in both conditions to reduce wheals, pruritus, and other histamine-driven symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Brain magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is performed in suspected Landau-Kleffner syndrome to exclude other structural pathologies—such as demyelinating lesions or intracranial tumours—because MRI provides high-resolution images that sensitively detect white-matter changes and mass lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "The liver is the primary site where apolipoproteins are produced and lipids are assembled into very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL), LDL, and HDL. These hepatic lipoproteins enter the circulation and determine plasma cholesterol and triglyceride levels; overproduction or impaired clearance of these liver-derived particles underlies most forms of hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucinous tubular and spindle cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "The renal cell carcinoma subtype that shows a marked female predominance and characteristically contains abundant mucinous or myxoid stroma is mucinous tubular and spindle cell carcinoma (MTSCC). Histologically, it displays tubular structures merging with spindle-cell areas embedded in mucin or myxoid matrix, and epidemiologic studies consistently report a higher incidence in women."
}

{
  "short_answer": "Vitamin A",
  "long_answer": "Deficiency of vitamin A impairs the maintenance of conjunctival and corneal epithelia, producing xerophthalmia, and disrupts the rhodopsin cycle in photoreceptor cells, leading to retinal dysfunction and damage. Thus inadequate vitamin A underlies both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "In vitro fertilization",
    "long_answer": "Children conceived by assisted reproductive technologies—especially in vitro fertilization (IVF) and related ICSI procedures—show a higher incidence of Silver–Russell syndrome. ART can disturb normal genomic imprinting during early embryo culture, leading to the hypomethylation at 11p15 or maternal uniparental disomy of chromosome 7 that underlies this growth-restriction disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FANCM",
    "long_answer": "FANCM encodes a DNA translocase that participates in the Fanconi anemia (FA) pathway for inter-strand cross-link repair; biallelic mutations cause the FA complementation group M. In multiple organisms, including Arabidopsis, yeast and mammals, FANCM suppresses excess meiotic crossovers by unwinding recombination intermediates, thereby functioning as a meiotic anti-crossover factor in addition to its canonical DNA-repair role."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa",
    "long_answer": "Walt Bodine’s blindness was the result of retinitis pigmentosa, a hereditary retinal dystrophy. In this genetic disorder, pathogenic mutations cause the photoreceptor cells (primarily rods, later cones) in the retina to progressively degenerate, leading first to night-blindness and peripheral vision loss and eventually to complete blindness, as occurred in Bodine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Jeune syndrome (asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy) causes a markedly narrow, rigid thoracic cage that leads to restrictive lung disease and life-threatening respiratory insufficiency in infancy and childhood. Because evaluation and management focus on ventilatory support, monitoring of lung function, and prevention/treatment of respiratory failure, the children are primarily cared for by specialists in pediatric pulmonology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GCH1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Segawa disease, the classic dopa-responsive dystonia, is caused in most patients by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the GCH1 gene, which encodes GTP-cyclohydrolase I—the rate-limiting enzyme for tetrahydrobiopterin and subsequently dopamine synthesis. GCH1 variants account for the great majority of familial and sporadic Segawa disease cases, making it the most common gene mutation associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eosinophils",
    "long_answer": "Helminthic parasites such as the filarial worms that cause lymphatic filariasis elicit a classic type-2 immune response characterized by interleukin-5 production and elevated IgE. The principal white blood cells recruited and activated in this response are eosinophils, whose granule proteins (e.g., major basic protein) are toxic to the surface of the worms and contribute to their clearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iris",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome ‘Aniridia-ataxia-renal agenesis-psychomotor retardation’ is characterized by aniridia, meaning the congenital absence or near-absence of the iris, the colored diaphragmatic structure that regulates light entry into the eye. Mutations disrupting PAX6 and related developmental pathways prevent normal iris formation, so affected individuals lack this ocular structure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLI3 and HOXD13 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Polysyndactyly most often results from pathogenic variants in the developmental patterning genes GLI3 (classically causing Greig cephalopolysyndactyly) and HOXD13 (causing synpolydactyly). Sequencing of these genes, or multigene panels that include them, readily detects the causative mutations in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dihydropteridine reductase",
    "long_answer": "In some hyperphenylalaninemia cases the defect is not in phenylalanine hydroxylase itself but in the recycling enzyme dihydropteridine reductase (DHPR). Loss of DHPR prevents regeneration of the co-factor tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4). BH4 is an essential co-factor for tyrosine hydroxylase, the enzyme that converts tyrosine to L-DOPA. Hence DHPR deficiency lowers available BH4 and directly impairs L-DOPA synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology (ENT)",
    "long_answer": "Erythroplakia is a premalignant red patch most often arising on the oral and oropharyngeal mucosa. Evaluation and management of such head-and-neck mucosal lesions—including biopsy, histopathologic assessment, and potential surgical excision—are routinely performed by otolaryngologists (ear, nose, and throat specialists). Consequently, the medical specialty of Otolaryngology is the one most commonly responsible for diagnosing and treating erythroplakia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blastocystis hominis",
    "long_answer": "Blastocystis hominis (now referred to as Blastocystis spp.) is a common intestinal protozoan frequently detected in stool samples from patients with irritable bowel syndrome. While some carriers remain asymptomatic, others experience diarrhea, abdominal pain, and bloating, leading to ongoing debate over whether Blastocystis is merely a commensal organism or a true gastrointestinal pathogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural crest cells",
    "long_answer": "The bones and connective tissues of the facial skeleton arise predominantly from cranial neural crest cells that migrate into the first and second pharyngeal arches during embryogenesis. Parry–Romberg syndrome involves progressive atrophy of tissues derived from these neural crest-origin cells, so the craniofacial abnormalities seen in the disorder reflect defects in neural crest–derived skeletal structures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Esophoria is a form of ocular misalignment (a type of heterophoria) involving an inward deviation tendency of the eyes. The diagnosis and management of ocular motility disorders, including esophoria, fall under the medical specialty of ophthalmology, where eye-movement testing, prism correction, vision therapy, or surgical intervention are provided by ophthalmologists (often in conjunction with orthoptists)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior cerebral artery stroke",
    "long_answer": "Anton syndrome—characterised by cortical blindness with denial of visual loss—most commonly arises after bilateral infarction of the occipital lobes. The occipital lobes are supplied by the posterior cerebral arteries; an ischemic stroke (occlusion) in these arteries restricts blood flow, produces bilateral occipital infarction, and can precipitate Anton syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leber hereditary optic neuropathy",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants of the mitochondrial complex-I genes MT-ND1, MT-ND4 and MT-ND6 are classically responsible for Leber hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON), a maternally inherited disorder that causes rapid, painless loss of central vision due to degeneration of retinal ganglion cells and the optic nerve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid gland",
    "long_answer": "Endocrine disorders of the thyroid gland—particularly hypothyroidism—are well-documented causes of delayed ejaculation. Altered thyroid hormone levels can impair sympathetic nervous system activity and dopamine-mediated pathways that govern the ejaculatory reflex, leading to a prolonged latency or inability to ejaculate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal interstitium",
    "long_answer": "IgG4-related disease characteristically produces IgG4-rich plasma-cell inflammation and fibrosis in the kidney’s tubulointerstitium, leading to IgG4-related tubulointerstitial nephritis; therefore, the renal interstitium is the part of the kidney that is affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "sacral agenesis",
    "long_answer": "Kurt Fearnley was born with sacral agenesis, a form of caudal regression syndrome in which the caudal (lower) segments of the axial skeleton fail to develop. This congenital malformation can result in partial or complete absence (agenesis) of the lumbar vertebrae, sacrum, and coccyx, leading to characteristic lower-limb and pelvic abnormalities that require wheelchair use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "In mucopolysaccharidoses, undegraded glycosaminoglycans accumulate within lysosomes of reticulo-endothelial cells, particularly in the liver, leading to marked hepatomegaly; thus the liver is characteristically enlarged in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "African trypanosomiasis",
    "long_answer": "African trypanosomiasis, commonly called sleeping sickness, is a parasitic disease produced by the protozoan Trypanosoma brucei and spread to humans through the bite of infected tsetse flies (Glossina spp.)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoidosis",
    "long_answer": "Sarcoidosis, a systemic granulomatous disease, typically produces peripheral‐blood lymphocytopenia because T-lymphocytes migrate from the circulation into affected tissues. At the same time, the chronic granulomatous inflammation stimulates the monocyte–macrophage system, leading to a reactive monocytosis. Thus sarcoidosis characteristically shows both low lymphocyte counts and elevated monocyte counts in the peripheral blood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Signs",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, objective findings that a clinician can detect during a physical examination—such as hirsutism, acne, or enlarged ovaries in polycystic ovary syndrome—are called clinical signs, distinguishing them from the patient-reported subjective symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Factor XIIa",
    "long_answer": "Activated Hageman factor (factor XIIa) converts plasma prekallikrein to kallikrein by proteolytically cleaving the same Arg–Ile bond in prekallikrein that is homologous to the activation site within factor XII itself. This specific cleavage yields the two-chain serine protease kallikrein, initiating the contact/kinin cascade."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L1CAM",
    "long_answer": "MASA syndrome (mental retardation, aphasia, shuffling gait and adducted thumbs) is an X-linked recessive neurodevelopmental disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the L1CAM gene located on Xq28. L1CAM encodes the L1 cell-adhesion molecule, critical for neuronal migration and axon guidance; loss-of-function mutations in this gene produce the characteristic MASA clinical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Warburg Micro syndrome is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in genes such as RAB3GAP1, RAB3GAP2, RAB18 or TBC1D20. Because two pathogenic alleles (one from each parent) are required for the phenotype, the condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium channel blockers",
    "long_answer": "Calcium channel blockers, especially the dihydropyridine subtype (e.g., amlodipine, nifedipine), are recommended for hypertension in the elderly because they dilate large conduit arteries and improve arterial compliance, making them particularly effective at counteracting the large-vessel stiffness that characterizes isolated systolic hypertension in this age group."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology",
    "long_answer": "Silent sinus syndrome is treated surgically by an otolaryngologist (ear, nose and throat surgeon) who performs functional endoscopic sinus surgery to re-establish maxillary sinus aeration and correct orbital floor changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wrist splinting",
    "long_answer": "Both repetitive strain injury (RSI) and carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS) are commonly managed by immobilising the wrist in a neutral position with a wrist splint. Splinting reduces repetitive motion and mechanical compression, lessening tendon inflammation in RSI and decreasing median-nerve pressure in CTS, thereby alleviating pain and facilitating tissue recovery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edema",
    "long_answer": "Edema—abnormal accumulation of fluid causing tissue swelling—is a frequent clinical sign in several inborn errors of metabolism (e.g., congenital disorders of glycosylation, lysosomal-storage or hepatic metabolic defects) because these disorders can lead to hypoalbuminemia or impaired lymphatic/venous drainage. The very same swelling is also characteristic of congestive heart failure, where increased venous pressure drives fluid into the interstitium, and of kidney disease, where salt-water retention or protein loss produces similar fluid overload. Thus edema is a shared, well-recognized symptom across these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valacyclovir",
    "long_answer": "Valacyclovir, the oral pro-drug of acyclovir used systemically for ocular herpes simplex infections (including keratitis), carries a boxed warning that high-dose therapy in severely immunocompromised patients (e.g., AIDS or bone-marrow transplant recipients) has precipitated thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura/hemolytic uremic syndrome due to widespread endothelial injury and platelet consumption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myositis ossificans",
    "long_answer": "Prematurely resuming vigorous activity after a muscle contusion or other soft-tissue injury can perpetuate bleeding and inflammation within the muscle, stimulating heterotopic bone formation inside the injured tissue. This pathological ossification, known as myositis ossificans, is a well-recognized complication of returning to activity too soon after an injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Singleton-Merten syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Singleton-Merten syndrome is a type-I interferonopathy caused by gain-of-function mutations in the pattern-recognition receptor genes IFIH1 (MDA5) and DDX58 (RIG-I). The chronic, inappropriate activation of interferon signalling produced by these variants leads to systemic inflammation, so affected individuals may present with recurrent, unexplained high fevers—i.e., fever of unknown origin—alongside the classic dental, skeletal, and vascular calcification features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alipogene tiparvovec",
    "long_answer": "Alipogene tiparvovec, marketed as Glybera, was the first gene therapy authorized by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) in 2012. It uses an adeno-associated virus (AAV1) vector to deliver a functional copy of the human LPL gene to muscle cells, restoring lipoprotein lipase activity in patients with hereditary lipoprotein lipase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kindlin-1",
    "long_answer": "KIND1 encodes the cytoskeletal adaptor protein kindlin-1. Pathogenic mutations in KIND1 (FERMT1) alter kindlin-1’s FERM domain, disrupting its normal binding to β-actin filaments. Thus, the interaction between kindlin-1 and actin is the one specifically impaired by KIND1 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Optic atrophy affects the optic nerve and visual pathways, so its evaluation and management fall under ophthalmology (often the subspecialty of neuro-ophthalmology). Ophthalmologists perform the necessary eye examinations, imaging, and vision-preserving interventions for patients with Hagemoser–Weinstein–Bresnick syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophages",
    "long_answer": "In polymyalgia rheumatica, biopsy and imaging studies of the shoulder and hip girdle show marked infiltration of the synovium and peri-articular bursae by activated macrophages. These macrophages produce pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-α, driving the characteristic pain, stiffness, and elevated acute-phase reactants seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic diabetes",
    "long_answer": "When the underlying etiology of a patient’s diabetes cannot be identified—no autoimmune markers, no clear insulin-resistance mechanism, and no secondary cause—the condition is classified as idiopathic diabetes (also called idiopathic type 1B diabetes mellitus). The term “idiopathic” is used in medicine to denote diseases of unknown cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azithromycin",
    "long_answer": "Current pertussis treatment guidelines advise that pregnant women who develop whooping cough within six weeks of symptom onset should receive the macrolide antibiotic azithromycin. Azithromycin is highly active against Bordetella pertussis, is taken for only 3–5 days, is better tolerated than erythromycin, and has an established safety record in pregnancy, making it the recommended first-line therapy in this setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "SHORT syndrome commonly includes ocular findings such as deep-set eyes (ocular depression) and anterior-segment anomalies; these eye manifestations are evaluated and managed by specialists in ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CCBE1",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous or compound-heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the CCBE1 gene (collagen and calcium-binding EGF domain-containing protein-1) have been identified in patients with Aagenaes syndrome, also called cholestasis-lymphedema syndrome. Defective CCBE1 disrupts normal lymphatic vessel development, producing neonatal intrahepatic cholestasis that can progressively damage the liver and culminate in fibrosis and cirrhosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AFib amyloidosis",
    "long_answer": "Congenital (hereditary) mutations in the fibrinogen α-chain gene (FGA) lead to the hereditary form of amyloidosis whose fibrils are composed of fibrinogen Aα fragments, designated AFib amyloidosis. This condition is a type of hereditary systemic amyloidosis, classically causing progressive renal amyloid deposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Prednisone, a systemic corticosteroid, has been formally studied and shown to stimulate erythropoiesis in Diamond–Blackfan (congenital hypoplastic) anemia; approximately 70-80 % of affected patients achieve a rise in hemoglobin when treated, making prednisone the standard first-line pharmacologic therapy for this congenital red-cell aplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diaphragmatic pacing",
    "long_answer": "In congenital central hypoventilation syndrome, implantation of a diaphragmatic (phrenic-nerve) pacemaker can electrically stimulate the diaphragm to generate adequate ventilation during sleep. This provides long-term ventilatory support without positive-pressure ventilation through a tracheotomy, allowing many patients to avoid or be decannulated from a tracheostomy tube."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Three",
    "long_answer": "Faciocardiorenal (Eastman-Bixler) syndrome is exceedingly rare; the literature records only one Northern Irish family—three affected siblings described in the 1980s. No further Northern Irish cases have been published, so the total remains three."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypokalemia",
    "long_answer": "Low serum potassium (hypokalemia) decreases the activity of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase because extracellular K⁺ is required for the pump’s normal function. Reduced pump activity alters the resting membrane potential, promotes intracellular Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ accumulation, and increases myocardial excitability. These electrophysiologic changes predispose the heart to rapid arrhythmias, including sinus and ventricular tachycardia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nerve conduction study",
    "long_answer": "Electrophysiological nerve conduction studies (NCV) measure the conduction velocity and amplitude of large myelinated peripheral nerve fibers; abnormalities in these parameters indicate large-fiber involvement and are therefore used to diagnose and quantify large fiber neuropathy in diabetic patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acetazolamide",
    "long_answer": "The first-line treatment for idiopathic intracranial hypertension is the carbonic anhydrase inhibitor acetazolamide, which lowers cerebrospinal-fluid production at the choroid plexus and thereby reduces intracranial pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA mutation; Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "The commonest familial partial lipodystrophy is Dunnigan-type (FPLD2), produced by heterozygous mutations in the LMNA gene that encodes lamin A/C. Because the disorder manifests with severe metabolic/endocrine derangements—insulin-resistant diabetes, hypertriglyceridaemia, fatty liver—it is principally evaluated and treated by clinicians in the specialty of endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute renal failure",
    "long_answer": "In Hemolytic–uremic syndrome, widespread microvascular thrombosis damages renal glomeruli and tubules, sharply reducing glomerular filtration and urine output. This acute loss of kidney function leads to acute renal failure (acute kidney injury), a life-threatening complication that often necessitates dialysis and can progress to chronic kidney disease if not resolved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysphania glomulifera",
    "long_answer": "Outbreaks of congenital biliary atresia in neonatal livestock were traced to pregnant animals grazing on the Australian herb Dysphania glomulifera (formerly classified in Chenopodium). The plant contains the isoflavonoid toxin biliatresone, which selectively injures developing extra-hepatic bile ducts; experimental exposure to biliatresone reproduces biliary atresia in zebrafish and mammalian models, firmly linking D. glomulifera to the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "35–40 years",
    "long_answer": "Current NCCN and ACOG guidelines advise BRCA1 mutation carriers to undergo risk-reducing salpingo-oophorectomy after completion of child-bearing, ideally between ages 35 and 40, because ovarian/tubal cancer risk begins to rise appreciably in the late 30s for BRCA1 carriers while still being low earlier; surgery at 35–40 years maximally lowers cancer incidence and improves survival."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) does not list or issue guidance on a discrete condition called “hypoactive sexual desire disorder” (HSDD). NICE produces guidelines on a wide range of recognised disorders—including several sexual-health topics such as erectile dysfunction, menopause-associated symptoms, and sexually transmitted infections—but it has never published a guideline or technology appraisal that designates HSDD as a formal disorder or provides specific recommendations for its management. Accordingly, within NICE documentation HSDD is not formally recognised as a distinct diagnostic entity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increases basal metabolic rate",
    "long_answer": "Excess thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) in hyperthyroidism up-regulate mitochondrial oxidative enzymes, stimulate Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity, and enhance catecholamine sensitivity. These actions elevate energy expenditure and heat production, so the basal metabolic rate rises markedly, leading to symptoms such as weight loss despite increased appetite and heat intolerance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bleomycin",
    "long_answer": "Bleomycin is a cytotoxic glycopeptide antibiotic derived from Streptomyces species that is widely used for intralesional sclerotherapy of cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformations). When injected into the lesion, it damages endothelial cells, incites an inflammatory response, and promotes fibrosis, thereby obliterating the cystic spaces and reducing the mass. Because of its proven efficacy and acceptable safety profile at the low doses used, bleomycin is a well-established sclerosing agent for treating cystic hygroma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Among the known causes of Waardenburg syndrome type 2, the most frequently identified are heterozygous mutations in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene. MITF controls melanocyte development; loss-of-function variants impair pigment cell migration and survival, producing the characteristic patchy depigmentation and sensorineural deafness of WS2. While other genes (e.g., SNAI2, SOX10) can also cause WS2, MITF mutations are the most common."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Insulin",
    "long_answer": "Administered insulin lowers serum potassium by stimulating Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase–mediated uptake of K⁺ into cells and is therefore a standard acute treatment for hyperkalemia. Conversely, when insulin is absent or insufficient (e.g., in diabetic ketoacidosis), this cellular uptake is impaired, potassium shifts out of cells, and hyperkalemia develops. Thus the same hormone used therapeutically to reduce potassium can be implicated in hyperkalemia when there is insulin deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pompe disease",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen-storage disease type II) results from mutations in the GAA gene located on chromosome 17q25.2-q25.3. Deficient acid α-glucosidase causes lysosomal glycogen accumulation, producing hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in the infantile form. The enlarged, glycogen-loaded myocardium predisposes affected infants to conduction disturbances and life-threatening arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATG16L1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "A common Crohn’s-disease–associated variant is the T300A mutation in the ATG16L1 gene. ATG16L1 encodes an autophagy protein essential for forming autophagosomes that digest and recycle cellular debris and microbial components. Defects in this gene impair the cell’s autophagy pathway—its main mechanism for handling and disposing of intracellular waste products—which contributes to the intestinal inflammation characteristic of Crohn’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dichlorphenamide",
    "long_answer": "Dichlorphenamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor marketed as KEVEYIS, is the only drug with FDA approval specifically for primary periodic paralysis, covering both the hypokalemic and hyperkalemic forms. Clinical trials showed it reduces the frequency and severity of paralytic attacks in patients with either subtype, leading to its dual-indication approval in 2015."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC6 (CSB)",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome results chiefly from pathogenic variants in the ERCC6 gene, which encodes the CSB protein involved in transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision repair. Loss-of-function ERCC6 mutations (Cockayne syndrome type B) are documented to produce not only the classic neuro-developmental and photosensitivity features but also gastrointestinal manifestations such as severe feeding difficulties, intestinal dysmotility, chronic pseudo-obstruction and Hirschsprung-like disease. Thus an ERCC6 (CSB) mutation is the Cockayne-syndrome–associated genetic defect known to affect the gastrointestinal tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKP2",
    "long_answer": "PKP2 encodes plakophilin-2, a key desmosomal protein. Loss-of-function variants in PKP2 are the most frequent genetic cause of arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (particularly arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy). The same variants impair intercalated-disc integrity and secondarily reduce cardiac sodium current, producing the characteristic ECG pattern and ventricular arrhythmias of Brugada syndrome in some carriers. Hence PKP2 is firmly associated with both Brugada syndrome and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerliponase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Cerliponase alfa (brand name Brineura) is a recombinant human tripeptidyl-peptidase 1 (TPP1) enzyme replacement therapy. In 2017 it became the first drug approved by the U.S. FDA specifically to treat the late-infantile (CLN2) form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis, slowing neurologic decline by supplementing the deficient TPP1 enzyme in cerebrospinal fluid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD4-positive helper T cells",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma is mycosis fungoides. This disease is characterized by a clonal proliferation of skin-homing CD4⁺ helper T lymphocytes, which infiltrate the epidermis and dermis to produce the typical patches, plaques, and tumors seen clinically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plastic surgeon",
    "long_answer": "Elephantiasis produces extreme, disfiguring enlargement of a limb or genitalia because of chronic lymphatic obstruction. Definitive management, after medical antiparasitic therapy, often requires excisional or reconstructive procedures such as the Charles operation or lymphatic-venous anastomosis. These complex soft-tissue and lymphatic reconstructions are typically performed by a plastic (reconstructive) surgeon, the specialist trained to remove the hypertrophic tissue and restore form and function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection is a well-documented infectious trigger for Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN). Although uncommon, HSV can disseminate beyond mucocutaneous sites and infect the lower respiratory tract, causing herpetic pneumonia, especially in immunocompromised or ventilated patients. Thus, HSV is the virus linked to both TEN and the potential development of pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The Peptidase D gene (PEPD) resides at 19q13.11 on chromosome 19. Deletions involving this region create PEPD haploinsufficiency, and such 19q13.11 microdeletions have been repeatedly reported in patients who present with aplasia cutis congenita, linking the condition specifically to chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous epithelium",
    "long_answer": "Squamous-cell carcinomas are malignant tumors that arise from squamous epithelial tissue—the flat, scale-like epithelial cells that line the epidermis of skin and mucosal surfaces throughout the body. Because they derive from these differentiated squamous epithelial cells, they are classified as epithelial (carcinomatous) cancers rather than sarcomas or other tumor types."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lisch nodules",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene) is characterized by multiple café-au-lait macules, cutaneous neurofibromas, and distinctive pigmented hamartomas of the iris called Lisch nodules. These melanocytic nodules are present in the majority of adult NF1 patients and are a classic ophthalmologic finding used diagnostically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Penicillin",
    "long_answer": "Strep throat is caused by group A Streptococcus, which remains universally susceptible to β-lactam antibiotics. Clinical guidelines (e.g., IDSA, AAP, CDC) recommend a 10-day course of oral penicillin V (or the equivalent amoxicillin) as first-line therapy because it eradicates the organism, shortens symptom duration, prevents rheumatic fever, and limits transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paresis",
    "long_answer": "Chiari malformation can compress the brainstem and upper cervical spinal cord, interrupting the motor pathways that control skeletal muscles. This may lead to paresis—partial loss or weakness of voluntary muscle movement—in the arms, legs, or facial muscles, making it a recognized neurological symptom of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testes",
    "long_answer": "Wilson-Turner syndrome is an X-linked condition featuring hypogonadism in affected males. The hypogonadism results from underdevelopment and impaired function of the testes—the male gonads—leading to small testicular size and decreased androgen production. Thus, the testes are the gonads specifically affected in males with Wilson-Turner syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edvard Munch",
    "long_answer": "Edvard Munch, the Norwegian Expressionist master, lost his mother and a sister to tuberculosis and documented hereditary mental instability in his family; his personal confrontation with illness and psychological distress profoundly influenced his art, culminating in the iconic 1893 painting 'The Scream'."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cayler cardiofacial syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Congenital hypoplasia or absence of the depressor anguli oris muscle produces an asymmetric crying facies. When this facial finding occurs together with congenital heart defects—most often conotruncal anomalies—the combined condition is termed Cayler cardiofacial syndrome, a phenotype within the 22q11.2 deletion spectrum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colchicine",
    "long_answer": "Colchicine is frequently prescribed for the mucocutaneous and articular manifestations of Behçet’s disease. By inhibiting microtubule polymerisation, it affects rapidly dividing cells; at higher or prolonged doses this can suppress hematopoiesis, leading to leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, or pancytopenia—i.e., bone-marrow suppression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Roberts syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Roberts syndrome (pseudothalidomide syndrome) is an autosomal-recessive disorder in which mutations in the ESCO2 gene disrupt limb bud development. A hallmark is mesomelic shortening or absence of both the ulna and radius, which secondarily causes severe hypoplasia or absence of the digits (oligodactyly/phocomelia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute hepatitis",
    "long_answer": "Hepatitis episodes that begin suddenly and last less than six months are classified as acute hepatitis, distinguishing them from chronic hepatitis where liver inflammation persists beyond six months."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diazepam",
    "long_answer": "In the management of tetanus, intravenous diazepam is routinely used to suppress the intense generalized muscle spasms produced by tetanospasmin. As a benzodiazepine, diazepam potentiates GABA-mediated inhibitory neurotransmission in the spinal cord and brainstem, decreasing motor neuron excitability and providing both muscle relaxation and sedation, which makes it the drug of choice for spasm control in tetanus patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncologist",
    "long_answer": "Leiomyomas are benign smooth-muscle tumors, but if they undergo malignant transformation they become leiomyosarcomas—a form of soft-tissue cancer. The diagnosis and management of any malignant tumor falls under the domain of oncology; therefore, an oncologist (often a surgical or medical oncologist specializing in soft-tissue sarcomas) would direct treatment, which may include surgery, chemotherapy, and/or radiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis",
    "long_answer": "Multicentric reticulohistiocytosis is grouped within the class of histiocytic proliferative disorders that lack Langerhans-cell markers; consequently, it is classified as a non-Langerhans cell histiocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Budgerigar",
    "long_answer": "Budgerigars (Melopsittacus undulatus) can form 'half-sider' individuals when two early embryos fuse, creating a tetragametic chimera. Because each cell line carries different color-mutation alleles, the resulting bird shows sharply divided or patchy plumage of two distinct colors, making the chimeric condition visibly obvious."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LYST",
    "long_answer": "Both Chédiak–Higashi syndrome and the disorder historically called Tietz syndrome are produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LYST gene (lysosomal trafficking regulator). Pathogenic variants in LYST impair vesicle trafficking and lysosome-related organelle formation, giving rise to the characteristic giant cytoplasmic granules, partial oculocutaneous albinism, and immune deficiency seen in these conditions. Thus, LYST is the gene whose mutation underlies both syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bile acid sequestrants",
    "long_answer": "Bile acid sequestrants (e.g., cholestyramine, colestipol, colesevelam) are anion-exchange resins that bind bile acids in the intestinal lumen. By preventing enterohepatic reabsorption of bile acids, they enhance conversion of hepatic cholesterol into new bile acids, up-regulate LDL receptors, and thereby reduce plasma LDL-cholesterol levels—an established therapy for hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caffeine",
    "long_answer": "Intake of caffeine, a central nervous system stimulant present in coffee, tea and many soft drinks, increases adrenergic activity and neuronal excitability; this heightened physiologic arousal is well documented to increase the amplitude of action tremors. Consequently, patients with essential tremor often experience noticeably worse tremor after consuming caffeine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium citrate",
    "long_answer": "In medullary sponge kidney, patients frequently have hypocitraturia, and citrate is a natural inhibitor of calcium stone formation. Oral potassium citrate supplements raise urinary citrate concentration and alkalinize the urine, allowing citrate to complex with calcium and prevent crystal nucleation and growth. Therefore, potassium citrate is routinely prescribed to prevent new kidney stones in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cortical collecting duct",
    "long_answer": "SCNN1A encodes the α-subunit of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), which mediates aldosterone-stimulated sodium reabsorption in the distal nephron. Loss-of-function mutations in SCNN1A disrupt ENaC activity specifically in the cortical collecting duct (and adjacent connecting tubule), leading to the salt-wasting and hyperkalemia characteristic of Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paracrine signaling",
    "long_answer": "In Gorham-Stout (vanishing bone) disease, the vascular/lymphatic tissue within the lesion releases osteoclast-activating cytokines such as interleukin-6 that act locally on neighbouring osteoclast precursors. This local cell-to-nearby-cell cytokine action is a classic example of paracrine signaling, which drives the excessive osteoclastic bone resorption seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electromyography",
    "long_answer": "Electromyography (EMG) measures the electrical activity generated by skeletal muscles in response to nerve stimulation, allowing clinicians to evaluate nerve conduction and muscle response. Abnormal EMG findings indicate impaired signal transmission, making the test a reliable way to assess the integrity of the peripheral nervous system in patients with peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Balsam of Peru",
    "long_answer": "Allergic contact cheilitis is often triggered by Balsam of Peru, a complex fragrance- and flavor-containing resin added to many foods, toothpastes, lipsticks, and topical products. Patch (allergy) testing routinely includes Balsam of Peru because it is one of the most common allergens producing lip inflammation; a positive patch test pinpoints it as the cause of the cheilitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polyuria",
    "long_answer": "Both nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and hyperglycemia cause excessive loss of water in the urine, leading to markedly increased urine output (polyuria). In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus the kidneys cannot respond to antidiuretic hormone, so they excrete large volumes of dilute urine, whereas in hyperglycemia the high glucose concentration produces an osmotic diuresis. Despite different mechanisms, the clinical manifestation of abnormally high urine volume—polyuria—is common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP24A1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CYP24A1 gene, which encodes 25-hydroxy-vitamin-D-24-hydroxylase, block the normal catabolism of 25-hydroxy- and 1,25-dihydroxy-vitamin D. With impaired degradation, physiologic or even low oral vitamin D intake leads to accumulation of active vitamin D metabolites, producing hypercalcemia, suppressed PTH and other signs of vitamin D toxicity without excessive supplementation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Headache",
    "long_answer": "Familial osteodysplasia, Anderson type frequently causes thickening and over-growth of cranial bones, which leads to recurrent headaches. Headache is also a well-recognized clinical manifestation of uncontrolled or severe arterial hypertension. Thus, headache is a symptom commonly encountered in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate",
    "long_answer": "Mevalonate kinase deficiency typically presents with systemic inflammation during febrile attacks, which is reflected by an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR). The ESR test gauges how quickly red blood cells settle in a tube; increased settling speed indicates heightened acute-phase reactants and inflammation, a common laboratory hallmark of this autoinflammatory disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate",
    "long_answer": "Erythema nodosum is an inflammatory panniculitis; during active disease acute-phase reactants rise, causing the erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) to increase. ESR is a classic laboratory test used clinically to quantify systemic inflammation and is characteristically elevated in patients with erythema nodosum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Pontocerebellar hypoplasia is characterized by underdevelopment of both the pons and, critically, the cerebellum. The cerebellum is the brain region responsible for integrating sensory and motor information to maintain bodily posture, balance, and smooth, coordinated locomotion. Its hypoplasia therefore explains the severe motor deficits seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Tick paralysis is not caused by an infectious agent but by neurotoxins secreted in the saliva of certain tick species (e.g., Dermacentor, Ixodes, Amblyomma). Because it is a toxin-mediated condition rather than an infection, vaccination is not a practical or established preventive strategy. Current prevention focuses on avoiding tick bites and promptly removing attached ticks, and treatment consists of tick removal and supportive care if paralysis develops. No licensed or experimentally validated human vaccine exists for tick paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Submucosal mucous glands",
    "long_answer": "In chronic bronchitis, hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the bronchial submucosal mucous glands markedly increase their secretory output, leading to the characteristic excess mucus within the airways."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C-reactive protein",
    "long_answer": "C-reactive protein (CRP) is an acute-phase protein produced by hepatocytes under the stimulation of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6. Serum CRP concentrations rise markedly during systemic or local inflammation, and numerous clinical studies report higher CRP levels in patients with IgA nephropathy, reflecting the inflammatory activity associated with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4.5 per 100 000",
    "long_answer": "Global Burden of Disease 2015 estimates attribute about 335 000 deaths worldwide to alcohol-related cirrhosis in 2015; with a world population of roughly 7.4 billion this corresponds to an age-standardised mortality rate of ≈4.5 deaths per 100 000 population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dietrich disease",
    "long_answer": "Avascular (aseptic) necrosis of a metacarpal head is classically referred to as Dietrich disease. In this rare disorder the end-arterial blood supply to the metacarpal head is compromised, leading to ischemia, subchondral collapse and arthritic changes of the metacarpophalangeal joint—hence the eponym Dietrich disease is the commonly used name."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clozapine",
    "long_answer": "Clozapine, an atypical antipsychotic used for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, carries a well-documented risk of potentially fatal agranulocytosis, requiring regular white-blood-cell and absolute neutrophil count monitoring in all patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pfizer",
    "long_answer": "The first medication approved in Europe for treating transthyretin-related familial amyloid polyneuropathy was tafamidis (brand name Vyndaqel). This drug was discovered at FoldRx Pharmaceuticals and completed clinical development after the company was acquired by Pfizer; the European Medicines Agency (EMA) lists Pfizer as the developer and marketing authorisation holder. Thus, Pfizer is the company that developed the approved treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KOH preparation",
    "long_answer": "A potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation involves placing the patient’s skin scraping in 10–20 % KOH on a slide; the alkali digests keratin, clearing the sample so branching septate hyphae of dermatophytes become visible under light microscopy, thereby confirming dermatophytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-neuraminidase",
    "long_answer": "Mucolipidosis type I, also called sialidosis, results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-neuraminidase (alpha-N-acetylneuraminidase, encoded by the NEU1 gene). Without this sialidase activity, sialylated oligosaccharides and glycoproteins accumulate in lysosomes, producing the characteristic storage disease symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBCE",
    "long_answer": "Sanjad–Sakati syndrome (hypoparathyroidism–retardation–dysmorphism syndrome) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TBCE gene, located on chromosome 1q42–q43. TBCE encodes tubulin-specific chaperone E, a protein required for proper microtubule assembly; pathogenic variants (e.g., R198X nonsense mutation) disrupt tubulin folding, leading to the characteristic hypoparathyroidism, growth retardation, and facial dysmorphism of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crizotinib",
    "long_answer": "Crizotinib is a small-molecule tyrosine-kinase inhibitor that targets the anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) enzyme. By binding to ALK’s ATP-binding site, it blocks the kinase activity of ALK fusion proteins that drive ALK-positive anaplastic large-cell lymphoma (ALCL). This inhibition halts downstream proliferative signaling, making crizotinib an approved treatment for relapsed or refractory ALK-positive ALCL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial septal defect",
    "long_answer": "In patients with tetrasomy 18p (isochromosome 18p), congenital heart disease is reported in roughly one-third of cases. Reviews of case series and the OMIM phenotype description consistently identify an atrial septal defect—most often the secundum type—as the single most frequently observed cardiac malformation in this chromosomal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia primarily affects ectoderm-derived structures—sweat glands, skin, hair, and nails—leading to deficient sweating, sparse hair, and other cutaneous manifestations. Because the core clinical problems are dermatologic, the specialty most directly responsible for evaluation and management of these patients is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coxsackie B virus",
    "long_answer": "Infection with Coxsackie B virus can initiate acute viral myocarditis; molecular mimicry between viral proteins and cardiac myosin triggers a sustained autoimmune attack on myocardial tissue. This chronic immune-mediated injury is a well-established pathway that progresses to dilated cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lateral eyebrows",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis pilaris atrophicans faciei (ulerythema ophryogenes) characteristically starts in early childhood with rough, erythematous follicular papules on the lateral portions of the eyebrows. This region is the first and most consistently affected site before the process may extend to adjacent facial areas such as the cheeks or forehead."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Foot drop",
    "long_answer": "The accessory deep peroneal nerve is typically a branch of the common (fibular) peroneal nerve. Injury to the common peroneal nerve abolishes the function of its deep and superficial branches, paralyzing the ankle dorsiflexors (tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus) and evertors. Loss of dorsiflexion causes the characteristic clinical condition known as foot drop."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmopressin",
    "long_answer": "Desmopressin (DDAVP) is a synthetic analogue of antidiuretic hormone (arginine vasopressin). It is the standard pharmacologic therapy for central diabetes insipidus because it binds renal V2 receptors, increasing water re-absorption and concentrating urine. In the diagnostic water deprivation test, desmopressin is administered after dehydration; a marked rise in urine osmolality confirms central (ADH-responsive) diabetes insipidus, distinguishing it from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Thus, the same drug is used both therapeutically and diagnostically in this context."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncology",
    "long_answer": "Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors are high-grade soft-tissue sarcomas; their management—surgery, radiotherapy, and systemic chemotherapy—falls under the care of oncologists (sarcoma specialists within medical and surgical oncology)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ixodes ricinus",
    "long_answer": "The European castor bean tick, Ixodes ricinus, is a proven vector for Anaplasma phagocytophilum—the bacterium that causes Human Granulocytic Anaplasmosis—and it also transmits Tick-borne Encephalitis virus. Its broad host range and wide geographic distribution in Europe make it the principal tick species capable of spreading both pathogens to humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beetlejuice",
    "long_answer": "The entertainer is Beetlejuice (born Lester Green), a well-known performer who has both dwarfism and microcephaly—conditions that result in short stature and a smaller-than-average head circumference due to limited brain growth. His unique appearance and personality led Howard Stern to induct him into the show’s recurring ensemble of eccentric guests known as the Wack Pack."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are routinely recommended for patients with chronic kidney disease, particularly those with albuminuria, because they lower intraglomerular pressure, reduce proteinuria, slow the decline in glomerular filtration rate, and concurrently lower blood pressure, thereby decreasing the risk of major cardiovascular events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ichthyosis",
    "long_answer": "Oliver–McFarlane syndrome is grouped among the syndromic forms of ichthyosis—genetic disorders characterized by abnormal epidermal cornification that produces generalized scaling of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Real-time abdominal ultrasound is routinely used to visualize the characteristic periportal fibrosis and enlarged portal vessels produced by hepatosplenic schistosomiasis, allowing non-invasive detection and grading of schistosomiasis-related liver damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple system atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Multiple system atrophy (MSA, formerly Shy-Drager syndrome) is an α-synuclein-related neurodegenerative disease that degenerates autonomic and brain-stem respiratory centers. Damage to medullary and pontine neurons impairs the automatic drive to breathe, especially during sleep, leading to central (alveolar) hypoventilation syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chiari I malformation",
    "long_answer": "Chiari I malformation is characterized by downward herniation of the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum. It is often asymptomatic but may become clinically apparent after a whiplash or other cervical acceleration–deceleration injury, when the transient rise in intracranial pressure or traction on neural tissue precipitates symptoms such as occipital headache, neck pain, or neurologic deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The majority of Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome cases result from microdeletions or pathogenic variants in the CREBBP gene, which is located on chromosome 16p13.3; therefore, chromosome 16 is the chromosome most commonly associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Canine distemper virus can invade a dog’s central nervous system, where it damages neurons and myelin sheaths. This neurotropism produces neurologic signs such as ataxia, loss of balance, and general incoordination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (ALD) is caused by mutations in the ABCD1 gene that impair peroxisomal β-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFAs). The resulting accumulation of VLCFAs in the central nervous system and adrenal cortex leads to widespread demyelination and adrenal insufficiency, making ALD the canonical genetic disorder linked to VLCFA-induced demyelination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Tranebjaerg–Svejgaard syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants on the X chromosome (the TIMM8A locus). Consequently, only males who inherit the mutant allele from a carrier mother manifest the full syndrome, while female carriers are usually asymptomatic and there is no male-to-male transmission—hallmarks of an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Struma",
    "long_answer": "In classical medical Latin, an enlargement of the thyroid gland that produces a visible swelling of the neck is termed a “struma.” The same root appears in modern pathology (e.g., “struma ovarii,” a thyroid-tissue teratoma), underscoring its specific association with goitre, the neck swelling caused by thyroid hypertrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gamma Knife radiosurgery",
    "long_answer": "After surgical debulking of his anaplastic astrocytoma, Lee Atwater traveled to Sweden’s Karolinska Institute to receive Gamma Knife stereotactic radiosurgery—a form of highly focused radiation therapy that delivers convergent beams of gamma radiation to the tumor while sparing surrounding brain tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAPN3 (calpain-3)",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies show that limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A, which results from pathogenic variants in the CAPN3 gene encoding the muscle-specific protease calpain-3, represents the largest share of LGMD cases reported across Europe and much of Asia. Therefore, CAPN3 mutation is considered the most common genetic cause of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy in the Euro-Asian population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-TK A8344G",
    "long_answer": "The vast majority (~80%) of patients with MERRF (Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers) harbor the m.8344A>G point mutation in the mitochondrial tRNA-Lysine gene (MT-TK). This mutation impairs mitochondrial protein synthesis, leading to defective oxidative phosphorylation and the characteristic neuromuscular manifestations of MERRF."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric oncology",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma is an aggressive soft-tissue sarcoma that occurs predominantly in children and adolescents. Management typically involves multimodal cancer therapy (chemotherapy, surgery, radiotherapy) coordinated by physicians trained in treating childhood malignancies—pediatric oncologists—who lead the therapeutic team and protocol-based care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "Trichothiodystrophy (TTD) is most often caused by mutations in the ERCC2 gene, also known as XPD. ERCC2 encodes one of the two helicase subunits of the multisubunit transcription-repair factor TFIIH. TFIIH is essential both for nucleotide-excision DNA repair and for transcription initiation by RNA polymerase II, placing ERCC2/XPD firmly within the TFIIH complex and explaining its central role in TTD pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glyoxylate",
    "long_answer": "In the human gut, microbial uricase and downstream enzymes oxidise uric acid to allantoin, allantoate and finally split ureidoglycolate to release glyoxylate. Thus glyoxylate is a direct product and key intermediate of intestinal uric-acid metabolism. Independently, glyoxylate (glyoxylic acid) is one of nature’s universal C2 building blocks: it enters the glyoxylate cycle, photorespiration and other central pathways to supply carbon for the biosynthesis of amino acids, carbohydrates, lipids and many other metabolites. Therefore, glyoxylate satisfies both parts of the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basilar invagination",
    "long_answer": "Basilar invagination is a cranio-vertebral junction abnormality in which the odontoid process and body of the C2 vertebra migrate upward toward the skull base, protruding through the foramen magnum. This vertical displacement narrows the foramen magnum and can compress the medulla and upper cervical spinal cord, producing neurologic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Annie Turnbo Malone",
    "long_answer": "Annie Turnbo Malone, an African-American chemist and entrepreneur, formulated and marketed the product she named “Wonderful Hair Grower” in the early 1900s. By combining sulfur, petrolatum and herbal extracts to combat scalp disease and stimulate hair growth, Malone’s creation laid the foundation for modern Black hair-care lines and the Poro system of beauty schools, thereby revolutionizing hair-care practices for African Americans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP1A3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATP1A3 gene, which encodes the α3-subunit of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, are a well-established cause of alternating hemiplegia of childhood (AHC). The same ATP1A3 mutations are also responsible for rapid-onset dystonia-parkinsonism and other dystonic phenotypes, linking this single gene to both dystonia and AHC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alphavirus",
    "long_answer": "The Togaviridae family currently contains a single recognized genus, Alphavirus. Alphaviruses include human pathogens such as chikungunya, Ross River, and Mayaro viruses that produce febrile illness with infectious arthritis, as well as Eastern, Western and Venezuelan equine encephalitis viruses that can cause severe encephalitis in humans. Because Alphavirus is the only genus remaining in Togaviridae and is responsible for both arthritogenic and encephalitic diseases, it is the correct answer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 17 (KRT17)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the keratin 17 gene (KRT17) are a well-established cause of Pachyonychia congenita type 2 and are also the principal genetic defect identified in familial and sporadic cases of Steatocystoma multiplex. KRT17 encodes the intermediate-filament protein keratin 17 expressed in nail beds, hair follicles, and sebaceous ducts; pathogenic variants disrupt filament assembly, producing the nail dystrophy and follicular/sebaceous cyst formation characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Familial ectrodactyly with tibia aplasia/hypoplasia corresponds to split-hand/foot malformation with long-bone deficiency type 1 (SHFLD1). Linkage and cytogenetic studies have localized this disorder to the long arm of chromosome 1, specifically the 1q42.2–q43 region, showing that pathogenic rearrangements of chromosome 1 underlie the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Stickler syndrome commonly causes severe myopia, cataracts, and a high risk of retinal detachment. Regular follow-up with an ophthalmologist is essential to monitor vision, detect early retinal tears, and manage other ocular complications that can threaten sight."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAG1",
    "long_answer": "Alagille syndrome is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the JAG1 gene, which encodes the Jagged-1 ligand of the Notch signaling pathway. JAG1 is located on chromosome 20p12, and loss-of-function mutations in this gene account for the vast majority of Alagille syndrome cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "Perimyositis belongs to the group of inflammatory myopathies—autoimmune disorders that inflame muscle fibres and their connective tissue. These systemic connective-tissue diseases are classically evaluated and managed by rheumatologists, who are trained in internal medicine and specialize in diagnosing and treating autoimmune and musculoskeletal inflammation. Consequently, a rheumatologist is the specialist most qualified to diagnose and treat perimyositis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Concussion",
    "long_answer": "A concussion is a mild traumatic brain injury produced by rapid acceleration-deceleration forces, such as those that occur in motor-vehicle crashes. It typically causes transient neurologic dysfunction, including confusion and both anterograde and retrograde amnesia, without overt structural brain damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALK gene",
    "long_answer": "The anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) gene, situated at chromosomal band 2p23 on chromosome 2, is characteristically rearranged or translocated in anaplastic large-cell lymphoma (ALCL), producing ALK fusion proteins that drive the malignant phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor—often fatal in early childhood",
    "long_answer": "SGFLD syndrome is a rare, multisystem congenital disorder that typically leads to progressive respiratory and multi-organ failure; most affected children die in infancy or early childhood despite supportive care, so the overall prognosis is very poor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diarrhea",
    "long_answer": "High-dose oral magnesium salts have an osmotic laxative effect in the gut; consequently, patients with Gitelman syndrome who take magnesium supplementation commonly experience diarrhea as a dose-limiting gastrointestinal side effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bis(chloromethyl) ether",
    "long_answer": "Bis(chloromethyl) ether (BCME) is a chloromethyl ether used industrially in ion-exchange resin production and other chemical processes. Occupational inhalation exposure to BCME is a well-documented cause of markedly increased lung cancer risk; it is classified as a Group 1 (human) carcinogen by the International Agency for Research on Cancer due to strong epidemiological and mechanistic evidence of its pulmonary carcinogenicity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthognathic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Möbius syndrome frequently develop jaw and occlusal abnormalities (e.g., mandibular/maxillary hypoplasia and malocclusion). Correcting these dental and skeletal problems often requires orthognathic surgery—repositioning of the maxilla and/or mandible—to achieve proper bite alignment and improve oral function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ODZ4 (TENM4)",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies of bipolar disorder have repeatedly identified the ODZ4 gene (also known as TENM4) as a significant risk locus. ODZ4 encodes a large transmembrane protein that contains multiple epidermal growth factor–like repeats characteristic of the Notch receptor family and has been shown to modulate canonical Notch signaling during neurodevelopment. Thus, ODZ4 is the bipolar-disorder-associated gene that functions within the Notch signaling pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NKX2-1",
    "long_answer": "Benign hereditary chorea is classically an autosomal-dominant movement disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the NKX2-1 gene (also called TITF-1 or TTF-1). Demonstration of an NKX2-1 mutation on molecular genetic testing is the standard confirmatory test for diagnosing benign hereditary chorea, which is often part of the broader ‘brain–lung–thyroid’ NKX2-1-related disorder spectrum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Major depressive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic and clinical studies show that mood disturbances, particularly major depressive disorder, are highly prevalent among people who use cocaine. Up to one-third of individuals with cocaine use disorder meet full DSM criteria for major depression, and the dysphoria that follows cocaine intoxication and withdrawal is driven by depletion of brain dopamine and serotonin. This makes major depressive disorder the most commonly documented mental disorder associated with cocaine use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acromesomelic dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous loss-of-function mutations in the GDF5 gene cause acromesomelic dysplasia, an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia characterized by severe shortening of the middle and distal segments of the limbs. Because the disorder is recessive, it is observed more frequently in the offspring of consanguineous parents, who have an increased chance of both carrying the same pathogenic GDF5 allele."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "In the World Health Organization (WHO) Classification of Tumours of Haematopoietic and Lymphoid Tissues, plasmacytomas (solitary plasmacytoma of bone and extramedullary plasmacytoma) are placed in the chapter on plasma-cell neoplasms, together with multiple myeloma and related disorders. The WHO lists Epstein–Barr virus (EBV)-associated lymphoproliferative diseases separately and includes entities such as EBV-positive mucocutaneous ulcer, EBV-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, EBV-positive polymorphic B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder, and a spectrum of EBV-positive T- and NK-cell disorders. Plasmacytomas are not part of that EBV-associated lymphoproliferative disease category, nor are they characteristically driven by EBV infection. Therefore, according to established WHO classification, plasmacytomas are not classified as EBV-associated lymphoproliferative diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinal cord compression",
    "long_answer": "In the cervical spine the bony canal encloses the spinal cord itself, so stenosis can compress the cord and endanger motor, sensory and even respiratory function throughout the body (tetraparesis, myelopathy). In the lumbar spine the cord has already ended, leaving only cauda-equina nerve roots; stenosis there affects legs and bladder but cannot injure the cord. Therefore cervical spinal stenosis, by risking direct spinal cord compression, is potentially far more dangerous."
}

{
    "short_answer": "University of California, San Diego",
    "long_answer": "The original description of Sakati–Nyhan–Tisdale syndrome was published by Nadia A. Sakati together with the metabolic geneticist William L. Nyhan and colleague Margaret Tisdale. At the time of publication, Dr. Nyhan’s group was based in the Department of Pediatrics, University of California, San Diego (UC San Diego) School of Medicine, making that pediatrics department the academic center involved in characterizing the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIGL",
    "long_answer": "Fryns-Aftimos syndrome, better known today as CHIME syndrome, is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PIGL gene (phosphatidylinositol glycan biosynthesis class L). PIGL encodes an enzyme required for the second step of GPI-anchor synthesis; its deficiency disturbs the surface expression of many proteins, producing the characteristic constellation of coloboma, heart defects, ichthyosiform skin changes, intellectual disability and ear anomalies. A common manifestation is bilateral sensorineural deafness, directly linking PIGL mutations with hearing loss in patients with Fryns-Aftimos (CHIME) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen (estradiol)",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Swyer syndrome (46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis) lack functional ovaries and therefore do not produce sex steroids needed for pubertal development. Standard management includes estrogen-replacement therapy—most commonly with estradiol—to induce breast development, stimulate uterine growth, and establish other secondary female sexual characteristics before sequentially adding progesterone for menstrual cycling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABHD5",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ABHD5 gene (also called CGI-58) cause neutral lipid storage disease with ichthyosis (Chanarin-Dorfman syndrome). Loss of ABHD5 impairs triglyceride hydrolysis, leading to lipid-filled vacuoles in leukocytes—a cytological feature known as Jordans' anomaly—making ABHD5 the gene classically linked to both the disease and the anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria cutanea tarda is strongly linked to iron overload states. The most common genetic cause of systemic iron accumulation is hereditary hemochromatosis, usually due to HFE gene mutations (e.g., C282Y, H63D). Excess hepatic iron from hemochromatosis generates oxidative stress that inhibits uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, precipitating or worsening porphyria cutanea tarda. Thus, hereditary hemochromatosis clearly increases the risk of developing PCT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is an autoimmune blistering skin disease characterized by granular IgA deposits in the dermal papillae. Diagnosis requires a skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence, and treatment involves dapsone plus a gluten-free diet—procedures and therapies that fall squarely within the expertise of dermatologists. Therefore, dermatology is the specialty most commonly responsible for diagnosing and managing this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Invariably fatal",
    "long_answer": "Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a prion-induced neurodegenerative disorder with no effective treatment; once clinical symptoms appear, progressive brain damage leads to death in virtually 100 % of cases, typically within about 12–24 months."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, classically of the nasal type, arises from cytotoxic natural killer (or occasionally γδ T) cells and is consistently associated with latent Epstein-Barr virus infection detected in tumor cells. EBV-encoded proteins drive proliferation and immune evasion, making this lymphoma uniquely linked to EBV and NK-cell origin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congestive heart failure",
    "long_answer": "In congestive heart failure the failing ventricles dilate, producing cardiomegaly on imaging, while elevated venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures cause transudation of fluid into the pleural space, leading to pleural effusions—classically bilateral or right-sided. Thus the same pathophysiologic process accounts for both an enlarged heart and pleural effusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygous recessive",
    "long_answer": "Fish-eye disease is a form of partial LCAT (lecithin–cholesterol acyltransferase) deficiency that follows an autosomal-recessive inheritance pattern. Clinical disease appears only when both copies of the LCAT gene carry pathogenic variants—i.e., in homozygous (recessive) individuals—while heterozygous carriers are typically asymptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Histone deacetylase 8",
    "long_answer": "Wilson-Turner syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-developmental disorder characterized by short stature, obesity and gynecomastia. It is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HDAC8 gene, which encodes the zinc-dependent enzyme histone deacetylase 8. Defective HDAC8 disrupts chromatin remodeling and gene expression, producing the clinical features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methacrylate ester",
    "long_answer": "Hydroxyethylmethacrylate (2-hydroxyethyl methacrylate, HEMA) is the ester formed from methacrylic acid and ethylene glycol. It therefore belongs to the class of acrylic/methacrylate esters, reactive monomers widely used to make polymeric materials such as soft contact lenses and dental resins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the homeobox gene HOXD13, which is situated on chromosome 2q31, cause synpolydactyly (also called Schmitt–Gillenwater–Kelly syndrome). Pathogenic variants disrupt distal limb patterning and can additionally perturb genital tract development, explaining the characteristic combination of limb malformations and urogenital abnormalities observed in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whole-exome sequencing",
    "long_answer": "3C (Ritscher-Schinzel) syndrome is produced by biallelic pathogenic variants in the WASHC5 (KIAA0196/strumpellin) gene. The standard way to detect these nucleotide-level mutations in clinical practice is next-generation whole-exome sequencing, which identifies the disease-causing variants and therefore confirms the diagnosis of 3C syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dural tail sign",
    "long_answer": "The “dural tail sign” – a linear, tapering contrast enhancement of dura mater extending away from the mass on post-contrast MRI or CT – was first reported in 1989 and is classically associated with intracranial meningiomas, helping to differentiate them from other extra-axial tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arnold–Chiari II malformation",
    "long_answer": "In most infants who require surgical closure of a myelomeningocele form of spina bifida, an Arnold–Chiari type II malformation is present. This hind-brain herniation syndrome involves downward displacement of the cerebellar vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum and is a well-recognized, frequently associated condition seen after closure of spina bifida defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Greig cephalopolysyndactyly syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the GLI3 gene, which is located on the short arm of chromosome 7 (7p13). Therefore, chromosome 7 is the chromosomal locus associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Cataracts are opacities of the eye’s lens and are managed surgically or medically by ophthalmologists. In patients with myotonic dystrophy, anterior subcapsular cataracts are common, and their evaluation and removal fall within the specialty of ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Social anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Fear of speaking or performing in front of others is a core symptom of Social Anxiety Disorder (social phobia). Cognitive models of this disorder emphasize how the sufferer’s negative expectations about being judged—essentially a self-fulfilling prophecy—lead to anxious behaviors (e.g., avoidance, stammering) that in turn increase the chance of negative evaluation. Thus, social anxiety disorder is directly connected both to fear of public speaking and to the self-fulfilling prophecy mechanism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "The ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the single most common congenital heart malformation and typically produces a left-to-right shunt as oxygenated blood in the higher-pressure left ventricle flows through the defect into the lower-pressure right ventricle, leading to increased pulmonary blood flow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "In SHORT syndrome, the “O” refers to ocular depression (deep-set or sunken eyes). Disorders and structural abnormalities of the eyes are evaluated and managed by ophthalmologists; therefore, the medical specialty of ophthalmology is responsible for treating the ocular depression associated with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AP4B1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the AP4B1 gene (β4-adaptin of the adaptor protein complex-4) cause autosomal-recessive hereditary spastic paraplegia type 47 (SPG47). The same AP4B1 defects have been repeatedly found in children originally diagnosed with spastic cerebral palsy because they present from birth with non-progressive spastic quadriplegia and intellectual disability. Thus, AP4B1 is a shared genetic cause of both hereditary spastic paraplegia and spastic cerebral palsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Imperial Japanese Navy",
    "long_answer": "In 1884 Surgeon-General Takaki Kanehiro replaced the white-rice-based diet of the Imperial Japanese Navy with one that included barley, meat and vegetables, providing sufficient thiamine. After the change, beriberi cases and deaths on naval voyages fell to virtually zero, whereas the Army, which kept its rice diet, continued to suffer. Thus, the Imperial Japanese Navy successfully eradicated beriberi among its sailors by dietary modification in the late 19th century."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Depression",
    "long_answer": "Cocaine blocks re-uptake of dopamine, norepinephrine and serotonin, producing an initial euphoria. Continued use rapidly depletes these monoamine neurotransmitters, so when the drug effect wanes the user often experiences a ‘crash’ characterized by dysphoria and clinical depression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Oculocerebrorenal (Lowe) syndrome causes central-nervous-system manifestations such as hypotonia, seizures, cognitive delay, and behavioral problems. Evaluation and management of these neurological features—imaging, anticonvulsant therapy, developmental monitoring—are carried out by a neurologist, typically a pediatric neurologist, who specializes in disorders of the brain and nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive neurodegenerative disease that selectively destroys upper and lower motor neurons. In the bulbar-onset form, degeneration of cranial motor nuclei controlling the tongue, pharynx, and larynx leads to dysarthria and dysphagia very early in the course of the illness, often before limb weakness appears. Thus, dysphagia can be an initial or early presenting symptom in ALS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scleritis",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a systemic autoimmune disorder that can cause inflammatory involvement of the sclera, leading to scleritis. Immune-mediated vasculitis in RA damages the episcleral and scleral vessels, producing severe ocular pain, redness, and potential vision loss—making scleritis a recognized complication of rheumatoid arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ben Barres",
    "long_answer": "Ben A. Barres (1954–2017) was a pioneering Stanford neurobiologist known for his groundbreaking work on glial cells. Assigned female at birth, he was born with Müllerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome), a condition in which the Müllerian ducts fail to develop, leaving no uterus or upper vagina. Discovering this congenital absence of female reproductive organs helped clarify his gender identity, and in 1997 he medically and socially transitioned to male, becoming one of the first openly transgender neuroscientists and later the first openly transgender member of the U.S. National Academy of Sciences."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cluttering",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Fragile X syndrome frequently exhibit ‘cluttering,’ a fluency disorder characterized by excessively rapid and/or irregular speech, verbal perseverations, and reduced intelligibility. Numerous clinical studies describe cluttered speech patterns—more so than true stuttering—in boys and men with the FMR1 full mutation, making cluttering the speech disorder most commonly associated with Fragile X syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enterolithiasis",
    "long_answer": "The term enterolithiasis refers to the pathologic process in which mineral concretions (enteroliths) form within the lumen of the gastrointestinal tract, most often in the small or large intestine. These stone-like deposits arise from precipitation of bile salts, calcium salts, or other insoluble material around a nidus, leading to true calculi within the gut—hence ‘lithiasis’ of the intestine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yersinia pseudotuberculosis-derived mitogen (YPM)",
    "long_answer": "Far East scarlet-like fever, a systemic illness caused by Yersinia pseudotuberculosis, is strongly associated with the bacterial superantigen Yersinia pseudotuberculosis-derived mitogen (YPM). YPM acts as a potent superantigen by cross-linking the Vβ region of T-cell receptors with MHC class II molecules, leading to massive cytokine release and the characteristic rash and systemic symptoms observed in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatal in infancy",
    "long_answer": "Pearson syndrome is a severe mitochondrial DNA deletion disorder characterized by sideroblastic anemia, pancreatic insufficiency, and multi-organ failure. Most affected infants succumb to bone-marrow failure, metabolic crises, or infections, and the majority die within the first year or two of life; only a small minority survive to childhood, sometimes later manifesting features of Kearns–Sayre syndrome. Thus, the typical clinical outcome is death during infancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clinical diagnostic criteria",
    "long_answer": "Kawasaki disease is diagnosed primarily by clinical evaluation—persistent fever for ≥5 days together with characteristic mucocutaneous and systemic signs (conjunctivitis, rash, oral changes, extremity changes, cervical lymphadenopathy). There is no definitive laboratory test, so clinicians rely on these established diagnostic criteria to make the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Argon plasma coagulation",
    "long_answer": "The dilated small blood vessels in the gastric antrum characterize gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE, or “watermelon stomach”). Endoscopic argon plasma coagulation (APC) is the standard, widely adopted procedure to ablate these ectatic vessels, reduce bleeding, and control chronic anemia associated with GAVE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Qualitative platelet dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "In uremia, retained nitrogenous and other toxic metabolites interfere with platelet adhesion and aggregation, producing a qualitative defect in platelet function. Although platelet count and coagulation factors are usually normal, this impaired platelet activity lengthens the bleeding time observed in uremic patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Breast MRI",
    "long_answer": "For suspected inflammatory breast cancer, contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (breast MRI) is recommended because it is highly sensitive for detecting the underlying tumour focus and defining its full extent despite the diffuse oedema and skin thickening that can obscure findings on mammography or ultrasound. MRI delineates dermal and chest-wall involvement, guides biopsy, and provides the most accurate evaluation for this aggressive form of breast cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin A deficiency is the principal cause of preventable childhood blindness in Southeast Asia. Insufficient vitamin A intake leads to xerophthalmia, progressing from night-blindness to corneal xerosis and keratomalacia, which permanently damages the cornea. WHO and UNICEF identify vitamin A deficiency as the most common, avoidable cause of blindness among children in this region, where dietary insufficiency remains prevalent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VACTERL association",
    "long_answer": "The VACTERL association (Vertebral defects, Anal atresia/imperforate anus, Cardiac anomalies, Tracheo-esophageal fistula, Renal anomalies, and Limb abnormalities) classically links imperforate anus with limb malformations such as radial ray defects, making it the syndrome most characteristically involving both features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antihelix",
    "long_answer": "In the congenital deformity known as Morel’s ear the antihelix—the inner curved ridge of cartilage that normally runs parallel to the helix—is completely or partly undeveloped. Because the antihelical fold is missing, the helix collapses toward the concha and the pinna appears cupped or shell-like."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Allopurinol",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Glycogen Storage Disease type I commonly develop hyperuricemia due to excess production of uric acid from increased nucleotide turnover. Allopurinol, a xanthine-oxidase inhibitor, is routinely prescribed to these patients because it blocks the conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid, thereby lowering serum urate levels and preventing gout or nephrolithiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sucrase-isomaltase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Sucrase-isomaltase is the brush-border enzyme complex responsible for hydrolyzing dietary sucrose and the α-1→6 branch points of starch. When this enzyme is deficient (congenital or acquired sucrase-isomaltase deficiency), ingested sucrose and starch reach the colon undigested, where they are fermented by bacteria, producing gas, osmotic load, diarrhea, abdominal pain, and other chronic gastrointestinal symptoms after sucrose- or starch-containing meals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD) is characterized by high penetrance but marked variable expressivity. Although most patients share the triad of ocular, dental and digital anomalies, the specific manifestations (e.g., microcornea, enamel hypoplasia, syndactyly), their combination, and their severity differ widely, even between members of the same family who carry the identical GJA1 mutation. Neurological involvement and craniofacial features are likewise present in some but absent in others. Therefore the disorder does not present with a uniform set of symptoms in all affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis",
    "long_answer": "Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter syndrome) is classically triggered by a preceding genitourinary infection with Chlamydia trachomatis. After the acute infection resolves, host immune responses to C. trachomatis antigens in genetically predisposed individuals (often HLA-B27–positive) can lead to sterile inflammation of joints, eyes, and the urogenital tract, producing the characteristic triad of arthritis, conjunctivitis/uveitis, and urethritis. Thus, Chlamydia trachomatis is a well-established bacterial cause of reactive arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "17-hydroxypregnenolone",
    "long_answer": "3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase catalyzes the conversion of 17-hydroxypregnenolone (a Δ5 steroid) to 17-hydroxyprogesterone (a Δ4 steroid). When the enzyme is deficient, this step is blocked and 17-hydroxypregnenolone accumulates markedly in blood. A high basal or ACTH-stimulated concentration of 17-hydroxypregnenolone is therefore the characteristic biochemical marker of congenital adrenal hyperplasia caused by 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intralobar sequestration",
    "long_answer": "Intralobar pulmonary sequestration is the form that can be acquired after repeated pulmonary infections. Chronic infection and inflammation can obstruct and isolate a segment of lung parenchyma, creating sequestered tissue that remains within the visceral pleura of an existing lobe and typically receives systemic arterial blood; extralobar sequestration, in contrast, is strictly congenital."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS activating mutation",
    "long_answer": "Fibrous dysplasia is classically caused by post-zygotic activating mutations of the GNAS gene (most often at codon R201), which increase Gsα signaling. The same somatic GNAS mutation underlies the polyostotic lesions that can deform the spine and produce scoliosis, making this mutation a shared genetic basis for both fibrous dysplasia and the scoliosis that frequently accompanies it."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complement component C5 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Leiner’s disease—an infantile syndrome characterized by severe, generalized seborrhoeic dermatitis with failure to thrive and recurrent infections—has repeatedly been associated with an inherited deficiency of the complement protein C5. Lack of C5 impairs formation of the membrane-attack complex and opsonisation, predisposing to infections and the marked inflammatory skin reaction seen in Leiner’s disease. The same C5 deficiency has been documented in a subset of patients presenting with severe seborrhoeic dermatitis alone, making complement component C5 deficiency the shared pathogenic link between the two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaline phosphatase",
    "long_answer": "Lyngstadaas syndrome is characterized by premature loss of teeth associated with defective formation of cementum. The underlying biochemical defect is a congenital deficiency of the enzyme tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase, the same enzyme lacking in hypophosphatasia. This alkaline phosphatase deficiency impairs mineralization of bone and dental tissues, producing the clinical features described by Lyngstadaas and colleagues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
    "long_answer": "In primary sclerosing cholangitis, contrast injection during endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) outlines alternating strictures and dilatations along the intra- and extra-hepatic bile ducts, producing the characteristic 'beading' appearance used to confirm the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNMT3A",
    "long_answer": "DNMT3A encodes DNA methyltransferase 3A, the enzyme that catalyzes de novo DNA methylation by adding methyl groups to unmethylated CpG sites. Somatic DNMT3A mutations, particularly at residue R882, occur in roughly 20–30 % of adult acute myeloid leukemia cases, making it one of the most frequently mutated genes in AML."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "More than three-quarters of pulmonary (lung) arteriovenous malformations occur in patients with hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome), an autosomal-dominant vascular disorder that predisposes to abnormal direct connections between arteries and veins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals",
    "long_answer": "In calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (pseudogout), the characteristic deposits within articular cartilage and synovial fluid are calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystals. These rhomboid, positively birefringent crystals trigger intense acute inflammatory reactions in the joint, producing the episodic pain and swelling typical of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy with proximal dominance is caused by pathogenic variants in the TFG (Trk-fused gene) locus, which is situated on the long arm of chromosome 3 (3q12-q13). Linkage studies in affected families demonstrated co-segregation of the disease with this chromosomal region, establishing chromosome 3 as the locus responsible for HMSN with proximal dominance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-phenylalanine diet",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria is caused by deficient phenylalanine hydroxylase, so phenylalanine accumulates and becomes neurotoxic. The cornerstone of management is lifelong restriction of dietary phenylalanine—achieved with a specially formulated, phenylalanine-free amino-acid supplement and avoidance of high-protein foods and aspartame—to keep blood phenylalanine within safe levels and prevent cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Child and adolescent psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "Reactive attachment disorder is classified in the DSM-5 as a trauma- and stressor-related psychiatric condition that arises in early childhood. Assessment, differential diagnosis, and management (which may include psychotherapy, family interventions, and, if indicated, pharmacotherapy for comorbidities) fall within the scope of child and adolescent psychiatry, the medical specialty trained to diagnose and treat mental disorders in children." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FOXL2 transcription factor cause Blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES). In BPES type I, females typically develop premature ovarian insufficiency because FOXL2 is crucial for granulosa-cell and ovarian follicle development. Thus, this FOXL2-related genetic disorder links characteristic eyelid malformations with early ovarian failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAPN3 (calpain-3)",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies covering both European and Asian cohorts show that autosomal-recessive limb-girdle muscular dystrophy due to CAPN3 mutations (historically LGMD2A, now LGMDR1) accounts for the largest share of genetically confirmed cases across Eurasia. CAPN3 encodes the muscle-specific calcium-dependent protease calpain-3, and loss-of-function variants in this gene are repeatedly reported as the most prevalent molecular defect responsible for limb-girdle muscle weakness in the region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-43, are the established cause of Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD). The very same GJA1 pathogenic variants have also been documented in families showing primarily isolated microphthalmia or microcornea, proving that a single GJA1 mutation can underlie both ODDD and an isolated microphthalmia phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Barrett esophagus",
    "long_answer": "Barrett esophagus is an acquired condition in which chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux leads to replacement of the normal distal oesophageal squamous epithelium by intestinal-type columnar mucosa. This metaplastic change occurs in the lower one-third of the oesophagus and confers a markedly elevated risk of progression through dysplasia to invasive adenocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Golden Retriever",
    "long_answer": "Among domestic dogs, Golden Retrievers show one of the highest breed-specific incidences of hemangiosarcoma (the veterinary counterpart of angiosarcoma). Multiple epidemiological studies have documented a several-fold greater risk in this breed compared with the general canine population, making Golden Retrievers the prototypical high-risk breed for developing angiosarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Both Watson syndrome and neurofibromatosis type 1 are caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene, which encodes the tumor-suppressor protein neurofibromin. Mutations that inactivate NF1 lead to dysregulated RAS signaling, producing the overlapping clinical features observed in these two disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) is a tumor-predisposition syndrome caused by germline mutations in the NF1 tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 17. Individuals with NF1 have a markedly increased risk of developing low-grade astrocytic tumors, particularly pilocytic astrocytomas, and optic pathway/optic nerve gliomas are one of the hallmark central nervous system manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baker cyst",
    "long_answer": "A Baker cyst (popliteal cyst) forms when synovial fluid from the knee joint herniates into the gastrocnemio-semimembranosus bursa. It is frequently associated with inflammatory arthritides such as rheumatoid arthritis, and the underlying synovitis can cause or exacerbate a knee joint effusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rocky Mountain spotted fever",
    "long_answer": "Rocky Mountain spotted fever, caused by Rickettsia rickettsii, frequently produces a CNS vasculitis that can manifest as meningo-encephalitis. The rickettsial infection damages vascular endothelium, leading to platelet activation and consumption, so thrombocytopenia is a characteristic laboratory finding in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Administration of thiamazole (methimazole) in cats can lead to formation of antinuclear antibodies. A persistently positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) test in cats is classically linked with the autoimmune disease systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE); therefore, thiamazole-treated cats that become ANA-positive are evaluated for drug-induced or spontaneous SLE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Undetectable = Untransmittable",
    "long_answer": "The concept is known as “Undetectable = Untransmittable” (U=U). Multiple large studies (e.g., HPTN 052, PARTNER, Opposites Attract) have shown that people living with HIV who maintain an undetectable plasma viral load—generally <200 copies/mL—through consistent antiretroviral therapy do not pass the virus to their sexual partners, establishing that when HIV is undetectable, it is untransmittable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudobulbar palsy",
    "long_answer": "Pseudobulbar palsy, also called pseudobulbar affect, arises when bilateral corticobulbar pathways are damaged—an injury pattern that can follow traumatic brain injury. Loss of frontal-brainstem inhibitory control produces uncontrolled bouts of laughing or crying, manifesting clinically as marked emotional lability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucose-driven thiamine depletion",
    "long_answer": "Oxidative metabolism of an IV glucose load requires thiamine as a co-factor (for pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase). In a thiamine-deficient patient, giving glucose alone abruptly increases metabolic demand for thiamine, rapidly exhausting the limited stores, leading to lactic acid accumulation and acute worsening or precipitation of Wernicke encephalopathy. Therefore thiamine must be administered before or with glucose to prevent this deterioration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-DQB1*06:02",
    "long_answer": "The HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele of the HLA-DQB1 gene is carried by >90 % of patients with narcolepsy type 1 and is the strongest genetic risk factor for the disease. This class II MHC molecule presents antigenic peptides to CD4⁺ T cells; its presence is thought to promote an autoimmune attack against orexin (hypocretin)–producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, leading to the orexin deficiency that characterizes narcolepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNH1",
    "long_answer": "Zimmermann–Laband syndrome type 1 is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the KCNH1 gene, which encodes the voltage-gated potassium channel Kv10.1 (EAG1). Gain-of-function mutations in KCNH1 disrupt normal channel activity, leading to the craniofacial, gingival, digital, and neurodevelopmental abnormalities characteristic of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type I collagen",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis Imperfecta is classically caused by mutations in the COL1A1 or COL1A2 genes that encode the α-chains of type I collagen, the major structural collagen of bone. Defective synthesis or structure of type I collagen weakens the bone matrix, producing the brittle-bone phenotype characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Promoter hypermethylation",
    "long_answer": "In many sporadic colorectal cancers the MGMT (O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase) gene is not inactivated by point mutations but by dense CpG-island promoter hypermethylation. This epigenetic change silences MGMT expression and is a characteristic alteration in colorectal tumours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prostaglandins",
    "long_answer": "Indometacin is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug that blocks cyclo-oxygenase-1 and ‑2 (COX-1/2) enzymes. By inhibiting COX activity it prevents the conversion of arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, thereby lowering tissue levels of prostaglandins—lipid signaling molecules that drive vasodilation, vascular permeability, pain and other inflammatory responses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Episodic memory",
    "long_answer": "Retrograde amnesia primarily disrupts the recall of autobiographical episodes—memories of specific personal events situated in time and place. This subtype of declarative (explicit) memory is called episodic memory, and it is the very material people draw on when writing or recounting autobiographies. While semantic knowledge (facts) may be spared, loss of episodic memory is a hallmark of retrograde amnesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CRX",
    "long_answer": "Whereas the large majority of Leber congenital amaurosis (LCA) genes cause disease only when both alleles are mutated (autosomal recessive), heterozygous mutations in the cone-rod homeobox gene CRX act in a dominant-negative fashion. As a result, a single pathogenic CRX allele is sufficient to produce the LCA7 subtype, giving Leber congenital amaurosis an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angiosarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Angiosarcoma is a malignant tumor that arises from the endothelial cells lining blood or lymphatic vessels. Because it originates in the soft connective tissues rather than in organs or epithelial surfaces, it is categorized under soft tissue sarcomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography (CT)",
    "long_answer": "Calcific deposits characteristic of chondrocalcinosis have high X-ray attenuation, so cross-sectional computed tomography clearly depicts these calcium-dense areas within intervertebral discs and spinal ligaments, making CT the standard modality for visualizing chondrocalcinosis in the spine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Panretinal photocoagulation",
    "long_answer": "Panretinal (scatter) laser photocoagulation is occasionally misapplied to cases of retinoschisis, where it offers no therapeutic benefit. However, it is the evidence-based treatment for proliferative and some severe non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy, because ablating peripheral ischemic retina lowers VEGF production and halts pathologic neovascularization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymidine phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalopathy syndrome (MNGIE) results from loss-of-function mutations in the TYMP gene, which encodes the enzyme thymidine phosphorylase. Deficiency of this enzyme blocks normal catabolism of thymidine and deoxyuridine, leading to their systemic accumulation, secondary mitochondrial DNA depletion/instability, and the characteristic neurologic and gastrointestinal manifestations of MNGIE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CT scan",
    "long_answer": "Computed tomography (CT) imaging is routinely used to evaluate congenital cystic neck lesions. For both cystic hygroma (lymphatic malformation) and branchial cleft cyst, a CT scan delineates the cystic, non-enhancing nature of the mass, its exact extent, and its relationship to adjacent structures, making it a standard diagnostic study for each condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyloid beta peptide",
    "long_answer": "The amyloid plaques characteristic of Alzheimer’s disease are largely composed of aggregated amyloid beta (Aβ) peptides—particularly the 40- and 42-amino-acid forms produced by proteolytic cleavage of the amyloid precursor protein (APP). Accumulation of Aβ in brain tissue is a hallmark of Alzheimer’s pathology and is also observed in individuals with mild cognitive impairment, linking this peptide directly to the early and late stages of the disease process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rituximab",
    "long_answer": "Rituximab, an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody, is widely used to induce and maintain remission in ANCA-associated vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis. By depleting CD20-positive B cells, it reduces production of pathogenic anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCAs) and has been shown in randomized trials (e.g., RAVE, RITUXVAS) to be at least as effective as cyclophosphamide with a more favorable toxicity profile."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysautonomia (Riley-Day syndrome) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the IKBKAP gene (also called ELP1). Cytogenetic mapping places IKBKAP at 9q31.3, so the gene resides on human chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal folate deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Heavy alcohol consumption during pregnancy impairs intestinal absorption and hepatic storage of folic acid, increasing its renal excretion and lowering circulating folate levels in the mother. Adequate folate is critical for DNA synthesis and neural tube closure between days 21–28 of embryogenesis; when maternal folate is deficient, the neural tube may fail to close properly, producing neural tube defects such as spina bifida."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L-carnitine",
    "long_answer": "L-carnitine is routinely given as a dietary supplement in patients with methylmalonic acidemias. Carnitine accepts excess acyl-CoA groups, forming acyl-carnitines that can be transported out of mitochondria and excreted, thereby lowering toxic acyl-CoA/organic acid accumulation and helping restore free CoA pools."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gout (gouty arthritis)",
    "long_answer": "Persistent hyperuricemia most often results in deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints and peri-articular tissues, producing the clinical syndrome of gouty arthritis. This manifestation occurs far more frequently than other complications such as uric-acid nephrolithiasis or chronic urate nephropathy, making gout the commonest consequence of elevated serum uric acid levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nucleocapsid",
    "long_answer": "In subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) the measles virus persists in neurons not as complete, infectious virions but as nucleocapsids—the protein shell tightly bound to the viral RNA genome. These nucleocapsids lack the envelope-associated matrix and glycoproteins needed for viral budding, so they evade immune clearance yet continue low-level replication, leading to the chronic, progressive brain disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encodes tyrosinase",
    "long_answer": "The mammalian TYR gene produces the enzyme tyrosinase, the rate-limiting catalyst that converts tyrosine to dopaquinone in the melanin biosynthetic pathway. Adequate tyrosinase activity is essential for synthesizing eumelanin and pheomelanin, which give skin, hair, and eye their color. Birds possess an orthologous TYR gene that performs the same enzymatic step; the amount or activity of this tyrosinase determines how much melanin is deposited in feathers, thereby controlling feather coloration. Thus, the gene’s melanin-synthesizing role in mammals directly parallels its role in avian plumage pigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wide local excision",
    "long_answer": "Aggressive fibromatosis (desmoid tumour) is a locally invasive lesion that does not metastasise but can recur. When surveillance reveals new tumours, current consensus recommends surgical management—specifically, repeat wide local excision with clear margins—to remove the new growth and prevent further local progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent monogenic cause of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis is an autosomal-dominant gain-of-function mutation in the STAT1 gene. STAT1 is located on chromosome 2 (band 2q32-q34). These mutations impair IL-17–mediated antifungal immunity, leading to persistent Candida infections of the skin, nails and mucous membranes that define chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NCSTN, PSENEN, PSEN1",
    "long_answer": "Familial hidradenitis suppurativa is most often linked to loss-of-function mutations in three genes that encode components of the γ-secretase complex—NCSTN (nicastrin), PSENEN (presenilin enhancer 2), and PSEN1 (presenilin-1). These mutations disrupt γ-secretase–mediated Notch signalling in follicular epithelium, predisposing to the follicular occlusion and chronic inflammation that characterize the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS), the clinical stage of HIV infection, is defined by a profound depletion of CD4+ T helper lymphocytes. When CD4+ counts fall below critical thresholds (e.g., <200 cells/µL), cell-mediated immunity is severely compromised, predisposing patients to opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, candidiasis, and cytomegalovirus disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 3 and 5",
    "long_answer": "Cytogenetic studies consistently show that the commonest abnormalities in angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma are whole-chromosome gains—most often trisomy 3 and trisomy 5—detected in a majority of karyotyped cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner: a child must receive two defective CFTR gene alleles—one from each carrier parent—for the disease to manifest. Heterozygous carriers are typically asymptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory failure",
    "long_answer": "Severe infantile hypophosphatasia produces poorly mineralized ribs and a small, soft thoracic cage. This compromises lung expansion and often leads to progressive respiratory failure, the most frequent and life-threatening pulmonary complication observed in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Biopsy",
    "long_answer": "The definitive diagnosis of oral cancer is established by performing a biopsy of the suspicious oral lesion and examining the tissue histopathologically; this procedure confirms malignant cellular changes and distinguishes cancer from benign conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly–ectodermal dysplasia–cleft (EEC) syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the transcription factor p63. TP63 is a member of the p53 gene family and shares significant sequence and functional homology with the tumor suppressor gene p53, explaining its description as a p53 homologue in the context of this congenital disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≥1000 IU/L",
    "long_answer": "Pleural fluid from an empyema typically shows very high lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) activity. Clinical guidelines and Light’s criteria state that an LDH concentration equal to or exceeding about 1000 international units per litre is strongly suggestive of a complicated parapneumonic effusion or frank empyema rather than a simple exudate or transudate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reflex sympathetic dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Paula Abdul revealed that after injuring her cervical spine in a high-school cheerleading accident she developed reflex sympathetic dystrophy (also called complex regional pain syndrome). RSD is a neurological pain disorder in which dysregulated sympathetic nervous system activity and neuro-inflammatory changes produce chronic, severe, often burning pain, hypersensitivity, and motor and trophic disturbances in the affected region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diazepam",
    "long_answer": "The neurologic manifestations of Lesch–Nyhan syndrome (dystonia, spasticity, self-injurious behavior) are managed with benzodiazepines—most commonly diazepam. By potentiating GABA-mediated inhibition in the CNS, diazepam can reduce abnormal movements and muscle tone, whereas drugs like allopurinol only address the hyperuricemia and have no effect on the neurologic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Portal hypertension",
    "long_answer": "In Niemann–Pick disease type C, unesterified cholesterol and sphingolipids accumulate in hepatocytes and Kupffer cells, producing progressive hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis. This hepatic involvement often increases portal venous pressure, making portal hypertension one of the most frequent and clinically significant complications observed in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blackwater fever",
    "long_answer": "Quinine therapy in patients with Plasmodium falciparum malaria can rarely precipitate Blackwater fever, an immune-mediated (hypersensitivity) reaction characterized by massive intravascular haemolysis and dark (‘black-water’) haemoglobinuria. This quinine-triggered event represents a severe form of hypersensitivity causing rapid red-cell destruction, acute anaemia and renal impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral retinoids",
    "long_answer": "Lesions in Epidermodysplasia verruciformis are commonly managed with systemic or topical retinoids (vitamin A derivatives such as acitretin or isotretinoin). Retinoids normalize keratinocyte differentiation and can reduce both the wart-like lesions caused by HPV and the risk of malignant transformation, making this drug class the standard pharmacologic therapy for EV skin lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "USH2A",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome type II is most frequently caused by pathogenic variants in the USH2A gene, which encodes the protein usherin. Targeted or whole-exome DNA sequencing readily detects these USH2A mutations, allowing molecular confirmation of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs",
    "long_answer": "In graft-versus-host disease, particularly its chronic form, the donor immune cells can attack the recipient’s lungs, leading to disorders such as bronchiolitis obliterans. The lungs are the organ responsible for gas exchange, transferring oxygen into the blood and removing carbon dioxide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscular dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Sam Schröder was born with muscular dystrophy, a group of rare inherited (genetic) disorders in which mutations in muscle-protein genes lead to progressive weakness and limited control of the arms and legs. This congenital impairment affects all four of his limbs, qualifying him for the quad division in wheelchair tennis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aminoglycosides",
    "long_answer": "Aminoglycoside antibiotics (e.g., gentamicin, amikacin, streptomycin) are well known to be ototoxic. They accumulate in the inner ear, generate reactive oxygen species, and damage cochlear hair cells and the vestibulocochlear nerve, producing irreversible sensorineural hearing loss in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "In severe neonatal jaundice, unconjugated bilirubin crosses the immature blood–brain barrier and deposits in certain deep brain nuclei, producing kernicterus. The principal site of damage is the basal ganglia—particularly the globus pallidus—whose circuits modulate and fine-tune voluntary motor activity. Injury here leads to the characteristic movement disorders seen in bilirubin encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Popliteal pterygium syndrome is an autosomal-dominant disorder most commonly produced by pathogenic variants in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6) located on chromosome 1q32.2. IRF6 encodes a transcription factor critical for epithelial and craniofacial development; loss-of-function mutations disrupt this pathway, leading to the cleft lip/palate, popliteal webbing, and genitourinary anomalies characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Storiform fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "IgG4-related disease is marked by a dense, whorled (“storiform”) pattern of fibrosis accompanying a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate rich in IgG4-positive plasma cells. This characteristic storiform fibrosis is a key histopathologic hallmark across the various organs affected by the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sweating",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary fructose intolerance leads to rapid drops in blood glucose after fructose ingestion; the resulting adrenergic response produces profuse sweating, a classic autonomic symptom that is equally characteristic of hypoglycemia in general."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "In the rare, severe infantile form of phosphofructokinase (PFK) deficiency (glycogen-storage disease type VII), impaired glycolysis deprives the developing brain of adequate energy. Affected infants can therefore go on to develop a progressive encephalopathy characterized by developmental regression, seizures and diffuse cerebral atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus amyloliquefaciens",
    "long_answer": "The L-tryptophan associated with the 1989 eosinophilia–myalgia syndrome outbreak was manufactured by Showa Denko using a genetically engineered strain of Bacillus amyloliquefaciens. This bacterium was modified to overproduce L-tryptophan, but changes in the strain and purification steps allowed novel trace contaminants (e.g., EBT, PAA) to pass into the final product, which were later linked to the development of EMS in consumers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hormone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "Case–control studies of essential blepharospasm have shown that women who receive systemic estrogen-containing hormone replacement therapy for relief of menopausal vasomotor symptoms have a significantly higher incidence of blepharospasm than non-users. Thus, menopausal hormone replacement therapy is recognized as a risk factor for developing blepharospasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "British Sjögren's Syndrome Association",
    "long_answer": "The UK Primary Sjögren's Syndrome Registry (UKPSSR) is a multicentre cohort and biobank for patients with primary Sjögren’s syndrome. Its establishment and continuing operation are funded and overseen by the British Sjögren's Syndrome Association (BSSA), the UK national patient charity that supports research and resources for Sjögren’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is an autoimmune connective-tissue disease in which immune-mediated vascular injury and fibrosis lead to episodic digital vasospasm. This produces the triphasic colour changes, numbness and pain characteristic of Raynaud phenomenon, so patients with systemic sclerosis often present with symptoms virtually identical to those of primary Raynaud syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Veins",
    "long_answer": "Factor V Leiden is a hereditary mutation that renders factor V resistant to inactivation by activated protein C, creating a hypercoagulable state that predominantly causes venous thrombosis (e.g., deep-vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism). Arterial vessels are much less affected, so the primary vessels involved are veins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Wallis–Zieff–Goldblatt syndrome is catalogued in OMIM (entry 275100) as a 2-series disorder, indicating autosomal recessive inheritance. Reported cases occurred among siblings born to unaffected parents, consistent with recessive transmission: each parent is an asymptomatic carrier, and the syndrome appears only when an individual inherits two mutated alleles, one from each parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 35 years",
    "long_answer": "Klippel–Feil syndrome itself usually does not shorten life, but when it is accompanied by significant congenital heart defects, survival is markedly reduced. Case-series that followed affected males report a mean age at death in the mid-thirties, giving an average life expectancy of roughly 35 years."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Both lead poisoning and long-term exposure to environmental noise are well-established causes of elevated blood pressure. Lead disrupts vascular nitric-oxide signaling and kidney function, while chronic noise activates the sympathetic nervous system and stress pathways; in each case the result is arterial constriction and sustained increases in systemic blood pressure, manifesting clinically as hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical tendon repair",
    "long_answer": "A complete quadriceps tendon rupture abolishes active knee extension; definitive management is prompt surgical re-attachment of the torn quadriceps tendon to the superior pole of the patella, usually with trans-osseous sutures or suture anchors, followed by immobilization and rehabilitation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Prenatal (in-utero) exposure to alcohol is a well-recognized teratogen. The spectrum of defects it produces—collectively termed Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders (FASD), including classic Fetal Alcohol Syndrome—frequently involves the visual system. Documented, consensus-accepted ocular and visual anomalies include microphthalmia (abnormally small eyes), optic nerve hypoplasia, coloboma, retinal vascular abnormalities, strabismus, ptosis, refractive errors, and reduced visual acuity. These defects arise because alcohol interferes with neural crest migration, retinoic-acid signaling, and vascular development during critical periods of embryogenesis, all of which are essential for normal eye morphogenesis. Therefore, established biomedical evidence supports that gestational alcohol exposure can indeed cause developmental problems affecting the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oropharyngeal dysphagia",
    "long_answer": "Difficulty initiating a swallow in the mouth and pharynx—termed oropharyngeal dysphagia—is frequently seen in patients with vascular dementia because multiple cerebral or brain-stem infarcts disrupt the neural control of the oropharyngeal phase of swallowing. In clinical classification of swallowing disorders, oropharyngeal dysphagia (versus esophageal dysphagia) is one of the two major types, making it both a key symptom in vascular dementia and a principal category of dysphagia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Child Behavior Checklist",
    "long_answer": "The Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL) is a widely validated parent-report questionnaire that screens for emotional and behavioral problems in youth. Its internalizing scales—especially the Anxious/Depressed and Withdrawn subscales—capture behaviors typical of separation anxiety disorder (e.g., excessive worry about separation, refusal to attend school, clinging). Clinicians therefore use the CBCL to identify and quantify problem behaviors in children presenting with separation anxiety disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PFAPA syndrome",
    "long_answer": "PFAPA (Periodic Fever, Aphthous Stomatitis, Pharyngitis, and Adenitis) syndrome is an autoinflammatory disorder seen in children that presents with recurrent high fevers. A hallmark of the syndrome is painful, enlarged cervical lymph nodes (cervical adenitis/lymphadenopathy) accompanying the periodic fever episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is most commonly caused by pathogenic mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes fibrillin-1 and is located on chromosome 15q21.1. Defective fibrillin-1 weakens connective tissue, particularly in the cardiovascular system, predisposing affected individuals to aortic root dilation and various arrhythmias that can manifest clinically as palpitations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Septic shock",
    "long_answer": "Septic (distributive) shock is the single most common form of shock seen in clinical practice. Its defining haemodynamic pattern is hypotension with a normal or, more often, markedly elevated cardiac output because widespread vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance. This paradoxically high cardiac output, in the setting of persistent hypotension and organ hypoperfusion, is a key feature that helps distinguish septic shock from other shock states where cardiac output is low."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vestibular neuritis",
    "long_answer": "Vestibular neuritis is inflammation of the vestibular branch of the eighth cranial nerve, most often triggered by a preceding viral infection (e.g., herpes simplex, influenza). The neuritis disrupts vestibular signaling, producing sudden, prolonged vertigo without hearing loss. Because it represents virus-related nerve inflammation, it parallels the way viral infections can inflame peripheral nerves and cause peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RS1 gene",
    "long_answer": "X-linked juvenile retinoschisis is caused by pathogenic mutations in the RS1 gene, which encodes the retina-specific cell-adhesion protein retinoschisin; loss-of-function RS1 variants disrupt retinal layer integrity and lead to the characteristic X-linked recessive form of retinoschisis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2p22-p25, 3q13",
    "long_answer": "Familial essential tremor shows autosomal-dominant linkage to two main loci: one on chromosome 2p22-p25 and another on chromosome 3q13. These chromosomal regions (designated ETM1 and ETM2 in genetic studies) are the best-validated genetic associations with essential tremor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNQ2 mutation; epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures are most frequently caused by heterozygous mutations in the KCNQ2 gene, which encodes the Kv7.2 voltage-gated potassium channel. While the neonatal seizures typically resolve within the first months of life, approximately 10–15 % of affected individuals develop epilepsy later in childhood, adolescence, or adulthood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skull X-ray",
    "long_answer": "Hyperostosis frontalis interna is typically an incidental finding during routine plain skull radiography (skull X-ray), where the characteristic thickening of the inner table of the frontal bone becomes readily visible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "Valbenazine (marketed as Ingrezza) received regulatory approval for the treatment of tardive dyskinesia from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in April 2017, making it the first FDA-approved therapy specifically indicated for this movement disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1 in 250,000",
    "long_answer": "Population studies based on U.S. newborn-screening data indicate that isovaleric acidemia occurs in roughly one newborn out of every 250,000, making the disorder an ultra-rare inborn error of leucine metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cetirizine",
    "long_answer": "Several case reports have shown that the second-generation H1-antihistamine cetirizine rapidly relieves the severe itching that accompanies Kimura’s disease. The drug not only blocks H1 receptors but also reduces histamine release from basophils and mast cells, mechanisms that fit the IgE- and eosinophil-driven pathophysiology of the disorder. Hence, cetirizine is regarded as an effective medication for controlling the pruritus associated with Kimura’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone",
    "long_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), released from the anterior pituitary gland, binds to receptors on Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules. This stimulation promotes tight-junction formation between Sertoli cells, establishing the blood-testis barrier, and supports spermatogenesis by providing nutrients and regulatory signals to developing germ cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)—for example paroxetine, sertraline, or fluoxetine—are the first-line antidepressant class for treating panic attacks and panic disorder. By inhibiting the serotonin transporter, SSRIs raise synaptic serotonin levels, which has been shown in controlled trials to reduce the frequency and severity of panic episodes and improve anticipatory anxiety more effectively and with better tolerability than older antidepressants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMP22",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsy (HNPP) is most often due to a deletion or inactivating mutation of the peripheral myelin protein 22 (PMP22) gene on chromosome 17p11.2. Frameshift mutations in PMP22 reduce functional PMP22 protein (haplo-insufficiency), disrupting myelin maintenance and producing the recurrent pressure-induced neuropathies characteristic of HNPP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tachypnea",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia triggers a sudden, extreme increase in skeletal-muscle metabolism, producing excess carbon dioxide and acidosis. To compensate, patients breathe much faster than normal—a clinical sign known as tachypnea (rapid breathing), commonly observed early during a malignant hyperthermia crisis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uremic pleuritis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic kidney disease, especially in advanced uremia or end-stage renal disease, can lead to inflammation of the pleura known as uremic pleuritis. Accumulation of uremic toxins and impaired fluid regulation cause a sterile, exudative pleural reaction, making uremic pleuritis the characteristic pleural disorder linked to chronic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prions",
    "long_answer": "Transmissible spongiform encephalopathies—including Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, kuru, and bovine spongiform encephalopathy—are caused by prions: misfolded, self-propagating isoforms of the host’s prion protein (PrP). Unlike bacteria or viruses, prions lack nucleic acids; they induce conformational change in normal PrP, leading to aggregates that produce the characteristic spongiform degeneration of neural tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Permanent pacemaker",
    "long_answer": "Severe (high-grade or complete) heart block disrupts the heart’s intrinsic conduction, risking profound bradycardia, syncope, and sudden death. Definitive therapy is electrical pacing: implantation of a permanent pacemaker restores reliable atrioventricular conduction and maintains adequate heart rate, making it the standard primary treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chrousos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Chrousos syndrome, or primary generalized glucocorticoid resistance, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NR3C1 gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor. The defective receptor makes peripheral tissues resistant to cortisol, while pituitary ACTH secretion rises in an attempt to compensate. Although serum cortisol is elevated, its signaling is ineffective, so the body experiences a functional (tissue-level) glucocorticoid deficiency at the same time that receptor-level resistance is present. Thus this single genetic disorder produces both glucocorticoid resistance and apparent glucocorticoid deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior cerebral artery",
    "long_answer": "Alien hand syndrome commonly arises after ischemic injury to the corpus callosum and medial frontal cortex, territories perfused by the anterior cerebral artery. Infarction of this artery disrupts interhemispheric motor control, producing the involuntary, autonomous limb movements characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurological disease",
    "long_answer": "While laryngomalacia is classically a congenital disorder of infants, an acquired form can appear in adolescents or adults. In these older patients the floppy supraglottic tissues result from impaired neuromuscular control of the larynx, most often secondary to an underlying neurological disease (e.g., stroke, Parkinson’s disease, multiple sclerosis, Chiari malformation). The neurologic impairment decreases tone in the aryepiglottic and other supraglottic structures, allowing them to collapse during inspiration and produce laryngomalacia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Carbuncles are deep, interconnected clusters of skin abscesses that almost always arise from infection by Staphylococcus aureus. This Gram-positive coccus colonizes hair follicles, produces cytotoxins (e.g., α-toxin), and elicits intense neutrophilic inflammation, leading to the coalescence of multiple furuncles into a single, larger carbuncle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Self-preservation instinct",
    "long_answer": "Self-defeating (masochistic) personality disorder involves habitual behaviors that bring about harm or failure to oneself. The direct opposite of this pattern is the innate self-preservation instinct—the basic biological and psychological drive to protect oneself from danger, seek safety, and maintain well-being."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve",
    "long_answer": "Fazio–Londe disease is a form of childhood-onset progressive bulbar palsy that leads to degeneration of lower motor neurons in several brain-stem nuclei. Involvement of the facial nucleus causes lower motor neuron palsy of cranial nerve VII—the facial nerve—resulting in facial paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKP2 (plakophilin-2)",
    "long_answer": "The majority of familial arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy cases carry loss-of-function mutations in the PKP2 gene, which encodes the desmosomal protein plakophilin-2. Defective plakophilin-2 weakens cardiomyocyte cell-cell adhesion, promoting myocyte detachment, fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, and ventricular arrhythmias characteristic of ARVC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical excision",
    "long_answer": "A chondroma is a benign, well-circumscribed cartilaginous tumour, so definitive management is simply to surgically excise the lesion (often by local excision or curettage with removal of the cartilaginous mass). Because it is encapsulated and non-invasive, complete excision reliably eliminates the unsightly tumour and prevents recurrence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Microcephaly-deafness syndrome is a rare congenital disorder involving developmental brain malformation and sensorineural hearing loss. Such syndromic, multisystem, hereditary conditions are typically evaluated, diagnosed, and coordinated in care by specialists trained in medical (clinical) genetics, who perform genetic testing, counseling, and guide multidisciplinary management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "In Uhl anomaly the near-absence of right-ventricular muscle can raise right-sided pressures, promoting right-to-left shunting of systemic venous blood across an interatrial communication. The admixture of de-oxygenated blood in the systemic arterial circulation produces arterial hypoxemia, which manifests clinically as cyanosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth retardation",
    "long_answer": "Children with Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (Hers disease) often experience impaired hepatic glycogen breakdown, leading to recurrent mild hypoglycaemia and chronic energy deficiency. This metabolic stress commonly manifests as poor linear growth and delayed attainment of normal stature—clinically described as growth retardation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Secretin stimulation test",
    "long_answer": "A standard confirmatory procedure for Zollinger–Ellison syndrome is the intravenous secretin stimulation test: after secretin is administered, patients with gastrin-secreting tumours show a paradoxical and marked rise in serum gastrin (>200 pg/mL), distinguishing the syndrome from other causes of hypergastrinaemia and peptic ulcer symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous immunoglobulin",
    "long_answer": "Prospective cohort series and multicentre retrospective studies of Clarkson-type systemic capillary leak syndrome show that monthly high-dose intravenous immunoglobulin (2 g/kg every 4‒8 weeks) markedly reduces attack frequency and improves survival, and it is therefore recommended as the first-line prophylactic therapy for SCLS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eosinophilic gastroenteritis",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic gastroenteritis is a rare gastrointestinal disorder in which eosinophils infiltrate the mucosal, muscular, and/or serosal layers of the stomach and intestines. When the serosal layer is involved, eosinophils and protein-rich fluid can leak into the peritoneal cavity, producing eosinophilic ascites. Thus ascites is a recognized complication of eosinophilic gastroenteritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Pre-gestational (type 1 or type 2) diabetes in the mother markedly raises the incidence of congenital heart defects—especially ventricular septal defects, transposition of the great arteries, and truncus arteriosus—in the fetus. Hyperglycaemia during early embryogenesis disrupts cardiac neural crest and endocardial cushion development, leading to these malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankle-foot orthosis",
    "long_answer": "Foot drop due to the distal muscle weakness typical of Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease is most often managed with an ankle-foot orthosis (AFO), which supports the ankle in neutral dorsiflexion, prevents plantar-flexion during swing phase, and thereby improves gait efficiency and safety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSV thymidine kinase",
    "long_answer": "Acyclovir is an inactive guanosine analogue that must be phosphorylated to exert its antiviral effect. In cells infected with Herpes simplex virus, the viral enzyme thymidine kinase catalyzes the first, rate-limiting phosphorylation of acyclovir to acyclovir monophosphate. Subsequent host-cell kinases convert it to the triphosphate form, which then selectively inhibits viral DNA polymerase. Thus, the HSV-encoded thymidine kinase is essential for the drug’s activation and antiviral specificity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ignaz Semmelweis",
    "long_answer": "In the late 1840s, Hungarian obstetrician Ignaz Semmelweis demonstrated that washing hands with a chlorinated lime solution before examining maternity patients lowered puerperal (post-partum) fever mortality from around 10–18 % to below 2 %. His work provided the first compelling evidence that physician-transmitted microbes caused postpartum infections, establishing hand hygiene as a fundamental preventive measure in clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Post-traumatic growth (PTG) is a well-described psychological phenomenon in which individuals report positive changes—such as greater appreciation of life, improved relationships, and new life priorities—after experiencing a highly stressful or life-threatening event. Serious medical illnesses, including different forms of cancer, are among the events most frequently studied in the PTG literature.\n\nEmpirical studies have specifically examined PTG in men treated for testicular cancer. Publications in psycho-oncology journals (e.g., Zwahlen et al., Psycho-Oncology 2010; Carswell & Lloyd, Eur J Cancer Care 2018) document that a notable proportion of testicular-cancer survivors report measurable PTG on standardized tools such as the Post-Traumatic Growth Inventory. Although not every patient experiences these benefits and some simultaneously struggle with psychological distress, the capacity for testicular cancer to act as a catalyst for post-traumatic growth is well established in the scientific literature. Therefore, the statement that testicular cancer can lead to post-traumatic growth is supported by current biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Grey Turner’s sign",
    "long_answer": "Grey Turner’s sign is a bluish-purplish ecchymosis of the flanks produced by blood tracking from a retroperitoneal hemorrhage (e.g., in acute pancreatitis or renal trauma). Because it presents as flank discoloration, it can easily be mistaken for a simple external bruise, yet it actually indicates significant internal bleeding in the retroperitoneal space."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proton magnetic resonance spectroscopy",
    "long_answer": "Proton magnetic resonance spectroscopy (¹H-MRS) can non-invasively measure brain metabolites such as N-acetylaspartate, a marker of neuronal integrity. In autosomal dominant (spinocerebellar) cerebellar ataxias, ¹H-MRS shows reduced NAA levels in the cerebellum and brainstem, providing a sensitive indicator of neuronal deterioration even before marked structural atrophy appears on routine MRI."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI) is an inherited connective-tissue disorder caused most commonly by COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations that weaken type I collagen. The resulting bone fragility leads to recurrent fractures from minimal trauma, which can mimic signs of non-accidental injury, making OI a well-recognized condition that is sometimes mistaken for child abuse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Combarros–Calleja–Leno syndrome is an eponym for the childhood-onset progressive bulbar palsy variant of spinal muscular atrophy (also known as Fazio–Londe disease), a rare motor-neuron disorder that causes progressive weakness of the cranial-nerve motor nuclei. Disorders of this type are diagnosed and managed by neurologists (often paediatric or neuromuscular specialists), making Neurology the medical specialty most likely to treat such a patient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematologist",
    "long_answer": "Purpura results from abnormal bleeding into the skin or mucous membranes, most often due to platelet disorders (e.g., immune thrombocytopenic purpura) or coagulation abnormalities. Evaluation of platelet counts, coagulation factors, bone-marrow function, and immune causes falls within the expertise of a hematologist, the specialist who diagnoses and treats blood and bleeding disorders. Therefore, a hematologist is the appropriate medical specialist for the diagnosis and management of purpura."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guangdong",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies consistently show that Guangdong Province in southern China has the world’s highest incidence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma, reaching 30–80 cases per 100,000 persons in some Cantonese populations. This province’s unusually high rates have made it the classic endemic area used in research on genetic, viral (EBV), and environmental risk factors for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transthoracic echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "Patients with a bicuspid aortic valve are at risk for progressive dilatation of the aortic root and ascending aorta. The standard way to follow these dimensions is with transthoracic echocardiography, a non-invasive ultrasound technique that can be repeated serially to measure aortic diameter and detect enlargement early."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basophils",
    "long_answer": "Basophils are a type of granulocytic white blood cell that store pre-formed histamine in their cytoplasmic granules. During allergic reactions, cross-linking of IgE on basophil surfaces triggers degranulation, releasing histamine and other mediators that cause vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, and other classic allergy symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antihistamines",
    "long_answer": "Allergic conjunctivitis is driven by IgE-mediated histamine release from mast cells. Topical or systemic antihistamines block H1 histamine receptors on conjunctival tissues, reducing itching, redness and tearing, and are therefore a standard, evidence-based treatment for allergic conjunctivitis."
}

{  
  "short_answer": "no",
  "long_answer": "Current evidence-based guidelines for the management of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) do not recommend aspirin as a therapeutic intervention. Standard ARDS care focuses on lung-protective mechanical ventilation strategies, prone positioning, conservative fluid management, and, in selected cases, neuromuscular blockade, corticosteroids, or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation. Although observational studies and small trials have examined aspirin’s antiplatelet and anti-inflammatory properties for ARDS prevention, the largest randomized trial to date (LIPS-A) showed no benefit in preventing ARDS, and no controlled studies demonstrate efficacy in treating established ARDS. Consequently, major societies (e.g., American Thoracic Society, European Society of Intensive Care Medicine, Society of Critical Care Medicine) do not include aspirin in their treatment recommendations for ARDS."  
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noonan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pediatric cardiologist Jacqueline Noonan noticed that many children she treated for congenital pulmonary valve stenosis shared characteristic facial features, short stature, and other anomalies. By linking the repeated finding of pulmonary valve stenosis with this consistent constellation of features, she delineated what is now called Noonan syndrome, first reported in 1963."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the CHD7 gene – the cause of CHARGE syndrome – disturb optic fissure closure during embryogenesis, leading to ocular coloboma. The resulting visual pathway and vestibular abnormalities often manifest clinically as congenital or infantile nystagmus. Thus, the same CHD7 mutation is well documented to produce both coloboma and nystagmus in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lichen myxedematosus",
    "long_answer": "Lichen myxedematosus, also called papular mucinosis, is classified among the cutaneous mucinoses because it is characterized by abnormal dermal deposition of mucin (acidic glycosaminoglycans) produced by fibroblasts, leading to waxy papules and skin thickening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Generally good",
    "long_answer": "With prompt diagnosis and lifelong intramuscular or high-dose oral vitamin B12 replacement, most patients achieve full hematologic recovery, reversal of neurological symptoms if treated early, and a normal life expectancy. The main prognostic considerations are adherence to ongoing B12 therapy and periodic monitoring for associated autoimmune conditions and increased gastric cancer risk, but overall outcome is favorable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinal column-shortening osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "In adults with recurrent tethered cord syndrome after prior intradural detethering, a spinal (vertebral) column-shortening osteotomy can be performed. By removing a wedge of bone—usually at the thoracolumbar junction—and closing the osteotomy, the vertebral column is shortened 1–2 cm, thereby reducing longitudinal tension on the spinal cord without reopening the dura. This indirect untethering has been shown to relieve symptoms and minimizes the risk of further scar-related retethering."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute myeloid leukemia",
    "long_answer": "Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) is a clonal disorder of hematopoietic stem cells characterized by ineffective blood cell production and dysplastic myeloblasts. When these myeloblasts accumulate and acquire additional genetic lesions, MDS can transform into acute myeloid leukemia (AML), the aggressive form of leukemia defined by ≥20% blasts in the bone marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inferiority complex",
    "long_answer": "An inferiority complex is defined by persistent feelings of inadequacy, self-doubt, and perceived inferiority relative to others. These core feelings overlap with the central diagnostic features of avoidant personality disorder, where individuals avoid social interaction largely because they expect criticism or rejection and view themselves as socially inept or inferior. Thus, the psychological condition most commonly linked to avoidant personality disorder through the shared theme of inadequacy is the inferiority complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Down syndrome (trisomy 21) have markedly higher rates of primary immunodeficiency—particularly defects in humoral and cellular immunity that lead to recurrent sinopulmonary and other infections. The syndrome results from an extra copy of chromosome 21, a cytogenetic abnormality that is readily identified by routine karyotyping (47,XX,+21 or 47,XY,+21), making karyotype analysis a definitive diagnostic test."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chondromalacia patellae",
    "long_answer": "A tight iliotibial band can pull the patella laterally, disturbing its tracking in the femoral groove. The resulting abnormal contact stresses lead to softening, irritation, and inflammation of the articular cartilage on the underside of the patella—a condition known as chondromalacia patellae (patellofemoral pain or “runner’s knee”)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clemastine fumarate",
    "long_answer": "Studies in mice in which the facial (VII) nerve was crushed have shown that treatment with the over-the-counter antihistamine clemastine fumarate significantly increases myelin basic protein expression, raises the number and thickness of myelin sheaths around regenerated axons, and improves functional recovery. These findings indicate that clemastine promotes Schwann-cell-mediated remyelination in damaged facial nerves."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium-independent phospholipase A2β",
    "long_answer": "Infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy (INAD) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PLA2G6 gene, which encodes the calcium-independent phospholipase A2β (iPLA2β). Impaired activity of this enzyme disrupts membrane phospholipid remodeling and leads to axonal spheroid formation and neurodegeneration characteristic of INAD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fewer than 200,000",
    "long_answer": "Axial osteosclerosis is classified in the United States as a rare (or ‘orphan’) disease. By federal definition, a rare disease affects fewer than 200,000 people nationwide; therefore, the best available estimate for the number of U.S. individuals with axial osteosclerosis is under this threshold."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin fibroblasts",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sulfatase deficiency is confirmed by measuring the activities of all sulfatase enzymes in cultured skin fibroblasts obtained from a patient’s skin biopsy. These fibroblasts show uniformly low activity of every sulfatase, providing the biochemical hallmark necessary for diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Small blood vessels",
    "long_answer": "Henoch-Schönlein purpura is an IgA-mediated leukocytoclastic vasculitis that primarily targets the body’s small blood vessels—especially capillaries, venules, and small arterioles—leading to the characteristic palpable purpura, gastrointestinal symptoms, and glomerulonephritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lowers HDL cholesterol",
    "long_answer": "Clinical and metabolic studies demonstrate that chronic supplementation with high doses of zinc (≈50 mg elemental zinc/day or more) produces a sustained reduction in circulating high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol levels, likely through alterations in lipoprotein metabolism associated with zinc-induced copper deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehrlichiosis",
    "long_answer": "In 2011 Minnesota health officials and the CDC documented human cases of ehrlichiosis caused by a newly recognized Ehrlichia muris–like bacterium transmitted by Ixodes scapularis (black-legged) ticks. Ehrlichiosis is a tick-borne bacterial illness in which Ehrlichia spp. infect leukocytes, leading to fever, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and elevated liver enzymes; the 2011 report marked the first confirmed occurrence of this bacterial tick-borne disease in Minnesota."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naxos disease",
    "long_answer": "Naxos disease (Naxos syndrome) is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the plakoglobin (JUP) gene. Patients are born with tightly curled “woolly” hair and, later in childhood or adolescence, develop arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy; many also exhibit diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma. The characteristic combination of woolly hair and cardiomyopathy is therefore diagnostic of Naxos disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "A transthoracic (or transesophageal) echocardiogram uses ultrasound to create real-time images of the heart and Doppler to assess blood flow. This non-invasive test visualizes the interventricular septum, reveals any discontinuity or hole, and shows left-to-right shunting, making it the standard method for diagnosing a ventricular septal defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infantile acid lipase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LIPA gene, which encodes lysosomal acid lipase, abolish enzymatic activity and lead to the severe infantile form of lysosomal acid lipase deficiency known as infantile acid lipase deficiency (also called Wolman disease). The defective enzyme prevents normal hydrolysis of cholesteryl esters and triglycerides within lysosomes, causing massive lipid accumulation in the liver, spleen, and other tissues and resulting in failure to thrive, hepatosplenomegaly, and early mortality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T. pallidum subsp. endemicum",
    "long_answer": "Treponema pallidum subsp. endemicum is the agent of endemic syphilis (bejel), which is usually spread non-sexually through close personal contact or shared utensils. Because it is genetically and antigenically almost identical to T. pallidum subsp. pallidum, the organism can also be transmitted by sexual intercourse and produce the clinical picture of venereal syphilis, making it the only primarily nonvenereal Treponema that may also cause classical syphilis when spread sexually."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testicular biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Sertoli cell-only syndrome is characterized by seminiferous tubules that lack germ cells and contain only Sertoli cells. Confirmation therefore requires direct histologic inspection of testicular tissue, which is obtained through a testicular biopsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholera",
    "long_answer": "Vibrio cholerae is the Gram-negative, comma-shaped bacterium that produces cholera toxin, leading to the acute diarrheal illness known as cholera. Infection of the small intestine causes profuse watery diarrhea ('rice-water stools'), rapid dehydration, and electrolyte loss, the hallmark clinical features of cholera."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SRY",
    "long_answer": "The SRY (Sex-determining Region Y) gene, located on the short arm of the Y chromosome, encodes a transcription factor that triggers the cascade of testis formation and subsequent male sexual differentiation. Loss-of-function or translocation of SRY results in sex reversal, underscoring its pivotal role in male development. Web pages describing SRY, like any other URL, can be preserved through the Internet Archive’s Wayback Machine, which explains the reference to it being “archived.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibial aplasia",
    "long_answer": "Hecht-Scott syndrome is a hereditary limb-malformation disorder whose cardinal feature is complete or partial absence of the tibia (tibial aplasia/hemimelia), often accompanied by pre-axial polydactyly and triphalangeal thumbs. Thus, the specific type of aplasia associated with the syndrome is tibial aplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gangrene",
    "long_answer": "In septicemic plague, Yersinia pestis circulates in the bloodstream, triggering disseminated intravascular coagulation and widespread thrombi. The compromised blood flow causes ischemic necrosis of peripheral tissues, leading to blackened, dead skin and digits—clinically recognized as gangrene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ghrelin",
    "long_answer": "People with Prader–Willi syndrome exhibit markedly elevated circulating levels of the orexigenic (appetite-stimulating) hormone ghrelin. This abnormal over-production of ghrelin correlates with the characteristic hyperphagia and insatiable appetite seen in the disorder, implicating ghrelin dysregulation as a key endocrine contributor to the syndrome’s severe drive to eat."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COVID-19-associated mucormycosis",
    "long_answer": "The surge of mucormycosis cases observed in India amid the COVID-19 pandemic is referred to as COVID-19-associated mucormycosis (CAM). This term denotes mucormycosis occurring in patients with recent or concurrent SARS-CoV-2 infection, often linked to factors such as steroid therapy, uncontrolled diabetes and immune dysregulation, leading to the sharp rise in infections during the pandemic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTC gene sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency is caused mainly by single-nucleotide variants or small insertions/deletions in the OTC gene. Direct sequencing of all coding exons and exon–intron boundaries (classically by Sanger sequencing or targeted NGS) detects about 80-90 % of pathogenic variants and is therefore the preferred first-line molecular test for diagnosing this X-linked urea-cycle disorder; deletion/duplication analysis is reserved for cases negative on sequencing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Tay-Sachs disease is caused by pathogenic variants in the HEXA gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner: an affected individual must receive a defective allele from each parent. Carriers, who possess only one mutated allele, are typically asymptomatic. Therefore the statement that Tay-Sachs disease is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DKC1",
    "long_answer": "The X-linked form of dyskeratosis congenita is caused by mutations in the DKC1 gene, located on chromosome Xq28. DKC1 encodes dyskerin, a nucleolar protein essential for telomerase RNA stability and ribosomal RNA modification; loss-of-function variants disrupt telomere maintenance, producing the characteristic mucocutaneous changes and bone-marrow failure of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Viljoen–Kallis–Voges syndrome is an extremely rare, presumably hereditary congenital syndrome involving skeletal abnormalities, growth impairment, and distinctive craniofacial features. Diagnosis requires recognition of a characteristic pattern of malformations and, when available, molecular genetic confirmation; long-term care focuses on genetic counselling and coordinated management of multisystem findings. These activities fall squarely within the expertise of medical (clinical) geneticists, making Medical Genetics the most relevant specialty for diagnosing and treating this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX10 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the neural-crest transcription factor SOX10 cause Waardenburg syndrome (especially type 4). Because SOX10 is required for proper development and function of enteric ganglion cells, the same mutation can impair gastrointestinal motility and present clinically as chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction, even in the absence of complete aganglionosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corneal clouding",
    "long_answer": "De Barsy syndrome (progeroid cutis laxa type III) is characterized by loose, inelastic skin and multiple systemic anomalies; among its distinctive ocular findings is bilateral corneal clouding (corneal opacification). This stromal opacity arises early in life and is consistently reported in patients, making corneal clouding a recognized symptom of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spastic paraplegia type 47",
    "long_answer": "Spastic paraplegia type 47 (SPG47) is caused by biallelic mutations in the AP4B1 gene. Affected individuals show profound developmental delay/severe intellectual disability from infancy and later develop progressive hereditary spastic paraplegia. Minor dysmorphic features frequently reported include fifth-finger clinodactyly, supporting SPG47 as the disorder fitting the described symptom constellation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital cutis laxa",
    "long_answer": "Berk–Tabatznik syndrome is an early description of the autosomal-recessive cutis laxa spectrum. Affected individuals present at birth with markedly loose, inelastic, redundant skin that hangs in folds (congenital cutis laxa). This lax connective tissue often leads to associated problems such as hernias and early-onset pulmonary emphysema, but the cardinal symptom that defines the syndrome is the generalized cutis laxa itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melanocytes",
    "long_answer": "The brown coloration of Becker’s nevus is caused by increased melanin production by epidermal melanocytes; these pigment-producing cells become hyperactive (though not necessarily increased in number), leading to the characteristic hyperpigmentation of the lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Aortic dissections in otherwise young, healthy adults are classically linked to the connective-tissue disorder Marfan syndrome, caused by pathogenic FBN1 variants that weaken the aortic media. Reports on Jonathan Larson’s death note that his tall, lanky habitus and sudden type-A dissection were compatible with previously undiagnosed Marfan syndrome, making it the most likely genetic contributor to his fatal aortic rupture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MHC class II deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome type II is caused by mutations in trans-acting regulators of MHC class II genes, so patients fail to express MHC class II molecules on antigen-presenting cells. Without MHC class II, CD4⁺ helper T cells cannot be positively selected in the thymus or activated in the periphery, leading to profoundly reduced helper T-cell numbers and impaired B-cell antibody responses. This combined cellular and humoral immunodeficiency makes affected individuals highly susceptible to recurrent, severe infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)",
    "long_answer": "After bilateral oophorectomy, loss of ovarian estrogen feedback causes serum FSH to rise to post-menopausal levels (>40 IU/L). If residual ovarian tissue is still secreting estrogen, FSH is inappropriately low or normal. Therefore, a relatively low serum FSH level compared with the expected high post-oophorectomy value is a biochemical marker pointing to ovarian remnant syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly-ectodermal dysplasia-clefting (EEC) syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor. TP63 regulates ectodermal and craniofacial development; its dysfunction leads to abnormalities of structures derived from surface ectoderm, including characteristic defects of the lower eyelids (e.g., punctal atresia, eyelid colobomas) observed in patients with EEC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET and NF2",
    "long_answer": "Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is produced by germ-line activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene (chromosome 10q11.2). Neurofibromatosis type 2, in contrast, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene that encodes merlin/schwannomin (chromosome 22q12). Hence the disorders are associated with mutations of the RET gene (MEN 2) and the NF2 gene (NF 2)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fahrenheit",
    "long_answer": "In the United States, safe-cooking recommendations for destroying Trichinella spiralis larvae are expressed in degrees Fahrenheit; using this scale (e.g., cooking pork to at least 145 °F with a 3-minute rest) ensures the internal temperature reaches levels proven to inactivate the parasite and prevent trichinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic cellulitis (Wells syndrome) is an inflammatory skin disorder characterized by erythematous, edematous plaques with prominent eosinophilic infiltration. Because it primarily manifests in the skin and requires clinical examination, skin biopsy, and topical or systemic therapies common to cutaneous diseases, it is most commonly diagnosed and managed by dermatologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysolecithin-mediated hemolysis",
    "long_answer": "In Zieve’s syndrome, abrupt mobilization of triglycerides produces lysolecithin and other free lipids that incorporate into red-cell membranes, altering their cholesterol-to-phospholipid ratio. This lipid-induced membrane instability makes erythrocytes mechanically fragile and complements their premature destruction, accounting for the characteristic hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Purpura commonly arises when the circulating platelet count is reduced (thrombocytopenia); the lack of adequate platelets impairs primary haemostasis, allowing capillary bleeding and the characteristic purple skin lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAQ",
    "long_answer": "Both isolated port-wine stains and the neurocutaneous disorder Sturge–Weber syndrome share the same post-zygotic activating mutation—most often c.548G>A (p.Arg183Gln)—in the GNAQ gene, which encodes the Gαq signaling protein. This somatic mosaic GNAQ mutation drives abnormal vascular development in skin and leptomeninges, explaining its presence in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHF6",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-disability disorder. Multiple affected families have been shown to carry loss-of-function mutations in the PHF6 gene, which encodes a plant-homeodomain zinc-finger protein located on Xq26. Disrupting PHF6 consistently produces the clinical picture originally described as Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome, establishing PHF6 mutation as its causal genetic defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Working memory",
    "long_answer": "Children and adults with spina bifida—especially those who have hydrocephalus treated with a cerebrospinal-fluid shunt—consistently show deficits in working memory. Neuropsychological studies demonstrate reduced capacity to hold and manipulate information over short intervals, reflecting disruption of fronto-parietal networks affected by ventricular enlargement and corpus callosum maldevelopment associated with shunted hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STK11 (LKB1)",
    "long_answer": "Peutz–Jeghers syndrome is classically caused by germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the STK11 (also called LKB1) gene, a tumor-suppressor serine/threonine kinase located on chromosome 19p13.3. Inactivation of STK11 disrupts cell-polarity and growth-control pathways, leading to the characteristic hamartomatous polyps throughout the gastrointestinal tract and mucocutaneous pigmentation seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Yemenite deaf-blind hypopigmentation syndrome is produced by a loss-of-function mutation in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene. Homozygous nonsense substitutions in MITF were identified in affected Yemenite Jewish families, and MITF is known to control melanocyte development in skin, eye and inner ear; its disruption therefore leads simultaneously to generalized hypopigmentation, congenital sensorineural deafness and severe visual impairment characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "the meninges",
    "long_answer": "Meningeal leukemia refers to leukemic cell infiltration of the meninges—the protective membranes (pia and arachnoid mater) surrounding the brain and spinal cord—so the affected anatomical site is the meninges of the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism is a metabolic disorder in which reduced secretion of parathyroid hormone leads to chronic hypocalcaemia. The resulting disturbances in calcium and phosphate homeostasis promote lens opacification, so infants born with hypoparathyroidism frequently present with congenital cataracts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome is a chromosomal disorder characterized by ovarian insufficiency and short stature. Management commonly requires growth hormone therapy, induction of puberty, and lifelong sex-steroid and metabolic monitoring, all of which are overseen by endocrinologists (especially pediatric endocrinology). Hence, the primary medical specialty involved in treating Turner syndrome is endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAT4",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic biallelic mutations in the FAT4 gene, which encodes a large protocadherin involved in planar cell polarity and lymphangiogenesis, have been documented in patients with Hennekam lymphangiectasia-lymphedema syndrome type 2 as well as in van Maldergem syndrome. Thus, alterations in FAT4 are a shared genetic cause of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tarsorrhaphy",
    "long_answer": "Patients with familial dysautonomia have reduced corneal sensation and inadequate tear production, predisposing them to exposure keratopathy and corneal ulceration. Surgically sewing part of the upper and lower eyelids together (tarsorrhaphy) narrows the palpebral fissure, decreasing corneal exposure and helping to protect the ocular surface in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor necrosis factor-α inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Biologic drugs that block TNF-α (such as etanercept, infliximab and adalimumab) are well documented to precipitate immune-mediated demyelinating events—optic neuritis, transverse myelitis, or multiple-sclerosis-like lesions—because TNF-α signalling is involved in myelin maintenance and repair; inhibiting it can trigger or unmask demyelination in the central and peripheral nervous systems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAGE3",
    "long_answer": "Among the genes known to cause Galloway–Mowat syndrome (GAMOS), only LAGE3 is located on the X chromosome (X-linked) and displays X-linked recessive inheritance (designated GAMOS2). Pathogenic variants in LAGE3, a component of the KEOPS complex, lead to the characteristic nephrotic syndrome and microcephaly of GAMOS, confirming it as the X-linked gene associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hair-shaft light microscopy",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant trait known as pili annulati (ringed hair) produces periodic air-filled cavities in the hair cortex. When an extracted hair is examined with incident or polarized light microscopy, these cavities alternately scatter and transmit light, giving a characteristic pattern of light and dark bands along the shaft and confirming the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tenosynovitis",
    "long_answer": "Inflammatory tenosynovitis causes edema and thickening of the flexor-tendon synovial sheaths that occupy the narrow tarsal tunnel. The resulting volumetric increase elevates intracompartmental pressure beneath the flexor retinaculum, mechanically compressing the posterior tibial nerve and its branches, thereby precipitating tarsal tunnel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nausea",
    "long_answer": "Nausea is frequently reported in pancreatic disorders such as acute or chronic pancreatitis, where inflammation of the pancreas irritates visceral afferents and triggers the vomiting centre. The same symptom can occur during hypoglycemia in diabetics because falling blood glucose activates autonomic (sympathoadrenal) responses that include nausea. Thus, nausea is a shared symptom between pancreatic disease and hypoglycemic episodes in diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brevetoxin",
    "long_answer": "Brevetoxins are lipid-soluble polyether neurotoxins produced by the dinoflagellate Karenia brevis that are responsible for neurotoxic shellfish poisoning (NSP). Their high lipophilicity allows them to readily traverse cellular membranes, including the blood-brain barrier, leading to central nervous system effects such as tingling, ataxia and reversal of hot-cold sensation observed in NSP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SBDS",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SBDS gene are the primary cause of Shwachman–Diamond syndrome. The SBDS protein participates in late‐stage maturation and release of the 60S ribosomal subunit, promoting its joining with the 40S subunit to form functional 80S ribosomes. Thus, SBDS links the clinical features of Shwachman–Diamond syndrome to defective ribosome assembly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transitional epithelium",
    "long_answer": "Uropathogenic Escherichia coli (UPEC) cause most urinary tract infections by attaching to and invading the urothelium—the specialized transitional epithelium that lines the bladder, ureters and renal pelvis. UPEC use type-1 and P fimbriae to bind uroplakin receptors on these transitional epithelial cells, initiating infection and inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CFTR gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the cystic fibrosis trans-membrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene—particularly the ΔF508 variant—are over-represented in asthmatic patients who develop allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. Impaired CFTR function hampers mucus hydration and clearance, favoring persistent Aspergillus colonization and exaggerated IgE-mediated immune responses, thereby increasing ABPA risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EZH2",
    "long_answer": "EZH2 is a key myelodysplastic-syndrome–associated gene that resides on chromosome 7q36.1. It encodes the catalytic histone-methyltransferase component of the Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2). Loss-of-function or missense mutations in EZH2 are frequent in myelodysplastic syndromes and contribute to defective epigenetic regulation and impaired hematopoietic differentiation, underscoring its pathogenic role in MDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NR0B1 (DAX1)",
    "long_answer": "X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita is caused by loss of the NR0B1 (DAX1) gene on Xp21. In many cases this deletion extends into the adjacent glycerol-kinase gene; the combined (contiguous) deletion produces glycerol-kinase deficiency, leading to marked hyperglycerolemia, together with adrenal hypoplasia. Hence, deletion of the NR0B1 (DAX1) region is characteristically associated with the simultaneous presence of hyperglycerolemia and X-linked adrenal hypoplasia congenita."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitiligo",
    "long_answer": "Vitiligo, an autoimmune disease in which cytotoxic immunity targets and destroys melanocytes, is the best-documented autoimmune condition found more often in patients who have congenital melanocytic nevi. The same immune response that produces depigmented patches in vitiligo can attack the melanocytes within a congenital nevus (halo or regressive change), so the occurrence of congenital melanocytic nevi is clinically associated with vitiligo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Variable expressivity",
    "long_answer": "In Oculodentodigital dysplasia, all affected individuals carry a pathogenic mutation (typically in the GJA1 gene encoding connexin-43), yet the range and severity of ocular, dental and digital abnormalities differ widely from person to person. This difference is due to variable expressivity—the phenomenon in which the same pathogenic allele produces a spectrum of clinical manifestations of differing severity among individuals who inherit it."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkaline phosphatase",
    "long_answer": "Rotor syndrome causes chronic conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia without true cholestasis, so hepatocyte-released or cholestatic enzymes remain within the reference range. In particular, serum alkaline phosphatase activity, which rises in obstructive or cholestatic liver disease, is characteristically normal in patients with Rotor syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies",
    "long_answer": "Hashimoto's encephalopathy—also called steroid-responsive encephalopathy associated with autoimmune thyroiditis—is characteristically linked to very high serum titers of anti-thyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO, formerly ‘antimicrosomal’) antibodies. The detection of these autoantibodies, common in Hashimoto’s thyroiditis, is a major laboratory clue used to support the diagnosis of this encephalopathic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLN3 gene",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile Batten disease, formally termed juvenile neuronal ceroid-lipofuscinosis (JNCL), is most commonly caused by mutations in the CLN3 gene located on chromosome 16p12.1. Over 80 % of affected individuals carry the characteristic 1-kilobase deletion in CLN3, making this gene the primary genetic determinant of the juvenile form of Batten disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle biopsy",
    "long_answer": "The definitive diagnosis of congenital myopathies relies on histopathological examination of a skeletal muscle biopsy, which reveals characteristic structural abnormalities (e.g., nemaline rods, central cores, central nuclei) that distinguish the various subtypes. While genetic tests can confirm the underlying mutation, biopsy-based morphology remains the primary diagnostic method."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aerosolized rodent excreta",
    "long_answer": "Junín virus is maintained in the drylands vesper mouse (Calomys musculinus). Humans acquire Argentine hemorrhagic fever primarily by inhaling or otherwise coming into contact with aerosolized urine, feces, or saliva from these infected rodents; this contaminated rodent excreta is the main route of human infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Dercum’s disease (adiposis dolorosa) sometimes receive short courses of the systemic corticosteroid prednisone. Because prednisone suppresses inflammation and modulates immune-mediated pain pathways, a brief regimen can lessen the chronic pain associated with the painful lipomatous nodules. The benefit is usually temporary, and prolonged use is avoided due to well-known corticosteroid side-effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)",
    "long_answer": "Cycloserine’s neuro-toxicity is linked to functional pyridoxine deficiency, which impairs synthesis of the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA and predisposes to seizures. Patients with sideroblastic anemia are often pyridoxine-deficient, so adding pharmacologic doses of vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) replenishes the cofactor, restores GABA production, and markedly lowers the incidence of cycloserine-induced convulsions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "General anesthesia",
    "long_answer": "Biopsy of a suspected laryngeal carcinoma is usually performed through direct or microlaryngoscopy, which requires complete airway control and patient immobility. Therefore the procedure is typically carried out under general anesthesia, allowing endotracheal intubation and a secure airway while the surgeon obtains the tissue specimen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary cilium",
    "long_answer": "Joubert syndrome belongs to the group of human ciliopathies. Mutations in genes that encode proteins of the primary (non-motile) cilium and its basal body impair ciliary assembly and signaling, leading to the characteristic neurodevelopmental, renal and retinal defects seen in Joubert syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteopathia striata",
    "long_answer": "The rare bone dysplasia osteopathia striata (also called Voorhoeve disease) is typified radiographically by multiple, fine, longitudinal linear striations running through the metaphyses and diaphyses of long or flat bones such as the femur, tibia, pelvis, and scapula. These symmetric, sclerotic striations are pathognomonic for the condition and usually discovered incidentally on plain X-rays, distinguishing it from other skeletal disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLT4 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the FLT4 gene, which encodes the lymphatic vascular endothelial growth factor receptor-3 (VEGFR-3), impair normal lymphatic vessel development. This congenital defect (seen in Milroy disease) results in lymphedema present from birth due to malformed or absent lymphatic channels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "The pancreas contains two distinct functional components: an extensive exocrine portion (acinar and ductal cells) that synthesizes and secretes digestive enzymes and bicarbonate into the pancreatic duct, which empties into the duodenum, and a smaller endocrine portion (islets of Langerhans) that releases hormones such as insulin and glucagon directly into the bloodstream. Because the exocrine part releases its secretions through a duct to an epithelial surface, it meets the classic definition of an exocrine gland. Therefore, on the basis of its secretion via ducts, the pancreas is indeed classified—at least in part—as an exocrine gland, even though it also has an endocrine function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nutritionist",
    "long_answer": "Because functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is commonly triggered by inadequate energy intake and excessive exercise, restoring normal menstrual function often requires increasing caloric intake and normalizing body weight. A nutritionist (dietitian) is therefore routinely involved to design an appropriate meal plan and monitor nutritional rehabilitation as part of the multidisciplinary treatment approach."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Otoacoustic emissions are produced by functioning outer hair cells in the cochlea. When these cells are damaged—as in sensorineural hearing loss—no emissions are generated or measurable. Thus, the absence of otoacoustic emissions is most commonly linked to cochlear sensorineural hearing loss rather than conductive or purely neural disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP1B1",
    "long_answer": "Primary congenital glaucoma is most frequently caused by loss-of-function mutations in the cytochrome P450 family gene CYP1B1. Defects in CYP1B1 disrupt metabolism of signaling molecules essential for anterior chamber and trabecular meshwork development, leading to the elevated intraocular pressure characteristic of primary congenital glaucoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary veins",
    "long_answer": "In transposition of the great arteries—where the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle—the lungs still oxygenate blood and return it to the heart through the four pulmonary veins that empty into the left atrium. Thus, the pulmonary veins are the vessels that deliver oxygen-rich blood to the heart in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inherited enzyme deficiencies",
    "long_answer": "Metabolic myopathies arise chiefly from germ-line mutations that impair enzymes required for muscle energy production—such as those in glycogen breakdown, fatty-acid β-oxidation, or mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. These inborn errors of metabolism limit ATP generation in skeletal muscle, producing the characteristic exercise intolerance, cramps, and weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome (also called Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome) is a multisystem condition that frequently includes urogenital anomalies such as hypospadias in affected males. The syndrome was first described in 1981 by Niikawa and colleagues in a Japanese family/patient series, making its initial recognition a Japanese discovery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈25 minutes",
    "long_answer": "Clinical stopwatch-based studies (e.g., Waldinger et al., 2005) show that men diagnosed with delayed ejaculation have a mean intravaginal ejaculation latency time of about 25 minutes—roughly five times longer than the general male median IELT (~5 minutes)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myoglobinuria",
    "long_answer": "Cocaine use can precipitate rhabdomyolysis, a destructive breakdown of skeletal muscle cells. The damaged fibers release large amounts of myoglobin into the circulation; the pigment is then filtered by the kidneys and appears in the urine, producing the condition known as myoglobinuria, which can further lead to acute renal failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyaline cartilage",
    "long_answer": "Chondromas are benign cartilaginous tumors composed of mature hyaline cartilage; thus, hyaline cartilage is the tissue involved in their formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent form of brachydactyly in the general population is brachydactyly type D, characterised by a short, broad distal phalanx of the thumb. Heterozygous mutations—typically poly-alanine expansions or missense changes—in the HOXD13 homeobox gene disrupt distal limb patterning during embryogenesis, leading to this common brachydactyly phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic anhydrase II",
    "long_answer": "Osteoclasts rely on carbonic anhydrase II to generate carbonic acid and release protons that dissolve the mineralized bone matrix. Inherited deficiency of carbonic anhydrase II impairs this acidification step, preventing normal bone resorption and producing the dense, brittle bones characteristic of osteopetrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythema migrans",
    "long_answer": "Geographic tongue (benign migratory glossitis) is classically associated with erythema migrans, a mucocutaneous condition characterized by erythema—redness produced by increased blood flow in superficial capillaries—appearing as migrating patches on the tongue or other oral mucosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Biliary atresia",
    "long_answer": "Biliary atresia—a progressive, idiopathic obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts leading to cholestasis and cirrhosis—is responsible for roughly 40–50 % of pediatric liver transplants in the United States, making it the single most common indication for liver transplantation in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11q deletion",
    "long_answer": "Paris–Trousseau syndrome occurs in patients who have a terminal deletion of the long arm of chromosome 11 (11q), as seen in Jacobsen syndrome. Loss of genes in the 11q23-qter region, including the hematopoietic transcription factor FLI1, underlies the characteristic congenital thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytokine release syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A major life-threatening toxicity of CD19-directed CAR-T cell therapy for diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is cytokine release syndrome (CRS). Following rapid in-vivo expansion of the infused CAR-T cells, massive release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6, IFN-γ and TNF-α can trigger high fever, hypotension, capillary leak, and multi-organ dysfunction. CRS frequently requires intensive monitoring and treatment with tocilizumab or corticosteroids to control the hyperinflammatory response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC9",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the ABCC9 gene, which encodes the SUR2 regulatory subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, are the well-established cause of Cantú syndrome (hypertrichotic osteochondrodysplasia). These same ABCC9 variants lead to one of the hallmark features of the disorder—generalized hypertrichosis—thereby linking a single gene mutation to both Cantú syndrome and hypertrichosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Prolactin, produced by the anterior pituitary, is essential for initiating and maintaining milk production after childbirth. In hypopituitarism (e.g., postpartum Sheehan syndrome), loss of prolactin secretion prevents stimulation of the mammary glands, resulting in agalactorrhea and an inability to breastfeed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antiepileptic (anticonvulsant) drugs",
    "long_answer": "Northern epilepsy syndrome (CLN8-related progressive epilepsy with mental retardation) manifests with generalized tonic-clonic seizures that are effectively managed with standard antiepileptic—or anticonvulsant—medications such as carbamazepine, valproate or other AEDs. Therefore, the therapeutic class employed to control these seizures is antiepileptic (anticonvulsant) drugs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Aldolase A is essential for glycolysis in erythrocytes. When the enzyme is deficient, red blood cells cannot generate enough ATP, become fragile, and are destroyed prematurely (hemolysis). The resulting excessive breakdown of red blood cells leads to a reduced circulating red-cell mass, producing hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypereosinophilic syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hypereosinophilic syndrome (HES) is defined by a persistently elevated peripheral‐blood eosinophil count (typically >1.5 × 10⁹/L for ≥6 months) associated with tissue or organ involvement. Pulmonary infiltration by eosinophils is common in HES and often manifests clinically as a persistent, non-productive (dry) cough, wheeze, or dyspnea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Compensatory hypertrophy",
    "long_answer": "In hydrocephalus, the ventricular system is distended and cerebrospinal-fluid dynamics are altered. The choroid plexus enlarges to compensate for the changed functional demand, a classic example of compensatory hypertrophy—hypertrophic enlargement of the remaining or still-functional tissue in response to altered conditions rather than true proliferation of new cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phototherapy",
    "long_answer": "In Crigler–Najjar syndrome the hepatic enzyme UDP-glucuronosyl-transferase is absent or greatly reduced, so bilirubin cannot be conjugated and accumulates in the unconjugated form. Intensive blue-light phototherapy is used because light converts unconjugated bilirubin in the skin into water-soluble photo-isomers that can be excreted without hepatic conjugation, thereby lowering serum bilirubin levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "urokinase-type plasminogen activator",
    "long_answer": "In Quebec platelet disorder a genetic defect causes megakaryocytes and platelets to greatly overproduce urokinase-type plasminogen activator (u-PA). The excess u-PA is stored in platelet α-granules and released at sites of injury, excessively converting plasminogen to plasmin and thereby accelerating fibrinolysis and breakdown of formed clots."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bilateral sagittal split osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "Mandibular prognathism—a protrusive lower jaw—is most commonly corrected by bilateral sagittal split osteotomy (BSSO). In this orthognathic procedure the mandibular rami are split sagittally, allowing the distal (tooth-bearing) segment to be set back and fixed in a more retruded position, thereby normalizing jaw relation and occlusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pituitary stalk interruption syndrome is a congenital disorder in which the pituitary stalk is absent or truncated. Because the stalk normally conveys hypothalamic releasing and inhibitory factors, its disruption abolishes the feedback mechanisms that regulate anterior-pituitary hormone secretion, leading to multiple pituitary hormone deficiencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IGF2",
    "long_answer": "Overexpression of the insulin-like growth factor 2 (IGF2) gene is the common molecular event that produces the generalized overgrowth seen in both syndromes. In Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, imprinting defects or paternal uniparental disomy at 11p15 lead to biallelic expression of IGF2. In Sotos syndrome, loss-of-function of the NSD1 histone-methyltransferase abolishes normal repression of IGF2. The resulting IGF2 excess drives the pre- and post-natal somatic overgrowth characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PSAT1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in PSAT1 (phosphoserine aminotransferase-1), an enzyme of the L-serine biosynthesis pathway, are the established genetic cause of Neu–Laxova syndrome type 2. Independent case reports have shown that the same PSAT1 mutations can present neonatally with the dramatic plate-like hyperkeratosis and ectropion typical of Harlequin-type ichthyosis. Thus, pathogenic variants in PSAT1 are molecularly linked to both Harlequin-type ichthyosis and Neu–Laxova syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto’s thyroiditis",
    "long_answer": "Hashimoto’s thyroiditis is an autoimmune disorder in which autoreactive T- and B-cells attack thyroid tissue, progressively destroying thyroid follicles. In populations with adequate iodine intake it is the leading cause of primary hypothyroidism. The disease is far more common in women, and immuno-endocrine research shows that elevated or unopposed estrogen levels can enhance Th2-mediated autoimmune responses, explaining its reported association with so-called “estrogen dominance.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT)",
    "long_answer": "Analysis of cell-free fetal DNA in maternal blood—commonly called non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT)—can screen for fetal aneuploidies such as trisomy 16 in the first trimester. By sequencing or targeted counting of chromosome-specific fragments circulating in maternal plasma, NIPT detects the relative over-representation of chromosome 16, allowing identification of trisomy 16 risk before resorting to invasive diagnostic procedures like chorionic villus sampling or amniocentesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bladder exstrophy",
    "long_answer": "Bladder exstrophy is a rare congenital anomaly in which the bladder mucosa is chronically exposed. The exposed urothelium frequently develops cystitis glandularis (and cystitis cystica); this metaplastic change is considered a precursor lesion that can progress to urinary-bladder adenocarcinoma. Thus, bladder exstrophy is the classic rare disease associated with cystitis glandularis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palliative care",
    "long_answer": "Fatal familial insomnia is an untreatable prion disease; no disease-modifying therapy exists. Clinical guidelines therefore recommend comprehensive palliative care—supportive measures that relieve insomnia, autonomic symptoms, pain, and psychological distress—so as to maximize comfort and overall quality of life for affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gemfibrozil",
    "long_answer": "Gemfibrozil, a fibric-acid derivative, activates PPAR-α to up-regulate lipoprotein lipase, thereby increasing clearance of very-low-density lipoproteins and lowering plasma triglyceride levels. It is routinely used to treat familial hypertriglyceridemia, a disorder characterized by elevated VLDL and triglycerides."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysinuric protein intolerance",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC7A7 gene disrupt the y+LAT-1 amino-acid transporter, causing the inherited metabolic disorder lysinuric protein intolerance (LPI). Patients with LPI develop defective transport of cationic amino acids, leading to chronic nutritional imbalance, impaired bone mineralization, and a high incidence of osteopenia or osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the type I collagen genes COL1A1 or COL1A2 disrupt normal collagen synthesis, weakening bone matrix. This heritable connective-tissue disorder, called osteogenesis imperfecta, often presents in childhood with low bone mass, recurrent fractures, and may mimic or manifest as juvenile osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatal hypoglycemia",
    "long_answer": "Severely low blood glucose in a newborn deprives the brain of its only significant energy substrate. When unrecognized, this metabolic emergency rapidly produces neurological depression, seizures, and can progress to deep coma and death. Routine screening and prompt intravenous glucose therapy are therefore critical immediately after birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pentoxifylline",
    "long_answer": "Pentoxifylline is a methylxanthine derivative prescribed to improve microcirculatory blood flow and reduce intermittent claudication (muscle pain) in peripheral artery disease. Although generally well-tolerated, case reports have documented drug-induced aseptic meningitis as a rare adverse effect of pentoxifylline therapy, establishing the link between this PAD medication and aseptic meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11p15 imprinting region",
    "long_answer": "Mosaic or germ-line abnormalities in the imprinting control domains on chromosome 11p15—containing the IGF2 and H19 genes—are a common molecular basis for both isolated hemihyperplasia and the over-growth disorder Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HCN4 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the HCN4 gene, which encodes the hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated channel 4 responsible for the sino-atrial node’s pacemaker ‘funny’ current (If), produce familial sinus node dysfunction (sick-sinus syndrome). The resulting profound bradycardia and sinus pauses predispose to atrial standstill and thrombo-embolism, markedly increasing the risk of cerebrovascular events such as transient ischemic attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBCK syndrome",
    "long_answer": "This rare autosomal-recessive neurodevelopmental disorder is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TBCK gene. It was first reported as a founder condition in Puerto Rican children who presented with profound infantile hypotonia, global developmental delay, seizures and progressive cerebral atrophy. Because of the initial cluster in Puerto Rico, the disorder is often referred to as the Puerto Rican infantile hypotonia syndrome, now formally recognised as TBCK-related encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic papular-plaque eruption",
    "long_answer": "Jessner lymphocytic infiltrate of the skin (Jessner-Kanof disease) classically presents as a benign, persistent eruption of erythematous papules and plaques—most often on the face, neck, or upper trunk. Histology shows a dense perivascular and periadnexal T-cell infiltrate, but clinically the disease is recognized by its chronic, non-scarring papular-plaque skin eruption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vesicoureteral reflux",
    "long_answer": "Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) is frequently present in children with congenital renal hypoplasia/dysplasia. The retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the ureters and kidneys facilitates bacterial ascent, predisposing patients to recurrent urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis. Repeated infections and intrarenal reflux produce progressive renal scarring (reflux nephropathy), which can ultimately progress to chronic kidney disease and end-stage renal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "Saal-Bulas syndrome is a rare congenital malformation syndrome whose cardinal neuroanatomical feature is complete agenesis of the corpus callosum. The corpus callosum, the major commissural tract connecting the cerebral hemispheres, fails to develop in affected individuals, and this absence is considered a defining characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Craniectomy",
    "long_answer": "Trigonocephaly is a form of craniosynostosis in which the metopic suture fuses prematurely. Surgical treatment commonly entails a craniectomy—removal of the fused strip of skull (often termed a strip craniectomy or cranial vault remodeling). By permanently removing that portion of bone, space is created so the underlying brain can expand normally, preventing further deformity and relieving intracranial constriction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Compartment syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Compartment syndrome occurs when increased pressure within a closed fascial compartment compromises blood flow and nerve function. The ischemia produces severe, disproportionate pain that is worsened by passive stretch, leading patients to guard the limb and develop a markedly limited range of motion. Progressive vascular and neural compression causes neurovascular symptoms such as paresthesias, pallor, decreased pulses, and eventual motor weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP7A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked copper-transporting ATPase gene ATP7A produce a spectrum that includes classical Menkes disease—a well-recognised neurocutaneous syndrome with neurodegeneration, hair abnormalities and skin involvement—and its allelic variant occipital horn syndrome, which presents with generalized cutis laxa. Thus the same ATP7A mutation is associated with both cutis laxa and a neurocutaneous disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor critical for ectodermal and craniofacial development, are the established cause of Ectrodactyly–ectodermal dysplasia–cleft (EEC) syndrome. The same gene is repeatedly implicated in isolated or syndromic craniofacial clefts, because p63 regulates epithelial adhesion and palatal shelf fusion. Thus, TP63 links both EEC syndrome and craniofacial clefting phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome is best known for its early-onset poikiloderma, photosensitivity, and later risk of cutaneous malignancies. Because these cutaneous manifestations dominate both diagnosis and long-term management, the specialty most consistently responsible for care and surveillance of affected patients is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melnick-Needles syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Melnick-Needles syndrome is an X-linked skeletal dysplasia caused by pathogenic variants in the FLNA gene. A hallmark radiographic finding is pronounced bowing of the fibulas giving them a characteristic “serpentine” appearance. Affected individuals also exhibit disproportionate short stature along with other skeletal and craniofacial anomalies, making this syndrome classically associated with the combination of serpentine fibulas and short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "cervical os",
    "long_answer": "Malignant mixed Müllerian tumors (uterine carcinosarcomas) often grow as large, polypoid masses within the uterine cavity. Because of their size and friable nature, they can extend downward and protrude through the cervical os (the opening of the cervix into the vagina), sometimes becoming visible in the vaginal canal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperkalemia",
    "long_answer": "In rhabdomyolysis, massive skeletal-muscle breakdown releases intracellular potassium into the bloodstream. The resulting rise in serum potassium is termed hyperkalemia, a life-threatening electrolyte disturbance because it can precipitate fatal cardiac arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Finkelstein reaction",
    "long_answer": "The Finkelstein reaction is a halogen-exchange reaction in which an alkyl chloride (or bromide) is treated with sodium iodide in a polar aprotic solvent such as dry acetone. The iodide ion performs an SN2 nucleophilic substitution on the alkyl chloride, and the reaction is driven to completion because the by-product sodium chloride is insoluble in acetone and precipitates, shifting the equilibrium toward formation of the alkyl iodide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "m.3243A>G mutation",
    "long_answer": "Maternally inherited diabetes and deafness (MIDD) is most often caused by the mitochondrial DNA point mutation m.3243A>G in the MT-TL1 gene, which encodes tRNALeu(UUR). This mutation impairs mitochondrial protein synthesis and oxidative phosphorylation, leading to progressive sensorineural deafness and early-onset diabetes that follow a maternal pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Borrelia burgdorferi serology",
    "long_answer": "Facial nerve (Bell’s) palsy can be an early neurologic manifestation of Lyme disease. Testing the patient’s serum for antibodies to Borrelia burgdorferi—typically by ELISA with confirmatory Western blot—can detect infection and thus identify Lyme disease as the underlying cause of the facial paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "In tricuspid atresia the right atrioventricular valve is absent, so systemic venous blood cannot enter the right ventricle and instead passes through an atrial septal defect directly into the left heart. This right-to-left shunt greatly reduces pulmonary blood flow and systemic arterial oxygen saturation. As a result, the newborn typically presents soon after birth with central cyanosis—a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes due to hypoxemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgM antibody",
    "long_answer": "Human infection with Toxoplasma gondii is most commonly confirmed serologically by demonstrating Toxoplasma-specific IgM antibodies in the patient’s serum. IgM appears early after primary infection, is readily detected by ELISA or immunofluorescence, and—because it does not cross the placenta—its presence indicates an active or recent infection, making it the key antibody class used in routine diagnosis of toxoplasmosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Repetitive nerve stimulation",
    "long_answer": "Electrodiagnostic testing with repetitive nerve stimulation (an EMG nerve-conduction study) characteristically shows a marked post-exercise or high-frequency ‘increment’—a >100 % rise in compound muscle action potential amplitude—in Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, reflecting facilitation of presynaptic acetylcholine release."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asymptomatic",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Pyle disease who show no clinical complaints or physical manifestations are described as asymptomatic, meaning the disease is present but does not produce noticeable symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior nasal choanae",
    "long_answer": "Choanal atresia is a congenital condition in which the posterior nasal apertures—called the choanae—fail to open, resulting in closure of the passage between the nasal cavities and the nasopharynx."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Automobile accidents",
    "long_answer": "Because narcolepsy causes uncontrollable daytime sleep attacks and sudden loss of muscle tone (cataplexy), affected individuals can fall asleep or lose control while driving. This markedly increases the risk of automobile accidents, making motor-vehicle injury a well-recognized complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brevibacterium linens",
    "long_answer": "Foot odor is chiefly produced when the skin-dwelling bacterium Brevibacterium linens breaks down sweat and skin proteins on the soles, releasing volatile compounds such as isovaleric acid and sulfur-containing molecules that give the characteristic cheesy smell. The same species is used in the ripening of pungent cheeses, underscoring its well-established role in generating strong odors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDL receptor (LDLR)",
    "long_answer": "Familial hyperlipoproteinemia type II, classically referred to as familial hypercholesterolemia, is most commonly caused by mutations in the LDL receptor (LDLR) gene. Loss-of-function variants in LDLR impair hepatic clearance of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) particles, leading to markedly elevated plasma LDL-cholesterol levels characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 1–2 %",
    "long_answer": "Modern series show that periprosthetic joint infection after primary total knee replacement occurs in roughly 1–2 out of every 100 operations (≈1–2 %), despite antibiotic prophylaxis and sterile operating-room protocols."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Cancer Institute",
    "long_answer": "The Bethesda Guidelines were created after a 1997 workshop convened by the U.S. National Cancer Institute (NCI). These guidelines specify clinical and pathological criteria for microsatellite-instability testing in colorectal tumors, helping clinicians recognize individuals at high risk for Lynch syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma can arise not only on sun-exposed skin but also on mucous membranes (e.g., lip, oral cavity, genital mucosa). Ultraviolet (UV) radiation produces pyrimidine dimers in DNA that frequently result in characteristic mutations in the p53 tumor-suppressor gene; these UV-induced p53 mutations are a well-established molecular hallmark in the pathogenesis of cutaneous and mucosal squamous cell carcinomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antisense oligonucleotide therapy",
    "long_answer": "Researchers are developing antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) therapy for facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy. These short, chemically-modified nucleic acids are designed to bind specifically to DUX4 mRNA, triggering its degradation or blocking its translation. By lowering DUX4 protein levels in muscle cells, ASOs aim to halt the downstream gene-dysregulation and muscle degeneration that characterize the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rituximab",
    "long_answer": "Rituximab, a chimeric anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody, depletes the IgM-secreting B-cell clone that produces anti-myelin-associated glycoprotein antibodies. In multiple prospective studies and randomized trials, rituximab has produced clinically meaningful improvements in strength, sensory function, and nerve conduction in patients with anti-MAG peripheral neuropathy, making it the most consistently effective and therefore most promising therapeutic option for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Donnan equilibrium",
    "long_answer": "Articular cartilage contains negatively charged proteoglycans that create a fixed charge density. According to the Donnan equilibrium (Donnan osmotic effect), these fixed charges attract mobile cations and water into the tissue to balance electrochemical potentials. In early osteoarthritis, disruption of the collagen network lessens the mechanical restraint on this osmotic swelling pressure, so more water is drawn into the cartilage despite some proteoglycan loss. Thus, the Donnan equilibrium principle explains the increased water content observed at the onset of osteoarthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phosphoserine",
    "long_answer": "Neu-Laxova syndrome results from mutations in the enzymes of the L-serine biosynthetic pathway, most notably phosphoserine phosphatase. This blocks the final step that converts the pathway intermediate phosphoserine into serine, implicating phosphoserine as the key accumulated/deficient intermediate associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Expanded tandem-mass-spectrometry newborn screening most frequently flags 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase (3-MCC) deficiency—an organic aciduria of leucine catabolism caused by mutations in MCCC1 or MCCC2. It is detected by the characteristic elevation of 3-hydroxy-isovalerylcarnitine (C5-OH) in dried blood spots and, in population data from Australia, North America and Europe, has the highest incidence of all screened organic acid disorders, exceeding that of propionic, methylmalonic, isovaleric and glutaric acidemias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ionizing radiation",
    "long_answer": "Prenatal exposure to ionizing radiation, such as diagnostic or therapeutic X-rays, is a well-recognized teratogenic environmental factor that can disrupt early optic vesicle development. This interruption of ocular morphogenesis can lead to complete absence of the eyes, i.e., anophthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus is a Gram-positive, coagulase-positive coccus that produces heat-stable enterotoxins responsible for rapid-onset food poisoning (gastroenteritis). The same organism is the leading cause of skin and soft-tissue infections, including impetigo, cellulitis, and abscesses, thereby linking it to both gastrointestinal illness and skin infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroesophageal reflux disease",
    "long_answer": "During pregnancy, elevated progesterone relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter and the growing uterus raises intra-abdominal pressure, leading many women to develop gastroesophageal reflux disease (heartburn). When hormone levels fall and abdominal pressure normalises after delivery, these reflux symptoms typically resolve on their own."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN5",
    "long_answer": "Dent’s disease is an X-linked recessive proximal tubulopathy most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CLCN5 gene on Xp11.23. CLCN5 encodes the renal chloride/proton exchanger ClC-5, and its dysfunction impairs endosomal acidification in proximal tubular cells, leading to low-molecular-weight proteinuria, hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis and progressive renal failure—the characteristic features of Dent’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone",
    "long_answer": "Before epispadias repair in boys with the bladder-exstrophy complex, surgeons commonly give a short course of androgen stimulation (intramuscular testosterone cypionate/enanthate injections or topical dihydrotestosterone). The exogenous testosterone enlarges the corporal bodies and glans, increasing penile length and tissue vascularity, which facilitates surgical reconstruction and improves cosmetic outcome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Milwaukee protocol",
    "long_answer": "The Milwaukee protocol is an experimental treatment for symptomatic rabies that places the patient in a medically induced coma while administering antiviral drugs and intensive supportive care. First used in 2004 on a teenage rabies survivor, it aims to protect the brain from viral damage while the immune system mounts a response. Because subsequent outcomes have been largely unsuccessful, it is not considered standard of care, but it remains the notable coma-based regimen attempted in post-symptom rabies cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rubella virus",
    "long_answer": "Congenital infection with rubella virus (the cause of German measles) can disrupt ocular development in the fetus, leading to anomalies such as cataracts, glaucoma, and microphthalmia. Maternal rubella during the first trimester is a well-established cause of microphthalmia in newborns, forming part of the classic congenital rubella syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent infections",
    "long_answer": "Waldmann disease (primary intestinal lymphangiectasia) causes loss of lymphocytes and immunoglobulins into the gut, producing lymphopenia and hypogammaglobulinemia. These immune deficits make patients prone to recurrent bacterial and viral infections—the hallmark clinical complaint in primary immunodeficiency disorders. Thus, both conditions commonly present with recurrent infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the homeobox gene HOXD13—most often poly-alanine expansions or missense changes—disturb distal limb patterning. Affected families typically present with opposable triphalangeal thumb duplication (a variant of synpolydactyly) and can also show bony fusion of adjacent metacarpals (metacarpal synostosis). Thus HOXD13 defects explain the coexistence of these two skeletal hand anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jaw osteomyelitis",
    "long_answer": "Because the bones in pycnodysostosis are abnormally dense and poorly vascularised, patients are prone to develop chronic osteomyelitis of the jaws, especially after dental extractions; this is the dental complication most characteristically linked to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cortisol",
    "long_answer": "Cortisol is the principal glucocorticoid released during physiologic or psychological stress. Elevated cortisol exerts potent immunosuppressive effects by promoting lymphocyte apoptosis and redistributing lymphocytes out of the peripheral blood, thereby leading to lymphocytopenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurologist",
    "long_answer": "Carotid artery dissection is primarily a cerebrovascular condition that presents with stroke-like neurological symptoms; therefore, its diagnosis (via CTA/MRA, duplex ultrasound) and first-line medical management (antithrombotic therapy, blood-pressure control) are overseen by a neurologist—typically a vascular neurologist—who coordinates any necessary endovascular or surgical interventions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human Immunodeficiency Virus",
    "long_answer": "The Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) selectively infects and destroys CD4+ T-lymphocytes, producing a progressive lymphocytopenia. Loss of these immune cells impairs both humoral and cell-mediated immunity, and persistent infection culminates in the clinical syndrome known as acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "The MLC1 gene, whose mutations cause Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts (MLC), is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 22 (22q13.3). This chromosomal localization has been confirmed by linkage analysis and genomic sequencing studies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Not recognized as a disease",
    "long_answer": "Reviews by the WHO Global Advisory Committee on Vaccine Safety (1999, 2003 and later) concluded that macrophagic myofasciitis is a local histological reaction at the vaccine injection site with no convincing evidence of a unique clinical syndrome. Consequently, the World Health Organization does not recognize macrophagic myofasciitis as a distinct disease entity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "The condition once called “lethal midline granuloma,” characterized by aggressive, destructive lesions of the nasal cavity and mid-facial structures, is now recognized to be an Epstein-Barr virus–associated extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (nasal type). Histology, immunophenotype (CD56+, cytotoxic molecules), and molecular studies confirmed it is a malignancy of natural killer / cytotoxic T cells rather than a granulomatous disease, so current lymphoma classifications place it under Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Japan",
    "long_answer": "In 2024 the Japanese Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (MHLW) granted the world’s first marketing authorisation for a medicine specifically indicated for distal myopathy (distal myopathy with rimmed vacuoles / GNE myopathy), making Japan the country that approved a drug for this rare muscle-wasting disorder in 2024."
}

{
    "short_answer": "St. Vitus’ dance",
    "long_answer": "Sydenham's chorea is a post-streptococcal movement disorder characterized by rapid, involuntary jerking movements that can follow rheumatic fever. In earlier centuries the same pattern of uncontrollable dancing-like motions was termed “St. Vitus’ dance,” after the patron saint invoked against such neurological afflictions. Thus, the historical name for Sydenham's chorea is St. Vitus’ dance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nyctophobia",
    "long_answer": "Nyctophobia is the clinical term for an intense, persistent fear of the dark or night. Although common in childhood, this specific phobia can remain or re-emerge in adulthood, leading to marked distress and avoidance behaviors that interfere with daily functioning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galen",
    "long_answer": "The 2nd-century Greco-Roman physician Galen of Pergamon taught that many disorders arose from a ‘plethora’—an excess of blood—which produced a strong, bounding pulse akin to what we now recognise as hypertension. To restore humoral balance he recommended venesection (bloodletting), and his authority ensured that bloodletting remained a standard treatment for elevated blood pressure and related conditions for well over a millennium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Lymphangioleiomyomatosis is a rare cystic lung disease characterized by abnormal smooth-muscle cell proliferation in the airways and lymphatics, leading to progressive respiratory decline. Evaluation (high-resolution CT, pulmonary function tests), medical management (e.g., sirolimus), and decisions about oxygen or lung transplantation are overseen by pulmonologists, the medical specialists in respiratory medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of Larsen syndrome are transmitted in an autosomal dominant pattern, usually due to pathogenic variants in the FLNB gene. Although rarer autosomal-recessive forms have been reported, the autosomal dominant mode of inheritance is by far the most frequent and well-documented presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Ventricular fibrillation is a life-threatening cardiac arrhythmia that demands rapid defibrillation, anti-arrhythmic drugs, and long-term rhythm management. These interventions fall within the expertise of cardiology, the medical specialty devoted to diagnosing and treating heart disorders, including malignant ventricular arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obstructive congenital defect",
    "long_answer": "Subvalvular (subaortic) aortic stenosis in dogs is categorized as an obstructive congenital heart defect because the malformation narrows the left-ventricular outflow tract, impeding blood ejection and creating pressure overload."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brain CT scan",
    "long_answer": "A computed tomography (CT) scan of the brain can reveal multiple small cortical and subcortical infarcts characteristic of multi-infarct (vascular) dementia, findings that are typically absent in Alzheimer’s disease, thereby helping to distinguish the two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-DR2",
    "long_answer": "Genetic association studies of acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy (APMPPE) have consistently shown an over-representation of the class II major histocompatibility complex serotype HLA-DR2—especially the DRB1*1501 allele—in affected patients compared with controls, indicating that HLA-DR2 is the immunogenetic marker most closely linked to this ocular inflammatory disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "315.32",
    "long_answer": "In the DSM-IV-TR classification of Communication Disorders, the diagnostic code assigned to Mixed Receptive-Expressive Language Disorder is 315.32."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Right colon",
    "long_answer": "In Asian populations diverticulitis most frequently involves right-sided colonic segments, particularly the cecum and ascending colon, whereas in Western countries it is usually left-sided (sigmoid). This geographic difference is well documented in surgical and epidemiological studies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATR",
    "long_answer": "The gene ATR (ataxia-telangiectasia and Rad3-related), located on human chromosome 3q22-q24, is the gene in which loss-of-function mutations were first shown to cause Seckel syndrome (SCKL1). ATR encodes a DNA damage–response kinase, and its deficiency leads to the characteristic growth retardation, microcephaly and developmental defects seen in Seckel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Routine prenatal ultrasonography (fetal ultrasound) can show absent calvarial ossification in Crane–Heise syndrome because the unmineralised skull transmits sound and appears poorly echoic. The same ultrasound technique, often with Doppler, is the standard tool for serial assessment and surveillance of fetuses with intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periodontics",
    "long_answer": "Papillon–Lefèvre syndrome causes aggressive, early-onset periodontitis that leads to rapid loss of primary and permanent teeth. Management of this severe periodontal disease is the domain of the dental specialty of periodontics, which focuses on preventing, diagnosing, and treating disorders of the supporting tissues of the teeth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleiotropy",
    "long_answer": "Agenesis of the corpus callosum can produce diverse neurological, cognitive, and systemic manifestations. This is best explained by pleiotropy, in which a single gene mutation influences multiple, seemingly unrelated phenotypic traits. Genes critical for corpus callosum formation (e.g., L1CAM, ARX) also participate in other developmental pathways; therefore, their disruption causes several different symptoms in the same individual."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Axenfeld–Rieger syndrome is classically inherited in an autosomal dominant manner, most commonly due to heterozygous pathogenic variants in genes such as PITX2 or FOXC1. In autosomal dominant inheritance a single mutant allele is sufficient to cause the syndrome, and it can be transmitted from an affected parent to offspring with a 50 % recurrence risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex",
    "long_answer": "Cardiac rhabdomyomas are benign tumors often seen in infants. Multiple studies show that approximately 80–90% of these tumors occur in patients with tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC), an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in TSC1 or TSC2. The mTOR-activating mutations characteristic of TSC drive hamartoma formation in multiple organs, including the heart, explaining the strong epidemiologic link between TSC and cardiac rhabdomyomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supportive physical therapy",
    "long_answer": "There is no disease-modifying drug for Bethlem myopathy; management is symptomatic. Regular, tailored supportive physical therapy (stretching, strengthening, contracture prevention) together with other supportive measures (orthotics, respiratory support as needed) is the standard approach to alleviate and control its muscle weakness and joint contractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ceftriaxone",
    "long_answer": "Ceftriaxone is a broad-spectrum, third-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that is routinely used as parenteral therapy for enteric fevers, including paratyphoid fever caused by Salmonella Paratyphi. Its extended activity against Gram-negative bacilli and proven clinical efficacy in multidrug-resistant Salmonella infections make it the standard cephalosporin choice for this indication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanide poisoning",
    "long_answer": "Cyanide binds to the ferric iron of cytochrome-c oxidase (Complex IV) in the mitochondrial electron-transport chain. This blocks oxidative phosphorylation, stopping ATP production and leading to premature cellular death (loss of cellular longevity) and severe metabolic derangements. The resulting energy failure and lactic acidosis rapidly precipitate dysfunction of multiple organs—brain, heart, lungs, kidneys, and liver—making cyanide poisoning a classic cause of widespread organ failure through inhibition of a critical metabolic enzyme system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices",
    "long_answer": "The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention’s Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP) issued guidance in 2017 advising that people identified as being at increased risk during a mumps outbreak receive a third dose of the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine to help boost waning immunity and control transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Appendix",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomyxoma peritonei almost always originates from a mucin-producing neoplasm of the vermiform appendix. Rupture of an appendiceal mucinous adenoma or adenocarcinoma releases copious mucin and tumor cells into the peritoneal cavity, leading to the characteristic gelatinous ascites of this syndrome; consequently, the appendix is the large-intestinal site most strongly linked to pseudomyxoma peritonei."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sulfur powder",
    "long_answer": "Before the advent of modern antifungals, elemental sulfur—often sold as \"flowers of sulfur\" powder—was routinely dusted onto ringworm (dermatophytosis) lesions. Sulfur’s slow oxidation in the skin produces acidic and polysulfide compounds that exert keratolytic and fungicidal effects, making sulfur powder a traditional remedy for dermatophyte infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "D-dimer",
    "long_answer": "Portal vein thrombosis produces cross-linked fibrin within the clot; as the endogenous fibrinolytic system degrades this fibrin, D-dimer fragments are released into the circulation. Because D-dimer is a specific degradation product of cross-linked fibrin, its blood concentration rises in active thrombosis and fibrin breakdown, making an elevated D-dimer a typical laboratory finding in portal vein thrombosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSM-III inclusion",
    "long_answer": "Dissociative identity disorder (then termed multiple personality disorder) was formally listed as a distinct diagnosis in the 1980 Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Third Edition (DSM-III). This official inclusion heightened clinical awareness, standardized criteria, and encouraged clinicians to look for and record the condition, leading to a sharp rise in reported and diagnosed cases during the late 20th century."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Knee osteoarthritis",
    "long_answer": "If genu varum (bow-leg deformity) is left uncorrected in adulthood, the varus alignment concentrates mechanical load on the medial tibio-femoral compartment. The chronic asymmetric stress accelerates cartilage wear, meniscal degeneration, and subchondral bone changes, commonly culminating in medial-compartment knee osteoarthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "lumen",
    "long_answer": "The lumen is the hollow interior space of an artery through which blood flows. In atherosclerosis, plaque builds up within the arterial wall, protruding into the lumen and thereby narrowing its diameter, which restricts blood flow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PDGFB",
    "long_answer": "Primary familial brain calcification (PFBC) is genetically heterogeneous. One of the causative genes, PDGFB (platelet-derived growth factor subunit B), maps to chromosome 22q13. Loss-of-function mutations in PDGFB impair PDGF-B/PDGFR-β signaling, disrupting blood–brain barrier integrity and leading to pathological calcium deposition in cerebral microvessels, which characterizes PFBC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Penetrating keratoplasty",
    "long_answer": "In Iridocorneal Endothelial (ICE) syndrome the abnormal endothelial cell proliferation can lead to advanced corneal edema and decompensation. When this occurs, visual rehabilitation often requires replacement of the full-thickness cornea; the standard surgical option is penetrating keratoplasty, which removes the diseased cornea and provides a clear graft."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Water deprivation test",
    "long_answer": "In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus the kidneys are unresponsive to vasopressin, so their ability to concentrate urine is impaired. The water deprivation test (also called the fluid-restriction or Miller‐Moses test) measures urine osmolality during controlled dehydration and thus directly evaluates renal concentrating capacity, providing a standard assessment of kidney function in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray",
    "long_answer": "Pneumoconiosis produces characteristic opacities and fibrotic changes in the lungs that are readily visualised on a standard chest X-ray; radiographic screening and grading (e.g., the ILO classification) rely on this imaging to document and monitor the disease-related lung damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Beta thalassemia can be cured by allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (bone-marrow or peripheral blood stem cell transplant), which replaces the patient’s defective hematopoietic stem cells with donor cells capable of producing normal β-globin chains and restoring effective erythropoiesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Many girls and women with Turner syndrome develop chronic hypertension; prevalence studies report rates exceeding 30–50%. Elevated blood pressure results from congenital vascular abnormalities (e.g., coarctation, aortic stiffness) and metabolic factors, and it is one of the strongest, well-established modifiable risk factors for cardiovascular disease, explaining part of the heightened cardiovascular morbidity seen in Turner syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acyclovir",
    "long_answer": "Neonatal herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection is managed with high-dose intravenous acyclovir, a guanine nucleoside analogue that is phosphorylated by viral thymidine kinase and inhibits viral DNA polymerase, thereby preventing HSV replication and reducing morbidity and mortality in affected newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low protein load in breast milk",
    "long_answer": "Human breast milk contains much less total protein—and therefore lower amounts of the cationic amino acids lysine, arginine, and ornithine—than cow-milk–based formula or solid foods. Because lysinuric protein intolerance causes defective intestinal and renal transport only when these amino acids are presented in higher amounts, the modest protein supply from breastfeeding does not overwhelm the impaired transport system, so metabolic disturbances (hyperammonemia, vomiting, failure to thrive) do not appear until protein intake increases at weaning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Quarter Horse",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen branching enzyme deficiency (GBED) is an autosomal recessive glycogen-storage disorder caused by mutations in the GBE1 gene; it has been documented almost exclusively in American Quarter Horses and closely related breeds (e.g., Paint Horses). Foals that inherit two mutant alleles cannot synthesize properly branched glycogen, leading to energy failure in skeletal muscle, heart, and liver, and they typically die in utero or shortly after birth. Consequently, the American Quarter Horse breed is the one characteristically affected by GBED."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia with ataxia is a neurocutaneous, often spinocerebellar, disorder in which the predominant disabling component is progressive cerebellar ataxia; diagnosis, genetic work-up and long-term management therefore fall mainly within the scope of a neurologist, who can then coordinate care with dermatology for the skin findings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epiphyseal growth plate",
    "long_answer": "Acromesomelic dysplasia is characterized by premature fusion of the epiphyseal growth plates (the epiphyses) of the long bones, which halts longitudinal growth and produces the marked shortening of the distal and middle limb segments typical of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDKN2A mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial atypical multiple mole melanoma (FAMMM) is most commonly caused by germline mutations in the CDKN2A tumor-suppressor gene (also known as p16/INK4A) on chromosome 9p21. Loss-of-function alterations in CDKN2A impair control of the G1-S cell-cycle checkpoint, markedly elevating an individual’s lifetime risk of developing cutaneous melanoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genu valgum",
    "long_answer": "Genu valgum (knock-knees) increases the Q-angle of the knee, which shifts the line of pull of the quadriceps laterally. This altered biomechanics reduces medial restraint on the patellae and permits them to glide farther to the lateral side during flexion–extension, so patients with genu valgum typically show increased lateral excursion (tracking) of the patellas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Puberty",
    "long_answer": "Although Poland syndrome can be detected at birth, many mild cases go unnoticed until puberty, when the normal adolescent growth of breast tissue and the pectoral muscles accentuates the unilateral chest wall underdevelopment, prompting individuals to recognize the abnormality for the first time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 8 syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The disorder produced by the presence of three copies of chromosome 8 is called Trisomy 8 syndrome, also known as Warkany syndrome 2. Complete or mosaic duplication of chromosome 8 leads to characteristic craniofacial abnormalities, skeletal malformations, intellectual disability, and other congenital anomalies that define this chromosomal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SH2D1A",
    "long_answer": "Dysgammaglobulinemia in patients with X-linked lymphoproliferative disease (XLP) is classically linked to mutations in the SH2D1A gene, which encodes the SLAM-associated protein (SAP). Loss-of-function SH2D1A mutations impair T- and NK-cell signaling through SLAM family receptors, leading to defective B-cell help and the characteristic abnormal immunoglobulin profile (dysgammaglobulinemia) seen in XLP1 patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippo signaling pathway",
    "long_answer": "The Hippo signaling pathway (MST1/2–LATS1/2–YAP/TAZ) is a conserved organ-size control cascade that limits cell proliferation and promotes apoptosis. In the developing cerebral cortex, Hippo activity restrains neural-progenitor expansion; loss-of-function of YAP/TAZ or other core Hippo components dramatically reduces progenitor pools and produces severe microcephaly in mouse models as well as human primary microcephaly syndromes. Therefore, Hippo signaling is a key regulator of brain size and is directly implicated in primary microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy",
    "long_answer": "Kidney stones that form in the anomalous collecting system of a horseshoe kidney can be fragmented non-invasively with extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL). The focused acoustic pulses pass through soft tissues and break the calculi into small fragments that can then be passed through the ureter, making ESWL a well-accepted treatment option for urolithiasis in patients with horseshoe kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baclofen",
    "long_answer": "Intrathecal baclofen therapy uses a surgically implanted pump to deliver the GABAB-receptor agonist baclofen directly into the cerebrospinal fluid, providing potent, adjustable control of severe muscle spasticity and secondary dystonia when oral medications are inadequate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric nephrology",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome is a disorder of the renal proximal tubules that causes excessive urinary loss of bicarbonate, glucose, phosphate, amino acids, and other solutes. Because it is fundamentally a kidney (renal tubular) disease occurring in children, the specialty that diagnoses and manages it is pediatric nephrology, the branch of medicine devoted to kidney disorders in pediatric patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Promonocyte",
    "long_answer": "In the monocytic lineage, the sequence is monoblast → promonocyte → monocyte. Promonocytes represent the first recognisable maturation stage that is largely confined to the bone marrow; mature monocytes circulate in peripheral blood. In acute myeloid leukaemia-M5 (acute monocytic/monoblastic leukaemia), promonocytes are therefore the earliest developmental stage that is predominantly encountered in the marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALK",
    "long_answer": "The ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) gene resides at chromosome 2p23. Chromosomal translocations such as t(2;5)(p23;q35) fuse ALK with partners like NPM1, creating oncogenic ALK fusion proteins that drive the pathogenesis of ALK-positive anaplastic large-cell lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rho(D) immune globulin",
    "long_answer": "Rho(D) immune globulin is an antibody preparation given to Rh-negative pregnant women to prevent Rh sensitization. It binds any fetal Rh-positive red blood cells that enter the maternal circulation, preventing the mother’s immune system from producing anti-D antibodies and thereby averting hemolytic disease of the newborn in current or subsequent pregnancies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronary angiography",
    "long_answer": "The definitive, catheter-based coronary angiography provides high-resolution, direct visualization of the coronary lumen and vessel contour, allowing precise measurement of abnormal dilatation. Because of this unmatched spatial resolution and diagnostic accuracy, it is universally regarded as the gold-standard imaging technique for identifying and characterizing coronary artery aneurysms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac arrhythmias",
    "long_answer": "In antisynthetase syndrome, immune-mediated inflammation can extend to the myocardium and conduction system, making supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias—as well as AV-conduction blocks—the most frequently reported cardiac complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "The TBX3 gene, whose loss-of-function mutations cause Ulnar–mammary syndrome, is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 12 (cytogenetic band 12q24.21)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek stoma + -itis",
    "long_answer": "The word ‘stomatitis’ is derived from classical Greek: ‘stoma’ (στόμα) meaning “mouth” and the inflammatory suffix ‘-itis’. Combined, they literally denote an inflammation of the mouth, which is exactly what the medical term describes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-CCP antibody",
    "long_answer": "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies are detected in a significant proportion of patients with palindromic rheumatism and are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis, where they serve as a key serological marker for diagnosis and prediction of disease progression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonazepam",
    "long_answer": "Clonazepam is a benzodiazepine that acts on GABA-A receptors and is widely prescribed for anxiety disorders and as an anticonvulsant. Because of its central inhibitory effects, clonazepam is also employed as a pharmacologic option for trigeminal neuralgia, particularly when first-line anticonvulsants are ineffective or not tolerated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulsed dye laser",
    "long_answer": "Port-wine stains are vascular malformations rich in capillary blood; their hemoglobin absorbs yellow light around 585–595 nm. The pulsed dye laser emits brief pulses at this wavelength range, achieving selective photothermolysis of the abnormal vessels while sparing surrounding skin, and is therefore the gold-standard treatment for port-wine stains."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dentin",
    "long_answer": "In X-linked hypophosphatemia, loss of PHEX activity prevents normal degradation of the mineralization inhibitor osteopontin (OPN). The excess OPN accumulates in the predentin/dentin matrix, where it blocks hydroxyapatite crystal growth, leading to hypomineralised dentin and the characteristic dental defects (enlarged pulp chambers, spontaneous abscesses) seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cri-du-chat syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cri-du-chat syndrome results from a partial deletion of the short arm (p) of chromosome 5 (designated 5p-). This chromosomal microdeletion disrupts genes important for neuronal and muscular development, producing the characteristic high-pitched “cat-like” cry, global developmental delay, microcephaly, and marked neonatal hypotonia in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothermia",
    "long_answer": "Hypoglycemia deprives tissues of glucose needed for metabolic heat production, lowering core body temperature and potentially producing hypothermia. Independent of blood-glucose levels, prolonged exposure to cold weather also leads to loss of body heat and the same clinical state. Thus, both hypoglycemia and cold exposure can culminate in hypothermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX10",
    "long_answer": "SOX10 encodes a transcription factor required for migration and differentiation of neural-crest–derived enteric neurons. Pathogenic SOX10 mutations are a well-established cause of Hirschsprung’s disease (often as part of Waardenburg-Shah syndrome). The same mutations have been documented in patients who also present with congenital esophageal achalasia, showing that SOX10 is involved in the development and function of the myenteric plexus in both the colon and the esophagus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isochromosome 8q (i(8)(q10))",
    "long_answer": "Both T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia and Sézary disease frequently show formation of an isochromosome for the long arm of chromosome 8 – designated i(8)(q10). This abnormality duplicates 8q material (often including the MYC locus) and deletes 8p, and is reported in the majority of T-PLL cases and in a substantial subset of Sézary/cutaneous T-cell lymphoma cases, making it the chromosomal change most commonly shared by these two disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BMPR1B",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the BMPR1B gene (bone morphogenetic protein receptor type 1B) are the recognised cause of the condition historically called triphalangeal thumbs-brachyectrodactyly syndrome (brachydactyly type A2). Loss- or change-of-function variants impair BMP signalling during limb bud development, leading to formation of a three-phalanged thumb and overall shortening or absence of phalanges in the fingers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii) causes a form of pneumonia that is one of the most frequent opportunistic infections seen when CD4⁺ T-cell counts fall below ≈200 cells/µL in people with HIV/AIDS. Because cell-mediated immunity is critical for clearing this ubiquitous fungus, immunocompromised patients—especially those with advanced HIV—are highly susceptible, making Pneumocystis pneumonia a prototypical and common AIDS-defining illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly involves abnormally short phalanges; when the shortened bones compromise hand or foot function, care is typically provided by an orthopedic surgeon, the specialty focused on diagnosing and surgically correcting disorders of the bones and joints, including congenital skeletal abnormalities of the digits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rubella vaccination",
    "long_answer": "Congenital rubella infection is a major cause of irreversible sensorineural hearing loss in children. Universal rubella vaccination—particularly immunising girls before child-bearing age—prevents maternal rubella infection during pregnancy, thereby eliminating congenital rubella syndrome and markedly lowering the incidence of childhood hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diastrophic dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Diastrophic dysplasia is a rare skeletal dysplasia marked by short-limb dwarfism, spinal deformities and other cartilage/bone abnormalities. It results from biallelic mutations in the SLC26A2 (DTDST) gene and follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern, meaning affected individuals inherit one pathogenic allele from each carrier parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Marinesco–Sjögren syndrome is marked by progressive cerebellar atrophy, especially involving the cerebellar vermis, leading to the characteristic cerebellar ataxia seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis is an autosomal-recessive disorder (commonly due to HFE gene mutations such as C282Y) in which excessive intestinal iron absorption leads to systemic iron overload, damaging the liver, pancreas, heart, skin, and joints despite its recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes insipidus nephrogenicus",
    "long_answer": "In medical Latin, the condition in which the kidney (nephro-) is unresponsive to the antidiuretic hormone is termed “Diabetes insipidus nephrogenicus.” The noun “diabetes” (m., Greek origin) is modified by the adjectives “insipidus” (“without taste,” i.e., producing dilute urine) and “nephrogenicus” (“arising from the kidney”), accurately denoting nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi anemia",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia is an inherited DNA-repair disorder that typically presents with progressive bone marrow failure (leading to aplastic anemia) and multiple congenital abnormalities such as radial-ray defects, short stature, microcephaly, and skin pigmentation changes. Its hallmark combination of marrow failure and birth defects makes it the classic genetic disorder fitting this description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bénédict-Augustin Morel",
    "long_answer": "Morel’s ear is named after the 19th-century French psychiatrist Bénédict-Augustin Morel, who catalogued distinctive physical stigmata—including a characteristic dysplastic external ear—as signs of hereditary mental degeneration in his 1857 work on ‘dégénérescence.’"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arylsulfatase G",
    "long_answer": "Sanfilippo syndrome type E (mucopolysaccharidosis IIIE) is caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase G, encoded by the ARSG gene. Loss of arylsulfatase G activity blocks a step in heparan-sulfate degradation, leading to the storage pathology characteristic of Sanfilippo disease. Types A–D involve other enzymes, but the type E subtype is specifically linked to arylsulfatase G deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactate",
    "long_answer": "Stimmler syndrome is a form of congenital lactic acidosis; the metabolic block causes large amounts of pyruvate to be reduced to lactate. Consequently, lactate accumulates in the blood and spills into the urine. The same compound is generated in exercising muscle when pyruvate is reduced by lactate dehydrogenase to regenerate NAD⁺ under anaerobic conditions. Thus the substance found at high levels in Stimmler syndrome and produced from pyruvate during intense exercise is lactate (lactic acid)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WAGR syndrome",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous-gene deletion at chromosome 11p13 that removes the WT1 and PAX6 loci. The syndrome is characterized by Wilms tumour, Aniridia, Genitourinary anomalies and intellectual disability (historically termed mental Retardation), making it the classic chromosome 11 deletion disorder associated with cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mixed hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Mixed hearing loss is characterized by the simultaneous presence of conductive deficits due to pathology in the middle ear (e.g., ossicular chain problems, otitis media) and sensorineural deficits arising from damage to the inner ear or auditory nerve. Thus, it involves changes in both the middle ear and the inner ear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4p deletion",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of congenital cicatricial (scarring) alopecia is aplasia cutis congenita of the scalp. A well-established syndromic association is Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome, which is caused by a terminal deletion of the short arm of chromosome 4 (4p-). Hence, loss of genetic material on chromosome 4p is the chromosomal deletion linked to this congenital scarring alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrops fetalis",
    "long_answer": "In achondrogenesis type II, the impaired development of cartilage and bone often leads to non-immune hydrops fetalis, a condition defined by abnormal accumulation of serous fluid in at least two fetal compartments such as the pleural, pericardial, or abdominal cavities and subcutaneous tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Van der Woude syndrome is an autosomal-dominant disorder characterized by congenital lower-lip pits with or without cleft lip and palate. The vast majority of cases are caused by pathogenic variants in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor-6) on chromosome 1q32, making IRF6 mutations the established molecular basis for the lip pits seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPA1-related optic atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the mitochondrial fusion gene OPA1 usually cause autosomal-dominant optic atrophy. When both alleles are mutated (biallelic/compound-heterozygous), the phenotype shifts to a severe infantile encephalopathy with lactic acidosis and characteristic symmetrical lesions in the basal ganglia and brain-stem — the radiological and clinical hallmarks of Leigh syndrome. Thus, the biallelic form of OPA1-related disease is classically associated with a Leigh-syndrome phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "In Lemierre’s syndrome, septicemia with Fusobacterium necrophorum can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). DIC causes widespread micro-thrombi and consumption of clotting factors, leading to ischemia, hemorrhage, and multi-organ dysfunction; when the liver is affected, this process can progress to acute liver failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia most commonly presents with recurrent epileptic seizures, and these seizures are characteristically refractory to antiepileptic medications, making drug-resistant epilepsy the hallmark clinical manifestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Furosemide",
    "long_answer": "Furosemide is a loop diuretic commonly prescribed to relieve edema. By blocking the Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, it greatly increases renal potassium excretion, often producing hypokalemia. Because fluctuations in serum potassium can provoke attacks in patients with hyperkalemic periodic paralysis, the hypokalemia induced by furosemide may paradoxically trigger or worsen their paralytic episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prosthetic limbs",
    "long_answer": "Acheiropodia is a rare congenital malformation in which the hands and feet (distal extremities) are absent. Fitting the affected individual with appropriately designed upper- and lower-extremity prosthetic limbs can substitute for the missing hands and feet, restoring functional abilities such as grasping, standing, and walking, thereby markedly improving mobility, independence, and overall quality of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diverticulectomy with cricopharyngeal myotomy",
    "long_answer": "For large (>5–6 cm) symptomatic Zenker's diverticula, the standard management is an open cervical diverticulectomy combined with a cricopharyngeal myotomy. Excision of the pouch removes the reservoir that causes regurgitation and aspiration, while myotomy eliminates the underlying upper-esophageal sphincter dysfunction that produced the diverticulum, yielding the highest long-term cure and lowest recurrence rates in this size category."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beta-carotene",
    "long_answer": "Erythropoietic protoporphyria is often treated with high-dose oral beta-carotene to increase light tolerance. Because beta-carotene is a lipophilic carotenoid that accumulates in the skin at high doses, it can impart a yellow-orange discoloration known as carotenoderma, a well-recognized side-effect of this therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa",
    "long_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa (RP) is an inherited retinal dystrophy whose penetrance varies markedly with the causative gene. For example, autosomal-dominant RHO mutations are nearly fully penetrant, whereas PRPF31 mutations show incomplete penetrance, and X-linked or autosomal-recessive mutations present different penetrance profiles. Thus, the likelihood that a carrier will manifest retinal degeneration depends on which RP gene is involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anthracyclines",
    "long_answer": "Anthracycline antibiotics (e.g., doxorubicin, daunorubicin) are cornerstone agents in lymphoma regimens such as CHOP and ABVD, but they are dose-dependently cardiotoxic. Their generation of free radicals and iron-dependent oxidative stress injures cardiac myocytes, leading to irreversible cardiomyopathy and congestive heart failure, making cardiotoxicity the hallmark adverse effect of this drug class."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal wall",
    "long_answer": "After appendicectomy, disruption of the cecal or appendiceal mucosa can allow Actinomyces species to invade contiguous tissues, producing abdominal actinomycosis that characteristically involves the abdominal wall, often presenting as firm masses or sinus-tracts at or near the surgical scar."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1 in 225,000",
    "long_answer": "Adams–Oliver syndrome is an exceedingly rare congenital disorder; epidemiological summaries such as those in Orphanet and clinical genetics reviews report its occurrence at roughly one case per 225,000 live births."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome) is a chromosomal disorder diagnosed by cytogenetic testing and requires coordinated counseling on prognosis, recurrence risk, and multisystem management. These tasks are typically performed by specialists in medical (clinical) genetics, who confirm the karyotype and guide ongoing care and family counseling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankle-foot orthosis",
    "long_answer": "Bethlem myopathy causes proximal muscle weakness and Achilles-tendon contractures that lead to toe-walking and gait instability. Wearing a custom ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) holds the ankle in neutral dorsiflexion, counteracts foot-drop, and stabilizes the lower limb, thereby improving walking efficiency and preserving mobility—making an AFO a beneficial aid for individuals with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RECQL4",
    "long_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome (particularly type II) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the RECQL4 gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase essential for DNA replication and repair. Defective RECQL4 leads to accumulation of DNA damage and genomic instability, phenomena central to the DNA damage theory of aging. Hence, mutations in RECQL4 link this syndrome to mechanisms proposed to drive organismal aging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ETDRS chart",
    "long_answer": "In clinical assessment and trials for choroideremia, best-corrected visual acuity is routinely measured with the Early Treatment Diabetic Retinopathy Study (ETDRS) chart—a standardized LogMAR eye-chart that yields precise, reproducible letter scores for patients with retinal dystrophies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ocrelizumab",
    "long_answer": "Ocrelizumab is a humanized anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody that is approved for both relapsing and primary-progressive multiple sclerosis. Because pemphigus vulgaris is driven by pathogenic autoantibody-producing B cells, ocrelizumab’s B-cell-depleting mechanism is being evaluated in clinical studies as a potential therapeutic option for this blistering autoimmune disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rectal irrigation",
    "long_answer": "In Intestinal neuronal dysplasia, the main conservative therapy is regular rectal irrigation (large-volume saline enemas) administered through the rectum to empty and decompress the colon, thereby relieving functional obstruction and preventing enterocolitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The bladder",
    "long_answer": "Dysautonomia disrupts autonomic (sympathetic and parasympathetic) control of the urinary bladder, so patients commonly experience neurogenic bladder manifestations such as urinary retention, urgency, or incontinence. Bladder dysfunction is therefore a well-recognized and frequent symptom of autonomic nervous system disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "In Alport syndrome, blockade of the renin–angiotensin system with angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors—such as ramipril or enalapril—is recommended because it lowers intraglomerular pressure, reduces proteinuria, and has been shown to slow the progression to chronic kidney failure in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPD helicase",
    "long_answer": "The XPD protein (encoded by ERCC2) is a DNA helicase that functions in the nucleotide-excision repair pathway. Mutations in XPD cause xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) and can also give rise to syndromes such as trichothiodystrophy and XP/Cockayne overlap, which feature segmental premature aging. Thus, XPD links defective DNA repair to both XP and premature-aging phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Myotonic dystrophy is a hereditary neuromuscular disorder; its hallmark manifestations—progressive muscle weakness, myotonia, and associated pain—are evaluated and treated by neurologists, particularly those with expertise in neuromuscular medicine. Neurology provides diagnostic electrophysiologic testing, guides symptomatic pharmacologic therapy, and coordinates multidisciplinary care for these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β2-glycoprotein I",
    "long_answer": "β2-Glycoprotein I (also called apolipoprotein H) is a circulating phospholipid-binding protein that attaches to negatively charged membrane phospholipids and interferes with the prothrombinase complex, thereby limiting the generation of thrombin. It is the principal antigen for antiphospholipid antibodies, and autoantibodies directed against β2-glycoprotein I constitute a diagnostic hallmark of antiphospholipid syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NMDA receptor",
    "long_answer": "Dextromethorphan acts as a non-competitive antagonist of the N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor. In non-ketotic hyperglycinemia (glycine encephalopathy), elevated glycine overactivates NMDA receptors in the brain; dextromethorphan helps by inhibiting this receptor’s activity, reducing excitotoxicity and improving neurological symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HCFC1",
    "long_answer": "The only clearly defined X-linked recessive form of methylmalonic acidemia is the cblX defect, which is caused by pathogenic variants in the HCFC1 (Host Cell Factor C1) gene on Xq28. HCFC1 is required for normal intracellular processing of cobalamin to its co-enzymatic forms (adenosyl- and methyl-cobalamin). Loss of HCFC1 function blocks this pathway, leading to deficient methylmalonyl-CoA mutase activity and accumulation of methylmalonic acid. Because HCFC1 resides on the X chromosome, the disorder follows X-linked recessive inheritance, distinguishing it from the more common autosomal forms of methylmalonic acidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC22A5",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency results from loss-of-function variants in the SLC22A5 gene, which encodes the high-affinity carnitine transporter OCTN2. Hepatic expression of SLC22A5 is transcriptionally up-regulated by the nuclear receptor peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-α (PPAR-α), linking PPAR-α activation to cellular carnitine uptake and thereby to fatty-acid oxidation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia",
    "long_answer": "Visual fibers running through the parietal lobe carry information from the inferior visual fields. A parietal lobe lesion therefore damages the superior optic radiations, producing loss of the lower (inferior) quadrant of the visual field on the side opposite the lesion—classically described as a contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia ('pie on the floor')."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radius and ulna",
    "long_answer": "In Nager acrofacial dysostosis the pre-axial limb defect typically involves hypoplasia or absence of the radius and, to a lesser extent, shortening of the ulna; involvement of these two forearm bones is what causes the characteristically shortened forearms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis",
    "long_answer": "Hemoglobin E is a structural variant that alters the charge of the β-globin chain. When a patient’s hemolysate is subjected to hemoglobin electrophoresis (alkaline or citrate agar), Hb E migrates at a characteristic position distinct from Hb A and Hb S, allowing its specific detection and quantification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progranulin (GRN) mutation",
    "long_answer": "The speech-related, non-fluent/agrammatic variant of primary progressive aphasia is most often linked to heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the GRN gene, which encodes progranulin. GRN mutations lead to reduced progranulin levels, triggering TDP-43 type A frontotemporal lobar degeneration that clinically presents with progressive speech production deficits characteristic of this PPA variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-contrast CT",
    "long_answer": "A low-dose, non-contrast computed tomography (CT) scan of the kidneys, ureters and bladder (CT-KUB) is the guideline-recommended study because it detects both the obstructing calculus and the resulting hydronephrosis with high sensitivity and specificity, without the need for intravenous contrast."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Globus pallidus",
    "long_answer": "In kernicterus from neonatal jaundice, unconjugated bilirubin preferentially deposits in the basal ganglia—most prominently in the globus pallidus. Bilateral pallidal damage disrupts extrapyramidal motor pathways, producing the writhing, athetoid movements characteristic of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Bowenoid papulosis is an HPV-related skin disorder that presents as pigmented papules on the genital skin. Dermatologists are the physicians specifically trained to recognize, biopsy, and manage diseases of the skin and mucocutaneous tissues, including HPV-associated lesions. Therefore, a dermatologist is the specialist who diagnoses and treats bowenoid papulosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease is caused by pathogenic mutations in the PRNP gene, which encodes the prion protein (PrP). In humans, the PRNP gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 20 (region 20p13). Hence, chromosome 20 harbors the gene responsible for this prion disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Symblepharon",
    "long_answer": "Symblepharon is a pathological adhesion in which the palpebral conjunctiva (lining the inner surface of the eyelids) becomes abnormally attached to the bulbar conjunctiva (covering the globe). This can follow trauma, chemical burns, cicatrizing conjunctivitis, or other inflammatory processes that cause conjunctival scarring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatigue",
    "long_answer": "Sheehan’s syndrome (post-partum hypopituitarism) typically leads to deficiencies of thyroid and adrenal hormones, producing marked fatigue and low energy. Generalized, persistent fatigue is likewise one of the most frequently reported constitutional symptoms across many autoimmune diseases (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, multiple sclerosis). Thus, fatigue is a shared, well-recognized symptom between Sheehan’s syndrome and numerous autoimmune disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POC5",
    "long_answer": "The centriolar protein gene POC5 has been linked to Adolescent Idiopathic Scoliosis. In affected families, a small deletion (e.g., c.1604_1605del) introduces a frameshift (p.Leu535Profs*17), altering the C-terminus of POC5. This frameshift impairs normal centriole elongation and ciliary signalling, offering a mechanistic explanation for its contribution to AIS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lines of Blaschko",
    "long_answer": "In ABCD syndrome the hypopigmented and other cutaneous lesions are distributed in a streaked, whorled pattern that maps to the embryonic skin-cell migration lines known as the lines of Blaschko."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "Cancer phobia is classified as a specific phobia, and clinical guidelines for anxiety disorders recommend cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)—particularly techniques such as gradual exposure and cognitive restructuring—as the first-line treatment. CBT targets the maladaptive beliefs that drive the fear of developing cancer and systematically reduces anxiety through controlled exposure to feared cancer-related stimuli, making it the recommended therapy for managing anxiety in individuals with cancer phobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "In Sanjad–Sakati syndrome, congenital absence or severe under-development of the parathyroid glands results in markedly low parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels. PTH is essential for maintaining serum calcium; its deficiency impairs renal calcium reabsorption and bone calcium mobilization, leading to persistent hypocalcemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Anton syndrome (cortical blindness with denial of blindness) results from bilateral occipital-lobe damage—typically due to stroke or other cerebral insults—placing it squarely within disorders of the central nervous system. Diagnosis of the cortical lesion, assessment of visual pathways, and management of the underlying cerebral pathology are handled by neurologists; therefore, the medical specialty of neurology is responsible for its diagnosis and treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apnea",
    "long_answer": "During benign familial neonatal seizures, affected infants can experience apnea—a brief, seizure-related cessation of breathing—alongside tonic limb stiffening and other ictal signs. The momentary lack of respiratory effort is a recognized clinical feature of these seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMM2-CDG",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PMM2 gene impair phosphomannomutase-2 activity, disrupting N-linked glycoprotein synthesis and causing PMM2-CDG (Congenital Disorder of Glycosylation type Ia). This multisystem genetic disease commonly presents with neurological features such as hypotonia, ataxia, and progressive peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANKH",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ANKH gene, which encodes a trans-membrane pyrophosphate transport regulator crucial for crystal-related inflammatory reactions, are strongly linked to calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystal deposition disease. Altered ANKH function elevates extracellular inorganic pyrophosphate levels, promoting CPP crystal formation in cartilage and driving the characteristic inflammation of CPPD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichophyton rubrum, KOH preparation",
    "long_answer": "Onychomycosis is most often caused by the dermatophyte Trichophyton rubrum. Confirmation is usually obtained by microscopic examination of nail scrapings placed in a potassium-hydroxide (KOH) preparation, which dissolves keratin and reveals the fungal hyphae characteristic of dermatophyte infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenic flexure",
    "long_answer": "The splenic (left colic) flexure lies at the junction of the transverse and descending colon. It is a vascular watershed zone supplied by terminal branches of the middle colic artery from the superior mesenteric artery and the left colic artery from the inferior mesenteric artery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of thyroid hormone (hypothyroidism) is a well-known cause of distal peripheral neuropathy owing to metabolic and myxedematous changes in peripheral nerves. The same condition blunts central respiratory drive and weakens respiratory muscles, so patients frequently develop hypoventilation or sleep-related hypopnea. Thus, hypothyroidism is a single pathological state that explains both clinical problems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary artery stenosis",
    "long_answer": "Keutel syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the matrix Gla protein gene. Besides cartilage calcification and brachytelephalangy, the characteristic cardiovascular manifestation is narrowing of the main and/or peripheral pulmonary arteries—pulmonary artery stenosis—making this the heart condition classically associated with Keutel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Etanercept",
    "long_answer": "Etanercept is a biologic agent that binds and neutralises tumour necrosis factor-α (TNF-α). It has been reported as an effective treatment in refractory cases of chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis (CRMO), where inhibition of TNF-α reduces the autoinflammatory bone lesions characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Enlarged lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic multicentric Castleman disease (iMCD) typically presents with generalized enlargement of lymph nodes. This lymphadenopathy—characterized by visibly or palpably enlarged lymph nodes—is a core clinical sign of iMCD and, by definition, a hallmark feature of lymphadenopathy itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Joint contractures",
    "long_answer": "CAMFAK syndrome includes arthrogryposis (the “A” in its name), which manifests as multiple congenital joint contractures. Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita itself is defined by the same fixed contractures of joints present at birth. Therefore, congenital joint contractures are a symptom found in both CAMFAK syndrome and arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fresh frozen plasma",
    "long_answer": "Kasabach–Merritt syndrome causes a consumptive coagulopathy with depletion of clotting factors. Transfusion of fresh frozen plasma supplies the missing coagulation factors and is therefore the blood product given to correct the coagulopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis (especially the common Freeman-Sheldon/Sheldon-Hall spectrum) is classically linked to heterozygous missense mutations in the MYH3 gene, which encodes the embryonic myosin heavy-chain protein. Defective MYH3 impairs skeletal-muscle contractile development, producing the congenital distal joint contractures that characterize distal arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bradycardia",
    "long_answer": "Both sinus node dysfunction (sick sinus syndrome) and systemic hypothermia depress the automaticity of the sino-atrial node, slowing its firing rate and thereby producing an abnormally low heart rate—clinically termed bradycardia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses are inherited lysosomal storage disorders caused by specific enzyme-gene defects; their diagnosis, enzyme replacement or gene-based management, and family genetic counseling are handled by the medical (clinical) genetics specialty, which deals with inborn errors of metabolism in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EFTUD2",
    "long_answer": "Mandibulofacial dysostosis-microcephaly syndrome (MFDM) is an autosomal-dominant craniofacial disorder produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions in the EFTUD2 gene, which encodes a core GTPase component of the U5 spliceosomal complex. Pathogenic EFTUD2 variants disrupt pre-mRNA splicing, leading to the characteristic mandibulofacial anomalies and congenital microcephaly seen in MFDM patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "In McArdle’s disease (glycogen-storage disease type V) skeletal muscle lacks functional glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase). Without this enzyme, muscle glycogen cannot be broken down to glucose-1-phosphate, so glycolysis is greatly curtailed during exercise. Because little pyruvate is generated, almost no pyruvate is reduced to lactate, and blood lactic acid levels fail to rise despite exertion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase; glycogen-storage disease type V, McArdle disease) produces exercise-induced muscle cramps, fatigue, myoglobinuria, and elevated muscle glycogen—clinical features that closely mirror those seen in phosphofructokinase (Tarui) deficiency because both defects block glycolysis in skeletal muscle and prevent efficient utilization of stored glycogen for ATP production during exercise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈49 hours",
    "long_answer": "Pharmacokinetic studies in psoriatic patients show acitretin is eliminated with a mean terminal half-life of about 49 hours (roughly two days; range 33–96 h). This value reflects acitretin itself, distinct from its alcohol-derived metabolite etretinate, which persists much longer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vomiting",
    "long_answer": "In acute hepatitis B, gastrointestinal upset is common; patients frequently experience nausea and especially vomiting. Repeated vomiting can lead to significant fluid loss, making it a well-recognized cause of dehydration during the acute phase of the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Amniocentesis allows retrieval of fetal cells and amniotic fluid in mid-pregnancy; enzyme activity assays or molecular genetic tests performed on this material can definitively identify or exclude specific inborn errors of metabolism, making the procedure a standard confirmatory diagnostic test when such disorders are suspected prenatally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gabapentin",
    "long_answer": "Gabapentin, an anticonvulsant that modulates voltage-gated calcium channels and dampens ectopic neuronal firing, has been shown to reduce the severe burning neuropathic pain characteristic of primary erythromelalgia and is commonly prescribed for symptomatic relief."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Escherichia coli O157:H7",
    "long_answer": "Shiga-toxin–producing Escherichia coli, classically the O157:H7 serotype, is a frequent bacterial cause of pediatric gastroenteritis. These strains colonize the intestinal mucosa, trigger watery and often bloody diarrhea in children, and carry phage-encoded stx genes that direct the synthesis of Shiga toxin. The toxin can damage intestinal endothelium and, in susceptible children, lead to hemolytic-uremic syndrome, underscoring the clinical significance of this organism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Humerus",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis pilaris characteristically manifests on the posterolateral (outer) aspects of the upper arms. The long bone that underlies and gives structure to this region of the upper limb is the humerus; therefore, the humerus is the bone located in the area where keratosis pilaris commonly appears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MGMT",
    "long_answer": "The O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) gene encodes a critical DNA repair enzyme that removes alkyl adducts from the O6 position of guanine. In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, the MGMT promoter CpG island is frequently hypermethylated, leading to transcriptional silencing of the gene. This epigenetic inactivation is well documented in multiple malignant neoplasms, underscoring MGMT’s role as a DNA repair gene whose loss contributes to tumorigenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Claudin-1",
    "long_answer": "Claudin-1 is a transmembrane tight-junction protein whose reduced expression in the epidermis has been repeatedly documented in patients with atopic dermatitis. Loss or down-regulation of claudin-1 disrupts tight-junction integrity, increases transepidermal water loss, and facilitates penetration of allergens and microbes, thereby contributing to the characteristic skin barrier dysfunction seen in this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naxos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Naxos syndrome exhibits the identical cutaneous pattern seen in Carvajal syndrome—woolly hair and diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma—while associating it with arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy. Because both conditions result from mutations in desmosomal proteins (plakoglobin in Naxos, desmoplakin in Carvajal), they share the same dermatologic phenotype of diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "> 0.2 (calcium/creatinine)",
    "long_answer": "A random (spot) urine calcium-to-creatinine ratio exceeding 0.20 mg Ca per mg creatinine is the accepted biochemical cut-off for hypercalciuria in children and adults; values above this level correspond to a 24-h urinary calcium excretion >4 mg/kg/day, confirming pathologic calcium loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium lauryl sulfate",
    "long_answer": "Sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS), a common detergent and foaming agent in many toothpastes, is well documented to irritate the oral mucosa. Clinical studies show that SLS can disrupt the mucosal barrier, increasing ulcer formation and recurrence of aphthous stomatitis (recurrent aphthous ulcers). Switching to SLS-free toothpaste often reduces the frequency and severity of these ulcers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type I (hypoxemic)",
    "long_answer": "Right-atrial isomerism is frequently accompanied by obstructed total anomalous pulmonary venous return and other lesions that cause severe pulmonary venous hypertension and alveolar flooding. These processes produce profound arterial hypoxaemia with normal or low PaCO₂, the pattern that defines Type I (hypoxemic) respiratory failure; therefore, patients with right atrial isomerism are predisposed to Type I respiratory failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Escherichia coli",
    "long_answer": "Escherichia coli is the standard bacterial work-horse in molecular biology and industrial biotechnology. Numerous E. coli genes—most famously the ribosomal RNA operons—possess two σ70 promoters that lie only a few nucleotides apart or even overlap. These closely spaced promoters make E. coli a textbook example for studying promoter architecture and regulation, while its ease of culture, well-mapped genetics and extensive toolkit have made it the most widely used microbial host in biotechnology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTDN1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TTDN1 gene (also known as C7orf11) are responsible for non-photosensitive trichothiodystrophy. This disorder has been described under several eponyms, including Tay syndrome and BIDS (Brittle hair-Intellectual impairment-Decreased fertility-Short stature) syndrome. Therefore, TTDN1 is the gene common to both Tay syndrome and BIDS syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Fountain syndrome is a rare genetic condition that results from pathogenic variants inherited in an autosomal recessive manner; affected individuals have two mutant alleles, one from each carrier parent. This mode of inheritance, rather than dominant or X-linked, defines Fountain syndrome as an autosomal recessive disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kaposi sarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Kaposi sarcoma is a vascular malignancy driven by HHV-8 infection. Its earliest cutaneous lesions are small purplish-red or violaceous macules and papules produced by proliferating blood-filled vascular channels; these can be mistaken for an ordinary bruise before they enlarge into nodules or plaques."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlorpropamide",
    "long_answer": "Chlorpropamide, a first-generation sulfonylurea, is sometimes used to treat partial central diabetes insipidus because it potentiates the renal action of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). The same ADH-potentiating effect can lead to excessive water reabsorption, dilutional hyponatremia, and the clinical picture of syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Freeman-Sheldon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Freeman-Sheldon syndrome (also called whistling-face or distal arthrogryposis type 2A) is defined by craniofacial anomalies such as microstomia, pursed lips and mid-face hypoplasia together with malformations of the extremities, notably severe contractures, camptodactyly and club-foot. Because these limb deformities arise from congenital distal arthrogryposis, the condition is classed among musculoskeletal abnormalities, fulfilling the association described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-Hodgkin lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Non-Hodgkin lymphoma characteristically produces painless peripheral lymphadenopathy, and the inguinal (groin) lymph nodes are among the nodal groups most often enlarged. In contrast, Hodgkin lymphoma more typically involves cervical or mediastinal nodes. Therefore, swollen lymph nodes in the groin are most commonly associated with Non-Hodgkin lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing spondylitis, a strongly HLA-B27–linked genetic spondyloarthropathy, carries a markedly increased risk of acute anterior uveitis; up to one-third of patients experience recurrent uveitic episodes due to the same immune-mediated inflammation that drives the axial arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Crome syndrome is a rare congenital-genetic disorder that presents in infancy with findings such as microcephaly, severe growth retardation, retinal degeneration and other developmental abnormalities. Because the condition is evident from birth or early childhood and requires coordinated evaluation of growth, development, vision and hearing, its diagnosis and longitudinal care are classically overseen by a pediatric specialist (often together with pediatric geneticists and other subspecialists). Thus, the medical specialty most likely to handle both diagnosis and management is Pediatrics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "World Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "In the WHO Classification of Tumours of Soft Tissue and Bone, the World Health Organization designates fibromatosis (also called desmoid-type fibromatosis) as an “intermediate (locally aggressive)” soft-tissue tumor, reflecting its tendency for local invasion without metastatic potential."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROR2",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal recessive Robinow syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ROR2 gene, which encodes the receptor tyrosine kinase–like orphan receptor 2 involved in WNT signaling and skeletal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is almost always caused by pathogenic mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1. FBN1 is located on chromosome 15q21, and defective fibrillin-1 disrupts microfibril formation in connective tissue, producing the skeletal, ocular, and cardiovascular manifestations characteristic of Marfan syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Superior thyroid artery",
    "long_answer": "The superior thyroid artery is the first anterior branch of the external carotid artery. It descends anterior to the neck structures to reach and vascularise the superior poles of the thyroid gland, providing its primary arterial supply in conjunction with the inferior thyroid artery from the thyrocervical trunk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dravet syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Dravet syndrome (severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy) is a genetic epilepsy most often caused by de-novo loss-of-function mutations in the SCN1A sodium-channel gene. The disorder characteristically starts in the first year of life with prolonged febrile seizures, making it a well-recognized genetic condition associated with febrile seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Office of Rare Diseases (NIH)",
    "long_answer": "Lyngstadaas syndrome is included in the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH) Office of Rare Diseases (ORD) database, which designates it as a rare disease within its Genetic and Rare Diseases (GARD) information resources."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX4",
    "long_answer": "Ischiopatellar dysplasia (small-patella syndrome) is an autosomal-dominant skeletal disorder caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the TBX4 gene, which is located on chromosome 17q23.1–q23.2. Mutations in TBX4, a T-box transcription factor essential for limb and pelvic development, underlie the characteristic absent or hypoplastic patellae and pelvic anomalies seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetic ketoacidosis",
    "long_answer": "In diabetes, especially during diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperglycaemia and acidosis impair neutrophil chemotaxis and phagocytosis while releasing free iron into serum; this combination both weakens innate immune defense and provides nutrients that stimulate Mucorales growth, making diabetics far more susceptible to mucormycosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abdominal pain",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatoblastoma usually grows to a considerable size before detection and its mass effect or ductal obstruction commonly produces marked upper-abdominal/epigastric pain. This pain is the symptom reported most often in published series and is the chief complaint that typically brings patients to the emergency room for evaluation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Doxycycline",
    "long_answer": "Amblyomma americanum (the lone star tick) transmits Ehrlichia chaffeensis, the cause of human monocytic ehrlichiosis. The established first-line therapy for this bacterial infection is doxycycline, which rapidly clears the intracellular Ehrlichia and improves outcomes when started promptly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric neurology",
    "long_answer": "Pachygyria is a neuronal-migration disorder that causes thickened cerebral gyri, developmental delay, and often refractory epilepsy. Assessment of brain malformations, seizure management, and neurodevelopmental follow-up fall within the expertise of pediatric neurologists, making pediatric neurology the specialty most commonly responsible for treating children with pachygyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cervical flexion MRI",
    "long_answer": "In Hirayama disease, dynamic magnetic resonance imaging of the cervical spine obtained in neck flexion shows anterior displacement of the posterior dura and engorged epidural veins compressing the spinal cord. This characteristic appearance on cervical flexion MRI confirms the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIGL",
    "long_answer": "CHIME syndrome (Coloboma, Heart defects, Ichthyosiform dermatosis, Mental retardation, and Ear anomalies) has been conclusively linked to loss-of-function mutations in the PIGL gene, which encodes an enzyme required for early steps of glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor biosynthesis. Pathogenic PIGL variants disrupt GPI-anchor formation, leading to the multisystem manifestations characteristic of CHIME syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Illusion",
    "long_answer": "An illusion is the misinterpretation of a real external stimulus (e.g., mistaking a coat on a chair for a person). Because an actual sensory input is present, the distortion is less severe than a true hallucination, in which perception occurs without any stimulus. Therefore, psychiatry classifies an illusion as a mild form of hallucination and lists it under perceptual disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets",
    "long_answer": "X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (caused by PHEX gene mutations) is an inherited disorder that increases fibroblast growth factor 23, producing excessive renal phosphate wasting. The resulting chronic hypophosphatemia impairs bone mineralization, leading to vitamin D-resistant rickets and osteomalacia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paraglottic space",
    "long_answer": "The paraglottic space is a fat-filled potential space that lies lateral to the laryngeal ventricle, between the mucosa and the thyroid cartilage. Because it contains loose connective tissue and lymphatics, laryngeal carcinomas—especially supraglottic and trans-glottic tumours—readily extend into this space and can then spread beyond the larynx into the neck."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin hyperpigmentation",
    "long_answer": "After bilateral adrenalectomy, loss of cortisol feedback can allow an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor to enlarge (Nelson’s syndrome). The excess ACTH is processed to melanocyte-stimulating peptides, producing diffuse skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation, which—together with visual field defects from the expanding tumor—is a classic clinical clue to Nelson’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric orthopedic surgeon",
    "long_answer": "Scoliosis is a musculoskeletal deformity of the spine; in children it is evaluated and managed by orthopedic surgeons who have additional training in pediatric musculoskeletal disorders. These pediatric orthopedic surgeons are experts in growth-related spinal curvature, bracing strategies, and corrective spine surgery, making them the specialists who treat scoliosis in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB11",
    "long_answer": "Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 2 is caused by mutations in the ABCB11 gene, which encodes the bile salt export pump (BSEP) located on the canalicular membrane of hepatocytes. Defective ABCB11 impairs biliary secretion of bile acids, leading to cholestasis characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aorta",
    "long_answer": "In levo-transposition of the great arteries (congenitally corrected TGA), there is atrioventricular and ventriculo-arterial discordance. The morphologic right ventricle is connected to the systemic outflow tract, so it ejects oxygenated blood into the aorta, which then delivers it to the body."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Platelets (thrombocytes)",
    "long_answer": "Acute megakaryoblastic leukemia is characterized by uncontrolled growth of malignant megakaryoblasts, the precursors of megakaryocytes. Megakaryocytes normally fragment their cytoplasm to generate platelets (thrombocytes). Thus, the cell fragments whose production is linked to the excessive proliferation of megakaryoblasts in this leukemia are platelets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis results from biallelic pathogenic mutations in the CFTR gene on chromosome 7. Because the disease manifests only when both alleles are defective, it follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern: carriers with one mutant allele are typically asymptomatic, and children of two carriers have a 25 % chance of being affected, a 50 % chance of being carriers, and a 25 % chance of being unaffected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blalock-Taussig shunt",
    "long_answer": "In a newborn with agenesis of a pulmonary artery, surgeons can establish blood flow to the affected lung by creating a systemic-to-pulmonary arterial shunt. The standard operation is the (modified) Blalock-Taussig shunt, which anastomoses the subclavian or innominate artery to the intrapulmonary artery with a small Gore-Tex tube. This redirects systemic blood into the absent-artery lung, relieves hypoxaemia, promotes pulmonary arterial growth and reduces pulmonary hypertension, making it an accepted initial surgical treatment for neonatal pulmonary artery agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXJ1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the transcription factor FOXJ1 disrupt the formation and motility of ependymal motile cilia that propel cerebrospinal fluid. The resulting ciliary dysfunction impairs CSF flow and clearance, leading to congenital or early-onset hydrocephalus. Thus, FOXJ1 is the key genetic factor linking defective cilia to the development of hydrocephalus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto’s encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "Hashimoto’s encephalopathy, also called steroid-responsive encephalopathy associated with autoimmune thyroiditis, is a rare neurological disorder that occurs in patients with autoimmune (Hashimoto’s) thyroiditis. It can manifest with fluctuating cognitive impairment, seizures, myoclonus, and neuropsychiatric symptoms, including acute psychosis. High serum antithyroid antibodies and a typically favorable response to corticosteroids support the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSPG2",
    "long_answer": "Schwartz–Jampel syndrome (SJS) type 1, the osteochondrodysplasia characterized by myotonia and skeletal anomalies, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HSPG2 gene. HSPG2 encodes perlecan, a heparan-sulfate proteoglycan essential for cartilage and neuromuscular junction integrity; pathogenic HSPG2 variants disrupt perlecan’s function, leading to the skeletal dysplasia and myotonic features typical of SJS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IKKα",
    "long_answer": "IκB kinase-α (IKKα, encoded by the CHUK gene) functions as a tumour-suppressor in epidermal keratinocytes. Experimental deletion of IKKα in mouse skin or marked reduction of its expression in human epidermis blocks normal keratinocyte differentiation, causes hyper-proliferation and markedly increases susceptibility to malignant transformation. Accordingly, loss or severe down-regulation of IKKα is frequently detected in cutaneous squamous-cell carcinomas, indicating that its absence is a key step in the progression of normal keratinocytes to malignant cSCC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WDHA syndrome",
    "long_answer": "A VIP-secreting tumour (VIPoma) characteristically produces WDHA syndrome—Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, and Achlorhydria. The resulting severe hypokalemia often manifests as lethargy, linking this syndrome directly to the symptom mentioned."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymus",
    "long_answer": "Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency causes accumulation of deoxyadenosine and dATP, which are toxic to developing lymphocytes. The ensuing severe combined immunodeficiency prevents normal T-cell maturation, resulting in a markedly hypoplastic or virtually absent thymus—the lymphoid organ where T cells normally develop."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GREB1L",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the GREB1L gene have been identified in families where some carriers present with Müllerian agenesis (MRKH syndrome) while others have different genitourinary anomalies or are unaffected, demonstrating marked variable expressivity. GREB1L encodes a transcriptional co-activator important for embryonic development of the reproductive and urinary tracts, and its mutations show incomplete penetrance and diverse phenotypic outcomes characteristic of variable expressivity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoplasmic reticulum",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the Bestrophin-1 (BEST1) chloride channel cause the protein to misfold and become retained within the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) instead of being trafficked to the basolateral plasma membrane of retinal pigment epithelium cells. This ER retention/mislocalization disrupts BEST1 function and underlies several retinal degenerative diseases such as Best vitelliform macular dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal-fetal medicine",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 9 is usually detected before birth through procedures such as high-resolution fetal ultrasound, chorionic villus sampling, or amniocentesis. These investigations are routinely ordered and interpreted by maternal-fetal medicine (perinatology) specialists, the obstetrics subspecialty focused on high-risk pregnancies and prenatal genetic diagnosis; hence they are the physicians most likely to make a prenatal diagnosis of trisomy 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene on chromosome 15. A single mutated copy of FBN1 is sufficient to produce the phenotype, and affected individuals have a 50% chance of passing the mutation to each offspring, fulfilling the criteria for autosomal-dominant inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatigue",
    "long_answer": "Fatigue is one of the most frequently reported somatic complaints in somatic symptom disorder, where patients experience distressing tiredness without an identifiable medical cause. The same symptom can arise in myasthenia gravis, an autoimmune disease of the neuromuscular junction that produces activity-induced muscle weakness and a characteristic sense of fatigue that improves with rest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The human PNPLA2 gene, which encodes adipose triglyceride lipase and whose mutations cause neutral lipid storage disease with myopathy, is mapped to chromosome 11 (cytogenetic band 11p15.5)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAB39B",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function of the X-linked vesicle-trafficking gene RAB39B, whether by pathogenic point mutation or Xq28 micro-deletion, produces a syndromic disorder characterized by intellectual disability and childhood epilepsy; affected males typically go on to develop early-onset parkinsonism. Hence, alteration or deletion of RAB39B is the genetic lesion that causes epilepsy in the early-onset parkinsonism-intellectual disability syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)",
    "long_answer": "Pain in nail–patella syndrome usually arises from joint dysplasia and early osteoarthritis; first-line pharmacologic management is with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, which reduce inflammation and provide analgesia for musculoskeletal pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase 2",
    "long_answer": "Apparent mineralocorticoid excess results from deficient activity of the renal enzyme 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which normally converts active cortisol to inactive cortisone. When this enzyme is reduced, cortisol accumulates and overstimulates mineralocorticoid receptors, producing sodium retention, hypertension, and the characteristic suppression of renin release."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dampa Tiger Reserve",
    "long_answer": "Camera-trap studies in Mizoram’s Dampa Tiger Reserve have estimated densities of around five clouded leopards (Neofelis nebulosa) per 100 km², a figure that ranks among the highest recorded anywhere in South or South-East Asia. Consequently, Dampa is recognised for harbouring one of the region’s most substantial clouded leopard populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome is a genetic disorder (most often from pathogenic variants in KMT2D or KDM6A) that presents with conductive hearing loss caused by middle-ear malformations, eyelid ptosis as part of its characteristic facial gestalt, and a spectrum of skeletal anomalies including vertebral defects, brachydactyly, and hip dislocation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical resection",
    "long_answer": "Hemangioblastomas are highly vascular, well-circumscribed tumors that can usually be removed en bloc. Complete surgical resection is therefore the standard first-line therapy; adjuvant radiosurgery or observation is reserved for unresectable or residual lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T-box (TBX) gene family",
    "long_answer": "Persistent truncus arteriosus is frequently observed in 22q11.2-deletion (DiGeorge) syndrome and in animal models lacking the transcription factor TBX1. TBX1 belongs to the T-box (TBX) gene family, whose members direct cardiac outflow-tract septation by controlling neural-crest cell migration and second heart-field development. Disruption of this gene family, particularly TBX1, therefore leads to failure of truncal septation and the congenital defect known as persistent truncus arteriosus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiofrequency catheter ablation",
    "long_answer": "In Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome, atrial fibrillation can conduct rapidly to the ventricles via the accessory pathway. Definitive treatment is achieved by radiofrequency catheter ablation, which targets and eliminates the accessory conduction pathway, thereby preventing both pre-excitation and AF-related rapid ventricular responses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orphanet",
    "long_answer": "The Orphanet rare-disease database entry for “Galactorrhea-hyperprolactinemia” (ORPHA:3103) enumerates pregnancy as a recognized physiological/medical cause of the condition, alongside other etiologies such as pituitary adenoma, hypothyroidism and drug-induced hyperprolactinemia. Thus, Orphanet explicitly lists pregnancy as a possible cause of galactorrhea with hyperprolactinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PDHA1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of congenital lactic acidosis accompanied by intellectual disability are due to loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked PDHA1 gene, which encodes the E1-α subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. Defective PDHA1 blocks the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, forcing excess pyruvate to be reduced to lactate, thereby causing severe neonatal lactic acidosis. Energy failure in the developing brain leads to neurodevelopmental delay and long-term intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apert syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome (acrocephalosyndactyly) is a congenital disorder caused mainly by activating mutations in the FGFR2 gene. It leads to premature fusion of cranial sutures, producing characteristic mid-face abnormalities, and to complex syndactyly that impairs normal formation of the fingers and toes—thereby affecting both facial features and digits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL6R locus (1q21.3)",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies have shown that the IL6R locus on chromosome 1q21.3, which encodes the interleukin-6 receptor, confers susceptibility to both Takayasu’s arteritis and atherosclerotic disease. Variants such as rs2228145 (Asp358Ala) modulate IL-6 signalling, influencing vascular inflammation in Takayasu’s arteritis and systemic inflammation that promotes or protects from atherosclerosis, linking this single chromosomal region to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus",
    "long_answer": "Epstein–Barr virus (EBV), a human gamma-herpesvirus that establishes latent infection in B lymphocytes, is detected in the Reed-Sternberg tumor cells of a substantial proportion of Hodgkin lymphoma cases. Epidemiological and molecular studies show that prior EBV infection increases the risk of developing Hodgkin lymphoma, making EBV the virus most clearly associated with this malignancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Agoraphobia",
    "long_answer": "Agoraphobia is an anxiety disorder marked by intense fear or avoidance of situations—such as public places, crowds, or traveling—where escape might be difficult or help unavailable if panic-like symptoms occur. Epidemiological studies consistently show high comorbidity between agoraphobia (with or without panic attacks) and substance use disorders, as individuals often turn to alcohol or drugs to cope with the distressing anxiety, fulfilling the criteria stated in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Extensive epidemiological data, clinical studies, and public-health guidance agree that people with diabetes (both type 1 and type 2) have a markedly higher likelihood of developing severe manifestations of COVID-19 compared with non-diabetic individuals of the same age. Large cohort studies from China, Europe, and the United States show that diabetes roughly doubles the risk of hospitalisation, admission to intensive-care units, and death. Meta-analyses published in peer-reviewed journals report pooled odds ratios for severe disease or mortality in the range of 1.5–3.0 after adjusting for age and other comorbidities. Mechanistically, chronic hyperglycaemia can impair innate and adaptive immune responses, promote a pro-inflammatory state, and is frequently accompanied by cardiovascular, renal, and obesity-related comorbidities, all of which compound COVID-19 severity. Consequently, authoritative bodies such as the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), the World Health Organization (WHO), and the International Diabetes Federation list diabetes as a high-risk condition for severe COVID-19. Therefore, the statement is supported by the established scientific consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complete surgical excision",
    "long_answer": "Onychomatricoma, a benign tumor of the nail matrix that produces xanthonychia (yellow nail discoloration) and can resemble an acral fibrokeratoma, is treated definitively by complete surgical excision of the lesion, which removes the proliferating matrix filaments and prevents recurrence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichinella spiralis",
    "long_answer": "Trichinella spiralis is a nematode parasite commonly called the “pork worm” because humans usually acquire it by eating under-cooked pork containing encysted larvae. After ingestion, larvae are released, mature in the intestine, and their newborn larvae disseminate hematogenously; during this migration they can invade the pulmonary parenchyma, provoking marked peripheral eosinophilia and the characteristic eosinophilic pneumonia seen in early trichinellosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is characterized by intellectual disability and distinctive physical features including brachydactyly (short fingers). Individuals with Down syndrome have a markedly increased incidence of congenital heart disease—particularly atrioventricular septal defects and ventricular or atrial septal defects—making it the genetic disorder classically linked to both short fingers and heightened risk of heart malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "Lung cancers can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), a paraneoplastic coagulopathy in which tumor-derived pro-coagulant factors activate the clotting cascade systemically. This results in the formation of microthrombi throughout the vasculature, consuming platelets and clotting factors and paradoxically leading to both widespread thrombosis and bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Freiberg disease is an osteochondrosis (avascular necrosis) of a metatarsal head, producing fore-foot pain and structural collapse of bone. Disorders of bones and joints of the foot are typically managed by orthopedic surgeons, especially those specializing in foot and ankle, making orthopedic surgery the specialty most commonly responsible for treating Freiberg disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleural effusion",
    "long_answer": "POEMS syndrome frequently presents with pleural effusion, the abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity surrounding the lungs. This effusion contributes to dyspnea and is part of the extravascular volume overload manifestations commonly observed in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Grebe type",
    "long_answer": "Among the recognized subtypes of acromesomelic dysplasia (Maroteaux, Hunter-Thompson, Grebe, etc.), the Grebe type is distinctive for segmentation defects of the carpal and tarsal bones that lead to congenital fusion (carpal and tarsal coalition). Radiographs classically show coalesced or block-like carpal bones, making carpal coalition a hallmark of the Grebe variety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uridine triacetate",
    "long_answer": "In September 2015 the U.S. FDA granted approval to uridine triacetate (brand name Xuriden) as the first specific therapy for hereditary orotic aciduria. Uridine triacetate is an orally-bioavailable prodrug of uridine that bypasses the deficiency of the enzyme UMP synthase, replenishing pyrimidine nucleotides, normalizing hematologic parameters and reducing urinary orotate accumulation in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3 missense mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous missense mutations in the MYH3 gene, which encodes the embryonic myosin heavy-chain 3, are a well-established cause of distal arthrogryposis syndromes. These disorders present with congenital joint contractures (arthrogryposis) and histologically abnormal skeletal muscle tissue. The pathogenic mechanism is a missense (gain-of-function) alteration in MYH3 that disrupts normal sarcomere function, leading to the characteristic contractures and myopathic changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital hypothyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Dalal et al. (2010) described a child with the characteristic features of Primrose syndrome who, from birth, had thyroid hormone deficiency fulfilling criteria for congenital hypothyroidism. This co-occurrence highlighted endocrine involvement as part of the phenotypic spectrum of Primrose syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MGP",
    "long_answer": "Keutel syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by abnormal cartilage calcification, brachytelephalangy, and distinctive facial abnormalities. The condition is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MGP gene (Matrix Gla Protein), which normally inhibits ectopic calcification. Disruption of MGP leads to the craniofacial and skeletal features observed in Keutel syndrome, linking this gene directly to the facial abnormalities characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3CA",
    "long_answer": "Klippel–Trénaunay syndrome is now recognized as part of the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum (PROS). Somatic activating mutations in the PIK3CA gene, which encodes the p110α catalytic subunit of phosphatidylinositol-3-kinase, hyperactivate the PI3K–AKT–mTOR signaling pathway and drive the combined vascular malformations and tissue overgrowth characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of inherited connective-tissue disorders in which mutations in collagen genes or in collagen-processing enzymes lead to structurally abnormal collagen fibers. The defective collagen weakens the dermal matrix, resulting in markedly increased skin elasticity (hyperextensible, fragile skin) and joint hypermobility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STAT3",
    "long_answer": "The classical autosomal-dominant form of hyper-IgE syndrome is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the STAT3 gene, which encodes the transcription factor Signal Transducer and Activator of Transcription 3. Defective STAT3 signaling impairs Th17 cell differentiation and cytokine responses, leading to recurrent staphylococcal infections, eczema, skeletal abnormalities, and markedly elevated serum IgE – the hallmarks of hyperimmunoglobulin E syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noonan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant developmental disorder caused most often by mutations in RAS–MAPK pathway genes (e.g., PTPN11). It produces characteristic facial features (hypertelorism, down-slanting palpebral fissures, low-set ears) together with multiple skeletal abnormalities such as short stature, pectus deformities and the typical increased carrying angle of the elbows known as cubitus valgus. The presence of cubitus valgus is therefore a well-described component of the skeletal phenotype in Noonan syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the IRF6 gene, which encodes the transcription factor Interferon Regulatory Factor 6, disrupt epithelial and craniofacial development. Such mutations are the primary cause of Van der Woude syndrome and account for a significant proportion of nonsyndromic cleft lip and cleft palate, firmly establishing IRF6 mutation as a key genetic driver of these malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Saint Helena Island",
    "long_answer": "St. Helena familial genu valgum was first identified in an extended family living on Saint Helena Island in the South Atlantic Ocean; the disorder takes its name from this isolated island where the initial kindred with the severe hereditary knock-knee deformity was described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NMDA receptor",
    "long_answer": "In hyperammonemia, excess ammonia increases extracellular glutamate and augments nitric-oxide production, leading to excessive stimulation of N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptors on neurons. Overactivation of these ionotropic glutamate receptors triggers calcium influx, oxidative/nitrosative stress, and excitotoxic neuronal damage, making NMDA receptor overactivation a key mechanism in ammonia-induced brain injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone tissue",
    "long_answer": "Avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis) occurs when the blood supply to a region of bone is interrupted. The resulting ischemia causes death of the bone tissue (osseous tissue) in the epiphysis, weakening the structural integrity of the bone, producing joint pain, and eventually leading to subchondral collapse and secondary arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenytoin",
    "long_answer": "Phenytoin, a classic antiepileptic drug, commonly produces nystagmus as a dose-related adverse effect; this ocular oscillation is the same symptom that appears in benign paroxysmal positional vertigo."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAPN3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CAPN3 gene, which encodes the muscle-specific protease calpain-3, are responsible for limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A (LGMD2A). LGMD2A accounts for the largest proportion of LGMD cases worldwide—reported in many cohorts to represent 20–40 % of all LGMD patients—making CAPN3 mutation the most frequent genetic cause of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy globally."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Iridocorneal Endothelial (ICE) syndrome is a disorder of the corneal endothelium and iris that can lead to secondary angle-closure glaucoma and corneal edema. Diagnosis and management—including slit-lamp examination, gonioscopy, intraocular pressure control, laser or surgical glaucoma procedures, and possible corneal transplantation—are carried out by ophthalmologists, the medical specialists trained to treat diseases of the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Von Meyenburg complex",
    "long_answer": "A bile duct hamartoma is classically referred to as a Von Meyenburg complex, an eponym honoring the Swiss pathologist Hanns von Meyenburg who first described these benign malformative lesions of intrahepatic bile ducts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Excess parathyroid hormone in hyperparathyroidism stimulates osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, progressively producing osteoporosis, and raises serum calcium and phosphate excretion, promoting nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis. The resulting calcific injury can progress to chronic kidney disease, so hyperparathyroidism is a single condition that can lead to both osteoporosis and chronic kidney disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrophage activation syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis, an uncommon but potentially life-threatening complication is macrophage activation syndrome (MAS). MAS represents a form of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis triggered by excessive activation of T-lymphocytes and macrophages, leading to high fever, cytopenias, liver dysfunction, coagulopathy, and markedly elevated ferritin levels. Prompt recognition and immunosuppressive treatment are critical because MAS carries a high mortality if untreated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis",
    "long_answer": "Cold abscesses are collections of pus that lack the heat, redness and acute inflammatory reaction typical of ordinary abscesses. They are classically produced in tuberculosis, where infection by Mycobacterium tuberculosis elicits a granulomatous rather than an acute neutrophilic response; necrotic material therefore accumulates quietly, giving rise to a ‘cold’ abscess without marked inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "dictionary",
    "long_answer": "Bathophobia, meaning an irrational fear of depths, is a lexicographic term rather than a formal diagnostic category; its definition is given in standard English and medical dictionaries (e.g., Merriam-Webster, Dorland’s). Thus, you would locate the definition in a dictionary."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Linoleic acid",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of linoleic acid, the principal omega-6 essential fatty acid, disrupts epidermal barrier lipid synthesis and eicosanoid production. This produces a scaly, erythematous dermatitis that closely mimics the rash observed in zinc or biotin deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Randomized controlled trials have demonstrated that selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs—e.g., paroxetine, fluvoxamine, sertraline) can reduce the urges and behaviors of problem gambling, while SSRIs are also an established first-line pharmacologic treatment for major depressive disorder. Their efficacy in both conditions is thought to stem from modulation of central serotonergic pathways implicated in impulse control and mood regulation."
}

{
  "short_answer": "yes",
  "long_answer": "Congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is one of the most common congenital viral infections and is well-documented to cause long-term neurologic and neurodevelopmental sequelae. Epidemiologic studies and clinical follow-up of infants with congenital CMV consistently list cognitive and motor developmental delay/intellectual disability among its major complications, alongside sensorineural hearing loss, microcephaly, seizures, and vision impairment. Therefore, developmental disability is a recognized and established potential complication of congenital CMV infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norwood procedure",
    "long_answer": "The Norwood procedure is the standard staged surgical palliation for hypoplastic left heart syndrome that develops after severe fetal aortic stenosis. In Stage I, surgeons create a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt (e.g., modified Blalock–Taussig or Sano shunt) to supply pulmonary blood flow. In Stage II (bidirectional Glenn/hemi-Fontan), that initial shunt is removed and superior vena caval blood is redirected to the pulmonary arteries. Thus the operation explicitly involves first creating and later removing shunts to manage the circulation in infants with HLHS secondary to fetal aortic stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq25–q26.1 locus",
    "long_answer": "Familial cases of Pentalogy of Cantrell have shown an X-linked pattern of inheritance. Linkage studies mapped the responsible region to the X-chromosomal locus Xq25–q26.1, implicating genes within this interval in the pathogenesis of the characteristic midline thoraco-abdominal and cardiac defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Van der Woude syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Van der Woude syndrome is the commonest syndromic form of orofacial clefting. It typically presents with cleft lip with or without cleft palate, and its mildest manifestation can be a sub-mucous cleft or a bifid uvula. Thus, the combination of a cleft lip and a bifid uvula is characteristic of Van der Woude syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lens luxation",
    "long_answer": "Iridodonesis—the trembling or vibration of the iris during eye movement—occurs when the iris loses the stabilising support normally provided by the crystalline lens. In dogs, this loss of support most commonly results from lens luxation (complete or partial displacement of the lens), allowing the iris to wobble freely with ocular motion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephron",
    "long_answer": "Acute tubular necrosis damages the renal tubules within the kidney. The kidney’s fundamental structural and functional unit is the nephron, which comprises the glomerulus and its associated tubule segments and is responsible for filtration, reabsorption, and secretion. Injury to tubular epithelial cells of the nephron underlies the pathophysiology of acute tubular necrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "X-linked Juvenile Retinoschisis is an inherited disorder that causes splitting of the retinal layers and progressive visual loss; evaluation and management require expertise in retinal imaging, electrophysiology, and potential surgical or low-vision interventions, all of which fall under the domain of ophthalmology (often a pediatric or retinal subspecialist)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autism spectrum disorder",
    "long_answer": "In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), several previously distinct pervasive developmental disorders—including childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified—were consolidated under the single diagnostic category ‘Autism spectrum disorder’. This change reflected evidence that these conditions share common symptom domains (social-communication deficits and restricted, repetitive behaviors) and differ mainly in severity rather than in qualitative features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-myeloperoxidase (MPO) ANCA",
    "long_answer": "Microscopic polyangiitis is a small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis strongly associated with perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (p-ANCA). The antigen most frequently targeted by these p-ANCAs is the neutrophil enzyme myeloperoxidase. Therefore, the antibody characteristically present in patients with microscopic polyangiitis is the anti-myeloperoxidase (MPO) ANCA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "If achalasia is not treated during the mid-stage, the chronically stagnant food and saliva cause longstanding mucosal irritation and inflammation. Over years this markedly increases the risk of malignant transformation of the esophageal epithelium, most often resulting in squamous-cell esophageal carcinoma, a life-threatening complication documented to be 10–30 times more common in untreated achalasia than in the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "Management of PHACE syndrome is multidisciplinary; when mental health issues such as anxiety, depression, or behavioral disorders arise, the medical specialty that evaluates and treats these conditions is psychiatry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Arakawa's syndrome II (also known as the hyper-ornithinaemia, hyper-ammonaemia, homocitrullinuria or HHH syndrome) results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC25A15 gene that encodes the mitochondrial ornithine transporter-1. Because the disorder manifests only when both copies of the gene are defective and carrier parents are typically unaffected, it follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngology (ENT)",
    "long_answer": "Wildervanck syndrome, also called cervico-oculo-acoustic syndrome, includes congenital sensorineural deafness as a core feature together with Duane retraction syndrome and Klippel-Feil cervical vertebral fusion. Because accurate diagnosis and management hinge on detailed assessment of the auditory system and provision of interventions such as audiologic testing, hearing aids, or cochlear implantation, the specialty most centrally involved is otolaryngology (ear, nose, and throat). ENT physicians are trained to evaluate and treat inner-ear malformations and related cranial-nerve anomalies, making them the primary specialists for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic diarrhea",
    "long_answer": "Omenn syndrome often presents with chronic diarrhea caused by intestinal inflammation and infections resulting from the underlying immunodeficiency. Chronic diarrhea is likewise a well-recognized driver of childhood malnutrition worldwide because persistent fluid and nutrient losses impair growth and nutrient absorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous (autosomal-dominant) mutations in the PAX6 gene are a well-established cause of Peters’ Anomaly type 2 and other anterior segment dysgeneses, making PAX6 the classic dominantly inherited gene linked to this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALK gene",
    "long_answer": "Activating germ-line mutations in the ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) proto-oncogene can arise at the moment of fertilisation, so the mutation is present in every cell of the embryo. Such de-novo (or inherited) germline ALK mutations are a well-established pre-disposing factor for the development of neuroblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pheochromocytoma",
    "long_answer": "Pheochromocytomas are catecholamine-secreting tumors of the adrenal medulla. Excess release of epinephrine and norepinephrine produces episodic or sustained, potentially life-threatening hypertension that often presents with classic symptoms such as severe headaches, palpitations, and sweating."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Simpson–Golabi–Behmel syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Simpson–Golabi–Behmel syndrome (SGBS) is an overgrowth/obesity condition caused by loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked glypican-3 (GPC3) gene. Because the gene is on the X chromosome, the disorder follows an X-linked recessive inheritance pattern: hemizygous males are affected, whereas carrier females are usually asymptomatic or only mildly affected. A characteristic clinical feature is excessive growth with progressive childhood obesity, alongside coarse facial features, macroglossia and organomegaly, fitting the description of an obesity-associated genetic disorder inherited as X-linked recessive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proton beam therapy",
    "long_answer": "Radiation-induced cerebral cavernous malformations are a documented late complication of cranial irradiation. Multiple clinical series have shown that patients treated with high-dose proton beam therapy for brain or skull-base tumors can develop de-novo CCMs years later. The particle radiation damages endothelial cells and small cerebral vessels, leading to capillary telangiectasia that evolve into cavernous malformations. Therefore, proton beam radiation therapy is a recognized cause of CCMs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PDE3A",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant hypertension with brachydactyly (Bilginturan syndrome) is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the PDE3A gene, which encodes phosphodiesterase-3A. PDE3A is expressed in vascular smooth muscle and cardiac tissue, where it regulates cyclic AMP signalling and thus vascular tone and cardiac contractility. Hyperactive PDE3A variants accelerate cAMP degradation, increasing peripheral vascular resistance and producing the severe, early-onset hypertension that characterises the syndrome alongside brachydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "TAR (thrombocytopenia-absent radius) syndrome is characterized by severe congenital thrombocytopenia that predisposes patients to bleeding; management centers on monitoring platelet counts and providing platelet transfusions or other blood-related therapies. These aspects fall squarely within the expertise of hematologists (typically pediatric hematologists), who are therefore the specialists most commonly responsible for treating patients with TAR syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baclofen",
    "long_answer": "Spasticity in infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy is treated symptomatically. The muscle relaxant baclofen, a GABA-B receptor agonist that acts at the spinal cord to inhibit excitatory neurotransmission, is commonly prescribed to reduce the heightened muscle tone and spasms seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonic contractions",
    "long_answer": "Jacksonian (simple partial motor) seizures originate in a discrete area of the motor cortex and produce repetitive, rhythmic jerking of the affected muscle groups. This repeated jerking is the clinical manifestation of clonic muscle contractions, which may progressively ‘march’ from one region to adjacent ones (the Jacksonian march)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fovea centralis",
    "long_answer": "The fovea centralis, a small depression at the center of the macula lutea, contains the highest density of cone photoreceptors and no rods. This dense packing of cones with minimal neural convergence provides maximal spatial resolution and color discrimination, making the fovea centralis responsible for sharp, high-resolution vision in bright (photopic) light required for tasks such as reading."
}

{
    "short_answer": "nidus",
    "long_answer": "In a cerebral arteriovenous malformation, the direct abnormal tangle where arterial blood passes into veins without an intervening capillary bed is called the nidus. This nidus comprises dysplastic vessels that create the pathological arteriovenous shunt characteristic of an AVM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "New South Wales",
    "long_answer": "In July 2022, two paediatric cases of toxin-producing Corynebacterium diphtheriae were reported in northern New South Wales, prompting NSW Health to declare a diphtheria outbreak—the first locally acquired cases in the state in many decades."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlorhexidine gluconate",
    "long_answer": "Chlorhexidine gluconate is a bis-biguanide antiseptic incorporated in prescription and some over-the-counter mouthwashes. Owing to its high substantivity, it binds to oral tissues and is slowly released, providing broad-spectrum antibacterial activity that suppresses dental plaque biofilm—the primary etiologic factor in gingivitis. Numerous controlled clinical trials and systematic reviews show that daily rinsing with 0.12–0.2 % chlorhexidine mouthwash significantly reduces plaque accumulation and gingival inflammation compared with placebo or essential-oil rinses, making it the most consistently effective anti-gingivitis ingredient in commercial mouthwashes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Essential fructosuria results from deficiency of hepatic ketohexokinase (KHK). Molecular mapping studies have placed the human KHK gene at cytogenetic band 2p23, establishing that the gene responsible for this benign metabolic trait resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "The commonest cause of virilisation and ambiguous external genitalia in otherwise 46,XX (genotypically normal female) infants is classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency. This enzymatic defect results from pathogenic mutations in the CYP21A2 gene, leading to excess adrenal androgen production that masculinises the external genitalia in utero."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fishy-smelling vaginal discharge",
    "long_answer": "In bacterial vaginosis, the hallmark clinical feature is a thin gray-white vaginal discharge that emits a characteristic fishy or amine odor, particularly after sexual intercourse or when potassium hydroxide is applied (the “whiff test”). Unlike vulvovaginal candidiasis, pruritus (itching) is usually absent, making the malodorous discharge the primary and most common symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iron overload",
    "long_answer": "Patients with pyruvate kinase deficiency often require chronic red-cell transfusions for hemolytic anemia. Each unit of transfused blood contains about 200–250 mg of elemental iron, and the body has no active mechanism to excrete excess iron. Over time, the cumulative iron load from repeated transfusions can exceed storage capacity, leading to systemic iron deposition (transfusional hemosiderosis/hemochromatosis) that may damage the liver, heart, and endocrine organs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent laryngeal nerve",
    "long_answer": "The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs in the tracheo-oesophageal groove immediately behind the thyroid gland. As a thyroid carcinoma enlarges it can breach the thyroid capsule and grow straight into adjacent tissues; the nerve is one of the first structures encountered. Consequently, thyroid cancers frequently infiltrate the recurrent laryngeal nerve directly, leading to vocal-cord paralysis without requiring lymphatic or blood-borne spread."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiamine (vitamin B1)",
    "long_answer": "Wernicke encephalopathy is classically caused by a deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1), which impairs cerebral energy metabolism. Crohn’s disease, through small-intestinal inflammation, resection, or malabsorption, can likewise lead to inadequate thiamine uptake. Thus, thiamine deficiency is the common nutritional deficit associated with both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spontaneous cytosine deamination",
    "long_answer": "Cytosine residues in DNA can spontaneously lose their amino group—a deamination reaction that converts cytosine to uracil (and 5-methyl-cytosine to thymine). If the resulting U•G or T•G mismatches are not repaired, they become C→T transition mutations. Defects in DNA repair pathways seen in progeroid syndromes (e.g., Werner, Cockayne) allow these deamination-induced lesions to accumulate, contributing to the mutational load characteristic of premature‐aging disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Telomeres",
    "long_answer": "Hoyeraal–Hreidarsson syndrome is a severe variant of dyskeratosis congenita caused by mutations in telomere-maintenance genes (e.g., DKC1, TERT, RTEL1). A hallmark of the disease is critically short telomeres in somatic cells, far below the age-matched normal range, leading to bone-marrow failure, immunodeficiency and other clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Brain magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is routinely employed to visualize and quantify cerebellar structures. High-resolution T1- and T2-weighted MRI can reveal cerebellar cortical and vermian atrophy, pontine atrophy, and other posterior-fossa structural changes characteristic of autosomal dominant (spinocerebellar) cerebellar ataxias, making it the imaging modality of choice for detecting such abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amygdala",
    "long_answer": "The amygdala is a medial-temporal lobe nucleus that receives convergent inputs from the sensory thalamus and cortical sensory areas, allowing it to integrate and evaluate sensory information. Functional imaging consistently shows hyper-activation of the amygdala in people with generalized anxiety disorder, reflecting its central role in generating and regulating fear and anxiety responses. Because it both processes sensory input and orchestrates emotional reactions, the amygdala is the structure implicated in sensory processing as well as in the pathophysiology of anxiety disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antiandrogen",
    "long_answer": "Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis) is driven by excess testicular steroid production that is independent of gonadotropin control. Therapy therefore focuses on blocking the peripheral action of these steroids. Antiandrogen drugs (e.g., spironolactone, flutamide, bicalutamide) antagonise the androgen receptor, thereby preventing the biological effects of the androgens produced by gonadal steroidogenesis and slowing or reversing the precocious pubertal changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Derek Baxby",
    "long_answer": "The British microbiologist Derek Baxby, a virologist at the University of Liverpool, analysed and published data on zoonotic orthopoxvirus infections and documented 12 confirmed human cases of cowpox that occurred in England between 1965 and 1976, highlighting transmission from domestic animals (especially cats) to humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary amoebic meningoencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Naegleria fowleri, a free-living thermophilic amoeba, can invade the nasal mucosa during exposure to contaminated warm freshwater. It migrates along the olfactory nerve through the cribriform plate into the brain, where it produces a rapidly progressive and often fatal infection called primary amoebic meningoencephalitis (PAM)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "The male infertility seen in Young’s syndrome is caused by obstructive azoospermia of the epididymis; investigation and treatment of such genitourinary tract obstructions are undertaken by urologists (andrology within urology). Therefore, the appropriate specialty for managing the reduced male fertility is Urology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiazide diuretics",
    "long_answer": "Thiazide-type diuretics (e.g., hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone) are widely prescribed antihypertensive agents. A well-documented metabolic adverse effect of this drug class is elevation of serum total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol and triglycerides, thereby producing hyperlipidemia in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-molecular-weight heparin",
    "long_answer": "During pregnancy, anticoagulation for women with mechanical (prosthetic) heart valves is achieved with low-molecular-weight heparin (e.g., enoxaparin) because heparin does not cross the placenta, avoids warfarin-associated teratogenicity, and provides effective prophylaxis against valve thrombosis and deep vein thrombosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apolipoprotein B",
    "long_answer": "Hypobetalipoproteinemia is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the APOB gene, which encodes apolipoprotein B. Apolipoprotein B-100 is the obligatory structural protein of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) particles; it wraps around the LDL lipid core and is required for their assembly, secretion, and receptor binding. Thus, the same protein—apolipoprotein B—is directly implicated in both the genetic disorder hypobetalipoproteinemia and the structural integrity of LDL particles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactin",
    "long_answer": "Prolactin is synthesized and secreted by lactotroph cells of the anterior pituitary gland. Elevated prolactin levels can disrupt the normal balance between estrogens and androgens, leading to relative estrogen excess and stimulation of breast tissue in males, thereby contributing to the development of gynecomastia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator (CFTR) gene is situated on the long arm (q arm) of human chromosome 7, specifically at cytogenetic band 7q31.2. Mutations in this gene disrupt chloride ion transport across epithelial membranes, leading to the multisystem manifestations characteristic of cystic fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Closed circulatory system",
    "long_answer": "Spirurida nematodes, like all nematodes, have no heart or blood vessels and therefore lack a true blood-vascular network. Transport of nutrients and gases occurs by diffusion through the pseudocoelomic fluid, so they do not possess a closed circulatory system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TCOF1",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome is most commonly caused by mutations in the TCOF1 gene, which encodes the nucleolar protein treacle. Genetic mapping and sequencing studies place TCOF1 on the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q32-q33). Pathogenic variants in this chromosomal locus disrupt ribosomal biogenesis during craniofacial development, leading to the characteristic features of Treacher Collins syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type I is caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene, which encodes neurofibromin. Neurofibromin functions as a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) that negatively regulates the RAS signaling pathway by accelerating the conversion of active RAS-GTP to inactive RAS-GDP. Loss of NF1 activity therefore leads to increased RAS pathway signaling, driving the clinical manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial tachycardia with AV block",
    "long_answer": "Digoxin toxicity often produces a combination of increased atrial automaticity and enhanced vagal inhibition of the AV node. This yields a rapid paroxysmal atrial tachycardia that is partially conducted, typically with a 2:1 or higher AV block. The pattern—atrial tachycardia with AV block—is considered the classic, most characteristic arrhythmia seen in digoxin intoxication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "In a subset of patients, chronic lymphocytic leukemia undergoes ‘Richter transformation,’ in which the CLL clone acquires additional genetic lesions (e.g., MYC activation, TP53 loss) and converts into diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL). DLBCL is a high-grade, rapidly proliferating lymphoma that is clinically much more aggressive than indolent CLL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease",
    "long_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is an autosomal-recessive inborn error of metabolism in which mutations in the branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex genes (BCKDHA, BCKDHB, DBT) block the catabolism of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine and valine. Their corresponding keto-acids accumulate, producing a toxic buildup of organic acids that leads to metabolic acidosis and the characteristic maple-syrup odor of urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "20q13.3",
    "long_answer": "In ring chromosome 20 (r(20)) syndrome the epileptic phenotype is linked to disturbance of genes in the distal long-arm subtelomeric segment of chromosome 20. Molecular studies repeatedly implicate the 20q13.3 region, which contains epilepsy-related genes such as KCNQ2 and CHRNA4. Altered dosage or epigenetic silencing of this 20q13.3 segment in the ring chromosome is therefore considered the chief chromosomal region associated with the characteristic epilepsy seen in r(20) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome, caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene encoding fibrillin-1, commonly features ectopia lentis—superior-temporal subluxation of the crystalline lens—due to weakened zonular fibers in the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type II hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "In autoimmune hemolytic anemia, auto-IgG or IgM antibodies bind antigens on the patient’s own red blood cell surface; this antibody coating triggers complement activation and/or Fc-mediated phagocytosis, a classic antibody-mediated (Type II) hypersensitivity reaction that destroys the red cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant salivary gland tumor",
    "long_answer": "Polymorphous low-grade adenocarcinoma is included in the World Health Organization classification as a malignant epithelial neoplasm of the salivary glands (a salivary gland carcinoma). It arises most often in minor salivary glands and, despite its generally indolent behavior, is histopathologically and clinically categorized as a malignant salivary gland tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "At least one well-documented case report describes a woman with a 48,XXXX (tetrasomy X) karyotype who developed systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). This observation supports the known association between excess X-chromosome dosage and increased risk of autoimmune disorders such as SLE, which is more common in females and in individuals carrying additional X chromosomes (e.g., Klinefelter 47,XXY males)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal electron microscopy",
    "long_answer": "Thin basement membrane disease is defined by diffuse thinning of the glomerular basement membrane, a change that is only visible at ultrastructural level. Performing electron microscopy on a renal (kidney) biopsy allows direct measurement of basement-membrane thickness and therefore distinguishes thin basement membrane disease from other causes of glomerular hematuria such as IgA nephropathy or Alport syndrome, which show normal or thickened/laminated membranes, respectively."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTDN1",
    "long_answer": "The non-photosensitive (non–DNA-repair‐deficient) form of trichothiodystrophy is most often caused by biallelic mutations in the TTDN1 gene (also called C7orf11). Unlike the photosensitive forms linked to NER genes such as ERCC2/XPD, ERCC3/XPB, and GTF2H5, TTDN1 mutations do not impair nucleotide-excision repair, so patients lack abnormal UV sensitivity while still exhibiting the characteristic brittle, sulfur-deficient hair and developmental features of trichothiodystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Langer–Giedion syndrome, also called trichorhinophalangeal syndrome type II, is a form of ectodermal dysplasia produced by a contiguous-gene deletion on chromosome 8q24. This microdeletion removes both the TRPS1 gene and neighboring genes such as EXT1, leading to sparse scalp hair, nail and skin changes typical of ectodermal dysplasia together with characteristic facial features and multiple exostoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Cayler cardiofacial syndrome presents with asymmetric crying facies caused by depressor-anguli-oris hypoplasia together with congenital heart defects (e.g., ventricular septal defect, tetralogy of Fallot). Because the major morbidity and need for intervention arise from these cardiac malformations, the specialty that routinely diagnoses, evaluates (echocardiography, genetic work-up) and treats such congenital heart disease is pediatric cardiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basilar invagination",
    "long_answer": "Basilar invagination (also called basilar impression or cranial settling) is a craniovertebral junction anomaly in which the floor of the skull (the basiocciput) is drawn upward, allowing the odontoid process and superior portion of the C2 vertebra to migrate into the foramen magnum. This upward displacement can compress neural structures and is the classic condition described as invagination of the skull base with superior migration of C2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Room-air ventilation",
    "long_answer": "Current WHO, ILCOR, and Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP) guidelines advise initiating positive-pressure ventilation with room air (≈21 % O₂) rather than 100 % oxygen for term or near-term newborns suffering perinatal asphyxia. Clinical trials have shown that bag-and-mask ventilation with room air achieves equivalent or better restoration of spontaneous breathing, reduces oxidative injury, and lowers mortality compared with pure oxygen; supplemental oxygen is added only if the infant fails to improve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈600 mL/min",
    "long_answer": "After a surgically created arteriovenous fistula matures, the low-resistance venous outflow causes a large rise in arterial inflow. Doppler studies show the brachial artery flow increases from roughly 50–100 mL/min at baseline to about 0.6 L per minute (≈600 mL/min), a value accepted clinically as the minimum adequate flow for a functioning haemodialysis fistula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Post-polio syndrome is a late neurologic complication of poliomyelitis that manifests with new muscle weakness, fatigue, and pain decades after the initial infection. Diagnosis requires exclusion of other neurologic conditions and electrophysiologic evaluation of lower-motor-neuron function, tasks that fall within the expertise of a neurologist. Management likewise relies on neurologic assessment of motor units and coordination with rehabilitation strategies, so the medical specialty primarily responsible is Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric neurology",
    "long_answer": "Aicardi–Goutières syndrome is an inherited encephalopathy that presents in infancy with cerebral calcifications, chronic neuro-inflammation, developmental delay, spasticity, and seizures. Because it primarily affects the central nervous system in children, diagnosis and ongoing care are typically directed by a pediatric neurologist, the medical specialty focused on neurologic disorders in childhood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Guillain–Barré syndrome is an acute immune-mediated polyneuropathy managed primarily by neurologists, who use disease-modifying therapies such as intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) or plasma exchange to halt immune attack on peripheral nerves."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Current epidemiological and neuropathological data do not place cerebral microinfarcts as the “fourth most important” independent cause of dementia. The major recognized aetiologies of chronic degenerative dementia in the population are, in rough order of prevalence: (1) Alzheimer’s disease, (2) vascular dementia (principally from larger-territory infarcts and diffuse small-vessel disease, which may include microinfarcts but does not treat them as a separate category), (3) dementia with Lewy bodies/Parkinson’s disease dementia, and (4) frontotemporal lobar degeneration.  \n\nMicroinfarcts are exceedingly common in the aged brain and are now understood to contribute to the overall burden of vascular cognitive impairment, often in combination with Alzheimer pathology (“mixed dementia”). Some autopsy studies show that the presence of microinfarcts adds substantially to the risk of dementia, in some analyses ranking second only to Alzheimer pathology in their statistical contribution. However, that contribution is considered part of the broader category of vascular dementia rather than a distinct, fourth-place cause after Alzheimer’s disease. Hence, the assertion that microinfarcts are the fourth most important cause of dementia after Alzheimer’s disease is not supported by prevailing consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Legs",
    "long_answer": "Spastic diplegia is a cerebral palsy subtype in which spasticity predominantly involves the lower limbs. The legs (lower extremities) are essential for weight-bearing and postural balance, so involvement of these muscles leads to the characteristic gait and stability problems seen in spastic diplegia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MSX1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the homeobox gene MSX1 are a well-established cause of hypodontia/anodontia, and the same gene is recurrently implicated in nonsyndromic cleft lip with or without cleft palate. Thus, MSX1 links both tooth agenesis and cleft lip-palate phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatitis E",
    "long_answer": "Hepatitis E virus (HEV) is mainly spread by the fecal-oral route through contaminated water or food. While it usually causes a self-limited acute hepatitis in the general population, infection during pregnancy—especially in the third trimester—can lead to fulminant hepatic failure with markedly increased maternal and fetal mortality, distinguishing it from other viral hepatitides."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL2RG gene sequencing",
    "long_answer": "X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the IL2RG gene, which encodes the common γ-chain of several interleukin receptors. Sequencing (mutation analysis) of the IL2RG gene in genomic DNA directly identifies these variants and therefore provides a definitive genetic diagnosis of X-SCID."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psammoma bodies",
    "long_answer": "Meningiomas frequently contain psammoma bodies—concentric, lamellated calcific spherules that form within whorled clusters of tumor cells and are a classic microscopic hallmark of these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aerobic exercise",
    "long_answer": "Aerobic exercise—such as brisk walking, jogging, cycling or swimming—raises heart and respiratory rates for sustained periods. This improves cardiovascular fitness, lowers blood pressure and LDL-cholesterol, enhances endothelial function, and increases insulin sensitivity and glucose uptake in skeletal muscle. These physiological adaptations reduce atherosclerotic risk and help maintain normal blood glucose levels, thereby decreasing the likelihood of developing heart disease and Type 2 diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tetra-amelia syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Prince Randian, billed as “The Human Torso,” was born without arms or legs. This complete absence of all four limbs is the defining feature of the congenital disorder known as tetra-amelia syndrome, a rare condition most often linked to mutations affecting the WNT3 signaling pathway during embryonic limb development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerliponase alfa",
    "long_answer": "On April 27, 2017 the U.S. FDA approved cerliponase alfa (brand name Brineura), an intraventricular enzyme-replacement therapy that supplies the deficient tripeptidyl-peptidase 1 (TPP1) enzyme in patients with late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (Jansky–Bielschowsky disease)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "Oculocerebrocutaneous (Delleman) syndrome frequently involves midline cerebral malformations, with partial or complete agenesis of the corpus callosum being one of the most consistently reported findings on neuro-imaging. This structure’s absence or hypoplasia contributes to the neurological manifestations seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type I cryoglobulinemia",
    "long_answer": "Type I cryoglobulinemia consists of a single (monoclonal) immunoglobulin that is typically produced in disorders such as monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS). MGUS is a clinically recognized premalignant (precancerous) plasma-cell condition that can progress to multiple myeloma or other lymphoproliferative malignancies. Therefore, the type of cryoglobulinemia linked to a precancerous condition is Type I."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CKAP2L",
    "long_answer": "Filippi syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by growth retardation, microcephaly, intellectual disability and syndactyly. Exome-sequencing studies have shown that affected individuals carry biallelic frameshift (loss-of-function) mutations in the CKAP2L gene (Cytoskeleton-Associated Protein 2-Like). CKAP2L encodes a mitotic spindle–associated protein; truncating frameshift variants impair proper spindle organization during neurodevelopment, providing the causal mechanism for Filippi syndrome. Hence, frameshift mutations in CKAP2L are responsible for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronary atherosclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Most myocardial infarctions occur when atherosclerotic plaques in the coronary arteries rupture or erode, triggering platelet aggregation and thrombus formation that abruptly occludes blood flow. Thus, the underlying blood-vessel disorder responsible for the majority of myocardial infarctions is coronary atherosclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin",
    "long_answer": "Studies of panic disorder consistently show dysregulation of the brain’s serotonergic system—e.g., altered 5-HT receptor binding and benefit from selective serotonin-reuptake inhibitors. Independent research on personality disorders (especially borderline and antisocial types) also implicates low or unstable serotonin activity in impulsivity and affective instability. Thus, irregular serotonin neurotransmission links panic disorder and several personality disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Barbiturates",
    "long_answer": "Barbiturates are sedative-hypnotic drugs that were historically prescribed for insomnia. Prolonged, daily use can lead to tolerance and physical dependence, and chronic intoxication is well documented to produce persistent cognitive deficits—memory loss, confusion and disorientation—described clinically as an organic brain syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Dermato-osteolysis, Kirghizian type causes progressive osteolysis of the carpal, tarsal and other bones in childhood, leading to deformity, instability and functional impairment. Treatment centers on musculoskeletal monitoring, bracing and corrective or stabilizing surgery—interventions that fall squarely within the expertise of pediatric orthopedic surgeons. Therefore, pediatric orthopedics is the specialty most likely to direct management of the disease’s symptoms in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonoxynol-9",
    "long_answer": "Nonoxynol-9, the most common surfactant compound in spermicidal foams, gels, and films, has been studied in epidemiologic cohorts and randomized trials; these data show no significant increase or decrease in the incidence of bacterial vaginosis among users compared with non-users. Therefore, exposure to Nonoxynol-9 does not alter a woman’s risk of developing bacterial vaginosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radius and ulna",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple osteochondromas (also called hereditary multiple exostoses) characteristically produce osteocartilaginous outgrowths at the metaphyses of long bones. In the forearm, both long bones—the radius and the ulna—can develop these osteochondromas, leading to ulnar shortening, radial bowing, and potential deformity or functional impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatic fever",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatic fever is an autoimmune sequela of group A streptococcal pharyngitis in which antibodies formed against streptococcal M-protein cross-react with cardiac tissue (molecular mimicry). The resulting pancarditis produces chronic inflammation and scarring of the heart valves—particularly the mitral and aortic valves—leading to rheumatic valvular heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulsed dye laser",
    "long_answer": "Keloid scars are commonly treated with the 585–595 nm pulsed dye laser (PDL). This vascular‐selective laser targets hemoglobin within the abundant microvasculature of keloids, producing selective photothermolysis that decreases blood supply, down-regulates fibroblast activity, and leads to gradual flattening and softening of the scar while also reducing redness and pruritus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metaphysis",
    "long_answer": "Metaphyseal chondrodysplasia Schmid type is characterized by abnormalities in the metaphyses—the flared, actively growing zones just below the growth plate of long bones. Mutations in the COL10A1 gene disrupt type X collagen in hypertrophic chondrocytes, and the resulting cartilage and bone matrix defects are most pronounced in the metaphyseal regions, leading to the typical widening and irregularity seen on radiographs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary fibrogenic cells",
    "long_answer": "Following liver injury hepatic stellate cells become activated, transform into myofibroblast-like cells and are the main source of type I/III collagen and other extracellular matrix components. Their sustained collagen deposition and matrix remodeling drive hepatic fibrosis, the pathological process that culminates in cirrhosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strabismus",
    "long_answer": "Strabismus—misalignment of the eyes, often presenting as esotropia or exotropia—is frequently observed in children with cerebral palsy. Neuromotor impairment of the extra-ocular muscles and their central control pathways makes ocular alignment unstable, so up to 40–60 % of cerebral palsy patients develop strabismus, far above the prevalence in the general pediatric population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HPA axis activation",
    "long_answer": "Chronic psychological or physiological stress persistently stimulates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, keeping cortisol and catecholamine levels chronically elevated. Prolonged cortisol exposure drives visceral fat accumulation, promotes insulin resistance, increases hepatic gluconeogenesis, raises blood pressure through mineralocorticoid effects, and alters lipid metabolism. Together these cortisol-mediated changes—central obesity, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, and dyslipidaemia—constitute the core abnormalities that define metabolic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) can indeed present with motor phenomena such as functional dystonia, so an association with dystonia is accepted. However, by definition the symptoms in conversion disorder are not consciously produced or feigned; intentional production of illness is characteristic of factitious disorder or malingering, not conversion disorder. Therefore, conversion disorder is not considered to be associated with feigning illness. Because the statement claims an association with both dystonia and feigning illness, it is not fully correct."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Excess intake of vitamin A or its analogues (e.g., isotretinoin) can raise intracranial pressure, producing idiopathic intracranial hypertension (also called pseudotumor cerebri). Elevated intracranial pressure leads to bilateral optic disc swelling (papilledema) and associated visual symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor lysis syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In high-grade lymphomas, rapid destruction of malignant cells can trigger tumor lysis syndrome. The sudden release of intracellular potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids causes hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, secondary hypocalcemia and other metabolic derangements. These electrolyte imbalances can precipitate fatal cardiac arrhythmias, making tumor lysis syndrome a life-threatening complication that may lead to sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectrodactyly",
    "long_answer": "Grady Stiles Jr., popularly known as “Lobster Boy,” was born with ectrodactyly, a congenital limb malformation in which one or more of the central digits of the hands or feet fail to develop. The resulting cleft gives the limbs a split-hand/split-foot appearance. Ectrodactyly is often inherited in an autosomal-dominant pattern and can involve genes such as TP63 that direct limb bud formation; the deficiency or complete absence of the central rays is its defining feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noonan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Noonan syndrome is a RAS-MAPK–pathway developmental disorder characterized by distinctive facial features, most notably down-slanting palpebral fissures, together with multiple skeletal anomalies such as pectus excavatum/carinatum, cubitus valgus and short stature. These hallmark findings make Noonan syndrome the classically recognized syndrome that links downslanting palpebral fissures with skeletal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cleft palate",
    "long_answer": "A congenital cleft of the soft palate disrupts the muscular sling (e.g., the levator veli palatini), preventing the soft palate from sealing against the posterior pharyngeal wall. This structural defect results in velopharyngeal inadequacy, allowing air to escape through the nose during speech and causing hypernasality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isocitrate dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the metabolic enzyme isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH1 or IDH2) are recurrently found in chondrosarcoma and in several leukemias, especially acute myeloid leukemia. These neomorphic IDH mutations produce the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which drives epigenetic dysregulation and contributes to tumorigenesis in both malignancies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Fryns-Aftimos syndrome is produced by mosaic tetrasomy of the short arm of chromosome 12 (12p), most often due to an extra isochromosome 12p. Hence, the clinical picture is directly linked to abnormalities of human chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macula lutea",
    "long_answer": "Maculopathy is any pathological condition that specifically involves the macula lutea—the small, central, yellow-pigmented area of the retina responsible for high-acuity and color vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dental fluorosis",
    "long_answer": "When fluoride is consumed chronically at concentrations higher than those used for community water fluoridation (~0.7–1 ppm), it disrupts normal enamel mineralization in developing teeth. This results in hypomineralized, opaque, and sometimes mottled enamel—a condition termed dental fluorosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAB39B",
    "long_answer": "RAB39B encodes a small Rab-family GTPase located on Xq28. Rab proteins orchestrate budding, movement and fusion of membrane vesicles, thereby controlling the trafficking of proteins and lipids between the endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, endosomes and the plasma membrane. Pathogenic loss-of-function mutations in RAB39B disrupt this vesicular trafficking and have been repeatedly documented in families with X-linked intellectual disability, confirming its role in both intracellular transport and the cognitive disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPAST gene (also known as SPG4 or spastin) are the single most frequent cause of hereditary spastic paraplegia. They account for roughly 40 % of autosomal-dominant HSP and about 25 % of all HSP cases across inheritance patterns, making SPAST the gene responsible for approximately one-quarter of hereditary spastic paraplegia overall."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome is caused by specific missense mutations (most commonly Ser252Trp or Pro253Arg) in the FGFR2 gene, which encodes fibroblast growth factor receptor 2. These gain-of-function mutations dysregulate FGFR2 signaling during craniofacial and limb development, leading to premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis) and syndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculocutaneous albinism type 1",
    "long_answer": "The TYR gene encodes tyrosinase, the rate-limiting enzyme in melanin biosynthesis. Pathogenic variants on both alleles—often as compound heterozygous mutations—abolish or markedly reduce tyrosinase activity, leading to the autosomal-recessive disorder oculocutaneous albinism type 1 (OCA1), characterized by hypopigmentation of skin, hair, and eyes and visual abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mustelidae",
    "long_answer": "Mustelidae is the mammalian family that encompasses weasels, otters, wolverines, ferrets, and related carnivores. Members of this family are known definitive hosts for several Diphyllobothrium species, whose life cycles involve piscivorous mammals such as mustelids becoming infected when they consume fish containing plerocercoid larvae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "14-3-3 protein",
    "long_answer": "14-3-3 proteins are abundant neuronal adaptor proteins whose presence in cerebrospinal fluid is a clinical marker of neurotoxicity (e.g., prion diseases). They also regulate apoptosis by binding the phosphorylated form of the pro-apoptotic protein BAD, keeping BAD sequestered in the cytosol and thereby modulating cell death signalling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Multiple occurrences of Crane–Heise syndrome have been reported in siblings born to unaffected parents, with no vertical transmission. This sib-clustering pattern with unaffected carriers is characteristic of autosomal recessive inheritance, so the syndrome is thought to represent an autosomal recessive genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type II (Hunter syndrome)",
    "long_answer": "Among the mucopolysaccharidoses, type II—Hunter syndrome—is classically associated with a high prevalence of obstructive sleep apnea. Glycosaminoglycan accumulation in the upper airway leads to adenotonsillar hypertrophy, macroglossia and airway narrowing, predisposing patients with MPS II to obstructive sleep apnea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Aromatase deficiency (caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CYP19A1 gene) leads to markedly reduced or absent conversion of androgens to estrogens. In both humans and animal models, the lack of estrogen results in metabolic disturbances that include insulin resistance and the compensatory development of hyperinsulinemia. Clinical reports of aromatase-deficient males and females describe elevated fasting insulin levels, impaired glucose tolerance, and, in some cases, progression to type 2 diabetes. Estrogen replacement in affected patients or in aromatase-knockout mice normalizes insulin sensitivity and lowers insulin concentrations, confirming a causal link. Therefore, hyperinsulinemia is an established metabolic manifestation associated with aromatase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Berdon syndrome, also called megacystis-microcolon-intestinal hypoperistalsis syndrome, is classically inherited in an autosomal recessive manner: affected children have pathogenic variants in both copies of a visceral smooth-muscle gene (e.g., MYH11, LMOD1, MYL9), received one from each heterozygous carrier parent. This recessive transmission pattern is the form most consistently reported in the medical literature for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absent (negative)",
    "long_answer": "Baló's concentric sclerosis generally lacks the polyspecific intrathecal antibody response to measles, rubella and varicella-zoster viruses (the MRZ-reaction) that is characteristic of typical multiple sclerosis. Hence, the MRZ-reaction is usually negative in Baló's concentric sclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pervasive developmental disorders",
    "long_answer": "In ICD-10, Disinhibited Attachment Disorder is coded as F94.2 within the group “Disorders of social functioning with onset specific to childhood and adolescence” (F94). The official exclusion note for this F94 category states that cases meeting criteria for pervasive developmental disorders (F84.–)—i.e., autism spectrum disorders—must not be classified here. Thus, pervasive developmental disorders are specifically excluded from the category that contains Disinhibited Attachment Disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Linezolid",
    "long_answer": "Linezolid, an oxazolidinone antibiotic, is FDA-approved and widely used in the United States for infections caused by vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE). It inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding the 50S ribosomal subunit, providing bacteriostatic activity against VRE and serving as a first-line therapeutic option in clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcal enterotoxin A",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus food-borne illness is caused by ingestion of pre-formed, heat-stable staphylococcal enterotoxins, most commonly enterotoxin A. This superantigenic toxin survives cooking, is rapidly absorbed in the gut, and triggers massive cytokine release from T cells and intestinal mast cells, producing abrupt gastrointestinal symptoms including intense abdominal cramping, profuse watery diarrhea, and vomiting within a few hours of food consumption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paul Gerson Unna",
    "long_answer": "The convoluted scalp disorder was first described in 1837, but it was the German dermatologist Paul Gerson Unna who, in 1907, introduced and formalized the name “cutis verticis gyrata,” referring to the scalp skin (cutis) of the vertex that is thrown into brain-like gyri."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Furocoumarins",
    "long_answer": "Phytophotodermatitis occurs after skin contact with plants that contain furocoumarins (psoralens). These molecules absorb UVA light and become highly reactive, producing free-radical damage in epidermal cells; this phototoxic action is what makes furocoumarins the key photosensitizers responsible for the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vegetations",
    "long_answer": "In infective endocarditis, friable valve lesions called vegetations form; they consist of a mesh of platelets and fibrin that traps bacterial microcolonies and contains only sparse inflammatory cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ashman phenomenon",
    "long_answer": "During atrial fibrillation, irregularly long-short R–R cycles can cause rate-dependent bundle-branch aberration, producing wide QRS complexes known as Ashman phenomenon. This aberrant conduction can also appear in patients with Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome experiencing atrial fibrillation, leading to intermittent wide-complex beats that mimic other tachyarrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematocrit ≥52%",
    "long_answer": "According to internationally accepted hematology criteria (e.g., the International Council for Standardization in Haematology), polycythemia in adult men is diagnosed when the hematocrit reaches or exceeds 52 %. This cutoff reflects a red-cell mass more than 25 % above predicted normal and reliably distinguishes true erythrocytosis from normal physiologic variation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plantar surface",
    "long_answer": "Calluses on the foot are most frequently produced on the plantar surface—the sole—because this weight-bearing region is subjected to repeated pressure and friction during standing and walking, prompting hyperkeratosis of the epidermis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Aniridia in humans is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PAX6 gene. PAX6 encodes a paired box/homeodomain transcription factor that governs eye formation; its nucleotide and protein sequences are highly conserved from zebrafish to humans. Disruption of this single gene impairs ocular development, leading to the characteristic absence of the iris seen in aniridia patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L1CAM",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked gene L1CAM cause the SPG1 form of hereditary spastic paraplegia. The L1 cell-adhesion molecule encoded by L1CAM is a key axon-guidance receptor; it partners with neuropilin-1 to transmit class-3 semaphorin (e.g., Sema3A) signals that steer growing corticospinal and other axons. This well-documented interaction and guidance role link L1CAM directly to both hereditary spastic paraplegia and neuropilin-1–dependent axon pathfinding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pons",
    "long_answer": "In cerebellar hypoplasia—particularly the pontocerebellar hypoplasia syndromes—the pons is characteristically under-developed along with the cerebellum. MRI and neuropathologic studies consistently show pontine hypoplasia, linking this specific brainstem region to cerebellar hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "Screwdriver- or peg-shaped incisors are a characteristic dental anomaly in hypohidrotic/anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia. This condition is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the EDA gene (ectodysplasin A) on chromosome Xq12–q13, which disrupt epithelial–mesenchymal signaling during tooth morphogenesis and results in conical, tapered (screwdriver-shaped) incisors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18 (18p11)",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant hereditary form of spasmodic torticollis (idiopathic cervical dystonia) has been mapped to the DYT7 locus on chromosome 18p11.3. Linkage studies in affected families demonstrated co-segregation of the disease with markers on the short arm of chromosome 18, establishing this chromosomal region as the primary genetic association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pretibial myxedema",
    "long_answer": "Pretibial myxedema, also called thyroid dermopathy, is a rare extrathyroidal manifestation of Graves' disease characterized by marked mucopolysaccharide deposition in the dermis of the lower legs. This leads to non-pitting edema and thickened, hyperpigmented skin that can resemble the massive lymphedematous swelling seen in elephantiasis, making misdiagnosis possible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cephalosporium",
    "long_answer": "The filamentous fungal genus Cephalosporium (now classified within Acremonium) has been documented to provoke hypersensitivity pneumonitis in exposed workers and patients. In 1945, Sardinian scientist Giuseppe Brotzu isolated Cephalosporium acremonium from sewage outflow and discovered that it produced cephalosporin C, the β-lactam compound that became the progenitor of the cephalosporin class of antibiotics. Thus, the same genus is linked both to hypersensitivity lung disease and to the origin of cephalosporin antimicrobials."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAS (CD95)",
    "long_answer": "In idiopathic aplastic anemia, autoreactive cytotoxic T-cells secrete IFN-γ and other cytokines that up-regulate the death receptor Fas (encoded by the FAS gene, also called CD95) on hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells. Engagement of Fas by Fas ligand on T-cells triggers caspase-mediated apoptosis, leading to immune-mediated destruction of the bone-marrow compartment. Thus, the FAS gene is centrally involved in the pathogenic immune attack responsible for aplastic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary cilia",
    "long_answer": "Hydrolethalus syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HYLS1 centrosomal protein, which is essential for assembling primary cilia. Failure to form functional primary cilia disrupts Hedgehog and other cilia-dependent signalling pathways during embryogenesis, leading to the characteristic severe developmental defects of the syndrome. Thus, dysfunction of the primary cilium is the proximate cellular cause of Hydrolethalus syndrome phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femur",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FLNB gene cause the lethal skeletal disorder known as boomerang dysplasia. Radiographically, the most characteristic finding is that each femur is grossly malformed and bowed into the shape of a boomerang, giving the condition its name. Thus, the femur is the bone that exhibits the distinctive “boomerang” malformation in FLNB-related disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melanoma",
    "long_answer": "Canvaxin is an allogeneic, polyvalent whole-cell melanoma vaccine derived from three human melanoma cell lines. Early-phase and some phase III trials reported improved immune responses and a trend toward longer survival in patients with stage III/IV melanoma, indicating that this skin cancer showed the most notable though ultimately limited success with Canvaxin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP6V1B1, ATP6V0A4, SLC4A1",
    "long_answer": "Inherited forms of distal (type I) renal tubular acidosis that arise in the collecting-duct intercalated cells are most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in genes encoding key acid–base transport proteins: ATP6V1B1 (B1 subunit) and ATP6V0A4 (a4 subunit) of the vacuolar H⁺-ATPase, and SLC4A1 (anion exchanger 1/AE1).  Defects in these proteins impair proton secretion or bicarbonate reclamation in the cortical collecting duct, producing the characteristic acidosis, alkaline urine and related sequelae of distal RTA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palovarotene",
    "long_answer": "Palovarotene, a selective retinoic-acid-receptor-γ agonist marketed as Sohonos, is the first therapy approved (in Canada) for fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva and had previously been granted orphan drug designation by both the U.S. FDA and the European Medicines Agency because of the disease’s extreme rarity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "After bilateral oophorectomy the normal physiologic response is loss of ovarian estrogen-inhibin feedback, leading to very high serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) concentrations (typically >40–50 IU/L, in the post-menopausal range). When functioning ovarian tissue is unintentionally left behind (ovarian remnant syndrome, ORS), that tissue can continue to secrete estradiol and inhibin and will partially suppress pituitary FSH release. Consequently, patients with ORS often show FSH values that are lower than expected for a surgically menopausal woman and may even fall into the pre-menopausal range. For this reason, measurement of FSH (usually together with estradiol) is recommended in the diagnostic work-up of suspected ORS: an unexpectedly low or normal FSH level raises suspicion for residual, hormone-producing ovarian tissue, whereas a consistently high FSH level makes ORS much less likely. Imaging and/or laparoscopic exploration are still needed for definitive localisation, but FSH is a useful biochemical indicator. Therefore, FSH can indeed indicate the presence of ovarian remnant syndrome after oophorectomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Felix Hoppe-Seyler",
    "long_answer": "The German physiologist and chemist Felix Hoppe-Seyler gave the first documented description of porphyria in 1871, when he isolated the red-purple pigment (haematoporphyrin) from the urine of an affected patient. His observation of this characteristic pigment and the accompanying clinical features laid the foundation for recognising porphyria as a distinct metabolic disease of the haem biosynthetic pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the heart valves and endocardial surface. Because it primarily involves cardiac structures and often requires expertise in valvular disease, hemodynamics, and possible surgical referral, patients are most commonly managed under the care of cardiologists, with additional input from infectious-disease specialists as needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary gland",
    "long_answer": "Acromegaly is caused by excessive secretion of growth hormone, almost always due to a functional adenoma of the anterior pituitary gland. Pierre Marie first described the syndrome, linking its characteristic skeletal overgrowth and soft-tissue enlargement to this hyperactive pituitary source."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal iodine deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Population studies have shown that inadequate iodine intake during pregnancy—the result of low environmental iodine levels in food and water—raises the incidence of congenital hypothyroidism in newborns. Iodine is essential for fetal thyroid hormone synthesis, and deficiency impairs thyroid development and hormone production, leading to statistically higher rates of congenital hypothyroidism in affected regions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Suprascapular nerve",
    "long_answer": "Parsonage–Turner syndrome (neuralgic amyotrophy) most frequently affects branches of the upper brachial plexus, with the suprascapular nerve being the nerve most commonly involved. The suprascapular nerve arises from the C5-C6 roots and provides motor innervation to the supraspinatus (and infraspinatus) muscles; therefore it both characterizes the typical neuropathy seen in Parsonage–Turner syndrome and is responsible for activating the supraspinatus muscle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ptosis",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, the term \"ptosis\" (from the Greek for 'falling') denotes the abnormal downward displacement or drooping of a body part or organ, such as eyelid ptosis or uterine ptosis. It is the standard term used in anatomy and clinical practice to describe this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SH2D1A",
    "long_answer": "Classic X-linked lymphoproliferative disease (XLP, also called XLP1) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SH2D1A gene, which encodes the SLAM-associated protein (SAP). Defective SAP disrupts signaling through SLAM family receptors on T, NK, and NKT cells, leading to uncontrolled immune activation—classically after Epstein-Barr virus infection—and the clinical manifestations of XLP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac arrhythmias",
    "long_answer": "If phenytoin is infused too rapidly intravenously (>50 mg/min in adults), the drug and its solvent (propylene glycol) can depress myocardial conduction and contractility, precipitating severe hypotension and life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias such as bradycardia, heart block, ventricular tachycardia, or cardiac arrest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pipecolic acidemia (primary hyperpipecolic acidemia), caused by deficiency of the peroxisomal enzyme pipecolic acid oxidase involved in lysine catabolism, is transmitted as an autosomal recessive disorder—both copies of the gene must be mutated for the condition to manifest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spirochete",
    "long_answer": "Pinta is caused by Treponema carateum, a member of the genus Treponema. Treponemes are slender, helically coiled bacteria—classified morphologically as spirochetes—giving them a characteristic spiral (corkscrew) shape."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electromyography (EMG)",
    "long_answer": "Needle electromyography is the standard diagnostic test for distal myopathies. By recording motor-unit potentials from the laryngeal and pharyngeal muscles, EMG reveals the small-amplitude, short-duration, polyphasic potentials that are characteristic of a primary myopathic process, allowing objective assessment of vocal-cord and pharyngeal involvement in distal myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triple A syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AAAS gene, which encodes the nuclear-pore protein ALADIN, cause Triple A syndrome (also called Allgrove syndrome). This autosomal-recessive disorder is typified by achalasia, alacrima, and adrenal insufficiency; the cortisol deficiency from adrenal failure frequently presents with unexplained, recurrent hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptococcus pneumoniae",
    "long_answer": "Streptococcus pneumoniae (the pneumococcus) is an encapsulated Gram-positive diplococcus that accounts for most cases of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection because splenectomized patients lose the spleen’s ability to clear opsonized encapsulated bacteria. The same organism is also the leading cause of community-acquired bacterial pneumonia worldwide, producing lobar pneumonia and other invasive pneumococcal diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CEP290",
    "long_answer": "The first in-vivo CRISPR human trial (EDIT-101) for Leber congenital amaurosis type 10 focused on excising the pathogenic intronic mutation (c.2991+1655A>G) in the CEP290 gene, whose defects are a well-established cause of this form of Leber congenital amaurosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jaundice",
    "long_answer": "In drug-induced liver injury, rising serum bilirubin signals impaired hepatic conjugation and excretion of bilirubin; its accumulation in blood and tissues produces the yellow discoloration known clinically as jaundice, a marker of significant hepatocellular or cholestatic damage and increased risk of liver failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAQ",
    "long_answer": "Sturge–Weber syndrome is almost always caused by a post-zygotic, somatic activating missense mutation in the GNAQ gene (classically c.548G>A, p.Arg183Gln). The mutation hyperactivates the Gαq signaling pathway, driving abnormal capillary–venous malformation in skin, leptomeninges and eye that characterizes the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus",
    "long_answer": "Several studies have detected herpes simplex virus (HSV) DNA in the corneal endothelium or aqueous humour of patients with iridocorneal endothelial (ICE) syndrome. This finding led to the hypothesis that a prior HSV infection of the corneal endothelium can trigger aberrant proliferation and migration of endothelial-like cells into the anterior chamber angle and over the iris surface, producing the characteristic abnormal endothelialization seen in ICE syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "The IL2RG gene encodes the common γ-chain shared by several interleukin receptors (IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, IL-21) that are essential for thymic maturation signals. A loss-of-function mutation in IL2RG disrupts these cytokine pathways, blocking normal development of T lymphocytes (and secondarily NK cells), so patients with X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency lack functional T cells while B-cell numbers are relatively preserved but ineffective."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia is a congenital malformation characterized by optic nerve hypoplasia, midline brain defects and possible pituitary dysfunction. Because the optic nerves are under-developed, visual input to the ocular motor system is abnormal, frequently leading to sensory nystagmus—an involuntary oscillation of the eyes. Thus, this condition links optic nerve hypoplasia with involuntary eye movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyprus",
    "long_answer": "Cyprus, an island nation in the eastern Mediterranean, has one of the world’s highest carrier frequencies for β-thalassemia (≈13–15 % of the population are heterozygotes). This high prevalence led Cyprus to pioneer nationwide screening and prenatal prevention programs for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WT1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Denys–Drash syndrome classically results from missense mutations in the Wilms-tumor suppressor gene WT1 on chromosome 11p13, which disrupt the zinc-finger DNA-binding domain. The same WT1 gene—when altered by different pathogenic variants such as splice-site changes—is also a recognized cause of 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis (e.g., Frasier/Swyer phenotypes). Thus, WT1 mutations underlie both Denys–Drash syndrome and 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diffuse choroidal hemangioma",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Sturge-Weber syndrome frequently harbor a diffuse choroidal hemangioma. Topical prostaglandin-F2α analogues such as latanoprost can augment choroidal blood flow and vascular permeability, predisposing the hemangioma to exudation or serous retinal detachment. Therefore, the presence of a diffuse choroidal hemangioma is considered a contraindication to prescribing latanoprost in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Suprachiasmatic nucleus",
    "long_answer": "The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus receives direct retinal input via the retino-hypothalamic tract, allowing it to sense environmental light—an essential element of visual information. This same nucleus serves as the body’s master circadian clock, generating and synchronizing daily rhythms of physiology and behavior. Because it both processes light signals from the visual system and orchestrates circadian timing, the SCN is the brain region that links visual processing with regulation of circadian rhythms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Howel–Evans syndrome (tylosis with oesophageal cancer) is produced by germ-line mutations in the RHBDF2 gene, which was mapped to the tylosis oesophageal cancer (TOC) locus at 17q25.1. Thus the disease-causing gene is located on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXT1",
    "long_answer": "Osteochondromas characteristically harbor loss-of-function mutations in the EXT1 (exostosin-1) gene, resulting in absent or markedly reduced EXT1 expression in the tumor. Trevor disease (dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica) lacks these EXT1 alterations and shows preserved EXT1 expression. Hence, analysis of the EXT1 gene (or its protein expression) is routinely used to distinguish Trevor disease from osteochondroma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autism",
    "long_answer": "Children born with congenital rubella syndrome have a markedly increased risk of developing autism later in childhood—epidemiologic studies show 7–13 % of CRS survivors meet criteria for autism spectrum disorder, reflecting rubella’s disruptive effects on fetal brain development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aspirin",
    "long_answer": "In thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) patients already have profound thrombocytopenia, so adding an antiplatelet agent such as aspirin, which irreversibly inhibits platelet cyclo-oxygenase and impairs aggregation, markedly heightens the risk of serious bleeding. Therefore aspirin is avoided until the platelet count has safely recovered."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGCA (alpha-sarcoglycan)",
    "long_answer": "Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2D (LGMD2D) is a sarcoglycanopathy produced by pathogenic mutations in the SGCA gene, which encodes the alpha-sarcoglycan subunit of the dystrophin–glycoprotein complex in skeletal muscle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehlers–Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Tethered cord syndrome is frequently reported in patients with Ehlers–Danlos syndrome, a hereditary connective-tissue disorder characterized by defects in collagen synthesis that lead to joint hypermobility, skin hyperextensibility, and tissue fragility. The abnormal connective-tissue elasticity can allow excessive elongation and fixation of the spinal cord and its filum terminale, predisposing individuals with Ehlers–Danlos syndrome to develop tethered cord pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dejerine–Sottas disease",
    "long_answer": "Dejerine–Sottas disease, the severe early-onset demyelinating neuropathy classified as Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease type 3, involves mutations in myelin-related genes such as PMP22, MPZ or EGR2. Because the disorder demyelinates not only peripheral nerves but also cranial nerves, the vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerve can be affected, leading to moderate to severe sensorineural hearing loss in many patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Middle nasal meatus",
    "long_answer": "Ethmoidal polyps originate from the mucosa of the ethmoidal air cells, which drain into and protrude through the middle nasal meatus; therefore, these polyps characteristically arise in the middle meatus region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2 duplication",
    "long_answer": "A recurrent duplication of the X-chromosomal segment Xq28 that includes the MECP2 gene—commonly termed MECP2 duplication—is a well-established cause of X-linked intellectual disability. Males with this copy-number gain typically present with severe cognitive impairment, hypotonia, seizures and recurrent infections, defining the MECP2 duplication (Xq28 microduplication) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infantile spasms",
    "long_answer": "Cortés-Lacassie syndrome is a rare congenital neurodevelopmental disorder whose neurological hallmark is the early appearance of epileptic infantile spasms (the seizure pattern that characterizes West syndrome). Affected infants develop flexor or extensor spasms in clusters during the first months of life, reflecting the severe encephalopathy that accompanies the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher’s disease commonly leads to hepatomegaly because Gaucher cells accumulate in the liver. The liver is the body’s main site for detoxifying blood-borne substances and for synthesizing plasma proteins such as albumin and clotting factors, making it the organ that fits both criteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candidiasis",
    "long_answer": "People with diabetes mellitus frequently have elevated blood and tissue glucose levels and impaired neutrophil function, conditions that favor overgrowth of Candida species. As a result, they are significantly more prone to develop candidiasis—yeast infections of the mouth (oral thrush), genital tract, or skin—making this the most common fungal infection associated with diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anisakiasis",
    "long_answer": "Anisakiasis is the human nematode infection acquired by eating raw or inadequately cooked fish or squid that contain third-stage larvae of Anisakis species. After ingestion, the larvae can penetrate the gastric or intestinal wall, producing acute gastrointestinal symptoms. Diagnosis is confirmed by recovering the larva endoscopically or by biochemical and immunological methods (e.g., ELISA, immunoblot, PCR) that identify Anisakis antigens or DNA, linking the infection specifically to consumption of contaminated seafood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human papillomavirus (HPV)",
    "long_answer": "The Pap (Papanicolaou) test examines cervical cells for characteristic abnormalities—such as koilocytosis and dysplasia—that are induced by infection with human papillomavirus (HPV), a common sexually transmitted infection. Detection of these HPV-related cytologic changes allows early identification of HPV infection and prevention of cervical cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obstetric ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "When a fetus is suspected of being large for gestational age, clinicians perform an obstetric ultrasound to obtain biometric measurements (e.g., abdominal circumference, head circumference, femur length). These data are entered into validated formulas to estimate fetal weight non-invasively, making ultrasound the standard prenatal diagnostic method for this purpose."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RT-PCR",
    "long_answer": "Reverse-transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) can directly detect SARS-CoV RNA in respiratory, stool or blood samples. A positive RT-PCR result provides laboratory confirmation of infection and upgrades a suspect case to a probable/confirmed SARS case even when chest X-ray findings are negative."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Petrus Gonsalvus, 16th-century",
    "long_answer": "The first medically documented case of hypertrichosis involved Petrus Gonsalvus, born on Tenerife in 1537 and described by Renaissance naturalists such as Ulisse Aldrovandi. His lanugo-type hair growth over the entire body was recorded and illustrated at European courts during the mid-to-late 16th century, making this Renaissance period the earliest verifiable report of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX3",
    "long_answer": "The PAX3 gene, located on chromosome 2q35-q36, encodes a paired-box transcription factor critical for neural-crest-derived craniofacial and limb development. Heterozygous pathogenic variants in PAX3 have been reported in families with acrofrontofacionasal dysostosis, producing the characteristic combination of frontonasal malformation and distal limb anomalies. The same gene is well known to cause Waardenburg and craniofacial-deafness-hand syndromes, where cleft lip and/or cleft palate are recurrent findings. Thus PAX3 satisfies both features mentioned in the question: involvement in acrofrontofacionasal dysostosis and the ability to produce cleft lip/palate, and it is situated on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CATSHL syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Qazi–Markouizos syndrome is the same clinical entity that geneticists now call CATSHL syndrome (Camptodactyly-Tall Stature-Hearing Loss syndrome). It is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function variants in the FGFR3 gene and is characterized by congenital camptodactyly, excessive linear growth, spinal curvature, and sensorineural hearing impairment. Because the original family was reported by Qazi and Markouizos, the disorder carries their names as an alternative eponym."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cor pulmonale",
    "long_answer": "Cor pulmonale—right-sided heart failure secondary to chronic pulmonary hypertension—can arise from both emphysema and the broader spectrum of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In these disorders, persistent airway obstruction and destruction of alveolar capillary beds elevate pulmonary arterial pressures. The sustained pressure overload forces the right ventricle to hypertrophy and eventually fail, producing cor pulmonale as a common and serious complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WAS gene",
    "long_answer": "X-linked thrombocytopenia is the mild allelic variant of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome and results from loss-of-function mutations in the WAS gene, which encodes the Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (WAS) protein involved in actin cytoskeleton organization of hematopoietic cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PMP22 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Roussy–Lévy syndrome and Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease type 1A share pathogenic alterations in the PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein 22) gene. Duplication or other mutations of PMP22 disrupt myelin integrity in peripheral nerves, producing the demyelinating neuropathy characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karyotype analysis",
    "long_answer": "Tetrasomy X is defined by the presence of four X chromosomes (48,XXXX). Clinical findings alone are not specific, so confirmation requires cytogenetic examination: a standard G-banded karyotype (chromosome analysis of peripheral blood lymphocytes) that visually demonstrates the 48,XXXX complement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wood’s lamp fluorescence",
    "long_answer": "Pure black-piedra hairs infected by Piedraia hortae do not fluoresce. If the affected hairs show fluorescence (typically a faint green-yellow glow) under a Wood’s lamp, this is taken as evidence that bacteria have secondarily colonised the hair shaft, because the porphyrins produced by these bacteria are what fluoresce."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "Cranial CT studies in patients with Pallister–Killian syndrome repeatedly show multiple small, punctate (‘spot’) calcifications localised to the basal ganglia (especially the globus pallidus/putamen region). These basal-ganglia calcifications are considered a characteristic, though not universal, neuroradiological feature of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatology",
    "long_answer": "Alagille syndrome is primarily a cholestatic liver disease caused by developmental paucity of intra-hepatic bile ducts and related JAG1/NOTCH2 mutations. Because its main manifestations involve chronic liver dysfunction, a hepatologist (often a pediatric hepatologist/gastroenterologist) is the specialist who evaluates liver enzymes, orders confirmatory genetic testing and liver biopsy, and manages complications such as pruritus, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, and potential need for liver transplantation. Thus, the medical specialty responsible for diagnosing and treating Alagille syndrome is hepatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cigarette smoking",
    "long_answer": "Both chronic bronchitis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) are strongly linked to long-term inhalation of tobacco smoke. Cigarette smoking induces persistent airway inflammation, mucus hypersecretion, and loss of elastic recoil, which drive the pathogenesis of chronic bronchitis and the airflow limitation characteristic of COPD. Consequently, smoking is recognized as the leading modifiable risk factor for developing both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Great-vessel anastomosis difficulty",
    "long_answer": "Because the heart and great vessels are mirror-imaged in dextrocardia situs inversus, implanting a normally oriented donor heart requires complex rerouting and reconstruction of the aorta, pulmonary artery, and caval veins. This anatomical mismatch makes the great-vessel anastomoses the principal technical challenge during transplantation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Western equine encephalitis can leave infants and children with permanent neurologic and developmental deficits after the acute infection. The ongoing evaluation and management of these serious post-encephalitic sequelae (e.g., cognitive impairment, motor dysfunction, seizures) fall under the scope of pediatrics, the medical specialty dedicated to the health care of infants, children, and adolescents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticonvulsant medications",
    "long_answer": "Guidelines from the American Academy of Pediatrics state that neither continuous nor intermittent antiepileptic (anticonvulsant) therapy—such as phenobarbital, valproate, or diazepam—should be used after a simple febrile seizure. These drugs only modestly lower recurrence risk but carry significant adverse-effect profiles; therefore, routine anticonvulsant prophylaxis is not recommended to prevent further simple febrile seizures in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Werner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Werner syndrome is an autosomal-recessive progeroid disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WRN helicase gene. A hallmark of the condition is accelerated aging, and affected individuals typically develop degenerative joint disease—including symptomatic osteoarthritis of large joints—in their 20s–30s, decades earlier than the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glanzmann thrombasthenia",
    "long_answer": "Glanzmann thrombasthenia is an inherited platelet function disorder caused by qualitative or quantitative defects in the platelet membrane glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin αIIbβ3). Because this receptor is essential for fibrinogen-mediated platelet aggregation, affected individuals have impaired clot formation and present with mucocutaneous bleeding manifestations, including epistaxis, easy bruising, and notably heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) in women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schleswig-Holstein",
    "long_answer": "Genetic studies have shown that many patients with paramyotonia congenita in northern Germany carry the same SCN4A founder mutation (e.g., p.T1313M). This single ancestral mutation arose in the Schleswig-Holstein region, leading to a markedly higher local prevalence of the disease compared with other areas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Early androgen therapy",
    "long_answer": "Controlled studies in infants and young boys with the sex-chromosome aneuploidy 49,XXXXY have demonstrated that a brief course of early androgen (testosterone) therapy leads to significant gains in gross and fine motor coordination, expressive language, and non-verbal cognitive (IQ) scores when compared with untreated peers. The improvement is attributed to replacing the androgen deficiency characteristic of this syndrome during a critical period of neurodevelopment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Auscultation",
    "long_answer": "Auscultation is the diagnostic technique that relies on listening to internal body sounds with a stethoscope. In suspected renal artery stenosis, clinicians auscultate the abdomen and flanks for a systolic-diastolic bruit produced by turbulent blood flow through a narrowed renal artery, providing a non-invasive clinical clue to the presence of the stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis",
    "long_answer": "Dermatitis herpetiformis is an autoimmune blistering skin disease in which IgA antibodies directed against epidermal transglutaminase deposit in the dermal papillae, producing intensely pruritic grouped vesicles and blisters, especially on extensor surfaces. It is the classic cutaneous manifestation of gluten-sensitive enteropathy and is strongly associated with coeliac disease; both conditions improve on a strict gluten-free diet."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pentoxifylline",
    "long_answer": "Pentoxifylline, a methylxanthine phosphodiesterase inhibitor, is routinely prescribed in oral submucous fibrosis because it improves micro-circulation, inhibits fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition, and enhances collagenase activity. These anti-fibrotic actions have been shown clinically to increase maximal mouth opening, thereby ameliorating the functional limitation characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trichromatic vision",
    "long_answer": "Humans are trichromats; their color vision relies on three distinct cone photoreceptor types in the retina—S-cones (short-wavelength, blue), M-cones (medium-wavelength, green), and L-cones (long-wavelength, red). The combined responses of these three cone classes enable full-color perception, a system termed trichromatic vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autologous hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "For severe, rapidly progressive systemic scleroderma (systemic sclerosis), high-dose immunoablation followed by reinfusion of the patient’s own hematopoietic stem cells—a procedure called autologous hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation (HSCT)—is employed. Randomized trials such as ASTIS and SCOT have shown that autologous HSCT can improve skin fibrosis, lung function, and overall survival by ‘resetting’ the aberrant immune response driving the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic necrotizing vasculitis affecting medium-sized arteries. Disorders of systemic vasculitis are most often diagnosed and treated by rheumatologists, the specialists in autoimmune and inflammatory connective-tissue diseases. Hence, management of polyarteritis nodosa is typically overseen by a rheumatologist, who coordinates immunosuppressive therapy and monitors organ involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Von Hansemann macrophage",
    "long_answer": "In malakoplakia, the characteristic Michaelis–Gutmann bodies are intracytoplasmic, partially calcified inclusions found within large foamy macrophages known as Von Hansemann cells. These defective macrophages accumulate undigested bacterial remnants, leading to the pathognomonic appearance of Michaelis–Gutmann bodies on microscopy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beare-Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beare-Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome is a rare FGFR2-related craniosynostosis disorder distinguished by premature cranial suture fusion and cerebriform skin folds (cutis gyrata). Patients frequently develop acanthosis nigricans, making this syndrome the established genetic condition that links craniosynostosis, cutis gyrata, and acanthosis nigricans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Addison disease",
    "long_answer": "Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) leads to deficient aldosterone and cortisol production, causing hyponatremia and volume depletion. Patients commonly develop hypergeusia (heightened taste perception) specifically for salt and manifest intense cravings for salty foods as a compensatory response to their low sodium state, making Addison disease classically associated with both features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle atrophy",
    "long_answer": "Muscle atrophy is the condition in which skeletal-muscle fibers shrink in cross-sectional area because protein breakdown exceeds synthesis, typically during disuse, immobilization, or aging. Regular exercise—especially resistance and weight-bearing training—reactivates anabolic signaling pathways (e.g., mTOR), stimulates protein synthesis, and thereby prevents or reverses the loss of muscle-cell size characteristic of muscle atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Letrozole",
    "long_answer": "Precocious puberty in McCune–Albright syndrome is gonadotropin-independent and driven by excess ovarian estrogen production. The current standard treatment is the third-generation aromatase inhibitor letrozole, which blocks conversion of androgens to estrogens, lowers circulating estradiol, slows bone-age advancement, and reduces episodes of vaginal bleeding. Clinical studies and guidelines now recommend letrozole as first-line therapy for this form of peripheral precocious puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ptosis",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, ptosis denotes the sagging or abnormal downward displacement of an organ or body part from its normal anatomical position. The suffix “-ptosis” (from the Greek πτῶσις, meaning “falling”) is used in terms such as blepharoptosis (drooping eyelid), gastroptosis (downward‐displaced stomach), and visceroptosis (sagging abdominal viscera), all illustrating this pathologic descent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC2A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC2A1 gene, which encodes the facilitative glucose transporter GLUT-1, cause GLUT-1 deficiency syndrome. Impaired GLUT-1 transport limits glucose entry into the developing brain, frequently leading to congenital or early-onset microcephaly along with seizures and developmental delay. Thus, SLC2A1 links defective glucose transport with the microcephaly phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic anterior uveitis",
    "long_answer": "In Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis, chronic anterior uveitis (also called iridocyclitis) is a well-recognized extra-articular manifestation. Persistent inflammation of the iris and ciliary body can damage the lens and promote posterior synechiae, ultimately leading to cataract formation if adequate ophthalmologic treatment is not instituted."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAP3K7",
    "long_answer": "Cardiospondylocarpofacial syndrome is produced by heterozygous mutations in the MAP3K7 gene, which encodes the kinase TAK1 involved in TGF-β/BMP signaling. These same MAP3K7 variants are documented to cause cranio-skeletal manifestations that include supernumerary teeth (hyperdontia), linking the mutation both to CSCFS and to dental anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "The majority of organic acidemias are caused by loss-of-function mutations in genes encoding metabolic enzymes. Disease manifests only when both alleles are defective, so affected individuals are typically homozygous or compound heterozygous, while heterozygous carriers remain asymptomatic. This classic pattern corresponds to autosomal recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leukocytes",
    "long_answer": "Neutral Lipid Storage Disease is marked by the cytoplasmic accumulation of triglyceride-filled lipid droplets in circulating white blood cells (leukocytes), a finding known as Jordans’ anomaly and used diagnostically for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parvovirus B19 IgM serology",
    "long_answer": "Fifth disease (erythema infectiosum) is caused by human parvovirus B19. Demonstrating virus-specific IgM antibodies in the patient’s serum by a serologic assay (e.g., ELISA) confirms an acute or recent parvovirus B19 infection, thereby verifying the diagnosis of fifth disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adolf von Strümpell",
    "long_answer": "Strümpell-Lorrain disease is the eponymous name for hereditary spastic paraplegia. The first detailed description of this inherited spastic gait disorder was published in 1893 by the German neurologist Adolf von Strümpell, for which he is credited with its identification; Maurice Lorrain later provided an independent report, giving rise to the combined eponym."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudogout",
    "long_answer": "Calcium-pyrophosphate deposition disease (pseudogout) frequently occurs in joints already affected by osteoarthritis and is also a recognized complication of Paget’s disease of bone, where increased bone turnover favors CPP crystal formation. Thus, pseudogout is associated with both Paget’s disease of bone and osteoarthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitor",
    "long_answer": "Renovascular hypertension is driven by increased renin-angiotensin-aldosterone activity from reduced renal perfusion. An ACE inhibitor (e.g., captopril, enalapril) blocks conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, decreasing vasoconstriction and aldosterone-mediated sodium retention, thus lowering blood pressure. ACE inhibitors are therefore commonly prescribed for medical management of renovascular hypertension, with monitoring of renal function and serum potassium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crouzon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder caused mainly by FGFR2 mutations. Premature fusion of the cranial sutures alters orbital anatomy, leading to ocular problems; strabismus (often a large-angle V-pattern) is reported in a majority of affected children. Thus, this genetic craniosynostosis syndrome is well known to present with strabismus in addition to skull and facial abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency impairs the carnitine-dependent shuttle that moves long-chain fatty acids into the mitochondrial matrix for β-oxidation. Because mitochondria cannot oxidize these fatty acids, energy production in heart, skeletal muscle and liver is compromised, producing the characteristic hypoketotic hypoglycemia, cardiomyopathy and myopathy. Thus, mitochondrial dysfunction underlies the clinical manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PCR of amniotic fluid",
    "long_answer": "Congenital varicella syndrome can be definitively confirmed prenatally by performing polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing on amniotic fluid obtained via amniocentesis; detection of varicella-zoster virus DNA in the fluid establishes fetal infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A supplementation",
    "long_answer": "Post-natal Vitamin A supplementation (e.g., 5 000 IU intramuscularly three times weekly) is given to extremely low-birth-weight, premature infants with or at risk for bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Vitamin A is essential for normal lung epithelial growth and alveolar development; supplementing deficient pre-term infants improves pulmonary mechanics and gas exchange, thereby lessening the severity of bronchopulmonary dysplasia and improving overall lung function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retina",
    "long_answer": "The retina can become inflamed during posterior uveitis (retinitis) and is the structure from which the vitreous gel separates in a posterior vitreous detachment. Thus, it is the ocular tissue common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyproterone acetate",
    "long_answer": "Hypersexual behaviour in Klüver–Bucy syndrome is often controlled pharmacologically with the steroidal anti-androgen cyproterone acetate. By blocking androgen receptors and suppressing testosterone production, cyproterone acetate lowers libido and reduces inappropriate sexual drive that characterises the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Chondrodystrophy (classically referring to achondroplasia/chondrodystrophic dwarfism) is produced by gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR3 gene and is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner—one mutant allele is sufficient to produce the phenotype, although many cases arise from new (de novo) mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Ollier disease (multiple enchondromatosis) is characterized by numerous benign cartilaginous tumors within bones, leading to limb deformities, pathologic fractures, and growth disturbances. Diagnosis, monitoring, and surgical management of these skeletal lesions fall under the expertise of orthopedic specialists, often in collaboration with orthopedic oncologists for tumor surveillance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trachea",
    "long_answer": "The aggressive, juvenile-onset form of laryngeal papillomatosis associated with HPV-11 often spreads beyond the larynx; among lower-airway sites, the trachea is the one most frequently involved, preceding extension to bronchi or lung parenchyma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome",
    "long_answer": "SARS-CoV-2 infection has been linked to Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), an acute immune-mediated polyneuropathy in which peripheral nerve demyelination causes rapidly progressive, often ascending muscle weakness, gait difficulty, and loss of reflexes. Numerous case reports and series have documented GBS emerging days to weeks after COVID-19 onset, consistent with the post-infectious autoimmune mechanism classically seen with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "World Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "The World Health Organization (WHO) has issued ‘Make Listening Safe’ guidelines noting that more than a billion adolescents and young adults risk noise-induced hearing loss, and therefore advises them to limit the volume and duration of exposure to loud sounds from personal audio devices, concerts and other recreational settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLEC",
    "long_answer": "Epidermolysis bullosa simplex Ogna type is an autosomal-dominant form of EBS caused by missense mutations in the PLEC gene, which encodes the cytolinker protein plectin. Defective plectin weakens the keratin–intermediate filament anchorage at hemidesmosomes, leading to skin fragility and the blistering phenotype characteristic of the Ogna variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Activates cystathionine β-synthase",
    "long_answer": "High cellular S-adenosyl methionine binds allosterically to cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), increasing CBS activity and shunting homocysteine into the trans-sulfuration pathway (conversion to cystathionine and then cysteine). This regulation prevents homocysteine accumulation when methionine/SAM levels are elevated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Mulibrey nanism results from loss-of-function mutations in the TRIM37 gene, which has been mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 17 at cytogenetic band 17q22–q23."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Impaired TCR signaling",
    "long_answer": "In ZAP70-deficient individuals the T-cell receptor is still expressed on the cell surface, but because the ZAP-70 tyrosine kinase is absent or non-functional the phosphorylation cascade normally triggered after TCR engagement cannot proceed. Consequently, antigen recognition by the TCR does not generate the intracellular signals required for thymic positive selection of CD8⁺ T cells or for peripheral T-cell activation and cytokine production, leading to a profound defect in cell-mediated immune responses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noggin",
    "long_answer": "Noggin is a secreted protein produced by the primitive node, notochord and adjacent mesoderm. It binds directly to bone morphogenetic proteins (especially BMP-4), blocking their interaction with BMP receptors. By antagonizing BMP signaling in the overlying ectoderm, Noggin removes the epidermal-inducing influence of BMPs, allowing that ectoderm to differentiate into neuroectoderm and form the neural plate during neurulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Analgesics",
    "long_answer": "Analgesic medications—such as acetaminophen or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) like ibuprofen—reduce pain by inhibiting cyclo-oxygenase enzymes and lowering prostaglandin levels, thereby relieving the headache, muscle aches, and sore throat commonly experienced during a cold."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis is an inherited hemolytic anemia caused by red-cell membrane protein defects. Diagnosis relies on specialized hematologic tests—peripheral blood smear, osmotic fragility or EMA binding assays, and interpretation of anemia and hemolysis parameters—all of which are routinely performed and interpreted by hematologists. Therefore, a hematologist (the hematology specialty) is the professional most likely to establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Porphyria",
    "long_answer": "Historians and medical researchers have suggested that King George III’s recurrent episodes of abdominal pain, dark-colored urine, and severe neuropsychiatric disturbances match the clinical picture of porphyria—a hereditary defect in heme biosynthesis that causes intermittent accumulation of neurotoxic porphyrin precursors. Genetic analyses of collateral royal descendants have identified mutations consistent with variegate porphyria, supporting the hypothesis that this autosomal-dominant porphyric disorder underlay the king’s celebrated ‘madness.’"
}

{
    "short_answer": "D-xylose test",
    "long_answer": "The D-xylose test assesses carbohydrate absorption by measuring the amount of orally administered D-xylose that appears in the urine over a set period. In small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, bacteria metabolize the D-xylose before it can be absorbed, so urinary excretion falls. A low urinary D-xylose level therefore indicates malabsorption related to SIBO."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Laminin-332 (laminin-5)",
    "long_answer": "Junctional epidermolysis bullosa results from mutations in genes (LAMA3, LAMB3, LAMC2) that encode laminin-332, formerly called laminin-5. Laminin-332 is a major anchoring-filament glycoprotein of the basement membrane zone; its loss weakens adhesion at the lamina lucida, producing the characteristic blistering of junctional EB."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium (Ca²⁺)",
    "long_answer": "In conditions such as rhabdomyolysis or malignant hyperthermia, an abnormal rise in intracellular Ca²⁺ in skeletal muscle fibers activates proteases and phospholipases that damage the sarcolemma. This membrane disruption allows myoglobin to leak out of the muscle cell into the bloodstream and urine, producing myoglobinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tubular basement membranes",
    "long_answer": "In light chain deposition disease (LCDD), monoclonal immunoglobulin light chains accumulate linearly along renal basement membranes, with the earliest, most intense, and diagnostic deposits occurring in the tubular (particularly distal tubular) basement membranes. These deposits later extend to glomerular and vascular walls, but the primary and hallmark renal site is the tubular basement membrane."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleomorphism",
    "long_answer": "Osteosarcoma typically shows highly pleomorphic malignant osteoblasts whose cells and nuclei vary greatly in size, shape and staining. This marked cellular and nuclear pleomorphism is a sign of anaplasia, a hallmark of cancer progression that indicates loss of normal differentiation and aggressive malignant potential."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD4+ T helper cells",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic CD4+ lymphocytopenia is defined by an unexplained, persistent depletion of CD4-positive T lymphocytes. It is the CD4+ T helper cell subset—responsible for coordinating adaptive immune responses—that is selectively and markedly reduced, while other lymphocyte populations such as CD8+ cytotoxic T cells or B cells are usually preserved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Propionyl-CoA carboxylase",
    "long_answer": "Propionic acidemia is caused by a deficiency of the mitochondrial enzyme propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Without this biotin-dependent enzyme, propionyl-CoA cannot be converted to methylmalonyl-CoA, leading to a buildup of propionyl-CoA and its metabolite propionic acid. The excess propionic acid enters the bloodstream, producing severe metabolic acidosis and other clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KAT6B",
    "long_answer": "Genitopatellar syndrome is caused by de novo truncating mutations in the KAT6B gene, which encodes the lysine acetyltransferase 6B histone-acetylating enzyme. Loss-of-function of this acetyltransferase disrupts chromatin remodeling and transcriptional regulation during development, leading to the characteristic skeletal, genital, and neurodevelopmental anomalies of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IFT43",
    "long_answer": "Sensenbrenner syndrome (cranioectodermal dysplasia) is a ciliopathy caused by pathogenic variants in several intraflagellar transport (IFT) genes. The IFT43 gene, located on chromosome 14q24.3, encodes a component of the IFT-A complex essential for retrograde ciliary transport. Loss-of-function mutations in IFT43 are a well-established cause of Sensenbrenner syndrome, and affected individuals commonly present with microcephaly in addition to the characteristic skeletal, craniofacial and ectodermal anomalies. Thus, IFT43 is the chromosome-14 gene whose disruption leads to Sensenbrenner syndrome and associated microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile nephronophthisis is a hereditary tubulointerstitial kidney disease whose cardinal clinical features are polyuria, polydipsia, secondary enuresis, growth retardation, anemia, and progressive loss of renal concentrating ability leading to chronic kidney failure. Because the primary pathology involves the renal tubules and interstitium rather than the glomeruli, the urine sediment is typically ‘bland’; significant hematuria or proteinuria is uncommon and, when present, tends to be only microscopic and incidental. Therefore, hematuria is not considered a common symptom of juvenile nephronophthisis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Psychiatric Association",
    "long_answer": "Schizophreniform disorder is classified and diagnosed according to criteria listed in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (currently the DSM-5). This manual is authored and published by the American Psychiatric Association, which is the authoritative organization responsible for its development and updates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apoptosis",
    "long_answer": "In Desmin-related myofibrillar myopathy, mutant or mis-folded desmin and associated proteins accumulate as cytoplasmic aggregates, disrupting the myofibrillar network. This stress activates the intrinsic (mitochondrial) caspase pathway, and affected muscle fibres show TUNEL-positive nuclei and cleaved caspase-3/-9, hallmarks of programmed cell death. Thus the predominant form of cell death in this disorder is apoptosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folic acid",
    "long_answer": "The common MTHFR (methylenetetrahydro­folate reductase) C677T polymorphism lowers enzymatic conversion of 5,10-methylene-THF to 5-methyl-THF, slowing remethylation of homocysteine to methionine and causing moderate hyperhomocysteinemia. Supplementation with folic acid (vitamin B9) increases circulating folate pools and 5-methyl-THF availability, thereby restoring remethylation capacity and reliably lowering plasma homocysteine levels in individuals with this MTHFR deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hwabyeong",
    "long_answer": "Hwabyeong (also written hwa-byung, literally “anger illness”) is a Korean culture-bound syndrome marked by chronic, suppressed anger that manifests as somatic and emotional symptoms such as chest pressure, heat sensations, palpitations, and anxiety–depression. It was still being clinically described and treated in South Korea into the mid-2000s, illustrating its persistence as a culturally specific disorder well after many other folk illnesses had declined."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a Gram-negative rod that frequently causes peritonitis, particularly in patients undergoing peritoneal dialysis, and is widely recognized as an opportunistic pathogen that exploits weakened host defenses in individuals with burns, cancer, neutropenia, cystic fibrosis, or other forms of immune compromise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is an autoimmune connective-tissue disease characterized by progressive fibrosis that causes hardening and tightening of the skin and can also affect internal organs. Pulmonary involvement, including interstitial lung disease and pulmonary hypertension, may lead to pleural inflammation and fluid accumulation, making pleural effusion a recognized complication of systemic sclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anakinra",
    "long_answer": "Familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome is part of the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) caused by gain-of-function mutations in NLRP3 that drive overproduction of interleukin-1β. Daily subcutaneous anakinra, a recombinant interleukin-1 receptor antagonist, blocks IL-1 signalling and reliably abolishes the fever, urticarial rash and arthralgia of FCAS, making it the standard targeted therapy for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FRAS1",
    "long_answer": "FRAS1 (Fraser extracellular matrix complex subunit 1) is one of the principal genes mutated in Fraser syndrome. Loss-of-function variants in FRAS1 disrupt basement-membrane integrity during embryogenesis, producing the typical Fraser features (cryptophthalmos, syndactyly, renal malformations) and, in a significant subset of affected fetuses, atypical or ambiguous genitalia. Because these genital anomalies are classified clinically as disorders of sex development (intersex), FRAS1 is a gene that links Fraser syndrome with intersex development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crohn disease",
    "long_answer": "Crohn disease, especially when the terminal ileum is inflamed or resected, impairs bile-salt reabsorption. Loss of bile salts reduces the solubilization of cholesterol in bile, leading to supersaturation and precipitation of cholesterol or mixed gallstones. Consequently, patients with Crohn disease have a markedly higher incidence of gallstone formation than the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "In Potter sequence, chronic oligohydramnios prevents normal lung expansion in utero, resulting in markedly underdeveloped lungs (pulmonary hypoplasia). At birth these infants cannot achieve adequate gas exchange, leading to severe respiratory failure, which is the most frequent cause of neonatal death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple carboxylase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Multiple carboxylase deficiency is an inherited defect (most often biotinidase or holocarboxylase-synthetase deficiency) that inactivates all biotin-dependent carboxylation enzymes. Loss of pyruvate, propionyl-CoA, and acetyl-CoA carboxylase activity disrupts energy and organic-acid metabolism, producing metabolic acidosis, seizures, dermatitis, and pronounced hypotonia in infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diaphysis",
    "long_answer": "Banki syndrome is characterised radiologically by congenitally slender shafts of the long bones; the diaphysis (the shaft portion) shows marked thinning while the metaphyses and epiphyses remain relatively normal. Thus, the thin part of the long bone in Banki syndrome is the diaphysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "In leukemia, malignant leukocyte proliferation crowds the bone marrow, suppressing normal megakaryocyte and platelet production. The resulting thrombocytopenia (markedly reduced platelet count) disrupts the blood-clotting cascade and predisposes patients to easy bruising, mucosal bleeding, and hemorrhage, making it a major clotting-related complication of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HERC2",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies have shown that variants in the HERC2 gene—particularly the rs12913832 allele that regulates the neighboring OCA2 pigment gene—are linked to increased risk of alcohol dependence. The very same variant is the principal determinant of blue versus brown eye color, making HERC2 a genetic factor common to both alcohol dependence susceptibility and eye-color determination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochlea",
    "long_answer": "Mondini dysplasia is a congenital malformation in which the cochlea fails to develop its normal 2½ turns and instead shows an incomplete, 1½-turn partition. This abnormal cochlear development is the hallmark of the Mondini deformity and underlies the sensorineural hearing loss seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conductive hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Hemifacial microsomia involves maldevelopment of the external ear canal, middle-ear ossicles, and surrounding temporal bone structures. These anatomic anomalies impede sound conduction to the inner ear, making conductive hearing loss the characteristic form of auditory impairment seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norepinephrine",
    "long_answer": "Bipolar II disorder is linked to dysregulation of the catecholaminergic system, notably altered norepinephrine signaling—elevated activity during hypomanic states and relative deficiency during depressive episodes—indicating that norepinephrine dysfunction is central to its pathophysiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "distal",
    "long_answer": "The lower (cardiac) esophageal sphincter lies at the distal, or inferior/caudal, end of the esophagus, marking the point where the esophagus joins the stomach. Thus, in anatomical terminology its position relative to the rest of the esophagus is described as distal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Hypermethioninemia is an inherited inborn error of methionine metabolism caused by mutations in genes such as MAT1A or GNMT. Because its diagnosis relies on genetic testing and its management requires counseling about hereditary transmission and metabolic treatment plans typical for genetic metabolic disorders, the condition falls under the expertise of the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "p40 (ΔNp63)",
    "long_answer": "Immunohistochemical staining for p40—the ΔNp63 isoform of the p63 transcription factor—is currently the most sensitive and highly specific marker for squamous differentiation. Because p40 is expressed in normal stratified squamous epithelium and is retained in malignant transformation, its strong nuclear positivity is used in surgical pathology to confirm the diagnosis of squamous-cell carcinoma and to distinguish it from adenocarcinoma or other non-squamous tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "New York, USA",
    "long_answer": "The Lennox–Gastaut Syndrome (LGS) Foundation is a U.S.-based 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization headquartered in New York, United States, where it coordinates education, advocacy, and research support for this severe epilepsy syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COCH",
    "long_answer": "The COCH gene, situated on human chromosome 14q11–q13, encodes the inner-ear protein cochlin. Pathogenic variants in COCH produce the DFNA9 form of autosomal-dominant, progressive, nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness (often with vestibular dysfunction). Thus COCH is the chromosome-14 gene classically associated with nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tic disorders",
    "long_answer": "Among Australian paediatric populations, transient and chronic tic disorders (including Tourette syndrome) are the most prevalent movement disorders. Community-based studies consistently show tics affecting up to 10–20 % of school-aged children at some stage, far exceeding the prevalence of other childhood movement disorders such as dystonia, chorea or cerebral palsy. Their characteristic brief, stereotyped, involuntary movements or vocalisations make them readily recognisable and account for the majority of movement-disorder presentations to Australian paediatric neurology services."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Binswanger's disease (subcortical leukoencephalopathy) is classically linked to long-standing, poorly controlled hypertension, which causes small-vessel arteriosclerosis and chronic cerebral ischemia leading to diffuse white-matter damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PANK2",
    "long_answer": "Pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration (PKAN) is an autosomal-recessive neurodegenerative disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the PANK2 gene, which encodes the mitochondrial isoform of pantothenate kinase—the rate-limiting enzyme of coenzyme A biosynthesis. Defective PANK2 activity disrupts CoA metabolism, leading to iron accumulation and progressive basal ganglia degeneration characteristic of PKAN."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the WISP3 gene lead to progressive pseudorheumatoid dysplasia. The disorder manifests only when an individual inherits two non-functional WISP3 alleles—one from each carrier parent—demonstrating an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids (glucocorticoids) dampen innate and adaptive immunity by inhibiting neutrophil migration, macrophage activation, and cytokine production. This immunosuppression reduces the host’s ability to contain invasive bacterial pathogens, thereby increasing the risk of rapidly progressive soft-tissue infections such as necrotizing fasciitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bullous pemphigoid",
    "long_answer": "Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune blistering skin disease in which IgG autoantibodies against hemidesmosomal proteins (BP180 and BP230) disrupt the dermal-epidermal junction. The resulting cleavage occurs below the epidermis, producing tense subepidermal blisters and widespread erythematous or urticarial lesions on the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in COL4A1, which encodes the α1 chain of type IV collagen in cerebral vessel basement membranes, cause fragile small vessels that bleed in utero. The resulting intracranial hemorrhage can cavitate into porencephalic cysts, and survivors typically present with congenital or early-onset hemiparesis due to the unilateral brain injury. Thus, COL4A1 mutations are the well-established genetic cause linking porencephaly with hemiparesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRCA1 and BRCA2",
    "long_answer": "Inherited pathogenic variants in the tumor-suppressor genes BRCA1 and BRCA2 greatly elevate lifetime breast-cancer risk—often to 50–80%—because loss of their DNA-repair (homologous recombination) function leads to genomic instability and malignant transformation of breast epithelial cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAOA gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Brunner syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) gene on the X chromosome. The defective MAOA enzyme fails to degrade serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine properly, leading to elevated neurotransmitter levels that manifest clinically as impulsive and aggressive behavior characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis",
    "long_answer": "Leri-Weill dyschondrosteosis is an autosomal-dominant skeletal dysplasia produced by haplo-insufficiency of the SHOX gene. The hallmark clinical feature is mesomelic dwarfism—marked shortening of the middle limb segments (radius/ulna and tibia/fibula)—often accompanied by Madelung deformity of the wrist. Because this condition specifically causes mesomelic limb shortening within the spectrum of genetic skeletal dysplasias, it is the classic disorder associated with mesomelic dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cesarean delivery",
    "long_answer": "Post-partum (puerperal) infections occur far more frequently after cesarean delivery than after vaginal birth; the uterine incision, devascularized tissue, and foreign bodies introduced during the operation create an optimal environment for bacterial contamination and growth. Consequently, cesarean delivery is recognized as the single most important and well-documented risk factor for postpartum infectious morbidity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventriculoperitoneal shunt",
    "long_answer": "Aqueductal stenosis blocks cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow between the third and fourth ventricles, leading to obstructive hydrocephalus. A ventriculoperitoneal shunt is surgically placed to divert excess CSF from the cerebral ventricles to the peritoneal cavity, relieving intracranial pressure and preventing further ventricular enlargement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fludrocortisone",
    "long_answer": "In the salt-wasting form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, deficient aldosterone production leads to hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia. Management therefore requires replacement of mineralocorticoid activity. The standard drug used is fludrocortisone, a potent synthetic analogue of aldosterone, which promotes renal sodium retention and potassium excretion, correcting the electrolyte imbalance in patients with salt-wasting CAH, including the rare variant caused by 11β-hydroxylase (CYP11B1) deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diltiazem",
    "long_answer": "Diltiazem, a benzothiazepine calcium-channel blocker, is commonly prescribed (often 240–480 mg/day) to reduce cutaneous calcium deposition in calcinosis cutis, especially the dystrophic form seen in systemic sclerosis and dermatomyositis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibula",
    "long_answer": "Acrorenal mandibular syndrome is characterized by limb malformations that include absence (aplasia) or severe hypoplasia of the fibula—the long bone situated lateral to the tibia in the lower leg."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supplementary motor area",
    "long_answer": "Neuroimaging and electrophysiological studies of Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy consistently demonstrate structural and functional abnormalities in the supplementary motor area (SMA), a medial frontal-lobe region that plans and initiates voluntary movement. Altered SMA activity within thalamo-cortical motor circuits is thought to help generate the characteristic myoclonic jerks of the disorder, linking this motor-control area directly to JME pathophysiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgM capture ELISA",
    "long_answer": "A virus-specific IgM antibody-capture enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (MAC-ELISA) detects newly produced IgM antibodies in serum or cerebrospinal fluid. Because IgM appears only after recent infection and there are no cross-reacting vaccines for most arboviruses, a positive MAC-ELISA result is accepted as definitive evidence of an arboviral infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal alcohol syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fetal alcohol syndrome (FAS) is a developmental disorder that results from prenatal exposure to alcohol. Alcohol teratogenicity disrupts neurodevelopment, leading to brain structural abnormalities and functional deficits. Children with FAS commonly show impaired fine motor skills, poor hand–eye coordination, and difficulties with tasks requiring precise motor control due to damage in cerebellar and cortical motor pathways."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lemons and limes",
    "long_answer": "Beginning in the 18th century, naval surgeons such as James Lind showed that juices from lemons and later West Indian limes cured and prevented scurvy because they supply vitamin C (ascorbic acid), which is lacking in the diet of sailors on long voyages. Lemon or lime juice therefore became a standard anti-scurvy ration in many navies, giving British sailors the nickname “limeys.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ureteroneocystostomy",
    "long_answer": "In patients with megaureter, the ureter is surgically re-implanted into the bladder through a new submucosal tunnel (ureteroneocystostomy). This antireflux reimplantation creates a length-to-diameter ratio that functions as a flap valve, preventing vesicoureteral reflux while allowing unobstructed urine drainage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Andrew Ross Sorkin",
    "long_answer": "Andrew Ross Sorkin, the New York Times financial columnist and DealBook founder, was born with an ocular coloboma, a congenital defect that leaves a key-hole–shaped gap in structures such as the iris and makes his left pupil appear unusually large and elongated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Mismatch repair cancer syndrome, more commonly referred to as constitutional mismatch repair deficiency (CMMRD), is caused by biallelic germ-line mutations in one of the DNA mismatch-repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2). Fewer than a few hundred families have been reported worldwide, and its estimated prevalence is well below 1 per 1,000,000 in the general population. It is listed in rare-disease catalogs such as Orphanet (Orpha number 180989) and the U.S. Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center. Therefore, by internationally accepted epidemiologic criteria and official designations, mismatch repair cancer syndrome is indeed considered a rare disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NS3 serine protease",
    "long_answer": "West Nile virus translates its genome as a single polyprotein that must be processed to yield the individual viral proteins. This processing is carried out by the virus-encoded NS3 serine protease (which functions with its cofactor NS2B). The NS3 protease recognizes specific cleavage sites in the polyprotein and catalyzes their hydrolysis, thereby releasing the separate structural and non-structural proteins required for viral replication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Swelling (edema)",
    "long_answer": "Right-atrial enlargement raises venous pressure, often producing peripheral edema—visible swelling of the ankles, legs, or abdomen. Swelling (tumor) is also one of the cardinal signs of inflammation, the body’s primary anti-infection defense mechanism. Thus, edema/swelling is a symptom common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHH",
    "long_answer": "The presence of duplicated, opposable triphalangeal thumbs indicates a disturbance in anterior–posterior limb-bud patterning. This phenotype is classically produced by mutations that up-regulate or mis-express Sonic Hedgehog (SHH)—often via point mutations in its ZPA enhancer—resulting in pre-axial polydactyly and conversion of the thumb into a three-phalanged digit. Thus, finding duplicated triphalangeal thumbs on physical examination strongly implicates an SHH gene mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Comprehensive Cancer Network",
    "long_answer": "The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) updated its Clinical Practice Guidelines to state that active surveillance (watch-and-wait) rather than immediate surgical resection should be the preferred initial approach for most desmoid (aggressive fibromatosis) tumors, reserving surgery for progressive or symptomatic disease. Thus, the NCCN no longer recommends surgery as the first-line treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Leukemias are malignancies that arise from blood-forming (hematopoietic) tissues in the bone marrow. Myeloid leukemia specifically involves the uncontrolled proliferation of cells of the myeloid lineage (e.g., granulocytes, monocytes, their precursors). Because it originates in and primarily affects the blood and bone marrow, it is classified within the broader category of hematologic (blood) diseases and disorders. This classification is universally accepted in hematology and oncology textbooks and by organizations such as the World Health Organization and the National Cancer Institute."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endotracheal intubation",
    "long_answer": "Endotracheal intubation often requires forceful, wide mouth opening maintained for several minutes while a laryngoscope and tube are placed. This sustained stretching of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) and its ligaments can produce capsular strain or subtle subluxation, leading to post-operative temporomandibular joint dysfunction in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical repair",
    "long_answer": "Encephaloceles are structural defects of the skull that expose brain tissue; definitive management is neurosurgical. The protruding sac is excised and the dura and skull defect are closed, often with cranial reconstruction, to protect the brain and prevent infection or CSF leakage. No medical therapy can correct the defect, so timely surgical repair is the primary treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basophils",
    "long_answer": "Basophils are a type of circulating granulocyte that contain cytoplasmic granules rich in histamine. When activated during immune responses such as infections or allergic reactions, basophils degranulate and release histamine, which increases capillary permeability and promotes vasodilation, facilitating the movement of immune cells and plasma proteins into affected tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The mediastinum",
    "long_answer": "The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity that houses vital structures including the heart, esophagus, trachea, and major blood vessels. Because it contains numerous lymph nodes and thymic tissue, it is a common site for both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphomas to arise or spread, often presenting as a mediastinal mass."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pineoblastoma",
    "long_answer": "Children with germ-line RB1 mutations who develop retinoblastoma are at particular risk for a primitive neuroectodermal tumor arising in the pineal gland, called a pineoblastoma. This pineal PNET together with ocular retinoblastoma constitutes the classic ‘trilateral retinoblastoma’ association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Androgen therapy",
    "long_answer": "Administration of synthetic androgens (e.g., oxymetholone, danazol) can transiently stimulate erythropoiesis and myelopoiesis in Fanconi anemia, raising red cell, neutrophil, and platelet counts and thus partially compensating for bone-marrow failure until definitive treatment such as hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is undertaken."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 6",
    "long_answer": "Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), particularly the HHV-6B variant, is well documented to reactivate after hematopoietic stem-cell or solid-organ transplantation and can invade the central nervous system, leading to post-transplant limbic encephalitis characterized by memory loss, seizures, and MRI abnormalities of the temporal lobes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tandem mass spectrometry",
    "long_answer": "Newborn screening programs quantify free carnitine (C0) in dried-blood-spot samples by acylcarnitine profiling with tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS). A markedly low C0 level detected by this MS/MS technique is the standard method for identifying systemic primary carnitine deficiency in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Functional neurological symptom disorder",
    "long_answer": "In DSM-5 the old diagnosis of conversion disorder was moved from the somatoform group and renamed functional neurological symptom disorder. This category covers neurologic-appearing symptoms—such as tremor, weakness, abnormal gait or other movement problems—that lack an identifiable organic cause, reflecting current clinical understanding of these functional movement disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FANCB",
    "long_answer": "The only confirmed X-linked subtype of Fanconi anemia is complementation group B. This form arises from loss-of-function mutations in the FANCB gene, which is located on the X chromosome at Xp22. Mutations in FANCB disrupt the Fanconi anemia DNA-repair pathway, producing the characteristic chromosomal instability and clinical features of X-linked Fanconi anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gout",
    "long_answer": "Elevated blood uric-acid levels (hyperuricaemia) can precipitate monosodium urate crystal deposition, producing gout. Epidemiologic and clinical studies list gout as a systemic disorder frequently associated with degenerative (calcific) aortic stenosis, making gout the condition linked to both aortic stenosis and high serum urate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Northern Cape",
    "long_answer": "The disorder was first reported in a multi-generation pedigree living near the town of Upington, which lies in South Africa’s Northern Cape Province; hence the condition was named “Upington disease.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "APOE E2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia (type III hyperlipoproteinemia) is typically caused by homozygosity for the APOE ε2 (ApoE2) variant, a missense mutation in the APOE gene (Arg158→Cys). The altered apolipoprotein E has markedly reduced affinity for the LDL receptor, leading to accumulation of remnant (β-VLDL/IDL) particles rich in cholesterol. This remnant accumulation promotes accelerated atherosclerosis, so the APOE E2 mutation confers the increased atherosclerotic risk seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Both types of hyperprolinemia (type I due to PRODH deficiency and type II due to ALDH4A1 deficiency) are transmitted in an autosomal-recessive fashion, meaning affected individuals inherit two pathogenic alleles—one from each carrier parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental isodisomy",
    "long_answer": "When both copies of the same homologous chromosome are inherited from a single heterozygous carrier parent (uniparental isodisomy), the child becomes homozygous for any recessive pathogenic variant present on that chromosome. This homozygosity can unmask rare autosomal-recessive disorders that would not appear if one normal allele were received from the other parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vagabond's disease",
    "long_answer": "Vagabond's disease is a chronic dermatosis caused by long-standing infestation with the body louse Pediculus humanus corporis. It occurs mainly in individuals with nutritional deficiency and poor personal hygiene, leading to widespread pruritus, excoriations, and diffuse, dirty-brown skin pigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Convalescent stage",
    "long_answer": "Whooping cough (pertussis) classically progresses through catarrhal, paroxysmal, and convalescent stages. In the convalescent stage the violent coughing wanes and the patient appears to recover, yet Bordetella pertussis organisms can still be present in the respiratory tract, so the individual may remain infectious for several weeks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alopecia areata",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia areata is an autoimmune disorder that produces sudden, painless loss of scalp hair. Abrupt diffuse shedding is also a classic manifestation of heavy-metal intoxications such as thallium or arsenic poisoning, so patients with alopecia areata have on occasion been erroneously suspected of being poisoned. Recognition of the characteristic exclamation-mark hairs and non-scarred smooth scalp helps differentiate alopecia areata from toxic (poisoning-related) alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs, including aspirin) inhibit cyclo-oxygenase enzymes, decreasing protective gastric prostaglandins and impairing platelet aggregation. This combination both predisposes the gastro-oesophageal mucosa to injury and hampers haemostasis, making NSAID use a well-established risk factor for upper gastrointestinal bleeding from Mallory-Weiss tears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertonia",
    "long_answer": "Sub-acute sclerosing panencephalitis produces pyramidal-tract damage, so patients develop increased muscle tone (hypertonia). Spasticity itself is defined as velocity-dependent hypertonia due to upper-motor-neuron lesions. Therefore, increased muscle tone (hypertonia) is a symptom seen in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the modality of choice for multiple system atrophy; it sensitively depicts pontocerebellar atrophy, showing reduced volume of the cerebellum and pons and characteristic signs such as the “hot-cross-bun” pontine signal."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Collecting duct",
    "long_answer": "Lithium accumulates in principal cells of the renal collecting duct, entering via the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). There it disrupts vasopressin (ADH) signaling and down-regulates aquaporin-2 water channels, impairing water reabsorption and producing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATM",
    "long_answer": "ATM (ataxia-telangiectasia mutated) is the serine/threonine-kinase gene whose loss-of-function mutations are the primary cause of classic ataxia–telangiectasia. The ATM protein also interacts directly with aprataxin and other single-strand break–repair factors; disruption of this ATM-centred repair pathway is integral to the pathogenesis of ataxia–ocular apraxia-1. Thus ATM is the common gene functionally linked to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Birdshot chorioretinopathy is an autoimmune posterior uveitis that is commonly managed with immunosuppression; systemic or peri-ocular corticosteroids are first-line agents because they dampen T-cell–mediated inflammation. Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids) are endogenous steroid hormones synthesized in the adrenal cortex of vertebrates, and the same class of molecules is formulated as drugs such as prednisone, prednisolone, or triamcinolone for clinical use."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "A lack of vitamin B12 (cobalamin) impairs myelin synthesis in peripheral nerves, producing demyelination and axonal degeneration. Clinically this manifests as symmetric distal paresthesias, numbness, and loss of proprioception—hallmarks of peripheral neuropathy. Correction of the B12 deficiency often halts or reverses these neurologic deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ligase IV syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LIG4 gene (encoding DNA ligase IV) lead to Ligase IV syndrome, a rare DNA-repair disorder characterized by severe microcephaly, growth retardation, immunodeficiency, and radiosensitivity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCAF17",
    "long_answer": "Woodhouse–Sakati syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by pathogenic mutations in the DCAF17 gene (formerly C2orf37). Molecular diagnosis relies on sequence analysis and dosage/duplication studies of DCAF17, because both point mutations and larger exonic duplications or deletions in this gene can be detected with gene duplication analysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Open lung biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Definitive confirmation of berylliosis (chronic beryllium disease) is obtained by an open lung biopsy, which provides a surgical sample of lung parenchyma that can be examined histologically for the characteristic non-caseating granulomas produced by beryllium exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium bicarbonate",
    "long_answer": "Glutathione-synthetase deficiency causes recurrent 5-oxoproline accumulation and a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. The acidosis is corrected acutely and chronically by administering sodium bicarbonate, which buffers excess acid and restores normal blood pH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Poor prognosis",
    "long_answer": "Meningococcal septicemia (meningococcemia) progresses rapidly to septic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation and multiorgan failure; even with prompt antibiotics and intensive care the case-fatality rate is about 10–40 %, and many survivors sustain major sequelae such as limb loss or neurologic deficits. Hence the overall prognosis is considered poor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease",
    "long_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease is an autosomal-recessive defect of the branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex. Inability to catabolize the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine leads to accumulation of their ketoacids, producing severe ketoacidosis along with the characteristic sweet-smelling urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increased GnRH pulse frequency",
    "long_answer": "In PCOS, the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator fires at an abnormally rapid rate. This high-frequency GnRH secretion drives the pituitary to release disproportionately more luteinising hormone (LH) than follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). Excess LH stimulates ovarian theca cells to overproduce androgens, while relatively low FSH fails to support normal follicular maturation. The resulting hyperandrogenism and arrested follicles underpin the cyst formation, anovulation and other core features of polycystic ovary syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylthioadenosine phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "The gene for the enzyme 5′-methylthioadenosine phosphorylase (MTAP) is located on chromosome 9p21. When an isochromosome 9p produces tetrasomy 9p, four copies of the MTAP gene are present, leading to a markedly increased MTAP enzymatic activity in blood or cultured cells. Quantifying this elevated MTAP activity therefore serves as a biochemical marker for confirming tetrasomy 9p."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Osteoma cutis is characterized by ectopic bone formation within the skin and subcutaneous tissue. Because it is a primary cutaneous disorder, initial evaluation, diagnosis (e.g., skin biopsy), and management are overseen by physicians trained in diseases of the skin—namely dermatologists (often pediatric dermatologists in newborns). Therefore, the specialty most likely to treat a newborn with osteoma cutis is Dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNAH5",
    "long_answer": "DNAH5 (dynein axonemal heavy chain 5) is one of the most frequently mutated genes in primary ciliary dyskinesia, where loss-of-function variants impair the outer dynein arm and ciliary motility. Transcriptomic and functional studies of nasal mucosa in allergic rhinitis have shown that DNAH5 expression is modulated during allergen-induced inflammation and participates in the epithelial inflammatory response, linking the gene to both primary ciliary dyskinesia and the pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Virilization",
    "long_answer": "Excessive production of androgens in females can lead to virilization, a condition in which typically male secondary sexual characteristics—such as deepening of the voice, increased muscle mass, hirsutism, and clitoral enlargement—develop in a woman."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)",
    "long_answer": "In XX gonadal dysgenesis the streak gonads produce virtually no sex steroids, so estrogen-dependent breast development is absent. Pubic and axillary hair, however, still appear because the adrenal cortex continues to secrete the weak androgen dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA, and its sulfate DHEAS). DHEA is converted peripherally to more potent androgens (testosterone, DHT) that act on hair follicles, making DHEA the hormone chiefly responsible for pubic hair development in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Umbilicated papules",
    "long_answer": "Molluscum contagiosum produces characteristic 2–5 mm dome-shaped, pink or flesh-colored papules that possess a central depression or dimple. Dermatologically, such small raised lesions with a central pit are termed “umbilicated papules,” which are pathognomonic for this poxvirus infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preservation of the alveolar basal lamina",
    "long_answer": "In cryptogenic organizing pneumonia the epithelial basal lamina is largely preserved after the initial lung injury. This intact basement-membrane scaffold prevents collapse and obliteration of the alveolar walls and permits granulation tissue to remain confined to airspaces, enabling reversible repair rather than permanent interstitial fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dementia with Lewy bodies",
    "long_answer": "Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) is well known to produce autonomic nervous system dysfunction—orthostatic hypotension, constipation, urinary and sweating abnormalities—because α-synuclein pathology involves brain-stem autonomic nuclei and peripheral autonomic ganglia. Dysautonomia is therefore a recognised clinical feature included in consensus diagnostic criteria for DLB, distinguishing it from Alzheimer’s disease and other dementias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aminoacidopathy",
    "long_answer": "Arakawa’s syndrome II is grouped with the inborn errors of amino-acid metabolism (aminoacidopathies). It represents a defect in a pathway that degrades specific amino acids, so it is classified as an amino acid metabolic disorder rather than an organic acidemia, carbohydrate disorder, or lipid/storage disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Kounis syndrome presents as an acute coronary syndrome—angina or myocardial infarction—triggered by an allergic reaction. Because its hallmark is coronary artery spasm or thrombosis requiring evaluation with ECG, cardiac biomarkers, and often coronary angiography, the specialty that routinely diagnoses and manages such acute coronary events is cardiology; cardiologists are therefore the clinicians most likely to recognize and treat Kounis syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cohen syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13B (also known as COH1) gene cause Cohen syndrome, an autosomal-recessive neurodevelopmental disorder. A proportion of individuals with Cohen syndrome experience epileptic seizures, linking the disorder to seizure susceptibility through defects in VPS13B."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMR vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Congenital rubella syndrome arises when a non-immune pregnant woman contracts rubella. Immunization with the live-attenuated rubella vaccine, routinely administered as part of the combined measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine, induces long-lasting immunity that prevents maternal rubella infection and thereby protects the fetus from congenital rubella syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Masson trichrome stain",
    "long_answer": "In collagenous colitis, subepithelial collagen thickening is suspected on routine H&E sections, but confirmation requires a special histochemical technique. Masson trichrome stain selectively binds collagen fibres, coloring them bright blue-green and clearly delineating the collagen layer between colonic glands, thereby confirming the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hermaphroditic (monoecious)",
    "long_answer": "Most Turbellaria flatworms possess both male and female reproductive organs in the same individual, making them simultaneous hermaphrodites (monoecious). This allows any two adults to cross-fertilize, a common reproductive strategy in this class."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant form of centronuclear myopathy is most commonly caused by mutations in the DNM2 (dynamin-2) gene, which is located on chromosome 19 (region 19p13). Hence, chromosome 19 is the chromosome associated with the autosomal dominant variant of this myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Motile cilia",
    "long_answer": "Endosalpingiosis consists of ectopic glands lined by fallopian-tube-type epithelium. These epithelial cells retain the same 9+2 microtubule ‘motile cilia’ (kinocilia) that normally propel the ovum within the tubal lumen. The presence of these motile cilia identifies the tissue as salpingotic and underlies its growth outside the fallopian tubes in endosalpingiosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heme b",
    "long_answer": "The cytochrome bc1 complex (Complex III of the respiratory chain) contains a cytochrome b subunit that carries two b-type protoheme IX groups (designated heme bL and heme bH). These b-type hemes are the characteristic heme species present in the bc1 complex and participate in electron transfer between ubiquinol and cytochrome c."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The MYOC (myocilin) gene is classically associated with juvenile-onset and adult primary open-angle glaucoma, not with primary congenital glaucoma (PCG). PCG, which presents at birth or within the first three years of life, is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in genes such as CYP1B1, LTBP2, TEK, ANGPT1, and FOXC1. While an isolated case report may occasionally describe very early glaucoma in a child carrying a MYOC variant, large genetic studies, clinical reviews, and authoritative databases (e.g., OMIM, GeneReviews, ClinGen) consistently show no established causal relationship between MYOC mutations and primary congenital glaucoma. Therefore, according to current scientific consensus, MYOC mutations are not linked to primary congenital glaucoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gum arabic",
    "long_answer": "Gum arabic (acacia gum) is the dried exudate of Acacia species and is widely employed in both foods and pharmaceutical preparations as a stabilising and emulsifying agent. Inhalation of the fine gum powder can lead to IgE-mediated sensitisation; numerous case reports have documented occupational rhinitis and asthma in workers handling or manufacturing gum arabic. Thus, gum arabic is the natural emulsifier-gum known to cause occupational asthma in gum-manufacturing personnel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ureteral stent",
    "long_answer": "When the ureter becomes abnormally dilated (a megaureter or hydroureter), placement of a ureteral stent—typically a double-J stent—creates an internal drainage channel that bypasses the obstructed or atonic segment. The stent allows urine to flow freely from the kidney to the bladder, relieves back-pressure, and gradually reduces the dilation of the ureter."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mepolizumab",
    "long_answer": "Mepolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that targets interleukin-5 (IL-5), a key cytokine driving eosinophil growth and survival. By neutralizing IL-5, mepolizumab markedly lowers blood and tissue eosinophil counts, reducing the organ damage characteristic of hypereosinophilic syndrome. Clinical trials demonstrated steroid-sparing efficacy and symptom control, leading to its FDA approval for treating hypereosinophilic syndrome without the FIP1L1-PDGFRA fusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arterial gas embolism",
    "long_answer": "During ascent, expanding air can rupture alveoli; gas then enters the pulmonary veins and is carried into the systemic arterial circulation. This arterial gas embolism is a well-recognized, potentially fatal complication of pulmonary barotrauma in scuba divers, leading to neurologic deficits, cardiovascular collapse, or stroke-like events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystic hygroma",
    "long_answer": "Historically, congenital lymphatic malformations were referred to as “cystic hygromas.” Modern ISSVA classifications recognize that these lesions are developmental malformations rather than true tumors, and current literature therefore abandons the term cystic hygroma in favor of “lymphatic malformation.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Jarcho-Levin syndrome is a form of spondylocostal/spondylothoracic dysostosis characterized by congenital malformations of the vertebrae and ribs that produce severe spinal curvature and thoracic deformity. Management focuses on correcting or stabilizing these skeletal abnormalities—often with procedures such as VEPTR implantation or spinal fusion—tasks that fall within the expertise of orthopedic surgeons, especially those specializing in pediatric spine disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jalili syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic (often compound-heterozygous) loss-of-function variants in the CNNM4 gene disrupt the magnesium transporter essential for both retinal photoreceptors and enamel-forming ameloblasts. This causes Jalili syndrome, a recessive disorder characterized by the combination of cone-rod dystrophy and amelogenesis imperfecta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paraplegia",
    "long_answer": "In mucopolysaccharidoses, accumulation of glycosaminoglycans and skeletal malformations (such as odontoid hypoplasia and vertebral canal narrowing) can compress the spinal cord. Progressive cord compression damages descending motor pathways, and the major neurological consequence is loss of motor function in the lower limbs—clinically presenting as paraplegia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mast cell",
    "long_answer": "Type I hypersensitivity (immediate allergy) is driven by IgE antibodies bound to surface FcεRI receptors on mast cells. Upon re-exposure to the allergen, cross-linking of these IgE molecules triggers mast cell degranulation, releasing histamine and other mediators that produce the characteristic allergic reactions such as urticaria, bronchoconstriction, and anaphylaxis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycerin (glycerol)",
    "long_answer": "Glycerin, also known as glycerol, is a humectant that attracts and retains water in the stratum corneum, making it an effective moisturizer frequently recommended for psoriatic skin care. Because of its excellent safety profile and skin-hydrating properties, glycerin is likewise a ubiquitous ingredient in baby lotions and a wide range of cosmetic products."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Restless legs syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Restless legs syndrome (RLS) disrupts nocturnal sleep and can produce excessive daytime sleepiness or hypersomnia. A well-established etiological factor for RLS is iron deficiency—low serum or CNS iron/ferritin levels increase RLS risk and treating the iron deficit often alleviates symptoms. Hence, RLS is both associated with hypersomnia and can be caused by iron deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-density lipoprotein (LDL)",
    "long_answer": "Elevated plasma levels of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol are strongly and independently correlated with the initiation and progression of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. LDL particles infiltrate the arterial intima, undergo oxidative modification, and trigger inflammatory processes that lead to plaque formation, making LDL the key lipoprotein risk factor for atherosclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Collagenous colitis is a form of microscopic colitis that typically responds to budesonide, antidiarrheals, or bile-acid sequestrants. For patients whose disease is refractory or intolerant to these agents, immunosuppressants and biologic anti-TNF therapies have been used. Multiple case reports and small case series, as well as both the American Gastroenterological Association (2016) and European Microscopic Colitis Group guidelines (2021), list infliximab as a therapeutic option for severe, steroid-refractory microscopic colitis, including collagenous colitis. Although the evidence base is limited and its use is off-label, infliximab has been documented to induce clinical and histologic remission in such patients. Therefore, it is scientifically accurate to say that infliximab can be used to treat collagenous colitis in selected, refractory cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling",
    "long_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling (CVS) obtains fetal trophoblast tissue early in pregnancy, providing DNA that can be analyzed for pathogenic mutations in genes such as LIS1 or DCX. Molecular testing of this CVS-derived fetal DNA allows definitive prenatal diagnosis of lissencephaly-causing genetic mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EYA1",
    "long_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome is most frequently caused by pathogenic variants in the EYA1 gene. EYA1 encodes a transcriptional co-activator essential for ear and kidney development. The EYA1 gene is located on human chromosome 8q13.3, and heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in EYA1 account for the majority of BOR syndrome cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocardial cushions",
    "long_answer": "During cardiac embryogenesis, the endocardial cushions form the atrioventricular septum and contribute to the lower (primum) portion of the atrial septum, the membranous part of the ventricular septum, and the atrioventricular valves. Maldevelopment of these cushions leads to atrioventricular septal defects that present as combined atrial and ventricular septal defects in congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zinc oxide",
    "long_answer": "Gunther disease (congenital erythropoietic porphyria) causes extreme photosensitivity because accumulated porphyrins in the skin are activated by ultraviolet and visible light. Sunscreens that rely on the physical blocker zinc oxide form an opaque layer that reflects and scatters a broad spectrum of UV radiation (UVA and UVB) and part of the visible range, thereby reducing the phototoxic reactions characteristic of the disease. Consequently, zinc oxide–containing sunscreens are recommended to help protect patients with Gunther disease from UV light exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papulopustular rosacea",
    "long_answer": "Papulopustular rosacea is the clinical subtype in which inflammatory, edematous papules and pustules develop on a background of central facial erythema. These lesions distinguish it from the erythematotelangiectatic form (mainly flushing and telangiectasias) and from other subtypes such as phymatous or ocular rosacea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Memory impairment",
    "long_answer": "Both dysexecutive syndrome and the various forms of dementia characteristically include memory impairment—particularly problems with working or recent (short-term) memory—as a core cognitive deficit. In dysexecutive syndrome this stems from disrupted frontal-lobe circuitry essential for working-memory maintenance and retrieval strategies, while in dementia (e.g., Alzheimer’s disease) progressive cortical and hippocampal degeneration compromises the encoding and recall of new information. Thus, impaired memory is a shared, well-documented symptom in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dilated cardiomyopathy",
    "long_answer": "Barth syndrome, caused by pathogenic variants in the TAZ (tafazzin) gene, classically presents with a severe form of cardiomyopathy. The most frequent cardiac manifestation is dilated cardiomyopathy—often with features of left-ventricular non-compaction—leading to ventricular dilation, impaired systolic function, and heart failure in affected males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candidiasis",
    "long_answer": "People with diabetes mellitus frequently develop candidiasis (infections by Candida species, e.g., oral thrush or vulvovaginal yeast infections). Hyperglycaemia and glycosuria provide an ideal growth medium for Candida, and diabetes-related immune dysfunction (impaired neutrophil and cytokine responses) further increases susceptibility, making candidiasis the most common fungal infection seen in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crouzon syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome is a syndromic craniosynostosis caused by FGFR2 mutations. Premature fusion of the cranial sutures produces mid-face hypoplasia and a narrow nasopharyngeal airway; this airway restriction frequently causes obstructive sleep apnea in affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topical corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Schamberg’s disease (a pigmented purpuric dermatosis) is often asymptomatic, but when itching occurs it is routinely managed with topical corticosteroid creams or ointments. The local anti-inflammatory effect of corticosteroids diminishes dermal inflammation and therefore relieves the pruritus associated with the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPAST gene (also called SPG4), which encodes the microtubule-severing protein spastin, are the single most common cause of hereditary spastic paraplegia and account for roughly 25 % of all inherited spastic paraplegia cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute pancreatitis",
    "long_answer": "Acute pancreatitis—an inflammatory condition of the pancreas—is classically associated with sudden, severe epigastric pain that often radiates to the back and is accompanied by nausea and persistent vomiting. The inflammation leads to release of pancreatic enzymes and local irritation, which stimulates the vomiting center via visceral afferent pathways, making vomiting a prominent symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Esotropia is a form of strabismus (inward deviation of one or both eyes). Diagnosis, optical correction, amblyopia therapy, and possible strabismus surgery are managed by ophthalmologists—particularly pediatric ophthalmologists—making ophthalmology the specialty that typically treats a child with esotropia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZEB2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mowat–Wilson syndrome is classically caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the ZEB2 (also called SIP1) gene. Defective ZEB2 disrupts neural-crest-derived enteric ganglia development, predisposing affected infants to Hirschsprung disease and related gastrointestinal dysmotility, which clinically presents as severe constipation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunoglobulin A (IgA)",
    "long_answer": "Patients with ankylosing spondylitis characteristically show elevated serum levels of Immunoglobulin A. Numerous studies have demonstrated that IgA concentrations correlate with disease activity and inflammation in AS, reflecting heightened mucosal and systemic immune responses typical of this spondyloarthropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Patients with polycystic kidney disease commonly develop renin-angiotensin–mediated hypertension; current nephrology guidelines recommend an angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitor as first-line therapy because RAAS blockade lowers blood pressure, reduces proteinuria, and may slow cyst-related decline in renal function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "California",
    "long_answer": "In 2002 the State of California enacted legislation that prohibits the prescription and sale of pharmaceutical lindane products, thereby banning its use for the treatment of scabies (and head-lice) because of concerns over neurotoxicity in patients and persistent contamination of water supplies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nasogastric intubation",
    "long_answer": "In cases of tricyclic antidepressant overdose, activated charcoal is typically delivered through nasogastric intubation; a tube is passed from the nostril into the stomach, allowing rapid gastric administration of the charcoal to adsorb the drug and limit systemic absorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenytoin",
    "long_answer": "Phenytoin, a hydantoin-class antiepileptic drug, is teratogenic when taken during pregnancy. In utero exposure disrupts embryonic folate metabolism and oxidative stress pathways, producing the characteristic craniofacial, limb, and growth defects collectively termed fetal hydantoin syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumocystis pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) is an opportunistic lung infection caused by the yeast-like fungus Pneumocystis jirovecii. It occurs predominantly in individuals with impaired cellular immunity—such as patients with advanced HIV/AIDS, those on prolonged corticosteroid therapy, or organ-transplant recipients—because their weakened immune defenses allow this normally harmless organism to proliferate in the alveoli, leading to diffuse interstitial pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PUVA therapy",
    "long_answer": "Acrokeratoelastoidosis is typically resistant to topical keratolytics and systemic retinoids, but multiple case reports and small series have shown marked flattening of the palmar and plantar papules after psoralen-UVA (PUVA) photochemotherapy. Consequently, PUVA has been cited in the dermatologic literature as the most consistently effective treatment for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow fibroblasts",
    "long_answer": "In primary myelofibrosis, the neoplastic megakaryocytes release profibrotic cytokines (e.g., PDGF, TGF-β) that stimulate non-neoplastic stromal fibroblasts in the marrow. These fibroblasts are the cells that synthesize and secrete the extracellular matrix proteins collagen (type I) and reticulin (type III), leading to the characteristic marrow fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediterranean diet",
    "long_answer": "Prospective cohort studies and randomized trials (e.g., the PREDIMED study) show that a Mediterranean diet—rich in vegetables, fruits, whole-grain cereals, legumes, nuts, olive oil, and fish, with limited red/processed meat and refined sugars—improves insulin sensitivity, reduces systemic inflammation, and is associated with a 20–30 % lower incidence of type 2 diabetes compared with low-fat or Western diets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myodesopsia",
    "long_answer": "Myodesopsia is the ophthalmic term for perceiving vitreous 'floaters'—mobile opacities within the vitreous humour that cast shadows on the retina—causing moving spots or threads in the patient’s visual field."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Social anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Glossophobia, the intense fear of speaking in front of others, is clinically categorized as a performance-only presentation of social anxiety disorder (also called social phobia). Social anxiety disorder involves marked fear of social or performance situations where one might be judged, making public-speaking anxiety one of its most common and well-documented manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral and maxillofacial surgery",
    "long_answer": "Rosenthal–Kloepfer syndrome is an historical name for florid cemento-osseous dysplasia (multiple cementoma), a benign fibro-osseous condition that affects the jaws. Evaluation, monitoring and any necessary surgical management of these jaw lesions are carried out by specialists in oral and maxillofacial surgery (dentistry), the discipline responsible for diseases and deformities of the facial bones and oral cavity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "GM1 gangliosidoses are rare, inherited lysosomal storage disorders caused by mutations in the GLB1 gene, so their diagnosis relies on genetic testing and enzymatic assays, and management centers on genetic counselling, metabolic monitoring, and coordination of supportive therapies. These activities fall primarily within the remit of the medical genetics (clinical/biochemical genetics) specialty, which is specifically trained to evaluate, diagnose, and guide treatment for inborn errors of metabolism such as GM1 gangliosidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "In familial lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) deficiency, defective esterification of plasma cholesterol alters red-cell membrane lipid composition, producing fragile target cells and stomatocytes. This leads to increased destruction of erythrocytes and manifests clinically as a mild to moderate hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDH1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Ollier disease (multiple enchondromatosis) is frequently caused by post-zygotic somatic mutations in the isocitrate dehydrogenase 1 (IDH1) gene. Patients who carry these mosaic IDH1 mutations not only develop multiple enchondromas but also have a markedly increased risk of developing intracranial gliomas, typically low-grade gliomas that harbor the same IDH1 (most often R132H) mutation. Thus, the presence of an IDH1 mutation is the molecular alteration linked to the higher incidence of cranial gliomas in Ollier disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Captopril renography",
    "long_answer": "A clinical prediction rule that weighs features such as resistant hypertension, abdominal bruit and rise in creatinine with ACE-inhibitors can be followed by captopril renography (ACE-inhibitor renal scintigraphy). This nuclear medicine scan compares renal perfusion and excretory curves before and after captopril; a fall in function or delayed tracer wash-out after the ACE inhibitor indicates haemodynamically significant renal artery stenosis, allowing the presence of the stenosis to be confirmed non-invasively."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1–2 % of children",
    "long_answer": "Population‐based studies indicate that reactive attachment disorder (RAD) is rare in the general child population, with epidemiological surveys in school-age cohorts finding DSM-defined RAD in roughly 1 %–2 % of children. Higher rates are observed only in high-risk groups such as maltreated or foster-care populations, but in the community the prevalence remains around one to two percent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Chronic toluene inhalation produces selective neurotoxicity in the cerebellum, especially loss of Purkinje cells, leading to cerebellar atrophy and the characteristic motor ataxia seen in long-term abusers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ectrodactyly",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly (split-hand/foot malformation) is an autosomal-dominant genetic disorder in which the central rays of the hand or foot fail to develop, leaving a cleft and functional peripheral digits. Because the remaining digits retain full sensation and proprioception, many individuals find that this natural, sensate grasp is more useful than an insensate prosthetic device, and therefore choose to keep their limb in its native form rather than use prosthetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folic acid",
    "long_answer": "Hemoglobin C disease produces chronic, low-grade hemolysis; the increased red-cell turnover raises the body’s demand for folate. Daily supplementation with folic acid (vitamin B9) prevents folate deficiency, supports erythropoiesis, and thereby helps lessen the anemia associated with hemoglobin C disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pregabalin",
    "long_answer": "Pregabalin is an α2δ-subunit calcium-channel modulator approved for the treatment of painful peripheral neuropathies (e.g., diabetic neuropathy, post-herpetic neuralgia). By dampening central neuronal excitability it can produce central nervous system depression, so commonly reported adverse effects include dizziness, somnolence, and clinically significant fatigue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "K76.7",
    "long_answer": "In the International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision (ICD-10), hepatorenal syndrome—a functional renal failure occurring in advanced liver disease—is specifically coded as K76.7 under the chapter for Diseases of the Digestive System (K70–K77)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis (Sweet syndrome) characteristically responds within 24–48 hours to oral systemic corticosteroids such as prednisone, which rapidly suppress the neutrophilic inflammation. Because of this prompt, reliable clinical improvement and extensive supporting evidence, systemic corticosteroid therapy is universally regarded as the gold-standard treatment for Sweet syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt",
    "long_answer": "A meta-analysis of randomized trials comparing large-volume paracentesis with other interventions for refractory ascites (e.g., Salerno et al., Ann Intern Med 2007) showed that placement of a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) achieved significantly higher rates of complete or partial resolution of ascites than repeated paracentesis. By creating a low-resistance channel between the portal and hepatic veins, TIPS lowers portal venous pressure and thereby reduces the formation and accumulation of ascitic fluid, making it more effective at removing (and preventing recurrence of) ascites than paracentesis alone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Viral illness",
    "long_answer": "In individuals with short-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase (SCAD) deficiency, metabolic stressors such as fasting or a febrile viral illness can precipitate energy demand that exceeds impaired fatty-acid oxidation capacity, thereby triggering clinical symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid storm",
    "long_answer": "In patients with untreated or poorly controlled hyperthyroidism, an intercurrent stress such as a systemic infection can precipitate a thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis). This acute exaggeration of thyrotoxic symptoms is life-threatening, characterized by hyperpyrexia, tachyarrhythmias, heart failure, and altered mental status, and requires emergent treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leg braces",
    "long_answer": "External orthotic leg braces are commonly prescribed for Becker muscular dystrophy. By spanning the hips, knees, and ankles, the braces support the legs and pelvis, helping to stabilize weak muscles, maintain proper alignment, and make walking easier, thereby reducing fatigue and other symptoms associated with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Smooth muscle",
    "long_answer": "Bronchogenic cysts are congenital malformations derived from the primitive foregut; their walls recapitulate normal bronchial wall components. Histologically they show ciliated pseudostratified columnar (respiratory) epithelium, cartilage, mucous glands, and bundles of smooth muscle— the same smooth muscle that forms the muscular layer of bronchi. No skeletal or cardiac muscle is present in these cysts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteotomy",
    "long_answer": "In brachydactyly, surgically realigning or lengthening the shortened phalanges with an osteotomy can improve finger alignment and joint function. By cutting and repositioning (and sometimes gradually distracting) the bone, the procedure restores more normal length or orientation, thereby enhancing grasp and dexterity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈1 in 40 000 live births",
    "long_answer": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy is a very rare autosomal-recessive lysosomal storage disorder; epidemiologic studies in Europe and North America consistently place its birth incidence at roughly one affected infant per 40 000 live births (ranging in different surveys from 1:40 000 to 1:100 000). Thus, in the general population the accepted incidence is about 1 in 40 000."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Although most cases of Goldenhar syndrome (oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum) occur sporadically, the families in which multiple first-degree relatives are affected can be accounted for by an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance with reduced penetrance and variable expressivity. This pattern permits transmission from an affected parent to offspring in successive generations while explaining the wide phenotypic variability and occasional skipping of generations observed in reported familial clusters."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection of the eye produces classic dendritic (branching, fern-like) epithelial ulcers on the cornea. These linear lesions with terminal bulbs are pathognomonic for HSV keratitis, reflecting viral replication in corneal epithelial cells and associated inflammatory response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pfam",
    "long_answer": "Pfam is the widely used Protein families database that organizes proteins into families and domains defined by hidden-Markov-model profiles. It contains dedicated entries for cytochrome c oxidase subunits (e.g., COX1, COX2, COX3), providing alignments, HMMs and functional annotations, so it is the appropriate resource for finding information on those protein families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate carboxylase",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PC gene impair the mitochondrial enzyme pyruvate carboxylase, whose normal role is to convert pyruvate to oxaloacetate. Loss of this activity blocks anaplerotic entry into the tricarboxylic acid cycle, causing accumulation of pyruvate that is instead reduced to lactate, producing severe lactic acidosis. Thus, deficiency of mitochondrial pyruvate carboxylase underlies the described clinical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHOX",
    "long_answer": "Langer mesomelic dysplasia results from homozygous or compound heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SHOX (short stature homeobox) gene. SHOX resides in the pseudoautosomal region 1 (PAR1) shared by the short arms of the X and Y chromosomes (Xp22.33/Yp11.32). Because it escapes X-inactivation and is present on both sex chromosomes, biallelic disruption of SHOX leads to the severe limb-shortening skeletal dysplasia characteristic of Langer mesomelic dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clostridium difficile colitis",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomembranous colitis is the classic endoscopic and pathologic manifestation of infection by Clostridium (Clostridioides) difficile. Toxins produced by C. difficile damage the colonic mucosa, leading to necrosis and the formation of yellow-white fibrinous plaques—pseudomembranes—on the mucosal surface. Hence, C. difficile-associated colitis is commonly termed pseudomembranous colitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Feingold syndrome type 2",
    "long_answer": "The triad of intestinal aganglionosis (Hirschsprung’s disease), hypoplastic or absent nails, and subtle facial dysmorphism corresponds to the microdeletion-8q24 disorder that is widely known as Feingold syndrome type 2. This autosomal dominant condition extends the classical Feingold phenotype to include Hirschsprung disease and nail hypoplasia, distinguishing it from type 1 that is caused by MYCN mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Chorea-acanthocytosis is caused by biallelic (homozygous or compound heterozygous) loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13A gene (encoding chorein). Because two pathogenic alleles are required for disease expression, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern, with carrier parents typically being asymptomatic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metaphyses of long bones",
    "long_answer": "In Metaphyseal dysostosis-intellectual disability-conductive deafness syndrome, the skeletal abnormality specifically involves the metaphyses—the widened, growth-plate–adjacent regions—of the long bones. These metaphyseal areas show abnormal modeling and radiographic irregularities characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroid replacement",
    "long_answer": "Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome causes bilateral adrenal hemorrhage that destroys the adrenal cortex, leading to acute and often permanent deficiency of cortisol (and frequently aldosterone). Survivors therefore may require lifelong corticosteroid replacement therapy—typically hydrocortisone (with or without fludrocortisone)—to compensate for the loss of endogenous adrenal hormones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "First and second pharyngeal arches",
    "long_answer": "Goldenhar syndrome (oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum) results from abnormal development of the first and second pharyngeal (branchial) arches, which give rise to facial bones, mandible, external ear structures, and related tissues affected in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "B-type natriuretic peptide",
    "long_answer": "Serum B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) – a cardiac hormone released from ventricular myocardium when it is stretched or failing – rises markedly in heart-failure states. Measuring BNP (or its inactive fragment NT-proBNP) is therefore routinely used to help confirm the diagnosis of peripartum cardiomyopathy, distinguishing it from non-cardiac causes of dyspnoea in late pregnancy or the early postpartum period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Astaxanthin",
    "long_answer": "Astaxanthin is a red-orange carotenoid pigment synthesized by certain microalgae (e.g., Haematococcus pluvialis). When these algae are consumed along the food chain, the astaxanthin accumulates in animals such as shrimp, krill, salmon, and flamingos, giving them their characteristic pink or reddish coloration. Because of its antioxidant properties, astaxanthin is also extracted and marketed as a human dietary supplement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meiotic nondisjunction",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental disomy most commonly originates when homologous chromosomes fail to segregate properly during meiosis I or II—a process called meiotic nondisjunction. This error creates a gamete that carries two copies of the same chromosome (or none). After fertilization, mechanisms such as trisomy rescue can leave the embryo with both chromosomes coming from one parent, producing uniparental disomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis",
    "long_answer": "Untreated or incompletely treated acute tonsillitis caused by Group A β-haemolytic Streptococcus can trigger an immune reaction in which circulating immune complexes deposit in the renal glomeruli, leading to inflammation and complement activation. This process produces post-streptococcal (acute) glomerulonephritis, typically appearing 1–3 weeks after the throat infection and characterised by haematuria, proteinuria, oedema and hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lesion biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Chronic multifocal Langerhans cell histiocytosis is definitively diagnosed by histologic and immunophenotypic examination of affected tissue; therefore, a biopsy of an involved lesion (typically a bone lesion) is performed to confirm the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epsilon-sarcoglycan",
    "long_answer": "Myoclonus-dystonia (DYT11) is most often caused by mutations in the SGCE gene, which encodes epsilon-sarcoglycan. Epsilon-sarcoglycan is a transmembrane glycoprotein component of the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex on the cell membrane. Pathogenic loss or dysfunction of this membrane protein alters cell-signalling in neurons, producing the characteristic myoclonic and dystonic movements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benign mesenchymal tumor",
    "long_answer": "A cardiac myxoma is classified as a benign neoplasm arising from mesenchymal (connective-tissue) cells in the endocardium. It is the most common primary benign tumor of the adult heart, distinguished from malignant sarcomas by its non-invasive, slow-growing behavior and lack of metastatic potential."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly type D (characterized by shortened thumbs) has been linked to pathogenic variants in the homeobox gene HOXD13. HOXD13 resides within the HOXD gene cluster on chromosome 2q31.1, and its dosage‐sensitive role in limb patterning explains the digital shortening seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheoesophageal fistula",
    "long_answer": "A tracheoesophageal fistula is a congenital malformation in which an aberrant passage forms between the esophagus and the trachea, allowing air to enter the gastrointestinal tract and gastric contents to enter the airway. This abnormal communication reflects faulty separation of the foregut into tracheal and esophageal tubes during embryogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. Beginning in the mid-1980s several peer-reviewed medical journal articles argued that Joseph Merrick (the \"Elephant Man\") most likely had Proteus syndrome rather than the earlier diagnosis of neurofibromatosis type 1.  The first widely cited paper was J.R. Tibbles and M.M. Cohen Jr., “The Proteus syndrome: the Elephant Man diagnosed,” published in 1986 in The British Medical Journal (later expanded in the American Journal of Medical Genetics).  Subsequent reviews and case analyses in journals such as the Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine, Clinical Genetics, and the American Journal of Medical Genetics have reiterated or supported that proposal.  Thus, medical journals have indeed proposed Proteus syndrome as the condition that afflicted Joseph Merrick."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pottery worker",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies have shown that people employed in pottery or ceramic production have a higher incidence of lung cancer. Pottery workers inhale large amounts of respirable crystalline silica (from clay and glazes) and, in some plants, radon-emitting materials and metallic pigments. Chronic silica dust exposure causes silicosis and persistent pulmonary inflammation, both established pathways that increase lung-cancer risk, explaining the excess cases observed in this occupation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) is brain injury caused by perinatal asphyxia. Newborns who experience HIE frequently develop persistent hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia; this perinatal-stress–induced form of hypoglycaemia is classified among the causes of congenital hyperinsulinism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenic macrophages",
    "long_answer": "In autoimmune hemolytic anemia, autoantibodies (often IgG) bind to red blood cells. These antibody-coated RBCs are recognized by Fc-receptors on splenic macrophages of the mononuclear phagocyte system, which then engulf and destroy the cells. Thus, splenic macrophages are the principal immune cells responsible for the phagocytosis of red blood cells in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Todd’s paralysis",
    "long_answer": "After a focal or generalized seizure, some patients experience a transient post-ictal neurological deficit—classically a focal weakness of a limb, one side of the body, or less commonly generalized flaccidity. This reversible deficit is known as Todd’s paralysis (Todd’s paresis, postictal paralysis), and typically lasts minutes to hours before full strength returns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNFRSF11A",
    "long_answer": "Dysosteosclerosis can result from loss-of-function mutations in TNFRSF11A, the gene encoding the RANK receptor. TNFRSF11A is situated on chromosome 18q21.33, and pathogenic variants impair RANK-mediated osteoclast differentiation, leading to the characteristic sclerosing bone changes of dysosteosclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 16",
    "long_answer": "Among spontaneous abortions, the single most frequent chromosomal abnormality detected is trisomy 16. Cytogenetic analyses of first-trimester miscarriages consistently show that this autosomal trisomy accounts for the largest proportion of chromosomally abnormal conceptions, making it the leading chromosomal cause of miscarriage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Viljoen–Kallis–Voges syndrome has been described in multiple affected siblings born to the same unaffected parents, with equal involvement of both sexes and no transmission through successive generations. This familial pattern led the original investigators to conclude that the disorder most likely follows an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth hormone therapy",
    "long_answer": "Children with Aarskog–Scott syndrome commonly present with proportionate short stature. Clinical reports and practice guidelines indicate that administering recombinant human growth hormone can stimulate linear growth and improve final height in these patients, making growth hormone therapy the standard treatment aimed specifically at their short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HIV-associated vacuolar myelopathy",
    "long_answer": "HIV-associated vacuolar myelopathy is a spinal cord disorder seen in advanced HIV infection. Degeneration of the lateral and posterior columns produces progressive spastic weakness of the legs and gait disturbance, mimicking paraplegia or spastic paraparesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography (CT)",
    "long_answer": "Brain CT scanning is the preferred imaging technique for diagnosing neurocysticercosis when calcified cysticerci are present, because CT is highly sensitive to intracranial calcifications and clearly delineates the small calcified cysts characteristic of the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lentivirus",
    "long_answer": "Visna-maedi virus is a member of the Lentivirus genus in the Retroviridae family, making it a lentivirus—a type of retrovirus that causes slow, progressive infections such as ovine progressive pneumonia in sheep."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈20 per 100 000",
    "long_answer": "Population-based epidemiological studies from Europe, North America, and Asia consistently report point prevalences for myasthenia gravis in the range of about 12–25 cases per 100 000 inhabitants. The value most frequently cited in reviews and textbooks is roughly 20 per 100 000 people, equivalent to about 1 affected individual in every 5 000 of the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Macroglobulinemia, particularly Waldenström macroglobulinemia, is a malignant disorder of B-cell lymphoplasmacytic origin that causes excess IgM (a macroglobulin) in the blood. Because it is a hematologic malignancy involving abnormal blood cells and immunoglobulins, diagnosis and management—including plasmapheresis, chemotherapy, and targeted agents—are handled by specialists in hematology (often combined with medical oncology)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is most often produced by pathogenic variants in the PKD1 gene, which is located on chromosome 16 at the 16p13-p12 region. Mutation of PKD1 leads to formation of numerous renal cysts, progressive enlargement of the kidneys, and ultimately impaired kidney function and renal failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastric mucosa",
    "long_answer": "In pernicious anemia, auto-antibodies attack the stomach’s parietal cells, which reside in the gastric mucosa. Destruction of this mucosal tissue eliminates intrinsic-factor production, preventing vitamin B12 absorption and leading to the characteristic megaloblastic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Choroid plexus papilloma",
    "long_answer": "Several case reports describe girls with Aicardi syndrome who developed choroid plexus papillomas within the lateral ventricles. This benign neuro-epithelial tumor of the choroid plexus is thus considered the tumor type most often reported in association with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is a chromosomal disorder characterized by brachydactyly—abnormally short fingers and toes—and about half of affected infants are born with congenital heart defects such as atrioventricular septal or ventricular septal defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Hydrogen cyanide (HCN) is a highly toxic, rapidly acting cellular asphyxiant. When inhaled, HCN binds to the ferric ion in cytochrome-c oxidase (complex IV) of the mitochondrial electron-transport chain, blocking oxidative phosphorylation and preventing cells from utilizing oxygen. The brainstem respiratory centers are extremely sensitive to this acute tissue hypoxia. Within seconds to minutes of significant exposure, victims can develop dyspnea, loss of consciousness, seizures, and apnea (temporary or complete cessation of breathing). If exposure continues or is severe, this apnea progresses to sustained respiratory arrest and death. Thus, according to toxicology texts and clinical reports, inhalation of hydrogen cyanide can indeed cause a temporary cessation of breathing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism",
    "long_answer": "In Klinefelter syndrome, the extra X chromosome causes primary testicular failure. Impaired Sertoli and Leydig cell function lowers testosterone output, removing negative feedback on the pituitary, so FSH and LH rise. The combination of low sex steroids with high gonadotropins defines hypergonadotropic (primary) hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urinary bladder",
    "long_answer": "Endometriosis can implant on the urinary tract, most commonly on the urinary bladder. The bladder’s primary physiological role is to act as a hollow muscular organ that collects and stores urine before micturition, making it the organ both affected by endometriotic lesions and responsible for urine storage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-L-fucosidase",
    "long_answer": "Fucosidosis is a lysosomal storage disorder caused by an inherited deficiency of the lysosomal hydrolase alpha-L-fucosidase. Lack of this enzyme prevents the normal degradation of fucose-containing glycoproteins and glycolipids, leading to pathological accumulation of fucose moieties in tissues and body fluids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Friedreich ataxia",
    "long_answer": "Friedreich ataxia is an autosomal-recessive neurodegenerative disorder caused by GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FXN gene located on chromosome 9q13. A well-recognized musculoskeletal complication of this chromosome 9–linked disease is progressive scoliosis, which develops in a majority of affected patients during childhood or adolescence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1",
    "long_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD) is classically caused by heterozygous pathogenic mutations in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin 43. These GJA1 mutations disrupt intercellular communication during limb development, leading to syndactyly that typifies the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental disomy",
    "long_answer": "Donnai–Barrow syndrome is autosomal-recessive. If a meiotic error leads to uniparental disomy—specifically isodisomy for chromosome 2 carrying the LRP2 mutation—the child can inherit two identical mutant alleles from a single carrier parent and none from the other parent, producing the disease without biparental inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PYCR1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PYCR1 gene have been documented in individuals diagnosed with De Barsy syndrome, and the same mutations define autosomal-recessive cutis laxa type 2B. PYCR1 encodes mitochondrial pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase 1, a key enzyme in proline biosynthesis; its deficiency disrupts connective-tissue integrity, producing the skin laxity and progeroid features characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychiatry",
    "long_answer": "Koro is classified as a culture-bound anxiety disorder in the DSM and ICD systems. Evaluation, diagnosis, and management—including reassurance, cognitive-behavioral therapy, and anxiolytic or antidepressant medication when needed—are carried out by psychiatrists and other mental-health professionals. Therefore the relevant medical specialty is Psychiatry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase",
    "long_answer": "Classical homocystinuria is most frequently caused by inherited deficiency of the enzyme cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), which normally converts homocysteine to cystathionine in the trans-sulfuration pathway. Loss of CBS activity leads to accumulation of homocysteine and methionine in blood and urine and accounts for the great majority of homocystinuria cases reported worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methotrexate",
    "long_answer": "Low-dose methotrexate is an established first-line systemic therapy for lymphomatoid papulosis because it suppresses the aberrant proliferation of CD30-positive T cells. The same antimetabolite drug is widely used as a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD) to control inflammation and slow disease progression in rheumatoid arthritis, making methotrexate a treatment common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX22",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TBX22 gene, which encodes a T-box transcription factor, are a well-established cause of X-linked cleft palate with or without ankyloglossia. Multiple peer-reviewed studies available in PubMed describe how loss-of-function or missense variants in TBX22 disrupt palate development, leading to isolated or syndromic cleft palate in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nail pitting",
    "long_answer": "In alopecia areata, the immune attack on hair follicles is often accompanied by involvement of the nail matrix, producing regular, finely punctate depressions in the nail plate known as nail pitting. This is the most characteristic and commonly reported nail change in patients with alopecia areata."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infantile hemangioma",
    "long_answer": "Infantile hemangiomas are benign vascular tumors that typically appear within the first weeks of life, occur in about 4–5% of infants, and grow rapidly before slowly involuting over several years. They are distinguished from congenital vascular malformations because they arise postnatally, are not present fully formed at birth, and therefore are not classified as birth defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nebulizer",
    "long_answer": "In severe croup, epinephrine (usually as racemic epinephrine) is delivered by aerosol through a nebulizer, which converts the liquid medication into a mist that can be inhaled directly into the upper airway to rapidly relieve airway obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ferroportin Q248H",
    "long_answer": "African iron overload has been linked to the Q248H missense mutation in the ferroportin gene (SLC40A1). This variant reduces the normal hepcidin-mediated inhibition of ferroportin, promoting excess intestinal iron absorption. Studies in African and African-American cohorts show that carriers of Q248H who develop type 2 diabetes frequently display elevated serum ferritin and hepatic iron, indicating the same mutation can affect individuals with diabetes as well."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLN3",
    "long_answer": "The CLN3 gene, situated on chromosome 16p12, encodes a lysosomal membrane protein; pathogenic mutations in CLN3 are the classic cause of juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (Batten disease). Thus, among NCL-associated genes, CLN3 is the one located on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Creatine kinase",
    "long_answer": "Schwartz–Jampel syndrome involves continuous myotonic muscle activity and myopathic changes; the resulting muscle-fiber leakage commonly leads to a moderate rise of the muscle enzyme creatine kinase (CK) in the bloodstream."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical repair",
    "long_answer": "A sternal cleft results from failed midline fusion of the sternum; the definitive and universally accepted management is early surgical repair, usually primary closure of the defect (sometimes with grafts or mesh) to protect intrathoracic organs and restore chest wall stability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5q35 microdeletion",
    "long_answer": "More than half of Japanese patients with Sotos syndrome carry a recurrent ~1.9–2.2 Mb microdeletion at chromosome 5q35 that removes the entire NSD1 gene. Loss of this region (and hence NSD1 haplo-insufficiency) is the documented cause in over 50 % of cases in Japan, whereas point mutations within NSD1 predominate in Western populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The CYLD tumor-suppressor gene, mutations of which cause CYLD cutaneous syndrome (Brooke-Spiegler syndrome and related disorders), is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 16 (16q12-q13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methionine",
    "long_answer": "Creatine is normally produced when guanidinoacetate is methyl-ated by guanidinoacetate methyltransferase (GAMT). The methyl donor in this reaction is S-adenosyl-methionine, which is formed from the essential amino acid methionine. Thus, methionine is the essential amino acid directly involved in the blocked methyl-transfer step of creatine synthesis in individuals with GAMT deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Southern Alberta",
    "long_answer": "3-Methylglutaconic aciduria type III (also known as DCMA syndrome caused by biallelic DNAJC19 mutations) shows a founder effect in the Hutterite colonies of southern Alberta, Canada, where the carrier frequency is about 1 in 12, resulting in the highest concentration of affected individuals in the country."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLCG2",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the PLCG2 gene (encoding phospholipase C-γ2) produce the syndrome known as PLAID/APLAID, characterized by cold-induced urticaria together with humoral immunodeficiency and other immune dysregulation features. Thus, PLCG2 mutations link cold urticaria and immunodeficiency in the same patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection",
    "long_answer": "Secondary cold agglutinin disease most frequently follows respiratory infection with Mycoplasma pneumoniae. This atypical bacterium stimulates production of high-titer IgM cold agglutinins, usually directed against the ‘I’ antigen on red cells, leading to complement-mediated hemolysis. Consequently, M. pneumoniae pneumonia is recognized as the commonest bacterial precipitant of secondary cold agglutinin disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical resection",
    "long_answer": "Paragangliomas are typically treated by complete surgical removal of the tumor. Because these neuroendocrine tumors are often well-circumscribed and can cause catecholamine excess or local mass effects, surgical resection offers definitive therapy, symptom control, and the best chance for cure or long-term disease-free survival."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reconstructive surgery",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly is usually just a cosmetic variation, but when the shortened phalanges limit grasping or other hand functions, orthopedic/hand surgeons may perform reconstructive surgery—such as bone-lengthening osteotomies or grafts—to restore proper finger length and improve functional range of motion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stroke",
    "long_answer": "Stroke, specifically an ischemic stroke, occurs when cerebral blood flow is cut off or greatly reduced (ischemia), depriving brain tissue of oxygen and glucose. Within minutes, neurons begin to malfunction and die, leading to neurological deficits. Thus, stroke is the classic brain condition that directly results from ischemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chondrosarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Chondrosarcoma is the malignant form of a cartilage-forming tumor. Unlike benign chondromas, chondrosarcomas infiltrate surrounding bone and soft tissue and possess the capacity to metastasize, most commonly to the lungs, making them locally aggressive and systemically dangerous."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The FBN1 gene, encoding the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1 whose mutations cause Marfan syndrome, is mapped to chromosome 15, specifically the 15q21.1 region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FcεRI (high-affinity IgE receptor)",
    "long_answer": "Mast cells express the high-affinity IgE receptor FcεRI on their surface. The α-chain of FcεRI specifically recognizes and binds the Fc (constant) region of IgE antibodies, allowing mast cells to become sensitized and to degranulate upon subsequent antigen-mediated cross-linking of bound IgE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SETBP1",
    "long_answer": "The SETBP1 gene encodes the SET-binding protein 1. Recurrent somatic missense mutations in SETBP1 are characteristic of several myeloid neoplasms, especially atypical chronic myeloid leukemia (aCML), where they promote leukemic transformation. Independently, de novo germline SETBP1 mutations that cluster in the same protein region cause the congenital disorder Schinzel-Giedion syndrome, establishing a shared genetic basis between these two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shortness of breath",
    "long_answer": "Both asbestosis and interstitial lung disease commonly present with progressive shortness of breath (dyspnea). In these fibrotic lung disorders, scarring of the alveolar–interstitial tissue stiffens the lungs and impairs gas exchange, leading patients to experience increasing breathlessness, especially during exertion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal trisomy",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 22 is a numerical chromosomal abnormality in which there are three copies of autosome 22 instead of the normal two, classifying it as an autosomal trisomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipoproteinaceous exudate",
    "long_answer": "Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy causes abnormal, leaky retinal vessels. Plasma rich in lipids and proteins seeps from these telangiectatic vessels, accumulating within or beneath the retina as yellow-white lipoproteinaceous exudative fluid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOS1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary gingival fibromatosis—a chronic, autosomal-dominant disorder marked by progressive gingival overgrowth—has been linked to loss-of-function mutations in the SOS1 gene (Son of Sevenless 1). Variants such as the c.3047C>T nonsense mutation in SOS1 disrupt RAS–MAPK signalling in gingival fibroblasts, driving the lifelong fibrotic enlargement of the gums."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asian elephant",
    "long_answer": "Fasciola jacksoni is a liver fluke species whose definitive host is the Asian elephant (Elephas maximus). Numerous veterinary reports from India and Sri Lanka document hepatic fascioliasis caused by F. jacksoni in captive and wild Asian elephants, establishing this species as the characteristic host for the parasite."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Menarche",
    "long_answer": "Menarche is the medical term for the first menstrual period, marking the onset of female reproductive capability. In Turner syndrome, gonadal dysgenesis leads to estrogen deficiency and ovarian failure, so most affected individuals do not experience menarche (primary amenorrhea)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Physical therapy",
    "long_answer": "Supervised physical therapy—particularly range-of-motion and strengthening exercises guided by a physiotherapist—reduces limb stiffness, enhances joint mobility, and promotes lymphatic flow, thereby improving range of motion in patients with lymphedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gomori trichrome stain",
    "long_answer": "The modified Gomori trichrome stain selectively colours skeletal-muscle fibres, highlighting abnormalities such as “ragged-red” fibres that are characteristic of mitochondrial and other myopathies; therefore, this trichrome dye is routinely used to identify muscle-fiber pathology when diagnosing myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), named after French neurologists Georges Guillain and Jean-Alexandre Barré, is an acute autoimmune polyradiculoneuropathy in which antibodies and immune cells mistakenly attack the myelin and/or axons of peripheral nerves. It typically follows respiratory or gastrointestinal infections (e.g., Campylobacter jejuni, CMV, influenza) and, more rarely, can be precipitated by vaccinations. The resulting demyelination causes rapid-onset muscle weakness and sensory disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heterocrine gland",
    "long_answer": "The pancreas contains acinar tissue that secretes digestive enzymes into ducts (exocrine function) and islets of Langerhans that release hormones such as insulin directly into the bloodstream (endocrine function), so it is classified as a heterocrine gland—one that has both exocrine and endocrine secretions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(2;13)(q35;q14)",
    "long_answer": "Among the two characteristic translocations of alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma—t(2;13)(q35;q14) producing the PAX3-FOXO1 fusion and t(1;13)(p36;q14) producing the PAX7-FOXO1 fusion—the t(2;13)(q35;q14) variant is consistently linked to higher metastatic potential and lower overall survival, thus conferring a poorer prognosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic allergic rhinitis",
    "long_answer": "Persistent allergic (IgE-mediated) rhinitis produces long-standing eosinophilic inflammation and edema of the nasal mucosa; over time this chronic inflammatory process promotes mucosal overgrowth, leading to the development of nasal polyps."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Reports of Zeichi-Ceide syndrome involve multiple affected siblings born to consanguineous, unaffected parents, a pattern that fits an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance—two carrier parents each pass on a mutant allele, producing the disorder in homozygous offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fine-needle aspiration biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Fine-needle aspiration biopsy (FNA) involves inserting a thin, hollow needle into the breast lump to withdraw a small sample of cells, which are then examined cytologically for malignant characteristics. Because it directly collects cells from the suspicious mass, FNA provides definitive pathological evidence to confirm or rule out breast cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HFE C282Y mutation",
    "long_answer": "The vast majority of hereditary hemochromatosis cases, particularly in populations of Northern European descent, are caused by homozygosity for the C282Y missense mutation in the HFE gene on chromosome 6. Because over 80–90 % of affected individuals carry this specific mutation, molecular testing for C282Y in HFE is the primary and most widely used genetic assay in the diagnostic work-up for hereditary hemochromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)",
    "long_answer": "Pain in torticollis arises from acute spasm and inflammation of the cervical muscles; first-line relief is provided by non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, which decrease prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and provide analgesia, thereby reducing both pain and muscle soreness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fowler's syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fowler's syndrome is a rare disorder of the urethral sphincter characterized by failure of sphincter relaxation and consequent chronic urinary retention in young women. Clinical studies have shown a strong association between Fowler's syndrome and polycystic ovary syndrome, making it the classical syndrome linked to PCOS that produces urinary retention."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antiphospholipid syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beyond the inherited Factor V Leiden mutation, acquired resistance to activated protein C is most often produced by antiphospholipid antibodies. In antiphospholipid syndrome, lupus-anticoagulant and anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies interfere with the protein C anticoagulant pathway, producing an acquired form of activated protein C resistance and a heightened risk of thrombosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pythiosis",
    "long_answer": "Pythium insidiosum is an aquatic oomycete that infects mammals and occasionally humans, producing a severe granulomatous disease known as pythiosis. The organism’s hyphae invade tissue, provoking eosinophilic inflammation and vascular or cutaneous lesions, which is why the condition is universally referred to as pythiosis in veterinary and medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHF6 gene, X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Börjeson-Forssman-Lehmann syndrome is caused by pathogenic mutations in the PHF6 gene located on the X chromosome (Xq26.3). Because the defective gene is on the X chromosome, the disorder is transmitted in an X-linked recessive manner: affected males show the full clinical picture, while heterozygous females are usually asymptomatic or only mildly affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intense pruritus",
    "long_answer": "Grover’s disease, or transient acantholytic dermatosis, which Ralph Wier Grover first described in 1970, is characterized chiefly by sudden-onset, severely itchy (pruritic) papules and vesicles on the trunk; the predominant and most troublesome symptom is intense pruritus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antiepileptic drugs",
    "long_answer": "When Alice in Wonderland syndrome arises secondary to epileptic activity, the underlying seizures are managed with antiepileptic (anticonvulsant) medications—such as carbamazepine, valproate, or other seizure-control agents. By stabilizing neuronal firing and preventing epileptic discharges in the temporal-occipital cortex, these drugs can eliminate the perceptual distortions characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome is almost always caused by de novo missense mutations in the FGFR2 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2) gene—most often the Ser252Trp or Pro253Arg substitutions. These gain-of-function mutations alter FGFR2 signaling, leading to premature craniosutural fusion and the characteristic craniofacial and limb abnormalities of Apert syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cigarette smoking",
    "long_answer": "The vast majority of chronic bronchitis cases are attributable to long-term cigarette smoking, whose inhaled toxins provoke persistent airway irritation, mucous gland hypertrophy, and chronic inflammation, leading to the characteristic productive cough that defines the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stanley Greenspan",
    "long_answer": "Floortime, formally known as the DIR/Floortime model, was created by child psychiatrist Dr. Stanley I. Greenspan. Greenspan designed the approach to foster emotional and developmental growth in young children by engaging them at their current developmental level and building ‘circles of communication.’ This technique is now widely applied to conditions involving attachment and social-emotional difficulties, including reactive attachment disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Digoxin toxicity is well-known to precipitate a variety of supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias. A characteristic manifestation is an accelerated junctional rhythm or junctional tachycardia (sometimes historically called the “digitalis rhythm”). The drug’s action of increasing intracellular calcium and enhancing automaticity of the atrioventricular (junctional) tissue, together with increased vagal tone, creates conditions that favor a junctional focus taking over the heart rate. Consequently, the appearance of a junctional tachycardia on the ECG is considered a classic sign of digoxin (digitalis) toxicity according to standard cardiology and pharmacology references."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal-dominant cancer-predisposition disorder caused by inherited germ-line mutations in the TP53 tumor-suppressor gene. Loss of normal p53 function impairs DNA-damage checkpoints and apoptosis, so affected individuals accumulate additional mutations more readily and develop a broad spectrum of malignancies (breast cancer, sarcomas, brain tumors, leukemias, etc.) at markedly elevated frequencies and at young ages. Thus this genetic disorder exemplifies the increased cancer risk that results from inherited genetic defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cutaneous sign of hyperlipidemia",
    "long_answer": "Xanthelasma palpebrarum consists of cholesterol-laden deposits in eyelid skin; their presence is strongly associated with elevated serum lipids (hypercholesterolemia/hypertriglyceridemia). Thus, xanthelasma often serves as a visible dermatologic marker indicating underlying hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acclimatization",
    "long_answer": "At high altitude the fall in inspired PO₂ disturbs oxygen homeostasis. The body undergoes acclimatization: peripheral chemoreceptors drive hyperventilation, kidneys dump bicarbonate to offset respiratory alkalosis, hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF-1α) up-regulates erythropoietin leading to increased red-cell mass, and 2,3-BPG rises to unload O₂ to tissues. Together these coordinated adjustments restore arterial O₂ content and pH, re-establishing systemic homeostasis despite the hypoxic environment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midbrain",
    "long_answer": "The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) arises from paired oculomotor nuclei located in the midbrain, specifically in the ventral periaqueductal gray at the level of the superior colliculus. Therefore, lesions of the midbrain can damage these nuclei and produce oculomotor nerve palsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Familial studies of primary palmar (focal) hyperhidrosis have mapped a susceptibility locus to the long arm of chromosome 14 (14q11.2-q13). This linkage indicates that genetic variants on chromosome 14 underlie the inherited form of excessive palmar sweating."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Marfanoid habitus can develop conductive hearing loss because lax connective tissue and recurrent otitis media may weaken or scar the tympanic membrane and ossicular chain. Damage or inefficient vibration of the tympanic membrane impairs sound transmission into the middle ear, so hearing loss is a recognized symptom attributable to tympanic-membrane pathology in these individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant connective-tissue disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene on chromosome 15, which encodes fibrillin-1. The abnormal connective tissue weakens the aortic root and cardiac valves, often causing cardiovascular manifestations such as aortic dilation, valve prolapse, and resultant heart palpitations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Essential pentosuria",
    "long_answer": "Essential pentosuria is a benign hereditary metabolic disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme L-xylulose reductase in the uronic acid pathway. Because L-xylulose cannot be efficiently reduced to xylitol, it accumulates and is excreted in the urine, leading to the characteristic finding of large amounts of L-xylulose in urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hugo Liepmann",
    "long_answer": "The German physiologist-neurologist Hugo Karl Liepmann (1863–1925) first defined and analyzed ideomotor apraxia, showing that damage to the dominant parietofrontal network disrupts the translation of an idea of movement into its motor execution. His classic studies laid the foundation for the modern understanding and classification of apraxic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Christopher E. M. Griffiths",
    "long_answer": "In the early 1980s, British dermatologist Professor Christopher E. M. Griffiths proposed the now-standard classification of pityriasis rubra pilaris into six clinical types (classical adult, atypical adult, classical juvenile, circumscribed juvenile, atypical juvenile, and later the HIV-associated form). His work provided a systematic framework that is still used to guide diagnosis and management of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious diseases",
    "long_answer": "Babesiosis is a tick-borne parasitic infection caused by Babesia species; management requires recognizing the pathogen, ordering specialized blood smears/PCR, and prescribing specific antimicrobials such as atovaquone-azithromycin or clindamycin-quinine. These diagnostic tests and treatment regimens fall within the expertise of an infectious diseases specialist, the medical specialty that routinely evaluates and treats parasitic and other systemic infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cysteine",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome most commonly results from missense mutations in FGFR2 that replace critical cysteine residues (e.g., Cys342 or Cys278). Loss or alteration of these cysteine residues disrupts disulfide-bond formation in the receptor’s extracellular domain, leading to constitutive FGFR2 signaling and the craniosynostosis characteristic of Crouzon syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APOL1 risk variants",
    "long_answer": "The Apolipoprotein L1 (APOL1) gene lies on chromosome 22q12.3. Two coding risk variants—G1 (S342G/I384M) and G2 (ΔN388/Y389)—are strongly associated with hypertension-attributed nephropathy and progression to end-stage renal disease, especially in people of African ancestry. Clinical and epidemiologic studies have also shown that carriers of these APOL1 risk alleles have a higher incidence of severe pruritus (itching), particularly in chronic kidney disease and haemodialysis settings, linking the same gene variants to both hypertensive nephropathy and itching."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the NLRP3 gene (also called CIAS1 or cryopyrin) are a well-established cause of the cryopyrin-associated periodic syndrome (CAPS) spectrum, which includes Familial Cold Autoinflammatory Syndrome, Muckle-Wells syndrome, and Neonatal-Onset Multisystem Inflammatory Disease/Chronic Infantile Neurologic Cutaneous and Articular syndrome. These conditions are prototypical hereditary periodic fever (autoinflammatory) syndromes characterized by recurrent episodes of fever, rash, and systemic inflammation. This relationship is documented in numerous genetic and clinical studies and is part of current medical consensus. Hence, NLRP3 mutations do cause periodic fever syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interstitial keratitis",
    "long_answer": "Late congenital syphilis classically causes interstitial keratitis—a chronic, immune-mediated inflammation of the corneal stroma that produces corneal vascularization and subsequent scarring, leading to visual impairment. This ocular manifestation is part of Hutchinson’s triad (interstitial keratitis, sensorineural deafness, and Hutchinson teeth) seen in late congenital syphilis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pruritus",
    "long_answer": "In mycosis fungoides—the most common form of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma—patients typically present with erythematous patches or plaques, but the symptom they report most often is intense itching. Multiple clinical studies and dermatologic reviews cite pruritus as the predominant, most frequently mentioned complaint, occurring in a large proportion of cases and often prompting patients to seek medical attention."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked sideroblastic anemia with ataxia",
    "long_answer": "The disorder is X-linked sideroblastic anemia with ataxia (XLSA/A). It was first reported by May et al. in 1997 in a single two-generation Mexican family in which only six hemizygous males were affected. All showed microcytic sideroblastic anemia accompanied by a mild, progressive cerebellar ataxia. The condition is caused by a missense mutation in the X-chromosomal ABCB7 gene, and no additional families have been documented, making these six males the only described cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto's thyroiditis",
    "long_answer": "People with coeliac disease frequently develop Hashimoto's thyroiditis—an autoimmune destruction of the thyroid gland—because both conditions share predisposing HLA-DQ2/DQ8 alleles and similar loss-of-tolerance mechanisms. Epidemiological studies show the prevalence of Hashimoto’s in coeliac patients is several-fold higher than in the general population, making it the autoimmune thyroid disease most commonly associated with coeliac disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hirschsprung disease",
    "long_answer": "Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) is characterized by developmental absence of enteric ganglion cells in the myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner) plexuses—the ‘tendinous plexus layer’ referred to. Without these neural plexuses, coordinated peristalsis in the affected segment fails, causing functional obstruction that manifests clinically as severe, chronic constipation and abdominal distension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Icosahedral",
    "long_answer": "Herpesviruses possess a nucleocapsid with icosahedral symmetry (T=16), built from 162 capsomeres. This icosahedral capsid is surrounded by the tegument layer and the viral envelope, but the core structural geometry of the capsid itself is distinctly icosahedral."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperkeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Hyperkeratosis is a pathologic thickening of the stratum corneum. Vitamin A deficiency impairs normal keratinocyte differentiation, leading to excessive keratin accumulation (follicular hyperkeratosis). Mechanical irritation from repeated friction or pressure similarly stimulates keratinocyte proliferation and cornification, producing callus-type hyperkeratosis in the affected skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (45,X or its variants) is definitively identified in utero by obtaining fetal cells through amniocentesis (or earlier by chorionic villus sampling) and performing karyotype or chromosomal microarray analysis. Demonstration of a monosomy X cell line confirms the diagnosis during pregnancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothalamus",
    "long_answer": "Shapiro syndrome (episodic spontaneous hypothermia with or without agenesis of the corpus callosum) is characterized by a failure of central thermoregulation. Thermoregulatory control in humans is governed mainly by the anterior pre-optic region of the hypothalamus, and neuroimaging as well as pathophysiological studies of Shapiro syndrome consistently implicate dysfunction of this hypothalamic thermoregulatory center as the cause of the profound hypothermia and hyperhidrosis attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Granulosa cells",
    "long_answer": "Within ovarian follicles, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) binds to FSH receptors expressed on granulosa cells. These cells are responsible for converting androgens to estrogens and supporting oocyte maturation. When there is FSH insensitivity—such as from loss-of-function mutations in the FSH receptor—the granulosa cells cannot respond to circulating FSH, disrupting follicular growth and estrogen production. Therefore, granulosa cells are the cell type specifically affected by FSH insensitivity in ovarian follicles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular microfibril protein fibrillin-1, are the established cause of acromicric dysplasia. The identical cluster of FBN1 missense variants (particularly within the TB5 domain) has also been documented in patients described clinically as having short-limb disproportionate short stature. Thus, pathogenic FBN1 mutations underlie both acromicric dysplasia and the short-limb disproportionate short-stature phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The SERPINA1 gene, which encodes alpha-1 antitrypsin and whose mutations cause alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, is located on the long arm of chromosome 14 (region 14q32.13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polysplenia syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Polysplenia syndrome is a heterotaxy (left-isomerism) disorder characterized by the presence of multiple small accessory spleens. It frequently coexists with other laterality defects such as dextrocardia, malpositioned abdominal organs, and can include hepatobiliary anomalies like biliary atresia. The combination of these findings firmly points to polysplenia syndrome as the underlying congenital condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mutualism",
    "long_answer": "When a microbe is passed vertically—i.e., from parent to offspring—its survival becomes tied to the host’s reproductive success. Natural selection therefore favors strains that do not harm, and often benefit, the host. Over evolutionary time this alignment of fitness interests drives a transition from parasitism toward a mutually beneficial symbiosis (mutualism), as seen in many insect endosymbionts and the ancestral origin of mitochondria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRCA2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic germ-line variants in the BRCA2 gene markedly increase a man’s lifetime risk of developing prostate cancer. BRCA2 encodes a key protein in the homologous-recombination pathway of DNA damage repair; loss-of-function mutations impair double-strand break repair, promoting genomic instability and cancer development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encephalitis lethargica",
    "long_answer": "Encephalitis lethargica (von Economo’s encephalitis) was the epidemic viral encephalitis that swept the world in the early 20th century. Survivors frequently developed a delayed parkinsonian syndrome—termed post-encephalitic parkinsonism—characterized by rigidity, bradykinesia, and tremor. Thus, encephalitis lethargica is the classic neurological disorder that causes post-encephalitic parkinsonism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dental floss",
    "long_answer": "Dental floss is routinely recommended in addition to tooth-brushing because it removes plaque and food debris from the interdental spaces that bristles cannot reach. Regular interdental cleaning with floss reduces bacterial biofilm accumulation, thereby lowering gingival inflammation and the risk of progression to periodontal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Regional lymph-node involvement",
    "long_answer": "In gastric (stomach) cancer staging, the TNM system uses the number of metastatic regional lymph nodes (N0, N1, N2, N3) to classify disease spread. The presence and count of positive lymph nodes directly determine the N category, which in turn up- or down-stages the overall stage and is a major prognostic indicator."
}

{
    "short_answer": "47,XXX",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy X is characterized by the presence of an extra X chromosome in each somatic cell, yielding a total of 47 chromosomes instead of the normal 46. The standard karyotype notation for this sex-chromosome aneuploidy is 47,XXX."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "The aqueous, bicarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid is produced by the epithelial cells lining the pancreatic ducts. Malignant transformation of these duct-secreting cells gives rise to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, the commonest form of pancreatic cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacral agenesis",
    "long_answer": "Kurt Fearnley was born with sacral agenesis—also called caudal regression syndrome—a congenital malformation in which parts of the sacrum and lower spine fail to form properly. This developmental defect causes severe impairment of lower-limb growth and function, explaining why Fearnley uses a wheelchair and competes in Paralympic events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The human RET proto-oncogene, implicated in familial and sporadic papillary thyroid carcinoma as well as multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, is mapped to band q11.2 on the long arm of chromosome 10."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "Conservative management of neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome typically starts with physical therapy and pain control. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen or naproxen are routinely prescribed because they reduce inflammation of surrounding soft tissues and provide analgesia, thereby alleviating the arm and shoulder pain characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondrial DNA deletion",
    "long_answer": "Kearns–Sayre syndrome is caused by a sporadic, large-scale deletion within mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), typically a single deletion of 1.3–10 kb (classically the 4,977-bp deletion). This loss of mtDNA segments impairs oxidative phosphorylation in affected tissues, producing the characteristic multisystem manifestations of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatrics",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome is a congenital chromosomal disorder identified at birth or in early infancy. Comprehensive care—including monitoring of growth, development, congenital heart defects, thyroid function, and hearing/vision—is coordinated from childhood onward. These responsibilities fall primarily to pediatricians, making Pediatrics the specialty most commonly responsible for treating and managing patients with Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marfan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant connective-tissue disorder caused mainly by mutations in the FBN1 gene encoding fibrillin-1. Affected individuals characteristically have a tall, thin (marfanoid) habitus with long limbs and fingers, and up to two-thirds develop scoliosis or other spinal curvatures due to weakened connective tissue in the vertebral column."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal balloon aortic valvuloplasty",
    "long_answer": "When severe aortic stenosis in the fetus threatens to arrest left-ventricular growth and evolve into hypoplastic left heart syndrome, the established intervention is ultrasound-guided fetal balloon aortic valvuloplasty. In this procedure a needle and guidewire are advanced through the maternal abdomen and fetal chest into the left ventricle; a balloon catheter is then inflated across the stenotic aortic valve to relieve the obstruction, promote antegrade flow, and allow the left heart structures to continue developing, thereby reducing progression to hypoplastic left heart syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1 (connexin-43)",
    "long_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD) is classically caused by heterozygous mutations in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-43. These mutations disrupt intercellular communication, producing the characteristic ocular, digital and dental anomalies. The defective enamel and dentin formed in ODDD patients make teeth highly susceptible to dental caries, so GJA1 mutations are directly linked to both the syndrome and the increased incidence of caries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Gastrocutaneous (Gardner) syndrome features numerous adenomatous polyps throughout the gastrointestinal tract in addition to cutaneous lesions. Because the dominant, life-threatening component is the gastrointestinal polyposis with a very high risk of colorectal carcinoma, patients are primarily managed and treated by gastroenterologists (often with colorectal surgical support) who perform endoscopic surveillance and prophylactic colectomy when needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningoencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Klüver–Bucy syndrome often arises after bilateral lesions of the medial temporal lobes. One condition capable of producing such damage is meningoencephalitis—the simultaneous inflammation of the meninges (meningitis) and brain parenchyma (encephalitis). Viral meningoencephalitis, particularly due to herpes simplex virus, can destroy temporal-lobe structures like the amygdala and hippocampus, precipitating the characteristic behavioural changes of Klüver–Bucy syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tamoxifen",
    "long_answer": "Tamoxifen, a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM), is commonly used in the medical management of desmoid tumors because it binds to and blocks estrogen receptors, reducing estrogen-driven cellular proliferation in these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution abdominal ultrasonography is the standard imaging technique for detecting intramural or intraluminal masses in the gallbladder. Smooth-muscle tumours such as leiomyomas produce a well-defined, homogeneously echogenic or hypoechoic mass that can be visualised on ultrasound, allowing non-invasive identification and differentiation from other gallbladder lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hamster",
    "long_answer": "The first enzyme-replacement drug for Gaucher’s disease (recombinant glucocerebrosidase, marketed as Cerezyme) is manufactured in cultured Chinese hamster ovary (CHO) cells. These hamster cells are engineered to produce the human enzyme, which is purified and administered to patients to replace the deficient lysosomal glucocerebrosidase characteristic of Gaucher’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenomegaly",
    "long_answer": "Primary myelofibrosis is characterized by bone-marrow fibrosis that drives extramedullary hematopoiesis, most prominently in the spleen. This results in marked splenic enlargement—splenomegaly—which is the classic organ enlargement associated with the disease and is present in the great majority of patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Walking pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Walking pneumonia—most often caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae—is an atypical form of pneumonia whose symptom complex commonly includes low-grade fever, persistent dry cough, fatigue, and characteristic night sweats. The systemic, slowly progressive infection provokes autonomic dysregulation and cytokine release that lead to intermittent sweating episodes during sleep, distinguishing it clinically from many other pneumonias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BMPR2",
    "long_answer": "BMPR2 (bone morphogenetic protein receptor type II) is the principal gene mutated in heritable and idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension. It encodes a receptor for BMP ligands that regulate bone morphogenesis and remodeling, directly linking the gene to bone metabolism as well as vascular function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTX2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the homeobox gene OTX2 are a recognised cause of septo-optic dysplasia. Because OTX2 is a master transcription factor for early eye field specification, the same mutations frequently produce severe bilateral ocular malformations—most notably anophthalmia or microphthalmia—together with the optic nerve hypoplasia and pituitary abnormalities that characterise septo-optic dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDOB gene mutation testing",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary fructose intolerance is caused by pathogenic variants in the aldolase-B (ALDOB) gene. Definitive confirmation is obtained by molecular genetic testing that identifies these ALDOB mutations, eliminating the need for risky fructose challenge tests or liver biopsy enzyme assays."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arterial switch operation",
    "long_answer": "When a double-outlet right ventricle presents with physiology of transposition of the great arteries (Taussig-Bing type), definitive repair is achieved with an arterial switch (Jatene) procedure, usually combined with an intraventricular tunnel to route left-ventricular blood to the neo-aorta. This restores normal ventriculo-arterial connections and separates the systemic and pulmonary circulations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDH18A1",
    "long_answer": "De Barsy syndrome (autosomal-recessive progeroid cutis laxa) has been associated with loss-of-function mutations in the ALDH18A1 gene, which encodes Δ¹-pyrroline-5-carboxylate synthase. Disruption of ALDH18A1 impairs proline biosynthesis, adversely affecting collagen and elastin maturation and leading to the characteristic skin laxity, progeroid appearance, and systemic findings of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inorganic pyrophosphate",
    "long_answer": "Generalized Arterial Calcification of Infancy (GACI) is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in ENPP1, the enzyme that generates inorganic pyrophosphate (PPi) from extracellular ATP. PPi is a small inorganic phosphate dimer that potently inhibits hydroxyapatite crystal nucleation and growth, thereby preventing extracellular matrix calcification. When PPi levels fall because ENPP1 is defective, calcification of arterial walls and other tissues occurs, making inorganic pyrophosphate the key physiological inhibitor whose deficiency underlies GACI."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spondylolisthesis",
    "long_answer": "Spondylolisthesis is the anterior (or posterior) slippage of one vertebra relative to the one below it. The displacement can narrow neural foramina or the spinal canal, compressing exiting nerve roots and producing radicular (nerve root) pain. When the slip is progressive or causes instability and neurological symptoms that do not respond to conservative treatment, surgical stabilization with spinal fusion is commonly performed to prevent further slippage and relieve nerve compression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactose intolerance",
    "long_answer": "A congenital genetic defect in the brush-border disaccharidase lactase prevents hydrolysis of lactose in the small intestine. The undigested lactose osmotically draws water into the lumen and is fermented by colonic bacteria, producing gas, bloating, abdominal pain, and osmotic diarrhea—clinical features collectively termed lactose intolerance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolactinoma",
    "long_answer": "Galactorrhea accompanied by hyperprolactinemia is most often produced by a prolactinoma—a lactotroph adenoma of the anterior pituitary that secretes excess prolactin. The elevated prolactin levels stimulate milk production (galactorrhea) even in non-lactating individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Short rib–polydactyly syndrome is an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia caused by mutations in genes that disrupt ciliogenesis. Because it is a congenital, heritable disorder requiring genetic diagnosis, carrier testing, prenatal counselling and coordination of multidisciplinary management, the specialty responsible is medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids such as prednisone are frequently prescribed to control the inflammatory and eosinophilic components of Kimura’s disease. Prolonged or high-dose glucocorticoid therapy raises circulating cortisol-like activity, producing classic iatrogenic (exogenous) Cushing’s syndrome with features such as moon face, central obesity, and skin thinning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin",
    "long_answer": "Intramuscular injection of botulinum toxin type A (e.g., onabotulinumtoxinA) is the main pharmacologic therapy for many forms of focal dystonia. Although it is FDA-approved for cervical dystonia and blepharospasm, clinicians routinely use it off-label for other focal dystonias such as writer’s cramp, musician’s hand dystonia, oromandibular and laryngeal dystonia. The toxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, weakening overactive muscles and reducing the involuntary postures characteristic of these disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Fibrous dysplasia arises from a post-zygotic activating mutation in the GNAS gene, which encodes the stimulatory G-protein α-subunit (Gsα). The mutation causes constitutive cAMP signaling in osteogenic precursors, leading to replacement of normal bone with fibrous tissue—hallmark of the disease and of related McCune-Albright syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Agoraphobia",
    "long_answer": "Long-term benzodiazepine use can lead to dependence and inter-dose withdrawal that provokes panic-like symptoms. To avoid these feelings, some patients begin to fear and avoid places where escape or help might be hard to obtain, fulfilling DSM criteria for agoraphobia—an anxiety disorder characterized by marked fear of situations such as crowds, bridges, or leaving home alone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Short-limb skeletal dysplasia with severe combined immunodeficiency (e.g., cartilage-hair hypoplasia caused by biallelic RMRP mutations) is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner: affected individuals have pathogenic variants on both alleles, while heterozygous parents are clinically unaffected carriers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-inflammatory diet",
    "long_answer": "Adopting an anti-inflammatory eating pattern (e.g., Mediterranean-style, rich in omega-3 fatty acids, fruits, vegetables, whole grains and low in refined sugar and saturated fat) lowers systemic levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-α, IL-1β and IL-6 and improves gut-microbiota balance. By dampening the autoimmune-driven inflammation that damages lacrimal and salivary glands, this dietary approach can reduce ocular and oral dryness, arthralgia and fatigue, thereby relieving the overall severity of Sjögren’s disease symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BRAF V600E mutation",
    "long_answer": "Most Erdheim–Chester disease lesions carry the somatic BRAF V600E point mutation, an activating change in the BRAF gene that substitutes valine with glutamic acid at codon 600. This alteration constitutively activates the MAPK/ERK signaling pathway and is detected in roughly 50–70% of patients, guiding the use of BRAF inhibitors as targeted therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Defective nutrition",
    "long_answer": "In dystrophy, the degeneration of tissue is mainly the result of faulty or inadequate nutrition of the cells—i.e., a metabolic disturbance that deprives the tissue of the substances it needs to maintain normal structure and function. This defective nutrition leads to progressive cellular damage and degeneration characteristic of dystrophic changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic liver cirrhosis can lead to several characteristic nail findings. Hypoalbuminemia from impaired hepatic protein synthesis produces paired white transverse bands known as Muehrcke’s lines. Altered nail-bed vascularity and connective-tissue changes give the nails a white, ‘ground-glass’ appearance with a distal brown band, termed Terry’s nails. Cirrhosis is also a recognized cause of digital clubbing, likely through hepatopulmonary and vascular mechanisms. Thus, liver cirrhosis is the single condition classically associated with the triad of Muehrcke’s lines, Terry’s nails, and nail clubbing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPS6KA3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Coffin–Lowry syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the X-linked RPS6KA3 gene (encoding the RSK2 kinase). Mutations in RPS6KA3 not only produce the characteristic neuro-skeletal phenotype of the syndrome but have also been documented in patients who developed dilated cardiomyopathy progressing to heart failure, linking RPS6KA3 mutations to cardiac decompensation in CLS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The RECQL4 gene, mutations of which cause Rothmund–Thomson syndrome, is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 8 (region 8q24.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome is a genetic disorder characterized by congenital sensorineural hearing loss together with pigmentary abnormalities, most notably poliosis (a white forelock or patches of depigmented hair). Mutations in genes involved in neural crest and melanocyte development (e.g., PAX3, MITF, SOX10, EDNRB/EDN3) underlie the syndrome, leading to both auditory defects and hair/skin depigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatoid nodules",
    "long_answer": "Firm, subcutaneous rheumatoid nodules are a classic extra-articular manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis; they appear on pressure points such as the elbows, fingers, or Achilles region and represent areas of granulomatous inflammation associated with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oxytocin",
    "long_answer": "Oxytocin is synthesized by neurons in the paraventricular and supra-optic nuclei of the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary. It promotes milk ejection (let-down reflex) and fosters maternal bonding. Damage or disease of the hypothalamus can impair oxytocin production or release, disrupting both lactation and bonding behaviors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Say–Meyer syndrome is classified among the X-linked intellectual-developmental disorders (OMIM: MRXSM). Reported families show affected males who inherit the pathogenic locus through carrier mothers, while heterozygous females are largely unaffected or only mildly affected—an inheritance pattern that is characteristic of X-linked recessive transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "St. Helena familial genu valgum is a hereditary bone-alignment deformity (knock-knees) that affects the growth and angulation of the lower limbs. Evaluation of limb alignment, imaging, growth-plate assessment, and corrective interventions such as osteotomy or guided growth are all within the scope of orthopedic surgeons, particularly pediatric orthopedists. Therefore, the orthopedic specialty is responsible for diagnosing and treating this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diagnostic paracentesis",
    "long_answer": "For every patient who presents to the hospital with new-onset ascites, guidelines recommend performing a diagnostic paracentesis on admission. Sampling and analyzing the ascitic fluid (cell count with differential, culture, albumin, total protein, etc.) is essential to detect spontaneous bacterial peritonitis or other etiologies and to guide immediate management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femur",
    "long_answer": "Hecht–Scott syndrome, also known as femoral hypoplasia–unusual facies (femoral–facial) syndrome, is characterized by marked hypoplasia or absence of the femurs, whereas other long bones are largely unaffected. Therefore, the femur is the bone primarily affected in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kidney",
    "long_answer": "Lachiewicz–Sibley syndrome is an eponym for benign familial hematuria (thin-basement-membrane nephropathy), a hereditary collagen IV defect that thins the glomerular basement membrane. The disorder manifests as persistent microscopic hematuria arising from the renal glomeruli, so the organ primarily affected is the kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sepsis",
    "long_answer": "Patients who experience spontaneous bouts of hypothermia accompanied by profuse sweating—as seen in Shapiro syndrome or spontaneous periodic hypothermia–hyperhidrosis—often present with low body temperature, tachycardia, diaphoresis and altered sensorium. These signs closely mimic the systemic inflammatory response seen in severe infection, so they are frequently misdiagnosed as sepsis before the true neurogenic thermoregulatory disorder is recognized."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "Cystic hygroma is typically identified in utero by high-resolution prenatal ultrasonography, which visualizes the characteristic multilocular, fluid-filled lymphatic cysts—often seen as increased nuchal translucency or a neck mass—during routine first- or second-trimester obstetric scans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the LMNA gene (coding for lamin A/C) produce an aggressive form of familial dilated cardiomyopathy that is distinguished by early-onset ventricular arrhythmias and rapid progression to end-stage heart failure. Because of these malignant electrical and mechanical complications, LMNA-positive patients show a markedly high rate of implantable defibrillator therapy and ultimately cardiac transplantation, features that substantially diminish quality of life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facio-oculo-acoustico-renal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Al-Gazali-Donnai-Mueller syndrome is the same disorder that was later termed facio-oculo-acoustico-renal (FOAR) syndrome, also known as Donnai-Barrow syndrome. It is an autosomal-recessive condition caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the LRP2 gene and is characterized by distinctive facial features, ocular anomalies (severe myopia, retinal degeneration), sensorineural deafness, and renal tubular proteinuria, often with agenesis of the corpus callosum and diaphragmatic hernia. Thus, the accepted alternate name for Al-Gazali-Donnai-Mueller syndrome is facio-oculo-acoustico-renal syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin",
    "long_answer": "Intramuscular injections of botulinum toxin (e.g., onabotulinumtoxinA) are a standard therapy for treating focal spasticity in patients with hemiparesis. The neurotoxin selectively blocks presynaptic release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, producing temporary chemodenervation of overactive muscles and thereby reducing hypertonia and improving functional movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephronophthisis",
    "long_answer": "Nephronophthisis is an autosomal-recessive cystic kidney disease in which mutations of NPHP (cilia-related) genes cause progressive tubulointerstitial damage. Because the same genes are expressed in cerebellar and ocular motor pathways, some affected patients develop the cerebellar vermis hypoplasia/​Joubert spectrum, manifesting oculomotor apraxia along with ataxia and abnormal breathing. Thus, nephronophthisis is the genetic kidney disorder that can be accompanied by oculomotor apraxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Developmental and epileptic encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "In the 2022 International League Against Epilepsy (ILAE) syndrome classification, Early Myoclonic Encephalopathy (EME) is grouped among the neonatal-onset developmental and epileptic encephalopathies (DEEs). These syndromes are characterized by early-life onset of intractable seizures accompanied by severe impairment of cerebral development, reflecting both developmental and epileptic contributions to the encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Focal cortical dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Focal cortical dysplasia is a malformation of cortical development that arises from disordered neuronal proliferation and migration during fetal life. Because the dysplastic cortex generates frequent epileptogenic discharges that are poorly responsive to antiseizure medications, it is recognized as the single most common pathological substrate underlying medically intractable (drug-resistant) epilepsy in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAGE3",
    "long_answer": "Among the genes known to cause Galloway–Mowat syndrome (GAMOS), the only one located on the X-chromosome is LAGE3 (L antigen family member 3). Because LAGE3 mutations are inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern, heterozygous females are typically unaffected carriers who can transmit the pathogenic allele to their children—sons may be affected and daughters may become carriers. Thus, LAGE3 is the gene that can be passed on specifically by female carriers in GAMOS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunohistochemistry",
    "long_answer": "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma lacks obvious line-age-specific features on routine light microscopy. To determine whether the tumor cells express markers of any particular differentiation (e.g., cytokeratin, desmin, S-100, CD34, vimentin), pathologists apply immunohistochemistry—antibody-based staining of tissue sections—which is the standard technique for examining and classifying these undifferentiated tumor cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Infantile Refsum disease belongs to the Zellweger spectrum of peroxisomal biogenesis disorders caused by biallelic (two-allele) loss-of-function mutations in PEX genes. Because disease manifests only when both copies of the gene are defective and carriers are asymptomatic, the condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Danazol",
    "long_answer": "Danazol is an attenuated androgen given prophylactically in hereditary angioedema to raise C1-esterase inhibitor levels. Because it retains androgenic activity, patients—especially females—may experience masculinizing adverse effects such as hirsutism, acne, voice deepening, and menstrual disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Torsades de pointes",
    "long_answer": "In Long QT syndrome, delayed ventricular repolarization sets up early after-depolarizations that precipitate the polymorphic ventricular tachycardia known as torsades de pointes. On ECG this arrhythmia shows a characteristic ‘twisting’ sinusoidal wave of QRS complexes around the baseline, making torsades de pointes the wave pattern classically associated with Long QT–related arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TYR (tyrosinase)",
    "long_answer": "Albinism in kangaroos is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TYR gene, which encodes the enzyme tyrosinase. Tyrosinase catalyzes the first committed steps of melanin biosynthesis; when the TYR gene is defective, melanocytes cannot produce melanin, resulting in the characteristic white hair and red eyes seen in albino kangaroos."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP7B1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CYP7B1 gene cause hereditary spastic paraplegia type 5 (SPG5). CYP7B1 encodes oxysterol-7α-hydroxylase, a key enzyme of the alternative bile-acid pathway that converts oxysterols such as 25-hydroxycholesterol and 27-hydroxycholesterol into bile-acid intermediates. This enzymatic step is essential for maintaining cholesterol catabolism and systemic cholesterol homeostasis, linking CYP7B1 directly to both hereditary spastic paraplegia and cholesterol regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemizygous",
    "long_answer": "Lenz microphthalmia syndrome is inherited in an X-linked recessive manner. Males possess only one X chromosome, so when they carry a pathogenic variant in the responsible gene (e.g., BCOR), they have just a single copy of that allele and are therefore hemizygous for the mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipoid pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Inhalation or aspiration of oil-based substances (e.g., mineral oil, petroleum jelly, oil-based nasal drops) introduces lipids into the airways and alveoli. These exogenous fats are taken up by alveolar macrophages, triggering an inflammatory response that produces the characteristic lipid-laden macrophages and radiographic infiltrates of lipoid pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papulosquamous lesions",
    "long_answer": "Mucha–Habermann disease (pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta) presents with crops of small erythematous-to-violaceous papules that acquire overlying scale and may become necrotic, forming a papulosquamous eruption. This papulosquamous skin abnormality—papules with scale that heal with varioliform scarring—is the characteristic cutaneous feature of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stomach rumbling",
    "long_answer": "Borborygmus refers to the loud, gurgling or rumbling noises produced by movement of gas and fluid in the intestines. MNGIE (mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomyopathy) patients frequently report this symptom, which is clinically described as stomach or bowel rumbling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GATA3",
    "long_answer": "Barakat syndrome, also called HDR (hypoparathyroidism-deafness-renal dysplasia) syndrome, is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the GATA3 gene. GATA3 encodes a zinc-finger transcription factor essential for embryonic development of the parathyroids, inner ear, and kidneys; disruption of this gene explains the triad of hypoparathyroidism, sensorineural deafness, and renal anomalies characteristic of Barakat syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clomipramine",
    "long_answer": "Clomipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant with strong serotonin-reuptake-inhibiting and anti-obsessional properties, has been reported in case series as beneficial for patients with the somatic subtype of delusional disorder (e.g., monosymptomatic hypochondriacal psychosis, olfactory reference syndrome). These observations led to the recommendation of clomipramine as a pharmacologic option for this specific delusional presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Troponin C",
    "long_answer": "Digoxin inhibits the cardiac Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, secondarily raising intracellular Ca²⁺ levels. The extra Ca²⁺ binds more readily to the contractile regulatory protein troponin C in cardiac myocytes, enhancing actin–myosin interaction and thereby increasing myocardial contractility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skeletal survey X-ray",
    "long_answer": "A comprehensive skeletal survey using conventional X-ray imaging is the standard method for detecting the characteristic long-bone slenderness, tall vertebral bodies, and other skeletal dysplasia features seen in 3-M syndrome, allowing confirmation of the disorder’s skeletal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations and common variants in the IRF6 (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6) gene are a well-established cause of Van der Woude and related syndromes that feature cleft lip and/or cleft palate, and genome-wide association studies have repeatedly shown IRF6 variants to be the strongest genetic risk factor for isolated, nonsyndromic orofacial clefts. IRF6 encodes a transcription factor essential for epithelial differentiation during craniofacial development, so disruption of its function directly predisposes to the formation of cleft lip and cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipoid pneumonia",
    "long_answer": "Lipoid pneumonia is a distinct type of pneumonia in which lipids—often cholesterol or cholesterol-rich oils—accumulate within the alveoli. The presence of these fats provokes a foreign-body inflammatory reaction, leading to consolidation and respiratory symptoms. It can arise exogenously (inhalation or aspiration of cholesterol-containing oils) or endogenously (cholesterol released from tissue breakdown), making cholesterol a well-recognized cause of this specific pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blind loop syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Following intestinal resection for short-bowel syndrome, parts of the remaining jejunum or ileum can become bypassed or dilated, leading to stasis and heavy bacterial overgrowth. This postoperative bacterial overgrowth produces malabsorption, steatorrhoea and vitamin-B12 deficiency – the clinical picture known as blind loop (small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Impetigo is a superficial bacterial infection of the skin most often caused by Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes. Because it is a cutaneous disorder, the medical specialist who routinely diagnoses and manages skin infections and other dermatologic conditions is a dermatologist. Dermatologists are trained to recognize the characteristic honey-colored crusts of impetigo, order or interpret necessary cultures if indicated, and prescribe appropriate topical or systemic antibiotics, making them the specialists most likely to handle its diagnosis and treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteopetrosis",
    "long_answer": "Osteopetrosis (especially the infantile malignant form) is a congenital disorder in which defective osteoclast-mediated bone resorption produces markedly sclerotic, abnormally dense bones. The excessive bone fragility, marrow failure, and associated complications commonly lead to severe morbidity and early death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periventricular leukomalacia",
    "long_answer": "Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) is a form of neonatal brain injury characterized by ischemic-hypoxic damage and softening of the white matter adjacent to the lateral ventricles. It is most frequently seen in premature infants and is strongly linked to later neurodevelopmental disorders such as spastic cerebral palsy. The hallmark lesion is focal necrosis and gliosis in the periventricular white matter, making PVL the classic disorder associated with white-matter damage near the brain’s ventricles in infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytoreductive surgery",
    "long_answer": "The standard treatment for pseudomyxoma peritonei is extensive cytoreductive surgery, often including peritonectomy procedures, aimed at removing all visible mucinous tumor deposits. This surgery is typically followed intra-operatively by heated intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC) to eradicate microscopic disease, but the key surgical component is the cytoreductive resection itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth hormone deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Septo-optic dysplasia involves malformations of midline brain structures and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis. The pituitary impairment most often affects the somatotroph cells, so growth hormone production is reduced; growth hormone deficiency is therefore the most prevalent endocrine abnormality seen in patients with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baclofen",
    "long_answer": "Baclofen, a selective GABA-B receptor agonist, is the standard pharmacologic agent used to reduce spasticity that accompanies upper-motor-neuron lesions such as post-stroke hemiparesis. By enhancing inhibitory GABAergic transmission in the spinal cord, it decreases tonic stretch reflexes and skeletal-muscle tone, thereby alleviating spasticity in hemiparetic patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Punctate inner choroiditis is an inflammatory disorder of the choroid and retina. Evaluation and management—including fundus examination, optical coherence tomography, fluorescein angiography, and treatment with corticosteroids or immunomodulatory agents—are performed by ophthalmologists, typically retina subspecialists. Therefore, the medical specialty most involved is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Van der Woude syndrome is most commonly produced by heterozygous mutations in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6). Many IRF6 mutations create truncated or missense proteins that act in a dominant-negative fashion, disrupting normal IRF6 transcription-factor activity and thereby interfering with the function of the remaining wild-type allele in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pegvisomant",
    "long_answer": "Pegvisomant is a growth-hormone receptor antagonist that binds to hepatic GH receptors and blocks their activation, thereby suppressing hepatic production of insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I). Clinical reports have shown that pegvisomant can normalize elevated IGF-I concentrations in patients with GH excess, and it has therefore been identified as a potential medical therapy for childhood gigantism when surgery or somatostatin analogues are insufficient."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heterotopic ossification",
    "long_answer": "Heterotopic ossification—the pathological formation of mature bone within peri-articular soft tissues—is a well-recognized complication after total hip arthroplasty and can likewise develop following traumatic hip dislocation, because both procedures involve substantial local tissue trauma that triggers aberrant osteogenic signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sepsis",
    "long_answer": "Because their skin barrier is severely disrupted and covered with fragile blistering lesions, neonates with Epidermolytic ichthyosis are highly susceptible to invasion by bacteria, which can rapidly enter the bloodstream and lead to life-threatening systemic infection (neonatal sepsis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "Infants with Laron syndrome commonly develop recurrent hypoglycemic episodes because of their profound growth-hormone resistance and low IGF-1 levels. These hypoglycemic events frequently precipitate seizures, making seizures a well-documented clinical feature of Laron syndrome and a classic neurologic manifestation of hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXT1-CDG",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple exostoses (multiple osteochondromas) is classically produced by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in the exostosin-1 gene (EXT1). Deficiency of this Golgi membrane glycosyl-transferase impairs heparan-sulfate chain elongation and is classified among the congenital disorders of glycosylation as EXT1-CDG (formerly CDG type IIj). Hence, EXT1-CDG is the CDG subtype associated with hereditary multiple exostoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptozocin",
    "long_answer": "Streptozocin is an alkylating chemotherapy agent of the nitrosourea class that is FDA-approved specifically for metastatic pancreatic neuroendocrine (islet-cell) tumors. It is preferentially taken up by pancreatic endocrine cells and induces DNA alkylation, leading to tumor cell death, making it a standard cytotoxic option for pNET treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triiodothyronine (T3)",
    "long_answer": "Allan–Herndon–Dudley syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MCT8 (SLC16A2) transporter, which is responsible for shuttling the thyroid hormone triiodothyronine (T3) into neurons. Without efficient T3 uptake, developing nerve cells cannot receive the thyroid-hormone signal required for proper brain growth and myelination, leading to the characteristic severe neurodevelopmental deficits of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diverticulitis",
    "long_answer": "Diverticulitis is an inflammatory complication of diverticulosis. When a colonic diverticulum becomes obstructed and infected, inflammation compromises the local blood supply; if not treated, this can progress to ischemia, full-thickness bowel wall necrosis, and perforation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary (autosomal-dominant) form of triphalangeal thumb maps to chromosome 7q36. Duplications or point mutations in the ZPA regulatory sequence (ZRS) within the LMBR1 gene on 7q36 disrupt Sonic Hedgehog (SHH) signaling during limb development, producing a thumb with three phalanges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxic encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "Mefloquine, quinacrine, and chloroquine are antimalarial drugs that can cross the blood–brain barrier; when taken in excessive doses they injure neuronal and glial cells, producing a diffuse brain dysfunction classified as toxic encephalopathy. This drug-induced encephalopathy is commonly evaluated in the field of neuropsychiatry, the discipline that integrates neurology and psychiatry to assess how chemical insults to the brain generate both neurological and psychiatric symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Irritable bowel syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder characterized by recurrent crampy abdominal pain accompanied by bloating, often related to altered bowel habits (diarrhea, constipation, or both). Visceral hypersensitivity and disordered gut motility lead to gas retention and bowel spasms, producing the typical combination of cramping pain and abdominal distension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trophoblasts",
    "long_answer": "Gestational trophoblastic disease originates from the trophoblastic cells of the blastocyst (cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast). These cells normally form the placenta, but abnormal proliferation of trophoblasts produces the spectrum of tumors and neoplastic lesions classified as gestational trophoblastic disease, including hydatidiform mole and choriocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low protein content",
    "long_answer": "Breast milk contains relatively little total protein and therefore much lower amounts of the cationic amino acids lysine, arginine and ornithine whose intestinal and renal transport is defective in lysinuric protein intolerance. The small amino-acid load does not overwhelm the impaired y+LAT-1 transporter, so metabolic disturbances (hyperammonemia, failure to thrive, etc.) do not develop until the infant is weaned to higher-protein formulas or solid foods."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Midbrain-hindbrain region",
    "long_answer": "Oculocerebrocutaneous (Delleman) syndrome is classically linked to developmental malformations of the midbrain-hindbrain region, such as cerebellar vermis agenesis, brainstem dysplasia, and other posterior fossa anomalies. These midbrain-hindbrain defects constitute the characteristic central nervous system component of the syndrome alongside its ocular and cutaneous features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray",
    "long_answer": "A standard chest radiograph (chest X-ray) is routinely obtained in suspected acute respiratory distress syndrome and shows bilateral, diffuse alveolar infiltrates that signify fluid accumulation (pulmonary edema) in the lungs, helping confirm the diagnosis of ARDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xanthine oxidase",
    "long_answer": "Xanthinuria results from a deficiency of xanthine oxidase (also known as xanthine dehydrogenase/oxidoreductase), the enzyme that normally oxidizes hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid in purine catabolism. When this enzyme is absent or inactive, xanthine cannot be further converted to uric acid, leading to its accumulation and increased excretion in the urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pinnal-pedal reflex",
    "long_answer": "In dogs with sarcoptic mange, gentle scratching of the ear margin (pinna) typically elicits an involuntary rhythmic hind-leg scratching motion (pedal response). This is called the pinnal-pedal reflex; a positive response is highly suggestive of mange and is commonly used by veterinarians as a quick diagnostic aid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochlear nerve",
    "long_answer": "The cochlear (auditory) nerve is the branch of cranial nerve VIII that carries the electrical impulses generated by cochlear hair cells to the auditory centers of the brain. In presbycusis—age-related sensorineural hearing loss—degeneration and loss of these cochlear nerve fibers contribute to the progressive decline in hearing, underscoring its central role in both signal transmission and the pathology of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachydactyly type A3",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly type A3, also termed brachymesophalangy V, is defined by shortening and wedge-shaping of the middle phalanx of the little finger. This structural change causes the distal phalanx of the fifth digit to curve toward the radial and volar (palmar) sides, producing the characteristic radial-volar clinodactyly described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Achondroplasia",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia is the most prevalent skeletal dysplasia, responsible for roughly 70 % of all dwarfism cases. Caused by gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR3 gene, it leads to disproportionate short stature characterized by shortened limbs with relative preservation of trunk length. Medical and advocacy groups—such as Little People of America and other dwarfism organizations—identify achondroplasia as the prototypical and most common form of dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-Amyloid accumulation",
    "long_answer": "The widely accepted amyloid-cascade model holds that Alzheimer’s disease is initiated by abnormal proteolytic processing of amyloid precursor protein, leading to extracellular buildup of insoluble β-amyloid (Aβ) peptides that aggregate into plaques. This Aβ accumulation triggers tau hyper-phosphorylation, synaptic loss, neuroinflammation, and progressive neuronal degeneration, making β-amyloid deposition the primary pathogenic driver identified for Alzheimer’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiac arrhythmias",
    "long_answer": "If phenytoin is infused too rapidly (exceeding about 50 mg/min in adults), the propylene-glycol–containing formulation can cause marked hypotension and severe disturbances of cardiac conduction, leading to life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias and possible cardiac arrest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homocystinuria",
    "long_answer": "Homocystinuria, an autosomal-recessive cystathionine-β-synthase deficiency, produces a marfanoid habitus with long, thin fingers (arachnodactyly). The disorder leads to accumulation of homocystine/homocysteine that is excreted in large amounts in urine; a simple urine cyanide-nitroprusside screening test detects the excess homocystine, allowing laboratory confirmation of the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Contact dermatitis is an inflammatory skin disorder caused by irritants or allergens; its diagnosis (often via patch testing) and management of severe cases fall under the expertise of dermatologists, the physicians specialized in diseases of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aberrant right subclavian artery",
    "long_answer": "An aberrant right subclavian artery (arteria lusoria) is a rare congenital anomaly of the aortic arch in which the right subclavian artery originates distally and courses posterior to the oesophagus and trachea. Its abnormal retro-oesophageal path can exert extrinsic compression on these mediastinal structures, producing symptoms such as dysphagia and atypical chest pain. Thus, chest pain arising from compressive effects of this anomalous vessel is a recognized, though uncommon, clinical presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dentistry",
    "long_answer": "Amelogenesis imperfecta is an inherited disorder that affects the formation and quality of tooth enamel. Management consists of dental interventions such as protective restorations, crowns, veneers, and, in children, preventive and restorative care—all procedures carried out by dental specialists (e.g., pediatric dentists or prosthodontists). Consequently, patients with amelogenesis imperfecta are most commonly treated within the field of dentistry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical excision",
    "long_answer": "For most solid malignant neoplasms (cancers), complete surgical excision of the tumor with adequate margins is the cornerstone and first-line ('primary') treatment, as it offers the best chance for cure or local control before adjuvant modalities such as chemotherapy or radiotherapy are considered."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idoxuridine",
    "long_answer": "Idoxuridine is a halogenated thymidine analogue first synthesized and developed for antiviral use by William H. Prusoff. Applied topically to the eye, it inhibits herpes simplex virus DNA replication and became the first approved antiviral drug, specifically indicated for treating herpes simplex keratitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Veralipride",
    "long_answer": "Veralipride is a dopamine-antagonist benzamide that was marketed in several countries to relieve vasomotor menopausal symptoms (e.g., hot flashes). By chronically blocking dopamine D2 receptors it markedly raises prolactin levels; the resulting hyperprolactinaemia suppresses gonadotropin secretion and oestrogen production, producing amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea and clinical hypogonadism in women. Because of these endocrine effects and additional neurological adverse events, veralipride was withdrawn from the market."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lung transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary capillary hemangiomatosis causes diffuse, irreversible proliferation of capillaries within the lung parenchyma, leading to severe pulmonary hypertension and respiratory failure. Pharmacologic vasodilators or anticoagulation provide little benefit and can precipitate fatal pulmonary edema. Because the pathologic process is confined to the lungs, replacing the diseased lungs is the only curative option; thus, bilateral lung transplantation is regarded as the sole definitive treatment for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain radiography",
    "long_answer": "Sprengel's deformity, a congenital elevation of the scapula, is clinically suspected from the high-riding, rotated scapula. Standard plain X-ray films of the shoulder girdle/cervical spine demonstrate the elevated, hypoplastic scapula and any associated omovertebral bone, thereby objectively confirming the clinical diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal ganglia",
    "long_answer": "Brain imaging in Cockayne syndrome characteristically shows intracranial calcifications concentrated in the basal ganglia (especially the putamen and caudate nuclei). These deposits are a well-documented radiologic hallmark of the disorder, reflecting neurodegenerative changes linked to the underlying DNA-repair defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR gene",
    "long_answer": "A mutation in the LDLR (low-density lipoprotein receptor) gene on chromosome 19q13 disrupts LDL receptor function, reducing hepatic clearance of LDL particles from the bloodstream. The resulting accumulation of LDL cholesterol produces familial hypercholesterolemia, a classic form of hyperlipidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The classic and most prevalent form of hereditary elliptocytosis is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner: a single pathogenic allele in genes encoding red-cell membrane proteins (most often α- or β-spectrin) is sufficient for the phenotype, and affected individuals typically have an affected parent and a 50% transmission risk to offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scoliosis",
    "long_answer": "In VACTERL association, congenital vertebral segmentation anomalies such as hemivertebrae or fused vertebrae are common. As the spine grows, these asymmetric defects can lead to progressive lateral curvature, so affected children are at increased risk of developing scoliosis during childhood or adolescence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Morningness–Eveningness Questionnaire",
    "long_answer": "The Horne-Östberg Morningness–Eveningness Questionnaire is widely used in sleep-medicine research and clinical practice to characterize an individual’s circadian phase preference. By scoring whether a person is a ‘morning type’ or ‘evening type’, it helps identify the delayed circadian phase typical of Delayed Sleep Phase Disorder, making it an appropriate tool for assessing the circadian rhythm in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schilder’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Schilder’s disease, also termed diffuse myelinoclastic sclerosis, is an acquired inflammatory demyelinating disorder considered a variant of multiple sclerosis. Its hallmark is large, bilateral cerebral white-matter plaques that resemble the symmetric involvement seen in hereditary leukodystrophies; hence neuropathology classifications list it as a “leukodystrophy-like” demyelinating disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Juvenile glaucoma",
    "long_answer": "Ackerman syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by gingival fibromatosis, facial and dental anomalies, and ocular involvement. One well-documented ocular manifestation is early-onset (juvenile) glaucoma, in which elevated intra-ocular pressure leads to buphthalmos and optic-nerve damage. Thus, juvenile glaucoma is a recognized eye condition that can accompany Ackerman syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Axial twist theory",
    "long_answer": "The axial twist theory proposes that, during early vertebrate development, the anterior head region undergoes a left-right rotational (twisting) process relative to the trunk, establishing the normal contralateral and asymmetric organization of the cerebral hemispheres. Failure or aberration of this twist disrupts midline formation and hemispheric separation; the theory therefore interprets holoprosencephaly—where the forebrain fails to cleave—as the most extreme outcome of disturbed brain asymmetry generated by the axial twist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Speech-language pathologist",
    "long_answer": "Nemaline myopathy can weaken the bulbar and oropharyngeal muscles, leading to dysarthria and dysphagia. A speech-language pathologist (also called a speech and language therapist) is the specialist trained to evaluate and treat speech and swallowing problems, providing exercises, compensatory strategies, and feeding recommendations to maintain safe swallowing and effective communication in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B12 injections",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) therapy for pernicious or severe vitamin B12–deficiency anemia is routinely administered by intramuscular injection, allowing direct replenishment of body stores when intestinal absorption is impaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Esophageal manometry",
    "long_answer": "Esophageal manometry (standard or high-resolution) directly measures lower esophageal sphincter pressure and its relaxation during swallowing, allowing precise assessment of motor function in children with achalasia-microcephaly syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein-losing enteropathy",
    "long_answer": "In aplasia cutis congenita-intestinal lymphangiectasia syndrome the congenital intestinal lymphangiectasia causes massive loss of serum proteins into the gut (protein-losing enteropathy). The ensuing profound hypoalbuminemia, edema, malnutrition, and immune compromise lead to life-threatening complications, and affected infants typically die from this protein-losing enteropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Real-time RT-PCR",
    "long_answer": "Kyasanur Forest disease virus is most sensitively diagnosed by a real-time reverse-transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) assay that amplifies a conserved region of the viral NS-5 gene, allowing rapid and specific detection of the infection in clinical specimens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Dermatology is the medical specialty devoted to the study, diagnosis, and treatment of disorders of the skin, hair, and nails, including conditions such as eczema, psoriasis, acne, and skin cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tofacitinib",
    "long_answer": "Tofacitinib, an oral Janus-kinase (JAK-1/3) inhibitor, has produced complete or near-complete hair regrowth in many patients with alopecia universalis by suppressing the γ-chain cytokine signals that drive the autoimmune attack on hair follicles. The same JAK-dependent cytokines (IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22, IFN-γ) are central to the type-2/Th1 inflammation of atopic dermatitis, and both systemic and topical tofacitinib have demonstrated significant reductions in eczema severity in clinical studies. Therefore, tofacitinib is a treatment effective in both alopecia universalis and atopic dermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory muscle weakness",
    "long_answer": "Freeman–Sheldon syndrome is a congenital myopathy; the same MYH3-related muscle weakness that produces facial and limb contractures also affects the intercostal and accessory respiratory muscles.  Because their cough is weak and chest expansion is restricted, patients clear airway secretions poorly, so viral or bacterial upper-respiratory infections linger and take longer to resolve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Listeria infection",
    "long_answer": "Listeria monocytogenes infection (listeriosis) often manifests in pregnant women as an influenza-like illness with fever, myalgia, and malaise. The bacterium can cross the placenta or contaminate the birth canal, allowing transmission to the neonate during delivery, where it may cause severe neonatal sepsis or meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Borderline personality disorder",
    "long_answer": "Borderline personality disorder (BPD) shows the highest and most consistent comorbidity with major depressive disorder; epidemiologic and clinical studies report lifetime co-occurrence rates exceeding 50–80 %. Shared affective dysregulation, impulsivity, and serotonergic deficits help explain why depressive episodes frequently arise in individuals with BPD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Fucosidosis results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the FUCA1 gene encoding α-L-fucosidase. Because disease manifests only when both copies of the gene are defective, it follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern: affected individuals inherit one mutated allele from each carrier parent, males and females are equally affected, and the risk of recurrence in siblings is 25%."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Across Europe and Asia, the single most common form of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy is LGMD2A/LGMDR1, caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the CAPN3 (calpain-3) gene. This disorder is transmitted only when an affected individual inherits one mutated CAPN3 allele from each heterozygous carrier parent, defining an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vasopressors",
    "long_answer": "After an acute spinal cord injury, loss of sympathetic tone can produce neurogenic shock with profound hypotension. Intravenous vasopressor drugs (e.g., norepinephrine, phenylephrine, dopamine) are administered to cause peripheral vasoconstriction, raise systemic vascular resistance, and keep the mean arterial pressure high enough to perfuse the spinal cord and other organs. Therefore, vasopressors are the medication class used to maintain blood pressure in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CFTR gene",
    "long_answer": "Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD) is regarded as a genital variant of cystic fibrosis. Over 75 % of men with this condition carry pathogenic mutations in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, which encodes an epithelial chloride channel. The National Institutes of Health (NIH) has extensively investigated CFTR mutations because they underlie both classic cystic fibrosis and isolated CBAVD, confirming CFTR mutation as the principal genetic cause of congenital absence of the vas deferens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondrial DNA mutations",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic alterations in the mitochondrial genome—such as point mutations or deletions in mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA)—impair genes that encode components of oxidative-phosphorylation complexes. These mtDNA mutations disrupt ATP production and are responsible for classic mitochondrial disorders (e.g., MELAS, LHON), which show maternal inheritance and tissue energy failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nevus simplex",
    "long_answer": "The vascular birthmark colloquially called a “stork bite” is medically classified as a nevus simplex, a benign capillary malformation that commonly appears on the nape of the neck, eyelids, or glabella in newborns and typically fades with time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "α-Sarcoglycanopathy",
    "long_answer": "The R77C (c.229C>T) missense mutation occurs in the SGCA gene encoding α-sarcoglycan. This variant disrupts α-sarcoglycan folding and integration into the sarcoglycan complex, leading to limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2D—the disease classically termed α-sarcoglycanopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low-dose aspirin",
    "long_answer": "In low-risk essential thrombocythemia, daily low-dose aspirin is recommended because it irreversibly inhibits platelet cyclo-oxygenase-1, suppressing thromboxane A₂ production and thereby decreasing platelet aggregation and the likelihood of arterial or venous thrombotic events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpesviridae",
    "long_answer": "Members of the Herpesviridae family—especially cytomegalovirus (CMV) and herpes simplex virus (HSV)—are well-documented causes of neonatal (congenital or perinatal) hepatitis. These enveloped DNA viruses can cross the placenta or be transmitted during delivery, infect the neonatal liver, and produce jaundice, hepatomegaly, and elevated transaminases characteristic of neonatal hepatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced sperm motility",
    "long_answer": "Protein–energy malnutrition lowers available amino acids and energy substrates, disrupts testosterone and gonadotropin production, and increases oxidative stress in the testes. These changes impair flagellar structure and mitochondrial ATP generation in spermatozoa, leading to decreased progressive movement—i.e., reduced sperm motility (asthenozoospermia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metabolic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "In 3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase deficiency, accumulated organic acids from impaired ketogenesis and leucine catabolism enter the blood, titrating bicarbonate and widening the anion gap. A widened (high) anion gap therefore signals metabolic acidosis due to this organic acidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent respiratory infections",
    "long_answer": "In primary ciliary dyskinesia the motile cilia of the airway epithelium beat ineffectively, so mucus, bacteria, and particulates are not propelled out of the respiratory tract. The resulting loss of mucociliary clearance leads to persistent colonisation of the airways and, therefore, to chronic and recurrent infections of the nose, sinuses, bronchi, and lungs (e.g., sinusitis, otitis media, bronchiectasis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Indonesia and Timor-Leste",
    "long_answer": "Under WHO’s current yaws (Treponema pallidum pertenue) eradication strategy, the only countries in the WHO South-East Asia Region that still report endemic transmission are Indonesia and Timor-Leste; consequently, these two nations are the specific Southeast Asian targets for yaws eradication campaigns such as the 2012 ‘Morges Strategy’ that uses mass azithromycin treatment and surveillance to interrupt transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leg pain",
    "long_answer": "Deep vein thrombosis often produces aching or cramping pain in the affected limb because the clot obstructs venous blood flow and raises venous pressure. Similar leg pain can also arise from poor circulation (e.g., peripheral arterial disease) or from nerve damage (neuropathy), making it a symptom common to all three conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FRMD7",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FRMD7 gene, located on Xq26 of the X chromosome, are a well-established cause of X-linked infantile (congenital) nystagmus. FRMD7 encodes a FERM-domain–containing protein important for neuronal development of ocular motor control, and its disruption leads specifically to the nystagmus phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beriberi",
    "long_answer": "Christiaan Eijkman showed that the peripheral neuropathy known as beriberi results from a dietary deficiency—specifically the absence of the anti-beriberi factor later identified as vitamin B₁ (thiamine) found in rice husks. This demonstration that a nutrient lack, rather than infection, caused the disease earned him the 1929 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "To enlarge the atrial septal opening",
    "long_answer": "Balloon (atrial) septostomy is performed to rip open or widen the foramen ovale/atrial septal defect so that oxygen-rich pulmonary venous blood can mix with systemic venous blood. This temporary increase in inter-atrial mixing raises systemic oxygen saturation and stabilizes cyanotic infants with defects such as transposition of the great arteries until definitive surgical repair can be done."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Malouf syndrome is a rare genetic condition originally described by Malouf et al. in 1979. The cardinal features include dilated cardiomyopathy together with hyper-gonadotropic hypogonadism caused by primary gonadal failure. Affected females typically have streak ovaries/ovarian dysgenesis, and affected males may have testicular atrophy—both forms representing gonadal dysgenesis. Therefore, gonadal dysgenesis is a defining component of Malouf syndrome, and the two conditions are indeed linked."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by deficiency of 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase, the final enzyme in the cholesterol biosynthetic pathway. The block in cholesterol synthesis leads to accumulation of 7-dehydrocholesterol and reduced cholesterol levels. Clinically, patients can show multiple congenital anomalies, developmental delay, and, owing to lipid accumulation and impaired hepatic function, may develop hepatomegaly or hepatosplenomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (classically 45,X monosomy) is characterized by streak or absent ovaries—i.e., gonadal agenesis—which leads to primary ovarian failure and estrogen deficiency. The absent/under-developed gonads result in hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, making Turner syndrome a well-recognized condition featuring both gonadal agenesis and hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ITGA8",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ITGA8 gene, which encodes the integrin-α8 subunit, have been shown to cause bilateral renal agenesis. The absence of both kidneys leads to severe oligohydramnios, producing the characteristic features of Potter sequence (pulmonary hypoplasia, limb and facial anomalies). Thus, ITGA8 mutations are a documented genetic cause of Potter sequence secondary to bilateral renal agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amblyopia",
    "long_answer": "Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon (a form of congenital ptosis) is frequently accompanied by unilateral amblyopia. The amblyopia often arises when the affected eye has significant anisometropia—unequal refractive error between the two eyes—which causes chronic image blur and cortical suppression of that eye, leading to reduced visual acuity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strict tree-nut avoidance",
    "long_answer": "Because IgE-mediated tree-nut allergy can provoke severe reactions and no curative therapy exists, standard clinical guidance is lifelong, strict avoidance of all tree nuts and products that may contain them, with vigilant label reading to prevent accidental exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scurvy (Barlow disease)",
    "long_answer": "Scurvy, also termed Barlow disease in infants, results from vitamin C deficiency. The consequent defective collagen synthesis leads to periosteal reactions, subperiosteal hemorrhage, and bone swelling that can closely resemble the radiographic and clinical findings of infantile cortical hyperostosis (Caffey disease), making scurvy a key diagnostic mimic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontonasal dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Frontonasal dysplasia (median cleft face syndrome) is a congenital malformation complex characterized by craniofacial anomalies such as severe ocular hypertelorism, broad nasal root, and midline facial clefting. Because the midline defect often extends through the anterior skull base, affected infants can present with an associated fronto-ethmoidal meningo-encephalocele. This well-established association makes frontonasal dysplasia the classic syndrome linking craniofacial anomalies with encephaloceles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benzathine penicillin G",
    "long_answer": "Treponema pallidum, the causative agent of syphilis, remains exquisitely sensitive to penicillin. Current CDC and WHO guidelines designate a single intramuscular dose of long-acting benzathine penicillin G as the first-line treatment for early syphilis (and appropriately dosed courses for later stages), because its sustained serum levels effectively eradicate the spirochete."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pectus carinatum",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Fitzsimmons–Guilbert syndrome characteristically present with a prominent chest wall deformity described as pectus carinatum (‘pigeon chest’). This protrusion of the sternum is one of the diagnostic skeletal features reported alongside short stature, microcephaly, brachydactyly and intellectual disability in the original clinical descriptions that define the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1",
    "long_answer": "The cause of Hallermann–Streiff syndrome remains unknown, but isolated case reports have identified de-novo heterozygous variants in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-43, in individuals diagnosed with this syndrome. Although these findings suggest a possible link, no clinical genetic test is yet available and the association has not been definitively established."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sleep-related eating disorder",
    "long_answer": "Sleep-related eating disorder (SRED) is a parasomnia in which individuals partially arouse from sleep and consume food or non-nutritive, even toxic, substances without full awareness. Episodes typically occur in the first half of the night, and sufferers often have no memory of the event on awakening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asn666Lys (N666K)",
    "long_answer": "In infantile myofibromatosis, the activating PDGFRB mutation Asn666Lys (N666K) lies in the kinase domain’s activation loop. This substitution locks PDGFR-β in an active conformation that imatinib, which binds only to the inactive (DFG-out) state, cannot effectively inhibit, rendering the tumor cells resistant to the drug."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Acute kidney injury resulting from obstruction of urine outflow is a post-renal problem that requires relief of the blockage (e.g., catheterization, stent placement, stone or prostate surgery). These diagnostic and interventional procedures fall under the scope of the urology specialty, which manages structural disorders of the urinary tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Familial renal amyloidosis belongs to the group of hereditary systemic amyloidoses caused by mutations in circulating plasma proteins (e.g., fibrinogen Aα-chain, apolipoprotein A-I). These disorders are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, meaning a single mutant allele inherited from one affected parent is sufficient to cause disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis syndrome (blepharophimosis–ptosis–epicanthus inversus syndrome, BPES) is usually caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the FOXL2 gene. These mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, showing high penetrance but variable expressivity in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylmalonic acid",
    "long_answer": "In vitamin B₁₂–dependent metabolism, methylmalonyl-CoA is converted to succinyl-CoA. When B₁₂ is lacking, this reaction slows and methylmalonic acid accumulates in blood and urine. Therefore, elevated methylmalonic acid is a sensitive and fairly specific biochemical marker of vitamin B₁₂ deficiency, often rising before overt hematologic or neurologic signs appear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spleen",
    "long_answer": "After a myocardial infarction, the body rapidly mobilises a large reservoir of monocytes stored in the spleen. These splenic monocytes are released into the bloodstream, migrate to the injured myocardium, and differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells that clear necrotic debris, modulate inflammation, and facilitate tissue repair. This spleen-derived monocyte contribution is a well-established mechanism in post-infarct cardiac healing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia",
    "long_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia is a malformation in which islands or nodules of cortical gray matter are ectopically located within the cerebral white matter. It is frequently reported on MRI in patients who have optic nerve hypoplasia and other midline brain developmental anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACE inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors block the formation of angiotensin II, the principal stimulant of aldosterone secretion by the adrenal zona glomerulosa. Reduced angiotensin II levels lead to markedly decreased aldosterone synthesis, so chronic ACE-inhibitor therapy can produce drug-induced hypoaldosteronism, often evidenced by hyperkalaemia and a mild metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase",
    "long_answer": "Both aminolevulinic acid dehydratase deficiency porphyria and tyrosinemia type I involve functional inhibition of the enzyme aminolevulinic acid dehydratase (ALAD, also called porphobilinogen synthase). In ALAD-deficiency porphyria the enzyme is genetically deficient, while in tyrosinemia type I the metabolite succinylacetone—accumulated because of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase deficiency—directly inhibits ALAD. Thus ALAD inhibition is the biochemical feature common to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Current evidence indicates that in-utero (vertical) transmission of SARS-CoV-2 can occur, although it appears to be uncommon. Several well-documented case reports and cohort studies have shown: (1) detection of SARS-CoV-2 RNA or infectious virus in placental tissue, amniotic fluid, umbilical-cord blood, and neonatal nasopharyngeal swabs obtained immediately after birth; (2) histopathologic placental findings consistent with viral infection; and (3) neonates with SARS-CoV-2-specific IgM antibodies, which cannot cross the placenta and therefore imply fetal synthesis after intra-uterine exposure. Large systematic reviews from the World Health Organization, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, and peer-reviewed meta-analyses conclude that while the absolute risk is low (generally <5% of pregnancies in infected mothers), true intra-uterine transmission is biologically plausible and has been confirmed. Thus, the established consensus is that vertical transmission is possible but rare."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dexamethasone",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune-mediated congenital heart block arises when maternal anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB antibodies injure the fetal atrioventricular node. The only drug shown to cross the placenta in therapeutic concentrations and used in attempts to halt or reverse early (first- or second-degree) block is the fluorinated glucocorticoid dexamethasone (typically 4 mg orally per day to the mother). Because dexamethasone is not extensively metabolized by placental 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, it reaches the fetus, dampening the antibody-induced inflammatory response and fibrosis. Hence, dexamethasone is the steroid drug employed in management of autoimmune congenital heart block."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate",
    "long_answer": "The Pediatric Crohn’s Disease Activity Index (PCDAI) includes the erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) as a laboratory parameter. ESR is a widely used blood test that rises with systemic inflammation, and its value is incorporated into the PCDAI scoring system to quantify inflammatory activity in children with Crohn’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Preserved (normal) ejection fraction",
    "long_answer": "In mitral valve prolapse, global left-ventricular systolic function is typically maintained; therefore the ejection fraction is usually normal (or even slightly elevated) despite the valvular abnormality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "Folliculitis decalvans is classified as a neutrophilic scarring alopecia; biopsy typically shows a dense perifollicular infiltrate of polymorphonuclear leukocytes. This neutrophil-dominated innate immune response drives the pustular inflammation and follicular destruction characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in COL4A1, the gene encoding the α1-chain of type IV collagen, weaken vascular basement membranes. Prenatal or perinatal cerebral hemorrhages caused by this defect often heal as cystic cavities, producing congenital or familial porencephaly. Affected newborns classically present with neurological deficits that include generalized hypotonia, linking a COL4A1 mutation to the combined findings of porencephaly and hypotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging",
    "long_answer": "Pontocerebellar hypoplasia involves structural under-development of the cerebellum and brainstem; high-resolution brain Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is the preferred modality because it provides superior soft-tissue contrast and detailed visualization of posterior fossa structures compared with CT or ultrasound, enabling accurate assessment of cerebellar and pontine hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autonomic nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Ogilvie syndrome (acute colonic pseudo-obstruction) is thought to arise from an imbalance in autonomic control of the colon. Loss or inhibition of sacral parasympathetic outflow—or relative sympathetic over-activity—diminishes colonic peristalsis, leading to massive dilatation in the absence of a mechanical obstruction. Thus, dysfunction of the autonomic nervous system is central to the regulation of colonic motor activity in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Donnai–Barrow syndrome (also called facio-oculo-acoustico-renal syndrome) is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by diaphragmatic hernia, ocular anomalies, sensorineural hearing loss, developmental delay, and low-molecular-weight proteinuria. Multiple genetic studies, including the original linkage and positional-cloning work in 2007, have shown that biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the LRP2 gene, which encodes the multi-ligand endocytic receptor megalin, are the causal defect. Subsequent case reports and functional analyses have confirmed that pathogenic variants in LRP2 disrupt megalin’s role in renal and developmental pathways, producing the Donnai-Barrow phenotype. Therefore, LRP2 is directly and conclusively associated with Donnai–Barrow syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration is an autoimmune paraneoplastic neurological syndrome in which antibodies generated against a systemic tumor (e.g., ovarian, breast, lung cancers) cross-react with neuronal antigens, leading to selective loss of Purkinje cells and other neurons within the cerebellum. The resulting cerebellar inflammation and neuronal death cause the hallmark subacute cerebellar ataxia, confirming that the cerebellum is the principal region of the nervous system affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malaria",
    "long_answer": "Global Burden of Disease studies consistently show that malaria accounts for by far the greatest number of disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) lost among all parasitic diseases—tens of millions annually—owing to its high incidence, severe morbidity and substantial mortality, particularly in young children in sub-Saharan Africa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hunter-Thompson type",
    "long_answer": "In acromesomelic dysplasia of the Hunter-Thompson type, radiographs characteristically show marked brachydactyly with absent or severely hypoplastic middle (second) phalanges of the fingers and toes. This hypoplasia of the second phalanges distinguishes the Hunter-Thompson variety from other acromesomelic dysplasia subtypes such as the Maroteaux or Grebe types."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OXCT1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OXCT1 gene, which encodes mitochondrial succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT), block the first step of ketone-body degradation. When SCOT activity is absent or markedly reduced, tissues cannot convert acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA, so acetoacetate accumulates in the bloodstream and is excreted in the urine, producing persistent ketonemia and ketonuria with markedly elevated acetoacetate levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1–4 %",
    "long_answer": "Adenosquamous carcinoma is an uncommon variant of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. Large clinicopathologic series and the WHO tumor classification agree that it represents only about 1 – 4 % of all exocrine pancreatic cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric orthopedics",
    "long_answer": "Pigeon toe (in-toeing) in children is an orthopedic alignment issue of the lower limbs or feet. Evaluation and management—ranging from physical examination and gait analysis to corrective casting, bracing, or surgery when necessary—are performed by pediatric orthopedic specialists, the branch of orthopedics focused on musculoskeletal disorders in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Synechia refers to adhesions within the eye—most commonly between the iris and either the lens (posterior synechia) or the cornea (anterior synechia). Evaluation, medical management (e.g., mydriatics, anti-inflammatory therapy), and surgical correction when necessary all fall under the expertise of an ophthalmologist. Therefore, the medical specialty that treats ocular synechiae is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "In the diagnostic work-up of a patient who presents with psychotic symptoms, clinicians routinely perform a panel of blood tests precisely to rule out or confirm medical conditions that can mimic or trigger psychosis. Standard investigations include complete blood count, electrolytes, renal and liver function tests, thyroid function, vitamin B12 and folate levels, inflammatory markers, serology for infections such as HIV or syphilis, and sometimes autoimmune or paraneoplastic antibody panels. Abnormalities in any of these tests can reveal metabolic disturbances, endocrine disorders, nutritional deficiencies, infections, or systemic autoimmune processes that may be responsible for the psychotic presentation. When results are normal, many of these potential organic causes are effectively excluded, guiding the clinician toward a primary psychiatric disorder. Therefore, blood investigations are an established and evidence-based means of excluding numerous potential medical causes of psychosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "A birth defect",
    "long_answer": "Constriction ring syndrome—also called amniotic band syndrome—occurs in utero when fibrous amniotic bands encircle parts of the fetus, producing constriction rings, limb deformities, or amputations present at birth. Because these anomalies originate during fetal development, constriction ring syndrome is classified as a congenital malformation, i.e., a birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniotic band syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In amniotic band syndrome, rupture of the amnion releases fibrous strands that wrap around developing fetal limbs or digits. These constricting bands impede blood flow and growth, leading to deep constriction rings, distal swelling, and, in severe cases, intrauterine (congenital) amputations of the affected extremities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The MYO7A gene, whose mutations cause Usher syndrome type 1B, is mapped to the long arm (q) of chromosome 11 at band 11q13.5. This chromosomal localization is well established through linkage and sequencing studies of affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autism spectrum disorder",
    "long_answer": "Jacobsen syndrome (11q deletion disorder) frequently involves neurodevelopmental impairments; studies show that roughly half of affected individuals meet diagnostic criteria for autism spectrum disorder (ASD), making ASD one of the most common neurological/behavioral conditions associated with this chromosomal deletion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Food and Agriculture Organization",
    "long_answer": "The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO), established in 1945, works jointly with the World Health Organization (WHO) through the FAO/WHO Codex Alimentarius Commission to develop internationally harmonized food standards, guidelines, and codes of practice that protect consumer health and facilitate fair trade."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pure red cell aplasia",
    "long_answer": "Erythroblastopenia is the hallmark of pure red cell aplasia (PRCA), a hematologic disorder in which erythroblasts are selectively absent or severely reduced in the bone marrow, leading to a marked decrease in circulating red blood cells while white blood cells and platelets remain normal. PRCA therefore presents clinically as a severe normocytic-normochromic anemia caused by failure of red cell production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maxillary sinusitis",
    "long_answer": "Infection of an upper-jaw tooth can spread to, and obstruct, the contiguous maxillary sinus. Obstruction prevents normal drainage and aeration, leading to inflammation of the sinus lining—clinically termed maxillary sinusitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase), as seen in McArdle disease, blocks the release of glucose-1-phosphate from muscle glycogen. Without this substrate, the glycolytic pathway cannot generate ATP during strenuous exercise. The resulting energy failure in skeletal muscle produces painful cramps, fatigue and muscle pain during heavy exertion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGF3",
    "long_answer": "Otodental syndrome (globodontia with ocular and auditory anomalies) has been shown to result from heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions involving the fibroblast growth factor 3 (FGF3) gene on chromosome 11q13. FGF3 is essential for proper development of both the inner ear and the dentition; its disruption therefore produces the characteristic combination of globodontia and congenital sensorineural hearing loss observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oxygen transport",
    "long_answer": "Hemoglobin’s primary role is to bind oxygen molecules in the lungs, carry them through the bloodstream, and release the oxygen to body tissues where it is needed for cellular respiration. Its iron-containing heme groups reversibly bind O₂, enabling efficient pickup in high-oxygen environments (lungs) and release in low-oxygen environments (peripheral tissues)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "The radius bones",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome (Nager acrofacial dysostosis) features pre-axial limb defects, most characteristically hypoplasia or complete absence of the radial bones of the forearms. This radial-ray deficiency is a core diagnostic finding and often accompanies thumb aplasia or hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngologist (ENT)",
    "long_answer": "Hearing loss related to Paget’s disease of bone results from bony overgrowth and deformity of the skull that impairs the structures of the middle and inner ear. Disorders of the ear and auditory system are managed by an otolaryngologist—also known as an ear, nose, and throat (ENT) specialist—who diagnoses and treats conductive or sensorineural hearing loss, including that secondary to skeletal diseases like Paget’s."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nitric oxide pathway",
    "long_answer": "Endothelial dysfunction in pulmonary arterial hypertension reduces production of nitric oxide from L-arginine, blunting the NO–soluble guanylate cyclase–cGMP pathway. Loss of this vasodilatory, anti-proliferative signalling promotes pulmonary vasoconstriction and vascular-smooth-muscle hyperplasia, driving the development of pulmonary arterial hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal smoking",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies have shown that mothers who smoke cigarettes during early pregnancy have a higher likelihood of giving birth to infants with limb-bud anomalies, including both pre-axial and post-axial polydactyly. Toxins in tobacco smoke can disturb key morphogen pathways (e.g., Sonic Hedgehog signaling) during limb development, and meta-analyses report a statistically significant increase (odds ratio ≈1.3–1.5) in polydactyly among newborns of smoking mothers compared with nonsmokers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coenzyme Q10",
    "long_answer": "Coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone) is frequently recommended as a dietary supplement for patients with mitochondrial diseases because it is an essential electron carrier in the mitochondrial respiratory chain. Supplementation can help bypass or lessen deficiencies in endogenous CoQ10 biosynthesis, improve electron transport efficiency, and enhance ATP production, which together may alleviate energy‐deficit symptoms characteristic of many mitochondrial disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasmodium falciparum",
    "long_answer": "Plasmodium falciparum is a mosquito-borne protozoan parasite that can cross the placenta or infect the mother peripartum, leading to congenital or neonatal malaria. It is transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes and is well documented as a cause of malaria in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical resection",
    "long_answer": "For patients whose non-small-cell lung cancer is confined and operable, the standard primary treatment is surgical resection (typically lobectomy or pneumonectomy), because removing the tumor offers the highest likelihood of cure and superior survival compared with other modalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glomus jugulare tumor",
    "long_answer": "Glomus jugulare tumors are highly vascular paragangliomas that arise in the region of the jugular bulb. Because of their rich arterial supply and close proximity to the middle and inner ear structures, blood flow within the tumor transmits a rhythmic sound that patients perceive as pulsatile tinnitus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myopia",
    "long_answer": "Numerous epidemiological and interventional studies have shown that children who spend more hours outdoors have a significantly lower risk of developing or progressing in myopia (nearsightedness). Bright outdoor light is thought to stimulate retinal dopamine release, which slows axial elongation of the eyeball—the key anatomical change that causes myopia. Thus, time spent outdoors during childhood is an important environmental factor influencing this very common refractive eye disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type",
    "long_answer": "This EBV-associated extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (nasal type) commonly involves the nasal cavity and hard palate. Its angiocentric, angio-destructive growth produces extensive tissue necrosis that can manifest clinically as painful ulcerations or even perforation of the palate, a classic presentation of this lymphoma subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome is a multisystem developmental disorder in which upper-limb skeletal anomalies such as radioulnar synostosis are common. Core clinical features also include marked prenatal-onset growth deficiency leading to short stature, microcephaly, progressive scoliosis, and moderate-to-severe intellectual disability. This constellation of findings matches all of the manifestations listed in the question, making Cornelia de Lange syndrome the established diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intrathecal administration",
    "long_answer": "To address the neurological manifestations of Hunter syndrome, clinical trials are delivering the enzyme idursulfase directly into the cerebrospinal fluid via intrathecal infusion. This intrathecal administration bypasses the blood–brain barrier, allowing the enzyme to reach the central nervous system and is being tested specifically to halt or slow cognitive decline in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPC3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Simpson–Golabi–Behmel syndrome is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations or deletions in the X-linked GPC3 (glypican-3) gene. Disruption of GPC3 alters growth-factor signaling during embryogenesis, producing the severe over-growth and fluid accumulation that can manifest prenatally as hydrops fetalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endotracheal intubation",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary angioedema, laryngeal swelling can rapidly obstruct the upper airway. The recommended emergency measure is early endotracheal intubation to secure and protect the airway; if intubation fails, a surgical airway (e.g., cricothyrotomy or tracheostomy) is performed. Prompt airway control is lifesaving while specific therapy (C1-inhibitor concentrate, icatibant, etc.) takes effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palatal petechiae",
    "long_answer": "Group A β-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis often produces tiny hemorrhagic spots—petechiae—on the soft palate. These palatal petechiae result from capillary rupture triggered by streptococcal exotoxins and are a classic, relatively specific physical-examination clue pointing toward streptococcal infection rather than viral causes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scotopic vision",
    "long_answer": "Scotopic vision is the form of sight generated solely by rod photoreceptors. Rods are highly sensitive to low-light levels but do not convey color information; therefore scotopic vision is monochromatic. In achromatopsia, cone function is absent or severely impaired, so affected individuals rely almost entirely on rod-mediated scotopic vision for both day and night vision, leading to total color blindness and reduced visual acuity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gingival fibromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Game-Friedman-Paradice syndrome is one of the hereditary gingival-overgrowth syndromes; its cardinal feature is massive, benign fibrous enlargement of the gingiva (gingival fibromatosis), often beginning in childhood and unassociated with drug use or systemic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NS1 protein",
    "long_answer": "Parvovirus B19’s multifunctional non-structural protein NS1 specifically recognizes and binds DNA motifs located upstream of the viral p6 promoter. Through this direct binding, NS1 acts as a transcriptional trans-activator, modulating initiation from the p6 promoter and thereby controlling early gene expression necessary for viral replication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome, caused by pathogenic variants in collagen IV genes (COL4A3, COL4A4, COL4A5), damages the basement membranes of the cochlea as well as the kidney glomeruli. This leads to bilateral, progressive sensorineural hearing loss rather than conductive or mixed loss, typically beginning in late childhood or adolescence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metastatic spread",
    "long_answer": "A benign insulinoma is confined to the pancreas, whereas a metastatic (malignant) insulinoma has spread beyond the pancreas—most often to regional lymph nodes or the liver—demonstrating true metastasis and thus malignant behavior."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Behçet disease",
    "long_answer": "Behçet disease is a systemic vasculitis that characteristically produces recurrent, painful ulcers on oral and genital mucous membranes and causes ocular inflammation, including uveitis and posterior segment involvement such as chorioretinitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver disease",
    "long_answer": "In acquired dysfibrinogenemia, abnormal fibrinogen molecules arise most often secondary to liver disease. Because the liver is the sole site of fibrinogen synthesis and post-translational modification, hepatic disorders (e.g., cirrhosis, hepatitis) can produce structurally altered fibrinogen that impairs clot formation, leading to the acquired form of dysfibrinogenemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Higher risk",
    "long_answer": "Maternal overweight or obesity before or during early pregnancy is consistently associated with an elevated incidence of congenital heart defects in the offspring. Large population-based studies show a dose-response pattern: compared with normal-weight women, being overweight raises the odds of CHDs by roughly 10–30 %, and maternal obesity raises it by 30–60 % or more. Proposed mechanisms include maternal hyperglycaemia and insulin resistance, chronic inflammation, oxidative stress, and impaired folate metabolism, all of which can disrupt cardiac morphogenesis during the first trimester."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Toxoplasmosis is an infectious disease produced by the protozoan parasite Toxoplasma gondii. This unicellular organism is a pathogenic agent that infects warm-blooded animals, including humans. Therefore, toxoplasmosis is indeed caused by a pathogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance",
    "long_answer": "Light chain deposition disease (LCDD) most often arises in patients who have a small B-cell/plasma-cell clone producing monoclonal light chains; the common underlying disorder is monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS). MGUS is a benign-appearing plasma cell dyscrasia that can remain stable for years but carries an annual risk of about 1% of transforming into overt multiple myeloma or another malignant plasma-cell neoplasm. Thus, MGUS is the plasma cell disorder classically linked to LCDD and recognized as a precursor state that may progress to multiple myeloma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal autotransplantation",
    "long_answer": "A widely used approach for relieving the intractable flank pain of Loin Pain Hematuria Syndrome is renal autotransplantation, in which the affected kidney is surgically removed and re-implanted in the pelvis. The procedure denervates the kidney and alters its blood supply, leading to sustained pain relief in a substantial proportion of LPHS patients when conservative analgesic therapy has failed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Classic forms of brachydactyly (types A–E) are usually caused by heterozygous mutations in genes such as IHH, ROR2, and HOXD13. These mutations are expressed when only one copy of the altered gene is present, so the trait is passed from an affected parent to offspring with a 50 % risk each pregnancy, demonstrating an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary hyperaldosteronism",
    "long_answer": "Primary hyperaldosteronism (also called Conn syndrome when due to an adrenal adenoma) is characterized by autonomous overproduction of the mineralocorticoid aldosterone by the adrenal cortex. While it can produce hypertension, hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis, many patients are initially asymptomatic, making it a classic example of an endocrine disorder that may go unnoticed despite excess aldosterone secretion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electroretinography",
    "long_answer": "Retinitis pigmentosa is characterized by progressive degeneration of rod and cone photoreceptors. The gold-standard test for detecting and monitoring this functional loss is full-field electroretinography (ERG), which records the retina’s electrical responses to flashes of light. In RP the ERG amplitudes progressively diminish and eventually become undetectable, providing an objective, sensitive measure of photoreceptor dysfunction before funduscopic changes are advanced."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "PFAPA (Periodic Fever, Aphthous Stomatitis, Pharyngitis, and Cervical Adenitis) is classified among the autoinflammatory periodic fever syndromes. These disorders are routinely diagnosed and managed in pediatric rheumatology clinics, where specialists have expertise in recurrent fever disorders, immune dysregulation, and related treatment strategies such as corticosteroid bursts or tonsillectomy. Therefore, children with PFAPA are most commonly treated by pediatric rheumatologists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertelorism",
    "long_answer": "Hypertelorism refers to an abnormally increased interpupillary and interorbital distance (wide-set eyes). In Apert syndrome, premature fusion of cranial sutures impairs normal skull and midfacial growth, producing midface dysplasia; the same aberrant craniofacial development frequently results in hypertelorism, making the two features commonly associated in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MSX1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the homeobox transcription factor gene MSX1 disrupt the epithelial-mesenchymal interactions needed for odontogenesis and nail formation. Pathogenic variants in MSX1 are a well-established cause of isolated hypodontia (selective tooth agenesis) and also produce the autosomal-dominant Tooth-and-Nail (Witkop) syndrome, which combines hypodontia with nail dysplasia. Thus, MSX1 is the gene common to both hypodontia and Witkop syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPC3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Simpson–Golabi–Behmel syndrome type 1 is classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked gene GPC3 (glypican-3). Because glypican-3 regulates Hedgehog and other morphogen signalling pathways that pattern the limbs, GPC3 mutations not only produce the overgrowth phenotype of Simpson–Golabi–Behmel syndrome but can also manifest with congenital anomalies such as postaxial polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Mondini dysplasia is a congenital malformation of the inner ear in which the cochlea is incompletely partitioned (roughly 1.5 turns instead of the normal 2.5). Because the defect involves the cochlea and other membranous labyrinth structures, it primarily disrupts the sensory (hair-cell/neural) mechanisms of hearing, leading classically to sensorineural hearing loss. Mixed or progressive losses can occur, but the characteristic and most commonly cited form is congenital sensorineural hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2",
    "long_answer": "Vestibular schwannomas typically harbor loss-of-function mutations in the NF2 (neurofibromin-2/merlin) gene. NF2 loss activates the mTOR signaling pathway, and everolimus—an mTOR inhibitor—is being evaluated to suppress growth of these NF2-mutant tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Delta waves",
    "long_answer": "In severe (advanced) hepatic encephalopathy the electro-encephalogram shows marked slowing, with the background rhythm dominated by high-amplitude generalized delta-frequency activity, indicating widespread cortical dysfunction characteristic of the later stages of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ancylostomatidae",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous larva migrans occurs when infective larvae of animal hookworms—most commonly Ancylostoma braziliense and Ancylostoma caninum—penetrate human skin. These hookworm species belong to the family Ancylostomatidae, whose members are unable to complete their life-cycle in humans but can migrate within the epidermis, producing the characteristic serpiginous lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Acquired hypoprothrombinemia can occur in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) when patients develop antiphospholipid-type autoantibodies (often termed lupus anticoagulant) that target prothrombin (coagulation factor II). These anti-prothrombin antibodies accelerate clearance of prothrombin, producing marked factor II deficiency and bleeding—known as the lupus-anticoagulant hypoprothrombinemia syndrome. Thus, SLE is the autoimmune disease classically linked to autoantibody-mediated acquired hypoprothrombinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 6",
    "long_answer": "In kidney (renal) transplant recipients, episodes of viremia with Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6) often precede and promote the reactivation of latent cytomegalovirus (CMV). HHV-6 immediate-early gene products can trans-activate CMV promoters and the virus impairs CMV-specific T-cell responses, making CMV reactivation and disease more likely in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FERMT1",
    "long_answer": "Kindler syndrome is an autosomal-recessive genodermatosis caused by loss-of-function mutations in the FERMT1 (also known as KIND1) gene, which encodes the focal-adhesion protein kindlin-1 that is essential for integrin activation and dermal-epidermal adhesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Daubenton’s bat",
    "long_answer": "In the United Kingdom, Daubenton’s bat (Myotis daubentonii) has repeatedly been found infected with European Bat Lyssavirus type 2, a rabies-like virus. Surveillance and the 2002 human fatality linked to this species established it as the primary UK bat host for rabies-related lyssaviruses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 18",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 18, also called Edwards syndrome, is a chromosomal disorder caused by the presence of an extra copy of chromosome 18. Characteristic craniofacial findings include hypertelorism (abnormally wide‐spaced eyes), micrognathia, low-set ears, and cleft lip/palate. The additional chromosome disrupts normal embryonic development, leading to these distinctive facial anomalies along with multiple systemic malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nesidioblastosis",
    "long_answer": "During the 1970s and 1980s, the term “nesidioblastosis” was commonly used as a synonym for congenital hyperinsulinism. Nesidioblastosis describes diffuse or focal proliferation of pancreatic β-cells (islet cell hyperplasia), leading to persistent hyperinsulinemic hypoglycaemia in neonates and infants. Although modern classification now prefers the umbrella term congenital hyperinsulinism, the historical term nesidioblastosis referred to the same disease process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthodontist",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with 3-M syndrome can have jaw-size discrepancies and malocclusion; an orthodontist is the dental specialist who evaluates and corrects such alignment and bite problems, helping manage the syndrome’s dental manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Copper",
    "long_answer": "Copper is the third most abundant essential trace metal in the human body, after iron and zinc. While small amounts are vital for the activity of enzymes such as cytochrome-c oxidase and superoxide dismutase, excess copper can accumulate in tissues, generating reactive oxygen species and leading to hepatic, neurologic, and renal toxicity (as seen in conditions like Wilson’s disease or from high-dose supplementation)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lithium carbonate",
    "long_answer": "Lithium carbonate is a prototypical mood-stabilizing drug that is first-line therapy for Bipolar I disorder, preventing manic and depressive relapses. Clinical reports and small series also demonstrate that lithium markedly reduces the frequency and severity of hypersomnia episodes in Kleine–Levin syndrome, making it one of the few medications effective in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NINDS",
    "long_answer": "The National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS), part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health, is the federal biomedical research organization that funds and conducts studies on neurological and lysosomal storage disorders such as sialidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "An ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Heterophoria (latent strabismus) is identified with tests such as the cover-uncover test, Maddox rod, and prism assessments, and its management may require prisms, vision therapy, or, rarely, surgery. All of these diagnostic procedures and treatments fall within the scope of an ophthalmologist—a medically trained eye specialist qualified to detect and manage binocular-vision disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tricuspid and mitral valves",
    "long_answer": "In both dogs and cats, congenital valvular dysplasia most frequently involves the atrioventricular valves—the tricuspid valve on the right side of the heart and the mitral valve on the left. Tricuspid valve dysplasia is particularly common in certain dog breeds such as Labrador Retrievers, while mitral valve dysplasia is also reported in several breeds and in cats. Dysplastic changes in these valves lead to malformed leaflets, chordae, or papillary muscles, resulting in regurgitation and characteristic murmurs detected in young animals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Streptococcus pyogenes",
    "long_answer": "Scleredema (scleredema adultorum of Buschke) classically develops after an acute upper-respiratory infection caused by Streptococcus pyogenes, the group A β-hemolytic streptococcus. Numerous case series document the temporal association between streptococcal pharyngitis and the abrupt onset of the woody skin induration characteristic of scleredema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatology",
    "long_answer": "Hemochromatosis type 4B causes systemic iron overload with especially heavy deposition in the liver, leading to hepatic fibrosis, cirrhosis, and risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. Monitoring liver injury, staging fibrosis, instituting surveillance for malignancy, and coordinating iron-removal therapy are core tasks of a hepatologist, making hepatology the specialty most directly involved in managing the major complications of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuropsychological testing",
    "long_answer": "HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders are formally confirmed with standardized neuropsychological testing, which evaluates performance across multiple cognitive domains. According to the Frascati diagnostic criteria, documenting impairment on a formal neuropsychological battery (along with functional assessment) is required to establish the presence and severity of HAND, whereas imaging or routine laboratory studies are only supportive or exclusionary."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SQSTM1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SQSTM1 gene, which encodes the autophagy-adaptor protein p62/sequestosome-1, are well established as a frequent cause of Paget’s disease of bone and have also been identified in familial and sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). Defective p62 impairs ubiquitin-mediated protein turnover and autophagy, pathogenic mechanisms shared by both disorders, making SQSTM1 mutation a common genetic link between ALS and Paget’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Iridocorneal Endothelial (ICE) syndrome is an ocular disorder that affects the corneal endothelium, iris, and anterior chamber angle, often leading to corneal edema and secondary glaucoma. Diagnosis, monitoring, and management—including slit-lamp examination, gonioscopy, intraocular pressure control, and possible surgical intervention—fall within the expertise of ophthalmologists (particularly cornea or glaucoma subspecialists). Therefore, the medical specialty that treats ICE syndrome is Ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coxsackievirus B",
    "long_answer": "Viral pleurisy (inflammation of the pleural membranes) is most frequently associated with infection by Coxsackievirus B, an enterovirus in the Picornaviridae family. Numerous studies and clinical reviews identify Coxsackie B as the leading viral agent in cases of idiopathic or uncomplicated pleurisy, often presenting with sharp pleuritic chest pain and sometimes termed Bornholm disease or epidemic pleurodynia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural progenitor cells",
    "long_answer": "Zika virus crosses the placenta, infects and replicates preferentially in fetal cortical neural progenitor cells (radial glia/neural stem cells). Viral infection triggers cell-cycle arrest, apoptosis and reduced neurogenesis, depleting the progenitor pool needed for brain growth, which culminates in the severe reduction of cranial size characteristic of microcephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rickets",
    "long_answer": "In Fanconi syndrome the proximal renal tubule loses phosphate, bicarbonate and other solutes. Phosphate wasting causes defective bone mineralization; in growing children this presents as rickets (while adults develop osteomalacia). Therefore, rickets is a well-recognized and common complication of Fanconi syndrome in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth plates",
    "long_answer": "In acromesomelic dysplasia, mutations that disrupt natriuretic-peptide signaling impair the cartilage of the epiphyseal (physeal) growth plates found at the ends of long bones. Damage to these growth plates hinders normal endochondral ossification, producing the characteristic abnormal bone growth and limb shortening seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Turner syndrome (45,X karyotype) have streak, dysgenetic gonads. This gonadal dysgenesis markedly increases the risk of malignant germ-cell tumors, particularly dysgerminoma, compared with women who have normal ovaries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strabismus",
    "long_answer": "A frequent ocular manifestation of Jacobsen syndrome is strabismus, a misalignment of the eyes (commonly “crossed eyes”). Clinical surveys report that roughly half of affected individuals exhibit strabismus, making it one of the most common eye-related problems associated with the 11q deletion characteristic of Jacobsen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNJ11 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Activating mutations in the KCNJ11 gene, which encodes the Kir6.2 subunit of the pancreatic ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channel, are a well-established cause of permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus. These same KCNJ11 mutations can also disrupt neuronal KATP channel function, leading to neurodevelopmental problems such as developmental delay, epilepsy, and the full DEND (Developmental delay, Epilepsy, and Neonatal Diabetes) syndrome, thereby increasing the risk of developmental disabilities in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebrospinal fluid",
    "long_answer": "Mumps meningitis is diagnosed by detecting mumps virus (viral RNA or virus-specific IgM) in cerebrospinal fluid obtained by lumbar puncture, because CSF directly reflects infection and inflammation of the meninges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malaise",
    "long_answer": "Among the typical clinical manifestations of swine influenza (e.g., fever, cough, sore throat), patients frequently report malaise—a nonspecific sensation of discomfort, uneasiness, or general ‘not feeling well.’ Because malaise reflects the systemic inflammatory response that accompanies influenza infection, it is consistently listed in medical literature as a common symptom of swine-origin H1N1 and other influenza viruses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nonoxynol-9",
    "long_answer": "Nonoxynol-9, the non-ionic surfactant used as the active spermicide in many contraceptive foams, gels, films and condoms, can provoke hypersensitivity reactions; contact allergy to this agent leads to vulvovaginal irritation and allergic vaginitis in susceptible women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anxious-ambivalent attachment",
    "long_answer": "In Bowlby’s framework, children with an anxious-ambivalent (insecure-resistant) attachment become markedly distressed when the caregiver leaves and remain clingy, fearful, and hard to soothe even after reunion. This exaggerated, prolonged separation distress parallels the core clinical features of separation anxiety disorder, making the anxious-ambivalent attachment style the closest analogue."  
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH7",
    "long_answer": "The MYH7 gene encodes the β-myosin heavy chain, a principal structural protein that forms the thick filaments of the cardiac‐muscle sarcomere. Pathogenic variants in MYH7 alter sarcomeric architecture and contractility, and are a well-established cause of left ventricular hypertrophy in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and related LVH phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "Intestinal lymphangiectasia causes leakage of intestinal lymph into the gut lumen. Because lymph is rich in lymphocytes, especially T-cell lymphocytes, their continuous loss leads to marked lymphopenia. Thus, the white blood cells lost in greatest quantity are lymphocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pernicious anemia",
    "long_answer": "Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune form of megaloblastic anemia in which patients produce autoantibodies against intrinsic factor (and parietal cell antigens). These antibodies block or destroy intrinsic factor, preventing vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum and leading to B12 deficiency and anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gadolinium-enhanced MRI",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium contrast is the test of choice because its superior soft-tissue resolution and enhancement patterns can detect vestibular schwannomas as small as 2–3 mm, delineate their extent along the VIII cranial nerve and cerebellopontine angle, and avoids ionizing radiation—features that CT or other modalities cannot match."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arylsulfatase B",
    "long_answer": "Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome is mucopolysaccharidosis type VI, which results from a hereditary deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase B (also called N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase). Lack of this enzyme blocks degradation of dermatan sulfate and related glycosaminoglycans, leading to their accumulation in tissues. The resultant storage in soft tissues such as the tongue produces the characteristic macroglossia seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shortness of breath",
    "long_answer": "When people with a specific phobia are exposed to the feared object or situation, they commonly develop autonomic arousal that produces shortness of breath or a smothering sensation. This identical complaint—shortness of breath—is the cardinal manifestation of dyspnea, which is medically defined as the subjective experience of breathing discomfort. Therefore, shortness of breath is a shared physical symptom of both specific phobia and dyspnea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Juvenile osteoporosis",
    "long_answer": "When osteoporosis occurs in the paediatric age-group—typically in pre-pubertal or early adolescent patients—it is termed juvenile osteoporosis (often specified as juvenile idiopathic osteoporosis). This condition is characterized by low bone mass and increased fracture risk in otherwise healthy children or adolescents, distinguishing it from the post-menopausal or senile forms seen in adults."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CCFDN syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Congenital cataracts-facial dysmorphism-neuropathy (CCFDN) syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by CTDP1 mutations. A defining triad is bilateral congenital cataracts, gradual sensorineural hearing loss, and a progressive demyelinating peripheral neuropathy; affected individuals often show facial dysmorphism and mild developmental delay as well. These features make CCFDN the prototypic genetic condition that combines congenital cataracts and progressive deafness with peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ventricular germinal zone",
    "long_answer": "Cortical neurons are born in the ventricular (germinal) zone lining the cerebral ventricles. In normal development they migrate radially from this periventricular region to the cortical plate. When this migration from the ventricular germinal zone is arrested or disordered, the cortex fails to form normal gyri and sulci, producing the ‘smooth brain’ appearance characteristic of lissencephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calsequestrin 2",
    "long_answer": "Calsequestrin 2 (encoded by the CASQ2 gene) is the main calcium-binding/buffering protein inside the cardiac sarcoplasmic reticulum. Loss-of-function or missense mutations in CASQ2 disrupt SR calcium storage and release, producing adrenergically driven ventricular arrhythmias characteristic of catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CPVT type 2). Thus the same protein links SR calcium buffering with the inherited arrhythmia syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciprofloxacin",
    "long_answer": "Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa is almost always caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa. The recommended therapy is a prolonged course of an antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone, most commonly ciprofloxacin, which achieves high bone and cartilage penetration and is active against Pseudomonas species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone replacement therapy",
    "long_answer": "In males with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, exogenous testosterone is given in gradually increasing doses to mimic normal pubertal androgen levels. Testosterone directly stimulates the epiphyseal growth plates, triggering the pubertal growth spurt, while simultaneously enlarging the penis, scrotum, and other androgen-dependent tissues and promoting development of secondary sexual characteristics (voice deepening, muscle mass, body hair). Thus, testosterone replacement therapy both advances skeletal maturation via the epiphyseal plate and improves genital and pubertal development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Filaggrin-2 (FLG2)",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the FLG2 gene, which encodes filaggrin-2, cause the ‘ichthyotic’ form of peeling skin syndrome. Because filaggrin-2 is essential for cornified-envelope formation and stratum-corneum hydration, its absence produces generalized superficial skin peeling and pronounced xerosis (dry, scaly skin). Thus, an FLG2 defect links peeling skin syndrome with clinical dryness of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rhizomelic dwarfism",
    "long_answer": "Rhizomelic dwarfism is defined by disproportionate shortening of the proximal limb segments—the humeri and femora—relative to the middle and distal segments. This pattern of limb involvement distinguishes it from mesomelic (middle‐segment) and acromelic (distal‐segment) forms of dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syphilis",
    "long_answer": "In tertiary (third-stage) syphilis, chronic endarteritis of the vasa vasorum leads to syphilitic aortitis. The resulting weakening of the aortic wall predisposes patients to formation of thoracic aortic aneurysms, a hallmark cardiovascular complication of late syphilis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia",
    "long_answer": "In catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CPVT), adrenergic stimulation causes intracellular Ca²⁺ overload, producing delayed afterdepolarizations (DADs). These DAD-triggered beats give rise to the characteristic arrhythmia of CPVT—bidirectional ventricular tachycardia, in which the QRS axis alternates beat-to-beat."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatosplenomegaly",
    "long_answer": "AIDS-related lymphoma can infiltrate and enlarge both the liver and spleen. The simultaneous abnormal enlargement of these two organs is called hepatosplenomegaly, and it is a recognized clinical sign that may accompany this lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retina",
    "long_answer": "The retina is the light-sensitive, visual-perception layer lining the back of the eye. At the point where the optic nerve fibers converge and exit the eye—the optic disc—there are no photoreceptors, producing the physiological blind spot. Thus, the retina is the ocular structure that both enables vision and contains the blind spot created by the optic nerve’s departure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYH3 gene, which encodes the embryonic myosin heavy-chain, are a well-established cause of multiple forms of distal arthrogryposis (e.g., Freeman-Sheldon syndrome/DA2A and Sheldon-Hall syndrome/DA2B). These mutations disrupt sarcomeric myosin function during fetal muscle development, leading to congenital contractures of the distal joints characteristic of distal arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome (Kabuki make-up syndrome) is a congenital genetic disorder whose classic skeletal findings include fifth-finger clinodactyly and characteristic cranial shape abnormalities such as trigono- or brachycephaly (‘trigonobrachycephaly’). The syndrome, usually caused by pathogenic variants in KMT2D or KDM6A, presents with distinctive facial features, post-natal growth deficiency, intellectual disability, and the skeletal anomalies noted above, making Kabuki syndrome the well-established condition that fits the described features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POLR3A",
    "long_answer": "Wiedemann–Rautenstrauch (neonatal-progeroid) syndrome has been traced to pathogenic variants in POLR3A, the gene encoding the largest catalytic subunit of RNA polymerase III. Patients with POLR3A mutations typically show congenital progeroid features together with relative or absolute macrocephaly, linking the same POLR3A defect to both the syndrome and the enlarged head size."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibromuscular dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) is the prototypical and most frequently encountered arterial dysplasia. It is a non-atherosclerotic, non-inflammatory disease in which abnormal growth of smooth-muscle and fibrous tissue within the arterial wall produces the classic ‘string-of-beads’ appearance, leading to arterial stenosis, aneurysm, or dissection—most commonly in the renal and carotid arteries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3p deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Loss of genetic material from the short arm of chromosome 3 (3p-) produces 3p deletion syndrome, a contiguous-gene disorder characterized by growth retardation, distinctive facial features and, importantly, moderate to severe intellectual disability due to haplo-insufficiency of multiple genes on 3p."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular surgery",
    "long_answer": "Abdominal aortic aneurysms are dilatations of the abdominal aorta that carry a risk of life-threatening rupture. Definitive management requires either open surgical repair or endovascular stent-graft placement, procedures that are planned and performed by vascular surgeons. Therefore, the medical specialty most commonly responsible for treating patients with an abdominal aortic aneurysm is vascular surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glutamic acid decarboxylase",
    "long_answer": "In a documented case of Satoyoshi syndrome, circulating auto-antibodies were found against the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD, particularly the 65-kDa isoform). Detection of anti-GAD antibodies supports an autoimmune mechanism for the muscle spasms and other features of the syndrome, paralleling their role in other neurologic autoimmune disorders such as stiff-person syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Telangiectasia",
    "long_answer": "Telangiectasia refers to clusters of abnormally dilated capillaries and venules visible in the skin or mucosa. These small, superficial blood vessels can appear anywhere on the body and are frequently noted around joints such as the knee, where repetitive trauma or venous pressure favors their development. The condition is characterized by fine red or purple lines or webs (so-called “spider veins”) that are the hallmark of dilated dermal capillaries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anaphase-promoting complex/cyclosome",
    "long_answer": "The anaphase-promoting complex/cyclosome (APC/C) is an E3 ubiquitin ligase that marks key regulatory proteins—most notably securin and mitotic cyclins—for proteasomal degradation at the end of metaphase. Destruction of securin releases the protease separase, which then cleaves cohesin rings and allows sister chromatids to separate, initiating anaphase. By ensuring that securin and cyclins are removed only when all chromosomes are properly attached to the spindle, the APC/C safeguards accurate chromosome segregation and thereby prevents chromosome nondisjunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Quantitative urinary free sialic acid analysis",
    "long_answer": "Infantile free sialic acid storage disease is confirmed by demonstrating markedly elevated levels of free (unconjugated) sialic acid in the patient’s urine. Quantitative analysis—typically performed by high-performance liquid chromatography or mass spectrometry—shows concentrations far above the normal range, establishing the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Saprophytic nutrition",
    "long_answer": "During maggot (larval) therapy, blow-fly larvae digest and absorb dead or decaying tissue rather than living tissue. They secrete proteolytic enzymes that liquefy the necrotic material outside their bodies and then ingest the breakdown products, a mode of heterotrophic feeding known as saprophytic (saprotrophic) nutrition. This saprophagy is what enables the maggots to debride and clean necrotic wounds."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Navicular bone",
    "long_answer": "Köhler disease is a childhood osteochondrosis characterized by avascular necrosis of the tarsal navicular bone in the foot, leading to pain and limping until re-ossification occurs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chorionic villus sampling",
    "long_answer": "Karyotypic analysis of placental cells obtained by chorionic villus sampling (CVS) can reveal an extra copy of chromosome 9. Because CVS is performed early in pregnancy and detects chromosomal complements in trophoblastic tissue, a finding of trisomy 9 (often mosaic) in the villi raises suspicion and therefore suggests the presence of trisomy 9 in the fetus, prompting confirmatory testing such as amniocentesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris",
    "long_answer": "Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune blistering disorder that shows increased prevalence among Ashkenazi Jews. The disease is characterized by IgG autoantibodies directed against the desmosomal cadherin desmoglein-3 (and often desmoglein-1), leading to loss of keratinocyte cohesion (acantholysis) and formation of flaccid mucocutaneous bullae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC6 gene",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoxanthoma elasticum (PXE) is overwhelmingly caused by pathogenic variants in the ATP-binding cassette sub-family C member 6 (ABCC6, formerly MRP6) gene on chromosome 16p13.1. More than 300 distinct ABCC6 mutations—missense, nonsense, splice-site, small insertions/deletions, and large rearrangements—have been catalogued in the literature and are indexed in PubMed reports. No other gene shows the same consistent, causal relationship with classic PXE, making ABCC6 the definitive gene whose mutations are documented for this disease in the PubMed database."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yersinia pseudotuberculosis",
    "long_answer": "Yersinia pseudotuberculosis is a zoonotic Gram-negative bacterium that, after ingestion, invades Peyer’s patches and mesenteric lymph nodes. The resulting inflammation of these lymph nodes produces mesenteric adenitis (lymphadenitis), a well-recognized clinical syndrome in humans that can mimic acute appendicitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "No lymphadenopathy",
    "long_answer": "In anaplastic (undifferentiated) thyroid carcinoma the regional cervical lymph nodes are usually not enlarged or clinically involved; therefore the absence of palpable cervical lymphadenopathy is characteristic and helps distinguish it from papillary or medullary thyroid cancers, which commonly show nodal metastases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oneirophobia",
    "long_answer": "The persistent, excessive and irrational fear of dreams is termed oneirophobia. Classified clinically as a type of specific phobia, individuals with oneirophobia experience marked anxiety or panic at the prospect of dreaming and may avoid sleep to prevent dreams, leading to distress and functional impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Brachydactyly type D (short distal phalanx of the thumb) has been linked to mutations or copy-number changes in the HOXD13 gene. HOXD13, a homeobox transcription factor critical for patterning of the distal limb, is located on chromosome 2q31. Disrupted HOXD13 function alters digit morphogenesis, producing the characteristic thumb shortening seen in brachydactyly type D."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XYLT1",
    "long_answer": "Angel-shaped phalango-epiphyseal dysplasia is an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the XYLT1 gene, which encodes xylosyltransferase-1, the enzyme that initiates glycosaminoglycan chain formation on proteoglycans. Defective XYLT1 activity disturbs cartilage matrix synthesis, producing the characteristic angel-shaped middle phalanges and other skeletal changes seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Revesz syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the TINF2 gene, a shelterin-complex component essential for telomere maintenance. The TINF2 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 14q12 on chromosome 14, making chromosome 14 the chromosomal location associated with Revesz syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Light-chain cast nephropathy",
    "long_answer": "In multiple myeloma, excess free κ or λ immunoglobulin light chains (Bence-Jones proteins) are filtered by the glomerulus and precipitate with Tamm-Horsfall protein in the distal nephron. The resulting obstructing casts and accompanying tubulointerstitial inflammation—termed light-chain (Bence-Jones) cast nephropathy—are the leading mechanism of acute and chronic kidney failure in myeloma patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Whole exome sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Cousin–Walbraum–Cegarra syndrome is a very rare Mendelian disorder; pinpointing the underlying pathogenic variant in the responsible gene allows a definitive diagnosis. Whole exome sequencing, a next-generation sequencing technology that reads all protein-coding regions of the genome, can detect that causal mutation and is therefore the appropriate tool for diagnosing the syndrome by uncovering its genetic cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Erythema nodosum is an acute inflammatory disorder of the subcutaneous fat that presents with tender, erythematous nodules on the shins and other extensor surfaces. Because it is primarily a cutaneous manifestation, patients are most commonly evaluated and managed by dermatologists, who diagnose the condition clinically and address underlying triggers and supportive treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Large myelinated fibers",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary motor and sensory neuropathies (e.g., Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease), weakness and impaired muscle control result from degeneration of the large-diameter, heavily myelinated A-alpha motor fibers (and their homologous large sensory fibers). Loss of these fast-conducting, myelinated axons disrupts efficient activation of skeletal muscle, producing the characteristic motor deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isovaleric acidemia",
    "long_answer": "Isovaleric acidemia (isovaleric aciduria) is an inborn error of leucine metabolism caused by deficiency of isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase. Accumulation of isovaleric acid and related metabolites produces the characteristic ‘sweaty-feet’ odor and precipitates acute metabolic crises with vomiting, lethargy, and metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Cutis laxa is a disorder of skin and elastic connective tissue that presents with loose, pendulous, inelastic skin folds. As a primary skin disease, its evaluation (clinical examination, skin biopsy, genetic testing) and management (symptom control, monitoring for systemic involvement, referral to other specialists when needed) are chiefly carried out within the field of dermatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beals syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beals syndrome, formally called congenital contractural arachnodactyly, is produced by pathogenic variants in the FBN2 gene.  Affected individuals can develop dilation and aneurysms of the ascending aorta, similar to Marfan-type disorders, and almost all show congenital joint contractures – most characteristically camptodactyly (permanent flexion of the fingers).  Thus this single genetic condition links aortic aneurysm formation with camptodactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Parkes Weber syndrome presents with cutaneous manifestations such as extensive capillary malformations (port-wine stains) and other vascular skin lesions. The medical specialist trained to diagnose and treat disorders of the skin, hair and nails—including vascular birthmarks—is a dermatologist, who therefore commonly manages the skin symptoms of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Orofaciodigital syndrome is an inherited disorder involving multiple congenital anomalies of the face, oral cavity, and digits. Because it results from pathogenic genetic variants and requires genetic testing for confirmation, risk assessment for relatives, and counseling, its diagnosis and ongoing management fall under the domain of the medical genetics specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spleen",
    "long_answer": "In familial chylomicronemia (type I hyperlipoproteinemia), massive accumulation of chylomicrons leads to triglyceride-laden macrophages sequestering in reticulo-endothelial tissues. This lipid deposition often produces splenomegaly, so the spleen can become visibly enlarged in patients with this severe hypertriglyceridemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ivermectin",
    "long_answer": "Ivermectin is the first-line anthelmintic for Strongyloides stercoralis infection and is specifically recommended in patients who may become immunosuppressed—such as those with acute radiation syndrome—to prevent or treat the potentially fatal Strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome. Its high efficacy and safety profile make it the drug of choice in this setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Azathioprine",
    "long_answer": "Azathioprine is a purine-analog immunosuppressant widely used as a steroid-sparing agent for myasthenia gravis. By inhibiting de-novo purine synthesis it suppresses T- and B-lymphocyte proliferation, improving neuromuscular transmission in MG. Long-term azathioprine therapy is well documented to raise the incidence of certain malignancies—particularly lymphomas and non-melanoma skin cancers—because its metabolites cause DNA damage and impair immune surveillance against tumor cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital brain malformation",
    "long_answer": "Agenesis of the corpus callosum is classified as a congenital malformation of the brain—a neurodevelopmental defect present at birth that results from abnormal formation of the corpus callosum during embryogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "Miosis refers to an abnormal constriction of the pupil, an ocular sign that can impair vision and often signals eye disease or systemic neurologic issues. Evaluation and management of pupillary abnormalities, including the use of pharmacologic agents (e.g., anticholinergics) or surgical intervention if needed, are within the scope of ophthalmology—the medical specialty dedicated to diagnosing and treating disorders of the eye and its adnexa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CT scan",
    "long_answer": "A computed tomography (CT) scan allows precise cross-sectional imaging of the lower-limb bones, letting clinicians calculate femoral anteversion and tibial torsion angles. These numerical torsion measurements are used to grade how severe an in-toeing (pigeon-toe) deformity is, making CT the standard imaging modality for quantifying its severity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caudal regression syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Zion Clark was born with caudal regression syndrome, a rare congenital disorder in which the lower (caudal) segments of the spine and associated structures fail to develop properly during early embryogenesis. This incomplete formation of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae can lead to severe malformations of the pelvis, lower limbs, and lower organs; in Clark’s case, it resulted in the complete absence of functional legs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lateral plantar nerve",
    "long_answer": "The muscle that produces abduction of the fifth toe is the abductor digiti minimi. This intrinsic foot muscle is supplied by the lateral plantar nerve, a terminal branch of the tibial nerve. Consequently, the lateral plantar nerve provides the motor innervation required for abducting the fifth toe."
}

{
    "short_answer": "22q11.2 deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Deletion of chromosome 22q11.2 (DiGeorge/velocardiofacial syndrome) causes thymic hypoplasia and impaired T-cell–mediated immunity. Individuals with this immune dysfunction have a markedly elevated lifetime risk of developing schizophrenia or schizo-affective disorders, making 22q11.2 deletion syndrome the best-established genetic condition linking immune deficits to schizophrenia." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glomerular filtration",
    "long_answer": "In the renal corpuscle, blood plasma is forced through the fenestrated glomerular capillaries into Bowman’s capsule by hydrostatic pressure. This process, called glomerular filtration, produces the initial filtrate that contains water and small solutes—including glucose—while excluding large proteins and cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibrin microthrombi",
    "long_answer": "A defining histologic feature of angiolipoma is the presence of fibrin microthrombi occluding many of its small capillary-sized vessels. These eosinophilic plugs consist of fibrin, the protein generated when thrombin cleaves fibrinogen, making them a unique morphologic hallmark of this tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral cortex",
    "long_answer": "Gray matter heterotopia arises when neurons that are generated in the periventricular germinal zone fail to complete their normal migration to the cerebral cortex during fetal development. The malformation therefore specifically involves (and reflects disordered formation of) the cerebral cortex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alcoholic Beverage Labeling Act",
    "long_answer": "The Alcoholic Beverage Labeling Act of 1988 amended the Federal Alcohol Administration Act to mandate that all alcoholic beverage containers sold in the United States display a government health warning. The required label warns that consumption of alcohol during pregnancy can cause birth defects, thereby informing consumers of the risk of fetal alcohol spectrum disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radius",
    "long_answer": "In Renal dysplasia-limb defects (RDL) syndrome, the elbow anomaly is a synostosis between the distal humerus and the proximal radius—termed radiohumeral fusion (humeroradial synostosis). The forearm bone that participates in this fusion is the radius."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatitis C",
    "long_answer": "Chronic hepatitis C infection is a recognised extra-hepatic cause of insulin resistance and new-onset type 2 diabetes mellitus. It is also one of the systemic diseases most often linked to chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP), with numerous reports describing CIDP as a neurological manifestation of hepatitis C. Thus, hepatitis C both predisposes to type 2 diabetes and is associated with CIDP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "In the United States nitrous oxide is considered a drug under the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act. When it is supplied or promoted for human inhalation—such as for recreational ‘whippet’ use—the product becomes an unapproved, misbranded drug. The federal agency that enforces this statute and therefore regulates the distribution and use of nitrous oxide for recreational purposes is the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA); nitrous oxide is not scheduled by the DEA, so primary federal oversight falls to the FDA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parvovirus B19 infection",
    "long_answer": "Parvovirus B19 transiently halts erythropoiesis by infecting and lysing erythroid precursor cells in the bone marrow. Because patients with sickle cell disease already have a shortened red-cell lifespan, this brief cessation of red-cell production precipitates a profound, rapid fall in hemoglobin known as an aplastic crisis, making Parvovirus B19 infection a well-recognized cause of severe anemia in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "Left ventricular wall thickness is most accurately and routinely quantified with transthoracic echocardiography, an ultrasound-based imaging technique that provides real-time cross-sectional views of the heart and allows direct measurement of myocardial thickness in patients with left ventricular hypertrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "In the Spanish type of heart-hand syndrome the affected individuals were born to unaffected parents and several sibs were involved, often in consanguineous families. The equal sex distribution and vertical non-transmission point to a recessive mode of inheritance, so the syndrome is considered to follow an autosomal recessive pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Christ-Siemens-Touraine syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia is classically known as Christ-Siemens-Touraine syndrome, named after the physicians who first characterized the triad of sparse hair, missing or cone-shaped teeth, and reduced or absent sweating that define this inherited ectodermal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Upstream open reading frames (uORFs)",
    "long_answer": "Cytomegaloviruses place short upstream open reading frames in the 5′-untranslated regions of several viral mRNAs. Scanning ribosomes initiate at these uORFs during the initiation step of eukaryotic translation, which reduces or delays re-initiation at the downstream main start codon. This uORF-mediated control lets the virus modulate the efficiency and timing of translation of its proteins, thereby regulating viral gene expression during infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator",
    "long_answer": "In hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias (such as sustained ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation) can occur. Current cardiology guidelines recommend implanting an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) in patients who develop these dangerous irregular heartbeats or are at high risk for them. The ICD continuously monitors cardiac rhythm and automatically delivers pacing or a shock to terminate the arrhythmia, thereby preventing sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mounier-Kuhn syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mounier-Kuhn syndrome, also called congenital tracheobronchomegaly, is a rare congenital disorder in which the trachea and main bronchi are markedly dilated due to atrophy or absence of elastic and smooth-muscle fibers in their walls. The resulting abnormally enlarged airways impair mucociliary clearance, predisposing patients to frequent, recurrent lower respiratory tract infections and bronchiectasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin",
    "long_answer": "Persistent or troublesome eyelid myokymia is commonly treated with local injections of botulinum toxin type A. The toxin blocks presynaptic release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, temporarily weakening the orbicularis oculi muscle and abolishing the involuntary twitching for several months."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Beare-Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Beare-Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome is an autosomal dominant FGFR2-related craniosynostosis disorder distinguished by premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis) and thick, furrowed scalp skin (cutis gyrata). Reported patients frequently have associated airway anomalies, including choanal atresia or stenosis, making this syndrome the well-known genetic cause that links all three features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts",
    "long_answer": "The uterus arises from the paired paramesonephric, also called Müllerian, ducts during embryogenesis. These ducts fuse in the midline to form the uterine corpus, cervix, fallopian tubes, and upper vagina. Because malignant mixed Müllerian tumors originate in tissues derived from this Müllerian system, the paramesonephric ducts are the developmental source of the uterus in which such tumors can occur."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrology",
    "long_answer": "Fibromuscular dysplasia most often affects the renal arteries, producing renovascular hypertension and possible loss of renal function. Evaluation and management of these renal-artery lesions—including blood-pressure control, renal function monitoring, and decisions about angioplasty—are typically overseen by a nephrologist, the medical specialist in kidney and renovascular diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)",
    "long_answer": "Both dysexecutive syndrome, which reflects frontal-lobe executive dysfunction, and fetal alcohol spectrum disorder frequently co-occur with attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Frontal-striatal circuit abnormalities that underlie executive deficits predispose individuals with either condition to the inattention, impulsivity and hyperactivity that define ADHD, making it a well-documented shared comorbidity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Color Doppler ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Color Doppler ultrasound is an ultrasonographic technique that superimposes color-coded velocity information on standard gray-scale images, allowing real-time visualization of vascularity. By detecting and mapping blood flow within and around an ovarian cyst, it helps differentiate benign from potentially malignant lesions and assess cyst perfusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RECQL4",
    "long_answer": "Rothmund–Thomson syndrome type II is most commonly caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the RECQL4 helicase gene. Because RECQL4 is essential for DNA replication and repair in rapidly dividing cells, its deficiency can impair germ-cell development, and multiple RTS patients with RECQL4 mutations have been reported to develop primary gonadal failure and consequent hypogonadism. Thus, the RECQL4 mutation links both Rothmund–Thomson syndrome and hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Fraser syndrome (cryptophthalmos-syndactyly) is classically produced by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in genes such as FRAS1, FREM2 and GRIP1. Because two pathogenic alleles—one inherited from each carrier parent—are required for the phenotype to appear, the condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burning mouth syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The idiopathic condition known as Burning Mouth Syndrome (BMS) is defined by a chronic, persistent burning or scalding sensation of the tongue, lips, palate, or entire oral mucosa in the absence of any clinically identifiable oral lesion or systemic cause. Because no local or systemic pathology can be found to explain the pain, BMS is classified as an idiopathic (primary) or neuropathic pain disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dental fusion",
    "long_answer": "Dental fusion occurs when two adjacent tooth germs unite during development, producing a single, enlarged tooth with a deep developmental groove at the line of union. This groove tends to trap plaque and food debris, making the fused tooth significantly more susceptible to dental caries (cavities)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Post-exertional malaise",
    "long_answer": "The disease referred to is myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS), which the World Health Organization has placed in the neurological chapter of the International Classification of Diseases since 1969. The cardinal, hallmark symptom that defines ME/CFS is post-exertional malaise: a characteristic worsening of fatigue, pain, cognitive dysfunction and other symptoms after even minor physical or mental exertion, often with delayed onset and prolonged recovery. This symptom is required in current diagnostic criteria and is considered pathognomonic for the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycogen branching enzyme deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen-storage disease type IV (Andersen disease) results from absence of the glycogen branching enzyme (amylo-α-1,4→α-1,6-transglucosidase). Lack of this enzyme yields very poorly branched glycogen that accumulates in the liver, skeletal muscle, and heart. The branching enzyme participates solely in glycogen synthesis and has no role in the hepatic or renal hydroxylations that activate vitamin D; therefore its deficiency is unrelated to vitamin D metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leukocytoclastic vasculitis",
    "long_answer": "Cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis is diagnosed histologically by a pattern of leukocytoclastic vasculitis, characterized by neutrophilic infiltration of post-capillary venules with nuclear dust (leukocytoclasia), fibrinoid necrosis of the vessel wall, and extravasated red cells. This histologic picture is the defining finding for cutaneous small-vessel (immune-complex) vasculitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonic stenosis is most often a congenital narrowing of the pulmonary valve or right ventricular outflow tract. Evaluation and management—including echocardiography, catheter-based balloon valvuloplasty, and coordination of surgical repair—are performed by specialists in pediatric cardiology, the branch of cardiology dedicated to heart disorders in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Diseases characterized by absence or dysfunction of motile respiratory cilia, such as primary ciliary dyskinesia/Kartagener syndrome, are classically inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, requiring biallelic pathogenic variants in genes encoding axonemal proteins (e.g., DNAI1, DNAH5)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patent ductus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "Because the narrowed pulmonary valve limits right-ventricular outflow, persistence of the ductus arteriosus creates an alternative channel from the aorta into the pulmonary artery distal to the obstruction. Left-to-right flow through this patent ductus arteriosus supplies additional blood to the lungs, partially compensating for the reduced pulmonary perfusion caused by pulmonic stenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Exercise-induced anaphylaxis is a well-described clinical disorder in which vigorous physical activity triggers an anaphylactic reaction. Major medical references (e.g., UpToDate, World Allergy Organization guidelines, the Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center, Orphanet) list it as a distinct condition, and these alphabetical disease indexes place it under the letter “E” because the name begins with “exercise-induced.” Therefore, it is indeed considered a disease entity that appears in lists of disorders starting with the letter “E.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inhaled treprostinil",
    "long_answer": "The only pharmacologic agent with regulatory approval specifically for pulmonary hypertension secondary to interstitial lung disease (PH-ILD) is inhaled treprostinil, a prostacyclin analogue shown in clinical trials (e.g., INCREASE study) to improve 6-minute walk distance and reduce NT-proBNP in adults with PH-ILD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "A frequent neurological manifestation of tetrasomy 18p is recurrent epileptic seizures. Clinical reviews of individuals with the isochromosome 18p consistently report epilepsy as one of the most common nervous-system symptoms, reflecting the chromosomal imbalance’s impact on brain excitability and development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missense mutation",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis is most often produced by heterozygous missense mutations in genes that encode skeletal-muscle contractile proteins (e.g., MYH3, TNNI2, TNNT3, TPM2). These single–nucleotide substitutions change a critical amino-acid residue, disrupting sarcomere function and leading to the characteristic distal joint contractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bordetella pertussis",
    "long_answer": "Bordetella pertussis is the Gram-negative coccobacillus classically responsible for whooping cough (pertussis). In addition to producing the characteristic paroxysmal cough, B. pertussis can invade the lower respiratory tract, leading to bronchopneumonia or lobar bacterial pneumonia, particularly in infants and unvaccinated individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High-resolution CT",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) provides detailed cross-sectional images of lung parenchyma, allowing detection and serial comparison of nodules, reticulation, and fibrotic changes characteristic of pneumoconiosis. Because it is more sensitive than standard chest radiography, HRCT is routinely used to track disease progression over time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L1CAM",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the L1CAM gene cause the X-linked form of hereditary spastic paraplegia designated SPG1. The same gene is responsible for several other X-linked neurodevelopmental syndromes collectively termed the L1-syndrome spectrum, which includes MASA syndrome, X-linked hydrocephalus, and agenesis of the corpus callosum. Thus, L1CAM is the gene shared between hereditary spastic paraplegia and multiple other X-linked disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cannabis use disorder",
    "long_answer": "During withdrawal from chronic cannabis use (Cannabis use disorder), patients commonly experience marked irritability, anger or aggression together with vivid, unpleasant dreams and nightmares. These features are part of the DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for cannabis withdrawal, distinguishing it from withdrawal syndromes of other substances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) granted approval for tamoxifen to be used as a chemopreventive agent to lower the incidence of breast cancer in women at elevated risk, a category that explicitly includes carriers of BRCA2 pathogenic mutations. This approval is based on large clinical trials (e.g., NSABP P-1) demonstrating that tamoxifen’s selective estrogen-receptor modulation can cut breast-cancer risk by roughly 40–50 % in these high-risk populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Safe-in-Sound Award",
    "long_answer": "The Safe-in-Sound Excellence in Hearing Loss Prevention Award, created by NIOSH and partner organizations, honors companies and institutions that have implemented exemplary programs to prevent noise-induced hearing loss—one of the leading causes of acquired hearing impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ovarian carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration most classically accompanies ovarian carcinoma. Auto-antibodies such as anti-Yo (PCA-1) generated against the tumor cross-react with Purkinje cells in the cerebellum, leading to rapid-onset ataxia and cerebellar dysfunction despite the cancer often being clinically silent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kaori Fujimiya",
    "long_answer": "In the manga and anime series “One Week Friends,” the heroine Kaori Fujimiya exhibits classic anterograde amnesia: each Monday her brain fails to consolidate new episodic memories of friends made the previous week, while older long-term memories and procedural knowledge remain intact. This inability to form and retain new personal memories after a specific point is the hallmark of anterograde amnesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cushing syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cushing syndrome is the clinical state produced by chronically elevated cortisol levels. Excess glucocorticoids promote abnormal redistribution of adipose tissue—central obesity with thinning of limb and gluteal fat—often described as centripetal lipodystrophy. Thus, the cortisol-driven Cushing syndrome can manifest both hypercortisolism and lipodystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrolethalus syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hydrolethalus syndrome (also called Salonen-Herva syndrome) is an autosomal-recessive, perinatally lethal disorder whose cardinal findings include marked intra-uterine growth delay, severe hydrocephaly, and pulmonary (lung) hypoplasia, together with craniofacial anomalies and post-axial polydactyly. The combination of growth retardation, massive hydrocephalus and under-developed lungs is characteristic and diagnostic for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skewed X-inactivation",
    "long_answer": "X-linked myotubular myopathy is caused by pathogenic variants in the MTM1 gene on the X chromosome. Females are typically asymptomatic carriers because random X-chromosome inactivation leaves enough cells expressing the normal allele. If X-inactivation is markedly skewed so that the X chromosome bearing the normal MTM1 allele is preferentially silenced, most cells express the mutant allele, leading a heterozygous female to manifest the full disease phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM5",
    "long_answer": "Acral peeling skin syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TGM5 gene, which encodes epidermal transglutaminase-5. Defective TG-5 impairs cross-linking of structural proteins in the cornified envelope, making the stratum corneum fragile and leading to characteristic peeling of acral (palms and soles) skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEN1",
    "long_answer": "Glucagonoma often occurs as part of the Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1 syndrome. This syndrome results from germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the MEN1 gene on chromosome 11q13, which encodes the tumor-suppressor protein menin. Individuals carrying MEN1 mutations have a substantially increased risk of developing pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors, including glucagonoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction in which antibodies target postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors. The reduced number of functional receptors causes transmission failure that becomes more pronounced with repeated stimulation, so patients develop fatigable muscle weakness that appears or worsens after exercise and improves with rest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek: eso- (inward) + tropia (turn)",
    "long_answer": "The medical term for inward-turning eyes is “esotropia,” derived from the Greek prefix “eso-,” meaning “within” or “inward,” and the Greek noun “tropē/-tropia,” meaning “turn” or “turning.” Thus, the word literally denotes an inward turn of the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenobarbital",
    "long_answer": "Phenobarbital is a long-acting barbiturate (elimination half-life ≈ 2–5 days) routinely used in detoxification protocols for barbiturate dependence. Its prolonged action provides stable plasma levels, allowing clinicians to substitute it for the shorter-acting abused barbiturate and then taper the dose gradually, thereby preventing withdrawal seizures and other abstinence symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jejunum",
    "long_answer": "Waldmann disease (primary intestinal lymphangiectasia) is characterized by marked dilatation of the intestinal lymphatic vessels, most prominently in the mucosa and submucosa of the jejunum, leading to protein-losing enteropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candida albicans",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies of candidemia and invasive candidiasis consistently show that Candida albicans accounts for the largest proportion of bloodstream and deep-seated systemic Candida infections worldwide. Although non-albicans species such as C. glabrata, C. parapsilosis, and C. tropicalis have increased in certain settings, C. albicans remains the predominant pathogen in most patient populations and is therefore considered responsible for the majority of systemic Candida infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Deafness-vitiligo-achalasia syndrome is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations (e.g., in the neural-crest transcription factor SOX10). Because a single mutant allele is sufficient to cause the phenotype, the condition is transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion, although penetrance can be variable."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GBE1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Andersen’s Disease (glycogen storage disease type IV) is caused by autosomal-recessive mutations in the GBE1 gene, which encodes the glycogen-branching enzyme. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to accumulation of abnormally structured glycogen in hepatocytes, driving progressive hepatic fibrosis and culminating in cirrhosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anal atresia",
    "long_answer": "Anal atresia (imperforate anus/anorectal malformation) is a defining component of VACTERL association and is also one element of the Currarino triad, making it the birth defect most characteristically shared by both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fungal infection",
    "long_answer": "Kerion is a severe inflammatory form of tinea capitis that arises when a dermatophyte (fungal) infection invades the hair follicle, provoking a marked immune response with boggy swelling, pustules, and crusting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Audiologist",
    "long_answer": "Stickler syndrome commonly causes sensorineural hearing loss because the collagen defects affect structures of the middle and inner ear. Assessment of the degree of loss, ongoing monitoring, and fitting of hearing aids are best handled by an audiologist—a clinician specialized in diagnosing and managing hearing problems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type III hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "In the Gell-Coombs classification, type III hypersensitivity reactions are driven by immune complexes—aggregates of antigen bound to IgG or IgM antibodies—that deposit in tissues or vessel walls. Complement activation and recruitment of neutrophils at these sites produce inflammation and tissue damage, exemplified by serum sickness, Arthus reaction, and some forms of vasculitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Custom orthotic shoe inserts",
    "long_answer": "Tarsal tunnel syndrome often worsens when excessive foot pronation or fallen arches increase pressure on the posterior tibial nerve. Custom orthotic shoe inserts (foot orthoses) support the medial arch, correct abnormal biomechanics, and redistribute plantar pressures, thereby reducing nerve compression within the tarsal tunnel and relieving pain and paresthesias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CECR2",
    "long_answer": "Cat eye syndrome is produced by a partial trisomy/tetrasomy of the proximal 22q11 region created by a supernumerary marker chromosome. Within this duplicated segment lies the gene CECR2 (Cat-Eye-Syndrome Chromosome Region, candidate 2) on chromosome 22q11.21. Because CECR2 dosage is increased in the marker chromosome and its disruption in animal models reproduces key CES features such as ocular coloboma, it is considered the principal gene associated with Cat eye syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWNK (Twinkle)",
    "long_answer": "Chronic Progressive External Ophthalmoplegia often results from mutations in the TWNK gene, which encodes the Twinkle mitochondrial DNA helicase. Twinkle is a key component of the mitochondrial replisome, unwinding mtDNA strands so that polymerase γ can synthesize new DNA. Pathogenic TWNK mutations impair mtDNA replication and maintenance, leading to multiple mtDNA deletions that characterize CPEO."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibrin",
    "long_answer": "Fibrin is the insoluble protein that forms the meshwork of a blood clot. It is generated when the enzyme thrombin cleaves circulating fibrinogen into fibrin monomers that polymerise into strands. In Haemophilia B, deficiency of clotting factor IX disrupts the cascade that normally produces thrombin, so adequate fibrin cannot be produced. Restoring the cascade (e.g., with factor IX concentrates) is aimed at enabling thrombin-mediated conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, making fibrin the critical end product for effective clotting in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Foramen magnum stenosis",
    "long_answer": "In achondroplasia, defective endochondral bone growth narrows the skull base, especially the foramen magnum. This stenosis can compress the cervicomedullary junction, leading to brain-stem dysfunction, central apnea and even sudden death, making it one of the most serious complications in affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Latex allergy",
    "long_answer": "People with spina bifida are frequently exposed to latex-containing medical devices from infancy (e.g., during repeated surgeries and catheterizations), predisposing them to IgE-mediated sensitization. Consequently, they carry a markedly increased risk of intra-operative latex allergy, which can manifest as severe anaphylactic reactions during surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Segmented negative-sense RNA",
    "long_answer": "Influenza A virus belongs to the Orthomyxoviridae family and contains eight separate segments of negative-sense, single-stranded RNA. This segmented, antisense RNA genome must be transcribed by the viral RNA-dependent RNA polymerase into positive-sense mRNA before host ribosomes can translate viral proteins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto thyroiditis",
    "long_answer": "In iodine-sufficient, developed countries, chronic autoimmune destruction of the thyroid—known as Hashimoto (autoimmune) thyroiditis—is the leading cause of primary hypothyroidism. Lymphocytic infiltration with anti-thyroid peroxidase and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies gradually destroys thyroid tissue, reducing hormone production and making it the most common etiology in these settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral vasculitis",
    "long_answer": "Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy (APMPPE) can be associated with cerebral vasculitis, an inflammatory vasculopathy of the central nervous system. This vascular inflammation may compromise cerebral blood flow, leading to neurological complications such as headaches, focal neurological deficits, or ischemic strokes, making cerebral vasculitis the key neurological condition linked to APMPPE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Russell-Silver syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Russell-Silver syndrome (Silver-Russell syndrome) is a genetic growth disorder marked by prenatal and post-natal proportionate short stature. A characteristic physical sign is fifth-finger clinodactyly produced by hypoplasia of the middle phalanx. A significant number of affected children also show delayed speech and language development, distinguishing the syndrome from disproportional skeletal dysplasias. These core features establish Russell-Silver syndrome as the condition fitting the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Guillain-Barré syndrome is an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy in which autoimmune inflammation targets peripheral nerve myelin. This immune-mediated attack disrupts nerve conduction, producing the classic rapidly progressive peripheral neuropathy characterized by weakness, sensory loss, and areflexia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Malignant rhabdoid tumours almost invariably show biallelic deletion or mutation of the SMARCB1 (INI1/SNF5) tumour-suppressor gene, which is located at 22q11.2. Consequently, cytogenetic studies frequently reveal monosomy or other abnormalities of chromosome 22 in these tumours, making chromosome 22 the chromosome most characteristically involved in their genetic pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TREX1",
    "long_answer": "Aicardi-Goutières syndrome can be caused by recessive or dominant loss-of-function mutations in the TREX1 gene, located on chromosome 3p21.31. TREX1 encodes the major 3′→5′ DNA exonuclease; when it is defective, excess cytosolic DNA accumulates, activating the type I interferon pathway and producing the encephalopathy and systemic inflammation characteristic of Aicardi-Goutières syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudolymphomatous cystitis",
    "long_answer": "Pseudolymphomatous (follicular) cystitis is a benign reactive lesion of the urinary bladder characterised by dense lymphoid follicle formation. It typically develops in the setting of chronic or recurrent cystitis and has been documented in several patients with a prior or concurrent malignant lymphoma, leading to its recognised association with a history of lymphoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thumb carpometacarpal joint",
    "long_answer": "In arthrogryposis a common deformity is thumb-in-palm caused by contracture of the first web space, limiting abduction at the thumb carpometacarpal (CMC) joint. The index rotation flap is a soft-tissue procedure that rotates a flap from the radial side of the index finger into the first web, lengthening the web space and freeing the thumb. By restoring web-space depth it directly addresses the mobility of the thumb CMC joint, allowing normal abduction and opposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The characteristic and diagnostically defining cytogenetic abnormality of synovial sarcoma is the reciprocal translocation t(X;18)(p11.2;q11.2), which creates the SS18-SSX1/SSX2/SSX4 fusion gene. This translocation involves chromosomes X and 18, not chromosome 7. Recurrent translocations involving chromosome 7 are not part of the established molecular profile of synovial sarcoma. Therefore, a chromosomal translocation of chromosome 7 is not considered to be associated with synovial sarcoma according to current biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GAIN",
    "long_answer": "Guillain-Barré & Associated Inflammatory Neuropathies (GAIN) is the recognized patient-advocacy charity that offers ongoing, specialist community support, information and networking for people with Multifocal Motor Neuropathy (as well as GBS and CIDP) across countries of the European Union."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giant cell tumor",
    "long_answer": "Giant cell tumor of bone (also called osteoclastoma) is a locally aggressive, benign primary bone tumor that commonly erodes cortical bone in the epiphysis of long bones. The cortical thinning and lytic ‘soap-bubble’ lesions weaken the bone, so patients frequently present after a minimal-trauma or spontaneous pathologic fracture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolonged endotracheal intubation",
    "long_answer": "Extended cuffed endotracheal intubation can exert sustained pressure on the tracheal wall, producing ischemic necrosis of the mucosa and underlying cartilaginous rings. Loss of this cartilaginous support weakens the airway, leading to acquired tracheobronchomalacia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Hypochondrogenesis is a lethal chondrodysplasia caused by mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes type II collagen. Type II collagen is the principal collagen of hyaline cartilage, and defects in COL2A1 impair the formation and stability of the cartilage matrix needed for normal endochondral ossification. The disrupted collagen network prevents cartilage from being properly replaced by bone, explaining the profound ossification defects characteristic of hypochondrogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Morphodifferentiation stage",
    "long_answer": "Zadik–Barak–Levin syndrome is characterized by dental anomalies such as generalized micro- and rudimentary teeth. These abnormalities arise when the size and shape of the tooth germ are being established, indicating a disturbance in the morphodifferentiation stage of odontogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stomach",
    "long_answer": "Anisakiasis is caused by ingestion of Anisakis larvae from raw or under-cooked seafood. After ingestion, the larvae typically penetrate and inflame the gastric mucosa, producing acute gastric pain and other symptoms; thus, the primary human organ affected is the stomach."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hodgkin lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Reed–Sternberg cells—large, bilobed or multinucleated B-cell–derived cells with prominent nucleoli—are the diagnostic hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma (formerly Hodgkin’s disease). Their presence in affected lymph nodes distinguishes Hodgkin lymphoma from non-Hodgkin lymphomas and underpins its pathological classification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plasmapheresis",
    "long_answer": "In cramp-fasciculation syndrome, therapeutic plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) can be used: a patient’s blood is withdrawn, the plasma component is separated and treated or replaced, and the cellular elements are then returned to the circulation. This procedure can remove circulating autoantibodies thought to contribute to the syndrome’s neuromuscular hyperexcitability, providing symptomatic relief in selected cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low molecular weight heparin",
    "long_answer": "Low-molecular-weight heparins (e.g., enoxaparin, dalteparin) are the standard anticoagulants given by subcutaneous injection to treat deep vein thrombosis. Their predictable anti-factor Xa activity allows weight-based dosing without routine laboratory monitoring, making them the preferred subcutaneous therapy over unfractionated heparin or oral agents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Sea-blue histiocytosis is a rare lipid storage disorder characterized by macrophages with blue-green (\"sea-blue\") cytoplasmic granules found on bone-marrow aspirate; patients often present with splenomegaly, cytopenias, and other marrow-related problems. Because diagnosis hinges on bone-marrow evaluation and management centers on treating the accompanying hematologic abnormalities or underlying hematologic diseases, the condition is primarily handled by the medical specialty of Hematology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-thalassemia X-linked intellectual disability syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATRX gene cause Alpha-thalassemia X-linked intellectual disability (ATR-X) syndrome. A characteristic clinical triad is intellectual disability, α-thalassemia, and distinctive craniofacial features—among them marked hypertelorism (widely spaced eyes). The link between ATRX mutations and this dysmorphic feature is well documented in genetic and clinical studies of ATR-X syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bactericidal action on oral bacteria",
    "long_answer": "Iodine is rapidly converted in saliva to free iodine (I₂), which penetrates and disrupts the cell walls and metabolic enzymes of cariogenic organisms such as Streptococcus mutans. By killing or severely inhibiting these acid-producing bacteria, iodine lowers plaque acid production and thus helps prevent the demineralization that leads to dental caries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Short rib–polydactyly syndrome is an inherited skeletal dysplasia. The medical specialty that focuses on diagnosing, counseling, and managing such hereditary disorders is Medical genetics, which addresses the molecular and clinical aspects of genetic diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LH and FSH",
    "long_answer": "Sheehan’s syndrome causes anterior pituitary failure, reducing secretion of multiple pituitary hormones. The two anterior-pituitary hormones that regulate gonadal function—luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)—are therefore deficient, fulfilling the definition of lost gonadotropins in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scapula",
    "long_answer": "Sprengel's deformity is a congenital condition characterized by an abnormally elevated and mal-rotated scapula (shoulder blade). The scapula fails to descend to its normal thoracic position during embryonic development, leading to its high placement on the back and associated cosmetic and functional impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burosumab",
    "long_answer": "ENPP1 deficiency in GACI survivors often causes FGF23-mediated hypophosphatemic rickets (ARHR2), which weakens bones. Burosumab is a human monoclonal antibody that binds and inhibits excess FGF23, raising serum phosphate and improving bone mineralization. Case reports and small series have shown that burosumab corrects hypophosphatemia and heals rickets in patients with ENPP1-related ARHR2, making it a logical therapeutic option for the bone-weakening condition seen in these GACI patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3p deletion syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Loss of genetic material from the short (p) arm of chromosome 3 produces 3p deletion syndrome (also called monosomy 3p). Individuals with this chromosomal deletion commonly present with global developmental delay and intellectual disability, along with characteristic craniofacial features and growth retardation. The neuro-cognitive impairment arises because dosage-sensitive genes located on 3p—such as CHL1 and CNTN4—are haploinsufficient after the deletion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial septal defect",
    "long_answer": "A fixed (i.e., respiration-independent) splitting of the second heart sound occurs classically in an atrial septal defect: the left-to-right shunt keeps right ventricular stroke volume chronically increased, prolonging right ventricular systole and delaying pulmonic valve closure on every beat, so the P2 component of S2 remains widely and consistently separated from A2 throughout the respiratory cycle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious disease specialist",
    "long_answer": "Septicemic plague is a life-threatening systemic infection caused by Yersinia pestis. Optimal care involves rapid microbiologic diagnosis, selection of appropriate intravenous antibiotics (e.g., aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones), and coordination with public-health authorities—tasks that fall squarely within the expertise of an infectious disease specialist. Consequently, this type of physician is routinely consulted to diagnose and treat septicemic plague."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alkylating agents",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is a DNA-repair disorder (defective nibrin/NBN) that renders cells exquisitely sensitive to double-strand DNA breaks. Chemotherapeutic alkylating agents (e.g., cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, busulfan) produce these breaks and therefore cause extreme toxicity in NBS patients. For this reason, alkylating agents should be avoided when treating malignancies in individuals with Nijmegen breakage syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-Hydroxytryptophan",
    "long_answer": "In dihydropteridine reductase deficiency, lack of tetrahydrobiopterin blocks tryptophan hydroxylase, preventing synthesis of serotonin. Therapeutic supplementation with the immediate serotonin precursor 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP) bypasses this enzymatic step and restores central serotonin levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "H3K27me3",
    "long_answer": "Cohen-Gibson syndrome arises from de novo missense mutations in EED, a core component of the Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 that catalyzes trimethylation of lysine 27 on histone H3 (H3K27me3). Loss or reduction of this specific histone mark underlies the epigenetic dysregulation observed in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "MERRF (Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged-Red Fibers) is a mitochondrial encephalomyopathy caused by pathogenic mutations in mitochondrial DNA—most commonly the MT-TK gene encoding mitochondrial tRNALys. These mutations impair oxidative phosphorylation, leading to defective energy production in mitochondria, the organelles responsible for ATP generation. Thus, mitochondrial dysfunction is the primary cellular defect underlying MERRF syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketosis",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), hepatic glycogen phosphorylase deficiency limits glucose release from liver glycogen. During fasting, falling blood glucose triggers lipolysis and fatty-acid oxidation; the resultant excess acetyl-CoA is converted to ketone bodies. Consequently, patients develop a ketotic metabolic state (ketotic hypoglycaemia) when fasted."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Mutations that impair mitochondrial oxidative-phosphorylation reduce energy supply to developing skeletal muscle and motor neurons, causing fetal akinesia; the lack of in-utero movement leads to multiple congenital joint contractures known as arthrogryposis. Numerous cases of arthrogryposis have been linked to defects in mitochondrial DNA or nuclear genes encoding mitochondrial proteins, so dysfunction of the mitochondria is a well-established pathogenic mechanism in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prostacyclin (PGI₂)",
    "long_answer": "Prostacyclin, also called PGI₂, is released from vascular endothelial cells and binds to platelet IP receptors. This activates adenylate-cyclase, raising intraplatelet cAMP. The cAMP-PKA pathway drives Ca²⁺ sequestration and limits Ca²⁺ influx, lowering cytosolic Ca²⁺ levels and thereby preventing platelet activation and aggregation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery",
    "long_answer": "Adenomatoid odontogenic tumors are benign epithelial tumors that arise in the tooth-bearing areas of the jaws. Definitive management is almost always conservative surgical enucleation or curettage. Surgical treatment of jaw and oral cavity lesions falls under the scope of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery, the specialty specifically trained to remove odontogenic tumors and reconstruct jaw defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive-behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "A recent meta-analysis pooling randomized and quasi-experimental studies of psychological interventions for death anxiety reported that cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) produced the largest and statistically significant reductions in death-anxiety scores compared with control conditions. CBT’s combination of cognitive restructuring (challenging catastrophic thoughts about death) and exposure or behavioral techniques directly targets the maladaptive cognitions and avoidance that maintain death anxiety, leading to measurable decreases in fear-of-death across diverse populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1, underlie Marfan syndrome. Defective fibrillin-1 weakens the ciliary zonular fibers that suspend the crystalline lens, predisposing affected individuals to ectopia lentis (lens dislocation) and making FBN1 mutation a well-established genetic cause of increased lens dislocation risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Onge",
    "long_answer": "The Onge, one of the indigenous peoples of Little Andaman Island, numbered several hundred in the late-19th century but fell to fewer than 100 by the late-20th century. The sharp decline was largely driven by introduced infectious diseases—especially measles and influenza—to which they had no prior immunity, leading to repeated epidemics and high mortality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ergonovine provocation test",
    "long_answer": "The definitive way to diagnose variant (Prinzmetal) angina is to demonstrate reversible coronary artery spasm. During coronary angiography, intracoronary or intravenous ergonovine is administered to provoke spasm; reproduction of chest pain with transient ST-segment elevation and angiographic narrowing that resolves with nitrates confirms the diagnosis. Therefore, coronary angiography with an ergonovine provocation test is considered the gold-standard diagnostic test."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypophosphatasia",
    "long_answer": "Hypophosphatasia is an inherited deficiency of tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (ALPL gene). The low alkaline-phosphatase activity impairs bone mineralization, producing rickets/osteomalacia, and affected infants commonly present with generalized muscle weakness and hypotonia. Thus this genetic disorder links hypotonia with the development of rickets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cherry-red macula",
    "long_answer": "Farber disease (Farber lipogranulomatosis) is a lysosomal storage disorder caused by acid ceramidase deficiency. Accumulation of sphingolipids in retinal ganglion cells leads to perifoveal retinal pallor with preservation of the foveal choroid, producing the characteristic cherry-red spot (cherry-red macula) observed on fundus examination."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Conjunctiva",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is characterized by multiple lentigines—small, dark, spot-like pigmentations. These lesions most often appear on periorificial skin, especially the eyelids, lips, and mucous membranes. Within the eye, the conjunctiva (the mucous membrane covering the sclera and inner eyelids) is a classic and frequently reported site of this spotty pigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle weakness",
    "long_answer": "A hallmark of myositis is proximal skeletal-muscle weakness. In juvenile dermatomyositis, chronic muscle inflammation can persist even after skin findings improve, leaving many patients with lasting muscle weakness. Thus, persistent muscle weakness is both a common long-term consequence of juvenile dermatomyositis and a defining symptom across the myositis spectrum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valoctocogene roxaparvovec",
    "long_answer": "Valoctocogene roxaparvovec (brand name Roctavian) is an adeno-associated virus serotype 5 (AAV5) gene therapy that delivers a B-domain-deleted human Factor VIII transgene to hepatocytes, enabling endogenous production of functional FVIII and thereby treating Haemophilia A. It received regulatory approval in 2022-2023 as the first licensed gene therapy for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIS1 (PAFAH1B1)",
    "long_answer": "Classic (type-1) lissencephaly and Miller-Dieker syndrome are most commonly caused by deletions or loss-of-function mutations of the LIS1 gene, formally named PAFAH1B1, which resides on chromosome 17p13.3. Disruption of LIS1 impairs neuronal migration during corticogenesis, producing the characteristic ‘smooth brain’ (lissencephaly) malformation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCA12",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ABCA12 gene cause Harlequin ichthyosis, the most severe, often lethal congenital form of ichthyosis. ABCA12 encodes a lipid transporter critical for moving glucosylceramides into lamellar granules of keratinocytes; without it, the skin barrier fails, producing the characteristic thick, plate-like scales and life-threatening complications seen in this extreme phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia results from a deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT). Without adequate GALT activity, galactose-1-phosphate cannot be converted to UDP-galactose, leading to accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate and toxic metabolites, which underlies the metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fournier's gangrene",
    "long_answer": "A very rare but serious adverse effect reported with SGLT2 inhibitor therapy is necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum, called Fournier's gangrene. While its overall incidence is extremely low, epidemiologic data show it occurs disproportionately in people with diabetes, and several post-marketing cases have been linked to canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, and empagliflozin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cheilitis granulomatosa",
    "long_answer": "Cheilitis granulomatosa (also called Miescher’s cheilitis) is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the lips in which non-caseating granulomas obstruct lymphatic drainage, leading to persistent edematous swelling. The resulting lip inflammation is therefore commonly accompanied by edema—abnormal fluid retention within the surrounding tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pacman dysplasia is a lethal skeletal dysplasia that has been documented to recur in siblings born to phenotypically normal, non-affected parents. This pattern of vertical transmission, coupled with reports from consanguineous families, establishes it as an autosomal recessive genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L-tryptophan",
    "long_answer": "The 1989 outbreak of eosinophilia–myalgia syndrome (EMS)—characterised by marked peripheral eosinophilia and incapacitating muscle pain—was epidemiologically traced to ingestion of over-the-counter L-tryptophan dietary supplements. Investigation by the CDC and FDA showed that virtually all affected patients had consumed L-tryptophan produced by a single manufacturer (Showa Denko), and chemical analysis revealed unique contaminants (e.g., 1,1'-ethylidenebis[tryptophan]) formed during its production. Consequently, L-tryptophan supplementation was temporarily withdrawn from the U.S. market, establishing this amino-acid supplement as the causal factor in EMS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "In the first description of Sonoda syndrome in a Japanese family, the index case (proband) was noted to have a congenital ventricular septal defect—an abnormal opening in the wall separating the right and left ventricles—which constituted the cardiac anomaly associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophil elastase",
    "long_answer": "In acquired cutis laxa, dermal inflammation rich in neutrophils is often seen. Neutrophils release the serine protease neutrophil elastase (also called leukocyte elastase), an enzyme capable of cleaving elastin and associated microfibrillar proteins. The prevailing hypothesis is that uncontrolled activity of neutrophil elastase during cutaneous inflammation degrades elastic fibers, leading to the lax, redundant skin characteristic of acquired cutis laxa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glutamate",
    "long_answer": "Dicarboxylic aminoaciduria results from loss-of-function mutations in the SLC1A1 gene that encodes the neuronal/renal transporter EAAT3 (also called EAAC1). EAAT3 is a high-affinity carrier for the dicarboxylic amino acids glutamate and aspartate. When this transporter is defective, re-uptake of the excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate is markedly reduced, leading to impaired glutamatergic synaptic transmission as well as excessive urinary loss of glutamate and related amino acids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fasciculations",
    "long_answer": "Both Hirayama disease (juvenile muscular atrophy of the distal upper limb) and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) commonly show muscle fasciculations—visible, involuntary twitches of muscle fibers—reflecting lower motor-neuron involvement in each disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ERCC2 gene, which encodes the DNA-repair helicase XPD (a core subunit of the TFIIH complex), can give rise to several nucleotide-excision-repair disorders. Depending on the nature of the mutation, ERCC2 defects cause xeroderma pigmentosum (XP), Cockayne syndrome (CS), or the combined XP-CS phenotype. Thus ERCC2 (XPD) is the gene whose mutation is associated with both xeroderma pigmentosum and Cockayne syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dandy-Walker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Dandy-Walker syndrome is a congenital malformation of the posterior fossa that includes cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle, agenesis or hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis, and enlargement of the posterior fossa. The majority of affected infants develop obstructive hydrocephalus, and the resulting raised intracranial pressure often leads to poor feeding, persistent vomiting, and inadequate weight gain. Consequently, failure to thrive is a well-recognized clinical feature accompanying the hydrocephalus in Dandy-Walker syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Photopsia",
    "long_answer": "Photopsia – brief flashes or flickering lights produced by retinal or vitreous traction – can resemble the shimmering lights of a migraine aura. Patients with eye disease may therefore mistake these peripheral light flashes for an aura, although photopsia usually reflects retinal pathology rather than cortical spreading depression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Indium-111",
    "long_answer": "Neuroendocrine tumors are commonly imaged with somatostatin-receptor scintigraphy (OctreoScan), in which the peptide octreotide is labeled with the γ-emitting isotope Indium-111 (¹¹¹In). The radiolabeled octreotide binds to somatostatin receptors over-expressed on neuroendocrine tumor cells, allowing sensitive nuclear medicine detection and staging of these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAI1",
    "long_answer": "Both Potocki–Lupski syndrome and Smith–Magenis syndrome map to the 17p11.2 chromosomal region. Duplication of this segment (gene over-dosage) causes Potocki–Lupski syndrome, whereas deletion of the same segment (gene haploinsufficiency) results in Smith–Magenis syndrome. Functional and molecular studies pinpoint the dosage-sensitive gene RAI1 (retinoic acid-induced 1) within this interval as the major driver of the clinical features in both disorders, making RAI1 the gene responsible for the contrasting phenotypes depending on copy number." }

{
    "short_answer": "BRAF V600E",
    "long_answer": "The activating V600E point mutation in the BRAF proto-oncogene is found in most cases of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and has also been reported as a driver lesion in a subset of clonal (neoplastic) hypereosinophilic syndromes/chronic eosinophilic leukaemia. In both disorders the BRAF V600E mutation constitutively activates the MAP-kinase pathway, accounting for their shared molecular pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SFRP4",
    "long_answer": "Pyle disease is now known to result from loss-of-function mutations in the SFRP4 gene, which encodes a secreted antagonist of Wnt signaling. SFRP4 is critical for normal bone modeling and craniofacial development; when mutated, the altered Wnt signaling can disturb ossification and pneumatization processes, potentially impairing formation of the paranasal sinuses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Circle of Willis",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is strongly associated with the development of saccular (berry) aneurysms in the Circle of Willis, the arterial ring at the base of the brain. Defects in vascular wall integrity due to PKD-related gene mutations predispose these cerebral arteries to aneurysm formation, placing this structure at particular risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin)",
    "long_answer": "The lipopolysaccharide (LPS) endotoxin present in the outer cell wall of Gram-negative bacteria within dental plaque is the principal trigger that activates host immune and inflammatory pathways. Recognition of LPS by immune cells (via TLR-4, CD14, etc.) induces cytokine release and initiates the gingival inflammatory response characteristic of gingivitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal (orbitofrontal) lobe",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of organic personality disorder arise after structural damage to the frontal lobes—especially the orbitofrontal cortex. Lesions, trauma, tumors or degenerative processes in this region disrupt executive control, emotional regulation and social behaviour, producing the characteristic disinhibition, irritability and personality change that define the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PORCN gene",
    "long_answer": "Focal dermal hypoplasia (Goltz syndrome) is an X-linked dominant disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PORCN gene located on Xp11.23 of the X chromosome. PORCN encodes a membrane-bound O-acyltransferase essential for Wnt ligand processing; its disruption leads to the characteristic skin, skeletal, and ocular anomalies seen in focal dermal hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Zori–Stalker–Williams syndrome is inherited in an X-linked manner; the causative mutation resides on the X chromosome, making this sex chromosome the one involved in transmission of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epithelial-myoepithelial carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "The salivary gland tumour that mirrors the biphasic appearance of breast adenomyoepithelioma is epithelial-myoepithelial carcinoma. Occurring mainly in the parotid gland, it shows ducts lined by an inner layer of epithelial cells and an outer layer of clear myoepithelial cells—the same dual-cell architecture characteristic of adenomyoepithelioma of the breast."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Achondroplasia",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia is the most common skeletal dysplasia producing disproportionate short-limb dwarfism. The abnormal endochondral bone growth not only shortens the long bones but also alters spinal vertebrae alignment, predisposing patients to spinal curvature abnormalities such as thoracolumbar kyphosis and scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Feline herpesvirus type 1",
    "long_answer": "Although most cases of feline eosinophilic granuloma complex are triggered by allergic hypersensitivity, several studies have detected feline herpesvirus type 1 (FHV-1) antigen or DNA within affected skin and oral lesions. This association indicates that FHV-1 infection can act as an initiating or exacerbating stimulus for the immune-mediated eosinophilic response seen in the granuloma complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the heart valves and endocardial surface; diagnosis, echocardiographic assessment, management of valvular complications, and coordination of surgical intervention all fall under the expertise of cardiology, making cardiologists the primary specialists responsible for these cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AHDC1",
    "long_answer": "Recent exome-sequencing studies in patients who meet the clinical diagnostic triad of Gómez–López-Hernández (cerebellar–trigeminal–dermal dysplasia) have revealed de-novo, loss-of-function mutations in the AHDC1 gene (AT-hook DNA-binding motif-containing 1). These variants disrupt AHDC1’s role in neurodevelopment and are the first and so far only gene mutations to be consistently implicated in the aetiology of Gómez–López-Hernández syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma",
    "long_answer": "Angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma (AITL) is classified as a systemic peripheral T-cell lymphoma that typically presents with generalized lymphadenopathy, constitutional symptoms and immune dysregulation. In a high proportion of cases, the background B-cell immunoblasts are infected by Epstein-Barr virus, making EBV involvement a characteristic feature of this lymphoma subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACSL4",
    "long_answer": "ACSL4 (acyl-CoA synthetase long-chain family member 4) is an X-chromosomal gene whose loss-of-function mutations cause non-syndromic X-linked intellectual disability. The encoded enzyme activates long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids by converting them to acyl-CoA thioesters, a key first step in fatty acid metabolism and phospholipid remodeling. Thus, ACSL4 links defective fatty-acid processing to the cognitive impairment seen in affected males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Autistic individuals may adopt extremely restricted diets, predisposing them to vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency. Cobalamin is required for myelin maintenance and axonal metabolism in the optic nerve; prolonged deficiency can therefore produce a bilateral, painless nutritional optic neuropathy (sometimes called nutritional optic atrophy). Case reports describe autistic patients with limited food repertoires developing vitamin B12 deficiency–related optic nerve damage, confirming the causal link."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1",
    "long_answer": "Patients with the autosomal-dominant genetic disorder Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) have a markedly higher incidence of intracranial vasculopathies, particularly stenosis and occlusion of the distal internal carotid and proximal cerebral arteries, predisposing them to the progressive cerebrovascular syndrome known as moyamoya disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "High endothelial venules",
    "long_answer": "Angioimmunoblastic T-cell lymphoma shows a polymorphous lymph-node infiltrate rich in plump, cuboidal endothelial cells that form an expanded network of high endothelial venules; these specialized post-capillary venule endothelial cells are a hallmark of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMAD4",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous gain-of-function missense mutations in the SMAD4 gene have been shown to cause classic Myhre syndrome. Subsequent genetic analyses of patients diagnosed with Laryngotracheal stenosis–Arthropathy–Prognathism–Short stature (LAPS) syndrome revealed the same recurrent SMAD4 mutations, establishing that both conditions result from pathogenic variants in SMAD4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fetal echocardiography",
    "long_answer": "A targeted fetal echocardiography—high-resolution ultrasound imaging of the fetal heart—allows visualization of the size and function of the right ventricle, tricuspid valve, and pulmonary outflow tract. This prenatal cardiac scan can demonstrate the characteristic under-development of right-sided structures, thereby confirming hypoplastic right heart syndrome before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tricho-dento-osseous syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the homeobox gene DLX3 cause tricho-dento-osseous syndrome, an autosomal-dominant disorder characterized by hypoplastic amelogenesis imperfecta (enamel hypoplasia), taurodontism of molars, increased bone density, and curly or kinky hair. The DLX3 mutation disrupts normal epithelial–mesenchymal signaling during tooth development, leading to the enamel defects that typify the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "McArdle’s disease (glycogen-storage disease type V) is caused by an inherited deficiency of the skeletal-muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, also called myophosphorylase. Because glycogen cannot be broken down during exercise, ATP generation from glycolysis falls, intracellular AMP rises, and AMP deaminase converts the excess AMP to inosine monophosphate (IMP). Thus, lack of muscle glycogen phosphorylase is the enzyme deficiency that leads to the characteristic overproduction of IMP during exercise in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 8",
    "long_answer": "The single most frequent cytogenetic change in T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia is gain of an entire copy of chromosome 8 (+8, trisomy 8), detected in well over half of cases. The same +8 abnormality—often reflecting gain of the 8q24 region harbouring MYC—is also a recurrent finding in Sézary syndrome, linking the two T-cell malignancies cytogenetically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PDGF-A",
    "long_answer": "In desmoplastic small-round-cell tumour, the EWS-WT1 fusion protein functions as an aberrant transcription factor that directly activates the platelet-derived growth factor-A (PDGF-A) gene. Over-expression of PDGF-A drives autocrine PDGF signalling, and this pathway can be therapeutically inhibited with PDGFR-blocking agents such as imatinib or other tyrosine-kinase inhibitors that are being evaluated in clinical trials. Thus, PDGF-A is the key protein target induced by the EWS/WT1 translocation product and provides a tractable target for trial-based therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Avoiding fatty foods",
    "long_answer": "High-fat meals decrease lower esophageal sphincter tone and slow gastric emptying, increasing the likelihood of acid reflux. Clinical GERD guidelines therefore advise limiting or eliminating fatty foods (e.g., fried foods, full-fat dairy, fatty meats) as a primary dietary modification to reduce heartburn and regurgitation symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease is an inherited lysosomal storage disorder (glycogen-storage disease type II) caused by pathogenic variants in the GAA gene; its diagnosis relies on enzymatic and genetic testing and long-term management involves enzyme-replacement therapy and multidisciplinary follow-up. These tasks are typically coordinated by specialists in medical genetics/biochemical genetics, the specialty responsible for diagnosing and treating inborn errors of metabolism such as Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome is a generalized dysfunction of the proximal renal tubules. Because the proximal tubule fails to reabsorb normally filtered solutes—including uric acid—patients excrete excessive amounts of uric acid in the urine, leading to hyperuricosuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B27",
    "long_answer": "Acute anterior uveitis—the most prevalent type of uveitis—is strongly linked to the class I major histocompatibility complex allele HLA-B27. Around half of patients with idiopathic or recurrent anterior uveitis carry this allele, and its presence is well established in association with spondyloarthropathies that frequently manifest uveitis. Therefore, HLA-B27 is the genetic marker most commonly associated with the most frequent form of uveitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rituximab",
    "long_answer": "Rituximab, an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody used in B-cell–targeted immunotherapy, has demonstrated clinical and electrophysiological improvement in patients with anti-MAG (myelin-associated glycoprotein) peripheral neuropathy by depleting the pathogenic IgM-secreting B-cells responsible for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV) can be detected prenatally by performing amniocentesis, which allows collection of fetal cells in amniotic fluid so that the deficient lysosomal enzyme (e.g., N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase for type IV A) or the disease-causing mutation can be assayed, confirming the disorder before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dysmorphic facies",
    "long_answer": "In Pentasomy X (49,XXXXX), patients typically show a constellation of cranio-facial anomalies—epicanthal folds, hypertelorism, flat nasal bridge, micrognathia, among others. Clinically, this characteristic set of facial abnormalities is grouped under the term “dysmorphic facies,” denoting an abnormal or distinctive facial appearance associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "North Carolina",
    "long_answer": "Analysis of U.S. death-certificate data for 1996–2005 (reported in the CDC’s MMWR article “Byssinosis-Related Mortality—United States, 1996–2005”) showed that more byssinosis was recorded among residents of North Carolina than any other state; North Carolina contributed the largest share of the nation’s byssinosis-related deaths during that decade, reflecting its historically large cotton-textile workforce where exposure to cotton dust can cause the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zika virus",
    "long_answer": "Zika virus can cross the placenta during pregnancy, infect the developing fetal nervous system, and cause congenital Zika syndrome. The resulting severe central- and peripheral-nervous-system damage reduces fetal movement, leading to neurogenic arthrogryposis (multiple joint contractures) observed in newborns of infected mothers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the BCOR (BCL-6 corepressor) gene on Xp11.4 are a well-established cause of Lenz microphthalmia syndrome (MCOPS2). This X-linked recessive disorder can be confirmed by molecular genetic testing that detects loss-of-function or missense mutations in BCOR, distinguishing it from other microphthalmia phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Akrasia",
    "long_answer": "Akrasia is the classic philosophical and psychological term for acting against one’s own better judgment. It captures the self-control failure whereby a person chooses an option they know is not in their best interest—such as delaying an intended task—making it closely associated with procrastination in behavioral science."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonazepam",
    "long_answer": "Clinical trials have demonstrated that clonazepam, a long-acting benzodiazepine, can lessen arousals and increase total sleep time and sleep efficiency in patients with Periodic Limb Movement Disorder, thereby improving objective and subjective sleep measures even though it may not markedly reduce the number of limb movements themselves."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunology",
    "long_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome is a form of severe combined immunodeficiency caused by absent MHC class II expression. Management centers on evaluating and restoring immune function (e.g., hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation, immunoglobulin replacement, infection prophylaxis). These diagnostic and therapeutic tasks are principally handled by specialists in Immunology (often termed Clinical or Allergy & Immunology), who focus on primary immunodeficiency disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transthyretin immunostaining",
    "long_answer": "Choroid plexus epithelium produces the transport protein transthyretin (pre-albumin). Immunohistochemical staining for transthyretin shows strong, diffuse cytoplasmic positivity in choroid plexus papilloma cells, providing a reliable marker that helps confirm the diagnosis and distinguish this tumor from other papillary neoplasms of the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chondrocalcinosis",
    "long_answer": "Magnesium deficiency decreases pyrophosphatase activity, allowing inorganic pyrophosphate to accumulate. The excess pyrophosphate combines with calcium to form calcium-pyrophosphate crystals that deposit in hyaline and fibro-cartilage, producing the characteristic cartilage calcification seen in chondrocalcinosis (calcium-pyrophosphate deposition disease/pseudogout)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-contrast CT scan",
    "long_answer": "Cystine stones may be faint on plain radiographs, but non-contrast helical computed tomography (CT) is highly sensitive to their sulfur-containing density and reliably visualises them; therefore, a non-contrast CT scan is the recommended imaging modality for detecting cystine stones in cystinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonorrhea",
    "long_answer": "In men, infection of the urethral epithelium by Neisseria gonorrhoeae (gonorrhea) produces acute urethritis. The inflammatory response leads to mucopurulent discharge and a characteristic burning or painful sensation during urination—clinical dysuria—which is one of the most common presenting symptoms of this sexually transmitted infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "Adrenocortical hyperfunction is characterized by excessive secretion of adrenal cortex hormones (e.g., cortisol, aldosterone). Disorders of hormone-producing glands are the domain of endocrinology; endocrinologists perform hormonal testing, imaging, and medical or surgical management for conditions such as Cushing’s syndrome and primary aldosteronism, making this the specialty responsible for diagnosing and treating adrenocortical hyperfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced activity",
    "long_answer": "The 677T (C677T) polymorphism replaces alanine with valine at codon 222, producing a thermolabile MTHFR isoform whose catalytic function is diminished; individuals carrying the T allele show only ~30–65 % of the normal enzyme activity, so the variant decreases MTHFR activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis",
    "long_answer": "Chlamydia trachomatis is a bacterium transmitted primarily through sexual contact. If the initial chlamydial infection is not diagnosed and treated, the organism can ascend from the cervix to the upper genital tract, producing inflammation of the uterus, fallopian tubes, and adjacent tissues—clinically recognized as pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). Chlamydia is the most common identifiable bacterial cause of PID, underscoring the importance of early detection and antibiotic therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dyschromatosis universalis hereditaria",
    "long_answer": "Both SASH1 and ABCB6 are causative genes for dyschromatosis universalis hereditaria (DUH), a dominantly inherited pigmentary disorder characterized by generalized mottled hyper- and hypopigmented macules. Pathogenic variants in ABCB6 define DUH type 1, while heterozygous mutations in SASH1 cause DUH type 2, establishing DUH as the pigmentation disorder linked to mutations in either gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the WNT4 gene, located on chromosome 1p36.12, have been identified in females with Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome. WNT4 encodes a Wnt signaling ligand crucial for Müllerian duct formation; disruption of this pathway leads to agenesis of the uterus and upper vagina characteristic of MRKH, firmly linking WNT4 on chromosome 1 to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transposition of the great arteries",
    "long_answer": "In transposition of the great arteries, the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, reversing the normal outflow tracts. This anatomic switch directs deoxygenated systemic venous blood back to the body while oxygen-rich pulmonary venous blood recirculates to the lungs, leading to severe systemic hypoxemia unless mixing occurs through a shunt such as a patent foramen ovale or ductus arteriosus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Persistent truncus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "During normal cardiac development, the truncus arteriosus is partitioned by the aorticopulmonary septum into the aorta and pulmonary trunk. When this septum fails to form and the truncus does not divide, a single arterial trunk exits the heart that supplies both systemic and pulmonary circulations. This congenital malformation is called persistent truncus arteriosus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SF3B4",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the splicing factor gene SF3B4 have been identified in patients with Rosselli–Gulienetti (Nager-type acrofacial dysostosis). Besides the characteristic craniofacial and limb anomalies, several reported individuals with SF3B4 mutations show developmental delay or intellectual disability, linking this specific gene defect to cognitive impairment in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maintaining corneal deturgescence",
    "long_answer": "The corneal endothelium acts as a metabolic pump–barrier that actively transports ions and fluid from the corneal stroma into the aqueous humor, thereby keeping the cornea relatively dehydrated (deturgescent) and optically clear. In Fuchs' endothelial dystrophy this pump function is lost as endothelial cells degenerate, leading to stromal and epithelial edema and loss of corneal transparency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus",
    "long_answer": "Several studies have detected herpes simplex virus (HSV) DNA and antigens in corneal and anterior segment tissues from patients with Iridocorneal Endothelial (ICE) syndrome. This has led to the hypothesis that a prior or latent HSV infection may trigger or promote the abnormal proliferation and migration of corneal endothelial cells characteristic of ICE syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic heterozygous variants in the ZSWIM6 gene cause acrofrontofacionasal (acromelic frontonasal) dysostosis. ZSWIM6 is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q12), so the disease-associated gene resides on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenytoin",
    "long_answer": "Phenytoin, a hydantoin anticonvulsant widely used for the management of tonic-clonic and focal seizures, has been repeatedly documented to cause selective decreases in serum immunoglobulin A levels. Numerous case series and pharmacovigilance reports describe reversible IgA deficiency developing during chronic phenytoin therapy, which normalizes after the drug is discontinued, establishing phenytoin as a classic cause of drug-induced IgA deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norwood procedure",
    "long_answer": "Staged palliation for single-ventricle congenital heart disease begins in the neonatal period with the Stage I Norwood procedure. This operation reconstructs the aorta, connects the single ventricle to the systemic circulation, and provides regulated pulmonary blood flow via a shunt, thereby stabilizing systemic and pulmonary circulations until the later Glenn and Fontan stages."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oocyte donation",
    "long_answer": "Although most women with Turner syndrome lack functional ovaries, their uterus can usually sustain a pregnancy. Using assisted reproduction, a fertile woman’s oocytes are fertilized (IVF) and the resulting embryos are transferred to the uterus of the woman with Turner syndrome. This donor-oocyte (egg donation) approach bypasses the ovarian failure and enables successful pregnancies in otherwise infertile Turner syndrome patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blunted sympathetic tone",
    "long_answer": "Chronic in-utero cocaine blocks catecholamine re-uptake, so fetal adrenergic receptors down-regulate. After birth, infants show lower baseline sympathetic outflow and a reduced ability to increase norepinephrine-mediated responses (e.g., smaller pupil size, attenuated heart-rate acceleration), demonstrating a blunted sympathetic nervous system activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Strongyloides stercoralis",
    "long_answer": "Strongyloides stercoralis, an intestinal nematode (helminth), commonly presents with chronic, non-bloody watery diarrhea. Because it is a tissue-invasive helminth, it elicits a strong Th2 immune response with marked peripheral eosinophilia—making eosinophilia a characteristic laboratory clue in patients with Strongyloides infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gestational diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Poorly controlled maternal hyperglycemia in gestational diabetes mellitus drives excess fetal insulin production, stimulating somatic growth and adiposity, which markedly increases the likelihood of a large-for-gestational-age infant. Screening, dietary counseling, glucose monitoring, and pharmacologic therapy during prenatal care can effectively manage gestational diabetes, thereby reducing LGA risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-A1-B8-DR3 haplotype",
    "long_answer": "Studies of Israeli Jewish patients with primary Sjögren’s syndrome have shown a marked increase in the extended MHC class I/II haplotype HLA-A1-B8-DRB1*03 (DR3), which also carries the linked DQA1*0501-DQB1*0201 alleles. This A1-B8-DR3 haplotype is therefore considered the principal genetic susceptibility factor for Sjögren’s disease in the Israeli population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OCRL1",
    "long_answer": "Oculocerebrorenal (Lowe) syndrome results from pathogenic mutations in the OCRL1 gene, which encodes an inositol-polyphosphate-5-phosphatase. Loss of OCRL1 disrupts phosphoinositide metabolism in cells such as the retinal pigment epithelium, leading to the characteristic ocular, cerebral and renal abnormalities of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Graves disease",
    "long_answer": "In Graves disease, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins trigger lymphocytic infiltration and glycosaminoglycan deposition in the extraocular muscles. The resulting muscle swelling and fibrosis restrict eye movements and produce misalignment, leading to binocular diplopia. Thus this autoimmune thyroid disorder can cause diplopia through its direct effect on the extraocular muscles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNGA3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in CNGA3, the α-subunit of the cone cyclic nucleotide-gated (CNG) channel, are a well-documented cause of congenital achromatopsia. CNGA3 forms the pore of the CNG channel that translates light-dependent changes in cGMP into ion flux, a pivotal step in the phototransduction cascade of cone photoreceptors. Disruption of CNGA3 therefore abolishes cone signal transduction, leading to the color blindness and photophobia characteristic of achromatopsia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Severe Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome is caused by mutations in the WAS gene that affect all hematopoietic lineages; replacing the patient’s immune and platelet-producing cells with donor cells corrects the underlying defect. Therefore, the established curative therapy is allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell (bone-marrow or cord-blood) transplantation, which can fully reconstitute normal immune function and platelet production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHARGE syndrome",
    "long_answer": "CHARGE syndrome, most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the CHD7 gene, is well known for its constellation of congenital anomalies. Cardiac malformations occur in roughly 75% of affected infants, with conotruncal defects such as Tetralogy of Fallot being among the most frequent. In the same cohort, anomalous pulmonary venous return—especially partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection—is another repeatedly reported cardiac manifestation. Thus, CHARGE syndrome uniquely links both Tetralogy of Fallot and partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection within a single genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Cerebral amyloid angiopathy involves deposition of β-amyloid in the walls of small cortical and leptomeningeal blood vessels of the brain. These vessels are part of the central nervous system, so the disease specifically affects CNS vasculature rather than the peripheral nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "baby shampoo",
    "long_answer": "For eyelid hygiene in patients with a stye (hordeolum), ophthalmologists commonly advise gently scrubbing the lid margins with a warm solution of diluted baby shampoo. Baby shampoo is tear-free, non-irritating, and mild enough to loosen and remove the crusted discharge without harming the ocular surface."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLEC (plectin)",
    "long_answer": "The Ogna form of epidermolysis bullosa simplex is being investigated for dominant missense or truncating mutations in the PLEC gene, which encodes the cytolinker protein plectin. Altered plectin weakens keratin–hemidesmosome linkage in basal keratinocytes, predisposing the skin to mechanical fragility and blistering characteristic of EBS-Ogna."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cough",
    "long_answer": "When urachal carcinoma disseminates, the lungs are the commonest site of distant spread. Pulmonary deposits frequently become symptomatic before other metastatic foci, and patients typically first notice a persistent or new-onset cough. Accordingly, cough is the most common initial symptom signaling metastatic urachal cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "More and more",
    "long_answer": "The acronym MOMO (Macrosomia-Obesity-Macrocephaly-Ocular anomalies) was chosen so it sounds like the phrase “more and more,” a playful reference to the syndrome’s characteristic excessive growth and size."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Propranolol",
    "long_answer": "Propranolol, a non-selective β-adrenergic blocker, is now first-line therapy for complicated infantile hemangiomas. It reduces lesion size and vascularity by causing vasoconstriction, down-regulating angiogenic factors such as VEGF, and promoting endothelial apoptosis, thereby lowering risks such as ulceration, visual obstruction, or airway compromise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone pain",
    "long_answer": "In POEMS syndrome, sclerotic or mixed lytic–sclerotic bone lesions are common; these lesions frequently give rise to persistent skeletal or bone pain, which is reported by a majority of patients and is considered a characteristic manifestation of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dishabiliophobia",
    "long_answer": "Dishabiliophobia is the specific phobic anxiety disorder defined as an excessive, irrational fear of undressing in front of other people. Derived from the French term \"déshabillé\" (state of undress) plus the Greek \"-phobia,\" it closely parallels gymnophobia (fear of nudity) but emphasizes the act or situation of removing one’s clothes in the presence of others."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1-chain of type II collagen, are the classical cause of spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita. Type II collagen is a major structural component of both cartilage and the vitreous body of the eye; therefore, COL2A1 mutations not only produce the skeletal abnormalities of SED congenita but also predispose patients to ocular complications such as high myopia and spontaneous retinal detachment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteophytes",
    "long_answer": "In canine hip dysplasia, chronic joint laxity leads to degenerative osteoarthritis. The cartilage damage and altered joint mechanics stimulate periarticular new bone formation, resulting in osteophytes—bony projections (bone spurs) that develop along the margins of the femoral head and acetabulum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal-dominant cancer-predisposition disorder caused by germ-line mutations in the TP53 tumor-suppressor gene. Affected individuals have a greatly increased lifetime incidence of several malignancies, notably brain tumors (e.g., astrocytomas, medulloblastomas, glioblastomas) and hematologic cancers, including childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia. The loss of normal p53 function compromises DNA-damage checkpoints, permitting accumulation of oncogenic mutations and explaining the high risk for both brain tumors and ALL in this inherited syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediastinal lymphadenopathy",
    "long_answer": "On a normal chest radiograph the aortopulmonary (AP) space appears lucent, because it is occupied by aerated lung. Appearance of a new soft-tissue radiodensity in this AP window almost always signifies tissue that should not be there, and the commonest cause is enlargement of the subaortic or mediastinal lymph nodes. Therefore a radiodensity in the aortopulmonary space typically indicates mediastinal lymphadenopathy, which may arise from lymphoma, metastatic lung cancer, sarcoidosis, or other nodal diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "In serum sickness, laboratory evaluation often includes a complete blood count. When this test reveals an abnormally low platelet count, the patient is diagnosed with thrombocytopenia—the condition defined by reduced circulating platelets. Identifying thrombocytopenia is important because immune-complex–mediated processes in serum sickness can consume or destroy platelets, leading to bleeding risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Albendazole",
    "long_answer": "Albendazole is a broad-spectrum benzimidazole anthelmintic recommended by the World Health Organization for treating ascariasis. A single 400 mg oral dose disrupts the parasite’s microtubule formation by binding β-tubulin, leading to impaired glucose uptake, energy depletion, and death of Ascaris lumbricoides."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L1 syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked L1CAM gene cause L1 syndrome (also called X-linked hydrocephalus with stenosis of the aqueduct of Sylvius). This disorder produces congenital aqueductal stenosis that leads to severe hydrocephalus and is characteristically accompanied by adducted thumbs, lower-limb spasticity, and other pyramidal signs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD40LG (CD40-ligand)",
    "long_answer": "The most prevalent form of hyper-IgM syndrome is the X-linked type (HIGM1), which results from mutations in the CD40-ligand gene (CD40LG, also called CD154) on the X chromosome. Loss of functional CD40L on activated T cells prevents interaction with CD40 on B cells, blocking class-switch recombination; this leaves patients with normal or elevated IgM but markedly reduced IgG, IgA and IgE levels, causing the characteristic immunodeficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tumor necrosis factor-α",
    "long_answer": "During Hantavirus hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS), circulating levels of the pro-inflammatory cytokine tumor necrosis factor-α (TNF-α) rise markedly. TNF-α released from activated monocytes/macrophages and endothelial cells promotes vascular permeability, fever, and other systemic inflammatory manifestations characteristic of HFRS, and its serum concentration correlates with disease severity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIPA gene",
    "long_answer": "Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency (including Wolman disease and cholesteryl-ester storage disease) results from pathogenic mutations in the LIPA gene, which encodes the enzyme lysosomal acid lipase. Loss of functional LIPA prevents hydrolysis of triglycerides and cholesterol esters within lysosomes, leading to their pathologic accumulation in tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asia",
    "long_answer": "Although the diagnosis of neurasthenia has been abandoned in North American and most European psychiatric classifications (e.g., it was removed from the DSM), it is still an official ICD-10 category (F48.0) and remains clinically recognized and used in several Asian countries—most prominently in China—so the continent where it continues to be acknowledged as a medical diagnosis is Asia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral neuropathy",
    "long_answer": "In Flynn–Aird syndrome, one of the earliest and most frequent manifestations is peripheral neuropathy—damage to the peripheral nerves that produces numbness, tingling, or weakness in the distal limbs. This sensory-motor nerve impairment typically appears before other neurological signs and reflects the syndrome’s characteristic progressive nerve degeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occupational noise exposure",
    "long_answer": "In the manufacturing industry, prolonged exposure to high-intensity industrial noise (e.g., from machinery, presses, and metal fabrication equipment) is the predominant factor that damages cochlear hair cells, leading to noise-induced hearing loss. Epidemiological studies consistently show that hazardous noise is the principal cause of work-related hearing impairment in this sector, far exceeding contributions from ototoxic chemicals or other hazards."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sotos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is an autosomal dominant over-growth disorder caused most commonly by NSD1 gene mutations. Affected children show rapid somatic growth and markedly advanced osseous (bone) maturation. Characteristic craniofacial features include a long, narrow face with down-slanting palpebral fissures, explaining the observed changes in the palpebral fissure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fuhrmann syndrome is a rare autosomal-recessive limb-malformation disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WNT7A gene. Its cardinal skeletal findings include complete or partial fibular aplasia/hypoplasia together with ectrodactyly (split-hand/foot malformation), often accompanied by syndactyly or oligodactyly. Because this specific combination of fibular absence and ectrodactyly is characteristic for Fuhrmann syndrome, it is the genetic condition classically associated with these anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ribavirin",
    "long_answer": "The exposed researcher was given intravenous ribavirin, a broad-spectrum antiviral nucleoside analogue that is active against arenaviruses such as Sabiá virus. The infectious-diseases specialist administered ribavirin early after exposure, and the patient recovered without developing severe Brazilian hemorrhagic fever, demonstrating the drug’s clinical efficacy in this case."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "long_answer": "First-line conservative management of lumbar spinal stenosis commonly includes non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). By inhibiting cyclo-oxygenase enzymes and reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation, NSAIDs diminish nerve-root and facet joint inflammation, thereby alleviating the back and leg pain characteristic of spinal canal narrowing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenytoin",
    "long_answer": "Maternal use of the anticonvulsant phenytoin (diphenylhydantoin) is a recognized cause of fetal hydantoin syndrome. This teratogenic syndrome includes skeletal defects such as hypoplastic distal phalanges and brachydactyly (short fingers and toes), arising from phenytoin’s interference with normal limb bone and nail development during early gestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "gp120",
    "long_answer": "HIV carries an envelope glycoprotein complex made of gp120 (surface) associated with gp41 (transmembrane). The outer subunit gp120 binds to the CD4 receptor and a chemokine co-receptor on the target cell, anchoring the virus. This receptor engagement triggers structural changes that bring gp41 into position to drive fusion of the viral envelope with the host plasma membrane, enabling viral entry. Thus, the gp120 envelope protein is the key viral component that permits attachment to target cells and initiates the membrane-fusion process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (45,X monosomy) is well known for cardiovascular abnormalities; about one-third of affected patients have left-sided heart lesions such as coarctation of the aorta, hypoplastic aortic arch, or bicuspid aortic valve. Thus, aortic hypoplasia is a characteristic congenital finding in individuals with this chromosomal disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTCH1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Nevoid basal-cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome is classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PTCH1 gene, the human homologue of the Drosophila Patched receptor in the Hedgehog signalling pathway. Besides predisposing to basal-cell carcinomas, PTCH1 haploinsufficiency disrupts normal craniofacial skeletogenesis; affected individuals often show mandibular overgrowth, presenting clinically as mandibular prognathism. Thus the same PTCH1 mutation responsible for NBCCS can directly lead to mandibular prognathism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythropoietin (EPO)",
    "long_answer": "Hemangioblastomas, particularly those arising in the cerebellum or spinal cord, can secrete erythropoietin ectopically. The excess erythropoietin stimulates red-blood-cell production, producing secondary polycythemia as a characteristic paraneoplastic syndrome associated with these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduces complication risk",
    "long_answer": "Quitting smoking lowers systemic inflammation, improves insulin sensitivity and endothelial function, and eliminates tobacco-induced oxidative stress. In people with diabetes, these changes translate into significantly lower rates of microvascular (nephropathy, retinopathy, neuropathy) and macrovascular (cardiovascular) complications, with risk progressively declining toward that of never-smokers the longer cessation is maintained."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultrasound imaging",
    "long_answer": "For cystic echinococcosis (hydatid disease), abdominal ultrasonography is the first-line and most widely recommended imaging technique because it can non-invasively detect, characterize and stage the typical cystic lesions—especially in the liver—guiding both diagnosis and management according to WHO-IWGE classifications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacrosidase oral solution",
    "long_answer": "Patients with genetic sucrase-isomaltase deficiency lack intestinal sucrase activity, so ingesting sucrose or some starches causes diarrhea, bloating and pain. Oral enzyme-replacement with sacrosidase (a yeast-derived sucrase preparation marketed as Sucraid) supplies the missing enzyme, allowing dietary sucrose to be digested and thereby alleviating the gastrointestinal symptoms of GSID."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pancreatic insufficiency",
    "long_answer": "In Johnson-Munson syndrome, the decisive factor for survival and long-term outcome is the severity of the exocrine pancreatic insufficiency: marked loss of pancreatic enzymes leads to intractable malabsorption, recurrent infections and failure to thrive, whereas patients in whom enzyme replacement controls the malabsorption can live into adulthood despite the other congenital anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topical corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Erosive pustular dermatosis of the scalp is typically managed with potent topical corticosteroids, whose anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive actions reduce pustulation, crusting, and erosions, promoting re-epithelialization and preventing further scarring alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electrocardiogram (ECG)",
    "long_answer": "Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is a congenital long-QT syndrome. The QT interval—the time from ventricular depolarization to repolarization—is quantified on a surface electrocardiogram (ECG). Recording a 12-lead ECG allows clinicians to measure the prolonged QT interval characteristic of this disorder and to assess the risk of malignant arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP53",
    "long_answer": "The TP53 gene, which encodes the p53 tumor-suppressor protein, is located on chromosome 17p13.1. Alterations in TP53 (mutations or loss of heterozygosity) are common molecular events detected in oral leukoplakia and are associated with its risk of malignant transformation, making TP53 a key gene implicated in leukoplakia pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exogenous ochronosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic application of skin-lightening creams that contain hydroquinone can induce exogenous ochronosis, a condition characterized by blue-black or grayish hyperpigmented macules due to polymerized homogentisic acid depositing in the dermis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schiller-Duval bodies",
    "long_answer": "Endodermal sinus (yolk sac) tumors are characterized by the presence of Schiller-Duval bodies—glomerulus-like structures in which a central capillary is surrounded by tumor cells and enclosed in a cystic space lined by additional tumor cells. This perivascular arrangement is considered pathognomonic for the diagnosis of an endodermal sinus tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNB1 duplication",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant leukodystrophy with autonomic disease (ADLD) is caused by a copy-number gain of the LMNB1 gene, encoding lamin B1. Duplication of LMNB1 on chromosome 5q23 leads to over-expression of lamin B1 in oligodendrocytes, disrupting nuclear lamina function and myelin maintenance, which produces the adult-onset leukodystrophy and characteristic autonomic dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tungiasis",
    "long_answer": "The flea known colloquially as the jiga (or jigger), Tunga penetrans, burrows into human skin. The resulting parasitic infestation is called tungiasis, characterized by painful, itchy, inflamed lesions—typically on the feet—where the gravid female flea embeds and enlarges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors",
    "long_answer": "Cataplexy is a REM-sleep–related phenomenon; antidepressants that strongly suppress REM sleep reduce its frequency. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs, e.g., fluoxetine, sertraline) increase central serotonergic tone, inhibit REM sleep, and are commonly prescribed to control cataplexy in patients with narcolepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP2A2",
    "long_answer": "Darier’s disease (keratosis follicularis) is an autosomal-dominant skin disorder produced by mutations in the ATP2A2 gene on chromosome 12q24.1. ATP2A2 encodes the SERCA2 Ca2+-ATPase, and loss-of-function variants disrupt intracellular calcium homeostasis in keratinocytes, leading to impaired cell adhesion and abnormal keratinization characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tyrosinase",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinase is the copper-dependent oxidase that catalyzes the first two steps of melanin biosynthesis—hydroxylation of tyrosine to L-DOPA and oxidation of L-DOPA to dopaquinone. Mutations that inactivate the tyrosinase gene prevent these reactions, so albino mammals lack functional tyrosinase and therefore cannot produce melanin pigment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal-recessive acute porphyria",
    "long_answer": "Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase deficiency porphyria is one of the acute hepatic porphyrias—an inborn error of heme biosynthesis—and it is transmitted in an autosomal recessive manner, requiring pathogenic variants in both ALAD gene alleles for disease expression."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic megacolon",
    "long_answer": "When a markedly dilated colon occurs without an identifiable mechanical obstruction, neurologic disorder, or systemic disease, the condition is termed idiopathic megacolon. The adjective “idiopathic” denotes that the cause is unknown."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastrointestinal stromal tumor",
    "long_answer": "After complete surgical excision of a gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST) from the colon or any gastrointestinal site, professional bodies such as the NCCN and ESMO recommend structured surveillance (e.g., CT or MRI every 3–6 months for several years) because GISTs carry a well-documented risk of local or metastatic recurrence despite apparent curative resection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lung auscultation",
    "long_answer": "During the physical examination, clinicians commonly perform lung auscultation with a stethoscope. In eosinophilic pneumonia this often reveals diffuse inspiratory crackles (rales), providing an immediate bedside clue that supports the diagnosis before imaging or laboratory tests are obtained."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tinea pedis",
    "long_answer": "The condition commonly called athlete’s foot is medically termed tinea pedis. It is a superficial dermatophyte infection—most often caused by Trichophyton species—that affects the skin of the feet, particularly the interdigital spaces and soles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otolaryngologist",
    "long_answer": "An otolaryngologist—also known as an ear, nose, and throat (ENT) or head-and-neck surgeon—is routinely part of the multidisciplinary team treating laryngeal cancer because this cancer arises within the throat. ENT physicians are surgically trained to manage tumors of the larynx and surrounding head-and-neck structures, and their residency curriculum includes operative procedures of the oral cavity and oropharynx, so they are qualified to perform oral surgery when needed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risk factor",
    "long_answer": "A risk factor is any attribute, characteristic, or exposure—such as age, genetics, lifestyle, or environmental condition—that is statistically associated with a higher probability of developing a particular disease or disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skewed X-chromosome inactivation",
    "long_answer": "Although MTM1 is inherited as an X-linked recessive trait, a heterozygous female can develop the full clinical picture if most of her cells have inactivated the chromosome bearing the normal MTM1 allele. This non-random (skewed) Lyonization leaves the mutant X active in the majority of muscle cells, so the defective myotubularin protein is expressed and the child manifests classic X-linked myotubular myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transient ischemic attack",
    "long_answer": "A transient ischemic attack (TIA) is a brief episode of cerebral ischemia in which restricted blood flow to part of the brain produces temporary neurological symptoms. Because TIAs can disrupt visual‐spatial processing in the occipital or parietal cortex, they may trigger perceptual distortions such as micropsia or macropsia that clinically resemble the hallmark features of Alice in Wonderland syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis produces dehydrated, densely packed red blood cells, so their hemoglobin is concentrated in a smaller cell volume. This leads to a characteristically elevated mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) on the complete blood count, a classic laboratory hallmark of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candidiasis",
    "long_answer": "Mixed (type II/III) cryoglobulinemia is most commonly infection-related, and among fungal infections the one repeatedly documented is candidiasis (systemic Candida infection, often Candida endocarditis). Immune complexes formed during invasive Candida infection can precipitate as cryoglobulins, producing the characteristic laboratory and clinical picture of mixed cryoglobulinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carl-Bertil Laurell and Sten Eriksson",
    "long_answer": "In 1963 the Swedish investigators Carl-Bertil Laurell and Sten Eriksson published the first report showing that patients with a hereditary deficiency of the serum protease inhibitor alpha-1 antitrypsin develop early-onset panacinar emphysema. Their work established the causal link between inherited alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and emphysematous lung disease that is now accepted as medical fact."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Torsades de pointes",
    "long_answer": "Long QT syndrome delays ventricular repolarization, promoting early after-depolarizations that trigger a distinctive form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia known as torsades de pointes. This arrhythmia can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation, producing hemodynamic collapse and sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory failure",
    "long_answer": "In Morquio syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IV), progressive skeletal and airway abnormalities—such as thoracic cage restriction, tracheobronchial narrowing, and cervical spine instability—lead to chronic respiratory insufficiency. This respiratory compromise ultimately results in respiratory failure, which is the most frequent cause of death in affected patients, surpassing cardiac or neurologic complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbon dioxide laser",
    "long_answer": "A carbon dioxide (CO₂) laser emits 10.6-µm infrared light that is intensely absorbed by water in skin cells, producing rapid vaporization and coagulation of tissue. This controlled photothermal effect allows dermatologists to ablate benign lesions with minimal bleeding, making the CO₂ laser a standard treatment option for common skin conditions such as viral warts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoidosis",
    "long_answer": "Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disease that forms non-caseating granulomas in affected tissues. Activated macrophages inside the granulomas produce extra-renal 1-α-hydroxylase, converting more 25-hydroxy-vitamin D to its active form (1,25-dihydroxy-vitamin D), which increases intestinal calcium absorption and can lead to hypercalcemia. Thus, sarcoidosis is well known for the dual features of granuloma formation and hypercalcemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metaphysis",
    "long_answer": "Sponastrime dysplasia is named for its characteristic skeletal changes—‘spon’ (spine), ‘nas’ (nasal), and ‘strime’ (striated metaphyses). Radiographs show prominent striations and irregular modeling in the metaphyses of long bones, making the metaphysis the part most consistently abnormal in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1000 cubic centimetres",
    "long_answer": "By definition, 1 litre (L) equals 1 cubic decimetre (dm³). Since 1 dm = 10 cm, the volume converts as (10 cm)³ = 1000 cm³. Thus a litre of water occupies exactly 1000 cubic centimetres."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Limb body wall complex",
    "long_answer": "The Van Allen criteria are used to diagnose and classify limb-body wall complex (body-stalk anomaly). According to these criteria, the presence of any two of three major findings—(1) craniofacial defects such as exencephaly or encephalocele with facial clefts, (2) large thoraco- or abdominoschisis, and (3) limb deformities—establishes the diagnosis. Thus, this severe malformation syndrome that includes craniofacial defects is the condition classified by the Van Allen criteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncologist",
    "long_answer": "Once an adenoma undergoes malignant transformation it is considered a cancer (adenocarcinoma). The diagnosis and treatment of cancers—including staging, surgery, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, and follow-up—fall under the responsibility of an oncologist, the physician specialized in managing malignant tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome is a contiguous-gene deletion disorder (17p13.3, including PAFAH1B1/LIS1) that produces classical lissencephaly. The LIS1 haploinsufficiency disrupts microtubule-dependent neuronal migration during cortical development, so the syndrome is recognized as a prototypical neuronal migration disorder associated with lissencephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN9A",
    "long_answer": "Primary (familial) erythromelalgia is most often caused by missense mutations in the SCN9A gene, which encodes the voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.7. These gain-of-function mutations enhance channel activity in peripheral sensory neurons, leading to neuronal hyperexcitability and the characteristic burning pain and erythema of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AGXT",
    "long_answer": "Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 — the commonest and most rapidly progressive form of the disease — is caused by pathogenic mutations in the AGXT gene, which encodes the hepatic enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase. Loss of AGXT activity leads to overproduction of oxalate and early-onset renal failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sternalis muscle",
    "long_answer": "The sternalis is an anatomical variant that lies superficial to the pectoralis major, running vertically beside the sternum. When present it can produce a palpable or radiodense parasternal mass that is easily mistaken for the breast-cancer symptom of a new breast lump. Recognising this muscle variation prevents misdiagnosis of breast cancer on physical examination or mammography."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sexophobia",
    "long_answer": "The medically recognized term for an irrational fear or aversion toward sexual organs, sexual acts, or sexuality in general is sexophobia. Individuals with sexophobia experience marked anxiety or avoidance when confronted with sexual topics, genitalia, or sexual activity, classifying it among specific phobias in anxiety-related disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Histoplasma antigen enzyme immunoassay",
    "long_answer": "Histoplasmosis can be rapidly confirmed with an antigen enzyme immunoassay (EIA/ELISA) that detects Histoplasma capsulatum polysaccharide antigens circulating in serum or excreted in urine. A positive result provides direct evidence of active infection and is highly sensitive, especially for disseminated or acute pulmonary disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive dystonia with pyramidal signs",
    "long_answer": "Wells-Jankovic syndrome is a rare, autosomal-dominant neurological disorder described in one large Balkan family. It begins in childhood with focal dystonia (usually a leg or the oromandibular region) and steadily spreads to produce generalized, sometimes laryngeal, dystonia. In addition, patients show mild pyramidal-tract involvement—spasticity, hyperreflexia and Babinski signs—while cognition, sensation and cerebellar function remain normal. The combination of slowly progressive primary dystonia together with corticospinal (pyramidal) signs is the hallmark of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "None identified",
    "long_answer": "Despite extensive genetic studies, no recurrent or disease-defining gene mutation has yet been found in Gómez–López-Hernández syndrome. Reported cases are sporadic, and genome-wide analyses have failed to implicate a specific gene; therefore its molecular basis remains unknown."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Catel–Manzke syndrome is caused by biallelic (homozygous or compound-heterozygous) loss-of-function mutations in the TGDS gene on chromosome 13q32. Because two pathogenic alleles are required for the phenotype, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL12B",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies of Takayasu's arteritis have repeatedly identified polymorphisms in the IL12B gene, which encodes the p40 subunit common to the cytokines interleukin-12 and interleukin-23. The IL-12/IL-23 pathway drives Th1 and Th17 immune responses that are central to granulomatous inflammation, the pathological hallmark of Takayasu's arteritis. Thus, IL12B is the gene linked to both the disease and the granulomatous inflammatory process."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudomembranous colitis (PMC)",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomembranous colitis is characterized by yellow-white plaques (pseudomembranes) on the colonic mucosa, typically caused by Clostridioides difficile toxin–mediated injury. Because of these diagnostic pseudomembranes, the condition is commonly abbreviated as PMC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acute respiratory distress syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Although most acute Q fever cases present with a nonspecific febrile illness or atypical pneumonia, the infection can rapidly worsen; within the first few days it may evolve into acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), a life-threatening condition characterized by severe hypoxemia and diffuse alveolar damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loud first heart sound",
    "long_answer": "In mitral stenosis the stiff yet still mobile mitral valve leaflets remain widely separated at the onset of ventricular systole and then snap shut abruptly, producing an accentuated, loud S1. This markedly heightened first heart sound is the most characteristic auscultatory heart-sound sign of the lesion, often preceding the opening snap and diastolic rumble in clinical importance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOX gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Caudal duplication syndrome has been hypothesized to arise from aberrant mutations affecting the caudal-patterning HOX (homeobox) genes; disruption of these genes’ normal expression could duplicate developmental fields and produce the characteristic duplication of distal spinal, gastrointestinal and genitourinary structures seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinol-binding protein",
    "long_answer": "In Leber congenital amaurosis, one proposed pathogenic mechanism is a deficiency of retinol-binding protein (also called interphotoreceptor retinoid-binding protein). Lack of this carrier impairs transport of vitamin-A–derived retinoids between the photoreceptors and the retinal pigment epithelium, disrupting the visual cycle and leading to the severe, early-onset photoreceptor dysfunction characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypokalemia",
    "long_answer": "In proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis, large oral doses of sodium bicarbonate are required because the proximal tubule continues to waste filtered bicarbonate. The increased distal delivery of sodium bicarbonate enhances sodium re-absorption via ENaC in the collecting duct, driving potassium secretion into the urine and shifting potassium intracellularly. Consequently, potassium loss intensifies, and any pre-existing low serum potassium is aggravated. Thus, oral bicarbonate supplementation can exacerbate hypokalemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LDLR (low-density lipoprotein receptor) gene on chromosome 19p13 cause familial hypercholesterolaemia. Defective or absent LDL receptors impair hepatic clearance of LDL particles, resulting in markedly elevated plasma LDL-cholesterol levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal smoking",
    "long_answer": "Large epidemiologic studies show that cigarette smoking during pregnancy approximately doubles the risk of congenital talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) in the infant. Nicotine-induced vasoconstriction and hypoxia can impair fetal musculoskeletal development, making maternal smoking a recognized modifiable risk factor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Atopic dermatitis (eczema) is understood to arise from a combination of genetic predisposition (e.g., filaggrin‐gene mutations), skin-barrier dysfunction, immune dysregulation (type-2/Th2-skewed inflammation), and environmental factors such as allergens, irritants, climate, and microbial exposure. Extensive epidemiologic studies and meta-analyses have found no causal relationship between routine vaccinations (e.g., MMR, DTaP, influenza, COVID-19, etc.) and the development of atopic dermatitis. Although some patients with pre-existing eczema may experience transient flare-ups triggered by fever or local skin irritation after an immunization, vaccines themselves are not a recognized etiologic factor in initiating the disease. Therefore, current scientific consensus is that vaccines do not cause atopic dermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mueller-Weiss disease",
    "long_answer": "Mueller-Weiss disease is an adult-onset spontaneous osteonecrosis of the tarsal navicular bone. It is regarded as the adult counterpart of Köhler disease, which is the osteochondrosis of the navicular seen in children and adolescents; both conditions share similar pathophysiology of avascular necrosis affecting the same bone, differing mainly in patient age."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate",
    "long_answer": "In Stimmler syndrome, impaired oxidative metabolism leads to an accumulation of pyruvate; this excess pyruvate is transaminated by alanine aminotransferase to form alanine, making pyruvate the presumed source of the markedly elevated alanine seen in patients’ blood and urine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "STK11",
    "long_answer": "Peutz–Jeghers syndrome arises from germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the tumor-suppressor gene STK11 (also called LKB1) on chromosome 19p13.3. Disruption of this serine/threonine kinase impairs regulation of cell polarity and growth, leading to the characteristic mucocutaneous pigmentation and hamartomatous gastrointestinal polyps seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Weight gain/obesity",
    "long_answer": "Physical inactivity promotes a positive energy balance that leads to weight gain and central obesity. Excess adipose tissue increases sympathetic nervous system activity, insulin resistance, sodium retention, and vascular stiffness, all of which raise peripheral vascular resistance and cardiac output, thereby increasing the likelihood of developing chronic hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "Photosensitive trichothiodystrophy arises from biallelic mutations in the ERCC2 gene, which encodes the XPD helicase of the TFIIH nucleotide-excision repair complex. Loss of XPD function blocks effective repair of UV-induced DNA damage, producing the characteristic photosensitivity seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin",
    "long_answer": "Poisoning with botulinum toxin classically produces early dysfunction of cranial nerves—manifesting as diplopia, ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, facial weakness, dysarthria, and dysphagia—because the toxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions. These characteristic cranial nerve abnormalities are a hallmark of botulism and help distinguish it from other toxic or neurologic conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GTP cyclohydrolase I",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant deficiency of GTP cyclohydrolase I, the rate-limiting enzyme in tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis, causes classic dopa-responsive dystonia (Segawa disease). Because BH4 is an essential cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, its lack reduces tyrosine hydroxylase activity and thereby impairs dopamine synthesis in the nigrostriatal pathway, producing the dystonic motor syndrome that reverses with low-dose levodopa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sclerosis (MS) is an autoimmune demyelinating disorder of the central nervous system in which autoreactive lymphocytes attack myelin. When this inflammatory process involves the optic nerve it produces optic neuritis, leading to optic neuropathy characterized by acute, often painful, loss of vision. Thus, MS is a well-established autoimmune disease that causes optic neuropathy through inflammation of the optic nerve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cold staphylococcal abscess",
    "long_answer": "Hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome is classically marked by recurrent skin and deep infections with Staphylococcus aureus that form so-called “cold” abscesses—collections of pus that lack the usual warmth, redness, and tenderness because neutrophil chemotaxis and Th17-mediated inflammation are defective. These cold staphylococcal abscesses are a hallmark clinical feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Risk of hypomagnesemia",
    "long_answer": "In diabetic ketoacidosis, osmotic diuresis causes large urinary magnesium losses, yet acidosis initially keeps serum Mg normal by shifting it extracellularly. When insulin and fluids are given and the acidosis corrects, magnesium moves back into cells and a marked fall in serum Mg can occur, producing hypomagnesemia that can precipitate arrhythmias, neuromuscular irritability and make hypokalemia refractory. Therefore magnesium levels must be closely monitored during DKA treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Rett syndrome is almost always caused by de novo loss-of-function mutations in the MECP2 gene on the X chromosome, which encodes the methyl-CpG–binding protein 2 critical for neuronal gene regulation. Pathogenic MECP2 alterations disrupt normal synaptic development and function, leading to the characteristic neurodevelopmental features of Rett syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metaphysis",
    "long_answer": "In pediatric SAPHO syndrome (often presenting as chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis), inflammatory and sclerotic lesions preferentially localize to the metaphyseal regions of long bones such as the tibia and femur."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippocampus",
    "long_answer": "Neuropathological studies of Japanese encephalitis virus-infected brains show marked reactive microgliosis in the hippocampus—particularly the CA3 and dentate gyrus regions—indicating that this area exhibits the most pronounced microglial activation during the infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macrolides",
    "long_answer": "Macrolide antibiotics (e.g., azithromycin, clarithromycin) are commonly prescribed during acute exacerbations of COPD because they cover the typical respiratory pathogens involved, including Haemophilus influenzae. Their spectrum against this Gram-negative coccobacillus and good lung tissue penetration make macrolides effective agents in this setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "the ears",
    "long_answer": "Prenatal exposure to the anticonvulsant trimethadione can produce the fetal trimethadione syndrome, characterized by multiple cranio-facial anomalies. A prominent and well-documented feature is developmental deformities of the external ears—such as low-set, posteriorly rotated, or malformed pinnae—demonstrating that the ears are a body part commonly affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trachea",
    "long_answer": "After skin penetration and hematogenous spread to the lungs, hookworm filariform larvae break into the alveoli and crawl upward through the bronchi and the trachea to reach the pharynx, from which they are swallowed into the gastrointestinal tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leukocytosis",
    "long_answer": "Leptospirosis can provoke a marked systemic inflammatory response, and many patients show a peripheral blood leucocyte count above the normal range. This elevated white-blood-cell state is termed leukocytosis and is a recognized laboratory finding during the acute phase of leptospiral infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myocardial bridging",
    "long_answer": "In myocardial bridging, a segment of a coronary artery—most often the left anterior descending—takes an intramyocardial, ‘tunneled’ course through the heart muscle instead of running on the epicardial surface. This congenital variant causes the artery to be compressed during systole and is classically described as a coronary artery tunneling through the myocardium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Pityriasis lichenoides chronica is an inflammatory skin disorder characterized by recurrent papules and scaly patches. Conditions affecting the skin are evaluated and managed by dermatologists, making Dermatology the appropriate medical specialty to treat this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langerhans cells",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic granuloma is the localized form of Langerhans cell histiocytosis. The lesion is characterized by a clonal, neoplastic-like proliferation of Langerhans cells—specialized antigen-presenting dendritic cells found in the epidermis and mucosa. Although the tissue also contains abundant eosinophils, the pathologic proliferation that defines the disease involves Langerhans cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Calpain-3–related limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (calpainopathy) often shows prominent scapular winging and shoulder-girdle weakness, clinical features that closely resemble those of facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (FSHD). Because both disorders produce early scapulo-humeral muscle involvement and preserved facial strength can be mild or absent early, patients with calpainopathy are frequently misclassified as having FSHD until genetic or protein studies identify CAPN3 deficiency. Thus, FSHD is the muscular dystrophy most commonly confused with calpainopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pectus carinatum",
    "long_answer": "A protruding, keel-shaped anterior chest wall is termed pectus carinatum (“pigeon chest”). This thoracic deformity is frequently described in connective-tissue and skeletal dysplasia syndromes such as Marfan, Noonan, and Morquio, where abnormal cartilage or rib growth pushes the sternum outward."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Keratosis follicularis-dwarfism-cerebral atrophy syndrome has been reported predominantly in siblings born to consanguineous parents, and the observed segregation pattern fits a recessive model located on an autosome. Unaffected parents are obligate heterozygous carriers, while affected individuals are homozygous for the pathogenic allele, establishing autosomal recessive inheritance for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Severe hyperthermia (e.g., exertional or classic heat-stroke, malignant hyperthermia) is a systemic illness that can progress to multiorgan dysfunction. Marked elevations in core temperature trigger endothelial injury, a systemic inflammatory response, and activation of the coagulation cascade. The resulting disseminated intravascular coagulation and hepatic failure lead to depletion of clotting factors and platelet consumption. Consequently, patients can develop clinically significant bleeding—gastro-intestinal hemorrhage, mucosal bleeding, or diffuse oozing—as part of the multiorgan failure picture. Therefore, bleeding that arises secondary to organ (especially hepatic and coagulation system) failure is recognized in the medical literature as one of the potentially life-threatening complications of severe hyperthermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blue cone monochromacy",
    "long_answer": "Blue cone monochromacy is an X-linked retinal disorder in which deleterious mutations or deletions in the OPN1LW (long-wave opsin) and OPN1MW (medium-wave opsin) genes, or their shared locus control region, abolish L- and M-cone function. Patients are left with only S-cone and rod activity, producing a form of monochromacy characterized by severely impaired color discrimination, reduced visual acuity, and photophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 18",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome arises when a zygote carries three copies of chromosome 18 instead of the normal two. This full or partial extra chromosome—termed trisomy 18—disrupts normal gene dosage and development, leading to the characteristic congenital anomalies and high neonatal mortality of Edwards syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Ringworm (tinea) is a superficial fungal infection that affects the skin, hair, or nails. The medical specialty focused on diagnosing and treating skin diseases—including fungal infections such as ringworm—is dermatology. Therefore, a dermatologist is the appropriate specialist to evaluate and manage the characteristic ring-shaped lesions and prescribe topical or systemic antifungal therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Ulna hypoplasia–intellectual disability syndrome (sometimes referred to as Schinzel syndrome) has been reported in multiple affected members of the same family across successive generations without sex bias. The vertical transmission pattern—each affected child having an affected parent—demonstrates autosomal inheritance, and the occurrence in every generation indicates dominance rather than recessiveness. Therefore, the established mode of inheritance is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection is very common, but CNS invasion and encephalitis are rare complications. Most HSV-1 infections present as oral/labial lesions and remain latent in peripheral sensory ganglia; most HSV-2 infections cause genital lesions. Only a small fraction of primary infections or reactivations spread along nerves or via the bloodstream to the brain, producing herpes simplex encephalitis. Therefore, HSV does not invariably reach the brain, and the vast majority of infected individuals never develop encephalitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucopolysaccharides",
    "long_answer": "In Sézary disease, the malignant CD4⁺ T lymphocytes (Sézary cells) show cytoplasmic vacuoles that stain strongly with periodic acid–Schiff and remain positive after diastase digestion, demonstrating that they contain acid mucopolysaccharides (glycosaminoglycans). This accumulation of mucopolysaccharide material is a characteristic cytochemical feature of the affected T cells in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Pigmented (bilirubin) gallstones form when excess unconjugated bilirubin from chronic red-cell destruction precipitates in bile. Any chronic hemolytic anemia—including hereditary spherocytosis, sickle cell disease, or thalassemias—produces this sustained hemolysis, so hemolytic anemia is classically associated with the presence of gallstones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia is an inherited pharmacogenetic reaction triggered by volatile anaesthetics or depolarising muscle relaxants during general anaesthesia. Although rare, it can cause rapid hyper-capnia, muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and cardiac arrest, making it a major, potentially fatal complication that must be considered when planning general anaesthesia for patients with dental phobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metoclopramide",
    "long_answer": "Metoclopramide, a pro-kinetic agent prescribed for gastro-oesophageal reflux and heartburn, antagonises dopamine D2 receptors. Dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release from the anterior pituitary; blocking D2 receptors therefore raises prolactin levels, which can lead to hyperprolactinaemia and manifest clinically as galactorrhea."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RASA1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the RASA1 gene (encoding p120-RASGAP) cause capillary malformation–arteriovenous malformation (CM-AVM) syndrome. This vascular developmental disorder is well documented to include brain arteriovenous shunts such as vein of Galen aneurysmal malformations as well as early-onset or extensive varicose veins in affected individuals, linking the same RASA1 mutation to both clinical manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AML1-ETO",
    "long_answer": "The M2 (acute myeloblastic leukemia with maturation) subtype of acute myeloid leukemia frequently carries the t(8;21)(q22;q22) chromosomal translocation, which fuses the RUNX1 (AML1) gene on chromosome 21 with the RUNX1T1 (ETO) gene on chromosome 8. The resultant chimeric protein, AML1-ETO, disrupts normal RUNX1-mediated transcriptional regulation of hematopoiesis and is a well-established molecular hallmark of M2 AML."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The human NADPH oxidase-5 (NOX5) gene is located on chromosome 15, specifically at cytogenetic band 15q23. Thus, chromosome 15 is the chromosomal location implicated when NOX5 variants are studied in connection with Hirschsprung's disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Little People of America",
    "long_answer": "Little People of America (LPA) is a national nonprofit organization that offers resources, medical and social support, advocacy, and community networking for people with achondroplasia and other forms of dwarfism, making it the best-known support group for individuals with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIT D816V mutation",
    "long_answer": "A somatic gain-of-function point mutation in the KIT proto-oncogene, most commonly the D816V substitution, is found in the vast majority of systemic mastocytosis cases. The same KIT D816V mutation is also linked to reduced bone density and a high prevalence of osteoporosis in these patients, because aberrant KIT signalling in mast cells increases release of mediators (e.g., histamine, tryptase, cytokines) that stimulate osteoclast activity and bone resorption. Thus, the KIT D816V mutation is a recognized genetic lesion associated with both mastocytosis and osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLG (filaggrin)",
    "long_answer": "Ichthyosis vulgaris is classically linked to loss-of-function mutations in the FLG gene, which encodes the epidermal protein filaggrin. Filaggrin is critical for keratin filament aggregation and formation of the skin’s stratum corneum barrier; deficiency results in the characteristic dry, scaly skin of ichthyosis vulgaris."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PLEC",
    "long_answer": "The Ogna form of epidermolysis bullosa simplex is linked to mutations in the PLEC gene, which encodes the cytolinker protein plectin. Plectin integrates intermediate filaments with hemidesmosomes in basal keratinocytes; pathogenic PLEC variants weaken this linkage, leading to the skin fragility characteristic of the Ogna subtype. Consequently, current research focuses on PLEC to understand and potentially treat this form of EBS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pacemaker implantation",
    "long_answer": "Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy frequently leads to progressive cardiac conduction defects and life-threatening arrhythmias. When these complications become severe, a permanent cardiac pacemaker is surgically implanted to maintain an adequate heart rhythm and prevent sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Borderline Personality Disorder",
    "long_answer": "Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD) is a personality disorder marked by pronounced emotional dysregulation, rapid mood shifts, impulsivity, and unstable interpersonal relationships. Because the affective instability in BPD can mimic depressive or manic episodes, it is frequently mistaken for mood disorders such as major depression or bipolar disorder. Distinguishing features include pervasive fear of abandonment, chronic feelings of emptiness, and identity disturbance, which help differentiate BPD from primary mood disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrinology",
    "long_answer": "In Erdheim–Chester disease, the most frequent central nervous system complication is central diabetes insipidus caused by histiocytic infiltration of the hypothalamic–pituitary axis. Diagnosis and management of diabetes insipidus—including water-balance assessment, desmopressin therapy, and endocrine follow-up—fall under the expertise of the medical specialty of Endocrinology. Therefore, endocrinologists are the specialists primarily responsible for diagnosing and treating this prevalent CNS manifestation in Erdheim–Chester disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLT3-ITD mutation",
    "long_answer": "Internal tandem duplication (ITD) mutations in the FLT3 receptor-tyrosine-kinase gene are one of the most frequent somatic lesions in AML (≈25–30% of adult cases). The duplicated sequence causes constitutive activation of FLT3 signaling pathways (RAS/MAPK, PI3K/AKT, STAT5) that drive uncontrolled proliferation of myeloid precursors, markedly increasing the likelihood of developing acute myeloid leukemia and conferring an adverse prognosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus anthracis",
    "long_answer": "Bacillus anthracis is the bacterium that causes anthrax, a disease documented since antiquity and notorious for historic livestock and human outbreaks. Its hardy endospores can survive in soil for decades, making soil contamination a persistent environmental and public-health concern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kartagener syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kartagener syndrome is the classical triad form of primary ciliary dyskinesia in which defective dynein-based ciliary motion leads to impaired mucociliary clearance. This results in chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis, while abnormal ciliary function during embryogenesis produces situs inversus. The concurrent presence of these three features is diagnostic for Kartagener syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) is classified as a primary 4-repeat tauopathy. Abnormally phosphorylated tau accumulates in neurons and glial cells not only in the brain-stem and basal ganglia—its most characteristic sites—but also in limbic structures, including the hippocampus (particularly CA2-CA4 and dentate gyrus). Post-mortem studies consistently report neuronal loss, gliosis and tau-positive neurofibrillary tangles and tufted astrocytes in these hippocampal subfields. Although hippocampal involvement is typically milder than in Alzheimer’s disease, the presence of tau aggregates indicates that the hippocampus is indeed affected in PSP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DLL3",
    "long_answer": "Jarcho-Levin syndrome (the autosomal-recessive form of spondylocostal dysostosis) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the DLL3 gene, which encodes the Delta-like 3 ligand of the Notch signaling pathway that regulates somitic segmentation of the vertebrae and ribs. Defective DLL3 disrupts normal vertebral and costal development, producing the characteristic short-trunk dwarfism and rib anomalies of Jarcho-Levin syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bright’s disease",
    "long_answer": "In 19th- and early 20th-century medicine, “Bright’s disease” was the umbrella term for chronic nephritis and related kidney disorders characterized by proteinuria, edema, and progressive renal damage. Clinicians recognized that patients with Bright’s disease commonly developed sustained high blood pressure, which in turn led to hypertensive heart disease and cardiac failure, establishing the historical link between this kidney ailment, hypertension, and heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deep peroneal nerve",
    "long_answer": "The deep peroneal (deep fibular) nerve supplies the extensor digitorum brevis muscle via its lateral terminal branch as it crosses the anterior aspect of the ankle and dorsum of the foot. Entrapment or injury of this nerve in the anterior tarsal tunnel or at the ankle joint often manifests as otherwise unexplained pain over the dorsal ankle and mid-foot region, confirming it as the nerve in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole",
    "long_answer": "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX, cotrimoxazole) is the first-line therapy for Pneumocystis jirovecii (Pneumocystis) pneumonia. Because it contains a sulfonamide, up to one-third of patients—especially those with HIV infection—develop cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions ranging from mild maculopapular rash to severe Stevens-Johnson syndrome. This well-documented dermatologic toxicity makes TMP-SMX the medication classically associated with rash in the treatment of Pneumocystis pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AKT3",
    "long_answer": "Somatic gain-of-function mutations in the AKT3 gene, a key kinase in the PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling pathway, have been found in brain tissue from patients with hemimegalencephaly. Hyperactivation of AKT3 promotes excessive neuronal and glial proliferation during cortical development, leading to the unilateral cerebral overgrowth that characterizes hemimegalencephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sirolimus",
    "long_answer": "Recent case series and prospective studies have shown that the mTOR inhibitor sirolimus (rapamycin) induces rapid, sustained shrinkage of kaposiform hemangioendothelioma and reverses the associated Kasabach-Merritt coagulopathy, even in lesions refractory to steroids or vincristine. Its targeted inhibition of the PI3K-AKT-mTOR pathway in abnormal endothelial cells accounts for the high response rates reported, making sirolimus the most consistently effective therapy for KHE in current literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dominant parietal lobe",
    "long_answer": "Ideomotor apraxia—the inability to correctly perform learned purposeful movements on command despite preserved strength and comprehension—most commonly follows injury to the dominant (usually left) parietal cortex, particularly the inferior parietal lobule. Lesions here disrupt the stored motor engrams and their transfer to premotor–motor areas, producing the characteristic apraxic errors observed clinically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parotidectomy",
    "long_answer": "Frey's syndrome—gustatory sweating and flushing over the parotid-mastoid region—most often occurs after parotidectomy. During removal of the parotid gland, post-ganglionic parasympathetic fibers in the auriculotemporal nerve can be damaged; when they regenerate, they aberrantly innervate sweat glands of the overlying skin, producing sweating and erythema during eating."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VACTERL association",
    "long_answer": "Imperforate anus (anal atresia) frequently occurs in the VACTERL association, a congenital syndrome characterized by Vertebral (spinal) defects, Anal atresia, Cardiac anomalies, Tracheo-esophageal fistula, Renal anomalies and Limb defects. The vertebral component accounts for the spinal defects linked to the imperforate anus in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Levodopa",
    "long_answer": "Levodopa, the main dopaminergic medication for Parkinson’s disease, can induce excessive dopaminergic stimulation of the striatum, leading to involuntary hyperkinetic movements such as chorea (levodopa-induced dyskinesias) in a significant proportion of treated patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Kidney stones that develop in primary hyperoxaluria are managed by urologists, the surgical specialists who diagnose and treat urinary-tract stone disease with procedures such as ureteroscopy, lithotripsy or percutaneous nephrolithotomy, while nephrologists handle the metabolic disorder itself. Thus, the medical specialty responsible for treating the stones is urology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Congenital muscular dystrophy-infantile cataract-hypogonadism syndrome is a rare, hereditary multisystem disorder. Conditions that are congenital, involve multiple organ systems, and arise from identifiable gene defects are routinely diagnosed, counseled, and managed by specialists in medical (clinical) genetics, who coordinate neuromuscular, ophthalmologic, and endocrine care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "ATR-16 syndrome, or alpha-thalassemia/mental retardation syndrome type 16, results from large deletions at the 16p13.3 region that remove the α-globin gene cluster (HBA1/HBA2) and neighboring genes; therefore, the disorder is specifically linked to abnormalities on human chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAI1",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Magenis syndrome most often results from a deletion of the 17p11.2 region, but research has shown that haploinsufficiency of the RAI1 (Retinoic acid-induced 1) gene within this interval is sufficient to reproduce the characteristic neurobehavioral, craniofacial and sleep-disturbance features of the syndrome. Patients with point mutations affecting only RAI1, without the larger chromosomal deletion, present with the typical Smith–Magenis phenotype, confirming that loss of RAI1 function is responsible for the majority of SMS manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "Severe or life-threatening Cogan syndrome, particularly when associated with systemic vasculitis or progressive auditory/ocular damage, is commonly managed with the alkylating immunosuppressant cyclophosphamide. Its potent suppression of B- and T-cell proliferation helps control the aggressive autoimmune inflammation when corticosteroids alone are inadequate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene (chromosome 10q11.2) are the single most frequent genetic cause of Hirschsprung’s disease. Among infants with Down syndrome who develop Hirschsprung’s, RET mutations are again the predominant molecular abnormality, making RET the gene most commonly implicated in both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Insulin antagonism",
    "long_answer": "Cortisol antagonizes insulin by enhancing hepatic gluconeogenesis, inhibiting GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake in muscle and adipose tissue, and raising circulating free fatty acids. These actions increase blood glucose and interfere with post-receptor insulin signaling pathways, progressively reducing tissue responsiveness to insulin and thereby promoting insulin resistance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AGXT gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of primary hyperoxaluria cases—and those that most often progress to nephrocalcinosis and chronic kidney disease—are due to loss-of-function mutations in the AGXT gene, which encodes the hepatic enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase. Defective AGXT (e.g., the common p.Gly170Arg variant) prevents conversion of glyoxylate to glycine, leading to systemic oxalate overproduction, calcium-oxalate deposition in the kidneys, and eventual renal failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental disomy",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental disomy (UPD) is a chromosomal anomaly in which both copies of a chromosome are inherited from the same parent instead of one copy from each parent. Case reports have described limb-body-wall complex in fetuses who had UPD—most often maternal UPD of chromosome 16—indicating that this error of inheritance can be associated with the severe malformation spectrum seen in limb body wall complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple Syrup Urine Disease",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex prevents normal catabolism of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine, producing Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD). Chronic MSUD is associated with impaired bone metabolism and reduced bone mineral density, predisposing patients to osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basal layer of epidermis",
    "long_answer": "Basal cell carcinomas, including those seen in Bazex–Dupré–Christol syndrome, originate from the basal keratinocytes located in the stratum basale (basal layer) of the epidermis—the outermost layer of the skin. This proliferative epidermal layer is where malignant transformation occurs, leading to the characteristic tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pigmented purpuric dermatosis",
    "long_answer": "Gougerot–Blum syndrome is the lichenoid variant of chronic benign capillaritis and belongs to the group of pigmented purpuric dermatoses—a set of disorders characterized by petechiae, purpura and brownish hyperpigmentation produced by hemosiderin deposition in the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC30A10",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the manganese efflux transporter gene SLC30A10 cause a form of primary familial brain calcification. Loss of SLC30A10 function leads to systemic hyper-manganesemia; excess manganese accumulates in the basal ganglia, producing symmetric calcifications seen on CT and an early-onset parkinsonian movement disorder. Thus SLC30A10 links PFBC with manganese-induced parkinsonism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thalidomide",
    "long_answer": "Thalidomide is an immunomodulatory drug used in combination regimens for multiple myeloma. It is highly teratogenic; exposure during pregnancy can disrupt limb and organ development, leading to severe birth defects such as phocomelia. Because of this risk, thalidomide is dispensed under strict pregnancy‐prevention programs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SOX2 gene, which encodes a transcription factor essential for early ocular and forebrain development, are a recognized cause of septo-optic dysplasia. Heterozygous SOX2 mutations frequently produce severe bilateral ocular anomalies such as microphthalmia or anophthalmia, alongside optic nerve hypoplasia and pituitary/midline brain defects characteristic of SOD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IBIDS syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Trichothiodystrophy is clinically classified into three acronyms: BIDS, IBIDS, and PIBIDS.  The IBIDS syndrome stands for Ichthyosis, Brittle hair, Intellectual impairment, Decreased fertility, and Short stature.  Because the first letter ‘I’ denotes ichthyosis, this subset of trichothiodystrophy specifically includes ichthyosis as one of its cardinal clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A3 COL4A4 COL4A5",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome results from pathogenic variants in the genes that encode the α3, α4 and α5 chains of type IV collagen. The X-linked form is caused by COL4A5 mutations, while autosomal recessive or dominant forms arise from mutations in COL4A3 or COL4A4. Defects in these genes disrupt the basement-membrane collagen network of the kidney, ear and eye, producing the characteristic nephropathy, hearing loss and ocular signs of Alport syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia, Strudwick type is an autosomal-dominant chondrodysplasia produced by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen. Defective type II collagen disrupts cartilage matrix organization, leading to the characteristic abnormalities of the spine, epiphyses and metaphyses that define the Strudwick form of spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 9 (and occasionally Keratin 1)",
    "long_answer": "Epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (Vorner type) is classically caused by dominant mutations in the KRT9 gene that encodes Keratin 9, the palmoplantar-specific intermediate-filament protein. Rare families with the same clinical and histological phenotype carry mutations in KRT1, encoding Keratin 1. Thus Keratin 9 is the primary, and Keratin 1 a less common, keratin associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytogenetic karyotyping",
    "long_answer": "Chromosomal anomalies characteristic of acute myeloid leukemia are identified by conventional cytogenetic karyotyping, in which metaphase chromosomes from leukemic cells are G-banded and examined for structural or numeric abnormalities such as t(8;21), inv(16), or t(15;17)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly–ectodermal dysplasia–cleft (EEC) syndrome results from heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor. p63 is crucial for limb development, ectodermal differentiation, and craniofacial formation; pathogenic variants disrupt these processes, leading to the characteristic limb malformations, ectodermal defects, and orofacial clefting seen in EEC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciliated columnar tubal epithelium",
    "long_answer": "Endosalpingiosis is defined by ectopic glands lined by Fallopian tube-type mucosa. These glands are composed of the same ciliated simple columnar epithelium normally found in the tubal mucosa, appearing outside the tube in peritoneal or other pelvic sites."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Odynophagia",
    "long_answer": "Severe aphthous stomatitis can extend lesions into the oropharynx, producing pain with each act of swallowing; this painful swallowing is clinically termed odynophagia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ataxia",
    "long_answer": "Ataxia is a neurological condition frequently observed in individuals with alcohol-related dementia. Chronic alcohol misuse can damage cerebellar structures, leading to impaired coordination, unsteady gait, and disturbed balance—hallmark features of ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liposuction",
    "long_answer": "In chronic, non-pitting lymphedema the excess limb volume is largely due to hypertrophied fibroadipose tissue that no longer responds to lymph-drainage therapies. Suction-assisted lipectomy (liposuction) surgically removes this solid tissue, producing sustained volume reduction and improved function, and is therefore an accepted surgical treatment for chronic non-pitting edema in lymphedema patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Centroblastic cells",
    "long_answer": "In the centroblastic variant of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, the neoplastic cells are large, non-cleaved germinal-center B cells that display multiple peripherally placed nucleoli and scant perinuclear cytoplasm—the same features seen in normal centroblasts. Therefore, the tumour cells in this variant (centroblastic cells) are the ones that most closely resemble centroblasts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myalgia",
    "long_answer": "Adrenal insufficiency often manifests with muscle aches, termed myalgia. The same symptom commonly arises after excessive muscle use and during many viral infections (e.g., influenza-associated muscle pain), so myalgia is a shared, well-recognized feature in all three situations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Medical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Medical genetics is the branch of medicine dedicated to the diagnosis, management, and counseling of hereditary disorders, including those caused by pathogenic variants in mitochondrial DNA or nuclear genes that affect mitochondrial function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenine",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent pathogenic variant causing medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency is the c.985A>G transition in the ACADM gene. In this mutation the adenine (A) nucleotide at position 985 is replaced by guanine (G), leading to the Lys304→Glu amino-acid substitution that disrupts enzyme function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Icosahedral",
    "long_answer": "Herpes simplex virus, like all members of the Herpesviridae family, encloses its double-stranded DNA genome within a highly symmetric icosahedral capsid (T=16), composed of 162 capsomeres arranged in the characteristic 20-triangular-face geometry of an icosahedron."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reticulocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Diamond–Blackfan anemia is a congenital failure of erythroid progenitor cells. Because the bone marrow cannot produce new red cells, the number of circulating reticulocytes (young red cells) falls dramatically—reticulocytopenia. Detecting this very low reticulocyte count confirms that the anemia results from impaired red-cell production rather than increased destruction or loss, making it a key diagnostic clue for Diamond–Blackfan anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Pedigree analyses of the families originally described with ankylosing vertebral hyperostosis with tylosis showed vertical transmission from an affected parent to offspring of either sex, with approximately a 50 % recurrence risk in each generation. No male-to-male transmission exclusion or sex bias was observed, establishing that the disorder follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymus (mediastinum)",
    "long_answer": "In T-lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma, the malignant T-cell lymphoblasts typically arise in and accumulate within the thymus, producing a bulky anterior mediastinal mass. This reflects the normal site of immature T-cell (thymocyte) development, making the thymus the characteristic location where these neoplastic lymphoblasts are most commonly found."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST2",
    "long_answer": "Barber-Say syndrome results from heterozygous, usually de-novo, gain-of-function mutations in the TWIST2 gene. TWIST2 encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor that regulates ectodermal and mesenchymal development; pathogenic variants disturb craniofacial, skin and hair-follicle morphogenesis, producing the hypertrichosis, macrostomia and redundant skin that characterize Barber-Say syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoparathyroidism is classically caused by inactivating mutations in the GNAS gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the stimulatory G protein (Gsα). Loss-of-function of Gsα prevents proper cyclic-AMP signaling downstream of the parathyroid hormone receptor, producing target-organ resistance to PTH and the clinical picture of pseudohypoparathyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarnat staging system",
    "long_answer": "Neonatal (hypoxic-ischemic) encephalopathy is clinically graded with the Sarnat staging system, which classifies affected newborns into Stage I (mild), Stage II (moderate), or Stage III (severe) based on level of consciousness, neuromuscular control, reflexes, autonomic function, and EEG findings. This system guides prognosis and therapeutic decisions such as the need for therapeutic hypothermia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mumps virus infection",
    "long_answer": "Mumps, caused by a paramyxovirus, can invade the inner ear and eighth cranial nerve. This viral infection may damage cochlear hair cells and the auditory nerve, producing sudden, often unilateral, irreversible sensorineural hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lens extraction with IOL implantation",
    "long_answer": "In Alport-related lenticonus the anterior lens capsule bulges and produces high irregular astigmatism that cannot be corrected adequately with glasses or contact lenses. Definitive treatment is surgical removal of the abnormal crystalline lens (clear-lens or cataract extraction) followed by implantation of an artificial intraocular lens (IOL), which restores a smooth optical surface and stable refraction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RB1",
    "long_answer": "Retinoblastoma is strongly linked to deletion or inactivation of the tumor-suppressor gene RB1 located on chromosome 13q14. Loss of one RB1 allele (germ-line deletion) predisposes individuals to a markedly higher risk of developing retinoblastoma, and subsequent somatic loss of the remaining allele leads to tumor formation according to the two-hit hypothesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cannabis (marijuana)",
    "long_answer": "Cannabis use commonly produces xerostomia, popularly called “cotton-mouth.” Δ⁹-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) binds to CB₁ and CB₂ receptors on salivary gland acinar cells, inhibiting parasympathetic stimulation of saliva secretion; this reduction in salivary flow manifests as dry mouth within minutes to hours of recreational consumption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karyotype analysis",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome is confirmed by cytogenetic karyotyping of peripheral blood (or prenatal samples), which shows a missing or structurally abnormal X chromosome—most commonly the 45,X karyotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Postaxial polydactyly",
    "long_answer": "In acro-oto-radial syndrome the limb phenotype includes an extra digit on the lateral side of the foot—postaxial polydactyly—along with radial-ray defects of the upper limbs and external-ear anomalies. This additional toe is the characteristic foot anomaly reported in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jigsaw puzzles",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with nonverbal learning disorder characteristically have deficits in visual-spatial organization and spatial reasoning. Completing jigsaw puzzles relies heavily on accurately perceiving shapes, spatial relationships and assembling pieces in a coherent visual pattern—skills that are commonly impaired in NVLD—so such puzzles are often especially challenging for them."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIEZO1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary xerocytosis (dehydrated hereditary stomatocytosis) is most commonly caused by missense gain-of-function mutations in the PIEZO1 gene. PIEZO1 encodes a large mechanosensitive, non-selective cation channel expressed in red blood cells; pathogenic variants increase cation leak, leading to red cell dehydration and the xerocytic phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST2",
    "long_answer": "Barber-Say syndrome and the related ablepharon-macrostomia spectrum share pathogenic heterozygous missense mutations in the TWIST2 gene, which encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor essential for craniofacial and skin development. Altered TWIST2 disrupts downstream gene regulation, leading to macrostomia, hypertrichosis, redundant skin, and other features characteristic of Barber-Say syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Insult",
    "long_answer": "In medical and neuropsychiatric contexts, an “insult” refers to any physical or psychological injury or damaging event (e.g., birth complications, head trauma, severe stress) that can disrupt brain development or function. Such developmental or environmental insults are well-recognized risk factors that can increase vulnerability to disorders like schizophrenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate",
    "long_answer": "During an acute episode of hereditary angioedema, intravenous infusion of purified C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate is given. By rapidly replacing the missing or dysfunctional C1-INH, it blocks uncontrolled activation of the complement and kallikrein–bradykinin pathways, stopping further bradykinin production and quickly relieving the edema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femur",
    "long_answer": "Myositis ossificans represents heterotopic bone formation in traumatized muscle and is seen most often in the quadriceps following injuries or fractures of the thigh. Because these lesions develop adjacent to the thigh musculature, the shaft and supracondylar region of the femur are the bone sites most frequently involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin 1",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (the classic Unna-Thost type) is produced by heterozygous mutations in the KRT1 gene, which encodes keratin 1. Keratin 1 is a suprabasal epidermal keratin abundantly expressed in palmoplantar skin; its mutation leads to marked hyperkeratosis without the epidermolytic cytolysis seen in keratin 9–related disease, hence the designation “nonepidermolytic.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a completely non-invasive scan that visualises muscle tissue; T2-weighted and STIR sequences reveal muscle edema and inflammation characteristic of myositis, allowing diagnosis and biopsy targeting without penetrating the tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Total anomalous pulmonary venous return",
    "long_answer": "In total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR), all pulmonary veins drain into the systemic venous circulation rather than the left atrium. This mis-routing forces oxygenated pulmonary venous blood back into the right side of the heart, creating volume overload and, when any part of the anomalous pathway is obstructed, marked pulmonary venous hypertension. Persistently elevated pulmonary venous pressures lead to pulmonary edema, progressive pulmonary vascular disease, right-sided volume and pressure overload, and ultimately right heart failure if the defect is not surgically corrected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDF6",
    "long_answer": "Growth Differentiation Factor 6 (GDF6), located at chromosome 8q22.1, has been linked to adolescent idiopathic scoliosis in genome-wide association and replication studies. Risk alleles in the regulatory region of GDF6 are more frequent in AIS patients, implicating this bone-morphogenetic factor in spinal growth and curvature development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal bossing",
    "long_answer": "Distal 2q37 monosomy (2q37 deletion syndrome) produces a recognizable cranio-facial dysmorphism; one of the most consistently reported features is a prominent, protruding forehead—clinically termed frontal bossing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clinical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) deficiency is an inherited enzymatic defect that causes life-threatening accumulation of fluoropyrimidine drugs (e.g., 5-FU, capecitabine). Because it is a pharmacogenetic condition, optimal risk management requires expert interpretation of DPYD genotype/phenotype results and counselling on dose adjustment or alternative therapy. Therefore, a clinical genetics (pharmacogenetics) specialist should be consulted to guide testing, interpret variants, and advise the oncology team on safe chemotherapy dosing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syringocystadenoma papilliferum",
    "long_answer": "A sebaceous nevus (nevus sebaceus of Jadassohn) is a congenital hamartoma that remains relatively flat in childhood but, under the influence of puberty-related hormones, often develops secondary adnexal tumors. The tumor that most characteristically and most frequently arises within a sebaceous nevus after puberty is the benign adnexal neoplasm syringocystadenoma papilliferum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LEMD3",
    "long_answer": "Osteopoikilosis (spotted bone disease) is an autosomal-dominant sclerosing bone dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LEMD3 gene (also called MAN1) on chromosome 12. LEMD3 encodes an inner nuclear membrane protein that antagonizes TGF-β/BMP signaling; pathogenic variants lead to the characteristic multiple bone islands seen in osteopoikilosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder",
    "long_answer": "Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) involves chronic, free-floating anxiety—a diffuse, persistent feeling of apprehension or dread that is not tied to any specific object or situation. Because the fear is non-specific, GAD is classified separately from the phobic disorders (social phobia, specific phobias, agoraphobia) in DSM-5 and ICD-10; it is therefore not listed as a type of phobia in standard medical references."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treponema pallidum subspecies endemicum",
    "long_answer": "Bejel (endemic syphilis) is an endemic treponematosis caused by the spirochete bacterium Treponema pallidum subspecies endemicum, a close relative of the agent of venereal syphilis. This subspecies is transmitted non-sexually, usually through direct skin or mucous contact in childhood, leading to chronic mucocutaneous and bone lesions characteristic of bejel."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periodontist",
    "long_answer": "Gingival fibromatosis is a massive, fibrous over-growth of the gingiva that impairs oral function and aesthetics. Definitive management is surgical gingivectomy and long-term periodontal maintenance, procedures carried out by a periodontist (the dental specialty dealing with the supporting tissues of the teeth). Although the syndrome may include hypertrichosis, the principal clinical problem requiring treatment is the gingival enlargement, so the patient is most appropriately treated in periodontics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inosine monophosphate",
    "long_answer": "Myophosphorylase (muscle glycogen phosphorylase) deficiency in McArdle disease limits ATP generation from glycogen during exercise. The resulting rise in AMP is rapidly deaminated in the purine-nucleotide cycle, leading to marked accumulation of the intermediate inosine monophosphate (IMP) and its degradation products. Thus, IMP is the purine-cycle intermediate directly associated with the metabolic disturbance seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mesoderm",
    "long_answer": "Malignant mixed Müllerian tumors (carcinosarcomas) arise in the uterus. The uterus develops from the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts, which are derived from the intermediate mesoderm germ layer. Therefore, the organ in which these tumors occur is of mesodermal origin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valine",
    "long_answer": "The hallmark JAK2 mutation in polycythemia vera is the V617F change, where the amino acid valine (V) at position 617 of the JAK2 tyrosine kinase is substituted by phenylalanine (F). This valine-to-phenylalanine substitution leads to constitutive activation of the JAK-STAT signaling pathway, driving uncontrolled hematopoietic proliferation characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "The superior thyroid artery is typically the first anterior branch of the external carotid artery. It descends toward the superior poles of the thyroid gland, where it enters the parenchyma and supplies arterial blood to the upper portions of both thyroid lobes as well as the isthmus. This relationship is a well-established anatomical fact described in standard texts and confirmed through surgical and radiologic studies. Therefore, the statement that the superior thyroid artery supplies blood to the thyroid gland is correct."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Warkany syndrome 2",
    "long_answer": "Presence of an extra copy of chromosome 8 in a mosaic pattern—trisomy 8 mosaicism—gives rise to the clinical entity called Warkany syndrome 2 (also known as trisomy 8 mosaicism syndrome). Individuals with this genetic condition exhibit characteristic facial dysmorphism, musculoskeletal anomalies, intellectual disability, and variable organ involvement, reflecting the proportion of cells that carry the third chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma",
    "long_answer": "The diffuse, epidermolytic form of palmoplantar keratoderma (often called Vörner-type PPK) is caused by dominant mutations in genes coding for suprabasal keratins that are selectively expressed in palmoplantar epidermis.  The commonest gene involved is KRT9, and rarer cases involve mutations in other palmoplantar keratins such as KRT16.  Thus, the palmoplantar keratoderma linked to both keratin 9 and keratin 16 is the epidermolytic (Vörner-type) variety."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) often includes major congenital heart defects—particularly complete atrioventricular septal defects and large ventricular septal defects—that can precipitate severe intra-uterine cardiac failure. The resulting high venous pressure and impaired lymphatic return can lead to non-immune hydrops fetalis, making Down syndrome a recognized genetic cause of hydrops secondary to congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cartilage-hair hypoplasia",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RMRP gene, which encodes the non-coding RNA component of the RNase MRP ribonucleoprotein complex, cause the inherited disorder cartilage-hair hypoplasia. These loss-of-function variants disrupt RNase MRP–mediated pre-rRNA processing and cell-cycle regulation, producing a characteristic phenotype that includes metaphyseal chondrodysplasia and markedly sparse, fine hair, hence the name of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chondrosarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple exostoses (hereditary multiple osteochondromas) is marked by numerous osteochondromas that can undergo malignant transformation. The predominant malignancy that arises from these cartilage-capped bony outgrowths is secondary peripheral chondrosarcoma, with a lifetime risk estimated at 1–5 %. Thus, people with this inherited condition are specifically predisposed to developing chondrosarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactiferous duct epithelium",
    "long_answer": "A subareolar abscess (periductal mastitis, Zuska’s disease) develops in the major lactiferous ducts that lie directly beneath the nipple-areolar complex. Squamous metaplasia of the duct epithelium produces keratin plugs, the ducts become obstructed, and secondary bacterial infection leads to abscess formation under the areola. Thus, the pathologic process involves the epithelium of the lactiferous (milk) ducts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Generally poor",
    "long_answer": "Once optic neuropathy has produced measurable visual loss, damaged retinal ganglion-cell axons cannot regenerate; therefore visual acuity, colour vision, and visual field defects usually persist. Although a minority of patients may experience slight subjective improvement if the causative factor (e.g., compression, inflammation, toxic exposure) is removed early, most cases of optic neuropathy carry a poor long-term visual prognosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome is an autosomal-dominant disorder (most often due to PAX3 or MITF mutations) in which defective neural-crest–derived melanocyte development leads to hypopigmentation of skin, hair and irides. Ocular manifestations include heterochromia, brilliantly blue or hypopigmented irides and, in many patients, microphthalmia. The concurrence of these pigmentary defects with microphthalmia distinguishes Waardenburg syndrome among neurocristopathies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rosai–Dorfman disease",
    "long_answer": "Rosai–Dorfman disease (sinus histiocytosis with massive lymphadenopathy) is a benign proliferative disorder in which S-100–positive histiocytes accumulate within the sinuses of lymph nodes. Although it classically presents with massive cervical nodes, axillary and inguinal lymphadenopathy is also common. The defining histologic feature is the marked infiltration of lymph-node sinuses by sheets of histiocytes, making this disease the canonical cause of inguinal lymphadenopathy associated with prominent histiocytes in lymph nodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyrantel pamoate",
    "long_answer": "For enterobiasis (pinworm) during pregnancy, pyrantel pamoate is preferred because it remains almost entirely within the intestinal lumen, resulting in negligible systemic absorption and no documented teratogenic risk. Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole or albendazole are avoided, especially in the first trimester, whereas pyrantel pamoate can be given safely at any stage of pregnancy to eradicate Enterobius vermicularis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Pericarditis secondary to mixed connective tissue disease is immune-mediated; systemic corticosteroids (e.g., oral prednisone) are routinely used because their anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects quickly control the pericardial inflammation and relieve symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated C-reactive protein",
    "long_answer": "Of the routinely available diagnostic tests, a markedly raised C-reactive protein level (≈ >2.5 mg/dL) has the highest positive likelihood ratio for giant cell arteritis, with LR+ values around 4, surpassing those of an ESR >100 mm/h, thrombocytosis, or other laboratory or imaging findings. This strong inflammatory marker therefore most powerfully increases the post-test probability of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eccrine sweat gland",
    "long_answer": "Acrospiromas (often called clear-cell hidradenomas or eccrine hidradenomas) are benign adnexal tumours that derive from the distal/excretory duct portion of eccrine sweat glands; hence they are classically associated with eccrine, not apocrine, sweat glands."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Roberts syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ESCO2 gene, which encodes an acetyltransferase required for proper sister-chromatid cohesion, cause Roberts syndrome (also known as SC phocomelia). This congenital disorder presents with widespread hypoplasia of limbs and other organs, growth retardation, and craniofacial anomalies. Loss of ESCO2 function disrupts chromatid cohesion during cell division, impairing normal tissue development and leading to the characteristic underdevelopment (hypoplasia) seen in Roberts syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hurler syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type I) results from deficiency of α-L-iduronidase, causing accumulation of dermatan and heparan sulfates in connective tissues. The storage of these glycosaminoglycans produces characteristic coarse facial features, including a markedly broad, bulky and flattened (saddle-shaped) nose, together with other facial abnormalities such as frontal bossing and thick lips."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin D deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic and clinical studies show that low serum 25-hydroxy-vitamin D levels are significantly associated with a higher incidence and greater severity of non-infectious (autoimmune) uveitis. Vitamin D modulates both innate and adaptive immunity; deficiency skews the immune response toward pro-inflammatory Th1/Th17 pathways that contribute to ocular inflammation, making vitamin D deficiency a recognized risk factor for non-infectious uveitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital generalized lipodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Berardinelli–Seip congenital generalized lipodystrophy is an autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by near-total absence of adipose tissue from birth. The lack of fat and secondary muscle hypertrophy give patients a striking, exaggerated musculature — a ‘pseudoathletic’ or ‘Herculean’ build that Italian authors have compared to the ancient statue known as the Warrior of Capestrano. Hence, this lipodystrophy is the disease classically linked to that distinctive appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "All reported cases of Anophthalmia-megalocornea-cardiopathy-skeletal anomalies syndrome have occurred in siblings born to consanguineous, unaffected parents, with equal involvement of males and females. This recurrence pattern and sex distribution are characteristic of autosomal recessive inheritance, in which two pathogenic alleles are required for the phenotype to manifest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cogan syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cogan syndrome is an autoimmune vasculitic disorder classically characterized by interstitial keratitis together with audiovestibular involvement that leads to progressive sensorineural hearing loss. The immune-mediated inflammation targets both ocular tissues and the inner ear, making hearing loss a well-recognized manifestation of this autoimmune disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arginine",
    "long_answer": "In ornithine aminotransferase deficiency, excess ornithine accumulates because it cannot be converted efficiently to pyrroline-5-carboxylate. Arginine is the chief dietary precursor of ornithine via the action of arginase, so restricting arginine intake lowers ornithine production and helps control hyperornithinemia and its retinal complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATL1 (Atlastin-1)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATL1 gene, which encodes the large GTPase atlastin-1, are a well-established cause of autosomal-dominant early-onset hereditary spastic paraplegia (locus SPG3A). The same ATL1 mutations have also been documented in families diagnosed with hereditary sensory neuropathy type 1 (designated HSN1D), showing that a single ATL1 pathogenic variant can produce either, or a combination of, spastic paraplegia and length-dependent sensory neuropathy. Thus, ATL1 links both disorders."  
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic anhydrase II",
    "long_answer": "A deficiency of carbonic anhydrase II impairs the osteoclast’s ability to generate the acidic environment required to dissolve hydroxyapatite, leading to defective bone resorption and the development of osteopetrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RUNX2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RUNX2 gene (also called CBFA1) are the established cause of cleidocranial dysostosis. RUNX2 is a master transcription factor that directs osteoblast differentiation and regulates bone formation during embryogenesis, including development of the clavicles, cranial sutures and the vertebral column. Consequently, pathogenic RUNX2 variants can produce the classic clavicular-cranial phenotype and, by impairing vertebral segmentation and ossification, can also manifest as congenital vertebral anomalies (e.g., hemivertebrae, fused or missing vertebrae). Thus the same RUNX2 mutation underlies both cleidocranial dysostosis and congenital vertebral malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renin",
    "long_answer": "In renal segmental hypoplasia (Ask-Upmark kidney), the hypoplastic segments develop juxtaglomerular cell hyperplasia that leads to excessive secretion of the enzyme renin. The resulting renin–angiotensin activation produces marked vasoconstriction and sodium retention, causing severe renovascular-type hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 (fibrillin-1)",
    "long_answer": "Microspherophakia in Weill–Marchesani syndrome is most often linked to pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene on chromosome 15. FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a key structural component of the ocular zonular microfibrils; loss-of-function mutations weaken these fibers, allowing the crystalline lens to become abnormally small and spherical, producing microspherophakia characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystine stone formation",
    "long_answer": "In cystinuria, defective renal tubular re-uptake of cystine leaves high levels of this poorly soluble amino acid in the urine. When cystine concentration rises above its solubility limit at physiologic urinary pH, it crystallizes and aggregates, producing characteristic hexagonal cystine crystals that grow into kidney/urinary tract calculi. Thus, higher urinary cystine directly increases the risk and frequency of cystine kidney stones (nephrolithiasis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infantile colic",
    "long_answer": "The brief, jack-knife flexor spasms of infantile epileptic spasms syndrome cause an infant to hunch forward, draw up the legs, and cry—movements that closely mimic the posture and behavior seen during episodes of infantile colic. Because of this similarity, the epileptic spasms are frequently mistaken for colic, delaying correct diagnosis and treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erysipelas",
    "long_answer": "St. Anthony’s fire is the medieval term for the acute, fiery-red skin infection we now call erysipelas—a superficial cellulitis classically caused by Streptococcus pyogenes. The intense burning pain and bright red rash it produces led to the historical association with ‘fire,’ and medieval sufferers often sought treatment in hospitals run by the Order of St. Anthony, giving rise to the name. Thus, the well-established alternative historical name is erysipelas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous epithelial cells",
    "long_answer": "Squamous-cell carcinoma is a malignant tumor that arises from squamous epithelial cells—the flat, keratinizing cells that line the epidermis of the skin and the mucosal surfaces of organs such as the oral cavity, esophagus, and lungs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocardial cushions",
    "long_answer": "Atrioventricular septal defect arises when the endocardial cushions—the tissue that normally fuses to form the atrioventricular (AV) septum and the AV valve leaflets—fail to develop and merge properly. This leads to a gap at the center of the heart between the atria and ventricles, producing the characteristic AVSD anatomy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheostomy",
    "long_answer": "Thanatophoric dysplasia produces a markedly small thorax and upper-airway obstruction, often leading to severe respiratory insufficiency. To establish a secure airway and allow long-term ventilatory support, affected infants may need a tracheostomy—surgical creation of an opening in the trachea through the neck—to facilitate breathing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Steatorrhea",
    "long_answer": "In eosinophilic gastroenteritis, eosinophilic infiltration of the small-bowel wall can impair fat absorption, producing fatty, pale, foul-smelling stools—termed steatorrhea. This malabsorptive, ‘fatty’ form of diarrhea is frequently reported as a hallmark gastrointestinal symptom in patients with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synovial membrane",
    "long_answer": "In synovial (osteo)chondromatosis, the synovial membrane lining the joint, bursa, or tendon sheath undergoes cartilaginous metaplasia, producing multiple cartilaginous nodules and loose bodies within the joint space."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increases basal metabolic rate",
    "long_answer": "In hyperthyroidism, excessive thyroid hormone (mainly T3 and T4) is released into the circulation. These hormones up-regulate mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, stimulate Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity, and enhance catecholamine sensitivity, all of which elevate energy expenditure. Consequently, resting (basal) metabolic rate rises significantly, leading to symptoms such as heat intolerance, weight loss despite increased appetite, and tachycardia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basilar artery",
    "long_answer": "The basilar artery is formed by the fusion of the vertebral arteries, runs along the ventral surface of the brainstem supplying its pons and midbrain structures, and terminates by bifurcating into the posterior cerebral arteries, thereby joining the Circle of Willis. Branch points in this region are classic sites for saccular (berry) aneurysms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow",
    "long_answer": "Hematopoiesis—the formation of red cells, white cells, and platelets—occurs in the red bone marrow found within spongy (cancellous) bone. Myelodysplastic syndrome is a clonal disorder of this marrow, where the stem and progenitor cells become dysplastic, leading to ineffective blood-cell production. Thus, the bone tissue involved and pathologically altered in myelodysplastic syndrome is the bone marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNQ1",
    "long_answer": "Romano-Ward syndrome is an autosomal-dominant long-QT syndrome. The commonest subtype, LQT1, results from mutations in the KCNQ1 gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the cardiac IKs potassium channel. KCNQ1 is situated on chromosome 11p15.5; pathogenic variants lengthen the ventricular action potential, producing the characteristic prolonged QT interval seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Psychotherapy",
    "long_answer": "Both dissociative identity disorder and major depressive disorder are commonly managed with psychotherapy. In DID, long-term trauma-focused psychotherapy is the mainstay of care to help integrate dissociated identities and process traumatic memories. For major depressive disorder, evidence-based psychotherapies such as cognitive-behavioral or interpersonal therapy are first-line treatments that can alleviate depressive symptoms and improve functioning. Thus, structured psychological therapy represents a standard intervention used for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SENP1",
    "long_answer": "Transcriptome analyses of Andean high-altitude residents have shown that the gene SENP1 (SUMO-specific protease 1) is expressed at significantly higher levels in individuals who develop chronic mountain sickness. Elevated SENP1 enhances de-SUMOylation and stabilization of HIF-α proteins, boosting erythropoietin signaling and driving the excessive erythrocytosis characteristic of the disorder. Thus, increased SENP1 expression is closely correlated with the presence and severity of chronic mountain sickness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coccidioides immitis",
    "long_answer": "Coccidioides immitis is a dimorphic soil fungus endemic to the arid regions of the southwestern United States (e.g., Arizona, California’s San Joaquin Valley). Infection (coccidioidomycosis or \"Valley Fever\") often triggers an immune-mediated reaction that manifests as erythema nodosum—sometimes called “desert bumps.” This geographic distribution and characteristic skin finding firmly link Coccidioides immitis to erythema nodosum in that region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ortner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ortner syndrome, also called cardiovocal syndrome, occurs when an enlarged cardiovascular structure (most often a dilated left atrium, pulmonary artery or aortic arch) compresses the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it loops under the aortic arch. This nerve palsy paralyses the vocal cord, leading not only to hoarseness but also to inspiratory stridor and shortness of breath because of impaired glottic opening. Thus dyspnea produced by recurrent-laryngeal-nerve compression is characteristic of Ortner syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Nephronophthisis typically produces small, echogenic kidneys with cysts at the corticomedullary junction. These characteristic structural changes are readily detected by renal (kidney) ultrasound, making ultrasonography a practical and widely used diagnostic tool for confirming nephronophthisis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Periapical (radicular) cyst",
    "long_answer": "Dentin dysplasia type I often leads to early pulpal necrosis and chronic periapical inflammation, producing multiple periapical radiolucencies that represent periapical, or radicular, cysts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced vaccination coverage",
    "long_answer": "COVID-19–related lockdowns, health-system diversion and supply-chain breaks interrupted immunization programs, causing global first-dose measles-containing vaccine (MCV1) coverage to fall from 86 % in 2019 to 83 % in 2020 and 81 % in 2021—the lowest in over a decade—leaving roughly 24–25 million children without their routine measles shots."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRNP gene",
    "long_answer": "Familial forms of prion diseases—such as familial Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, Gerstmann-Sträussler-Scheinker syndrome, and fatal familial insomnia—are caused by pathogenic mutations in the PRNP gene on chromosome 20, which encodes the prion protein (PrP). Missense, insertion, or stop-codon mutations in PRNP lead to an autosomal-dominant predisposition to prion misfolding and neurodegeneration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Sandhoff disease is a GM2 gangliosidosis that produces progressive neurodegeneration, seizures, spasticity, and other central-nervous-system manifestations. Because its presentation, diagnosis (enzyme assays, neuroimaging), and ongoing symptomatic care (seizure control, spasticity management, developmental assessment) are centered on the nervous system, the specialty most directly responsible for diagnosing and managing affected patients is Neurology (typically pediatric neurology)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCS1L",
    "long_answer": "GRACILE syndrome, one of the Finnish heritage diseases, is caused by recessive mutations in the BCS1L gene, which encodes a mitochondrial inner-membrane chaperone essential for proper assembly of respiratory chain complex III. Defective BCS1L leads to impaired oxidative phosphorylation, explaining the syndrome’s growth retardation, lactic acidosis, and multi-organ failure characteristic of GRACILE patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygous (Hb C/C)",
    "long_answer": "Hemoglobin C disease occurs when both β-globin genes carry the Hb C point mutation (β6 Glu→Lys). Thus the individual is homozygous for the Hb C allele (genotype CC), whereas heterozygotes (AC) have the benign Hb C trait."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Yes. XPB (also known as ERCC3) encodes one of the ATP-dependent helicase subunits of the transcription/repair factor TFIIH.  Pathogenic variants in XPB/ERCC3 have been repeatedly documented in patients with trichothiodystrophy (TTD), a rare neuroectodermal disorder characterized by sulfur-deficient brittle hair, ichthyosis, growth and developmental delay, and photosensitivity.  Along with XPD (ERCC2) and TTDA (GTF2H5), XPB is one of the three TFIIH genes whose loss-of-function mutations are firmly established causes of the photosensitive form of TTD in the medical genetics literature and in authoritative databases such as OMIM (TTD group 2, OMIM #616390).  Therefore, the statement that XPB is a gene linked to trichothiodystrophy is supported by well-accepted scientific evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Activated protein C resistance assay",
    "long_answer": "Activated protein C (APC) resistance is diagnosed with an aPTT-based functional test called the APC resistance assay, in which the prolongation of activated partial thromboplastin time after adding APC is measured; a reduced prolongation indicates APC resistance (most often due to the Factor V Leiden mutation)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Up-regulates OPG expression",
    "long_answer": "Estrogen binding to its receptor (mainly ERα) in osteoblast-lineage cells activates estrogen-responsive elements in the OPG promoter, leading to increased transcription, higher OPG mRNA and protein production, and consequent suppression of osteoclastogenesis. This estrogen-dependent up-regulation is blocked by anti-estrogens or ER knockdown, demonstrating that estrogen positively regulates OPG expression in osteoblasts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography",
    "long_answer": "High-resolution cranio-facial computed tomography clearly demonstrates whether the mandibular bones are completely absent; this imaging finding distinguishes true agnathia from conditions such as micrognathia or mandibulofacial dysostoses where the mandible is present but hypoplastic or malformed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciprofloxacin",
    "long_answer": "Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa is almost always caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, so therapy must have reliable antipseudomonal activity and good bone penetration. The fluoroquinolone ciprofloxacin is the standard first-line agent (given orally or intravenously for several weeks), achieving high tissue levels and demonstrating proven efficacy against P. aeruginosa in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPTLC1",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant form of hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy (HSAN type I) is most commonly caused by missense mutations in the SPTLC1 gene, which encodes the long-chain-base subunit-1 of serine palmitoyl-transferase. These mutations impair normal sphingolipid biosynthesis, leading to the sensory and autonomic deficits characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is the commonest red-cell enzyme defect producing episodic hemolytic anemia. Acute hemolysis is often precipitated by oxidative stress generated during infections, especially by ubiquitous pathogens such as influenza virus. Because preventing the infection eliminates this oxidative trigger, routine vaccination against influenza (and other common vaccine-preventable organisms) markedly reduces or completely prevents hemolytic crises in people with G6PD deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1",
    "long_answer": "Infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (the CLN1 form) results from mutations in the PPT1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1. Loss of PPT1 activity prevents removal of thioester-linked palmitate from proteins, leading to accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigments in neurons and the characteristic neurodegeneration of the infantile disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Thymomas arise from thymic epithelial cells, and the autoimmune disease most frequently linked to these tumors is myasthenia gravis. Approximately one-third of patients with thymoma develop myasthenia gravis, and thymectomy can improve neuromuscular symptoms, underscoring the close pathogenetic association between the tumor and the autoimmune disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATXN7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATXN7 gene cause spinocerebellar ataxia type 7, an autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia characterized by progressive ataxia together with pigmentary macular degeneration that leads to retinal degeneration and vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metabolic syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Metabolic syndrome is a clinical cluster that includes central (abdominal) obesity together with high blood pressure, insulin resistance or hyperglycaemia, and atherogenic dyslipidaemia. These inter-related cardiometabolic abnormalities commonly occur together, so the presence of both hypertension and abdominal obesity is a defining feature of metabolic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Glypican-1 (GPC1) has been implicated in biliary atresia by several genetic and functional studies; variants or reduced expression of GPC1 can disturb bile-duct development in animal models and human association studies have reported links to disease risk. However, the human GPC1 gene is not on chromosome 6. It is located on the long arm of chromosome 2 (2q37 region). Because the statement requires both conditions to be true, and the chromosomal location is incorrect, the overall claim is not accurate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is produced by biallelic mutations in the NBN (NBS1, nibrin) gene, which is located on chromosome 8 at cytogenetic band 8q21. Loss of nibrin disrupts the MRN complex needed for DNA double-strand break repair, causing the chromosomal instability, immunodeficiency and cancer predisposition characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral palsy",
    "long_answer": "Periventricular leukomalacia is a white-matter injury that primarily affects preterm infants. The resultant loss of oligodendrocytes and damage to descending corticospinal fibers near the ventricles disrupts motor pathway development, so survivors frequently go on to develop cerebral palsy—most commonly the spastic diplegic form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgG antibodies",
    "long_answer": "In the chronic (indeterminate or late) phase of Chagas disease, the parasite is rarely seen in blood, so diagnosis depends on serologic tests that detect long-lived, high-affinity IgG antibodies directed against Trypanosoma cruzi antigens. Therefore, detection of specific IgG antibodies is the standard method to confirm chronic Chagas infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POLG",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in POLG, the gene encoding the mitochondrial DNA polymerase γ, have been reported to give rise to an MNGIE-like phenotype featuring gastrointestinal dysmotility, cachexia, external ophthalmoplegia and peripheral neuropathy, yet with normal cerebral white matter on MRI—i.e., without the characteristic leukoencephalopathy seen in classic TYMP-related MNGIE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes insipidus",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) prevents normal water re-absorption in the kidney’s collecting ducts. This produces large volumes of dilute urine (polyuria), leading to excessive thirst, dehydration, and possible hypernatremia—classic features of central (ADH-deficient) diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone morphogenetic protein-2",
    "long_answer": "In myositis ossificans, local trauma and inflammation lead to over-expression of bone morphogenetic protein-2 (BMP-2). BMP-2 is a potent osteogenic growth factor that drives mesenchymal fibroblasts to differentiate into osteoblast-like cells, resulting in heterotopic (ectopic) bone formation within muscle tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infundibulum",
    "long_answer": "The infundibulum, also called the pituitary stalk, is the narrow stalk of neural tissue that carries the hypothalamo-hypophyseal (supraopticoparaventricular) tract from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary. A tuber cinereum hamartoma arises in the floor of the hypothalamus adjacent to this structure and can distort or interrupt the infundibulum, disrupting neuro-endocrine signaling between the hypothalamus and the posterior (neuro)-hypophysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuronal cell death",
    "long_answer": "In Alzheimer’s disease, intrinsic and extrinsic apoptotic pathways become aberrantly activated by factors such as amyloid-β oligomers, hyperphosphorylated tau, oxidative stress, and mitochondrial dysfunction. Caspase activation and other downstream events of apoptosis lead to systematic, programmed elimination of neurons and synaptic connections. This apoptosis-mediated neuronal cell death drives cortical and hippocampal neurodegeneration, directly contributing to the cognitive decline characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome is a neural-crest–related disorder of melanocyte development that produces patchy skin and hair depigmentation, heterochromia iridis and deafness. Although usually caused by a germ-line mutation in genes such as PAX3, MITF or SOX10, many patients have been described in whom the mutation arose post-zygotically; these individuals are somatic mosaics, showing the typical features only in portions of the body. Thus, Waardenburg syndrome is a genetic condition involving melanocyte development that can be associated with mosaicism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Aplasia cutis-myopia (Knobloch) syndrome results from biallelic pathogenic variants—most often in the COL18A1 gene—and is transmitted in an autosomal recessive manner, requiring both parents to be carriers for an affected child to manifest the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diamond-Blackfan anemia",
    "long_answer": "Diamond-Blackfan anemia (congenital pure red-cell aplasia) is frequently accompanied by congenital anomalies; approximately half of affected infants show physical malformations such as craniofacial defects, thumb abnormalities, short stature, and a webbed neck. This characteristic association makes Diamond-Blackfan anemia the congenital anemia linked with a webbed neck."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic short stature",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic short stature (ISS) denotes children whose height is ≥2 SD below the mean but in whom no endocrine, systemic, nutritional, chromosomal or skeletal disorder can be identified. ISS—encompassing familial short stature and constitutional delay of growth—is the single most frequent explanation when a diagnostic work-up fails to reveal an underlying pathology, making it the commonest cause of short stature without a definable diagnostic etiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lead poisoning",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome can be acquired when toxic levels of lead damage the renal proximal tubule. Lead poisoning produces direct tubular cell injury, leading to impaired re-absorption of glucose, amino acids, phosphate, and bicarbonate—hallmark features of Fanconi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Syringocystadenoma papilliferum is confirmed histologically. A skin biopsy of the lesion, followed by microscopic examination, reveals the characteristic papillary projections lined by two layers of epithelium with a dense plasma-cell-rich stroma, allowing definitive identification of this adnexal tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial septal defect",
    "long_answer": "Saito–Kuba–Tsuruta syndrome, also known as the Japanese type of heart-hand syndrome, is classically characterized by brachydactyly and a congenital heart malformation. The specific cardiac lesion repeatedly reported in the original and subsequent case descriptions is an atrial septal defect (ASD), making this the hallmark congenital heart defect associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Milia",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria cutanea tarda often heals with tiny, white keratin-filled cysts called milia. These superficial epidermal cysts also arise secondarily after skin injury such as ultraviolet (sun) damage or inflammatory blistering, making milia the shared condition in both scenarios."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type IV hypersensitivity",
    "long_answer": "The tuberculin (PPD) skin test elicits a delayed-type reaction mediated by sensitized Th1 T-lymphocytes that release cytokines and recruit/activate macrophages 24–72 hours after antigen exposure. This cellular, non-antibody–mediated response is the hallmark of a Type IV hypersensitivity reaction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography angiography",
    "long_answer": "Computed tomography angiography (CTA) provides high-resolution, contrast-enhanced images of the cervical and intracranial vasculature. By directly displaying the expected course of the carotid artery—and demonstrating that the vessel and its bony canal are absent—CTA can definitively diagnose carotid agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ragged red fiber myopathy",
    "long_answer": "In mitochondrial DNA defects, abnormal aggregates of mitochondria accumulate beneath the sarcolemma, producing the characteristic \"ragged-red\" appearance on muscle biopsy. This mitochondrial dysfunction reduces oxidative phosphorylation, so patients develop early fatigue and exercise intolerance. Therefore, the myopathy that typifies both mitochondrial disease and exercise intolerance is ragged red fiber myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular dementia",
    "long_answer": "After Alzheimer’s disease, the leading cause of chronic, progressive cognitive decline is vascular dementia. It arises from cerebrovascular pathology—such as multiple cortical or subcortical infarcts, strategic single-infarcts, or chronic small-vessel ischemic changes—that cumulatively damage brain tissue and disrupt neural circuits essential for memory and executive function. Epidemiological studies consistently place vascular dementia as the second most prevalent form of dementia worldwide, accounting for roughly 15–20 % of cases, behind Alzheimer’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCAF17",
    "long_answer": "Woodhouse-Sakati syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the DCAF17 gene (formerly C2orf37). Diagnostic molecular testing therefore relies on sequence analysis of DCAF17 to detect these mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increased self-diagnosis via social contagion",
    "long_answer": "The proliferation of YouTube channels featuring sensationalized or dramatized personal accounts of dissociative identity disorder has created a social-contagion effect: viewers, especially adolescents, adopt the language and behaviors shown online and begin to self-diagnose with DID. Clinicians report a surge of patients presenting with textbook-like ‘parts’ after heavy YouTube consumption, often without the developmental trauma or dissociative history usually required for a formal DSM-5 diagnosis. Thus, YouTube has shifted public perception toward thinking DID is common and easily self-identifiable, leading to more self-diagnoses and diagnostic confusion in clinical practice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oculodentodigital dysplasia",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-43, are a well-established cause of Oculodentodigital dysplasia (ODDD). This rare developmental syndrome frequently presents with ocular anomalies such as microphthalmia in addition to its characteristic dental, digital and facial features, linking GJA1 involvement to a microphthalmia-associated syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "X-linked Juvenile Retinoschisis is an inherited retinal disorder that causes splitting of the retinal layers and progressive loss of central vision. Diagnosis, monitoring (via optical coherence tomography, electroretinography) and management—including low-vision rehabilitation and potential future gene or retinal therapies—are handled by ophthalmologists, particularly retina subspecialists. Therefore, Ophthalmology is the medical specialty most directly involved in treating this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin A (retinoids) is used therapeutically in oral leukoplakia because its active metabolites modulate epithelial cell differentiation and keratinization, leading to regression of the premalignant lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical debridement",
    "long_answer": "Zygomycosis (mucormycosis) often invades tissue rapidly, so standard management combines antifungal drugs with surgical debridement—physical excision of necrotic, infected tissue—to reduce fungal load and improve drug penetration, making it a common and essential treatment step."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone conduction hearing aid",
    "long_answer": "In anotia the external ear canal is absent, so air-conduction amplification cannot reach the cochlea. Because the cochlea and auditory nerve are intact, sound can be efficiently transmitted through the skull. Therefore, a bone conduction (bone-anchored) hearing aid, which vibrates the skull to deliver sound directly to the inner ear, is the standard recommendation for affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC8 (CSA)",
    "long_answer": "Cockayne syndrome has several genetic subtypes. The comparatively moderate, so-called type A form is caused by biallelic mutations in the ERCC8 gene, which encodes the CSA protein involved in transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision repair. Patients with ERCC8/CSA defects typically manifest a milder clinical course than those with ERCC6/CSB mutations, making ERCC8 the gene linked to the more moderate form of Cockayne syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal bone",
    "long_answer": "Trigonocephaly arises from premature fusion of the metopic suture that joins the two halves of the frontal bone. Surgical correction (fronto-orbital advancement) therefore involves removing and remodeling the fused frontal bone segments, reshaping them to restore a normal, rounded forehead contour."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sotos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is an overgrowth-intellectual-disability disorder most often caused by NSD1 mutations. Besides characteristic postnatal overgrowth, macrocephaly and developmental delay, up to 15–20 % of affected males have genitourinary anomalies; reported abnormalities include cryptorchidism and hypospadias. Thus Sotos syndrome is the overgrowth/ID syndrome that can present with hypospadias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Usher syndrome mutations",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent inherited cause of combined deafness and blindness from birth in the United States is Usher syndrome, produced by pathogenic variants in the USH gene family (e.g., USH2A, MYO7A, USH1C, CDH23, PCDH15). These mutations lead to congenital sensorineural hearing loss and early-onset retinitis pigmentosa, accounting for the majority of congenital deafblindness cases nationwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome is a congenital disorder typically caused by mutations in the CREBBP or EP300 genes. A cardinal physical feature is the presence of broad, angulated thumbs and great toes, accompanied by facial dysmorphism, growth retardation, and marked developmental delay or intellectual disability. These hallmark findings distinguish it from other syndromes with overlapping features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macula lutea",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome frequently produces a characteristic ‘dot-and-fleck’ retinopathy and thinning that localize to the macula. The macula lutea, especially its central fovea, is the retinal area that mediates central, high-resolution, color vision, so pathology here explains the visual findings in Alport patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gabapentin",
    "long_answer": "Gabapentin is an anticonvulsant that has been reported to stop the intermittent torsional oscillations of superior oblique myokymia. Beyond this ophthalmic use, gabapentin is an established therapy for focal (partial-onset) seizures and is widely prescribed for neuropathic pain syndromes such as post-herpetic neuralgia and diabetic neuropathy, making it the medication that fits all three indications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈30% five-year survival",
    "long_answer": "Population-based SEER and American Cancer Society data show that men whose prostate cancer has already spread to distant organs have a five-year relative survival of roughly 30 % (about 3 in 10 patients). Localized or regional disease carries virtually 100 % five-year survival, but once metastases are present the prognosis drops sharply to this ~30 % figure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Astrocytes",
    "long_answer": "In gangliogliomas, malignant progression almost always arises from the glial component, and specifically the astrocytic glial cells are the ones that undergo anaplastic transformation, giving rise to anaplastic ganglioglioma or glioblastoma-like changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2B and Homocystinuria",
    "long_answer": "A Marfanoid body habitus (tall stature, long limbs, arachnodactyly, joint laxity) is classically seen in two well-defined conditions: Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2B, caused by activating RET proto-oncogene mutations, and Homocystinuria due to cystathionine-β-synthase deficiency. Both disorders can mimic the skeletal appearance of Marfan syndrome while having distinct biochemical and clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Compression therapy",
    "long_answer": "In Parkes Weber syndrome, the affected limb is chronically hyper-perfused and prone to venous congestion and edema, which drive progressive soft-tissue and bony overgrowth. Regular use of graduated compression garments or bandaging (compression therapy) counteracts venous stasis, reduces swelling, and can slow the hypertrophic enlargement of the limb, making it a standard supportive measure to manage limb overgrowth in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "L2HGDH",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the L2HGDH gene, which encodes L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase, prevent conversion of L-2-hydroxyglutarate to α-ketoglutarate. The resulting accumulation of L-2-hydroxyglutarate produces L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria. Patients classically present in infancy or early childhood with neurologic features that include global developmental delay and generalized hypotonia, linking a single L2HGDH mutation to both 2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria and hypotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Joubert syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Joubert syndrome is a primary ciliopathy: mutations in genes that encode proteins of the primary cilium–basal body complex disrupt ciliary signalling. This leads to malformation of the cerebellar vermis and brain‐stem (‘molar-tooth sign’ on MRI), producing hypotonia and the characteristic truncal and gait ataxia seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Peyronie’s disease causes fibrous plaque formation in the tunica albuginea of the penis, leading to penile curvature and erectile dysfunction. Disorders of the male genital tract are evaluated and managed by urologists—the surgical specialists in urinary and male reproductive systems—who provide both medical therapies (e.g., intralesional collagenase) and corrective penile surgery. Therefore, urology is the medical specialty responsible for treating Peyronie’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous penicillin G",
    "long_answer": "Neurosyphilis is treated with high-dose aqueous crystalline penicillin G given intravenously (typically 18–24 million units/day, 3–4 million units every 4 hours for 10–14 days). This regimen achieves treponemicidal concentrations in the cerebrospinal fluid, eradicates Treponema pallidum from the central nervous system, and is endorsed by CDC and WHO guidelines as the standard therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coal dust",
    "long_answer": "Coal worker's pneumoconiosis (also called ‘black lung disease’) develops after chronic inhalation of coal dust particles in underground or surface mining. The carbon-rich coal dust accumulates in the lungs, leading to macrophage activation, fibrosis, and characteristic nodular lesions, which together produce the clinical and radiographic picture of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WT1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Congenital nephrotic syndrome that results from germ-line mutations in the Wilms tumor 1 (WT1) tumor-suppressor gene—as seen in Denys-Drash syndrome—confers an exceptionally high lifetime risk of Wilms’ tumor. Because of this risk, affected infants are routinely offered prophylactic bilateral nephrectomy to remove all renal tissue and thus prevent the development of Wilms’ tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Andersen disease",
    "long_answer": "Dorothy Hansine Andersen first described Glycogen Storage Disease Type IV, now commonly called Andersen disease. The disorder results from loss-of-function mutations in the GBE1 gene, which encodes the glycogen-branching enzyme. Defective GBE1 impairs normal glycogen branching, leading to accumulation of abnormal, poorly branched glycogen in liver, muscle, and other tissues, which underlies the clinical manifestations of Andersen disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketotic hypoglycaemia",
    "long_answer": "Because hepatic glycogen phosphorylase is deficient in Glycogen Storage Disease VI, liver glycogen cannot be mobilised to maintain blood-glucose levels during fasting. Consequently, patients develop hypoglycaemia, and to supply energy the body accelerates fatty-acid oxidation, generating excess ketone bodies. This results in the characteristic metabolic state of ketotic hypoglycaemia during periods of fasting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Large T antigen",
    "long_answer": "In polyomaviruses the early-region transcript undergoes alternative splicing to generate several proteins; the principal splice product is the Large T antigen. Large T antigen interacts with key cellular regulators such as p53 and the retinoblastoma (Rb) family proteins, driving the host cell into S phase and thereby controlling the cell cycle to favor viral replication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tandem mass spectrometry",
    "long_answer": "In modern newborn-screening programs, dried blood spots are analyzed by tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS). This technique measures characteristic acyl-carnitine and amino-acid patterns that reveal the accumulation of specific organic acids, allowing early detection of organic acidemias such as methylmalonic, propionic and isovaleric acidemia in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ADGRV1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ADGRV1 gene (also known as VLGR1/GPR98) cause Usher syndrome type II (subtype USH2C) and, in animal and human studies, disrupt maturation and organization of the auditory cortex, leading to cortical auditory processing defects. ADGRV1 encodes the very-large adhesion GPCR VLGR1, expressed in inner-ear hair cells, retina, and developing cerebral cortex; its disruption therefore links the peripheral Usher phenotype with central auditory cortex developmental abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "nevus sebaceous",
    "long_answer": "Schimmelpenning (Schimmelpenning-Feuerstein-Mims) syndrome is a type of epidermal-nevus syndrome in which the hallmark cutaneous lesion is a linear nevus sebaceous of Jadassohn, typically present at birth and following Blaschko’s lines. This sebaceous nevus is the dermatologic finding classically associated with the multisystem manifestations of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increases afterload",
    "long_answer": "High systemic arterial pressure means the left ventricle must pump against a greater resistance. This elevated vascular resistance raises the afterload—the pressure the failing heart must overcome to eject blood—thereby further straining the myocardium and reducing stroke volume in heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Williams syndrome commonly causes cardiovascular abnormalities such as supravalvular aortic stenosis and other congenital vascular lesions. These heart defects are typically evaluated and managed by physicians trained in cardiology (often pediatric cardiologists in children), making cardiology the specialty most likely to treat the cardiac problems associated with Williams syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX22",
    "long_answer": "Abruzzo–Erickson syndrome was re-classified as the same X-linked disorder now known as cleft palate with or without ankyloglossia (CPX). Both conditions are produced by loss-of-function mutations in the T-box transcription-factor gene TBX22; affected males frequently show cleft palate and the tongue-tie (ankyloglossia) phenotype, confirming that TBX22 mutation is responsible for both syndromic presentations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diaphysis",
    "long_answer": "Weismann-Netter–Stuhl syndrome is a diaphyseal dysplasia: the characteristic broadening and anterior bowing chiefly affect the shafts (diaphyses) of the tibiae and fibulae, and sometimes other long bones. Thus, the primary abnormality is located in the diaphysis of these bones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inferior oblique muscle",
    "long_answer": "In many cases of childhood hypertropia, the vertical deviation is produced by overaction of the inferior oblique extra-ocular muscle. Excessive contraction of this muscle elevates the eye in adduction, creating the characteristic up-drift (hypertropia) frequently seen in infantile strabismus and congenital superior oblique palsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Severe aplastic anemia caused by bone-marrow failure is treated definitively with allogeneic bone marrow (hematopoietic stem-cell) transplantation, which replaces the patient’s depleted marrow with healthy donor stem cells capable of re-establishing normal blood cell production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Directly proportional",
    "long_answer": "Noise-induced hearing loss follows a dose-response pattern: the higher the sound intensity and the longer the exposure, the greater the irreversible damage to cochlear hair cells and therefore the greater the hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sulfur tetrafluoride",
    "long_answer": "Sulfur tetrafluoride (SF₄) is a powerful electrophilic fluorinating agent. In organic synthesis it substitutes the oxygen of –C-OH groups to give C–F bonds and replaces a carbonyl oxygen (C=O) with two fluorine atoms to generate a –CF₂– unit. Industrially, SF₄ is also applied in processes that make acetic-acid-based products, such as the preparation of fluoroacetic acid used in acetic-acid chemistry. These properties make sulfur tetrafluoride the reagent described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydrocephalus, endocardial fibroelastosis and cataracts",
    "long_answer": "HEC syndrome is defined by the triad of congenital hydrocephalus (or severe ventriculomegaly), diffuse endocardial fibroelastosis of the heart, and bilateral congenital cataracts; these three findings constitute its diagnostic hallmarks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cephalic disorder",
    "long_answer": "Hydranencephaly is a rare congenital brain malformation in which most of the cerebral hemispheres are absent and replaced by cerebrospinal fluid. Because it arises from abnormal development of the fetal head and brain, it is classified within the group of congenital neurodevelopmental conditions known as cephalic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disease X",
    "long_answer": "The World Health Organization introduced the placeholder term “Disease X” in its list of priority diseases to represent any as-yet-unknown pathogen that could emerge and trigger a serious international epidemic, underscoring the need for preparedness even when the exact microbe is not yet identified."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teeth",
    "long_answer": "Barodontalgia is a toothache precipitated by changes in atmospheric pressure, such as those experienced during ascent or descent in an aircraft cabin. The pressure differential acts on air trapped within the dental pulp or under restorations, producing acute pain specifically in the teeth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol",
    "long_answer": "Calcinogenic plants like Cestrum diurnum and Solanum malacoxylon contain the active vitamin D3 metabolite 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol). Ingesting this compound drives excessive intestinal calcium absorption and bone resorption, producing persistent hypercalcemia in affected animals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Triangular face",
    "long_answer": "Tsao-Ellingson syndrome is a rare growth- and neurodevelopmental disorder whose clinical descriptions consistently note a distinctive triangular facial configuration—broad forehead with the face tapering to a narrow chin. This triangular face is one of the most frequently reported and recognizable craniofacial features in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchiectasis",
    "long_answer": "Patients with hypogammaglobulinemia lack sufficient immunoglobulins to clear respiratory pathogens, leading to recurrent sinopulmonary infections. Repeated infections and inflammation damage the bronchial walls, producing permanent airway dilation known as bronchiectasis, which is one of the most frequent chronic lung complications seen in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PKLR gene impair the erythrocyte-specific isoform of pyruvate kinase, the enzyme that catalyzes conversion of phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate with generation of ATP in glycolysis. Loss of this activity lowers ATP levels in red blood cells, causing the well-known disorder pyruvate kinase deficiency and resulting in chronic hemolytic anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cervical spine",
    "long_answer": "The cervical portion of the spinal canal contains the high-level segments of the spinal cord that control all four limbs and the phrenic nerve roots for respiration, yet the bony canal here is relatively narrow. Consequently, even modest deformation of the thecal sac in the cervical region can quickly compress the cord and produce life-threatening or catastrophic neurologic deficits, making the cervical spine the area most at risk for serious symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ofloxacin",
    "long_answer": "Ofloxacin, a broad-spectrum fluoroquinolone antibiotic, is one of the standard oral agents recommended (usually together with metronidazole) for outpatient management of mild-to-moderate pelvic inflammatory disease. Because it inhibits bacterial DNA-gyrase and topoisomerase IV, ofloxacin is active against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, making it useful for many other systemic and genitourinary infections as well."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Near-normal life expectancy",
    "long_answer": "Scheie syndrome (the attenuated form of mucopolysaccharidosis type I) progresses slowly; intelligence is normal and serious organ failure is uncommon. Most affected individuals reach adulthood and can live a near-normal lifespan, though they may develop treatable complications such as joint stiffness, corneal clouding, or cardiac valvular disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertensive retinopathy",
    "long_answer": "Sustained systemic hypertension damages retinal arterioles, leading to arteriolar narrowing, hemorrhages, exudates and sometimes macular edema. This pathology—termed hypertensive retinopathy—compromises the retinal photoreceptors and neural tissue, resulting in reduced visual acuity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cannabis sativa",
    "long_answer": "Case reports describe that smoking or ingesting Cannabis sativa (marijuana) markedly lessens the frequency and intensity of paroxysmal non-kinesigenic dyskinesia attacks. Cannabinoids act on CB1 receptors within basal-ganglia circuitry, dampening abnormal neuronal excitability and thereby ‘softening’ the dyskinetic episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Patent ductus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "Ramos-Arroyo syndrome has been reported to include congenital heart defects, most notably patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). PDA results when the fetal ductus arteriosus—a blood vessel that normally shunts blood from the pulmonary artery to the aorta—does not close after birth, leading to abnormal circulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Onychogryphosis is a disorder of the nail unit characterized by marked hypertrophy and curvature of the nail plate. Diseases of the nails are within the scope of dermatology, the medical specialty that diagnoses and treats conditions of the skin, hair, and nails. Severe congenital cases often require a dermatologist for evaluation, conservative debridement, or surgical nail ablation, sometimes in coordination with a dermatologic or plastic surgeon. Thus, a dermatologist is the specialist most likely to treat severe congenital onychogryphosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Severe metabolic acidosis",
    "long_answer": "3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency is an organic acidemia; during catabolic stress, accumulation of organic acids (e.g., 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid, 3-methylcrotonylglycine) can overwhelm the body’s buffering systems and lead to a life-threatening episode of severe metabolic acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Femur",
    "long_answer": "Facial femoral (femoral-facial) syndrome, also termed femoral hypoplasia–unusual facies syndrome, is characterized by marked hypoplasia or complete absence of the femur in one or both legs. This consistent femoral underdevelopment is the hallmark skeletal abnormality of the disorder, making the femur the principal bone affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Creatine kinase",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Neutral Lipid Storage Disease frequently show increased serum creatine kinase (CK) activity, a reflection of the underlying skeletal-muscle involvement that characterizes both the ichthyosis and myopathy variants of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine",
    "long_answer": "The pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (e.g., PCV13/PCV15/PCV20) targets multiple serotypes of Streptococcus pneumoniae, the bacterium responsible for pneumococcal meningitis as well as pneumonia, sepsis, and otitis media. By inducing serotype-specific antibodies and mucosal immunity, the vaccine markedly reduces invasive pneumococcal disease, including meningitis, in vaccinated populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈43%",
    "long_answer": "A population-based twin study reported in Web of Science (Monzani et al., 2011, Twin Research and Human Genetics) found that additive genetic factors explained about 43 % of the variance in dysmorphic concerns, giving an estimated heritability of roughly 0.43 for body dysmorphic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Adrenoleukodystrophy is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the ABCD1 gene, which is located on the long arm of the X chromosome (region Xq28). Hence, the disease is linked to the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudoephedrine",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoephedrine is a sympathomimetic decongestant commonly found in cold and allergy remedies (e.g., Sudafed). By increasing presynaptic release of norepinephrine, it stimulates α- and β-adrenergic receptors, which can raise heart rate and produce CNS activation. This adrenergic surge may precipitate anxiety, jitteriness, and, in susceptible individuals, full-blown panic attacks, making pseudoephedrine a well-recognized trigger for such events while treating nasal congestion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDKN1C (p57KIP2)",
    "long_answer": "The gene CDKN1C, also known as p57KIP2, resides in the imprinted 11p15 region implicated in Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. CDKN1C encodes a cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor that suppresses G1-to-S phase progression, thereby acting as a negative regulator of cell proliferation. Loss or reduced expression of this maternally expressed gene contributes to the overgrowth features characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Psychiatric Association",
    "long_answer": "The diagnostic criteria for opioid use disorder are contained in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5). This manual—and therefore the official guidelines clinicians use to diagnose opioid use disorder—is published by the American Psychiatric Association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Baylor College of Medicine",
    "long_answer": "Researchers at Baylor College of Medicine genetically engineered a mouse carrying a duplication of the chromosomal region orthologous to human 17p11.2, creating the first animal model of Potocki–Lupski syndrome. This model, developed in Dr. James R. Lupski’s laboratory, reproduces the gene-dosage increase (including RAI1) that causes PTLS, allowing detailed study of the syndrome’s neurodevelopmental and metabolic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Latanoprost",
    "long_answer": "Glaucoma in Sturge-Weber syndrome arises from elevated episcleral venous pressure, which impairs the conventional trabecular outflow pathway. The prostaglandin-F2α analogue latanoprost lowers intra-ocular pressure by enhancing uveoscleral outflow, a route that bypasses the episcleral venous system. Because of this pressure-independent mechanism, topical latanoprost is widely used and considered first-line pharmacologic therapy for the glaucoma associated with Sturge-Weber syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-ray radiography",
    "long_answer": "Bhaskar–Jagannathan syndrome produces characteristic skeletal abnormalities that can be visualised on plain X-ray films. X-ray radiography uses high-energy electromagnetic radiation (X-rays) to penetrate tissues and create images of bones, making it a suitable diagnostic tool to detect the syndrome’s bony changes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brainstem",
    "long_answer": "In Leigh syndrome, French-Canadian type, mutations in the LRPPRC gene cause mitochondrial respiratory-chain failure that leads to progressive loss of the myelin sheath in the white matter of the brainstem, producing the characteristic neurologic signs of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ameloblasts",
    "long_answer": "During amelogenesis, the enamel-forming stage of tooth development, tall columnar epithelial cells called ameloblasts synthesize and secrete the enamel matrix proteins (e.g., amelogenin, enamelin, ameloblastin) onto the developing tooth surface. These proteins later mineralize to form mature enamel, making ameloblasts the definitive cells responsible for enamel protein secretion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Catechol-O-methyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "The COMT gene encodes the enzyme catechol-O-methyltransferase, which catalyzes the O-methylation of catecholamines such as dopamine, epinephrine, and norepinephrine, thereby contributing to their metabolic degradation and clearance from neural and peripheral tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex (mutations in TSC1 or TSC2) is an autosomal-dominant disorder whose cutaneous hallmarks include facial angiofibromas (adenoma sebaceum) that frequently resemble or coexist with acneiform lesions. Thus this single genetic disease is classically linked with both acne-like eruptions and true angiofibromas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈0.3 per 1 000 000",
    "long_answer": "According to the epidemiological data collected by Orphanet for Fryns syndrome, the prevalence calculated from the number of diagnosed patients in France is about 0.3 cases per 1 000 000 inhabitants, underscoring the extreme rarity of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈75%",
    "long_answer": "Twin- and family-based studies in European Union populations consistently estimate that about three-quarters of the variance in liability to attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder is genetic. Meta-analyses of EU cohorts place the narrow-sense heritability of ADHD in the 70–80 % range, yielding a pooled point estimate of roughly 75 %, which is the figure commonly cited by European public-health authorities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonioscopy",
    "long_answer": "Gonioscopy uses a special contact lens to visualize the anterior chamber angle directly. This allows the clinician to see whether the iris is abnormally adherent to the trabecular meshwork or cornea—peripheral anterior synechia—making gonioscopy the standard technique for detecting synechiae in the eye."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schizencephaly",
    "long_answer": "Schizencephaly is a cortical brain malformation in which abnormal, full-thickness clefts extend from the ventricular surface to the pia mater and are lined by dysplastic grey matter. It is commonly associated with optic nerve hypoplasia and other midline defects, fitting the description given."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ivemark syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Right atrial isomerism accompanied by absence of the spleen defines Ivemark syndrome, a heterotaxy disorder in which bilateral right-sided (right atrial) morphology is associated with congenital heart malformations and asplenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Head impulse test",
    "long_answer": "The head impulse (head-thrust) test evaluates the vestibulo-ocular reflex. An abnormal response— a corrective saccade after the rapid head movement— shows that the peripheral vestibular apparatus or VIII-cranial nerve is impaired, thereby indicating a peripheral rather than central problem."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibrates",
    "long_answer": "Clinical guidelines for severe hypertriglyceridemia, including the genetic form familial hypertriglyceridemia, recommend fibric-acid derivatives (fibrates such as fenofibrate or gemfibrozil) as first-line drug therapy. Fibrates activate PPAR-α, up-regulating lipoprotein lipase and enhancing VLDL catabolism, which typically lowers plasma triglycerides by 30–50 %, thereby rapidly reducing the risk of pancreatitis associated with very high triglyceride levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sleep drunkenness",
    "long_answer": "In idiopathic hypersomnia and other hypersomnolence disorders, patients often experience sleep drunkenness (also termed sleep inertia), a prolonged period of confusion and impaired alertness after awakening. This awakening disturbance typically includes marked irritability, linking the symptom directly to the complaint of irritability in hypersomniac patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parkinson’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Parkinson’s disease is a neurodegenerative movement disorder in which patients exhibit involuntary muscle activity—most notably the classic resting tremor—and simultaneous hypokinetic features such as bradykinesia and overall reduced spontaneous movement. The combination of hyperkinetic (tremor, rigidity) and hypokinetic (slowness, decreased amplitude of movement) signs is characteristic of this dopaminergic basal ganglia disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia",
    "long_answer": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency causes episodic hemolysis, flooding the circulation with unconjugated bilirubin. Gilbert’s syndrome, due to reduced UGT1A1 activity, impairs hepatic conjugation of that bilirubin. Together, the excess production plus impaired clearance can drive bilirubin levels abnormally high, producing severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (marked jaundice with risk of kernicterus)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "m.3243A>G in MT-TL1",
    "long_answer": "The m.3243A>G point mutation in the mitochondrial tRNA-Leu(UUR) gene (MT-TL1) is the classic pathogenic change in MELAS syndrome and is also well documented in patients who show a combined MELAS/MERRF clinical picture. This single nucleotide substitution impairs mitochondrial protein synthesis, leading to the overlapping encephalomyopathic features characteristic of both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellar vermis",
    "long_answer": "Joubert syndrome is a congenital neurodevelopmental disorder characterized on brain MRI by the so-called “molar tooth sign,” which reflects marked hypoplasia or complete agenesis of the midline portion of the cerebellum—the cerebellar vermis. This underdevelopment or absence of the cerebellar vermis disrupts normal brainstem–cerebellar connections and accounts for the ataxia, hypotonia, and abnormal eye movements seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin of the lower leg",
    "long_answer": "After mating, the gravid female Dracunculus medinensis migrates through connective tissues and locates in the subcutaneous tissue of the distal lower limbs—most often the skin of the lower leg, ankle, or foot—where it produces a painful blister that ulcerates before the worm emerges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Splenectomy",
    "long_answer": "In patients with Evans syndrome who fail or relapse after first-line immunosuppressive therapy (e.g., corticosteroids, IVIG), removal of the spleen—splenectomy—is a recognized treatment option. The spleen is a major site where antibody-coated erythrocytes and platelets are destroyed; its surgical removal can reduce hemolysis and thrombocytopenia, leading to durable remissions in a subset of cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Teratoma",
    "long_answer": "In epidemiologic series of pediatric germ cell tumors, teratomas account for the great majority of cases presenting during the neonatal period, infancy, and the first 3–4 years of life. These tumors, composed of mature or immature derivatives of all three germ layers and most often arising in the sacrococcygeal region, are far more frequent than other germ cell tumor subtypes (e.g., yolk-sac tumor, embryonal carcinoma) in this age group, making teratoma the most common germ cell tumor diagnosed in neonates, infants, and children younger than 4 years."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dapsone",
    "long_answer": "Dapsone is a sulfone antibiotic with anti-inflammatory properties. Clinically, it is employed to control cutaneous small-vessel (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis by inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis and oxidative burst, thereby reducing vessel wall damage. The same drug is a cornerstone of multidrug therapy for Mycobacterium leprae infection (leprosy), where it inhibits folate synthesis in the bacillus. Thus, a single agent—dapsone—serves both indications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae",
    "long_answer": "Organizing pneumonia is usually idiopathic, but it can occur secondarily after certain infections. One well-documented infectious trigger is Mycoplasma pneumoniae, an atypical bacterium; pulmonary infection with this organism can lead to the development of secondary (formerly called cryptogenic) organizing pneumonia through persistent airway–alveolar inflammation and fibroblast proliferation within the distal airspaces."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staghorn calculus",
    "long_answer": "A staghorn calculus is a large, branched renal stone that occupies the renal pelvis and extends into multiple renal calyces, resembling the antlers of a stag. This term specifically denotes kidney stones that spread into the calyceal system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Capillary hemangioma",
    "long_answer": "Capillary hemangioma is a benign vascular tumour composed of proliferating capillaries. It usually presents as a red-to-purple lesion in the skin (the classic “strawberry” birthmark). When the lesion involves the eyelid or orbit, the expanding capillary mass can occlude the visual axis, induce astigmatism or compress the optic nerve, and, if untreated, may result in irreversible amblyopia and blindness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTEN",
    "long_answer": "Bannayan–Riley–Ruvalcaba syndrome is part of the PTEN-hamartoma tumor syndrome spectrum. Germline loss-of-function mutations in the tumor-suppressor gene PTEN (chromosome 10q23) disrupt PI3K/AKT signaling, leading to multiple hamartomas, including cutaneous and visceral hemangiomas characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Segmental demyelination",
    "long_answer": "Radiation therapy can injure Schwann cells, leading to loss of myelin over discrete segments of peripheral nerve fibres while sparing the axons. This pattern of injury is termed segmental demyelination and is a recognised radiation-induced neuropathic change."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA (lamin A/C)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear-envelope proteins lamin A and lamin C, are a frequent cause of autosomal-dominant limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 1B in northern European populations. The same LMNA mutations also produce the classical autosomal-dominant form of Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (EDMD2), linking the two clinical entities through a shared molecular defect in lamin A/C."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dasatinib",
    "long_answer": "Dasatinib is a second-generation BCR-ABL tyrosine-kinase inhibitor. It is specifically approved for Philadelphia chromosome-positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+ CML) in patients whose disease is resistant to, or who cannot tolerate, first-line treatment with imatinib. By inhibiting BCR-ABL with higher potency and binding both active and inactive kinase conformations, dasatinib overcomes most imatinib-resistance mutations and provides an effective therapeutic option for these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus is the chief bacterial cause of skin abscesses and furuncles (boils). When this bacterium invades tissue it can trigger systemic inflammatory responses, leading to symptoms such as fever, malaise and pronounced fatigue in addition to the localized boil."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alcian blue stain",
    "long_answer": "Macular corneal dystrophy features accumulations of acid mucopolysaccharides (glycosaminoglycans) within the corneal stroma and endothelium. These deposits bind the cationic dye Alcian blue at pH 2.5, giving an intense blue coloration, whereas other routine stains are less specific. Therefore, Alcian blue histochemical staining is routinely used to demonstrate these deposits and confirm the diagnosis of Macular Corneal Dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura",
    "long_answer": "Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is a hematologic disorder characterized by immune-mediated destruction of platelets, leading to marked thrombocytopenia. When corticosteroids or other medical therapies fail to maintain adequate platelet counts, removal of the spleen—the primary site of platelet destruction and autoantibody production—can provide long-term remission, making splenectomy a well-established second-line treatment for ITP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL9A1",
    "long_answer": "Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (MED) is genetically heterogeneous; one of its established causative genes is COL9A1, which encodes the α1 chain of type IX collagen. COL9A1 is mapped to chromosome 6q13, and pathogenic variants in this gene are known to produce a form of MED characterized by delayed ossification and joint pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Restless legs syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ekbom syndrome is the historical eponym for restless legs syndrome, a neurological sensorimotor disorder in which uncomfortable leg sensations create an irresistible urge to move the limbs, especially at night. First detailed by Swedish neurologist Karl-Axel Ekbom in the 1940s, it is now also termed Willis–Ekbom disease, reflecting its established place in neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Normal phenylalanine levels",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant GTP-cyclohydrolase I deficiency retains sufficient enzyme activity to support phenylalanine hydroxylase, so blood phenylalanine remains normal. Because routine newborn screening for BH₄-related disorders depends on detecting hyperphenylalaninemia, the absence of elevated phenylalanine means the condition is not flagged and can be missed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant inheritance",
    "long_answer": "Heart-hand syndrome type 5, the form of Pierre Robin sequence that combines congenital heart defects with hand/upper-limb anomalies, is a familial disorder that segregates as an autosomal dominant trait; although the responsible gene has not yet been identified, transmission from an affected parent to affected offspring in successive generations establishes an autosomal dominant genetic basis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologists and cardiologists",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in AGK or, more rarely, SLC25A4 cause Sengers syndrome, whose cardinal signs are congenital cataracts and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. An ophthalmologist identifies the early-onset cataracts, while a cardiologist evaluates the cardiomyopathy; together their findings prompt molecular testing that confirms the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Cyclosporine is a calcineurin-inhibiting immunosuppressant that has been employed in immune-mediated myocarditis (e.g., giant-cell myocarditis) to dampen T-cell–driven myocardial injury, and it is a mainstay drug for preventing acute and chronic rejection following solid-organ transplantation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOTCH2",
    "long_answer": "Hajdu-Cheney syndrome, a rare connective-tissue disorder marked by progressive and severe bone resorption (acro-osteolysis and generalized osteoporosis), is caused by heterozygous truncating mutations in the NOTCH2 gene. Loss of the C-terminal PEST domain stabilizes NOTCH2, leading to excessive osteoclast activity and skeletal degradation, a well-documented finding in medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈4 hours",
    "long_answer": "The antithyroid drug most commonly prescribed for feline hyperthyroidism is methimazole. Pharmacokinetic studies in cats show that methimazole is cleared rapidly, with an elimination (biological) half-life of roughly 4 hours (reported range about 3–5 hours). This short half-life is why the drug is typically administered twice daily in cats to maintain adequate suppression of thyroid hormone synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heart failure",
    "long_answer": "Many congenital heart defects eventually produce volume or pressure overload of the myocardium, leading to ventricular dysfunction and clinical heart failure. Heart failure is also one of the leading reasons older adults are admitted to the hospital, making it a complication of congenital heart disease that is likewise a very common cause of hospitalization in the elderly population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Histidine",
    "long_answer": "Histidinemia results from deficiency of the enzyme histidase (histidine ammonia-lyase), which catalyzes the first step in the catabolic pathway of the amino acid histidine. Loss of this enzyme blocks histidine degradation, causing elevated levels of histidine in blood and urine; therefore, it is histidine metabolism that is specifically affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The drug that is the active metabolite of etretinate and is prescribed orally for severe psoriasis is acitretin. Pharmacokinetic studies consistently show that acitretin has a mean elimination half-life of roughly 49 hours (about 2 days), with reported ranges in most references of approximately 33–96 hours. Its predecessor, etretinate, is much more lipophilic and has a markedly longer half-life (≈120 days), but acitretin itself does not. Therefore, stating that the half-life of this drug is 4 days (≈96 hours) is not in line with the established consensus value of ~2 days, so the statement is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebellum",
    "long_answer": "Marinesco–Sjögren syndrome is characterized by progressive cerebellar atrophy with marked loss of Purkinje cells, leading to the cerebellar ataxia that typifies the disorder. Thus, the cerebellum is the principal brain region affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Retinal detachment",
    "long_answer": "Retinopathy of prematurity causes abnormal vascular proliferation and subsequent cicatricial changes in the peripheral retina. The resultant vitreoretinal scarring and traction persist throughout life, pre-disposing affected individuals to rhegmatogenous or tractional retinal detachment years after the initial neonatal disease has regressed. Consequently, adults who had ROP as infants have a markedly higher lifetime risk of developing retinal detachment compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Nance-Horan syndrome is an X-linked developmental disorder characterized by congenital cataracts, distinctive facial features, and dental anomalies. The disease-causing gene, NHS, is located on the short arm of the X chromosome (Xp22.13). Pathogenic variants in this X-linked gene, not in a gene on chromosome 7, are responsible for the syndrome. Therefore, the statement that Nance-Horan syndrome is caused by mutations on chromosome 7 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydroxyurea",
    "long_answer": "Hydroxyurea is an antineoplastic (chemotherapy) agent that readily crosses the placenta. Several documented first-trimester exposures in pregnant women treated for myeloproliferative disorders have resulted in infants born with severe limb reduction defects, including split-hand/split-foot malformations (ectrodactyly), alongside other anomalies such as microcephaly and ear defects. These clinical reports establish hydroxyurea as a teratogen capable of causing ectrodactyly in humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sodium bicarbonate",
    "long_answer": "Administering intravenous sodium bicarbonate alkalinizes the urine, reducing precipitation of myoglobin and iron-mediated free-radical injury in the renal tubules. This urinary alkalinization is commonly used as a prophylactic measure to lessen the risk of acute kidney injury in rhabdomyolysis-associated myoglobinuria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HESX1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the HESX1 homeobox gene, which encodes a transcription factor essential for early forebrain and pituitary development, are well documented to cause septo-optic dysplasia and can simultaneously lead to combined pituitary hormone deficiency, manifesting clinically as hypopituitarism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Café-au-lait spots",
    "long_answer": "McCune–Albright syndrome is classically characterized by the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, endocrine hyperfunction, and distinctive café-au-lait skin macules with irregular, “coast-of-Maine” borders. These pigmented lesions are a hallmark cutaneous feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gumma",
    "long_answer": "In the late (tertiary-like) phase of bejel—an endemic treponemal infection—patients may develop gummatous lesions, which are chronic granulomatous masses that can destroy skin, mucous membranes, and bone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type Ib, the most common form that produces marked hepatomegaly, is characterized by chronic neutropenia and defective neutrophil function. The standard therapy for this immunodeficiency component is recombinant granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF), which raises circulating neutrophil counts and improves their bactericidal activity, thereby reducing the frequency and severity of infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oncology",
    "long_answer": "Primitive neuroectodermal tumors are malignant neoplasms that require multimodal cancer therapy (surgery, chemotherapy, radiotherapy). Management is therefore directed by oncologists—often pediatric or neuro-oncology subspecialists—who coordinate systemic chemotherapy and adjuvant treatments for these aggressive tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wilson disease",
    "long_answer": "Wilson disease is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the ATP7B gene, leading to impaired biliary excretion of copper. The resulting systemic copper accumulation particularly affects the liver and basal ganglia/cerebellum. Neurological manifestations include tremor, dystonia and cerebellar ataxia, making Wilson disease a well-known genetic, copper-accumulation disorder that can present with ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rosacea",
    "long_answer": "Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory dermatosis of the central face in which biopsies and surface scrapings consistently show markedly higher densities of Demodex folliculorum mites than in normal skin. Proliferation of these mites is thought to trigger host inflammatory and vascular responses, leading to the characteristic persistent erythema, papules-pustules, telangiectasia and intermittent soft-tissue edema that gives the appearance of facial swelling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology-oncology",
    "long_answer": "Lymphomatoid granulomatosis is an Epstein-Barr virus–driven B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder that can progress to malignant lymphoma; its management typically requires systemic immunochemotherapy overseen by hematology-oncology specialists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Francisco Javier de Balmis",
    "long_answer": "Surgeon Francisco Javier de Balmis led the 1803-1810 Royal Philanthropic Vaccine Expedition, carrying live cowpox (vaccinia) in a relay of orphaned children and successfully introducing the smallpox vaccine throughout Spain’s dominions in North and South America."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Organization for Rare Disorders (NORD)",
    "long_answer": "Schimmelpenning syndrome is included in the rare-disease database maintained by the National Organization for Rare Disorders (NORD), which compiles and disseminates information on conditions classified as rare in the United States."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pacinian corpuscle",
    "long_answer": "Morton’s neuroma is an entrapment-type neuroma of the plantar interdigital nerve lying beneath the deep transverse metatarsal ligament. Histologic studies show that this nerve segment contains an unusually high density of Pacinian (lamellated) corpuscles—rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors that respond to deep pressure and vibration. Mechanical compression of these Pacinian corpuscles by weight-bearing or footwear provokes the sharp, electric-like pain characteristic of Morton’s neuroma, making them the mechanoreceptors most closely implicated in the condition’s painful symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "Gustavson syndrome, an X-linked neurodevelopmental disorder, frequently presents with recurrent seizure episodes. Epilepsy is defined by the occurrence of unprovoked, recurrent seizures. Thus, seizures are a common clinical feature shared by both Gustavson syndrome and epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLB1",
    "long_answer": "Both GM1 gangliosidoses and Morquio syndrome Type B (also called MPS IVB) are autosomal-recessive disorders caused by pathogenic variants in the GLB1 gene. GLB1 encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-galactosidase; different mutations in this single gene can reduce β-galactosidase activity to varying degrees, leading to the neurovisceral accumulation of GM1 ganglioside seen in GM1 gangliosidosis or the predominantly skeletal manifestations characteristic of Morquio Type B."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mandible",
    "long_answer": "In sclerosteosis (loss-of-function mutations in the SOST gene), bone formation is markedly increased throughout the skeleton, but the mandible shows the greatest and most conspicuous overgrowth, producing the characteristic massive jaw (mandibular hyperostosis) seen in nearly all patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced hypothalamic dopamine",
    "long_answer": "Chronic psychological or physical stress activates central stress pathways (CRH, serotonin, opioids), which dampen activity of the tuberoinfundibular dopaminergic neurons in the hypothalamus. Dopamine is the principal prolactin-inhibiting factor; when its release into the pituitary portal blood falls, the tonic inhibition on pituitary lactotrophs is lifted, causing sustained over-secretion of prolactin and thus hyperprolactinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ribs",
    "long_answer": "Jeune syndrome, or asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy, is defined by markedly shortened, horizontally oriented ribs that constrict the thoracic cage. Although other skeletal elements can be involved, the ribs are the primary bones affected, and their maldevelopment is what produces the characteristic small, narrow chest and respiratory compromise seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aplastic anemia",
    "long_answer": "The virus that causes fifth disease, Parvovirus B19, infects and destroys erythroid precursor cells in the bone marrow. This abrupt halt in red-blood-cell production can precipitate an aplastic crisis (pure red-cell aplasia), manifesting clinically as aplastic anemia, especially in individuals with underlying hemolytic disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita is a hereditary condition in which reduced fetal movement (fetal hypokinesia) leads to multiple joint contractures, muscle atrophy and fibrous replacement of muscle tissue. The combination of congenital joint deformities, progressive muscle degeneration and their primary association with hypokinesia makes this the disorder that fits the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COMT Val158Met",
    "long_answer": "The Val158Met single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP rs4680) in the catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) gene alters enzymatic degradation of dopamine, especially in the prefrontal cortex. Studies have shown that the Met/Val variation is linked to schizotypal personality traits and diagnosed Schizotypal Personality Disorder, and the same polymorphism has repeatedly been associated with susceptibility to Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder, indicating a shared dopaminergic genetic risk factor for both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerebral palsy",
    "long_answer": "Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive central motor disorder in which damage to the developing brain produces upper motor-neuron signs; spasticity is the most common clinical manifestation. Because the abnormal, asymmetric muscle tone and weakness around the spine persist during growth, children with cerebral palsy frequently develop neuromuscular scoliosis. Thus cerebral palsy is a well-recognized condition that is both associated with scoliosis and capable of causing spasticity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Consanguinity",
    "long_answer": "Laron syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the growth-hormone receptor gene. The chief epidemiologic factor that increases the chance of a child inheriting two mutant alleles is consanguineous mating (parents who are close relatives), making consanguinity the primary recognized risk factor for Laron syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sebaceous gland",
    "long_answer": "Steatocystoma multiplex consists of numerous dermal cysts that are considered hamartomas of the pilosebaceous unit. The cyst wall shows sebaceous differentiation, indicating that an abnormal lining of the sebaceous gland (specifically, its duct portion) is responsible for the development of these sebum-filled cysts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Positive selection",
    "long_answer": "Sequence analyses of Parvovirus B19 show an excess of non-synonymous over synonymous substitutions in exposed regions of the VP1 capsid protein. This pattern indicates positive (Darwinian) selection, driven largely by host immune pressure, which promotes amino-acid changes that diversify surface epitopes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRRT2",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial infantile epilepsy is most commonly caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PRRT2 gene, which is located on chromosome 16p11.2. PRRT2 encodes proline-rich transmembrane protein 2; pathogenic variants disrupt synaptic vesicle exocytosis, producing the characteristic self-limited infantile seizures seen in this familial epilepsy syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Unani medicine",
    "long_answer": "In the Greco-Arabic Unani system of traditional medicine, egg yolk oil (Raughan Baiza-e-Murgh) is routinely applied to the scalp as a remedy for alopecia, with the oil’s lipids and bioactive phospholipids believed to nourish hair follicles and reduce hair loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Reardon-Hall-Slaney syndrome (also known as postaxial acrofacial dysostosis/Miller syndrome) is transmitted in an autosomal-recessive manner: affected individuals inherit two pathogenic alleles, one from each phenotypically normal carrier parent. This pattern explains the occurrence in siblings of unaffected parents and the equal incidence in males and females."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BDNF",
    "long_answer": "In WAGR syndrome, large 11p13–p14 deletions that extend beyond WT1 and PAX6 to include the BDNF gene (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) cause haploinsufficiency of BDNF. Loss of one BDNF allele disrupts central appetite-regulating pathways, leading to hyperphagia and early-onset obesity; thus, BDNF on chromosome 11 is the gene specifically linked to obesity in WAGR patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemolytic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency causes chronic destruction of red blood cells, producing hemolytic anemia. The accelerated hemolysis releases large amounts of unconjugated bilirubin, which accumulates in tissues and leads to the yellow discoloration characteristic of jaundice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPL13",
    "long_answer": "The Pakistani type of spondylo-epimetaphyseal dysplasia (SEMD-PK) is caused by homozygous or compound-heterozygous pathogenic variants in the RPL13 gene, which encodes ribosomal protein L13. Multiple affected Pakistani families have been shown by linkage analysis and sequencing to harbor loss-of-function RPL13 mutations, establishing this ribosomal protein gene as the molecular basis of the SEMD Pakistani phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angiotensin-converting enzyme",
    "long_answer": "Bradykinin is rapidly inactivated in the pulmonary circulation by kininase II, more commonly known as angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). ACE cleaves bradykinin into inactive fragments while simultaneously converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II, making it the principal physiological enzyme responsible for bradykinin degradation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth retardation",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen storage disease type VI (Hers disease), children commonly present with poor linear growth—often called growth retardation or short stature. Tracking height and weight is a standard developmental milestone; failure to reach expected growth percentiles is therefore both a typical symptom of GSD VI and a clinical indicator that a developmental milestone is delayed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Thrombocytopenia is defined as an abnormally low number of circulating platelets. Because platelets are essential for primary hemostasis and clot formation, their deficiency impairs clotting, leading to prolonged or excessive bleeding. Clinically, thrombocytopenia is categorized as a coagulopathy (a disorder of blood clotting) and may arise from decreased platelet production, increased destruction, or sequestration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic-resonance imaging (particularly MR angiography) provides high-resolution, three-dimensional visualization of the thoracic aorta, allowing precise identification of the characteristic narrowing and any collateral vessels, and is therefore routinely used to confirm and define coarctation of the aorta."
}

{
  "short_answer": "Pennsylvania Department of Health",
  "long_answer": "Legionnaires' disease was first recognized after a 1976 outbreak among American Legion conventioneers in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. In that state, authority over public health programs—such as infectious-disease surveillance, outbreak investigation, and prevention—resides with the Pennsylvania Department of Health, the state agency charged with managing disease control and overall public health services."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Upington",
    "long_answer": "Upington disease was first identified in families living in the Northern Cape town of Upington, South Africa; the disorder was therefore named after this city."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arteriovenous fistula",
    "long_answer": "An arteriovenous fistula is an abnormal direct connection between an artery and a vein. It can be produced by penetrating or blunt trauma that disrupts adjacent vessels, allowing arterial blood to shunt into the venous system and bypass the high-resistance arteriolar and capillary beds. Because blood now flows through this low-resistance pathway, total peripheral resistance falls, which can lead to high-output heart failure if the shunt is large."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transketolase",
    "long_answer": "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is precipitated by thiamine (vitamin B₁) deficiency, and the critical biochemical lesion is reduced activity of the thiamine-dependent enzyme transketolase in brain and erythrocytes. Impaired transketolase limits the pentose-phosphate pathway and cerebral energy metabolism, leading to the neurologic manifestations characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pericardial effusion",
    "long_answer": "Erdheim–Chester disease frequently infiltrates the pericardium, leading to pericardial effusion. Accumulation of fluid around the heart restricts cardiac filling and causes dyspnea (shortness of breath), making pericardial effusion a recognized cardiovascular complication of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDA type II",
    "long_answer": "Among the hereditary dyserythropoietic anemias, congenital dyserythropoietic anemia type II (also called HEMPAS) accounts for roughly 60–70 % of reported cases, making it the most frequent form. CDA II is autosomal-recessive, caused mainly by SEC23B mutations, and is characterized by binucleated erythroblasts and a positive acidified serum (Ham) test."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aporophobia",
    "long_answer": "The word “aporophobia,” coined from the Greek aporos (without resources) and-phobia (fear), designates the specific fear, rejection or aversion directed at poor or destitute people themselves, rather than at poverty as a condition. It is widely used in social science and ethics to describe discrimination against individuals who lack economic means."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UGT1A1",
    "long_answer": "The congenital (familial) form of Lucey–Driscoll syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the UGT1A1 gene, which encodes bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. Loss-of-function mutations in UGT1A1 reduce or abolish hepatic glucuronidation of unconjugated bilirubin, leading to the severe transient neonatal hyperbilirubinemia characteristic of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lower socioeconomic status",
    "long_answer": "Extensive epidemiological studies consistently demonstrate an inverse socioeconomic gradient: individuals with lower socioeconomic status have a markedly higher incidence and earlier onset of myocardial infarction. This elevated risk is mediated by a clustering of modifiable cardiovascular risk factors (e.g., smoking, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidaemia, obesity), chronic psychosocial stress, limited access to preventive care, and adverse environmental conditions, all of which accelerate atherosclerosis and precipitate coronary events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type VII (Sly syndrome)",
    "long_answer": "The rare lysosomal storage disorder mucopolysaccharidosis type VII (Sly syndrome), caused by β-glucuronidase deficiency, can present in utero with massive glycosaminoglycan accumulation that disrupts lymphatic return and cardiac function. This often manifests as non-immune hydrops fetalis in affected newborns, a distinctive feature reported in many MPS VII cases but rarely seen in other mucopolysaccharidoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trigeminal nerve",
    "long_answer": "Cluster headaches are classified as trigeminal-autonomic cephalalgias. Functional imaging and physiologic studies show activation of the trigeminovascular system, with afferent input carried primarily by the ophthalmic branch of cranial nerve V (the trigeminal nerve). This activation triggers parasympathetic outflow and release of vasoactive peptides, producing the unilateral orbital pain and autonomic signs characteristic of cluster headache."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHH",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SHH (Sonic hedgehog) gene, the secreted morphogen that initiates the hedgehog signalling cascade, are a well-documented cause of holoprosencephaly spectrum disorders that frequently include syndromic microphthalmia or anophthalmia. SHH is a central, upstream component of the hedgehog pathway, and loss-of-function mutations disrupt early ocular and craniofacial patterning, producing the microphthalmic phenotype seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Branchial fistulae",
    "long_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal syndrome commonly involves maldevelopment of the second branchial cleft. This can leave epithelial-lined tracts that form abnormal connections—branchial fistulae—between the skin on the lateral sides of the neck and the pharynx, producing openings or discharge along the neck."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63 gene",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly–ectodermal dysplasia–cleft (EEC) syndrome is most commonly caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 transcription factor. p63 is a master regulator of limb, craniofacial, and ectodermal development; pathogenic TP63 variants disrupt these developmental pathways, producing the characteristic limb clefts, ectodermal dysplasia, and orofacial clefting seen in EEC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAPSS2",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia reported in multiple Pakistani kindreds is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PAPSS2 gene, which encodes 3′-phosphoadenosine-5′-phosphosulfate synthase 2. Defective PAPSS2 impairs production of the universal sulfate donor PAPS, leading to undersulfation of cartilage proteoglycans and the characteristic skeletal abnormalities of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome is the partial hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl-transferase (HGPRT) deficiency variant of Lesch–Nyhan disease. The inherited enzyme defect causes chronic over-production of uric acid, leading to early-onset gout and uric-acid nephrolithiasis. Crystal-induced inflammation can involve the axial skeleton and produce a spondyloarthropathy, so patients often present with chronic low back pain in addition to gouty attacks. Thus this genetic disorder links gout and low back pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Emetophobia",
    "long_answer": "Emetophobia is an anxiety disorder characterized by an intense, persistent fear of vomiting or seeing others vomit. People with emetophobia commonly avoid situations they associate with a higher risk of nausea—such as airplane travel—so the fear of vomiting can lead them to refuse to fly even when no actual gastrointestinal threat exists."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cucurbita",
    "long_answer": "The genus Cucurbita includes carotenoid-rich edible species such as pumpkins, squashes and some gourds. These fruits contain high levels of β-carotene and other provitamin A carotenoids; excessive ingestion can elevate circulating carotenoids and deposit them in skin, producing the benign orange discoloration characteristic of carotenosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≤110 pg/mL",
    "long_answer": "Narcolepsy type 1 is defined by loss of hypothalamic orexin-producing neurons, reflected in a markedly reduced cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) concentration of orexin-A (hypocretin-1). International diagnostic criteria (e.g., ICSD-3) set the threshold at a CSF orexin-A level of 110 picograms per millilitre or lower (or < 1/3 of normal mean). Values at or below 110 pg/mL therefore confirm the diagnosis of narcolepsy type 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angiokeratoma corporis diffusum",
    "long_answer": "Schindler disease (α-N-acetylgalactosaminidase deficiency), particularly the adult/Type II form known as Kanzaki disease, characteristically presents with multiple angiokeratomas distributed over the trunk and extremities. These hyperkeratotic vascular papules constitute the skin condition called angiokeratoma corporis diffusum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ivermectin",
    "long_answer": "Human and veterinary infections produced by warble-fly larvae (Hypoderma lineatum and Hypoderma bovis) respond to a single oral dose of the macrocyclic lactone ivermectin (≈ 200 µg/kg), which rapidly kills the migrating larvae and resolves the cutaneous or systemic manifestations of hypodermosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VACTERL association",
    "long_answer": "In VACTERL association (Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-oesophageal, Renal and Limb anomalies), the limb component commonly manifests as radial dysplasia and can also include absence or hypoplasia of the tibia. Thus, the syndrome linked to radial dysplasia that specifically features a missing or underdeveloped tibia is the VACTERL association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anterior horn cell degeneration",
    "long_answer": "In congenital distal spinal muscular atrophy the primary lesion is degeneration and loss of the α-motor neurons located in the anterior horns of the spinal cord. This denervation of skeletal muscle fibres removes their trophic neural input, leading directly to muscle fibre wasting and the characteristic distal muscle atrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto's thyroiditis",
    "long_answer": "Physical or chronic urticaria is sometimes associated with autoimmune thyroid disease. In Hashimoto's thyroiditis, the immune system produces antibodies (e.g., anti-TPO, anti-TG) that attack and progressively destroy thyroid tissue. This autoimmune activity can alter mast-cell function and increase histamine release, predisposing patients to recurrent urticaria. Thus, persistent physical urticaria can be a clinical clue to underlying Hashimoto's thyroiditis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lyme disease",
    "long_answer": "Lyme disease is an infection caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, transmitted by Ixodes ticks. Neurologic dissemination (Lyme neuroborreliosis) can affect cranial nerves, most commonly resulting in unilateral or bilateral facial nerve (VII) palsy, making Lyme disease a well-recognized infectious cause of facial paralysis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibialis anterior",
    "long_answer": "Inclusion body myositis characteristically weakens ankle dorsiflexor muscles, especially the tibialis anterior. Loss of tibialis anterior strength prevents active dorsiflexion of the foot during the swing phase of gait, producing the clinical manifestation of foot drop."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yersinia pestis",
    "long_answer": "The Black Death, the devastating pandemic that swept across Europe and Asia in the mid-14th century, was caused by the Gram-negative bacterium Yersinia pestis. This pathogen is typically transmitted to humans via the bite of infected fleas that parasitize rodents, leading to bubonic, septicemic, or pneumonic plague forms. Extensive genomic analyses of ancient plague victims’ dental pulp have confirmed the presence of Y. pestis DNA, establishing it as the etiologic agent of the Black Death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Remission",
    "long_answer": "In clinical terminology, a \"remission\" is the phase during which the clinical manifestations of a disease—such as the skin lesions of Mucha–Habermann disease (pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta)—lessen markedly or disappear altogether. This term is routinely used across dermatology and other medical specialties to denote the temporary abatement of signs and symptoms before possible relapse or recurrence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant disorder",
    "long_answer": "Ankylosing vertebral hyperostosis with tylosis is a rare inherited skeletal dysplasia that follows an autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance—one mutant allele is sufficient to cause the excessive vertebral hyperostosis and the associated palmoplantar keratoderma (tylosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ganser syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ganser syndrome is a rare dissociative disorder (ICD-10 F44.8) whose core features include ‘approximate answers’ (Vorbeireden) together with additional symptoms such as clouding of consciousness, pseudoneurological complaints, hallucinations, echolalia, and echopraxia. Because echopraxia is specifically listed among its characteristic manifestations, Ganser syndrome is the dissociative disorder most clearly associated with this symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrichosis cubiti",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrichosis cubiti, also called hairy-elbow syndrome, is a benign form of localized congenital hypertrichosis in which dense terminal hairs appear symmetrically over the extensor surfaces of both elbows in infants and young children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intensive care unit",
    "long_answer": "Delirium tremens is a severe form of alcohol-withdrawal marked by autonomic instability, profound agitation, seizures, and risk of respiratory failure. Because it demands continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring, rapid titration of benzodiazepines or barbiturates, and immediate management of complications, clinical guidelines recommend that these patients be managed in an intensive care unit (ICU), where such close observation and advanced supportive therapy are available."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEN type 2B",
    "long_answer": "Among the subtypes of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2, the 2B variant (also called MEN-2B or MEN-3) is characterized by mucosal and intestinal ganglioneuromas. These neural tumors in the gastrointestinal tract commonly cause abdominal distension, altered bowel habits, and excessive intestinal gas production, making flatulence a recognized clinical feature of MEN type 2B."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The FMR1 gene, whose CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion causes Fragile X syndrome, is located on the long arm of the X chromosome at band Xq27.3. Because it resides on the X chromosome, the disorder shows X-linked inheritance, with males typically more severely affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Canker sores",
    "long_answer": "Canker sores (recurrent aphthous ulcers) are small, painful ulcerations on the oral mucosa. They frequently emerge during nicotine withdrawal because the loss of tobacco-induced mucosal keratinisation and changes in local immunity make the mouth more prone to these ulcers. Thus, canker sores are both a recognized nicotine-withdrawal symptom and a well-known type of mouth ulcer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheoesophageal fistula",
    "long_answer": "In the VACTERL association (Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-Esophageal, Renal, Limb anomalies), the tracheoesophageal fistula—usually occurring with esophageal atresia—prevents the newborn from safely swallowing milk, leading to choking, aspiration, and inability to pass a nasogastric tube. Definitive correction requires urgent surgical repair so that normal feeding and breathing can proceed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Viral meningitis",
    "long_answer": "Viral meningitis is an infection—most commonly by enteroviruses, herpesviruses, or arboviruses—that targets the meninges, the protective membranes enveloping the brain and spinal cord. The ensuing immune response leads to inflammation of these membranes, producing the characteristic clinical syndrome of meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatic cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "In hereditary fructose intolerance, aldolase B deficiency causes accumulation of fructose-1-phosphate in hepatocytes, leading to phosphate trapping, inhibition of gluconeogenesis/glycogenolysis, and chronic cellular injury. Persistent fructose exposure therefore produces progressive liver damage that can culminate in hepatic cirrhosis, a well-documented long-term sequela of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "17β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 3",
    "long_answer": "Conversion of androstenedione to testosterone in the testes is catalyzed mainly by the enzyme 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 3 (17β-HSD-3). When this enzyme is deficient, androstenedione cannot be efficiently reduced to testosterone, so circulating androstenedione rises while testosterone remains low, producing a markedly elevated androstenedione-to-testosterone ratio that becomes especially evident at puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neural crest cells",
    "long_answer": "Primitive neuroectodermal tumours (PNETs) are thought to arise from primitive, undifferentiated neuroectodermal cells that originate in the embryonic neural crest. Accordingly, the bulk of cells found in these tumours represent malignant derivatives of neural crest cells rather than mature neuronal or glial lineages."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymoma",
    "long_answer": "On the lateral chest radiograph the retrosternal clear space—bounded laterally by the mediastinal pleura—should appear lucent (air-filled lungs). Soft-tissue radiodensity within this space signifies an anterior mediastinal mass; in adults the most characteristic cause is enlargement of the thymus, i.e., a thymoma. Thus opacity in this region strongly suggests the presence of a thymoma (an anterior mediastinal tumour)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Mutations—most often poly-alanine expansions—in the homeobox gene HOXD13 disrupt anterior-posterior patterning of the limb bud. This causes synpolydactyly (polysyndactyly), in which fused digits are accompanied by one or more extra fingers or toes, especially in the feet. Thus HOXD13 mutations are classically linked to the presence of extra toes in polysyndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)",
    "long_answer": "Prurigo nodularis is frequently observed in patients infected with Human immunodeficiency virus. Numerous dermatologic studies identify PN as a common cutaneous manifestation of HIV disease, especially in advanced immunosuppression, supporting a well-established association between HIV infection and prurigo nodularis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈16 % of under-5 deaths",
    "long_answer": "Population-based data from Uganda indicate that sickle cell disease accounts for roughly 15–16 % of all deaths in children younger than five years. Because more than two-thirds of affected infants die before their fifth birthday in the absence of early diagnosis and care, sickle cell disease represents a major contributor to childhood mortality in Uganda."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fovea centralis",
    "long_answer": "The fovea centralis is a small central pit in the retina packed exclusively with cone photoreceptors. Cones function optimally in bright (photopic) light and are responsible for high-resolution, color vision. Because the fovea contains the highest density of cones and minimal overlying tissue, it provides the greatest visual acuity, enabling precise perception of fine details in well-lit conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "None",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic hypercalciuria is driven by increased intestinal calcium absorption and reduced tubular re-uptake of filtered calcium; glomerular filtration remains normal. Therefore, the glomerulus itself does not participate in the pathogenesis of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Squamous cell carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Bowen’s disease—a psoriasiform, scaly erythematous plaque—can be mistaken for psoriasis. Histologically it is an in-situ form of squamous cell carcinoma, and untreated lesions may invade to become overt squamous cell carcinoma of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PITX2",
    "long_answer": "PITX2 encodes a paired-like homeodomain transcription factor that directs neural-crest–derived mesenchyme to form the corneal stroma and endothelium during eye development. Pathogenic variants in PITX2 are a well-established cause of anterior-segment dysgenesis syndromes, and several patients with such mutations present clinically with Peters’ Anomaly, confirming its implication in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greek and Latin",
    "long_answer": "The malformation is called “ectopia cordis”, a hybrid term formed from the Greek word ‘ektopos’ (ἐκτός + τόπος, meaning ‘out of place’) and the Latin word ‘cor, cordis’ (“heart”). Thus its linguistic origin is a combination of Greek and Latin roots."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippopotamophobia",
    "long_answer": "In the nomenclature of specific phobias, the Greek or Latin name of the feared object is combined with the suffix “-phobia.” The animal’s classical name is hippopotamus (from Greek hippos, “horse,” and potamos, “river”), so the irrational and persistent fear of these animals is termed “hippopotamophobia.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Adenomyosis can be non-invasively diagnosed by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). On T2-weighted MRI the uterine junctional zone is clearly visualised; a junctional zone thickness greater than about 12 mm or irregular thickening with high-signal myometrial foci is characteristic of adenomyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cerliponase alfa",
    "long_answer": "Cerliponase alfa (brand name Brineura) is a recombinant form of the lysosomal enzyme tripeptidyl-peptidase-1 (TPP1). In April 2017 the U.S. FDA approved it as the first disease-specific therapy for neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (CLN2), providing intracerebroventricular enzyme-replacement treatment that slows the progressive neurologic decline characteristic of this TPP1-deficiency disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT2) deficiency arises when both alleles of the CPT2 gene carry pathogenic variants. Heterozygous carriers are typically asymptomatic, while individuals with bi-allelic (compound heterozygous or homozygous) mutations develop the disease, establishing an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital dyserythropoietic anemia type II",
    "long_answer": "Among the defined congenital dyserythropoietic anemias (types I, II, III and the very rare type IV), type II—historically called HEMPAS (hereditary erythroblastic multinuclearity with a positive acidified serum lysis test)—accounts for roughly 50–60 % of all reported CDA cases, making it the most prevalent subtype worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ARMS2",
    "long_answer": "The ARMS2 gene (Age-Related Maculopathy Susceptibility 2), formerly called LOC387715, lies on chromosome 10q26. Variants in ARMS2 are strongly associated with increased risk of age-related macular degeneration, making it one of the principal genetic loci implicated in this retinal disorder alongside CFH on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) is an autosomal-dominant craniofacial disorder caused most commonly by pathogenic variants in the TCOF1 gene. It is characterized by hypoplasia of the zygomatic (cheek) bones and mandible, and about one-third of affected individuals also have a cleft palate, making it the classic genetic condition that presents with both cheek–jaw underdevelopment and cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi anemia",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia is an inherited bone-marrow failure syndrome caused by mutations in the FANCA/FANCC and related genes that impair the DNA inter-strand cross-link repair pathway. The resulting genomic instability affects rapidly dividing germ-cells, producing hypogonadism, diminished spermatogenesis or premature ovarian failure, and thus a high rate of infertility in affected males and females."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNMB staging system",
    "long_answer": "Mycosis fungoides, the most common cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, is staged with the tumor–node–metastasis–blood (TNMB) classification endorsed by the American Joint Committee on Cancer and the International Society for Cutaneous Lymphomas. This system evaluates extent of skin lesions (T), lymph-node involvement (N), visceral metastasis (M) and the presence of malignant T cells in peripheral blood (B) to assign an overall stage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Electromyography",
    "long_answer": "Guillain–Barré syndrome is confirmed by performing electromyography with nerve conduction studies, which measure the speed and pattern of electrical impulses along peripheral nerves; the characteristic finding of slowed conduction or conduction block supports the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental disomy",
    "long_answer": "In this case, both copies of the chromosome 2 segment containing the LRP2 gene were inherited from the same parent (isodisomy), a phenomenon called uniparental disomy. This made the child homozygous for the parent’s pathogenic LRP2 mutation and produced Donnai–Barrow syndrome even though the other parent carried no mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lung adenocarcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (digital clubbing with periostitis of long bones) occurs as a paraneoplastic syndrome in lung cancers. Among the histologic subtypes, non-small-cell tumors—particularly peripheral lung adenocarcinoma—account for the majority of cases, making adenocarcinoma the type most commonly linked to hypertrophic osteoarthropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary dwarfism",
    "long_answer": "Pituitary dwarfism results from deficient secretion of growth hormone by the anterior pituitary gland—a primary endocrine disorder—leading to proportionate but markedly reduced stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CAPN3 gene, which encodes the muscle-specific calcium-dependent protease calpain-3, cause limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A (LGMD2A, also called calpainopathy). This autosomal-recessive muscular dystrophy is characterized by progressive weakness and wasting of the shoulder and pelvic girdle muscles and is the well-established clinical consequence of CAPN3 loss-of-function variants worldwide, including in Eurasian populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "In the acquired form of C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency, patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) can develop IgG autoantibodies that bind to and neutralize the C1 esterase inhibitor protein. This antibody-mediated inactivation lowers functional C1-INH levels, leading to complement activation and episodes of angio-edema characteristic of acquired angio-edema associated with SLE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human papillomavirus",
    "long_answer": "High-risk types of human papillomavirus (especially HPV-16 and HPV-18) are the established causal agents of virtually all cervical cancers and are also frequently detected in HPV-positive oropharyngeal and oral squamous cell carcinomas, making the virus etiologically linked to both oral and cervical cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Respiratory failure",
    "long_answer": "Saito–Kuba–Tsuruta syndrome is a lethal congenital contracture/fetal akinesia sequence. Affected infants are born with severe pulmonary hypoplasia and extremely weak respiratory muscles; they are unable to establish adequate ventilation after birth. Consequently, death invariably occurs from respiratory failure within the perinatal period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bronchiolitis obliterans",
    "long_answer": "After heart-lung transplantation, the most frequent chronic rejection complication is bronchiolitis obliterans (also called bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome), in which inflammation and fibrotic scarring progressively narrow and obliterate the terminal bronchioles—the smallest airways—leading to irreversible airflow obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tonic seizures",
    "long_answer": "Lennox–Gastaut syndrome is characterized by multiple seizure types, but tonic seizures are the most prevalent, affecting the great majority of patients. These seizures typically consist of brief, generalized muscle stiffening and are especially common during periods of non-REM (slow-wave) sleep, where they may cluster or occur repetitively. Their high frequency in this sleep stage and overall predominance make tonic seizures the hallmark seizure type of Lennox–Gastaut syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plakoglobin (JUP)",
    "long_answer": "Naxos syndrome—characterized by arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, palmoplantar keratoderma, and woolly hair—is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the plakoglobin gene (JUP). Plakoglobin is a desmosomal protein essential for cell-to-cell adhesion in skin and cardiac tissue; its mutation disrupts desmosomal integrity, leading to the hair shaft abnormalities (woolly hair) and associated cutaneous and cardiac manifestations typical of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Stomach",
    "long_answer": "In normal anatomy the stomach lies in the left upper quadrant, just medial to the spleen. In patients with situs ambiguus (heterotaxy), the stomach bubble is frequently displaced to an atypical side or location; this abnormal position is one of the standard imaging clues used to recognise the disorder. Therefore, malposition of the stomach is a common and easily observed indicator of situs ambiguus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lingual gyrus",
    "long_answer": "Cortical (central) achromatopsia occurs when the colour-processing centre in the ventral occipital cortex—specifically area V4 located on the lingual and posterior fusiform gyri—is damaged. Lesions of the lingual gyrus therefore abolish colour perception even though the eyes and optic nerves remain intact."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgG4 autoantibodies",
    "long_answer": "Certain atypical variants of chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) are mediated by IgG4-class autoantibodies that target paranodal cell-adhesion proteins such as neurofascin-155 or contactin-1. These IgG4 autoantibodies drive the autoimmune response and underlie the distinct clinical and therapeutic features of these CIDP subtypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Discoid lupus erythematosus",
    "long_answer": "Discoid lupus erythematosus (DLE) is an autoimmune form of cutaneous lupus in which lymphocyte-mediated inflammation targets the skin and hair follicles. When DLE lesions develop on the scalp they produce erythematous, inflamed plaques that destroy follicular units, leading to permanent cicatricial (scarring) alopecia. Thus, this autoimmune disease is a well-recognized cause of hair loss accompanied by scarring and marked inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACADS",
    "long_answer": "Short-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase (SCAD) deficiency is an autosomal recessive fatty-acid oxidation disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the ACADS gene, which encodes the mitochondrial enzyme short-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase. Mutations in ACADS impair the β-oxidation of short-chain fatty acids, leading to accumulation of butyrylcarnitine and ethylmalonic acid that characterize the clinical condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lichen planus–like lesions",
    "long_answer": "In chronic graft-versus-host disease, donor T-cells attack host squamous epithelium, and in the oral cavity this produces a lichenoid mucositis that clinically and histologically resembles lichen planus—white reticular or plaque-like striae with areas of atrophy and ulceration—representing the characteristic tissue damage of chronic GVHD in the mouth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 6–7",
    "long_answer": "In reproductive-age women the vaginal pH is acidic (≈4.0–4.5) because estrogen supports lactobacilli that produce lactic acid. After menopause, estrogen withdrawal diminishes lactobacilli, so the environment becomes less acidic; in atrophic vaginitis the pH typically rises to about 6–7 (i.e., ≥5). This elevated pH is a characteristic finding used clinically to confirm postmenopausal atrophic change."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Aphalangy-syndactyly-microcephaly syndrome (OMIM 102510) has been described in multiple affected siblings born to phenotypically normal, often consanguineous parents. The equal occurrence in males and females and the absence of vertical transmission indicate an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cervical lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "Both Hodgkin and most non-Hodgkin lymphomas typically present with painless enlargement of the cervical (often including the supraclavicular) lymph nodes, making this nodal group the one most commonly affected at initial diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Penicillamine",
    "long_answer": "Penicillamine is a thiol-containing copper-chelating drug widely used as first-line therapy for Wilson’s disease. Case reports have documented development of the characteristic triad of yellow nails, lymphedema and pleural effusions in patients receiving penicillamine, linking the medication to yellow nail syndrome. Thus, penicillamine is both associated with yellow nail syndrome and routinely employed to treat Wilson’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-DR2 and HLA-DR3",
    "long_answer": "Systemic lupus erythematosus shows its strongest and most reproducible genetic link to particular class II major-histocompatibility-complex alleles—specifically the HLA-DR2 (e.g., DRB1*1501) and HLA-DR3 (e.g., DRB1*0301) haplotypes. Individuals carrying these alleles have a several-fold higher risk of developing lupus, reflecting altered presentation of self-nuclear antigens to CD4⁺ T cells and consequent loss of immune tolerance. While many other loci contribute to risk, DR2 and DR3 remain the variants most commonly cited in clinical and immunology literature as being associated with lupus erythematosus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PCNT",
    "long_answer": "In January 2008, researchers reported that loss-of-function mutations in the PCNT gene, which encodes the centrosomal protein pericentrin, are responsible for microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II (a form of primordial dwarfism). PCNT mutations disrupt centrosome organisation and mitotic spindle assembly, leading to markedly reduced cell proliferation and the extreme intra-uterine and postnatal growth restriction characteristic of primordial dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-galactosidase A",
    "long_answer": "Fabry disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GLA gene, which markedly reduce the activity of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-galactosidase A. Deficiency of this enzyme prevents normal degradation of globotriaosylceramide (Gb3), allowing its accumulation in cardiomyocytes, vascular endothelium and conduction tissue. The resulting glycosphingolipid buildup produces left-ventricular hypertrophy, arrhythmias and other cardiac complications characteristic of Fabry disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Angular gyrus",
    "long_answer": "Lesions in the dominant (usually left) angular gyrus of the inferior parietal lobule characteristically produce Gerstmann syndrome, whose core features include agraphia (inability to write) and finger agnosia (inability to recognize or name fingers). Therefore, the angular gyrus is the brain region linked to both deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Navicular bone",
    "long_answer": "Köhler disease is an osteochondrosis characterized by avascular necrosis of the tarsal navicular bone in children, leading to mid-foot pain and swelling until the bone re-vascularizes and remodels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AMH or AMHR2 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome results when anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) signalling is lost. Autosomal-recessive mutations in either the AMH gene itself or in its type II receptor gene (AMHR2) prevent production or action of AMH produced by fetal Sertoli cells. Without functional AMH, the embryonic Müllerian ducts do not regress, so a uterus and fallopian tubes are retained in otherwise 46,XY males, producing the characteristic PMDS phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Legionnaires' disease",
    "long_answer": "Legionella pneumophila infection presents primarily as Legionnaires' disease, an atypical form of pneumonia. Because the bacteria cause inflammatory damage to alveolar tissues, patients can develop pulmonary infiltrates that occasionally bleed, leading to hemoptysis (coughing up blood). This symptom is well documented in severe cases of Legionnaires' disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "S-adenosyl-homocysteine",
    "long_answer": "High plasma homocysteine is rapidly converted intracellularly to S-adenosyl-homocysteine (SAH). SAH is a potent competitive inhibitor of DNA methyltransferases; its accumulation reduces the S-adenosyl-methionine : SAH ratio, causing global DNA hypomethylation. Hypomethylation alters expression of vascular‐protective genes (e.g., eNOS) and increases pro-atherogenic, pro-inflammatory gene expression, thereby promoting endothelial dysfunction, smooth-muscle proliferation, and atherogenesis that culminate in cardiovascular disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ring chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Sugarman syndrome is the eponym used for the congenital disorder caused by formation of a ring chromosome derived from human chromosome 15. The ring configuration results in deletion of terminal segments of chromosome 15 and produces the characteristic clinical picture of growth retardation, craniofacial anomalies and developmental delay that define the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Boudhina-Yedes-Khiari syndrome is a hereditary neuro-ophthalmologic disorder that has been reported exclusively in consanguineous families; linkage analyses and pedigree studies show affected individuals only when both parents are carriers, establishing an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fluorosis",
    "long_answer": "Chronic, long-term ingestion of excessive fluoride overwhelms normal mineral metabolism and leads to fluorosis. Depending on age and dose, this manifests as enamel hypomineralization (dental fluorosis) or progressive bone changes (skeletal fluorosis), both of which are well-documented consequences of sustained high fluoride exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genomic imprinting",
    "long_answer": "Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy is caused by heterozygous inactivating mutations in the GNAS gene, whose expression is regulated by parent-of-origin–specific methylation marks. Because only the maternal allele is active in key endocrine tissues, a mutation inherited from the mother produces the full phenotype with hormone resistance (pseudohypoparathyroidism Ia), whereas the same mutation from the father yields the skeletal features alone (pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism). This parent-of-origin effect is the classic example of genomic imprinting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Progressive chorioretinal degeneration",
    "long_answer": "In choroideremia, loss-of-function mutations in the CHM gene lead to deficiency of Rab escort protein-1. This disrupts vesicular trafficking in retinal cells, causing gradual atrophy of the choroid, the retinal pigment epithelium, and overlying photoreceptors. The ensuing progressive degeneration of these chorioretinal tissues is the direct, primary reason patients lose night vision, peripheral vision, and eventually central vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spleen",
    "long_answer": "Although Pneumocystis jirovecii most often causes pneumonia, in immunocompromised patients it can disseminate; the spleen is a well-recognized extrapulmonary site, where nodular or calcified lesions due to pneumocystosis are frequently documented."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary multiple exostoses (HME) is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in EXT1 or EXT2, enzymes required for heparan-sulfate biosynthesis. In a published mouse model that mimics the human condition (Ext1+/– mice), researchers demonstrated reduced brain heparan sulfate, impaired formation of excitatory synapses, and a constellation of behavioural abnormalities: diminished social interaction, lack of preference for social novelty, reduced ultrasonic vocalisations and increased stereotyped/repetitive activities. These findings were interpreted as an autism-like phenotype in the mice. On the basis of those results, the authors suggested that children who carry EXT1/EXT2 mutations and present with HME could potentially exhibit similar autism-like social difficulties. Hence, according to the cited mouse-model study, autism-like social problems may indeed be observed in children with hereditary multiple exostoses."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rajidae",
    "long_answer": "Leucoraja naevus, commonly called the cuckoo ray, is a skate species. Skates are grouped within the order Rajiformes, and Leucoraja is classified in the family Rajidae—the principal family that comprises most skate species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
    "long_answer": "Sclerosing mesenteritis is an uncommon disorder in which the adipose tissue of the intestinal mesentery undergoes chronic, nonspecific inflammation followed by fat necrosis and progressive fibrosis. This fibro-inflammatory process can produce a thickened, scarred mesentery visible on imaging and responsible for abdominal pain or obstructive symptoms, fitting the description of a condition that affects the mesentery with both inflammation and fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) is a congenital craniofacial defect characterized by maldevelopment of the first and second pharyngeal arches. Typical features include hypoplastic or absent external and middle ear structures (microtia, atresia of the auditory canal), leading to conductive hearing loss and visibly incomplete development of the ear."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome (also called mandibulofacial dysostosis) is an autosomal-dominant genetic disorder caused most often by pathogenic variants in the TCOF1 gene (or, less commonly, POLR1D/POLR1C). The mutation disrupts neural-crest cell development derived from the first and second pharyngeal arches, leading to underdevelopment of the zygomatic complex, mandible, ears, and lower eyelids. As a result, affected individuals show striking, characteristic malformations of the head and face—including downward-slanting palpebral fissures, hypoplastic cheek and jaw bones, ear anomalies, and cleft palate—making it the prototypical genetic disorder defined by distinctive craniofacial abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry",
    "long_answer": "Methylmalonic acidemias are diagnosed by demonstrating excessive methylmalonic acid in body fluids. Routine metabolic screening labs use gas chromatography coupled with mass spectrometry (GC-MS) to separate urinary organic acids and detect the characteristic mass fragments of methylmalonic acid, allowing accurate quantification of the elevated metabolite."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Otitis media",
    "long_answer": "Hypogammaglobulinemia causes a deficiency of serum immunoglobulins, leaving patients unable to opsonize and clear encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. These pathogens commonly infect the mucosa-lined air-containing spaces of the upper respiratory tract, especially the middle ear cavity, leading to recurrent or chronic otitis media that is classically noted in patients with antibody deficiencies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corticotropin-releasing hormone",
    "long_answer": "Tuber cinereum hamartomas are hypothalamic lesions capable of autonomously producing hypothalamic releasing factors. When they secrete corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), this drives pituitary corticotrophs to release excessive adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), leading to ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism (Cushing’s disease). Thus, CRH from the hamartoma is the hormone responsible for the excessive ACTH production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRT1 gene mutations",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse nonepidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (the classic Unna-Thost type) is produced by heterozygous mutations in the keratin 1 (KRT1) gene. Altered KRT1 disrupts the keratin intermediate-filament network in palmoplantar epidermis, leading to hyperkeratosis without the histological epidermolysis seen with KRT9 mutations, thus giving the nonepidermolytic phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LPIN2",
    "long_answer": "The syndromic form of chronic recurrent multifocal osteomyelitis seen in Majeed syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LPIN2 gene, which encodes the lipid-metabolizing enzyme lipin-2. Patients with biallelic LPIN2 mutations develop the triad of CRMO, congenital dyserythropoietic anemia, and neutrophilic dermatosis, establishing LPIN2 as the gene definitively linked to this syndromic presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Karyotype analysis",
    "long_answer": "Pentasomy X is characterized by the constitutional chromosome complement 49,XXXXX. Performing a standard cytogenetic karyotype (G-banded chromosome analysis) on peripheral blood lymphocytes allows direct visualization and counting of the five X chromosomes, thereby definitively confirming the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudotumor cerebri",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, elevates intracranial pressure without a mass lesion. The raised pressure can stretch the long intracranial course of the abducens (sixth cranial) nerve along the clivus, producing a sixth nerve palsy that manifests as horizontal diplopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HACNS1",
    "long_answer": "HACNS1 (Human-Accelerated Conserved Non-coding Sequence 1) is a limb-development enhancer that acquired a cluster of human-specific mutations. Experimental work shows these changes increase its activity in the embryonic forelimb, a shift proposed to have helped shape the fully opposable human thumb. Because the enhancer’s altered function depends on many coordinated nucleotide substitutions, investigators describe its structure as being as fragile and finely balanced as \"a house of cards.\""
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmosomes",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary mucoepithelial dysplasia is an autosomal-dominant disorder in which epithelial cells show markedly reduced intercellular adhesion. Ultrastructural studies demonstrate absent or malformed desmosomes and poorly organized tonofilaments, indicating that faulty desmosome formation is the primary cellular defect responsible for the disease’s mucosal and cutaneous manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A4",
    "long_answer": "Thin basement membrane disease (benign familial hematuria) has been linked to heterozygous mutations in the COL4A4 gene, which encodes the α4 chain of type IV collagen; such defects impair the type IV collagen network of the glomerular basement membrane, leading to its uniform thinning and persistent microscopic haematuria observed in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optical Coherence Tomography",
    "long_answer": "Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) provides high-resolution cross-sectional images of the retina, allowing visualization of the small schisis cavities and microcystic changes characteristic of X-linked Juvenile Retinoschisis. It is therefore the preferred tool to detect the tiny retinal cysts produced by this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amblyopia",
    "long_answer": "In Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon (a congenital ptosis with synkinetic eyelid elevation), the drooping lid or associated strabismus/anisometropia can block or blur visual input during early development. This sensory deprivation frequently leads to amblyopia—permanently decreased visual acuity in the affected eye—unless it is detected and treated early."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial amyloidosis, Finnish type",
    "long_answer": "Dermatochalasis (baggy, redundant eyelid skin) is a hallmark of the autosomal-dominant gelsolin (AGel) amyloidosis known as familial amyloidosis, Finnish type (Meretoja syndrome). Mutant gelsolin deposits as amyloid in skin and other tissues, leading to characteristic cutis laxa and eyelid drooping alongside lattice corneal dystrophy and cranial neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO5A",
    "long_answer": "Elejalde syndrome (neuroectodermal melanolysosomal disorder) is caused by pathogenic mutations in the MYO5A gene, which encodes the motor protein myosin-Va responsible for intracellular melanosome transport. Loss-of-function variants in MYO5A disrupt melanin distribution and neuronal vesicle trafficking, producing the characteristic pigmentary dilution and severe neurologic manifestations of Elejalde syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seckel syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Seckel syndrome, also called ‘bird-headed dwarfism,’ is classically characterized by pronounced intra-uterine and post-natal growth retardation (producing marked short stature) together with craniofacial anomalies that include a prominent beak-like nose, microcephaly, and a striking micrognathia. The coexistence of severe short stature and micrognathia makes Seckel syndrome the well-recognized disorder fitting the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lethargy",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia often presents with lethargy—a marked lack of energy and decreased responsiveness—because toxic metabolites of galactose impair hepatic and cerebral energy metabolism. The same feeling of sluggishness or tiredness (lethargy) is frequently experienced by otherwise healthy individuals after too little sleep or excessive physical exertion, making it a symptom common to both situations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Loose anagen syndrome is usually diagnosed on the basis of clinical findings (easily extractable hair in young children) combined with a hair-pull test and light-microscopic examination of the plucked anagen hairs, which show the characteristic ruffled cuticle and absent inner and outer root sheaths. A scalp biopsy is rarely required and is not considered necessary for routine diagnosis; it is reserved only for atypical cases when another hair-loss disorder must be ruled out. Therefore, a biopsy is not necessary for diagnosing loose anagen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Fechtner syndrome is an MYH9-related disorder characterized by renal disease, sensorineural deafness, cataracts, and a hematologic finding of macro-thrombocytopenia. This blood abnormality is defined by a reduced platelet count (thrombocytopenia) often accompanied by enlarged platelets, and is a hallmark feature of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "4p deletion",
    "long_answer": "Aplasia cutis congenita is a recognised cutaneous sign of Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome, which results from a terminal deletion of the short arm of chromosome 4 (4p-). Numerous case series document scalp ACC as part of the clinical spectrum of this 4p deletion, establishing the chromosomal loss of 4p as the deletion linked to this dermatological manifestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shortness of breath",
    "long_answer": "In laryngeal papillomatosis, papillomas can narrow the airway, producing dyspnea—felt by patients as shortness of breath. This symptom is not unique to the disorder; it is likewise a hallmark of other respiratory illnesses such as asthma and pneumonia, making it a common but nonspecific clinical feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus type 1",
    "long_answer": "Eczema herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption) is most frequently triggered by herpes simplex virus type 1 (HSV-1); epidemiologic studies show HSV-1 accounts for the vast majority of cases, whereas HSV-2 is a much less common cause."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occipital horn syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Occipital horn syndrome (formerly Ehlers-Danlos type IX) is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the ATP7A copper-transport gene. A hallmark of the condition is generalized cutis laxa together with characteristic skeletal abnormalities such as occipital horns, joint laxity, and bony exostoses, fitting the description in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCP and MRCP",
    "long_answer": "Pancreas divisum is most accurately demonstrated by imaging studies that delineate the pancreatic ductal anatomy. Endoscopic Retrograde Cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) has traditionally served as the gold-standard, directly opacifying the dorsal and ventral ducts and showing their separate drainage. Magnetic Resonance Cholangiopancreatography (MRCP), especially with secretin stimulation, is now widely used because it is non-invasive yet provides high-resolution images of the pancreatic ductal system and reliably depicts the failure of ductal fusion characteristic of pancreas divisum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RHBDF2",
    "long_answer": "Howel–Evans syndrome (tylosis with oesophageal cancer) is caused by germ-line mutations in the RHBDF2 gene, which encodes the inactive rhomboid protein iRhom2. The same RHBDF2 mutations identified in affected families have been reported in individuals who also presented with congenital pulmonary stenosis, suggesting that altered iRhom2-mediated EGFR-ligand signalling can contribute to maldevelopment of the pulmonary valve in addition to producing the characteristic palmoplantar keratoderma and oesophageal cancer susceptibility seen in Howel–Evans syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Equatorial Guinea",
    "long_answer": "The World Health Organization reported that health authorities in Equatorial Guinea confirmed an outbreak of Marburg virus disease in mid-February 2023, marking the country’s first recorded occurrence of the filovirus infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB6 (connexin-30)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GJB6 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-30, are the classical cause of Clouston’s hidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (HED2). The same gene is implicated in certain forms of palmoplantar keratoderma, where connexin-30 defects disturb epidermal gap-junction communication and lead to hyperkeratosis of the palms and soles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adrenocortical adenoma",
    "long_answer": "Cushing’s syndrome results from chronic excess cortisol. An adrenocortical (adrenal cortex) adenoma that autonomously secretes cortisol can independently raise blood cortisol levels, producing the characteristic manifestations of Cushing’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperhidrosis",
    "long_answer": "After a spinal cord injury the descending autonomic pathways that normally modulate sympathetic outflow may be disrupted.  The result can be a chronic sympathetic over-activity in the preserved segments, producing persistently increased eccrine sweat-gland stimulation.  This leads to permanent, excessive sweating—clinically termed hyperhidrosis—in the areas whose sympathetic supply remains intact."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SBDS",
    "long_answer": "Shwachman–Diamond syndrome is most commonly caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SBDS gene. SBDS (Shwachman-Bodian-Diamond syndrome gene) resides on the long arm of chromosome 7 at band q11. It encodes a protein critical for ribosome biogenesis and mitotic spindle stabilization, and its deficiency leads to the characteristic exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, bone-marrow failure, and skeletal abnormalities seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve palsy",
    "long_answer": "Lattice corneal dystrophy type II (Meretoja syndrome) results from a gelsolin mutation that produces systemic amyloid deposition. The amyloid accumulates in cranial nerves, especially the seventh (facial) nerve, leading to a progressive bilateral facial nerve palsy, a well-recognized neurological manifestation of this corneal dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genetic factors",
    "long_answer": "Although the precise cause of most scoliosis cases is termed ‘idiopathic,’ multiple family and twin studies show a clear hereditary component, and several susceptibility genes (e.g., CHD7, LBX1, PAX1) have been linked to curve development. These observations support the consensus that genetic factors play a central role in the onset and progression of scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXC1",
    "long_answer": "Iridogoniodysgenesis, dominant type (anterior-segment dysgenesis with early-onset glaucoma) is caused by heterozygous mutations or dosage alterations in the FOXC1 gene on chromosome 6p25. FOXC1 encodes the forkhead box C1 transcription factor that controls neural-crest–derived tissue development in the eye; when mutated, it impairs formation of the iris stroma and trabecular meshwork, leading to the characteristic glaucoma phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Monocytes",
    "long_answer": "Acute myeloblastic leukemia without maturation (FAB subtype M1) arises from the granulocytic (myeloid) lineage and consists almost entirely of myeloblasts with little or no maturation into neutrophils. Importantly, no monocytic component is present; monocytic white blood cells are not involved in this leukemia subtype, distinguishing it from subtypes M4 (myelomonocytic) and M5 (monocytic)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prudence Sarn",
    "long_answer": "In Mary Webb’s novel ‘Precious Bane’, the protagonist Prudence (“Prue”) Sarn is born with a congenital cleft lip—referred to in the book as a ‘hare-lip’. This physical difference shapes her experiences and relationships throughout the story, making her the character specifically identified with a cleft lip."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scalp",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia totalis is a severe form of alopecia areata characterized by complete loss of hair on the scalp, while body hair elsewhere is typically spared."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemosiderin deposition",
    "long_answer": "In Schamberg's disease, leaky dermal capillaries allow red blood cells to escape into the skin; their hemoglobin breaks down to hemosiderin, whose brown-rust pigment gives the lesions their characteristic rust-colored appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ankyloblepharon",
    "long_answer": "Ankyloblepharon is the condition in which fibrous bands develop between the opposing lid margins, leading to partial or complete fusion of the upper and lower eyelids. These adhesions can be congenital or acquired after inflammation, trauma, or burns, and may restrict eyelid opening and normal ocular function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anemia",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile nephronophthisis, an autosomal-recessive cystic kidney disease, commonly presents with normochromic-normocytic anemia long before advanced renal failure. Reduced erythropoietin production by the damaged renal tubules leads to the blood disorder, making anemia a characteristic systemic symptom of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jean Chrétien",
    "long_answer": "Jean Chrétien, who served as Canada’s 20th Prime Minister from 1993 to 2003, contracted Bell’s palsy in childhood. The acute, idiopathic paralysis of the facial nerve left him with a lifelong partial facial weakness and the characteristic asymmetrical smile that remained visible throughout his political career."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1, are known to cause several connective-tissue disorders. Specific pathogenic variants in FBN1 have been documented in patients with Stiff skin syndrome, characterized by rock-hard skin and joint contractures, and in the autosomal-dominant form of Weill–Marchesani syndrome, marked by short stature, brachydactyly, and ocular anomalies. Thus, FBN1 is the gene causally linked to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pathologia",
    "long_answer": "In classical medical terminology, the study of disease is called “pathologia,” the Latinised form of the Greek roots pathos (suffering or disease) and-logia (study). This term underlies the modern English word “pathology,” denoting the discipline that investigates the causes, mechanisms and effects of diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK)",
    "long_answer": "In gluconeogenesis, oxaloacetate is first formed from pyruvate, then it is decarboxylated and phosphorylated to yield phosphoenolpyruvate. The enzyme responsible for this decarboxylation step is phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK), which uses GTP (or ATP in some organisms) to remove CO₂ from oxaloacetate while transferring a phosphate, producing phosphoenolpyruvate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma",
    "long_answer": "Cushing’s disease is specifically caused by a corticotroph (ACTH-producing) pituitary adenoma. The tumor releases excess adrenocorticotropic hormone, which overstimulates the adrenal cortex to produce cortisol, leading to the clinical features of Cushing’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vocal cord dysfunction",
    "long_answer": "Vocal cord dysfunction (paradoxical vocal fold motion) involves inappropriate closure of the vocal cords during inspiration, producing episodic shortness of breath, wheeze-like noises, and throat tightness. Because these attacks mimic asthma but do not improve with bronchodilators and show normal spirometry outside episodes, the condition is frequently misdiagnosed as asthma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatigue",
    "long_answer": "Persistent fatigue is one of the most frequently reported systemic symptoms in autoimmune disorders such as systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis, where chronic inflammation and cytokine release impair energy metabolism. The same profound tiredness can arise in HIV/AIDS patients because opportunistic infections (e.g., cytomegalovirus, Mycobacterium avium complex) and the associated immune activation place high metabolic demands on the body, leading to marked fatigue. Therefore, fatigue is a shared, well-recognized symptom in both settings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinal fusion",
    "long_answer": "In children with severe spina bifida, marked spinal instability or scoliosis can develop. To stabilise and straighten the vertebral column, surgeons may perform a spinal fusion, permanently joining two or more vertebrae with bone grafts and metal rods/screws. This orthopaedic procedure reduces deformity, protects the spinal cord, and prevents further neurological compromise, making it the accepted surgical option for the most severe cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corpus callosum",
    "long_answer": "The corpus callosum is the principal commissural fiber bundle that physically connects the left and right cerebral hemispheres. A hallmark neuroanatomical abnormality in septo-optic dysplasia is hypoplasia or agenesis of this structure, reflecting impaired midline brain development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MCAD deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency is an inherited fatty-acid-oxidation disorder in which the enzyme needed to break down medium-chain fatty acids is absent. Because these fats cannot be converted into acetyl-CoA for energy, they accumulate in the liver, especially during fasting or illness, leading to fatty infiltration and hepatomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glomerular basement membrane",
    "long_answer": "In diabetic nephropathy, chronic hyperglycemia induces non-enzymatic glycation and accumulation of extracellular matrix proteins, leading to diffuse and nodular thickening specifically of the glomerular basement membrane in the renal glomerulus. This alteration impairs the filtration barrier and is a hallmark histopathologic change of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIPA gene",
    "long_answer": "Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency (both Wolman disease and cholesterol-ester storage disease) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LIPA gene, which encodes lysosomal acid lipase. Without functional LIPA, the enzyme cannot hydrolyze cholesteryl esters and triglycerides within lysosomes, leading to their pathological accumulation in cells and tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytomegalovirus infection",
    "long_answer": "Congenital infection with human cytomegalovirus (CMV) can cross the placenta during pregnancy and disrupt fetal brain development, frequently resulting in microcephaly as well as other neurodevelopmental defects such as sensorineural hearing loss and intracranial calcifications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Impaired clearance of encapsulated bacteria",
    "long_answer": "Neisseria meningitidis is protected by a polysaccharide capsule. The spleen is the main site where IgM-opsonized, complement-coated encapsulated organisms are filtered and phagocytosed. Without a spleen, these opsonizing antibodies, complement activation, and splenic macrophage functions are markedly reduced, so encapsulated pathogens like N. meningitidis are not efficiently cleared, leading to a greatly increased risk of invasive infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Decompressive laminectomy",
    "long_answer": "Because hypochondroplasia can cause short pedicles and narrowing of the spinal canal, affected patients who develop symptomatic spinal stenosis are typically treated with a decompressive laminectomy. Removing the lamina relieves pressure on the spinal cord and nerve roots and is the standard surgical intervention for stenosis in this skeletal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)",
    "long_answer": "During the late follicular phase, FSH acts on granulosa cells of the dominant follicle. This stimulation triggers transcription of the luteinizing hormone/choriogonadotropin receptor (LHCGR) gene, leading to a marked increase in LH receptor numbers. The newly expressed LH receptors allow the granulosa cells to respond to the mid-cycle LH surge, an essential step for final oocyte maturation and ovulation. Hence, FSH is the hormone required for up-regulating the LH/CG receptor in preparation for ovulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bevacizumab",
    "long_answer": "Bevacizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds vascular endothelial growth factor-A (VEGF-A), is employed as an adjuvant treatment for recurrent respiratory papillomatosis. By inhibiting VEGF-mediated angiogenesis within papillomatous lesions, intralesional or systemic bevacizumab can reduce lesion vascularity and growth, thereby decreasing the frequency of surgical debulking in patients with this HPV-driven airway disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultrasound and CT scan",
    "long_answer": "Wandering spleen is usually identified first with abdominal ultrasonography, which shows absence of the spleen in its normal position and a mobile splenic mass elsewhere. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT) is then used to confirm the diagnosis, delineating the ectopic spleen, its vascular pedicle, and any associated torsion or infarction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TIMP3",
    "long_answer": "Sorsby's fundus dystrophy is classically caused by heterozygous mutations in the TIMP3 gene, which encodes tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinases-3 on chromosome 22q12.3. These gain-of-function or misfolding mutations in TIMP3 are transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion, producing the characteristic macular degeneration seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cleft palate",
    "long_answer": "In reported cases of Cryptorchidism-arachnodactyly-intellectual disability syndrome, an orofacial cleft—most often a cleft palate—recurred alongside the core findings of undescended testes, long slender fingers, and developmental delay. Because this palatal cleft appears in multiple affected individuals, it is considered a common accompanying birth defect in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maternal antisocial behavior",
    "long_answer": "Women with antisocial or conduct-related traits are more likely both to smoke during pregnancy and to transmit genetic and environmental risks for conduct disorder to their children, making maternal antisocial behavior a key confounding variable in the observed association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Necrosis",
    "long_answer": "When blood flow is restored to an ischemic tissue, the sudden influx of oxygen and inflammatory cells generates large amounts of reactive oxygen species, calcium overload, and complement activation. These events disrupt cell membranes and mitochondria, causing uncontrolled loss of ion homeostasis and swelling—hallmarks of necrotic cell death. Thus, the tissue damage observed in reperfusion injury is chiefly mediated by necrosis (although some apoptosis can also occur)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Australia",
    "long_answer": "The first documented case of Fasciola hepatica resistance to triclabendazole was reported in Australia, with Overend and Bowen’s 1995 Veterinary Record paper describing treatment failure on a Victorian sheep farm. Subsequent studies in other countries followed, but this Australian report is accepted as the earliest evidence of triclabendazole-resistant F. hepatica."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Norwood procedure",
    "long_answer": "The Norwood procedure is the first stage in the surgical palliation of hypoplastic left heart syndrome and is usually carried out within the first few days of life. It reconstructs the aorta and establishes a reliable source of systemic blood flow while creating a shunt to provide pulmonary circulation, allowing the right ventricle to assume the role of the systemic ventricle until later staged operations (Glenn and Fontan) can be performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eye-poking behavior",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic hypertryptophanemia is a rare metabolic disorder characterized by markedly elevated plasma tryptophan and neuro-developmental impairment. A distinctive feature repeatedly described in affected patients is repetitive, self-injurious poking or pressing of the eyes (the oculodigital sign). This abnormal eye-directed behavior is considered a characteristic clinical clue to the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS",
    "long_answer": "Activating missense mutations in the GNAS gene – classically involving codon 201 of the Gsα-subunit – constitute the hallmark molecular lesion in fibrous dysplasia and have likewise been detected in cherubism-type gnathic giant-cell/fibro-osseous lesions. Thus, alteration of GNAS provides the common genetic link between these two fibro-osseous disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gene therapy",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IGHMBP2 gene cause the neuromuscular disorder spinal muscular atrophy with respiratory distress type 1 (SMARD1). Research groups have shown that delivering a functional IGHMBP2 cDNA with adeno-associated virus (AAV9) vectors rescues motor neurons and prolongs survival in animal models, and early clinical trials are now pursuing this AAV-mediated gene replacement approach. Thus, the therapy under investigation is gene therapy aimed at restoring normal IGHMBP2 function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lysosome",
    "long_answer": "Multiple sulfatase deficiency results from a lack of active sulfatase enzymes, which normally reside in lysosomes and degrade sulfated lipids and glycosaminoglycans. When lysosomal sulfatases are inactive, these substrates accumulate, so the key organelle whose dysfunction underlies the buildup of lipids and mucopolysaccharides is the lysosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous infusion",
    "long_answer": "Factor VIII replacement for Haemophilia A is delivered directly into a vein. Because the clotting protein is large and would be digested if taken orally, it must enter the bloodstream intact; therefore, both prophylactic and on-demand treatments use intravenous infusion (often through a peripheral vein or implanted venous access device) as the standard route of administration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pyridoxine",
    "long_answer": "Isoniazid inactivates pyridoxal-5′-phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6, producing a functional vitamin B6 deficiency that manifests as sensory peripheral neuropathy. Administering pyridoxine (vitamin B6) restores pyridoxal phosphate levels and reliably prevents or alleviates the neuropathy induced by isoniazid treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anxiety",
    "long_answer": "Children and adults with dysgraphia often struggle with writing tasks, which can lead to repeated frustration and fear of failure. Over time this stress frequently evolves into anxiety—especially school- or performance-related anxiety—making it one of the most common emotional problems observed alongside dysgraphia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-antithrombin",
    "long_answer": "Antithrombin circulates as two glycoforms: the common α-antithrombin and the high-heparin-affinity β-antithrombin. β-Antithrombin lacks one N-glycan, giving it a 5- to 10-fold higher affinity for heparin and heparan-sulfate chains that become exposed or released at sites of tissue injury. This allows β-antithrombin to localise rapidly to the damaged vascular surface and to inactivate thrombin and factor Xa more efficiently than the α-form. Consequently, β-antithrombin is considered the principal isoform for limiting thrombogenic events that arise after tissue injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycerol kinase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Glycerol kinase deficiency (GKD) is an X-linked metabolic disorder produced by mutations or deletions in the glycerol kinase (GK) gene on Xp21. Loss of GK activity blocks phosphorylation of glycerol to glycerol-3-phosphate, leading to hyperglycerolaemia, episodes of hypoglycaemia, and neuromuscular features such as neonatal or infantile hypotonia. Thus, the genetic defect in the glycerol kinase gene directly causes the hypotonia-associated disorder known as glycerol kinase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Conditions characterized by absent or immotile cilia in the respiratory epithelium, such as primary ciliary dyskinesia (including Kartagener syndrome), are classically inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern: both alleles of an associated gene (e.g., DNAI1, DNAH5) must be mutated to abolish functional cilia, and parents are typically asymptomatic carriers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GARS1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 2D is classically caused by dominant missense mutations in the GARS1 gene, which encodes glycyl-tRNA synthetase. These GARS1 mutations produce a toxic gain-of-function that preferentially injures peripheral motor and sensory axons. The resulting axonal degeneration often manifests clinically as neuropathic pain, making GARS1 mutation the established genetic cause linking CMT2D to pain symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Draining lesion exudate",
    "long_answer": "In cats infected with Sporothrix spp., the purulent exudate that drains from cutaneous nodules and ulcers can be collected and examined cytologically. Smears of this exudative fluid typically reveal large numbers of characteristic cigar-shaped yeast cells within macrophages, making the draining lesion exudate the fluid of choice for rapid cytological diagnosis of feline sporotrichosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Physical examination",
    "long_answer": "Trigger finger is most commonly diagnosed through a focused physical (clinical) examination in which the clinician palpates the flexor tendon sheath, observes catching or locking of the affected digit, and feels the characteristic tender nodule at the A1 pulley. Imaging is usually unnecessary because these clinical findings alone reliably establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leber congenital amaurosis",
    "long_answer": "Leber congenital amaurosis (LCA) is an inherited retinal dystrophy that typically presents in the first months of life with nystagmus, poor pupillary responses, and markedly reduced or absent electroretinogram signals. Mutations in any of more than 20 genes (e.g., RPE65, CEP290) disrupt photoreceptor or retinal pigment epithelium function, producing severe, progressive vision loss that often culminates in childhood blindness. Because symptoms manifest in infancy and vision loss is a principal feature, LCA fits the description of a genetic disorder with infancy-onset symptoms that can lead to vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SQSTM1 (p62)",
    "long_answer": "Paget’s disease of bone is most often linked to mutations in the SQSTM1 gene, which encodes the p62 protein. While SQSTM1 mutations account for a significant proportion of familial and some sporadic cases in people of European ancestry, genetic surveys show that such mutations are rare among individuals of Asian descent, making them much less common in these populations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAR1A",
    "long_answer": "Carney complex is most commonly caused by inactivating germ-line mutations in the PRKAR1A gene located on chromosome 17q22-24. Loss of PRKAR1A disrupts the regulatory subunit type 1α of cAMP-dependent protein kinase A (PKA), leading to constitutive PKA activation that drives the endocrine and cardiac myxoma manifestations characteristic of Carney complex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTEN",
    "long_answer": "Bannayan–Riley–Ruvalcaba syndrome is part of the PTEN-hamartoma tumor syndrome spectrum. Germline loss-of-function mutations in the PTEN tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 10q23 lead to hyperactivation of the PI3K/AKT signaling pathway, producing the characteristic features of BRRS such as macrocephaly, hamartomatous intestinal polyps, lipomas, and pigmented penile macules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Gougerot–Blum syndrome is a pigmented purpuric dermatosis, a chronic inflammatory disorder that affects the skin. Diagnosis, management, and research of such cutaneous diseases fall under the medical specialty of dermatology, the branch of medicine devoted to conditions of the skin, hair, and nails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoarthritis",
    "long_answer": "In dogs, osteochondritis dissecans produces cartilage flaps and joint inflammation that, over time, progress to degenerative joint disease. This chronic degeneration of articular cartilage and underlying bone is clinically recognized as osteoarthritis, a long-term condition particularly seen in large and rapidly growing breeds affected by OCD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I deficiency is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the CPS1 gene; because disease manifests only when both alleles are defective, it follows an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giemsa stain",
    "long_answer": "Loa loa infection is routinely diagnosed by identifying its sheathed microfilariae in peripheral blood. Thick or thin blood smears stained with Giemsa allow visualization of the characteristic microfilariae with nuclei extending to the tail tip, making Giemsa staining the standard technique for laboratory confirmation of Loa loa filariasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sialyl oligosaccharides",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-N-acetyl neuraminidase is the lysosomal sialidase that removes terminal sialic acid (N-acetyl-neuraminic acid) residues from glycoproteins and oligosaccharides. When this enzyme is deficient (sialidosis), the sialic-acid–containing oligosaccharides cannot be degraded and therefore accumulate within lysosomes of neurons and other cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cocaine use",
    "long_answer": "Maternal use of cocaine—commonly smoked as crack cocaine—is a well-established risk factor for placental abruption. The potent sympathomimetic action of cocaine causes intense vasoconstriction of uterine and placental vessels, leading to placental ischemia and premature separation (abruption) of the placenta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3–7 weeks’ gestation",
    "long_answer": "Bronchogenic cysts arise from abnormal budding of the ventral foregut and tracheobronchial tree during the early embryonic period, specifically between the 3rd and 7th weeks of gestation, when the respiratory diverticulum is forming."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Infectious disease specialist",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a gram-negative opportunistic pathogen that often causes hospital-acquired infections such as ventilator-associated pneumonia, bloodstream infections, and complicated urinary tract or wound infections. Management typically requires selection of appropriate anti-pseudomonal antibiotics, assessment of resistance patterns, and coordination of infection-control measures—tasks that fall within the expertise of an infectious disease specialist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central nervous system",
    "long_answer": "Organic acidemias cause accumulation of toxic organic acids that readily cross the blood–brain barrier, producing metabolic acidosis, hyperammonemia and energy deficiency in neural tissue. These disturbances manifest as encephalopathy, seizures and developmental delay, making the central nervous system the organ system most frequently and prominently affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Glycine encephalopathy – more commonly called non-ketotic hyperglycinemia (NKH) – is an autosomal-recessive defect of the glycine-cleavage system and therefore belongs to the group of inborn errors of amino-acid metabolism (aminoacidopathies). Population studies performed in the 1970s-1990s in Finland, Canada and several European countries estimated its birth prevalence at roughly 1 : 55 000-110 000. On the basis of these data it has routinely been cited, in textbooks and review articles, as the fourth most frequent congenital amino-acid disorder, ranking after phenylketonuria (PKU), maple-syrup-urine disease (MSUD) and homocystinuria. Consequently, describing “the fourth most common congenital disorder of amino-acid metabolism” as glycine encephalopathy is consistent with the established medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deaf-mutism",
    "long_answer": "Congenital iodine deficiency syndrome (cretinism) often damages the developing auditory pathway and laryngeal function, leading to sensorineural deafness together with an inability to develop speech—termed deaf-mutism. This classic symptom impairs the child’s capacity to communicate without spoken words and is one of the most frequently described clinical features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular dementia",
    "long_answer": "Binswanger’s disease is a subcortical leukoencephalopathy caused by chronic small-vessel ischemic changes in the brain’s white matter. Because its pathogenesis is cerebrovascular (arteriosclerosis, hypertension), it is classified as a form of vascular dementia rather than an Alzheimer-type or other neurodegenerative dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kallmann syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kallmann syndrome is a congenital form of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism in which failure of embryonic migration of GnRH neurons and olfactory bulbs leads to two hallmark findings: complete or severe loss of smell (anosmia) and markedly reduced or absent development of secondary sexual hair, often described clinically as alopecia. The concurrence of alopecia with anosmia is therefore characteristic of Kallmann syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cephalic and basilic veins",
    "long_answer": "A brachiocephalic arteriovenous fistula is fashioned by connecting the brachial artery to the cephalic vein at the ante-cubital fossa. The resultant arterial pressure increases flow up the cephalic vein and, via the median-cubital crossover, into the basilic venous system. Thus both the cephalic vein (proximal arm) and the basilic vein receive the augmented blood flow that characterises this fistula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Convergence excess",
    "long_answer": "Esophoria is a latent tendency for the eyes to drift inward when fusion is disrupted. A classic ocular-muscle imbalance producing this condition is convergence excess, where the medial rectus (convergence) activity is relatively over-strong—or divergence amplitudes are insufficient—compared with the lateral rectus (divergence) system. This imbalance shifts the resting ocular alignment toward an inward position, giving rise to esophoria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Barium swallow study",
    "long_answer": "A contrast barium swallow (esophagram) can outline the esophageal lumen and mucosal irregularities; it often reveals masses, strictures, or filling defects caused by esophageal carcinoma when an upper-GI endoscope cannot pass or has missed the lesion, making it the next diagnostic step if endoscopy is unsuccessful."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Candidal intertrigo",
    "long_answer": "Excessive sweating keeps the axillary folds warm and moist, conditions that allow Candida albicans to overgrow and produce an erythematous, macerated rash known as candidal intertrigo. Endocrine disorders that impair immunity and raise tissue glucose, such as Cushing’s syndrome, are well-recognized predisposing factors for this cutaneous Candida infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Propionic acidemia results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PCCA or PCCB genes encoding the two subunits of propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Because disease manifests only when both copies of the gene are defective and carriers are asymptomatic, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PYGM",
    "long_answer": "The most frequently diagnosed muscle-specific glycogen storage disease is McArdle disease (GSD type V). It results from autosomal-recessive mutations in the PYGM gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase). Loss of PYGM activity blocks glycogen breakdown in muscle, leading to exercise intolerance, cramps, and myoglobinuria—hallmark features that make GSD V the commonest myopathic glycogen storage disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "I-123 (iodine-123) is a gamma-emitting radioisotope used almost exclusively for diagnostic nuclear-medicine imaging studies, particularly of the thyroid. It delivers radiation to the body and has no pharmacologic or biological action that mitigates radiation injury or removes radioactive contaminants. In cases of radiation exposure or internal contamination with radioactive iodine, the recommended countermeasure is administration of non-radioactive potassium iodide (stable iodine), which saturates the thyroid and blocks uptake of the radioactive isotope. Other decorporation agents (e.g., Prussian blue, Ca- or Zn-DTPA) are used for different radionuclides. Giving I-123 to a patient who has radiation poisoning would add additional radiation dose and provide no therapeutic benefit. Therefore, I-123 is not a useful treatment for radiation poisoning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "In the few families reported with Katz syndrome, the affected children are born to clinically normal parents, recurrence is seen among siblings, and both males and females are affected in equal numbers. The absence of vertical (parent-to-child) transmission together with sibling clustering is characteristic of an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance, indicating that two mutated copies of the responsible gene are required for the syndrome to manifest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cognitive-behavioral therapy",
    "long_answer": "Blood-injection-injury phobia is typically treated with cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT), which combines cognitive restructuring to correct distorted catastrophic thoughts about blood with graduated exposure and behavioral techniques (e.g., applied tension). CBT has strong empirical support for reducing fear and avoidance in specific phobias, making it the standard intervention for blood phobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alzheimer disease",
    "long_answer": "In Alzheimer disease, progressive cortical neurodegeneration eventually involves motor-controlling cortical and subcortical networks. In this advanced (late) stage, patients often develop generalized myoclonus—brief, shock-like muscle jerks—alongside other late manifestations such as seizures. Thus, myoclonus is a recognized late-stage clinical feature of Alzheimer disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Reduced self-efficacy",
    "long_answer": "Research on learning disabilities shows that the persistent writing difficulties characteristic of dysgraphia often lead to repeated academic failures and negative feedback. These experiences lower individuals’ confidence in their ability to perform writing-related tasks, generalizing to broader academic and personal domains. Consequently, dysgraphia is consistently associated with diminished self-efficacy—the belief in one’s capability to organize and execute actions required for desired outcomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydroxyurea",
    "long_answer": "Hydroxyurea (hydroxycarbamide) is the standard cytoreductive drug for essential thrombocythemia. It inhibits the enzyme ribonucleotide reductase, preventing conversion of ribonucleotides to deoxyribonucleotides, thereby blocking DNA synthesis and reducing excessive platelet production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complete blood count",
    "long_answer": "A complete blood count (CBC) is the standard laboratory test that quantitatively measures circulating blood cells, including platelets. In Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura, the CBC reveals an isolated low platelet count, which is essential for confirming the diagnosis and excluding other cytopenias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "World Health Organization",
    "long_answer": "The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) is maintained and published by the World Health Organization (WHO). Dercum's disease (also called adiposis dolorosa) is listed in the ICD—currently under code E88.2 in ICD-10—reflecting the WHO's formal recognition of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical excision",
    "long_answer": "Choroid plexus papillomas are benign intraventricular tumors that commonly cause obstructive hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure. The accepted, curative treatment for symptomatic lesions is complete surgical excision (total resection), which simultaneously removes the tumor and alleviates CSF obstruction, providing long-term control without the need for adjuvant therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prothrombin complex concentrate",
    "long_answer": "Congenital or acquired factor VII deficiency can be treated with a prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC). PCC preparations provide not only factor VII but also the other vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors (II, IX and X), thereby correcting the deficiency while supplying additional coagulation proteins needed for haemostasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uterine fibroid",
    "long_answer": "Uterine fibroids (leiomyomas) are benign smooth-muscle tumors of the uterus. They commonly cause menorrhagia—excessive or prolonged menstrual bleeding—which can result in anemia and disrupt the normal uterine environment. By distorting the uterine cavity or impairing endometrial receptivity, fibroids can interfere with implantation and embryo development, making them a well-recognized cause of infertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Lymphoma is a malignant disorder of the lymphatic and hematopoietic systems, so its evaluation and management fall under the subspecialty of Hematology (often combined with Oncology). Hematologists are trained to perform diagnostic procedures such as lymph node biopsy interpretation, staging with blood and marrow studies, and to administer systemic therapies like chemotherapy, monoclonal antibodies, and hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation for lymphoma patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glomerular filtration rate",
    "long_answer": "The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) quantifies how much plasma the kidneys filter per minute and therefore directly reflects overall renal excretory capacity. As GFR declines, nitrogenous and other uremic toxins accumulate, so the level of GFR is the accepted standard for staging chronic kidney disease and gauging the severity of uremia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Oculofaciocardiodental (OFCD) syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants in the BCOR gene. BCOR encodes the BCL-6 corepressor, a transcriptional coregulatory protein essential for normal ocular, facial, cardiac and dental development; loss-of-function mutations in BCOR underlie the characteristic anomalies seen in OFCD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel (NaV1.4), disrupt normal sodium influx in muscle fibres. The persistent or aberrant Na+ entry forces the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (sodium-potassium pump) to work harder to re-establish ionic gradients, linking SCN4A mutations to pump-dependent membrane excitability disorders such as hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis and paramyotonia congenita."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Delirium and dementia",
    "long_answer": "Organic brain syndrome is classically divided into two principal clinical categories: delirium, representing an acute, fluctuating confusional state, and dementia, representing a chronic, progressive loss of cognitive functions. This acute-versus-chronic distinction underlies the standard psychiatric classification of organic mental disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GOSR2",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous or compound-heterozygous mutations in the GOSR2 gene (encoding Golgi SNAP-receptor complex member 2) are a recognized cause of progressive myoclonus epilepsy type 6. A hallmark of this disorder is the co-occurrence of cerebellar ataxia, reflecting GOSR2’s role in neuronal vesicle trafficking; loss-of-function disrupts cerebellar Purkinje cell function, producing gait and limb ataxia alongside the myoclonus and generalized seizures characteristic of progressive myoclonus epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TorsinA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DYT1 gene cause a defect in the ATP-binding chaperone protein TorsinA. This defective TorsinA is the established cause of early-onset generalized torsion dystonia and can also present as focal dystonias such as writer’s cramp."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ollier disease",
    "long_answer": "Enchondromatosis is characterized by the development of multiple intramedullary cartilage tumors (enchondromas) within the bones; this condition is classically known as Ollier disease. The term 'Ollier disease' is widely used in orthopedic and oncologic literature as the synonymous designation for enchondromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Encapsulated bacteria",
    "long_answer": "The spleen contains marginal-zone macrophages and IgM-producing B cells that opsonize and clear encapsulated bacteria (e.g., Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis). After splenectomy this immune function is lost, so these organisms are not efficiently removed from the bloodstream, leading to a markedly increased risk of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B2 (riboflavin)",
    "long_answer": "Vitamin B2, also known as riboflavin, is converted to the flavin cofactors FMN and FAD that shuttle electrons in many oxidation–reduction (redox) reactions. FAD is the required cofactor for methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR); in riboflavin-responsive forms of homocystinuria, high-dose riboflavin enhances residual MTHFR activity and lowers homocysteine/homocystine levels, making riboflavin an established therapeutic vitamin for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Spinal Muscular Atrophy with Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsy is a neuro‐degenerative condition characterized by lower-motor-neuron weakness and epileptic myoclonic seizures. Diagnosis, seizure control, and management of neuromuscular degeneration fall primarily under the expertise of neurologists (often pediatric or neuromuscular subspecialists), making Neurology the specialty most involved in caring for these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum and melanoma",
    "long_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) is an autosomal-recessive disorder in which mutations in nucleotide-excision-repair genes (e.g., XPA–XPG) sharply reduce the cell’s ability to remove UV-induced thymine dimers, making patients extremely sensitive to sunlight. One well-recognized consequence of severe or repeated sunburn in the general population is malignant melanoma, a pigment-cell skin cancer whose risk rises markedly after intense intermittent UV exposure. Thus, XP is the DNA-repair defect, and melanoma is a common skin condition that can follow severe sunburn."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-thalassemia major",
    "long_answer": "Deletion of all four α-globin genes (α-thalassemia major) produces severe fetal anemia. The profound anemia forces the fetal heart to pump a much larger volume of blood to maintain oxygen delivery, leading to high-output cardiac failure and generalized edema—manifesting as hydrops fetalis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serum protein electrophoresis",
    "long_answer": "In multiple myeloma the malignant plasma cells secrete a monoclonal immunoglobulin (M-protein). When the patient’s serum is subjected to protein electrophoresis, this abnormal antibody migrates as a sharp, discrete band—an “M-spike.” Therefore, serum protein electrophoresis is the standard laboratory technique used to detect and quantify these abnormal antibody proteins and is a key component of multiple myeloma diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontal bone",
    "long_answer": "In trigonocephaly the metopic (frontal) suture fuses prematurely, forcing the two halves of the frontal bone to close early. Because the frontal bone forms the anterior part of the skull—commonly called the forehead—the abnormal fusion produces the characteristic triangular-shaped front of the head. Thus, the anatomical structure occupying the front of the head in a child with trigonocephaly is the frontal bone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythema nodosum",
    "long_answer": "Erythema nodosum is a panniculitis characterized by tender, red nodules—typically on the shins—whose occurrence is classically linked to a diverse set of conditions. It is well-documented as a hypersensitivity reaction in patients with Hodgkin’s disease (Hodgkin lymphoma) and also arises in certain infections, including typhoid fever caused by Salmonella enterica serotype Typhi. Hence, erythema nodosum is the condition associated with both Hodgkin’s disease and S. Typhi infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ketogenic diet",
    "long_answer": "In congenital lactic acidosis, especially in disorders such as pyruvate-dehydrogenase or other mitochondrial defects, a high-fat, very low-carbohydrate ketogenic diet is sometimes prescribed. By shifting energy metabolism away from glycolysis toward fatty-acid β-oxidation and ketone utilization, it decreases pyruvate accumulation and subsequent lactate formation, thereby helping to mitigate chronic lactic acidosis in some patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gram-negative",
    "long_answer": "Neisseria meningitidis possesses a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane rich in lipooligosaccharide, so it does not retain the crystal-violet dye during Gram staining and instead takes up the counter-stain. This staining behavior classifies it as a Gram-negative bacterium (more specifically, a Gram-negative diplococcus)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "5-Aminolevulinic acid",
    "long_answer": "Topical 5-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) is used to treat skin lesions in patients with Epidermodysplasia verruciformis. ALA is a pro-photosensitiser that cells convert to protoporphyrin IX; when exposed to specific light wavelengths it produces reactive oxygen species that destroy abnormal cells. The same light-activated mechanism underlies its established use in photodynamic therapy for several cancers and precancerous skin conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypercalcemia",
    "long_answer": "Many patients with primary hyperparathyroidism are asymptomatic; the disorder is frequently discovered only when serum calcium is checked as part of an osteoporosis evaluation. The unexpectedly elevated calcium level (hypercalcemia) prompts further testing, leading to the diagnosis of primary hyperparathyroidism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carotid artery stenting",
    "long_answer": "When antithrombotic therapy fails or is contraindicated, placing an endovascular stent across the dissected segment of the carotid artery re-apposes the intimal flap, restores luminal patency, prevents thrombus formation and thereby lowers the risk of recurrent ischemic stroke."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia with ataxia is a hereditary neuro-cutaneous syndrome in which progressive cerebellar ataxia (a neurologic disorder) is the principal disabling feature, while the erythrokeratodermic skin plaques are secondary manifestations. Because the core clinical problem involves cerebellar dysfunction and coordination impairment, patients are most appropriately evaluated and managed by a neurologist; dermatologic care is adjunctive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thoracic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Acute mediastinitis resulting from an esophageal perforation requires urgent operative management—primary repair of the esophageal tear, mediastinal drainage and debridement—procedures that are performed by thoracic surgeons. Consequently, the specialty of thoracic surgery is directly involved in treating this life-threatening condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DAZ gene",
    "long_answer": "Microdeletions in the AZF (Azoospermia Factor) region of the Y chromosome frequently remove the DAZ (Deleted in Azoospermia) gene cluster. DAZ encodes RNA-binding proteins expressed exclusively in germ cells and is essential for the development and maturation of spermatozoa; loss of DAZ disrupts spermatogenesis and is a common genetic cause of male infertility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tetracycline",
    "long_answer": "Tetracycline is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that has historically been used in long courses to treat Whipple’s disease. It binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit of bacteria, blocking aminoacyl-tRNA attachment and thereby inhibiting protein synthesis, which suppresses growth of Tropheryma whipplei, the causative organism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Betulin",
    "long_answer": "Betulin is a triterpene that constitutes the major active component of birch bark extract. Formulated as the topical gel Oleogel-S10 (Filsuvez), betulin promotes re-epithelialisation and has been studied and used clinically as a treatment to accelerate wound healing in patients with epidermolysis bullosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal alcohol exposure",
    "long_answer": "Fetal alcohol spectrum disorders are caused by maternal consumption of ethanol during pregnancy. Alcohol freely crosses the placenta and interferes with neural crest migration and ocular morphogenesis, leading to eye defects such as microphthalmia, optic nerve hypoplasia, retinal dysplasia, strabismus, and reduced visual acuity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior basal segment of the left lower lobe",
    "long_answer": "Intralobar (intrapulmonary) sequestrations overwhelmingly arise in the lower lobes, and roughly two-thirds are found in the left lung. The classic and most frequent site is the posterior basal segment of the left lower lobe, where an isolated portion of lung parenchyma receives systemic arterial blood rather than the normal pulmonary arterial supply."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yolk sac tumor",
    "long_answer": "The yolk sac tumor (also called an endodermal-sinus tumor) is a malignant germ-cell neoplasm whose cells recapitulate yolk-sac differentiation and characteristically produce and secrete alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), making elevated AFP a key diagnostic marker for this malignancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Schimke syndrome, also called Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia, is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SMARCAL1 gene. Because disease manifests only when both alleles are mutated, the condition is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, with unaffected carrier parents each contributing one defective allele to an affected child."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "May-Hegglin anomaly is a rare inherited platelet disorder characterized by thrombocytopenia, giant platelets and Döhle-like inclusions in granulocytes. Because it affects blood cell morphology and platelet number/function, diagnosis and management (e.g., bleeding risk assessment, transfusion planning, genetic counseling) fall within the expertise of a hematologist; therefore, the medical specialty involved is Hematology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autoimmune disease",
    "long_answer": "Alopecia totalis is considered an autoimmune disorder in which the immune system—especially cytotoxic T-cells—mistakenly targets and attacks hair follicle structures, leading to complete loss of scalp hair. This classification is supported by the presence of perifollicular lymphocytic infiltrates, association with other autoimmune conditions, and responsiveness to immunomodulatory therapies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTCH1",
    "long_answer": "Basal-cell carcinomas commonly harbor loss-of-function mutations in the PTCH1 gene, a tumor-suppressor that encodes the Hedgehog receptor Patched-1. PTCH1 is located on chromosome 9q22.3, and its inactivation drives constitutive Hedgehog pathway signaling, promoting the development of sporadic basal-cell carcinomas as well as the nevoid basal-cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastrostomy tube",
    "long_answer": "Children and adults with Aicardi syndrome frequently have severe oral-motor and swallowing problems that make it hard to take in enough nutrition by mouth. A surgically placed gastrostomy tube (G-tube) allows liquid nutrition to be delivered directly into the stomach, bypassing oral feeding difficulties and reducing the risk of aspiration, so it is commonly recommended for nutritional support in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ERCC2 gene, which encodes the XPD helicase subunit of the TFIIH complex, can cause classic xeroderma pigmentosum (XP-D) as well as the combined XP/Cockayne syndrome phenotype. Defective ERCC2 impairs nucleotide-excision repair and transcription-coupled repair, explaining its involvement in both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kwashiorkor",
    "long_answer": "Kwashiorkor is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition in children that develops when dietary protein is inadequate despite adequate caloric intake. Deficient apolipoprotein synthesis in the liver impairs export of triglycerides, producing fatty liver (hepatic steatosis) and hepatomegaly, hallmark features of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thymidine phosphorylase",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalopathy (MNGIE) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TYMP gene, which encodes the enzyme thymidine phosphorylase. Deficient thymidine phosphorylase activity prevents normal degradation of thymidine and deoxyuridine, leading to systemic accumulation of these nucleosides that damages mitochondrial DNA and produces the characteristic clinical features of MNGIE."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human papillomavirus",
    "long_answer": "The condition described—multiple pigmented, verrucous papules on the penile shaft—corresponds to Bowenoid papulosis, which is etiologically linked to infection with high-risk types of Human papillomavirus (especially HPV-16, but also types 18, 31, and 33). HPV DNA is regularly identified in these lesions, establishing the virus as the associated pathogen."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supportive symptomatic therapy",
    "long_answer": "Bethlem myopathy has no disease-modifying or curative drug; management relies on supportive symptomatic therapy—primarily physiotherapy, stretching, orthopedic interventions, and respiratory support—to preserve mobility and manage contractures and other complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastrointestinal hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Blue Rubber Bleb Nevus Syndrome is characterized by multiple venous malformations, most abundantly in the gastrointestinal tract. Recurrent or massive bleeding from these gut lesions leads to chronic anemia and, in severe episodes, fatal exsanguination. Consequently, uncontrolled gastrointestinal hemorrhage is the leading cause of death in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Thrombocytopenia is a disorder characterized by abnormally low platelet counts in the blood. Assessment, diagnosis, and treatment of platelet, red-cell, and white-cell abnormalities fall under hematology—the branch of medicine that studies blood, blood-forming organs, and blood disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fenestrations",
    "long_answer": "In cor triatriatum, the abnormal fibro-muscular membrane that divides the atrium is usually perforated by one or more small openings. These openings are referred to as “fenestrations,” a term commonly used in cardiology and pathology to denote small, window-like perforations in a membrane or septum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratinocytes",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus epidermidis, a common skin commensal, binds to Toll-like receptor-2 (TLR2) on epidermal keratinocytes. This interaction triggers expression of cytokines and antimicrobial peptides such as β-defensins, thereby modulating both innate and adaptive immune responses in the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Insulin",
    "long_answer": "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy arises when chronically high blood-glucose damages peripheral nerves; the hyperglycaemia is a direct result of inadequate or ineffective secretion of the pancreatic hormone insulin. For this reason, clinicians routinely include a serum insulin (or C-peptide) determination—alongside glucose/HbA1c—in the laboratory work-up to confirm that the neuropathy is secondary to diabetes and impaired insulin regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gaucher disease",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher disease is a lysosomal storage disorder caused by deficiency of the enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase. The accumulated substrate (glucocerebroside) leads to massive hepatosplenomegaly, anemia, thrombocytopenia and bone crises. Standard treatment is enzyme replacement therapy with recombinant β-glucocerebrosidase (e.g., imiglucerase, velaglucerase), which reduces organomegaly and improves hematologic and skeletal manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Addison’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Addison’s disease, also called primary adrenal insufficiency, results from destruction or dysfunction of the adrenal cortex, leading to deficient secretion of the glucocorticoid cortisol and the mineralocorticoid aldosterone. The lack of these hormones impairs energy metabolism and sodium-potassium balance, producing symptoms such as weakness, fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, and hyperpigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inattentive presentation, working memory",
    "long_answer": "In adults, ADHD most commonly manifests as the predominantly inattentive presentation—hyperactive / impulsive symptoms tend to lessen with age, while inattention persists. Neuropsychological studies consistently show that people with ADHD have marked deficits in executive functions, especially working memory, which underlies tasks such as holding and manipulating information, planning, and goal-directed behavior."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adrenal crisis",
    "long_answer": "In congenital adrenal hyperplasia, deficient cortisol and often aldosterone production can precipitate a life-threatening adrenal crisis, characterized by severe hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and shock. If not rapidly treated with glucocorticoids and fluid/electrolyte replacement, this salt-wasting crisis can lead to death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudopterygium",
    "long_answer": "A pseudopterygium is an inflammatory or post-traumatic adhesion of conjunctiva to the peripheral cornea that can look very similar to a true pterygium on clinical examination—appearing as a triangular fold of conjunctiva encroaching onto the cornea. Because the two share location and shape, a pseudopterygium is often mistaken for pterygium unless specific distinguishing features (such as its mobility on probing and occurrence at any clock-hour position) are carefully assessed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is a hereditary channelopathy characterized by episodic muscle weakness or paralysis associated with low serum potassium. As a neuromuscular disorder involving ion-channel dysfunction of skeletal muscle, its diagnosis (electrophysiologic studies, genetic testing) and management (acute potassium replacement, preventive therapies such as carbonic-anhydrase inhibitors) are primarily within the expertise of neurologists, especially those specializing in neuromuscular medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain radiography",
    "long_answer": "Chondrocalcinosis caused by calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystal deposition is most readily visualised as linear or punctate calcifications within articular cartilage on standard plain radiographs (conventional X-rays), making this imaging technique the diagnostic mainstay for detecting the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In medical terminology, a syndrome refers to a consistent cluster of signs, symptoms, and clinical findings that occur together and characterize a particular condition or disorder. For example, microcephaly-deafness syndrome denotes the specific grouping of microcephaly and sensorineural hearing loss that reliably co-occur, illustrating how a syndrome captures a distinctive pattern of clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Grade 2⁄3 astrocytoma",
    "long_answer": "In December 2005, Matt Cappotelli’s doctors identified his brain tumor as a grade II/III astrocytoma—a diffuse astrocytic glioma arising from astrocytes. This intermediate-grade tumor (between low-grade II and anaplastic grade III) explained his neurological symptoms and, although initially resected, later progressed to a higher-grade glioblastoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 6",
    "long_answer": "Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6) is the principal causative agent of roseola infantum (exanthem subitum) in infants. In adolescents and adults, primary HHV-6 infection or reactivation can produce an infectious-mononucleosis–like syndrome with fever, lymphadenopathy, atypical lymphocytosis, and positive heterophile-negative serology. Thus the same virus is recognized to cause both roseola and a form of infectious mononucleosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Cloacal exstrophy is typically identified during routine obstetric imaging. High-resolution prenatal ultrasound reveals the characteristic midline abdominal wall defect, non-visualization of the urinary bladder, and associated omphalocele, allowing definitive diagnosis before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aldosterone",
    "long_answer": "CYP17A1 encodes 17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase, the enzyme that channels pregnenolone and progesterone toward cortisol and sex-steroid synthesis. When this enzyme is deficient, those pathways are blocked and steroid precursors are shunted into the mineralocorticoid pathway, markedly increasing production and secretion of the mineralocorticoid hormone aldosterone (as well as its precursor 11-deoxycorticosterone). The resulting excess aldosterone is responsible for the hypertension and hypokalaemia characteristic of 17α-hydroxylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPM1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the TRPM1 gene (Transient Receptor Potential Cation Channel, Subfamily M, Member 1) disrupt ON-bipolar-cell signaling. In horses, a large insertion in TRPM1 linked to the Leopard Complex (LP) coat pattern causes congenital stationary night blindness. Human genetic studies have also identified TRPM1 variants in genome-wide association analyses of refractive error, implicating the same gene in susceptibility to myopia. Thus, TRPM1 is the common genetic link between equine CSNB and human myopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarpa salpa",
    "long_answer": "Sarpa salpa, commonly called the salema porgy or \"dreamfish,\" is a coastal sparid fish of the Mediterranean and eastern Atlantic. In rare cases its flesh contains indole alkaloids that induce ichthyoallyeinotoxism, producing vivid, LSD-like hallucinations when eaten, making it the best-known hallucinogenic coastal fish species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary embolism",
    "long_answer": "A ruptured appendix can trigger severe inflammation, infection, and prolonged immobilization, creating a hypercoagulable state in which a deep-vein thrombus may form. If part of this clot dislodges and travels through the venous circulation to lodge in the pulmonary arteries, it produces a pulmonary embolism—a potentially fatal complication characterized by sudden obstruction of blood flow in the lungs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Interleukin-1β",
    "long_answer": "In mevalonate kinase deficiency, shortage of isoprenoid intermediates prevents normal prenylation of small GTP-binding proteins, leading to spontaneous NLRP3–caspase-1 inflammasome activation. This results in excessive maturation and secretion of the pro-inflammatory cytokine interleukin-1β (IL-1β), which drives the periodic fever and systemic inflammation characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methemoglobinemia",
    "long_answer": "Methemoglobinemia is the clinical condition characterized by an abnormally high concentration of methemoglobin—an oxidized form of hemoglobin that contains ferric (Fe3+) iron—in circulating blood. Because methemoglobin cannot bind oxygen effectively, its accumulation leads to impaired oxygen delivery to tissues, resulting in cyanosis and hypoxic symptoms. The disorder can be congenital (e.g., CYB5R3 deficiency, Hb M variants) or acquired from exposure to oxidizing agents such as nitrates, aniline dyes, certain local anesthetics, or sulfonamide antibiotics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI) is a hereditary collagen-type I disorder. Defective collagen weakens bone matrix, producing extreme bone fragility with recurrent fractures. The same abnormal collagen makes ligaments and joint capsules lax, giving marked joint hypermobility that predisposes to repeated subluxations and dislocations. Thus OI combines hypermobility with frequent joint dislocations and fractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary hyperaldosteronism",
    "long_answer": "Conn’s syndrome is the classic form of primary hyperaldosteronism, an endocrine disorder in which autonomous overproduction of aldosterone by the adrenal cortex (usually due to an aldosterone-producing adenoma) leads to sodium retention, hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Hagemoser–Weinstein–Bresnick syndrome (optic atrophy with sensorineural deafness and peripheral neuropathy) segregates in families in a vertical fashion, including father-to-son transmission, demonstrating an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance rather than mitochondrial or autosomal recessive transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metacercaria",
    "long_answer": "Fasciolosis is acquired when humans ingest the encysted metacercarial stage of Fasciola spp. (usually on contaminated water-plants or in water). The metacercaria is the infective larval form that survives outside the snail intermediate host, adheres to vegetation, and initiates infection once swallowed, making it the stage responsible for transmission to humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "African Americans",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies in the United States show that the incidence of acquired thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura is roughly 2- to 3-fold higher in individuals of African ancestry than in White or other racial groups, with African-American women being particularly affected. This increased risk has been linked to population-specific genetic variants in the ADAMTS13 gene and HLA alleles that predispose to the autoimmune inhibition of ADAMTS13, the central pathogenic mechanism in TTP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Harding ataxia is a hereditary cerebellar ataxia that affects the nervous system, producing gait disturbance, dysarthria, and other neurological deficits. Evaluation, diagnosis (clinical examination, neuro-imaging, genetic testing), and symptomatic management of ataxias are the responsibility of neurologists; hence the medical specialty involved is Neurology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bisphosphonates",
    "long_answer": "Multicentric carpotarsal osteolysis syndrome involves excessive osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. Anti-resorptive bisphosphonate drugs (such as pamidronate or zoledronate) can inhibit osteoclast activity and are therefore employed to treat or stabilize the progressive bone lesions seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNE",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal recessive form of hereditary inclusion body myopathy (hIBM2/Nonaka myopathy) is caused by mutations in the GNE gene, which encodes the bifunctional enzyme UDP-N-acetylglucosamine 2-epimerase/N-acetylmannosamine kinase. Loss-of-function mutations in GNE reduce sialic acid synthesis, leading to progressive muscle fiber degeneration with rimmed vacuoles characteristic of this recessive myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epsilon-aminocaproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Superficial mucocutaneous bleeding in congenital afibrinogenemia can be controlled with the antifibrinolytic agent epsilon-aminocaproic acid, which inhibits plasminogen activation and stabilizes the nascent platelet plug when fibrinogen replacement is not urgently required."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST1",
    "long_answer": "Saethre–Chotzen syndrome is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder that arises from heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions in the TWIST1 gene. TWIST1 encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor essential for normal cranial suture patterning, and its disruption leads to the characteristic skull, facial, and limb anomalies seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Irritability",
    "long_answer": "Infants with Krabbe disease typically present with marked irritability—persistent, inconsolable crying due to progressive demyelination. Irritability is also widely recognized as a transdiagnostic symptom in mental health, appearing in depression, anxiety, bipolar disorder, ADHD, and other conditions, making it a shared clinical feature across these disparate disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF4 rs12203592",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide association studies have shown that the intronic variant rs12203592 in the IRF4 gene increases susceptibility to actinic keratosis and cutaneous squamous-cell carcinoma. The same T-allele of rs12203592 is a major determinant of lighter skin, hair and eye colour in humans, linking this single IRF4 mutation to both actinic keratosis risk and light skin pigmentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAPN3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CAPN3 gene, encoding the muscle-specific protease calpain-3, are the most prevalent cause of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A in Europe and large parts of Asia. Early in the disease CAPN3-deficient patients often show pronounced hypertrophy of calf and shoulder-girdle muscles, creating a ‘pseudo-athletic’ appearance before weakness becomes evident."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmic artery",
    "long_answer": "In giant cell (temporal) arteritis, granulomatous inflammation often involves the branches of the external and internal carotid arteries. When the ophthalmic artery—an internal carotid branch supplying the optic nerve and retina—is inflamed and occluded, ischemia of the optic nerve head or retina ensues, producing sudden, potentially permanent vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clonus",
    "long_answer": "Clonus is a rhythmic, involuntary pattern of alternating muscle contractions and relaxations. It reflects hyper-excitability of the stretch-reflex pathway after an upper motor-neuron lesion and is frequently observed in cerebral palsy, where damage to the developing motor cortex and descending tracts produces spasticity and associated clonic jerks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric oncology",
    "long_answer": "Primitive neuroectodermal tumors are aggressive childhood malignancies that require multimodal cancer therapy (chemotherapy, radiotherapy, surgery). The medical specialty that directs and coordinates such cancer treatment in children is pediatric oncology, making pediatric oncologists central to the management of these tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a systemic autoimmune inflammatory joint disease. Physicians trained in rheumatology—rheumatologists—specialize in diagnosing and treating autoimmune and inflammatory disorders of the joints, muscles, and connective tissues, making them the recognized experts for managing rheumatoid arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diabetes mellitus",
    "long_answer": "Diabetes mellitus causes peripheral neuropathy that can involve the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. Loss of corneal sensory innervation diminishes trophic support and blinking reflexes, impairing epithelial maintenance and wound healing, which can culminate in neurotrophic keratitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antitubercular drugs",
    "long_answer": "Tuberculous pleurisy is caused by infection of the pleural space with Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The cornerstone of therapy is a full course of antitubercular drugs (isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol, etc.), which eradicate the mycobacteria and lead to resolution of the pleural effusion and inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolonged aPTT uncorrected by mixing",
    "long_answer": "Acquired haemophilia A is caused by auto-antibodies that inhibit factor VIII. This produces an isolated prolongation of the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). When the patient’s plasma is mixed 1:1 with normal plasma, the inhibitor prevents correction, so the aPTT remains prolonged. An isolated, prolonged aPTT that fails to correct in a mixing study in a person with no prior bleeding history is the classic laboratory finding that points to acquired haemophilia A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alport syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the type IV collagen genes COL4A3, COL4A4 (autosomal forms) and COL4A5 (X-linked form) disrupt the α3, α4 and α5 chains of basement-membrane collagen, producing the hereditary nephropathy known as Alport syndrome. Besides progressive renal disease and sensorineural deafness, Alport syndrome frequently manifests ocular abnormalities such as anterior lenticonus and macular hypoplasia, linking these collagen gene defects to the observed retinal finding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gray matter",
    "long_answer": "In Alzheimer’s disease, extracellular amyloid-β plaques accumulate predominantly within the brain’s cortical gray matter—the regions rich in neuronal cell bodies such as the hippocampus and neocortex—rather than in the myelinated white matter. This gray-matter deposition is a hallmark pathological feature that contributes to synaptic dysfunction and neurodegeneration in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Autoimmune-mediated (anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB–associated) congenital heart block often eventually requires pacing, but the need for a pacemaker in the immediate neonatal period is not the rule. Large series and registry data show that about 60–70 % of affected children will receive a pacemaker at some time during childhood, yet only 15–30 % undergo implantation in the first days-to-weeks of life. Most infants are initially managed with medical therapies (maternal steroids, β-agonists, intravenous immunoglobulin, etc.) and close monitoring; pacing is deferred unless the newborn has symptomatic bradycardia, ventricular dysfunction, or very low ventricular rates. Consequently, early pacemaker implantation is required in a minority of treated patients, while the majority either receive a device later in childhood or never need one at all. This agrees with current consensus statements and outcome studies on immune-mediated congenital AV block."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dentigerous cyst",
    "long_answer": "Holmes-Collins syndrome is a rare hereditary disorder in which many permanent teeth fail to erupt; cystic enlargement of the follicle surrounding these unerupted teeth is characteristic, giving rise to dentigerous cysts. Consequently, the odontogenic lesion typically linked to Holmes-Collins syndrome is the dentigerous cyst."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Increased intra-abdominal pressure",
    "long_answer": "Excess visceral fat in obesity elevates intra-abdominal pressure, which mechanically stresses the lower esophageal sphincter, promotes transient sphincter relaxations, and favors hiatal hernia formation. These changes allow gastric contents to reflux into the esophagus more readily, thereby raising the likelihood of gastroesophageal reflux disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Single-nucleotide polymorphisms",
    "long_answer": "Single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) are single–base changes in DNA that vary between individuals. SNPs can alter gene expression or protein function, affecting how patients metabolize or respond to drugs (pharmacogenomics), and specific SNPs in genes such as APOE are strongly linked to the risk of Alzheimer’s disease and other conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anaphylactic shock",
    "long_answer": "Anaphylactic shock is an immune-mediated (type I hypersensitivity) form of distributive shock in which antigen-induced IgE degranulation of mast cells and basophils releases histamine, leukotrienes, and other mediators. These substances markedly increase capillary permeability and cause widespread vasodilation, leading to rapid intravascular volume loss and hypotension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PiZZ SERPINA1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Homozygosity for the PiZZ (Glu342Lys) mutation in the SERPINA1 gene causes severe alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Misfolded A1AT protein accumulates in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, provoking cellular injury that presents in the neonatal period with cholestasis and can rapidly progress to acute or chronic liver failure in infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Histopathological examination",
    "long_answer": "Paracoccidioidomycosis is definitively confirmed by histopathological examination of a biopsy specimen. When the tissue is stained (e.g., with Grocott-Gomori methenamine silver or PAS) and examined microscopically, the characteristic multiple-budding \"pilot-wheel\" yeast cells of Paracoccidioides spp. are visualised, providing a specific diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Brachyspira",
    "long_answer": "Human intestinal spirochetosis is produced by colon-colonizing spirochetes, chiefly Brachyspira species such as Brachyspira aalborgi and Brachyspira pilosicoli. These organisms adhere to the colonic epithelial surface, forming a characteristic fringe or ‘false brush border’ seen histologically and leading to the condition termed intestinal spirochetosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric pulmonology",
    "long_answer": "Congenital tracheobronchomalacia causes dynamic collapse of the infant’s trachea and bronchi, producing recurrent wheeze, stridor and respiratory distress. Diagnostic bronchoscopy, airway-function evaluation, and long-term management with positive-pressure ventilation or surgical referral are routinely coordinated by pediatric pulmonologists, the subspecialists devoted to lower-airway disorders in children. Hence infants with this condition are most commonly treated by pediatric pulmonology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antihistamine eye drops",
    "long_answer": "Allergic conjunctivitis results from histamine release when an allergen activates conjunctival mast cells. Topical antihistamine eye drops block ocular H1-histamine receptors, rapidly reducing itching, redness, and swelling, making them a standard treatment for inflammation of the conjunctiva caused by allergens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Irritant contact dermatitis",
    "long_answer": "Strong bases such as sodium hydroxide or potassium hydroxide are caustic chemicals that directly damage the stratum corneum and deeper epidermal layers. This non-immune, chemical injury provokes erythema, burning, and sometimes necrosis characteristic of irritant contact dermatitis, not the delayed T-cell–mediated allergic form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rabies",
    "long_answer": "Rabies is an acute zoonotic disease caused by rabies virus (an RNA virus of the Lyssavirus genus). Infection leads to a rapidly progressive, invariably fatal encephalomyelitis—the simultaneous inflammation of the brain (encephalitis) and spinal cord (myelitis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alport syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Alport syndrome is an inherited type IV-collagen disorder (most often X-linked COL4A5 mutations) in which basement-membrane defects produce adult-onset sensorineural hearing loss from cochleosaccular degeneration of the inner ear and characteristic ocular changes, notably anterior lenticonus that progresses to cataracts. These two hallmark findings—cochleosaccular degeneration and progressive cataracts—are therefore diagnostic features of Alport syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Biliary obstruction",
    "long_answer": "In cholangiocarcinoma the malignant tumor grows within and around the bile ducts, narrowing or occluding their lumen. This mechanical blockage prevents normal outflow of bile from the liver into the intestine, leading to intra-hepatic cholestasis, accumulation of conjugated bilirubin in the blood, and hence the clinical manifestation of jaundice."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adenosine",
    "long_answer": "Intravenous adenosine transiently blocks AV-nodal conduction. This reliably terminates atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia (which depends on the AV node for the re-entry circuit) but usually fails to terminate junctional ectopic tachycardia, an automatic focus in the AV junction. Thus the differential response to adenosine distinguishes AVNRT from JET."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Hennekam syndrome is caused by biallelic (homozygous or compound-heterozygous) pathogenic variants in genes such as CCBE1 or FAT4. Because two defective copies of the gene must be inherited—one from each carrier parent—the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyaluronidase",
    "long_answer": "Cutis verticis gyrata is characterized by thickened, folded scalp skin in which excess dermal ground-substance (rich in hyaluronic acid and other glycosaminoglycans) and connective-tissue hypertrophy are observed. Because hyaluronidase enzymatically degrades hyaluronic acid, small intralesional injections of hyaluronidase have been proposed and reported as a conservative, nonsurgical means of softening and flattening the scalp folds. Thus, hyaluronidase has been suggested as a possible therapeutic enzyme for cutis verticis gyrata."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GATA2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the transcription factor gene GATA2 cause pulmonary alveolar proteinosis by impairing the development and maintenance of alveolar macrophages. Complete loss of GATA2 activity is embryonically lethal in mice and humans, underscoring its critical role in early hematopoietic and vascular development. Thus, GATA2 mutations link pulmonary alveolar proteinosis with a gene essential for embryonic development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral and maxillofacial surgery",
    "long_answer": "Arrhinia is a rare congenital craniofacial anomaly characterized by complete absence of the external nose and nasal passages. Surgical reconstruction of such complex mid-facial defects falls within the scope of oral and maxillofacial surgery, the dental specialty specifically trained to perform operative management of craniofacial and oro-facial skeletal abnormalities. Oral and maxillofacial surgeons routinely collaborate with plastic and ENT surgeons to create a functional airway and reconstruct nasal form in arrhinia patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Birth trauma to the sternocleidomastoid",
    "long_answer": "Congenital muscular torticollis often arises after difficult or assisted deliveries (e.g., forceps, breech), where stretching or ischemic injury to the sternocleidomastoid muscle produces a hematoma that later fibroses and shortens the muscle, causing the characteristic head tilt in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatologist",
    "long_answer": "CREST syndrome is the limited-cutaneous form of systemic sclerosis, an autoimmune connective-tissue disease. Rheumatologists are the specialists trained to evaluate and treat systemic autoimmune and rheumatic disorders, so they are the physicians who typically diagnose CREST syndrome, monitor its progression, and coordinate multisystem management (e.g., vascular, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, pulmonary involvement)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colonic epithelial mucosa",
    "long_answer": "Human intestinal spirochetosis is characterized by colonization of the large-bowel lining; the spirochetes attach to and infect the surface epithelium of the colonic mucosa, producing a dense fringe on that tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiology",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary valve stenosis is a valvular heart defect that obstructs blood flow from the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery. Diagnosis, medical management, balloon valvuloplasty, and coordination of surgical repair are typically performed by cardiologists—often pediatric or adult congenital cardiology specialists—making cardiology the primary specialty involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antidiuretic hormone",
    "long_answer": "Small-cell carcinoma of the lung often secretes ectopic antidiuretic hormone (also called arginine vasopressin). Excessive ADH released into the circulation increases water reabsorption in the kidneys, diluting serum sodium and producing the hyponatremia and water retention characteristic of the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone hypersecretion (SIADH)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB3 and GJB4",
    "long_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia variabilis is most commonly caused by pathogenic mutations in the gap-junction protein genes GJB3 (encoding connexin-31) and GJB4 (encoding connexin-30.3). Defective connexins impair intercellular communication in the epidermis, leading to the characteristic transient erythematous patches and fixed hyperkeratotic plaques of EKV."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type I results from germ-line mutations in the NF1 gene on chromosome 17. NF1 encodes neurofibromin, a regulator that normally suppresses RAS signalling; loss of its function promotes uncontrolled cell growth. Because its alteration drives tumor development, NF1 is classified among cancer-associated genes (tumor-suppressor/oncogene class)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HINT1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the HINT1 gene (histidine triad nucleotide-binding protein 1) are the established cause of autosomal recessive axonal neuropathy with neuromyotonia. Multiple independent families have been shown to carry homozygous or compound-heterozygous HINT1 variants, and functional studies demonstrate that these mutations abolish HINT1 enzymatic activity, leading to the characteristic motor-sensory axonal neuropathy accompanied by spontaneous muscle activity (neuromyotonia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease",
    "long_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is an inborn error of branched-chain amino acid metabolism caused by deficiency of the branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex. Accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, valine and their ketoacids leads to early clinical signs that include poor feeding and recurrent vomiting, along with lethargy, seizures, and the characteristic sweet odor of urine. Thus, MSUD is a metabolic disorder of amino acid metabolism in which vomiting is a prominent symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "E. coli O157:H7",
    "long_answer": "Hemolytic–uremic syndrome is most frequently triggered by infection with the Shiga toxin–producing Escherichia coli serotype O157:H7, which releases Shiga-like toxins that damage renal endothelial cells and precipitate the triad of hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury characteristic of HUS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iodine deficiency",
    "long_answer": "The great majority of cases of congenital iodine-deficiency syndrome (formerly called cretinism) and the associated neonatal or childhood goitre result from insufficient dietary iodine in the mother and fetus. Lack of iodine prevents adequate thyroid-hormone synthesis, causing hypothyroidism and chronic TSH stimulation that enlarges the thyroid gland, producing goitre."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "For food allergies—including peanut allergy—the double-blind, placebo-controlled oral food challenge (DBPCFC) is widely regarded by allergy and immunology societies as the diagnostic gold standard. Skin-prick testing, serum specific-IgE measurement, and component-resolved diagnostics can suggest sensitization, but they cannot definitively establish clinical reactivity because of their limited predictive values. In contrast, a DBPCFC directly and objectively documents whether ingestion of the suspected food elicits reproducible symptoms, while blinding and a placebo control minimize bias and the influence of subjective expectations. Therefore, under current consensus guidelines (e.g., EAACI, AAAAI/ACAAI, WAO), the DBPCFC remains the reference method for confirming or ruling out peanut allergy when the diagnosis is uncertain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exposure therapy",
    "long_answer": "Fear of fire (pyrophobia) is classified as a specific phobia. The first-line, evidence-based treatment for specific phobias is exposure therapy—usually delivered within a cognitive-behavioural therapy framework—where the patient is gradually and repeatedly exposed to fire-related stimuli in a controlled, safe setting until the anxiety response extinguishes. This graded exposure approach has the strongest empirical support and is therefore the most commonly used intervention."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sildenafil",
    "long_answer": "Sildenafil is a phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE-5) inhibitor originally marketed for erectile dysfunction (Viagra). The same drug, under the brand name Revatio, is FDA-approved at different dosing to treat pulmonary arterial hypertension because PDE-5 inhibition increases cyclic GMP in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, producing vasodilation and lowering pulmonary arterial pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maintain ductus arteriosus",
    "long_answer": "In transposition of the great vessels the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left, so the pulmonary and systemic circuits run in parallel. Intravenous prostaglandin E1 is given to keep the ductus arteriosus from closing, providing an essential shunt that allows mixing of oxygenated and de-oxygenated blood and supports systemic oxygenation until definitive surgical repair can be performed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcolemma fragility",
    "long_answer": "Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in proteins of the dystrophin–glycoprotein complex (e.g., sarcoglycans, dysferlin) that normally stabilize the sarcolemma. The resulting sarcolemma fragility means muscle fibers tear easily during the mechanical stress of exercise, allowing Ca²⁺ influx, necrosis, and inflammation. Instead of building strength, strenuous activity therefore accelerates muscle fiber damage and disease progression in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis",
    "long_answer": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (extrinsic allergic alveolitis) often presents with work-related cough, wheeze and breathlessness that begin hours after exposure to organic dusts, closely mimicking occupational asthma. Because both conditions are triggered by inhaled workplace antigens yet differ in pathophysiology (alveolar immune inflammation vs. bronchial hyper-responsiveness), hypersensitivity pneumonitis is a well-recognized disorder that can be mistaken for occupational asthma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren's syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren's syndrome is an autoimmune disorder characterized by lymphocytic destruction of the salivary and lacrimal glands, leading to xerostomia (dry mouth) and keratoconjunctivitis sicca (dry eyes). It is one of the systemic autoimmune diseases most frequently associated with chronic spontaneous urticaria, making it the strongly supported answer to the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq27.3",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome—the most common inherited form of intellectual disability—is caused by CGG-repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene, which is located at cytogenetic band Xq27.3 on the long arm of the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis is characterized by multifocal strictures and ‘beading’ of the intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts. These characteristic biliary changes are best demonstrated with cholangiography; the standard diagnostic cholangiographic procedure is endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP), which directly visualizes and outlines the bile ducts, allowing confirmation of PSC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arsenic",
    "long_answer": "Inorganic arsenic compounds—particularly those in chromated copper arsenate (CCA) used to pressure-treat lumber—are recognized Group 1 carcinogens. Epidemiological studies of workers handling arsenic-based wood preservatives show significantly elevated lung-cancer incidence, and inhaled arsenic dust or fumes cause genotoxic effects in pulmonary tissue, explaining the increased risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B8-DR3 haplotype",
    "long_answer": "Selective IgA deficiency shows a strong association with the ancestral MHC class-II haplotype that includes HLA-B8 and HLA-DR3 (often A1-B8-DR3-DQ2 on chromosome 6). The same HLA-B8-DR3 (8.1) haplotype is a well-established genetic risk factor for several autoimmune diseases such as coeliac disease, type 1 diabetes and systemic lupus erythematosus, linking these genetic differences to both IgA deficiency and autoimmunity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediastinal compression",
    "long_answer": "Chest pain in patients with thymoma is commonly produced when the enlarging anterior mediastinal tumor compresses or directly invades adjacent mediastinal structures—such as pleura, pericardium, or great vessels—eliciting pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chlorhexidine",
    "long_answer": "Chlorhexidine, a cationic biguanide antiseptic widely formulated as chlorhexidine gluconate mouthwash, is well-documented to cause reversible dysgeusia (taste disturbance). Its strong affinity for oral mucosa and taste bud membranes can blunt taste perception for several hours to days after use, making taste alteration a recognized adverse effect listed in clinical guidelines and product labeling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urachal cyst",
    "long_answer": "A urachal cyst is formed from a persistent mid-portion of the fetal urachus between the bladder and the umbilicus. These cysts often become infected with urinary pathogens, so patients commonly present with urinary tract infection–like symptoms. If an infected urachal cyst ruptures, its purulent contents can spill into the peritoneal cavity, precipitating acute peritonitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatology",
    "long_answer": "Steatocystoma multiplex is a cutaneous disorder characterized by multiple cystic lesions arising from sebaceous ducts within the skin. Diagnosis, counseling, and treatments such as surgical excision, laser ablation, or oral retinoids are routinely managed by dermatologists, making Dermatology the primary specialty involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The RPS19 gene, whose mutations are the most common cause of Diamond–Blackfan anemia, is mapped to chromosome 19 at cytogenetic band 19q13.2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Concrescence",
    "long_answer": "Concrescence is a developmental dental anomaly in which the roots of two adjacent teeth become united exclusively by the cementum layer, without involvement of dentin or enamel. This cemental fusion distinguishes concrescence from true fusion (which involves dentin) and other tooth-joining abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Crouzonodermoskeletal (Crouzon-acanthosis nigricans) syndrome is caused by a specific activating mutation (Ala391Glu) in the FGFR3 gene. This FGFR3 mutation underlies the craniofacial dysostosis and the odontogenic lesions, including the development of periapical cemental dysplasias (cementomas) seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Serotonin",
    "long_answer": "Brunner syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) gene, the enzyme responsible for degrading monoamines. Without functional MAOA, serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is inadequately metabolized, leading to chronically elevated serotonin levels (and high urinary 5-HIAA), which are characteristic of the disorder and contribute to its behavioral manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Non-ketotic hyperglycinemia",
    "long_answer": "Non-ketotic hyperglycinemia, also called glycine encephalopathy, is a neurometabolic disorder caused by defects in the glycine cleavage system. The resulting accumulation of glycine in blood, cerebrospinal fluid, and brain tissue disrupts neurotransmission. In neonates and young infants, this excess glycine depresses brain-stem respiratory centers, commonly producing episodes of life-threatening apnea along with seizures and hypotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DHODH",
    "long_answer": "Miller syndrome (post-axial acrofacial dysostosis), which features malformations of the external ears along with limb and craniofacial anomalies, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the DHODH gene encoding dihydro-orotate dehydrogenase. Pathogenic variants in DHODH disrupt de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis during embryonic development, leading to the characteristic ear malformations observed in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXC2",
    "long_answer": "Meige disease, a form of hereditary (primary) lymphedema with onset around puberty, is most commonly linked to mutations in the FOXC2 gene. FOXC2 is a forkhead‐box transcription factor essential for lymphatic valve and vessel development. It is located on chromosome 16q24.3, and pathogenic variants in FOXC2 disrupt normal lymphatic flow, producing the characteristic limb swelling seen in Meige disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC39A4",
    "long_answer": "Acrodermatitis enteropathica is caused by autosomal-recessive mutations in the SLC39A4 gene, which encodes the ZIP4 zinc transporter located in the apical membrane of intestinal mucosal cells. Loss-of-function mutations in SLC39A4 impair ZIP4-mediated uptake of dietary zinc across the mucous membrane, leading to systemic zinc deficiency and the characteristic skin, hair, and gastrointestinal manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Loculi",
    "long_answer": "A cystic hygroma is a lymphatic malformation composed of many interconnected, thin-walled cystic spaces. These small cavities that contain clear lymphatic fluid are called loculi (singular: loculus). The multiloculated architecture filled with lymph is a defining microscopic and gross feature of cystic hygromas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertriglyceridemia",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen Storage Disease type I (von Gierke disease), impaired glucose-6-phosphatase activity traps glucose-6-phosphate in hepatocytes, driving excess lipogenesis and very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) production. This results in markedly elevated serum triglycerides—hypertriglyceridemia—which is one of the most frequent metabolic complications in these patients. Because severe hypertriglyceridemia can cause pancreatitis and other vascular problems, it must be actively managed as part of hyperlipidemia treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exposure therapy",
    "long_answer": "Specific phobias, including blood-injection-injury phobia, are most effectively treated with cognitive-behavioral exposure therapy, in which the patient is systematically and repeatedly exposed to the feared stimulus (often in a graded hierarchy) until the anxiety response extinguishes. This approach has the strongest empirical support and is recommended as first-line treatment by clinical guidelines."
}

{
    "short_answer": "YopJ",
    "long_answer": "The Yersinia-specific small regulatory RNA Ysr141 has been shown to base-pair with the 5′ region of the yopJ messenger RNA, modulating its stability and translation. As a result, expression of the type III–secreted effector protein YopJ (Yersinia outer protein J) is directly regulated by Ysr141 in Yersinia pestis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NKX2-5",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the cardiac homeobox gene NKX2-5 are a well-established cause of multiple congenital heart defects—including atrial and ventricular septal defects and tetralogy of Fallot—which typically present with systolic heart murmurs. Loss-of-function in this transcription factor disrupts cardiac morphogenesis and conduction, so NKX2-5 mutation is classically associated with the murmurs heard in patients with congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Schwannoma",
    "long_answer": "Tumors that arise inside the dura but remain outside the spinal cord itself are classified as intradural-extramedullary. Population series show that benign nerve-sheath tumors—predominantly schwannomas—make up the largest proportion of lesions in this compartment, accounting for roughly 30-55 % of cases, ahead of meningiomas and other entities. Therefore, schwannoma is the single most common tumor found within the dura yet external to the spinal cord parenchyma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Growth hormone therapy",
    "long_answer": "Recombinant human growth hormone (GH) treatment is the established intervention used to improve growth velocity and augment final adult stature in children with Noonan syndrome, who commonly present with short stature due to dysregulated RAS-MAPK signalling. Clinical trials and long-term follow-up studies have shown that daily subcutaneous GH administration can increase height SDS and lead to a higher final height compared with untreated cohorts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylmalonate semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ALDH6A1 gene abolish mitochondrial methylmalonate-semialdehyde dehydrogenase activity, blocking the valine and thymine degradation pathways. The resulting accumulation of organic acids (e.g., 3-hydroxyisobutyric and methylmalonic acids) precipitates recurrent episodes of metabolic acidosis with marked ketoacidosis, the clinical hallmark of methylmalonate semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human herpesvirus 8",
    "long_answer": "Kaposi’s sarcoma is etiologically linked to infection with Human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8), also called Kaposi’s sarcoma-associated herpesvirus (KSHV). The virus infects endothelial and B cells, expressing latent and lytic genes that drive angiogenesis and cell proliferation, which are hallmarks of Kaposi’s sarcoma lesions. Epidemiological, molecular, and serological studies consistently demonstrate HHV-8 DNA and proteins in virtually all forms of Kaposi’s sarcoma, establishing it as the causative agent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy is an inherited seizure disorder affecting the brain’s frontal lobes; evaluation, diagnosis (e.g., EEG, neuroimaging, genetic testing), and anticonvulsant management are core functions of neurologists, the medical specialists who treat epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary gland",
    "long_answer": "The pituitary gland (hypophysis) secretes hormones such as ACTH, TSH, GH, LH, FSH, and prolactin under direct regulation by hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting factors delivered through the hypophyseal portal system. Because its cells are neuroendocrine in origin, the pituitary can develop neuroendocrine tumors (pituitary adenomas), making it the gland whose hormone secretion is controlled by the hypothalamus and that is implicated in neuroendocrine tumors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TDP-43",
    "long_answer": "In over 95 % of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) cases, spinal motor neurons contain cytoplasmic inclusions composed of the RNA-binding protein TDP-43 (TAR DNA-binding protein 43 kDa). These inclusions reflect abnormal mislocalization, phosphorylation, ubiquitination, and aggregation of TDP-43, making its pathology a defining molecular feature of most ALS patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sugarman syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Oral-facial-digital syndrome type III is most commonly referred to as Sugarman syndrome. This nomenclature is well established in the clinical genetics literature, distinguishing it from other oral-facial-digital subtypes such as type I (Papillon-Léage-Psaume) and type II (Mohr syndrome)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcium gluconate",
    "long_answer": "Acute, severe hypocalcemia in hypoparathyroidism is treated with intravenous 10 % calcium gluconate to rapidly raise serum ionized calcium and relieve neuromuscular and cardiac symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Capnocytophaga canimorsus",
    "long_answer": "Capnocytophaga canimorsus is a Gram-negative rod that inhabits the oral flora of healthy dogs and cats. When transmitted to humans through bites, scratches, or even licks, it can invade the bloodstream and, in susceptible individuals (e.g., those with liver disease, alcoholism, or asplenia), progress to fulminant infections including sepsis and necrotizing fasciitis. Numerous clinical reports document C. canimorsus as a cause of rapidly progressive necrotizing soft-tissue disease following canine or feline exposure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Crystal violet",
    "long_answer": "Gram staining begins with the primary dye crystal violet, which penetrates and binds the thick peptidoglycan layer of Gram-positive bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus. After subsequent iodine treatment and decolorization, cells that have retained the crystal violet appear purple, allowing their classification as Gram-positive organisms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency results from mutations in the functional steroid 21-hydroxylase gene CYP21A2 (historically called CYP21B). This gene resides in the class III region of chromosome 6 at 6p21.3, immediately adjacent to the major histocompatibility complex (HLA) loci, explaining its tight linkage with HLA haplotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacterial culture",
    "long_answer": "When ELISA immunoassay is not available, yersiniosis can be diagnosed by conventional bacteriological culture—isolating Yersinia spp. from stool or tissue on selective media (often after cold enrichment) and identifying the characteristic colonies. Direct culture provides definitive proof of the causative bacteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ellis–van Creveld syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ellis–van Creveld syndrome is a chondro-ectodermal dysplasia characterized by polydactyly and marked brachydactyly; more than half of affected patients have structural congenital heart defects (classically a common atrium). Thus, the presence of brachydactyly in this syndrome signals a significant risk for congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Imatinib mesylate",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic leukemia driven by the FIP1L1-PDGFRA fusion is exquisitely sensitive to the tyrosine-kinase inhibitor imatinib; even low doses (≈100 mg/day) rapidly induce complete hematologic and molecular remission, making imatinib the standard first-line therapy for such cases, including presentations with myeloid sarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GCH1",
    "long_answer": "Dopamine-responsive dystonia (Segawa disease) is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the GCH1 gene, which encodes GTP-cyclohydrolase I, the rate-limiting enzyme for tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis. Reduced BH4 diminishes tyrosine hydroxylase activity and dopamine production, producing the characteristic dystonia that improves dramatically with low-dose levodopa. Therefore, GCH1 is the primary gene associated with dopamine-responsive dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Developmental delay",
    "long_answer": "Premature fusion of cranial sutures can restrict brain growth and elevate intracranial pressure, leading to impaired neurocognitive maturation. This manifests as developmental delay, which is routinely quantified with standardized developmental or neuropsychological tests (e.g., Bayley Scales, IQ assessments)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melkersson–Rosenthal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Melkersson–Rosenthal syndrome is a rare neuro-mucocutaneous condition whose classic triad includes recurrent unilateral or bilateral facial nerve paralysis, persistent orofacial swelling (cheilitis granulomatosa), and a fissured (plicated) tongue. Because it is a granulomatous disorder of unknown etiology, it is frequently reported in association with other granulomatous diseases such as Crohn’s disease and sarcoidosis, making it the neurological disorder that fits the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Desmopressin",
    "long_answer": "Desmopressin, a synthetic analogue of vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone), selectively stimulates renal V2 receptors to increase water reabsorption in the collecting ducts. This action replaces the deficient ADH in central diabetes insipidus, reducing polyuria and polydipsia. The same antidiuretic effect decreases nighttime urine production, making desmopressin an established therapy for nocturnal polyuria (including nocturnal enuresis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Haim–Munk syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the cathepsin C (CTSC) gene. CTSC is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 11 (region 11q14), so the pathogenic alleles responsible for Haim–Munk syndrome reside on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Contrast-enhanced CT scan",
    "long_answer": "The standard first-line imaging for suspected pancreatic cancer is a contrast-enhanced multi-detector computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen. CT provides high-resolution cross-sectional images that delineate pancreatic masses, assess local vascular invasion, detect metastatic spread, and guide staging and treatment planning, making it the most commonly employed diagnostic technique for this malignancy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Takayasu arteritis",
    "long_answer": "Takayasu arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis that predominantly involves the aorta and its major branches. It classically occurs in young women, with a particular predilection for those of Asian descent, leading to its description as “pulseless disease” due to stenosis or occlusion of proximal arteries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Katz syndrome (also classified as an orofaciodigital syndrome subtype) is transmitted in an autosomal-recessive manner: affected children inherit one pathogenic allele from each phenotypically normal carrier parent, and the disorder tends to recur among siblings while usually skipping generations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Burning pain",
    "long_answer": "Peripheral neuropathy commonly damages small sensory fibers, leading to neuropathic pain that patients describe as a persistent or intermittent burning sensation. This ‘burning pain’ is therefore both a hallmark symptom of peripheral neuropathy and a well-recognized subtype of neuropathic pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vemurafenib",
    "long_answer": "Vemurafenib, a BRAF-V600E kinase inhibitor prescribed for unresectable or metastatic (late-stage) melanoma, frequently produces cutaneous adverse events. Among the most characteristic is hyperkeratosis—excessive thickening of the stratum corneum—reported in clinical trials in up to 30–40 % of treated patients. The effect is linked to paradoxical MAP-kinase pathway activation in wild-type keratinocytes triggered by the drug."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia results from an inherited deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT). Absence of GALT blocks conversion of galactose-1-phosphate to UDP-galactose, causing accumulation of galactose and its metabolites, which produces the characteristic hepatic, neurologic, and ocular manifestations of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypogonadism",
    "long_answer": "Elevated prolactin inhibits hypothalamic GnRH secretion, leading to reduced pituitary release of LH and FSH; the fall in these gonadotropins diminishes ovarian or testicular steroidogenesis and gametogenesis, producing clinical hypogonadism (amenorrhea, infertility, low libido)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "the jim-jams",
    "long_answer": "Delirium tremens—severe alcohol-withdrawal marked by tremor and hallucinations—has long been colloquially called “the jim-jams.”  Eugene O’Neill uses this nickname in his barroom drama The Iceman Cometh, where characters suffering from withdrawal complain of having “the jim-jams,” directly referencing delirium tremens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia",
    "long_answer": "Patients with Ramos-Arroyo syndrome have shown a marked susceptibility to malignant hyperthermia when exposed to halothane-succinylcholine (volatile) general anesthesia. The syndrome therefore carries a well-documented association with malignant-hyperthermia–triggering anesthetic agents, making this anesthetic complication a key clinical consideration in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatology",
    "long_answer": "Thanatophoric dysplasia is a lethal skeletal dysplasia that presents at birth with severe limb shortening, thoracic constriction, and respiratory failure. Immediate care occurs in a neonatal intensive-care setting, so the specialty most commonly responsible for managing these infants is neonatology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lumbar puncture",
    "long_answer": "In patients with optic papillitis (a form of optic neuritis), a lumbar puncture is performed to collect cerebrospinal fluid. Examination of the CSF can reveal inflammatory markers, oligoclonal bands, or infections that help establish the underlying cause of the optic disc inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Recurrent respiratory infections",
    "long_answer": "Infants with mucolipidosis type I frequently experience recurrent respiratory infections (e.g., repeated pneumonias or upper-airway infections). Accumulation of undegraded sialylated substrates in airway mucosa impairs mucociliary clearance and immune defense, making these infants prone to repeated infections of the respiratory tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Lenz microphthalmia syndrome is an X-linked condition caused by loss-of-function mutations in the BCOR (BCL6 corepressor) gene located at Xp11.4. Pathogenic variants in BCOR interfere with early ocular development and other embryonic pathways, leading to the characteristic microphthalmia/anophthalmia and associated skeletal, craniofacial, and urogenital anomalies seen in affected males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary angioedema due to C1-INH deficiency is treated by replacing the missing protein. Intravenous C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate (e.g., Berinert, Cinryze) supplies functional C1-INH, halting uncontrolled kallikrein activation and bradykinin production, thereby reducing or aborting HAE attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSCAM",
    "long_answer": "The Down syndrome cell adhesion molecule gene (DSCAM), located on chromosome 21q22, is over-expressed in trisomy 21 and therefore linked to Down syndrome. Genetic studies have also shown that sequence variants and altered dosage of DSCAM increase susceptibility to Hirschsprung’s disease, helping explain the elevated incidence of Hirschsprung’s in individuals with Down syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "98%",
    "long_answer": "Stage 0 bladder cancer is confined to the innermost lining of the bladder and has not invaded the muscle wall. According to large U.S. population-based data (e.g., SEER statistics reported by the American Cancer Society), the five-year relative survival rate for patients diagnosed at this stage is about 98 %, reflecting the excellent prognosis when the disease is detected before muscle invasion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "U.S. Food and Drug Administration",
    "long_answer": "Valbenazine (Ingrezza), a selective VMAT2 inhibitor, received marketing authorization in April 2017 from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) as the first approved therapy for treating tardive dyskinesia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated aspergillosis",
    "long_answer": "In immunocompromised patients, Aspergillus can invade pulmonary blood vessels and spread hematogenously, producing multifocal infection in distant organs such as the brain, kidneys, liver and heart. This widespread, angio-invasive spread—disseminated aspergillosis—is a serious, often fatal complication of aspergillosis in the immunocompromised host."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation",
    "long_answer": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a consumptive co-agulopathy in which systemic activation of the coagulation cascade produces widespread microvascular thrombi while simultaneously depleting platelets and clotting factors. In neonates, DIC can be triggered by sepsis, birth asphyxia, or placental complications and typically presents with thrombocytopenia together with both clotting and bleeding manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)",
    "long_answer": "Myxoid liposarcoma is a soft-tissue sarcoma with a high water and fat content that shows characteristic signal patterns on T1- and T2-weighted sequences. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is therefore the imaging modality of choice for its diagnosis, providing superior soft-tissue contrast, delineation of tumor extent, and detection of the myxoid and fatty components that typify this tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1",
    "long_answer": "The FMR1 gene resides at Xq27.3 on the X chromosome.  Pathogenic expansions of its CGG repeat cause the classic fragile X syndrome, and mutations or disruptions in FMR1 have also been reported in families classified clinically as having FG (Opitz-Kaveggia) syndrome, placing FMR1 on the list of X-linked genes associated with FG syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Current international consensus statements and guideline documents (e.g., 2015 ESC Guidelines on Ventricular Arrhythmias/Sudden Cardiac Death and 2022 HRS/APHRS/LAHRS Expert Consensus on Inherited Arrhythmia Syndromes) define short QT syndrome primarily by electrocardiographic criteria (markedly shortened QTc on a resting 12-lead ECG) together with clinical, familial and genetic findings. An invasive electrophysiology study (programmed electrical stimulation) is neither required nor routinely recommended for making the diagnosis because: (1) it adds little diagnostic specificity beyond the surface ECG; (2) the inducibility of arrhythmias and measured refractory periods lack validated threshold values for SQTS; and (3) EPS results do not reliably alter patient management in this disorder. Therefore, an electrophysiology study is not recommended as a diagnostic test for short QT syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Scimitar syndrome is a rare congenital cardiopulmonary malformation characterized by partial anomalous pulmonary venous return of the right lung to the inferior vena cava, right lung hypoplasia, and associated cardiac defects. Because of the abnormal pulmonary venous drainage and frequent coexistence of other congenital heart lesions (e.g., atrial septal defect, patent ductus arteriosus, pulmonary hypertension), the condition is primarily evaluated, monitored, and treated within the subspecialty of pediatric and adult congenital cardiology. Cardiologists perform the diagnostic work-up (echocardiography, cardiac MRI/CT, catheterization), manage medical therapy for heart failure or pulmonary hypertension when needed, and coordinate surgical or transcatheter interventions with cardiothoracic surgeons. Thus, management of Scimitar syndrome clearly falls under cardiology care."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCDC2 and KIAA0319",
    "long_answer": "The DYX2 susceptibility locus on chromosome 6p21.3 harbors two well-replicated dyslexia-associated genes: DCDC2 and KIAA0319. Multiple linkage and association studies show that sequence variants or regulatory changes in these genes are linked to impaired phonological processing and neuronal migration abnormalities that underlie developmental dyslexia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HFE C282Y mutation",
    "long_answer": "The C282Y missense mutation in the HFE gene (c.845G>A, p.Cys282Tyr) is the principal genetic variant underlying classic hereditary hemochromatosis and shows its highest allele frequency in Northern European populations, including those of Scandinavian ancestry."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topical capsaicin",
    "long_answer": "Topical application of capsaicin—the pungent compound derived from chili peppers—has been shown to give prolonged symptomatic relief in chromhidrosis by depleting neuropeptides (e.g., substance P) in cutaneous nerves, thereby reducing the abnormal, pigment-laden secretions from apocrine glands."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sulfate-reducing bacteria",
    "long_answer": "In oxygen-poor sediments and water columns, anaerobic sulfate-reducing bacteria carry the hgcAB gene pair that enzymatically transfers a methyl group to inorganic Hg(II), forming toxic methylmercury. Numerous field and laboratory studies show that these bacteria dominate mercury methylation in lakes, estuaries, and wetlands, making them the principal biological source of methylmercury in aquatic systems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis-ptosis-epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES) type I is caused by mutations that truncate (shorten) the FOXL2 gene product. Such FOXL2 shortening disrupts its transcription-factor function in ovarian follicles, leading to premature ovarian insufficiency in affected women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse",
    "long_answer": "Larsen syndrome, caused most commonly by pathogenic variants in the FLNB gene, involves generalized connective-tissue weakness. In addition to the characteristic multiple joint dislocations and facial features, this weakness often affects valvular tissue of the heart; the best-documented valvular abnormality is mitral valve prolapse, in which myxomatous degeneration of the mitral leaflets leads to systolic billowing and possible regurgitation. Hence, mitral valve prolapse is the heart valve disease classically associated with Larsen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome involves generalized dysfunction of the proximal renal tubule. This defect impairs bicarbonate reabsorption, producing a type 2 (proximal) renal tubular acidosis, and simultaneously reduces reabsorption of amino acids, leading to marked aminoaciduria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Classic 3-Methylglutaconic aciduria (type I) results from biallelic pathogenic variants in the AUH gene involved in leucine catabolism. Because disease manifests only when both alleles are mutated, it follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern, as described in the medical genetics literature for most forms of 3-methylglutaconic aciduria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanotic congenital heart disease",
    "long_answer": "Digital (nail) clubbing together with an increased hematocrit most commonly reflects long-standing systemic hypoxemia. Chronic arterial desaturation from a right-to-left shunt in a cyanotic congenital heart defect (e.g., Tetralogy of Fallot) stimulates erythropoietin production, raising the hematocrit, and produces the vascular and connective-tissue changes responsible for clubbing. Thus, the coexistence of these two findings strongly points to cyanotic congenital heart disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frontotemporal dementia",
    "long_answer": "Frontotemporal dementia (FTD) is recognized as the second‐most common cause of dementia with onset before 65 years, accounting for roughly 10–20 % of early-onset cases; only Alzheimer’s disease is more prevalent in this age group. FTD is characterized by progressive degeneration of the frontal and/or temporal lobes, leading to prominent behavioral or language disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Smoking",
    "long_answer": "Cigarette smoking promotes atherosclerosis and thrombosis, making it a well-established modifiable risk factor for myocardial infarction. The same tobacco smoke impairs mucociliary clearance and local immune defenses in the airways, increasing colonization by pathogenic bacteria and thus the incidence of community-acquired pneumonia. Because it independently elevates the likelihood of both conditions, smoking is a common risk factor shared by myocardial infarction and pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dizygotic twins",
    "long_answer": "The Brazilian case report described two male siblings with Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome who were fraternal (dizygotic) twins, meaning they arose from two separate eggs fertilised by two different sperm and therefore are genetically no more similar than ordinary brothers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ebstein anomaly",
    "long_answer": "Ebstein anomaly is a malformation of the tricuspid valve in which the septal and posterior leaflets are displaced apically, leaving much of the right ventricle ‘atrialized’ and functionally small (hypoplastic). Because the degree of leaflet displacement, valve dysplasia, and right-sided hypoplasia vary greatly among patients, surgical correction must be tailored to the individual anatomy; definitive management therefore involves individualized open-heart surgery (e.g., cone reconstruction or tricuspid valve replacement)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated intra-ocular pressure",
    "long_answer": "Persistently raised intra-ocular pressure (IOP) is the chief, well-established risk factor for developing glaucoma. Excess pressure compromises retinal and optic-nerve perfusion; when markedly elevated it can also disturb macular circulation and structure, producing central visual defects or edema. Thus increased IOP is both the most important driver of glaucomatous optic neuropathy and a pressure-related insult that can secondarily involve the macula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Low birth weight",
    "long_answer": "Infants affected by fetal hydantoin (phenytoin) embryopathy typically show marked intra-uterine growth retardation, emerging at delivery as low birth weight babies. Low birth weight is likewise the defining clinical consequence of intrauterine growth restriction itself, since IUGR limits fetal mass accumulation. Thus, reduced birth weight is a cardinal clinical feature common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipofuscin",
    "long_answer": "Retinal pigmented epithelial (RPE) cells normally accumulate the autofluorescent pigment lipofuscin. Modern fundus-autofluorescence imaging detects the distribution and amount of RPE lipofuscin, and characteristic patterns (e.g., a hyper-autofluorescent ring) are used to diagnose and monitor retinitis pigmentosa. Thus, the lipofuscin stored in RPE cells is the substance directly involved in the clinical diagnosis of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor (the main calcium-release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum), have been documented in patients with congenital fiber type disproportion. The same RYR1 mutations are a well-established genetic cause of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility because the altered channel aberrantly releases Ca²⁺ during anesthetic exposure. Thus, RYR1 links these two clinical entities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Miller-Dieker syndrome, caused by a microdeletion of 17p13.3 that includes the LIS1 (PAFAH1B1) gene, produces a lissencephaly spectrum with pachygyria, intractable epilepsy, and severe developmental delay. Patients commonly have associated congenital anomalies, and congenital heart defects—most often ventricular or atrial septal defects and patent ductus arteriosus—are well-documented features of this genetic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dendritic cell vaccine",
    "long_answer": "Researchers are testing a dendritic cell vaccine in which a patient’s own tumor cells are lysed and loaded onto dendritic cells derived from that patient’s white blood cells; the primed dendritic cells are then re-infused to elicit a targeted immune attack against brainstem glioma cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Symptomatic epilepsy",
    "long_answer": "Encephalitis produces inflammatory damage to cerebral tissue; when seizures result from such a known structural or metabolic brain insult, the condition is classified as symptomatic (acquired) epilepsy rather than idiopathic or cryptogenic forms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fourth week of gestation",
    "long_answer": "Hemifacial microsomia arises during the early development of the first and second branchial (pharyngeal) arches, which occurs in the fourth week of embryogenesis (about 28–30 days). Vascular disruption or other insults at this stage impair normal formation of the mandibular and hyoid arch derivatives, producing the characteristic facial asymmetry observed at birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibialis posterior tendon transfer",
    "long_answer": "In patients with distal myopathy, weakness of the ankle dorsiflexors produces foot drop. A well-established surgical option is transfer of the tibialis posterior tendon through the interosseous membrane to the dorsum of the foot, where it is attached to the cuneiforms or extensor tendons. This re-positions the functioning tibialis posterior muscle to act as a dorsiflexor, restoring active ankle dorsiflexion and reducing the need for an ankle-foot orthosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome (aHUS) is a complement-mediated thrombotic microangiopathy in which germ-line mutations or polymorphisms in complement-regulatory genes (e.g., CFH, CFI, MCP) create a genetic predisposition to TMA. Various stresses can precipitate an episode; among the recognized triggers are autoimmune flares, particularly systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), which can initiate uncontrolled complement activation and lead to the clinical onset of aHUS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antibiotic therapy",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis patients are prone to chronic and acute bacterial infections of the airways, most commonly by Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus. The cornerstone of managing these lung infections is timely, targeted antibiotic therapy—delivered orally, intravenously, or by inhalation—to eradicate or suppress bacterial growth, reduce inflammation, and preserve lung function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with Acrocallosal syndrome may develop systemic arterial hypertension, often related to their associated renal anomalies. This elevated blood pressure can be managed with standard antihypertensive drugs (e.g., ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers), making hypertension the treatable condition linked to the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cone-shaped epiphyses",
    "long_answer": "Fitzsimmons–Guilbert syndrome is a form of spondylometaphyseal dysplasia in which the ossification centers of the long bones and phalanges taper to a point, giving rise to characteristic cone-shaped epiphyses on radiographs. The presence of these cone-shaped epiphyses is a defining skeletal feature of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral artery disease",
    "long_answer": "Intermittent claudication—exercise-induced leg pain relieved by rest—results from inadequate arterial blood flow to working muscles. The most common underlying cause is peripheral artery disease (PAD), in which atherosclerotic narrowing of the lower-extremity arteries limits perfusion, producing ischemic pain during exertion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DST",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the DST gene (coding for dystonin/BPAG1) are the established cause of hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type VI. Independent families carrying the same or similar DST truncating variants have also been reported with a clinical picture dominated by a length-dependent axonal peripheral neuropathy that fulfils diagnostic criteria for axonal Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT2). Hence, DST mutations are linked to both HSAN VI and axonal CMT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial short stature",
    "long_answer": "When a child’s heights are plotted on a standard growth chart, the most frequent pattern that explains persistent stature below the 3rd percentile is familial (genetic) short stature. In these children growth velocity is normal and the height curve runs parallel to, but below, the standard percentiles; parental heights predict a similarly low target adult height. Because this characteristic pattern can be seen directly on the growth chart without laboratory tests, familial short stature is considered the commonest identifiable cause of dwarfism using growth-chart assessment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cesarean section",
    "long_answer": "When a mother has active genital herpes lesions during labor, delivery by Cesarean section is recommended to bypass the infected birth canal and markedly reduce the risk of neonatal herpes transmission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Criterion validity",
    "long_answer": "In psychometrics, a new autism screening or assessment tool is judged valid if its results correspond to an accepted ‘gold-standard’ outcome. The DSM-5 diagnostic criteria supply that external standard: when an instrument’s scores accurately predict or concur with a DSM-5 clinical diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder, the tool is said to demonstrate criterion validity. Thus the DSM-5 criteria serve as the criterion against which the validity of autism tests is evaluated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome is a congenital Müllerian duct anomaly in which the uterus and upper two-thirds of the vagina fail to develop, leading to vaginal agenesis or severe shortening and absence of the uterus despite normal ovarian function and 46,XX karyotype. Thus, MRKH is the syndrome classically linked to absence or malformation of the female reproductive tract, including the vagina."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coenzyme Q10",
    "long_answer": "Glutaric acidemia type 2 (multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) disrupts the flow of electrons from flavoprotein dehydrogenases to the mitochondrial respiratory chain, leading to reduced ATP generation. Many patients—particularly those with ETFDH mutations—develop a secondary deficiency of coenzyme Q10 (ubiquinone), the lipid-soluble electron carrier that shuttles electrons between complexes I/II and complex III. Oral supplementation with coenzyme Q10 can replenish this electron carrier, improve electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation, and thereby help restore cellular energy production in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmologist",
    "long_answer": "Lattice corneal dystrophy is a hereditary disorder of the corneal stroma that must be evaluated with slit-lamp biomicroscopy, corneal imaging, and managed with measures such as topical therapy or corneal transplantation; these diagnostic and therapeutic procedures are performed by an ophthalmologist (often a corneal subspecialist)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudo-athletic appearance",
    "long_answer": "Madelung’s disease (multiple symmetric lipomatosis) causes large, symmetric, unencapsulated fat deposits around the neck, shoulders and upper trunk. These fatty masses mimic marked muscular hypertrophy, giving patients the illusion of being heavily muscled although they are not. This illusion is medically termed a “pseudo-athletic appearance.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Constitutional growth delay",
    "long_answer": "The majority of otherwise healthy children who present with both reduced height percentile and late onset of secondary sexual characteristics have Constitutional Growth Delay (also called constitutional delay of growth and puberty). This benign variant of growth is characterized by a temporary slowing of linear growth in early childhood, a delayed but normal pubertal surge in growth hormone and sex steroids, and eventual attainment of normal adult stature. It is far more prevalent than pathological causes such as growth-hormone deficiency or chronic systemic disease, making it the most common cause of short stature combined with delayed puberty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatology",
    "long_answer": "Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic necrotizing vasculitis—an autoimmune-mediated inflammation of medium-sized arteries. Systemic vasculitides and other connective-tissue autoimmune diseases are primarily diagnosed and managed by rheumatologists, who have the expertise to use immunosuppressive therapies such as glucocorticoids and cyclophosphamide that are standard of care for polyarteritis nodosa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the WNT4 gene are known to cause renal agenesis or other congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract. The same mutations disrupt Müllerian duct development, resulting in uterine and vaginal agenesis (Müllerian aplasia) and consequent infertility or primary amenorrhoea in affected women."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF23",
    "long_answer": "Congenital dyserythropoietic anemia type III is most often caused by heterozygous mutations in the KIF23 gene, which encodes the mitotic kinesin MKLP1. Defective MKLP1 disrupts cytokinesis in erythroid precursors, so cell division fails to complete and very large, multinucleated erythroblasts accumulate—a hallmark of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Surgical reconstruction",
    "long_answer": "Exstrophy of the bladder results from failure of the paired mesodermal folds to fuse in the ventral midline during embryogenesis. Definitive therapy is surgical reconstruction/repair of the bladder and anterior abdominal wall, performed soon after birth to restore urinary continence and protect renal function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noma (cancrum oris)",
    "long_answer": "Noma, also called cancrum oris, is a gangrenous infection of the oral and facial tissues that classically arises in severely malnourished individuals. It typically begins as necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis; when host defenses are impaired by protein-energy malnutrition, the infection extends rapidly, causing extensive tissue necrosis. Thus, noma is the condition that can result from malnutrition and is directly linked to necrotizing gingivitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lungs, liver, and bones",
    "long_answer": "Adrenocortical carcinoma disseminates hematogenously and via lymphatics; the organs most frequently seeded are the lungs and liver, followed by skeletal sites (bones). Clinical series consistently report these three sites as the predominant locations of distant metastases from this tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasound",
    "long_answer": "Multicystic dysplastic kidney is typically identified during routine antenatal screening by prenatal ultrasonography, which visualizes the fetus and reveals the characteristic multiple non-communicating renal cysts and absent normal renal parenchyma before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exercise blood lactate test",
    "long_answer": "In McArdle’s disease (myophosphorylase deficiency), muscle glycogen cannot be broken down to supply glycolysis. When venous blood lactate is measured before and after an ischemic forearm or similar exercise challenge, patients show little-to-no rise in lactate. This ‘flat’ exercise blood lactate response signals a severe muscle glycolytic block characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LYST",
    "long_answer": "Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LYST (lysosomal trafficking regulator) gene. Defective LYST disrupts formation and transport of lysosome-related organelles in neurons, leading to progressive neurodegeneration that frequently manifests as peripheral neuropathy in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Platelet transfusion",
    "long_answer": "When bleeding is significant or an invasive procedure is planned, both congenital giant-platelet disorders (e.g., Bernard-Soulier syndrome) and immune thrombocytopenic purpura are managed by transfusing donor platelets. The transfusion supplies functional platelets, temporarily boosting the circulating count and improving hemostasis, so platelet transfusion is a treatment common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3R1",
    "long_answer": "SHORT syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous mutations in the PIK3R1 gene, which encodes the p85α regulatory subunit of phosphatidylinositol-3-kinase (PI3K). These mutations impair the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling pathway, a central route controlling cell growth, proliferation, and metabolism, thereby producing the growth and developmental features characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiology",
    "long_answer": "Hyperostosis frontalis interna is usually discovered as a characteristic, symmetrical thickening of the inner table of the frontal bone on skull radiographs or CT scans. Because interpretation of these imaging studies is the province of radiologists, the diagnosis is most commonly made within the medical specialty of radiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyloid-β (Aβ)",
    "long_answer": "The hallmark amyloid plaques in Alzheimer’s disease are primarily composed of amyloid-β (Aβ) peptide, a 40–42-amino-acid fragment cleaved from the larger amyloid precursor protein (APP). Extracellular accumulation and aggregation of Aβ into insoluble fibrils form these plaques, which are a central pathological feature of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ASPM",
    "long_answer": "The ASPM gene (abnormal spindle-like microcephaly associated) is the single most frequently mutated gene in autosomal recessive primary microcephaly (MCPH). Pathogenic variants in ASPM account for roughly 40 % of documented MCPH cases worldwide, making it the most common genetic cause of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Auditory brainstem response",
    "long_answer": "Auditory brainstem response (ABR) testing records the electrical activity generated in the auditory nerve and brainstem after a sound stimulus. Because the amplitude and latency of the ABR waves change systematically with stimulus intensity, the lowest intensity that still evokes a reproducible waveform closely approximates the infant’s behavioral hearing threshold. This objective, non-invasive test is therefore routinely used to estimate hearing thresholds in newborns and infants with nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome is a hereditary disorder characterized by congenital sensorineural deafness, poliosis (white forelock), and other pigmentation anomalies such as heterochromia of the irises or patchy skin hypopigmentation. These features arise from defective development and migration of neural crest-derived melanocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIAA0196",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the KIAA0196 gene (also called strumpellin, WASHC5) cause hereditary spastic paraplegia type 8 (SPG8). Cytogenetic mapping places KIAA0196 at 8q24.13 on human chromosome 8, making it the HSP-associated gene located on this chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Skin biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic folliculitis in people living with HIV is confirmed histologically. A skin biopsy of an active lesion shows a perifollicular and intrafollicular infiltrate rich in eosinophils, which distinguishes this entity from other pruritic papular eruptions. Therefore, performing a skin biopsy is the diagnostic procedure required to establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GABAA receptor",
    "long_answer": "Barbiturates act as positive allosteric modulators of the GABAA receptor, and prolonged modulation of this receptor underlies the development of barbiturate dependence. The same GABAA receptor-mediated enhancement of inhibitory neurotransmission in the cerebellum and brainstem is a well-known mechanism for the incoordination and gait disturbance (ataxia) that occur after ingestion of sedative or other exogenous substances. Thus, the GABAA receptor is implicated in both barbiturate dependence and ataxia caused by exogenous substances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytotoxic T lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "In idiopathic aplastic anemia, the marrow failure is largely immune-mediated: autoreactive cytotoxic CD8⁺ T lymphocytes become activated and secrete perforin, granzymes, and pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IFN-γ, TNF-α) that directly attack and eliminate hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells in the bone marrow, leading to pancytopenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome is classically caused by activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor-2 (FGFR2) gene. In experimental settings, expression or modulation of FGFR2 in mesenchymal stem cells is employed to regulate their osteogenic differentiation and subsequent ossification, reflecting the receptor’s central role in cranial bone development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypovolemic shock",
    "long_answer": "In shigellosis, profuse diarrhoea can cause rapid fluid and electrolyte loss. Severe dehydration may critically reduce circulating blood volume, leading to hypovolemic (circulatory) shock, a life-threatening complication marked by hypotension and organ hypoperfusion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pediatric radiology",
    "long_answer": "A non-ossifying fibroma is a benign bone lesion that almost always comes to attention as an incidental finding on plain radiographs or other imaging studies obtained in growing children. Because the diagnosis is characteristically made from its pathognomonic imaging appearance—without the need for biopsy—the clinician most likely to recognize and diagnose it is the pediatric radiologist (or radiologist interpreting the child’s imaging)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valeriana",
    "long_answer": "Valeriana, the genus that includes Valeriana officinalis (valerian), was reportedly employed medicinally by the Roman emperor Publius Licinius Valerianus and is currently investigated in clinical studies for its anxiolytic effects in generalized anxiety disorder, thought to stem from constituents such as valerenic acid that modulate GABA-A receptor activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperosmolar sugary fluids",
    "long_answer": "Soft drinks contain very high concentrations of sucrose/fructose (osmolarity >600 mOsm/L) and virtually no sodium or potassium. In gastroenteritis this hyperosmolar sugar load draws water into the intestinal lumen, increases osmotic diarrhea, and fails to replace lost electrolytes, thereby worsening dehydration in children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronary artery disease",
    "long_answer": "X-linked ichthyosis is caused by loss of the steroid sulfatase (STS) gene, leading to lifelong accumulation of cholesterol-sulfate and altered lipid handling. Epidemiological studies of affected males show a markedly increased incidence of premature atherosclerotic events, particularly ischemic heart disease; thus, coronary artery disease is a well-recognized systemic association of X-linked ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Primary focal hyperhidrosis is classically inherited as an autosomal-dominant trait with variable penetrance.  Familial studies of anxiety-prone individuals show the same autosomal-dominant pattern of transmission.  Thus the genetic characteristic shared by both conditions is autosomal-dominant inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCA4",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal recessive Stargardt disease (STGD1) is most commonly caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the ABCA4 gene, which encodes a photoreceptor-specific ATP-binding cassette transporter. Loss of ABCA4 function impairs retinal vitamin-A derivative clearance, leading to lipofuscin accumulation in the retinal pigment epithelium and the characteristic macular degeneration seen in Stargardt disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha granules",
    "long_answer": "Platelet alpha granules are membrane-bound storage organelles that contain coagulation and adhesion proteins, including von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, and factor V. Upon platelet activation they release these contents, contributing directly to hemostasis, making alpha granules the platelet granule type responsible for both hemostatic function and von Willebrand factor storage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Saethre-Chotzen syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Saethre-Chotzen syndrome is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder caused chiefly by TWIST1 mutations. Patients typically show premature fusion of the coronal sutures (craniosynostosis) together with characteristic eyelid anomalies such as ptosis and blepharophimosis, as well as facial asymmetry and a low frontal hairline. These hallmark features firmly associate Saethre-Chotzen syndrome with the combination of craniosynostosis and blepharophimosis described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endomyocardial biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic giant-cell myocarditis can only be definitively confirmed by histologic demonstration of multinucleated giant cells within myocardial tissue, which is obtained through an endomyocardial biopsy. Imaging and laboratory tests may suggest myocarditis, but biopsy provides the pathognomonic evidence required for diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alpha-fetoprotein",
    "long_answer": "Endodermal sinus tumors (also called yolk sac tumors) are germ-cell neoplasms that characteristically synthesize and secrete the fetal serum protein alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). The AFP produced by the tumor is released into the circulation, where it can be measured in blood plasma and serves as a clinically useful tumor marker."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anthrax",
    "long_answer": "The American thrash-metal band Anthrax includes the track “Anthrax” on their 1984 debut album. The song’s title refers to the bacterium Bacillus anthracis, whose spores are a well-known toxic agent that has been weaponised and used in bioterrorism attacks (e.g., the 2001 U.S. mail attacks)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in both copies of the MMUT gene (which encodes methylmalonyl-CoA mutase) are required to cause methylmalonic acidemia, the disorder that can include dysmorphic features. Because the disease manifests only when two mutant alleles are present and carriers are typically asymptomatic, the condition follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Loss of 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 3 activity blocks the conversion of androstenedione to testosterone in the fetal testis. The resulting low testosterone levels prevent normal masculinization of the external genitalia, so 46,XY fetuses present with ambiguous or female-appearing genitalia (undervirilization). Thus impaired testosterone biosynthesis caused by 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency is a well-established cause of XY undervirilization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Turner syndrome (45,X monosomy) is the single most common chromosomal abnormality identified in first-trimester spontaneous abortions, accounting for roughly 15–20 % of karyotyped miscarriages. It is a well-characterised aneuploid condition studied in medical genetics because surviving individuals display a distinct constellation of somatic and reproductive features, while most conceptions with monosomy X abort early in gestation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren’s syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Primary biliary cirrhosis (PBC) frequently co-exists with several autoimmune disorders. One of the commonest is Sjögren’s syndrome, an autoimmune exocrinopathy that can also produce systemic autoimmune phenomena, including warm-antibody (IgG) autoimmune haemolytic anaemia. Thus, the Sjögren’s–PBC association explains how a patient with PBC may additionally develop haemolytic anaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ultrasound scan",
    "long_answer": "Quadriceps tendon rupture is usually apparent clinically, but high-frequency musculoskeletal ultrasound can directly visualise a discontinuity in the quadriceps tendon and associated haematoma. Therefore, if the clinical picture is uncertain, an ultrasound scan is commonly used to confirm or exclude the rupture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absence of digital phalanges",
    "long_answer": "Aphalangia is a congenital limb malformation in which one or more phalanges of the fingers or toes fail to develop, resulting in their partial or complete absence. This lack of digital phalanges is the defining, characteristic feature of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCHS1",
    "long_answer": "Van Maldergem syndrome (also known as the Verloes-Van Maldergem-Marneffe syndrome) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the planar-cell-polarity gene DCHS1 (Dachsous Cadherin-Related 1). Disruption of DCHS1 impairs neuronal migration and cortical organization, leading to the characteristic global developmental delay and intellectual disability seen in affected individuals, along with craniofacial, skeletal and brain malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Larynx, trachea, esophagus",
    "long_answer": "Opitz G/BBB syndrome often involves midline malformations of the upper aerodigestive tract. Structural defects of the larynx (voice box), trachea (windpipe) and/or esophagus can narrow or create abnormal connections (e.g., laryngeal cleft, tracheo-esophageal fistula), leading to airway obstruction and aspiration. These abnormalities therefore produce marked difficulty with breathing and swallowing in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diffuse macular erythematous rash",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus toxic shock syndrome classically presents with a sudden, widespread, sunburn-like rash—described as a diffuse, blanching, macular erythematous eruption—that later undergoes desquamation, especially on the palms and soles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygous recessive",
    "long_answer": "Oculocutaneous albinism type I is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TYR gene encoding tyrosinase. To express the disease phenotype, an individual must carry two mutant alleles—either identical mutations or two different pathogenic variants—making the zygosity effectively homozygous (or compound heterozygous) for the recessive mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pierre Robin syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pierre Robin syndrome causes severe mandibular micrognathia and glossoptosis. The receding jaw makes laryngoscopy and tracheal intubation technically difficult, and the posteriorly displaced tongue narrows the upper airway, producing episodes of obstructive sleep apnea, especially during sleep or when the patient is supine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fanconi syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi syndrome is a disorder of the renal proximal convoluted tubule that impairs re-absorption of multiple solutes. As a result, substances normally reclaimed—including glucose, amino acids, phosphate, bicarbonate and uric acid—are inappropriately lost in the urine, leading to increased urinary excretion of uric acid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 80–90%",
    "long_answer": "Epilepsy is the most frequent neurological manifestation of tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC); cohort studies consistently show that about four-fifths to nine-tenths of individuals with TSC experience seizures at some point, giving a prevalence of roughly 80–90%."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome (CdLS) is a multisystem congenital disorder characterized by distinctive facial features, including ptosis, growth retardation, limb abnormalities, and developmental delay. The majority of classical CdLS cases result from heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the NIPBL gene, which encodes a cohesin loading factor essential for proper chromosome cohesion and gene regulation. Thus, NIPBL mutations are the primary genetic cause linking CdLS to clinical findings such as ptosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cortisol",
    "long_answer": "During the stress response, the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis triggers adrenal secretion of the glucocorticoid hormone cortisol. Endogenous cortisol rapidly redistributes and suppresses circulating lymphocytes, leading to the characteristic stress-induced lymphocytopenia observed in blood counts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ramsay Hunt syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ramsay Hunt syndrome, also called herpes zoster oticus, occurs when latent varicella-zoster virus reactivates in the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve. The resultant inflammation produces peripheral facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) paralysis, often accompanied by ear pain and a vesicular rash in the external ear canal or oropharynx."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inferior gluteal artery",
    "long_answer": "The coccygeus (ischiococcygeus) muscle receives its principal arterial supply from muscular branches of the inferior gluteal artery, a terminal branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery that courses posterior to the pelvic diaphragm to nourish both the levator ani and coccygeus muscles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bloom syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bloom syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function frameshift (or other inactivating) mutations in the BLM gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase. Truncation of BLM leads to genomic instability, elevated sister-chromatid exchange, growth deficiency, sun-sensitive skin rash, immunodeficiency, and a markedly increased cancer risk, defining the clinical picture of Bloom syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberculosis",
    "long_answer": "In pre-modern medical literature, the term “phthisis” (from the Greek for ‘wasting’) referred to the progressive wasting lung disease now recognized as tuberculosis, most commonly pulmonary tuberculosis caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alcoholic fatty liver disease",
    "long_answer": "Chronic heavy alcohol consumption directly causes fatty liver disease—termed alcoholic fatty liver disease—by altering hepatic metabolism (elevating the NADH/NAD⁺ ratio, inhibiting β-oxidation, and increasing triglyceride synthesis), leading to reversible triglyceride accumulation in hepatocytes in virtually all heavy drinkers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Folic acid",
    "long_answer": "In tropical sprue, malabsorption causes folate deficiency, contributing to anorexia, weight loss and megaloblastic changes. Oral folic acid replacement rapidly corrects the deficiency, often producing a prompt increase in appetite within days and subsequent weight gain, making folic acid supplementation a hallmark of therapy for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Auricular hypertrichosis",
    "long_answer": "Auricular hypertrichosis (also called hypertrichosis pinnae auris) is the trait in which coarse terminal hair grows excessively on the external ear. Early reports suggested Y-linked inheritance, but pedigree and linkage analyses in southern Indian families demonstrated that the condition does not cosegregate with the Y chromosome, indicating it is not Y-linked in that population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Celiac disease",
    "long_answer": "Celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) damages small-intestinal villi, producing malabsorption of calcium and vitamin D. Chronic deficiency of these nutrients decreases bone mineralization, so patients with untreated celiac disease frequently develop osteopenia and osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX6 gene, which encodes a paired-box transcription factor essential for eye morphogenesis, have repeatedly been identified in patients with Peters’ Anomaly. Loss-of-function or dosage alterations of PAX6 disrupt neural-crest–derived anterior segment development, producing the corneal opacity and iridocorneal adhesions that characterise this form of anterior segment mesenchymal dysgenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Stickler syndrome is classically inherited in an autosomal dominant manner: a single heterozygous pathogenic variant in genes such as COL2A1, COL11A1 or COL11A2 is sufficient to cause the characteristic cranio-facial, ocular, auditory and skeletal features, and the trait is passed from an affected parent to offspring with a 50 % recurrence risk. (Rare autosomal-recessive forms exist but are exceptional.)"
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "In an Italian family with Waardenburg syndrome type 2, a constitutional chromosomal translocation was found to break within the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) locus on chromosome 3p14. This disruption of MITF led to its identification as the gene whose mutation causes WS type 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fosdenopterin",
    "long_answer": "The FDA-approved therapy for Molybdenum Cofactor Deficiency Type A is fosdenopterin (marketed as NULIBRY). It is an IV formulation of cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate that replaces the missing precursor of the molybdenum cofactor in patients with MOCS1 mutations, thereby restoring enzyme activity and reducing the high mortality associated with this ultra-rare metabolic disorder. Approved in 2021, it is the first and only disease-specific treatment for MoCD Type A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cesarean section",
    "long_answer": "Because a large cystic hygroma can bulk the fetal neck and threaten airway patency or become traumatized during vaginal birth, obstetric consensus recommends planning delivery by cesarean section (often with an EXIT airway–securing procedure) to minimize compression, rupture, and respiratory compromise immediately after birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wet beriberi",
    "long_answer": "Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency can lead to beriberi; in the cardiovascular form known as wet beriberi, lack of thiamine impairs myocardial energy metabolism, causing peripheral vasodilation, high-output heart failure, and tachycardia (abnormally fast heart rate)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naxos disease",
    "long_answer": "Naxos disease (also called the Naxos syndrome) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the desmosomal proteins plakoglobin (JUP) and, in some families, desmoplakin (DSP). Like Carvajal syndrome, it presents with the triad of woolly hair and diffuse palmoplantar keratoderma accompanied by cardiomyopathy, giving the two disorders an almost identical cutaneous phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Duodenal wall",
    "long_answer": "Ectopic (accessory) pancreatic tissue most frequently occurs in the proximal small intestine, classically within the wall of the duodenum—often near the minor papilla—where it has no ductal connection to the main gland."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dup15q syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Dup15q (chromosome 15q11-q13 duplication) syndrome is produced by an extra copy of part of chromosome 15. Clinical findings often include intellectual disability, autism, seizures, and skeletal anomalies such as overlapping or unusually shaped toes, making it the chromosome-15 duplication disorder associated with toe abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratin retention",
    "long_answer": "In infants, the small skin-coloured or translucent papules (milia) arise because keratin becomes trapped within the superficial epidermis or immature pilosebaceous units, forming tiny inclusion cysts. This retention of keratin is the primary and well-established cause of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B3 (niacin)",
    "long_answer": "Canadian psychiatrist Abram Hoffer was a founder of orthomolecular psychiatry and advocated very high-dose therapy with niacin—vitamin B3, specifically nicotinic acid or its amide nicotinamide—to reduce psychotic symptoms and improve outcomes in patients with schizophrenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carpal bones",
    "long_answer": "Opsismodysplasia is marked by profoundly delayed skeletal maturation; radiographs typically show absent or extremely delayed secondary ossification centers in the wrist. This defect involves the carpal bones— the eight small bones that normally ossify in childhood— distinguishing the disorder from other skeletal dysplasias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral leukokeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Pachyonychia congenita frequently presents with oral leukokeratosis, a condition in which excessive keratinisation produces thick, white patches on the oral mucosa (buccal mucosa, tongue, palate). These white plaques are a hallmark mucosal manifestation of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aplastic anemia",
    "long_answer": "Hoyeraal–Hreidarsson syndrome is considered a severe variant of dyskeratosis congenita and is characterized by progressive bone-marrow failure. A common and life-threatening hematologic manifestation in these patients is severe aplastic anemia, marked by pancytopenia due to hypocellular bone marrow."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cephalosporins",
    "long_answer": "Cephalosporin antibiotics—most notably the second-generation agent cefaclor—are recognized causes of serum sickness (a type III immune-complex hypersensitivity). After 1–2 weeks of therapy, immune complexes formed against the drug deposit in tissues, producing fever, urticarial or morbilliform rash, arthralgias, lymphadenopathy, and hypocomplementemia, the classic clinical picture of serum sickness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optical coherence tomography",
    "long_answer": "Optical coherence tomography (OCT) offers high-resolution, cross-sectional imaging of the retina and choroid. In uveal melanoma, serial OCT scans can document changes in tumor-related retinal elevation and thickness, and they sensitively reveal radiation-induced complications such as macular edema or retinopathy after plaque brachytherapy or proton beam therapy. Hence, OCT is routinely employed to follow the course of uveal melanoma and to detect ocular side effects of its treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM5",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the TGM5 gene, which encodes epidermal transglutaminase 5, impair cross-linking of corneocyte proteins in the stratum corneum. This defect produces the superficial, painless desquamation characteristic of acral peeling skin syndrome (a form of peeling skin syndrome) that has long been synonymously described in the dermatology literature as keratolysis exfoliativa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anticonvulsant drugs",
    "long_answer": "Fountain syndrome can include epileptic seizures as part of its neurological manifestations. Standard medical management for such seizures is the administration of anticonvulsant (antiepileptic) drugs, which stabilize neuronal activity and prevent seizure episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hallermann-Streiff syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Hallermann-Streiff syndrome is a rare congenital disorder characterised by bilateral congenital cataracts together with microphthalmia (reduced eye size). The syndrome’s classic ocular features—microphthalmia, microcornea and dense cataracts present at birth—make it the prototypical condition linking small globes with bilateral cataract formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bulbospongiosus muscle",
    "long_answer": "The bulbospongiosus (bulbocavernosus) muscle, a superficial pelvic floor muscle implicated in the spasmodic tightening seen in vaginismus, also surrounds the clitoral bulbs. When it contracts, it compresses the erectile tissue and deep dorsal vein, promoting clitoral engorgement, and it participates in the rhythmic contractions characteristic of female orgasm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanosis",
    "long_answer": "Central hypoventilation syndrome causes inadequate ventilation, leading to hypoxemia; the resulting low oxygen saturation turns skin and mucous membranes a bluish-purple color, a symptom known medically as cyanosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC20A2",
    "long_answer": "The SLC20A2 gene, mapped to chromosome 8p11, is the most common genetic cause of Primary Familial Brain Calcification (PFBC). Pathogenic variants disrupt the PiT-2 phosphate transporter, leading to abnormal intracerebral calcium–phosphate deposition; affected individuals frequently manifest neurologic symptoms such as movement disorders and seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Migraine",
    "long_answer": "Phonophobia—an abnormal intolerance or fear of everyday sounds—is a classic accompanying symptom of migraine attacks. The International Classification of Headache Disorders lists phonophobia (often together with photophobia) as a diagnostic criterion for migraine because many patients experience heightened auditory sensitivity during an episode. This sound sensitivity arises from central nervous system hypersensitivity linked to activation of the trigeminovascular pathway in migraine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Blurred vision",
    "long_answer": "In myopia (near-sightedness) the eye’s optical focus falls in front of the retina, so distant objects appear indistinct—producing blurred vision. During many headache disorders, especially migraine or headaches provoked by ocular strain, patients likewise report transient blurring of vision. Hence blurred vision is a symptom that can accompany both myopia and headaches."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T lymphocytes",
    "long_answer": "In Marek's disease, a herpesvirus-induced lymphoma of chickens, the peripheral nerves become grossly enlarged because they are infiltrated by neoplastic T lymphocytes (predominantly CD4⁺ cells). These lymphoid cells accumulate within and around the nerve fibers, leading to the characteristic paralysis seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTC7A",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia is most often caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TTC7A gene, which encodes a tetratricopeptide repeat–containing protein essential for intestinal epithelial development and immune function. Disruption of TTC7A impairs epithelial polarity and survival, leading to the characteristic multiple atresias seen in affected neonates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sagittal craniosynostosis",
    "long_answer": "Cardiocranial syndrome, Pfeiffer type (also called CCSP) is a rare autosomal-recessive multiple-anomaly disorder whose hallmark cranial abnormality is premature fusion of the sagittal suture. This sagittal craniosynostosis produces the characteristic long, narrow (dolichocephalic) skull seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitreous",
    "long_answer": "Intermediate uveitis is characterized by inflammation centered in the vitreous cavity, particularly the vitreous gel and adjacent pars plana/peripheral retina, leading to vitreous cells and snowbanking, rather than primarily involving the iris or choroid."
}

{
    "short_answer": "400 micrograms",
    "long_answer": "UK Department of Health guidance advises all women who might become pregnant to take a 400 µg (0.4 mg) folic acid supplement daily, starting before conception and continuing through the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, as this dose is proven to lower the incidence of neural tube defects such as spina bifida in the developing fetus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver biopsy",
    "long_answer": "Although imaging studies and serum alpha-fetoprotein can suggest hepatocellular carcinoma, definitive confirmation is obtained by liver biopsy, where a core of hepatic tissue is sampled and histologically examined for malignant hepatocyte morphology characteristic of hepatocellular carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scanning electron microscopy",
    "long_answer": "Woolly hair is characterised by tightly coiled, flattened hair shafts with an oval-elliptical cross-section. Scanning electron microscopy allows high-resolution surface imaging of individual hair shafts, revealing their flattened, twisted morphology and irregular cuticle scales; these distinctive ultrastructural features confirm the diagnosis of woolly hair."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Isotretinoin",
    "long_answer": "Oral isotretinoin, a systemic retinoid, is prescribed to patients with xeroderma pigmentosum to reduce the development and recurrence of ultraviolet-induced skin cancers such as basal-cell and squamous-cell carcinomas. It promotes normal keratinocyte differentiation and exerts antiproliferative effects, thereby lowering the skin-cancer burden in this DNA-repair-deficient disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoplasmic reticulum",
    "long_answer": "In skeletal muscle cells, excessive release of Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum elevates cytosolic calcium, activating proteases and phospholipases that damage muscle fibers. The resulting rhabdomyolysis releases myoglobin into the bloodstream, which is filtered into urine, producing myoglobinuria. Thus, abnormal calcium ion movement from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is central to the disorder’s pathophysiology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypocalcemia",
    "long_answer": "During rhabdomyolysis, massive muscle breakdown releases intracellular phosphate and allows calcium to be taken up or precipitated within damaged muscle. The resulting fall in circulating ionized calcium produces hypocalcemia, a condition defined by abnormally low serum calcium levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cronkhite-Canada syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Cronkhite-Canada syndrome is a rare, non-hereditary GI polyposis disorder marked by hundreds of hamartomatous polyps throughout the stomach, small intestine and colon. The diffuse mucosal involvement causes excessive loss of serum proteins into the gut lumen, producing protein-losing enteropathy with hypoalbuminemia, edema and weight loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Integrase",
    "long_answer": "Visna-maedi virus is a lentiviral retrovirus; once its RNA is reverse-transcribed to double-stranded DNA, the viral enzyme integrase (encoded by the pol gene) catalyzes the cutting of host chromosomal DNA and ligation of the viral DNA into it, achieving stable proviral integration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sensorineural hearing loss",
    "long_answer": "Survivors of bacterial or viral meningitis often develop sensorineural hearing loss due to inflammation-induced damage of the cochlea and auditory nerve. Preterm infants have an elevated baseline vulnerability of the auditory system and are frequently exposed to ototoxic drugs and cardiorespiratory instability, making them at even greater risk for this long-term sequela of meningitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uterine fibroid",
    "long_answer": "Uterine fibroids, also called leiomyomas, are benign smooth-muscle tumors of the uterus. By enlarging the endometrial surface and disrupting normal uterine contractility, they commonly cause heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia). Chronic excessive blood loss can deplete iron stores and lead to iron-deficiency anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cardiothoracic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Interrupted aortic arch is a critical congenital heart defect that can be corrected only by open surgical reconstruction of the aortic arch and great vessels, usually within the first days or weeks of life. This operation is performed by cardiothoracic (congenital/pediatric cardiac) surgeons; therefore, infants with an interrupted aortic arch are most often managed under the care of the cardiothoracic surgery specialty."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Orthopedic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Septic arthritis is an acute bacterial infection of a joint that can rapidly destroy cartilage; in children the standard care involves prompt surgical irrigation and debridement together with intravenous antibiotics. The specialty that performs joint drainage and manages musculoskeletal infections is orthopedic surgery (specifically pediatric orthopedics), so orthopedic surgeons are the clinicians most commonly responsible for treating children with septic arthritis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wear shower sandals",
    "long_answer": "Dermatophytosis fungi are commonly transmitted in communal showers, locker rooms, and pool decks when bare skin contacts contaminated damp surfaces. Using shower sandals (flip-flops) creates a physical barrier between the skin and these surfaces, preventing dermatophyte spores from reaching and colonising the stratum corneum, thereby markedly reducing the risk of infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn",
    "long_answer": "Warfarin crosses the placenta and blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase in the fetus. This prevents γ-carboxylation of the vitamin K–dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X, leaving the neonate profoundly deficient in these factors. The deficiency produces spontaneous, sometimes life-threatening bleeding after birth—classically referred to as hemorrhagic disease of the newborn."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune neuromuscular disease in which antibodies target components of the acetylcholine receptor complex at the neuromuscular junction. Because extraocular muscles are especially susceptible, patients commonly develop fluctuating weakness in these muscles, manifesting as ptosis and double vision (diplopia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myasthenia gravis",
    "long_answer": "Maternal autoimmune myasthenia gravis can cause trans-placental blockade of fetal acetylcholine receptors. The resulting fetal akinesia leads to multiple joint contractures (arthrogryposis), and the same neuromuscular transmission defect characteristically produces weakness or complete paralysis of the extra-ocular muscles (ophthalmoplegia/ptosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kabuki syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Kabuki syndrome (Niikawa-Kuroki syndrome) is a congenital genetic disorder, usually caused by pathogenic variants in KMT2D or KDM6A, that produces distinctive facial features. A hallmark is abnormal eyebrows—highly arched, sparse, or irregularly distributed, sometimes with partial or complete absence of the lateral third. This eyebrow anomaly is widely recognized as a diagnostic clue for Kabuki syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synaptobrevin",
    "long_answer": "Tetanus toxin is a zinc-dependent endopeptidase that enters inhibitory interneurons and specifically proteolyzes synaptobrevin (also called vesicle-associated membrane protein, VAMP). By cleaving synaptobrevin, an essential v-SNARE required for synaptic vesicle fusion with the presynaptic membrane, the toxin prevents formation of the SNARE complex, thereby blocking release of inhibitory neurotransmitters such as GABA and glycine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcitriol",
    "long_answer": "Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D₃) is the active vitamin D₃ analog routinely given to patients with chronic kidney disease–related renal osteodystrophy to treat secondary hyperparathyroidism. Because it bypasses renal 1-α-hydroxylation, calcitriol restores intestinal calcium absorption, raises serum calcium, and directly suppresses parathyroid hormone release, thereby correcting the metabolic bone changes of renal osteodystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CNNM4",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the CNNM4 gene (Cyclin M4), which encodes a magnesium transporter expressed in retinal photoreceptors and enamel-forming ameloblasts, cause Jalili syndrome (cone–rod dystrophy with amelogenesis imperfecta) and are also documented in families presenting with non-syndromic amelogenesis imperfecta. Thus, CNNM4 mutations link both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frequent handwashing",
    "long_answer": "Scarlet fever is caused by group A Streptococcus, which is transmitted mainly through respiratory droplets and contaminated hands or objects. Regular, thorough handwashing with soap and water physically removes the bacteria before they can be passed to others, making it one of the most effective, evidence-based measures for limiting person-to-person spread of scarlet fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy",
    "long_answer": "Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (usually caused by mutations in the emerin or LMNA genes) is well known for early-onset cardiac conduction system disease. Patients commonly develop atrioventricular block and life-threatening arrhythmias, so prophylactic implantation of a permanent pacemaker (or an ICD) is frequently required to prevent sudden cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dehydroepiandrosterone",
    "long_answer": "Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) is an endogenous adrenal steroid that serves as a biochemical precursor for both androgens and estrogens via peripheral conversion (e.g., aromatization to estrone and estradiol). An intravaginal formulation of DHEA (prasterone) is FDA-approved to treat vulvovaginal atrophy/atrophic vaginitis in postmenopausal women, where it alleviates symptoms by locally increasing estrogenic and androgenic activity in vaginal tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fistulography",
    "long_answer": "A collaural fistula is a first-branchial cleft tract that runs between the neck (collum) and the external auditory canal. Injecting a water-soluble contrast medium into the cutaneous opening and obtaining radiographic images—i.e., performing a fistulogram—delineates the entire course of the tract and confirms the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Straight eyebrows",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with 1p36 deletion syndrome show a characteristic cranio-facial gestalt in which the most recognisable single feature is straight, laterally continuous eyebrows that give the face a ‘straight-eyebrow’ appearance; this, together with deep-set eyes, mid-face hypoplasia, a broad nasal root and a pointed chin, forms the distinctive facies of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bone marrow transplantation",
    "long_answer": "In malignant (severe infantile) osteopetrosis, paralysis results from bony overgrowth that compresses the spinal cord and cranial nerves. Allogeneic bone marrow (hematopoietic stem-cell) transplantation supplies normal osteoclast precursors, restores bone resorption, remodels the excessively dense bone, and can relieve or reverse the resulting neurologic paralysis. It is the only established curative therapy for this form of osteopetrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Seizures",
    "long_answer": "In Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (Hers disease), recurrent fasting hypoglycemia can lead to neuro-glycopenia and seizures. Maintaining euglycemia through frequent carbohydrate-rich meals or uncooked cornstarch therapy prevents hypoglycemic episodes and thereby controls or eliminates seizure occurrence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epithelial-myoepithelial carcinoma",
    "long_answer": "Epithelial-myoepithelial carcinoma of the salivary glands shows a distinctive biphasic pattern: each small tubule has an inner lining of eosinophilic ductal epithelial cells surrounded by a rim of clear, glycogen-rich myoepithelial cells. This dual epithelial-myoepithelial architecture is the defining histologic hallmark of the tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edwards syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome, or trisomy 18, is caused by the presence of an extra copy of chromosome 18. A common clinical feature in affected neonates and infants is generalized hypotonia (reduced muscle tone), alongside characteristic dysmorphic features and multiple congenital anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Menorrhagia",
    "long_answer": "Hypodysfibrinogenemia is a hereditary qualitative defect of fibrinogen that impairs clot formation, predisposing affected individuals to excessive mucocutaneous bleeding. When this disorder leads to abnormally heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding, the clinical term used is menorrhagia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microsporidian fungi",
    "long_answer": "Microsporidiosis is an infection produced by microsporidia, a group of obligate intracellular, spore-forming fungi. These eukaryotic parasites invade host cells, where they replicate and can cause gastrointestinal, ocular, or disseminated disease, particularly in immunocompromised individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is an inherited connective-tissue disorder caused by defects in collagen synthesis or structure. The faulty collagen weakens skin and ligaments, producing cutis laxa-like hyperextensible, fragile skin and markedly hypermobile (double-jointed) joints—both cardinal clinical features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Minamata disease",
    "long_answer": "The United Nations Environment Programme’s Global 500 Roll of Honour included the victims of Minamata disease, acknowledging their persistent activism that exposed industrial mercury pollution in Minamata Bay, Japan. Their efforts led to landmark environmental regulations and international awareness of heavy-metal contamination, which the award recognized as a significant environmental achievement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dentin",
    "long_answer": "True tooth fusion occurs when two separate developing tooth germs join so that their dentin becomes continuous. Mere union of cementum is termed concrescence; fusion specifically requires the dentinal tissues of the teeth to fuse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Computed tomography (CT)",
    "long_answer": "In Urbach–Wiethe disease, characteristic bilateral calcifications—particularly of the amygdala—are readily visualised with computed tomography. CT imaging has high sensitivity for intracranial calcium deposits, making it the standard neuro-imaging method for detecting the brain calcifications associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Klebsiella pneumoniae",
    "long_answer": "Klebsiella pneumoniae is a well-known cause of severe, necrotising bacterial pneumonia. The intense inflammation and tissue destruction it provokes can damage bronchial walls and impair mucociliary clearance, predisposing the affected lung segments to permanent dilatation of the bronchi—i.e., bronchiectasis. Thus, an infection by this common pneumonia pathogen can subsequently lead to the development of bronchiectasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rifampin prophylaxis",
    "long_answer": "Close household or intimate contacts should receive chemoprophylaxis with rifampin, which rapidly eradicates Neisseria meningitidis from the nasopharynx and prevents secondary cases of meningococcal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Broca’s aphasia",
    "long_answer": "Lesions in Broca’s area—the posterior inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant (usually left) hemisphere—produce Broca’s aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia. Patients have markedly reduced, effortful speech output while comprehension remains relatively intact, reflecting the critical role of Broca’s area in speech production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Synechia",
    "long_answer": "In surgical parlance an unplanned, pathological fusion between two normally separate tissue surfaces is called a synechia (plural synechiae). Congenital syngnathia represents a synechia in which the maxillary and mandibular tissues adhere to each other, preventing normal jaw opening."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematoxylin-eosin (H&E)",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic gastroenteritis is diagnosed histologically by counting eosinophils in mucosal biopsies. Routine hematoxylin-eosin staining is used, because eosinophil granules bind the acid dye eosin and appear bright pink-red, allowing their numbers and distribution to be readily evaluated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radius",
    "long_answer": "Acrorenal-mandibular syndrome is classically defined by a triad of limb, renal and mandibibular malformations. The hallmark limb defect is bilateral radial aplasia—complete absence of the radius—while the ulna and other long bones are usually present. Hence, the radius is the bone characteristically missing in individuals with this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyaluronidase",
    "long_answer": "In cutis verticis gyrata the scalp skin is markedly thickened because of excess dermal mucopolysaccharides, particularly hyaluronic acid. Intradermal or subcutaneous injection of the enzyme hyaluronidase is used therapeutically because it specifically hydrolyzes hyaluronic acid, thereby reducing the bulk and thickness of the involved skin folds."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria cutanea tarda results from a deficiency of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase, the fifth enzyme of the hepatic heme biosynthetic pathway. Loss of this enzyme’s activity leads to accumulation of uroporphyrinogens that generate photosensitizing porphyrins, producing the characteristic blistering skin lesions of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heel-cord stretching",
    "long_answer": "For children with flexible flatfoot, daily heel-cord (Achilles tendon) stretching exercises are routinely advised. Tightness of the gastrocnemius–soleus–Achilles complex contributes to excessive hind-foot pronation and collapse of the medial longitudinal arch. Lengthening this complex through heel-cord stretching reduces pronatory forces, allowing the arch to elevate and improving the flat-foot deformity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPR56",
    "long_answer": "Bilateral frontoparietal polymicrogyria is most commonly caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the GPR56 gene (also called ADGRG1). GPR56 encodes an adhesion G-protein–coupled receptor required for proper assembly of the pial basement membrane during cortical development; its disruption leads to the characteristic symmetrical frontoparietal polymicrogyria seen in BFPP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Photophobia",
    "long_answer": "In vernal keratoconjunctivitis, disease-severity scales describe mild disease as predominantly itching and tearing, while moderate disease adds light sensitivity; thus photophobia is the characteristic symptom that appears with moderate vernal keratoconjunctivitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benign fasciculation syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Benign fasciculation syndrome (BFS) presents with persistent, visible muscle fasciculations (twitches) but lacks the sensory loss, weakness, and electrophysiologic evidence of axonal damage that define peripheral neuropathy. Because patients with BFS often fear they have a neuropathic or motor-neuron disorder, clinicians must carefully distinguish this benign condition from peripheral neuropathy through neurologic examination and normal nerve-conduction studies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TORCH syndrome",
    "long_answer": "TORCH syndrome (also called the TORCH complex) refers to the constellation of congenital defects that arise from in-utero infections with Toxoplasma gondii, Rubella virus, Cytomegalovirus, Herpes simplex virus, and other vertically transmitted pathogens such as HIV, syphilis, and parvovirus B19. These agents share the capacity to cross the placenta, disrupt fetal development, and produce a similar spectrum of clinical manifestations, hence they are grouped under the acronym TORCH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous methylprednisolone",
    "long_answer": "The standard initial management of acute optic neuritis is a course of high-dose intravenous methylprednisolone (typically 1 g/day for 3 days). Data from the Optic Neuritis Treatment Trial demonstrated that IV corticosteroids speed visual recovery and lower short-term risk of recurrent demyelinating events, making this regimen the accepted first-line therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "H+-ATPase & ENaC genes",
    "long_answer": "Inherited distal renal tubular acidosis (dRTA) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in genes that encode the vacuolar H+-ATPase of α-intercalated cells—ATP6V1B1 and ATP6V0A4—or, less often, the chloride-bicarbonate exchanger SLC4A1. In contrast, pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 (PHA-1) results from mutations that blunt aldosterone-mediated sodium reabsorption: the renal form involves the mineralocorticoid-receptor gene NR3C2, while the systemic form is due to mutations in any of the three epithelial sodium-channel (ENaC) subunit genes—SCNN1A (α), SCNN1B (β) or SCNN1G (γ). Hence, dRTA is linked to H+-ATPase–related genes, whereas PHA-1 is linked to ENaC or NR3C2 mutations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Mucha–Habermann disease (pityriasis lichenoides et varioliformis acuta) is a rare inflammatory skin disorder characterized by distinctive cutaneous papules and necrotic lesions. Because it is confined to the skin and requires clinical examination, dermatoscopic assessment, and skin-biopsy interpretation for definitive diagnosis—followed by management with topical or systemic therapies such as corticosteroids, antibiotics, or phototherapy—the physician most qualified to diagnose and treat it is a dermatologist, the medical specialist in diseases of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paroxetine",
    "long_answer": "Scopophobia is categorized as a form of social anxiety. Selective serotonin-reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are first-line pharmacologic treatment for social anxiety disorders. Paroxetine, an SSRI approved for social anxiety disorder, elevates synaptic serotonin levels and has been shown in controlled trials to lessen anticipatory anxiety, fear of scrutiny, and the physical symptoms (e.g., palpitations, sweating) that accompany phobic reactions—thereby helping patients manage the symptoms of scopophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease",
    "long_answer": "After phenylketonuria, the congenital amino-acid-metabolism disorder most frequently detected worldwide is Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD). MSUD is an autosomal-recessive deficiency of the branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex, preventing normal catabolism of leucine, isoleucine and valine; newborn-screening data show it is consistently the second most prevalent inborn error of amino-acid metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Invasin-mediated adherence",
    "long_answer": "Yersinia pseudotuberculosis produces the outer-membrane adhesin ‘invasin’, which binds with high affinity to β1-integrins on M cells of the intestinal epithelium. This tight attachment anchors the bacteria to the mucosa, preventing them from being flushed out by normal peristaltic movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome)",
    "long_answer": "Fetal choroid plexus cysts seen on mid-trimester ultrasound are a well-known soft marker for the autosomal aneuploidy trisomy 18, also called Edwards syndrome. Although many cysts are benign and resolve, their presence increases the likelihood that the fetus carries an extra copy of chromosome 18; large series show a markedly higher prevalence of trisomy 18 in fetuses with persistent or multiple choroid plexus cysts compared with the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycogen debranching enzyme",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type III (Cori/Forbes disease) is caused by deficiency of the glycogen debranching enzyme, also called amylo-1,6-glucosidase/4-α-glucanotransferase (encoded by the AGL gene). Lack of this enzyme impairs breakdown of branched glycogen, leading to abnormal glycogen accumulation in liver, skeletal muscle, and cardiac muscle; the cardiac deposition can produce hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis",
    "long_answer": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Churg–Strauss syndrome) is a small- to medium-vessel necrotizing vasculitis marked by prominent peripheral and tissue eosinophilia. Clinically, it almost always involves a history of asthma or allergic rhinitis, and the triad of asthma, eosinophilia, and systemic vasculitis is characteristic."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Balkh",
    "long_answer": "“Balkh sore” is a regional name for cutaneous leishmaniasis that takes its name from the ancient city of Balkh in northern Afghanistan, where the disease has been endemic for centuries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prolonged apnea",
    "long_answer": "Individuals with pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) deficiency cannot rapidly hydrolyze depolarizing neuromuscular blockers such as succinylcholine or mivacurium. As a result, the drug-induced neuromuscular blockade persists far longer than normal, producing prolonged apnea and respiratory paralysis that necessitate extended mechanical ventilation until the drug is spontaneously cleared."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anger",
    "long_answer": "In paranoid personality disorder, interpersonal interactions are typically colored by pervasive distrust and projection of aggressive feelings; consequently, anger (and associated hostility) is the dominant affect. Individuals are quick to perceive slights and react with anger or counter-attack, making this emotion central to their relational dynamics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC8 and KCNJ11",
    "long_answer": "The majority of congenital hyperinsulinism cases arise from loss-of-function mutations in the KATP-channel genes ABCC8 (encoding the SUR1 subunit) and KCNJ11 (encoding the Kir6.2 subunit). Defective KATP channels keep β-cell membranes depolarized, causing unregulated insulin secretion and persistent hypoglycaemia; together these two genes account for over half of all mutation-positive congenital hyperinsulinism patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHMP1A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in CHMP1A (Charged Multivesicular Body Protein 1A) lead to a form of pontocerebellar hypoplasia. CHMP1A is an ESCRT-III subunit that binds the MIT domain of the AAA-ATPase VPS4A, a key interaction required for ESCRT-III filament disassembly. Thus, CHMP1A is the pontocerebellar-hypoplasia gene that interacts directly with VPS4A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxic encephalopathy",
    "long_answer": "Prolonged or intense exposure to neurotoxic organic solvents (e.g., toluene, xylene, trichloroethylene) can injure neuronal membranes, disturb myelin integrity, and disrupt neurotransmission, leading to toxic encephalopathy. This solvent-induced brain disorder manifests with cognitive decline, memory loss, mood changes, and motor deficits, and the neurological damage is often irreversible, making it a form of permanent brain injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)",
    "long_answer": "In Swyer syndrome (46,XY pure gonadal dysgenesis) the streak gonads fail to produce sex steroids. The absent negative feedback on the pituitary causes markedly increased secretion of the gonadotropins, particularly follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), so serum FSH levels are characteristically elevated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zona fasciculata",
    "long_answer": "11β-Hydroxylase (CYP11B1) catalyses the last step in cortisol synthesis, an action that takes place in the zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex. When this enzyme is congenitally deficient, cortisol falls, ACTH rises, and the zona fasciculata undergoes marked hyperplasia; this is the characteristic lesion in congenital adrenal hyperplasia caused by 11β-hydroxylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sonic hedgehog (SHH)",
    "long_answer": "Preaxial polydactyly often results from mutations in the regulatory ZRS enhancer of the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) gene. These mutations drive ectopic anterior (preaxial) expression of SHH in the limb bud, creating a new zone of polarizing activity–like organizer and producing extra thumb-side digits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLNB",
    "long_answer": "Young–Madders syndrome, also known as spondylocarpotarsal synostosis syndrome, has been traced to biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the FLNB gene. FLNB encodes filamin B, an actin-binding cytoskeletal protein essential for normal vertebral segmentation and limb bone development. Disruption of FLNB interferes with endochondral ossification, leading to the characteristic vertebral fusions and carpal/tarsal synostoses seen in Young–Madders syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Right atrium",
    "long_answer": "During embryogenesis the right horn of the sinus venosus gives rise both to the superior and inferior venae cavae and to the smooth-walled portion of the right atrium (sinus venarum). If the vena cava fails to form (agenesis), the same developmental field is absent, so the part of the heart that is affected is the smooth portion of the right atrium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transient elastography (FibroScan)",
    "long_answer": "Transient elastography (FibroScan) is a non-invasive, ultrasound-based imaging method that measures liver stiffness; elevated stiffness readings correlate with advanced fibrosis and cirrhosis, making it a standard tool for detecting cirrhosis in patients with primary biliary cholangitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuromyelitis optica",
    "long_answer": "Neuromyelitis optica, also called Devic’s disease, is an autoimmune demyelinating disorder in which pathogenic IgG autoantibodies target the water-channel protein aquaporin-4 on astrocytes. The disease frequently presents with severe optic neuritis and transverse myelitis, and serum aquaporin-4 IgG is a diagnostic biomarker distinguishing it from multiple sclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gap junctions in canaliculi",
    "long_answer": "Osteocytes sit in lacunae and send thin cytoplasmic processes through microscopic channels called canaliculi. Where these processes touch those of neighboring osteocytes or cells on the bone surface, they form gap junctions. These gap junctions create direct cytoplasmic connections that let small molecules, ions, nutrients, and metabolic waste diffuse from cell to cell, ensuring metabolic exchange throughout the bone matrix."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acromesomelic dysplasia, Grebe type",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the GDF5 gene cause acromesomelic dysplasia, Grebe type. This skeletal dysplasia is characterized by severe shortening of the distal limb segments and frequently includes hand- and foot-malformations such as polydactyly, linking the condition directly to both GDF5 dysfunction and the presence of extra digits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plastic surgery",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome is a congenital craniofacial disorder characterized by hypoplastic zygomatic arches, mandibular underdevelopment, ear malformations, and other facial anomalies. Management focuses on reconstructing the facial skeleton, soft-tissue contour, and external ear, as well as correcting airway and palate defects. These reconstructive procedures fall within the domain of craniofacial plastic surgery; therefore, a plastic (craniofacial) surgeon is the specialist most commonly responsible for treating and coordinating care for patients with Treacher Collins syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thyroid ectopy",
    "long_answer": "Thyroglossal cysts arise from persistence of the thyroglossal duct, the pathway along which the thyroid primordium normally migrates from the foramen cecum to the neck. Failure or abnormality of this migration leads to ectopic thyroid tissue along the duct’s course; hence, the form of thyroid dysgenesis classically linked to thyroglossal cysts is thyroid ectopy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (also called pseudotumor cerebri) produces chronically elevated intracranial pressure in the absence of an intracranial mass, hydrocephalus, or other structural cause. The raised cerebrospinal fluid pressure can flatten the pituitary gland and distend the sella turcica, creating an ‘empty sella’ appearance that closely imitates CSF build-up seen in empty sella syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Leptomeningeal angiomas",
    "long_answer": "In Sturge-Weber syndrome, congenital capillary-venous malformations (leptomeningeal angiomas) overlie the cerebral cortex. These vascular lesions impair venous drainage and chronically ischemize the underlying brain, leading to cortical atrophy and dystrophic mineralization. The resulting deposition of calcium within the atrophic cortex produces the characteristic gyriform (“tram-track”) calcifications seen on imaging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylalanine hydroxylase",
    "long_answer": "Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH₄) is an essential cofactor for the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase, which converts phenylalanine to tyrosine. When BH₄ is deficient, phenylalanine hydroxylase activity is functionally impaired, leading to accumulation of phenylalanine in the blood (hyperphenylalaninemia). Thus, deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase activity—secondary to lack of BH₄—causes the elevated phenylalanine levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Physiotherapists",
    "long_answer": "Physiotherapists form the core professional group responsible for the rehabilitation of Guillain–Barré syndrome patients; they design and implement graded mobility, muscle-strengthening, range-of-motion, and respiratory exercises that prevent contractures, maintain joint function, and facilitate functional recovery as peripheral nerves remyelinate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mechanical ventilation",
    "long_answer": "In a myasthenic crisis, auto-antibody–mediated failure of neuromuscular transmission can paralyze the diaphragm and other respiratory muscles. When this causes impending or actual respiratory failure, the standard life-saving intervention is endotracheal intubation with mechanical ventilation to support breathing until therapies such as plasmapheresis, IVIG, or anticholinesterase medication restore adequate muscle strength."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX3",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg syndrome type 1 is most frequently caused by pathogenic mutations in the PAX3 gene, which disrupt neural crest development. These PAX3 mutations can impair melanocyte formation in the inner ear, resulting in congenital sensorineural hearing loss characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dura mater",
    "long_answer": "Experimental and clinical studies show that the dura mater underlying a cranial suture secretes molecular signals that either maintain the suture’s patency or trigger osteogenic fusion. When the dura is removed or replaced, sutures that normally close can remain open and vice-versa, implicating the dura mater as the meningeal layer that regulates suture closure in craniosynostosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ellobius talpinus",
    "long_answer": "In the northern mole vole, Ellobius talpinus, the Y chromosome and the Sry gene have disappeared entirely. Cytogenetic analyses show that both males and females possess an XX karyotype (2n = 54), demonstrating that sex determination in this species occurs without a Y chromosome; males and females each carry two X chromosomes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclosporine",
    "long_answer": "Several case series and reports have shown that the calcineurin-inhibiting immunosuppressant cyclosporine can markedly reduce lymphoid masses, eosinophilia, and serum IgE levels in Kimura’s disease, supporting its clinical efficacy for this rare inflammatory disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Estrogen production",
    "long_answer": "In 46,XX gonadal dysgenesis the ovaries fail to develop into functional steroid-producing tissue, so ovarian secretion of estrogens (and consequently other ovarian steroids such as progesterone) is markedly impaired. This loss of estrogen production leads to primary amenorrhea, absent pubertal development, and elevated gonadotropins characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Zeichi-Ceide syndrome has been reported in multiple affected siblings born to consanguineous parents with no affected individuals in previous generations. The clustering in siblings and the necessity of both parents being carriers point to an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Honeycombing",
    "long_answer": "On high-resolution CT of chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis, fibrotic remodeling produces subpleural clusters of 3–10 mm cystic airspaces with irregularly thickened walls; this characteristic appearance is called honeycombing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pericentrin",
    "long_answer": "Microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II (MOPD II) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PCNT gene, which encodes the centrosomal scaffolding protein pericentrin. Defective pericentrin disrupts centrosome integrity and cell-cycle progression, leading to the extreme growth restriction and brain size reduction characteristic of this rare genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy",
    "long_answer": "Steven C. Hayes is the originator of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), a form of cognitive-behavioral psychotherapy that focuses on psychological flexibility through acceptance, mindfulness, and commitment to valued actions. ACT has been applied to impulse-control disorders such as trichotillomania, where it helps patients accept urges without acting on them and commit to alternative behaviors, thereby reducing hair-pulling episodes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APC gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis is classically caused by germline mutations in the APC (adenomatous polyposis coli) tumor-suppressor gene; screening tests in preventive medicine can identify pathogenic APC mutations before clinical disease develops."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Melatonin",
    "long_answer": "Exogenous melatonin taken at the same time each day serves as a strong zeitgeber (time cue) that can entrain or reset the internal circadian clock in individuals with non-24-hour sleep-wake disorder, helping their sleep–wake cycle align with the 24-hour day."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary amenorrhea",
    "long_answer": "Müllerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome) is characterized by congenital absence of the uterus and upper vagina in phenotypic females with normal ovarian function. Because endometrial tissue is absent, menstrual bleeding never begins, leading to primary amenorrhea—the failure to achieve menarche by the expected age."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hydranencephaly",
    "long_answer": "Hydranencephaly is a rare congenital malformation in which the cerebral hemispheres fail to develop or are destroyed in utero, usually after bilateral occlusion of the internal carotid arteries. The cranial vault remains intact, but the space that would normally contain the cerebral cortex is instead occupied almost entirely by cerebrospinal fluid, giving the appearance of a skull ‘filled’ with CSF and virtually absent cerebral hemispheres."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Marked hypotension",
    "long_answer": "During seizures in Panayiotopoulos syndrome the autonomic discharge commonly produces pronounced parasympathetic activation, leading to bradycardia and a significant fall in systemic arterial pressure; hence blood pressure drops (ictal hypotension)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type II is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NF2 gene on chromosome 22q12. The NF2 gene encodes the tumor-suppressor protein merlin (schwannomin); when NF2 is inactivated, cells—especially Schwann cells, meningeal cells, and ependymal cells—lose growth control, leading to the characteristic benign tumors such as bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal ependymomas seen in NF2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alzheimer’s disease",
    "long_answer": "Alzheimer’s disease is a neurodegenerative disorder in which abnormal aggregation and hyper-phosphorylation of tau protein (a tauopathy) occurs alongside β-amyloid pathology. Progressive tau-driven neurofibrillary tangles disrupt neuronal function and connectivity, leading to the characteristic progressive cognitive decline that meets DSM-5 criteria for a major neurocognitive disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sphingolipidosis",
    "long_answer": "Gangliosidosis is a lysosomal lipid storage disease in which gangliosides (a class of complex sphingolipids) accumulate because the enzymes required for their degradation are deficient. Disorders characterized by pathologic buildup of sphingolipids are collectively termed sphingolipidoses, so gangliosidosis is classified as a sphingolipidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastroenterology",
    "long_answer": "Congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents with chronic osmotic diarrhea, bloating, and failure to thrive due to absent brush-border disaccharidase activity. Definitive diagnosis requires specialized gastrointestinal tests—small-bowel biopsy for disaccharidase assays or sucrose hydrogen breath testing—that are ordered, performed, and interpreted by gastroenterologists (often pediatric gastroenterologists). Thus, gastroenterology is the specialty most commonly responsible for diagnosing CSID."
}

{
    "short_answer": "George Mendoza",
    "long_answer": "George Mendoza is a celebrated American painter and author who became legally blind in his teens after developing a complex eye disorder that includes macular coloboma. Coloboma is a congenital defect in which normal ocular tissue fails to develop, leaving a gap in structures such as the retina or optic nerve; when the macula is affected it can severely impair central vision and result in legal blindness. Despite this vision loss, Mendoza has gained national recognition for his vividly colored paintings, many of which are inspired by the kaleidoscopic visual phenomena produced by his coloboma-related retinal damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCX",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the DCX (doublecortin) gene disrupt neuronal migration, producing a lissencephaly-pachygyria spectrum in males and subcortical band heterotopia in heterozygous females. Individuals with DCX-related pachygyria almost invariably develop early-onset, often drug-resistant epilepsy, making DCX mutation the well-recognized genetic link between pachygyria and pharmacoresistant seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOX7",
    "long_answer": "The 8p23.1 region that is duplicated in this syndrome contains the SOX7 gene, an SRY-box transcription factor critical for early cardiogenesis. Experimental over-expression of SOX7 in model systems (e.g., zebrafish embryos) produces atrioventricular septal and other cardiac defects, indicating that increased SOX7 dosage is pathogenic. Consequently, SOX7 over-dosage is regarded as the major molecular cause of the congenital heart disease seen in 8p23.1 duplication syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive disorder",
    "long_answer": "HEC syndrome (Hydrocephalus-Endocardial fibroelastosis-Cataract) has been reported in multiple affected siblings born to consanguineous parents, with equal involvement of males and females, and without male-to-male transmission. These pedigree features are characteristic of an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance, so the condition is classified as an autosomal recessive genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VHL",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the von Hippel–Lindau (VHL) tumor-suppressor gene block degradation of hypoxia-inducible factor-α (HIF-α). Stabilized HIF transcriptionally up-regulates glucose transporters and glycolytic enzymes, causing a switch to high glycolytic flux even in the presence of oxygen (Warburg effect). This metabolic reprogramming promotes cell survival, proliferation, and angiogenesis, markedly increasing the lifetime risk of cancers such as clear-cell renal cell carcinoma, hemangioblastoma, and pheochromocytoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Missouri River",
    "long_answer": "In Jefferson’s written instructions for the 1804-06 Lewis and Clark Expedition, he ordered Meriwether Lewis to carry preserved kinepox (cowpox) matter so that the party could vaccinate Native peoples encountered while ascending the Missouri River, the principal route of their exploration from St. Louis toward the northwest. Jefferson saw the portable cowpox vaccine as a means to protect against smallpox and specifically directed that it be taken up the Missouri for use along that river corridor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "A ventricular septal defect (VSD) is the congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening (hole) in the interventricular septum, allowing blood to flow between the left and right ventricles."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scissoring gait",
    "long_answer": "In spastic diplegic cerebral palsy the hip adductor muscles are hyper-tonic, forcing the thighs into adduction and internal rotation. When the child walks, the legs cross in front of each other with a narrow base, resembling the blades of a pair of scissors—hence the classic “scissoring” gait pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fusobacterium nucleatum",
    "long_answer": "Fusobacterium nucleatum is consistently found at elevated levels in colorectal adenomas and carcinomas. Its abundance correlates with the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, and stool or biopsy detection of F. nucleatum DNA has shown high sensitivity and specificity for distinguishing precancerous bowel lesions from normal tissue, making it a valuable early-prediction biomarker for colorectal cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH11",
    "long_answer": "The MYH11 gene, encoding smooth-muscle myosin heavy chain 11, is one of the genes mutated in Berdon syndrome (megacystis-microcolon-intestinal hypoperistalsis syndrome). Pathogenic variants in the same gene are a well-established cause of familial thoracic aortic aneurysms and dissections, reflecting the critical role of smooth-muscle contractile proteins in both visceral and vascular tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALX1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ALX1 homeobox gene cause autosomal-recessive frontonasal dysplasia type 3. ALX1 encodes a transcription factor that directs migration and patterning of neural-crest-derived mesenchyme in the frontonasal prominence. Functional studies in humans, mice, and zebrafish have shown that without normal ALX1 activity the midline face fails to fuse properly, producing severe hypertelorism and median clefts. Thus, ALX1 is both the gene mutated in this subtype of frontonasal dysplasia and is indispensable for normal facial development." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal syndrome",
    "long_answer": "An ear pit (preauricular sinus) is a classic external-ear anomaly seen in Branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome, a congenital disorder caused most commonly by mutations in EYA1, SIX1, or SIX5 genes. Over 80–90 % of affected individuals display these characteristic ear pits, along with branchial cleft cysts, hearing loss, and renal malformations, making the presence of an ear pit a key diagnostic clue for BOR syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Elevated endostatin",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 21 increases dosage of the COL18A1 gene, leading to chronically high circulating levels of endostatin—a potent endogenous inhibitor of angiogenesis. This anti-angiogenic environment suppresses the retinal neovascularization and microvascular leakage that drive diabetic retinopathy, so individuals with Down syndrome rarely develop the complication even when they have diabetes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Ganser syndrome is not recognized as an independent, stand-alone diagnosis in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition (DSM-5). In the DSM-5 dissociative disorders chapter, the only specifically named disorders are dissociative identity disorder, dissociative amnesia, and depersonalization/derealization disorder. Rare or atypical presentations such as Ganser syndrome are mentioned only as illustrative examples that can be coded under “Other Specified Dissociative Disorder (OSDD)” (code 300.15) or “Unspecified Dissociative Disorder.” Therefore, while clinicians may record Ganser-type presentations within an OSDD designation, the syndrome itself is not explicitly listed or coded as a separate disorder in the DSM-5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatophytid lesions",
    "long_answer": "In some patients with ringworm (dermatophyte) infections, circulating fungal antigens provoke a delayed-type hypersensitivity response. This induced sensitization produces secondary, sterile eruptions known as dermatophytid (id) lesions—typically pruritic papulovesicular or eczematous lesions that appear at sites distant from the primary fungal focus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Halothane",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia is precipitated by depolarizing muscle relaxants and halogenated volatile anesthetics, with halothane being the classic and most notorious trigger. In genetically susceptible patients (often carrying RYR1 mutations), exposure to halothane can cause uncontrolled skeletal-muscle calcium release, leading to hypermetabolism, muscle rigidity, hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis. Therefore, halothane—and other volatile agents of its class—must be avoided in these individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclophosphamide",
    "long_answer": "In rheumatoid vasculitis, life-threatening or severe organ system involvement (e.g., neuropathy, gut or renal ischemia) is managed with aggressive immunosuppression. The standard agent used is cyclophosphamide—usually given in high-dose pulses together with corticosteroids—because this alkylating drug potently suppresses immune-mediated necrotizing vasculitis and improves survival in severe systemic disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT4 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the WNT4 gene have been repeatedly documented in women with Mayer–Rokitansky–Küster–Hauser syndrome (Müllerian agenesis). WNT4 encodes a signaling protein essential for development of the Müllerian ducts; disruptive variants impair duct formation, leading to congenital absence of the uterus and upper vagina and, in some cases, hyperandrogenism. Thus, a WNT4 mutation is the best-established genetic lesion associated with Müllerian agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "GMS syndrome is defined by the triad of goniodysgenesis (an anterior-segment eye malformation that predisposes to congenital glaucoma), intellectual disability (historically called mental retardation), and short stature. Tall stature is not a recognized feature of this disorder in the medical literature. Therefore, the statement that GMS syndrome is characterized by goniodysgenesis, intellectual disability, and tall stature is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FUS-DDIT3 fusion",
    "long_answer": "Most myxoid liposarcomas harbor the t(12;16)(q13;p11) chromosomal translocation, which joins the FUS (TLS) gene on 16p11 to the DDIT3 (CHOP) gene on 12q13. The resulting FUS-DDIT3 chimeric transcription factor dysregulates adipocytic differentiation and is considered the central oncogenic driver of myxoid liposarcoma pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular septal defect",
    "long_answer": "In levo-transposition (congenitally corrected transposition) of the great arteries, the lesion most frequently found in association is a ventricular septal defect; studies show that 60–80 % of patients with L-TGA have a VSD, making it the commonest accompanying cardiac defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wenckebach phenomenon",
    "long_answer": "In second-degree atrioventricular (AV) block type I, also called Mobitz I, there is a characteristic progressive prolongation of the PR interval with each successive beat until one atrial impulse fails to conduct to the ventricles (a ‘dropped’ QRS complex). This repeating pattern is termed the Wenckebach phenomenon."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tryptophan",
    "long_answer": "In Type III properdin deficiency a single-base substitution in the CFP gene replaces a critical arginine residue with tryptophan. The bulky, hydrophobic tryptophan disrupts the thrombospondin-type repeat fold of properdin, so the protein is present but functionally inactive, producing the characteristic dysfunctional phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Collagen",
    "long_answer": "Connective tissue diseases (often termed “collagen-vascular” disorders) chiefly involve damage, autoimmunity against, or genetic defects in collagen—the main structural protein that gives strength and integrity to skin, tendons, ligaments, blood vessels, and other connective tissues. Whether through autoimmune attack (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, scleroderma) or inherited mutations (e.g., Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta), dysfunction of collagen is the central molecular event underlying these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gabapentin",
    "long_answer": "Gabapentin, an anticonvulsant that binds the α2δ-subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, is routinely prescribed for partial epilepsy and a variety of neuropathic pain conditions. Case reports and small series have shown that it reduces peripheral nerve hyperexcitability, making it an effective symptomatic treatment for cramp fasciculation syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal oncocytoma",
    "long_answer": "Renal oncocytoma is a benign renal tumor that originates from the intercalated (acid-secreting) cells of the collecting ducts. Histologically it is composed of oncocytic cells rich in mitochondria, reflecting its derivation from these specialized cells in the kidney’s collecting system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urology",
    "long_answer": "Renal cell carcinoma is a malignancy of the kidney that is primarily managed surgically with partial or radical nephrectomy; these operations and the overall oncologic care of genitourinary tumors fall within the scope of urology. Therefore, a urologist (often a urologic oncologist) is the specialist most commonly responsible for treating renal cell carcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Scuba diving",
    "long_answer": "During scuba diving the external ambient pressure rises and falls rapidly with changes in depth. These large, rapid pressure shifts can create barotrauma that forces perilymph through the oval or round window, rupturing these membranes and producing a perilymphatic (labyrinthine) fistula."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPD (ERCC2)",
    "long_answer": "XPD, encoded by the ERCC2 gene, is a 5′→3′ DNA helicase within the TFIIH complex that unwinds damaged DNA during nucleotide-excision repair. Loss-of-function mutations in XPD cause xeroderma pigmentosum (XP group D), and other hypomorphic alleles of the same gene produce progeroid disorders such as trichothiodystrophy or XP/Cockayne-overlap syndromes that show marked premature aging. Hence XPD is the DNA-repair protein linked to both xeroderma pigmentosum and premature aging."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNA",
    "long_answer": "Chromosomal deletions, such as a deletion on the long arm (q) of chromosome 2, represent loss of a segment of chromosomal material. Human chromosomes are made of deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), so the genetic abnormality involves missing stretches of DNA sequences and the genes they contain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD30",
    "long_answer": "Both lymphomatoid papulosis type A and anaplastic large-cell lymphoma belong to the spectrum of CD30-positive T-cell lymphoproliferative disorders. Immunohistochemical staining shows strong membrane/cytoplasmic expression of the activation marker CD30 (Ki-1 antigen) in the atypical large lymphoid cells of each condition, making CD30 positivity a shared diagnostic feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Plain radiography",
    "long_answer": "Chondrocalcinosis—the deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals in articular cartilage—is most readily detected on plain radiographic X-ray images, where the calcified cartilage appears as linear or punctate densities. Conventional radiography therefore serves as the standard diagnostic method to identify chondrocalcinosis in calcium pyrophosphate deposition (CPPD) disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "McCune–Albright syndrome results from post-zygotic activating mutations in the GNAS gene, which encodes the stimulatory G-protein α-subunit (Gsα). The GNAS locus is situated on the long arm of chromosome 20 at band 20q13.3, making chromosome 20 the chromosomal location of the gene whose mutation leads to the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Pyroluria (also called "kryptopyrroluria" or the "mauve‐factor") is a condition promoted mainly within orthomolecular or alternative‐medicine circles, in which excess urinary pyrroles are said to deplete vitamin B6 and zinc and thereby cause a variety of psychiatric or behavioural problems. The entity is not recognized in ICD-10/11, DSM-5, or any major clinical classification system, and there are no validated diagnostic criteria or standardized laboratory assays for it.

With regard to attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), authoritative consensus statements and evidence-based clinical guidelines (e.g., American Academy of Pediatrics, National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Canadian ADHD Practice Guidelines) do not cite pyroluria as an etiologic factor, diagnostic marker, or treatment consideration. Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of ADHD pathophysiology and treatment likewise make no mention of pyroluria, and there are no high-quality, peer-reviewed controlled studies demonstrating a reproducible association between urinary pyrrole levels and ADHD symptoms.

Most available literature linking “pyroluria” to ADHD consists of anecdotal reports, uncontrolled case series, or publications in non-indexed journals, none of which meet the methodological standards required for medical consensus. Consequently, the claim that pyroluria is linked to ADHD is not accepted by mainstream, evidence-based medicine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal facial skin",
    "long_answer": "In Cyprus facial neuromusculoskeletal (Setleis) syndrome, the ‘mephistophelian’ appearance refers to bilateral, atrophic scar-like lesions over the temples; thus, the temporal facial skin is the area characteristically affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calcitonin gene-related peptide",
    "long_answer": "Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from parafollicular C-cells that over-express the calcitonin gene. Alternative splicing of this gene yields calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), a potent vasodilatory neuropeptide. Intermittent surges of CGRP enter the systemic circulation, producing marked peripheral vasodilation and the characteristic flushing episodes seen in patients with medullary thyroid cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXA11",
    "long_answer": "During Müllerian duct development, region-specific expression of HOX genes patterns the future female reproductive tract. The homeobox gene HOXA11 is expressed in the lower uterine segment and developing cervix; mutations or dysregulation of HOXA11 have been linked to forms of Müllerian agenesis (e.g., MRKH), in which cervical structure is absent or malformed. Thus, HOXA11 is the gene whose disruption affects the cervix in Müllerian agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fallopian tubes",
    "long_answer": "Adult Enterobius vermicularis (pinworms) can migrate from the perianal region into the vagina, through the uterus, and as far as the fallopian tubes. Their presence in the tubes elicits a marked inflammatory reaction, producing salpingitis and, in some reported cases, granulomatous lesions. Thus, the fallopian tubes are a recognized site that pinworms can reach and inflame in the female reproductive tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Darbepoetin alfa",
    "long_answer": "Darbepoetin alfa is a hyper-glycosylated erythropoiesis-stimulating agent used to manage anemia in patients with myelodysplastic syndromes. Its additional carbohydrate chains prolong circulation, giving it a serum half-life roughly two to three times longer than that of epoetin alpha, which allows less frequent dosing while stimulating red-blood-cell production."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atrial fibrillation",
    "long_answer": "Graves’ disease–induced hyperthyroidism raises circulating thyroid hormone levels, which enhance β-adrenergic sensitivity and increase atrial automaticity and conduction velocity. This sympathetic overactivity predisposes to rapid, disorganized atrial electrical activity, making atrial fibrillation the most frequent cardiac complication of hyperthyroidism, occurring in roughly 10–20 % of affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Watery discharge",
    "long_answer": "Viral (especially adenoviral) conjunctivitis typically produces a clear, watery ocular discharge and is characteristically accompanied by pre-auricular lymphadenopathy, i.e., swelling of nearby lymph nodes. Thus the symptom ‘watery discharge’ is the form of conjunctival involvement that correlates with lymph node swelling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trisomy 9",
    "long_answer": "The condition caused by the presence of three copies of chromosome 9 is termed Trisomy 9 (also called Trisomy 9 syndrome). This chromosomal aneuploidy produces multiple congenital anomalies, one of which is micrognathia (an abnormally small mandible). The defining cytogenetic feature is complete or mosaic trisomy of chromosome 9, distinguishing it from other trisomies such as Patau (trisomy 13) or Edwards (trisomy 18)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Abciximab",
    "long_answer": "Abciximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody Fab fragment that binds with high affinity to the platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin αIIbβ3) receptor. By occupying this receptor, it prevents fibrinogen (and von Willebrand factor) from binding, thereby blocking the final common pathway of platelet aggregation and exerting its antiplatelet effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈85%",
    "long_answer": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is the most prevalent leukemia among adults in Western countries. Population-based data (e.g., SEER 2012-2018) show a 5-year relative survival rate of roughly 85 %, reflecting the generally indolent nature of CLL and advances in targeted therapies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Supportive care",
    "long_answer": "Because there is no definitive curative therapy for Gorham-Stout (Gorham’s) disease, management focuses on controlling pain, preventing complications, and maintaining function. Therefore, treatment is primarily supportive care, with interventions such as analgesics, physical therapy, and other symptom-directed measures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Warfarin",
    "long_answer": "X-linked recessive chondrodysplasia punctata (CDPX1) is caused by deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase E. Coumarin-derivative anticoagulants such as warfarin are known to inhibit arylsulfatase E activity; when taken during pregnancy they can produce the same skeletal phenotype (warfarin embryopathy) characterised by stippled epiphyses and nasal hypoplasia.  Thus, warfarin is the medication that can inhibit the enzyme associated with CDPX1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transmission electron microscope",
    "long_answer": "Definitive diagnosis of microvillus inclusion disease requires visualization of characteristic intracellular microvillus inclusions within enterocytes. These inclusions are only resolvable at the ultrastructural level, so a transmission electron microscope is employed to examine intestinal-biopsy specimens and confirm the presence of the diagnostic microvillus inclusions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Photopsia",
    "long_answer": "Acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy often presents with photopsia—brief flashes of light perceived by the patient. Photopsia is likewise a classic warning sign of retinal detachment, caused by mechanical stimulation of the retina as it separates. Therefore, the overlapping symptom between these two ocular conditions is photopsia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transforming growth factor-β1",
    "long_answer": "Transforming growth factor-β1 (TGF-β1) is consistently found in high levels within Peyronie’s disease plaques and is regarded as the principal profibrotic cytokine driving the disorder. TGF-β1 promotes fibroblast activation, extracellular matrix deposition, and collagen cross-linking, making it a central mediator of fibrosis in multiple tissues and the most prominently overexpressed cytokine in Peyronie’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSM-III-R",
    "long_answer": "The American Psychiatric Association’s revised third edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-III-R, published in 1987) was the first to list the “blood-injury-injection” subtype of simple (specific) phobia. This designation formally recognized fear of needles and injections—‘needle phobia’—as an official specific phobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Edwards syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18) is a rare chromosomal disorder in which affected infants typically have characteristic low-set, posteriorly rotated ears and a high incidence of congenital heart disease (e.g., ventricular septal defects, patent ductus arteriosus). These features are part of the well-described clinical constellation resulting from the presence of an extra copy of chromosome 18."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemochromatosis is an autosomal-recessive disorder (often due to HFE mutations) in which excessive intestinal iron absorption leads to systemic iron overload. Iron deposition in the anterior pituitary and in the gonads disrupts gonadotropin secretion and steroidogenesis, frequently resulting in secondary hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "The microphthalmia-associated transcription factor (MITF) is normally expressed in retinal pigment epithelium cells, where it controls genes required for melanogenesis and early eye morphogenesis. Experimental loss or mutation of MITF abolishes its expression in the RPE, disrupting normal ocular development and producing markedly small eyes—i.e., microphthalmia—in both animal models and affected humans."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pancreatic α cells",
    "long_answer": "Within the pancreatic islets of Langerhans, the α (alpha) cells synthesize and secrete the peptide hormone glucagon, which raises blood glucose levels by promoting hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Indomethacin therapy",
    "long_answer": "A short postoperative course of the NSAID indomethacin (e.g., 25 mg three times daily for several weeks) is routinely recommended after total hip arthroplasty because it inhibits prostaglandin-mediated osteogenic activity, thereby markedly reducing the incidence of heterotopic ossification around the hip joint."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The classical LIS1 gene, whose official symbol is PAFAH1B1, is mapped to chromosome 17p13.3. Deletions or pathogenic variants in PAFAH1B1 (LIS1) are a well-established cause of type-1 (classical) lissencephaly and Miller-Dieker lissencephaly syndrome. No portion of this gene resides on chromosome 2; chromosome 2 harbors other loci that can cause cortical malformations (e.g., RELN on 2q24 for lissencephaly with cerebellar hypoplasia), but not LIS1. Therefore, stating that the LIS1 gene is located on chromosome 2 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Will Bayley",
    "long_answer": "British table-tennis player Will Bayley, who won Paralympic gold in the men’s singles Class 7 event at the 2016 Rio Games, was born with arthrogryposis multiplex congenita—a congenital condition characterized by joint contractures and muscle weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B6",
    "long_answer": "Isoniazid therapy can produce peripheral neuropathy manifested as numbness, because the drug forms a complex with pyridoxal-5′-phosphate and increases urinary loss of pyridoxine. Supplementing with Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) restores pyridoxine levels, prevents or reverses the neuropathy, and is routinely co-prescribed with isoniazid for this reason."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TGM1 gene, which encodes epidermal transglutaminase-1, are a well-established cause of autosomal recessive congenital ichthyoses. Pathogenic TGM1 variants have been documented in patients presenting clinically as lamellar ichthyosis as well as in those with congenital (non-bullous) ichthyosiform erythroderma, linking this single gene to both disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperthyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Excess thyroid hormone in hyperthyroidism stimulates osteoclast-mediated bone resorption and shortens the bone-remodeling cycle. The resulting high bone turnover leads to net loss of bone mineral density, making hyperthyroidism a well-recognized endocrine cause of secondary osteoporosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Galactosemia",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia—caused by deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase—leads to accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate and galactitol after lactose ingestion. Toxic build-up damages the liver, kidneys and brain, producing vomiting, jaundice and, if untreated, marked failure to thrive in the newborn. Eliminating galactose/lactose from the diet prevents the growth failure and other life-threatening complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wenckebach phenomenon",
    "long_answer": "Karel Frederik Wenckebach described the progressive prolongation of AV nodal conduction culminating in a dropped ventricular beat—now called the Wenckebach phenomenon (also termed Mobitz type I or Luciani-Wenckebach second-degree AV block)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemoglobin S (HbS)",
    "long_answer": "The Glu6Val point mutation in the β-globin chain produces hemoglobin S in red blood cells. Individuals carrying one HbS allele (sickle-cell trait) are significantly protected against severe Plasmodium falciparum malaria because the altered hemoglobin impairs parasite growth and enhances immune clearance of infected erythrocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological data show that the leading causes of traumatic spinal cord injury worldwide are motor-vehicle crashes, falls (particularly in the elderly and in construction settings), acts of violence (gunshot or stab wounds), and sports/recreational mishaps such as diving accidents. All of these mechanisms occur largely outside the body and can be significantly reduced through well-established preventive measures—seat-belt and helmet use, traffic-safety enforcement, environmental modifications to prevent falls, workplace safety regulations, firearm-injury prevention strategies, and sports safety guidelines. Because the majority of spinal cord injuries arise from these external, modifiable events rather than from unavoidable internal disease processes, the most common causes are considered preventable by public-health authorities (e.g., WHO, CDC, National Spinal Cord Injury Statistical Center)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parietal lobe cortex",
    "long_answer": "Corticobasal degeneration is characterized pathologically and on MRI by marked, unilateral-predominant loss of gray matter in the parietal cortex (often extending to adjacent posterior frontal areas). This asymmetric parietal atrophy corresponds to the lateralized motor and cortical sensory deficits that typify the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lameness",
    "long_answer": "Osteochondritis (commonly osteochondritis dissecans/osteochondrosis) disrupts normal cartilage and subchondral bone in developing joints such as the shoulder, elbow, stifle, and hock. The resulting pain, inflammation, and joint incongruity frequently manifest clinically as lameness, one of the most common orthopedic conditions observed in dogs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Culex mosquitoes",
    "long_answer": "Pogosta disease is caused by Sindbis virus, an alphavirus that circulates between passerine birds and mosquitoes. In Finland and other parts of northern Europe the virus is mainly transmitted to humans by mosquitoes of the genus Culex (especially Culex torrentium/Culex pipiens), making Culex mosquitoes the principal vector for Pogosta disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurology",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile primary lateral sclerosis is a rare upper-motor-neuron form of motor neuron disease. Diagnosis, monitoring of spasticity and weakness, and coordination of symptomatic therapies are managed by a neurologist—often one with neuromuscular or pediatric-neurology expertise—making neurology the primary medical specialty involved in its treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Henoch–Schönlein purpura",
    "long_answer": "Henoch–Schönlein purpura (IgA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis) frequently involves the kidneys; IgA immune-complex deposition in glomerular mesangium produces an IgA nephropathy–like picture that can manifest as hematuric or nephritic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) commonly presents with widened cranial sutures and a markedly large or late-closing anterior fontanelle. The extra chromosome 21 alters bone and connective-tissue development, delaying membranous ossification of the skull; this characteristic finding is routinely listed among the dysmorphic features used to clinically recognize the genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antibiotics",
    "long_answer": "Hyper-IgE (Job) syndrome predisposes patients to recurrent bacterial, particularly Staphylococcus aureus, infections of the skin and lungs. Because no curative therapy exists, the cornerstone of management is continuous or prophylactic antibiotic therapy—most commonly agents such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole—to prevent and control these infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spondylodiscitis",
    "long_answer": "Spondylodiscitis denotes concurrent inflammation or infection of the vertebral bodies (spondylitis) and the adjacent intervertebral disc space (discitis). It is the accepted medical term when both structures are involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Potassium bicarbonate",
    "long_answer": "When hypokalemia co-exists with metabolic acidosis, the preferred supplement is potassium bicarbonate because it supplies potassium while its bicarbonate anion directly counteracts the acidotic state, helping to restore both serum potassium and systemic acid-base balance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cholelithiasis",
    "long_answer": "Patients with alkaptonuria frequently accumulate homogentisic acid in bile, which polymerizes and precipitates, leading to pigmented gallstones. Thus, the development of gallbladder stones (cholelithiasis) is a well-recognized complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Draining lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "In allergic contact dermatitis, cutaneous lymphatic vessels carry hapten-loaded Langerhans cells/dendritic cells and soluble allergen from the epidermis to the regional (draining) lymph nodes. Within these lymph nodes the dendritic cells present processed antigen to naïve T-lymphocytes, initiating their activation, clonal expansion, and differentiation into allergen-specific effector and memory T cells. This priming step in the draining lymph nodes is essential for generating the delayed-type (type IV) hypersensitivity response that manifests clinically as allergic contact dermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Andersen-Tawil syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Andersen-Tawil syndrome is an autosomal dominant KCNJ2 channelopathy whose clinical triad includes frequent ventricular extrasystoles and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia causing syncopal episodes, episodic muscle weakness, and characteristic dysmorphic features such as a low anterior hairline, micrognathia, and short stature. This combination of cardiac arrhythmia with a low anterior hairline makes Andersen-Tawil syndrome the established genetic disorder fitting the description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLURP1",
    "long_answer": "Mal de Meleda, an autosomal-recessive palmoplantar keratoderma, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLURP1 gene (Secreted Ly6/U-PAR-related protein 1) located on chromosome 8q24.3. Pathogenic variants in SLURP1 impair epidermal homeostasis and lead to the characteristic diffuse thickening of the palms and soles observed in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sterile milk content",
    "long_answer": "A galactocele is a closed retention cyst filled only with breast milk. Human milk inside the obstructed duct remains sterile and contains bacteriostatic factors (e.g., lactoferrin, lysozyme, secretory IgA). Because bacteria neither enter nor thrive in this medium, galactoceles very rarely become infected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral nervous system",
    "long_answer": "In Infantile Refsum disease defective peroxisomal biogenesis impairs lipid metabolism needed for Schwann-cell myelin formation, producing marked hypomyelination of peripheral nerves; nerve conduction studies show slow velocities and pathology reveals thin or absent myelin sheaths in the peripheral nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diarrhea",
    "long_answer": "Patients with MPI-CDG (mannose-phosphate isomerase congenital disorder of glycosylation) frequently experience chronic diarrhea due to intestinal involvement and protein-losing enteropathy. Diarrhea leads to excessive fluid loss from the gastrointestinal tract, making it a well-recognized cause of dehydration in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mexiletine",
    "long_answer": "LQT3 results from gain-of-function mutations in the cardiac sodium channel gene SCN5A, producing a persistent late Na⁺ current and prolonging repolarisation. The class IB anti-arrhythmic mexiletine selectively blocks this late sodium current, thereby shortening the QT interval and reducing arrhythmic risk. Consequently, mexiletine is the medication specifically employed to treat the LQT3 subtype of Romano–Ward syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral mucosa",
    "long_answer": "Oral lichen planus is a chronic inflammatory, immune-mediated disorder that specifically involves the stratified squamous epithelium lining the mouth—tongue, buccal mucosa, gingiva, and other intra-oral surfaces—rather than skin or other organs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Voiding cystourethrogram",
    "long_answer": "Posterior urethral valves create an obstructing membrane in the posterior urethra of male infants. The gold-standard test is a voiding (micturating) cystourethrogram, which fills the bladder with contrast and images the urethra during voiding. This study reveals the characteristic dilated posterior urethra and valve leaflets, definitively diagnosing posterior urethral valve disorder in newborn boys."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Familial isolated vitamin E deficiency is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TTPA gene, which encodes the α-tocopherol transfer protein. Because disease manifests only when both alleles are altered and carriers are asymptomatic, it follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3q13.3 deletion",
    "long_answer": "In one reported Primrose syndrome case, cytogenetic analysis revealed an interstitial microdeletion at chromosome 3q13.3. This segment includes the ZBTB20 locus, now known to be the critical gene whose haploinsufficiency or de novo point mutation causes Primrose syndrome, thereby linking the 3q13.3 deletion with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Addison’s disease",
    "long_answer": "In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison’s disease) cortisol production is lost, removing the negative feedback on the pituitary. This drives markedly increased secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). Addison’s disease is therefore an endocrine dysfunction linked to acanthosis nigricans that is specifically characterized by elevated ACTH levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar capillary dysplasia with misalignment of pulmonary veins (ACDMPV) is most often caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions involving the transcription factor gene FOXF1. The FOXF1 gene is situated at cytogenetic band 16q24.1 on human chromosome 16, so the pathogenic variants responsible for this disorder map to chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Immunoglobulin D (IgD)",
    "long_answer": "Mevalonate kinase deficiency manifests clinically as Hyper-IgD Syndrome. A distinguishing laboratory feature of this condition is persistently elevated serum levels of immunoglobulin D (IgD), often exceeding 100 IU/mL, making IgD elevation a characteristic biomarker for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Selenium sulfide",
    "long_answer": "Selenium sulfide is routinely formulated as 1-2.5 % medicated shampoo to eradicate the fungal infection (Piedraia hortae) responsible for black piedra. Animal studies have shown that chronic exposure to selenium sulfide produces hepatocellular carcinomas in mice and rats, leading regulatory agencies (e.g., IARC, OSHA) to classify it as a potential human carcinogen. Thus, the same compound used therapeutically for black piedra carries a recognized carcinogenic hazard."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Combined oral contraceptives",
    "long_answer": "Estrogen-containing combined oral contraceptive pills markedly raise the risk of thrombo-embolic events. In people who already have migraine with aura—a condition that independently increases ischemic stroke risk—the added estrogen further elevates cerebrovascular risk. Therefore, combined oral contraceptives (those with both estrogen and progestin) are considered contraindicated in this population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈25 per 100 000 inhabitants",
    "long_answer": "Population-based analyses of German statutory health-insurance claims data show that systemic scleroderma (systemic sclerosis) affects roughly 23–25 individuals per 100 000 residents, equivalent to about 1 case in every 4 000 people in Germany."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "MacDermot-Winter syndrome is an extremely rare, X-linked intellectual-developmental disorder that has been described in only a small number of families worldwide. Fewer than a dozen affected males have been documented in the medical literature, and no population-based incidence or prevalence data exist because cases are so scarce. Consequently, it is considered an ultra-rare condition and is not common among live births."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Capillary",
    "long_answer": "Telangiectasia represents a persistent, focal dilation of small superficial blood vessels—most commonly the capillaries (and sometimes adjacent venules). When these capillaries become widened and tortuous, they appear as fine red ‘spider-like’ lines on skin or mucous membranes, which is the hallmark of telangiectasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "White matter",
    "long_answer": "Diffusion-tensor MRI studies have shown reduced fractional anisotropy and other diffusion changes in multiple cerebral white-matter tracts (e.g., posterior corona radiata, superior longitudinal fasciculus) in children diagnosed with sensory processing disorder, indicating that the microstructure of white matter—not gray matter—is abnormally altered in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Connective tissue disorder",
    "long_answer": "Larsen syndrome is classified as a connective tissue disorder: mutations in the FLNB gene disrupt filamin-B, a cytoskeletal protein critical for the integrity of connective tissues, leading to joint dislocations, skeletal abnormalities, and characteristic facial features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malonyl-CoA decarboxylase",
    "long_answer": "Loss of the mitochondrial enzyme malonyl-CoA decarboxylase (MCD) blocks conversion of malonyl-CoA to acetyl-CoA. The excess malonyl-CoA is hydrolyzed to malonic acid, producing marked malonic aciduria. Malonic acid is a potent inhibitor of succinate dehydrogenase (complex II) and, together with malonyl-CoA–mediated inhibition of carnitine palmitoyl-transferase I, interferes with β-oxidation and electron-transport activity, thereby compromising oxidative phosphorylation and cellular ATP generation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gout",
    "long_answer": "During fasting, patients with glycogen storage disease type VI break down increased amounts of purine nucleotides, leading to hyperuricemia. The excess uric acid can crystallize in joints, producing gout—a painful inflammatory arthritis that is a recognized complication of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Hypoparathyroidism is a well-known secondary cause of bilateral basal ganglia and other intracranial calcifications. Chronic deficiency of parathyroid hormone leads to hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia, promoting calcium-phosphate deposition in brain tissues; on neuroimaging this pattern is virtually indistinguishable from the calcifications seen in Primary Familial Brain Calcification (Fahr disease)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ionizing radiation",
    "long_answer": "Reports of femoral-facial (femoral hypoplasia–unusual facies) syndrome consistently list maternal exposure to ionizing radiation—such as diagnostic or therapeutic X-rays—during early pregnancy as a recognized teratogenic risk factor. The DNA-damaging effects of ionizing radiation on the developing embryo can disturb limb-bud and craniofacial morphogenesis, contributing to the anomalies that characterize facial femoral syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hematology",
    "long_answer": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is an inherited red-blood-cell enzyme defect that produces chronic hemolytic anemia. Diagnosis, monitoring of hemoglobin levels, management with transfusions, splenectomy, or emerging enzyme-activating drugs all fall under the expertise of hematologists, the specialists who treat blood disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Appendiceal lumen",
    "long_answer": "Acute appendicitis typically starts when the appendiceal lumen—the hollow interior passage of the vermiform appendix—is obstructed by a fecalith, lymphoid hyperplasia, or other material. Obstruction of this lumen causes mucus accumulation, increased intraluminal pressure, ischemia, bacterial overgrowth, and ultimately inflammation of the appendix."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "In Langerhans cell histiocytosis, an MRI of the head can simultaneously show the three components of the Hand-Schüller-Christian triad: lytic skull lesions, orbital/orbital apex involvement producing exophthalmos, and hypothalamic-pituitary stalk infiltration responsible for central diabetes insipidus. Because MRI provides high-resolution soft-tissue contrast as well as bone detail with appropriate sequences, it is the preferred imaging technique to detect this classic triad."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Both mercury poisoning and long-term exposure to environmental noise have been shown to raise systemic arterial blood pressure. Mercury can induce oxidative stress and autonomic dysfunction, promoting vasoconstriction and leading to hypertension. Chronic noise activates the sympathetic–adrenal system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, similarly causing sustained elevations in blood pressure. Thus, the cardiovascular condition common to both exposures is hypertension (high blood pressure)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Species",
    "long_answer": "Bothrops lanceolatus is the binomial name of the Martinique lancehead. In the Linnaean hierarchy a two-part (genus + specific epithet) Latin name designates the species level; therefore Bothrops lanceolatus occupies the taxonomic rank of species."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trench fever",
    "long_answer": "Body lice (Pediculus humanus corporis) transmit the bacterium Bartonella quintana. This infection, known as trench fever, is frequently reported in modern urban homeless populations where body-louse infestation is common. The lice become infected by feeding on an infected person’s blood and pass the bacteria to others through their feces, which enter the skin via scratching; outbreaks of trench fever have been documented repeatedly among homeless individuals worldwide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IgG4 antibodies, Golgi apparatus",
    "long_answer": "Pemphigus foliaceus is mediated by pathogenic IgG autoantibodies, with the IgG4 subclass being the dominant and most pathogenic isotype. After synthesis in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, IgG4 molecules are conveyed to and selectively sorted in the Golgi apparatus—specifically the trans-Golgi network—before being packaged into secretory vesicles for extracellular release. These secreted IgG4 autoantibodies then bind desmoglein-1 on keratinocytes, driving the autoimmune blistering characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Valproic acid",
    "long_answer": "Valproic acid (sodium valproate) is the first-line broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug for myoclonic seizures in generalized epilepsies, such as juvenile myoclonic epilepsy. It raises brain GABA levels, stabilizes neuronal firing, and is consistently recommended in treatment guidelines for controlling myoclonic jerks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EMA/EP chemotherapy",
    "long_answer": "An interval of more than 48 months from the antecedent pregnancy places a placental site trophoblastic tumor in a high-risk category. International Society for the Study of Trophoblastic Diseases and FIGO guidelines advise treating such high-risk or metastatic PSTT with the multi-agent, platinum–etoposide-based regimen EMA/EP (etoposide-methotrexate-actinomycin D / etoposide-cisplatin), because single-agent regimens are inadequate for this relatively chemoresistant tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Polydactyly",
    "long_answer": "Polydactyly is a congenital genetic disorder in which mutations affecting limb patterning genes (such as SHH or GLI3) lead to the development of supernumerary digits, resulting in more than the usual five fingers (or toes) on a hand or foot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B27",
    "long_answer": "The human leukocyte antigen HLA-B27 allele is present in about 90 % of patients with ankylosing spondylitis, making it a characteristic genetic marker for this spondyloarthropathy. In contrast, diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) shows no association with HLA-B27, distinguishing the two conditions genetically and pathogenetically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperopia",
    "long_answer": "Hyperopia, also called farsightedness, is a refractive error in which near objects appear blurry while distant objects remain clearer. Although genetic factors are most common, systemic conditions such as diabetes mellitus can alter the eye’s refractive state by changing lens shape or thickness and thereby precipitate or exacerbate hyperopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Benign prognosis",
    "long_answer": "Dubin–Johnson syndrome causes lifelong, fluctuating conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to impaired hepatic secretion of conjugated bilirubin, but it does not damage the liver or other organs. Affected individuals remain asymptomatic aside from mild jaundice and have a normal life expectancy, so the overall prognosis is considered benign."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heritable connective tissue disorder",
    "long_answer": "Hypermobility spectrum disorder belongs to the group of heritable connective tissue disorders, the same category that includes conditions such as the various types of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. These disorders are characterized by genetically based abnormalities in collagen or related extracellular-matrix components that lead to systemic manifestations like joint hypermobility, skin findings, and tissue fragility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Langerhans cells",
    "long_answer": "Congenital self-healing reticulohistiocytosis is a benign variant of Langerhans cell histiocytosis. The disorder is characterized by clonal proliferation and activation of epidermal antigen-presenting Langerhans cells (CD1a⁺, langerin⁺ dendritic cells), which leads to the transient cutaneous lesions that spontaneously regress. Thus, the primary immune system component activated is the population of Langerhans cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "Cirrhosis—especially alcoholic liver cirrhosis—is a chronic liver disease in which fibrosis and regenerative nodules distort hepatic architecture. This increases intrahepatic vascular resistance, making cirrhosis the most common cause of portal hypertension and a well-known long-term complication of chronic alcohol abuse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ichthyosis",
    "long_answer": "In Conan Doyle’s story Holmes discovers that Godfrey Emsworth was not a leper but had ichthyosis—a non-contagious ‘pseudo-leprosy’ in which the skin becomes thickened, dry and scaly, giving a blanched, white appearance that can be mistaken for Hansen’s disease. This benign dermatological condition explains Emsworth’s pallor without the dangers associated with true leprosy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wearing protective footwear",
    "long_answer": "Hookworm larvae hatch in soil contaminated with human feces and penetrate bare skin, usually through the feet. Consistently wearing shoes or other protective footwear creates a physical barrier between skin and contaminated soil, interrupting the parasite’s transmission cycle and markedly reducing new infections in areas lacking adequate sanitation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Connective tissue",
    "long_answer": "Larsen syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants in the FLNB gene, which encodes filamin-B, a cytoskeletal protein essential for the structure and function of connective tissue—particularly cartilage and the supporting tissues around joints. The defect therefore primarily compromises connective tissue, leading to the characteristic multiple joint dislocations, craniofacial anomalies, and other skeletal abnormalities seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gemcitabine",
    "long_answer": "Gemcitabine is a nucleoside analogue chemotherapy agent that is routinely combined with cisplatin (the GC regimen) as first-line treatment for advanced or metastatic transitional cell (urothelial) carcinoma. Clinical trials showed that gemcitabine-cisplatin provides response rates and overall survival comparable to older MVAC regimens but with significantly less toxicity, so guidelines list gemcitabine plus cisplatin as a standard option for this disease setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adipose tissue lipolysis",
    "long_answer": "In fatty liver disease—particularly non-alcoholic fatty liver disease—most hepatic triglyceride originates from free fatty acids released by white adipose tissue. Insulin-resistant, inflamed adipose tissue undergoes heightened lipolysis, flooding the liver with fatty acids that are re-esterified and stored, leading to hepatic steatosis. Thus, dysfunctional adipose tissue is a primary upstream driver of fat accumulation in the liver."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase",
    "long_answer": "Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) occurs when hepatic activity of the enzyme-protein uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD) is reduced. UROD normally converts uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III in the heme-biosynthetic pathway. Its deficiency leads to hepatic and cutaneous accumulation of uroporphyrins, producing the blistering photosensitivity typical of PCT. Because UROD is a liver-synthesised protein whose normal function is also needed for proper secretion of retinol-binding complexes, its deficiency disrupts both heme formation and systemic retinol (vitamin A) transport, linking the enzyme defect to the clinical and biochemical features seen in PCT."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phenylketonuria",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal-recessive inborn error of metabolism caused by deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase. Toxic accumulation of phenylalanine in the brain begins soon after birth and classically produces early neurologic signs, including irritability, seizures, and coarse or fine tremors in infants. Prompt dietary restriction of phenylalanine prevents these tremor manifestations, underscoring the genetic basis of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ampulla of Vater",
    "long_answer": "In annular pancreas a ring of pancreatic tissue encircles and narrows the second part of the duodenum. The constriction is usually proximal to the entry point of the common bile and pancreatic ducts, so the major duodenal papilla—also called the ampulla of Vater—lies just distal (below) the site of obstruction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TYR gene sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Oculocutaneous albinism type I is caused by pathogenic variants in the TYR gene; therefore, molecular genetic testing that sequences the TYR gene’s coding regions (and, if necessary, deletion/duplication analysis) is performed to establish the diagnosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neutrophils",
    "long_answer": "The Anaplasma species that affect ruminants, dogs, and horses is Anaplasma phagocytophilum; it multiplies within the cytoplasmic vacuoles of neutrophilic granulocytes. This tropism for neutrophils (rather than red blood cells or monocytes) underlies tick-borne fever in cattle and sheep, canine granulocytic anaplasmosis, and equine granulocytic anaplasmosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome is caused by a 22q11.2 microdeletion on chromosome 22. The deletion disrupts genes critical for development of the pharyngeal arches, leading to craniofacial anomalies, cleft palate and recurrent otitis media, which together predispose to conductive and sometimes sensorineural hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bloom syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Bloom syndrome is an autosomal-recessive chromosomal-instability disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the BLM helicase gene. The defective DNA-repair helicase leads to excessive chromosomal breakage and markedly elevated sister-chromatid exchange, producing genome instability. Because of this instability, affected individuals have a greatly increased lifetime risk of many malignancies, including leukemias, lymphomas and solid tumours."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRNP P102L",
    "long_answer": "The prototypical Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker (GSS) mutation is a proline-to-leucine substitution at codon 102 of the prion protein gene (PRNP P102L). This mutation causes the inherited prion disease characterized by early cerebellar degeneration, which manifests clinically as gait ataxia and dysarthria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chest X-ray",
    "long_answer": "In infantile Pompe disease, excessive glycogen deposition in the myocardium causes generalized cardiac enlargement. A standard postero-anterior chest X-ray is routinely used to visualise the cardiac silhouette, and an enlarged heart shadow on this radiograph is the classic, first-line means of detecting cardiomegaly in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "The original description of Combarros–Calleja–Leno syndrome involved several affected siblings born to clinically unaffected, non-consanguineous parents, with both males and females showing the same phenotype and no evidence of vertical (parent-to-child) transmission. This pattern—multiple affected children of unaffected parents, equal sex distribution, and absence of male-to-male transmission—best fits an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "YAP1",
    "long_answer": "Porocarcinomas characteristically harbor recurrent gene fusions involving YAP1 (e.g., YAP1-MAML2 or YAP1-NUTM1). These fusions drive strong nuclear accumulation of the Hippo-pathway transcription co-activator YAP1, and immunohistochemical detection of YAP1 has proven highly sensitive and specific for both eccrine poroma and its malignant counterpart, porocarcinoma. Consequently, the YAP1 protein family is now routinely used as a diagnostic marker to identify porocarcinoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vision loss",
    "long_answer": "When a glioma infiltrates or compresses the optic nerve, it disrupts transmission of visual signals from the eye to the brain, leading to loss or reduction of vision (visual acuity/field defects). Thus, affected patients commonly experience vision loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Body dysmorphic disorder",
    "long_answer": "Body dysmorphic disorder (BDD) is classified among the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Patients are preoccupied with imagined or slight defects in appearance, experience intrusive thoughts about these perceived flaws, and engage in repetitive or compulsive behaviors—such as mirror checking, excessive grooming, or camouflaging—to hide or fix them."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclooxygenase (COX)",
    "long_answer": "Aspirin and related non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs irreversibly acetylate the enzyme cyclooxygenase (also called prostaglandin-endoperoxide synthase, COX-1/COX-2). By blocking COX, the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H₂—the precursor of all prostaglandins—is prevented. This reduction in prostaglandin production, particularly of PGD₂, is why low-dose aspirin is often given with niacin therapy for hyperlipidemia to lessen prostaglandin-mediated flushing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging",
    "long_answer": "Pilocytic astrocytoma is usually confirmed radiologically with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). MRI provides high-resolution, multiplanar views of the brain, and with gadolinium contrast it characteristically shows a well-circumscribed, often cystic mass with an enhancing mural nodule—findings that are diagnostic for pilocytic astrocytoma and guide surgical planning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex, an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by pathogenic variants in TSC1 or TSC2, classically presents with a triad of (1) intellectual disability, (2) epilepsy/seizures, and (3) characteristic facial angiofibromas (formerly called adenoma sebaceum) that clinicians often mistake for acne or psoriatic skin lesions. The combination of neurological impairment and psoriasiform cutaneous findings makes tuberous sclerosis the recognized genetic syndrome described."
}
